[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4e8da80a-29dc-45d9-b6d7-c95c1fb98ddc
name: Protein Nuclear Import and Export
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:nucleus ! Nucleus
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
is_a: urn:agi-folder:p ! P
is_a: urn:agi-folder:proteins_turnover ! Proteins turnover
xref: PS:Description "Macromolecules greater than 40kDa are transported across the nuclear pore complexes (NPCs) actively using soluble transport factors or carrier molecules that cycle between the nucleus and cytoplasm. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Macromolecules greater than 40kDa are transported across the nuclear pore complexes (NPCs) actively using soluble transport factors or carrier molecules that cycle between the nucleus and cytoplasm. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4e8da80a-29dc-45d9-b6d7-c95c1fb98ddc"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4e8da80a-29dc-45d9-b6d7-c95c1fb98ddc" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "generic"
synonym: "Organ_System: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "generic"
synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Macromolecules greater than 40kDa are transported across the nuclear pore complexes (NPCs) actively using soluble transport factors or carrier molecules that cycle between the nucleus and cytoplasm.    Signaling description: NPCs are large macromolecular assemblies with eight-fold rotational symmetry that allow the transport of RNA and ribosomes from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. NPCs also allow transport of proteins, carbohydrates, signal molecules, and lipids into the nucleus. They are built from 30 different proteins called nucleoporins. The nuclear protein import process involves cargo-carrier import complex assembly in the cytoplasm, its translocation through NPC, disassembly of this complex in the nucleus, and recycling of importins. Most nuclear transport pathways use a homologous family of carrier molecules collectively called karyopherins, with import carriers called importins and export carriers called exportins. Many proteins are imported using importin beta (KPNB1), often using importin alpha (KPNA2) as an adaptor while some are imported using other importins such as TNPO1. Cargo proteins are targeted for nuclear import by short NLS (nuclear localization signal) sequence motifs. In the cytoplasm, cargo proteins that contain a classic NLS form an import complex with KPNB1-KPNA2 heterodimer, which facilitates movement through NPCs.    Outcome effects: In the nucleus, RAN binds to KPNB1 which dissociates and releases the cargo. Nucleoporins such as NUP50 catalyze cargo dissociation and are involved in importin recycling. RAN then binds to importins and is exported to the cytoplasm, where cytoplasmic RANGAP1 stimulates the dissociation of RAN from the importins and thereby releases them for another import cycle. Cytoplasmic RAN is imported into the nucleus by Gm10349. KPNA2 is recycled to the cytoplasm by its nuclear export factor, CSE1L. Many proteins are exported using XPO1; however pre-microRNA and tRNA are exported by XPO5 and XPOT. The nuclear export of mRNA is performed by NXF1/NXT1 heterodimer."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Macromolecules greater than 40kDa are transported across the nuclear pore complexes (NPCs) actively using soluble transport factors or carrier molecules that cycle between the nucleus and cytoplasm.    Signaling description: NPCs are large macromolecular assemblies with eight-fold rotational symmetry that allow the transport of RNA and ribosomes from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. NPCs also allow transport of proteins, carbohydrates, signal molecules, and lipids into the nucleus. They are built from 30 different proteins called nucleoporins. The nuclear protein import process involves cargo-carrier import complex assembly in the cytoplasm, its translocation through NPC, disassembly of this complex in the nucleus, and recycling of importins. Most nuclear transport pathways use a homologous family of carrier molecules collectively called karyopherins, with import carriers called importins and export carriers called exportins. Many proteins are imported using importin beta (KPNB1), often using importin alpha (KPNA2) as an adaptor while some are imported using other importins such as TNPO1. Cargo proteins are targeted for nuclear import by short NLS (nuclear localization signal) sequence motifs. In the cytoplasm, cargo proteins that contain a classic NLS form an import complex with KPNB1-KPNA2 heterodimer, which facilitates movement through NPCs.    Outcome effects: In the nucleus, RAN binds to KPNB1 which dissociates and releases the cargo. Nucleoporins such as NUP50 catalyze cargo dissociation and are involved in importin recycling. RAN then binds to importins and is exported to the cytoplasm, where cytoplasmic RANGAP1 stimulates the dissociation of RAN from the importins and thereby releases them for another import cycle. Cytoplasmic RAN is imported into the nucleus by Gm10349. KPNA2 is recycled to the cytoplasm by its nuclear export factor, CSE1L. Many proteins are exported using XPO1; however pre-microRNA and tRNA are exported by XPO5 and XPOT. The nuclear export of mRNA is performed by NXF1/NXT1 heterodimer." EXACT []

