xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ubiquitin-dependent proteolysis has a central role in cytosolic degradation of short-lived and regulatory proteins important in a variety of basic cellular processes.    Signaling description: The degradation of a protein by this pathway involves two steps including covalent attachment of multiple ubiquitin molecules to the target protein and degradation of the tagged protein by the 26S proteasome complex. Ubiquitin is a small protein of 76 amino acids encoded in mammals by four different genes including UBA52 and RPS27A, which code for a single copy of ubiquitin and UBB and UBC genes, which code for polyubiquitin precursor proteins. Proteins destined for degradation through ubiquitylation are first labeled by ubiquitin by the sequential action of a multi-enzymatic system consisting of ubiquitin-activating enzyme E1, E2 (ubiquitin-conjugating enzymes), and E3 (ubiquitin-protein ligase). E3 enzymes function as the substrate recognition modules of the system and are capable of interaction with both E2 and target protein. E3s mediate the covalent attachment of multiple ubiquitin moieties to an NH2 group of a lysine residue of the protein substrate to generate an isopeptide bond.    Outcome effects: Additional ubiquitin moieties may be transferred to the previously conjugated ubiquitin, resulting in a polyubiquitin chain formation. This chain is recognized by the proteasome and directs the target protein to the 26S proteasome complex for degradation. 26S proteasome is composed of 28 subunits to form a cylindrical structure. The 19S proteasome regulatory particle is cupped at each end of the 20S core proteasome to form the core of the 26S proteasome. Once ubiquitin-tagged, proteins bind to 19S proteasome regulatory particle of the proteasome and then enter the catalytic inner chamber."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ubiquitin-dependent proteolysis has a central role in cytosolic degradation of short-lived and regulatory proteins important in a variety of basic cellular processes.    Signaling description: The degradation of a protein by this pathway involves two steps including covalent attachment of multiple ubiquitin molecules to the target protein and degradation of the tagged protein by the 26S proteasome complex. Ubiquitin is a small protein of 76 amino acids encoded in mammals by four different genes including UBA52 and RPS27A, which code for a single copy of ubiquitin and UBB and UBC genes, which code for polyubiquitin precursor proteins. Proteins destined for degradation through ubiquitylation are first labeled by ubiquitin by the sequential action of a multi-enzymatic system consisting of ubiquitin-activating enzyme E1, E2 (ubiquitin-conjugating enzymes), and E3 (ubiquitin-protein ligase). E3 enzymes function as the substrate recognition modules of the system and are capable of interaction with both E2 and target protein. E3s mediate the covalent attachment of multiple ubiquitin moieties to an NH2 group of a lysine residue of the protein substrate to generate an isopeptide bond.    Outcome effects: Additional ubiquitin moieties may be transferred to the previously conjugated ubiquitin, resulting in a polyubiquitin chain formation. This chain is recognized by the proteasome and directs the target protein to the 26S proteasome complex for degradation. 26S proteasome is composed of 28 subunits to form a cylindrical structure. The 19S proteasome regulatory particle is cupped at each end of the 20S core proteasome to form the core of the 26S proteasome. Once ubiquitin-tagged, proteins bind to 19S proteasome regulatory particle of the proteasome and then enter the catalytic inner chamber." EXACT []

