xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Apoptosis is the genetically regulated programmed cell death which occurs in mulaticellular organisms as part of their normal development and functioning. The nuclear lamina is filamentous meshwork underlying the nuclear membrane that provides mechanical stability for the nucleus.    Signaling description: During apoptosis, chromatin, the macromolecular complex in which DNA is condensed into a more compact structure and associated with histones and other proteins, is cleaved to oligonucleosomal fragments and the nuclear lamina undergoes proteolysis. The disassembly of the nuclear lamina is induced by phosphorylation during mitosis. In apoptosis, the nuclear lamina (LMNA) is degraded by caspases via proteolytic cleavage. Specifically, the caspases recognize aspartic acid (Asp) residues in lamins. CASP6, CASP3, and CASP7 act in concert with PRKCD which is needed for lamin hyperphosphorylation, and together they bring about an efficient disassembly of the lamina.    Outcome effects: Lamin-binding proteins are an important architectural component involved in cell cycle-dependent nuclear structure dynamics. The degradation of lamin-binding proteins is not a general prerequisite for apoptotic events; however the most important structural proteins involved in chromatin organization must be selectively targeted by the apoptotic caspases."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Apoptosis is the genetically regulated programmed cell death which occurs in mulaticellular organisms as part of their normal development and functioning. The nuclear lamina is filamentous meshwork underlying the nuclear membrane that provides mechanical stability for the nucleus.    Signaling description: During apoptosis, chromatin, the macromolecular complex in which DNA is condensed into a more compact structure and associated with histones and other proteins, is cleaved to oligonucleosomal fragments and the nuclear lamina undergoes proteolysis. The disassembly of the nuclear lamina is induced by phosphorylation during mitosis. In apoptosis, the nuclear lamina (LMNA) is degraded by caspases via proteolytic cleavage. Specifically, the caspases recognize aspartic acid (Asp) residues in lamins. CASP6, CASP3, and CASP7 act in concert with PRKCD which is needed for lamin hyperphosphorylation, and together they bring about an efficient disassembly of the lamina.    Outcome effects: Lamin-binding proteins are an important architectural component involved in cell cycle-dependent nuclear structure dynamics. The degradation of lamin-binding proteins is not a general prerequisite for apoptotic events; however the most important structural proteins involved in chromatin organization must be selectively targeted by the apoptotic caspases." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-be878e67-d551-45ea-a23d-6170599173a9
