xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The three major classes of intracellular RNA-degrading enzymes (ribonucleases or RNases) include endonucleases which cut RNA internally, 5' exonucleases which hydrolyze RNA from the 5' end, and 3' exonucleases which degrade RNA from the 3' end.    Signaling description: Nearly all mRNA degradation mechanisms are initiated by the shortening of mRNA 3'-poly(A) tails removed by deadenylases. The major deadenylase activity in yeast and humans is most likely the CCR4-NOT transcription complex. Another deadenylase (PARN) may play a more important role in regulated deadenylation. Following poly(A) tail removal, the mRNA body may be subject to 3' degradation. In eukaryotes, 3' degradation activity is provided by the exosome complex. TRAMP polyadenylation complexes act as major cofactors for the exosome complex. In human cells, homologs of TRAMP components are PAPD7, PAPD5, and ZCCHC3. The TRAMP-exosome combination exhibits exonuclease and endonuclease activities and function together with helicase family members such as SKIV2L2, SUPV3L1, and SKIV2L.    Outcome effects: Eukaryotic mRNAs carry protective 5'-cap structures that must be removed prior to 5' exonuclease degradation by decapping enzymes DCP1A and XRN1. Individual LSM proteins assemble into a seven-member ring (torus), which usually binds to a small RNA molecule to form a ribonucleoprotein complex. Ring-shaped complexes LSM 1-7 act as chaperones promoting RNA degradation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The three major classes of intracellular RNA-degrading enzymes (ribonucleases or RNases) include endonucleases which cut RNA internally, 5' exonucleases which hydrolyze RNA from the 5' end, and 3' exonucleases which degrade RNA from the 3' end.    Signaling description: Nearly all mRNA degradation mechanisms are initiated by the shortening of mRNA 3'-poly(A) tails removed by deadenylases. The major deadenylase activity in yeast and humans is most likely the CCR4-NOT transcription complex. Another deadenylase (PARN) may play a more important role in regulated deadenylation. Following poly(A) tail removal, the mRNA body may be subject to 3' degradation. In eukaryotes, 3' degradation activity is provided by the exosome complex. TRAMP polyadenylation complexes act as major cofactors for the exosome complex. In human cells, homologs of TRAMP components are PAPD7, PAPD5, and ZCCHC3. The TRAMP-exosome combination exhibits exonuclease and endonuclease activities and function together with helicase family members such as SKIV2L2, SUPV3L1, and SKIV2L.    Outcome effects: Eukaryotic mRNAs carry protective 5'-cap structures that must be removed prior to 5' exonuclease degradation by decapping enzymes DCP1A and XRN1. Individual LSM proteins assemble into a seven-member ring (torus), which usually binds to a small RNA molecule to form a ribonucleoprotein complex. Ring-shaped complexes LSM 1-7 act as chaperones promoting RNA degradation." EXACT []

