xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases.    Signaling description: When the S phase is complete, all of the chromosomes have been replicated, i.e., each chromosome has two (sister) chromatids. DNA replication takes place only at specific loci on the chromosomal DNA termed replication origins. Newly synthesized CDC6/CDC18 temporally associates with ORC (Origin Recognition Complexes) and is essential for the assembly of preRC (pre Replication Complex) at origins of replication, prior to the initiation of DNA synthesis. Histone production occurs during the S phase, but rates of RNA transcription and protein synthesis are very low during this phase. In the S-phase, CycA - CDK2 complex initiates DNA replication. CycE regulates the transcription of genes that encode the replication machinery, but has also been implicated in the initiation DNA replication process in mammalian cells. The synthesis and accumulation of CycB also occurs during S-phase. In G1 phase, CycB is synthesized but stays inactive due to FZR1/APC-dependent degradation.    Outcome effects: During the S/G2/M state, CycA - CDK2 turns off FZR1, allowing CycB to accumulate, so that the cell will eventually be able to enter mitosis. In G2, the cell prepares for mitosis. Spoecifically, significant biosynthesis occurs during this phase, mainly involving the production of microtubules, which are required during the process of mitosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases.    Signaling description: When the S phase is complete, all of the chromosomes have been replicated, i.e., each chromosome has two (sister) chromatids. DNA replication takes place only at specific loci on the chromosomal DNA termed replication origins. Newly synthesized CDC6/CDC18 temporally associates with ORC (Origin Recognition Complexes) and is essential for the assembly of preRC (pre Replication Complex) at origins of replication, prior to the initiation of DNA synthesis. Histone production occurs during the S phase, but rates of RNA transcription and protein synthesis are very low during this phase. In the S-phase, CycA - CDK2 complex initiates DNA replication. CycE regulates the transcription of genes that encode the replication machinery, but has also been implicated in the initiation DNA replication process in mammalian cells. The synthesis and accumulation of CycB also occurs during S-phase. In G1 phase, CycB is synthesized but stays inactive due to FZR1/APC-dependent degradation.    Outcome effects: During the S/G2/M state, CycA - CDK2 turns off FZR1, allowing CycB to accumulate, so that the cell will eventually be able to enter mitosis. In G2, the cell prepares for mitosis. Spoecifically, significant biosynthesis occurs during this phase, mainly involving the production of microtubules, which are required during the process of mitosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

