xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. The G1/S transition determines the whether the cell should divide or not.    Signaling description: At the G1/S checkpoint, the cell cycle arrest is induced by DNA damage and is p53-dependent. Namely, ATM and ATR 'recognize' DNA damage. These kinases phosphorylate p53, which stimulates the induction of CDKN1A, causes CDKs inhibition and cell cycle arrest, preventing the replication of damaged DNA. MDM2 inhibits p53 transcriptional activity and contributes to the proteolytic degradation of p53. CDKN2A binds to MDM2 and this prevents p53 proteolysis.    Outcome effects: In the case of severely damaged cells, p53 induces cell death by activating genes (e.g. Bax, Fas and genes involved in oxidative stress pathways) involved in apoptotic signaling. DNA protein kinase (PRKDC), a DNA double-strand break repair enzyme is related to ATM and ATR, but it is unknown whether it also has a role at the G1/S checkpoint. TGF-Beta, DNA damage, contact inhibition, replicative senescence and growth factors are all stimuli for the G1/S DNA damage checkpoint. TGF-Beta additionally inhibits the transcription of CDC25A, a phosphatase that activates the cell cycle kinases. ATM-mediated phosphorylation of NBN may be required to induce S phase arrest during the S phase checkpoint."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. The G1/S transition determines the whether the cell should divide or not.    Signaling description: At the G1/S checkpoint, the cell cycle arrest is induced by DNA damage and is p53-dependent. Namely, ATM and ATR 'recognize' DNA damage. These kinases phosphorylate p53, which stimulates the induction of CDKN1A, causes CDKs inhibition and cell cycle arrest, preventing the replication of damaged DNA. MDM2 inhibits p53 transcriptional activity and contributes to the proteolytic degradation of p53. CDKN2A binds to MDM2 and this prevents p53 proteolysis.    Outcome effects: In the case of severely damaged cells, p53 induces cell death by activating genes (e.g. Bax, Fas and genes involved in oxidative stress pathways) involved in apoptotic signaling. DNA protein kinase (PRKDC), a DNA double-strand break repair enzyme is related to ATM and ATR, but it is unknown whether it also has a role at the G1/S checkpoint. TGF-Beta, DNA damage, contact inhibition, replicative senescence and growth factors are all stimuli for the G1/S DNA damage checkpoint. TGF-Beta additionally inhibits the transcription of CDC25A, a phosphatase that activates the cell cycle kinases. ATM-mediated phosphorylation of NBN may be required to induce S phase arrest during the S phase checkpoint." EXACT []

