xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. The G1/S transition determines the whether the cell should divide or not. Many cells stop at this stage and reenter the resting state G0. The restriction point (R) is defined as a point of no return in G1, following which the cell is committed to enter the cell cycle (S phase). G1 ends when DNA synthesis begins and S phase starts with DNA synthesis.    Signaling description: The CycE/CDK2 complex accumulates during late G1-phase and triggers the passage into S-phase. When CycE is abundant it interacts with the CDK2 and phosphorylates RB1. When dephosphorylated in G0, RB1 blocks the transcription via E2F transcription factors; however, when CycD - CDK4/6 and CycE - CDK2 complexes phosphorylate RB1, it dissociates from E2F, allowing E2F to activate the transcription of genes required for S-phase. One of the genes activated by E2F is CycE itself. CycE (and CycA) inactivates the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1B (CDKN1B) forming inactive trimmers with CDK2. At this stage, the cell is past the restriction point.    Outcome effects: The decision by the cell to progress into S-phase is the result, in part of the balance between CycE production and its proteolytic degradation in the proteosome. Cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor proteins (CKI, CDKN) are regulated both by internal and external signals. ATAD2, HELLS, RAD54B, RAD54L are consistently highly expressed in G1/S. They stimulate G1 to S phase transitions. HELLS and ATAD2 both co-activate E2F transcription factors and recruit DNA repair and chromatin-modifying proteins and DNA double strand break repair proteins. In Myc- dependent cancers ATAD2 stimulate cell cycle progression. FOXM1 promotes G1/S transition by releasing the inhibition of CDKN1B and by the transcriptional regulation of the CDC25A. BMyb also plays an important role during the late G1 and early S phases of the cell cycle."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. The G1/S transition determines the whether the cell should divide or not. Many cells stop at this stage and reenter the resting state G0. The restriction point (R) is defined as a point of no return in G1, following which the cell is committed to enter the cell cycle (S phase). G1 ends when DNA synthesis begins and S phase starts with DNA synthesis.    Signaling description: The CycE/CDK2 complex accumulates during late G1-phase and triggers the passage into S-phase. When CycE is abundant it interacts with the CDK2 and phosphorylates RB1. When dephosphorylated in G0, RB1 blocks the transcription via E2F transcription factors; however, when CycD - CDK4/6 and CycE - CDK2 complexes phosphorylate RB1, it dissociates from E2F, allowing E2F to activate the transcription of genes required for S-phase. One of the genes activated by E2F is CycE itself. CycE (and CycA) inactivates the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1B (CDKN1B) forming inactive trimmers with CDK2. At this stage, the cell is past the restriction point.    Outcome effects: The decision by the cell to progress into S-phase is the result, in part of the balance between CycE production and its proteolytic degradation in the proteosome. Cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor proteins (CKI, CDKN) are regulated both by internal and external signals. ATAD2, HELLS, RAD54B, RAD54L are consistently highly expressed in G1/S. They stimulate G1 to S phase transitions. HELLS and ATAD2 both co-activate E2F transcription factors and recruit DNA repair and chromatin-modifying proteins and DNA double strand break repair proteins. In Myc- dependent cancers ATAD2 stimulate cell cycle progression. FOXM1 promotes G1/S transition by releasing the inhibition of CDKN1B and by the transcriptional regulation of the CDC25A. BMyb also plays an important role during the late G1 and early S phases of the cell cycle." EXACT []

