xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle includes four discrete phases including the S phase in which DNA is replicated, M phase when the chromosomes are separated in the process of mitosis, and two Gap phases, G1 and G2 in which the cell prepares for mitosis.    Signaling description: In mammalian cells, CCND and CDK4/6 are needed for G1 progression, CCNE and CDK2 for G1-S transition, CCNA and CDK2 for S phase progression, and CCNB and CDK1 for entry of the cell into M phase. The disruption of cyclin action leads to either cell cycle arrest or to uncontrolled cell proliferation. The G1 phase is marked by the synthesis of enzymes required for the S phase. During G1/S transition, phosphorylation of RB1 by CDK4/6 and CDK2 disrupts the RB1-repressor complex, permitting transcription of the S phase genes which are required for further DNA replication. Many diverse stimuli exert G1/S checkpoint control and most of them act by inducing members of the INK4 family of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors including CDKN2D, CDKN2C, CDKN2A, and CDKN2B or CIP/KIP family of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors such as CDKN1A and CDKN1B. The S phase begins when DNA synthesis commences, and when it is complete all of the chromosomes have been replicated. In the S phase, CCNA and CCNE are required to induce DNA replication and synthesis. The G2 phase continues until the cell enters mitosis. Again, significant biosynthesis occurs during this phase, mainly involving the production of microtubules for mitosis. The G2/M checkpoint is determined by a threshold level of the active CCNB1-CDK1 complex. CDK1 activity is modulated primarily through the regulation of its inhibitory phosphorylation sites by WEE1 and MYT1. Multiple feedback loops function in parallel to enhance CCNB-CDK1 activation. The G2/M checkpoint prevents the cell from entering mitosis if the genome is damaged. The M phase (mitosis) promoting factor activates the anaphase-promoting complex (APC/C) which allows its sister chromatids to move to the poles (anaphase), completing mitosis.    Outcome effects: The anaphase begins when APC/C destroys PTTG1. APC/C activity maintains low CCNB levels through G1 phase until silencing of APC/C in the S phase through direct inactivation by CCNA-CDK2. The spindle assembly checkpoint (SAC) stops the cell cycle in the M phase. The proteins responsible for the SAC signal make up the mitotic checkpoint complex (MCC). The proteolysis of CCNB and inactivation of the CCNB-CDK1 kinase can inhibit SAC activity."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle includes four discrete phases including the S phase in which DNA is replicated, M phase when the chromosomes are separated in the process of mitosis, and two Gap phases, G1 and G2 in which the cell prepares for mitosis.    Signaling description: In mammalian cells, CCND and CDK4/6 are needed for G1 progression, CCNE and CDK2 for G1-S transition, CCNA and CDK2 for S phase progression, and CCNB and CDK1 for entry of the cell into M phase. The disruption of cyclin action leads to either cell cycle arrest or to uncontrolled cell proliferation. The G1 phase is marked by the synthesis of enzymes required for the S phase. During G1/S transition, phosphorylation of RB1 by CDK4/6 and CDK2 disrupts the RB1-repressor complex, permitting transcription of the S phase genes which are required for further DNA replication. Many diverse stimuli exert G1/S checkpoint control and most of them act by inducing members of the INK4 family of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors including CDKN2D, CDKN2C, CDKN2A, and CDKN2B or CIP/KIP family of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors such as CDKN1A and CDKN1B. The S phase begins when DNA synthesis commences, and when it is complete all of the chromosomes have been replicated. In the S phase, CCNA and CCNE are required to induce DNA replication and synthesis. The G2 phase continues until the cell enters mitosis. Again, significant biosynthesis occurs during this phase, mainly involving the production of microtubules for mitosis. The G2/M checkpoint is determined by a threshold level of the active CCNB1-CDK1 complex. CDK1 activity is modulated primarily through the regulation of its inhibitory phosphorylation sites by WEE1 and MYT1. Multiple feedback loops function in parallel to enhance CCNB-CDK1 activation. The G2/M checkpoint prevents the cell from entering mitosis if the genome is damaged. The M phase (mitosis) promoting factor activates the anaphase-promoting complex (APC/C) which allows its sister chromatids to move to the poles (anaphase), completing mitosis.    Outcome effects: The anaphase begins when APC/C destroys PTTG1. APC/C activity maintains low CCNB levels through G1 phase until silencing of APC/C in the S phase through direct inactivation by CCNA-CDK2. The spindle assembly checkpoint (SAC) stops the cell cycle in the M phase. The proteins responsible for the SAC signal make up the mitotic checkpoint complex (MCC). The proteolysis of CCNB and inactivation of the CCNB-CDK1 kinase can inhibit SAC activity." EXACT []

