xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. G1 is the first phase of the new cell cycle during which the cell is preparing for DNA synthesis. The resting phase in which the cell has left the cycle and is not going to divide is called G0.    Signaling description: The G0/G1 transition starts with the activation of growth factor receptors and focal adhesion receptors by extracellular signals. Activation of Ras/PI3K/Raf and Rho leads to the transcriptional induction of D-type cyclins (CycD) in early G1. Also, the expression of CycD may be activated through the MAPK cascade (via SP1, c-Fos, c-Jun) and/or through the NF-KB pathway. In addition, AKT may inhibit GSK3 beta, thus preventing the degradation of CycD. The eukaryotic cell cycle is dependent upon the activities of CDKs (Cyclin Dependent Kinases). CDK activity is controlled by fluctuations in levels of cyclins and cell cycle kinase inhibitors (CDKN), and by reversible phosphorylation. In mammalian cells, CycD - CDK4/6 complex is involved in the regulation of G1 phase progression. The accumulation of CycD - CDK4/6 complexes is activated via the phosphorylation of CDKs by the CAK complex.CAK is composed of three subunits: CDK7, CycH and MAT1. Along with six other subunits, CAK is also a part of the general transcription factor TFIIH.    Outcome effects: CycD - CDK4/6 complex is responsible for the first phosphorylation of RB1 in G1 phase thereby, liberating E2Fs (for example, E2F1 or E2F4). E2Fs form a heterodimeric complex of the E2F polypeptide and TFDPs. E2F-TFDPs complex induces the expression of Cyclin E, Cyclin A, DNA polymerase, thymidine kinase and some other proteins that are required in S phase."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. G1 is the first phase of the new cell cycle during which the cell is preparing for DNA synthesis. The resting phase in which the cell has left the cycle and is not going to divide is called G0.    Signaling description: The G0/G1 transition starts with the activation of growth factor receptors and focal adhesion receptors by extracellular signals. Activation of Ras/PI3K/Raf and Rho leads to the transcriptional induction of D-type cyclins (CycD) in early G1. Also, the expression of CycD may be activated through the MAPK cascade (via SP1, c-Fos, c-Jun) and/or through the NF-KB pathway. In addition, AKT may inhibit GSK3 beta, thus preventing the degradation of CycD. The eukaryotic cell cycle is dependent upon the activities of CDKs (Cyclin Dependent Kinases). CDK activity is controlled by fluctuations in levels of cyclins and cell cycle kinase inhibitors (CDKN), and by reversible phosphorylation. In mammalian cells, CycD - CDK4/6 complex is involved in the regulation of G1 phase progression. The accumulation of CycD - CDK4/6 complexes is activated via the phosphorylation of CDKs by the CAK complex.CAK is composed of three subunits: CDK7, CycH and MAT1. Along with six other subunits, CAK is also a part of the general transcription factor TFIIH.    Outcome effects: CycD - CDK4/6 complex is responsible for the first phosphorylation of RB1 in G1 phase thereby, liberating E2Fs (for example, E2F1 or E2F4). E2Fs form a heterodimeric complex of the E2F polypeptide and TFDPs. E2F-TFDPs complex induces the expression of Cyclin E, Cyclin A, DNA polymerase, thymidine kinase and some other proteins that are required in S phase." EXACT []

