synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). During the M phase (mitosis) the mother cell divides into two daughter cells, genetically identical to each other and to their parent cell.    Signaling description: The CycB - CDK1 complex activates the anaphase-promoting complex (APC/C) which allows the sister chromatids at the metaphase plate to separate and move to the poles. The separation of sister chromatids depends on the breakdown of cohesin that holds the chromatids together. Cohesin breakdown is caused by ESPL1 which is kept inactive until late metaphase by an inhibitory PTTG1. Anaphase begins when APC/C destroys PTTG1. Once the cell has split into two daughter cells, it enters G1. During the M phase, the PLKs activate certain functions of the APC/C. The spindle assembly checkpoint (SAC) during M phase stops the cell cycle (see Spindle Assembly Pathway). SAC occurs in metaphase when all the chromosomes have/should have aligned at the mitotic plate and are under bipolar tension. The proteins responsible for the SAC include MAD2/MAD3, BUB3, and CDC20. CDC20 and the SAC proteins concentrate at the kinetochores before attachment to the spindle assembly. Another regulator of checkpoint activation is kinetochore tension. Aurora-B functions as the tension sensor for improper kinetochore attachments.    Outcome effects: Once activated, SAC blocks anaphase entry by inhibiting the APC/C. Following silencing of the spindle checkpoint in mitosis, CycB is degraded by the APC/C or via ubiquitin/proteasome - dependent degradation. Inactivation of the CycB - CDK1 inhibits SAC activity. APC/C activity maintains low CycB levels through the G1 phase until silencing of APC/C in the S phase via direct inactivation by CycA-CDK2."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). During the M phase (mitosis) the mother cell divides into two daughter cells, genetically identical to each other and to their parent cell.    Signaling description: The CycB - CDK1 complex activates the anaphase-promoting complex (APC/C) which allows the sister chromatids at the metaphase plate to separate and move to the poles. The separation of sister chromatids depends on the breakdown of cohesin that holds the chromatids together. Cohesin breakdown is caused by ESPL1 which is kept inactive until late metaphase by an inhibitory PTTG1. Anaphase begins when APC/C destroys PTTG1. Once the cell has split into two daughter cells, it enters G1. During the M phase, the PLKs activate certain functions of the APC/C. The spindle assembly checkpoint (SAC) during M phase stops the cell cycle (see Spindle Assembly Pathway). SAC occurs in metaphase when all the chromosomes have/should have aligned at the mitotic plate and are under bipolar tension. The proteins responsible for the SAC include MAD2/MAD3, BUB3, and CDC20. CDC20 and the SAC proteins concentrate at the kinetochores before attachment to the spindle assembly. Another regulator of checkpoint activation is kinetochore tension. Aurora-B functions as the tension sensor for improper kinetochore attachments.    Outcome effects: Once activated, SAC blocks anaphase entry by inhibiting the APC/C. Following silencing of the spindle checkpoint in mitosis, CycB is degraded by the APC/C or via ubiquitin/proteasome - dependent degradation. Inactivation of the CycB - CDK1 inhibits SAC activity. APC/C activity maintains low CycB levels through the G1 phase until silencing of APC/C in the S phase via direct inactivation by CycA-CDK2." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-d76dcf08-4a1b-4d66-908a-fedff76e60fb
name: G2/M Phase Transition
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:nucleus ! Nucleus
is_a: urn:agi-folder:cell_cycle_regulation ! Cell cycle regulation
is_a: urn:agi-folder:g ! G
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
xref: PS:Description "The mammalian cell cycle divides into two stages: mitosis (M) - the process of nuclear division and interphase.Interphase is the interlude between two M phases. Interphase includes G1, S and G 2 phases. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: The mammalian cell cycle divides into two stages: mitosis (M) - the process of nuclear division and interphase.Interphase is the interlude between two M phases. Interphase includes G1, S and G 2 phases. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-d76dcf08-4a1b-4d66-908a-fedff76e60fb"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-d76dcf08-4a1b-4d66-908a-fedff76e60fb" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "generic"
synonym: "Organ_System: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "generic"
synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. The G2/M checkpoint occurs at the end of G2 phase, triggering the start of mitosis.    Signaling description: The mitotic entry is determined by activation of the CycB - CDK1 complex. CycB is absent in G1 and accumulates in S/G2/M phases of the cycle. CycB - CDK1 activities are held in check until DNA synthesis is completed. During the S/G2/M state CycA - CDK2 turns off FZR1, allowing CycB to accumulate, so that the cell will eventually be able to enter mitosis. Wee1 and PKMYT1 phosphorylate and inhibit CDK1. Further, CDK1 is dephosphorylated primarily by Cdc25. Multiple feedback loops function in parallel to enhance the CycB - CDK1 activation. For example, CycB - CDK1-mediated activation of Plk1 increases the production of several CycB - CDK1 activators, thereby further stimulating CycB - CDK1 activation. FOXM1 is a critical regulator of G2/M cell cycle transitions. FoxM1 stimulates the expression of multiple proteins in the mitotic entry network including CycB. FOXM1 regulates Cdc25B and Cdc25c genes transcription via direct promoter binding. Cdc25B has a role in activating the mitotic kinase CycB - CDK1 in the cytoplasm. Active CycB - CDK1 then phosphorylates and activates Cdc25C leading to a positive feedback mechanism and to entry into mitosis.    Outcome effects: The CycB - CDK1 complex phosphorylates many targets in preparation for mitosis, including Histone H1, nuclear lamins, centrosomal proteins. CycB - CDK1 complex induces chromosome condensation, assembly of the mitotic spindle apparatus, and alignment of condensed chromosomes at the metaphase plate. During the G2-phase, CDC2 is maintained in an inactive state by the kinases Wee1 and Myt1. As cells approach the M-phase, the phosphatase CDC25 is activated by PLK1. CDC25 then activates CDC2, establishing a feedback amplification loop that efficiently drives the cell into mitosis. 14-3-3 proteins bind to the phosphorylated CycB-CDC2 kinase and export it from the nucleus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. The G2/M checkpoint occurs at the end of G2 phase, triggering the start of mitosis.    Signaling description: The mitotic entry is determined by activation of the CycB - CDK1 complex. CycB is absent in G1 and accumulates in S/G2/M phases of the cycle. CycB - CDK1 activities are held in check until DNA synthesis is completed. During the S/G2/M state CycA - CDK2 turns off FZR1, allowing CycB to accumulate, so that the cell will eventually be able to enter mitosis. Wee1 and PKMYT1 phosphorylate and inhibit CDK1. Further, CDK1 is dephosphorylated primarily by Cdc25. Multiple feedback loops function in parallel to enhance the CycB - CDK1 activation. For example, CycB - CDK1-mediated activation of Plk1 increases the production of several CycB - CDK1 activators, thereby further stimulating CycB - CDK1 activation. FOXM1 is a critical regulator of G2/M cell cycle transitions. FoxM1 stimulates the expression of multiple proteins in the mitotic entry network including CycB. FOXM1 regulates Cdc25B and Cdc25c genes transcription via direct promoter binding. Cdc25B has a role in activating the mitotic kinase CycB - CDK1 in the cytoplasm. Active CycB - CDK1 then phosphorylates and activates Cdc25C leading to a positive feedback mechanism and to entry into mitosis.    Outcome effects: The CycB - CDK1 complex phosphorylates many targets in preparation for mitosis, including Histone H1, nuclear lamins, centrosomal proteins. CycB - CDK1 complex induces chromosome condensation, assembly of the mitotic spindle apparatus, and alignment of condensed chromosomes at the metaphase plate. During the G2-phase, CDC2 is maintained in an inactive state by the kinases Wee1 and Myt1. As cells approach the M-phase, the phosphatase CDC25 is activated by PLK1. CDC25 then activates CDC2, establishing a feedback amplification loop that efficiently drives the cell into mitosis. 14-3-3 proteins bind to the phosphorylated CycB-CDC2 kinase and export it from the nucleus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

