xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Transcription is the process in which a DNA sequence is enzymatically processed to produce a complementary RNA.    Signaling description: RNA Polymerase II transcribes messenger RNA (mRNA) and most small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs). Transcription is divided into three stages including initiation, elongation, and termination. To initiate transcription, the TBP subunit of TFIID transcription factor must recognize and bind to the TATA box, a core promoter element at the DNA. GTF2B and GTF2A1 stabilize this interaction between TATA and TFIID. RNA Polymerase II, along with transcription factors TFIIE, TFIIF, and TFIIH, is then recruited to form the initiation complex. Chromatin structure inhibition of transcriptional initiation is the process where the promoter is hidden by a chromatin structure. RNA Polymerase II catalyzes elongation upon clearing from the TFIID-complex. Once transcription begins, the 5' end of the initial RNA product synthesized by RNA Polymerase II is capped with 7-methyl-guanylate. The 3' end of a complete mRNA transcript is generated by endonucleolytic cleavage at a specific sequence, the Poly-A site, located at the 3' end the final exon. The transcriptional machinery continues up to 2kb beyond this signaling sequence and then dissociates.    Outcome effects: The initial pre-mRNA product undergoes several processing steps including polyadenylation and splicing. Polyadenylation is the covalent linking of a polyadenylyl moiety to the 3' ends of mRNA molecule. CPSF forms an unstable complex with the upstream AU-rich poly(A) signal (AAUAAA sequence) and initiates cleavage resulting in free 3' ends that are rapidly polyadenylated by PABPN1. Finally, introns are removed by RNA splicing before the complete RNA is transported to the cytoplasm. Most introns start from the sequence GU and end with the sequence AG, in the 5' to 3' direction. Splicing can be broadly divided into two stages including recognition of consensus sequences and assembly of complex and reactions of cleavage and ligation that change the structure of substrate RNA. Splicing small nuclear ribonucleoprotein particles (snRNPs) such as U1, U2, U4, U5, and U6 associate with pre-mRNA and with each other in a certain order to form the spliceosome. This large ribonucleoprotein complex then catalyzes two transesterification reactions that result in splicing of the exons and excision of the intron."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Transcription is the process in which a DNA sequence is enzymatically processed to produce a complementary RNA.    Signaling description: RNA Polymerase II transcribes messenger RNA (mRNA) and most small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs). Transcription is divided into three stages including initiation, elongation, and termination. To initiate transcription, the TBP subunit of TFIID transcription factor must recognize and bind to the TATA box, a core promoter element at the DNA. GTF2B and GTF2A1 stabilize this interaction between TATA and TFIID. RNA Polymerase II, along with transcription factors TFIIE, TFIIF, and TFIIH, is then recruited to form the initiation complex. Chromatin structure inhibition of transcriptional initiation is the process where the promoter is hidden by a chromatin structure. RNA Polymerase II catalyzes elongation upon clearing from the TFIID-complex. Once transcription begins, the 5' end of the initial RNA product synthesized by RNA Polymerase II is capped with 7-methyl-guanylate. The 3' end of a complete mRNA transcript is generated by endonucleolytic cleavage at a specific sequence, the Poly-A site, located at the 3' end the final exon. The transcriptional machinery continues up to 2kb beyond this signaling sequence and then dissociates.    Outcome effects: The initial pre-mRNA product undergoes several processing steps including polyadenylation and splicing. Polyadenylation is the covalent linking of a polyadenylyl moiety to the 3' ends of mRNA molecule. CPSF forms an unstable complex with the upstream AU-rich poly(A) signal (AAUAAA sequence) and initiates cleavage resulting in free 3' ends that are rapidly polyadenylated by PABPN1. Finally, introns are removed by RNA splicing before the complete RNA is transported to the cytoplasm. Most introns start from the sequence GU and end with the sequence AG, in the 5' to 3' direction. Splicing can be broadly divided into two stages including recognition of consensus sequences and assembly of complex and reactions of cleavage and ligation that change the structure of substrate RNA. Splicing small nuclear ribonucleoprotein particles (snRNPs) such as U1, U2, U4, U5, and U6 associate with pre-mRNA and with each other in a certain order to form the spliceosome. This large ribonucleoprotein complex then catalyzes two transesterification reactions that result in splicing of the exons and excision of the intron." EXACT []

