synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Transfer RNA (tRNA) is a small RNA chain of 74-93 nucleotides that transfers a specific amino acid to a growing polypeptide chain during translation. A multi-subunit complex TFIIIC initiates tRNA transcription by RNA polymerase III.    Signaling description: TFIIIC recognizes tRNA gene promoters that consist of two separated 10bp elements (A-BOX and B-BOX) located downstream of the transcription start site. The binding of TFIIIC recruits TFIIIB which is a complex of polypeptides including TBP, BRF1, and BDP1. TFIIIB then recruits RNA polymerase III. This DNA/TFIIIB/RNA polymerase III initiation complex is stable enough to pass through many rounds of tRNA transcription. Once the initiation complex is assembled, transcription and elongation begins. RNA polymerase III slides along the DNA molecule and matches the DNA nucleotides with complementary RNA nucleotides generating a new RNA molecule that is patterned after the DNA. Unlike RNA polymerase I and II, RNA polymerase III termination occurs efficiently at four or more T residues without other protein factors.    Outcome effects: The initial transcripts of tRNA genes must be properly processed into mature tRNAs. Pre-tRNA has four loops, a 5'-terminal 16-nucleotide sequence and a 3'-terminal U nucleotide with -OH group. During processing, one loop (14-nucleotide intron) is removed by splicing, the 16-nucleotide sequence at the 5' end is cleaved by ribonuclease P, and U residues at the 3' end are replaced by the CCA sequence. Therefore, tRNA consists of a 5'-terminal phosphate group, a CCA sequence at the 3' end of the tRNA molecule which is important for the recognition of tRNA by enzymes critical in translation, and three loops (D-arm, anticodon arm, and T-arm). Some pre-tRNAs contain introns that are spliced out during processing, and all pre-tRNAs undergo modification of some bases into dihydrouridine and pseudouridine. Finally, mature tRNAs are exported to the cytoplasm to participate in protein synthesis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Transfer RNA (tRNA) is a small RNA chain of 74-93 nucleotides that transfers a specific amino acid to a growing polypeptide chain during translation. A multi-subunit complex TFIIIC initiates tRNA transcription by RNA polymerase III.    Signaling description: TFIIIC recognizes tRNA gene promoters that consist of two separated 10bp elements (A-BOX and B-BOX) located downstream of the transcription start site. The binding of TFIIIC recruits TFIIIB which is a complex of polypeptides including TBP, BRF1, and BDP1. TFIIIB then recruits RNA polymerase III. This DNA/TFIIIB/RNA polymerase III initiation complex is stable enough to pass through many rounds of tRNA transcription. Once the initiation complex is assembled, transcription and elongation begins. RNA polymerase III slides along the DNA molecule and matches the DNA nucleotides with complementary RNA nucleotides generating a new RNA molecule that is patterned after the DNA. Unlike RNA polymerase I and II, RNA polymerase III termination occurs efficiently at four or more T residues without other protein factors.    Outcome effects: The initial transcripts of tRNA genes must be properly processed into mature tRNAs. Pre-tRNA has four loops, a 5'-terminal 16-nucleotide sequence and a 3'-terminal U nucleotide with -OH group. During processing, one loop (14-nucleotide intron) is removed by splicing, the 16-nucleotide sequence at the 5' end is cleaved by ribonuclease P, and U residues at the 3' end are replaced by the CCA sequence. Therefore, tRNA consists of a 5'-terminal phosphate group, a CCA sequence at the 3' end of the tRNA molecule which is important for the recognition of tRNA by enzymes critical in translation, and three loops (D-arm, anticodon arm, and T-arm). Some pre-tRNAs contain introns that are spliced out during processing, and all pre-tRNAs undergo modification of some bases into dihydrouridine and pseudouridine. Finally, mature tRNAs are exported to the cytoplasm to participate in protein synthesis." EXACT []

