synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The function of rRNA is to provide a mechanism for coding mRNA into amino acids at the center of a small ribosomal subunit and to interact with tRNAs during translation.    Signaling description: Three rRNA genes including 18S, 5.8S, and 28S rRNA are transcribed by RNA polymerase I while 5S rRNA is transcribed by RNA polymerase III. 18S, 5.8S, and 28S rRNAs are synthesized as part of a single large precursor that undergoes a series of enzymatic cleavage steps to produce mature rRNA molecules. The promoter of rRNA precursor gene consists of two regions, a core promoter element (CPE) and an upstream control element (UCE). UBTF recognizes UCE, whereas SL1 complex (TBP, TAF1A, TAF1B, and TAF1C) recognizes CPE. SL1 complex is activated by phosphorylation in mitosis, while UBTF is phosphorylated and activated in the G1 phase. RNA polymerase I and additional factors bind UBTF and SL1 complex, thus forming the initiation complex. TTF1 protein binds to eukaryotic transcription units for ribosomal genes which contain the SalI restriction site (Sal BOX) and causes the termination of transcription and disassembly of the RNA polymerase I complex.    Outcome effects: Following transcriptional termination, 80S ribonucleoprotein (RNP) precursor is formed, which contains a 5' leader sequence and 18S, 5.8S and 28S rRNAs separated by spacer RNAs. The position of cleavage sites in ribosomal RNA precursor (pre-rRNA) and the specific sites of 2-O-methylation and pseudouridine formation are determined by approximately 150 different small nucleolar RNAs (snoRNAs or RNA modification guide). Like small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs), snoRNAs associate with proteins and form snoRNPs. One large class of snoRNAs, called ribonuclease MRP, catalyzes one of the cleavages by which transcribed spacer sequences are removed from pre-rRNA. The cleavage of rRNA precursor leads to 20S (containing 18S rRNA) and 60S ribosomal subunits (containing 5.8S, 28S and 5S rRNAs). Unlike all other rRNAs, 5S rRNA transcription is performed by RNA polymerase III. The process is similar to tRNA transcription, starting with the binding of GTF3A to the intragenic (lying within the transcribed DNA sequence) 5S rRNA control sequence box C. GTF3A binding is required for subsequent TFIIIC positioning. In turn, TFIIIC causes the correct positioning of TFIIIB (TBP, BRF, and BDP1), which then recruits RNA polymerase III. Transcription begins immediately following the assembly of the initiation complex and continues until RNA polymerase III reaches a termination signal. Without further processing, 5S rRNA diffuses to the nucleolus where it assembles with the 28S and 5.8S rRNAs and proteins into large ribosomal subunits. The rRNAs are associated with ribosomal proteins in a self-organizing mode, forming both large and small ribosomal subunits which are then separately transported to the cytoplasm to take part in protein synthesis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The function of rRNA is to provide a mechanism for coding mRNA into amino acids at the center of a small ribosomal subunit and to interact with tRNAs during translation.    Signaling description: Three rRNA genes including 18S, 5.8S, and 28S rRNA are transcribed by RNA polymerase I while 5S rRNA is transcribed by RNA polymerase III. 18S, 5.8S, and 28S rRNAs are synthesized as part of a single large precursor that undergoes a series of enzymatic cleavage steps to produce mature rRNA molecules. The promoter of rRNA precursor gene consists of two regions, a core promoter element (CPE) and an upstream control element (UCE). UBTF recognizes UCE, whereas SL1 complex (TBP, TAF1A, TAF1B, and TAF1C) recognizes CPE. SL1 complex is activated by phosphorylation in mitosis, while UBTF is phosphorylated and activated in the G1 phase. RNA polymerase I and additional factors bind UBTF and SL1 complex, thus forming the initiation complex. TTF1 protein binds to eukaryotic transcription units for ribosomal genes which contain the SalI restriction site (Sal BOX) and causes the termination of transcription and disassembly of the RNA polymerase I complex.    Outcome effects: Following transcriptional termination, 80S ribonucleoprotein (RNP) precursor is formed, which contains a 5' leader sequence and 18S, 5.8S and 28S rRNAs separated by spacer RNAs. The position of cleavage sites in ribosomal RNA precursor (pre-rRNA) and the specific sites of 2-O-methylation and pseudouridine formation are determined by approximately 150 different small nucleolar RNAs (snoRNAs or RNA modification guide). Like small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs), snoRNAs associate with proteins and form snoRNPs. One large class of snoRNAs, called ribonuclease MRP, catalyzes one of the cleavages by which transcribed spacer sequences are removed from pre-rRNA. The cleavage of rRNA precursor leads to 20S (containing 18S rRNA) and 60S ribosomal subunits (containing 5.8S, 28S and 5S rRNAs). Unlike all other rRNAs, 5S rRNA transcription is performed by RNA polymerase III. The process is similar to tRNA transcription, starting with the binding of GTF3A to the intragenic (lying within the transcribed DNA sequence) 5S rRNA control sequence box C. GTF3A binding is required for subsequent TFIIIC positioning. In turn, TFIIIC causes the correct positioning of TFIIIB (TBP, BRF, and BDP1), which then recruits RNA polymerase III. Transcription begins immediately following the assembly of the initiation complex and continues until RNA polymerase III reaches a termination signal. Without further processing, 5S rRNA diffuses to the nucleolus where it assembles with the 28S and 5.8S rRNAs and proteins into large ribosomal subunits. The rRNAs are associated with ribosomal proteins in a self-organizing mode, forming both large and small ribosomal subunits which are then separately transported to the cytoplasm to take part in protein synthesis." EXACT []

