xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. During the S phase, each chromosome is replicated to produce two identical chromosomes termed sister chromatids.    Signaling description: Sister chromatids are paired along their entire length by the interaction of structural cohesins and the establishment factors. Cohesin is loaded onto chromatin in telophase and early G1 phase. The cohesin complex holds replicated sister chromatids together according to its ring shape which encircles and thereby topologically traps DNA. With the aid of accessory factors, such as PCNA, the replication fork-associated acetyltransferase ESCO1 facilitates its activation and thus cohesion between sisters is established. In mammalian cells, PDS5 retains centromeric cohesion and WAPAL facilitates the removal of centromeric cohesin. BUB1, through SGOL1, protects centromeric cohesin from the prophase cohesin removal pathway in a PP2A-dependent manner. At the beginning of metaphase, microtubules exert a pulling force on the chromosomes towards the opposite ends of the cells, while the cohesion between the sister chromatids oppose this force. PLK1 and AURKB are required for the chromosome-arm cohesin removal during metaphase.    Outcome effects: When anaphase is imminent, cohesion is cleaved by ESPL1 followed by its release from the inhibitory partner, PTTG1 and the separated chromatids are pulled to opposite sides of the cell by the spindle microtubules. The physical movement of the spindle poles themselves causes further separation of the chromatids. MAD2L1 is a key effector of the spindle assembly checkpoint through the inhibition of APC/C."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). The interphase is further divided into three periods G1, S and G 2 phases. During the S phase, each chromosome is replicated to produce two identical chromosomes termed sister chromatids.    Signaling description: Sister chromatids are paired along their entire length by the interaction of structural cohesins and the establishment factors. Cohesin is loaded onto chromatin in telophase and early G1 phase. The cohesin complex holds replicated sister chromatids together according to its ring shape which encircles and thereby topologically traps DNA. With the aid of accessory factors, such as PCNA, the replication fork-associated acetyltransferase ESCO1 facilitates its activation and thus cohesion between sisters is established. In mammalian cells, PDS5 retains centromeric cohesion and WAPAL facilitates the removal of centromeric cohesin. BUB1, through SGOL1, protects centromeric cohesin from the prophase cohesin removal pathway in a PP2A-dependent manner. At the beginning of metaphase, microtubules exert a pulling force on the chromosomes towards the opposite ends of the cells, while the cohesion between the sister chromatids oppose this force. PLK1 and AURKB are required for the chromosome-arm cohesin removal during metaphase.    Outcome effects: When anaphase is imminent, cohesion is cleaved by ESPL1 followed by its release from the inhibitory partner, PTTG1 and the separated chromatids are pulled to opposite sides of the cell by the spindle microtubules. The physical movement of the spindle poles themselves causes further separation of the chromatids. MAD2L1 is a key effector of the spindle assembly checkpoint through the inhibition of APC/C." EXACT []

