xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The spindle fibers help separate the chromosomes into daughter cells during the process of cell division. The cellular spindle apparatus consists of the spindle microtubules, centrosomes or asters present at the spindle poles, and associated proteins.    Signaling description: Spindle poles are the pointed ends of the spindle where spindle microtubules are nucleated by the centrosomes. In the wide middle portion, known as the spindle midzone, antiparallel microtubules are bundled by the minus-end-directed kinesin ATPase proteins. The two ends of the microtubule differ in growth rate and chromosomes attach to the spindle by binding the faster growing (plus) ends of microtubules. The centrosome is held to the minus ends of microtubules by additional proteins. Dynein-dependent transport of free microtubule bundles from the cytoplasm into the forming spindle continues through prometaphase and metaphase stages of mitosis. Microtubules are stabilized by dynein/dynactin associated with the nuclear envelope and facilitate breakup of the envelope. The major function of the centrosome is to dominate spindle assembly by nucleating microtubules.    Outcome effects: Once spindle assembly has progressed to anaphase, centrosomes are no longer required for spindle maintenance."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The spindle fibers help separate the chromosomes into daughter cells during the process of cell division. The cellular spindle apparatus consists of the spindle microtubules, centrosomes or asters present at the spindle poles, and associated proteins.    Signaling description: Spindle poles are the pointed ends of the spindle where spindle microtubules are nucleated by the centrosomes. In the wide middle portion, known as the spindle midzone, antiparallel microtubules are bundled by the minus-end-directed kinesin ATPase proteins. The two ends of the microtubule differ in growth rate and chromosomes attach to the spindle by binding the faster growing (plus) ends of microtubules. The centrosome is held to the minus ends of microtubules by additional proteins. Dynein-dependent transport of free microtubule bundles from the cytoplasm into the forming spindle continues through prometaphase and metaphase stages of mitosis. Microtubules are stabilized by dynein/dynactin associated with the nuclear envelope and facilitate breakup of the envelope. The major function of the centrosome is to dominate spindle assembly by nucleating microtubules.    Outcome effects: Once spindle assembly has progressed to anaphase, centrosomes are no longer required for spindle maintenance." EXACT []

