xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Kinetochores are unique protein complexes assembled on a single centromere DNA region of each sister chromatid during mitosis. The primary function of kinetochores is to create attachments between chromosomes and microtubules in a dividing mother cell. The accurate partitioning of sister chromatids to the daughter cells relies on these attachments.    Signaling description: In vertebrates, kinetochores have three the main subcomponents including the inner kinetochore which contributes a sturdy interface with centromeric chromatin, the outer kinetochore which contributes a microtubule-binding interface, and the spindle assembly checkpoint which is a feedback control mechanism that monitors the state of kinetochore-microtubule attachment to control the progression of the cell cycle. At the heart of the kinetochore is a specialized nucleosome that contains centromere protein CENPA, a histone H3 homologue. CENPB is required to establish centromeric chromatin, but is not necessary for its maintenance. OIP5 is crucial for chromatin incorporation of CENPA during the mitotic exit. CASC5, MIS12, and NDC80 have emerged as crucial components of the specialized machinery involved in microtubule binding. INCENP and AURKB are part of a complex that is commonly referred to as the chromosome passenger complex (CPC) which performs essential functions on chromosomes throughout mitosis. Cytoplasmic dynein, a minus-end directed motor, and CENPE, a plus-end directed motor, are needed for the congression of chromosomes to the metaphase plate. Several inner kinetochore components associate with kinetochores throughout the cell cycle. Many other proteins are recruited to the outer kinetochore specifically in mitosis. They provide a landing platform for the spindle-assembly checkpoint (SAC) proteins. At the attached kinetochore, MAD2L1/BUB1B complex binds to CDC20 and form the mitotic checkpoint complex (MCC), which binds to and inhibits the anaphase promoting complex (APC/C).    Outcome effects: In the metaphase, APC/C-mediated CDC20 inhibition is released by SAC silencing. This event is followed by the onset of anaphase when sister chromatids are separated and pulled toward opposite poles of the cell."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Kinetochores are unique protein complexes assembled on a single centromere DNA region of each sister chromatid during mitosis. The primary function of kinetochores is to create attachments between chromosomes and microtubules in a dividing mother cell. The accurate partitioning of sister chromatids to the daughter cells relies on these attachments.    Signaling description: In vertebrates, kinetochores have three the main subcomponents including the inner kinetochore which contributes a sturdy interface with centromeric chromatin, the outer kinetochore which contributes a microtubule-binding interface, and the spindle assembly checkpoint which is a feedback control mechanism that monitors the state of kinetochore-microtubule attachment to control the progression of the cell cycle. At the heart of the kinetochore is a specialized nucleosome that contains centromere protein CENPA, a histone H3 homologue. CENPB is required to establish centromeric chromatin, but is not necessary for its maintenance. OIP5 is crucial for chromatin incorporation of CENPA during the mitotic exit. CASC5, MIS12, and NDC80 have emerged as crucial components of the specialized machinery involved in microtubule binding. INCENP and AURKB are part of a complex that is commonly referred to as the chromosome passenger complex (CPC) which performs essential functions on chromosomes throughout mitosis. Cytoplasmic dynein, a minus-end directed motor, and CENPE, a plus-end directed motor, are needed for the congression of chromosomes to the metaphase plate. Several inner kinetochore components associate with kinetochores throughout the cell cycle. Many other proteins are recruited to the outer kinetochore specifically in mitosis. They provide a landing platform for the spindle-assembly checkpoint (SAC) proteins. At the attached kinetochore, MAD2L1/BUB1B complex binds to CDC20 and form the mitotic checkpoint complex (MCC), which binds to and inhibits the anaphase promoting complex (APC/C).    Outcome effects: In the metaphase, APC/C-mediated CDC20 inhibition is released by SAC silencing. This event is followed by the onset of anaphase when sister chromatids are separated and pulled toward opposite poles of the cell." EXACT []

