synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: A centriole is a cylinder shaped cell structure found in most eukaryotic cells that is comprised of nine triplets of microtubules in the cytoplasm. A centrosome consists of an associated pair of centrioles which are arranged perpendicularly and surrounded by an amorphous mass of pericentriolar material (PCM).    Signaling description: In the centrosome, subdistal appendages anchor microtubule minus ends, while distal appendages are required to attach the centriole to the plasma membrane (such as CEP170, PCNT, and NIN). The PCM contains a number of proteinaceous complexes, such as DCTN1, dynactin complex, MAPRE1, and centrin, which are responsible for microtubule nucleation (gamma tubulin ring complex) and the anchoring of microtubule minus ends to PCM. Cells begin the cell cycle with a single centrosome containing a pair of centrioles. The older centriole in a pair is termed mother centriole, whereas the younger one is termed daughter centriole. A new centriole grows from the side of each mother centriole. The replication of centrioles proceeds through G1/S transition until the onset of mitosis. The phosphorylation of NPM1, TTK, and PLK4 by CDK2 results in percentriole formation. The activation of PLK4 on the centrosome is required for the sequential recruitment of SASS6 and CENPJ.    Outcome effects: The distance between mother and daughter centrioles increases early in mitosis and NEK2 induces centrosome separation. In anaphase, the mother and daughter centrioles lose their orthogonal relationship and disengage. From this point onward, each centriole is surrounded by its own cloud of PCM. The molecular mechanisms responsible for centriole disengagement are poorly understood, however the cleavage may be mediated by separase ESPL1."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: A centriole is a cylinder shaped cell structure found in most eukaryotic cells that is comprised of nine triplets of microtubules in the cytoplasm. A centrosome consists of an associated pair of centrioles which are arranged perpendicularly and surrounded by an amorphous mass of pericentriolar material (PCM).    Signaling description: In the centrosome, subdistal appendages anchor microtubule minus ends, while distal appendages are required to attach the centriole to the plasma membrane (such as CEP170, PCNT, and NIN). The PCM contains a number of proteinaceous complexes, such as DCTN1, dynactin complex, MAPRE1, and centrin, which are responsible for microtubule nucleation (gamma tubulin ring complex) and the anchoring of microtubule minus ends to PCM. Cells begin the cell cycle with a single centrosome containing a pair of centrioles. The older centriole in a pair is termed mother centriole, whereas the younger one is termed daughter centriole. A new centriole grows from the side of each mother centriole. The replication of centrioles proceeds through G1/S transition until the onset of mitosis. The phosphorylation of NPM1, TTK, and PLK4 by CDK2 results in percentriole formation. The activation of PLK4 on the centrosome is required for the sequential recruitment of SASS6 and CENPJ.    Outcome effects: The distance between mother and daughter centrioles increases early in mitosis and NEK2 induces centrosome separation. In anaphase, the mother and daughter centrioles lose their orthogonal relationship and disengage. From this point onward, each centriole is surrounded by its own cloud of PCM. The molecular mechanisms responsible for centriole disengagement are poorly understood, however the cleavage may be mediated by separase ESPL1." EXACT []

