synonym: "Organ_System: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "generic"
synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). During mitosis, the nuclear envelope (NE) is completely disassembled and removed from the chromatin.    Signaling description: The major components of the NE are the inner and outer membranes, nuclear pore complex, and nuclear lamina which is a filamentous scaffold formed by lamins. Inner nuclear membrane (INM) proteins link the NE membrane to chromatin and the lamina, whereas outer nuclear membrane (ONM) proteins provide a connection from the nucleus to the cytoskeleton. Nuclear envelope proteins that are bound to the lamina include SYNE1, SYNE2, EMD, LBR, and members of the LEM-domain family. LBR interacts with both B-type lamins and CBX5 in conjunction with core histones. TMPO, TOR1AIP1, EMD, and LEMD3, which are members of the LEM-domain family, bind to lamins and interact with chromatin through BANF1. Transcription factors such as RB1, SREBF1, and ZNF239 also bind to the lamina. The lamina is essential for the organization of nuclear pore complexes in the nuclear membrane. Nuclear pore complexes are large macromolecular assemblies with eight-fold rotational symmetry that allow the transport of RNA and ribosomes from the nucleus to the cytoplasm and proteins, carbohydrates, signal molecules, and lipids into the nucleus. They are built from 30 different proteins known as nucleoporins. During mitosis, dynein/dynactin complexes attach to the NE and migrate towards the minus ends of microtubules assembled from cytoplasmic centrosomes in NE invaginations. This generates a tension that tears the NE open at sites distal to the centrosomes.    Outcome effects: Lamins and lamin-binding proteins are then phosphorylated by mitotic kinases and dissociate from the lamina and chromatin. Nuclear reassembly after sister chromatid separation requires a timely coordinated and dephosphorylation-dependent association of lamin-binding proteins and lamins with chromosomal proteins and targeting of membranes to specific sites on chromosomes."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, the cell cycle consists of two major phases: the mitotic (M) phase (the process of nuclear division) and interphase (the interlude between two M phases). During mitosis, the nuclear envelope (NE) is completely disassembled and removed from the chromatin.    Signaling description: The major components of the NE are the inner and outer membranes, nuclear pore complex, and nuclear lamina which is a filamentous scaffold formed by lamins. Inner nuclear membrane (INM) proteins link the NE membrane to chromatin and the lamina, whereas outer nuclear membrane (ONM) proteins provide a connection from the nucleus to the cytoskeleton. Nuclear envelope proteins that are bound to the lamina include SYNE1, SYNE2, EMD, LBR, and members of the LEM-domain family. LBR interacts with both B-type lamins and CBX5 in conjunction with core histones. TMPO, TOR1AIP1, EMD, and LEMD3, which are members of the LEM-domain family, bind to lamins and interact with chromatin through BANF1. Transcription factors such as RB1, SREBF1, and ZNF239 also bind to the lamina. The lamina is essential for the organization of nuclear pore complexes in the nuclear membrane. Nuclear pore complexes are large macromolecular assemblies with eight-fold rotational symmetry that allow the transport of RNA and ribosomes from the nucleus to the cytoplasm and proteins, carbohydrates, signal molecules, and lipids into the nucleus. They are built from 30 different proteins known as nucleoporins. During mitosis, dynein/dynactin complexes attach to the NE and migrate towards the minus ends of microtubules assembled from cytoplasmic centrosomes in NE invaginations. This generates a tension that tears the NE open at sites distal to the centrosomes.    Outcome effects: Lamins and lamin-binding proteins are then phosphorylated by mitotic kinases and dissociate from the lamina and chromatin. Nuclear reassembly after sister chromatid separation requires a timely coordinated and dephosphorylation-dependent association of lamin-binding proteins and lamins with chromosomal proteins and targeting of membranes to specific sites on chromosomes." EXACT []

