xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Junctions between adjacent cells include gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, or tight junctions. The tight junctions are located in the uppermost portion of the lateral plasma membrane apical to adherens junctions, and serve to hold cells together, act as a barrier, and prevent the lateral diffusion of membrane proteins between the apical and lateral/basal surfaces which allows the specialized functions of each cell surface, endocytosis at the apical surface and exocytosis at the basolateral surface, to be preserved.    Signaling description: Tight junctions are represented by linear co-polymerized molecules of occluding, claudins, and junctional adhesion molecules that have a high affinity for the C-terminal sequences of PDZ domain-containing cytoplasmic proteins. Occludin (OCLN) is expressed in the tight junctions of both endothelial and epithelial cells. This hydrophilic phosphoprotein has four transmembrane domains and two extracellular loops exposed to the cytosol. OCLN forms complexes with TJP1, TJP2, VAPA, EPB41, CTNNB1, CTNNA1, MLLT4, CGN, and SYMPK. RAB13 and other RAB GTPases facilitate vesicular trafficking of occludins. RAB13 interacts directly with PKA and inhibits PKA-dependent phosphorylation of VASP which is essential for actin remodeling. Similarly, the phosphorylation of SNAP receptors by PKA is implicated in the regulation of vesicle release. The cytoplasmic plaque protein, SYMPK participates in nuclear as well as cytoplasmic polyadenylation and activates mRNA cleavage stimulating factor and HSF1 to regulate mRNA stability/localization and cell adhesion/apoptosis, respectively.    Outcome effects: MAGI2 and MAGI3 bind to CTNNB1 at occludin junctions and prevent PTEN degradation. This significantly decreases the proliferative effect of AKT1 and prevents junction disassembly. The increase in PTEN activity also affects ITG/ILK/GSK3/MAPK8 signaling and thus modulates the level of OCLN near tight junctions."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Junctions between adjacent cells include gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, or tight junctions. The tight junctions are located in the uppermost portion of the lateral plasma membrane apical to adherens junctions, and serve to hold cells together, act as a barrier, and prevent the lateral diffusion of membrane proteins between the apical and lateral/basal surfaces which allows the specialized functions of each cell surface, endocytosis at the apical surface and exocytosis at the basolateral surface, to be preserved.    Signaling description: Tight junctions are represented by linear co-polymerized molecules of occluding, claudins, and junctional adhesion molecules that have a high affinity for the C-terminal sequences of PDZ domain-containing cytoplasmic proteins. Occludin (OCLN) is expressed in the tight junctions of both endothelial and epithelial cells. This hydrophilic phosphoprotein has four transmembrane domains and two extracellular loops exposed to the cytosol. OCLN forms complexes with TJP1, TJP2, VAPA, EPB41, CTNNB1, CTNNA1, MLLT4, CGN, and SYMPK. RAB13 and other RAB GTPases facilitate vesicular trafficking of occludins. RAB13 interacts directly with PKA and inhibits PKA-dependent phosphorylation of VASP which is essential for actin remodeling. Similarly, the phosphorylation of SNAP receptors by PKA is implicated in the regulation of vesicle release. The cytoplasmic plaque protein, SYMPK participates in nuclear as well as cytoplasmic polyadenylation and activates mRNA cleavage stimulating factor and HSF1 to regulate mRNA stability/localization and cell adhesion/apoptosis, respectively.    Outcome effects: MAGI2 and MAGI3 bind to CTNNB1 at occludin junctions and prevent PTEN degradation. This significantly decreases the proliferative effect of AKT1 and prevents junction disassembly. The increase in PTEN activity also affects ITG/ILK/GSK3/MAPK8 signaling and thus modulates the level of OCLN near tight junctions." EXACT []

