xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Junctions between adjacent cells include gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, or tight junctions. The tight junctions are located in the uppermost portion of the lateral plasma membrane apical to adherens junctions. The tight junctions hold cells together, act as a barrier, and prevent the lateral diffusion of membrane proteins between the apical and lateral/basal surfaces which allows the specialized functions of each surface, endocytosis at the apical surface and exocytosis at the basolateral surface, to be preserved.    Signaling description: Tight junctions are represented by linear co-polymerized molecules of occluding, claudins, and junctional adhesion molecules that have a high affinity for the C-terminal sequences of PDZ domain-containing cytoplasmic proteins. Claudins have two extracellular loops and four hydrophobic transmembrane domains. The extracellular loops vary in different claudins and may additionally contribute to the formation of ion-selective channels. The proteins that form a complex with the C-terminus of claudins include TJP1, TJP2, TJP3, MPDZ, MLLT4, EPB41, CSDA, INADL, and spectrin/fodrin. INADL and MPDZ interact with CRB1 and CRB3 to form a tight junction complex involved in epithelial cell polarity. The PARD3/PARD6A/ atypical PKC/CRB/INADL/TIAM1 polarity complex interacts with GTP-bound CDC42, a key modulator of the actin cytoskeleton, polarizing membrane transport and tight junction assembly. Furthermore, atypical PKC, PARD6A, and LLGL1 form another multi-protein complex that is required for regulating direct protein trafficking.    Outcome effects: Tight junctions regulate epithelial proliferation by different molecular mechanisms which generally suppress proliferation and increase cell density. TJP1 regulates proliferation and interacts with CSDA which regulates G1/S phase progression by the reduction of nuclear CDK4. CSDA functions in the transcriptional regulation of cell cycle regulators. TJP2 enters the nucleus in proliferating cells and subsequently binds with mRNA-binding proteins. SAFB binds Jun/Fos to regulate epithelial cell proliferation and differentiation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Junctions between adjacent cells include gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, or tight junctions. The tight junctions are located in the uppermost portion of the lateral plasma membrane apical to adherens junctions. The tight junctions hold cells together, act as a barrier, and prevent the lateral diffusion of membrane proteins between the apical and lateral/basal surfaces which allows the specialized functions of each surface, endocytosis at the apical surface and exocytosis at the basolateral surface, to be preserved.    Signaling description: Tight junctions are represented by linear co-polymerized molecules of occluding, claudins, and junctional adhesion molecules that have a high affinity for the C-terminal sequences of PDZ domain-containing cytoplasmic proteins. Claudins have two extracellular loops and four hydrophobic transmembrane domains. The extracellular loops vary in different claudins and may additionally contribute to the formation of ion-selective channels. The proteins that form a complex with the C-terminus of claudins include TJP1, TJP2, TJP3, MPDZ, MLLT4, EPB41, CSDA, INADL, and spectrin/fodrin. INADL and MPDZ interact with CRB1 and CRB3 to form a tight junction complex involved in epithelial cell polarity. The PARD3/PARD6A/ atypical PKC/CRB/INADL/TIAM1 polarity complex interacts with GTP-bound CDC42, a key modulator of the actin cytoskeleton, polarizing membrane transport and tight junction assembly. Furthermore, atypical PKC, PARD6A, and LLGL1 form another multi-protein complex that is required for regulating direct protein trafficking.    Outcome effects: Tight junctions regulate epithelial proliferation by different molecular mechanisms which generally suppress proliferation and increase cell density. TJP1 regulates proliferation and interacts with CSDA which regulates G1/S phase progression by the reduction of nuclear CDK4. CSDA functions in the transcriptional regulation of cell cycle regulators. TJP2 enters the nucleus in proliferating cells and subsequently binds with mRNA-binding proteins. SAFB binds Jun/Fos to regulate epithelial cell proliferation and differentiation." EXACT []

