xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The extracellular matrix (ECM), a complex meshwork of proteins and proteoglycans, provides structure and support for cells and tissues. ECM can have properties of a soft gel (eyes), a polymer fiber rope (ligaments), or a rock-hard composite (bones).    Signaling description: ECM proteins are grouped as glycoproteins, proteoglycans, and collagens and the majority of them are collagen, fibronectin (FN1), and laminin. Glycosaminoglycans and polysaccharides can covalently attach to the core proteins. ECM polymerization is contingent on the synthesizing activity of fibroblasts and osteoblasts. The degradation of the ECM is a critical process during cell morphogenesis, differentiation, cell migration, and apoptosis. Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) are a group of 23 related proteinases that have a central role in the degradation of ECM. The activity of MMPs can be regulated by nitric oxide (NO) and by TIMPs which inhibit MMP activity. In addition, MMPs are nonspecifically inhibited by their association with A2M and once in a complex, MMPs can no longer bind to substrate and are readily cleared by endocytosis through LRP1. TFPI2 has also been described as an MMP binding agent.    Outcome effects: RECK inhibits MMPs which suppresses the invasion of tissues, metastasis, and tumor angiogenesis. This interaction is required for normal development. ECM degradation is often accomplished via protrusions of the plasma membrane known as podosomes or invadopodia. These cellular structures are responsible for cell invasion by degradation of the ECM barriers. Podosomes are composed of an F-actin core, surrounded by actin-associated proteins, signal transduction proteins, and MMPs which include membrane-bound MMPs such as MMP14 and secreted MMPs such as MMP2 and MMP9. FAP, in association with DPP4, is involved in the pericellular proteolysis of the ECM as well as the migration and invasion of endothelial cells into the ECM."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The extracellular matrix (ECM), a complex meshwork of proteins and proteoglycans, provides structure and support for cells and tissues. ECM can have properties of a soft gel (eyes), a polymer fiber rope (ligaments), or a rock-hard composite (bones).    Signaling description: ECM proteins are grouped as glycoproteins, proteoglycans, and collagens and the majority of them are collagen, fibronectin (FN1), and laminin. Glycosaminoglycans and polysaccharides can covalently attach to the core proteins. ECM polymerization is contingent on the synthesizing activity of fibroblasts and osteoblasts. The degradation of the ECM is a critical process during cell morphogenesis, differentiation, cell migration, and apoptosis. Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) are a group of 23 related proteinases that have a central role in the degradation of ECM. The activity of MMPs can be regulated by nitric oxide (NO) and by TIMPs which inhibit MMP activity. In addition, MMPs are nonspecifically inhibited by their association with A2M and once in a complex, MMPs can no longer bind to substrate and are readily cleared by endocytosis through LRP1. TFPI2 has also been described as an MMP binding agent.    Outcome effects: RECK inhibits MMPs which suppresses the invasion of tissues, metastasis, and tumor angiogenesis. This interaction is required for normal development. ECM degradation is often accomplished via protrusions of the plasma membrane known as podosomes or invadopodia. These cellular structures are responsible for cell invasion by degradation of the ECM barriers. Podosomes are composed of an F-actin core, surrounded by actin-associated proteins, signal transduction proteins, and MMPs which include membrane-bound MMPs such as MMP14 and secreted MMPs such as MMP2 and MMP9. FAP, in association with DPP4, is involved in the pericellular proteolysis of the ECM as well as the migration and invasion of endothelial cells into the ECM." EXACT []

