synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The focal adhesions are large protein complexes that connect the cell cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix (ECM).    Signaling description: Focal adhesions are formed around a transmembrane core of an alpha/beta integrin heterodimer which binds to a component of the ECM on its extracellular region, constitutes the site of anchorage of the actin cytoskeletons to the cytoplasmic side of the membrane, and mediates various intracellular signaling pathways. Integrins bind to ECM proteins such as fibronectin (FN1), laminin, vitronectin (VTN), or collagen. The intracellular domain of integrin binds to the cytoskeleton via adapter proteins such as TLN1, ACTN1, filamin, VCL, and PXN. TLN1, a key component of focal adhesions, is required for the initial link between integrins and the cytoskeleton.    Outcome effects: TLN1 binding to the integrin cytoplasmic tail promotes focal adhesion clustering. Integrin adapter proteins, including filamin and ITGB1BP1, regulate integrin activation at the plasma membrane. Filamin binding inhibits cell migration and is also implicated in the sensing of mechanical forces and adhesion strengthening. Tyrosine phosphorylation is required for focal adhesion assembly. The two major kinases found in focal adhesions are PTK2 and SRC. Phosphorylated PIP5K1C binds to TLN1 and inhibits its ability to bind integrin. This, along with the phosphorylation of integrin leads to focal adhesion disassembly. PTPN12 is a major phosphatase found in focal adhesions. BCAR1 and PXN function as signaling scaffolds for the components of focal adhesions. Calpain (CAPN1), through the interaction with PTK2, cleaves several proteins found within focal adhesions."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The focal adhesions are large protein complexes that connect the cell cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix (ECM).    Signaling description: Focal adhesions are formed around a transmembrane core of an alpha/beta integrin heterodimer which binds to a component of the ECM on its extracellular region, constitutes the site of anchorage of the actin cytoskeletons to the cytoplasmic side of the membrane, and mediates various intracellular signaling pathways. Integrins bind to ECM proteins such as fibronectin (FN1), laminin, vitronectin (VTN), or collagen. The intracellular domain of integrin binds to the cytoskeleton via adapter proteins such as TLN1, ACTN1, filamin, VCL, and PXN. TLN1, a key component of focal adhesions, is required for the initial link between integrins and the cytoskeleton.    Outcome effects: TLN1 binding to the integrin cytoplasmic tail promotes focal adhesion clustering. Integrin adapter proteins, including filamin and ITGB1BP1, regulate integrin activation at the plasma membrane. Filamin binding inhibits cell migration and is also implicated in the sensing of mechanical forces and adhesion strengthening. Tyrosine phosphorylation is required for focal adhesion assembly. The two major kinases found in focal adhesions are PTK2 and SRC. Phosphorylated PIP5K1C binds to TLN1 and inhibits its ability to bind integrin. This, along with the phosphorylation of integrin leads to focal adhesion disassembly. PTPN12 is a major phosphatase found in focal adhesions. BCAR1 and PXN function as signaling scaffolds for the components of focal adhesions. Calpain (CAPN1), through the interaction with PTK2, cleaves several proteins found within focal adhesions." EXACT []

