xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Desmosomes are molecular complexes that attach the cell surface adhesion proteins to intracellular keratin cytoskeletal filaments. Desmosomes are the most common type of intercellular junction in vertebrate epithelial cells that link the intermediate filament network of one cell to its neighbors.    Signaling description: The cell adhesion proteins of the desmosome, desmoglein (DSG) and desmocollin (DSC), are members of the cadherin family that bridge the space between adjacent epithelial cells by way of homophilic binding of their extracellular domains to other desmosomal cadherins on the adjacent cell. Both of them have five extracellular domains and calcium-binding motifs. The extracellular domain of the desmosome is called the extracellular core domain (ECD) and contains DSG and DSC proteins bound to each other.    Outcome effects: There are two dense structures on the cytoplasmic side of the plasma membrane, the outer dense plaque (ODP) and the inner dense plaque (IDP). At the ODP, the cytoplasmic domains of the cadherins attach to desmoplakin (DSP) via plakoglobin (JUP) and plakophilin (PKP). The IDP is where DSP attaches to the intermediate filaments of the cell. JUP and PKP may bind to other members of the plakin family of cytolinkers which also includes plectin (PLEC), envoplakin (EVPL), and periplakin (PPL)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Desmosomes are molecular complexes that attach the cell surface adhesion proteins to intracellular keratin cytoskeletal filaments. Desmosomes are the most common type of intercellular junction in vertebrate epithelial cells that link the intermediate filament network of one cell to its neighbors.    Signaling description: The cell adhesion proteins of the desmosome, desmoglein (DSG) and desmocollin (DSC), are members of the cadherin family that bridge the space between adjacent epithelial cells by way of homophilic binding of their extracellular domains to other desmosomal cadherins on the adjacent cell. Both of them have five extracellular domains and calcium-binding motifs. The extracellular domain of the desmosome is called the extracellular core domain (ECD) and contains DSG and DSC proteins bound to each other.    Outcome effects: There are two dense structures on the cytoplasmic side of the plasma membrane, the outer dense plaque (ODP) and the inner dense plaque (IDP). At the ODP, the cytoplasmic domains of the cadherins attach to desmoplakin (DSP) via plakoglobin (JUP) and plakophilin (PKP). The IDP is where DSP attaches to the intermediate filaments of the cell. JUP and PKP may bind to other members of the plakin family of cytolinkers which also includes plectin (PLEC), envoplakin (EVPL), and periplakin (PPL)." EXACT []

