xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Junctions between adjacent cells include gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, or tight junctions. The tight junctions are located at the uppermost portion of the lateral plasma membrane apical to adherens junctions, and serve to hold cells together, act as a barrier, and prevent the lateral diffusion of membrane proteins between the apical and lateral/basal surfaces which allows the specialized functions of each cell surface, endocytosis at the apical surface and exocytosis at the basolateral surface, to be preserved.    Signaling description: Tight junctions are represented by linear co-polymerized molecules of occluding, claudins, and junctional adhesion molecules that have a high affinity for the C-terminal sequences of PDZ domain-containing cytoplasmic proteins. Junctional adhesion molecules (JAMs) are found at tight junctions of polarized epithelial and endothelial cells. JAMs are characterized by the molecular structure composed of two extracellular immunoglobulin loops, a single transmembrane domain, and a cytoplasmic domain with a PDZ binding motif. In addition, other cell adhesion regulators such as TJP1, CSDA, EPB41, MLLT4, spectrin/fodrin, TJAP1, CGN, CASK, MAGI1, F-actin, and myosin also form plaques near the cytoplasmic domains of JAMs to promote firm adhesions.    Outcome effects: The PARD3/PARD6A/ atypical PKC complex that is adjacent to JAMs regulates epithelial polarization. PARD3 regulates tight junction assembly through a PARD6A/ atypical PKC-independent mechanism by regulating RAC1 activation through TIAM1 to induce F-actin/myosin binding and cell adhesion. Atypical PKC and CDC42 regulate vesicular trafficking, organization of the microtubule network, and polarized membrane traffic. The JAMs/CGN complex contributes to the regulation of RHOA signaling and this influences cell migration and cell cycle progression."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Junctions between adjacent cells include gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, or tight junctions. The tight junctions are located at the uppermost portion of the lateral plasma membrane apical to adherens junctions, and serve to hold cells together, act as a barrier, and prevent the lateral diffusion of membrane proteins between the apical and lateral/basal surfaces which allows the specialized functions of each cell surface, endocytosis at the apical surface and exocytosis at the basolateral surface, to be preserved.    Signaling description: Tight junctions are represented by linear co-polymerized molecules of occluding, claudins, and junctional adhesion molecules that have a high affinity for the C-terminal sequences of PDZ domain-containing cytoplasmic proteins. Junctional adhesion molecules (JAMs) are found at tight junctions of polarized epithelial and endothelial cells. JAMs are characterized by the molecular structure composed of two extracellular immunoglobulin loops, a single transmembrane domain, and a cytoplasmic domain with a PDZ binding motif. In addition, other cell adhesion regulators such as TJP1, CSDA, EPB41, MLLT4, spectrin/fodrin, TJAP1, CGN, CASK, MAGI1, F-actin, and myosin also form plaques near the cytoplasmic domains of JAMs to promote firm adhesions.    Outcome effects: The PARD3/PARD6A/ atypical PKC complex that is adjacent to JAMs regulates epithelial polarization. PARD3 regulates tight junction assembly through a PARD6A/ atypical PKC-independent mechanism by regulating RAC1 activation through TIAM1 to induce F-actin/myosin binding and cell adhesion. Atypical PKC and CDC42 regulate vesicular trafficking, organization of the microtubule network, and polarized membrane traffic. The JAMs/CGN complex contributes to the regulation of RHOA signaling and this influences cell migration and cell cycle progression." EXACT []

