xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The hemidesmosomes are multi-protein adhesion complexes that provide the attachment of basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane, a meshwork of extracellular macromolecules separating the dermis from the epidermis. The hemidesmosomal protein complex is responsible for constant adhesion between the epidermis and the underlying dermis and provides resistance to mechanical stress.    Signaling description: Hemidesmosomes contain ITGA6, ITGB4, COL17A1, and CD151 and the two plakin family members, PLEC and DST. It is the complex of ITGA6 and ITGB4 integrins that connects the cells to a major component of the basement membrane laminin-332. The cytoplasmic proteins PLEC and DST connect to the intermediate filaments, thereby creating a stable anchoring complex. CD151 is an early component of hemidesmosomal structures strongly associated with another laminin-binding integrin on keratinocytes, VLA-3 receptor (integrin). Although the VLA-3 receptor does not appear to participate directly in hemidesmosome assembly, it may serve as a nucleation site for the assembly of hemidesmosomes by ITGA6 and ITGB4.    Outcome effects: ERBB2IP may have a general role in the establishment and maintenance of cell-cell and cell-basement membrane adhesions. In studies considering carcinoma cell invasion of keratinocyte migration, hemidesmosomes are broken down and their components are redistributed. The requirements for the regulation of each assembly and disassembly step are still unknown. Serine phosphorylation of the ITGB4 cytoplasmic domain by PKC is involved in the disassembly of hemidesmosomes in keratinocytes to initiate migration."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The hemidesmosomes are multi-protein adhesion complexes that provide the attachment of basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane, a meshwork of extracellular macromolecules separating the dermis from the epidermis. The hemidesmosomal protein complex is responsible for constant adhesion between the epidermis and the underlying dermis and provides resistance to mechanical stress.    Signaling description: Hemidesmosomes contain ITGA6, ITGB4, COL17A1, and CD151 and the two plakin family members, PLEC and DST. It is the complex of ITGA6 and ITGB4 integrins that connects the cells to a major component of the basement membrane laminin-332. The cytoplasmic proteins PLEC and DST connect to the intermediate filaments, thereby creating a stable anchoring complex. CD151 is an early component of hemidesmosomal structures strongly associated with another laminin-binding integrin on keratinocytes, VLA-3 receptor (integrin). Although the VLA-3 receptor does not appear to participate directly in hemidesmosome assembly, it may serve as a nucleation site for the assembly of hemidesmosomes by ITGA6 and ITGB4.    Outcome effects: ERBB2IP may have a general role in the establishment and maintenance of cell-cell and cell-basement membrane adhesions. In studies considering carcinoma cell invasion of keratinocyte migration, hemidesmosomes are broken down and their components are redistributed. The requirements for the regulation of each assembly and disassembly step are still unknown. Serine phosphorylation of the ITGB4 cytoplasmic domain by PKC is involved in the disassembly of hemidesmosomes in keratinocytes to initiate migration." EXACT []

