xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The SCF complex (SKP, Cullin, F-box containing complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in cell cycle and also marks various other cellular proteins for destruction. The SCF complex consists of four components: three constant subunits SKP1, RBX1 and CUL1, and a variable subunit, the F-box-protein. CUL1 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the subunits RBX1 and SKP1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and SKP1 binds to the F-box-protein. Each F-box protein binds to a specific subset of substrate proteins, promoting their degradation. In SCF/CCNF complex, CCNF (FBXO1) acts as the substrate recognition F-box protein.    Signaling description: Through a specific subset of substrate proteins, the core of SCF/CCNF related signaling leads to regulation of DNA repair, centrosome duplication and spindle assembly. The precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: During G2 phase, SCF/CCNF targets and degrades the CP110 to limit centrosome duplication to once per cell cycle. SCF/CCNF promotes the degradation of NUSAP1, a protein involved in mitotic spindle organization. Also, SCF/CCNF mediates the degradation of RRM2 in G2 phase prevents genome instability.    Animal data: Role of SCF/CCNF in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The SCF complex (SKP, Cullin, F-box containing complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in cell cycle and also marks various other cellular proteins for destruction. The SCF complex consists of four components: three constant subunits SKP1, RBX1 and CUL1, and a variable subunit, the F-box-protein. CUL1 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the subunits RBX1 and SKP1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and SKP1 binds to the F-box-protein. Each F-box protein binds to a specific subset of substrate proteins, promoting their degradation. In SCF/CCNF complex, CCNF (FBXO1) acts as the substrate recognition F-box protein.    Signaling description: Through a specific subset of substrate proteins, the core of SCF/CCNF related signaling leads to regulation of DNA repair, centrosome duplication and spindle assembly. The precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: During G2 phase, SCF/CCNF targets and degrades the CP110 to limit centrosome duplication to once per cell cycle. SCF/CCNF promotes the degradation of NUSAP1, a protein involved in mitotic spindle organization. Also, SCF/CCNF mediates the degradation of RRM2 in G2 phase prevents genome instability.    Animal data: Role of SCF/CCNF in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments." EXACT []

