xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The Anaphase-Promoting Complex/Cyclosome (APC/C) is an E3 ubiquitin ligase that marks target cell cycle proteins for degradation by the 26S proteasome. Many proteins of the APC/C still have unknown functions. APC/C is basically organized into three structural domains, called the 'platform', the 'catalytic' and the 'TPR-arm, lobe or arc lamp'. The platform subcomplex (ANAPC1, ANAPC4, ANAPC5, and ANAPC15) forms a base to join the other subunits of the APC/C. The catalytic core subcomplex (ANAPC2, ANAPC11, and ANAPC10) with E2 enzyme can provide ubiquitylation activity to the APC/C. The TPR-arm consists of several structurally related proteins with multiple tetratricopeptide (TPR) repeats (ANAPC7, ANAPC13, ANAPC16, CDC16, CDC23, CDC26, CDC27) and is required to coordinate assembly of the APC/C and mediate important interactions with regulator proteins that modulate APC/C activity.     Signaling description: The core of APC/C-CDC20 complex related signaling through specific subset of substrate proteins leads to metaphase/anaphase transition. Precise mechanism of the molecular signaling on this pathway is partially known and is associated with activation of CDC20._    Outcome effects: Activating regulator protein CDC20 associates with the phosphorylated APC/C in early mitosis. As metaphase begins, the spindle checkpoint inhibits the APC/C until all sister-kinetochores are attached to opposite poles of the mitotic spindle. CDK1 phosphorylates subunits on APC/C that promotes binding to CDC20. The APC/C-CDC20 complex ubiquitinates PTTG1 (securin) and CCNB1 (cyclin B1) for degradation. Once PTTG1 is degraded, ESPL1 (separin) is released and cleaves RAD21. In turn, RAD21 is bound to a member of the cohesin complex (STAG1, STAG2, SMC1A, SMC3) which regulates separation of sister chromatids. Subsequent to cohesin degradation, the sister chromatids are separated and metaphase proceeds to anaphase. When the spindle assembly checkpoint is activated, CDC20 is inhibited in a MAD2L1-dependent process.     Animal data: Role of APC/C-CDH1 in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The Anaphase-Promoting Complex/Cyclosome (APC/C) is an E3 ubiquitin ligase that marks target cell cycle proteins for degradation by the 26S proteasome. Many proteins of the APC/C still have unknown functions. APC/C is basically organized into three structural domains, called the 'platform', the 'catalytic' and the 'TPR-arm, lobe or arc lamp'. The platform subcomplex (ANAPC1, ANAPC4, ANAPC5, and ANAPC15) forms a base to join the other subunits of the APC/C. The catalytic core subcomplex (ANAPC2, ANAPC11, and ANAPC10) with E2 enzyme can provide ubiquitylation activity to the APC/C. The TPR-arm consists of several structurally related proteins with multiple tetratricopeptide (TPR) repeats (ANAPC7, ANAPC13, ANAPC16, CDC16, CDC23, CDC26, CDC27) and is required to coordinate assembly of the APC/C and mediate important interactions with regulator proteins that modulate APC/C activity.     Signaling description: The core of APC/C-CDC20 complex related signaling through specific subset of substrate proteins leads to metaphase/anaphase transition. Precise mechanism of the molecular signaling on this pathway is partially known and is associated with activation of CDC20._    Outcome effects: Activating regulator protein CDC20 associates with the phosphorylated APC/C in early mitosis. As metaphase begins, the spindle checkpoint inhibits the APC/C until all sister-kinetochores are attached to opposite poles of the mitotic spindle. CDK1 phosphorylates subunits on APC/C that promotes binding to CDC20. The APC/C-CDC20 complex ubiquitinates PTTG1 (securin) and CCNB1 (cyclin B1) for degradation. Once PTTG1 is degraded, ESPL1 (separin) is released and cleaves RAD21. In turn, RAD21 is bound to a member of the cohesin complex (STAG1, STAG2, SMC1A, SMC3) which regulates separation of sister chromatids. Subsequent to cohesin degradation, the sister chromatids are separated and metaphase proceeds to anaphase. When the spindle assembly checkpoint is activated, CDC20 is inhibited in a MAD2L1-dependent process.     Animal data: Role of APC/C-CDH1 in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments" EXACT []

