xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The CRL complex (Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligase, SCF4 complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in the cell cycle progression, apoptosis, chromatin remodeling and DNA damage response.The SCF4 complex consists of four components: three constant subunits CUL4, DDB1 and RBX, and a variable subunit which includes substrate-recognition proteins. CUL4 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the RING-finger protein RBX1 and adaptor protein DDB1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and DDB1 binds to the substrate-recognition proteins. Each substrate-recognition protein binds to a specific substrate protein. In the CUL4/CRL complex, WDR5, DTL, DDB2 and RFWD2 act as the substrate recognition proteins. DDB1 can bind a substrate directly, and can function as both an adaptor and a substrate-recognition protein._    Signaling description: The core of CUL4/CRL related signaling through specific subset of substrate proteins regulates the cell cycle progression and DNA damage response. Precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: CUL4/CRL targets and degrades the DTL, a protein that controls cell cycle progression, DNA damage and DNA repair via CDKN1A, CDT1 and PCNA. Degradation of DDB2 and RFWD2 leads to control of DNA repair. CUL4/CRL complex degrades WDR5, a protein involved in a variety of cellular processes, including cell cycle progression and chromatin remodeling through the interaction of the HDAC1/2/3. CUL4/CRL can also promote degradation of DET1 required to apoptosis and chromatin remodeling.    Animal data: Role of CUL4/CRL in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The CRL complex (Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligase, SCF4 complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in the cell cycle progression, apoptosis, chromatin remodeling and DNA damage response.The SCF4 complex consists of four components: three constant subunits CUL4, DDB1 and RBX, and a variable subunit which includes substrate-recognition proteins. CUL4 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the RING-finger protein RBX1 and adaptor protein DDB1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and DDB1 binds to the substrate-recognition proteins. Each substrate-recognition protein binds to a specific substrate protein. In the CUL4/CRL complex, WDR5, DTL, DDB2 and RFWD2 act as the substrate recognition proteins. DDB1 can bind a substrate directly, and can function as both an adaptor and a substrate-recognition protein._    Signaling description: The core of CUL4/CRL related signaling through specific subset of substrate proteins regulates the cell cycle progression and DNA damage response. Precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: CUL4/CRL targets and degrades the DTL, a protein that controls cell cycle progression, DNA damage and DNA repair via CDKN1A, CDT1 and PCNA. Degradation of DDB2 and RFWD2 leads to control of DNA repair. CUL4/CRL complex degrades WDR5, a protein involved in a variety of cellular processes, including cell cycle progression and chromatin remodeling through the interaction of the HDAC1/2/3. CUL4/CRL can also promote degradation of DET1 required to apoptosis and chromatin remodeling.    Animal data: Role of CUL4/CRL in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments." EXACT []

