xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: DNA in eukaryotic cells is packaged into chromatin, a complex structure in which DNA is associated with histones and a multitude of other proteins. SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex contains either BRG1 (SMARCA4) or BRM (SMARCA2) as the central catalytic ATPase subunit. SWI/SNF can further be subdivided into the BAF and PBAF (polybromo-associated BAF) complexes. The only difference between these complexes is the presence of specific subunits ARID1A or PBRM1, found in BAF or PBAF, respectively.    Signaling description: A variety of mechanisms can be used to remodel chromatin; some act locally on a single nucleosome and others act more broadly. Chromatin remodeling can be accomplished by covalent modification of histones or by the action of ATP-dependent remodeling complexes. SWI/SNF complex disrupts the rotational phasing of DNA on the surface of the histone octamer in an ATP-dependent manner and then targets promoters through the interactions with sequence-specific transcription factors such as glucocorticoid receptor (NR3C1).    Outcome effects: SMARCA4 (or SMARCA2) can recognize acetylated lysine residues within histone H3 and H4 tails. SMARCA4 (or SMARCA2) associates with numerous chromatin-modifying complexes including co-activators and co-repressors of transcription."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: DNA in eukaryotic cells is packaged into chromatin, a complex structure in which DNA is associated with histones and a multitude of other proteins. SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex contains either BRG1 (SMARCA4) or BRM (SMARCA2) as the central catalytic ATPase subunit. SWI/SNF can further be subdivided into the BAF and PBAF (polybromo-associated BAF) complexes. The only difference between these complexes is the presence of specific subunits ARID1A or PBRM1, found in BAF or PBAF, respectively.    Signaling description: A variety of mechanisms can be used to remodel chromatin; some act locally on a single nucleosome and others act more broadly. Chromatin remodeling can be accomplished by covalent modification of histones or by the action of ATP-dependent remodeling complexes. SWI/SNF complex disrupts the rotational phasing of DNA on the surface of the histone octamer in an ATP-dependent manner and then targets promoters through the interactions with sequence-specific transcription factors such as glucocorticoid receptor (NR3C1).    Outcome effects: SMARCA4 (or SMARCA2) can recognize acetylated lysine residues within histone H3 and H4 tails. SMARCA4 (or SMARCA2) associates with numerous chromatin-modifying complexes including co-activators and co-repressors of transcription." EXACT []

