xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The SCF complex (SKP, Cullin, F-box containing complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in cell cycle and also marks various other cellular proteins for destruction. The SCF complex consists of four components: three constant subunits SKP1, RBX1 and CUL1, and a variable subunit, the F-box-protein. CUL1 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the subunits RBX1 and SKP1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and SKP1 binds to the F-box-protein. Each F-box protein binds to a specific subset of substrate proteins, promoting their degradation. In SCF/BTRC complex, BTRC (FBXW1) acts as a substrate recognition F-box protein.    Signaling description: Through a specific subset of substrate proteins, the core SCF/BTRC related signaling regulates the cell cycle progression, apoptosis and autophagy. The precise mechanism of the molecular signaling on this pathway is well known and is associated with ubiquitination and degradation of WEE1, FBXO5, CDC25A, CLSPN, DEPTOR, MDM2, ATF4, NFKBIA, CTNNB1, REST. In addition, SCF/BTRC may regulate circadian rhythm. The precise mechanism of circadian rhythm regulation through degradation of PER1/2 is poorly understood.    Outcome effects: SCF/BTRC targets and degrades the WEE1, FBXO5, CDC25A, and CLSPN which regulate cyclins/CDK1 complex and thus, causes G2/M transition. SCF/BTRC promotes the ubiquitination of PDCD4, MDM2, ATF4, NFKBIA and CTNNB1 which leads to block of apoptosis. SCF/BTRC regulates autophagy by controlling the ubiquitination and destruction of DEPTOR, an endogenous inhibitor of the MTOR pathway.    Animal data: Role of SCF/BTRC in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The SCF complex (SKP, Cullin, F-box containing complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in cell cycle and also marks various other cellular proteins for destruction. The SCF complex consists of four components: three constant subunits SKP1, RBX1 and CUL1, and a variable subunit, the F-box-protein. CUL1 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the subunits RBX1 and SKP1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and SKP1 binds to the F-box-protein. Each F-box protein binds to a specific subset of substrate proteins, promoting their degradation. In SCF/BTRC complex, BTRC (FBXW1) acts as a substrate recognition F-box protein.    Signaling description: Through a specific subset of substrate proteins, the core SCF/BTRC related signaling regulates the cell cycle progression, apoptosis and autophagy. The precise mechanism of the molecular signaling on this pathway is well known and is associated with ubiquitination and degradation of WEE1, FBXO5, CDC25A, CLSPN, DEPTOR, MDM2, ATF4, NFKBIA, CTNNB1, REST. In addition, SCF/BTRC may regulate circadian rhythm. The precise mechanism of circadian rhythm regulation through degradation of PER1/2 is poorly understood.    Outcome effects: SCF/BTRC targets and degrades the WEE1, FBXO5, CDC25A, and CLSPN which regulate cyclins/CDK1 complex and thus, causes G2/M transition. SCF/BTRC promotes the ubiquitination of PDCD4, MDM2, ATF4, NFKBIA and CTNNB1 which leads to block of apoptosis. SCF/BTRC regulates autophagy by controlling the ubiquitination and destruction of DEPTOR, an endogenous inhibitor of the MTOR pathway.    Animal data: Role of SCF/BTRC in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments." EXACT []

