xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The Anaphase-Promoting Complex/Cyclosome (APC/C) is an E3 ubiquitin ligase that marks target cell cycle proteins for degradation by the 26S proteasome. Many proteins of the APC/C still have unknown functions. APC/C is largely organized into three structural domains, called the 'platform', the 'catalytic' and the 'TPR-arm, lobe or arc lamp'. The platform subcomplex (ANAPC1, ANAPC4, ANAPC5, and ANAPC15) forms a base to join the other subunits of the APC/C. The catalytic core subcomplex (ANAPC2, ANAPC11, and ANAPC10) with an E2 enzyme can provide ubiquitylation activity to the APC/C. The TPR-arm consists of several structurally related proteins with multiple tetratricopeptide (TPR) repeats (ANAPC7, ANAPC13, ANAPC16, CDC16, CDC23, CDC26, CDC27) and is required to coordinate assembly of the APC/C and mediate important interactions with regulator proteins which modulate APC/C activity.    Signaling description: The core of APC/C-FZR1 (APC/C-CDH1) complex related signaling leads to M/G1 transition through a specific subset of substrate proteins. The precise mechanism of the molecular signaling on this pathway is partially known and is associated with activation of FZR1 (CDH1).    Outcome effects: In the beginning of the cell cycle FZR1 (CDH1) is phosphorylated and inhibited by CDK1, preventing it from attaching to APC/C. APC/C is then free to attach CDC20 and usher the transition from metaphase to anaphase. As CDK1 gets degraded later in mitosis, CDC20 gets released and FZR1 (CDH1) can bind to APC/C, keeping it activated through the M/G1 transition. APC/C-CDH1 then continues is working in G1 phase and degrading CDC20, AURKA/B, CDC25A, GMNN and SKP2. CCNB1 is accumulated throughout this phase and activates CDK1 leads to inhibition of FZR1 (CDH1) and inactivation of APC/C-CDH1.    Animal data: Role of APC/C-CDH1 in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The Anaphase-Promoting Complex/Cyclosome (APC/C) is an E3 ubiquitin ligase that marks target cell cycle proteins for degradation by the 26S proteasome. Many proteins of the APC/C still have unknown functions. APC/C is largely organized into three structural domains, called the 'platform', the 'catalytic' and the 'TPR-arm, lobe or arc lamp'. The platform subcomplex (ANAPC1, ANAPC4, ANAPC5, and ANAPC15) forms a base to join the other subunits of the APC/C. The catalytic core subcomplex (ANAPC2, ANAPC11, and ANAPC10) with an E2 enzyme can provide ubiquitylation activity to the APC/C. The TPR-arm consists of several structurally related proteins with multiple tetratricopeptide (TPR) repeats (ANAPC7, ANAPC13, ANAPC16, CDC16, CDC23, CDC26, CDC27) and is required to coordinate assembly of the APC/C and mediate important interactions with regulator proteins which modulate APC/C activity.    Signaling description: The core of APC/C-FZR1 (APC/C-CDH1) complex related signaling leads to M/G1 transition through a specific subset of substrate proteins. The precise mechanism of the molecular signaling on this pathway is partially known and is associated with activation of FZR1 (CDH1).    Outcome effects: In the beginning of the cell cycle FZR1 (CDH1) is phosphorylated and inhibited by CDK1, preventing it from attaching to APC/C. APC/C is then free to attach CDC20 and usher the transition from metaphase to anaphase. As CDK1 gets degraded later in mitosis, CDC20 gets released and FZR1 (CDH1) can bind to APC/C, keeping it activated through the M/G1 transition. APC/C-CDH1 then continues is working in G1 phase and degrading CDC20, AURKA/B, CDC25A, GMNN and SKP2. CCNB1 is accumulated throughout this phase and activates CDK1 leads to inhibition of FZR1 (CDH1) and inactivation of APC/C-CDH1.    Animal data: Role of APC/C-CDH1 in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments." EXACT []

