xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The SCF complex (SKP, Cullin, F-box containing complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in cell cycle and also marks various other cellular proteins for destruction. The SCF complex consists of four components: three constant subunits SKP1, RBX1 and CUL1, and a variable subunit, the F-box-protein. CUL1 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the subunits RBX1 and SKP1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and SKP1 binds to the F-box-protein. Each F-box protein binds to a specific substrate proteins. In SCF/SKP2 complex, SKP2 acts as the substrate recognition F-box protein.    Signaling description: Through a specific subset of substrate proteins, the core of SCF/SKP2 related signaling leads to regulation of cell cycle progression, apoptosis and DNA damage. The precise mechanism of molecular signaling depicted on this pathway is well known and is associated with CDKN1A, CDKN1B, CDKN1C, CCNE1, CCND1, FOXO1/3 and NBN protein recognition.    Outcome effects: SCF/SKP2 targets and degrades the nuclear pools of CDKN1A, CDKN1B, CDKN1C and CDKN1D bound to cyclins/CDKs and causes G1/S transition. Degradation of FOXO1/3 and NBN by SCF/SKP2 leads to block of apoptosis. Disruption of this proteolytic system can lead to genomic instability and cancer.    Animal data: Role of SCF/SKP2 in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The SCF complex (SKP, Cullin, F-box containing complex) is a multi-protein E3-ubiquitin ligase complex catalyzing the ubiquitination of proteins destined for proteasomal degradation. It has important roles in the ubiquitination of proteins involved in cell cycle and also marks various other cellular proteins for destruction. The SCF complex consists of four components: three constant subunits SKP1, RBX1 and CUL1, and a variable subunit, the F-box-protein. CUL1 (Cullin) is the core subunit on which the subunits RBX1 and SKP1 are assembled. RBX1 connects to E2 and SKP1 binds to the F-box-protein. Each F-box protein binds to a specific substrate proteins. In SCF/SKP2 complex, SKP2 acts as the substrate recognition F-box protein.    Signaling description: Through a specific subset of substrate proteins, the core of SCF/SKP2 related signaling leads to regulation of cell cycle progression, apoptosis and DNA damage. The precise mechanism of molecular signaling depicted on this pathway is well known and is associated with CDKN1A, CDKN1B, CDKN1C, CCNE1, CCND1, FOXO1/3 and NBN protein recognition.    Outcome effects: SCF/SKP2 targets and degrades the nuclear pools of CDKN1A, CDKN1B, CDKN1C and CDKN1D bound to cyclins/CDKs and causes G1/S transition. Degradation of FOXO1/3 and NBN by SCF/SKP2 leads to block of apoptosis. Disruption of this proteolytic system can lead to genomic instability and cancer.    Animal data: Role of SCF/SKP2 in the signaling was revealed partially from animal model experiments." EXACT []

