xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Actin belongs to a highly conserved eukaryotic protein family which consists of three broad classes including alpha-, beta-, and gamma-isoforms. The highest concentrations of actin are in striated muscles; however, there are significant quantities of actin present in nonmuscle cells where it has a variety of functions including myosin-independent changes of cell shape, motor-based organelle transport, regulation of ion transport, and receptor-mediated responses of the cell to external signals.    Signaling description: Actin exists in two principal forms: globular monomeric (G-actin) and filamentous polymeric (F-actin). G-actin contains an ATP binding site in the center of the molecule and can form dimers or trimers that serve as sites for nucleation and growth of F-actin. Actin binds to a significant number of proteins collectively known as actin-binding proteins (ABPs) which bind to actin filaments and modulate their properties and functions. ABPs can be classified into several groups depending upon their activity towards actin. Cross-linking proteins, such as actin related protein 2/3 complex, ACTN1, FSCN1, and filamin, force the elongation of filament bundles, branching of filaments, and formation of three-dimensional networks. Actin related protein 2/3 complex binds to the side of ATP-F-actin near the growing, or barbed end, of the filament and causes nucleation of a new F-actin branch. In addition, WASP-family proteins, including WASF1, bring G-actin and actin related protein 2/3 complex together to regulate elongation while monomer-binding proteins bind to and prevent the polymerization of G-actin. Some examples of monomer-binding proteins include profilin, thymosin, and ABP-50.    Outcome effects: The filament-severing proteins such as GSN and CFL1/2 shorten the average length of filaments by binding to the side of F-actin and cutting it into two pieces. Filament end-binding proteins, such as CAPZA1, cap the ends of F-actin and prevent the exchange of monomers. Lastly, stabilizing proteins, including tropomyosin and tropomodulin, prevent actin depolymerization by binding to the sides of F-actin. The actin cytoskeleton may be rearranged in response to the activation of CDC42 and Rac signal transduction pathways which leads to the formation of filopodia and lamellipodia, respectively."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Actin belongs to a highly conserved eukaryotic protein family which consists of three broad classes including alpha-, beta-, and gamma-isoforms. The highest concentrations of actin are in striated muscles; however, there are significant quantities of actin present in nonmuscle cells where it has a variety of functions including myosin-independent changes of cell shape, motor-based organelle transport, regulation of ion transport, and receptor-mediated responses of the cell to external signals.    Signaling description: Actin exists in two principal forms: globular monomeric (G-actin) and filamentous polymeric (F-actin). G-actin contains an ATP binding site in the center of the molecule and can form dimers or trimers that serve as sites for nucleation and growth of F-actin. Actin binds to a significant number of proteins collectively known as actin-binding proteins (ABPs) which bind to actin filaments and modulate their properties and functions. ABPs can be classified into several groups depending upon their activity towards actin. Cross-linking proteins, such as actin related protein 2/3 complex, ACTN1, FSCN1, and filamin, force the elongation of filament bundles, branching of filaments, and formation of three-dimensional networks. Actin related protein 2/3 complex binds to the side of ATP-F-actin near the growing, or barbed end, of the filament and causes nucleation of a new F-actin branch. In addition, WASP-family proteins, including WASF1, bring G-actin and actin related protein 2/3 complex together to regulate elongation while monomer-binding proteins bind to and prevent the polymerization of G-actin. Some examples of monomer-binding proteins include profilin, thymosin, and ABP-50.    Outcome effects: The filament-severing proteins such as GSN and CFL1/2 shorten the average length of filaments by binding to the side of F-actin and cutting it into two pieces. Filament end-binding proteins, such as CAPZA1, cap the ends of F-actin and prevent the exchange of monomers. Lastly, stabilizing proteins, including tropomyosin and tropomodulin, prevent actin depolymerization by binding to the sides of F-actin. The actin cytoskeleton may be rearranged in response to the activation of CDC42 and Rac signal transduction pathways which leads to the formation of filopodia and lamellipodia, respectively." EXACT []

