xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The actin filaments associated with myosin are required for many types of cell movements.    Signaling description: Myosins are motor proteins that travel toward the plus end of the actin filaments in an ATP-dependent fashion. Myosin II, a conventional two-headed myosin, has an important role in muscle contractions. The sarcomere or unit of contraction is composed of parallel arrays of actin filaments (thin filaments) and bundles of myosin II (thick filaments). A cross-bridge between the thick and thin filaments is formed by the head group of myosin binding to actin. In contrast to myosin II, other types of myosin do not form filaments and therefore are not involved in contraction. Myosin I can attach to an organelle or to the plasma membrane and move it along the actin filament. In addition to myosin I and II, at least 12 other classes of unconventional myosins (III through XIV) have been identified but their functions have yet to be determined.    Outcome effects: Troponin complex is essential for muscle contraction for skeletal and cardiac muscles. When calcium (Ca2+) binds to troponin, the positions of troponin and tropomyosin are altered on the thin filament and myosin then has access to its binding site on actin. However, in nonmuscle and smooth muscle cells, contraction is regulated primarily by Ca2+-mediated activation of myosin light-chain kinases."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The actin filaments associated with myosin are required for many types of cell movements.    Signaling description: Myosins are motor proteins that travel toward the plus end of the actin filaments in an ATP-dependent fashion. Myosin II, a conventional two-headed myosin, has an important role in muscle contractions. The sarcomere or unit of contraction is composed of parallel arrays of actin filaments (thin filaments) and bundles of myosin II (thick filaments). A cross-bridge between the thick and thin filaments is formed by the head group of myosin binding to actin. In contrast to myosin II, other types of myosin do not form filaments and therefore are not involved in contraction. Myosin I can attach to an organelle or to the plasma membrane and move it along the actin filament. In addition to myosin I and II, at least 12 other classes of unconventional myosins (III through XIV) have been identified but their functions have yet to be determined.    Outcome effects: Troponin complex is essential for muscle contraction for skeletal and cardiac muscles. When calcium (Ca2+) binds to troponin, the positions of troponin and tropomyosin are altered on the thin filament and myosin then has access to its binding site on actin. However, in nonmuscle and smooth muscle cells, contraction is regulated primarily by Ca2+-mediated activation of myosin light-chain kinases." EXACT []

