xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intermediate filaments, which are long molecules composed of many monomers, are a diverse group of cytoskeletal proteins that enable animal cells to withstand mechanical stress.    Signaling description: There are five different types of intermediate filaments including Types I and II which contain acidic keratin and basic keratin and Type III which contains vimentin (VIM), desmin (DES), glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP), and peripherin (PRPH). Type IV contains neurofilament H (heavy), M (medium) and L (low) while Type V contains lamins which support inside the inner nuclear membrane. Two monomers twist around one another to form a dimer and two dimers align laterally to form a tetrameric protofilament. The protofilaments then associate in a staggered fashion to form a long protofilament.    Outcome effects: Lastly, the protofilaments are arranged in parallel to form the intermediate filament."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intermediate filaments, which are long molecules composed of many monomers, are a diverse group of cytoskeletal proteins that enable animal cells to withstand mechanical stress.    Signaling description: There are five different types of intermediate filaments including Types I and II which contain acidic keratin and basic keratin and Type III which contains vimentin (VIM), desmin (DES), glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP), and peripherin (PRPH). Type IV contains neurofilament H (heavy), M (medium) and L (low) while Type V contains lamins which support inside the inner nuclear membrane. Two monomers twist around one another to form a dimer and two dimers align laterally to form a tetrameric protofilament. The protofilaments then associate in a staggered fashion to form a long protofilament.    Outcome effects: Lastly, the protofilaments are arranged in parallel to form the intermediate filament." EXACT []

