xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human telomeres are the ends of chromosomes which consist of many kilobases of (TTAGGG)n tandemly-repeated DNA sequences with various associated proteins. Small amounts of these terminal sequences are lost from the tips of the telomeres with each cell division during each S phase of DNA replication.    Signaling description: A cell stops dividing when the telomere length reaches a critical point and the enzyme telomerase can compensate this loss. Mammalian telomerases are composed of the TTAGGG sequence, an RNA molecule TERC, and a catalytic subunit TERT. TERC has an RNA template sequence which pairs with the terminal TTAGGG repeat to initiate elongation of the 3' DNA end. In a single elongation, only one repeat of the sequence can be added. Circularly-permuted sequence GGTTAG terminates the synthesis. TERT determines the availability of the telomerase function. The phosphorylation status of TERT is involved in the modulation of the catalytic activity of telomerase. In mammals, a variety of proteins like DKC1, TEP1, and HNRNPA1 assist in telomerase assembly and facilitate interactions between telomerase and the telomere. Telomeric structural proteins interact with the telomere and regulate the opening and closing of the free telomere end.    Outcome effects: Telomeres that end in a 3' overhang (G-strand overhang) bend back and anneal with the complementary sequences in the 5' end of the opposite strand. This creates a telomere T-loop which is stabilized by a set of specialized proteins such as TERF1, TERF2, TINF2, and TNKS. Complex interactions between these proteins, telomeric DNA, and other cellular factors are necessary for maintenance of the telomere. MRE11A complex, the Ku complex (XRCC6 and XRCC5), and ATM function in the detection of short telomeres and trigger DNA damage response pathways or the repair of telomere sequences. A telomere that becomes too short fails to protect the chromosome from DNA damage and when the damage occurs, proteins like TP53 respond by discontinuing cell replication and forcing it into senescence."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human telomeres are the ends of chromosomes which consist of many kilobases of (TTAGGG)n tandemly-repeated DNA sequences with various associated proteins. Small amounts of these terminal sequences are lost from the tips of the telomeres with each cell division during each S phase of DNA replication.    Signaling description: A cell stops dividing when the telomere length reaches a critical point and the enzyme telomerase can compensate this loss. Mammalian telomerases are composed of the TTAGGG sequence, an RNA molecule TERC, and a catalytic subunit TERT. TERC has an RNA template sequence which pairs with the terminal TTAGGG repeat to initiate elongation of the 3' DNA end. In a single elongation, only one repeat of the sequence can be added. Circularly-permuted sequence GGTTAG terminates the synthesis. TERT determines the availability of the telomerase function. The phosphorylation status of TERT is involved in the modulation of the catalytic activity of telomerase. In mammals, a variety of proteins like DKC1, TEP1, and HNRNPA1 assist in telomerase assembly and facilitate interactions between telomerase and the telomere. Telomeric structural proteins interact with the telomere and regulate the opening and closing of the free telomere end.    Outcome effects: Telomeres that end in a 3' overhang (G-strand overhang) bend back and anneal with the complementary sequences in the 5' end of the opposite strand. This creates a telomere T-loop which is stabilized by a set of specialized proteins such as TERF1, TERF2, TINF2, and TNKS. Complex interactions between these proteins, telomeric DNA, and other cellular factors are necessary for maintenance of the telomere. MRE11A complex, the Ku complex (XRCC6 and XRCC5), and ATM function in the detection of short telomeres and trigger DNA damage response pathways or the repair of telomere sequences. A telomere that becomes too short fails to protect the chromosome from DNA damage and when the damage occurs, proteins like TP53 respond by discontinuing cell replication and forcing it into senescence." EXACT []

