xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: About 50% of mammalian genome composed of repetitive sequences. Multiple copies of the same sequence can hinder precise chromosomal pairing during mitosis. A transposon or a retroposon that inserts itself into a functional gene will most likely disable that gene.    Signaling description: The most abundant repeats in mammals are the retrotransposons, which self-replicate through the reverse transcription of RNA intermediates.The retrotransposon class includes two subgroups: the LTR (long terminal repeat) type, which is flanked by direct repeats, and the non-LTR type, which includes LINEs (long interspersed elements) and SINEs (short interspersed elements). Mechanisms for the suppression of retrotransposon transcription include cooperation of de novo CpG methylation, histone modification and RNA-based silencing pathways - piRNA and endo-siRNA. The piRNA pathway relies on the specificity to recognize complementary transposable element targets; piRNAs are generated either from RNA transcripts of active transposable elements or from transcripts originating from specialized loci in the genome called piRNA clusters.    Outcome effects: PIWI proteins function at the chromatin level in the nucleus to silence transposable elements transcription by directing deposition of repressive histone marks and DNA methylation to copies of transposable elements.    Animal data: mouse model."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: About 50% of mammalian genome composed of repetitive sequences. Multiple copies of the same sequence can hinder precise chromosomal pairing during mitosis. A transposon or a retroposon that inserts itself into a functional gene will most likely disable that gene.    Signaling description: The most abundant repeats in mammals are the retrotransposons, which self-replicate through the reverse transcription of RNA intermediates.The retrotransposon class includes two subgroups: the LTR (long terminal repeat) type, which is flanked by direct repeats, and the non-LTR type, which includes LINEs (long interspersed elements) and SINEs (short interspersed elements). Mechanisms for the suppression of retrotransposon transcription include cooperation of de novo CpG methylation, histone modification and RNA-based silencing pathways - piRNA and endo-siRNA. The piRNA pathway relies on the specificity to recognize complementary transposable element targets; piRNAs are generated either from RNA transcripts of active transposable elements or from transcripts originating from specialized loci in the genome called piRNA clusters.    Outcome effects: PIWI proteins function at the chromatin level in the nucleus to silence transposable elements transcription by directing deposition of repressive histone marks and DNA methylation to copies of transposable elements.    Animal data: mouse model." EXACT []

