xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In eukaryotes, DNA replication only takes place during S phase of the cell cycle; however, the primary initiation of replication occurs in G1 phase. The binding of the origin of replication recognition complex (ORC) to certain DNA loci called origins of replication is necessary for replication initiation. In the subsequent step of initiation, ORC becomes bound to prereplication complex (pre-RC) that may contain at least 14 distinct proteins conserved in all eukaryotes including CDT1, CDC6, and MCM complex.    Signaling description: When the process of DNA synthesis is activated, initiator complexes promote unwinding of double-stranded DNA and formation of replicative fork. Each parent DNA strand may be the matrix for synthesis of a new complementary strand. When DNA strands are separated, DNA polymerase alpha (POLA1) recruits RNA oligonucleotides (primers) and initiates new DNA chain synthesis. During replication, two growing strands (leading and lagging) are formed by different mechanisms of DNA synthesis. For the leading strand formation, one RNA primer per one active origin of replication is used, whereas the lagging strand synthesis requires several RNA primers to produce separate serial fragments of complementary DNA (Okazaki fragments). DNA polymerase epsilon (POLE) drives polymerization of DNA in the leading strand while DNA polymerase delta (Pol delta) synthesizes the lagging strand. The activity of the polymerases requires the participation of a complex that consists of PCNA, replication factor A, and DNA replication factor C proteins. These proteins form torus-shaped mobile platform that keeps Pol delta anchored on the DNA strand during the polymerization process.    Outcome effects: When DNA synthesis is complete, FEN1, DNA2, and DNA ligase are recruited to fill a single nick in the leading strand and multiple nicks in the lagging strand."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In eukaryotes, DNA replication only takes place during S phase of the cell cycle; however, the primary initiation of replication occurs in G1 phase. The binding of the origin of replication recognition complex (ORC) to certain DNA loci called origins of replication is necessary for replication initiation. In the subsequent step of initiation, ORC becomes bound to prereplication complex (pre-RC) that may contain at least 14 distinct proteins conserved in all eukaryotes including CDT1, CDC6, and MCM complex.    Signaling description: When the process of DNA synthesis is activated, initiator complexes promote unwinding of double-stranded DNA and formation of replicative fork. Each parent DNA strand may be the matrix for synthesis of a new complementary strand. When DNA strands are separated, DNA polymerase alpha (POLA1) recruits RNA oligonucleotides (primers) and initiates new DNA chain synthesis. During replication, two growing strands (leading and lagging) are formed by different mechanisms of DNA synthesis. For the leading strand formation, one RNA primer per one active origin of replication is used, whereas the lagging strand synthesis requires several RNA primers to produce separate serial fragments of complementary DNA (Okazaki fragments). DNA polymerase epsilon (POLE) drives polymerization of DNA in the leading strand while DNA polymerase delta (Pol delta) synthesizes the lagging strand. The activity of the polymerases requires the participation of a complex that consists of PCNA, replication factor A, and DNA replication factor C proteins. These proteins form torus-shaped mobile platform that keeps Pol delta anchored on the DNA strand during the polymerization process.    Outcome effects: When DNA synthesis is complete, FEN1, DNA2, and DNA ligase are recruited to fill a single nick in the leading strand and multiple nicks in the lagging strand." EXACT []

