xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Endocytosis is a process where cells ingest material including macromolecules, low-molecular weight molecules, and particles from outside the cell by enclosing it with a portion of their cell membrane and bringing it into the cell in a membrane-bound vesicle. Endocytosis is characterized as clathrin-dependent and clathrin-independent.    Signaling description: Clathrin-dependent endocytosis (CDE) is the process when proteins located in the plasma membrane specifically interact with clathrin adaptor AP-1, -2, -3, and -4 localized in the membranes of clathrin-coated vesicles. This interaction promotes the movement of extracellular proteins towards the cytoplasm. Transferrin (TF) receptor (TFRC) and LDLR are classic examples of proteins transferred by means of CDE. Clathrin-independent endocytosis (CIE) is less studied. CIE involves CDC42 and ARF1 proteins which are responsible for the endocytosis of fluid-phase markers, glycosyl phosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored proteins, and bacterial toxins. CIE also recruits ARF6 protein to transfer the membrane proteins that carry no domains for binding to clathrin adapter proteins. During endocytosis, after relocating into primary clathrin-dependent or clathrin-independent vesicles, the molecules are transferred into early endosomes. Early endosomes are characterized by the presence of RAB5A and EEA1 proteins, along with PIK3CG and its product PtdIns3P. ESCRT complex is also located in early endosomes and binds monoubiquitylated proteins of the cytoplasm. Other important players of the endocytosis machinery are small GTPase proteins of the RAB family. These GTPases can exist in a GDP-bound \"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"off\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\" state or a GTP-bound \"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"on\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\" state. When GTP-bound, RAB proteins are capable of activating various proteins that control vesicle trafficking. Proteins in early endosomes are either directed to lysosomes for further degradation or to late endosomes and trans-Golgi network (TGN) or returned to the plasma membrane. In order to recycle endosomes back to the plasma membrane, ARF6 must activate PLD1 and 1-phosphatidylinositol-4-phosphate 5-kinase. ARF6 also activates RAB11FIP3 that in turn promotes endosomal recycling. Molecules transferred by clathrin-coated vesicles are recycled back to the plasma membrane by means of a fast mechanism that requires RAB4A and RAB35 proteins. RAB10, RAB11A, RAB22A, and RAB35 proteins are associated with microtubule-dependent endosomal recycling. This process also requires EHD1 protein in complex with PDCD6IP and RAB11FIP2."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Endocytosis is a process where cells ingest material including macromolecules, low-molecular weight molecules, and particles from outside the cell by enclosing it with a portion of their cell membrane and bringing it into the cell in a membrane-bound vesicle. Endocytosis is characterized as clathrin-dependent and clathrin-independent.    Signaling description: Clathrin-dependent endocytosis (CDE) is the process when proteins located in the plasma membrane specifically interact with clathrin adaptor AP-1, -2, -3, and -4 localized in the membranes of clathrin-coated vesicles. This interaction promotes the movement of extracellular proteins towards the cytoplasm. Transferrin (TF) receptor (TFRC) and LDLR are classic examples of proteins transferred by means of CDE. Clathrin-independent endocytosis (CIE) is less studied. CIE involves CDC42 and ARF1 proteins which are responsible for the endocytosis of fluid-phase markers, glycosyl phosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored proteins, and bacterial toxins. CIE also recruits ARF6 protein to transfer the membrane proteins that carry no domains for binding to clathrin adapter proteins. During endocytosis, after relocating into primary clathrin-dependent or clathrin-independent vesicles, the molecules are transferred into early endosomes. Early endosomes are characterized by the presence of RAB5A and EEA1 proteins, along with PIK3CG and its product PtdIns3P. ESCRT complex is also located in early endosomes and binds monoubiquitylated proteins of the cytoplasm. Other important players of the endocytosis machinery are small GTPase proteins of the RAB family. These GTPases can exist in a GDP-bound \"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"off\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\" state or a GTP-bound \"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"on\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\" state. When GTP-bound, RAB proteins are capable of activating various proteins that control vesicle trafficking. Proteins in early endosomes are either directed to lysosomes for further degradation or to late endosomes and trans-Golgi network (TGN) or returned to the plasma membrane. In order to recycle endosomes back to the plasma membrane, ARF6 must activate PLD1 and 1-phosphatidylinositol-4-phosphate 5-kinase. ARF6 also activates RAB11FIP3 that in turn promotes endosomal recycling. Molecules transferred by clathrin-coated vesicles are recycled back to the plasma membrane by means of a fast mechanism that requires RAB4A and RAB35 proteins. RAB10, RAB11A, RAB22A, and RAB35 proteins are associated with microtubule-dependent endosomal recycling. This process also requires EHD1 protein in complex with PDCD6IP and RAB11FIP2." EXACT []

