xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Proteins traffic between the trans-Golgi network (TGN) and endosomes in a multi-step process consisting of membrane-bound vesicle formation (budding), targeting, tethering, and membrane fusion.     Signaling description: Cargo proteins are concentrated at a specialized region on the donor membrane and packed into a nascent vesicle. Vesicles with a clathrin coat from the plasma membrane and the TGN move to late endosomes. The receptors that use clathrin-coated vesicle endocytosis have a unique peptide signal, Phe-Arg-X-Tyr. This signal mediates clathrin adaptors AP-1, AP-2, AP-3, AP-4, GGA1, and GGA2 thus providing cargo selectivity in the transport vesicle from TGN to the endosome. CLINT1, PICALM, and calmodulin also contain binding motifs for clathrin allowing them to participate in clathrin-mediated budding at the TGN, endosome, or the plasma membrane. Vesicle-membrane SNAREs (SNAP receptors) are a set of proteins that cycle between the TGN and endosomes that mediate membrane fusion. There are over 30 SNAP receptors in humans, each associated with a particular membrane-enclosed organelle involved in the endocytic pathway. These transmembrane proteins exist as complementary sets of vesicle membrane SNAP receptors (v-SNAREs) and target membrane SNAP receptors (t-SNAREs). The complex formed brings the two membranes in close proximity allowing fusion of the lipid bilayers. The combination of SNAP receptors and tethering factors such as Golgi transport complex, TRAPP complex, GOLGA1, and GOLGA4 is largely responsible for the specificity of organelle-fusion events. Tethering factors are recruited from the cytosol to the TGN by small GTPases including RAB9A, RAB5A, or ARFRP1. Intracellular sorting receptors, M6PR and IGF2R, play an essential role in retrograde endosome-Golgi transport. Other important proteins in retrograde transport from endosomes to the TGN include transmembrane peptidases such as FURIN, CPD, and vacuolar sorting proteins. Vacuolar sorting proteins form a retromer protein complex which consists of two discrete complexes, a core assembly containing VPS35, VPS26A, and VPS29 and an associated dimer of proteins from the sorting nexin (SNX) protein family, SNX1 and SNX2. In addition to acid-hydrolase receptors, some bacterial and plant toxins internalized from the extracellular space also undergo retrograde transport. In human cells, two SNAP receptor complexes have been implicated in the transport of Shiga toxin from early and/or recycling endosomes to the TGN. The first includes BET1L, STX5, GOSR1, and YKT6 proteins, while the second consists of STX16, STX6, VTI1A, and VAMP4 proteins."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Proteins traffic between the trans-Golgi network (TGN) and endosomes in a multi-step process consisting of membrane-bound vesicle formation (budding), targeting, tethering, and membrane fusion.     Signaling description: Cargo proteins are concentrated at a specialized region on the donor membrane and packed into a nascent vesicle. Vesicles with a clathrin coat from the plasma membrane and the TGN move to late endosomes. The receptors that use clathrin-coated vesicle endocytosis have a unique peptide signal, Phe-Arg-X-Tyr. This signal mediates clathrin adaptors AP-1, AP-2, AP-3, AP-4, GGA1, and GGA2 thus providing cargo selectivity in the transport vesicle from TGN to the endosome. CLINT1, PICALM, and calmodulin also contain binding motifs for clathrin allowing them to participate in clathrin-mediated budding at the TGN, endosome, or the plasma membrane. Vesicle-membrane SNAREs (SNAP receptors) are a set of proteins that cycle between the TGN and endosomes that mediate membrane fusion. There are over 30 SNAP receptors in humans, each associated with a particular membrane-enclosed organelle involved in the endocytic pathway. These transmembrane proteins exist as complementary sets of vesicle membrane SNAP receptors (v-SNAREs) and target membrane SNAP receptors (t-SNAREs). The complex formed brings the two membranes in close proximity allowing fusion of the lipid bilayers. The combination of SNAP receptors and tethering factors such as Golgi transport complex, TRAPP complex, GOLGA1, and GOLGA4 is largely responsible for the specificity of organelle-fusion events. Tethering factors are recruited from the cytosol to the TGN by small GTPases including RAB9A, RAB5A, or ARFRP1. Intracellular sorting receptors, M6PR and IGF2R, play an essential role in retrograde endosome-Golgi transport. Other important proteins in retrograde transport from endosomes to the TGN include transmembrane peptidases such as FURIN, CPD, and vacuolar sorting proteins. Vacuolar sorting proteins form a retromer protein complex which consists of two discrete complexes, a core assembly containing VPS35, VPS26A, and VPS29 and an associated dimer of proteins from the sorting nexin (SNX) protein family, SNX1 and SNX2. In addition to acid-hydrolase receptors, some bacterial and plant toxins internalized from the extracellular space also undergo retrograde transport. In human cells, two SNAP receptor complexes have been implicated in the transport of Shiga toxin from early and/or recycling endosomes to the TGN. The first includes BET1L, STX5, GOSR1, and YKT6 proteins, while the second consists of STX16, STX6, VTI1A, and VAMP4 proteins." EXACT []

