xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intraflagellar transport (IFT) is a specialized intracellular process essential for the formation and maintenance of eukaryotic cilia and flagella. IFT is a bidirectional transport (anterograde or orthograde and retrograde transport) along axonemal microtubules. Anterograde activities deliver IFT particles (IFT subcomplexes, IFT trains) to the tip of cilium and retrograde activities return them back to the cell body. Both motilities are tightly regulated to keep cilia intact and maintain them at their specified lengths.    Signaling description: The retrograde transport of individual cargoes along the doublet microtubules of the flagellar axoneme is mediated by dynein motor (DYNC2LI1/DYNC2H1). Dynein motor moves IFT-associated proteins and cargo, BBSome and IFT-A/B subcomplexes. In cooperation with this motor, IFT-A subcomplex binds to protein cargo through protein interaction domains. For many cargoes it is not yet understood how they interact with the IFT machinery. IFT-A subcomplex contains IFT144 (WDR19), IFT140, IFT139 (TTC21B), IFT122, IFT121 (WDR35) and IFT43. IFT144 (WDR19) is required for the association of IFT-A subcomplex with dynein motor. BBSome may play a role in holding IFT-A and IFT-B together. The precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Dynein motor, IFT-A subcomplex and BBSome are involved in retrograde transport.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intraflagellar transport (IFT) is a specialized intracellular process essential for the formation and maintenance of eukaryotic cilia and flagella. IFT is a bidirectional transport (anterograde or orthograde and retrograde transport) along axonemal microtubules. Anterograde activities deliver IFT particles (IFT subcomplexes, IFT trains) to the tip of cilium and retrograde activities return them back to the cell body. Both motilities are tightly regulated to keep cilia intact and maintain them at their specified lengths.    Signaling description: The retrograde transport of individual cargoes along the doublet microtubules of the flagellar axoneme is mediated by dynein motor (DYNC2LI1/DYNC2H1). Dynein motor moves IFT-associated proteins and cargo, BBSome and IFT-A/B subcomplexes. In cooperation with this motor, IFT-A subcomplex binds to protein cargo through protein interaction domains. For many cargoes it is not yet understood how they interact with the IFT machinery. IFT-A subcomplex contains IFT144 (WDR19), IFT140, IFT139 (TTC21B), IFT122, IFT121 (WDR35) and IFT43. IFT144 (WDR19) is required for the association of IFT-A subcomplex with dynein motor. BBSome may play a role in holding IFT-A and IFT-B together. The precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Dynein motor, IFT-A subcomplex and BBSome are involved in retrograde transport.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

