xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Synthesized proteins can be transported to a different organelle depending on the protein by co-translational or post-translational translocation. In the case of co-translational endoplasmic reticulum (ER) protein import, the N-terminal signal sequence of the protein is bound by the nascent polypeptide-associated complex (NAC) shortly after protein synthesis but before its complete emergence from the ribosome. NAC appears to be displaced from the growing polypeptide chain by the signal recognition particle (SRP) when the signal emerges from the ribosome.    Signaling description: Proteins are translocated into the ER lumen or integrated into the ER membrane through a channel formed by the heterotrimeric SEC61 translocon. Within the ER, the SRP sequence in secretory proteins and type I transmembrane proteins is immediately cleaved from the nascent polypeptide by the signal peptidase. The signal sequence of some polytopic membrane proteins is not cleaved off. Most newly synthesized polypeptides are co-translationally modi?ed with N-glycans in the ER. When nascent polypeptides enter the ER lumen, glucose, mannose, and N-acetylglucosamine oligosaccharides are attached to the asparagine residues of the polypeptides by the dolichyl-diphosphooligosaccharide-protein glycotransferase. The two outer glucoses are removed immediately by glucosidase I and glucan 1,3-alpha-glucosidase. The resulting monoglucosylated N-glycans on the polypeptides are recognized by the ER-resident lectin-like chaperones, CANX, CALR, and HSPA5, which promote folding of the glycoproteins.    Outcome effects: After the removal of the third glucose by glucan 1,3-alpha-glucosidase, the substrates dissociate from CANX and are transported to the Golgi apparatus through vesicular transport. However, if the polypeptides are incorrectly or immaturely folded, they are re-glucosylated by glycosyl transferase, and again recognized and folded by CANX."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Synthesized proteins can be transported to a different organelle depending on the protein by co-translational or post-translational translocation. In the case of co-translational endoplasmic reticulum (ER) protein import, the N-terminal signal sequence of the protein is bound by the nascent polypeptide-associated complex (NAC) shortly after protein synthesis but before its complete emergence from the ribosome. NAC appears to be displaced from the growing polypeptide chain by the signal recognition particle (SRP) when the signal emerges from the ribosome.    Signaling description: Proteins are translocated into the ER lumen or integrated into the ER membrane through a channel formed by the heterotrimeric SEC61 translocon. Within the ER, the SRP sequence in secretory proteins and type I transmembrane proteins is immediately cleaved from the nascent polypeptide by the signal peptidase. The signal sequence of some polytopic membrane proteins is not cleaved off. Most newly synthesized polypeptides are co-translationally modi?ed with N-glycans in the ER. When nascent polypeptides enter the ER lumen, glucose, mannose, and N-acetylglucosamine oligosaccharides are attached to the asparagine residues of the polypeptides by the dolichyl-diphosphooligosaccharide-protein glycotransferase. The two outer glucoses are removed immediately by glucosidase I and glucan 1,3-alpha-glucosidase. The resulting monoglucosylated N-glycans on the polypeptides are recognized by the ER-resident lectin-like chaperones, CANX, CALR, and HSPA5, which promote folding of the glycoproteins.    Outcome effects: After the removal of the third glucose by glucan 1,3-alpha-glucosidase, the substrates dissociate from CANX and are transported to the Golgi apparatus through vesicular transport. However, if the polypeptides are incorrectly or immaturely folded, they are re-glucosylated by glycosyl transferase, and again recognized and folded by CANX." EXACT []

