synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Transcytosis is the process where membrane-bound carriers selectively transport molecules between different parts of the cell. The transport is rapid and a unique vesicle acts as the shuttle between apical and basal surfaces. There are two types of transcytosis including caveolae-mediated and clathrin-mediated, which differ in mechanisms of vesicle formation.    Signaling description: Caveolae are pits in the basal and apical membranes of endothelial cells. These vesicles transport cargo, usually fluid, from one surface to another in the cell. Caveolae appear enriched in sphingolipids, cholesterol, and proteins associated with the membrane via lipid anchors such as glycosylphosphatidylinositol diacylglycerol-lyase and non-receptor tyrosine kinases including CSK. Caveolin (CAV) is the major structural component of caveolae. CAV1, CAV2, and CAV3 are important for the structure of caveolae due to their ability to oligomerize and bind cholesterol. Caveolae are most numerous in the microvascular endothelia of the lung and relatively uncommon in the highly restrictive microvascular endothelia of the brain, retina, and testes. CAV1 can bind and promote the assembly of G-proteins, including Src, GRB7, and GNAQ, at the plasma membrane. CAV1 plays a role as a negative regulator of many proteins through the scaffolding motif. Through the inhibition of NOS3 and TRPC1, TRPC4, and TRPV4 channels, CAV1 is involved in calcium (Ca2 ) and nitric oxide (NO) translocation. In addition to their coat proteins, caveolae also contain adapter proteins called cavins including PTRF, SDPR, PRKCDBP, and MURC which may be important in regulating CAV expression and caveolar morphology. Micronutrients do not generally use vesicle-mediated transport to reach the blood stream with the exception of iron (Fe2 ) and vitamin B12. Cargo in the clathrin-mediated transcytotic pathway avoids degradation in lysosomes. Proteins tagged for the lysosome are marked for this pathway by the addition of ubiquitin that is recognized by ESCRT-I which sorts the protein into forming luminal vesicles. The membranes of lysosomes and late endosomes are enriched in a specific set of LAMPs. Lysosomes contain more than 40 hydrolases in an acidic environment (pH of about 5). During synthesis in the ER, lysosomal enzymes such as LIPA are decorated with mannose-6-phosphate residues which are recognized by M6PR and packaged into clathrin-coated endosomes.    Outcome effects: When the endosomes lose RAB5A and acquire RAB7A, they become capable of fusion with lysosomes. Lysosomes are acidified by a V-type H -ATPase (V-ATPase) to promote enzymatic activity and control biogenesis steps such as the maturation of hydrolytic enzymes. A lysosomal chaperone (HSPA8) is required for the complete translocation of the substrate protein into the lysosomal matrix."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Transcytosis is the process where membrane-bound carriers selectively transport molecules between different parts of the cell. The transport is rapid and a unique vesicle acts as the shuttle between apical and basal surfaces. There are two types of transcytosis including caveolae-mediated and clathrin-mediated, which differ in mechanisms of vesicle formation.    Signaling description: Caveolae are pits in the basal and apical membranes of endothelial cells. These vesicles transport cargo, usually fluid, from one surface to another in the cell. Caveolae appear enriched in sphingolipids, cholesterol, and proteins associated with the membrane via lipid anchors such as glycosylphosphatidylinositol diacylglycerol-lyase and non-receptor tyrosine kinases including CSK. Caveolin (CAV) is the major structural component of caveolae. CAV1, CAV2, and CAV3 are important for the structure of caveolae due to their ability to oligomerize and bind cholesterol. Caveolae are most numerous in the microvascular endothelia of the lung and relatively uncommon in the highly restrictive microvascular endothelia of the brain, retina, and testes. CAV1 can bind and promote the assembly of G-proteins, including Src, GRB7, and GNAQ, at the plasma membrane. CAV1 plays a role as a negative regulator of many proteins through the scaffolding motif. Through the inhibition of NOS3 and TRPC1, TRPC4, and TRPV4 channels, CAV1 is involved in calcium (Ca2 ) and nitric oxide (NO) translocation. In addition to their coat proteins, caveolae also contain adapter proteins called cavins including PTRF, SDPR, PRKCDBP, and MURC which may be important in regulating CAV expression and caveolar morphology. Micronutrients do not generally use vesicle-mediated transport to reach the blood stream with the exception of iron (Fe2 ) and vitamin B12. Cargo in the clathrin-mediated transcytotic pathway avoids degradation in lysosomes. Proteins tagged for the lysosome are marked for this pathway by the addition of ubiquitin that is recognized by ESCRT-I which sorts the protein into forming luminal vesicles. The membranes of lysosomes and late endosomes are enriched in a specific set of LAMPs. Lysosomes contain more than 40 hydrolases in an acidic environment (pH of about 5). During synthesis in the ER, lysosomal enzymes such as LIPA are decorated with mannose-6-phosphate residues which are recognized by M6PR and packaged into clathrin-coated endosomes.    Outcome effects: When the endosomes lose RAB5A and acquire RAB7A, they become capable of fusion with lysosomes. Lysosomes are acidified by a V-type H -ATPase (V-ATPase) to promote enzymatic activity and control biogenesis steps such as the maturation of hydrolytic enzymes. A lysosomal chaperone (HSPA8) is required for the complete translocation of the substrate protein into the lysosomal matrix." EXACT []

