xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of chronic diseases characterized by a high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period.    Signaling description: Insulin release from pancreatic beta-cells proceeds in two steps. Increased blood glucose levels rapidly trigger the first step of insulin release. Upregulation of glucose metabolism activates the ATP/ADP ratio in beta-cells, which promotes the ATP-sensitive potassium channel (KCNJ11) closure, membrane depolarization, and opens voltage-gated calcium (Ca2 ) channels.    Outcome effects: Insulin is stored in large dense-core secretory granules known as beta-granules, which constantly turn over: if a beta-granule does not undergo exocytosis, it is retired by intracellular degradation with a half-life of 3 to 5 days. The release of insulin from the pancreas is not continuous, and oscillates with a period of 3 to 6 minutes. Pulsatile insulin secretion from individual beta-cells is driven by oscillations of Ca2+ concentration in the cell, as well as cyclic variations of the cell metabolism and ATP/ADP ratio. An increase of intracellular Ca2+ concentrations triggers the exocytosis of insulin granules, which occurs at different rates and is controlled by two Ca2+ sensing mechanisms: the low affinity Ca2+ sensor and the high affinity Ca2+ sensor. Exocytosis which occurs at high Ca2+ is controlled by the low affinity Ca2+ sensor. SYT9 has been identified as the high-affinity Ca2+ sensor in beta cells, whereas PCLO may act as a low-affinity Ca2+ sensor. PCLO facilitates rapid Ca2+-induced exocytosis by its interacting with RAPGEF3/RAPGEF4. The fusion of granules involves a complex interaction between SNAP25 and STX1A at the plasma membrane, and VAMP2 probably modulated by synaptotagmin V and VII. Upon stimulation, proteolysis of SNAP25 seems to be crucial for the secretion of insulin. In addition, cyclic AMP (cAMP) promotes insulin exocytosis by facilitating the generation of Ca2+ signals by stimulating the mobilization and priming of granules via PKA- and RAPGEF4/RAPGEF3-dependent pathways. In addition, both cAMP and Ca2+ are dependent on the intracellular ATP level. RAPGEF4/RAPGEF3 may increase the intracellular Ca2+ channel sensitivity to Ca2+ or Ca2+ mobilizing signals. RAPGEF4 may act through RAP1A, ABCC8, and RIMS2. Only 1% of insulin granules are immediately available for the glucose triggered release (readily releasable pool). The remaining 99% of granules belong to the reserve pool which has to undergo preparatory reactions before release. L-type Ca2+V1.2 channels (CACNA1C) are responsible for the immediate release of insulin, while R-type CaV2.3 channels (CACNA1E) are insufficient to initiate insulin exocytosis, but accelerate granule mobilization. UNC13A is vital for the second wave of secretion. KIAA1244-deficient insulin-secreting cells display increased insulin content and granule number and elevated insulin secretion upon stimulation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of chronic diseases characterized by a high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period.    Signaling description: Insulin release from pancreatic beta-cells proceeds in two steps. Increased blood glucose levels rapidly trigger the first step of insulin release. Upregulation of glucose metabolism activates the ATP/ADP ratio in beta-cells, which promotes the ATP-sensitive potassium channel (KCNJ11) closure, membrane depolarization, and opens voltage-gated calcium (Ca2 ) channels.    Outcome effects: Insulin is stored in large dense-core secretory granules known as beta-granules, which constantly turn over: if a beta-granule does not undergo exocytosis, it is retired by intracellular degradation with a half-life of 3 to 5 days. The release of insulin from the pancreas is not continuous, and oscillates with a period of 3 to 6 minutes. Pulsatile insulin secretion from individual beta-cells is driven by oscillations of Ca2+ concentration in the cell, as well as cyclic variations of the cell metabolism and ATP/ADP ratio. An increase of intracellular Ca2+ concentrations triggers the exocytosis of insulin granules, which occurs at different rates and is controlled by two Ca2+ sensing mechanisms: the low affinity Ca2+ sensor and the high affinity Ca2+ sensor. Exocytosis which occurs at high Ca2+ is controlled by the low affinity Ca2+ sensor. SYT9 has been identified as the high-affinity Ca2+ sensor in beta cells, whereas PCLO may act as a low-affinity Ca2+ sensor. PCLO facilitates rapid Ca2+-induced exocytosis by its interacting with RAPGEF3/RAPGEF4. The fusion of granules involves a complex interaction between SNAP25 and STX1A at the plasma membrane, and VAMP2 probably modulated by synaptotagmin V and VII. Upon stimulation, proteolysis of SNAP25 seems to be crucial for the secretion of insulin. In addition, cyclic