xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) and Golgi complex are the main components of the secretory pathway. Fully-folded proteins transport from ER to the Golgi complex. Proteins travel through the secretory pathway in membrane-bound vesicles. The process of vesicle budding requires coat proteins that pinch off the vesicle from the donor membrane.    Signaling description: There are three types of coated vesicles including vesicles with a COPII coat, vesicles with a COPI coat, and vesicles with a clathrin coat. The inner shell of the coat, or adaptor complex, sorts cargo into the vesicle and recognizes the tethering. Tethers provide a physical connection between the vesicle and target membrane. After tethering is established, fusion is mediated by SNAP receptors that are located on the vesicle and target membrane. RAB proteins may promote the transition from tethering to fusion by recruiting tethers to fusion sites, where they promote SNAP receptor complex assembly. Vesicles with a COPII coat transport proteins from the rough ER to the Golgi and COPII coat vesicles interact with ERGIC. The first step along the mammalian secretory pathway involves the homotypic fusion of COPII vesicles to form vesicular tubular clusters (VTCs), also called the pre-Golgi compartment. Tether protein TRAPPC3 is important for this homotypic membrane fusion event. Secretory cargo is concentrated at ERGIC and may be delivered to pre-Golgi intermediates or directly into tubular membrane clusters due to the activity of COPII complex. SEC23/24 and SEC13, 23, 24, and 31 heterodimers form a COPII-coated bud on the ER membrane. The pre-Golgi structures are associated with microtubules and move in a minus-end-directed manner. COPI machinery regulates retrograde transport from the Golgi complex to the ER and intra-Golgi transport. COPI is a heptameric cytosolic protein complex that, in conjunction with ARF1, assembles onto Golgi membranes. COPI regulates the composition and concentration of SNAP receptor proteins and lipids in the cisternae. Mammalian multi-subunit TRAPP complex is associated with COPI-coated vesicles within the early Golgi, but not at the late Golgi. RAB1A is the proposed main target of TRAPP activity. RAB2A promotes the formation of retrograde-directed vesicles. GAPDH is also involved in COPI vesicle formation and has a critical role at the fission stage. The Golgi transport complex acts as a tethering factor and the increase of Ca2 during the late phase of intra-Golgi transport stimulates the fusion of membranes containing cargo proteins and Golgi cisternae. Ca2 -transporting ATPases play an essential role at this stage. The cis face, or entry face, of the Golgi is where pre-Golgi intermediates first merge together.    Outcome effects: The cargo molecules then move through polarized stacks of flattened cisternae enriched in glycoprotein and glycolipid-processing enzymes to the trans-Golgi network. There protein and lipid are packaged into transport intermediates that move through the cytoplasm to fuse with the cell surface or with other compartments of the endosomal system.    References:1). Lodish H, Berk A, Zipursky SL, et al. Molecular Cell Biology. 4th edition. New York: W. H. Freeman; 2000."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) and Golgi complex are the main components of the secretory pathway. Fully-folded proteins transport from ER to the Golgi complex. Proteins travel through the secretory pathway in membrane-bound vesicles. The process of vesicle budding requires coat proteins that pinch off the vesicle from the donor membrane.    Signaling description: There are three types of coated vesicles including vesicles with a COPII coat, vesicles with a COPI coat, and vesicles with a clathrin coat. The inner shell of the coat, or adaptor complex, sorts cargo into the vesicle and recognizes the tethering. Tethers provide a physical connection between the vesicle and target membrane. After tethering is established, fusion is mediated by SNAP receptors that are located on the vesicle and target membrane. RAB proteins may promote the transition from tethering to fusion by recruiting tethers to fusion sites, where they promote SNAP receptor complex assembly. Vesicles with a COPII coat transport proteins from the rough ER to the Golgi and COPII coat vesicles interact with ERGIC. The first step along the mammalian secretory pathway involves the homotypic fusion of COPII vesicles to form vesicular tubular clusters (VTCs), also called the pre-Golgi compartment. Tether protein TRAPPC3 is important for this homotypic membrane fusion event. Secretory cargo is concentrated at ERGIC and may be delivered to pre-Golgi intermediates or directly into tubular membrane clusters due to the activity of COPII complex. SEC23/24 and SEC13, 23, 24, and 31 heterodimers form a COPII-coated bud on the ER membrane. The pre-Golgi structures are associated with microtubules and move in a minus-end-directed manner. COPI machinery regulates retrograde transport from the Golgi complex to the ER and intra-Golgi transport. COPI is a heptameric cytosolic protein complex that, in conjunction with ARF1, assembles onto Golgi membranes. COPI regulates the composition and concentration of SNAP receptor proteins and lipids in the cisternae. Mammalian multi-subunit TRAPP complex is associated with COPI-coated vesicles within the early Golgi, but not at the late Golgi. RAB1A is the proposed main target of TRAPP activity. RAB2A promotes the formation of retrograde-directed vesicles. GAPDH is also involved in COPI vesicle formation and has a critical role at the fission stage. The Golgi transport complex acts as a tethering factor and the increase of Ca2 during the late phase of intra-Golgi transport stimulates the fusion of membranes containing cargo proteins and Golgi cisternae. Ca2 -transporting ATPases play an essential role at this stage. The cis face, or entry face, of the Golgi is where pre-Golgi intermediates first merge together.    Outcome effects: The cargo molecules then move through polarized stacks of flattened cisternae enriched in glycoprotein and glycolipid-processing enzymes to the trans-Golgi network. There protein and lipid are packaged into transport intermediates that move through the cytoplasm to fuse with the cell surface or with other compartments of the endosomal system.    References:1). Lodish H, Berk A, Zipursky SL, et al. Molecular Cell Biology. 4th edition. New York: W. H. Freeman; 2000." EXACT []

