xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Peroxisomes are ubiquitous cellular compartments or organelles with a single membrane involved in the metabolism of hydrogen peroxide and the beta-oxidation of fatty acids. All peroxisomal proteins are synthesized at free ribosomes and are post-translationally transferred to the cytosol and imported into peroxisomes. The main function in detoxifying peroxides or peroxide radicals into water belongs to catalase (CAT).    Signaling description: The newly synthesized proteins contain peroxisomal targeting signals (PTSs). Almost all matrix proteins have a peroxisome targeting signal 1 (PTS1) which is a C-terminal tripeptide with a consensus sequence (S/A/C)-(K/R/H)-(L/A). PTS1 is specifically recognized by receptor PEX5. Peroxisome targeting signal 2 (PTS2) is a non-peptide with a consensus sequence (R/K)-(L/V/I)-XXXXX-(H/Q)-(L/A/F) located near the N-terminus (X can be any amino acid). PTS2 is specifically recognized by receptor PEX7. The import process involves three distinct steps. The import receptors, PEX5 and PEX7, bind proteins with PTS1 and PTS2 in the cytosol and direct them to the peroxisomal membrane-bound PEX13/PEX14 receptor complex. In mammals, an isoform of PEX5 (PEX5L) is required as an accessory protein for PEX7. This complex is part of a larger complex known as the peroxisomal importomer which includes the membrane proteins PEX2, PEX10, and PEX12. Cargoes are then translocated into the peroxisomal matrix in a manner that exposes the receptor to the matrix of the peroxisome. Whether the receptors translocate completely into the matrix or retain association with the translocation complex is still unknown. The docking and RING complexes (PEX2, PEX10, and PEX12) are involved in translocation. Cargoes are released on the matrix side of the membrane and receptors are recycled to the cytoplasm. The recycling of cargo-released PEX5 to the cytosol requires PEX1 and PEX6. Membrane assembly and insertion of peroxisomal membrane proteins (PMPs), containing targeting signals of PMPs (mPTS), requires PEX19 and PEX16 which may function as receptor/chaperone which binds the PMPs in the cytosol and interacts with PEX3 at the peroxisomal membrane. The ubiquitylation of the import receptor is emerging as an important aspect of import. Peroxisomes can evidently arise from the fission of preexisting peroxisomes. PEX11A, PEX11B, and PEX11G are the only peroxins known to be involved in the regulation of peroxisome size and number (proliferation) in mammals. The division of peroxisomes is mediated by DNM1L. It has been demonstrated that the fission of preexisting peroxisomes is coupled to the transfer of membrane material from the ER to peroxisomes through PEX3-containing vesicles. Mammalian peroxisomes move along microtubules and bind kinesin and dynein motors by a mechanism that is not clear."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Peroxisomes are ubiquitous cellular compartments or organelles with a single membrane involved in the metabolism of hydrogen peroxide and the beta-oxidation of fatty acids. All peroxisomal proteins are synthesized at free ribosomes and are post-translationally transferred to the cytosol and imported into peroxisomes. The main function in detoxifying peroxides or peroxide radicals into water belongs to catalase (CAT).    Signaling description: The newly synthesized proteins contain peroxisomal targeting signals (PTSs). Almost all matrix proteins have a peroxisome targeting signal 1 (PTS1) which is a C-terminal tripeptide with a consensus sequence (S/A/C)-(K/R/H)-(L/A). PTS1 is specifically recognized by receptor PEX5. Peroxisome targeting signal 2 (PTS2) is a non-peptide with a consensus sequence (R/K)-(L/V/I)-XXXXX-(H/Q)-(L/A/F) located near the N-terminus (X can be any amino acid). PTS2 is specifically recognized by receptor PEX7. The import process involves three distinct steps. The import receptors, PEX5 and PEX7, bind proteins with PTS1 and PTS2 in the cytosol and direct them to the peroxisomal membrane-bound PEX13/PEX14 receptor complex. In mammals, an isoform of PEX5 (PEX5L) is required as an accessory protein for PEX7. This complex is part of a larger complex known as the peroxisomal importomer which includes the membrane proteins PEX2, PEX10, and PEX12. Cargoes are then translocated into the peroxisomal matrix in a manner that exposes the receptor to the matrix of the peroxisome. Whether the receptors translocate completely into the matrix or retain association with the translocation complex is still unknown. The docking and RING complexes (PEX2, PEX10, and PEX12) are involved in translocation. Cargoes are released on the matrix side of the membrane and receptors are recycled to the cytoplasm. The recycling of cargo-released PEX5 to the cytosol requires PEX1 and PEX6. Membrane assembly and insertion of peroxisomal membrane proteins (PMPs), containing targeting signals of PMPs (mPTS), requires PEX19 and PEX16 which may function as receptor/chaperone which binds the PMPs in the cytosol and interacts with PEX3 at the peroxisomal membrane. The ubiquitylation of the import receptor is emerging as an important aspect of import. Peroxisomes can evidently arise from the fission of preexisting peroxisomes. PEX11A, PEX11B, and PEX11G are the only peroxins known to be involved in the regulation of peroxisome size and number (proliferation) in mammals. The division of peroxisomes is mediated by DNM1L. It has been demonstrated that the fission of preexisting peroxisomes is coupled to the transfer of membrane material from the ER to peroxisomes through PEX3-containing vesicles. Mammalian peroxisomes move along microtubules and bind kinesin and dynein motors by a mechanism that is not clear." EXACT []

