xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Base excision repair (BER) is the primary DNA repair pathway that corrects base lesions that arise due to deamination, oxidation, alkylation, or spontaneous loss of the DNA base itself.    Signaling description: BER facilitates the repair of damaged DNA via two general pathways including short-patch and long-patch, both of which are initiated by a DNA glycosylase. These DNA glycosylases specifically hydrolyze the N-glycosylic bond of a target base which causes the release of the ineligible base while keeping the sugar phosphate backbone of the DNA intact. Glycosylase action creates an apurinic/apyrimidinic site (AP) or abasic site, which is processed by APEX1, which cleaves the phosphodiester backbone immediately 5' to the AP site, resulting in a 3' hydroxyl group and a transient 5' abasic deoxyribose phosphate (dRP). In the short-patch pathway, the removal of the dRP is accomplished by the action of POLB which adds one nucleotide to the 3' end of the nick and removes the dRP moiety through its associated AP lyase activity.    Outcome effects: The integrity of the DNA is then restored by DNA ligase (LIG3) which seals the strand nick. POLB and LIG3 are brought together at the site of repair by means of XRCC1 that acts as a scaffold protein. In the long-patch pathway, DNA synthesis of multiple nucleotides occurs and the displaced oligonucleotide is removed by the FEN1 endonuclease. The strand integrity is finally restored by a DNA ligase (LIG1)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Base excision repair (BER) is the primary DNA repair pathway that corrects base lesions that arise due to deamination, oxidation, alkylation, or spontaneous loss of the DNA base itself.    Signaling description: BER facilitates the repair of damaged DNA via two general pathways including short-patch and long-patch, both of which are initiated by a DNA glycosylase. These DNA glycosylases specifically hydrolyze the N-glycosylic bond of a target base which causes the release of the ineligible base while keeping the sugar phosphate backbone of the DNA intact. Glycosylase action creates an apurinic/apyrimidinic site (AP) or abasic site, which is processed by APEX1, which cleaves the phosphodiester backbone immediately 5' to the AP site, resulting in a 3' hydroxyl group and a transient 5' abasic deoxyribose phosphate (dRP). In the short-patch pathway, the removal of the dRP is accomplished by the action of POLB which adds one nucleotide to the 3' end of the nick and removes the dRP moiety through its associated AP lyase activity.    Outcome effects: The integrity of the DNA is then restored by DNA ligase (LIG3) which seals the strand nick. POLB and LIG3 are brought together at the site of repair by means of XRCC1 that acts as a scaffold protein. In the long-patch pathway, DNA synthesis of multiple nucleotides occurs and the displaced oligonucleotide is removed by the FEN1 endonuclease. The strand integrity is finally restored by a DNA ligase (LIG1)." EXACT []

