xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Double strand DNA breaks are common events in eukaryotic cells and the two major pathways for repairing them are homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous DNA end joining (NHEJ). NHEJ double strand repair recovers two broken DNA ends without using a homologous template.    Signaling description: In NHEJ, XRCC6/XRCC5 heterodimer protein associates with free DNA ends and recruits other factors including DNA-dependent protein kinase (PRKDC). During DNA binding, PRKDC becomes activated and phosphorylates multiple proteins including DNA ligase IV (LIG4) cofactor XRCC4. Microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) is XRCC6/XRCC5-independent NHEJ that makes use of short homologous sequences called microhomologies, existing in single-stranded overhangs on the ends of double-stranded gaps. MMEJ is based on ligating hung mismatched DNA ends that remove overhanging nucleotides and fills the gaps. MMEJ results in removing mutations from the genetic code. In most cases, MMEJ is used only when NHEJ is impossible.    Outcome effects: The final step of NHEJ involves the ligation of DNA ends by LIG4."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Double strand DNA breaks are common events in eukaryotic cells and the two major pathways for repairing them are homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous DNA end joining (NHEJ). NHEJ double strand repair recovers two broken DNA ends without using a homologous template.    Signaling description: In NHEJ, XRCC6/XRCC5 heterodimer protein associates with free DNA ends and recruits other factors including DNA-dependent protein kinase (PRKDC). During DNA binding, PRKDC becomes activated and phosphorylates multiple proteins including DNA ligase IV (LIG4) cofactor XRCC4. Microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) is XRCC6/XRCC5-independent NHEJ that makes use of short homologous sequences called microhomologies, existing in single-stranded overhangs on the ends of double-stranded gaps. MMEJ is based on ligating hung mismatched DNA ends that remove overhanging nucleotides and fills the gaps. MMEJ results in removing mutations from the genetic code. In most cases, MMEJ is used only when NHEJ is impossible.    Outcome effects: The final step of NHEJ involves the ligation of DNA ends by LIG4." EXACT []

