synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Nucleotide excision repair (NER) requires the action of more than 30 proteins in a stepwise manner that includes damage recognition, local opening of the DNA duplex around the lesion, dual incision of the damaged DNA strand, gap repair synthesis, and strand ligation. The most significant lesions are pyrimidine dimers caused by the UV component of sunlight. There are two distinct forms of NER including global genomic NER (GG-NER), which corrects damage in transcriptionally silent areas of the genome and transcription-coupled NER (TC-NER), which repairs lesions on the actively transcribed strand of the DNA.    Signaling description: The two NER pathways are generally identical except in their mechanism of damage recognition. In GG-NER, the XPC protein complex is responsible for the initial detection of damaged DNA. TC-NER occurs when the transcription apparatus stops at the site of injury and does not require XPC. The following steps of both GG-NER and TC-NER proceed identically. XPA and replication factor A bind at the site of injury and further aid in damage recognition. Subsequently, ERCC3 and ERCC2 helicases, which are the components of TFIIH transcription factor, unwind the DNA duplex close to the lesion and allow RNA polymerase II to initiate transcription. One strand of the DNA is consequently cleaved by ERCC5 and ERCC1/ ERCC4 endonucleases at positions 3' and 5' to the damage, respectively, generating an oligonucleotide containing the lesion. This oligonucleotide is displaced and allows gap repair synthesis machinery, including DNA polymerase delta and epsilon (POLE) along with several replication accessory factors, to act.    Outcome effects: NER is finalized by DNA ligase (LIG1) which seals the nick in the repaired strand."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Nucleotide excision repair (NER) requires the action of more than 30 proteins in a stepwise manner that includes damage recognition, local opening of the DNA duplex around the lesion, dual incision of the damaged DNA strand, gap repair synthesis, and strand ligation. The most significant lesions are pyrimidine dimers caused by the UV component of sunlight. There are two distinct forms of NER including global genomic NER (GG-NER), which corrects damage in transcriptionally silent areas of the genome and transcription-coupled NER (TC-NER), which repairs lesions on the actively transcribed strand of the DNA.    Signaling description: The two NER pathways are generally identical except in their mechanism of damage recognition. In GG-NER, the XPC protein complex is responsible for the initial detection of damaged DNA. TC-NER occurs when the transcription apparatus stops at the site of injury and does not require XPC. The following steps of both GG-NER and TC-NER proceed identically. XPA and replication factor A bind at the site of injury and further aid in damage recognition. Subsequently, ERCC3 and ERCC2 helicases, which are the components of TFIIH transcription factor, unwind the DNA duplex close to the lesion and allow RNA polymerase II to initiate transcription. One strand of the DNA is consequently cleaved by ERCC5 and ERCC1/ ERCC4 endonucleases at positions 3' and 5' to the damage, respectively, generating an oligonucleotide containing the lesion. This oligonucleotide is displaced and allows gap repair synthesis machinery, including DNA polymerase delta and epsilon (POLE) along with several replication accessory factors, to act.    Outcome effects: NER is finalized by DNA ligase (LIG1) which seals the nick in the repaired strand." EXACT []

