xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The mismatch repair (MMR) system is responsible for the post-replicative repair of mismatches and small single-stranded DNA loops.    Signaling description: MMR recognizes a wide spectrum of mismatches in base-pairing nucleotides and removes a patch of DNA including the misincorporated nucleotide. New DNA is synthesized using the complementary strand as a template. The mismatch is initially bound by heterodimer MSH2/MSH6 for base-base mismatches and one- or two-base loops or MutS beta heterodimer (MSH2/MSH3) for loops of 2-14bp. The mismatch-bound MSH2/MSH6 heterodimer is converted to a sliding clamp capable of translocating along the DNA molecule in an ATP-dependent manner. The MSH2/MSH6/ATP/DNA complex is bound by a second heterodimer, MLH1/PMS2. This complex searches a strand discontinuity and is able to translocate in either direction. MSH2 also forms a heterodimer with MSH3. This MutS beta complex binds to insertion/deletion mispairs but displays little if any affinity for base-base mismatches. In strand resynthesis, the MSH2/MSH6 sliding clamp stimulates the activity of EXO1, starting from a nick situated 5' from the mispair and traveling towards the mispair.    Outcome effects: Polymerases and their cofactors, PCNA and DNA replication factor C fill in the resulting single-stranded gap and LIG1 seals the remaining nick. Any defect in this system dramatically increases mutation rates."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The mismatch repair (MMR) system is responsible for the post-replicative repair of mismatches and small single-stranded DNA loops.    Signaling description: MMR recognizes a wide spectrum of mismatches in base-pairing nucleotides and removes a patch of DNA including the misincorporated nucleotide. New DNA is synthesized using the complementary strand as a template. The mismatch is initially bound by heterodimer MSH2/MSH6 for base-base mismatches and one- or two-base loops or MutS beta heterodimer (MSH2/MSH3) for loops of 2-14bp. The mismatch-bound MSH2/MSH6 heterodimer is converted to a sliding clamp capable of translocating along the DNA molecule in an ATP-dependent manner. The MSH2/MSH6/ATP/DNA complex is bound by a second heterodimer, MLH1/PMS2. This complex searches a strand discontinuity and is able to translocate in either direction. MSH2 also forms a heterodimer with MSH3. This MutS beta complex binds to insertion/deletion mispairs but displays little if any affinity for base-base mismatches. In strand resynthesis, the MSH2/MSH6 sliding clamp stimulates the activity of EXO1, starting from a nick situated 5' from the mispair and traveling towards the mispair.    Outcome effects: Polymerases and their cofactors, PCNA and DNA replication factor C fill in the resulting single-stranded gap and LIG1 seals the remaining nick. Any defect in this system dramatically increases mutation rates." EXACT []

