xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mitochondrial proteins are synthesized as precursor proteins in the cytosol and are imported mainly by a post-translational mechanism. Mitochondrial precursor proteins can be subdivided into two major classes including preproteins that carry N-terminal cleavable extensions called presequences and the second class of precursor proteins that are synthesized without cleavable extensions and have the same primary structure as the mature protein.    Signaling description: The mitochondrial preproteins are believed to be maintained in a translocation competent state by cytosolic chaperones, particularly members of the HSP70 protein family (HSPA1A). The presence of a peptide in the binding domain stimulates the ATPase activity of HSPA1A, increasing its rate of ATP hydrolysis which is normally low. The translocase of the outer mitochondrial membrane (TOM complex) specifically recognizes and binds the precursors prior to their translocation. The TOM complex consists of seven different subunits including TOMM20, TOMM22, TOMM70A, TOMM40, TOMM5, TOMM6, and TOMM7. One half of the amphipathic alpha-helix of a typical presequence possesses a hydrophobic surface that is recognized by a binding groove within TOMM20, whereas the other half is positively charged and recognized by the TOMM22 receptor.    Outcome effects: The preprotein is then transported to the general import pore formed by the essential beta-barrel protein TOMM40. The precursor proteins follow one of three major pathways after passing through the TOM complex. Preproteins with presequence are transferred to TIMM23 complex, the presequence translocase of the inner membrane. Most inner membrane proteins are synthesized without a presequence. After recognition by TOMM70A and translocation by the TOM pore across the outer membrane, these precursor proteins are bound by a complex of small TIM proteins in the intermembrane space, the Tim9-Tim10 complex. The precursors of outer membrane proteins, such as the abundant protein porin, are integrated into the outer membrane by the sorting and assembly machinery (SAM complex)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mitochondrial proteins are synthesized as precursor proteins in the cytosol and are imported mainly by a post-translational mechanism. Mitochondrial precursor proteins can be subdivided into two major classes including preproteins that carry N-terminal cleavable extensions called presequences and the second class of precursor proteins that are synthesized without cleavable extensions and have the same primary structure as the mature protein.    Signaling description: The mitochondrial preproteins are believed to be maintained in a translocation competent state by cytosolic chaperones, particularly members of the HSP70 protein family (HSPA1A). The presence of a peptide in the binding domain stimulates the ATPase activity of HSPA1A, increasing its rate of ATP hydrolysis which is normally low. The translocase of the outer mitochondrial membrane (TOM complex) specifically recognizes and binds the precursors prior to their translocation. The TOM complex consists of seven different subunits including TOMM20, TOMM22, TOMM70A, TOMM40, TOMM5, TOMM6, and TOMM7. One half of the amphipathic alpha-helix of a typical presequence possesses a hydrophobic surface that is recognized by a binding groove within TOMM20, whereas the other half is positively charged and recognized by the TOMM22 receptor.    Outcome effects: The preprotein is then transported to the general import pore formed by the essential beta-barrel protein TOMM40. The precursor proteins follow one of three major pathways after passing through the TOM complex. Preproteins with presequence are transferred to TIMM23 complex, the presequence translocase of the inner membrane. Most inner membrane proteins are synthesized without a presequence. After recognition by TOMM70A and translocation by the TOM pore across the outer membrane, these precursor proteins are bound by a complex of small TIM proteins in the intermembrane space, the Tim9-Tim10 complex. The precursors of outer membrane proteins, such as the abundant protein porin, are integrated into the outer membrane by the sorting and assembly machinery (SAM complex)." EXACT []

