xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mitochondria in mammals are organized into tubular networks that undergo frequent shape change. Mitochondrial fusion and fission are the main components that mediate mitochondrial shape change.    Signaling description: As soon as the mitochondrion has sufficient mtDNA, membrane area, and membrane proteins, it can undergo fission. Mitochondria can also fuse with each other in order to exchange genetic material. FIS1 supports the dynamic assembly of DNM1L on the mitochondrial surface. DNM1L self-assembles into curved filaments and its function is comparable to other dynamin family members that act in numerous intracellular membrane scission events.    Outcome effects: During mitochondrial fusion, MFN1 and MFN2 bind via coiled-coil domain interactions between mitofusins that are anchored into the outer mitochondrial membrane, with their GTPase domains facing the cytosol. OPA1 is an intermembrane space protein anchored to the inner membrane. The coordinated activity of the machineries in the outer and inner membranes is necessary to ensure the reliability of double membrane fusion. The second step in membrane fusion is lipid bilayer mixing."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mitochondria in mammals are organized into tubular networks that undergo frequent shape change. Mitochondrial fusion and fission are the main components that mediate mitochondrial shape change.    Signaling description: As soon as the mitochondrion has sufficient mtDNA, membrane area, and membrane proteins, it can undergo fission. Mitochondria can also fuse with each other in order to exchange genetic material. FIS1 supports the dynamic assembly of DNM1L on the mitochondrial surface. DNM1L self-assembles into curved filaments and its function is comparable to other dynamin family members that act in numerous intracellular membrane scission events.    Outcome effects: During mitochondrial fusion, MFN1 and MFN2 bind via coiled-coil domain interactions between mitofusins that are anchored into the outer mitochondrial membrane, with their GTPase domains facing the cytosol. OPA1 is an intermembrane space protein anchored to the inner membrane. The coordinated activity of the machineries in the outer and inner membranes is necessary to ensure the reliability of double membrane fusion. The second step in membrane fusion is lipid bilayer mixing." EXACT []

