xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mitochondrial replication is controlled by the chromosomes in the nucleus. POLG is involved in copying mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) during replication.    Signaling description: In order for mtDNA replication to occur, the two-subunit POLG, mitochondrial helicase, and single-stranded DNA (ssDNA)-binding protein SSBP are absolutely required. When POLG and C10orf2 are combined, they form a replisome which can utilize duplex DNA as a template to synthesize ssDNA molecules of about 2kb. The addition of SSBP1 further stimulates the reaction and generates DNA products of about 16kb which is the size of the mammalian mtDNA molecule. Circular mtDNA molecule replicates in a D-loop (displacement) configuration because the heavy and light strands have different origins of replication. One strand replicates first, displacing the other strand, until the origin of replication on the other strand is reached. At this point, the other strand begins replicating in the opposite direction until two new mtDNA molecules are synthesized. D-loop has additional functions including attaching mtDNA to the inner membrane and initiation of transcription since promoters for transcription of both DNA chains are located in this region.    Outcome effects: mtDNA encodes 22 transfer RNAs (tRNAs), 2 ribosomal RNAS (rRNAs), and 13 polypeptides essential for oxidative phosphorylation and electron transport. It has three promoters including heavy strand 1 (H1), heavy strand 2 (H2), and light strand (L). Transcription on the heavy strand results in the synthesis of a polycistronic transcript while the light strand produces either one long transcript or small transcripts which can be used as primers. Subsequently, the transcripts are cut into functional RNA molecules. Prior to transcription, POLRMT, TFAM, and TFB1M or TFB2M proteins assemble at mitochondrial promoters. These factors are required for basal transcription, although the actual molecular events involved in transcriptional initiation are unknown. Lastly, PolyA sequences associate with 3'-tips of mRNA while 5'-tips of these mRNA molecules do not form caps. Splicing does not occur because mitochondrial genes in mammals lack introns."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mitochondrial replication is controlled by the chromosomes in the nucleus. POLG is involved in copying mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) during replication.    Signaling description: In order for mtDNA replication to occur, the two-subunit POLG, mitochondrial helicase, and single-stranded DNA (ssDNA)-binding protein SSBP are absolutely required. When POLG and C10orf2 are combined, they form a replisome which can utilize duplex DNA as a template to synthesize ssDNA molecules of about 2kb. The addition of SSBP1 further stimulates the reaction and generates DNA products of about 16kb which is the size of the mammalian mtDNA molecule. Circular mtDNA molecule replicates in a D-loop (displacement) configuration because the heavy and light strands have different origins of replication. One strand replicates first, displacing the other strand, until the origin of replication on the other strand is reached. At this point, the other strand begins replicating in the opposite direction until two new mtDNA molecules are synthesized. D-loop has additional functions including attaching mtDNA to the inner membrane and initiation of transcription since promoters for transcription of both DNA chains are located in this region.    Outcome effects: mtDNA encodes 22 transfer RNAs (tRNAs), 2 ribosomal RNAS (rRNAs), and 13 polypeptides essential for oxidative phosphorylation and electron transport. It has three promoters including heavy strand 1 (H1), heavy strand 2 (H2), and light strand (L). Transcription on the heavy strand results in the synthesis of a polycistronic transcript while the light strand produces either one long transcript or small transcripts which can be used as primers. Subsequently, the transcripts are cut into functional RNA molecules. Prior to transcription, POLRMT, TFAM, and TFB1M or TFB2M proteins assemble at mitochondrial promoters. These factors are required for basal transcription, although the actual molecular events involved in transcriptional initiation are unknown. Lastly, PolyA sequences associate with 3'-tips of mRNA while 5'-tips of these mRNA molecules do not form caps. Splicing does not occur because mitochondrial genes in mammals lack introns." EXACT []

