xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS) is an autosomal dominant autoinflammatory disease associated with enhanced innate immune responsiveness. TRAPS is characterized clinically by recurrent fevers, abdominal pain, migratory rash, myalgia, arthralgia, and systemic amyloidosis. TRAPS is caused by mutations in the extracellular domain of the gene encoding TNFRSF1A.    Signaling description: In TRAPS, mutant TNFRSF1A leads to enhanced inflammation through the increased activation of MAPKs, independent of TNF. Cells harboring TRAPS-associated TNFRSF1A mutations display spontaneous MAPK activation, as well as enhanced and prolonged MAPK activation following LPS stimulation. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) can potentiate MAPK activation by inactivation of MAPK phosphatases through the oxidation of their catalytic cysteine residues. TNF is a major cytokine involved in systemic inflammation. It is now proposed that abnormal oligomerization or misfolding of TNFRSF1A leads to retention in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), decreased binding to TNF, ligand-independent signaling, and a reduction in TNF-induced apoptosis which underlies the pathogenesis of the disease. The increased activation of proinflammatory MAPKs secondary to stress-induced overproduction of mitochondrial oxygen species has also been demonstrated. The failure of mutant TNFRSF1A to be localized at the plasma membrane is associated with TNFRSF1A accumulation and aggregation in the ER. As autophagy is suggested to be the only mechanism effective in the clearance of TNFRSF1A aggregates, it has been proposed that the resulting autophagy defect may underlie TRAPS-associated induction of NF-kB.    Outcome effects: In TRAPS, heterozygous TNFRSF1A mutant cells produce higher levels of several inflammatory cytokines including IL1B, TNF, and IL6 in response to LPS stimulation when compared to wild-type cells. TRAPS-associated induction of NF-kB triggers innate immune responses and excessive IL1B secretion which leads to chronic inflammation. In addition, increased levels of the ER stress response and XBP1 together with high ROS generation may contribute to the proinflammatory state associated with TRAPS.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS) is an autosomal dominant autoinflammatory disease associated with enhanced innate immune responsiveness. TRAPS is characterized clinically by recurrent fevers, abdominal pain, migratory rash, myalgia, arthralgia, and systemic amyloidosis. TRAPS is caused by mutations in the extracellular domain of the gene encoding TNFRSF1A.    Signaling description: In TRAPS, mutant TNFRSF1A leads to enhanced inflammation through the increased activation of MAPKs, independent of TNF. Cells harboring TRAPS-associated TNFRSF1A mutations display spontaneous MAPK activation, as well as enhanced and prolonged MAPK activation following LPS stimulation. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) can potentiate MAPK activation by inactivation of MAPK phosphatases through the oxidation of their catalytic cysteine residues. TNF is a major cytokine involved in systemic inflammation. It is now proposed that abnormal oligomerization or misfolding of TNFRSF1A leads to retention in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), decreased binding to TNF, ligand-independent signaling, and a reduction in TNF-induced apoptosis which underlies the pathogenesis of the disease. The increased activation of proinflammatory MAPKs secondary to stress-induced overproduction of mitochondrial oxygen species has also been demonstrated. The failure of mutant TNFRSF1A to be localized at the plasma membrane is associated with TNFRSF1A accumulation and aggregation in the ER. As autophagy is suggested to be the only mechanism effective in the clearance of TNFRSF1A aggregates, it has been proposed that the resulting autophagy defect may underlie TRAPS-associated induction of NF-kB.    Outcome effects: In TRAPS, heterozygous TNFRSF1A mutant cells produce higher levels of several inflammatory cytokines including IL1B, TNF, and IL6 in response to LPS stimulation when compared to wild-type cells. TRAPS-associated induction of NF-kB triggers innate immune responses and excessive IL1B secretion which leads to chronic inflammation. In addition, increased levels of the ER stress response and XBP1 together with high ROS generation may contribute to the proinflammatory state associated with TRAPS.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

