xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS) include three diseases, namely familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome (FCAS), Muckle-Wells syndrome (MWS), and neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID; also known as chronic infantile neurologic cutaneous articular [CINCA] syndrome). Among the three syndromes, FCAS is the mildest in the clinical spectrum. It is characterized by cold-induced episodes of inflammation, associated with fever, chills, urticarial, and joint symptoms. Patients with MWS present with fever, urticarial rash, and arthritis, as well as subsequent hearing loss, and often have eye manifestations, namely conjunctivitis and episcleritis. In addition, amyloidosis develops in many patients with MWS. NOMID is one of the most severe monogenic autoinflammatory diseases. It has a more chronic and persistent course than FCAS and MWS, rather than clear-cut episodes. Patients display ongoing fever, continuous rash, optic disc edema, uveitis, abnormal bony overgrowth of the knees, and a variety of central nervous system manifestations. The common symptoms observed in patients with CAPS are fever, urticarial rash, musculoskeletal symptoms, high levels of acute-phase reactants, and conjunctivitis.    Signaling description: All three syndromes are associated with mutations in NLRP3 gene, which codes for a key protein of the NLRP3 inflammasome which activates caspase 1 (CASP1). Activated NLRP3 oligomerizes and interacts with PYCARD and pro-caspase 1, resulting in the enzymatic activation of CASP1.    Outcome effects: Activated CASP1 can convert pro-IL-1beta to its mature form (IL1B). IL-1beta maturation and secretion activates inflammatory response.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS) include three diseases, namely familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome (FCAS), Muckle-Wells syndrome (MWS), and neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID; also known as chronic infantile neurologic cutaneous articular [CINCA] syndrome). Among the three syndromes, FCAS is the mildest in the clinical spectrum. It is characterized by cold-induced episodes of inflammation, associated with fever, chills, urticarial, and joint symptoms. Patients with MWS present with fever, urticarial rash, and arthritis, as well as subsequent hearing loss, and often have eye manifestations, namely conjunctivitis and episcleritis. In addition, amyloidosis develops in many patients with MWS. NOMID is one of the most severe monogenic autoinflammatory diseases. It has a more chronic and persistent course than FCAS and MWS, rather than clear-cut episodes. Patients display ongoing fever, continuous rash, optic disc edema, uveitis, abnormal bony overgrowth of the knees, and a variety of central nervous system manifestations. The common symptoms observed in patients with CAPS are fever, urticarial rash, musculoskeletal symptoms, high levels of acute-phase reactants, and conjunctivitis.    Signaling description: All three syndromes are associated with mutations in NLRP3 gene, which codes for a key protein of the NLRP3 inflammasome which activates caspase 1 (CASP1). Activated NLRP3 oligomerizes and interacts with PYCARD and pro-caspase 1, resulting in the enzymatic activation of CASP1.    Outcome effects: Activated CASP1 can convert pro-IL-1beta to its mature form (IL1B). IL-1beta maturation and secretion activates inflammatory response.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

