xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Transient and permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus (TNDM and PNDM) are rare conditions which occur in 1:300,000 to 400,000 live births. TNDM infants develop diabetes in the first few weeks of life, then go into remission in a few months, with possible relapse to a permanent diabetes state usually around adolescence or adulthood. The pancreatic dysfunction in this condition may be maintained throughout life, with a relapse initiated at times of metabolic stress such as puberty or pregnancy. In PNDM, insulin secretory failure occurs in the late fetal or early post-natal period and does not go into remission. Very early onset diabetes mellitus seems to be unrelated to autoimmunity in most instances.    Signaling description: One third to one half of all PNDM cases involve mutations in KCNJ11 and ABCC8 genes responsible for pancreatic ATP-sensitive potassium (K ) channels (KATP) involved in the regulation of insulin secretion. Normally, glucose is transported into the beta cell by SLC2A2. GCK phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate as the rate-limiting step in glucose metabolism. Glycolysis increases the ATP/ADP ratio which is sensed by the KATP channels. The increase of the ATP/ADP ratio leads to KATP channel closure, followed by membrane depolarization which results in the opening of voltage-gated Ca2 channels. The influx of Ca2 triggers the exocytosis of insulin.    Outcome effects: The activating mutations in genes encoding the two subunits of the pancreatic KATP channel (namely KCNJ11 and ABCC8) lead to the channel opening. Increased K outflow leads to hyperpolarization and stabilization of membrane potential, thus blocking insulin exocytosis. SLC2A2 and GCK may also be mutated in rare cases of neonatal diabetes. Mutations in genes encoding transcription factors including PDX1, PTF1A, RFX6, GLIS3, PAX6, NEUROD1, NEUROG3, HNF1B, and GATA6 cause abnormal development of the pancreatic endocrine cells. PDX1 mutation is associated with pancreas agenesis; while PTF1A mutation is associated with pancreatic hypoplasia, neonatal diabetes, and cerebellar hypoplasia. In addition, GLIS3 mutation is associated with neonatal diabetes, hypothyroidism, congenital glaucoma, kidney cysts, and hepatic fibrosis. FOXP3 mutation is associated with immunodysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X linked (IPEX) syndrome. Furthermore, EIF2AK3 mutation is associated with Wolcott-Rallison syndrome, an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by infancy onset diabetes associated with a spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia. EIF2AK3 mutation leads to an accumulation of misfolded proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) causing increased stress on ER folding machinery. Mutations in the INS gene cause abnormal proinsulin processing within the ER which leads to beta cell toxicity.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Transient and permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus (TNDM and PNDM) are rare conditions which occur in 1:300,000 to 400,000 live births. TNDM infants develop diabetes in the first few weeks of life, then go into remission in a few months, with possible relapse to a permanent diabetes state usually around adolescence or adulthood. The pancreatic dysfunction in this condition may be maintained throughout life, with a relapse initiated at times of metabolic stress such as puberty or pregnancy. In PNDM, insulin secretory failure occurs in the late fetal or early post-natal period and does not go into remission. Very early onset diabetes mellitus seems to be unrelated to autoimmunity in most instances.    Signaling description: One third to one half of all PNDM cases involve mutations in KCNJ11 and ABCC8 genes responsible for pancreatic ATP-sensitive potassium (K ) channels (KATP) involved in the regulation of insulin secretion. Normally, glucose is transported into the beta cell by SLC2A2. GCK phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate as the rate-limiting step in glucose metabolism. Glycolysis increases the ATP/ADP ratio which is sensed by the KATP channels. The increase of the ATP/ADP ratio leads to KATP channel closure, followed by membrane depolarization which results in the opening of voltage-gated Ca2 channels. The influx of Ca2 triggers the exocytosis of insulin.    Outcome effects: The activating mutations in genes encoding the two subunits of the pancreatic KATP channel (namely KCNJ11 and ABCC8) lead to the channel opening. Increased K outflow leads to hyperpolarization and stabilization of membrane potential, thus blocking insulin exocytosis. SLC2A2 and GCK may also be mutated in rare cases of neonatal diabetes. Mutations in genes encoding transcription factors including PDX1, PTF1A, RFX6, GLIS3, PAX6, NEUROD1, NEUROG3, HNF1B, and GATA6 cause abnormal development of the pancreatic endocrine cells. PDX1 mutation is associated with pancreas agenesis; while PTF1A mutation is associated with pancreatic hypoplasia, neonatal diabetes, and cerebellar hypoplasia. In addition, GLIS3 mutation is associated with neonatal diabetes, hypothyroidism, congenital glaucoma, kidney cysts, and hepatic fibrosis. FOXP3 mutation is associated with immunodysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X linked (IPEX) syndrome. Furthermore, EIF2AK3 mutation is associated with Wolcott-Rallison syndrome, an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by infancy onset diabetes associated with a spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia. EIF2AK3 mutation leads to an accumulation of misfolded proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) causing increased stress on ER folding machinery. Mutations in the INS gene cause abnormal proinsulin processing within the ER which leads to beta cell toxicity.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

