xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is a group of inherited disorders affecting 1 in 3000-7000 people and characterized by abnormalities in the photoreceptors (rods and cones) or retinal pigment epithelium, which lead to progressive visual loss. The most common form of RP is a rod-cone dystrophy, in which the first symptom is night blindness, followed by the progressive loss in the peripheral visual field in daylight, and eventually leading to blindness after several decades. RP can be inherited in an autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive or X-linked manner. RP may occur as part of a syndrome as in Usher syndrome and Bardet-Biedl syndrome.    Signaling description: Over 50 genes have been associated with RP to date, with the majority of them expressed in either the photoreceptors or the retinal pigment epithelium. Many proteins mutated in RP normally take part in either phototransduction or in retinol metabolism (visual cycle). Disease causative proteins implicated in phototransduction include rhodopsin (RHO), phosphodiesterases 6A/B/G (PDE6A, PDE6B, and PDE6G), guanylate cyclases 2D and 2F (GUCY2D and GUCY2F), guanylate cyclase activators 1A/B (GUCA1A and GUCA1B), cyclic nucleotide gated channels alpha 1 and beta 1 (CNGA1 and CNGB1), and arrestin 1 (SAG).    Outcome effects: The apoptosis of photoreceptors is a common event in retinal degeneration independently of implicated mutated genes, although the triggering signals leading to retinal cell apoptosis remain largely unknown.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is a group of inherited disorders affecting 1 in 3000-7000 people and characterized by abnormalities in the photoreceptors (rods and cones) or retinal pigment epithelium, which lead to progressive visual loss. The most common form of RP is a rod-cone dystrophy, in which the first symptom is night blindness, followed by the progressive loss in the peripheral visual field in daylight, and eventually leading to blindness after several decades. RP can be inherited in an autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive or X-linked manner. RP may occur as part of a syndrome as in Usher syndrome and Bardet-Biedl syndrome.    Signaling description: Over 50 genes have been associated with RP to date, with the majority of them expressed in either the photoreceptors or the retinal pigment epithelium. Many proteins mutated in RP normally take part in either phototransduction or in retinol metabolism (visual cycle). Disease causative proteins implicated in phototransduction include rhodopsin (RHO), phosphodiesterases 6A/B/G (PDE6A, PDE6B, and PDE6G), guanylate cyclases 2D and 2F (GUCY2D and GUCY2F), guanylate cyclase activators 1A/B (GUCA1A and GUCA1B), cyclic nucleotide gated channels alpha 1 and beta 1 (CNGA1 and CNGB1), and arrestin 1 (SAG).    Outcome effects: The apoptosis of photoreceptors is a common event in retinal degeneration independently of implicated mutated genes, although the triggering signals leading to retinal cell apoptosis remain largely unknown.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

