xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is a group of inherited disorders affecting 1 in 3000-7000 people and characterized by abnormalities in the photoreceptors (rods and cones) or retinal pigment epithelium, which lead to progressive visual loss. The most common form of RP is a rod-cone dystrophy, in which the first symptom is night blindness, followed by the progressive loss in the peripheral visual field in daylight, and eventually leading to blindness after several decades. RP can be inherited in an autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive or X-linked manner. RP may occur as part of a syndrome as in Usher syndrome and Bardet-Biedl syndrome.    Signaling description: Missense mutations in the carbonic anhydrase IV (CA4) gene have been identified in patients with an autosomal dominant form of retinitis pigmentosa (RP). CA4 is synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) as a precursor. Following post-translational modifications, the mature form is expressed in the plasma membranes of epithelial and endothelial cells of different organs including the choriocapillaris of the eye.    Outcome effects: Mutated CA4 has been shown to elevate levels of ER stress markers including HSPA5 and DDIT3, which can lead to cell death through apoptosis. Since CA4 is highly expressed in the choriocapillaris of the eye, but not in the retina, it has been proposed that ER stress-induced apoptosis of endothelial cells in the choriocapillaris may cause secondary damage in the overlying retina, which eventually leads to RP.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is a group of inherited disorders affecting 1 in 3000-7000 people and characterized by abnormalities in the photoreceptors (rods and cones) or retinal pigment epithelium, which lead to progressive visual loss. The most common form of RP is a rod-cone dystrophy, in which the first symptom is night blindness, followed by the progressive loss in the peripheral visual field in daylight, and eventually leading to blindness after several decades. RP can be inherited in an autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive or X-linked manner. RP may occur as part of a syndrome as in Usher syndrome and Bardet-Biedl syndrome.    Signaling description: Missense mutations in the carbonic anhydrase IV (CA4) gene have been identified in patients with an autosomal dominant form of retinitis pigmentosa (RP). CA4 is synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) as a precursor. Following post-translational modifications, the mature form is expressed in the plasma membranes of epithelial and endothelial cells of different organs including the choriocapillaris of the eye.    Outcome effects: Mutated CA4 has been shown to elevate levels of ER stress markers including HSPA5 and DDIT3, which can lead to cell death through apoptosis. Since CA4 is highly expressed in the choriocapillaris of the eye, but not in the retina, it has been proposed that ER stress-induced apoptosis of endothelial cells in the choriocapillaris may cause secondary damage in the overlying retina, which eventually leads to RP.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

