xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Tangier disease (E78.6) is a rare, inherited disorder characterized by significantly reduced levels of high-density lipoprotein (HDL) in the blood. Symptoms of Tangier disease include enlarged, orange-colored tonsils, a slightly elevated amount of fat in the blood (mild hypertriglyceridemia) and disturbances in nerve function (neuropathy). Other features of this condition may include an enlarged spleen (splenomegaly), an enlarged liver (hepatomegaly), clouding of the clear covering of the eye (corneal clouding), and type 2 diabetes. In addition, affected individuals often develop atherosclerosis, which is an accumulation of fatty deposits and scar-like tissue in the lining of the arteries.    Signaling description: HDL transports cholesterol and phospholipids from the body's tissues to the liver, where they are removed from the blood. Mutations in ABCA1 gene are associated with Tangier disease. With cholesterol as its substrate, this protein functions as a cholesterol efflux pump in the cellular lipid removal pathway. Mutations in ABCA1 gene prevent the release of cholesterol and phospholipids from the cells.    Outcome effects: As a result, cholesterol and phospholipids accumulate within the cells and cause certain body tissues to enlarge and the tonsils to acquire a yellowish-orange color. A buildup of cholesterol can be toxic to cells and lead to impaired cell function or cell death. In addition, the inability to transport cholesterol and phospholipids out of the cells results in very low HDL levels, which increases the risk of cardiovascular disease. These combined factors cause the symptoms of Tangier disease.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Tangier disease (E78.6) is a rare, inherited disorder characterized by significantly reduced levels of high-density lipoprotein (HDL) in the blood. Symptoms of Tangier disease include enlarged, orange-colored tonsils, a slightly elevated amount of fat in the blood (mild hypertriglyceridemia) and disturbances in nerve function (neuropathy). Other features of this condition may include an enlarged spleen (splenomegaly), an enlarged liver (hepatomegaly), clouding of the clear covering of the eye (corneal clouding), and type 2 diabetes. In addition, affected individuals often develop atherosclerosis, which is an accumulation of fatty deposits and scar-like tissue in the lining of the arteries.    Signaling description: HDL transports cholesterol and phospholipids from the body's tissues to the liver, where they are removed from the blood. Mutations in ABCA1 gene are associated with Tangier disease. With cholesterol as its substrate, this protein functions as a cholesterol efflux pump in the cellular lipid removal pathway. Mutations in ABCA1 gene prevent the release of cholesterol and phospholipids from the cells.    Outcome effects: As a result, cholesterol and phospholipids accumulate within the cells and cause certain body tissues to enlarge and the tonsils to acquire a yellowish-orange color. A buildup of cholesterol can be toxic to cells and lead to impaired cell function or cell death. In addition, the inability to transport cholesterol and phospholipids out of the cells results in very low HDL levels, which increases the risk of cardiovascular disease. These combined factors cause the symptoms of Tangier disease.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

