xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hirschsprung disease (HSCR) is a congenital disorder that is characterized by intestinal dysmotility due to an absence of enteric ganglia along variable lengths of the intestinal tract, preferably in the colon. HSCR results from aberrant migration, proliferation, or differentiation of vagal neural crest cells.    Signaling description: SOX10 is one of the key regulators of enteric nervous system development. ASCL1 and PHOX2B, which are both required for a neural progenitor cell to differentiate into an autonomic neuron, are not expressed in the absence of SOX10. SOX10, PHOX2B, PAX3, and TLX2 regulate the expression of RET, ERBB3, and L1CAM. SIM2 and ARNT2 also regulate the expression of RET and are regarded as RET modifier genes.    Outcome effects: Mutations of SOX10, PHOX2B, PAX3, and ZEB2 impair migration, proliferation, or differentiation of enteric neural crest progenitor cells leading to HSCR development. The expression of neurogenic transcription factors is largely controlled by BMP signaling.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hirschsprung disease (HSCR) is a congenital disorder that is characterized by intestinal dysmotility due to an absence of enteric ganglia along variable lengths of the intestinal tract, preferably in the colon. HSCR results from aberrant migration, proliferation, or differentiation of vagal neural crest cells.    Signaling description: SOX10 is one of the key regulators of enteric nervous system development. ASCL1 and PHOX2B, which are both required for a neural progenitor cell to differentiate into an autonomic neuron, are not expressed in the absence of SOX10. SOX10, PHOX2B, PAX3, and TLX2 regulate the expression of RET, ERBB3, and L1CAM. SIM2 and ARNT2 also regulate the expression of RET and are regarded as RET modifier genes.    Outcome effects: Mutations of SOX10, PHOX2B, PAX3, and ZEB2 impair migration, proliferation, or differentiation of enteric neural crest progenitor cells leading to HSCR development. The expression of neurogenic transcription factors is largely controlled by BMP signaling.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

