xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hirschsprung disease (HSCR) is a congenital disorder characterized by intestinal dysmotility due to the absence of enteric ganglia along variable lengths of the intestinal tract, preferably in the colon. HSCR results from aberrant migration, proliferation, or differentiation of vagal neural crest cells.    Signaling description: Mutations of RET tyrosine kinase receptor, GDNF, NRTN, NRG1/3, EDN3, EDNRB, ECE1, and L1CAM lead to HSCR development. RET mutations are the most frequent genetic abnormities in HSCR. In about 30% of cases, HSCR is associated with other malformations. The syndromic forms of HSCR with Mendelian inheritance include Shah-Waardenburg syndrome, Mowat-Wilson syndrome, congenital central hypoventilation syndrome, and Goldberg-Shprintzen syndrome. Shah-Waardenburg syndrome is caused by mutations in EDNRB, EDN3, and SOX10 and is characterized by sensorineural hearing loss and balance problems in some patients as well as pigmentary abnormalities of the hair, skin, and eyes. Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome and neuroblastoma are caused by mutations in PHOX2B. Mowat-Wilson syndrome is caused by ZEB2 mutations and is characterized by mental retardation, microcephaly, epilepsy, and corpus callosum agenesis. The protein encoded by KIAA1279 is responsible for Goldberg-Shprintzen megacolon syndrome which is characterized by microcephaly, mental retardation, and polymicrogyria. Lastly, L1CAM mutations may be associated with X-linked hydrocephalus and RET mutations may lead to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2a/b.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hirschsprung disease (HSCR) is a congenital disorder characterized by intestinal dysmotility due to the absence of enteric ganglia along variable lengths of the intestinal tract, preferably in the colon. HSCR results from aberrant migration, proliferation, or differentiation of vagal neural crest cells.    Signaling description: Mutations of RET tyrosine kinase receptor, GDNF, NRTN, NRG1/3, EDN3, EDNRB, ECE1, and L1CAM lead to HSCR development. RET mutations are the most frequent genetic abnormities in HSCR. In about 30% of cases, HSCR is associated with other malformations. The syndromic forms of HSCR with Mendelian inheritance include Shah-Waardenburg syndrome, Mowat-Wilson syndrome, congenital central hypoventilation syndrome, and Goldberg-Shprintzen syndrome. Shah-Waardenburg syndrome is caused by mutations in EDNRB, EDN3, and SOX10 and is characterized by sensorineural hearing loss and balance problems in some patients as well as pigmentary abnormalities of the hair, skin, and eyes. Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome and neuroblastoma are caused by mutations in PHOX2B. Mowat-Wilson syndrome is caused by ZEB2 mutations and is characterized by mental retardation, microcephaly, epilepsy, and corpus callosum agenesis. The protein encoded by KIAA1279 is responsible for Goldberg-Shprintzen megacolon syndrome which is characterized by microcephaly, mental retardation, and polymicrogyria. Lastly, L1CAM mutations may be associated with X-linked hydrocephalus and RET mutations may lead to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2a/b.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

