xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hirschsprung disease (HSCR) is a congenital disorder that is characterized by intestinal dysmotility due to an absence of enteric ganglia along variable lengths of the intestinal tract, preferably in the colon. HSCR results from aberrant migration, proliferation, or differentiation of vagal neural crest cells.Headnote: Mutations in the RET tyrosine kinase receptor gene are the most frequent genetic abnormities in HSCR. Normally, RET receptor triggers one of the most important signal transduction routes which is crucial for the migration, proliferation, and differentiation of the neural crest stem cells. In addition, GDNF, a ligand of RET, normally exerts a chemoattractive effect on enteric crest-derived precursors which migrate along a GDNF gradient. Furthermore, NRTN is a ligand in the GDNF family, which normally binds GFRA1 and RET to promote the projection and branching of some enteric neuron axons. Endothelin signaling is also usually necessary for normal neural crest stem cell migration. EDN3 is a secreted peptide expressed by gut mesenchyme that binds to the G-protein coupled receptor, EDNRB on migrating cells. ECE1 post-translationally modifies the immature form of EDN3 into the active form. Additionally, NRG1 signals through ERBB2 and ERBB3 receptors to regulate neural crest cell development. The role of modifier genes appears to be significant in HSCR. L1CAM is a modifier of RET and EDN3/EDNRB genes. A mutation of L1CAM in conjunction with a mutation in EDN3 or EDNRB significantly increases the severity of intestinal aganglionosis compared to a loss of EDN3 or EDNRB alone. NRG1 may also act as a modifier gene for RET. Individuals that possess a specific NRG1 haplotype have an increased risk of HSCR conferred by RET.    Outcome effects: Mutations of RET, GDNF, NRTN, NRG1/3, EDN3, EDNRB, ECE1, and L1CAM lead to HSCR development. All of these mutations lead to loss of function and impairment of enteric neural crest cells to migrate and proliferate which leads to aganglionosis.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hirschsprung disease (HSCR) is a congenital disorder that is characterized by intestinal dysmotility due to an absence of enteric ganglia along variable lengths of the intestinal tract, preferably in the colon. HSCR results from aberrant migration, proliferation, or differentiation of vagal neural crest cells.Headnote: Mutations in the RET tyrosine kinase receptor gene are the most frequent genetic abnormities in HSCR. Normally, RET receptor triggers one of the most important signal transduction routes which is crucial for the migration, proliferation, and differentiation of the neural crest stem cells. In addition, GDNF, a ligand of RET, normally exerts a chemoattractive effect on enteric crest-derived precursors which migrate along a GDNF gradient. Furthermore, NRTN is a ligand in the GDNF family, which normally binds GFRA1 and RET to promote the projection and branching of some enteric neuron axons. Endothelin signaling is also usually necessary for normal neural crest stem cell migration. EDN3 is a secreted peptide expressed by gut mesenchyme that binds to the G-protein coupled receptor, EDNRB on migrating cells. ECE1 post-translationally modifies the immature form of EDN3 into the active form. Additionally, NRG1 signals through ERBB2 and ERBB3 receptors to regulate neural crest cell development. The role of modifier genes appears to be significant in HSCR. L1CAM is a modifier of RET and EDN3/EDNRB genes. A mutation of L1CAM in conjunction with a mutation in EDN3 or EDNRB significantly increases the severity of intestinal aganglionosis compared to a loss of EDN3 or EDNRB alone. NRG1 may also act as a modifier gene for RET. Individuals that possess a specific NRG1 haplotype have an increased risk of HSCR conferred by RET.    Outcome effects: Mutations of RET, GDNF, NRTN, NRG1/3, EDN3, EDNRB, ECE1, and L1CAM lead to HSCR development. All of these mutations lead to loss of function and impairment of enteric neural crest cells to migrate and proliferate which leads to aganglionosis.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

