xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Huntington's disease (HD) is a neurodegenerative disorder caused by a CAG-repeat expansion in the huntingtin (HTT) gene. The striatum is one of the regions most affected by neurodegeneration, resulting in the loss of the medium spiny neurons (MCN). Glutamate (Glu) and dopamine (Dop) signaling pathways act synergistically to induce Ca2+ overload and apoptosis of MCN.     Signaling description: Under normal physiological conditions Glu is released from presynaptic terminals via Ca2+- dependant exocytosis and activates NMDAR (N-Methyl D-Aspartate Receptor) expressed on postsynaptic motor neurons and voltage dependent Ca2+ channels. This leads to Ca2+ influx into the cell.Neuronal death may depend on excitotoxicity that results from increased glutamate (Glu) release from cortical neurons and increased activity of the glutamate receptor (NMDAR). Mutant huntingtin (mHTT) can enhance the NMDA receptor-mediated exitotoxicity interacting with PSD95 (DLG4) postsynaptic scaffold protein. mHTT also induces phosphorylation of the NR2B subunit contributing to overactivation of NMDAR. The number and subunit composition of NMDAR are not static, but in response to neuronal activity, this process is called NMDAR trafficking. NMDAR trafficking is impaired in HD by destabilization of the clathrin-mediated endocytotic complex that involves NMDAR, mHTT, and HIP-1.The stimulation of glutamate metabotropic receptor GRM5 by Glu activates the IP3 signaling pathway causes increased Ca2+ release from endoplasmic reticulum by ITPR1. mHTT binds to ITPR1 and renders the receptors more sensitive to IP3. Dop released from substantia nigra dopaminergic neurons stimulates D1- and D2- class receptors (DRD1/2), which are abundantly expressed in MSNs. DRD1 is coupled to GNAS/ADCY, cAMP production and PKA activation. PKA potentiates glutamate-induced Ca2+ signals by activating of ITPR1. DRD2 acts through PI3K/IP3 signaling leading to the same results. As a result, simultaneous activation of Glu and Dop receptors leads to cytosolic and mitochondrial Ca2+ overload in HD MSNs. Downregulation of pre-synaptic cannabinoid receptors type 1 (CNR1) and DRD2 which normally block Glu release make impact in HD development.    Outcome effects: When mitochondrial Ca2+ storage capacity is exceeded, mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP) opens, leading to release of cytochrome c (CYCS) into the cytosol and activation of caspases 9 and 3, which leads to progression of apoptosis. The mitochondrial Ca2+ handling is further destabilized by direct association of HTT with mitochondria. mHTT was shown to directly induce the opening of mPTP. Adenosine A2 receptors (A2AR) are abundant on the corticostriatal pre-synaptic terminals, where, when activated, they increase Glu release. Reduced BDNF production and release from the cortical afferents contribute to neuronal death. Striatal BDNF is produced in the cortex where its transcription is downregulated in the presence of mHTT. In addition, mHTT reduces BDNF vesicle transport. Both mechanisms result in loss of BDNF trophic support to striatal neurons.Decreased Glu uptake in glial cells by GLT-1 (SLC1A2) contributes to neuronal excitotoxicity.Quinolinate is an endogenous metabolite at the kynurenine pathway of glial cells acting as a selective agonist at the NMDA receptor, and produces membrane depolarization and Ca2+ influx into neuronal cells. Moreover, quinolinate induces ROS synthesis and increases oxidative stress. Quinolinate level is increased in HD due to high kynurenine 3-monooxygenase (KMO) activity induced by mHTT. Cholinergic system is dysfunctional in HD patients: expression of vesicular acetylcholine transporter (SLC18A3) and choline acetyltransferase (CHAT) are decreased in the striatum and cortex. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red and compound"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Huntington's disease (HD) is a neurodegenerative disorder caused by a CAG-repeat expansion in the huntingtin (HTT) gene. The striatum is one of the regions most affected by neurodegeneration, resulting in the loss of the medium spiny neurons (MCN). Glutamate (Glu) and dopamine (Dop) signaling pathways act synergistically to induce Ca2+ overload and apoptosis of MCN.     Signaling description: Under normal physiological conditions Glu is released from presynaptic terminals via Ca2+- dependant exocytosis and activates NMDAR (N-Methyl D-Aspartate Receptor) expressed on postsynaptic motor neurons and voltage dependent Ca2+ channels. This leads to Ca2+ influx into the cell.Neuronal death may depend on excitotoxicity that results from increased glutamate (Glu) release from cortical neurons and increased activity of the glutamate receptor (NMDAR). Mutant huntingtin (mHTT) can enhance the NMDA receptor-mediated exitotoxicity interacting with PSD95 (DLG4) postsynaptic scaffold protein. mHTT also induces phosphorylation of the NR2B subunit contributing to overactivation of NMDAR. The number and subunit composition of NMDAR are not static, but in response to neuronal activity, this process is called NMDAR trafficking. NMDAR trafficking is impaired in HD by destabilization of the clathrin-mediated endocytotic complex that involves NMDAR, mHTT, and HIP-1.The stimulation of glutamate metabotropic receptor GRM5 by Glu activates the IP3 signaling pathway causes increased Ca2+ release from endoplasmic reticulum by ITPR1. mHTT binds to ITPR1 and renders the receptors more sensitive to IP3. Dop released from substantia nigra dopaminergic neurons stimulates D1- and D2- class receptors (DRD1/2), which are abundantly expressed in MSNs. DRD1 is coupled to GNAS/ADCY, cAMP production and PKA activation. PKA potentiates glutamate-induced Ca2+ signals by activating of ITPR1. DRD2 acts through PI3K/IP3 signaling leading to the same results. As a result, simultaneous activation of Glu and Dop receptors leads to cytosolic and mitochondrial Ca2+ overload in HD MSNs. Downregulation of pre-synaptic cannabinoid receptors type 1 (CNR1) and DRD2 which normally block Glu release make impact in HD development.    Outcome effects: When mitochondrial Ca2+ storage capacity is exceeded, mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP) opens, leading to release of cytochrome c (CYCS) into the cytosol and activation of caspases 9 and 3, which leads to progression of apoptosis. The mitochondrial Ca2+ handling is further destabilized by direct association of HTT with mitochondria. mHTT was shown to directly induce the opening of mPTP. Adenosine A2 receptors (A2AR) are abundant on the corticostriatal pre-synaptic terminals, where, when activated, they increase Glu release. Reduced BDNF production and release from the cortical afferents contribute to neuronal death. Striatal BDNF is produced in the cortex where its transcription is downregulated in the presence of mHTT. In addition, mHTT reduces BDNF vesicle transport. Both mechanisms result in loss of BDNF trophic support to striatal neurons.Decreased Glu uptake in glial cells by GLT-1 (SLC1A2) contributes to neuronal excitotoxicity.Quinolinate is an endogenous metabolite at the kynurenine pathway of glial cells acting as a selective agonist at the NMDA receptor, and produces membrane depolarization and Ca2+ influx into neuronal cells. Moreover, quinolinate induces ROS synthesis and increases oxidative stress. Quinolinate level is increased in HD due to high kynurenine 3-monooxygenase (KMO) activity induced by mHTT. Cholinergic system is dysfunctional in HD patients: expression of vesicular acetylcholine transporter (SLC18A3) and choline acetyltransferase (CHAT) are decreased in the striatum and cortex. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red and compound" EXACT []

