xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mutant HTT (mHTT) is cleaved at several points to generate toxic fragments with abnormal compact alpha conformation. Proteolytic cleavage of mHTT may be one of the keys for disease pathogenesis.     Signaling description: Proteins involved in mHTT cleavage are caspase 3 and 6 (CASP3/6) and calpains 1 and 2 (CAPN1/2). IKBKB may directly regulate the cleavage and neurotoxicity of mHTT. Substantial evidence exists to indicate that Rhes (RASD2) promotes striatal-specific neurodegeneration in Huntington's Disease by sumoylating mHTT and preventing aggregate formation. Golgi protein acyl-CoA binding domain containing 3 (ACBD3) mediates mHTT cytotoxicity via formation of a RASD2/mHTT/ACBD3 complex.RASD2 physiologically active tes mTOR, exerting a major trophic influence. In Huntington Desease, mHTT sequesters RASD2 away from mTOR, thus promoting striatal atrophy.Neuroprotection by HTT is mediated by its sequestration of histone deacetylase-3 (HDAC3), a protein known to promote neuronal death. In contrast to the normal HTT, mHTT interacts poorly with HDAC3, liberating HDAC3 from HTT, thus de-repressing its neurotoxic activity. SUMO-1 modification of mHTT was associated with increased toxicity and decreased aggregation.SUMO-2 and PIAS1 modulate insoluble mHTT accumulation. Phosphorylation of mHTT by AKT1 may abrogate mHTT toxicity.Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mutant HTT (mHTT) is cleaved at several points to generate toxic fragments with abnormal compact alpha conformation. Proteolytic cleavage of mHTT may be one of the keys for disease pathogenesis.     Signaling description: Proteins involved in mHTT cleavage are caspase 3 and 6 (CASP3/6) and calpains 1 and 2 (CAPN1/2). IKBKB may directly regulate the cleavage and neurotoxicity of mHTT. Substantial evidence exists to indicate that Rhes (RASD2) promotes striatal-specific neurodegeneration in Huntington's Disease by sumoylating mHTT and preventing aggregate formation. Golgi protein acyl-CoA binding domain containing 3 (ACBD3) mediates mHTT cytotoxicity via formation of a RASD2/mHTT/ACBD3 complex.RASD2 physiologically active tes mTOR, exerting a major trophic influence. In Huntington Desease, mHTT sequesters RASD2 away from mTOR, thus promoting striatal atrophy.Neuroprotection by HTT is mediated by its sequestration of histone deacetylase-3 (HDAC3), a protein known to promote neuronal death. In contrast to the normal HTT, mHTT interacts poorly with HDAC3, liberating HDAC3 from HTT, thus de-repressing its neurotoxic activity. SUMO-1 modification of mHTT was associated with increased toxicity and decreased aggregation.SUMO-2 and PIAS1 modulate insoluble mHTT accumulation. Phosphorylation of mHTT by AKT1 may abrogate mHTT toxicity.Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

