xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Huntington's disease is a progressive, fatal, neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the huntingtin gene (HGT), which encodes an abnormally long polyglutamine repeat in the huntingtin protein. It is monogenic, inherited in an autosomal dominant manner with age-dependent penetrance. Clinical features of Huntington's disease include progressive motor dysfunction (chorea, dystonia, bradykinesia, or incoordination), cognitive decline, and psychiatric disturbance caused by both neuronal dysfunction and neuronal cell death. Within the brain, there is massive striatal neuronal cell death with up to 95% loss of GABAergic medium spiny projection neurons; other affected neuronal populations include the pyramidal neurons of layers III and V of the cortex. HTT is widely expressed in neuronal and non-neuronal tissues and is particularly abundant in neurons of the striatum and layers III and V of the cortex. The lack of neurotrophic action of BDNF is regarded to be one of the main reasons of selected striatal neurons damage in HD.    Signaling description: Munatant HTT (mHTT) interacts with many proteins leading to impairment of multiple cellular functions. mHTT infracts with transcription factors SP1, CREB1, REST, SIN3A, NCOR1, and TP53. Interaction of mHTT with CREB1 and REST is responsible for decreased synthesis of neurotrophic factor BDNF, and CREB1. Interaction of mHTT with REST, SIN3A, and NCOR1 impairs chromatin remodeling. mHTT impairs DNA transcription and RNA processing via interaction with TFIIF, TFIID, TBP, TCERG1, PRPF40A and PRPF40B, all these factors make impact in regulation of RNA polymerase II function. mHTT interaction with TORC1 impairs translation and may induce autophagy. Activation of TP53 and induction of caspases via HIPPI (IFT57) may induce apoptosis.Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Huntington's disease is a progressive, fatal, neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the huntingtin gene (HGT), which encodes an abnormally long polyglutamine repeat in the huntingtin protein. It is monogenic, inherited in an autosomal dominant manner with age-dependent penetrance. Clinical features of Huntington's disease include progressive motor dysfunction (chorea, dystonia, bradykinesia, or incoordination), cognitive decline, and psychiatric disturbance caused by both neuronal dysfunction and neuronal cell death. Within the brain, there is massive striatal neuronal cell death with up to 95% loss of GABAergic medium spiny projection neurons; other affected neuronal populations include the pyramidal neurons of layers III and V of the cortex. HTT is widely expressed in neuronal and non-neuronal tissues and is particularly abundant in neurons of the striatum and layers III and V of the cortex. The lack of neurotrophic action of BDNF is regarded to be one of the main reasons of selected striatal neurons damage in HD.    Signaling description: Munatant HTT (mHTT) interacts with many proteins leading to impairment of multiple cellular functions. mHTT infracts with transcription factors SP1, CREB1, REST, SIN3A, NCOR1, and TP53. Interaction of mHTT with CREB1 and REST is responsible for decreased synthesis of neurotrophic factor BDNF, and CREB1. Interaction of mHTT with REST, SIN3A, and NCOR1 impairs chromatin remodeling. mHTT impairs DNA transcription and RNA processing via interaction with TFIIF, TFIID, TBP, TCERG1, PRPF40A and PRPF40B, all these factors make impact in regulation of RNA polymerase II function. mHTT interaction with TORC1 impairs translation and may induce autophagy. Activation of TP53 and induction of caspases via HIPPI (IFT57) may induce apoptosis.Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

