xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) (I78.0) is an autosomal dominant genetic disorder of the blood vessels which affects approximately 1 in 5,000 people (males and females from all racial and ethnic groups). The disorder is also referred to as Osler-Weber-Rendu disease (OWRD), named after several doctors who studied HHT over 100 years ago. Currently , three general types of the disease are described including HHT1 caused by a mutation in the ENG gene, HHT2 caused by a mutation in the ACVRL1 gene, and JPHT (juvenile polyposis hemorrhagic telangiectasia) caused by a mutation in the SMAD4 gene, as well as PPH (pulmonary primary hypertension) combined with HHT caused by a mutation in the BMPR2 gene. In each case, extensive vascular growth occurs with blood vessels having an abnormal structure and more than 70% of them lacking the muscle layer. This is caused by extensive proliferation and migration of endothelial cells and the absence of vessel maturation phase.    Signaling description: The receptors for TGFB1 and GDF2, such as ACVRL1, TGFBR1, and TGFBR2, as well as their co-receptors ENG and BMP2, are co-located on the surface of endothelial cells. ACVRL1 and TGFBR1 receptors trigger the opposite cellular responses: TGFB1 and ACVRL1/TGFBR2 promote migration and proliferation of endothelial cells, while TGFB1 and TGFBR1/TGFBR2 inhibit these effects and support the formation of extracellular matrix. These two opposite effects correspond to two different phases of angiogenesis: activation (proliferation and migration of endothelial cells with formation of new vessels) and dormancy state (absence of proliferation, generation of extracellular matrix, recruiting of smooth muscle cells, and stabilization of blood vessels). This antagonism is regulated on the molecular level by the SMAD family proteins. The opposite actions of ACVRL1 and TGFBR1 in endothelial cells constitute the basis for the complex mechanism of equilibrium controlled by TGFB1. Low doses of this cytokine stimulate the proliferation and migration of endothelial cells via the ACVRL1/SMAD1/SMAD5 pathway, while high doses of TGFB1 inhibit these reactions and activate the TGFBR1/SMAD2/SMAD3 pathway. A mutation in the ENG gene activates the ENG/PIP3/AKT1 signaling, which leads to SMAD3 repression. Specifically, this pathway stimulates the activation of AKT1 which blocks the SMAD3/ TGFBR1 interaction.    Outcome effects: Non-phosphorylated SMAD3 does not form a complex with SMAD4, SMAD2, and ATF3, and thus cannot be moved into the nucleus. SMAD3 is also the activator of the ENG transcription, and thus, upon SMAD3 activation, the feedback loop is triggered. An increase in ENG subsequently blocks ENG/AKT1/SMAD pathway. Therefore, mutation in the ENG gene leads to the arrest of ENG/AKT1/SMAD pathway and activation of SMAD3 and SMAD4 which leads to prolonged cell proliferation. Mutations in ACVRL1 gene generally affect intracellular kinase domain which is presumably required for the ENG activation. The absence of ENG activation results in the same consequences as mutations in the ENG gene in which the ENG/PIP3/AKT1 pathway leading to SMAD3 repression becomes blocked. As a result, SMAD2/SMAD3/SMAD4 complex cannot be activated. This is supported by reduced ENG expression in patients with mutations in ACVRL1. In addition, mutations in the SMAD4 gene prevent the formation of SMAD2/SMAD3/SMAD4 complex and translocation of SMAD3 into the nucleus. SMAD2/SMAD3/SMAD4 complex is also required for recruiting ATF3, the repressor of ID1 gene transcription. The absence of ID1 transcriptional repression leads to its continuous transcriptional activation through SMAD1, SMAD5, and SMAD9. This results in further proliferation and migration of endothelial cells. Also, the activation of ENG expression and arrest of proliferation of endothelial cells fails to occur. Mutations in the BMPR2 gene have recently been discovered which possibly prevent the ENG/BMPR2 complex formation. Fu"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT) (I78.0) is an autosomal dominant genetic disorder of the blood vessels which affects approximately 1 in 5,000 people (males and females from all racial and ethnic groups). The disorder is also referred to as Osler-Weber-Rendu disease (OWRD), named after several doctors who studied HHT over 100 years ago. Currently , three general types of the disease are described including HHT1 caused by a mutation in the ENG gene, HHT2 caused by a mutation in the ACVRL1 gene, and JPHT (juvenile polyposis hemorrhagic telangiectasia) caused by a mutation in the SMAD4 gene, as well as PPH (pulmonary primary hypertension) combined with HHT caused by a mutation in the BMPR2 gene. In each case, extensive vascular growth occurs with blood vessels having an abnormal structure and more than 70% of them lacking the muscle layer. This is caused by extensive proliferation and migration of endothelial cells and the absence of vessel maturation phase.    Signaling description: The receptors for TGFB1 and GDF2, such as ACVRL1, TGFBR1, and TGFBR2, as well as their co-receptors ENG and BMP2, are co-located on the surface of endothelial cells. ACVRL1 and TGFBR1 receptors trigger the opposite cellular responses: TGFB1 and ACVRL1/TGFBR2 promote migration and proliferation of endothelial cells, while TGFB1 and TGFBR1/TGFBR2 inhibit these effects and support the formation of extracellular matrix. These two opposite effects correspond to two different phases of angiogenesis: activation (proliferation and migration of endothelial cells with formation of new vessels) and dormancy state (absence of proliferation, generation of extracellular matrix, recruiting of smooth muscle cells, and stabilization of blood vessels). This antagonism is regulated on the molecular level by the SMAD family proteins. The opposite actions of ACVRL1 and TGFBR1 in endothelial cells constitute the basis for the complex mechanism of equilibrium controlled by TGFB1. Low doses of this cytokine stimulate the proliferation and migration of endothelial cells via the ACVRL1/SMAD1/SMAD5 pathway, while high doses of TGFB1 inhibit these reactions and activate the TGFBR1/SMAD2/SMAD3 pathway. A mutation in the ENG gene activates the ENG/PIP3/AKT1 signaling, which leads to SMAD3 repression. Specifically, this pathway stimulates the activation of AKT1 which blocks the SMAD3/ TGFBR1 interaction.    Outcome effects: Non-phosphorylated SMAD3 does not form a complex with SMAD4, SMAD2, and ATF3, and thus cannot be moved into the nucleus. SMAD3 is also the activator of the ENG transcription, and thus, upon SMAD3 activation, the feedback loop is triggered. An increase in ENG subsequently blocks ENG/AKT1/SMAD pathway. Therefore, mutation in the ENG gene leads to the arrest of ENG/AKT1/SMAD pathway and activation of SMAD3 and SMAD4 which leads to prolonged cell proliferation. Mutations in ACVRL1 gene generally affect intracellular kinase domain which is presumably required for the ENG activation. The absence of ENG activation results in the same consequences as mutations in the ENG gene in which the ENG/PIP3/AKT1 pathway leading to SMAD3 repression becomes blocked. As a result, SMAD2/SMAD3/SMAD4 complex cannot be activated. This is supported by reduced ENG expression in patients with mutations in ACVRL1. In addition, mutations in the SMAD4 gene prevent the formation of SMAD2/SMAD3/SMAD4 complex and translocation of SMAD3 into the nucleus. SMAD2/SMAD3/SMAD4 complex is also required for recruiting ATF3, the repressor of ID1 gene transcription. The absence of ID1 transcriptional repression leads to its continuous transcriptional activation through SMAD1, SMAD5, and SMAD9. This results in further proliferation and migration of endothelial cells. Also, the activation of ENG expression and arrest of proliferation of endothelial cells fails to occur. Mutations in the BMPR2 gene have recently been discovered which possibly prevent the ENG/BMPR2 complex formation. Fu" EXACT []

