xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alström syndrome (AS) is among the rarest genetic disorders in the world, with only 266 reported cases in medical literature and over 501 known cases in 47 countries. AS is an autosomal recessive disease characterized by multi-organ dysfunction. The key features are childhood obesity, blindness due to congenital retinal dystrophy, and sensorineural hearing loss. Associated endocrinologic features include hyperinsulinemia, early-onset type 2 diabetes, and hypertriglyceridemia. Dilated cardiomyopathy occurs in approximately 70% of patients during infancy or adolescence. Renal failure, pulmonary, hepatic, and urologic dysfunction are often observed and systemic fibrosis develops with age.    Signaling description: Mutations in ALMS1 gene have been found to be causative for AS. ALMS1 protein localizes intracellularly to the centrosome and may therefore influence microtubular function and impair the transport of SLC2A1-5 receptors to the cell surface. This may be the cause of diabetes development."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alström syndrome (AS) is among the rarest genetic disorders in the world, with only 266 reported cases in medical literature and over 501 known cases in 47 countries. AS is an autosomal recessive disease characterized by multi-organ dysfunction. The key features are childhood obesity, blindness due to congenital retinal dystrophy, and sensorineural hearing loss. Associated endocrinologic features include hyperinsulinemia, early-onset type 2 diabetes, and hypertriglyceridemia. Dilated cardiomyopathy occurs in approximately 70% of patients during infancy or adolescence. Renal failure, pulmonary, hepatic, and urologic dysfunction are often observed and systemic fibrosis develops with age.    Signaling description: Mutations in ALMS1 gene have been found to be causative for AS. ALMS1 protein localizes intracellularly to the centrosome and may therefore influence microtubular function and impair the transport of SLC2A1-5 receptors to the cell surface. This may be the cause of diabetes development." EXACT []

