xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Usher syndrome is a rare genetic disorder characterized by hearing loss and a gradual vision loss due to retinitis pigmentosa (progressive degeneration of photoreceptor cells in the retina).    Signaling description: Usher proteins localize to the periciliary region and basal bodies next to the connecting cilia and the synaptic region of the photoreceptors. Many of the Usher protein variants for PCDH15, CDH23 and GPR96 found in the cochlea are also found in the retina. Based on the protein interactions and localization at the periciliary membrane complex, an area close to the connecting cilium, implicated in membrane trafficking to the outer segments, it has been proposed that the Usher protein complexes might have a role in the trafficking of vesicular cargo between the inner and outer segments of the photoreceptors. A direct role for the actin binding molecular motor, myosin VIIA (MYO7A), in opsin (RHO) transport through the cilium, has been suggested. This may be a sign of the general involvement for Usher proteins in the transport of cargo between the inner and outer segments of the photoreceptors.    Outcome effects: In the absence of MYO7A, there is a significant decrease in outer disk phagocytosis by pigment epithelial cells. MYRIP, a protein that binds MYO7A and directly interacts with RAB27A might be involved in the process of melanosomes transport to the apical microvilli of the retinal pigment epithelium cells.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Usher syndrome is a rare genetic disorder characterized by hearing loss and a gradual vision loss due to retinitis pigmentosa (progressive degeneration of photoreceptor cells in the retina).    Signaling description: Usher proteins localize to the periciliary region and basal bodies next to the connecting cilia and the synaptic region of the photoreceptors. Many of the Usher protein variants for PCDH15, CDH23 and GPR96 found in the cochlea are also found in the retina. Based on the protein interactions and localization at the periciliary membrane complex, an area close to the connecting cilium, implicated in membrane trafficking to the outer segments, it has been proposed that the Usher protein complexes might have a role in the trafficking of vesicular cargo between the inner and outer segments of the photoreceptors. A direct role for the actin binding molecular motor, myosin VIIA (MYO7A), in opsin (RHO) transport through the cilium, has been suggested. This may be a sign of the general involvement for Usher proteins in the transport of cargo between the inner and outer segments of the photoreceptors.    Outcome effects: In the absence of MYO7A, there is a significant decrease in outer disk phagocytosis by pigment epithelial cells. MYRIP, a protein that binds MYO7A and directly interacts with RAB27A might be involved in the process of melanosomes transport to the apical microvilli of the retinal pigment epithelium cells.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

