xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Autoimmune polyglandular syndromes (or autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes) are a group of rare diseases which affect several endocrine organs. Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (APS-1) is also known as autoimmune polyendocrinopathy-candidiasis-ectodermal dystrophy (APECED), Whitaker syndrome, and candidiasis-hypoparathyroidism-Addison's disease-syndrome. APS-1 is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder and is caused by a mutation in AIRE gene. The main components of the syndrome are chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease). APS-1 often includes type 1 diabetes as part of the disease phenotype.     Signaling description: AIRE gene controls the expression of a set of self-Ag, including insulin in the thymus, leading to the negative selection of self-reactive thymocytes and immune tolerance. Insulin as the target antigen of the autoimmune islet destruction may lead to type 1 diabetes. Thus, metabolic and immune mechanisms may both contribute to different extents to the development of different forms of diabetes. MAFA has also been shown to induce insulin expression in the thymus. It has been speculated that thymic insulin expression may be regulated by AIRE/MAFA. Mutation in FOXP3 in Treg cells causes immunodysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X linked (IPEX) syndrome. IPEX syndrome is a rare disease which is characterized by hyperactive T cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Autoimmune polyglandular syndromes (or autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes) are a group of rare diseases which affect several endocrine organs. Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (APS-1) is also known as autoimmune polyendocrinopathy-candidiasis-ectodermal dystrophy (APECED), Whitaker syndrome, and candidiasis-hypoparathyroidism-Addison's disease-syndrome. APS-1 is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder and is caused by a mutation in AIRE gene. The main components of the syndrome are chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease). APS-1 often includes type 1 diabetes as part of the disease phenotype.     Signaling description: AIRE gene controls the expression of a set of self-Ag, including insulin in the thymus, leading to the negative selection of self-reactive thymocytes and immune tolerance. Insulin as the target antigen of the autoimmune islet destruction may lead to type 1 diabetes. Thus, metabolic and immune mechanisms may both contribute to different extents to the development of different forms of diabetes. MAFA has also been shown to induce insulin expression in the thymus. It has been speculated that thymic insulin expression may be regulated by AIRE/MAFA. Mutation in FOXP3 in Treg cells causes immunodysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X linked (IPEX) syndrome. IPEX syndrome is a rare disease which is characterized by hyperactive T cells." EXACT []

