xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in PAX8, FOXE1, GLIS3, NKX2-1, and some mutations in thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor (TSHR) prevent or disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth. These account for only 2% of thyroid dysgenesis cases, while 98% are of unknown cause. The pathway depicts thyroid gland development in relation to mutations in NKX2-1. The proteins regulated by NKX2-1 were added following the analysis of existing neighbors of NKX2-1 in Pathway Studio. Only those that are significant in the development of thyroid cells were included.    Outcome effects: Mutations in NKX2-1 also known as thyroid transcription factor 1 (TTF-1), have been associated with congenital hypothyroidism, hyaline membrane disease (HMD) (more commonly called respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)), and the type of ataxia also known as choreoathetosis. Recent reports describe an NKX2-1 mutation with congenital hypothyroidism and benign chorea. .    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in PAX8, FOXE1, GLIS3, NKX2-1, and some mutations in thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor (TSHR) prevent or disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth. These account for only 2% of thyroid dysgenesis cases, while 98% are of unknown cause. The pathway depicts thyroid gland development in relation to mutations in NKX2-1. The proteins regulated by NKX2-1 were added following the analysis of existing neighbors of NKX2-1 in Pathway Studio. Only those that are significant in the development of thyroid cells were included.    Outcome effects: Mutations in NKX2-1 also known as thyroid transcription factor 1 (TTF-1), have been associated with congenital hypothyroidism, hyaline membrane disease (HMD) (more commonly called respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)), and the type of ataxia also known as choreoathetosis. Recent reports describe an NKX2-1 mutation with congenital hypothyroidism and benign chorea. .    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

