xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in PAX8, FOXE1, GLIS3, NKX2-1, and some mutations in thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor (TSHR) prevent or disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth. These account for only 2% of thyroid dysgenesis cases, while 98% are of unknown cause. The pathway describes thyroid gland development in relation to a mutation in GLIS3. The proteins that are regulated by GLIS3 were added to the pathway following the analysis of existing neighbors of GLIS3 in Pathway Studio. Only those that are important for the development of thyroid cells were added. Although there is some evidence that cells have no more than 2-3 cascades of transcription factors, the path of GLIS3-RUNX2-SMAD3-NKX2-1 transcription factors is shown because it may be activated consistently in different cells during morphogenesis.    Outcome effects: GLIS3 functions as both a repressor and activator of transcription and is specifically involved in the development of pancreatic beta cells, thyroid, eye, liver and kidney. Mutations in GLIS3 can cause a recessive form of neonatal diabetes and congenital hypothyroidism. .    Mutated gene: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in PAX8, FOXE1, GLIS3, NKX2-1, and some mutations in thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor (TSHR) prevent or disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth. These account for only 2% of thyroid dysgenesis cases, while 98% are of unknown cause. The pathway describes thyroid gland development in relation to a mutation in GLIS3. The proteins that are regulated by GLIS3 were added to the pathway following the analysis of existing neighbors of GLIS3 in Pathway Studio. Only those that are important for the development of thyroid cells were added. Although there is some evidence that cells have no more than 2-3 cascades of transcription factors, the path of GLIS3-RUNX2-SMAD3-NKX2-1 transcription factors is shown because it may be activated consistently in different cells during morphogenesis.    Outcome effects: GLIS3 functions as both a repressor and activator of transcription and is specifically involved in the development of pancreatic beta cells, thyroid, eye, liver and kidney. Mutations in GLIS3 can cause a recessive form of neonatal diabetes and congenital hypothyroidism. .    Mutated gene: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

