xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in the DUOX2, SLC5A5, TG, TPO, and TSHB genes prevent or reduce the production of thyroid hormones. In iodine sufficient countries, 85% of congenital hypothyroidism is due to thyroid dysgenesis. The remaining 10-15% of cases can be attributed to the inborn errors of thyroid hormone synthesis called dyshormonogenesis. Most commonly, dyshormonogenesis is due to defects in thyroid peroxidase (TPO) activity. TPO uses hydrogen peroxide to couple iodine to thyroglobulin within the thyroid gland forming T3 and T4; while the passage of thyroid hormone into cells is facilitated by thyroid hormone plasma membrane transporters.    Outcome effects: Severe defects in TPO lead to total iodide organification defects (TIOD), while less severe mutations cause partial iodide organification defects (PIOD). Pendred's syndrome is caused by a genetic defect in the transmembrane protein, pendrin (SLC26A4), which acts as a chloride-iodide transporter in both the thyroid gland and the inner ear. Mutations in the enzyme dual oxidase 2 (DUOX2) will lead to dyshormonogenesis due to insufficient hydrogen peroxide generation and can be autosomal dominant. The phenotype is heterogeneous and can be permanent or transient and cause TIOD or PIOD. Mutations in the dual oxidase maturation factor (DUOXA2) gene can also lead to deficient iodide organification through similar mechanisms and can cause PIOD. Further, a mutation in monocarboxylase transporter 8 (MCT8) has been reported in five cases as a cause of X-linked hypothyroidism associated with mental retardation and neurologic abnormalities including quadriplegia and is known as Allan-Herndon-Dudley syndrome. The defective transporter appears to impair the passage of T3 into neurons and is characterized by elevated serum T3 levels, low T4 and normal TSH. Other rare causes of dyshormonogenesis include defects in SLC5A5, thyroglobulin (TG), and IDA. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in the DUOX2, SLC5A5, TG, TPO, and TSHB genes prevent or reduce the production of thyroid hormones. In iodine sufficient countries, 85% of congenital hypothyroidism is due to thyroid dysgenesis. The remaining 10-15% of cases can be attributed to the inborn errors of thyroid hormone synthesis called dyshormonogenesis. Most commonly, dyshormonogenesis is due to defects in thyroid peroxidase (TPO) activity. TPO uses hydrogen peroxide to couple iodine to thyroglobulin within the thyroid gland forming T3 and T4; while the passage of thyroid hormone into cells is facilitated by thyroid hormone plasma membrane transporters.    Outcome effects: Severe defects in TPO lead to total iodide organification defects (TIOD), while less severe mutations cause partial iodide organification defects (PIOD). Pendred's syndrome is caused by a genetic defect in the transmembrane protein, pendrin (SLC26A4), which acts as a chloride-iodide transporter in both the thyroid gland and the inner ear. Mutations in the enzyme dual oxidase 2 (DUOX2) will lead to dyshormonogenesis due to insufficient hydrogen peroxide generation and can be autosomal dominant. The phenotype is heterogeneous and can be permanent or transient and cause TIOD or PIOD. Mutations in the dual oxidase maturation factor (DUOXA2) gene can also lead to deficient iodide organification through similar mechanisms and can cause PIOD. Further, a mutation in monocarboxylase transporter 8 (MCT8) has been reported in five cases as a cause of X-linked hypothyroidism associated with mental retardation and neurologic abnormalities including quadriplegia and is known as Allan-Herndon-Dudley syndrome. The defective transporter appears to impair the passage of T3 into neurons and is characterized by elevated serum T3 levels, low T4 and normal TSH. Other rare causes of dyshormonogenesis include defects in SLC5A5, thyroglobulin (TG), and IDA. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

