xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in the DUOX2, SLC5A5, TG, TPO, and TSHB genes prevent or reduce the production of thyroid hormones. In iodine sufficient countries, 85% of congenital hypothyroidism is due to thyroid dysgenesis. The remaining 10-15% of cases can be attributed to the inborn errors of thyroid hormone synthesis called dyshormonogenesis. Thyroglobulin (TG) is a precursor protein used by the thyroid gland to produce thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3).    Outcome effects: TG gene autosomal recessive mutations cause dysmorphogenesis with a wide phenotype variation and is characterized usually by the presence of congenital goiter or goiter appearing shortly after birth, high I uptake, negative perchlorate discharge test, low serum TG and elevated serum TSH with simultaneous low serum T4 and low, normal or high serum T3.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in the DUOX2, SLC5A5, TG, TPO, and TSHB genes prevent or reduce the production of thyroid hormones. In iodine sufficient countries, 85% of congenital hypothyroidism is due to thyroid dysgenesis. The remaining 10-15% of cases can be attributed to the inborn errors of thyroid hormone synthesis called dyshormonogenesis. Thyroglobulin (TG) is a precursor protein used by the thyroid gland to produce thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3).    Outcome effects: TG gene autosomal recessive mutations cause dysmorphogenesis with a wide phenotype variation and is characterized usually by the presence of congenital goiter or goiter appearing shortly after birth, high I uptake, negative perchlorate discharge test, low serum TG and elevated serum TSH with simultaneous low serum T4 and low, normal or high serum T3.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

