xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in PAX8, FOXE1, GLIS3, NKX2-1, and some mutations in thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor (TSHR) prevent or disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth. These account for only 2% of thyroid dysgenesis cases, while 98% are of unknown cause. The pathway displays thyroid gland development and cleft palate syndrome in relation to a mutation in FOXE1 forkhead box E1 (thyroid transcription factor-2 (TTF-2)). FOXE1 functions as a thyroid transcription factor which likely plays a crucial role in thyroid morphogenesis. However, the exact role for FOXE1 in thyroid development is poorly understood. Here, its expression targets TSHR, TPO, TG, and THPO are shown to be important for thyroid gland function.    Outcome effects: A homozygous missense mutation in FOXE1 causes a syndrome of thyroid dysgenesis, choanal atresia, cleft palate, and spiky hair also known as Bamforth-Lazarus syndrome. Phenotypic features include low set ears, extensive cleft palate, hypertelorism, spiky hair, and low posterior hairline. .    Mutated gene: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hypothyroidism refers to thyroid hormone deficiency present at birth. Approximately 1 out of 3000-4000 newborn infants has a severe deficiency of thyroid function. If left untreated, severe congenital hypothyroidism can lead to growth failure and permanent mental retardation. Treatment consists of a daily oral dosage of thyroxin. Most cases of congenital hypothyroidism are sporadic and an estimated 15 to 20 percent of cases are inherited.    Signaling description: Gene mutations cause loss of thyroid function in one of two ways: 1) mutations which disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth and 2) mutations which reduce the production of thyroid hormones, while the thyroid gland is present. Mutations in PAX8, FOXE1, GLIS3, NKX2-1, and some mutations in thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor (TSHR) prevent or disrupt the normal development of the thyroid gland before birth. These account for only 2% of thyroid dysgenesis cases, while 98% are of unknown cause. The pathway displays thyroid gland development and cleft palate syndrome in relation to a mutation in FOXE1 forkhead box E1 (thyroid transcription factor-2 (TTF-2)). FOXE1 functions as a thyroid transcription factor which likely plays a crucial role in thyroid morphogenesis. However, the exact role for FOXE1 in thyroid development is poorly understood. Here, its expression targets TSHR, TPO, TG, and THPO are shown to be important for thyroid gland function.    Outcome effects: A homozygous missense mutation in FOXE1 causes a syndrome of thyroid dysgenesis, choanal atresia, cleft palate, and spiky hair also known as Bamforth-Lazarus syndrome. Phenotypic features include low set ears, extensive cleft palate, hypertelorism, spiky hair, and low posterior hairline. .    Mutated gene: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

