xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital secondary or central hypothyroidism generally results from defects of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) production and is most commonly part of a disorder causing congenital hypopituitarism. Congenital hypopituitarism is often associated with midline defects such as septo-optic dysplasia or cleft lip and/or palate, and can be part of a larger genetic syndrome.    Signaling description: Mutations in genes regulating pituitary gland development, which include HESX1, LHX3, LHX4, PIT1, and PROP1, have been reported to be a cause of familial hypopituitarism. The mechanism of their action is poorly understood. In addition to TSH deficiency, other pituitary hormones are often deficient including growth hormone, adrenocorticotrophic hormone, and antidiuretic hormone. Yet again, the mechanism of their decrease is not clearly understood. Here, several possible signaling routes are shown.    Outcome effects: In some cases, specific gene defects lead to central hypothyroidism. These include isolated TSH deficiency (autosomal recessive caused by mutations in the TSH ß subunit (TSHB) gene), and thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH) resistance resulting from mutations in the TRH receptor gene. Pituitary stalk interruption syndrome (PSIS) is a congenital abnormality of the pituitary that is responsible for pituitary deficiency. Mutations in the transcription factors, HESX1 and LHX4 have been associated with PSIS. PSIS is diagnosed on the basis of clinical findings and a low growth hormone (GH) peak after stimulation test and low insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF1).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital secondary or central hypothyroidism generally results from defects of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) production and is most commonly part of a disorder causing congenital hypopituitarism. Congenital hypopituitarism is often associated with midline defects such as septo-optic dysplasia or cleft lip and/or palate, and can be part of a larger genetic syndrome.    Signaling description: Mutations in genes regulating pituitary gland development, which include HESX1, LHX3, LHX4, PIT1, and PROP1, have been reported to be a cause of familial hypopituitarism. The mechanism of their action is poorly understood. In addition to TSH deficiency, other pituitary hormones are often deficient including growth hormone, adrenocorticotrophic hormone, and antidiuretic hormone. Yet again, the mechanism of their decrease is not clearly understood. Here, several possible signaling routes are shown.    Outcome effects: In some cases, specific gene defects lead to central hypothyroidism. These include isolated TSH deficiency (autosomal recessive caused by mutations in the TSH ß subunit (TSHB) gene), and thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH) resistance resulting from mutations in the TRH receptor gene. Pituitary stalk interruption syndrome (PSIS) is a congenital abnormality of the pituitary that is responsible for pituitary deficiency. Mutations in the transcription factors, HESX1 and LHX4 have been associated with PSIS. PSIS is diagnosed on the basis of clinical findings and a low growth hormone (GH) peak after stimulation test and low insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF1).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

