xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypothyroidism is a pathological state characterized by the deficiency of thyroid hormones. The pituitary secretes thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) which stimulates the thyroid gland to secrete thyroid hormones. TSH resistance can cause hypothyroidism and is characterized by high levels of serum TSH, normal or reduced levels of serum thyroid hormone, and a normal/hypoplastic thyroid gland. Depending on the degree of TSH insensitivity, the presentations may be extremely variable, ranging from severe congenital hypothyroidism to mild elevations of TSH in the absence of hypothyroidism. The defect may affect any step along the cascade of events following the binding of TSH to its receptor (TSHR) on thyroid cell membranes.The pathway describes the three major signaling routes up-regulated by TSHR: via G proteins to cAMP, G proteins to Ca2, and STAT3 activation.    Outcome effects: There exist several forms of TSH resistance with the most common being a hereditary syndrome of congenital TSH resistance due to mutations in the TSH receptor gene leading to thyroid hypoplasia. Twenty three different TSHR loss-of-function mutations have been shown to be responsible for TSH resistance. Mutations in Gs alpha (GNAS) can lead to pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a and defective TSH signaling. Mutations and polymorphisms in other genes, such as PDE-8B may be involved in TSH resistance. The reduced function of mutated genes may lead to a down-regulation of thyroid hormone synthesis and a diminished rate of thyroid cell proliferation. Low levels of thyroid hormones can cause thyroid dysgenesis. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypothyroidism is a pathological state characterized by the deficiency of thyroid hormones. The pituitary secretes thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) which stimulates the thyroid gland to secrete thyroid hormones. TSH resistance can cause hypothyroidism and is characterized by high levels of serum TSH, normal or reduced levels of serum thyroid hormone, and a normal/hypoplastic thyroid gland. Depending on the degree of TSH insensitivity, the presentations may be extremely variable, ranging from severe congenital hypothyroidism to mild elevations of TSH in the absence of hypothyroidism. The defect may affect any step along the cascade of events following the binding of TSH to its receptor (TSHR) on thyroid cell membranes.The pathway describes the three major signaling routes up-regulated by TSHR: via G proteins to cAMP, G proteins to Ca2, and STAT3 activation.    Outcome effects: There exist several forms of TSH resistance with the most common being a hereditary syndrome of congenital TSH resistance due to mutations in the TSH receptor gene leading to thyroid hypoplasia. Twenty three different TSHR loss-of-function mutations have been shown to be responsible for TSH resistance. Mutations in Gs alpha (GNAS) can lead to pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a and defective TSH signaling. Mutations and polymorphisms in other genes, such as PDE-8B may be involved in TSH resistance. The reduced function of mutated genes may lead to a down-regulation of thyroid hormone synthesis and a diminished rate of thyroid cell proliferation. Low levels of thyroid hormones can cause thyroid dysgenesis. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

