xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital generalized lipodystrophy type 2 (CGL2, also known as Berardinelli-Seip syndrome) is a rare autosomal recessive condition. CGL2 belongs to a group of extreme insulin resistance syndromes characterized by generalized lipodystrophy, hepatomegaly, acromegaloid features, severe low levels of leptin and adiponectin, and hypertriglyceridemia from birth or early infancy. Other clinical and biologic features include acanthosis nigricans, muscular hypertrophy, abnormal accumulation of fat in internal organs, hirsutism, mental retardation, bone cysts with spontaneous fractures, and precocious puberty.     Signaling description: Different forms of congenital generalized lipodystrophy may be caused by the disruption of different pathways. CGL type1 is caused by a mutation in the AGPAT2 gene, encoding a key enzyme in triglyceride biosynthesis (1-acyl-glycerol-3-phosphate-O-acyltransferase-2). CGL type 2 is caused by a mutation in the BSCL2 (Berardinelli-Seip congenital lipodystrophy 2 (seipin)). Patients with BSCL2 mutations have lower serum leptin levels, an earlier onset of diabetes, and a higher prevalence of mild mental retardation and cardiomyopathy compared with other subtypes. CGL type 3 is caused by a mutation in the CAV1 gene encoding the caveolin 1 protein. CGL type 4 is caused by a mutation in the PTRF gene which encodes the cavin protein (polymerase I and transcript release factor). Patients with BSCL2 mutations have a higher prevalence of cardiomyopathy and muscular dystrophy compared with other subtypes.The mechanisms of this disease are not fully understood. Studies suggest that BSCL2 may play a critical role in the early development of adipocytes. Normally, BSCL2 binds to LPIN1 and induces PPARG and SREBF1. LPIN1 catalyzes the ultimate step in triglyceride synthesis. The expression of LPIN1 is required for adipocyte differentiation and also functions as a nuclear transcriptional coactivator with nuclear receptor PPARG to modulate expression of other genes involved in lipid metabolism. PPARG promotes AGPAT2, LPL, UCP3, RETN, and LEP. AGPAT2, LPL, and UCP3 are involved in adipocyte differentiation while RETN and LEP play an endocrine role. In addition, PPARG promotes SLC2A4 thus increasing glucose uptake. SREBF1 promotes FASN, ACACB, and ACACA which are involved in lipid metabolism. CAV1 plays an important role in caveolae formation and organization. PTRF is essential for normal oligomerization of caveolin 1. Changes in the function of CAV1 leads to the disruption of lipid transport, formation of lipid droplets, and adipocytes differentiation. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital generalized lipodystrophy type 2 (CGL2, also known as Berardinelli-Seip syndrome) is a rare autosomal recessive condition. CGL2 belongs to a group of extreme insulin resistance syndromes characterized by generalized lipodystrophy, hepatomegaly, acromegaloid features, severe low levels of leptin and adiponectin, and hypertriglyceridemia from birth or early infancy. Other clinical and biologic features include acanthosis nigricans, muscular hypertrophy, abnormal accumulation of fat in internal organs, hirsutism, mental retardation, bone cysts with spontaneous fractures, and precocious puberty.     Signaling description: Different forms of congenital generalized lipodystrophy may be caused by the disruption of different pathways. CGL type1 is caused by a mutation in the AGPAT2 gene, encoding a key enzyme in triglyceride biosynthesis (1-acyl-glycerol-3-phosphate-O-acyltransferase-2). CGL type 2 is caused by a mutation in the BSCL2 (Berardinelli-Seip congenital lipodystrophy 2 (seipin)). Patients with BSCL2 mutations have lower serum leptin levels, an earlier onset of diabetes, and a higher prevalence of mild mental retardation and cardiomyopathy compared with other subtypes. CGL type 3 is caused by a mutation in the CAV1 gene encoding the caveolin 1 protein. CGL type 4 is caused by a mutation in the PTRF gene which encodes the cavin protein (polymerase I and transcript release factor). Patients with BSCL2 mutations have a higher prevalence of cardiomyopathy and muscular dystrophy compared with other subtypes.The mechanisms of this disease are not fully understood. Studies suggest that BSCL2 may play a critical role in the early development of adipocytes. Normally, BSCL2 binds to LPIN1 and induces PPARG and SREBF1. LPIN1 catalyzes the ultimate step in triglyceride synthesis. The expression of LPIN1 is required for adipocyte differentiation and also functions as a nuclear transcriptional coactivator with nuclear receptor PPARG to modulate expression of other genes involved in lipid metabolism. PPARG promotes AGPAT2, LPL, UCP3, RETN, and LEP. AGPAT2, LPL, and UCP3 are involved in adipocyte differentiation while RETN and LEP play an endocrine role. In addition, PPARG promotes SLC2A4 thus increasing glucose uptake. SREBF1 promotes FASN, ACACB, and ACACA which are involved in lipid metabolism. CAV1 plays an important role in caveolae formation and organization. PTRF is essential for normal oligomerization of caveolin 1. Changes in the function of CAV1 leads to the disruption of lipid transport, formation of lipid droplets, and adipocytes differentiation. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

