xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Warts, Hypogammaglobulinemia, Infections and Myelokathexis (WHIM) Syndrome is a rare congenital immunodeficiency disorder characterized by chronic noncyclic neutropenia. WHIM patients exhibit increased susceptibility to bacterial and viral infections, especially from human papilloma virus common serotypes, resulting in warts on the hands and feet starting in childhood. Myelokathexis refers to retention (kathexis) of neutrophils in the bone marrow (myelo). In addition, lymphocytes and IgG antibody levels are often deficient.    Signaling description: WHIM syndrome results from autosomal dominant mutations in the CXCR4 gene. CXCR4 protein possesses potent chemotactic properties towards lymphocytes and is expressed on most mature human leukocyte subtypes and hematopoietic progenitor cells. CXCR4 ligand (CXCL12) is important in hematopoietic stem cell homing to the bone marrow and in hematopoietic stem cell quiescence. When CXCL12 binds to CXCR4, signal transduction activates G-proteins which activate the downstream effectors. This process is regulated by desensitization whereby ADRBK2 mediates the phosphorylation of the C-terminus of the CXCR4 cytoplasmic domain. This leads to the recruitment of ARRB2 to prevent further G-protein activation which subsequently results in receptor internalization and ubiquitination. This signaling plays a critical role in bone marrow homing in addition to myelopoiesis and lymphopoiesis. Point mutations in CXCR4 gene leads to a premature stop codon, thus causing a truncation within the last 10-19 amino acids of the C-end of CXCR4. Additional CXCR4 signaling steps have been suggested to be involved in WHIM syndrome. ADRBK2 plays a role as a regulator of CXCL12-promoted desensitization of CXCR4. In the presence of a truncating mutation of the C-terminal domain of CXCR4, the enhanced binding of ARRB2 to other regions of the receptor paradoxically resulted in prolonged activation of the receptor and failure to internalize it via endocytosis.    Outcome effects: Activated downstream effectors of CXCR4 such as paxillin (PXN), AKT1, and MAPK3/MAPK1 and calcium (Ca2) flux trigger adhesion and cell migration. In cells of patients with WHIM syndrome, mutations result in the enhanced production of IP3 and prolonged ligand-stimulated release of intracellular Ca2 flux in response to CXCL12 binding.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Warts, Hypogammaglobulinemia, Infections and Myelokathexis (WHIM) Syndrome is a rare congenital immunodeficiency disorder characterized by chronic noncyclic neutropenia. WHIM patients exhibit increased susceptibility to bacterial and viral infections, especially from human papilloma virus common serotypes, resulting in warts on the hands and feet starting in childhood. Myelokathexis refers to retention (kathexis) of neutrophils in the bone marrow (myelo). In addition, lymphocytes and IgG antibody levels are often deficient.    Signaling description: WHIM syndrome results from autosomal dominant mutations in the CXCR4 gene. CXCR4 protein possesses potent chemotactic properties towards lymphocytes and is expressed on most mature human leukocyte subtypes and hematopoietic progenitor cells. CXCR4 ligand (CXCL12) is important in hematopoietic stem cell homing to the bone marrow and in hematopoietic stem cell quiescence. When CXCL12 binds to CXCR4, signal transduction activates G-proteins which activate the downstream effectors. This process is regulated by desensitization whereby ADRBK2 mediates the phosphorylation of the C-terminus of the CXCR4 cytoplasmic domain. This leads to the recruitment of ARRB2 to prevent further G-protein activation which subsequently results in receptor internalization and ubiquitination. This signaling plays a critical role in bone marrow homing in addition to myelopoiesis and lymphopoiesis. Point mutations in CXCR4 gene leads to a premature stop codon, thus causing a truncation within the last 10-19 amino acids of the C-end of CXCR4. Additional CXCR4 signaling steps have been suggested to be involved in WHIM syndrome. ADRBK2 plays a role as a regulator of CXCL12-promoted desensitization of CXCR4. In the presence of a truncating mutation of the C-terminal domain of CXCR4, the enhanced binding of ARRB2 to other regions of the receptor paradoxically resulted in prolonged activation of the receptor and failure to internalize it via endocytosis.    Outcome effects: Activated downstream effectors of CXCR4 such as paxillin (PXN), AKT1, and MAPK3/MAPK1 and calcium (Ca2) flux trigger adhesion and cell migration. In cells of patients with WHIM syndrome, mutations result in the enhanced production of IP3 and prolonged ligand-stimulated release of intracellular Ca2 flux in response to CXCL12 binding.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

