xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phenylalanine and tyrosine are the precursors of several neurotransmitters (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine) and melanin; deficiencies in the enzymes involved in their metabolism lead to a variety of syndromes. Phenylalanine is essential (indispensable) amino acid, and therefore must be supplied in its diet. A series of chemical reactions lead to synthesis of neurotransmitters dopamine, noradrenaline and epinephrine, melanin pigment, thyroid hormones and other important substances from phenylalanine (Phe) and tyrosine (Tyr). A number of inherited metabolic disorders is caused by mutations in genes which encode enzymes taking part in Phe and tyrosine Tyr metabolism. These disorders include phenylketonuria, tyrosinemias, alkaptonuria and oculocutaneous albinism.     Signaling description: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder characterized by a mutation in the gene for the hepatic enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), this enzyme is necessary to metabolize Phe to Tyr. A rarer form of PKU occurs when the PAH enzyme is normal, but there is a defect in the biosynthesis or recycling of the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin caused by mutation of quinoid dihydropteridine reductase (QDPR). Tyrosinemia is an error of metabolism in which the body cannot effectively break down the amino acid Tyr. Untreated, tyrosinemia can be fatal. There are three types of tyrosinemia, caused by the deficiency of a different enzyme. Type I Tyrosinemia, also known as hepatorenal tyrosinemia, is the most severe form of the desease. Symptoms include liver and kidney disturbances and mental retardation. It is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH). By inhibiting this enzyme, the accumulation of the fumarylacetoacetate is prevented. Type II Tyrosinemia (oculocutaneous tyrosinemia, Richner-Hanhart syndrome) is an autosomal recessive condition with onset between ages 2 and 4 years. This form of the disorder can affect the eyes, skin, and mental development. Type II Tyrosinemia is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme tyrosine aminotransferase (TAT). Type III tyrosinemia is a rare disorder caused by a deficiency of the enzyme 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPD), encoded by the gene HPD. Characteristic features of type III tyrosinemia include mild mental retardation, seizures, and periodic loss of balance and coordination (intermittent ataxia). Alkaptonuria (black urine disease) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder of Phe and Tyr metabolism due to a defect in the enzyme homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase (HGD), which participates in the degradation of tyrosine. As a result, homogentisic acid and its oxide, called alkapton, accumulate in the blood and are excreted in urine in large amounts. Excessive homogentisic acid causes damage to cartilage (ochronosis, leading to osteoarthritis) and heart valves as well as precipitating as kidney stones. Oculocutaneous albinism (OCA) is a form of albinism involving the eyes, skin, and hair. Four types of OCA have been described, all caused by a disruption of melanin synthesis and all autosomal recessive disorders. OCA1,2,3,4 are caused by mutations in tyrosinase (TYR), OCA2, tyrosinase-related protein 1 (TYRP1) and solute carrier family 45, member 2 (SLC45A2), respectively. All these proteins are involved in melanin synthesis / transportation.    Outcome effects: Untreated PKU can lead to intellectual disability, seizures, and other serious medical problems. The mainstream treatment for classic PKU patients is a strict Phe-restricted diet supplemented by a medical formula containing amino acids and other nutrients. The primary treatment for type I tyrosinemia is nitisinone (Orfadin). Nitisinone inhibits the conversion of 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate to homogentisic acid by 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPD), the second step in tyrosine degradation.Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phenylalanine and tyrosine are the precursors of several neurotransmitters (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine) and melanin; deficiencies in the enzymes involved in their metabolism lead to a variety of syndromes. Phenylalanine is essential (indispensable) amino acid, and therefore must be supplied in its diet. A series of chemical reactions lead to synthesis of neurotransmitters dopamine, noradrenaline and epinephrine, melanin pigment, thyroid hormones and other important substances from phenylalanine (Phe) and tyrosine (Tyr). A number of inherited metabolic disorders is caused by mutations in genes which encode enzymes taking part in Phe and tyrosine Tyr metabolism. These disorders include phenylketonuria, tyrosinemias, alkaptonuria and oculocutaneous albinism.     Signaling description: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder characterized by a mutation in the gene for the hepatic enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), this enzyme is necessary to metabolize Phe to Tyr. A rarer form of PKU occurs when the PAH enzyme is normal, but there is a defect in the biosynthesis or recycling of the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin caused by mutation of quinoid dihydropteridine reductase (QDPR). Tyrosinemia is an error of metabolism in which the body cannot effectively break down the amino acid Tyr. Untreated, tyrosinemia can be fatal. There are three types of tyrosinemia, caused by the deficiency of a different enzyme. Type I Tyrosinemia, also known as hepatorenal tyrosinemia, is the most severe form of the desease. Symptoms include liver and kidney disturbances and mental retardation. It is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH). By inhibiting this enzyme, the accumulation of the fumarylacetoacetate is prevented. Type II Tyrosinemia (oculocutaneous tyrosinemia, Richner-Hanhart syndrome) is an autosomal recessive condition with onset between ages 2 and 4 years. This form of the disorder can affect the eyes, skin, and mental development. Type II Tyrosinemia is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme tyrosine aminotransferase (TAT). Type III tyrosinemia is a rare disorder caused by a deficiency of the enzyme 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPD), encoded by the gene HPD. Characteristic features of type III tyrosinemia include mild mental retardation, seizures, and periodic loss of balance and coordination (intermittent ataxia). Alkaptonuria (black urine disease) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder of Phe and Tyr metabolism due to a defect in the enzyme homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase (HGD), which participates in the degradation of tyrosine. As a result, homogentisic acid and its oxide, called alkapton, accumulate in the blood and are excreted in urine in large amounts. Excessive homogentisic acid causes damage to cartilage (ochronosis, leading to osteoarthritis) and heart valves as well as precipitating as kidney stones. Oculocutaneous albinism (OCA) is a form of albinism involving the eyes, skin, and hair. Four types of OCA have been described, all caused by a disruption of melanin synthesis and all autosomal recessive disorders. OCA1,2,3,4 are caused by mutations in tyrosinase (TYR), OCA2, tyrosinase-related protein 1 (TYRP1) and solute carrier family 45, member 2 (SLC45A2), respectively. All these proteins are involved in melanin synthesis / transportation.    Outcome effects: Untreated PKU can lead to intellectual disability, seizures, and other serious medical problems. The mainstream treatment for classic PKU patients is a strict Phe-restricted diet supplemented by a medical formula containing amino acids and other nutrients. The primary treatment for type I tyrosinemia is nitisinone (Orfadin). Nitisinone inhibits the conversion of 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate to homogentisic acid by 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPD), the second step in tyrosine degradation.Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

