xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Deficiency of HPRT1 Leads to Lesch-Nyhan Syndrome. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome - a rare disorder of purine metabolism, inherited is linked to the X chromosome and the resulting lack of hypoxanthine phosphoribosyltransferase 1. The frequency of classic Lesch-Nyhan syndrome ranges from 1: 100000-1: 300000; the prevalence of the variant forms of the disease is unknown. Patients with the classic syndrome have a kidney damage and respiratory system and rarely live up to 30 years. Signaling Descriptions: Normally, HPRT1 is present in all cells, in the largest number contained in the brain, particularly in the basal ganglia. Manifestations of the syndrome include hyperuricemia, mental retardation, cerebral palsy, and choreoathetosis with early and later muscle spasticity and dystonia, dysarthria. HGPRT is the \"salvage enzyme\" for the purines: it channels hypoxanthine and guanine back into DNA synthesis. Striatal dopaminergic pathways may be central to the neurological deficits, especially the choreoathetoid dyskinesia and self-mutilation. The link between dopamine and purine synthesis is a nucleotide guanosine triphosphate (GTP). The first step of dopamine synthesis is GTP cyclohydrolase, and significantly a deficiency of this step produces a syndrome that has a neuropathology similar to LNS. Thus a lack of HGPRT may produce a nucleotide deficiency (specifically: GTP deficiency) disorder, resulting in dopamine deficiency. Also 6-hydroxydopamine (oxidopamine) toxicity in rodents used as animal model for the syndrome. Oxidopamine inhibits BDNF - the major factor for grow and homeostasis of neurons. The hyperuricemia is accompanying Lesch-Nyhan syndrome. The increased uric acid levels in tissue of patients with Lesch-Nyhan disease might be toxic to the developing nervous system.Mutated gene is shown in white-out style. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Deficiency of HPRT1 Leads to Lesch-Nyhan Syndrome. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome - a rare disorder of purine metabolism, inherited is linked to the X chromosome and the resulting lack of hypoxanthine phosphoribosyltransferase 1. The frequency of classic Lesch-Nyhan syndrome ranges from 1: 100000-1: 300000; the prevalence of the variant forms of the disease is unknown. Patients with the classic syndrome have a kidney damage and respiratory system and rarely live up to 30 years. Signaling Descriptions: Normally, HPRT1 is present in all cells, in the largest number contained in the brain, particularly in the basal ganglia. Manifestations of the syndrome include hyperuricemia, mental retardation, cerebral palsy, and choreoathetosis with early and later muscle spasticity and dystonia, dysarthria. HGPRT is the \"salvage enzyme\" for the purines: it channels hypoxanthine and guanine back into DNA synthesis. Striatal dopaminergic pathways may be central to the neurological deficits, especially the choreoathetoid dyskinesia and self-mutilation. The link between dopamine and purine synthesis is a nucleotide guanosine triphosphate (GTP). The first step of dopamine synthesis is GTP cyclohydrolase, and significantly a deficiency of this step produces a syndrome that has a neuropathology similar to LNS. Thus a lack of HGPRT may produce a nucleotide deficiency (specifically: GTP deficiency) disorder, resulting in dopamine deficiency. Also 6-hydroxydopamine (oxidopamine) toxicity in rodents used as animal model for the syndrome. Oxidopamine inhibits BDNF - the major factor for grow and homeostasis of neurons. The hyperuricemia is accompanying Lesch-Nyhan syndrome. The increased uric acid levels in tissue of patients with Lesch-Nyhan disease might be toxic to the developing nervous system.Mutated gene is shown in white-out style. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red." EXACT []

