xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Wolfram syndrome, also refered to as DIDMOAD (diabetes insipidus, diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy, and deafness), is a rare genetic disorder which is characterized by a range of symptoms including diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy, and deafness. Wolfram syndrome is also associated with an increased incidence of psychiatric disorders and a significantly shortened life span, averaging only 30 years. Wolfram syndrome is caused by mutations in the WFS1 and CISD2 genes.    Signaling description: WFS1 is related to endoplasmic reticulum (ER) dysfunction in beta cells and the progression of type 2 diabetes. WFS1-deficient beta cells from individuals with Wolfram syndrome show increased levels of ER stress molecules and decreased insulin content. In Wolfram subject samples, XBP1, ATF4, nuclear ATF6 alpha, EIF2AK3, and HSPA5 were increased.    Outcome effects: ER stress did not reduce insulin secretion by inducing cell death, but rather by affecting insulin secretion. In mice, mutated CISD2 causes ER stress and the unfolded protein response. In addition, loss of CISD2 causes a depletion of ER Ca2 stores, a dramatic increase in mitochondrial Ca2 load, increased reactive oxygen and nitrogen species, an increase in the glutathione disulfide/ glutathione and NAD /NADH ratios, and an increase in the ADP/ATP ratio consistent with enhanced ATP utilization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Wolfram syndrome, also refered to as DIDMOAD (diabetes insipidus, diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy, and deafness), is a rare genetic disorder which is characterized by a range of symptoms including diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy, and deafness. Wolfram syndrome is also associated with an increased incidence of psychiatric disorders and a significantly shortened life span, averaging only 30 years. Wolfram syndrome is caused by mutations in the WFS1 and CISD2 genes.    Signaling description: WFS1 is related to endoplasmic reticulum (ER) dysfunction in beta cells and the progression of type 2 diabetes. WFS1-deficient beta cells from individuals with Wolfram syndrome show increased levels of ER stress molecules and decreased insulin content. In Wolfram subject samples, XBP1, ATF4, nuclear ATF6 alpha, EIF2AK3, and HSPA5 were increased.    Outcome effects: ER stress did not reduce insulin secretion by inducing cell death, but rather by affecting insulin secretion. In mice, mutated CISD2 causes ER stress and the unfolded protein response. In addition, loss of CISD2 causes a depletion of ER Ca2 stores, a dramatic increase in mitochondrial Ca2 load, increased reactive oxygen and nitrogen species, an increase in the glutathione disulfide/ glutathione and NAD /NADH ratios, and an increase in the ADP/ATP ratio consistent with enhanced ATP utilization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

