xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial partial lipodystrophies (FPL) are a heterogeneous group of disorders with several distinct phenotypes. FPL type 3 (FPL3) results from an autosomal dominantly inherited mutation in the PPARG gene. FPL3 is associated with a milder phenotype, with the onset of lipodystrophy beginning in the second decade or later in life. Unlike patients with FPL1, excess fat deposition in areas such as the face, neck, and trunk does not occur. In general, patients with FPL3 develop more severe metabolic derangements compared to FPL2 patients. It is characterized by lipodystrophy associated with skeletal abnormalities including hypoplasia of the mandible and clavicle.    Signaling description: PPARG is a member of the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor subfamily of nuclear receptors which binds to PPARA. PPARG promotes AGPAT2, LPL, UCP3, RETN, and LEP. AGPAT2, LPL, and UCP3 are involved in adipocyte differentiation while RETN and LEP play an endocrine role. In addition, PPARG promotes SLC2A4, thus increasing glucose uptake. PPARA promotes ACOX1 and UCP2 which are involved in lipid metabolism. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial partial lipodystrophies (FPL) are a heterogeneous group of disorders with several distinct phenotypes. FPL type 3 (FPL3) results from an autosomal dominantly inherited mutation in the PPARG gene. FPL3 is associated with a milder phenotype, with the onset of lipodystrophy beginning in the second decade or later in life. Unlike patients with FPL1, excess fat deposition in areas such as the face, neck, and trunk does not occur. In general, patients with FPL3 develop more severe metabolic derangements compared to FPL2 patients. It is characterized by lipodystrophy associated with skeletal abnormalities including hypoplasia of the mandible and clavicle.    Signaling description: PPARG is a member of the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor subfamily of nuclear receptors which binds to PPARA. PPARG promotes AGPAT2, LPL, UCP3, RETN, and LEP. AGPAT2, LPL, and UCP3 are involved in adipocyte differentiation while RETN and LEP play an endocrine role. In addition, PPARG promotes SLC2A4, thus increasing glucose uptake. PPARA promotes ACOX1 and UCP2 which are involved in lipid metabolism. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

