xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial partial lipodystrophies (FPL) are a heterogeneous group of disorders with several distinct phenotypes. The most prevalent of these is FPL type 2 (Dunnigan syndrome), which is characterized by a normal distribution of body fat in childhood with a gradual and progressive lipoatrophy of the upper and lower extremities beginning at puberty, resulting in a muscular appearance. Some patients may gain excess fat in the face and chin as well as neck which leads to a cushingoid appearance. Fatty liver and acute pancreatitis may also occur. Metabolic complications may occur later in life, including diabetes mellitus, hypertriglyceridemia, low levels of high-density lipoprotein, and atherosclerotic disease. These are more common in women with Dunnigan syndrome than in affected men.    Signaling description: FPL2 results from an autosomal dominant missense mutation in LMNA, which encodes lamin A and lamin C. LMNA regulates PPARG and SREBF1. PPARG promotes AGPAT2, LPL, UCP3, RETN, and LEP. AGPAT2, LPL, and UCP3 are involved in adipocyte differentiation while RETN and LEP play an endocrine role. In addition, PPARG promotes SLC2A4 which increases glucose uptake. SREBF1 promotes FASN, ACACB, and ACACA which are involved in lipid metabolism. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial partial lipodystrophies (FPL) are a heterogeneous group of disorders with several distinct phenotypes. The most prevalent of these is FPL type 2 (Dunnigan syndrome), which is characterized by a normal distribution of body fat in childhood with a gradual and progressive lipoatrophy of the upper and lower extremities beginning at puberty, resulting in a muscular appearance. Some patients may gain excess fat in the face and chin as well as neck which leads to a cushingoid appearance. Fatty liver and acute pancreatitis may also occur. Metabolic complications may occur later in life, including diabetes mellitus, hypertriglyceridemia, low levels of high-density lipoprotein, and atherosclerotic disease. These are more common in women with Dunnigan syndrome than in affected men.    Signaling description: FPL2 results from an autosomal dominant missense mutation in LMNA, which encodes lamin A and lamin C. LMNA regulates PPARG and SREBF1. PPARG promotes AGPAT2, LPL, UCP3, RETN, and LEP. AGPAT2, LPL, and UCP3 are involved in adipocyte differentiation while RETN and LEP play an endocrine role. In addition, PPARG promotes SLC2A4 which increases glucose uptake. SREBF1 promotes FASN, ACACB, and ACACA which are involved in lipid metabolism. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

