xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Donohue syndrome (also known as Leprechaunism) is an extremely rare and severe genetic disorder. Leprechaunism is an autosomal recessive condition with mutations in the insulin receptor gene (INSR). It is characterized by slow physical and mental development and endocrine abnormalities such as insulin resistance, hyperinsulinemia, lipodystrophy, and precocious puberty. Death usually occurs in utero or in the first years of life. Affected infants may have distinctive abnormalities of the head and face (Leprechaun's face), low birth weight, skin abnormalities, hirsutism, low-set prominent ears, broad nose, acanthosis nigricans, flared nostrils, and thick lips. The cause of the disease is the lack of a fully functional insulin receptor, which has major effects during fetal development and thereafter.     Signaling description: Normally, insulin (INS) binds to its receptors, INSR and IGF1R. The activation of INSR and IGF1R leads to activation of PI3K pathway, which via PDPK1, results in promotion of downstream targets such as AKT1, MAPK-cascade, and JAK-cascade. AKT1 phosphorylates FOXO1/3 and MTOR which are involved in the control of protein degradation. AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits GSK3B which leads to increased activity of FASN, ACACB and ACACA which are involved in lipid metabolism. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates TBC1D4 and promotes SLC2A4 in adipose and muscle tissues, thus increasing glucose uptake. MAPK-cascade and JAK-cascade regulate cell proliferation. Many of the issues associated with Donohue syndrome may be due to IGF1R, which regulates growth of the embryo in addition to its well-known role in regulating blood sugar. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Donohue syndrome (also known as Leprechaunism) is an extremely rare and severe genetic disorder. Leprechaunism is an autosomal recessive condition with mutations in the insulin receptor gene (INSR). It is characterized by slow physical and mental development and endocrine abnormalities such as insulin resistance, hyperinsulinemia, lipodystrophy, and precocious puberty. Death usually occurs in utero or in the first years of life. Affected infants may have distinctive abnormalities of the head and face (Leprechaun's face), low birth weight, skin abnormalities, hirsutism, low-set prominent ears, broad nose, acanthosis nigricans, flared nostrils, and thick lips. The cause of the disease is the lack of a fully functional insulin receptor, which has major effects during fetal development and thereafter.     Signaling description: Normally, insulin (INS) binds to its receptors, INSR and IGF1R. The activation of INSR and IGF1R leads to activation of PI3K pathway, which via PDPK1, results in promotion of downstream targets such as AKT1, MAPK-cascade, and JAK-cascade. AKT1 phosphorylates FOXO1/3 and MTOR which are involved in the control of protein degradation. AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits GSK3B which leads to increased activity of FASN, ACACB and ACACA which are involved in lipid metabolism. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates TBC1D4 and promotes SLC2A4 in adipose and muscle tissues, thus increasing glucose uptake. MAPK-cascade and JAK-cascade regulate cell proliferation. Many of the issues associated with Donohue syndrome may be due to IGF1R, which regulates growth of the embryo in addition to its well-known role in regulating blood sugar. The abnormality in glucose uptake and the dysregulation of lipid and protein metabolism causes insulin resistance which ultimately leads to diabetes mellitus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

