xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder characterized by increased levels of phenylalanine in the blood. Classical PKU is inherited in a strictly autosomal recessive manner and results from mutations in the PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase) gene. PAH is responsible for the conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine. Low levels of PAH result in the accumulation of dietary phenylalanine in the blood. PKU if left untreated leads to intellectual disability, seizures and behavioral problems. Brain dysfunction in PKU occurs due to phenylalanine toxicity to neurons. In addition, PKU is characterized by impaired myelination.    Signaling description: Protein and cholesterol are essential parts of myelin. High concentrations of phenylalanine in the brain may disrupt protein and cholesterol biosynthesis. Phenylalanine enters the brain using the L-type amino acid transporter SLC7A5 in competition with other L-type amino acids (valine, isoleucine, leucine, and methionine).    Outcome effects: In PKU, increased levels of L-phenylalanine in the brain coincide with the lack of other large L-type amino acids which may cause a disruption of proper protein biosynthesis in the neuron. Also, phenylalanine at high concentrations reduces the activity of 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A reductase (HMGCR), the rate-controlling enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. Cholesterol is one of the primary constituents of the myelin lipid, thus decreased HMGCR activity results in reduced myelination in PKU.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder characterized by increased levels of phenylalanine in the blood. Classical PKU is inherited in a strictly autosomal recessive manner and results from mutations in the PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase) gene. PAH is responsible for the conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine. Low levels of PAH result in the accumulation of dietary phenylalanine in the blood. PKU if left untreated leads to intellectual disability, seizures and behavioral problems. Brain dysfunction in PKU occurs due to phenylalanine toxicity to neurons. In addition, PKU is characterized by impaired myelination.    Signaling description: Protein and cholesterol are essential parts of myelin. High concentrations of phenylalanine in the brain may disrupt protein and cholesterol biosynthesis. Phenylalanine enters the brain using the L-type amino acid transporter SLC7A5 in competition with other L-type amino acids (valine, isoleucine, leucine, and methionine).    Outcome effects: In PKU, increased levels of L-phenylalanine in the brain coincide with the lack of other large L-type amino acids which may cause a disruption of proper protein biosynthesis in the neuron. Also, phenylalanine at high concentrations reduces the activity of 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A reductase (HMGCR), the rate-controlling enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. Cholesterol is one of the primary constituents of the myelin lipid, thus decreased HMGCR activity results in reduced myelination in PKU.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

