xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder characterized by increased levels of phenylalanine in the blood. Classical PKU is inherited in a strictly autosomal recessive manner and results from mutations in the PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase) gene. PAH is responsible for the conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine. Low levels of PAH result in the accumulation of dietary phenylalanine in the blood. PKU if left untreated leads to intellectual disability, seizures and behavioral problems. Brain dysfunction in PKU occurs due to phenylalanine toxicity to neurons.    Signaling description: Phenylalanine enters the brain using the L-type amino acid transporter SLC7A5 in competition with eight other L-type amino acids (i.e. tyrosine, tryptophan, valine, isoleucine, leucine, threonine, methionine, and histidine). Among thse amino acids, tyrosine and tryptophan are listed on the pathway since their deficiencies severely affect the pathology manifestation and progress. The SLC7A5 transporter has a high affinity for phenylalanine, resulting in a high transport velocity even if blood phenylalanine level is only slightly elevated.    Outcome effects: Increased brain phenylalanine inhibits the activities of tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) and tryptophan hydroxylase (TPH2) leading to decreased dopamine and serotonin biosynthesis. The lack of dopamine and serotonin disrupts synaptic plasticity and transmission of nerve impulse. In the neuron, phenylalanine is converted to phenylpyruvate by aromatic-amino-acid-glyoxylate transaminase. Phenylpyruvate downregulates pyruvate carboxylase (PC) causing decreased L-aspartate and L-glutamate formation which are important for the listed processes.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder characterized by increased levels of phenylalanine in the blood. Classical PKU is inherited in a strictly autosomal recessive manner and results from mutations in the PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase) gene. PAH is responsible for the conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine. Low levels of PAH result in the accumulation of dietary phenylalanine in the blood. PKU if left untreated leads to intellectual disability, seizures and behavioral problems. Brain dysfunction in PKU occurs due to phenylalanine toxicity to neurons.    Signaling description: Phenylalanine enters the brain using the L-type amino acid transporter SLC7A5 in competition with eight other L-type amino acids (i.e. tyrosine, tryptophan, valine, isoleucine, leucine, threonine, methionine, and histidine). Among thse amino acids, tyrosine and tryptophan are listed on the pathway since their deficiencies severely affect the pathology manifestation and progress. The SLC7A5 transporter has a high affinity for phenylalanine, resulting in a high transport velocity even if blood phenylalanine level is only slightly elevated.    Outcome effects: Increased brain phenylalanine inhibits the activities of tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) and tryptophan hydroxylase (TPH2) leading to decreased dopamine and serotonin biosynthesis. The lack of dopamine and serotonin disrupts synaptic plasticity and transmission of nerve impulse. In the neuron, phenylalanine is converted to phenylpyruvate by aromatic-amino-acid-glyoxylate transaminase. Phenylpyruvate downregulates pyruvate carboxylase (PC) causing decreased L-aspartate and L-glutamate formation which are important for the listed processes.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

