xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder characterized by increased levels of phenylalanine in the blood. Classical PKU is inherited in a strictly autosomal recessive manner and results from mutations in the PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase) gene. PAH is responsible for the conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine. Low levels of PAH result in the accumulation of dietary phenylalanine in the blood. PKU if left untreated leads to intellectual disability, seizures and behavioral problems. Brain dysfunction in PKU occurs due to phenylalanine toxicity to neurons.    Signaling description: The conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine catalyzed by PAH requires a tetrahydrobiopterin cofactor which converts to dihydrobiopterin in the reaction. Dihydrobiopterin is recycled to tetrahydrobiopterin by the enzyme ihydropteridine reductase (QDPR). Tetrahydrobiopterin deficiency caused by a lack of function mutation in the QDPR gene results in tetrahydrobiopterin-deficient hyperphenylalaninemia. Elevated intracellular L-phenylalanine is metabolized by different routes. First, it can be converted to phenylacetate by the consequential action of DDC and MAOB. Secondly, it can be converted to phenylpyruvate by aromatic-amino-acid-glyoxylate transaminase.    Outcome effects: Phenylpyruvate enters neurons where it exerts its toxic effect.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder characterized by increased levels of phenylalanine in the blood. Classical PKU is inherited in a strictly autosomal recessive manner and results from mutations in the PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase) gene. PAH is responsible for the conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine. Low levels of PAH result in the accumulation of dietary phenylalanine in the blood. PKU if left untreated leads to intellectual disability, seizures and behavioral problems. Brain dysfunction in PKU occurs due to phenylalanine toxicity to neurons.    Signaling description: The conversion of L-phenylalanine to L-tyrosine catalyzed by PAH requires a tetrahydrobiopterin cofactor which converts to dihydrobiopterin in the reaction. Dihydrobiopterin is recycled to tetrahydrobiopterin by the enzyme ihydropteridine reductase (QDPR). Tetrahydrobiopterin deficiency caused by a lack of function mutation in the QDPR gene results in tetrahydrobiopterin-deficient hyperphenylalaninemia. Elevated intracellular L-phenylalanine is metabolized by different routes. First, it can be converted to phenylacetate by the consequential action of DDC and MAOB. Secondly, it can be converted to phenylpyruvate by aromatic-amino-acid-glyoxylate transaminase.    Outcome effects: Phenylpyruvate enters neurons where it exerts its toxic effect.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

