xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Raynaud's disease is a rare disorder in which cold-induced constriction of cutaneous arteries causes episodes of reduced blood flow in the affected areas of the body, typically the fingers, and less commonly the toes. Women are more commonly affected than men.    Signaling description: Cold-induced vasoconstriction in the cutaneous circulation is normally a protective response that acts to reduce the heat loss. Cold exposure initiates cutaneous vasoconstriction through a reflex increase in sympathetic nerve activity and through a direct action on the blood vessel, which enhances the sympathetic constriction. Norepinephrine is the major sympathetic neurotransmitter, which initiates cutaneous vasoconstriction by activating two distinct families of receptors, namely the alpha 1- and alpha 2-adrenoceptors on vascular smooth muscle (VSM) cells. At the same time, cold exposure amplifies constriction mediated by alpha 2-adrenoceptors, while simultaneously reducing the activity of alpha 1-adrenoceptors. Although alpha 2-adrenoceptors represent a family of three distinct subtypes (a2A, a2B, and a2C), only one subtype, the a2C-adrenoceptor (ADRA2C), appears to be 'thermosensitive'. In contrast to the a2A-adrenoceptors and a2B-adrenoceptors, which are directed to the plasma membrane, ADRA2C is predominantly localized to the ER/Golgi compartments. Cold causes a remarkable functional and spatial rescue of ADRA2C from the trans-Golgi to the cell surface, where they can respond to stimulation. Immediately upon cooling, there is an increased generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) from VSM mitochondria in cutaneous arteries. This represents the earliest detectable cold-induced response in cutaneous arteries, suggesting that the mitochondria may be the 'thermosensor' responsible for initiating cold-induced vasoconstriction in cutaneous blood vessels. These mitochondrial ROS do not appear to be pathogenic, but instead activate the novel REDOX signaling through the RhoA/Rho kinase (ROCK) pathway.    Outcome effects: RHOA is a member of the Ras family of small GTP-binding proteins and has a central role in regulating actin myosin-dependent processes in VSMs including contractility and motility.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Raynaud's disease is a rare disorder in which cold-induced constriction of cutaneous arteries causes episodes of reduced blood flow in the affected areas of the body, typically the fingers, and less commonly the toes. Women are more commonly affected than men.    Signaling description: Cold-induced vasoconstriction in the cutaneous circulation is normally a protective response that acts to reduce the heat loss. Cold exposure initiates cutaneous vasoconstriction through a reflex increase in sympathetic nerve activity and through a direct action on the blood vessel, which enhances the sympathetic constriction. Norepinephrine is the major sympathetic neurotransmitter, which initiates cutaneous vasoconstriction by activating two distinct families of receptors, namely the alpha 1- and alpha 2-adrenoceptors on vascular smooth muscle (VSM) cells. At the same time, cold exposure amplifies constriction mediated by alpha 2-adrenoceptors, while simultaneously reducing the activity of alpha 1-adrenoceptors. Although alpha 2-adrenoceptors represent a family of three distinct subtypes (a2A, a2B, and a2C), only one subtype, the a2C-adrenoceptor (ADRA2C), appears to be 'thermosensitive'. In contrast to the a2A-adrenoceptors and a2B-adrenoceptors, which are directed to the plasma membrane, ADRA2C is predominantly localized to the ER/Golgi compartments. Cold causes a remarkable functional and spatial rescue of ADRA2C from the trans-Golgi to the cell surface, where they can respond to stimulation. Immediately upon cooling, there is an increased generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) from VSM mitochondria in cutaneous arteries. This represents the earliest detectable cold-induced response in cutaneous arteries, suggesting that the mitochondria may be the 'thermosensor' responsible for initiating cold-induced vasoconstriction in cutaneous blood vessels. These mitochondrial ROS do not appear to be pathogenic, but instead activate the novel REDOX signaling through the RhoA/Rho kinase (ROCK) pathway.    Outcome effects: RHOA is a member of the Ras family of small GTP-binding proteins and has a central role in regulating actin myosin-dependent processes in VSMs including contractility and motility.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

