xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Raynaud's disease is a rare disorder in which cold-induced constriction of cutaneous arteries causes episodes of reduced blood flow in the affected areas of the body, typically the fingers, and less commonly the toes. Women are more commonly affected than men.    Signaling description: Nitric oxide (NO), a free radical, is an important physiological signaling molecule, potent vasodilator, and mediator of oxidative stress. NO is synthesized from L-arginine by NO synthase (NOS), and three main isoforms of NOS have been identified. In patients with Raynaud Disease (RD), NO production by NOS3 in endothelial cells is decreased.    Outcome effects: Reduced NOS3 expression and microcirculatory dysfunction contribute to the development of RD. NOS3 levels are controlled by NOS inhibitor asymmetric dimethylarginine (ADMA). There is evidence to indicate increased circulatory levels of ADMA in the serum of RD patients, suggesting a NOS reduction later on in the disease. Further, S-nitrosylation of the ADMA regulating enzyme dimethylarginine dimethylaminohydrolase (DDAH) diminishes DDAH activity and leads to an accumulation of ADMA. Thus, it appears that NO deficiency contributes to the pathogenesis of RD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Raynaud's disease is a rare disorder in which cold-induced constriction of cutaneous arteries causes episodes of reduced blood flow in the affected areas of the body, typically the fingers, and less commonly the toes. Women are more commonly affected than men.    Signaling description: Nitric oxide (NO), a free radical, is an important physiological signaling molecule, potent vasodilator, and mediator of oxidative stress. NO is synthesized from L-arginine by NO synthase (NOS), and three main isoforms of NOS have been identified. In patients with Raynaud Disease (RD), NO production by NOS3 in endothelial cells is decreased.    Outcome effects: Reduced NOS3 expression and microcirculatory dysfunction contribute to the development of RD. NOS3 levels are controlled by NOS inhibitor asymmetric dimethylarginine (ADMA). There is evidence to indicate increased circulatory levels of ADMA in the serum of RD patients, suggesting a NOS reduction later on in the disease. Further, S-nitrosylation of the ADMA regulating enzyme dimethylarginine dimethylaminohydrolase (DDAH) diminishes DDAH activity and leads to an accumulation of ADMA. Thus, it appears that NO deficiency contributes to the pathogenesis of RD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

