xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. FHM3 is caused by mutations in the SCN1A gene which encodes the pore forming alpha-1-subunit of the voltage-dependent sodium (Na+) channel. This type of ion channel is localized mainly to the body and proximal dendrites of inhibitory neurons.    Signaling description: The specific location of the SCN1A channel plays a key role in the development of the hyperexcitability of dendrites which is an essential component of synaptic transmission. To date, five mutations have been identified in this gene in five families. Gln1489Lys mutation leads to a faster recovery after a quick blockade of SCN1A channels which causes an increase in neuronal excitability. The rise in intracellular Na+ increases the release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft. The increase of glutamate in the synaptic cleft leads to the activation of NMDA receptors on the postsynaptic neurons and metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluR) on the surface of astrocytes.    Outcome effects: NMDA receptor activation leads to the release of K+ from the cell. Hyperdepolarization is important for the progression of cortical depression. Aura preceding migraine attack is the consequence of cortical spreading depression. In addition, the release of K+ causes constriction of proximal blood vessels. According to the vascular theory of migraine pathogenesis, vasoconstriction precedes vasodilation and occurs simultaneously with cortical spreading depression. The activation of neuronal cytosolic calcium-dependent phospholipase A2 (PLA2G4A) leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid can block the K+ large conductance calcium-activated channel (KCNMA1). The main function of KCNMA1 is the removal of K+ from the extracellular space to decrease membrane depolarization. The excess of glutamate is removed from the synaptic cleft by astrocytes, which convert glutamate into glutamine. Glutamine is then transmitted to neurons where it is converted back to glutamate. Thus, Na+ channel mutations lead to the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft causing the emergence of cortical spreading depression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. FHM3 is caused by mutations in the SCN1A gene which encodes the pore forming alpha-1-subunit of the voltage-dependent sodium (Na+) channel. This type of ion channel is localized mainly to the body and proximal dendrites of inhibitory neurons.    Signaling description: The specific location of the SCN1A channel plays a key role in the development of the hyperexcitability of dendrites which is an essential component of synaptic transmission. To date, five mutations have been identified in this gene in five families. Gln1489Lys mutation leads to a faster recovery after a quick blockade of SCN1A channels which causes an increase in neuronal excitability. The rise in intracellular Na+ increases the release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft. The increase of glutamate in the synaptic cleft leads to the activation of NMDA receptors on the postsynaptic neurons and metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluR) on the surface of astrocytes.    Outcome effects: NMDA receptor activation leads to the release of K+ from the cell. Hyperdepolarization is important for the progression of cortical depression. Aura preceding migraine attack is the consequence of cortical spreading depression. In addition, the release of K+ causes constriction of proximal blood vessels. According to the vascular theory of migraine pathogenesis, vasoconstriction precedes vasodilation and occurs simultaneously with cortical spreading depression. The activation of neuronal cytosolic calcium-dependent phospholipase A2 (PLA2G4A) leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid can block the K+ large conductance calcium-activated channel (KCNMA1). The main function of KCNMA1 is the removal of K+ from the extracellular space to decrease membrane depolarization. The excess of glutamate is removed from the synaptic cleft by astrocytes, which convert glutamate into glutamine. Glutamine is then transmitted to neurons where it is converted back to glutamate. Thus, Na+ channel mutations lead to the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft causing the emergence of cortical spreading depression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

