xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. FHM1 is caused by mutations in the CACNA1A gene which encodes a subunit of a P/Q voltage-dependent calcium (Ca2+). Mutations in this gene lead to a partial or complete loss of function.    Signaling description: The general function of CACNA1A is modulating the release of excitatory neurotransmitters in the neuromuscular synapse and central synapses (predominantly in the cerebellum, stem, and cerebral cortex). In FHM1, specific mutations in the CACNA1A gene cause diverse forms of ion channel malfunction such as conductivity disturbance, alteration of its structure, or shifted kinetics. The altered Ca2+ channels open at a lower voltage, as compared to wild type, and ion flow into the cell occurs following lower depolarization level. The altered ion channels also remain open longer, which results in increased Ca2+ ion flow into the cell. Increased intracellular Ca2+ leads to the release of neurotransmitters, primarily glutamate, into the synaptic cleft. High amounts of glutamate trigger ionotropic receptors (NMDA and AMPA) on postsynaptic neurons, as well as metabotropic receptors (mGluR) on the astrocyte surface.    Outcome effects: NMDA receptor activation leads to the release of potassium (K+) from the cell. Hyperdepolarization is important for the progression of cortical depression. Aura preceding migraine attack is the consequence of cortical spreading depression. In addition, the release of K+ causes constriction of proximal blood vessels. According to the vascular theory of migraine pathogenesis, vasoconstriction precedes vasodilation and occurs simultaneously with cortical spreading depression. The activation of neuronal cytosolic Ca2+-dependent phospholipase A2 (PLA2G4A) leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid, which can block K+ large conductance Ca2+-activated channel (KCNMA1). The main function of KCNMA1 is decreasing membrane depolarization by removing K+ from the extracellular space. The excess of glutamate is removed by astrocytes which convert it to glutamine. Glutamine is then transferred into the neurons where it is then converted back to glutamate. Thus, the effects of Ca2+ channel mutations cause the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft where it causes the emergence of cortical spreading depression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. FHM1 is caused by mutations in the CACNA1A gene which encodes a subunit of a P/Q voltage-dependent calcium (Ca2+). Mutations in this gene lead to a partial or complete loss of function.    Signaling description: The general function of CACNA1A is modulating the release of excitatory neurotransmitters in the neuromuscular synapse and central synapses (predominantly in the cerebellum, stem, and cerebral cortex). In FHM1, specific mutations in the CACNA1A gene cause diverse forms of ion channel malfunction such as conductivity disturbance, alteration of its structure, or shifted kinetics. The altered Ca2+ channels open at a lower voltage, as compared to wild type, and ion flow into the cell occurs following lower depolarization level. The altered ion channels also remain open longer, which results in increased Ca2+ ion flow into the cell. Increased intracellular Ca2+ leads to the release of neurotransmitters, primarily glutamate, into the synaptic cleft. High amounts of glutamate trigger ionotropic receptors (NMDA and AMPA) on postsynaptic neurons, as well as metabotropic receptors (mGluR) on the astrocyte surface.    Outcome effects: NMDA receptor activation leads to the release of potassium (K+) from the cell. Hyperdepolarization is important for the progression of cortical depression. Aura preceding migraine attack is the consequence of cortical spreading depression. In addition, the release of K+ causes constriction of proximal blood vessels. According to the vascular theory of migraine pathogenesis, vasoconstriction precedes vasodilation and occurs simultaneously with cortical spreading depression. The activation of neuronal cytosolic Ca2+-dependent phospholipase A2 (PLA2G4A) leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid, which can block K+ large conductance Ca2+-activated channel (KCNMA1). The main function of KCNMA1 is decreasing membrane depolarization by removing K+ from the extracellular space. The excess of glutamate is removed by astrocytes which convert it to glutamine. Glutamine is then transferred into the neurons where it is then converted back to glutamate. Thus, the effects of Ca2+ channel mutations cause the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft where it causes the emergence of cortical spreading depression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

