xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene.    Signaling description: Increased amounts of PGE2 and histamine are found in patients prior to and during migraine attack. Histamine is a ubiquitous messenger molecule released from mast cells, enterochromaffin-like cells, and neurons. Histamine from histamine-rich foods has been shown to provoke a migraine attack. The effects of histamine in vascular endothelial cells are mediated by histamine receptor H1 (HRH1), which activates phospholipase C, beta 1 (PLCB1) through the G protein q polypeptide (GNAQ). Inositol 1,4,5-triphosphate (IP3) synthesized by this enzyme activates inositol 1,4,5-triphosphate receptor 1 (ITPR1), which releases calcium (Ca2+) from the endoplasmic reticulum. In turn, prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) interacts with prostaglandin E2 receptor (PTGER2) and activates adenylate cyclase (ADCY) through Gs alpha (GNAS) initiating the production of cAMP, which then activates protein kinase A (PKA).    Outcome effects: Activated PKA phosphorylates and thus activates endothelial nitric oxide synthase (NOS3 or eNOS), and also triggers the transient receptor potential cation channel (TRPV1) pathway which leads intracellular Ca2+ flux. Ca2+ then activates calmodulin resulting in the phosphorylation of NOS3 by calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase (CAMK2A). The activation of NOS3 leads to the overproduction of nitric oxide (NO). Increased endothelial NO amounts cause vasodilation in dura mater.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene.    Signaling description: Increased amounts of PGE2 and histamine are found in patients prior to and during migraine attack. Histamine is a ubiquitous messenger molecule released from mast cells, enterochromaffin-like cells, and neurons. Histamine from histamine-rich foods has been shown to provoke a migraine attack. The effects of histamine in vascular endothelial cells are mediated by histamine receptor H1 (HRH1), which activates phospholipase C, beta 1 (PLCB1) through the G protein q polypeptide (GNAQ). Inositol 1,4,5-triphosphate (IP3) synthesized by this enzyme activates inositol 1,4,5-triphosphate receptor 1 (ITPR1), which releases calcium (Ca2+) from the endoplasmic reticulum. In turn, prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) interacts with prostaglandin E2 receptor (PTGER2) and activates adenylate cyclase (ADCY) through Gs alpha (GNAS) initiating the production of cAMP, which then activates protein kinase A (PKA).    Outcome effects: Activated PKA phosphorylates and thus activates endothelial nitric oxide synthase (NOS3 or eNOS), and also triggers the transient receptor potential cation channel (TRPV1) pathway which leads intracellular Ca2+ flux. Ca2+ then activates calmodulin resulting in the phosphorylation of NOS3 by calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase (CAMK2A). The activation of NOS3 leads to the overproduction of nitric oxide (NO). Increased endothelial NO amounts cause vasodilation in dura mater.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

