xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. In FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3, the proposed increased amounts of glutamate in the synaptic cleft are caused by mutations in CACNA1A, ATP1A2, and SCN1A genes, respectively.    Signaling description: At the postsynaptic membrane, glutamate interacts with ionotropic glutamate receptors, AMPA (GRIA) and NMDA. The activation of these receptors leads to intracellular calcium (Ca2+) flux and activation of calmodulin. Calmodulin triggers the NF-kB-dependent pathway that activates the transcription of inducible nitric oxide (NO) synthase (NOS2 or iNOS) through phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K). In addition, calmodulin directly binds to and phosphorylates neuronal NO synthase (NOS1 or nNOS) bringing it to an activated state.    Outcome effects: Increased NO synthesis leads to the dilation of vessels in dura mater causing headache. Thus, mutations in FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3 result in the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft and the activation of NO synthesis in neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. In FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3, the proposed increased amounts of glutamate in the synaptic cleft are caused by mutations in CACNA1A, ATP1A2, and SCN1A genes, respectively.    Signaling description: At the postsynaptic membrane, glutamate interacts with ionotropic glutamate receptors, AMPA (GRIA) and NMDA. The activation of these receptors leads to intracellular calcium (Ca2+) flux and activation of calmodulin. Calmodulin triggers the NF-kB-dependent pathway that activates the transcription of inducible nitric oxide (NO) synthase (NOS2 or iNOS) through phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K). In addition, calmodulin directly binds to and phosphorylates neuronal NO synthase (NOS1 or nNOS) bringing it to an activated state.    Outcome effects: Increased NO synthesis leads to the dilation of vessels in dura mater causing headache. Thus, mutations in FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3 result in the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft and the activation of NO synthesis in neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

