xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. FHM2 is caused by mutations in the ATP1A2 that encodes an alpha-2 subunit of the neuronal and glial Na+/K+-ATPase. This ATPase is a transmembrane ion-pump and contributes to the accumulation of membrane potential by pumping potassium (K+) into the cell and sodium (Na+) out of the cell. Up to now, over 50 mutations in ATP1A2 have been identified. These are mostly deletions and reading frame shifts. The reduced activity of Na+/K+-ATPase leads to the disruption of ion balance and glutamate reuptake from the synaptic cleft by glial cells. This results in a slow recovery of neuronal activity after stimulation. The most severe clinical phenotype observed with Leu764Pro, Trp887Arg, and Gly615Arg mutations is characterized by the full loss of Na+/K+-ATPase activity. The Thr345Ala mutation causes a partial reduction of the Na+/K+-ATPase activity, a decrease in affinity to intracellular K+, slower current of K+ from the intercellular space, and a slower recovery after neuronal excitation. The reduction or loss of Na /K -ATPase activity leads to the accumulation of K+ in the extracellular space and Na+ within the cell. Met731Thr and Arg689Gln mutations reduce catalytic turnover, which also leads to the accumulation of K+ in the extracellular space     Outcome effects: The accumulation of K+ in the extracellular space and Na+ within the cell disrupts the glutamate transporters and increases the glutamate concentration in the synaptic cleft which leads to the permanent activation of NMDA receptors on the postsynaptic neurons and metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluR) on the surface of astrocytes. NMDA receptor activation leads to the release of K+ from the cell. Hyperdepolarization is important for the progression of cortical depression. Aura preceding migraine attack is the consequence of cortical spreading depression. In addition, the release of K+ causes constriction of proximal blood vessels. According to the vascular theory of migraine pathogenesis, vasoconstriction precedes vasodilation and occurs simultaneously with cortical spreading depression. The activation of neuronal cytosolic calcium-dependent phospholipase A2 (PLA2G4A) leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid, whichcan block the K large conductance calcium-activated channel (KCNMA1). The main function of KCNMA1 is the removal of K+ from the extracellular space to decrease membrane depolarization. The excess of glutamate is removed from the synaptic cleft by astrocytes, which convert glutamate into glutamine. Glutamine is then transmitted to neurons where it is converted back to glutamate. Thus, the effects of Na+/K+-ATPase mutations result from the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft where one consequence is the emergence of cortical spreading depression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. FHM2 is caused by mutations in the ATP1A2 that encodes an alpha-2 subunit of the neuronal and glial Na+/K+-ATPase. This ATPase is a transmembrane ion-pump and contributes to the accumulation of membrane potential by pumping potassium (K+) into the cell and sodium (Na+) out of the cell. Up to now, over 50 mutations in ATP1A2 have been identified. These are mostly deletions and reading frame shifts. The reduced activity of Na+/K+-ATPase leads to the disruption of ion balance and glutamate reuptake from the synaptic cleft by glial cells. This results in a slow recovery of neuronal activity after stimulation. The most severe clinical phenotype observed with Leu764Pro, Trp887Arg, and Gly615Arg mutations is characterized by the full loss of Na+/K+-ATPase activity. The Thr345Ala mutation causes a partial reduction of the Na+/K+-ATPase activity, a decrease in affinity to intracellular K+, slower current of K+ from the intercellular space, and a slower recovery after neuronal excitation. The reduction or loss of Na /K -ATPase activity leads to the accumulation of K+ in the extracellular space and Na+ within the cell. Met731Thr and Arg689Gln mutations reduce catalytic turnover, which also leads to the accumulation of K+ in the extracellular space     Outcome effects: The accumulation of K+ in the extracellular space and Na+ within the cell disrupts the glutamate transporters and increases the glutamate concentration in the synaptic cleft which leads to the permanent activation of NMDA receptors on the postsynaptic neurons and metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluR) on the surface of astrocytes. NMDA receptor activation leads to the release of K+ from the cell. Hyperdepolarization is important for the progression of cortical depression. Aura preceding migraine attack is the consequence of cortical spreading depression. In addition, the release of K+ causes constriction of proximal blood vessels. According to the vascular theory of migraine pathogenesis, vasoconstriction precedes vasodilation and occurs simultaneously with cortical spreading depression. The activation of neuronal cytosolic calcium-dependent phospholipase A2 (PLA2G4A) leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid, whichcan block the K large conductance calcium-activated channel (KCNMA1). The main function of KCNMA1 is the removal of K+ from the extracellular space to decrease membrane depolarization. The excess of glutamate is removed from the synaptic cleft by astrocytes, which convert glutamate into glutamine. Glutamine is then transmitted to neurons where it is converted back to glutamate. Thus, the effects of Na+/K+-ATPase mutations result from the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft where one consequence is the emergence of cortical spreading depression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

