xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. In FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3, the proposed increased amounts of glutamate in the synaptic cleft are caused by mutations in CACNA1A, ATP1A2, and SCN1A genes, respectively.    Signaling description: Glutamate activates ionotropic receptors (NMDA) on postsynaptic neurons and metabotropic receptors (GRM5 or mGluR) on astrocyte membrane leading to the activation of pathways for arachidonic acid synthesis. In astrocytes, activated mGluR, acting through GNAQ, activates MAPK14 which in turn phosphorylates phospholipase A2. The product of the enzymatic activity of phospholipase A2 is arachidonic acid which is the precursor of prostaglandin H2 (PGH2), synthesized by prostaglandin synthase (PTGS1) in astrocytes. In neurons, PGH2 synthesis involves another mechanism. Activated ionotropic glutamate receptors provide intracellular calcium flux and activation of protein kinase C, gamma (PRKCG) and calmodulin. This in turn leads to the activation of phospholipase A2, group IVA (PLA2G4A) and the product of this phospholipase is also arachidonic acid. The synthesis of PGH2 from arachidonic acid is conducted by prostaglandin synthase (PTGS2) in neurons.    Outcome effects: The following chemical reactions are identical in astrocytes and neurons. PGH2 is the substrate for prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) synthesis catalyzed by prostaglandin-E synthase enzyme (PTGES). PGH2 is considered to be a strong vasodilator due to its capability to activate endothelial NO synthase (NOS3). Thus, mutations in FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3 result in the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft and the activation of PGE2 synthesis in astrocytes and neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene. In FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3, the proposed increased amounts of glutamate in the synaptic cleft are caused by mutations in CACNA1A, ATP1A2, and SCN1A genes, respectively.    Signaling description: Glutamate activates ionotropic receptors (NMDA) on postsynaptic neurons and metabotropic receptors (GRM5 or mGluR) on astrocyte membrane leading to the activation of pathways for arachidonic acid synthesis. In astrocytes, activated mGluR, acting through GNAQ, activates MAPK14 which in turn phosphorylates phospholipase A2. The product of the enzymatic activity of phospholipase A2 is arachidonic acid which is the precursor of prostaglandin H2 (PGH2), synthesized by prostaglandin synthase (PTGS1) in astrocytes. In neurons, PGH2 synthesis involves another mechanism. Activated ionotropic glutamate receptors provide intracellular calcium flux and activation of protein kinase C, gamma (PRKCG) and calmodulin. This in turn leads to the activation of phospholipase A2, group IVA (PLA2G4A) and the product of this phospholipase is also arachidonic acid. The synthesis of PGH2 from arachidonic acid is conducted by prostaglandin synthase (PTGS2) in neurons.    Outcome effects: The following chemical reactions are identical in astrocytes and neurons. PGH2 is the substrate for prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) synthesis catalyzed by prostaglandin-E synthase enzyme (PTGES). PGH2 is considered to be a strong vasodilator due to its capability to activate endothelial NO synthase (NOS3). Thus, mutations in FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3 result in the continuous release of glutamate into the synaptic cleft and the activation of PGE2 synthesis in astrocytes and neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

