xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. Mutations in PRRT2 (ICCA) are strongly associated with hemiplegic migraine.    Signaling description: The molecular mechanism for the effect of PRRT2 (ICCA) gene mutations on migrane pathogenesis has not yet been determined. One of the hypotheses which involves dysregulation of exocytosis is depicted on the pathway.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
