xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene.    Signaling description: Nitric oxide (NO) activates signaling pathways that lead to the relaxation of smooth muscles in the blood vessels of meninges. NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) leading to the activation of protein kinase G (PKG) through cGMP. PKG phosphorylates myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP) which can also be phosphorylated by protein kinase A (PKA). PKA is activated by cAMP and cAMP synthesis is mediated by the interaction between histamine and histamine receptor H2 (HRH2) in addition to the activation of Gs alpha (GNAS) and adenylate cyclase (ADCY).    Outcome effects: Activated MLCP dephosphorylates light chains of myosin causing muscle relaxation such as vasodilation. The administration of one of the most effective anti-migraine drugs called sumatriptan in the beginning of a migraine seizure can stop an attack. This can be explained by the return of blood vessels to a normal state prior to pain signals since sumatriptan is an agonist of serotonin receptors 1B and 1D. The activation of these receptors triggers calcium/calmodulin-dependent processes that lead to the arrest of MLCP, phosphorylation of myosin light chains, and muscle contraction such as vasoconstriction. Thus, increased amounts of NO and histamine activate pathological vasodilation of blood vessels in dura mater causing headache.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is a form of migraine headache that runs in families. Migraines usually cause intense, throbbing pain in one area of the head, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and extreme sensitivity to light and sound. In FHM, a pattern of neurological symptoms called an aura precedes the headache. There exist three identified forms of FHM known as FHM1, FHM2, and FHM3. Each type of the disease is caused by mutations in a particular gene.    Signaling description: Nitric oxide (NO) activates signaling pathways that lead to the relaxation of smooth muscles in the blood vessels of meninges. NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) leading to the activation of protein kinase G (PKG) through cGMP. PKG phosphorylates myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP) which can also be phosphorylated by protein kinase A (PKA). PKA is activated by cAMP and cAMP synthesis is mediated by the interaction between histamine and histamine receptor H2 (HRH2) in addition to the activation of Gs alpha (GNAS) and adenylate cyclase (ADCY).    Outcome effects: Activated MLCP dephosphorylates light chains of myosin causing muscle relaxation such as vasodilation. The administration of one of the most effective anti-migraine drugs called sumatriptan in the beginning of a migraine seizure can stop an attack. This can be explained by the return of blood vessels to a normal state prior to pain signals since sumatriptan is an agonist of serotonin receptors 1B and 1D. The activation of these receptors triggers calcium/calmodulin-dependent processes that lead to the arrest of MLCP, phosphorylation of myosin light chains, and muscle contraction such as vasoconstriction. Thus, increased amounts of NO and histamine activate pathological vasodilation of blood vessels in dura mater causing headache.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

