xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Loss-of-function mutations in either parkin (PARK2) or PTEN-induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) can result in familial autosomal recessive forms of PD while a mutation in ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal esterase L1 (UCHL1) causes an autosomal dominant form. PARK2 is a component of the multiprotein ubiquitin-proteasome system that mediates the targeting of proteins for degradation. Mutations in this gene lead to the accumulation of its substrates which causes neurodegeneration. Mutated PARK2 is unable to bind zinc finger protein 746 (ZNF746) which in free form represses the expression of the transcriptional co-activator, PGC-1-alpha (PPARGC1A) by binding to its promoter. Mutations in PARK2 impair normal mitochondrion organization and biogenesis. PINK1 and PARK2 act in concert to utilize the mitochondrial fission-fusion cycle to initiate the segregation and removal of irreversibly damaged/dysfunctional mitochondria with reduced mitochondrial membrane potential.    Outcome effects: Such mitochondria accumulate PINK1 on the outer mitochondrial membrane which in turn recruits the cytosolic ubiquitin ligase, PARK2; therefore mutations in both genes disturb mitophagy. In addition, UCHL1 is an ubiquitin-recycling enzyme that cleaves the carboxy-terminal peptide bond of polyubiquitine chains. Thus, the supply of ubiquitin for 26S proteasome (PSMD4) may be reduced by mutations in UCHL1. Also, mutated UCHL1 cannot bind to LAMP2 which disrupts chaperone-mediated autophagy.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Loss-of-function mutations in either parkin (PARK2) or PTEN-induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) can result in familial autosomal recessive forms of PD while a mutation in ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal esterase L1 (UCHL1) causes an autosomal dominant form. PARK2 is a component of the multiprotein ubiquitin-proteasome system that mediates the targeting of proteins for degradation. Mutations in this gene lead to the accumulation of its substrates which causes neurodegeneration. Mutated PARK2 is unable to bind zinc finger protein 746 (ZNF746) which in free form represses the expression of the transcriptional co-activator, PGC-1-alpha (PPARGC1A) by binding to its promoter. Mutations in PARK2 impair normal mitochondrion organization and biogenesis. PINK1 and PARK2 act in concert to utilize the mitochondrial fission-fusion cycle to initiate the segregation and removal of irreversibly damaged/dysfunctional mitochondria with reduced mitochondrial membrane potential.    Outcome effects: Such mitochondria accumulate PINK1 on the outer mitochondrial membrane which in turn recruits the cytosolic ubiquitin ligase, PARK2; therefore mutations in both genes disturb mitophagy. In addition, UCHL1 is an ubiquitin-recycling enzyme that cleaves the carboxy-terminal peptide bond of polyubiquitine chains. Thus, the supply of ubiquitin for 26S proteasome (PSMD4) may be reduced by mutations in UCHL1. Also, mutated UCHL1 cannot bind to LAMP2 which disrupts chaperone-mediated autophagy.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

