xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra. Dopamine is the neurotransmitter that helps control muscle movement.    Signaling description: Normally, dopamine is synthesized from L-tyrosine by tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) and then transported from the cytosol into synaptic vesicles by VMAT2 (SLC18A2) to be released when necessary. In the synaptic cleft, dopamine interacts with its receptors on the postsynaptic neuron and triggers the transmission of the nerve impulse. The duration of dopaminergic action is limited by dopamine inactivation, i.e. mainly methylation by catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) in the prefrontal cortex and re-uptake via the dopamine transporter, DAT1 (SLC6A3) and autoreceptors, DRD2 in the striatum. SLC18A2 is responsible for sequestering dopamine into storage vesicles within the neurons, however it is estimated that about 10% of dopamine escape into the neuronal cytoplasm and is subsequently metabolized by MAOA or MAOB, ALDH1A1 (in cytoplasm), ALDH2 (in mitochondria), and COMT. In addition, it is suggested that ALDH1A1 maintains reduced intraneuronal levels of 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde by catalyzing its metabolism to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid. This homeostatic function is crucial because the accumulation of 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde may be neurotoxic and result in neuronal cell death.    Outcome effects: The lack of dopamine due to dopaminergic neuron loss causes motor dysfunction in PD. The use of pharmacological agents that affect the mechanism of dopamine synthesis and degradation is the most common strategy for treatment of PD symptoms."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra. Dopamine is the neurotransmitter that helps control muscle movement.    Signaling description: Normally, dopamine is synthesized from L-tyrosine by tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) and then transported from the cytosol into synaptic vesicles by VMAT2 (SLC18A2) to be released when necessary. In the synaptic cleft, dopamine interacts with its receptors on the postsynaptic neuron and triggers the transmission of the nerve impulse. The duration of dopaminergic action is limited by dopamine inactivation, i.e. mainly methylation by catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) in the prefrontal cortex and re-uptake via the dopamine transporter, DAT1 (SLC6A3) and autoreceptors, DRD2 in the striatum. SLC18A2 is responsible for sequestering dopamine into storage vesicles within the neurons, however it is estimated that about 10% of dopamine escape into the neuronal cytoplasm and is subsequently metabolized by MAOA or MAOB, ALDH1A1 (in cytoplasm), ALDH2 (in mitochondria), and COMT. In addition, it is suggested that ALDH1A1 maintains reduced intraneuronal levels of 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde by catalyzing its metabolism to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid. This homeostatic function is crucial because the accumulation of 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde may be neurotoxic and result in neuronal cell death.    Outcome effects: The lack of dopamine due to dopaminergic neuron loss causes motor dysfunction in PD. The use of pharmacological agents that affect the mechanism of dopamine synthesis and degradation is the most common strategy for treatment of PD symptoms." EXACT []

