xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Microglia is a type of glial cells that act as the first and main form of active immune defense in the central nervous system (CNS). Microglia activation and the subsequent increase in proinflammatory molecules are observed in Parkinson's disease (PD). Microglia activation occurs when aggregates of alpha-synuclein (SNCA) form intermediate-state oligomers (protofibrils) which upon release from neurons are taken up by microglial cells. Subsequently, SNCA protofibrils activate microglia by triggering NF-kB-dependent production of proinflammatory mediators. Mutations in the transcription factor, NR4A2 are associated with a rare familial form of PD. NR4A2 acts as a transrepressor in microglia and astrocytes attenuating inflammatory gene transcription by NF-kB.    Outcome effects: Proinflammatory mediators act directly on dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra, leading to cell death in PD. The positive feedback loop between proinflammatory mediators and microglia further amplify the inflammatory response. Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) of dead neurons, acting primarily through toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), also induce an inflammatory response in the substantia nigra resulting in the loss of dopaminergic neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Microglia is a type of glial cells that act as the first and main form of active immune defense in the central nervous system (CNS). Microglia activation and the subsequent increase in proinflammatory molecules are observed in Parkinson's disease (PD). Microglia activation occurs when aggregates of alpha-synuclein (SNCA) form intermediate-state oligomers (protofibrils) which upon release from neurons are taken up by microglial cells. Subsequently, SNCA protofibrils activate microglia by triggering NF-kB-dependent production of proinflammatory mediators. Mutations in the transcription factor, NR4A2 are associated with a rare familial form of PD. NR4A2 acts as a transrepressor in microglia and astrocytes attenuating inflammatory gene transcription by NF-kB.    Outcome effects: Proinflammatory mediators act directly on dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra, leading to cell death in PD. The positive feedback loop between proinflammatory mediators and microglia further amplify the inflammatory response. Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) of dead neurons, acting primarily through toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), also induce an inflammatory response in the substantia nigra resulting in the loss of dopaminergic neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

