xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: PARK7 is a ubiquitously expressed protein that is mutated in an autosomal recessive, early-onset form of Parkinson's disease (PD). Mutations disrupt PARK7 protein folding, resulting in a misfolded protein that is prone to aggregation. Misfolded proteins sequestered into pericentriolar inclusions are called aggresomes. Parkin (PARK2) cooperates with heterodimeric E2 enzyme (UBE2N) to mediate K63-linked polyubiquitination of misfolded PARK7. LRRK2 (also known as dardarin)-linked is another form of young onset autosomal dominant PD. It is the most common form of disease, still there is little information about protein function and its pathology. Recent research data suggest that increased kinase activity of mutated LRRK2 contributes to an autophagy deregulation, possibly through the activation of the Ca2 /CaMKK/AMPK pathway.    Outcome effects: The polyubiquitination of misfolded PARK7 serves as a signal for the interaction with HDAC6, an adaptor protein that binds the dynein-dynactin complex. Through this interaction, misfolded PARK7 is transported to aggresomes. PARK7 has strong anti-oxidative properties, while a mutated protein is unable to neutralize reactive oxygen species (ROS) which leads to the opening of mPTP and release of CYCS which results in cell death. Normally, PARK7 is processed by caspase-6 (CASP6) and the caspase-6-derived C-terminal fragment of PARK7 prevents p53 (TP53)-dependent cell death. This pathway is blocked due to mutations in PARK7.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: PARK7 is a ubiquitously expressed protein that is mutated in an autosomal recessive, early-onset form of Parkinson's disease (PD). Mutations disrupt PARK7 protein folding, resulting in a misfolded protein that is prone to aggregation. Misfolded proteins sequestered into pericentriolar inclusions are called aggresomes. Parkin (PARK2) cooperates with heterodimeric E2 enzyme (UBE2N) to mediate K63-linked polyubiquitination of misfolded PARK7. LRRK2 (also known as dardarin)-linked is another form of young onset autosomal dominant PD. It is the most common form of disease, still there is little information about protein function and its pathology. Recent research data suggest that increased kinase activity of mutated LRRK2 contributes to an autophagy deregulation, possibly through the activation of the Ca2 /CaMKK/AMPK pathway.    Outcome effects: The polyubiquitination of misfolded PARK7 serves as a signal for the interaction with HDAC6, an adaptor protein that binds the dynein-dynactin complex. Through this interaction, misfolded PARK7 is transported to aggresomes. PARK7 has strong anti-oxidative properties, while a mutated protein is unable to neutralize reactive oxygen species (ROS) which leads to the opening of mPTP and release of CYCS which results in cell death. Normally, PARK7 is processed by caspase-6 (CASP6) and the caspase-6-derived C-terminal fragment of PARK7 prevents p53 (TP53)-dependent cell death. This pathway is blocked due to mutations in PARK7.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

