xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: The exposure of neurotoxins, such as rotenone and Cyperquat, can cause symptoms that are similar to those seen in Parkinson's disease (PD). These neurotoxins are commonly used in toxin-induced animal models of the disease. 1-methyl-4-phenyl-1,2,3,6-tetrahydropyridine (MPTP) is a by-product of the recreational drug, 1-methyl-4-phenyl-4-propionoxypiperidine (desmethylprodine), while rotenone is a broad-spectrum pesticide. MPTP itself is non-toxic and as a lipophilic compound, it can penetrate the blood-brain barrier. Once inside the brain, it is converted by monoamine oxidase B (MAOB) into Cyperquat, which is toxic. Cyperquat and rotenone attack dopaminergic neurons and inhibit NADH dehydrogenase-respiratory chain complex I, resulting in ATP depletion. Respiratory chain disruption leads to a massive superoxide radical burst resulting in the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS). One of the initial events triggered by ATP depletion is loss of energy required for dopamine trafficking. With regards to energy, a lack of ATP diminishes the capability of intracellular ATPase pumps to sequester dopamine into synaptic vesicles. In addition, Cyperquat can cause further insult due to its ability to bind directly to a vesicular monoamine transporter, VMAT2 (SLC18A2), gain entrance into the synaptic vesicles, and release dopamine back into the cytoplasmic compartment.    Outcome effects: In the cytoplasm, dopamine is oxidized by tyrosinase (TYR) and xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH) to dopaminoquinone which in turn is oxidized to aminochrome. The oxidation of dopamine in the cytoplasm causes the formation of oxidopamine that also inhibits NADH dehydrogenase. Free radicals which are formed in the dopamine oxidation reaction then contribute to alpha-synuclein (SNCA) aggregation. Peroxinitrite also inhibits glutathione synthesis which disrupts ROS detoxification in mitochondria.    Animal data: Rotenone and Cyperquat are neurotoxins commonly used in toxin-induced animal models of PD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with incresed level or activity is highlighted in red and entities with decresed level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: The exposure of neurotoxins, such as rotenone and Cyperquat, can cause symptoms that are similar to those seen in Parkinson's disease (PD). These neurotoxins are commonly used in toxin-induced animal models of the disease. 1-methyl-4-phenyl-1,2,3,6-tetrahydropyridine (MPTP) is a by-product of the recreational drug, 1-methyl-4-phenyl-4-propionoxypiperidine (desmethylprodine), while rotenone is a broad-spectrum pesticide. MPTP itself is non-toxic and as a lipophilic compound, it can penetrate the blood-brain barrier. Once inside the brain, it is converted by monoamine oxidase B (MAOB) into Cyperquat, which is toxic. Cyperquat and rotenone attack dopaminergic neurons and inhibit NADH dehydrogenase-respiratory chain complex I, resulting in ATP depletion. Respiratory chain disruption leads to a massive superoxide radical burst resulting in the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS). One of the initial events triggered by ATP depletion is loss of energy required for dopamine trafficking. With regards to energy, a lack of ATP diminishes the capability of intracellular ATPase pumps to sequester dopamine into synaptic vesicles. In addition, Cyperquat can cause further insult due to its ability to bind directly to a vesicular monoamine transporter, VMAT2 (SLC18A2), gain entrance into the synaptic vesicles, and release dopamine back into the cytoplasmic compartment.    Outcome effects: In the cytoplasm, dopamine is oxidized by tyrosinase (TYR) and xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH) to dopaminoquinone which in turn is oxidized to aminochrome. The oxidation of dopamine in the cytoplasm causes the formation of oxidopamine that also inhibits NADH dehydrogenase. Free radicals which are formed in the dopamine oxidation reaction then contribute to alpha-synuclein (SNCA) aggregation. Peroxinitrite also inhibits glutathione synthesis which disrupts ROS detoxification in mitochondria.    Animal data: Rotenone and Cyperquat are neurotoxins commonly used in toxin-induced animal models of PD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with incresed level or activity is highlighted in red and entities with decresed level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

