xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Early-onset Parkinson's disease (PD) is characterized by alpha-synuclein (SNCA) aggregation and Lewy body formation. Normally, SNCA is involved in vesicle trafficking, dopamine release, and chaperone-mediated autophagy. The protein is also a competitive inhibitor of tyrosine hydroxylase (TH), the rate-limiting enzyme in L-DOPA biosynthesis from L-tyrosine. Mutations in the SNCA gene produce incorrect protein folding and lead to its aggregation and disruption of dopamine release which can ultimately cause cell death. Two missense mutations (A30P and A53T) in the gene encoding SNCA have been found in cases of the autosomal dominant form of disease. A triplication of the wild-type SNCA locus was also discovered suggesting that overexpression of SNCA may be sufficient to trigger the disease.    Outcome effects: Misfolded SNCA has an increased tendency to aggregate with calpain and tubulin with TGM2 proteins facilitating this process as well as 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde (DOPAL). An elevated concentration of DOPAL is found in the brains of PD patients with mutated SNCA. Aggregated protein with its interacting substances, mainly ubiquitin, forms Lewy bodies which are the pathological hallmarks of PD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with incresed level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Early-onset Parkinson's disease (PD) is characterized by alpha-synuclein (SNCA) aggregation and Lewy body formation. Normally, SNCA is involved in vesicle trafficking, dopamine release, and chaperone-mediated autophagy. The protein is also a competitive inhibitor of tyrosine hydroxylase (TH), the rate-limiting enzyme in L-DOPA biosynthesis from L-tyrosine. Mutations in the SNCA gene produce incorrect protein folding and lead to its aggregation and disruption of dopamine release which can ultimately cause cell death. Two missense mutations (A30P and A53T) in the gene encoding SNCA have been found in cases of the autosomal dominant form of disease. A triplication of the wild-type SNCA locus was also discovered suggesting that overexpression of SNCA may be sufficient to trigger the disease.    Outcome effects: Misfolded SNCA has an increased tendency to aggregate with calpain and tubulin with TGM2 proteins facilitating this process as well as 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde (DOPAL). An elevated concentration of DOPAL is found in the brains of PD patients with mutated SNCA. Aggregated protein with its interacting substances, mainly ubiquitin, forms Lewy bodies which are the pathological hallmarks of PD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with incresed level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

