xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Glutamate excitotoxicity is one of the pathological processes leading to cell death in PD. The persistent presence of glutamate in the interneuron space occurs due to microglia activation. Glutamate stimulation of NMDA receptors triggers calcium (Ca2+) influx into the cell. Further, Ca2+ activates the NF-kB dependent nitric oxide synthase 1 (NOS1) transcription via the calmodulin/PI3K/Akt pathway. In addition, Ca2+ influx promotes the release of cytochrome c (CYCS) from mitochondria via calpain and calcineurin,     Outcome effects: S-nitrosylation with excessive nitric oxide (NO) generated by NOS1 contributes to protein misfolding. The increased formation of misfolded proteins and release of CYCS commit the neurons to apoptosis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Parkinson's disease (PD) is a chronic and progressive degenerative disorder of the central nervous system which generally affects the motor sphere. Early symptoms include involuntary shaking, muscle rigidity and movement slowness, with demetntia being more common in the advanced stages. PD is characterized by the dramatic loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra.    Signaling description: Glutamate excitotoxicity is one of the pathological processes leading to cell death in PD. The persistent presence of glutamate in the interneuron space occurs due to microglia activation. Glutamate stimulation of NMDA receptors triggers calcium (Ca2+) influx into the cell. Further, Ca2+ activates the NF-kB dependent nitric oxide synthase 1 (NOS1) transcription via the calmodulin/PI3K/Akt pathway. In addition, Ca2+ influx promotes the release of cytochrome c (CYCS) from mitochondria via calpain and calcineurin,     Outcome effects: S-nitrosylation with excessive nitric oxide (NO) generated by NOS1 contributes to protein misfolding. The increased formation of misfolded proteins and release of CYCS commit the neurons to apoptosis." EXACT []

