xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Orexin A/B (hypocretin, HCRT) is a neuropeptide responsible for regulation of sleep / arousal. Elevated activity in HCRT neurons has been hypothesized to underlie sleep and arousal dysregulation in primary insomnia. Elevated HCRT level in haemodialysis patients was associated with insomnia. Insomnia is a common side effect of MAOIs.    Signaling description: Orexins have an excitatory activity in monoaminergic neurons. Noradrenergic cells of the locus coeruleus, dopaminergic cells of the ventral tegmental area, serotonergic cells of the dorsal raphe, histaminergic cells from tuberomammillary nucleus and cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain have been shown to be activated by orexins and are implicated in regulation of arousal. Monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) is an enzyme that degrades norepinephrine (noradrenaline), epinephrine (adrenaline), serotonin, and dopamine. Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) were the first type of developed antidepressant. Because of potentially lethal dietary and drug interactions, monoamine oxidase inhibitors have historically been reserved as a last line of treatment, used only when other classes of antidepressant drugs are not effective."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Orexin A/B (hypocretin, HCRT) is a neuropeptide responsible for regulation of sleep / arousal. Elevated activity in HCRT neurons has been hypothesized to underlie sleep and arousal dysregulation in primary insomnia. Elevated HCRT level in haemodialysis patients was associated with insomnia. Insomnia is a common side effect of MAOIs.    Signaling description: Orexins have an excitatory activity in monoaminergic neurons. Noradrenergic cells of the locus coeruleus, dopaminergic cells of the ventral tegmental area, serotonergic cells of the dorsal raphe, histaminergic cells from tuberomammillary nucleus and cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain have been shown to be activated by orexins and are implicated in regulation of arousal. Monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) is an enzyme that degrades norepinephrine (noradrenaline), epinephrine (adrenaline), serotonin, and dopamine. Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) were the first type of developed antidepressant. Because of potentially lethal dietary and drug interactions, monoamine oxidase inhibitors have historically been reserved as a last line of treatment, used only when other classes of antidepressant drugs are not effective." EXACT []

