xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acetylcholine (ACh) is a neurotransmitter widely distributed in the central (and also peripheral, autonomic and enteric) nervous system (CNS). In the CNS, ACh facilitates many functions, such as learning, memory, attention, sleep regulation and motor control.    Signaling description: Cholinesterase inhibitors are commonly used to treat memory loss and mental changes in individuals with Alzheimer's disease and other types of dementia. One of the main side effects of these drugs is sleep disturbance. These drugs inhibit acetylcholinesterase (ACHE), the enzyme that breaks down ACh and thus boosting the amount available to brain cells.     Outcome effects: The excess of ACh in the synaptic cleft may lead to sleep disturbances.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acetylcholine (ACh) is a neurotransmitter widely distributed in the central (and also peripheral, autonomic and enteric) nervous system (CNS). In the CNS, ACh facilitates many functions, such as learning, memory, attention, sleep regulation and motor control.    Signaling description: Cholinesterase inhibitors are commonly used to treat memory loss and mental changes in individuals with Alzheimer's disease and other types of dementia. One of the main side effects of these drugs is sleep disturbance. These drugs inhibit acetylcholinesterase (ACHE), the enzyme that breaks down ACh and thus boosting the amount available to brain cells.     Outcome effects: The excess of ACh in the synaptic cleft may lead to sleep disturbances.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

