xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Oxidative stress (OS) is proven to be one of the mechanisms for ALS pathogenesis. OS is caused by an imbalance between the generation and removal of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species (ROS/RNS) and causes structural damage and changes in redox-sensitive signaling. About 20-25% of family ALS cases arise from mutations in the superoxide dismutase 1 (SOD1) gene. Normally, SOD1 converts the superoxide anion to hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and protects the cell from OS. This enzyme plays an important role as a cellular antioxidant, and mutant SOD1 (mSOD1) can selectively kill motor neurons due to loss of dismutase function. At the same time, mSOD1 can impair multiple cellular functions including the disruption of CYBB regulation via direct interaction with RAC1. CYBB is a superoxide anion producer, and its dysregulation allows persistent superoxide production. NFE2L2 antioxidant response pathway is the primary cellular defense against the cytotoxic effects of OS.    Outcome effects: NFE2L2 normally elevates the expression of several antioxidant enzymes such as GSTA2, NQO1, GSR, GCLM, GCLC, and HMOX1. NFE2L2 is retained in the cytoplasm by KEAP1 and CUL3 which degrade NFE2L2 by ubiquitylation. KPNA6 has been identified as a protein which facilitates nuclear import and attenuates NFE2L2 signaling. NFE2L2 has been found to be downregulated in ALS. In addition, PPARG is an important neuroprotective factor in ALS and is upregulated in ALS. PPARG is activated by lipid peroxidation end product 4-hydroxynonenal, whose levels are elevated in the cerebrospinal fluid and spinal cord from ALS patients. PPARG triggers GSTA2 and HMOX1 production.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Oxidative stress (OS) is proven to be one of the mechanisms for ALS pathogenesis. OS is caused by an imbalance between the generation and removal of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species (ROS/RNS) and causes structural damage and changes in redox-sensitive signaling. About 20-25% of family ALS cases arise from mutations in the superoxide dismutase 1 (SOD1) gene. Normally, SOD1 converts the superoxide anion to hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and protects the cell from OS. This enzyme plays an important role as a cellular antioxidant, and mutant SOD1 (mSOD1) can selectively kill motor neurons due to loss of dismutase function. At the same time, mSOD1 can impair multiple cellular functions including the disruption of CYBB regulation via direct interaction with RAC1. CYBB is a superoxide anion producer, and its dysregulation allows persistent superoxide production. NFE2L2 antioxidant response pathway is the primary cellular defense against the cytotoxic effects of OS.    Outcome effects: NFE2L2 normally elevates the expression of several antioxidant enzymes such as GSTA2, NQO1, GSR, GCLM, GCLC, and HMOX1. NFE2L2 is retained in the cytoplasm by KEAP1 and CUL3 which degrade NFE2L2 by ubiquitylation. KPNA6 has been identified as a protein which facilitates nuclear import and attenuates NFE2L2 signaling. NFE2L2 has been found to be downregulated in ALS. In addition, PPARG is an important neuroprotective factor in ALS and is upregulated in ALS. PPARG is activated by lipid peroxidation end product 4-hydroxynonenal, whose levels are elevated in the cerebrospinal fluid and spinal cord from ALS patients. PPARG triggers GSTA2 and HMOX1 production.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

