xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles. Most incidences (90%) of ALS are sporadic and around 10% are familial.    Signaling description: About 20-25% of family ALS cases arise from mutations in the superoxide dismutase 1 (SOD1) gene. Normally, SOD1 converts the superoxide anion to hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and protects the cell from OS. This enzyme plays an important role as a cellular antioxidant, and mutant SOD1 (mSOD1) can selectively kill motor neurons due to loss of dismutase function. At the same time, mSOD1 can impair multiple cellular functions with misfolding and aggregation being the most probable source of mSOD1 toxicity. mSOD1 is directly involved in the initiation of caspase activation and apoptotic cell death as it sequesters the anti-apoptotic protein BCL2 in aggregates at the mitochondria making it nonfunctional and toxic. mSOD1 also impairs the association of CYCS with the inner membrane of the mitochondrion leading to cytoplasmic CYCS release and subsequent caspase activation. In addition, mSOD1 can activate TP53, a nuclear phosphoprotein that may play a causative role in apoptosis. Furthermore, the activation of caspase 1 (CASP1) is involved in both apoptosis and inflammation and its prolonged activation exacerbates chronic neural inflammation. mSOD1 disrupts the regulation of CYBB (which is a producer of superoxide anion) through a direct interaction with RAC1. Mitochondrial dysfunction is prominent in ALS. A fraction of toxic mSOD1 aggregated is localized in the mitochondria and may directly damage them. mSOD1 may impair the function of the protein translocation machinery (TOM and TIMM complexes) of the mitochondrion and limit the import of functional proteins into the mitochondrion. Additionally, mSOD1 directly binds to VDAC1 and depolarizes the mitochondrion membrane and disrupts the normal functioning of the electron transport chain. mSOD1 also impairs the anterograde and retrograde axonal transport through the interaction with KIFAP3 and dynein, respectively. Also, DCTN1 is responsible for retrograde axonal transport and may be mutated in some ALS cases. In addition, protein quality control by ER-associated degradation (ERAD) is impaired in ALS. mSOD1 binds to DERL1, a transmembrane protein responsible for the translocation of misfolded proteins from the ER lumen. Dysfunction or overloading of ERAD results in the accumulation of unfolded proteins and triggers the unfolded protein response (UPR). mSOD1 also induces the overexpression of HSPA5, a key regulator of UPR. mSOD1 interacts with heat shock proteins such as HSPA1A, DNAJB1, HSPB1, and CRYAB impairing chaperone function of these proteins. Additionally, mSOD1 may bind to TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TARDBP) and recruit it to aggregates thereby disrupting RNA processing. TARDBP may also be mutated and form aggregates in SOD1-independent cases of ALS. mSOD1 directly contributes to VEGFA deficiency leading to the lack of its neuroprotective function. In addition, mSOD1 may be secreted from the motor neuron through an interaction with CHGA/B. At the same time, RNF19A and HSPB8 were found to reduce the amount of intracellular mSOD1.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, the above-described molecular events lead to the dysfunction and apoptotic death of motor neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles. Most incidences (90%) of ALS are sporadic and around 10% are familial.    Signaling description: About 20-25% of family ALS cases arise from mutations in the superoxide dismutase 1 (SOD1) gene. Normally, SOD1 converts the superoxide anion to hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and protects the cell from OS. This enzyme plays an important role as a cellular antioxidant, and mutant SOD1 (mSOD1) can selectively kill motor neurons due to loss of dismutase function. At the same time, mSOD1 can impair multiple cellular functions with misfolding and aggregation being the most probable source of mSOD1 toxicity. mSOD1 is directly involved in the initiation of caspase activation and apoptotic cell death as it sequesters the anti-apoptotic protein BCL2 in aggregates at the mitochondria making it nonfunctional and toxic. mSOD1 also impairs the association of CYCS with the inner membrane of the mitochondrion leading to cytoplasmic CYCS release and subsequent caspase activation. In addition, mSOD1 can activate TP53, a nuclear phosphoprotein that may play a causative role in apoptosis. Furthermore, the activation of caspase 1 (CASP1) is involved in both apoptosis and inflammation and its prolonged activation exacerbates chronic neural inflammation. mSOD1 disrupts the regulation of CYBB (which is a producer of superoxide anion) through a direct interaction with RAC1. Mitochondrial dysfunction is prominent in ALS. A fraction of toxic mSOD1 aggregated is localized in the mitochondria and may directly damage them. mSOD1 may impair the function of the protein translocation machinery (TOM and TIMM complexes) of the mitochondrion and limit the import of functional proteins into the mitochondrion. Additionally, mSOD1 directly binds to VDAC1 and depolarizes the mitochondrion membrane and disrupts the normal functioning of the electron transport chain. mSOD1 also impairs the anterograde and retrograde axonal transport through the interaction with KIFAP3 and dynein, respectively. Also, DCTN1 is responsible for retrograde axonal transport and may be mutated in some ALS cases. In addition, protein quality control by ER-associated degradation (ERAD) is impaired in ALS. mSOD1 binds to DERL1, a transmembrane protein responsible for the translocation of misfolded proteins from the ER lumen. Dysfunction or overloading of ERAD results in the accumulation of unfolded proteins and triggers the unfolded protein response (UPR). mSOD1 also induces the overexpression of HSPA5, a key regulator of UPR. mSOD1 interacts with heat shock proteins such as HSPA1A, DNAJB1, HSPB1, and CRYAB impairing chaperone function of these proteins. Additionally, mSOD1 may bind to TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TARDBP) and recruit it to aggregates thereby disrupting RNA processing. TARDBP may also be mutated and form aggregates in SOD1-independent cases of ALS. mSOD1 directly contributes to VEGFA deficiency leading to the lack of its neuroprotective function. In addition, mSOD1 may be secreted from the motor neuron through an interaction with CHGA/B. At the same time, RNF19A and HSPB8 were found to reduce the amount of intracellular mSOD1.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, the above-described molecular events lead to the dysfunction and apoptotic death of motor neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

