xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Mutations in a number of genes including TARDBP, FUS, SETX, ANG, C9orf72, and TAF15 are related to ALS pathogenesis. TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TARDBP) is implicated in multiple aspects of RNA processing including transcriptional regulation and alternative splicing via interactions with ADARB1, SRSF2, HNRNPA2B1, and other proteins. Fused in sarcoma protein (FUS) is an RNA-DNA-binding protein that is involved in transcriptional regulation and RNA processing. FUS associates with multiple splicing factors including YBX1, SRSF2, SRSF10, and HNRNPA1. FUS also associates with TBP, which is essential for RNA polymerase I, II, and III transcription activation. Both TARDBP and FUS are involved in microRNA processing. Both associate with DROSHA, the nuclear RNase III-type protein that mediates the first step in microRNA maturation. In addition, TARDBP may be involved in the cytoplasmic cleavage step of microRNA biogenesis by its association with DDX17. TARDBP and FUS also form insoluble toxic aggregates. It is unclear whether the motor neuron injury in ALS is caused by a loss of normal nuclear functions of TARDBP and FUS in RNA processing or by toxic gains of function or both. Senataxin (SETX) is a component of large ribonucleoprotein complexes and is involved in maintaining DNA repair and RNA processing. SETX mutation affects the function of RNA polymerase I and II. Furthermore, TAF15 interacts with the components of the RNA polymerase II complex. Angiogenin (ANG) acts as a transfer RNA-specific ribonuclease and regulates ribosomal RNA transcription; thereby ANG acts as a permissive factor for other angiogenic proteins.    Outcome effects: A number of genes which are responsible for RNA transcription, processing, and splicing or DNA repair were shown to be mutated in ALS. Their association with ALS indicates an important role for these fundamental molecular processes in neurodegeneration.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Mutations in a number of genes including TARDBP, FUS, SETX, ANG, C9orf72, and TAF15 are related to ALS pathogenesis. TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TARDBP) is implicated in multiple aspects of RNA processing including transcriptional regulation and alternative splicing via interactions with ADARB1, SRSF2, HNRNPA2B1, and other proteins. Fused in sarcoma protein (FUS) is an RNA-DNA-binding protein that is involved in transcriptional regulation and RNA processing. FUS associates with multiple splicing factors including YBX1, SRSF2, SRSF10, and HNRNPA1. FUS also associates with TBP, which is essential for RNA polymerase I, II, and III transcription activation. Both TARDBP and FUS are involved in microRNA processing. Both associate with DROSHA, the nuclear RNase III-type protein that mediates the first step in microRNA maturation. In addition, TARDBP may be involved in the cytoplasmic cleavage step of microRNA biogenesis by its association with DDX17. TARDBP and FUS also form insoluble toxic aggregates. It is unclear whether the motor neuron injury in ALS is caused by a loss of normal nuclear functions of TARDBP and FUS in RNA processing or by toxic gains of function or both. Senataxin (SETX) is a component of large ribonucleoprotein complexes and is involved in maintaining DNA repair and RNA processing. SETX mutation affects the function of RNA polymerase I and II. Furthermore, TAF15 interacts with the components of the RNA polymerase II complex. Angiogenin (ANG) acts as a transfer RNA-specific ribonuclease and regulates ribosomal RNA transcription; thereby ANG acts as a permissive factor for other angiogenic proteins.    Outcome effects: A number of genes which are responsible for RNA transcription, processing, and splicing or DNA repair were shown to be mutated in ALS. Their association with ALS indicates an important role for these fundamental molecular processes in neurodegeneration.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

