xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS. Under normal physiological conditions, glutamate is released from presynaptic terminals via Ca2+- dependent exocytosis and activates receptors expressed on postsynaptic motor neurons including AMPA receptor, N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptor, and voltage-gated Ca2 channels. This leads to Ca2+ influx into the cell. Typically, the GluR2 (GRIA2) subunit regulates and restricts Ca2+ permeability in AMPA receptors. Since this subunit is deficient in motor neurons, the motor neuronal AMPA receptor is unusually Ca2+ permeable and therefore vulnerable to excessive glutamate stimulation. The action of glutamate in the cleft is terminated by its rapid reuptake via glutamate transporter proteins. SLC1A3 and SLC1A2 are expressed on glial cells, with astrocytes and SLC1A2 being primarily responsible for the most part of glutamate reuptake. SLC1A1 is located mainly on the presynaptic motor neurons. Glutamate excitotoxicity is a neuronal injury, which results from excessive glutamate-induced stimulation of postsynaptic glutamate receptors. It is largely caused by the impairment of glutamate reuptake. In most cases (~65/%) of sporadic ALS, there is a profound reduction in the expression and activity of SLC1A2 both in the cortex and spinal cord. One suggestion is that the loss of SLC1A2 arises from splicing errors in SLC1A2 mRNA. CASP3 is overexpressed in ALS astrocytes and cleaves SLC1A2. In addition, HNRNPK is required for SLC1A2 transcriptional activation and its reduced expression is found in ALS. Increased reactive oxygen species (ROS) in ALS inhibit the function of SLC1A2 and SLC1A3. A high level of lipid peroxidation product 4-hydroxynonenal has been found in the spinal cords of ALS patients and this has been linked to the inhibition of SLC1A2. Furthermore, D-amino acid oxidase (DAO) is responsible for the oxidative deamination of D-serine, an activator and co-agonist of NMDA receptor. Mutations in DAO may potentially contribute to excitotoxic motor neuron injury.    Outcome effects: Excitotoxicity leads to increased intracellular Ca2+, which in turn leads to increased nitric oxide (NO) formation. Peroxynitrite, which is generated by the reaction of superoxide radical and NO, can subsequently lead to oxidative damage. Ca2+-dependent Ca2+ release from endoplasmic reticulum by RYR3 may accelerate intracellular Ca2+ overload. Normally, mitochondrial Ca2+ uptake responds dynamically and sensitively to changes in cytosolic Ca2+ levels and plays a crucial role in sequestering the large Ca2+ loads. The excessive influx of Ca2+ into mitochondria may result in mitochondrial dysfunction. In ALS, mitochondria may be a critical intracellular target of injury after intense Ca2+ channel stimulation and may trigger apoptosis.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Le Verche V, et al.(2011) Glutamate pathway implication in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: what is the signal in the noise? Journal of Receptor, Ligand and Channel Research.4:1-22."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS. Under normal physiological conditions, glutamate is released from presynaptic terminals via Ca2+- dependent exocytosis and activates receptors expressed on postsynaptic motor neurons including AMPA receptor, N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptor, and voltage-gated Ca2 channels. This leads to Ca2+ influx into the cell. Typically, the GluR2 (GRIA2) subunit regulates and restricts Ca2+ permeability in AMPA receptors. Since this subunit is deficient in motor neurons, the motor neuronal AMPA receptor is unusually Ca2+ permeable and therefore vulnerable to excessive glutamate stimulation. The action of glutamate in the cleft is terminated by its rapid reuptake via glutamate transporter proteins. SLC1A3 and SLC1A2 are expressed on glial cells, with astrocytes and SLC1A2 being primarily responsible for the most part of glutamate reuptake. SLC1A1 is located mainly on the presynaptic motor neurons. Glutamate excitotoxicity is a neuronal injury, which results from excessive glutamate-induced stimulation of postsynaptic glutamate receptors. It is largely caused by the impairment of glutamate reuptake. In most cases (~65/%) of sporadic ALS, there is a profound reduction in the expression and activity of SLC1A2 both in the cortex and spinal cord. One suggestion is that the loss of SLC1A2 arises from splicing errors in SLC1A2 mRNA. CASP3 is overexpressed in ALS astrocytes and cleaves SLC1A2. In addition, HNRNPK is required for SLC1A2 transcriptional activation and its reduced expression is found in ALS. Increased reactive oxygen species (ROS) in ALS inhibit the function of SLC1A2 and SLC1A3. A high level of lipid peroxidation product 4-hydroxynonenal has been found in the spinal cords of ALS patients and this has been linked to the inhibition of SLC1A2. Furthermore, D-amino acid oxidase (DAO) is responsible for the oxidative deamination of D-serine, an activator and co-agonist of NMDA receptor. Mutations in DAO may potentially contribute to excitotoxic motor neuron injury.    Outcome effects: Excitotoxicity leads to increased intracellular Ca2+, which in turn leads to increased nitric oxide (NO) formation. Peroxynitrite, which is generated by the reaction of superoxide radical and NO, can subsequently lead to oxidative damage. Ca2+-dependent Ca2+ release from endoplasmic reticulum by RYR3 may accelerate intracellular Ca2+ overload. Normally, mitochondrial Ca2+ uptake responds dynamically and sensitively to changes in cytosolic Ca2+ levels and plays a crucial role in sequestering the large Ca2+ loads. The excessive influx of Ca2+ into mitochondria may result in mitochondrial dysfunction. In ALS, mitochondria may be a critical intracellular target of injury after intense Ca2+ channel stimulation and may trigger apoptosis.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Le Verche V, et al.(2011) Glutamate pathway implication in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: what is the signal in the noise? Journal of Receptor, Ligand and Channel Research.4:1-22." EXACT []

