xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: The dysregulation of endosomal trafficking and autophagy-endolysosomal pathway has been implicated in several genetic subtypes of ALS. Endocytosis is the process by which extracellular molecules are captured at the cell membrane and taken into the cell. Autophagy is a process which among other functions is responsible for protein degradation. Several proteins implicated in endosomal transport and autophagy may be mutated in ALS. Mutations in the alsin gene (ALS2) are associated with a form of autosomal recessive juvenile-onset ALS. ALS2 interacts with RAB5A and RAC1 and is involved in endosomal fusion/trafficking and microtubule assembly. FIG4 plays a role in the turnover of phosphatidylinositol-3,5-bisphosphate (PtdIns 3,5-P2) which is involved in endocytosis and autophagy. In addition, valosin containing protein (VCP) forms a complex with clathrin coat and is implicated in multiple cellular processes including endocytosis, autophagy, and ubiquitin-dependent protein degradation. Charged multivesicular protein 2B (CHMP2B) is a component of ESCRT-III complex. The ESCRT complexes are known to play a role in multivesicular body biogenesis and autophagosomal-endolysosomal maturation. Additionally, dynactin 1 (DCTN1) functions as an adaptor between dynein and various cargos, thereby regulating the efficiency of dynein motor and taking part in axonal cargo transport. There are also rare ALS-associated mutations that dysregulate endosomal trafficking including vesicle-associated membrane protein-associated protein B (VAPB), optineurin (OPTN), and microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT).    Outcome effects: Autophagy is impaired in ALS leading to the accumulation of insoluble protein aggregates which is one of the key pathological features of ALS.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: The dysregulation of endosomal trafficking and autophagy-endolysosomal pathway has been implicated in several genetic subtypes of ALS. Endocytosis is the process by which extracellular molecules are captured at the cell membrane and taken into the cell. Autophagy is a process which among other functions is responsible for protein degradation. Several proteins implicated in endosomal transport and autophagy may be mutated in ALS. Mutations in the alsin gene (ALS2) are associated with a form of autosomal recessive juvenile-onset ALS. ALS2 interacts with RAB5A and RAC1 and is involved in endosomal fusion/trafficking and microtubule assembly. FIG4 plays a role in the turnover of phosphatidylinositol-3,5-bisphosphate (PtdIns 3,5-P2) which is involved in endocytosis and autophagy. In addition, valosin containing protein (VCP) forms a complex with clathrin coat and is implicated in multiple cellular processes including endocytosis, autophagy, and ubiquitin-dependent protein degradation. Charged multivesicular protein 2B (CHMP2B) is a component of ESCRT-III complex. The ESCRT complexes are known to play a role in multivesicular body biogenesis and autophagosomal-endolysosomal maturation. Additionally, dynactin 1 (DCTN1) functions as an adaptor between dynein and various cargos, thereby regulating the efficiency of dynein motor and taking part in axonal cargo transport. There are also rare ALS-associated mutations that dysregulate endosomal trafficking including vesicle-associated membrane protein-associated protein B (VAPB), optineurin (OPTN), and microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT).    Outcome effects: Autophagy is impaired in ALS leading to the accumulation of insoluble protein aggregates which is one of the key pathological features of ALS.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

