xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Normally, motor neurons are characterized by a high level of CX3CL1 and CD200 expression, which prevents microglia from becoming neurotoxic. CX3CL1 can be either membrane bound or soluble (the latter results from the cleavage of membrane-bound CX3CL1 by ADAM10/17). CX3CL1 interacts with CX3CR1 on the surface of microglia cells resulting in microglia inhibition. The precise mechanism of this effect is unclear; most likely CX3CL1/CX3CR1 interaction modulates the release of proinflammatory cytokines from microglia. CD200/CD200R1 interactions lead to the activation of DOK1 and DOK2 proteins, binding of Ras-GAP and INPP5D, and subsequent downstream inhibition of Ras/MAPK and PI3K/AKT1 pathways.    Outcome effects: Inhibition of Ras/MAPK and PI3K/AKT1 pathways results in a decreased expression of proinflammatory cytokines and NOS2. In amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, CX3CL1 and CD200 are downregulated in motor neurons resulting in the reversal of microglia inhibition and activation of neuroinflammation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1).Gorczynski RM (2012) CD200:CD200R-Mediated Regulation of Immunity. ISRN Immunology.Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.5402/2012/682168 2). Holmannova D, et al.(2012) CD200/CD200R paired potent inhibitory molecules regulating immune and inflammatory responses; Part I: CD200/CD200R structure, activation, and function. Acta Medica (Hradec Kralove).55(1):12-7."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive degenerative lethal disorder of both the upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive atrophy and paralysis of skeletal muscles.    Signaling description: Normally, motor neurons are characterized by a high level of CX3CL1 and CD200 expression, which prevents microglia from becoming neurotoxic. CX3CL1 can be either membrane bound or soluble (the latter results from the cleavage of membrane-bound CX3CL1 by ADAM10/17). CX3CL1 interacts with CX3CR1 on the surface of microglia cells resulting in microglia inhibition. The precise mechanism of this effect is unclear; most likely CX3CL1/CX3CR1 interaction modulates the release of proinflammatory cytokines from microglia. CD200/CD200R1 interactions lead to the activation of DOK1 and DOK2 proteins, binding of Ras-GAP and INPP5D, and subsequent downstream inhibition of Ras/MAPK and PI3K/AKT1 pathways.    Outcome effects: Inhibition of Ras/MAPK and PI3K/AKT1 pathways results in a decreased expression of proinflammatory cytokines and NOS2. In amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, CX3CL1 and CD200 are downregulated in motor neurons resulting in the reversal of microglia inhibition and activation of neuroinflammation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1).Gorczynski RM (2012) CD200:CD200R-Mediated Regulation of Immunity. ISRN Immunology.Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.5402/2012/682168 2). Holmannova D, et al.(2012) CD200/CD200R paired potent inhibitory molecules regulating immune and inflammatory responses; Part I: CD200/CD200R structure, activation, and function. Acta Medica (Hradec Kralove).55(1):12-7." EXACT []

