xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm is triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and death of neurons. Chronic vasoconstriction which lasts several days after SAH causes energy and oxygen (O2) starvation which leads to lack of adenosine triphosphate (ATP), which in turn leads to the arrest of ion pumps, primarily Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase.    Outcome effects: The misbalance between K+ and Na+ ions results in uncontrolled neuronal necrosis. Other significant processes activated by O2 starvation in cells include the appearance of H2O2 and a decrease in ATP synthesis. The products of neuronal death cause vasoconstriction of adjacent blood vessels which extends the injury. Thus, in the late stages of vasospasm necrosis of neurons adjacent to the SAH lesions occurs. The extent of necrosis is directly correlated to the severity of SAH.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm is triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and death of neurons. Chronic vasoconstriction which lasts several days after SAH causes energy and oxygen (O2) starvation which leads to lack of adenosine triphosphate (ATP), which in turn leads to the arrest of ion pumps, primarily Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase.    Outcome effects: The misbalance between K+ and Na+ ions results in uncontrolled neuronal necrosis. Other significant processes activated by O2 starvation in cells include the appearance of H2O2 and a decrease in ATP synthesis. The products of neuronal death cause vasoconstriction of adjacent blood vessels which extends the injury. Thus, in the late stages of vasospasm necrosis of neurons adjacent to the SAH lesions occurs. The extent of necrosis is directly correlated to the severity of SAH.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

