xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). Complement activation plays an important role in the pathogenesis of AD.    Signaling description: The complement cascade is an essential component of the innate immune response which assists in the removal of invading pathogens and cellular debris as well as in the processing of immune complexes. In AD, extracellular aggregated Abeta binds to complement component C1q and so triggers the classical complement pathway. The pathway includes consequent C1S, C2, C4B, and C3 activation.    Outcome effects: The interaction of C3 with CR1 and ITGAM on the surface of astrocytes promotes the phagocytosis of Abeta aggregates by the cell. The acquired polymorphisms in CR1 gene are associated with extracellular Abeta accumulation. Activated C3 also induces the inflammatory response through the activation of C3AR1. Therefore, complement activation is implicated in AD pathogenesis via initiation of chronic inflammation and, at the same time has a role in removing Abeta aggregates."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). Complement activation plays an important role in the pathogenesis of AD.    Signaling description: The complement cascade is an essential component of the innate immune response which assists in the removal of invading pathogens and cellular debris as well as in the processing of immune complexes. In AD, extracellular aggregated Abeta binds to complement component C1q and so triggers the classical complement pathway. The pathway includes consequent C1S, C2, C4B, and C3 activation.    Outcome effects: The interaction of C3 with CR1 and ITGAM on the surface of astrocytes promotes the phagocytosis of Abeta aggregates by the cell. The acquired polymorphisms in CR1 gene are associated with extracellular Abeta accumulation. Activated C3 also induces the inflammatory response through the activation of C3AR1. Therefore, complement activation is implicated in AD pathogenesis via initiation of chronic inflammation and, at the same time has a role in removing Abeta aggregates." EXACT []

