xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: It is not completely clear whether AD related mitochondrial dysfunction is a cause or a disease consequence.The aspects of mitochondrial dysfunction are as follows: mitochondria release higher amounts of reactive oxygen species (ROS) into the cytoplasm; mitochondrial respiratory chain produces lower amounts of ATP; and mitochondria extend in size. Amyloid precursor protein (APP) and Abeta can alter the transport of proteins and small molecules into the mitochondria through their interaction with the membrane and TOM pore complex. Abeta can stimulate the passage of small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Calcium (Ca2 ) depolarizes the membrane and alters the respiratory chain activity. In addition, APP and Abeta can directly and indirectly alter the activity of mitochondrial metabolic enzymes causing an increase in the synthesis of reactive oxygen and hydrogen species and a decrease in the synthesis of ATP by the respiratory chain. APP also participates in the process of mitochondrial fusion which leads to the accumulation of mitochondrial dysfunction. Furthermore, cholesterol and estrogens play a neuroprotective role in AD through the inhibition of Abeta. Under certain conditions, Abeta can eliminate the neuroprotective role of estrogens. However, estrogens can also increase mitochondrial dysfunction by producing ROS during catechol-quinone redox cycling. Additionally, Ca2 and glucose that enter mitochondria from the cytoplasm can activate signaling pathways of apoptosis in neuronal mitochondria.    Outcome effects: Finally, the poorly functioning enlarged mitochondria gradually displace normal organelles, eventually causing decreased ATP production and failing autophagocytosis. This triggers oxidative stress and apoptosis, resulting in degeneration and death of neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: It is not completely clear whether AD related mitochondrial dysfunction is a cause or a disease consequence.The aspects of mitochondrial dysfunction are as follows: mitochondria release higher amounts of reactive oxygen species (ROS) into the cytoplasm; mitochondrial respiratory chain produces lower amounts of ATP; and mitochondria extend in size. Amyloid precursor protein (APP) and Abeta can alter the transport of proteins and small molecules into the mitochondria through their interaction with the membrane and TOM pore complex. Abeta can stimulate the passage of small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Calcium (Ca2 ) depolarizes the membrane and alters the respiratory chain activity. In addition, APP and Abeta can directly and indirectly alter the activity of mitochondrial metabolic enzymes causing an increase in the synthesis of reactive oxygen and hydrogen species and a decrease in the synthesis of ATP by the respiratory chain. APP also participates in the process of mitochondrial fusion which leads to the accumulation of mitochondrial dysfunction. Furthermore, cholesterol and estrogens play a neuroprotective role in AD through the inhibition of Abeta. Under certain conditions, Abeta can eliminate the neuroprotective role of estrogens. However, estrogens can also increase mitochondrial dysfunction by producing ROS during catechol-quinone redox cycling. Additionally, Ca2 and glucose that enter mitochondria from the cytoplasm can activate signaling pathways of apoptosis in neuronal mitochondria.    Outcome effects: Finally, the poorly functioning enlarged mitochondria gradually displace normal organelles, eventually causing decreased ATP production and failing autophagocytosis. This triggers oxidative stress and apoptosis, resulting in degeneration and death of neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

