xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The elevated expression of mitochondrial Abeta-binding alcohol dehydrogenase and the translocation protein (TSPO), shown in AD, may affect the mechanisms of neuroprotective action of estrogen and cholesterol. Normally estrogen and cholesterol act as neuroprotectors restricting the release of reactive oxygen species (ROS) from mitochondria and decreasing cytotoxic effects of Abeta. At the same time, estrogens can themselves produce cytotoxic effects since they participate in the control of mitochondrial fusion and production of ROS during the catechol - quinone redox cycling. In neurons of patients with AD, Abeta in the mitochondrial matrix decreases the activity of antioxidant enzymes such as SOD1 and SOD2, while cholesterol and estrogens enhance their expression.    Outcome effects: Reduced activity of SOD1 and SOD2 leads to the increased synthesis of ROS. Therefore, cholesterol and estrogens have a neuroprotective in AD role not only through the inhibition of Abeta, but also through the enhanced expression of SOD1 and SOD2.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The elevated expression of mitochondrial Abeta-binding alcohol dehydrogenase and the translocation protein (TSPO), shown in AD, may affect the mechanisms of neuroprotective action of estrogen and cholesterol. Normally estrogen and cholesterol act as neuroprotectors restricting the release of reactive oxygen species (ROS) from mitochondria and decreasing cytotoxic effects of Abeta. At the same time, estrogens can themselves produce cytotoxic effects since they participate in the control of mitochondrial fusion and production of ROS during the catechol - quinone redox cycling. In neurons of patients with AD, Abeta in the mitochondrial matrix decreases the activity of antioxidant enzymes such as SOD1 and SOD2, while cholesterol and estrogens enhance their expression.    Outcome effects: Reduced activity of SOD1 and SOD2 leads to the increased synthesis of ROS. Therefore, cholesterol and estrogens have a neuroprotective in AD role not only through the inhibition of Abeta, but also through the enhanced expression of SOD1 and SOD2.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

