xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene encodes the precursor to Abeta, the cetral protein in the biogenesis of AD. The synthesis of Abeta occurs mainly in the membranes of cellular vesicles. The three major isoforms containing the Abeta sequence are produced by alternative splicing of the APP gene primary transcript. The two larger isoforms consisting of 751 and 770 amino acid residues (APP751 and APP770, respectively) contain a Kunitz protease inhibitor (KPI) domain and are ubiquitously expressed. The APP695 isoform does not contain a KPI domain and is predominantly expressed in neurons. The first scenario of APP processing does not include the formation of Abeta peptides and thus is called non-amyloidogenic. APP is initially cleaved by alpha-secretases (ADAM9, ADAM10, and ADAM17) to generate C83 carboxyl-terminal fragment. Then, C83 can undergo further cleavage by gamma-secretase to produce peptide P3 and the APP intracellular domain (AICD). According to the alternative scenario, APP is cleaved sequentially by beta-secretase (BACE1) and gamma-secretase, releasing an ectodomain and retaining the last 99 amino acids of APP (known as C99) within the membrane. The first amino acid of C99 corresponds to the first amino acid of Abeta. C99 is subsequently cleaved by gamma-secretase to release Abeta. This cleavage predominantly produces amyloid peptides of 40 amino acid residues (Abeta 40) and a more amyloidogenic peptide of 42 amino acid residues (Abeta 42), at a ratio of 10:1. In the AD state, the altered structure of APP causes a release of higher amounts of the Abeta 42 after sequential cleavage by BACE1 and gamma-secretase. The presenilin proteins, PSEN1 and PSEN2, which are a part of the gamma-secretase complex may also be mutated and increase the yield of the neurotoxic form, Abeta 42. Mutations in APP, PSEN1, and PSEN2 account for the majority of early-onset familial AD cases. However, most AD cases are the late-onset sporadic form that cannot be explained by a defect in these genes. AICD is a product of both non-amyloidogenic and amyloidogenic processing and participates in the activation of transcription. It interacts with APBB1 and KAT5 to regulate transcription in the nucleus. The role of APP with regard to the intracellular signaling pathways is poorly understood.    Outcome effects: Abeta 42 peptides can be released into the extracellular space where they oligomerize into Abeta 40/42 and aggregate causing plaque formation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene encodes the precursor to Abeta, the cetral protein in the biogenesis of AD. The synthesis of Abeta occurs mainly in the membranes of cellular vesicles. The three major isoforms containing the Abeta sequence are produced by alternative splicing of the APP gene primary transcript. The two larger isoforms consisting of 751 and 770 amino acid residues (APP751 and APP770, respectively) contain a Kunitz protease inhibitor (KPI) domain and are ubiquitously expressed. The APP695 isoform does not contain a KPI domain and is predominantly expressed in neurons. The first scenario of APP processing does not include the formation of Abeta peptides and thus is called non-amyloidogenic. APP is initially cleaved by alpha-secretases (ADAM9, ADAM10, and ADAM17) to generate C83 carboxyl-terminal fragment. Then, C83 can undergo further cleavage by gamma-secretase to produce peptide P3 and the APP intracellular domain (AICD). According to the alternative scenario, APP is cleaved sequentially by beta-secretase (BACE1) and gamma-secretase, releasing an ectodomain and retaining the last 99 amino acids of APP (known as C99) within the membrane. The first amino acid of C99 corresponds to the first amino acid of Abeta. C99 is subsequently cleaved by gamma-secretase to release Abeta. This cleavage predominantly produces amyloid peptides of 40 amino acid residues (Abeta 40) and a more amyloidogenic peptide of 42 amino acid residues (Abeta 42), at a ratio of 10:1. In the AD state, the altered structure of APP causes a release of higher amounts of the Abeta 42 after sequential cleavage by BACE1 and gamma-secretase. The presenilin proteins, PSEN1 and PSEN2, which are a part of the gamma-secretase complex may also be mutated and increase the yield of the neurotoxic form, Abeta 42. Mutations in APP, PSEN1, and PSEN2 account for the majority of early-onset familial AD cases. However, most AD cases are the late-onset sporadic form that cannot be explained by a defect in these genes. AICD is a product of both non-amyloidogenic and amyloidogenic processing and participates in the activation of transcription. It interacts with APBB1 and KAT5 to regulate transcription in the nucleus. The role of APP with regard to the intracellular signaling pathways is poorly understood.    Outcome effects: Abeta 42 peptides can be released into the extracellular space where they oligomerize into Abeta 40/42 and aggregate causing plaque formation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

