xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). In AD, the newly formed Abeta plaques are thought to activate and recruit microglia, the glial cells which act as the first and main form of active immune defense in the central nervous system.    Signaling description: Extracellular oligomers of Abeta activate microglia preferably through the Toll-like receptor (TLR4). The presence of CD14 and LY96 next to TLR4 on the cell surface is important for signal transduction. Abeta oligomers bind to TLR4 which interacts with MYD88. The signal is transduced to IRAK4 and then to IRAK1. Following activation by IRAK1, TRAF6 affects MAP3K7 which phosphorylates IKBK and provokes NF-kB release from its inhibitor, NFKBIA.    Outcome effects: Free NF-kB promotes the expression of TNF, IL6, IL1, and PTGS2 genes. All of these proteins provoke inflammation in the brain, as well as the pathology progression.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). In AD, the newly formed Abeta plaques are thought to activate and recruit microglia, the glial cells which act as the first and main form of active immune defense in the central nervous system.    Signaling description: Extracellular oligomers of Abeta activate microglia preferably through the Toll-like receptor (TLR4). The presence of CD14 and LY96 next to TLR4 on the cell surface is important for signal transduction. Abeta oligomers bind to TLR4 which interacts with MYD88. The signal is transduced to IRAK4 and then to IRAK1. Following activation by IRAK1, TRAF6 affects MAP3K7 which phosphorylates IKBK and provokes NF-kB release from its inhibitor, NFKBIA.    Outcome effects: Free NF-kB promotes the expression of TNF, IL6, IL1, and PTGS2 genes. All of these proteins provoke inflammation in the brain, as well as the pathology progression.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

