xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). The energetic function of mitochondria is altered in the AD brain.    Signaling description: The mitochondria of patients with AD produce less ATP and more reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the presence of Abeta and mutant forms of amyloid precursor protein (APP). Abeta and APP co-localize at the mitochondria, however, it is not known whether they penetrate into the mitochondrion or alter the efficiency of protein transport conducted by the TOM complex from outside. Cytotoxic forms of Abeta and APP stimulate the passage of calcium and other small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Calcium causes membrane depolarization and decreases the respiratory chain activity. In neurons of patients with AD, Abeta in the mitochondrial matrix decreases activity of antioxidant enzymes, such as SOD1, SOD2, and CAT, which leads to the increased synthesis of ROS.    Outcome effects: Finally, mitochondria dysfunction and damage causes the release of toxic radicals into the cytoplasm of neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). The energetic function of mitochondria is altered in the AD brain.    Signaling description: The mitochondria of patients with AD produce less ATP and more reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the presence of Abeta and mutant forms of amyloid precursor protein (APP). Abeta and APP co-localize at the mitochondria, however, it is not known whether they penetrate into the mitochondrion or alter the efficiency of protein transport conducted by the TOM complex from outside. Cytotoxic forms of Abeta and APP stimulate the passage of calcium and other small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Calcium causes membrane depolarization and decreases the respiratory chain activity. In neurons of patients with AD, Abeta in the mitochondrial matrix decreases activity of antioxidant enzymes, such as SOD1, SOD2, and CAT, which leads to the increased synthesis of ROS.    Outcome effects: Finally, mitochondria dysfunction and damage causes the release of toxic radicals into the cytoplasm of neurons.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

