xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: Amyloid plaques are mainly composed of aggregated Abeta, which is derived from the amyloid precursor protein (APP) via a sequential proteolysis by secretases to form a number of peptides. APP is cleaved by beta-secretase (BACE1) with the formation of the C Terminal Fragment (APP-C99), which in turn is cleaved by gamma-secretase resulting in the formation of Abeta. In addition, MME, ACE, IDE, and ECE1 normally cleave Abeta, but become decreased in AD.    Outcome effects: The gamma secretase generates a number of isoforms, including Abeta 40 and Abeta 42, with the most common being Abeta 40. However, mutations in the presenilin proteins (PSEN1 and PSEN2) and APP have an effect on gamma-secretase activity resulting in an increased production of the longer form of amyloid beta, Abeta 42, which is the main component of amyloid deposits found in AD brains. Additionally, interactions of SNCA and TGM2 with Abeta result in Abeta plaque formation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: Amyloid plaques are mainly composed of aggregated Abeta, which is derived from the amyloid precursor protein (APP) via a sequential proteolysis by secretases to form a number of peptides. APP is cleaved by beta-secretase (BACE1) with the formation of the C Terminal Fragment (APP-C99), which in turn is cleaved by gamma-secretase resulting in the formation of Abeta. In addition, MME, ACE, IDE, and ECE1 normally cleave Abeta, but become decreased in AD.    Outcome effects: The gamma secretase generates a number of isoforms, including Abeta 40 and Abeta 42, with the most common being Abeta 40. However, mutations in the presenilin proteins (PSEN1 and PSEN2) and APP have an effect on gamma-secretase activity resulting in an increased production of the longer form of amyloid beta, Abeta 42, which is the main component of amyloid deposits found in AD brains. Additionally, interactions of SNCA and TGM2 with Abeta result in Abeta plaque formation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

