xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: APP gene encodes the precursor to amyloid beta (Abeta). The synthesis of Abeta occurs mainly in the membranes of cellular vesicles. The three major isoforms containing the Abeta sequence are produced by alternative splicing of the APP gene primary transcript. The two larger isoforms consisting of 751 and 770 amino acid residues (APP751 and APP770, respectively) contain a Kunitz protease inhibitor (KPI) domain and are ubiquitously expressed. The APP695 isoform does not contain a KPI domain and is predominantly expressed in neurons. The first scenario of APP processing does not include the formation of Abeta peptides, and thus is called non-amyloidogenic. APP is initially cleaved by alpha-secretase (ADAM9, ADAM10, and ADAM17) to generate C83 carboxyl-terminal fragment. Then C83 can also undergo cleavage by gamma-secretase into the peptide P3 and the APP intracellular domain (AICD). According to the alternative scenario, APP is cleaved sequentially by BACE1 and gamma-secretase, releasing an ectodomain and retaining the last 99 amino acids of APP (known as C99) within the membrane. The first amino acid of C99 is the first amino acid of Abeta. C99 is subsequently cleaved by gamma-secretase to 38-43 amino acids from the amino terminus to release Abeta. This cleavage predominantly produces amyloid peptides of 40 amino acid residues (Abeta 40) and a more amyloidogenic peptide of 42 amino acid residues (Abeta 42), at a ratio of 10:1. In the AD state, the altered structure of APP causes a releas