xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The central protein involved in the pathogenesis of AD is amyloid precursor protein (APP). The APP gene encodes the precursor protein of Abeta. SorL1, a protein genetically linked to AD, regulates the APP translocation inside the cell. Further, there are polymorphisms in several genes encoding the retromer complex (RC) or RC-associated proteins that are genetically associated with AD. In addition, LPR1, a lipoprotein receptor, also plays an important role in AD. Generally, it affects the metabolism of cholesterol and apolipoprotein E, the major risk factors for spontaneous AD. Human apolipoprotein E (APOE) has three common isoforms (ApoE2, ApoE3, and ApoE4) with different effects on lipid and neuronal homeostasis. ApoE4 is the major known genetic risk factor for AD. This allele is over-represented in all late-onset familial AD patients. Brain ApoE4 is synthesized and secreted primarily by astrocytes. Finally, mutations and altered function of proteins involved in endocytosis in neurons are also implicated in AD.    Outcome effects: SorL1 associates with APP in the Golgi apparatus. In the absence or diminished function of SorL1, APP is delivered to a late-endosome where it can be cleaved to produce Abeta peptides. Further, the obstruction of the RC by inhibiting RC proteins such as VPS26A or VPS35 also increases Abeta production. Then, LPR1 also stimulates the formation of Abeta through binding to the APP. Additionally, one of the potential roles for ApoE4 is an enhanced Abeta production. Finally, proteins involved in endocytosis in neurons may also enhance Abeta synthesis in the cell.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The central protein involved in the pathogenesis of AD is amyloid precursor protein (APP). The APP gene encodes the precursor protein of Abeta. SorL1, a protein genetically linked to AD, regulates the APP translocation inside the cell. Further, there are polymorphisms in several genes encoding the retromer complex (RC) or RC-associated proteins that are genetically associated with AD. In addition, LPR1, a lipoprotein receptor, also plays an important role in AD. Generally, it affects the metabolism of cholesterol and apolipoprotein E, the major risk factors for spontaneous AD. Human apolipoprotein E (APOE) has three common isoforms (ApoE2, ApoE3, and ApoE4) with different effects on lipid and neuronal homeostasis. ApoE4 is the major known genetic risk factor for AD. This allele is over-represented in all late-onset familial AD patients. Brain ApoE4 is synthesized and secreted primarily by astrocytes. Finally, mutations and altered function of proteins involved in endocytosis in neurons are also implicated in AD.    Outcome effects: SorL1 associates with APP in the Golgi apparatus. In the absence or diminished function of SorL1, APP is delivered to a late-endosome where it can be cleaved to produce Abeta peptides. Further, the obstruction of the RC by inhibiting RC proteins such as VPS26A or VPS35 also increases Abeta production. Then, LPR1 also stimulates the formation of Abeta through binding to the APP. Additionally, one of the potential roles for ApoE4 is an enhanced Abeta production. Finally, proteins involved in endocytosis in neurons may also enhance Abeta synthesis in the cell.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

