xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). In AD, alterations in the energetic function of mitochondria are observed.    Signaling description: Abeta is thought to decrease the activity of the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle enzymes in AD. Abeta causes a decrease in the activity of pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (OGDH), decreasing NADH reduction and hydrogen traffic from the matrix to the intermembrane space. This impairs the mitochondrial membrane potential. Conversely, the expression of mitochondrial Abeta-binding alcohol dehydrogenase (HSD17B10), which participates in the TCA cycle in the mitochondrial matrix, is increased in AD. Cytotoxic forms of Abeta and amyloid precursor protein (APP) stimulate the passage of calcium (Ca2+) and other small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Ca2+ causes membrane depolarization and decreases activity of the respiratory chain.    Outcome effects: As a result, the respiratory chain of mitochondria produces less ATP and more reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the presence of cytotoxic effects of Abeta, causing oxidative stress."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). In AD, alterations in the energetic function of mitochondria are observed.    Signaling description: Abeta is thought to decrease the activity of the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle enzymes in AD. Abeta causes a decrease in the activity of pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (OGDH), decreasing NADH reduction and hydrogen traffic from the matrix to the intermembrane space. This impairs the mitochondrial membrane potential. Conversely, the expression of mitochondrial Abeta-binding alcohol dehydrogenase (HSD17B10), which participates in the TCA cycle in the mitochondrial matrix, is increased in AD. Cytotoxic forms of Abeta and amyloid precursor protein (APP) stimulate the passage of calcium (Ca2+) and other small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Ca2+ causes membrane depolarization and decreases activity of the respiratory chain.    Outcome effects: As a result, the respiratory chain of mitochondria produces less ATP and more reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the presence of cytotoxic effects of Abeta, causing oxidative stress." EXACT []

