xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: Protein clearance is critical for the maintenance of neuronal cell integrity and a disruption of this process will lead to the accumulation of misfolded proteins and aggregates. Defective removal of Abeta from the brain may be a decisive event in the pathogenesis of AD. Amyloid precursor protein (APP) cleavage creates mainly the amyloid peptides of 40 amino acid residues (Abeta 40) and a more amyloidogenic peptide of 42 amino acid residues (Abeta 42). This cleavage is localized to different subcellular compartments. The Abeta peptides are released to the extracellular space where they can form cytotoxic oligomers and aggregate into amyloid deposits. In the extracellular space, Abeta aggregates are partly removed by the surrounding cells including astrocytes and microglia cells. Abeta binds to A2M, APOE, and CLU which are the ligands of the LRP1 and LRP2 receptors. LRP binds to clathrin adaptor AP-2 which interacts with the clathrin coat.    Outcome effects: The activation of clathrin coat proteins promotes endocytosis of the receptor-ligand complex with Abeta."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: Protein clearance is critical for the maintenance of neuronal cell integrity and a disruption of this process will lead to the accumulation of misfolded proteins and aggregates. Defective removal of Abeta from the brain may be a decisive event in the pathogenesis of AD. Amyloid precursor protein (APP) cleavage creates mainly the amyloid peptides of 40 amino acid residues (Abeta 40) and a more amyloidogenic peptide of 42 amino acid residues (Abeta 42). This cleavage is localized to different subcellular compartments. The Abeta peptides are released to the extracellular space where they can form cytotoxic oligomers and aggregate into amyloid deposits. In the extracellular space, Abeta aggregates are partly removed by the surrounding cells including astrocytes and microglia cells. Abeta binds to A2M, APOE, and CLU which are the ligands of the LRP1 and LRP2 receptors. LRP binds to clathrin adaptor AP-2 which interacts with the clathrin coat.    Outcome effects: The activation of clathrin coat proteins promotes endocytosis of the receptor-ligand complex with Abeta." EXACT []

