xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). The key aspect of the amyloidogenic (Hypothesis) of AD pathogenesis is the toxic effect of amyloid fibrils that are assembled in the extracellular space of a patient's brain.    Signaling description: Abeta can exist in multiple assembly states: monomers, oligomers, protofibrils, and fibrils. Monomeric Abeta is soluble, while oligomeric and cross linked Abeta is not, ultimately generating highly insoluble and stable plaques. Diffuse senile plaques have been shown to consist of mainly Abeta 42(43), and classic senile plaques contain both Abeta 40 and Abeta 42(43), which is less soluble and forms fibrils faster than the shorter isoforms. Amyloid fibrils have toxic effects on the brain, at the same time soluble forms of Abeta can also produce neurotoxic effects. The mechanism by which nonfibrillar oligomers cause the loss of neurons is not yet clear, but the experimental evidence points to its toxicity at the synapses.The degradation of fibrillar Abeta can be accomplished via two major pathways: proteolytic degradation and receptor-mediated transport. In case of degradation, Abeta can be internalized and degraded by activated microglia in the brain. Low-density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 1 (LRP1) and the receptor for advanced glycosylation end products (AGER) are both involved in receptor-mediated flux of Abeta across the blood-brain barrier.    Outcome effects: Once Abeta enters the bloodstream, it can either reenter the brain via AGER receptor, or be delivered to peripheral sites of degradation (e.g. liver and kidney) by chaperone molecules such as APOE or A2M. Chaperones such as clusterin (CLU) and APOE are required for soluble amyloid trafficking into neurons and astrocytes.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). The key aspect of the amyloidogenic (Hypothesis) of AD pathogenesis is the toxic effect of amyloid fibrils that are assembled in the extracellular space of a patient's brain.    Signaling description: Abeta can exist in multiple assembly states: monomers, oligomers, protofibrils, and fibrils. Monomeric Abeta is soluble, while oligomeric and cross linked Abeta is not, ultimately generating highly insoluble and stable plaques. Diffuse senile plaques have been shown to consist of mainly Abeta 42(43), and classic senile plaques contain both Abeta 40 and Abeta 42(43), which is less soluble and forms fibrils faster than the shorter isoforms. Amyloid fibrils have toxic effects on the brain, at the same time soluble forms of Abeta can also produce neurotoxic effects. The mechanism by which nonfibrillar oligomers cause the loss of neurons is not yet clear, but the experimental evidence points to its toxicity at the synapses.The degradation of fibrillar Abeta can be accomplished via two major pathways: proteolytic degradation and receptor-mediated transport. In case of degradation, Abeta can be internalized and degraded by activated microglia in the brain. Low-density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 1 (LRP1) and the receptor for advanced glycosylation end products (AGER) are both involved in receptor-mediated flux of Abeta across the blood-brain barrier.    Outcome effects: Once Abeta enters the bloodstream, it can either reenter the brain via AGER receptor, or be delivered to peripheral sites of degradation (e.g. liver and kidney) by chaperone molecules such as APOE or A2M. Chaperones such as clusterin (CLU) and APOE are required for soluble amyloid trafficking into neurons and astrocytes.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

