xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The enlargement of mitochondria and apoptosis, cellular processes seen in aging neurons, are observed in AD. Normally, mitochondria are dynamically fused and dissociated. The functioning of the entire cell is altered when this equilibrium shifts towards fusion or dissociation. Mutant forms of amyloid precursor protein (APP) and Abeta can either penetrate the mitochondrion or cause alterations of protein transport into the mitochondrion, which is conducted by the TOM complex. This process occurs through the interaction with BAG1 and mPTP. Whether directly or indirectly (the details of the process are not fully understood), Abeta and mutant forms of APP inhibit the expression of OPA1 and DNM1L , which are required for the dissociation. At the same time, the expression of FIS1, required for fusion, is upregulated. Further, Abeta and APP can stimulate the passage of calcium and other small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Calcium induces the inhibition of H- ATPase-mediated ATP production through cytochrome c oxidase. In addition, calcium activates CASP3 by a yet to be identified mechanism. The activation may occur through interaction with BAD and CYCS.    Outcome effects: The altered expression of OPA1, DNM1L, and FIS1 results in mitochondrial fusion (OPA1) and dissociation (DNM1L, and FIS1). Inhibition of H- ATPase-mediated ATP production causes membrane depolarization and decreases activity of the respiratory chain and mitochondrial dysfunction. Finally, the poorly functioning enlarged mitochondria gradually displace normal organelles, eventually causing decreased ATP production and failing autophagocytosis. This triggers oxidative stress and apoptosis, resulting in the degeneration and death of neurons.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The enlargement of mitochondria and apoptosis, cellular processes seen in aging neurons, are observed in AD. Normally, mitochondria are dynamically fused and dissociated. The functioning of the entire cell is altered when this equilibrium shifts towards fusion or dissociation. Mutant forms of amyloid precursor protein (APP) and Abeta can either penetrate the mitochondrion or cause alterations of protein transport into the mitochondrion, which is conducted by the TOM complex. This process occurs through the interaction with BAG1 and mPTP. Whether directly or indirectly (the details of the process are not fully understood), Abeta and mutant forms of APP inhibit the expression of OPA1 and DNM1L , which are required for the dissociation. At the same time, the expression of FIS1, required for fusion, is upregulated. Further, Abeta and APP can stimulate the passage of calcium and other small molecules into the intermembrane space through the mPTP pore complex. Calcium induces the inhibition of H- ATPase-mediated ATP production through cytochrome c oxidase. In addition, calcium activates CASP3 by a yet to be identified mechanism. The activation may occur through interaction with BAD and CYCS.    Outcome effects: The altered expression of OPA1, DNM1L, and FIS1 results in mitochondrial fusion (OPA1) and dissociation (DNM1L, and FIS1). Inhibition of H- ATPase-mediated ATP production causes membrane depolarization and decreases activity of the respiratory chain and mitochondrial dysfunction. Finally, the poorly functioning enlarged mitochondria gradually displace normal organelles, eventually causing decreased ATP production and failing autophagocytosis. This triggers oxidative stress and apoptosis, resulting in the degeneration and death of neurons.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

