xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). Metals are believed to have a role in the pathogenesis of AD.    Signaling description: Abeta protein monomers can bind to copper (Cu), iron (Fe), and zinc (Zn) ions and enzymatically decrease their valence by oxidation. Abeta-Cu2 exhibits both antioxidant activity and prooxidant activity depending on the conditions. In the early stages of AD, monomeric Abeta may protect neurons by binding to potentially toxic Cu2 released at the synapse. In long-term AD, oligomeric Abeta could concentrate the redox-active form of Cu2 at the membrane and cause lipid peroxidation and eventually cell death. Similar to Cu, Fe interacts with Abeta. Fe2 and Fe3 accumulate in senile plaques in patients with AD and can lead to the generation of free radicals. In addition, Zn2 also binds to Abeta and accelerates its aggregation. Zn is a competitive inhibitor of Cu uptake, while Cu competes for Zn uptake. Oxidized Cu and Fe anions participate in the Fenton reaction that results in the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS). In addition, aluminum (Al) induces a conformational change in Abeta which has been shown to inhibit the degradation of Abeta. Abeta coupled with Al is more toxic than normal Abeta, causing membrane disruption or perturbation of neural Ca2 homeostasis and mitochondrial respiration. Furthermore, selenium (Se) protects against 4-hydroxynonenal and Abeta-mediated toxicity in neurons. A deficiency in Se intake was largely observed in patients with AD.    Outcome effects: One of general effects of ROS inside a cell is the peroxidation of lipids in cellular membranes. In turn, lipid peroxidation causes the release of 4-hydroxynonenal or propanedial, which is toxic to the cell. The activation of JUN N-terminal kinases and p38 pathways mediate the 4-hydroxynonenal dependent up-regulation of BACE-1 expression. 4-hydroxynonenal, an oxidative stress mediator, was detected in vivo in the brains of AD patients. HSPB8-BAG3 expression levels in post-mortem human brain tissue from patients with AD were found to be up-regulated. In conclusion, the interaction of Abeta and redox-active metals enhances Abeta aggregation or facilitates the generation of ROS, and causes neuronal death and the activation of an inflammatory mechanism in the brain.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). Metals are believed to have a role in the pathogenesis of AD.    Signaling description: Abeta protein monomers can bind to copper (Cu), iron (Fe), and zinc (Zn) ions and enzymatically decrease their valence by oxidation. Abeta-Cu2 exhibits both antioxidant activity and prooxidant activity depending on the conditions. In the early stages of AD, monomeric Abeta may protect neurons by binding to potentially toxic Cu2 released at the synapse. In long-term AD, oligomeric Abeta could concentrate the redox-active form of Cu2 at the membrane and cause lipid peroxidation and eventually cell death. Similar to Cu, Fe interacts with Abeta. Fe2 and Fe3 accumulate in senile plaques in patients with AD and can lead to the generation of free radicals. In addition, Zn2 also binds to Abeta and accelerates its aggregation. Zn is a competitive inhibitor of Cu uptake, while Cu competes for Zn uptake. Oxidized Cu and Fe anions participate in the Fenton reaction that results in the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS). In addition, aluminum (Al) induces a conformational change in Abeta which has been shown to inhibit the degradation of Abeta. Abeta coupled with Al is more toxic than normal Abeta, causing membrane disruption or perturbation of neural Ca2 homeostasis and mitochondrial respiration. Furthermore, selenium (Se) protects against 4-hydroxynonenal and Abeta-mediated toxicity in neurons. A deficiency in Se intake was largely observed in patients with AD.    Outcome effects: One of general effects of ROS inside a cell is the peroxidation of lipids in cellular membranes. In turn, lipid peroxidation causes the release of 4-hydroxynonenal or propanedial, which is toxic to the cell. The activation of JUN N-terminal kinases and p38 pathways mediate the 4-hydroxynonenal dependent up-regulation of BACE-1 expression. 4-hydroxynonenal, an oxidative stress mediator, was detected in vivo in the brains of AD patients. HSPB8-BAG3 expression levels in post-mortem human brain tissue from patients with AD were found to be up-regulated. In conclusion, the interaction of Abeta and redox-active metals enhances Abeta aggregation or facilitates the generation of ROS, and causes neuronal death and the activation of an inflammatory mechanism in the brain.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

