xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) impair cytoskeleton organization and disrupt intracellular trafficking.NFT formation occurs by several mechanisms: Ca2+ activation of calpain cleaves CDK5R1 (p35) to give rise to CDK5 and p25, and thus controls CDK5 activation. CDK5 then phosphorylates MAPT, which is also phosphorylated by CASP3 and GSK3B. There is a high level of GSK3B expression in the neurons, increased by the APP intracellular domain (AICD) which is formed during gamma-secretase cleavage of APP. NFTs are formed when MAPT becomes pathologically hyper-phosphorylated and dissociates from microtubules which then depolymerize.    Outcome effects: The hyper-phosphorylated MAPT self-aggregates and accumulates in the cell body where it forms paired-helical filaments (NFTs). The hyper-phosphorylated MAPT binds MAP2 and forms insoluble complexes and is cross-linked by the enzymatic activity of TGM2. In addition, heat shock protein 27 (HSPB1) preferentially binds hyper-phosphorylated MAPT and paired helical filaments directly. The formation of this complex alters the conformation of the hyper-phosphorylated MAPT and reduces its concentration by facilitating its degradation and dephosphorylation. MAPT is also dephosphorylated by PPP2CB, but its expression is decreased in AD. Finally, NFTs cause a disruption in intracellular transport and dendrite degradation triggering cell death.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons).    Signaling description: The neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) impair cytoskeleton organization and disrupt intracellular trafficking.NFT formation occurs by several mechanisms: Ca2+ activation of calpain cleaves CDK5R1 (p35) to give rise to CDK5 and p25, and thus controls CDK5 activation. CDK5 then phosphorylates MAPT, which is also phosphorylated by CASP3 and GSK3B. There is a high level of GSK3B expression in the neurons, increased by the APP intracellular domain (AICD) which is formed during gamma-secretase cleavage of APP. NFTs are formed when MAPT becomes pathologically hyper-phosphorylated and dissociates from microtubules which then depolymerize.    Outcome effects: The hyper-phosphorylated MAPT self-aggregates and accumulates in the cell body where it forms paired-helical filaments (NFTs). The hyper-phosphorylated MAPT binds MAP2 and forms insoluble complexes and is cross-linked by the enzymatic activity of TGM2. In addition, heat shock protein 27 (HSPB1) preferentially binds hyper-phosphorylated MAPT and paired helical filaments directly. The formation of this complex alters the conformation of the hyper-phosphorylated MAPT and reduces its concentration by facilitating its degradation and dephosphorylation. MAPT is also dephosphorylated by PPP2CB, but its expression is decreased in AD. Finally, NFTs cause a disruption in intracellular transport and dendrite degradation triggering cell death.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

