xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). The imbalance of calcium (Ca2+) homeostasis which includes Ca2+ overload and Ca2+ exitotoxicity is observed in Alzheimer's disease (AD).    Signaling description: Normally, Ca2+ plays an important role in signal transduction and neuron excitation. Ca2+ overload results from the opening of specific ion channels when Abeta binds to them. These are the voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCCs) and several ligand-gated ion channels (N-methyl-D-aspartate selective glutamate receptor or NMDAR, CHRM1, and CHRNA7). These channels promote Ca2+ influx. In addition, the blockage of Ca2+ ATPase due to lipid peroxidation caused by H2O2 synthesis prevents Ca2+ efflux. Oligomeric Abeta can also form a pore for Ca2+ influx. Furthermore, APP intracellular domain (AICD) is translocated by APBB1 to the nucleus and modifies gene transcription in ways that upset Ca2+ homeostasis. Ca2+ is released into the cytosol from endoplasmic reticulum (ER) via the activation of ITPR1 and RYR3. Basal cytosolic Ca2+ levels are in part maintained by the active uptake by the sarco/endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA) at the ER membrane, which is normally regulated by presenilin protein, and mutations in this protein lead to an abnormal increase in the size of ER Ca2+ stores.    Outcome effects: Ca2+ overload leads to various consequences: Ca2+ activates NOS1 in neurons leading to NO generation and initiating pathological processes such as mitochondrial depolarization, DNA damage, and others. Specifically, Ca2+ enters the mitochondria via MCU channel and disturbs the respiratory chain. The released electrons are accepted by O2 and superoxide radicals are generated. These events lead to ATP depletion the generation of ROS. Superoxide anion also causes lipid peroxidation. Ca2+ also promotes CYCS release by activating BAD through calmodulin. Increased cytosolic Ca2+ concentration initiates the activation of MAPK signaling cascades leading to CREB activation, a process critical for protein synthesis-dependent synaptic plasticity and long-term potentiation (LTP). Ca2+ also triggers neurofibrillary tangle (NFT) formation through calpain/CDK5/MAPT axis and increases beta-secretase (BACE1) activity. Finally, the affected processes including DNA damage, NFT formation, and CYCS release eventually lead to neuron death.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with symptoms including short-term memory loss, disorientation, problems with self care, mood swings, and behavioural issues. AD is characterized by the formation of amyloid plaques (deposits of amyloid beta peptide (Abeta) outside the cells) and neurofibrillary tangles (aggregates of hyperphosphorylated microtubule-associated protein tau (MAPT) inside the neurons). The imbalance of calcium (Ca2+) homeostasis which includes Ca2+ overload and Ca2+ exitotoxicity is observed in Alzheimer's disease (AD).    Signaling description: Normally, Ca2+ plays an important role in signal transduction and neuron excitation. Ca2+ overload results from the opening of specific ion channels when Abeta binds to them. These are the voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCCs) and several ligand-gated ion channels (N-methyl-D-aspartate selective glutamate receptor or NMDAR, CHRM1, and CHRNA7). These channels promote Ca2+ influx. In addition, the blockage of Ca2+ ATPase due to lipid peroxidation caused by H2O2 synthesis prevents Ca2+ efflux. Oligomeric Abeta can also form a pore for Ca2+ influx. Furthermore, APP intracellular domain (AICD) is translocated by APBB1 to the nucleus and modifies gene transcription in ways that upset Ca2+ homeostasis. Ca2+ is released into the cytosol from endoplasmic reticulum (ER) via the activation of ITPR1 and RYR3. Basal cytosolic Ca2+ levels are in part maintained by the active uptake by the sarco/endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA) at the ER membrane, which is normally regulated by presenilin protein, and mutations in this protein lead to an abnormal increase in the size of ER Ca2+ stores.    Outcome effects: Ca2+ overload leads to various consequences: Ca2+ activates NOS1 in neurons leading to NO generation and initiating pathological processes such as mitochondrial depolarization, DNA damage, and others. Specifically, Ca2+ enters the mitochondria via MCU channel and disturbs the respiratory chain. The released electrons are accepted by O2 and superoxide radicals are generated. These events lead to ATP depletion the generation of ROS. Superoxide anion also causes lipid peroxidation. Ca2+ also promotes CYCS release by activating BAD through calmodulin. Increased cytosolic Ca2+ concentration initiates the activation of MAPK signaling cascades leading to CREB activation, a process critical for protein synthesis-dependent synaptic plasticity and long-term potentiation (LTP). Ca2+ also triggers neurofibrillary tangle (NFT) formation through calpain/CDK5/MAPT axis and increases beta-secretase (BACE1) activity. Finally, the affected processes including DNA damage, NFT formation, and CYCS release eventually lead to neuron death.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

