xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Adenosine is an inhibitory modulator of brain activity with neuroprotective and anticonvulsant properties. Under physiological conditions, a major source of synaptic adenosine is the vesicular release of ATP from astrocytes and neurons followed by its extracellular degradation to adenosine by a cascade of ectonucleotidases (ENTPD1 and NT5C1A). Adenosine kinase (ADK) is responsible for the removal of adenosine via phosphorylation to AMP. ADK undergoes rapid coordinated changes following brain injury such as epileptic seizures or stroke. The transient downregulation of ADK after acute brain injury protects the brain from seizures. Conversely, chronic overexpression of ADK causes seizures. Normally, the inhibitory effects of adenosine are primarily mediated by the activation of ADORA1 that is linked to inhibitory G proteins, which activate G-protein-dependent inwardly rectifying K channels and inhibit Ca2+ channels. At the same time, downregulation of ADORA1 receptors has been described as a consequence of chronic seizures. In chronic recurrent seizures, astrogliosis (neuroinflammation) leads to the upregulation of ADK.    Outcome effects: Normally, as a consequence of ADORA action, the release of various neurotransmitters, particularly glutamate is blocked; whereas, in chronic recurrent seizures, the upregulation of ADK results in a decrease of adenosine and spontaneous recurrent seizures develop.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Adenosine is an inhibitory modulator of brain activity with neuroprotective and anticonvulsant properties. Under physiological conditions, a major source of synaptic adenosine is the vesicular release of ATP from astrocytes and neurons followed by its extracellular degradation to adenosine by a cascade of ectonucleotidases (ENTPD1 and NT5C1A). Adenosine kinase (ADK) is responsible for the removal of adenosine via phosphorylation to AMP. ADK undergoes rapid coordinated changes following brain injury such as epileptic seizures or stroke. The transient downregulation of ADK after acute brain injury protects the brain from seizures. Conversely, chronic overexpression of ADK causes seizures. Normally, the inhibitory effects of adenosine are primarily mediated by the activation of ADORA1 that is linked to inhibitory G proteins, which activate G-protein-dependent inwardly rectifying K channels and inhibit Ca2+ channels. At the same time, downregulation of ADORA1 receptors has been described as a consequence of chronic seizures. In chronic recurrent seizures, astrogliosis (neuroinflammation) leads to the upregulation of ADK.    Outcome effects: Normally, as a consequence of ADORA action, the release of various neurotransmitters, particularly glutamate is blocked; whereas, in chronic recurrent seizures, the upregulation of ADK results in a decrease of adenosine and spontaneous recurrent seizures develop.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

