xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Genetic factors are thought to account for approximately 70% of the risk of developing epilepsy.    Signaling description: Epilepsy genetics has two broad categories: genes and loci discovered in association with primary epilepsy syndromes, in which the epilepsy is a principal presenting feature and genes discovered in association with disorders of brain development that are associated with epilepsy. Most of the genes identified in the latter group encode ion channels (channelopathies). Specifically, mutations in voltage-dependent Na+ channel genes including SCN1A, SCN2A, and SCN1B are found in epilepsy. SCN1A mutations are found in febrile seizures plus (GEFS+), severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy (Dravet syndrome/SMEI), and intractable childhood epilepsy with frequent generalized tonic-clonic seizures (ICEGTC). SCN2A mutations are associated with benign familial neonatal-infantile seizures (BFNIS), GEFS, and SMEI-like phenotype. SCN1B mutations are also reported in GEFS+. In addition, mutations of K channels including KCNA1, KCNQ2/3, KCNMA1, and KCNJ11 are found in epilepsy. KCNA1 mutations are associated with episodic ataxia type 1 (EA1). KCNQ2/3 mutations are found in benign familial neonatal convulsions (BFNC). KCNMA1 mutations are described in generalized epilepsy and paroxysmal dyskinesia. KCNJ11 mutations cause neonatal diabetes mellitus accompanied by developmental delay and seizures. Furthermore, hyperpolarization activated cyclic nucleotide-gated K channels 1 and 2 (HCN1/2) mutations may contribute to epilepsy development. LGI1 is a secreted non-ion channel protein that is mutated in autosomal dominant lateral temporal lobe epilepsy (ADLTLE, also known as autosomal dominant partial epilepsy with auditory features, ADPEAF). LGI1 can modulate the function of K channels including KCNA1, KCNAB1, and KCNA4. Wild-type LGI1 has been reported to increase the surface expression of excitatory AMPA-type glutamate receptors, via an interaction with the transmembrane adhesion protein ADAM22. As mutant LGI1 appears unable to bind ADAM22, this mutation could result in a decrease in excitatory AMPA currents. Mutations of Ca2+ channels such as CACNA1A, CACNA1H, and CACNB4 are also described in epilepsy. CACNA1A mutations may be associated with familial hemiplegic migraine, spinocerebellar ataxia type 6, and episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2). Some patients with EA2 also suffer from absence seizures. CACNB4 mutations are associated with juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME). CACNA1H mutations occasionally occur in association with childhood absence epilepsy (CAE). EFHC1 is a protein associated with Ca2+ channel CACNA1E. Mutations of EFHC1 are associated with JME. In addition, Cl- channel CLCN2 mutations are associated with different forms of idiopathic generalized epilepsy including CAE, JME, juvenile absence epilepsy (JAE), and epilepsy with grand mal seizures on awakening (EGMA). Furthermore, nicotinic acetylcholine receptors are ligand-gated ion channels. Mutations in CHRNA and CHRNB2 genes are found in patients suffering from nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy (NFLE), while CHRNA7 mutations are found in JME. Lastly, GABA-A receptor in the brain is most often composed of GABRA1, GABRB2, and GABRG2 subunits. GABRA1 mutations are associated with JME and CAE, while GABRG2 mutations are found in GEFS+, CAE, and febrile seizures. GABRD encodes the delta subunit that forms heteromeric extra- or peri-synaptic channels that differ from the synaptic GABA-A receptor channels. GABRD mutations are found in GEFS+ seizures.    Outcome effects: Seizures may result from gain-of-function mutations of sodium (Na+) and calcium (Ca2+) channels or loss-of-function mutation"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Genetic factors are thought to account for approximately 70% of the risk of developing epilepsy.    Signaling description: Epilepsy genetics has two broad categories: genes and loci discovered in association with primary epilepsy syndromes, in which the epilepsy is a principal presenting feature and genes discovered in association with disorders of brain development that are associated with epilepsy. Most of the genes identified in the latter group encode ion channels (channelopathies). Specifically, mutations in voltage-dependent Na+ channel genes including SCN1A, SCN2A, and SCN1B are found in epilepsy. SCN1A mutations are found in febrile seizures plus (GEFS+), severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy (Dravet syndrome/SMEI), and intractable childhood epilepsy with frequent generalized tonic-clonic seizures (ICEGTC). SCN2A mutations are associated with benign familial neonatal-infantile seizures (BFNIS), GEFS, and SMEI-like phenotype. SCN1B mutations are also reported in GEFS+. In addition, mutations of K channels including KCNA1, KCNQ2/3, KCNMA1, and KCNJ11 are found in epilepsy. KCNA1 mutations are associated with episodic ataxia type 1 (EA1). KCNQ2/3 mutations are found in benign familial neonatal convulsions (BFNC). KCNMA1 mutations are described in generalized epilepsy and paroxysmal dyskinesia. KCNJ11 mutations cause neonatal diabetes mellitus accompanied by developmental delay and seizures. Furthermore, hyperpolarization activated cyclic nucleotide-gated K channels 1 and 2 (HCN1/2) mutations may contribute to epilepsy development. LGI1 is a secreted non-ion channel protein that is mutated in autosomal dominant lateral temporal lobe epilepsy (ADLTLE, also known as autosomal dominant partial epilepsy with auditory features, ADPEAF). LGI1 can modulate the function of K channels including KCNA1, KCNAB1, and KCNA4. Wild-type LGI1 has been reported to increase the surface expression of excitatory AMPA-type glutamate receptors, via an interaction with the transmembrane adhesion protein ADAM22. As mutant LGI1 appears unable to bind ADAM22, this mutation could result in a decrease in excitatory AMPA currents. Mutations of Ca2+ channels such as CACNA1A, CACNA1H, and CACNB4 are also described in epilepsy. CACNA1A mutations may be associated with familial hemiplegic migraine, spinocerebellar ataxia type 6, and episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2). Some patients with EA2 also suffer from absence seizures. CACNB4 mutations are associated with juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME). CACNA1H mutations occasionally occur in association with childhood absence epilepsy (CAE). EFHC1 is a protein associated with Ca2+ channel CACNA1E. Mutations of EFHC1 are associated with JME. In addition, Cl- channel CLCN2 mutations are associated with different forms of idiopathic generalized epilepsy including CAE, JME, juvenile absence epilepsy (JAE), and epilepsy with grand mal seizures on awakening (EGMA). Furthermore, nicotinic acetylcholine receptors are ligand-gated ion channels. Mutations in CHRNA and CHRNB2 genes are found in patients suffering from nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy (NFLE), while CHRNA7 mutations are found in JME. Lastly, GABA-A receptor in the brain is most often composed of GABRA1, GABRB2, and GABRG2 subunits. GABRA1 mutations are associated with JME and CAE, while GABRG2 mutations are found in GEFS+, CAE, and febrile seizures. GABRD encodes the delta subunit that forms heteromeric extra- or peri-synaptic channels that differ from the synaptic GABA-A receptor channels. GABRD mutations are found in GEFS+ seizures.    Outcome effects: Seizures may result from gain-of-function mutations of sodium (Na+) and calcium (Ca2+) channels or loss-of-function mutation" EXACT []

