xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: At an excitatory glutamatergic synapse, the neurotransmitter glutamate is released from presynaptic terminal vesicles. Glutamate binds to AMPA and N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptors on the postsynaptic membrane and opens ion channels allowing the inward flow of calcium (Ca2+) and sodium (Na+) leading to action potential generation. Astrocytes are known to aid in homeostatic functions including the clearance of neuronally-released potassium (K+) and glutamate from the synapse. The propagation of action potentials and synaptic activity after local depolarization lead to fast fluctuations of the extracellular K+ concentration. If increases in K+ remain uncorrected, the resting potential may become more positive and affect activation of transmembrane ion channels, receptors, and transporters and may generate epileptiform activity. Two different mechanisms are thought to balance K+ including K+ uptake and spatial K+ buffering. Spatial buffering is dependent on proper distribution and function of astrocytic K+ channels, water channels, and gap junctions. In astrocytes, the inward rectifying K+ channel Kir4.1 (KCNJ10) allows K+ influx at negative membrane potentials. Elevated levels of K are taken up by perisynaptic astrocytes along with water by KCNJ10 and AQP4 channels, respectively. The loss or redistribution of AQP4 and KCNJ10 away from astrocytic endfeet observed in epilepsy would exacerbate astrocyte swelling since water flux into astrocytes at synapses would be increased while efflux via endfeet into the blood vessels would be decreased. GJA1 and GJB6 comprise the main connexins forming gap junctions of astrocytes. The lack of these gap junction proteins observed in epilepsy leads to the generation of spontaneous epileptiform activity due to impaired ion special buffering.    Outcome effects: Astrocytes are responsible for glutamate uptake. The reduced excitatory amino acid transporter 1/2 (SLC1A3/SLC1A2) expression in epilepsy may lead to delayed clearance of glutamate from the synapse. Glutamate is converted into glutamine through glutamine synthetase (GLUL). The reduced expression of GLUL in epilepsy elevates the concentration of glutamate in astrocytes, providing more glutamate to be released. Elevated extracellular glutamate decreases the threshold for seizure induction. In addition, glutamine is used as a precursor for the synthesis of GABA in GABAergic neurons. The loss of GLUL in reactive astrocytes leads to a decrease in glutamine and GABA levels in GABAergic terminals. Consequently, GABAergic inhibition is reduced which leads to a reduction in the seizure threshold.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Clasadonte J, Haydon PG (2012) Astrocytes and Epilepsy. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition. 2). Coulter DA (2009). Neurotransmitter function. Consequences of Diminished Function of the Glutamine Cycle on Inhibitory Function in the Epileptic Brain. Encyclopedia of Basic Epilepsy Research. pp 877-882.Avialable from: http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123739612003453 Hubbard JA, Hsu MS, 3). 23376026"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: At an excitatory glutamatergic synapse, the neurotransmitter glutamate is released from presynaptic terminal vesicles. Glutamate binds to AMPA and N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptors on the postsynaptic membrane and opens ion channels allowing the inward flow of calcium (Ca2+) and sodium (Na+) leading to action potential generation. Astrocytes are known to aid in homeostatic functions including the clearance of neuronally-released potassium (K+) and glutamate from the synapse. The propagation of action potentials and synaptic activity after local depolarization lead to fast fluctuations of the extracellular K+ concentration. If increases in K+ remain uncorrected, the resting potential may become more positive and affect activation of transmembrane ion channels, receptors, and transporters and may generate epileptiform activity. Two different mechanisms are thought to balance K+ including K+ uptake and spatial K+ buffering. Spatial buffering is dependent on proper distribution and function of astrocytic K+ channels, water channels, and gap junctions. In astrocytes, the inward rectifying K+ channel Kir4.1 (KCNJ10) allows K+ influx at negative membrane potentials. Elevated levels of K are taken up by perisynaptic astrocytes along with water by KCNJ10 and AQP4 channels, respectively. The loss or redistribution of AQP4 and KCNJ10 away from astrocytic endfeet observed in epilepsy would exacerbate astrocyte swelling since water flux into astrocytes at synapses would be increased while efflux via endfeet into the blood vessels would be decreased. GJA1 and GJB6 comprise the main connexins forming gap junctions of astrocytes. The lack of these gap junction proteins observed in epilepsy leads to the generation of spontaneous epileptiform activity due to impaired ion special buffering.    Outcome effects: Astrocytes are responsible for glutamate uptake. The reduced excitatory amino acid transporter 1/2 (SLC1A3/SLC1A2) expression in epilepsy may lead to delayed clearance of glutamate from the synapse. Glutamate is converted into glutamine through glutamine synthetase (GLUL). The reduced expression of GLUL in epilepsy elevates the concentration of glutamate in astrocytes, providing more glutamate to be released. Elevated extracellular glutamate decreases the threshold for seizure induction. In addition, glutamine is used as a precursor for the synthesis of GABA in GABAergic neurons. The loss of GLUL in reactive astrocytes leads to a decrease in glutamine and GABA levels in GABAergic terminals. Consequently, GABAergic inhibition is reduced which leads to a reduction in the seizure threshold.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Clasadonte J, Haydon PG (2012) Astrocytes and Epilepsy. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition. 2). Coulter DA (2009). Neurotransmitter function. Consequences of Diminished Function of the Glutamine Cycle on Inhibitory Function in the Epileptic Brain. Encyclopedia of Basic Epilepsy Research. pp 877-882.Avialable from: http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123739612003453 Hubbard JA, Hsu MS, 3). 23376026" EXACT []

