xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus. A ketogenic diet (KD) is a high-fat, adequate-protein, and low-carbohydrate diet used to treat refractory epilepsy in children.    Signaling description: The exact mechanism of anticonvulsant action for KD remains unknown, and several hypotheses have been suggested. KD decreases insulin levels, and thus is expected to inhibit MTOR activity indirectly by decreasing the PI3K/AKT1/MTOR signaling. Energy and nutrient deprivation may also lead to the inhibition of MTOR via the STK11/AMPK/TSC1/TSC2 activation. In addition, polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) boost the activity of PPARA, which in turn activates brain-specific uncoupling protein UCP2 and downregulates NF-kB. Further, reduced glucose levels activate PANX1, which releases ATP into the extracellular space. ATP is converted into adenosine which subsequently activates adenosine receptor ADORA1. Lastly, leptin (LEP) inhibits seizures possibly through the blockade of AMPA receptor-mediated synaptic transmission. Chronic ketosis is predicted to cause increased levels of acetone which may activate KCNK2 channels.    Outcome effects: MTOR inhibition has been shown to prevent epileptogenesis. Further, UCP2 activation results in limited ROS generation and inhibition of neurodegeneration, whereas reduction of IL1B, TNF, and IL6 expression via NF-kB inhibition leads to reduced neuroinflammation. ADORA1 activation is coupled to ATP-activated inward rectifier potassium (K+) channels. Ultimately, the opening of these K channels hyperpolarizes neurons and diminishes neuronal excitability and contributes to the anticonvulsant actions of KD. Activation of KCNK2 channels results in hyperpolarizion of neurons and limits their excitability. Acting directly, PUFAs may inhibit both voltage-gated Na and Ca2+ channels, activate a lipid-sensitive class of KCNK2 K channels, and enhance the activity of Na /K -exchanging ATPase to limit neuronal excitability and dampen seizure activity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Masino SA, Rho JM (2012) Mechanisms of Ketogenic Diet Action. Jasper's basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition. 2).Wong M (2010) Mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) inhibition as a potential antiepileptogenic therapy: From tuberous sclerosis to common acquired epilepsies. Epilepsia.51(1):27-36."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus. A ketogenic diet (KD) is a high-fat, adequate-protein, and low-carbohydrate diet used to treat refractory epilepsy in children.    Signaling description: The exact mechanism of anticonvulsant action for KD remains unknown, and several hypotheses have been suggested. KD decreases insulin levels, and thus is expected to inhibit MTOR activity indirectly by decreasing the PI3K/AKT1/MTOR signaling. Energy and nutrient deprivation may also lead to the inhibition of MTOR via the STK11/AMPK/TSC1/TSC2 activation. In addition, polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) boost the activity of PPARA, which in turn activates brain-specific uncoupling protein UCP2 and downregulates NF-kB. Further, reduced glucose levels activate PANX1, which releases ATP into the extracellular space. ATP is converted into adenosine which subsequently activates adenosine receptor ADORA1. Lastly, leptin (LEP) inhibits seizures possibly through the blockade of AMPA receptor-mediated synaptic transmission. Chronic ketosis is predicted to cause increased levels of acetone which may activate KCNK2 channels.    Outcome effects: MTOR inhibition has been shown to prevent epileptogenesis. Further, UCP2 activation results in limited ROS generation and inhibition of neurodegeneration, whereas reduction of IL1B, TNF, and IL6 expression via NF-kB inhibition leads to reduced neuroinflammation. ADORA1 activation is coupled to ATP-activated inward rectifier potassium (K+) channels. Ultimately, the opening of these K channels hyperpolarizes neurons and diminishes neuronal excitability and contributes to the anticonvulsant actions of KD. Activation of KCNK2 channels results in hyperpolarizion of neurons and limits their excitability. Acting directly, PUFAs may inhibit both voltage-gated Na and Ca2+ channels, activate a lipid-sensitive class of KCNK2 K channels, and enhance the activity of Na /K -exchanging ATPase to limit neuronal excitability and dampen seizure activity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Masino SA, Rho JM (2012) Mechanisms of Ketogenic Diet Action. Jasper's basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition. 2).Wong M (2010) Mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) inhibition as a potential antiepileptogenic therapy: From tuberous sclerosis to common acquired epilepsies. Epilepsia.51(1):27-36." EXACT []

