xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Several studies demonstrated impaired integrity of the blood-brain barrier (BBB) as an important hallmark in brain injuries. Albumin (ALB) binds to TGFBR1/TGFBR2 in astrocytes and activates the SMAD/NF-kB signaling. Further, thrombin (F2) was shown activate of persistent inward sodium (Na+) current via voltage-gated channels affecting resting membrane potential. In addition, excessive glutamate release following BBB disrupting events may result in N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptor-mediated calcium (Ca2+)-calmodulin-dependent activation of PI3K/AKT1 and activation of MTOR. Finally, signaling through metabotropic glutamate receptor GRM5 may have an impact on the increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels.    Outcome effects: The activation of TGFBR/SMAD/NF-kB axis by ALB induces rapid transcriptional modifications, astrocytic transformation, and may decrease seizure threshold. Then, persistent sodium current induced by thrombin also facilitates seizures. Further, PI3K/AKT1 and MTOR activation causes a cascade of cellular events that contribute to epileptogenesis including axonal sprouting and increased expression of ion channels and neurotransmitter receptors. Lastly, the increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels via GMR5 signaling may also promote seizures.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Bianchi R, Wong RK, Merlin LR (2012) Glutamate Receptors in Epilepsy: Group I mGluR-Mediated Epileptogenesis. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Several studies demonstrated impaired integrity of the blood-brain barrier (BBB) as an important hallmark in brain injuries. Albumin (ALB) binds to TGFBR1/TGFBR2 in astrocytes and activates the SMAD/NF-kB signaling. Further, thrombin (F2) was shown activate of persistent inward sodium (Na+) current via voltage-gated channels affecting resting membrane potential. In addition, excessive glutamate release following BBB disrupting events may result in N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptor-mediated calcium (Ca2+)-calmodulin-dependent activation of PI3K/AKT1 and activation of MTOR. Finally, signaling through metabotropic glutamate receptor GRM5 may have an impact on the increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels.    Outcome effects: The activation of TGFBR/SMAD/NF-kB axis by ALB induces rapid transcriptional modifications, astrocytic transformation, and may decrease seizure threshold. Then, persistent sodium current induced by thrombin also facilitates seizures. Further, PI3K/AKT1 and MTOR activation causes a cascade of cellular events that contribute to epileptogenesis including axonal sprouting and increased expression of ion channels and neurotransmitter receptors. Lastly, the increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels via GMR5 signaling may also promote seizures.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Bianchi R, Wong RK, Merlin LR (2012) Glutamate Receptors in Epilepsy: Group I mGluR-Mediated Epileptogenesis. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition." EXACT []

