xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Proconvulsant events such as brain trauma or infection lead to the activation of microglia, astrocytes, and neurons. Activated glia cells release the proinflammatory cytokine IL1B, and neurons release HMGB1, initiating a cascade of inflammatory events in glia cells via the activation of IL1R1 (IL1B) and TLR4 (HMGB1), which activates the TRAF6/NF-kB pathway.    Outcome effects: NF-kB activation results in the increased secretion of proinflammatory factors such as TNF, IL1B, HMGB1, IL6, and CCL2 which leads to the exacerbation of neuroinflammation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Proconvulsant events such as brain trauma or infection lead to the activation of microglia, astrocytes, and neurons. Activated glia cells release the proinflammatory cytokine IL1B, and neurons release HMGB1, initiating a cascade of inflammatory events in glia cells via the activation of IL1R1 (IL1B) and TLR4 (HMGB1), which activates the TRAF6/NF-kB pathway.    Outcome effects: NF-kB activation results in the increased secretion of proinflammatory factors such as TNF, IL1B, HMGB1, IL6, and CCL2 which leads to the exacerbation of neuroinflammation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

