xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus. A ketogenic diet (KD) is a high-fat, adequate-protein, and low-carbohydrate diet used to treat refractory epilepsy in children.    Signaling description: The release of excessive glutamate during status epilepticus may result in N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptor-mediated Ca2+-calmodulin-dependent activation of PI3K/AKT1 and relieve TSC1/TSC2 inhibition of MTOR.    Outcome effects: This causes a cascade of cellular events that contribute to epileptogenesis, including axonal sprouting and increased expression of ion channels and neurotransmitter receptors. The signaling through metabotropic glutamate receptors GRM1 and GRM5 may also have an impact on the increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Bianchi R, Wong RK, Merlin LR (2012) Glutamate Receptors in Epilepsy: Group I mGluR-Mediated Epileptogenesis. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus. A ketogenic diet (KD) is a high-fat, adequate-protein, and low-carbohydrate diet used to treat refractory epilepsy in children.    Signaling description: The release of excessive glutamate during status epilepticus may result in N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptor-mediated Ca2+-calmodulin-dependent activation of PI3K/AKT1 and relieve TSC1/TSC2 inhibition of MTOR.    Outcome effects: This causes a cascade of cellular events that contribute to epileptogenesis, including axonal sprouting and increased expression of ion channels and neurotransmitter receptors. The signaling through metabotropic glutamate receptors GRM1 and GRM5 may also have an impact on the increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Bianchi R, Wong RK, Merlin LR (2012) Glutamate Receptors in Epilepsy: Group I mGluR-Mediated Epileptogenesis. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition." EXACT []

