xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: An excess of extracellular glutamate is found in human epileptogenic tissue and can induce recurrent seizures and neuronal cell death. This may occur through the activation of neuronal and glial glutamate receptors. Glutamate activates astrocyte metabotropic receptors GRM1/5 leading to intracellular calcium (Ca2+) elevation and Ca2+-dependent release of glutamate, D-serine, and ATP from astrocytes. Astrocyte swelling is typical for epilepsy and opens volume-regulated anion channels (VRACs) which releases glutamate into the extracellular space.    Outcome effects: The increased level of glutamate and D-serine in the synaptic cleft leads to postsynaptic motor neuron hyperexcitation. ATP release may lead to astrocyte activation via P2Y receptors (P2RY1/P2RY2) in an autocrine manner, and at the same time, inhibitory signaling to postsynaptic motor neurons via ATP metabolite adenosine.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Clasadonte J, Haydon PG (2012) Astrocytes and Epilepsy. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition. Coulter DA (2009). 2). Neurotransmitter function. Consequences of Diminished Function of the Glutamine Cycle on Inhibitory Function in the Epileptic Brain. Encyclopedia of Basic Epilepsy Research. pp 877-882.Avialable from: http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123739612003453 3)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: An excess of extracellular glutamate is found in human epileptogenic tissue and can induce recurrent seizures and neuronal cell death. This may occur through the activation of neuronal and glial glutamate receptors. Glutamate activates astrocyte metabotropic receptors GRM1/5 leading to intracellular calcium (Ca2+) elevation and Ca2+-dependent release of glutamate, D-serine, and ATP from astrocytes. Astrocyte swelling is typical for epilepsy and opens volume-regulated anion channels (VRACs) which releases glutamate into the extracellular space.    Outcome effects: The increased level of glutamate and D-serine in the synaptic cleft leads to postsynaptic motor neuron hyperexcitation. ATP release may lead to astrocyte activation via P2Y receptors (P2RY1/P2RY2) in an autocrine manner, and at the same time, inhibitory signaling to postsynaptic motor neurons via ATP metabolite adenosine.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Clasadonte J, Haydon PG (2012) Astrocytes and Epilepsy. Jasper's Basic Mechanisms of the Epilepsies. 4th edition. Coulter DA (2009). 2). Neurotransmitter function. Consequences of Diminished Function of the Glutamine Cycle on Inhibitory Function in the Epileptic Brain. Encyclopedia of Basic Epilepsy Research. pp 877-882.Avialable from: http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123739612003453 3)." EXACT []

