xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Seizures induce the massive influx of calcium (Ca2+) through N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptors and voltage-gated Ca2+ channels. Elevated intracellular Ca2+ levels may directly trigger mitochondrial dysfunction or activate Ca2+ phosphatases such as calcineurin, which releases BAD from mitochondria leading to BAX activation. Subsequently, CYCS is released which triggers caspase-9 (CASP9) activation and apoptosis. Alternatively, death receptor activation leads to the formation of a death-inducing signaling complex and caspase-8 (CASP8) processing. Both TNF and FASLG are present in the brain following seizures. Seizures also activate caspase-independent apoptosis pathways in the brain where calpains are thought to be critical. Calpain I (CAPN1) has been shown to be responsible for triggering the release of AIFM1 during excitotoxicity. In addition, BBC3 upregulation following seizures is mainly TP53-mediated. DNA damage may be the trigger for TP53 accumulation after seizures. Serotonin and dopamine signaling modulate seizure-induced CREB1 phosphorylation via the activation of PPP1R1B and MAPK1/3. Dopamine acts through two different types of G-protein coupled receptors, D1-like and D2-like receptors. The activation of D1-like (DRD1 and DRD5) receptors results in the reduction of seizure threshold and increased seizure severity. Conversely, the effect of D2-like (including DRD2, DRD3, and DRD4) receptors on seizure modulation is predominantly inhibitory. The canonical pathway of dopamine receptor activity in neurons involves the modulation of adenylate cyclase (ADCY) to regulate cAMP production and subsequent activation of protein kinase A (PKA). D1-like receptors are coupled to GNAS and stimulate ADCY which leads to increased levels of cAMP. Conversely, D2-like receptors are coupled to Gi/o proteins and inhibit ADCY and decrease cAMP production. Recent evidence suggests a role for an alternative pathway in DRD2-modulated inhibition of seizures. A role of signaling complex composed of DRD2, AKT1, PP2A, and ARRB2 has been demonstrated. The activation of this pathway results in the dephosphorylation of AKT1 (inactivation), followed by dephosphorylation (activation) of GSK3B. Decreased dopamine/DRD2/DRD3 binding has been shown in temporal lobe epilepsy and juvenile monoclinic epilepsy. In addition, serotonin acts through a family of G-protein coupled receptors to modulate neuronal excitability. Similar to dopamine, most serotonin receptors act on the ADCY/cAMP/PKA pathway. The influx of Ca2+ leads to CREB1 phosphorylation via Ras/MAPK and calmodulin-dependent signaling.    Outcome effects: CREB1 promotes the induction of \"immediate early genes\" JUN-FOS and switch on apoptotic cascades. In addition, CREB1 induces the synthesis of BDNF which may contribute to long-term excitability. NPY induced by CREB1 has been shown to have anticonvulsant properties. Several downstream effector proteins including NMDA, AMPA, GABA receptors, and Ca2+ and Na+ channels may be induced by \"immediate early genes\" which is proposed to be the mechanism of proconvulsant action.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epilepsy is a common neurological condition defined by recurrent, unprovoked seizures and affects 1% of the world's population. Epileptogenesis is common following brain trauma, ischemia, and infection. However, the mechanisms underlying injury-related epileptogenesis remain unknown. Seizures damage the brain and if they are prolonged (status epilepticus) or repeatedly evoked they can cause neuronal death, especially within vulnerable brain regions such as the hippocampus.    Signaling description: Seizures induce the massive influx of calcium (Ca2+) through N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) selective glutamate receptors and voltage-gated Ca2+ channels. Elevated intracellular Ca2+ levels may directly trigger mitochondrial dysfunction or activate Ca2+ phosphatases such as calcineurin, which releases BAD from mitochondria leading to BAX activation. Subsequently, CYCS is released which triggers caspase-9 (CASP9) activation and apoptosis. Alternatively, death receptor activation leads to the formation of a death-inducing signaling complex and caspase-8 (CASP8) processing. Both TNF and FASLG are present in the brain following seizures. Seizures also activate caspase-independent apoptosis pathways in the brain where calpains are thought to be critical. Calpain I (CAPN1) has been shown to be responsible for triggering the release of AIFM1 during excitotoxicity. In addition, BBC3 upregulation following seizures is mainly TP53-mediated. DNA damage may be the trigger for TP53 accumulation after seizures. Serotonin and dopamine signaling modulate seizure-induced CREB1 phosphorylation via the activation of PPP1R1B and MAPK1/3. Dopamine acts through two different types of G-protein coupled receptors, D1-like and D2-like receptors. The activation of D1-like (DRD1 and DRD5) receptors results in the reduction of seizure threshold and increased seizure severity. Conversely, the effect of D2-like (including DRD2, DRD3, and DRD4) receptors on seizure modulation is predominantly inhibitory. The canonical pathway of dopamine receptor activity in neurons involves the modulation of adenylate cyclase (ADCY) to regulate cAMP production and subsequent activation of protein kinase A (PKA). D1-like receptors are coupled to GNAS and stimulate ADCY which leads to increased levels of cAMP. Conversely, D2-like receptors are coupled to Gi/o proteins and inhibit ADCY and decrease cAMP production. Recent evidence suggests a role for an alternative pathway in DRD2-modulated inhibition of seizures. A role of signaling complex composed of DRD2, AKT1, PP2A, and ARRB2 has been demonstrated. The activation of this pathway results in the dephosphorylation of AKT1 (inactivation), followed by dephosphorylation (activation) of GSK3B. Decreased dopamine/DRD2/DRD3 binding has been shown in temporal lobe epilepsy and juvenile monoclinic epilepsy. In addition, serotonin acts through a family of G-protein coupled receptors to modulate neuronal excitability. Similar to dopamine, most serotonin receptors act on the ADCY/cAMP/PKA pathway. The influx of Ca2+ leads to CREB1 phosphorylation via Ras/MAPK and calmodulin-dependent signaling.    Outcome effects: CREB1 promotes the induction of \"immediate early genes\" JUN-FOS and switch on apoptotic cascades. In addition, CREB1 induces the synthesis of BDNF which may contribute to long-term excitability. NPY induced by CREB1 has been shown to have anticonvulsant properties. Several downstream effector proteins including NMDA, AMPA, GABA receptors, and Ca2+ and Na+ channels may be induced by \"immediate early genes\" which is proposed to be the mechanism of proconvulsant action.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

