xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways. Primary GBM is the most frequent type of glioblastoma and develops rapidly following a short clinical history and without evidence of a less malignant precursor.    Signaling description: The central signaling systems deregulated in GBM are receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK), TP53, and RB1 pathways. The amplification and/or activating mutations of the gene that encodes EGFR are the most frequent genetic defects that is associated with GBM. Also, other RTKs such as PDGFRA, FGFR, IGF1R, and MET are abnormally expressed in GBM. RTKs activate proliferation via PI3K/AKT1/MTOR and Ras/MAPK pathways. MAPK activation results in the activation of various transcription factors such as ELK1, MYC, ETS1, and STAT1/3 which induce cell transformation and inhibit apoptosis. TP53 pathway is disrupted through the deletion of CDKN2A or amplification of MDM2 and MDM4. TP53 mutations may occur as well. The disruption of the RB1 pathway occurs through deletions of CDK4 or CDKN2A. In addition, PIK3CA and PIK3R1, which are members of the PI3K family, and PTEN may be mutated. Furthermore, mutations and/or deletions in NF1 are present in 15-18% of GBM cases.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, all of these genetic events ultimately lead to the activation of proliferation and block of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways. Primary GBM is the most frequent type of glioblastoma and develops rapidly following a short clinical history and without evidence of a less malignant precursor.    Signaling description: The central signaling systems deregulated in GBM are receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK), TP53, and RB1 pathways. The amplification and/or activating mutations of the gene that encodes EGFR are the most frequent genetic defects that is associated with GBM. Also, other RTKs such as PDGFRA, FGFR, IGF1R, and MET are abnormally expressed in GBM. RTKs activate proliferation via PI3K/AKT1/MTOR and Ras/MAPK pathways. MAPK activation results in the activation of various transcription factors such as ELK1, MYC, ETS1, and STAT1/3 which induce cell transformation and inhibit apoptosis. TP53 pathway is disrupted through the deletion of CDKN2A or amplification of MDM2 and MDM4. TP53 mutations may occur as well. The disruption of the RB1 pathway occurs through deletions of CDK4 or CDKN2A. In addition, PIK3CA and PIK3R1, which are members of the PI3K family, and PTEN may be mutated. Furthermore, mutations and/or deletions in NF1 are present in 15-18% of GBM cases.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, all of these genetic events ultimately lead to the activation of proliferation and block of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

