xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways.    Signaling description: The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Research Network recently provided a new molecular classification of GBM. Genomic profiling resulted in a gene-expression-based molecular classification of GBM into 4 distinct subtypes including proneural, neural, classical, and mesenchymal. These subtypes were shown to have divergent clinical courses, prognoses, and treatment responses. The classical subtype is described by genetic alterations in EGFR, RB1, CDK4, and CCND2 genes with a distinct lack of TP53 mutations. There is also an overexpression of NES in addition to Notch (NOTCH3, JAG1, and LFNG) and Sonic hedgehog (SMO, GAS1, and GLI2) proteins.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways.    Signaling description: The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Research Network recently provided a new molecular classification of GBM. Genomic profiling resulted in a gene-expression-based molecular classification of GBM into 4 distinct subtypes including proneural, neural, classical, and mesenchymal. These subtypes were shown to have divergent clinical courses, prognoses, and treatment responses. The classical subtype is described by genetic alterations in EGFR, RB1, CDK4, and CCND2 genes with a distinct lack of TP53 mutations. There is also an overexpression of NES in addition to Notch (NOTCH3, JAG1, and LFNG) and Sonic hedgehog (SMO, GAS1, and GLI2) proteins.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

