xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways.    Signaling description: The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Research Network recently provided a new molecular classification of GBM. Genomic profiling resulted in a gene-expression-based molecular classification of GBM into 4 distinct subtypes including proneural, neural, classical, and mesenchymal. These subtypes were shown to have divergent clinical courses, prognoses, and treatment responses. The neural subtype does not have typical mutations but in a small percentage of cases mutations in TP53, NF1, PTEN, EGFR, IDH1, PIK3R1, PIK3CA, ERBB2, and RB1 may be found. This subtype is characterized by the expression of neuron markers such as NEFL, GABRA1, SYT1, and SLC12A5.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways.    Signaling description: The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Research Network recently provided a new molecular classification of GBM. Genomic profiling resulted in a gene-expression-based molecular classification of GBM into 4 distinct subtypes including proneural, neural, classical, and mesenchymal. These subtypes were shown to have divergent clinical courses, prognoses, and treatment responses. The neural subtype does not have typical mutations but in a small percentage of cases mutations in TP53, NF1, PTEN, EGFR, IDH1, PIK3R1, PIK3CA, ERBB2, and RB1 may be found. This subtype is characterized by the expression of neuron markers such as NEFL, GABRA1, SYT1, and SLC12A5.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

