xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways. Secondary GBM develops from astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, or ependymoma.    Signaling description: The most important signaling systems deregulated in GBM are receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK), TP53, and RB1 pathways. Isocitrate dehydrogenase 1/2 (IDH1/2) mutations are early and frequent genetic alterations in secondary GBMs, as well as in oligodendroglial tumors. Loss of IDH1 function induces HIF1A, which is implicated in tumor growth. IDH1 proteins also exhibit a gain-of-function phenotype by generating 2-hydroxyglutarate, a toxic metabolite associated with an increased risk of malignant brain tumors in patients. TP53 inactivation due to mutation is also an early event in secondary GBM development. Other mechanisms include PDGFR, MDM2, and CDK4 amplifications, CDKN2A and RB1 deletions, as well as PTEN and IDH2 mutations. In addition, mutations in neurofibromin 2 (NF2) are the major cause of ependymomas, which in the long term develop into secondary GBMs.    Outcome effects: Ultimately, these genetic events lead to the activation of proliferation and block of apoptosis resulting in carcinogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways. Secondary GBM develops from astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, or ependymoma.    Signaling description: The most important signaling systems deregulated in GBM are receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK), TP53, and RB1 pathways. Isocitrate dehydrogenase 1/2 (IDH1/2) mutations are early and frequent genetic alterations in secondary GBMs, as well as in oligodendroglial tumors. Loss of IDH1 function induces HIF1A, which is implicated in tumor growth. IDH1 proteins also exhibit a gain-of-function phenotype by generating 2-hydroxyglutarate, a toxic metabolite associated with an increased risk of malignant brain tumors in patients. TP53 inactivation due to mutation is also an early event in secondary GBM development. Other mechanisms include PDGFR, MDM2, and CDK4 amplifications, CDKN2A and RB1 deletions, as well as PTEN and IDH2 mutations. In addition, mutations in neurofibromin 2 (NF2) are the major cause of ependymomas, which in the long term develop into secondary GBMs.    Outcome effects: Ultimately, these genetic events lead to the activation of proliferation and block of apoptosis resulting in carcinogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

