xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways. The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Research Network recently provided a new molecular classification of GBM. Genomic profiling resulted in a gene-expression-based molecular classification of GBM into 4 distinct subtypes including proneural, neural, classical, and mesenchymal. These subtypes were shown to have divergent clinical courses, prognoses, and treatment responses. The proneural subtype is characterized by genetic alterations in PDGFRA, IDH1, PIK3CA, and PIK3R1 genes, and a high expression of oligodendrocyte development genes including PDGFRA, NKX2-2, and OLIG2.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most frequent and malignant subtype of glioma. It is subdivided into primary and secondary GBM with distinct disease subtypes that affect patients of different age and develop through different genetic pathways. The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Research Network recently provided a new molecular classification of GBM. Genomic profiling resulted in a gene-expression-based molecular classification of GBM into 4 distinct subtypes including proneural, neural, classical, and mesenchymal. These subtypes were shown to have divergent clinical courses, prognoses, and treatment responses. The proneural subtype is characterized by genetic alterations in PDGFRA, IDH1, PIK3CA, and PIK3R1 genes, and a high expression of oligodendrocyte development genes including PDGFRA, NKX2-2, and OLIG2.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

