xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Astrocytoma is a malignant brain tumor that originates from a specific type of neuroglial cells called astrocytes. This type of tumor does not typically spread outside the brain and spinal cord and usually does not affect other organs. Astrocytomas are the most common glioma and can occur in most parts of the brain and occasionally in the spinal cord. The World Health Organization (WHO) system assigns astrocytomas a grade from I to IV, with I being the least aggressive and IV being the most aggressive.    Signaling description: Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO I) is a slow-growing tumor where activating BRAF mutations or inactivating NF1 mutations may be found in a subset of patients. Low-grade (diffuse) astrocytoma (WHO II) is characterized by TP53 mutations and/or PDGFRA amplification and overexpression. IDH1 mutations may also be observed in low-grade astrocytomas. Anaplastic astrocytoma (WHO III) is characterized by additional gain of CDK4/6 amplification and overexpression, as well as RB1 mutations. Additional genetic events lead to the development of glioblastoma multiforme (GBM, WHO IV).    Outcome effects: The final tumorigenic events caused by these genetic alterations are the activation of proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Astrocytoma is a malignant brain tumor that originates from a specific type of neuroglial cells called astrocytes. This type of tumor does not typically spread outside the brain and spinal cord and usually does not affect other organs. Astrocytomas are the most common glioma and can occur in most parts of the brain and occasionally in the spinal cord. The World Health Organization (WHO) system assigns astrocytomas a grade from I to IV, with I being the least aggressive and IV being the most aggressive.    Signaling description: Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO I) is a slow-growing tumor where activating BRAF mutations or inactivating NF1 mutations may be found in a subset of patients. Low-grade (diffuse) astrocytoma (WHO II) is characterized by TP53 mutations and/or PDGFRA amplification and overexpression. IDH1 mutations may also be observed in low-grade astrocytomas. Anaplastic astrocytoma (WHO III) is characterized by additional gain of CDK4/6 amplification and overexpression, as well as RB1 mutations. Additional genetic events lead to the development of glioblastoma multiforme (GBM, WHO IV).    Outcome effects: The final tumorigenic events caused by these genetic alterations are the activation of proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

