xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glioma is a broad category of brain and spinal cord tumors that arise from glial cells, the most abundant cell type in the central nervous system. Glioma is highly invasive by nature but does not typically spread outside the brain and spinal cord and generally does not affect other organs.    Signaling description: The brain parenchyma has a unique composition, mainly hyaluronan, and is devoid of rigid protein barriers composed of collagen, fibronectin, and laminin. Invasion signaling in glioma is induced by several types of receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs), integrins, CD44, and G-protein-coupled receptors. RTKs such as EGFR, PDGFR, MET, TIE1, DDR1, EPH, and AXL are expressed at very high levels in glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) and play a major role in glioma invasion. In addition, hyaluronic acid receptor (CD44) and hyaluronan-mediated motility receptor (HMMR) play a major role in glioma cell-matrix interactions since neoplastic cells use these receptors to adhere to and migrate along the components of the brain extracellular matrix (ECM). Glioma cells activate plasminogen (PLG) and plasminogen activator (PLAU). PLAU converts PLG to the active plasmin, whereas, plasmin as a proteolytic enzyme enhances the ability of PLAUR to localize the proteolytic activity of PLAU on the cell surface which is extremely important for the invasive ability of tumor cells. In addition, integrins, particularly vitronectin receptor and VLA-3 receptor, are overexpressed in gliomas and appear to be significant to processes of invasion and angiogenesis. Furthermore, gliomas overexpress ephrin receptors including EPHB2, EPHA2, and EPHA4 and their ligands, the ephrins, which also regulate migration and invasion. Lastly, plasmin activates protease activated receptor-1 (F2R) to enhance cell invasion and proliferation.    Outcome effects: Plasmin, as a broad-specificity protease, degrades several ECM components such as fibronectin, laminin, and collagens. In addition, PLAU triggers a proteolytic cascade that involves the activation of MMP1, 2, 3, and 9, which are responsible for ECM degradation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glioma is a broad category of brain and spinal cord tumors that arise from glial cells, the most abundant cell type in the central nervous system. Glioma is highly invasive by nature but does not typically spread outside the brain and spinal cord and generally does not affect other organs.    Signaling description: The brain parenchyma has a unique composition, mainly hyaluronan, and is devoid of rigid protein barriers composed of collagen, fibronectin, and laminin. Invasion signaling in glioma is induced by several types of receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs), integrins, CD44, and G-protein-coupled receptors. RTKs such as EGFR, PDGFR, MET, TIE1, DDR1, EPH, and AXL are expressed at very high levels in glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) and play a major role in glioma invasion. In addition, hyaluronic acid receptor (CD44) and hyaluronan-mediated motility receptor (HMMR) play a major role in glioma cell-matrix interactions since neoplastic cells use these receptors to adhere to and migrate along the components of the brain extracellular matrix (ECM). Glioma cells activate plasminogen (PLG) and plasminogen activator (PLAU). PLAU converts PLG to the active plasmin, whereas, plasmin as a proteolytic enzyme enhances the ability of PLAUR to localize the proteolytic activity of PLAU on the cell surface which is extremely important for the invasive ability of tumor cells. In addition, integrins, particularly vitronectin receptor and VLA-3 receptor, are overexpressed in gliomas and appear to be significant to processes of invasion and angiogenesis. Furthermore, gliomas overexpress ephrin receptors including EPHB2, EPHA2, and EPHA4 and their ligands, the ephrins, which also regulate migration and invasion. Lastly, plasmin activates protease activated receptor-1 (F2R) to enhance cell invasion and proliferation.    Outcome effects: Plasmin, as a broad-specificity protease, degrades several ECM components such as fibronectin, laminin, and collagens. In addition, PLAU triggers a proteolytic cascade that involves the activation of MMP1, 2, 3, and 9, which are responsible for ECM degradation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

