xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Muscular dystrophy refers to a group of progressive muscular disorders with muscle weakening and breakdown being the major symptom.    Signaling description: Interleukin 6 (IL6) is a pleiotropic cytokine that plays a key role in a broad range of processes including inflammation, immune response, metabolism, muscle function, and oncogenesis. In addition, IL6 has emerged as a factor involved in insulin resistance. In obesity, adipose tissue infiltrated by immune cells starts producing excessive amount of IL6. In the liver, IL6 inhibits insulin signaling leading to insulin resistance. This effect is mediated through the JAK/STAT pathway. IL6-induced activation of STAT3 leads to the up-regulation of SOCS3.    Outcome effects: SOCS3 is a negative regulator of insulin signaling. Some other downstream targets of IL6, such as MAPK8, NOS2, and PTPN1, can also affect activity of the immediate targets of the insulin receptor (insulin receptor substrates), blocking insulin's action. However, in muscles, IL6 can be beneficial for insulin signaling. Increased IL6 production caused by muscle contraction stimulates AMPK that can increase glucose uptake and repress MTOR, thereby abolishing its negative regulation of insulin receptor substrates.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Muscular dystrophy refers to a group of progressive muscular disorders with muscle weakening and breakdown being the major symptom.    Signaling description: Interleukin 6 (IL6) is a pleiotropic cytokine that plays a key role in a broad range of processes including inflammation, immune response, metabolism, muscle function, and oncogenesis. In addition, IL6 has emerged as a factor involved in insulin resistance. In obesity, adipose tissue infiltrated by immune cells starts producing excessive amount of IL6. In the liver, IL6 inhibits insulin signaling leading to insulin resistance. This effect is mediated through the JAK/STAT pathway. IL6-induced activation of STAT3 leads to the up-regulation of SOCS3.    Outcome effects: SOCS3 is a negative regulator of insulin signaling. Some other downstream targets of IL6, such as MAPK8, NOS2, and PTPN1, can also affect activity of the immediate targets of the insulin receptor (insulin receptor substrates), blocking insulin's action. However, in muscles, IL6 can be beneficial for insulin signaling. Increased IL6 production caused by muscle contraction stimulates AMPK that can increase glucose uptake and repress MTOR, thereby abolishing its negative regulation of insulin receptor substrates.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

