synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Muscular dystrophy refers to a group of progressive muscular disorders with muscle weakening and breakdown being the major symptom. Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is the most common type of muscular dystrophy caused by various mutations in the gene coding for dystrophin. The disease is generally inherited in the X-linked recessive manner and manifests before the age of six in boys. The disease eventually leads to inability to walk, weakness of breathing due to respiratory muscle weakness and severely decreased life expectancy.    Signaling description: One of the functions of dystrophin is the proper scaffolding of various proteins including members of signaling cascades. The lack of DGC results in mislocalization and changes in the activity of proteins normally associated with DGC. NOS1 activity becomes decreased in dystrophin-deficient muscles. Caveolin 3 (CAV3) levels are also increased in dystrophin-deficient muscles. It has been documented that such an increase can alone cause Duchenne-like symptoms. DGKZ, calmodulin, and GRB2 also interact with DGC and may represent a link between DGC impairment and muscle cell survival.    Outcome effects: Nitric oxide (NO) produced by NOS1 is an important anti-inflammatory and cytoprotective agent. The restoration of NO synthesis or treatment with NO donors improves symptoms of dystrophin deficiency in models of DMD.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Muscular dystrophy refers to a group of progressive muscular disorders with muscle weakening and breakdown being the major symptom. Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is the most common type of muscular dystrophy caused by various mutations in the gene coding for dystrophin. The disease is generally inherited in the X-linked recessive manner and manifests before the age of six in boys. The disease eventually leads to inability to walk, weakness of breathing due to respiratory muscle weakness and severely decreased life expectancy.    Signaling description: One of the functions of dystrophin is the proper scaffolding of various proteins including members of signaling cascades. The lack of DGC results in mislocalization and changes in the activity of proteins normally associated with DGC. NOS1 activity becomes decreased in dystrophin-deficient muscles. Caveolin 3 (CAV3) levels are also increased in dystrophin-deficient muscles. It has been documented that such an increase can alone cause Duchenne-like symptoms. DGKZ, calmodulin, and GRB2 also interact with DGC and may represent a link between DGC impairment and muscle cell survival.    Outcome effects: Nitric oxide (NO) produced by NOS1 is an important anti-inflammatory and cytoprotective agent. The restoration of NO synthesis or treatment with NO donors improves symptoms of dystrophin deficiency in models of DMD.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

