xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB), the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood, is derived from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.    Signaling description: Normally, autonomic neuron development is controlled by a network of transcription factors which includes PHOX2A, PHOX2B, ASCL1, HAND2, and GATA3. These transcription factors are essential for the initial differentiation of autonomic neurons and also directly activate genes of the noradrenalin synthesis route such as dopamine beta hydroxylase (DBH) and tyrosine hydroxylase (TH). The expression of PHOX2B, PHOX2A, ASCL1, and HAND2 is controlled by bone morphogenetic proteins (BMP2/4/7) and Notch signaling. Mutations in PHOX2B gene have been observed in familial and sporadic forms of neuroblastoma (NB). NB patients with PHOX2B mutations may also have familial disorders of the neural crest such as Hirschsprung disease and congenital central hypoventilation syndrome.    Outcome effects: Mutated PHOX2B fails to inactivate SOX10, which normally blocks differentiation. Mutations disrupt the interaction of PHOX2B with the neuronal calcium sensor, HPCAL1 and thereby also impede cellular differentiation in NB. In addition, PHOX2B downregulates MSX1, which in turn upregulates Notch pathway genes including DLK1, NOTCH3, and HEY1 and downregulates NEUROD1. Finally, PHOX2B mutations lead to block of differentiation and activation of proliferation. It may also lead to the impairment of sympathoadrenal ganglia formation as observed in Hirschsprung disease. In NB, neuroblasts often retain the ability to secrete noradrenaline (norepinephrine).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB), the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood, is derived from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.    Signaling description: Normally, autonomic neuron development is controlled by a network of transcription factors which includes PHOX2A, PHOX2B, ASCL1, HAND2, and GATA3. These transcription factors are essential for the initial differentiation of autonomic neurons and also directly activate genes of the noradrenalin synthesis route such as dopamine beta hydroxylase (DBH) and tyrosine hydroxylase (TH). The expression of PHOX2B, PHOX2A, ASCL1, and HAND2 is controlled by bone morphogenetic proteins (BMP2/4/7) and Notch signaling. Mutations in PHOX2B gene have been observed in familial and sporadic forms of neuroblastoma (NB). NB patients with PHOX2B mutations may also have familial disorders of the neural crest such as Hirschsprung disease and congenital central hypoventilation syndrome.    Outcome effects: Mutated PHOX2B fails to inactivate SOX10, which normally blocks differentiation. Mutations disrupt the interaction of PHOX2B with the neuronal calcium sensor, HPCAL1 and thereby also impede cellular differentiation in NB. In addition, PHOX2B downregulates MSX1, which in turn upregulates Notch pathway genes including DLK1, NOTCH3, and HEY1 and downregulates NEUROD1. Finally, PHOX2B mutations lead to block of differentiation and activation of proliferation. It may also lead to the impairment of sympathoadrenal ganglia formation as observed in Hirschsprung disease. In NB, neuroblasts often retain the ability to secrete noradrenaline (norepinephrine).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

