xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB), the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood, originates from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.    Signaling description: Neurotrophin receptor signaling was found to be important in malignant transformation of sympathetic neuroblasts. The family of neurotrophin receptors includes neurotrophic tyrosine kinase receptor type 1, 2, and 3 (NTRK1/2/3). The ligands of NTRK include nerve growth factor (NGF), brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), and neurotrophin 3 and 4 (NTF3/4). These ligand-receptor interactions induce PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/MAPK signaling pathwyas. Another transmembrane receptor known as nerve growth factor receptor (NGFR) binds all members of the neurotrophin family with low affinity. This receptor is a member of the tumor necrosis factor receptor (TNFR) or death receptor family. NGFR could lead to either cell death or differentiation in response to the ligand depending on whether NTRK receptors were co-expressed.    Outcome effects: High levels of NTRK1 in association with very low/no MYCN expression are detected in favorable neuroblastoma (NB) tumors which often spontaneously regress. These favorable NB tumor cells usually express a small amount of NGF as do some of the surrounding cells. Cells that express the most NGFs are thought to undergo differentiation, while those that express less NGF undergo apoptosis. NTRK1 expression is dramatically decreased in MYCN-amplified NB tumors. In contrast, NTRK2 is preferentially expressed in clinically unfavorable NB tumors and the expression of NTRK2 strongly correlates with MYCN amplification. BDNF is also highly expressed in these tumors. NTRK3 is commonly expressed in favorable NB tumors. These tumors also express very limited amounts of NTF3 and co-express NTRK1. In addition to NGFs, other growth factors are also significant in the development of sympathoadrenal lineage cells and transformed neuroblasts. This includes EGF which is expressed in neural crest cells and is thought to contribute to the formation of neurons, in addition to VEGFA which is expressed by surrounding cells and IGF1/2 which is expressed in the neural crest, dorsal root, sympathetic ganglia, and adrenal medulla. Overall, these growth factors promote cell survival and proliferation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB), the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood, originates from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.    Signaling description: Neurotrophin receptor signaling was found to be important in malignant transformation of sympathetic neuroblasts. The family of neurotrophin receptors includes neurotrophic tyrosine kinase receptor type 1, 2, and 3 (NTRK1/2/3). The ligands of NTRK include nerve growth factor (NGF), brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), and neurotrophin 3 and 4 (NTF3/4). These ligand-receptor interactions induce PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/MAPK signaling pathwyas. Another transmembrane receptor known as nerve growth factor receptor (NGFR) binds all members of the neurotrophin family with low affinity. This receptor is a member of the tumor necrosis factor receptor (TNFR) or death receptor family. NGFR could lead to either cell death or differentiation in response to the ligand depending on whether NTRK receptors were co-expressed.    Outcome effects: High levels of NTRK1 in association with very low/no MYCN expression are detected in favorable neuroblastoma (NB) tumors which often spontaneously regress. These favorable NB tumor cells usually express a small amount of NGF as do some of the surrounding cells. Cells that express the most NGFs are thought to undergo differentiation, while those that express less NGF undergo apoptosis. NTRK1 expression is dramatically decreased in MYCN-amplified NB tumors. In contrast, NTRK2 is preferentially expressed in clinically unfavorable NB tumors and the expression of NTRK2 strongly correlates with MYCN amplification. BDNF is also highly expressed in these tumors. NTRK3 is commonly expressed in favorable NB tumors. These tumors also express very limited amounts of NTF3 and co-express NTRK1. In addition to NGFs, other growth factors are also significant in the development of sympathoadrenal lineage cells and transformed neuroblasts. This includes EGF which is expressed in neural crest cells and is thought to contribute to the formation of neurons, in addition to VEGFA which is expressed by surrounding cells and IGF1/2 which is expressed in the neural crest, dorsal root, sympathetic ganglia, and adrenal medulla. Overall, these growth factors promote cell survival and proliferation." EXACT []

