xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB) is the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood and it originates from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.    Signaling description: Sonic hedgehog signaling is one of the key pathways in neuroblastoma development. Sonic hedgehog protein ligand (SHH) interacts with PTCH1 and SMO receptors. PTCH1 inhibits the activation of SMO, when SHH signaling is switched off. Canonical Sonic hedgehog signaling acts through the GLI1/2/3 transcription factors, and GLI1 is the agent which induces the expression of many other target genes. GLI2 acts as an activator or repressor of this pathway with its action dependent on the presence of GLI1. GLI3 always acts as repressor and in the absence of SHH signaling, GLI3 enters the nucleus with the help of SUFU and shows reversal as compared to GLI1 on SHH target genes.    Outcome effects: The downstream target genes of SHH-GLI include CCND1/2, CCNB1, PAX6, NKX2-2, MYCN, and others. The activation of these genes leads to the enhancement of proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB) is the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood and it originates from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain.    Signaling description: Sonic hedgehog signaling is one of the key pathways in neuroblastoma development. Sonic hedgehog protein ligand (SHH) interacts with PTCH1 and SMO receptors. PTCH1 inhibits the activation of SMO, when SHH signaling is switched off. Canonical Sonic hedgehog signaling acts through the GLI1/2/3 transcription factors, and GLI1 is the agent which induces the expression of many other target genes. GLI2 acts as an activator or repressor of this pathway with its action dependent on the presence of GLI1. GLI3 always acts as repressor and in the absence of SHH signaling, GLI3 enters the nucleus with the help of SUFU and shows reversal as compared to GLI1 on SHH target genes.    Outcome effects: The downstream target genes of SHH-GLI include CCND1/2, CCNB1, PAX6, NKX2-2, MYCN, and others. The activation of these genes leads to the enhancement of proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

