xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB), the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood, originates from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain. MYCN amplification is found in about 22% of NB cases.    Signaling description: MYCN forms a heterodimer with MAX and the protein complex functions as a transcriptional activator. MYCN expression targets are ODC1, MCM7, PTMA, TERT, PAX3, ID2, MDM2, and others. MYCN activation also leads to the overexpression of ABCB1 and ABCC1 and is responsible for drug resistance. The amplification of MDM2, DDX1, and MYCL1 are found in a small subset of NB cases. The loss of the short arm of chromosome 1 occurs in about 25-35% of NB tumors. Tumor suppressors including CHD5, MIR34A, and KIF1B are localized in chromosome 1 and they all affect cell growth. In addition, TP53 or CDKN2A may be mutated in rare cases of NB. Although TP53 is a transcriptional target of MYCN, MYCN also inhibits its function through increased levels of TP53 negative regulators such as MDM2 and TWIST1.    Outcome effects: The genetic rearrangements result in the inhibition of apoptosis and activation of proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neuroblastoma (NB), the most common extracranial solid tumor in childhood, originates from precursor cells of the peripheral (sympathetic) nervous system (neural crest neuroblasts). Tumors are most frequently found in the adrenal medulla and paraspinal ganglia, but can arise anywhere along the sympathetic chain. MYCN amplification is found in about 22% of NB cases.    Signaling description: MYCN forms a heterodimer with MAX and the protein complex functions as a transcriptional activator. MYCN expression targets are ODC1, MCM7, PTMA, TERT, PAX3, ID2, MDM2, and others. MYCN activation also leads to the overexpression of ABCB1 and ABCC1 and is responsible for drug resistance. The amplification of MDM2, DDX1, and MYCL1 are found in a small subset of NB cases. The loss of the short arm of chromosome 1 occurs in about 25-35% of NB tumors. Tumor suppressors including CHD5, MIR34A, and KIF1B are localized in chromosome 1 and they all affect cell growth. In addition, TP53 or CDKN2A may be mutated in rare cases of NB. Although TP53 is a transcriptional target of MYCN, MYCN also inhibits its function through increased levels of TP53 negative regulators such as MDM2 and TWIST1.    Outcome effects: The genetic rearrangements result in the inhibition of apoptosis and activation of proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

