xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Age-related macular degeneration (AMD) is the most common cause of blindness in developed countries and is characterized by extensive loss of the retinal pigment epithelium cells (RPE) and overlying photoreceptors. Oxidative stress is regarded to be one of the major factors in the development of AMD. Cigarette smoking is a well-known rick factor for AMD and generates a lot of chemical components, some of which are strong oxidants.     Signaling description: A high flux of retinoids through the retinoid cycle, as occurs during intense light exposure, can cause elevated levels of toxic retinoid intermediates, especially all-trans-retinal, that can trigger retinal pigment epithelium cells (RPE) degeneration. All-trans-retinal was found to induce NADPH oxidase-mediated ROS generation. It was shown that all-trans-retinal may acts via G-protein coupled HTR2A and CHRM3 to induce NADPH oxidase. While ROS are short-lived, oxidatively damaged molecules termed oxidation specific epitopes (OSEs), can be long-lived and a source of chronic stress that activates the immune system. Oxidative damage, mediated by reactive oxygen species, generates deleterious reactive aldehydic byproducts including carboxyethylpyrrole (CEP), malondialdehyde (MDA), 4-hydroxynonenal (4-HNE) and advanced glycation end (AGE) products such as carboxymethyllysine and pentosidine.    Outcome effects: Proteins modified with aldehydic adducts (MDA, 4-HNE) or CEP adducts become more immunogenic. Therefore, oxidation of lipids and proteins could induce the generation of new autoantigens to which the immune system has not been \"tolerized\". CEP is a byproduct generated from the oxidation of docosahexaenoic (DHA) acid in the retina. DHA is typically a precursor for neuroprotective factors neuroprotectin D1 and resolvin D1 that normally inhibit apoptosis and inflammation, but oxidative damage in the retina is capable of diverting DHA for use in these protective functions towards generation of pathogenic intermediates. Normally neuroprotectin D1 represses amyloid-beta-42-triggered activation of pro-inflammatory genes and upregulated the antiapoptotic genes encoding BCL2, BCL2L1, and BCL2A1. When the level of neuroprotectin D1 is decreased, it does not happen.Lipofuscin granules are byproducts of photoreceptor outer segment turnover and are seen within both healthy and pathological RPE. Lipofuscin in RPE accumulates throughout life, and is readily detectable after age 40. Lipofuscin pigments are generated from random non-enzymatic reactions of retinaldehyde in photoreceptors throughout life and enter RPE via phagocytosis, from which point pigment-containing phagosomes fuse with lysosomes. However, the random nature of lipofuscin pigment generation results in compounds that evade lysosomal digestion and thus accumulate within the RPE. Accumulation of lipofuscin results in a significant increase in retinal phototoxicity with age. The RPE has developed a robust antioxidant system driven by the transcription factor Nrf2 (NFE2L2). NFE2L2 controls target antioxidant genes HMOX1, NQO1, GCLM. Aging can reduce NFE2L2 activity. Impaired NFE2L2 signaling can lead to oxidative damage and RPE apoptosis. HIF1A was shown to be induced by ROS and hypoxia in AMD and controls expression of VEGFA making impact to choroidal neovascularization.Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Age-related macular degeneration (AMD) is the most common cause of blindness in developed countries and is characterized by extensive loss of the retinal pigment epithelium cells (RPE) and overlying photoreceptors. Oxidative stress is regarded to be one of the major factors in the development of AMD. Cigarette smoking is a well-known rick factor for AMD and generates a lot of chemical components, some of which are strong oxidants.     Signaling description: A high flux of retinoids through the retinoid cycle, as occurs during intense light exposure, can cause elevated levels of toxic retinoid intermediates, especially all-trans-retinal, that can trigger retinal pigment epithelium cells (RPE) degeneration. All-trans-retinal was found to induce NADPH oxidase-mediated ROS generation. It was shown that all-trans-retinal may acts via G-protein coupled HTR2A and CHRM3 to induce NADPH oxidase. While ROS are short-lived, oxidatively damaged molecules termed oxidation specific epitopes (OSEs), can be long-lived and a source of chronic stress that activates the immune system. Oxidative damage, mediated by reactive oxygen species, generates deleterious reactive aldehydic byproducts including carboxyethylpyrrole (CEP), malondialdehyde (MDA), 4-hydroxynonenal (4-HNE) and advanced glycation end (AGE) products such as carboxymethyllysine and pentosidine.    Outcome effects: Proteins modified with aldehydic adducts (MDA, 4-HNE) or CEP adducts become more immunogenic. Therefore, oxidation of lipids and proteins could induce the generation of new autoantigens to which the immune system has not been \"tolerized\". CEP is a byproduct generated from the oxidation of docosahexaenoic (DHA) acid in the retina. DHA is typically a precursor for neuroprotective factors neuroprotectin D1 and resolvin D1 that normally inhibit apoptosis and inflammation, but oxidative damage in the retina is capable of diverting DHA for use in these protective functions towards generation of pathogenic intermediates. Normally neuroprotectin D1 represses amyloid-beta-42-triggered activation of pro-inflammatory genes and upregulated the antiapoptotic genes encoding BCL2, BCL2L1, and BCL2A1. When the level of neuroprotectin D1 is decreased, it does not happen.Lipofuscin granules are byproducts of photoreceptor outer segment turnover and are seen within both healthy and pathological RPE. Lipofuscin in RPE accumulates throughout life, and is readily detectable after age 40. Lipofuscin pigments are generated from random non-enzymatic reactions of retinaldehyde in photoreceptors throughout life and enter RPE via phagocytosis, from which point pigment-containing phagosomes fuse with lysosomes. However, the random nature of lipofuscin pigment generation results in compounds that evade lysosomal digestion and thus accumulate within the RPE. Accumulation of lipofuscin results in a significant increase in retinal phototoxicity with age. The RPE has developed a robust antioxidant system driven by the transcription factor Nrf2 (NFE2L2). NFE2L2 controls target antioxidant genes HMOX1, NQO1, GCLM. Aging can reduce NFE2L2 activity. Impaired NFE2L2 signaling can lead to oxidative damage and RPE apoptosis. HIF1A was shown to be induced by ROS and hypoxia in AMD and controls expression of VEGFA making impact to choroidal neovascularization.Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

