xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cataract is clouding of the eye's lens that affects vision and is the leading cause of blindness in older individuals. The pathology generally develops due to the gradual accumulation of molecular modifications in crystallins, the major proteins in the lens, which changes crystallin structure and function and leads to clouding of the lens. Calcium (Ca2+) - mediated toxicity in the lens cells is one of the major pathological mechanisms leading to different forms of cataract including congenital, age-related, and diabetes-induced cataract.    Signaling description: The disease develops as a result of the disruption of Ca2+ efflux due to the impairment of Ca2+ ATPases by reactive oxygen species (ROS). Normally, a high Ca2+ concentration is observed in the human aqueous humor. Therefore, the ions constantly enter the cell in an inwardly directed gradient across the plasma membrane. There are several mechanisms which help maintain a low Ca2+ concentration in lens cells. Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1) and Ca2+ ATPases including ATP2B1, ATP2B2, and ATP2B4 in healthy lenses are activated by Ca2+-dependent calmodulin and promote Ca2+ efflux from the cell. In contrast, ATP2A2 and ATP2A3 sequester Ca2+ in the endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ store. Also, lipids bind ions on the outer membrane and decrease the ion concentration in the cell vicinity. ROS are accumulated in lens cells due to a number of factors in diabetic, age-related, and congenital forms of cataract and attack the membrane lipids causing their peroxidation.    Outcome effects: Lipids peroxidation results in the dysfunction of Ca2+ ATPases and Ca2+ release from lipids. CASR and voltage-gated Ca2+ channels promote free Ca2+ entrance into the cell. Increased intracellular Ca2+ binds crystallins leading to their aggregation; and in addition, activates calpains, preferentially CAPN2, which cleave VIM and CRYAA. This leads to cytoskeleton disorganization and block of chaperone activity in the cell causing light scattering and lens opacities.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level of activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level of activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cataract is clouding of the eye's lens that affects vision and is the leading cause of blindness in older individuals. The pathology generally develops due to the gradual accumulation of molecular modifications in crystallins, the major proteins in the lens, which changes crystallin structure and function and leads to clouding of the lens. Calcium (Ca2+) - mediated toxicity in the lens cells is one of the major pathological mechanisms leading to different forms of cataract including congenital, age-related, and diabetes-induced cataract.    Signaling description: The disease develops as a result of the disruption of Ca2+ efflux due to the impairment of Ca2+ ATPases by reactive oxygen species (ROS). Normally, a high Ca2+ concentration is observed in the human aqueous humor. Therefore, the ions constantly enter the cell in an inwardly directed gradient across the plasma membrane. There are several mechanisms which help maintain a low Ca2+ concentration in lens cells. Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1) and Ca2+ ATPases including ATP2B1, ATP2B2, and ATP2B4 in healthy lenses are activated by Ca2+-dependent calmodulin and promote Ca2+ efflux from the cell. In contrast, ATP2A2 and ATP2A3 sequester Ca2+ in the endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ store. Also, lipids bind ions on the outer membrane and decrease the ion concentration in the cell vicinity. ROS are accumulated in lens cells due to a number of factors in diabetic, age-related, and congenital forms of cataract and attack the membrane lipids causing their peroxidation.    Outcome effects: Lipids peroxidation results in the dysfunction of Ca2+ ATPases and Ca2+ release from lipids. CASR and voltage-gated Ca2+ channels promote free Ca2+ entrance into the cell. Increased intracellular Ca2+ binds crystallins leading to their aggregation; and in addition, activates calpains, preferentially CAPN2, which cleave VIM and CRYAA. This leads to cytoskeleton disorganization and block of chaperone activity in the cell causing light scattering and lens opacities.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level of activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level of activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

