xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cataract is clouding of the eye's lens that affects vision. Diabetes-induced cataract is a common complication of the diabetes.    Signaling description: Lens glucose uptake is an insulin-independent process. Glucose enters the lens epithelium through the SLC2A1 inwardly-directed gradient. In diabetes, hexokinase, the primary enzyme in the cell glucose metabolism, becomes saturated and excess glucose enters the sorbitol pathway which includes two reactions. In the first step, the AKR1B1 catalysis occurs via the NADPH-dependent reduction of glucose to its sugar alcohol, sorbitol. This reaction has a central role in diabetic cataract formation. In addition, sorbitol dehydrogenase (SORD) catalyzes NAD -dependent oxidation of sorbitol to fructose. The increased sorbitol pathway activity in the lens leads to intracellular sorbitol accumulation. Sorbitol is hydrophilic and does not diffuse through the cell membrane; therefore it accumulates intracellularly causing osmotic stress to the cell. The fructose produced by the polyol pathway can be phosphorylated to fructose-3-P, which is broken down to 3-deoxyglucosone. Both compounds are powerful glycosylating agents that are involved in the formation of advanced glycation end products. When they accumulate, they can modify structural proteins such as crystallins in the cataractous lenses of human subjects with diabetes mellitus and provoke lens opacities. The usage of NADPH by AKR1B1 may result in less cofactor available for glutathione reductase (GSR), which is critical for the maintenance of the intracellular pool of reduced glutathione.    Outcome effects: Decreased antioxidant defenses and the generation of oxidant species initiate multiple mechanisms of cellular damage including structural protein modification, dysfunction of membrane proteins, and Ca2+ toxicity. Glucose-induced elevation of diacylglycerol concentration leads to PRKCG activation which phosphorylates GJA3, disrupting lens gap junction transfer activity. It causes the impairment of water circulation that leads to its accumulation and thereby provokes lens opacities.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level of activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level of activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cataract is clouding of the eye's lens that affects vision. Diabetes-induced cataract is a common complication of the diabetes.    Signaling description: Lens glucose uptake is an insulin-independent process. Glucose enters the lens epithelium through the SLC2A1 inwardly-directed gradient. In diabetes, hexokinase, the primary enzyme in the cell glucose metabolism, becomes saturated and excess glucose enters the sorbitol pathway which includes two reactions. In the first step, the AKR1B1 catalysis occurs via the NADPH-dependent reduction of glucose to its sugar alcohol, sorbitol. This reaction has a central role in diabetic cataract formation. In addition, sorbitol dehydrogenase (SORD) catalyzes NAD -dependent oxidation of sorbitol to fructose. The increased sorbitol pathway activity in the lens leads to intracellular sorbitol accumulation. Sorbitol is hydrophilic and does not diffuse through the cell membrane; therefore it accumulates intracellularly causing osmotic stress to the cell. The fructose produced by the polyol pathway can be phosphorylated to fructose-3-P, which is broken down to 3-deoxyglucosone. Both compounds are powerful glycosylating agents that are involved in the formation of advanced glycation end products. When they accumulate, they can modify structural proteins such as crystallins in the cataractous lenses of human subjects with diabetes mellitus and provoke lens opacities. The usage of NADPH by AKR1B1 may result in less cofactor available for glutathione reductase (GSR), which is critical for the maintenance of the intracellular pool of reduced glutathione.    Outcome effects: Decreased antioxidant defenses and the generation of oxidant species initiate multiple mechanisms of cellular damage including structural protein modification, dysfunction of membrane proteins, and Ca2+ toxicity. Glucose-induced elevation of diacylglycerol concentration leads to PRKCG activation which phosphorylates GJA3, disrupting lens gap junction transfer activity. It causes the impairment of water circulation that leads to its accumulation and thereby provokes lens opacities.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level of activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level of activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

