xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cataract is clouding of the eye's lens that affects vision. Cataract caused by steroid administration is a common side effect of the therapy used to treat various pathologies. The characteristic features of steroid-induced cataract (SIC) are aberrant lens epithelial cell migration, proliferation, and a central posterior location.    Signaling description: Normally, lens epithelial cells continuously proliferate and differentiate into fiber cells via the synthesis of specific proteins such as crystallins, MIP, BFSP1, and BFSP2 in addition to the elimination of organelles and cell migration to the lens nucleus. The process of cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration is upregulated by FGF2 in a dose-dependent manner. A low cytokine concentration initiates epithelial cell proliferation, while moderate cytokine levels lead to migration, and higher concentrations cause differentiation. FGF2, (through MAP2K1/MAP2K2/MAPK1/MAPK3) promotes cell proliferation via the activation of RPS6KB1 and CREBBP transcription factors. At higher concentrations of FGF2, MAF is involved through the same pathway and initiates the transcription of lens specific proteins including crystallins, MIP, BFSP1, and BFSP2.    Outcome effects: Steroids can indicrectly influence cataract development by moderately decreasing FGF2 expression, which leads to block of cell differentiation, but the level of FGF2 is still sufficient to promote cell proliferation. In the direct mechanism of steroid-induced cataractogenesis, steroids (glucocorticoids) bind to their intracellular receptor, NR3C1 and initiate the expression of TSC22D3, which is a repressor of cell differentiation. NR3C1 inhibits CDH1 through CTNNB1 and results in the disruption of cell adhesion processes. Lastly, elevated levels of MMP2 and MMP9 are also observed in SIC. The expression of metalloproteinases is initiated by TGFB1.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cataract is clouding of the eye's lens that affects vision. Cataract caused by steroid administration is a common side effect of the therapy used to treat various pathologies. The characteristic features of steroid-induced cataract (SIC) are aberrant lens epithelial cell migration, proliferation, and a central posterior location.    Signaling description: Normally, lens epithelial cells continuously proliferate and differentiate into fiber cells via the synthesis of specific proteins such as crystallins, MIP, BFSP1, and BFSP2 in addition to the elimination of organelles and cell migration to the lens nucleus. The process of cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration is upregulated by FGF2 in a dose-dependent manner. A low cytokine concentration initiates epithelial cell proliferation, while moderate cytokine levels lead to migration, and higher concentrations cause differentiation. FGF2, (through MAP2K1/MAP2K2/MAPK1/MAPK3) promotes cell proliferation via the activation of RPS6KB1 and CREBBP transcription factors. At higher concentrations of FGF2, MAF is involved through the same pathway and initiates the transcription of lens specific proteins including crystallins, MIP, BFSP1, and BFSP2.    Outcome effects: Steroids can indicrectly influence cataract development by moderately decreasing FGF2 expression, which leads to block of cell differentiation, but the level of FGF2 is still sufficient to promote cell proliferation. In the direct mechanism of steroid-induced cataractogenesis, steroids (glucocorticoids) bind to their intracellular receptor, NR3C1 and initiate the expression of TSC22D3, which is a repressor of cell differentiation. NR3C1 inhibits CDH1 through CTNNB1 and results in the disruption of cell adhesion processes. Lastly, elevated levels of MMP2 and MMP9 are also observed in SIC. The expression of metalloproteinases is initiated by TGFB1.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

