xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Retinal and choroidal vascular diseases constitute the most common causes of moderate and severe vision loss. They can be divided into retinal vascular diseases, in which there is neovascularization (NV) of retina, and subretinal NV, in which new vessels grow into the normally avascular outer retina and subretinal space. The first category of diseases includes diabetic retinopathy, retinal vein occlusions, and retinopathy of prematurity and the second category includes neovascular age-related macular degeneration (AMD), ocular histoplasmosis, pathologic myopia, and other related diseases.     Signaling description: Retinal hypoxia is a key feature of retinal NV resulting in elevated levels of hypoxia-inducible factor-1 (HIF-1) which stimulates expression of vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), platelet-derived growth factor-B (PDGFB), placental growth factor (PGF), stromal-derived growth factor-1 (CXCL12) and their receptors as well as other hypoxia-regulated gene products such as angiopoietin-2 (ANGPT2). Hypoxia has not been demonstrated as part of choroidal NV. VEGF also diffuse to the anterior chamber leading to NV and open- or close-angle glaucoma development. Anti-VEGF treatment may be beneficial in NV disorders of the eye."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Retinal and choroidal vascular diseases constitute the most common causes of moderate and severe vision loss. They can be divided into retinal vascular diseases, in which there is neovascularization (NV) of retina, and subretinal NV, in which new vessels grow into the normally avascular outer retina and subretinal space. The first category of diseases includes diabetic retinopathy, retinal vein occlusions, and retinopathy of prematurity and the second category includes neovascular age-related macular degeneration (AMD), ocular histoplasmosis, pathologic myopia, and other related diseases.     Signaling description: Retinal hypoxia is a key feature of retinal NV resulting in elevated levels of hypoxia-inducible factor-1 (HIF-1) which stimulates expression of vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), platelet-derived growth factor-B (PDGFB), placental growth factor (PGF), stromal-derived growth factor-1 (CXCL12) and their receptors as well as other hypoxia-regulated gene products such as angiopoietin-2 (ANGPT2). Hypoxia has not been demonstrated as part of choroidal NV. VEGF also diffuse to the anterior chamber leading to NV and open- or close-angle glaucoma development. Anti-VEGF treatment may be beneficial in NV disorders of the eye." EXACT []

