xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. Neovascular glaucoma (NVG) is a secondary glaucoma and can present through either secondary open-angle or secondary closed-angle mechanisms contingent on the extent of neovascularization.    Signaling description: NVG is associated with a wide range of conditions including retinal ischemia, which accounts for the majority of the cases, and central retinal vein occlusion and diabetes retinopathy, which produces nearly two thirds of all cases. Neovascularization is a growth of vessels into tissues normally not vascularized. In NVG, neovascularization is localized in the iris and anterior angle of the eye. Retinal ischemia triggers the production of HIF1A, which in turn leads to the expression of different factors such as VEGFA, which has been shown to play a major role in NVG pathogenesis.    Outcome effects: VEGFA induces different signaling pathways, resulting in the upregulation of genes involved in proliferation, migration, and survival of endothelial cells and vascular permeability. All of these events contribute to neovascularization. Anti-VEGFA therapy (bevacizumab) has been demonstrated as being beneficial in NVG.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    References: 1) Kurt Spiteri Cornish (2011). Neovascular Glaucoma, Glaucoma - Current Clinical and Research Aspects, Dr. Pinakin Gunvant (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-307-263-0, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/18497.Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/glaucoma-current-clinical-and-research-aspects/neovascular-glaucoma 4)"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. Neovascular glaucoma (NVG) is a secondary glaucoma and can present through either secondary open-angle or secondary closed-angle mechanisms contingent on the extent of neovascularization.    Signaling description: NVG is associated with a wide range of conditions including retinal ischemia, which accounts for the majority of the cases, and central retinal vein occlusion and diabetes retinopathy, which produces nearly two thirds of all cases. Neovascularization is a growth of vessels into tissues normally not vascularized. In NVG, neovascularization is localized in the iris and anterior angle of the eye. Retinal ischemia triggers the production of HIF1A, which in turn leads to the expression of different factors such as VEGFA, which has been shown to play a major role in NVG pathogenesis.    Outcome effects: VEGFA induces different signaling pathways, resulting in the upregulation of genes involved in proliferation, migration, and survival of endothelial cells and vascular permeability. All of these events contribute to neovascularization. Anti-VEGFA therapy (bevacizumab) has been demonstrated as being beneficial in NVG.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    References: 1) Kurt Spiteri Cornish (2011). Neovascular Glaucoma, Glaucoma - Current Clinical and Research Aspects, Dr. Pinakin Gunvant (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-307-263-0, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/18497.Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/glaucoma-current-clinical-and-research-aspects/neovascular-glaucoma 4)" EXACT []

