xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss.    Signaling description: NF-kB activity is elevated in the cells of trabecular meshwork obtained from the eyes of glaucoma patients of diverse etiology. The binding of TNF to its receptors leads to receptor trimerization, which promotes the assembly of a multimolecular complex on the TNF receptor, in which ubiquitination of RIPK1 takes place. At that point, IKBKG is recruited to ubiquitinated RIPK1, which leads to the activation of IKBKB and CHUK.    Outcome effects: Active IKBKB phosphorylates NFKBIA, which acts as a trigger for ubiquitination and degradation of NFKBIA. This leads to NF-kB activation and recruitment to the nucleus, which causes activation of transcription. OPTN regulates this process by acting as a competitive inhibitor of IKBKG and binds to ubiquitinated RIPK1 by displacing IKBKG. OPTN then recruits CYLD (a deubiquitinase) to the molecular complex thus facilitating deubiquitination of polyubiquitinated RIPK1 by CYLD leading to the down regulation of downstream pathways causing cell apoptosis. Mutated OPTN also triggers the release of cytochrome c (CYCS), loss of mitochondrial membrane potential, and activation of the mitochondrial pathway in neuronal cell apoptosis.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss.    Signaling description: NF-kB activity is elevated in the cells of trabecular meshwork obtained from the eyes of glaucoma patients of diverse etiology. The binding of TNF to its receptors leads to receptor trimerization, which promotes the assembly of a multimolecular complex on the TNF receptor, in which ubiquitination of RIPK1 takes place. At that point, IKBKG is recruited to ubiquitinated RIPK1, which leads to the activation of IKBKB and CHUK.    Outcome effects: Active IKBKB phosphorylates NFKBIA, which acts as a trigger for ubiquitination and degradation of NFKBIA. This leads to NF-kB activation and recruitment to the nucleus, which causes activation of transcription. OPTN regulates this process by acting as a competitive inhibitor of IKBKG and binds to ubiquitinated RIPK1 by displacing IKBKG. OPTN then recruits CYLD (a deubiquitinase) to the molecular complex thus facilitating deubiquitination of polyubiquitinated RIPK1 by CYLD leading to the down regulation of downstream pathways causing cell apoptosis. Mutated OPTN also triggers the release of cytochrome c (CYCS), loss of mitochondrial membrane potential, and activation of the mitochondrial pathway in neuronal cell apoptosis.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

