xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. Approximately 4% of adult-onset primary open-angle glaucoma cases and more than 10% of juvenile-onset cases are associated with myocilin (MYOC) mutations.    Signaling description: MYOC is a secreted glycoprotein of unknown function. Most likely, mutations in MYOC have a pathogenic effect due to the inability of the protein to fold properly. When misfolded, this mutated protein forms aggregates in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) as well as in the cytoplasm. The misfolded protein ultimately results in unfolded protein response (UPR) and activates mitochondria-independent apoptosis pathway. In addition, MYOC mutations greatly reduce the quantity of MYOC that is secreted into the AH which supports the idea that failure to secrete MYOC is one of the central events in the pathogenesis of MYOC-associated glaucoma. Normally, MYOC is cleaved by CAPN2 into two fragments: a 20 kDa N-terminal fragment and a 35 kDa C-terminal olfactomedin-containing fragment. The 35-kDa fragment and full length MYOC have been shown to be excreted into the extracellular matrix (ECM), and thus hypothesized to maintain a normal ECM structure. Furthermore, data suggests that MYOC can control the adhesive properties of the cell. MYOC was shown to be a modulator of WNT signaling pathway, and the protein also affects the TM adhesion properties through RHOA. MYOC interacts with the components of the WNT signaling pathways, including WNT receptors of the Frizzled (FZD) family, WNT antagonists of the secreted FZD-related protein (SFRP1) family, and WNT inhibitory factor 1 (WIF1), which modulate the organization of actin cytoskeleton and stimulate the formation of stress fibers critical for the contractility of TM and IOP regulation. MYOC expression is known to be upregulated by glucocorticoids in TM cells and altered MYOC levels may be the culprit in conditions such as corticosteroid glaucoma. The expression of MYOC may also be induced by TGFB1, OPTN, and mechanical stress.    Outcome effects: Mutations in MYOC disturb its normal cleavage by CAPN2, and thus cause the breakdown of ECM structure in TM which ultimately results in higher intraocular pressure (IOP) and glaucoma. In addition, the mitochondria-independent apoptosis pathway caused by misfolded MYOC leads to cell death and the breakdown of the trabecular meshwork (TM) cell structure, obstruction of aqueous humor (AH) outflow pathway, and also leads to ocular hypertension and glaucoma.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. Approximately 4% of adult-onset primary open-angle glaucoma cases and more than 10% of juvenile-onset cases are associated with myocilin (MYOC) mutations.    Signaling description: MYOC is a secreted glycoprotein of unknown function. Most likely, mutations in MYOC have a pathogenic effect due to the inability of the protein to fold properly. When misfolded, this mutated protein forms aggregates in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) as well as in the cytoplasm. The misfolded protein ultimately results in unfolded protein response (UPR) and activates mitochondria-independent apoptosis pathway. In addition, MYOC mutations greatly reduce the quantity of MYOC that is secreted into the AH which supports the idea that failure to secrete MYOC is one of the central events in the pathogenesis of MYOC-associated glaucoma. Normally, MYOC is cleaved by CAPN2 into two fragments: a 20 kDa N-terminal fragment and a 35 kDa C-terminal olfactomedin-containing fragment. The 35-kDa fragment and full length MYOC have been shown to be excreted into the extracellular matrix (ECM), and thus hypothesized to maintain a normal ECM structure. Furthermore, data suggests that MYOC can control the adhesive properties of the cell. MYOC was shown to be a modulator of WNT signaling pathway, and the protein also affects the TM adhesion properties through RHOA. MYOC interacts with the components of the WNT signaling pathways, including WNT receptors of the Frizzled (FZD) family, WNT antagonists of the secreted FZD-related protein (SFRP1) family, and WNT inhibitory factor 1 (WIF1), which modulate the organization of actin cytoskeleton and stimulate the formation of stress fibers critical for the contractility of TM and IOP regulation. MYOC expression is known to be upregulated by glucocorticoids in TM cells and altered MYOC levels may be the culprit in conditions such as corticosteroid glaucoma. The expression of MYOC may also be induced by TGFB1, OPTN, and mechanical stress.    Outcome effects: Mutations in MYOC disturb its normal cleavage by CAPN2, and thus cause the breakdown of ECM structure in TM which ultimately results in higher intraocular pressure (IOP) and glaucoma. In addition, the mitochondria-independent apoptosis pathway caused by misfolded MYOC leads to cell death and the breakdown of the trabecular meshwork (TM) cell structure, obstruction of aqueous humor (AH) outflow pathway, and also leads to ocular hypertension and glaucoma.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

