xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. Glutamate is the predominant excitatory amino acid in various regions of the central nervous system (CNS) including the retina. Glutamate excitotoxicity is believed to be one of the reasons for glaucomatous retinal ganglion cell (RGC) death.    Signaling description: Glutamate is released from the pre-synaptic photoreceptor membrane and binds to glutamate receptors, including N-methyl-D-aspartate selective glutamate receptor (NMDA), AMPA, and metabotropic glutamate receptors in the post-synaptic membrane. This in turn induces the rapid build-up of cytosolic calcium (Ca2+) levels. The prolonged activation of glutamate receptors by increased levels of glutamate leads to high Ca2+ levels in the cytosol and thus facilitates the opening of mitochondrial mPTP.    Outcome effects: The opening of mPTP leads to the passage of cytochrome C (CYCS), AIFM1, and ENDOG to the cytosol and initiates the cascade of apoptotic cell death. Muller glial cells are responsible for the uptake of excess glutamate in the retina via glutamate/aspartate transporters (SLC1A2/3). These transporters have been shown to be downregulated in glaucomatous retina. Muller cells are extraordinarily sensitive to NMDA and respond rapidly by upregulating NF-kB activity and TNF production. TNF increases the surface levels of AMPA receptors on RGCs. Wild-type OPTN has been shown to inhibit GRM1/5 signaling. When OPTN is mutated, this function may be impaired leading to the further rise in intracellular Ca2+.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. Glutamate is the predominant excitatory amino acid in various regions of the central nervous system (CNS) including the retina. Glutamate excitotoxicity is believed to be one of the reasons for glaucomatous retinal ganglion cell (RGC) death.    Signaling description: Glutamate is released from the pre-synaptic photoreceptor membrane and binds to glutamate receptors, including N-methyl-D-aspartate selective glutamate receptor (NMDA), AMPA, and metabotropic glutamate receptors in the post-synaptic membrane. This in turn induces the rapid build-up of cytosolic calcium (Ca2+) levels. The prolonged activation of glutamate receptors by increased levels of glutamate leads to high Ca2+ levels in the cytosol and thus facilitates the opening of mitochondrial mPTP.    Outcome effects: The opening of mPTP leads to the passage of cytochrome C (CYCS), AIFM1, and ENDOG to the cytosol and initiates the cascade of apoptotic cell death. Muller glial cells are responsible for the uptake of excess glutamate in the retina via glutamate/aspartate transporters (SLC1A2/3). These transporters have been shown to be downregulated in glaucomatous retina. Muller cells are extraordinarily sensitive to NMDA and respond rapidly by upregulating NF-kB activity and TNF production. TNF increases the surface levels of AMPA receptors on RGCs. Wild-type OPTN has been shown to inhibit GRM1/5 signaling. When OPTN is mutated, this function may be impaired leading to the further rise in intracellular Ca2+.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

