xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. OPTN is a multifunctional protein that may be mutated in normal-tension glaucoma and adult-onset primary open-angle glaucoma.    Signaling description: Optineurin (OPTN) is involved in regulating various cellular functions including signal transduction, membrane vesicle trafficking, autophagy, NF-kB signaling, and cell survival. These functions are mediated through interactions with a variety of proteins. A glaucoma-causing mutant of OPTN, named E50K, selectively induces death in retinal ganglion cells. OPTN may function in vesicle trafficking and Golgi organization/biogenesis through its interactions with RAB8A, HTT, and MYO6. RAB8A recruits OPTN to link HTT and MYO6 to coordinate the movement of vesicles along microtubules and actin tracks. OPTN also interacts with TBC1D17, which does not bind directly to RAB8A and requires OPTN for this interaction, thereby leading to the inactivation of RAB8A. OPTN is localized in the Golgi apparatus and upon apoptotic stimuli, it translocates to the nucleus. This translocation is mediated by RAB8A. OPTN is involved in mitosis regulation through the interaction with PLK1. During mitosis, OPTN is phosphorylated by PLK1, which leads to its re-localization to the nucleus from the Golgi. In the nucleus, OPTN enhances phosphorylation of PPP1R12A by CDK1 leading to binding of PPP1R12A and PLK1 and, subsequently PLK1 inactivation. OPTN also inhibits GRM1/5 signaling.    Outcome effects: The knockdown of OPTN leads to defects in chromosome separation and the formation of multinucleate cells. In addition, OPTN is involved in autophagy through the interaction with MAP1LC3A and GABARAP. Mutated OPTN may inhibit NF-kB signaling by acting as a competitive inhibitor of IKBKG and binding to ubiquitinated RIPK1 by displacing IKBKG. OPTN then recruits CYLD (a deubiquitinase) to the molecular complex, thus facilitating deubiquitination of polyubiquitinated RIPK1 by CYLD leading to the down regulation of downstream pathways causing cell apoptosis. Mutated OPTN triggers the release of cytochrome c (CYCS), loss of mitochondrial membrane potential, and activation of the mitochondrial pathway in neuronal cell apoptosis. The knockdown of endogenous OPTN has been shown to induce apoptotic cell death due to the reduced secretion of NTF3 and CNTF.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style.    References: 1).Ghanshyam Swarup, Vipul Vaibhava, Ananthamurthy Nagabhushana (2013). Functional Defects Caused by Glaucoma - Associated Mutations in Optineurin, Glaucoma - Basic and Clinical Aspects, Dr Shimon Rumelt (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-1064-4, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/52692.Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/glaucoma-basic-and-clinical-aspects/functional-defects-caused-by-glaucoma-associated-mutations-in-optineurin"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. OPTN is a multifunctional protein that may be mutated in normal-tension glaucoma and adult-onset primary open-angle glaucoma.    Signaling description: Optineurin (OPTN) is involved in regulating various cellular functions including signal transduction, membrane vesicle trafficking, autophagy, NF-kB signaling, and cell survival. These functions are mediated through interactions with a variety of proteins. A glaucoma-causing mutant of OPTN, named E50K, selectively induces death in retinal ganglion cells. OPTN may function in vesicle trafficking and Golgi organization/biogenesis through its interactions with RAB8A, HTT, and MYO6. RAB8A recruits OPTN to link HTT and MYO6 to coordinate the movement of vesicles along microtubules and actin tracks. OPTN also interacts with TBC1D17, which does not bind directly to RAB8A and requires OPTN for this interaction, thereby leading to the inactivation of RAB8A. OPTN is localized in the Golgi apparatus and upon apoptotic stimuli, it translocates to the nucleus. This translocation is mediated by RAB8A. OPTN is involved in mitosis regulation through the interaction with PLK1. During mitosis, OPTN is phosphorylated by PLK1, which leads to its re-localization to the nucleus from the Golgi. In the nucleus, OPTN enhances phosphorylation of PPP1R12A by CDK1 leading to binding of PPP1R12A and PLK1 and, subsequently PLK1 inactivation. OPTN also inhibits GRM1/5 signaling.    Outcome effects: The knockdown of OPTN leads to defects in chromosome separation and the formation of multinucleate cells. In addition, OPTN is involved in autophagy through the interaction with MAP1LC3A and GABARAP. Mutated OPTN may inhibit NF-kB signaling by acting as a competitive inhibitor of IKBKG and binding to ubiquitinated RIPK1 by displacing IKBKG. OPTN then recruits CYLD (a deubiquitinase) to the molecular complex, thus facilitating deubiquitination of polyubiquitinated RIPK1 by CYLD leading to the down regulation of downstream pathways causing cell apoptosis. Mutated OPTN triggers the release of cytochrome c (CYCS), loss of mitochondrial membrane potential, and activation of the mitochondrial pathway in neuronal cell apoptosis. The knockdown of endogenous OPTN has been shown to induce apoptotic cell death due to the reduced secretion of NTF3 and CNTF.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style.    References: 1).Ghanshyam Swarup, Vipul Vaibhava, Ananthamurthy Nagabhushana (2013). Functional Defects Caused by Glaucoma - Associated Mutations in Optineurin, Glaucoma - Basic and Clinical Aspects, Dr Shimon Rumelt (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-1064-4, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/52692.Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/glaucoma-basic-and-clinical-aspects/functional-defects-caused-by-glaucoma-associated-mutations-in-optineurin" EXACT []

