xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Trabecular meshwork (TM) and Schlemm's canal is the outflow system for aqueous humor (AH) in the eye. TM is a network of beams covered with endothelial cells. The outflow resistance is dependent on the TM extracellular matrix state and on the size and volume of endothelial cells.    Signaling description: Endothelial cell nitric oxide synthase (NOS3) has been shown to be activated, as a result of intraocular pressure (IOP) increase, and produce nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC), formation of cGMP, and activation of protein kinase G (PKG) with the subsequent phosphorylation of target proteins including BK channels and K+:Cl- symporters with subsequent K+ efflux. In addition, water efflux is mediated by AQP1.    Outcome effects: As a result, cell volume decreases allowing easy outflow of AH leading to IOP decrease. TM is a contractile tissue itself with properties similar to those of smooth muscle. TM contractility is linked to RHOA, which inhibits myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP), resulting in the accumulation of phosphorylated myosin light chain, which is then capable of interacting with actin to produce contraction. The contraction of TM cells decreases the permeability of the TM due to the reduced size of the intercellular spaces. Similarly, when TM cells relax, the opposite effect appears and the permeability of the tissue increases. Elevated concentrations of endothelin-1 (EDN1) have been documented in the AH of patients with glaucoma. EDN1 is capable of inducing the contraction of both TM cells and the cellular matrix. The contraction seems to be triggered by the activation of the RHOA/ROCK1 pathway which leads to the increase in outflow resistance and consequently to IOP elevation. Rho kinase (ROCK1) inhibitors are a novel potential class of glaucoma therapeutics, and endothelin receptor antagonists would be ideal to lower IOP.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red.    References: 1) Rocha-Sousa A, et al. (2013) New therapeutic targets for intraocular pressure lowering. ISRN Ophthalmol."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Trabecular meshwork (TM) and Schlemm's canal is the outflow system for aqueous humor (AH) in the eye. TM is a network of beams covered with endothelial cells. The outflow resistance is dependent on the TM extracellular matrix state and on the size and volume of endothelial cells.    Signaling description: Endothelial cell nitric oxide synthase (NOS3) has been shown to be activated, as a result of intraocular pressure (IOP) increase, and produce nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC), formation of cGMP, and activation of protein kinase G (PKG) with the subsequent phosphorylation of target proteins including BK channels and K+:Cl- symporters with subsequent K+ efflux. In addition, water efflux is mediated by AQP1.    Outcome effects: As a result, cell volume decreases allowing easy outflow of AH leading to IOP decrease. TM is a contractile tissue itself with properties similar to those of smooth muscle. TM contractility is linked to RHOA, which inhibits myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP), resulting in the accumulation of phosphorylated myosin light chain, which is then capable of interacting with actin to produce contraction. The contraction of TM cells decreases the permeability of the TM due to the reduced size of the intercellular spaces. Similarly, when TM cells relax, the opposite effect appears and the permeability of the tissue increases. Elevated concentrations of endothelin-1 (EDN1) have been documented in the AH of patients with glaucoma. EDN1 is capable of inducing the contraction of both TM cells and the cellular matrix. The contraction seems to be triggered by the activation of the RHOA/ROCK1 pathway which leads to the increase in outflow resistance and consequently to IOP elevation. Rho kinase (ROCK1) inhibitors are a novel potential class of glaucoma therapeutics, and endothelin receptor antagonists would be ideal to lower IOP.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red.    References: 1) Rocha-Sousa A, et al. (2013) New therapeutic targets for intraocular pressure lowering. ISRN Ophthalmol." EXACT []

