xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. The aqueous humor (AH) of the eye is a transparent fluid consisting of water (98%), ions, amino acids, and biomolecules such as signaling molecules, growth factors, and extracellular matrix molecules.    Signaling description: Three mechanisms are involved in AH formation including diffusion, ultrafiltration, and active secretion.The AH fills the anterior and posterior chambers of the eye and has two main functions: nutrient supply to the avascular structures of the eye, cornea, and lens and intraocular pressure (IOP) homeostatsis. AH undergoes a constant turnover. It is secreted to the posterior chamber of the eye by the ciliary body epithelium (CBE), circulates throughout the posterior and anterior chambers, and drains through the trabecular meshwork in the iridocorneal angle. A number of transporters are involved in AH production including Na+/K+ ATPases, Na+-K+-Cl- symporters, Na+/H+ antiporters, Cl-/ HCO3- exchangers, and other channels.    Outcome effects: The balance between the production and outflow of AH ultimately determines IOP. Carbonic anhydrases is a family of enzymes that catalyze the reversible conversion of carbon dioxide and water to bicarbonate and protons. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease the amount of AH production and are used to treat glaucoma.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Glaucoma refers to a group of eye disoders in which ocular hypertension damages the optic nerve eventually leading to vision loss. The aqueous humor (AH) of the eye is a transparent fluid consisting of water (98%), ions, amino acids, and biomolecules such as signaling molecules, growth factors, and extracellular matrix molecules.    Signaling description: Three mechanisms are involved in AH formation including diffusion, ultrafiltration, and active secretion.The AH fills the anterior and posterior chambers of the eye and has two main functions: nutrient supply to the avascular structures of the eye, cornea, and lens and intraocular pressure (IOP) homeostatsis. AH undergoes a constant turnover. It is secreted to the posterior chamber of the eye by the ciliary body epithelium (CBE), circulates throughout the posterior and anterior chambers, and drains through the trabecular meshwork in the iridocorneal angle. A number of transporters are involved in AH production including Na+/K+ ATPases, Na+-K+-Cl- symporters, Na+/H+ antiporters, Cl-/ HCO3- exchangers, and other channels.    Outcome effects: The balance between the production and outflow of AH ultimately determines IOP. Carbonic anhydrases is a family of enzymes that catalyze the reversible conversion of carbon dioxide and water to bicarbonate and protons. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease the amount of AH production and are used to treat glaucoma.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

