xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Endometrial carcinoma (EC) is the most common gynecological malignant tumor in the westen world. Overall, approximately 2-3% of women develop EC. The two major categories of EC include type I estrogen-dependent endometrioid endometrial carcinoma (EEC) and type II estrogen-independent EC with serous papillary or clear cell morphology. EEC is the most common subtype of gynecological cancer.    Signaling description: EEC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, PIK3CA, PTEN, ARID1A, and beta-catenin (CTNNB1) genes, as well as mutations in mismatch repair genes such as MLH1, MSH2, MSH3, and MSH6. In addition, type I EEC is characterized by a reduced expression of PGR (progesterone receptor) and overexpression of growth factor receptors and their ligands such as EGFRs (the EGFR family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4)), as well as IGF1R, FGFR2, WNT, ESR1 (estrogen receptor 1), and CTNNB1. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of the AKT1 cascades, mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades, and the WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway. IN particular, ligand-bound membrane EGFRs, IGF1R, FGFR2, and ESR1 activate PIK3CA, which in turn activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT1-mediated signaling, and mutations in PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: The constitutive signaling activation in EEC leads to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of endometrial epithelial cells; whereas mutations in mismatch repair genes cause block of DNA repair. Specifically, activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and the suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs mediate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in cell growth. EGFRs and IGF1R also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. In addition, ligand-bound cytoplasmic ESR1 moves into the nucleus. Then, nuclear ESR1 interacts with transcription factors, NCOA1/NCOA3/EP300 and forms the transcription complex which induces cell proliferation. The WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway controls various cellular functions including proliferation, differentiation, apoptosis, survival, migration, embryonic development, tumorigenesis, and polarity. In the absence of WNTs, CTNNB1 is associated with a complex which includes the adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) tumor suppressor, axin1 (AXIN1), glycogen synthase kinase-3beta (GSK3B), and casein kinase 1 (CSNK1A1). This complex formation induces the phosphorylation of CTNNB1 by CSNK1A1 and GSK3B, which results in the ubiquitination and degradation of CTNNB1 protein by the proteasome. When WNT proteins bind to LRP5/6 and FZD, DVL1 is activated and inhibits GSK3B, which results in the cytosolic CTNNB1 stabilization. Also, AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic CTNNB1 protein. Activated CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus wh"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Endometrial carcinoma (EC) is the most common gynecological malignant tumor in the westen world. Overall, approximately 2-3% of women develop EC. The two major categories of EC include type I estrogen-dependent endometrioid endometrial carcinoma (EEC) and type II estrogen-independent EC with serous papillary or clear cell morphology. EEC is the most common subtype of gynecological cancer.    Signaling description: EEC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, PIK3CA, PTEN, ARID1A, and beta-catenin (CTNNB1) genes, as well as mutations in mismatch repair genes such as MLH1, MSH2, MSH3, and MSH6. In addition, type I EEC is characterized by a reduced expression of PGR (progesterone receptor) and overexpression of growth factor receptors and their ligands such as EGFRs (the EGFR family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4)), as well as IGF1R, FGFR2, WNT, ESR1 (estrogen receptor 1), and CTNNB1. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of the AKT1 cascades, mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades, and the WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway. IN particular, ligand-bound membrane EGFRs, IGF1R, FGFR2, and ESR1 activate PIK3CA, which in turn activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT1-mediated signaling, and mutations in PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: The constitutive signaling activation in EEC leads to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of endometrial epithelial cells; whereas mutations in mismatch repair genes cause block of DNA repair. Specifically, activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and the suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs mediate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in cell growth. EGFRs and IGF1R also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. In addition, ligand-bound cytoplasmic ESR1 moves into the nucleus. Then, nuclear ESR1 interacts with transcription factors, NCOA1/NCOA3/EP300 and forms the transcription complex which induces cell proliferation. The WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway controls various cellular functions including proliferation, differentiation, apoptosis, survival, migration, embryonic development, tumorigenesis, and polarity. In the absence of WNTs, CTNNB1 is associated with a complex which includes the adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) tumor suppressor, axin1 (AXIN1), glycogen synthase kinase-3beta (GSK3B), and casein kinase 1 (CSNK1A1). This complex formation induces the phosphorylation of CTNNB1 by CSNK1A1 and GSK3B, which results in the ubiquitination and degradation of CTNNB1 protein by the proteasome. When WNT proteins bind to LRP5/6 and FZD, DVL1 is activated and inhibits GSK3B, which results in the cytosolic CTNNB1 stabilization. Also, AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic CTNNB1 protein. Activated CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus wh" EXACT []

