xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Headnote: Endometrial carcinoma (EC) is the most common gynecological cancer in the western countries. The two major categories of EC include type I estrogen-dependent endometrioid endometrial carcinoma and type II estrogen-independent EC with papillary serous or clear cell morphology. Type II estrogen-independent EC is a rare type of aggressive EC. Patients with Type II estrogen-independent EC have a lower overall and disease-free survival rate than patients with type I estrogen-dependent EC.    Signaling description: Type II estrogen-independent EC is characterized by mutations in the TP53 gene, overexpression of ERBB2, CLDN3/4, EPCAM, and CDKN2A in addition to the reduced expression of CDH1 (E-cadherin). The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members including epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple the extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. Ligand-bound membrane EGFRs activate PIK3CA, which in turn activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1), which then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and the activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs regulate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in cell growth. EGFRs also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. Lastly, CLDN3/4 and EPCAM form tight junctions and their overexpression may increase cell motility and contribute to the aggressive phenotype of EC of serous papillary or clear-cell histology.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Headnote: Endometrial carcinoma (EC) is the most common gynecological cancer in the western countries. The two major categories of EC include type I estrogen-dependent endometrioid endometrial carcinoma and type II estrogen-independent EC with papillary serous or clear cell morphology. Type II estrogen-independent EC is a rare type of aggressive EC. Patients with Type II estrogen-independent EC have a lower overall and disease-free survival rate than patients with type I estrogen-dependent EC.    Signaling description: Type II estrogen-independent EC is characterized by mutations in the TP53 gene, overexpression of ERBB2, CLDN3/4, EPCAM, and CDKN2A in addition to the reduced expression of CDH1 (E-cadherin). The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members including epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple the extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. Ligand-bound membrane EGFRs activate PIK3CA, which in turn activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1), which then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and the activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs regulate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in cell growth. EGFRs also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. Lastly, CLDN3/4 and EPCAM form tight junctions and their overexpression may increase cell motility and contribute to the aggressive phenotype of EC of serous papillary or clear-cell histology.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

