xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Endometriosis is an estrogen-dependent gynecological disease characterized by endometrial-like tissue growing outside the uterine cavity, typically on the pelvic peritoneum, in the ovaries and in the rectovaginal septum. Endometriosis typically results in extensive pelvic adhesions and deformation of pelvic anatomy, often leading to pain and infertility. The incidence of endometriosis is estimated to be up to 10% in women of reproductive age.    Signaling description: Endometriotic tissue proliferates in response to both systemic and locally produced estrogens. Endometriotic cells were shown to have their own estrogen production, which enhances the growth of the diseased tissue. Ectopic endometrium induces inflammation within the peritoneal cavity, which results in the release of cytokines. IL-1B and the VEGF produced by macrophages or endometriotic lesions induce COX-2 (PTGS2). PTGS2 catalyzes in macrophages and ectopic endometrial cells the formation of prostaglandin (PG) G2, which is further converted to PGE2. PGE2 increases estrogen synthesis by up-regulating steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (STAR), aromatase (CYP19A1) and other steroidogenic enzymes. In the endometrium of healthy women STAR and CYP19A1 are not expressed because NR5A1 is switched off and inhibitory factors (NR2F1/2, WT-1) are present. These inhibitory factors are down-regulated in endometriosis. Endometriosis is able to locally release hormones and pro-hormones from their sulfate conjugates (estrone from estrone sulfate, estradiol from estradiol sulfate and dehydroepiandrosterone from dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate) due to the presence of steroid sulfatase (STS) activity. Estrogen receptor beta (ESR2) was shown to be up-regulated in endometriosis and inhibits expression of estrogen receptor alpha (ESR1) and progesterone receptor (PGR).    Outcome effects: PTGS2 induction increases local concentration of estradiol (E2), which further up-regulates PTGS2 and CYP19A1 and establishes a positive-feedback loop, as a vicious cycle in favor of continuous estrogen and PG formation. Normally E2 can be inactivated also by sulfate conjugation catalyzed by estrogen sulfotransferase (SULT1E1). SULT1E1 was shown to be down-regulated in endometriosis. Also E2 can be inactivated through conversion to estrone by oxidative HSD17B2 -activity, and via the formation of sulfate- or glucorine conjugates. In the healthy endometrium, the expression of HSD17B2 is highly increased in the secretory phase, due to its regulation by progesterone. Its aberrant expression, i.e. the lack of increase at the secretory phase, has been confirmed in endometriosis. The aberrant expression of HSD17B2 in endometriosis is assumed to be caused by an impaired action of progesterone in the endometriotic cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Endometriosis is an estrogen-dependent gynecological disease characterized by endometrial-like tissue growing outside the uterine cavity, typically on the pelvic peritoneum, in the ovaries and in the rectovaginal septum. Endometriosis typically results in extensive pelvic adhesions and deformation of pelvic anatomy, often leading to pain and infertility. The incidence of endometriosis is estimated to be up to 10% in women of reproductive age.    Signaling description: Endometriotic tissue proliferates in response to both systemic and locally produced estrogens. Endometriotic cells were shown to have their own estrogen production, which enhances the growth of the diseased tissue. Ectopic endometrium induces inflammation within the peritoneal cavity, which results in the release of cytokines. IL-1B and the VEGF produced by macrophages or endometriotic lesions induce COX-2 (PTGS2). PTGS2 catalyzes in macrophages and ectopic endometrial cells the formation of prostaglandin (PG) G2, which is further converted to PGE2. PGE2 increases estrogen synthesis by up-regulating steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (STAR), aromatase (CYP19A1) and other steroidogenic enzymes. In the endometrium of healthy women STAR and CYP19A1 are not expressed because NR5A1 is switched off and inhibitory factors (NR2F1/2, WT-1) are present. These inhibitory factors are down-regulated in endometriosis. Endometriosis is able to locally release hormones and pro-hormones from their sulfate conjugates (estrone from estrone sulfate, estradiol from estradiol sulfate and dehydroepiandrosterone from dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate) due to the presence of steroid sulfatase (STS) activity. Estrogen receptor beta (ESR2) was shown to be up-regulated in endometriosis and inhibits expression of estrogen receptor alpha (ESR1) and progesterone receptor (PGR).    Outcome effects: PTGS2 induction increases local concentration of estradiol (E2), which further up-regulates PTGS2 and CYP19A1 and establishes a positive-feedback loop, as a vicious cycle in favor of continuous estrogen and PG formation. Normally E2 can be inactivated also by sulfate conjugation catalyzed by estrogen sulfotransferase (SULT1E1). SULT1E1 was shown to be down-regulated in endometriosis. Also E2 can be inactivated through conversion to estrone by oxidative HSD17B2 -activity, and via the formation of sulfate- or glucorine conjugates. In the healthy endometrium, the expression of HSD17B2 is highly increased in the secretory phase, due to its regulation by progesterone. Its aberrant expression, i.e. the lack of increase at the secretory phase, has been confirmed in endometriosis. The aberrant expression of HSD17B2 in endometriosis is assumed to be caused by an impaired action of progesterone in the endometriotic cells." EXACT []

