xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Angiogenesis is a key factor for the successful establishment and growth of endometriotic lesions. The two main regulators of angiogenesis are vascular endothelial growth factors (VEGF) and angiopoietins (ANGPT).    Signaling description: VEGFA and VEGFC its principal receptor VEGFR2 (KDR) are strongly up-regulated in endometriotic lesions. VEGFA specifically acts as endothelial cells (EC) mitogen, enhances EC migration, and increases vascular permeability. Inhibition of VEGF leads to a significant decrease in the number of endometriotic lesions. Levels of Angiopoeitin-1 and -2 (ANGPT1/2) are both increased in the endometrium of patients with endometriosis. The shift of the ANGPT1/2 balance in favor of ANGPT2 destabilizes pre-existing vessels and makes them prone to sprout."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Angiogenesis is a key factor for the successful establishment and growth of endometriotic lesions. The two main regulators of angiogenesis are vascular endothelial growth factors (VEGF) and angiopoietins (ANGPT).    Signaling description: VEGFA and VEGFC its principal receptor VEGFR2 (KDR) are strongly up-regulated in endometriotic lesions. VEGFA specifically acts as endothelial cells (EC) mitogen, enhances EC migration, and increases vascular permeability. Inhibition of VEGF leads to a significant decrease in the number of endometriotic lesions. Levels of Angiopoeitin-1 and -2 (ANGPT1/2) are both increased in the endometrium of patients with endometriosis. The shift of the ANGPT1/2 balance in favor of ANGPT2 destabilizes pre-existing vessels and makes them prone to sprout." EXACT []

