xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Environmental toxicants can act as endocrine disrupters on the female reproductive system. 2,3,7,8-Tetrachlorodibenzo-p-dioxin (TCDD, dioxin) is resistant to degradation and due to its lipophilic nature, accumulates in the fat tissue and in the food chain. Human and animal exposure to TCDD affects levels of the steroid receptors and steroid-responsive gene expression and has an impact on metabolism and serum transport of steroids.    Signaling description: The effects of dioxin exposure are mediated by binding to the cytosolic aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR). Upon ligand binding, the receptor translocates to the nucleus and heterodimerizes with aryl hydrocarbon nuclear translocator (ARNT). AHR can affect cellular signaling through interactions with various regulatory and signaling proteins, including those mediated by the estrogen receptors (ESR1/2) and NF-kB. Activated AHR complexes associate directly with ESR1 and ESR2 in the absence of estrogen resulting in transcriptional activation of canonically estrogen-dependent genes. The ligand-dependent transcriptional activity of ESR1 has been found to be stimulated by g-synuclein (SNCG); furthermore, human ESR1 requires SNCG for efficient transcriptional activity. AHR also interacts with nuclear factor kappa-B (NF-kB) signaling pathways. The pleiotropic transcription factor, NF-kB has been identified to protect cells from apoptosis. B-cell lymphoma/ leukemia-2 (BCL2) is a well-known anti-apoptotic signaling protein. In normal endometrium, BCL2 demonstrates cyclical expression decreasing during the menstrual and late proliferative phases, indicating hormonal regulation. However, this regulation is lost in endometriosis.Dioxins via AHR induce estrogen-metabolizing enzymes CYP1A1 and CYP1B1. Glycodelin (PAEP) is an endometrium-derived protein known for its angiogenic, immunosuppressive, and contraceptive effects. It is up-regulated by AHR and impairs binding sperm to the egg making impact into dioxin - and endometriosis- associated infertility."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Environmental toxicants can act as endocrine disrupters on the female reproductive system. 2,3,7,8-Tetrachlorodibenzo-p-dioxin (TCDD, dioxin) is resistant to degradation and due to its lipophilic nature, accumulates in the fat tissue and in the food chain. Human and animal exposure to TCDD affects levels of the steroid receptors and steroid-responsive gene expression and has an impact on metabolism and serum transport of steroids.    Signaling description: The effects of dioxin exposure are mediated by binding to the cytosolic aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR). Upon ligand binding, the receptor translocates to the nucleus and heterodimerizes with aryl hydrocarbon nuclear translocator (ARNT). AHR can affect cellular signaling through interactions with various regulatory and signaling proteins, including those mediated by the estrogen receptors (ESR1/2) and NF-kB. Activated AHR complexes associate directly with ESR1 and ESR2 in the absence of estrogen resulting in transcriptional activation of canonically estrogen-dependent genes. The ligand-dependent transcriptional activity of ESR1 has been found to be stimulated by g-synuclein (SNCG); furthermore, human ESR1 requires SNCG for efficient transcriptional activity. AHR also interacts with nuclear factor kappa-B (NF-kB) signaling pathways. The pleiotropic transcription factor, NF-kB has been identified to protect cells from apoptosis. B-cell lymphoma/ leukemia-2 (BCL2) is a well-known anti-apoptotic signaling protein. In normal endometrium, BCL2 demonstrates cyclical expression decreasing during the menstrual and late proliferative phases, indicating hormonal regulation. However, this regulation is lost in endometriosis.Dioxins via AHR induce estrogen-metabolizing enzymes CYP1A1 and CYP1B1. Glycodelin (PAEP) is an endometrium-derived protein known for its angiogenic, immunosuppressive, and contraceptive effects. It is up-regulated by AHR and impairs binding sperm to the egg making impact into dioxin - and endometriosis- associated infertility." EXACT []

