xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Retinoic acid (RA) plays an important role in normal function of the human endometrium. RA may promote survival or apoptosis of cells, depending on the levels of binding proteins: apoptosis-inducing cellular RA binding protein 2 (CRABP2) and cell survival-promoting fatty acid binding protein 5 (FABP5). Endometriotic tissues and cells from women with endometriosis are characterized by altered expression of genes involved in the regulation of synthesis and action of RA.    Signaling description: CRABP2 directs RA toward RARA, which results in growth arrest and apoptosis, whereas FABP5 directs RA to bind PPARD, which promotes expression of pro-survival genes. In endometriosis, there is a decrease of the expression of CRABP2 but not FABP5. Endometriosis is also associated with a greatly reduced expression of a gene regulating the cellular uptake of retinol (STRA6) and therefore decreased the availability of the substrate for RA synthesis. Progesterone receptor (PGR) is necessary for high STRA6 and CRABP2 expression in endometrial stromal cells. PGR is down-regulated by ESR2. The retinoic acid catabolic enzyme CYP26A1, is a progesterone responsive gene that is upregulated in women with endometriosis. CYP26A1 inactivates retinoic acid and contributes to establishment of endometriotic lesions.HSD17B2 catalyzes the conversion of estradiol (E2) to estrone (E1) and is downregulated in endometriosis due to low expression of PGR. The result is deficient metabolism of E2 in endometriotic tissues giving rise to high local concentrations of E2.    Outcome effects: The consequent decrease of the ratio of CRABP2 to FABP5 may be important in directing actions of RA toward increasing growth and reducing apoptosis of endometriotic tissues."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Retinoic acid (RA) plays an important role in normal function of the human endometrium. RA may promote survival or apoptosis of cells, depending on the levels of binding proteins: apoptosis-inducing cellular RA binding protein 2 (CRABP2) and cell survival-promoting fatty acid binding protein 5 (FABP5). Endometriotic tissues and cells from women with endometriosis are characterized by altered expression of genes involved in the regulation of synthesis and action of RA.    Signaling description: CRABP2 directs RA toward RARA, which results in growth arrest and apoptosis, whereas FABP5 directs RA to bind PPARD, which promotes expression of pro-survival genes. In endometriosis, there is a decrease of the expression of CRABP2 but not FABP5. Endometriosis is also associated with a greatly reduced expression of a gene regulating the cellular uptake of retinol (STRA6) and therefore decreased the availability of the substrate for RA synthesis. Progesterone receptor (PGR) is necessary for high STRA6 and CRABP2 expression in endometrial stromal cells. PGR is down-regulated by ESR2. The retinoic acid catabolic enzyme CYP26A1, is a progesterone responsive gene that is upregulated in women with endometriosis. CYP26A1 inactivates retinoic acid and contributes to establishment of endometriotic lesions.HSD17B2 catalyzes the conversion of estradiol (E2) to estrone (E1) and is downregulated in endometriosis due to low expression of PGR. The result is deficient metabolism of E2 in endometriotic tissues giving rise to high local concentrations of E2.    Outcome effects: The consequent decrease of the ratio of CRABP2 to FABP5 may be important in directing actions of RA toward increasing growth and reducing apoptosis of endometriotic tissues." EXACT []

