xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Endometrial ovarian carcinomas (EOC) or endometrioid adenocarcinomas of the ovary are rare ovarian tumors (less than 15% of ovarian carcinomas) with histological semblance to endometrial carcinomas of the uterus. Approximately, 5-10% of EOC cases are associated with endometriosis. A pathologist must differentiate EOCs from mucinous ovarian carcinomas and since squamous differentiation is very rare in mucinous tumors, this feature would favor an EOC.    Signaling description: EOC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, BRAF, PTEN, and ARID1A genes. It has also been shown that the overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2), FBXO5, and LPARs are associated with EOC. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to the intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs activate PIK3CA through SRC. In turn, PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT-mediated signaling, and thus, mutations in the PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1) protein, which then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD, which leads to BCL2 and BCL2L1 activation and the suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and apoptotic caspase cascade activation. EGFRs regulates Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in cell growth. EGFRs also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which in turn activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. LPARs (lysophosphatidic acid receptor 2/3) promote AKT1 the activation. Anaphase promoting complex (APC/C) is essentially required to exit from mitosis as it induces proteolysis of diverse cell cycle regulators. The activation of APC/C is mediated by CDC20, while FBXO5 is a negative regulator of CDC20. The overexpression of FBXO5 inhibits APC/C activation and induces cell proliferation. AT rich interactive domain 1A (ARID1A) is a component of the SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex, which also includes SMARCA4, ACTB, ACTL6A, ACTL6B, ARID1B, and SMARCD"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Endometrial ovarian carcinomas (EOC) or endometrioid adenocarcinomas of the ovary are rare ovarian tumors (less than 15% of ovarian carcinomas) with histological semblance to endometrial carcinomas of the uterus. Approximately, 5-10% of EOC cases are associated with endometriosis. A pathologist must differentiate EOCs from mucinous ovarian carcinomas and since squamous differentiation is very rare in mucinous tumors, this feature would favor an EOC.    Signaling description: EOC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, BRAF, PTEN, and ARID1A genes. It has also been shown that the overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2), FBXO5, and LPARs are associated with EOC. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to the intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs activate PIK3CA through SRC. In turn, PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT-mediated signaling, and thus, mutations in the PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1) protein, which then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD, which leads to BCL2 and BCL2L1 activation and the suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and apoptotic caspase cascade activation. EGFRs regulates Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in cell growth. EGFRs also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which in turn activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. LPARs (lysophosphatidic acid receptor 2/3) promote AKT1 the activation. Anaphase promoting complex (APC/C) is essentially required to exit from mitosis as it induces proteolysis of diverse cell cycle regulators. The activation of APC/C is mediated by CDC20, while FBXO5 is a negative regulator of CDC20. The overexpression of FBXO5 inhibits APC/C activation and induces cell proliferation. AT rich interactive domain 1A (ARID1A) is a component of the SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex, which also includes SMARCA4, ACTB, ACTL6A, ACTL6B, ARID1B, and SMARCD" EXACT []

