xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Clear cell ovarian carcinoma (CCOC) is also known as clear cell adenocarcinoma and clear cell mesonephroma. Approximately 6% of ovarian cancers are clear cell carcinoma, and this histological subtype is one of the most aggressive forms of ovarian cancer. CCOC is generally resistant to standard therapy.    Signaling description: CGOC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, BRAF, PTEN, and ARID1A genes. In addition, the overexpression of PDGFA/B, MET, HGF, PPM1D, and FBXO5 are also associated with CCOC. The platelet-derived growth factor receptors (PDGFR alpha/beta) are transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinases that are activated by platelet-derived growth factors (PDGFs). The met proto-oncogene (MET) is a transmembrane hepatocyte growth factor receptor. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound MET activates PIK3CA, which activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses the AKT-mediated signaling, and mutations in PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1. In addition, PDGFRs and MET mediate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2), which regulate cell growth. PDGFRs and MET also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the CTNNB1 stabilization. CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD, whuch leads to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and apoptotic caspase cascade activation. Further, the oncogenic PPM1D protein is an important regulator in the DNA damage pathway. PPM1D dephosphorylates and inactivates ATM, which then phosphorylates CHEK1 and CHEK2, required for checkpoint mediated cell cycle arrest. CHEK1 and CHEK2 regulate TP53 and BRCA1 activity, and BRCA1 plays a central role in DNA repair. Anaphase promoting complex (APC/C) is required to exit from mitosis, as it induces proteolysis of different cell cycle regulators. The activation of APC/C is mediated by CDC20, while FBXO5 is a negative regulator of CDC20. The overexpression of FBXO5 inhibits the activation of APC/C and induces cell proliferation. AT rich interactive domain 1A (ARID1A) is a component of SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex which also includes SMARCA4, ACTB, ACTL6A, ACTL6B, ARID1B, and SMARCD1/SMARCD2/SMARCD3/SMARCC1/SMARCC2/SMARCB1. Mutations in ARID1A result in the dysfunction of chromatin remodeling.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red an"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Clear cell ovarian carcinoma (CCOC) is also known as clear cell adenocarcinoma and clear cell mesonephroma. Approximately 6% of ovarian cancers are clear cell carcinoma, and this histological subtype is one of the most aggressive forms of ovarian cancer. CCOC is generally resistant to standard therapy.    Signaling description: CGOC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, BRAF, PTEN, and ARID1A genes. In addition, the overexpression of PDGFA/B, MET, HGF, PPM1D, and FBXO5 are also associated with CCOC. The platelet-derived growth factor receptors (PDGFR alpha/beta) are transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinases that are activated by platelet-derived growth factors (PDGFs). The met proto-oncogene (MET) is a transmembrane hepatocyte growth factor receptor. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound MET activates PIK3CA, which activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses the AKT-mediated signaling, and mutations in PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1. In addition, PDGFRs and MET mediate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2), which regulate cell growth. PDGFRs and MET also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the CTNNB1 stabilization. CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD, whuch leads to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and apoptotic caspase cascade activation. Further, the oncogenic PPM1D protein is an important regulator in the DNA damage pathway. PPM1D dephosphorylates and inactivates ATM, which then phosphorylates CHEK1 and CHEK2, required for checkpoint mediated cell cycle arrest. CHEK1 and CHEK2 regulate TP53 and BRCA1 activity, and BRCA1 plays a central role in DNA repair. Anaphase promoting complex (APC/C) is required to exit from mitosis, as it induces proteolysis of different cell cycle regulators. The activation of APC/C is mediated by CDC20, while FBXO5 is a negative regulator of CDC20. The overexpression of FBXO5 inhibits the activation of APC/C and induces cell proliferation. AT rich interactive domain 1A (ARID1A) is a component of SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex which also includes SMARCA4, ACTB, ACTL6A, ACTL6B, ARID1B, and SMARCD1/SMARCD2/SMARCD3/SMARCC1/SMARCC2/SMARCB1. Mutations in ARID1A result in the dysfunction of chromatin remodeling.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red an" EXACT []

