xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Breast cancer is cancer that develops from breast tissue. Signs of breast cancer may include a lump in the breast, a change in breast shape, dimpling of the skin, fluid coming from the nipple, or a red scaly patch of skin. Multiple pathways are involved in ovarian cancer pathogenesis, including the EGFR and PTAFR signaling. In ovarian cancer EGFR and PTAFR are cross talking and activating each other leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Signaling description: EGFR is activated by EGF and HBEGF and lead to downstream RAS-MAPK signaling. PTAFR is activated by 1-O-Stearoyl-2-acetyl-sn-glyceryl-3-phosphorylcholine (platelet activating factor) leading to GNAQ - PLCB1 - RAS - MAPK signaling. EGFR and PTAFR are cross talking and activating each other. MAPK1 and MAPK3 may also activate phospholipase A2, leading to hyper production of 1-O-Stearoyl-2-acetyl-sn-glyceryl-3-phosphorylcholine and forming a vicious circle. Proteins EGF, HBEGF, EGFR and PTAFR with red highlights have increased expression in ovarian cancer cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Breast cancer is cancer that develops from breast tissue. Signs of breast cancer may include a lump in the breast, a change in breast shape, dimpling of the skin, fluid coming from the nipple, or a red scaly patch of skin. Multiple pathways are involved in ovarian cancer pathogenesis, including the EGFR and PTAFR signaling. In ovarian cancer EGFR and PTAFR are cross talking and activating each other leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Signaling description: EGFR is activated by EGF and HBEGF and lead to downstream RAS-MAPK signaling. PTAFR is activated by 1-O-Stearoyl-2-acetyl-sn-glyceryl-3-phosphorylcholine (platelet activating factor) leading to GNAQ - PLCB1 - RAS - MAPK signaling. EGFR and PTAFR are cross talking and activating each other. MAPK1 and MAPK3 may also activate phospholipase A2, leading to hyper production of 1-O-Stearoyl-2-acetyl-sn-glyceryl-3-phosphorylcholine and forming a vicious circle. Proteins EGF, HBEGF, EGFR and PTAFR with red highlights have increased expression in ovarian cancer cells." EXACT []

