xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. High-grade serous ovarian carcinoma (HGSOC) is the most common and lethal form of epithelial ovarian cancer. HGSOC is a highly aggressive histotype of ovarian cancer that exhibits distinct genetic instability.    Signaling description: HGSOC is characterized by mutations in TP53, BRCA1, and BRCA2 genes with no characteristic mutations in KRAS, BRAF, or PTEN genes. In addition, it has been shown that overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2), VEGFRs, LPARs, NTRK2, and NOTCH3 is associated with HGSOC. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members isuch as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). The family of vascular endothelial growth factor receptors (VEGFRs) includes fms-related tyrosine kinase 1/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 1 (FLT1/VEGFR1), kinase insert domain receptor/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (KDR/VEGFR2), and fms-related tyrosine kinase 4/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 3 (FLT4/VEGFR3). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs, VEGFRs and NTRK2 activate PIK3CA via SRC. In turn, PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. In addition, LPARs (lysophosphatidic acid receptor 2/3) regulate AKT1 activation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival which responds to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Furthwer, activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and the JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD leading to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of the apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs and VEGFRs regulate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in the regulation of cell growth. In addition, EGFRs interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which then activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. Further, NOTCH signaling is a crucial regulator of multiple cellular processes including differentiation, proliferation, motility, and survival. NOTCH3 receptors interact with surface-bound ligands JAG1, JAG2, DLL1, DLL3, and DLL4. Activated membrane-bound NOTCH3 receptor is cleaved by gamma-secretase complex. NOTCH intracellular domain (NICD) is transported into the nucleus and participates in the formation of the NICD-"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. High-grade serous ovarian carcinoma (HGSOC) is the most common and lethal form of epithelial ovarian cancer. HGSOC is a highly aggressive histotype of ovarian cancer that exhibits distinct genetic instability.    Signaling description: HGSOC is characterized by mutations in TP53, BRCA1, and BRCA2 genes with no characteristic mutations in KRAS, BRAF, or PTEN genes. In addition, it has been shown that overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2), VEGFRs, LPARs, NTRK2, and NOTCH3 is associated with HGSOC. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members isuch as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). The family of vascular endothelial growth factor receptors (VEGFRs) includes fms-related tyrosine kinase 1/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 1 (FLT1/VEGFR1), kinase insert domain receptor/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (KDR/VEGFR2), and fms-related tyrosine kinase 4/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 3 (FLT4/VEGFR3). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs, VEGFRs and NTRK2 activate PIK3CA via SRC. In turn, PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. In addition, LPARs (lysophosphatidic acid receptor 2/3) regulate AKT1 activation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival which responds to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Furthwer, activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and the JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD leading to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of the apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs and VEGFRs regulate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in the regulation of cell growth. In addition, EGFRs interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which then activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. Further, NOTCH signaling is a crucial regulator of multiple cellular processes including differentiation, proliferation, motility, and survival. NOTCH3 receptors interact with surface-bound ligands JAG1, JAG2, DLL1, DLL3, and DLL4. Activated membrane-bound NOTCH3 receptor is cleaved by gamma-secretase complex. NOTCH intracellular domain (NICD) is transported into the nucleus and participates in the formation of the NICD-" EXACT []

