xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Low-grade serous ovarian carcinoma (LGSOC) represents approximately 10% of all serous ovarian carcinomas. There are a number of important differences between LGSOC and high-grade serous ovarian carcinomas in their clinical and molecular characteristics. Patients with LGSOC are diagnosed at a younger age, have a longer overall survival, and have lower response rates to conventional chemotherapy.    Signaling description: LGSOC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, BRAF, and PTEN genes in addition to the overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2) and NOTCH3. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs activate PIK3CA via SRC. In turn, PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT-mediated signaling; thus, mutations in the PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival, which responds to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus, where it interacts with T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and the JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD leading to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of the apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs regulate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in the regulation of cell growth. In addition, EGFRs interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which in turn activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. NOTCH signaling is a crucial regulator of multiple cellular processes including differentiation, proliferation, motility, and survival. NOTCH3 receptors interact with surface-bound ligands JAG1, JAG2, DLL1, DLL3, and DLL4. Activated membrane-bound NOTCH3 receptor is cleaved by the gamma-secretase complex. NOTCH intracellular domain (NICD) is transported into the nucleus and participates in the formation of the NICD-Mastermind complex by interacting with mastermind-like 1/3 proteins (MAML1/3). NICD-Mastermind complex promotes transcription factors MYC and HES1 and binds TP53. HES1 inhibits CDKN1B, which participates in the regulation of cell proliferation and differentiation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Low-grade serous ovarian carcinoma (LGSOC) represents approximately 10% of all serous ovarian carcinomas. There are a number of important differences between LGSOC and high-grade serous ovarian carcinomas in their clinical and molecular characteristics. Patients with LGSOC are diagnosed at a younger age, have a longer overall survival, and have lower response rates to conventional chemotherapy.    Signaling description: LGSOC is characterized by mutations in KRAS, BRAF, and PTEN genes in addition to the overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2) and NOTCH3. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs activate PIK3CA via SRC. In turn, PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT-mediated signaling; thus, mutations in the PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival, which responds to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 protein then translocates into the nucleus, where it interacts with T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and the JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD leading to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of the apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs regulate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in the regulation of cell growth. In addition, EGFRs interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF, which in turn activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation. NOTCH signaling is a crucial regulator of multiple cellular processes including differentiation, proliferation, motility, and survival. NOTCH3 receptors interact with surface-bound ligands JAG1, JAG2, DLL1, DLL3, and DLL4. Activated membrane-bound NOTCH3 receptor is cleaved by the gamma-secretase complex. NOTCH intracellular domain (NICD) is transported into the nucleus and participates in the formation of the NICD-Mastermind complex by interacting with mastermind-like 1/3 proteins (MAML1/3). NICD-Mastermind complex promotes transcription factors MYC and HES1 and binds TP53. HES1 inhibits CDKN1B, which participates in the regulation of cell proliferation and differentiation." EXACT []

