xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Mucinous ovarian carcinomas (MOC) share some similarities with serous ovarian carcinomas. MOC develop slowly and unexpectedly transform from benign precursors.    Signaling description: MOC is characterized primarily by mutations in the KRAS gene, and mutations in BRAF and PTEN genes may also rarely occur. In addition, the overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2) is also associated with MOC. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs activate PIK3CA via SRC. PIK3CA then activates V- AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT-mediated signaling, and mutations in the PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. Namely, AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2, and MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival, which responds to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 protein then translocates to the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and the JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD leading to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of the apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs regulates Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in the regulation of cell growth. EGFRs also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ovarian cancer is the second most common malignancy among the gynecological cancers and the most common cause of cancer death, yet the etiology of ovarian malignant transformation remains poorly understood. Different types of ovarian cancer include serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, undifferentiated, and unclassifiable. Mucinous ovarian carcinomas (MOC) share some similarities with serous ovarian carcinomas. MOC develop slowly and unexpectedly transform from benign precursors.    Signaling description: MOC is characterized primarily by mutations in the KRAS gene, and mutations in BRAF and PTEN genes may also rarely occur. In addition, the overexpression of EGFRs (EGFR and ERBB2) is also associated with MOC. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases includes four related members such as epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR/HER1), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2 (ERBB2, HER2/neu), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homologs 3 and 4 (ERBB3/HER3 and ERBB4/HER4). These receptors couple extracellular growth factor ligands to intracellular signaling pathways and, thus regulate cell processes including proliferation, differentiation, cell migration, and survival. The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades leading to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, hyperplastic growth, and migration of ovarian epithelial cells. Specifically, ligand-bound EGFRs activate PIK3CA via SRC. PIK3CA then activates V- AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1. Normally, PTEN dephosphorylates PIP3 and suppresses AKT-mediated signaling, and mutations in the PTEN gene lead to the constitutive activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. Namely, AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR, while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2, and MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival, which responds to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 protein then translocates to the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3), mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), and the JNK-cascade. In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD leading to the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of the apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs regulates Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) involved in the regulation of cell growth. EGFRs also interact with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activate BRAF which activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

