xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) and lutenising hormone (LH) are produced in the anterior lobe of pituitary gland and their function in gonads is mediated over distinct FSH and LH receptors (FSHR and LHCGR), respectively. LH receptors are found on both granulosa and thecal cells; the expression of FSH receptors is restricted to the granulose cells. Loss of function mutations in these receptors cause gonadotropin resistance with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Mutations of beta subunits of FSH or LH (FSHB and LHB respectively) also cause hypogonadism and infertility.    Signaling description: LH stimulates female progesterone synthesis and ovulation and also theca cell androgen production. FSH is required for follicle maturation and stimulation of ovarian estrogen production. During the process of folliculogenesis, the conversion of cholesterol into ovarian hormones takes place. Conversion of steroids to androgens occurs within the theca cells. Androgens and are converted to estrogens by aromatase enzyme (CYP19A1) in the granulosa cells. NR5A1 is a key transcriptional regulator involved in production of key steroidogenic enzymes and transporters, including STAR, CYP11A1, CYP17A1, and CYP19A. Mutations in the NR5A1 have also been associated with impairment of ovarian development and function. CYP19A1 mutation causes follicle maturation arrest and subsequently ovarian dysgenesis. FOXL2 is involved in the development of granulosa cells and controls CYP19A. If mutated, it may cause female infertility. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) and lutenising hormone (LH) are produced in the anterior lobe of pituitary gland and their function in gonads is mediated over distinct FSH and LH receptors (FSHR and LHCGR), respectively. LH receptors are found on both granulosa and thecal cells; the expression of FSH receptors is restricted to the granulose cells. Loss of function mutations in these receptors cause gonadotropin resistance with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Mutations of beta subunits of FSH or LH (FSHB and LHB respectively) also cause hypogonadism and infertility.    Signaling description: LH stimulates female progesterone synthesis and ovulation and also theca cell androgen production. FSH is required for follicle maturation and stimulation of ovarian estrogen production. During the process of folliculogenesis, the conversion of cholesterol into ovarian hormones takes place. Conversion of steroids to androgens occurs within the theca cells. Androgens and are converted to estrogens by aromatase enzyme (CYP19A1) in the granulosa cells. NR5A1 is a key transcriptional regulator involved in production of key steroidogenic enzymes and transporters, including STAR, CYP11A1, CYP17A1, and CYP19A. Mutations in the NR5A1 have also been associated with impairment of ovarian development and function. CYP19A1 mutation causes follicle maturation arrest and subsequently ovarian dysgenesis. FOXL2 is involved in the development of granulosa cells and controls CYP19A. If mutated, it may cause female infertility. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

