xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Inhibins plays important role in regulating ovarian function either as a negative modulators of pituitary FSH synthesis or as a paracrine factor.     Signaling description: Inhibin consists of alpha (a) and beta (b) subunits. The b-subunit is of two types, A and B. Thus there are two forms of inhibin: inhibin A (a-bA) and inhibin B (a-bB). Inhibins are structurally related to activins. Activin is a homodimer of two b subunits, which antagonizes the effect of inhibin. The genes INHA, INHBA and INHBB encode for inhibin a, inhibin bA and inhibin bB subunits, respectively. The gene products of INHA, INHBA and INHBB are restricted to granulosa cells from the primordial follicle stage. If there is any mutation in the INHA gene, the association of a and b will be severely diminished, the homodimerization of two b subunits will take place dominantly. In other words, the concentration of inhibin will diminish and concentration of activins will rise and ultimately FSH concentration will rise.    Outcome effects: High FSH level resulting in increased number of primordial follicles being recruited for folliculogenesis, enhanced follicular development to antral stages with increased granulosa cell proliferation, cumulus cell differentiation and expansion, and a dysregulation in steroid hormone production at the theca and granulosa cell level. This condition may also lead to the rapid depletion of follicles, causing premature ovarian failure.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Inhibins plays important role in regulating ovarian function either as a negative modulators of pituitary FSH synthesis or as a paracrine factor.     Signaling description: Inhibin consists of alpha (a) and beta (b) subunits. The b-subunit is of two types, A and B. Thus there are two forms of inhibin: inhibin A (a-bA) and inhibin B (a-bB). Inhibins are structurally related to activins. Activin is a homodimer of two b subunits, which antagonizes the effect of inhibin. The genes INHA, INHBA and INHBB encode for inhibin a, inhibin bA and inhibin bB subunits, respectively. The gene products of INHA, INHBA and INHBB are restricted to granulosa cells from the primordial follicle stage. If there is any mutation in the INHA gene, the association of a and b will be severely diminished, the homodimerization of two b subunits will take place dominantly. In other words, the concentration of inhibin will diminish and concentration of activins will rise and ultimately FSH concentration will rise.    Outcome effects: High FSH level resulting in increased number of primordial follicles being recruited for folliculogenesis, enhanced follicular development to antral stages with increased granulosa cell proliferation, cumulus cell differentiation and expansion, and a dysregulation in steroid hormone production at the theca and granulosa cell level. This condition may also lead to the rapid depletion of follicles, causing premature ovarian failure.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

