xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a disease generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. Impaired steroidogenesis, particularly excessive androgen production, is one of the main features in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).    Signaling description: Normally, androgens are synthesized in the theca cells of ovarian follicles under the exposure to luteinizing hormone (LH) to be later converted into estrogens by CYP19A1 in granulosa cells. In PCOS, elevated LH or LH/FSH ratio is observed. Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GNRH1) has increased amplitude of secretion in the pathology. The hormone provokes LH release in the pituitary and elevated LH triggers steroid hormone synthesis by binding to its receptor, LHCGR. It promotes STAR involvement in the process through PRKAR1A activation. STAR is a protein, which transports cholesterol (the substrate for hormone synthesis) into the mitochondrion where the process occurs. Several proteins, including CYP11A1, CYP17A1, HSD3B2, and HSD17B1, are involved in the subsequent conversion of cholesterol into testosterone in the theca cells. In addition, insulin (INS) synergistically increases LH action. INS induces the expression of steroidogenic enzymes through the activation of NR5A1 transcription factor and also promotes LDLR synthesis in the theca cells. LDLR transports cholesterol into the cell. IGF1 and IGF2 are involved in PCOS pathogenesis by activating the expression of steroidogenic enzymes through MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway. RBP4, which is also elevated in PCOS, delivers retinol to the follicle. Retinol uptake into the cell, where it contributes to CYP17A1 expression, occurs via STRA6. The next step of steroidogenesis in women occurs in granulosa cells where testosterone and its derivatives are converted into estrogens. Normally, the conversion is promoted by CYP19A1. The lack of the enzyme due to a deficiency of stimulatory signals from FSH leads to androgen excess. In some cases, testosterone may be converted into dihydrotestosterone by SRD5A1, when the protein is elevated.    Outcome effects: The excess of androgens, particularly dihydrotestosterone, explain hirsutism and acne in PCOS women.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a disease generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. Impaired steroidogenesis, particularly excessive androgen production, is one of the main features in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).    Signaling description: Normally, androgens are synthesized in the theca cells of ovarian follicles under the exposure to luteinizing hormone (LH) to be later converted into estrogens by CYP19A1 in granulosa cells. In PCOS, elevated LH or LH/FSH ratio is observed. Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GNRH1) has increased amplitude of secretion in the pathology. The hormone provokes LH release in the pituitary and elevated LH triggers steroid hormone synthesis by binding to its receptor, LHCGR. It promotes STAR involvement in the process through PRKAR1A activation. STAR is a protein, which transports cholesterol (the substrate for hormone synthesis) into the mitochondrion where the process occurs. Several proteins, including CYP11A1, CYP17A1, HSD3B2, and HSD17B1, are involved in the subsequent conversion of cholesterol into testosterone in the theca cells. In addition, insulin (INS) synergistically increases LH action. INS induces the expression of steroidogenic enzymes through the activation of NR5A1 transcription factor and also promotes LDLR synthesis in the theca cells. LDLR transports cholesterol into the cell. IGF1 and IGF2 are involved in PCOS pathogenesis by activating the expression of steroidogenic enzymes through MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway. RBP4, which is also elevated in PCOS, delivers retinol to the follicle. Retinol uptake into the cell, where it contributes to CYP17A1 expression, occurs via STRA6. The next step of steroidogenesis in women occurs in granulosa cells where testosterone and its derivatives are converted into estrogens. Normally, the conversion is promoted by CYP19A1. The lack of the enzyme due to a deficiency of stimulatory signals from FSH leads to androgen excess. In some cases, testosterone may be converted into dihydrotestosterone by SRD5A1, when the protein is elevated.    Outcome effects: The excess of androgens, particularly dihydrotestosterone, explain hirsutism and acne in PCOS women.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

