xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. Anovulation or block of ovulation in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is due to the disruption of the two stages of follicular development. The first is excessive follicular growth and the second is violation of the selection of one of the follicles from the increased pool and its further maturation into a dominant follicle.    Signaling description: Normally, FSH promotes folliculogenesis by means of granulosa cell proliferation and differentiation, which surround the oocyte. In the pathology, signaling is blocked by AMH, estrogens, FST, and inhibin complex. The arrested signaling includes the PI3K, PKC, and PKA routes, which normally lead to proliferation and differentiation of granulosa cells. PLCB1 provokes Ca2+-dependent PKC activation and affects BRAF, which promotes the MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway. The pathway recruits CREB1, FOS, and SRF transcription factors, which initiate cell division. In addition, the MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway, along with PKA, promotes GATA4 activation. GATA4 and SRF are responsible for cell differentiation. The impaired FSH signaling prevents AKT1 activation, which normally inhibits FOXO1, a negative regulator of cell proliferation and differentiation. The excess of androgens bind to their intracellular receptor (AR) and trigger IGF1R appearance on granulosa cell surfaces. Elevated IGF1 binds to its receptor, which induces PI3K activation. PI3K promotes AKT1 phosphorylation of CDKN1B, BAD, and GSK3B resulting in their inhibition. These proteins normally promote granulosa cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: Granulosa cells are necessary for appropriate follicle development, thus the arrest of their proliferation and differentiation stops follicle growth with this being one of the major causes of anovulation in PCOS.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level of activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level of activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. Anovulation or block of ovulation in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is due to the disruption of the two stages of follicular development. The first is excessive follicular growth and the second is violation of the selection of one of the follicles from the increased pool and its further maturation into a dominant follicle.    Signaling description: Normally, FSH promotes folliculogenesis by means of granulosa cell proliferation and differentiation, which surround the oocyte. In the pathology, signaling is blocked by AMH, estrogens, FST, and inhibin complex. The arrested signaling includes the PI3K, PKC, and PKA routes, which normally lead to proliferation and differentiation of granulosa cells. PLCB1 provokes Ca2+-dependent PKC activation and affects BRAF, which promotes the MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway. The pathway recruits CREB1, FOS, and SRF transcription factors, which initiate cell division. In addition, the MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway, along with PKA, promotes GATA4 activation. GATA4 and SRF are responsible for cell differentiation. The impaired FSH signaling prevents AKT1 activation, which normally inhibits FOXO1, a negative regulator of cell proliferation and differentiation. The excess of androgens bind to their intracellular receptor (AR) and trigger IGF1R appearance on granulosa cell surfaces. Elevated IGF1 binds to its receptor, which induces PI3K activation. PI3K promotes AKT1 phosphorylation of CDKN1B, BAD, and GSK3B resulting in their inhibition. These proteins normally promote granulosa cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: Granulosa cells are necessary for appropriate follicle development, thus the arrest of their proliferation and differentiation stops follicle growth with this being one of the major causes of anovulation in PCOS.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level of activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level of activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

