xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a disease generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. The origin of hyperandrogenemia arises from excessive luteinizing hormone (LH) stimulation of follicle theca cells.    Signaling description: SHBG and IGFBP1 are proteins involved in the pathogenesis of polycystic ovary syndrome. SHBG is a key circulating protein, which controls the bioavailability of testosterone; whereas IGFBP1 is the protein, which binds free IGF1, thus preventing its action. Insulin (INS) can augment ovarian steroidogenesis indirectly by reducing the production of IGFBP1 in the liver and therefore elevating the levels of free IGF1 and reducing hepatic biosynthesis of SHBG. The mechanism of SHBG downregulation remains elusive. The mechanism of insulin-induced inhibition of IGFBP1 synthesis includes the initiation of the PI3K pathway leading to AKT1 activation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits FOXO1 which is a positive regulator of IGFBP1 transcription.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a disease generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. The origin of hyperandrogenemia arises from excessive luteinizing hormone (LH) stimulation of follicle theca cells.    Signaling description: SHBG and IGFBP1 are proteins involved in the pathogenesis of polycystic ovary syndrome. SHBG is a key circulating protein, which controls the bioavailability of testosterone; whereas IGFBP1 is the protein, which binds free IGF1, thus preventing its action. Insulin (INS) can augment ovarian steroidogenesis indirectly by reducing the production of IGFBP1 in the liver and therefore elevating the levels of free IGF1 and reducing hepatic biosynthesis of SHBG. The mechanism of SHBG downregulation remains elusive. The mechanism of insulin-induced inhibition of IGFBP1 synthesis includes the initiation of the PI3K pathway leading to AKT1 activation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits FOXO1 which is a positive regulator of IGFBP1 transcription.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

