xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a disease generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. Impaired steroidogenesis, particularly excessive androgen production, is one of the main features in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).    Signaling description: Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) controls granulosa cell proliferation and differentiation and thus promotes folliculogenesis. The hormone also participates in steroidogenesis. The FSH hormone signaling promotes AMH expression, and the elevated protein in turn reduces the FSH receptor mRNA expression and inhibits the signaling. In polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), FSH signaling in granulosa cells activates cell proliferation and differentiation of many follicles at the earliest stages of their development. The signaling is blocked at the stage of small preantral follicle due to elevated AMH and estrogen, which block the FSH synthesis.    Outcome effects: The FSH signaling includes PI3K, PKC, and PKA activation, which normally leads to proliferation and differentiation of granulosa cells. PLCB1 provokes Ca2+-dependent PKC activation and affects BRAF, which promotes the MAPK1/MAPK3 signaling route. The pathway recruits CREB1, FOS, and SRF transcription factors, which initiate cell division. In addition, the MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway, along with PKA, promotes GATA4 activation. GATA4 and SRF are responsible for cell differentiation. FSH signaling, through PI3K, leads to AKT1 activation which inhibits FOXO1, a negative regulator of cell proliferation and differentiation. FSH plays an important role in steroidogenesis through the activation of GATA4 and CREB1.    Outcome effects:     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a disease generally characterized by hyperandrogenemia, anovulation/oligoovulation, and polycystic ovary. Impaired steroidogenesis, particularly excessive androgen production, is one of the main features in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).    Signaling description: Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) controls granulosa cell proliferation and differentiation and thus promotes folliculogenesis. The hormone also participates in steroidogenesis. The FSH hormone signaling promotes AMH expression, and the elevated protein in turn reduces the FSH receptor mRNA expression and inhibits the signaling. In polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), FSH signaling in granulosa cells activates cell proliferation and differentiation of many follicles at the earliest stages of their development. The signaling is blocked at the stage of small preantral follicle due to elevated AMH and estrogen, which block the FSH synthesis.    Outcome effects: The FSH signaling includes PI3K, PKC, and PKA activation, which normally leads to proliferation and differentiation of granulosa cells. PLCB1 provokes Ca2+-dependent PKC activation and affects BRAF, which promotes the MAPK1/MAPK3 signaling route. The pathway recruits CREB1, FOS, and SRF transcription factors, which initiate cell division. In addition, the MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway, along with PKA, promotes GATA4 activation. GATA4 and SRF are responsible for cell differentiation. FSH signaling, through PI3K, leads to AKT1 activation which inhibits FOXO1, a negative regulator of cell proliferation and differentiation. FSH plays an important role in steroidogenesis through the activation of GATA4 and CREB1.    Outcome effects:     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

