xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The trophoblast is the peripheral part of the human conceptus, which exerts a crucial role in implantation and placentation. Antiphospholipid antibodies (aPL) increase the risk of recurrent miscarriage, intrauterine growth restriction and preterm birth. It is associated with decreased trophoblast viability, syncytialization and invasion, deranged production of hormones and signalling molecules by trophoblasts, as well as activation of coagulation and complement pathways.     Signaling description: aPL are preferentially targeted to the trophoblast cells of the placenta where they activate complement through the classical pathway. The complement cascade is initiated; C3 and, subsequently, C5 are activated. C5a is generated, which attracts and activates neutrophils and monocytes and stimulates the release of inflammatory mediators, including reactive oxidants, proteolytic enzymes, chemokines and cytokines, which results in further influx of inflammatory cells and, ultimately, fetal injury.Intraplacental thrombosis is considered one of the main pathogenic mechanism mediating foetal loss in antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). This (Hypothesis) of placental damage was supported by the finding of thrombosis and infarction in placentas from women with APS and by the demonstration of aPL capability to induce a procoagulant state in vitro through several mechanisms including the ability of the aPL antibodies (specifically, Aanti-beta-2GPI / anti-APOH antibodies) to disrupt the anticoagulant annexin A5 (ANXA5) shield on trophoblast and endothelial cell monolayers. Normally ANXA5 forms a shield around negatively-charged phospholipid molecules, thus reducing their availability for coagulation. Outcome Effects; anti-ANXA5 antibodies increase phospholipid-dependent coagulation steps. ANXA5 expression is decreased on trophoblasts of preeclamptic placentas."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The trophoblast is the peripheral part of the human conceptus, which exerts a crucial role in implantation and placentation. Antiphospholipid antibodies (aPL) increase the risk of recurrent miscarriage, intrauterine growth restriction and preterm birth. It is associated with decreased trophoblast viability, syncytialization and invasion, deranged production of hormones and signalling molecules by trophoblasts, as well as activation of coagulation and complement pathways.     Signaling description: aPL are preferentially targeted to the trophoblast cells of the placenta where they activate complement through the classical pathway. The complement cascade is initiated; C3 and, subsequently, C5 are activated. C5a is generated, which attracts and activates neutrophils and monocytes and stimulates the release of inflammatory mediators, including reactive oxidants, proteolytic enzymes, chemokines and cytokines, which results in further influx of inflammatory cells and, ultimately, fetal injury.Intraplacental thrombosis is considered one of the main pathogenic mechanism mediating foetal loss in antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). This (Hypothesis) of placental damage was supported by the finding of thrombosis and infarction in placentas from women with APS and by the demonstration of aPL capability to induce a procoagulant state in vitro through several mechanisms including the ability of the aPL antibodies (specifically, Aanti-beta-2GPI / anti-APOH antibodies) to disrupt the anticoagulant annexin A5 (ANXA5) shield on trophoblast and endothelial cell monolayers. Normally ANXA5 forms a shield around negatively-charged phospholipid molecules, thus reducing their availability for coagulation. Outcome Effects; anti-ANXA5 antibodies increase phospholipid-dependent coagulation steps. ANXA5 expression is decreased on trophoblasts of preeclamptic placentas." EXACT []

