xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer) and the second leading cause of cancer death in women. Men may also develop BC, but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: The WNT/beta-catenin (WNT/CTNNB1) signaling pathway controls various cellular functions including proliferation, differentiation, and migration. In particular, WNT/CTNNB1 signaling was shown to be involved in mammary gland development and mammary oncogenesis. In the absence of WNT proteins, CTNNB1 is associated with a complex which comprises AXIN1, GSK3B, and CSNK1A1. The formation of this complex induces the phosphorylation of CTNNB1 by CSNK1A1 and GSK3B leading to CTNNB1 protein ubiquitination and degradation by the proteasome. When WNT proteins bind to LRP5/6 and FZD7, DVL1 is activated and inhibits GSK3B, which results in the stabilization of cytosolic CTNNB1 protein.    Outcome effects: Further, activated CTNNB1 protein translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor/lymphoid enhancing factor (TCF/LEF) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. It has been demonstrated that FZD7 and LRP6 overexpression plays an essential role in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC). The overexpression of FZD7 and LRP6 causes the constitutive activation of WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway causing loss of differentiation, redundant proliferation, and migration of mammary epithelial cells. In addition, WNT/FZD7 induces breast cancer cell migration and growth via the MAPK-cascade: WNT/FZD7 activates the DVL1/DAAM1 complex, which in turn activates RAC1 and RHOA, which induce the MAPK-cascade.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer) and the second leading cause of cancer death in women. Men may also develop BC, but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: The WNT/beta-catenin (WNT/CTNNB1) signaling pathway controls various cellular functions including proliferation, differentiation, and migration. In particular, WNT/CTNNB1 signaling was shown to be involved in mammary gland development and mammary oncogenesis. In the absence of WNT proteins, CTNNB1 is associated with a complex which comprises AXIN1, GSK3B, and CSNK1A1. The formation of this complex induces the phosphorylation of CTNNB1 by CSNK1A1 and GSK3B leading to CTNNB1 protein ubiquitination and degradation by the proteasome. When WNT proteins bind to LRP5/6 and FZD7, DVL1 is activated and inhibits GSK3B, which results in the stabilization of cytosolic CTNNB1 protein.    Outcome effects: Further, activated CTNNB1 protein translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor/lymphoid enhancing factor (TCF/LEF) to induce the expression of downstream target genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. It has been demonstrated that FZD7 and LRP6 overexpression plays an essential role in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC). The overexpression of FZD7 and LRP6 causes the constitutive activation of WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway causing loss of differentiation, redundant proliferation, and migration of mammary epithelial cells. In addition, WNT/FZD7 induces breast cancer cell migration and growth via the MAPK-cascade: WNT/FZD7 activates the DVL1/DAAM1 complex, which in turn activates RAC1 and RHOA, which induce the MAPK-cascade.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

