xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer). Men may also develop BC but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. BC is a heterogeneous disease. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: ERBB2, EGFR, and VEGFRs play a significant role in breast cancer (BC). The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 signaling which prevents apoptosis and differentiation of mammary epithelial cells in BC and leads to hyperplastic glandular growth. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases consists of four related members including epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2, neuro/glioblastoma derived oncogene homolog (ERBB2), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 3/4 (ERBB3 and ERBB4). The family of vascular endothelial growth factor receptors (VEGFRs) consists of fms-related tyrosine kinase 1/vascular endothelial growth factor 1 (FLT1/VEGFR1), kinase insert domain receptor/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (KDR/VEGFR2), and fms-related tyrosine kinase 4/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 3 (FLT4/VEGFR3). Ligand-bound EGFRs and VEGFRs activate PIK3CA via insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS1). PIK3CA via PIP3 and PDPK1 activates v-akt murine thymoma viral oncogene (AKT1).    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 7 (TCF7) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs and VEGFRs mediate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2), which are involved in cell growth and activate RAF1 via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1. RAF1 activates ERK (MAPK1/3) signaling pathway which induces genes involved in cell proliferation and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer). Men may also develop BC but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. BC is a heterogeneous disease. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: ERBB2, EGFR, and VEGFRs play a significant role in breast cancer (BC). The overexpression of these receptors causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 signaling which prevents apoptosis and differentiation of mammary epithelial cells in BC and leads to hyperplastic glandular growth. The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases consists of four related members including epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR), v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 2, neuro/glioblastoma derived oncogene homolog (ERBB2), and v-erb-b2 erythroblastic leukemia viral oncogene homolog 3/4 (ERBB3 and ERBB4). The family of vascular endothelial growth factor receptors (VEGFRs) consists of fms-related tyrosine kinase 1/vascular endothelial growth factor 1 (FLT1/VEGFR1), kinase insert domain receptor/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (KDR/VEGFR2), and fms-related tyrosine kinase 4/vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 3 (FLT4/VEGFR3). Ligand-bound EGFRs and VEGFRs activate PIK3CA via insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS1). PIK3CA via PIP3 and PDPK1 activates v-akt murine thymoma viral oncogene (AKT1).    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with the T-cell factor 7 (TCF7) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. EGFRs and VEGFRs mediate Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2), which are involved in cell growth and activate RAF1 via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1. RAF1 activates ERK (MAPK1/3) signaling pathway which induces genes involved in cell proliferation and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

