xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer) and the second leading cause of cancer death in women. Men may also develop BC, but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: It has been demonstrated that breast cancer is linked to the overexpression of insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor (IGF1R). The overexpression of IGF1R causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation, and migration of mammary epithelial cells. Ligand-bound IGF1R activates PIK3CA via insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS1). PIK3CA via PIP3 and PDPK1 activates v-akt murine thymoma viral oncogene (AKT1).    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor 7 (TCF7) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. IGF1R mediates Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2), which are involved in cell growth. IGF1R also interacts with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activates RAF1, which in turn activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer) and the second leading cause of cancer death in women. Men may also develop BC, but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: It has been demonstrated that breast cancer is linked to the overexpression of insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor (IGF1R). The overexpression of IGF1R causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation, and migration of mammary epithelial cells. Ligand-bound IGF1R activates PIK3CA via insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS1). PIK3CA via PIP3 and PDPK1 activates v-akt murine thymoma viral oncogene (AKT1).    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor 7 (TCF7) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. IGF1R mediates Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2), which are involved in cell growth. IGF1R also interacts with SHC1/GRB2/SOS1 and activates RAF1, which in turn activates ERK (MAPK1/3). This signaling pathway induces genes involved in cell proliferation and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

