xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer). Men may also develop BC but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. BC is a heterogeneous disease. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: Luminal breast tumors are subdivided into two different types called luminal A and luminal B cancers. Luminal A cancer has ER (estrogen receptor) and/or PR (progesterone receptor) positive, human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2/neu) negative status. Luminal B cancer has ER and/or PR, ERBB2 positive, HER2/neu positive status. Women with luminal B cancer are often diagnosed at a younger age than those with luminal A cancer and have poorer prognosis factors including high tumor grade, larger and more aggressive tumors, and lymph node metastasis. The crucial pathogenic processes involved in luminal BC are cell proliferation, block of apoptosis, cell invasion, chromatin remodeling, dysfunction of DNA repair, and loss of cell differentiation. ESR1/ERBB-positive luminal BC demonstrates overexpression of ESR1 and growth factor receptors such as EGFRs (ERBB1/2), IGF1R, and VEGFRs (FLT1/VEGFR1, KDR/VEGFR2, and FLT4/VEGFR3). In addition, there may be mutations in PTEN (antagonist of PIK3CA and suppressor of AKT-mediated signaling pathways) and DNA repair genes such as BRCA1, BRCA2, PALB2, RAD51, NBN, BRIP1, PARP1, and MLH1. Almost half of all familial breast cancer cases have mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes.    Outcome effects: The overexpression of membrane-bound ESR1, EGFRs, VEGFRs, and IGF1R causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation, and migration of mammary epithelial cells. Nuclear ESR1 induces genes involved in cell proliferation and differentiation. Normally, BRCA1 and BRCA2 play a central role in DNA repair, response to DNA damage, prevention of chromosome breaks, and genomic stability. BRCA1 interacts with DNA mismatch repair protein complex MLH1/MSH2/MSH3/MSH6 and genes that participate in homologous recombination and double-strand break repair such as BRCA2, RAD51, BRIP1, PALB2, MRN complex (RAD50/MRE11A/NBN), and PARP1/2 via NBN. Mutations in these genes lead to block of DNA repair and dysfunction in response to DNA damage. Luminal BC, particularly in post-menopausal women, is responsive to hormones. ESR1 antagonist (Fulvestrant) and selective ESR1 modulators (Tamoxifen and Toremifene) inhibit tumor growth and increase survival. MTOR inhibitors (Everolimus and Temsirolimus), inhibitor of SRC (Dasatinib), inhibitors of EGFRs (Iressa (Gefitinib), Trastuzumab, Lapatinib, and Cetuximab), inhibitor of VEGFA (Bevacizumab), inhibitors of KDR (Bevacizumab and Sutent (Sunitinib)) are used for the treatment of BC.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer). Men may also develop BC but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. BC is a heterogeneous disease. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: Luminal breast tumors are subdivided into two different types called luminal A and luminal B cancers. Luminal A cancer has ER (estrogen receptor) and/or PR (progesterone receptor) positive, human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2/neu) negative status. Luminal B cancer has ER and/or PR, ERBB2 positive, HER2/neu positive status. Women with luminal B cancer are often diagnosed at a younger age than those with luminal A cancer and have poorer prognosis factors including high tumor grade, larger and more aggressive tumors, and lymph node metastasis. The crucial pathogenic processes involved in luminal BC are cell proliferation, block of apoptosis, cell invasion, chromatin remodeling, dysfunction of DNA repair, and loss of cell differentiation. ESR1/ERBB-positive luminal BC demonstrates overexpression of ESR1 and growth factor receptors such as EGFRs (ERBB1/2), IGF1R, and VEGFRs (FLT1/VEGFR1, KDR/VEGFR2, and FLT4/VEGFR3). In addition, there may be mutations in PTEN (antagonist of PIK3CA and suppressor of AKT-mediated signaling pathways) and DNA repair genes such as BRCA1, BRCA2, PALB2, RAD51, NBN, BRIP1, PARP1, and MLH1. Almost half of all familial breast cancer cases have mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes.    Outcome effects: The overexpression of membrane-bound ESR1, EGFRs, VEGFRs, and IGF1R causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation, and migration of mammary epithelial cells. Nuclear ESR1 induces genes involved in cell proliferation and differentiation. Normally, BRCA1 and BRCA2 play a central role in DNA repair, response to DNA damage, prevention of chromosome breaks, and genomic stability. BRCA1 interacts with DNA mismatch repair protein complex MLH1/MSH2/MSH3/MSH6 and genes that participate in homologous recombination and double-strand break repair such as BRCA2, RAD51, BRIP1, PALB2, MRN complex (RAD50/MRE11A/NBN), and PARP1/2 via NBN. Mutations in these genes lead to block of DNA repair and dysfunction in response to DNA damage. Luminal BC, particularly in post-menopausal women, is responsive to hormones. ESR1 antagonist (Fulvestrant) and selective ESR1 modulators (Tamoxifen and Toremifene) inhibit tumor growth and increase survival. MTOR inhibitors (Everolimus and Temsirolimus), inhibitor of SRC (Dasatinib), inhibitors of EGFRs (Iressa (Gefitinib), Trastuzumab, Lapatinib, and Cetuximab), inhibitor of VEGFA (Bevacizumab), inhibitors of KDR (Bevacizumab and Sutent (Sunitinib)) are used for the treatment of BC.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

