xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer). Men may also develop BC but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. BC is a heterogeneous disease. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: The estrogen receptor alpha (ESR1) signaling pathway plays a key role in BC. ESR1 is involved in cell differentiation, apoptosis, and proliferation through diverse mechanisms. The overexpression of membrane-bound ESR1 causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 signaling preventing apoptosis and differentiation of mammary epithelial cells in BC and leads to hyperplastic glandular growth. Nuclear ESR1 induces genes involved in cell proliferation and differentiation. 17beta-estradiol binds and activates membrane-bound ESR1 which activates PIK3CA via PIK3R1. PIK3CA via PIP3 and PDPK1 activates v-akt murine thymoma viral oncogene homologs (AKT1).    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 which regulates the transcription of BCL2 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor/lymphoid enhancing factor (TCF/LEF) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10), and BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. ESR1 also activates PRKCA via PIK3CA/PIP3/PDPK1. PRKCA activates v-raf-1 murine leukemia viral oncogene homolog 1 (RAF1), which activates ERK (MAPK1/3) signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation and migration. ESR1 binds src protein tyrosine kinase (SRC) mediated Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) which are involved in cell growth. Further, ligand-bound cytoplasmic ESR1 moves to the nucleus. 17beta-estradiol/ESR1 co-activator complex recruits transcription factors, SP1- and SP3, which induce the progesterone receptor (PGR) involved in cell differentiation and chromatin remodeling. Nuclear ESR1 interacts with transcription factors, NCOA1/NCOA3/EP300 and forms a transcription complex that binds with KAT2B and JUN/FOS and induces chromatin remodeling and cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Breast cancer (BC) is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women (excluding skin cancer). Men may also develop BC but this accounts for less than 1% of all BCs. BC is a heterogeneous disease. Most BCs are luminal tumors that start in the inner (luminal) cells lining the mammary ducts.    Signaling description: The estrogen receptor alpha (ESR1) signaling pathway plays a key role in BC. ESR1 is involved in cell differentiation, apoptosis, and proliferation through diverse mechanisms. The overexpression of membrane-bound ESR1 causes the constitutive activation of AKT1 signaling preventing apoptosis and differentiation of mammary epithelial cells in BC and leads to hyperplastic glandular growth. Nuclear ESR1 induces genes involved in cell proliferation and differentiation. 17beta-estradiol binds and activates membrane-bound ESR1 which activates PIK3CA via PIK3R1. PIK3CA via PIP3 and PDPK1 activates v-akt murine thymoma viral oncogene homologs (AKT1).    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several antiapoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 which regulates the transcription of BCL2 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). CTNNB1 then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor/lymphoid enhancing factor (TCF/LEF) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5), mediated JNK-cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10), and BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. ESR1 also activates PRKCA via PIK3CA/PIP3/PDPK1. PRKCA activates v-raf-1 murine leukemia viral oncogene homolog 1 (RAF1), which activates ERK (MAPK1/3) signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation and migration. ESR1 binds src protein tyrosine kinase (SRC) mediated Janus kinases 1/2 (JAK1/2) which are involved in cell growth. Further, ligand-bound cytoplasmic ESR1 moves to the nucleus. 17beta-estradiol/ESR1 co-activator complex recruits transcription factors, SP1- and SP3, which induce the progesterone receptor (PGR) involved in cell differentiation and chromatin remodeling. Nuclear ESR1 interacts with transcription factors, NCOA1/NCOA3/EP300 and forms a transcription complex that binds with KAT2B and JUN/FOS and induces chromatin remodeling and cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

