xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cholesteatoma is a destructive disease characterized by the progressive expansion of keratinizing squamous epithelium in the middle ear and mastoid and chronic inflammatory reaction of the subepithelial connective tissue, which gradually expands and causes serious complications due to destruction of nearby bony structures.     Signaling description: Cholesteatoma growing in a space-limited area of the middle ear can exceed the capacity of the existing vessel supply. This might result in molecular changes such as apoptosis or hypoxia and necrotic cell death within the cholesteatoma tissue, followed by release of HMGB1. HMGB1 is released extracellularly in response to stress or necrosis and behaves as a cytokine, promoting inflammation. Higher expression of RAGE within cholesteatoma perimatrix, which is adjacent to ossicle surface or to other parts of the temporal bone, might be interpreted as indicative of the RAGE/HMGB1 involvement in this process. HMGB1 triggers paracrine activation of RAGE expressed in the cholesteatoma matrix but not in normal skin. It is possible that keratinocyte proliferation is promoted and sustained by HMGB1 via its interaction with RAGE on keratinocytes. It is also plausible that RAGE/HMGB1 interactions may trigger the signaling pathways involved in the regulation of chronic inflammation as well as bone resorption in cholesteatoma. The cholesteatoma might be related to the co-expression and functional interactions between HMGB1 and RAGE.The PI3K/Akt/NF-kB pathway is now recognized as one of the critical pathways in regulating cell survival/apoptosis, migration, and proliferation in pathological conditions such as chronic inflammation. It was shown that triggering of RAGE by HMGB1 activated Akt, induced NF-kB p65 translocation, and resulted in increased proliferation and decreased sensitivity to drug-induced apoptosis in keratinocytes. Levels of pAkt and pNF-kB molecules were significantly increased and the phosphorylated forms of c-Jun, MEK1, MEK2, MAPKp44/p42 (Erk1/2), and STAT3 were overexpressed in cholesteatoma tissue vs. normal skin epithelium. Furthermore, phosphorylation of all the above-listed molecules was triggered by HMGB1/RAGE interactions in cell lines and correspondingly also in cholesteatoma tissues. The abnormal behavior of cholesteatoma epithelium seems to be related to the presence of infiltrating immune cells releasing high levels of various cytokines and growth factors. Although IL1b and TNFa were not secreted by cells induced HMGB1, HMGB1 triggered IL8 production in these cells, confirming reports that HMGB1/RAGE interactions can induce IL8, a cytokine best known for its pro-inflammatory effects on immune cells. There is speculation that RAGE and HMGB1 regulate bone remodeling in chronic inflammation via up-regulation of the IL8 production, a mediator of bone destruction, in tissue samples of cholesteatoma.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cholesteatoma is a destructive disease characterized by the progressive expansion of keratinizing squamous epithelium in the middle ear and mastoid and chronic inflammatory reaction of the subepithelial connective tissue, which gradually expands and causes serious complications due to destruction of nearby bony structures.     Signaling description: Cholesteatoma growing in a space-limited area of the middle ear can exceed the capacity of the existing vessel supply. This might result in molecular changes such as apoptosis or hypoxia and necrotic cell death within the cholesteatoma tissue, followed by release of HMGB1. HMGB1 is released extracellularly in response to stress or necrosis and behaves as a cytokine, promoting inflammation. Higher expression of RAGE within cholesteatoma perimatrix, which is adjacent to ossicle surface or to other parts of the temporal bone, might be interpreted as indicative of the RAGE/HMGB1 involvement in this process. HMGB1 triggers paracrine activation of RAGE expressed in the cholesteatoma matrix but not in normal skin. It is possible that keratinocyte proliferation is promoted and sustained by HMGB1 via its interaction with RAGE on keratinocytes. It is also plausible that RAGE/HMGB1 interactions may trigger the signaling pathways involved in the regulation of chronic inflammation as well as bone resorption in cholesteatoma. The cholesteatoma might be related to the co-expression and functional interactions between HMGB1 and RAGE.The PI3K/Akt/NF-kB pathway is now recognized as one of the critical pathways in regulating cell survival/apoptosis, migration, and proliferation in pathological conditions such as chronic inflammation. It was shown that triggering of RAGE by HMGB1 activated Akt, induced NF-kB p65 translocation, and resulted in increased proliferation and decreased sensitivity to drug-induced apoptosis in keratinocytes. Levels of pAkt and pNF-kB molecules were significantly increased and the phosphorylated forms of c-Jun, MEK1, MEK2, MAPKp44/p42 (Erk1/2), and STAT3 were overexpressed in cholesteatoma tissue vs. normal skin epithelium. Furthermore, phosphorylation of all the above-listed molecules was triggered by HMGB1/RAGE interactions in cell lines and correspondingly also in cholesteatoma tissues. The abnormal behavior of cholesteatoma epithelium seems to be related to the presence of infiltrating immune cells releasing high levels of various cytokines and growth factors. Although IL1b and TNFa were not secreted by cells induced HMGB1, HMGB1 triggered IL8 production in these cells, confirming reports that HMGB1/RAGE interactions can induce IL8, a cytokine best known for its pro-inflammatory effects on immune cells. There is speculation that RAGE and HMGB1 regulate bone remodeling in chronic inflammation via up-regulation of the IL8 production, a mediator of bone destruction, in tissue samples of cholesteatoma.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

