xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Noise-induced hearing loss is the result of prolonged exposure to excessive noise, triggering apoptosis in cochlear sensory hair cells. Generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) is regarded as one the key events in noise-induced hearing loss.    Signaling description: The probable trigger for elevated ROS is Ca2+. Free Ca2+ increases in outer hair cells immediately after acoustic overstimulation, probably both via entry through ion channels and liberation from intracellular stores. In agreement with Ca2+ as a decisive factor in noise trauma is the success of attenuating noise-induced hearing loss through a blockade of L-type or T-type voltage-gated calcium channels. At the same time, previous observational studies investigating the role of race and hearing loss have consistently demonstrated that black race is associated with a 60-70% lower odd of noise-induced hearing loss and AHL compared to white subjects. Melanin produced by strial melanocytes (intermediate cells) in the cochlea has been hypothesized to serve a protective role as a free radical scavenger. Further, traumatic noise induces transient ATP depletion probably through mitochondrial mechanisms. AMPK is an energy sensor; when cellular ATP levels decrease, the levels of AMP increase and AMPK is activated. This leads to RAC1 increases and RHOA decrease. The increase in activated RAC1 leads to ROS formation through activation of NOX3. Traumatic noise diminishes both RHOA/DIAPH1 complex and total DIAPH1. The mammalian homolog of diaphanous (DIAPH1) is a RHO effector that regulates F-actin polymerization. Diminished levels of RHOA decrease ROCK2 levels. Phosphorylation of ezrin (EZR)-radixin(RDX)-moesin (MSN) by ROCK2 normally allows EZR-RDX-MSN to cross-link actin filaments and the plasma membrane. Noise-decreased levels of ROCK results in reduction of phosphorylation of EZR-RDX-MSN leading to depolymerization of actin filaments.    Outcome effects: Calcineurin, a calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein phosphatase, is activated following noise exposure and can, in turn, activate mitochondria-mediated cell death pathways via BAD. Oxidative stress can damage mitochondria which, in turn, release pro-apoptotic factors AIFM1 and ENDOG. ROS induce DNA damage and upregulates TP53 causing chronic activation of the mitochondrial BAK1. Noise-induced ROS produce vasoactive lipid peroxidation products such as isoprostanes, which may contribute to the decrease in cochlear blood flow observed following exposure to noise. The resulting ischemia and subsequent reperfusion would add to increased mitochondrial ROS formation and oxidative stress in a self-enhancing cycle. Noise exposure also induces up-regulation of NOX1 and DUOX2 in the cochlea. The activity of antioxidant defenders such as SOD2, GSTM1, GSTT1 and UCP2 may diminish with age and may be implicated in mechanisms of hearing loss. Traumatic noise was shown to induce hair cell apoptosis through transient cellular energy depletion. Finally, actin rearrangements following traumatic noise appear to change the gross morphology of hair cell structure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Noise-induced hearing loss is the result of prolonged exposure to excessive noise, triggering apoptosis in cochlear sensory hair cells. Generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) is regarded as one the key events in noise-induced hearing loss.    Signaling description: The probable trigger for elevated ROS is Ca2+. Free Ca2+ increases in outer hair cells immediately after acoustic overstimulation, probably both via entry through ion channels and liberation from intracellular stores. In agreement with Ca2+ as a decisive factor in noise trauma is the success of attenuating noise-induced hearing loss through a blockade of L-type or T-type voltage-gated calcium channels. At the same time, previous observational studies investigating the role of race and hearing loss have consistently demonstrated that black race is associated with a 60-70% lower odd of noise-induced hearing loss and AHL compared to white subjects. Melanin produced by strial melanocytes (intermediate cells) in the cochlea has been hypothesized to serve a protective role as a free radical scavenger. Further, traumatic noise induces transient ATP depletion probably through mitochondrial mechanisms. AMPK is an energy sensor; when cellular ATP levels decrease, the levels of AMP increase and AMPK is activated. This leads to RAC1 increases and RHOA decrease. The increase in activated RAC1 leads to ROS formation through activation of NOX3. Traumatic noise diminishes both RHOA/DIAPH1 complex and total DIAPH1. The mammalian homolog of diaphanous (DIAPH1) is a RHO effector that regulates F-actin polymerization. Diminished levels of RHOA decrease ROCK2 levels. Phosphorylation of ezrin (EZR)-radixin(RDX)-moesin (MSN) by ROCK2 normally allows EZR-RDX-MSN to cross-link actin filaments and the plasma membrane. Noise-decreased levels of ROCK results in reduction of phosphorylation of EZR-RDX-MSN leading to depolymerization of actin filaments.    Outcome effects: Calcineurin, a calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein phosphatase, is activated following noise exposure and can, in turn, activate mitochondria-mediated cell death pathways via BAD. Oxidative stress can damage mitochondria which, in turn, release pro-apoptotic factors AIFM1 and ENDOG. ROS induce DNA damage and upregulates TP53 causing chronic activation of the mitochondrial BAK1. Noise-induced ROS produce vasoactive lipid peroxidation products such as isoprostanes, which may contribute to the decrease in cochlear blood flow observed following exposure to noise. The resulting ischemia and subsequent reperfusion would add to increased mitochondrial ROS formation and oxidative stress in a self-enhancing cycle. Noise exposure also induces up-regulation of NOX1 and DUOX2 in the cochlea. The activity of antioxidant defenders such as SOD2, GSTM1, GSTT1 and UCP2 may diminish with age and may be implicated in mechanisms of hearing loss. Traumatic noise was shown to induce hair cell apoptosis through transient cellular energy depletion. Finally, actin rearrangements following traumatic noise appear to change the gross morphology of hair cell structure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

