xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hearing loss is a hearing loss present at birth or developed later due to genetic causes. Genetic factors are thought to cause more than 50% of all incidents of congenital hearing loss. Hereditary hearing loss and deafness may be syndromic (associated with malformations of the external ear or other organs or with medical problems involving other organ systems) or nonsyndromic (with no associated visible abnormalities of the external ear or any related medical problems). Hereditary hearing loss can be inherited in an autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked recessive manner, as well as by mitochondrial inheritance.    Signaling description: Many genes mutated in congenital hearing loss are implicated in actin-myosin complex organization or associated with it's functioning. A number of myosins may be mutated, such as MYO3A (recessive hearing loss DFNB30), MYO6 (dominant and recessive hearing loss DFNA22 and DFNB37, respectively), MYO7A (dominant and recessive hearing loss DFNA11 and DFNB2, respectively) and MYO15A (recessive hearing loss DFNB3), MYO1A (dominant hearing loss DFNA48), MYO1C and MYO1F. Two non-muscle conventional myosins, MYH9 (dominant hearing loss DFNA17) and MYH14 (dominant hearing loss DFNA4), are associated with non-syndromic hearing loss. MYO7A mutations are also responsible for Usher syndrome type IB. Actin, gamma 1 (ACTG1) mutations are associated with DFNA20/26, a subtype of autosomal dominant hearing loss. HOMER2 exerts its function by regulating actin dynamics in stereocilia through its interaction with CDC42 and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. DIAPH1 have a role in the regulation of actin polymerization and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. TPRN may modulate actin dynamics through direct or indirect interaction with pointed ends of actin filaments, particularly within the hair cell stereocilia and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. CLIC5 stabilizes membrane-actin filament linkages at the base of hair cell stereocilia in a molecular complex with RDX, TPRN and MYO6. RDX may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. CEACAM16 is localizes to the tips of the tallest stereocilia and the tectorial membrane (TM). This specific localization suggests a role in maintaining the integrity of the TM as well as in the association of the stereocilia and TM, a linkage essential for mechanical amplification. In agreement with this role, CEACAM16 colocalizes and coimmunoprecipitates with the TM protein TECTA (alpha-tectorin). Mutation of CEACAM16 leads to autosomal dominant nonsyndromic deafness (DFNA4). Mutations in the TECTA gene have been shown to be responsible for autosomal dominant nonsyndromic hearing impairment and a recessive form of sensorineural pre-lingual non-syndromic deafness. PTPRQ and STRC are implicated in side binding between stereocilia. The cytoplasmic domain of PTPRQ associates with a juxtamembrane protein complex (blue spheres), which includes CLIC5, RDX, MYO6, and/or TPRN, thereby linking PTPRQ to actin filaments at the tapered base of stereocilia, as well as linking actin filaments of the cuticular plate and the interstereocilia membrane domain. PTPRQ and STRC mutation cause non-syndromic hearing loss. MARVELD2 (deafness autosomal recessive type 49 (DFNB49)), TJP2 (autosomal dominant deafness-51) and CLDN14 normally take part in tight junctions assembly and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. SLC26A5 (prestin) is a transmembrane protein and an incomplete anion transporter, it does not allow anions to cross the cell membrane, but instead undergoes a conformational change in response to changes in intracellular Cl- levels that results in a change in cell length. SLC26A5 contracts and elongates leading to electromotility of outer hair cells. Electromotility is the driving force behind the somatic motor of the cochlear amplifier, which is a mammalian evolution product that increases sensitivity to incoming sound wave freq"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hearing loss is a hearing loss present at birth or developed later due to genetic causes. Genetic factors are thought to cause more than 50% of all incidents of congenital hearing loss. Hereditary hearing loss and deafness may be syndromic (associated with malformations of the external ear or other organs or with medical problems involving other organ systems) or nonsyndromic (with no associated visible abnormalities of the external ear or any related medical problems). Hereditary hearing loss can be inherited in an autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked recessive manner, as well as by mitochondrial inheritance.    Signaling description: Many genes mutated in congenital hearing loss are implicated in actin-myosin complex organization or associated with it's functioning. A number of myosins may be mutated, such as MYO3A (recessive hearing loss DFNB30), MYO6 (dominant and recessive hearing loss DFNA22 and DFNB37, respectively), MYO7A (dominant and recessive hearing loss DFNA11 and DFNB2, respectively) and MYO15A (recessive hearing loss DFNB3), MYO1A (dominant hearing loss DFNA48), MYO1C and MYO1F. Two non-muscle conventional myosins, MYH9 (dominant hearing loss DFNA17) and MYH14 (dominant hearing loss DFNA4), are associated with non-syndromic hearing loss. MYO7A mutations are also responsible for Usher syndrome type IB. Actin, gamma 1 (ACTG1) mutations are associated with DFNA20/26, a subtype of autosomal dominant hearing loss. HOMER2 exerts its function by regulating actin dynamics in stereocilia through its interaction with CDC42 and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. DIAPH1 have a role in the regulation of actin polymerization and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. TPRN may modulate actin dynamics through direct or indirect interaction with pointed ends of actin filaments, particularly within the hair cell stereocilia and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. CLIC5 stabilizes membrane-actin filament linkages at the base of hair cell stereocilia in a molecular complex with RDX, TPRN and MYO6. RDX may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. CEACAM16 is localizes to the tips of the tallest stereocilia and the tectorial membrane (TM). This specific localization suggests a role in maintaining the integrity of the TM as well as in the association of the stereocilia and TM, a linkage essential for mechanical amplification. In agreement with this role, CEACAM16 colocalizes and coimmunoprecipitates with the TM protein TECTA (alpha-tectorin). Mutation of CEACAM16 leads to autosomal dominant nonsyndromic deafness (DFNA4). Mutations in the TECTA gene have been shown to be responsible for autosomal dominant nonsyndromic hearing impairment and a recessive form of sensorineural pre-lingual non-syndromic deafness. PTPRQ and STRC are implicated in side binding between stereocilia. The cytoplasmic domain of PTPRQ associates with a juxtamembrane protein complex (blue spheres), which includes CLIC5, RDX, MYO6, and/or TPRN, thereby linking PTPRQ to actin filaments at the tapered base of stereocilia, as well as linking actin filaments of the cuticular plate and the interstereocilia membrane domain. PTPRQ and STRC mutation cause non-syndromic hearing loss. MARVELD2 (deafness autosomal recessive type 49 (DFNB49)), TJP2 (autosomal dominant deafness-51) and CLDN14 normally take part in tight junctions assembly and may be mutated in non-syndromic hearing loss. SLC26A5 (prestin) is a transmembrane protein and an incomplete anion transporter, it does not allow anions to cross the cell membrane, but instead undergoes a conformational change in response to changes in intracellular Cl- levels that results in a change in cell length. SLC26A5 contracts and elongates leading to electromotility of outer hair cells. Electromotility is the driving force behind the somatic motor of the cochlear amplifier, which is a mammalian evolution product that increases sensitivity to incoming sound wave freq" EXACT []

