xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Age-related hearing loss ARHL is the result of genetic predispositions combined with various insults to the inner ear accumulated during a lifetime.    Signaling description: Hearing depends on a high K+ concentration in the endolymph bathing the apical membranes of sensory hair cells. During sound stimulation, K+ enters the hair cells via apical mechanoelectrical transducer channels (MTCs). TMC1, TMC2, LHFPL5, PCDH15 and TMIE proteins are likely to be involved in the organization of MTCs, although the exact molecular composition of the MTCs is yet to be completely elucidated. PCDH15 polymorphism was shown to be associated with ARHL.    Outcome effects: K+ exits the hair cells through KCNQ4 and KCNMA1 channels, and KCNQ4 is a candidate gene for ARHL predisposition. In addition, KCNQ4 mutations are found in patients with autosomal dominant nonsyndromic hearing loss (ARNSHL). Following K+ efflux from the hair cell, it is taken up by supporting Deiters cells via KCNJ10 and then exported via SLC12A6 and SLC12A7. K+ passes fibrocytes of the lateral wall through gap junctions. The common GJB2 (Connexin 26, gap junction protein) 35delG mutation might contribute to the development of age-related hearing impairment (ARHI) and noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL). GJB2 mutations were also described in ARNSHL. In strial vascularis marginal cells, SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 raise intracellular K+ concentration. In parallel, CLCNKA/ BSND and CLCNKB/ BSND channels recycle Cl-. Then, K+ exits through KCNQ1 and KCN1 apical channels back to the endolymph. SLC12A2, ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 heterozygous deletEion were shown to cause age-dependent hearing loss in mice."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Age-related hearing loss ARHL is the result of genetic predispositions combined with various insults to the inner ear accumulated during a lifetime.    Signaling description: Hearing depends on a high K+ concentration in the endolymph bathing the apical membranes of sensory hair cells. During sound stimulation, K+ enters the hair cells via apical mechanoelectrical transducer channels (MTCs). TMC1, TMC2, LHFPL5, PCDH15 and TMIE proteins are likely to be involved in the organization of MTCs, although the exact molecular composition of the MTCs is yet to be completely elucidated. PCDH15 polymorphism was shown to be associated with ARHL.    Outcome effects: K+ exits the hair cells through KCNQ4 and KCNMA1 channels, and KCNQ4 is a candidate gene for ARHL predisposition. In addition, KCNQ4 mutations are found in patients with autosomal dominant nonsyndromic hearing loss (ARNSHL). Following K+ efflux from the hair cell, it is taken up by supporting Deiters cells via KCNJ10 and then exported via SLC12A6 and SLC12A7. K+ passes fibrocytes of the lateral wall through gap junctions. The common GJB2 (Connexin 26, gap junction protein) 35delG mutation might contribute to the development of age-related hearing impairment (ARHI) and noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL). GJB2 mutations were also described in ARNSHL. In strial vascularis marginal cells, SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 raise intracellular K+ concentration. In parallel, CLCNKA/ BSND and CLCNKB/ BSND channels recycle Cl-. Then, K+ exits through KCNQ1 and KCN1 apical channels back to the endolymph. SLC12A2, ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 heterozygous deletEion were shown to cause age-dependent hearing loss in mice." EXACT []

