xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hearing loss (HL) is total or partial inability to hear. HL can be categolized by the part of the hearing system that is affected and is generally divided into conductive and sensorineural HL.    Signaling description: The most commonly used drugs with ototoxic effects are aminoglycosides (AGs) (eg., gentamicin) and platinum-based chemotherapeutica (eg., cisplatin), loop diuretcs, salicylates, macrolide antibiotics and antimalarials. AGs and cisplatin effect on the hearing apparatus is the most severe and leads to permanent HL. AGs and cisplatin causes HL through the induction of apoptosis in sensory hair cells. Apoptosis activation has been attributed to the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and reactive nitrogen species (RNS) initiated by ototoxic drugs. Supposedly, aminoglycosides (AGs) are taken up by sensory hair cells via e both endocytosis and transport through ion channels. MYO7A was hypothesized to have a role in endocytosis-mediated AG uptake. Formation of an AG-iron complex can react with electron donors to form ROS.    Outcome effects: ROS trigger downstream mechanisms leading to apoptosis. ROS can activate JNK (MAPK1/3), which can translocate to the nucleus to activate genes in the cell death pathway. In addition, oxidative stress can damage mitochondria which, in turn, release pro-apoptotic factors AIFM1 and ENDOG. Gentamicin activates MAPK1/3 (ERK1/2), which leads to upregulation of HRK and finally results in cell death. Cisplatin is proposed to enter the hair cells through cation transporters SLC22A1, SLC22A2, SLC31A1 and mechanotransducer channels. NADPH oxidase 3 (NOX3) is upregulated following cisplatin treatment and was shown to play a key role in cisplatin-mediated ototoxicity. Cisplatin has been shown to increase the expression of inflammatory mediators such as NOS2, COX2 and TNF, which are downstream targets of STAT1. Cisplatin-induced activation of STAT1 is dependent on ROS generation through NOX3.    Animal data: Mouse models."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hearing loss (HL) is total or partial inability to hear. HL can be categolized by the part of the hearing system that is affected and is generally divided into conductive and sensorineural HL.    Signaling description: The most commonly used drugs with ototoxic effects are aminoglycosides (AGs) (eg., gentamicin) and platinum-based chemotherapeutica (eg., cisplatin), loop diuretcs, salicylates, macrolide antibiotics and antimalarials. AGs and cisplatin effect on the hearing apparatus is the most severe and leads to permanent HL. AGs and cisplatin causes HL through the induction of apoptosis in sensory hair cells. Apoptosis activation has been attributed to the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and reactive nitrogen species (RNS) initiated by ototoxic drugs. Supposedly, aminoglycosides (AGs) are taken up by sensory hair cells via e both endocytosis and transport through ion channels. MYO7A was hypothesized to have a role in endocytosis-mediated AG uptake. Formation of an AG-iron complex can react with electron donors to form ROS.    Outcome effects: ROS trigger downstream mechanisms leading to apoptosis. ROS can activate JNK (MAPK1/3), which can translocate to the nucleus to activate genes in the cell death pathway. In addition, oxidative stress can damage mitochondria which, in turn, release pro-apoptotic factors AIFM1 and ENDOG. Gentamicin activates MAPK1/3 (ERK1/2), which leads to upregulation of HRK and finally results in cell death. Cisplatin is proposed to enter the hair cells through cation transporters SLC22A1, SLC22A2, SLC31A1 and mechanotransducer channels. NADPH oxidase 3 (NOX3) is upregulated following cisplatin treatment and was shown to play a key role in cisplatin-mediated ototoxicity. Cisplatin has been shown to increase the expression of inflammatory mediators such as NOS2, COX2 and TNF, which are downstream targets of STAT1. Cisplatin-induced activation of STAT1 is dependent on ROS generation through NOX3.    Animal data: Mouse models." EXACT []

