xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: A number of genes, encoding proteins implicated in synaptogenesis and synapse function may be mutated in congenital hearing loss: otoferlin (OTOF), GIPC PDZ domain containing family, member 3 (GIPC3), solute carrier family 17, vesicular glutamate transporter, member 8 (SLC17A8), calcium channel, voltage-dependent, L type, alpha 1D subunit (CACNA1D), MYO6 (myosin VI).    Signaling description: OTOF has been proposed to be the calcium sensor in hair cell exocytosis, compensating for the classic synaptic fusion proteins synaptotagmin-1 (SYT1) and synaptotagmin-2 (SYT2). OTOF binds Ca(2+) and displays Ca(2+)-dependent interactions with the SNARE proteins syntaxin-1 (STX1A) and SNAP25. Mutations in OTOF cause prelingual deafness DFNB9 and temperature-sensitive synaptic hearing impairment. Myosin VI (MYO6) was shown to be a novel OTOF-binding partner. Unlike in other synapses, hair cell ribbon synapses use CaV1.3 L-type Ca2+ channels (CACNA1D) for stimulus-secretion coupling. Recently, a loss of function mutation in the CACNA1D gene has been identified in families with congenital deafness and bradycardia, signifying the importance of CaV1.3 for hearing and atrial pacemaking. A mutation of the CABP2 gene has been demonstrated to cause a moderate sensorineural hearing impairment. CABP2 might play a role in regulating CACNA1D and inner hair cell synaptic transmission. The glutamatergic ribbon synapses of hair cells use the transporter SLC17A8 (VGLUT3) to load their synaptic vesicles with glutamate. This gene encodes a vesicular glutamate transporter. The encoded protein transports the neurotransmitter glutamate into synaptic vesicles before it is released into the synaptic cleft. Mutations in this gene are the cause of autosomal-dominant nonsyndromic type 25 deafness. GIPC3 distribution in hair cells is reminiscent of vesicular staining and shows a similar localization as SLC17A8 and MYO6, proteins that regulate synaptic glutamate release and Ca2 + -dependent exocytosis at the hair cell ribbon synapse. GIPC3 may be part of a protein complex involved in vesicle trafficking that transports factors critical for signal aquisition and propagation in cochlear hair cells. Age-related hearing loss (ARHL), also known as presbycusis, is a multifactorial symmetric sensorineural loss that affects adults older than 50 years of age. The causes of presbycusis are likely a combination of environmental and genetic factors. A few loci have been identified as having a likely role in susceptibility to ARHL, and GRM7 is the only gene that has achieved genome-wide significance. A 500K-single nucleotide polymorphism genome-wide association study of a large European cohort of older adults resulted in the identification of one haplotype within GRM7 as harboring a significant risk allele for ARHL. L-glutamate is the major excitatory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system, and it activates both ionotropic and metabotropic glutamate receptors. GRM7 is localized at the pre-synaptic membrane and was shown to inhibit cyclic AMP cascade and synaptic gluatamate exocytisis providing a negative feedback. Lack of GRM7 function leads to neuronal damage due to glutamate exitotoxicity resulting in hearing loss.Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: A number of genes, encoding proteins implicated in synaptogenesis and synapse function may be mutated in congenital hearing loss: otoferlin (OTOF), GIPC PDZ domain containing family, member 3 (GIPC3), solute carrier family 17, vesicular glutamate transporter, member 8 (SLC17A8), calcium channel, voltage-dependent, L type, alpha 1D subunit (CACNA1D), MYO6 (myosin VI).    Signaling description: OTOF has been proposed to be the calcium sensor in hair cell exocytosis, compensating for the classic synaptic fusion proteins synaptotagmin-1 (SYT1) and synaptotagmin-2 (SYT2). OTOF binds Ca(2+) and displays Ca(2+)-dependent interactions with the SNARE proteins syntaxin-1 (STX1A) and SNAP25. Mutations in OTOF cause prelingual deafness DFNB9 and temperature-sensitive synaptic hearing impairment. Myosin VI (MYO6) was shown to be a novel OTOF-binding partner. Unlike in other synapses, hair cell ribbon synapses use CaV1.3 L-type Ca2+ channels (CACNA1D) for stimulus-secretion coupling. Recently, a loss of function mutation in the CACNA1D gene has been identified in families with congenital deafness and bradycardia, signifying the importance of CaV1.3 for hearing and atrial pacemaking. A mutation of the CABP2 gene has been demonstrated to cause a moderate sensorineural hearing impairment. CABP2 might play a role in regulating CACNA1D and inner hair cell synaptic transmission. The glutamatergic ribbon synapses of hair cells use the transporter SLC17A8 (VGLUT3) to load their synaptic vesicles with glutamate. This gene encodes a vesicular glutamate transporter. The encoded protein transports the neurotransmitter glutamate into synaptic vesicles before it is released into the synaptic cleft. Mutations in this gene are the cause of autosomal-dominant nonsyndromic type 25 deafness. GIPC3 distribution in hair cells is reminiscent of vesicular staining and shows a similar localization as SLC17A8 and MYO6, proteins that regulate synaptic glutamate release and Ca2 + -dependent exocytosis at the hair cell ribbon synapse. GIPC3 may be part of a protein complex involved in vesicle trafficking that transports factors critical for signal aquisition and propagation in cochlear hair cells. Age-related hearing loss (ARHL), also known as presbycusis, is a multifactorial symmetric sensorineural loss that affects adults older than 50 years of age. The causes of presbycusis are likely a combination of environmental and genetic factors. A few loci have been identified as having a likely role in susceptibility to ARHL, and GRM7 is the only gene that has achieved genome-wide significance. A 500K-single nucleotide polymorphism genome-wide association study of a large European cohort of older adults resulted in the identification of one haplotype within GRM7 as harboring a significant risk allele for ARHL. L-glutamate is the major excitatory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system, and it activates both ionotropic and metabotropic glutamate receptors. GRM7 is localized at the pre-synaptic membrane and was shown to inhibit cyclic AMP cascade and synaptic gluatamate exocytisis providing a negative feedback. Lack of GRM7 function leads to neuronal damage due to glutamate exitotoxicity resulting in hearing loss.Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

