xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Congenital hearing loss is a hearing loss present at birth or developed later due to genetic causes. Multiple mutations in genes, involved in cation transport in the inner ear, are found in congenital syndromic and non-syndromic deafness.    Signaling description: Hearing depends on a high K+ concentration in the endolymph bathing the apical membranes of sensory hair cells. During sound stimulation, K+ enters the hair cells via apical mechanoelectrical transducer channels (MTCs). TMC1, TMC2, LHFPL5, PCDH15 and TMIE proteins are likely to be involved in the organization of MTCs, although the exact molecular composition of the MTCs is yet to be completely elucidated. Mutations in the PCDH15, LHFPL5, TMIE, TMC1 are associated with different forms of congenital deafness. Mutations in the PCDH15 gene are responsible for non-syndromic hearing loss (DFNB23) and Usher syndrome type I (USH1F). TMIE mutation is associated with autosomal recessive nonsyndromic hearing loss (ARNSHL), the most common form of congenitally acquired inherited hearing impairment. TMC1 mutation is associated with progressive postlingual hearing loss and profound prelingual deafness.    Outcome effects: K+ exits from the hair cells through the KCNQ4 and KCNMA1 channels. KCNQ4 mutations are found in patients with nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness type 2 (DFNA2), an autosomal dominant form of progressive hearing loss. K+ is then taken up via KCNJ10 by supporting Deiters cells and exported out by SLC12A6 and SLC12A7. Mutations in KCNJ10 cause the autosomal recessive EAST syndrome characterized by epilepsy, ataxia, sensorineural deafness, and a salt-wasting tubulopathy. The knockout of either SLC12A6 or SLC12A7 genes causes deafness in mice. K+ passes fibrocytes of the lateral wall through gap junctions. At least three connexin genes, GJB2, GJB3 and GJB6, all of which are expressed in this gap junction system, are involved in human genetic deafness. Mutations in GJB2 are responsible for as much as 50% of pre-lingual, recessive deafness. In strial vascularis marginal cells, SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 raise intracellular K+ concentration. In parallel, CLCNKA/ BSND and CLCNKB/ BSND channels recycle Cl-. Further, K+ exits through apical channels KCNQ1 and KCNE1 back to the endolymph. Mutations in Barttin (BSND) have been associated with Bartter syndrome with sensorineural deafness (Bartter syndrome type IV). Barttin is thought to be an accessory subunit of a chloride channel and if mutated disrupts the activity of CLCNKA and CLCNKB. Finally, mutations in the KCNE1 and KCNQ1 genes cause Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome (long QT syndrome, associated with bilateral sensorineural hearing loss).    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Congenital hearing loss is a hearing loss present at birth or developed later due to genetic causes. Multiple mutations in genes, involved in cation transport in the inner ear, are found in congenital syndromic and non-syndromic deafness.    Signaling description: Hearing depends on a high K+ concentration in the endolymph bathing the apical membranes of sensory hair cells. During sound stimulation, K+ enters the hair cells via apical mechanoelectrical transducer channels (MTCs). TMC1, TMC2, LHFPL5, PCDH15 and TMIE proteins are likely to be involved in the organization of MTCs, although the exact molecular composition of the MTCs is yet to be completely elucidated. Mutations in the PCDH15, LHFPL5, TMIE, TMC1 are associated with different forms of congenital deafness. Mutations in the PCDH15 gene are responsible for non-syndromic hearing loss (DFNB23) and Usher syndrome type I (USH1F). TMIE mutation is associated with autosomal recessive nonsyndromic hearing loss (ARNSHL), the most common form of congenitally acquired inherited hearing impairment. TMC1 mutation is associated with progressive postlingual hearing loss and profound prelingual deafness.    Outcome effects: K+ exits from the hair cells through the KCNQ4 and KCNMA1 channels. KCNQ4 mutations are found in patients with nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness type 2 (DFNA2), an autosomal dominant form of progressive hearing loss. K+ is then taken up via KCNJ10 by supporting Deiters cells and exported out by SLC12A6 and SLC12A7. Mutations in KCNJ10 cause the autosomal recessive EAST syndrome characterized by epilepsy, ataxia, sensorineural deafness, and a salt-wasting tubulopathy. The knockout of either SLC12A6 or SLC12A7 genes causes deafness in mice. K+ passes fibrocytes of the lateral wall through gap junctions. At least three connexin genes, GJB2, GJB3 and GJB6, all of which are expressed in this gap junction system, are involved in human genetic deafness. Mutations in GJB2 are responsible for as much as 50% of pre-lingual, recessive deafness. In strial vascularis marginal cells, SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 raise intracellular K+ concentration. In parallel, CLCNKA/ BSND and CLCNKB/ BSND channels recycle Cl-. Further, K+ exits through apical channels KCNQ1 and KCNE1 back to the endolymph. Mutations in Barttin (BSND) have been associated with Bartter syndrome with sensorineural deafness (Bartter syndrome type IV). Barttin is thought to be an accessory subunit of a chloride channel and if mutated disrupts the activity of CLCNKA and CLCNKB. Finally, mutations in the KCNE1 and KCNQ1 genes cause Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome (long QT syndrome, associated with bilateral sensorineural hearing loss).    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

