xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL) is the result of prolonged exposure to excessive noise that damages cochlear sensory hair cells.    Signaling description: Hearing depends on a high K+ concentration in the endolymph bathing the apical membranes of sensory hair cells. During sound stimulation, K+ enters the hair cells via apical mechanoelectrical transducer channels (MTCs). TMC1, TMC2, LHFPL5, PCDH15 and TMIE proteins are likely to be involved in the organization of MTCs, although the exact molecular composition of the MTCs is yet to be completely elucidated. Genetic variations of PCDH15 are associated with susceptibility to NIHL.    Outcome effects: K+ exits the hair cells through KCNQ4 and KCNMA1 channels, and single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in KCNQ4 are strongly associated with NIHL. Further, K+ is taken up via KCNJ10 by supporting Deiters cells and exported by SLC12A6 and SLC12A7. Fibrocytes in the lateral wall of the cochlea are connected by gap junctions. Most likely K+ is transported through these gap junctions from cell to cell. The common GJB2 (Connexin 26, gap junction protein) 35delG mutation might contribute to the development of age-related hearing impairment and NIHL. In strial vascularis marginal cells, SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 raise intracellular K+ concentration. In parallel, CLCNKA/ BSND and CLCNKB/ BSND channels recycle Cl-. K+ exits through the apical channels KCNQ1 and KCNE1 back to the endolymph. SNPs in KCNQ1 and KCNE1 were shown to be strongly asscociated with NIHL."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Noise-induced hearing loss (NIHL) is the result of prolonged exposure to excessive noise that damages cochlear sensory hair cells.    Signaling description: Hearing depends on a high K+ concentration in the endolymph bathing the apical membranes of sensory hair cells. During sound stimulation, K+ enters the hair cells via apical mechanoelectrical transducer channels (MTCs). TMC1, TMC2, LHFPL5, PCDH15 and TMIE proteins are likely to be involved in the organization of MTCs, although the exact molecular composition of the MTCs is yet to be completely elucidated. Genetic variations of PCDH15 are associated with susceptibility to NIHL.    Outcome effects: K+ exits the hair cells through KCNQ4 and KCNMA1 channels, and single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in KCNQ4 are strongly associated with NIHL. Further, K+ is taken up via KCNJ10 by supporting Deiters cells and exported by SLC12A6 and SLC12A7. Fibrocytes in the lateral wall of the cochlea are connected by gap junctions. Most likely K+ is transported through these gap junctions from cell to cell. The common GJB2 (Connexin 26, gap junction protein) 35delG mutation might contribute to the development of age-related hearing impairment and NIHL. In strial vascularis marginal cells, SLC12A2 (NKCC1), ATP1A1 and ATP1A2 raise intracellular K+ concentration. In parallel, CLCNKA/ BSND and CLCNKB/ BSND channels recycle Cl-. K+ exits through the apical channels KCNQ1 and KCNE1 back to the endolymph. SNPs in KCNQ1 and KCNE1 were shown to be strongly asscociated with NIHL." EXACT []

