xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is a neoplasm of large transformed B-cells and accounts for 30-40% of all adult non-Hodgkin lymphomas, making it the most common type. DLBCL is subdivided into many categories based on biological properties and localization. Activated B-cell-like DLBCL (ABC DLBCL) is a subtype of DLBCL identified by gene-expression profile.    Signaling description: The pathogenetic hallmark of ABC DLBCL is the constitutive activation of the NF-kB signaling pathway that promotes cell survival, proliferation, and inhibition of apoptosis. Approximately 30% of ABC DLBCLs harbor mutations/deletions of TNFAIP3 (A20) which acts as a negative regulator of NF-kB signaling. NFKBIZ amplification is also detected in 9% of ABC DLBCL cases which activates targets in the NF-kB signaling pathway. About 30% of ABC DLBCL tumors have activating mutations in MYD88, leading to the constitutive activation of NF-kB signaling and JAK/STAT3 pathway. About 10% of ABC DLBCL patients harbor activating mutations in CARD11 and 20% of ABC lymphomas have mutations in CD79A or CD79B leading to chronic active B-cell receptor signaling. In conclusion, ABC DLBCL has an unfavorable prognosis and remains the least curable form of this malignancy despite many recent advances in therapy.    Outcome effects: ABC DLBCLs are characterized by amplifications and consecutive overexpression of the antiapoptotic BCL2 gene as well as deletions affecting CDKN2A and CDKN2B. BCL6 dependency occurs in ABC cell lines, with or without a BCL6 translocation. BCL2 and NFATC1 are consistently upregulated by the gain/amplification of 18q while the gain/amplification of 19q leads to the overexpression of SPIB. IL6 and IL10 are overexpressed in an autocrine manner and play an important role in pathogenesis. ABC DLBCLs characteristically express several genes that are normally expressed in plasma cells. However, full differentiation into plasma cells seems to be blocked by genetic abnormalities involving the function of PRDM1 (Blimp-1), the master regulator of plasmacytic differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is a neoplasm of large transformed B-cells and accounts for 30-40% of all adult non-Hodgkin lymphomas, making it the most common type. DLBCL is subdivided into many categories based on biological properties and localization. Activated B-cell-like DLBCL (ABC DLBCL) is a subtype of DLBCL identified by gene-expression profile.    Signaling description: The pathogenetic hallmark of ABC DLBCL is the constitutive activation of the NF-kB signaling pathway that promotes cell survival, proliferation, and inhibition of apoptosis. Approximately 30% of ABC DLBCLs harbor mutations/deletions of TNFAIP3 (A20) which acts as a negative regulator of NF-kB signaling. NFKBIZ amplification is also detected in 9% of ABC DLBCL cases which activates targets in the NF-kB signaling pathway. About 30% of ABC DLBCL tumors have activating mutations in MYD88, leading to the constitutive activation of NF-kB signaling and JAK/STAT3 pathway. About 10% of ABC DLBCL patients harbor activating mutations in CARD11 and 20% of ABC lymphomas have mutations in CD79A or CD79B leading to chronic active B-cell receptor signaling. In conclusion, ABC DLBCL has an unfavorable prognosis and remains the least curable form of this malignancy despite many recent advances in therapy.    Outcome effects: ABC DLBCLs are characterized by amplifications and consecutive overexpression of the antiapoptotic BCL2 gene as well as deletions affecting CDKN2A and CDKN2B. BCL6 dependency occurs in ABC cell lines, with or without a BCL6 translocation. BCL2 and NFATC1 are consistently upregulated by the gain/amplification of 18q while the gain/amplification of 19q leads to the overexpression of SPIB. IL6 and IL10 are overexpressed in an autocrine manner and play an important role in pathogenesis. ABC DLBCLs characteristically express several genes that are normally expressed in plasma cells. However, full differentiation into plasma cells seems to be blocked by genetic abnormalities involving the function of PRDM1 (Blimp-1), the master regulator of plasmacytic differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

