xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is a neoplasm of large transformed B-cells and accounts for 30-40% of all adult non-Hodgkin lymphomas, making it the most common type. DLBCL is subdivided into many categories based on biological properties and localization. Germinal center B-cell-like DLBCL (GCB DLBCL) is a subtype of DLBCL identified by gene-expression profile. It has an expression profile typical for germinal center cells.    Signaling description: There are several oncogenic mechanisms distinguishing GCB DLBCL from other types of DLBCL. A significant fraction of GCB DLBCL have a translocation, t(14;18) (q32; q21), which activates the antiapoptotic BCL2 gene. PTEN deletions are also detected in approximately 15% of GCB DLBCL cases. As an alternative mechanism for deregulating PTEN nearly 15% of GCB DLBCL cases have amplifications of the miR-17-92 microRNA (miRNA) cluster resulting in PTEN suppression. The inactivation of PTEN leads to the constitutive activation of PI3K/Akt signaling pathway. In more than 10% of samples of GCB DLBCLs, amplification of the nuclear-localized E3 ubiquitin ligase MDM2 is also observed. The MDM2 gene is transcriptionally regulated by TP53. In addition, genetic aberrations affecting CREBBP and EP300 have also been identified. TP53 mutations are also revealed in GCB DLBCL. The REL locus is recurrently amplified in approximately 30% of GCB DLBCL cases. REL is a transcription factor that is a member of the REL/NF-kB family which is responsible for DNA binding, dimerization, nuclear localization, and binding to the NF-kB inhibitor. However, in contrast to ABC DLBCL in which the NF-kB pathway is constitutively activated in virtually all cases, GCB DLBCL does not seem to depend on NF-kB signaling. Thus, the functional role of this aberration in the biology of GCB DLBCL needs to be functionally clarified. In conclusion, GCB DLBCL has a more favorable prognosis when compared to ABC DLBCL. It may be successfully treated with conventional chemotherapy plus rituximab.    Outcome effects: MDM2 can promote tumor formation by targeting tumor suppressor proteins, such as TP53 for proteasomal degradation. Mutant CREBBP and EP300 proteins are deficient in acetylating BCL6 and TP53 which leads to the constitutive activation of BCL6 and to decreased TP53 tumor suppressor activity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is a neoplasm of large transformed B-cells and accounts for 30-40% of all adult non-Hodgkin lymphomas, making it the most common type. DLBCL is subdivided into many categories based on biological properties and localization. Germinal center B-cell-like DLBCL (GCB DLBCL) is a subtype of DLBCL identified by gene-expression profile. It has an expression profile typical for germinal center cells.    Signaling description: There are several oncogenic mechanisms distinguishing GCB DLBCL from other types of DLBCL. A significant fraction of GCB DLBCL have a translocation, t(14;18) (q32; q21), which activates the antiapoptotic BCL2 gene. PTEN deletions are also detected in approximately 15% of GCB DLBCL cases. As an alternative mechanism for deregulating PTEN nearly 15% of GCB DLBCL cases have amplifications of the miR-17-92 microRNA (miRNA) cluster resulting in PTEN suppression. The inactivation of PTEN leads to the constitutive activation of PI3K/Akt signaling pathway. In more than 10% of samples of GCB DLBCLs, amplification of the nuclear-localized E3 ubiquitin ligase MDM2 is also observed. The MDM2 gene is transcriptionally regulated by TP53. In addition, genetic aberrations affecting CREBBP and EP300 have also been identified. TP53 mutations are also revealed in GCB DLBCL. The REL locus is recurrently amplified in approximately 30% of GCB DLBCL cases. REL is a transcription factor that is a member of the REL/NF-kB family which is responsible for DNA binding, dimerization, nuclear localization, and binding to the NF-kB inhibitor. However, in contrast to ABC DLBCL in which the NF-kB pathway is constitutively activated in virtually all cases, GCB DLBCL does not seem to depend on NF-kB signaling. Thus, the functional role of this aberration in the biology of GCB DLBCL needs to be functionally clarified. In conclusion, GCB DLBCL has a more favorable prognosis when compared to ABC DLBCL. It may be successfully treated with conventional chemotherapy plus rituximab.    Outcome effects: MDM2 can promote tumor formation by targeting tumor suppressor proteins, such as TP53 for proteasomal degradation. Mutant CREBBP and EP300 proteins are deficient in acetylating BCL6 and TP53 which leads to the constitutive activation of BCL6 and to decreased TP53 tumor suppressor activity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

