xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) is a subtype of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL). PMBL mainly affects young female patients with a median age of 30-35 years with the mediastinum being the predominant site of lymphoma manifestation. PMBL tumor cells seem to originate from a rare B-cell subpopulation which resides in the thymus. In the past, PMBL patients were mainly diagnosed by clinical and pathological criteria. Several gene expression profiling studies have demonstrated that PMBLs are characterized by a specific gene expression profile that clearly distinguishes them from GCB and ABC DLBCLs. However, these studies also indicate that PMBL cannot be diagnosed reliably by current diagnostic methods alone since approximately 25% of cases assigned as PMBL by conventional criteria was not confirmed by gene expression profiling. These cases represented other DLBCL subtypes with mediastinal involvement. Interestingly, PMBLs clinically and biologically resemble nodular sclerosis Hodgkin's lymphoma.    Signaling description: PMBL is characterized by the constitutive activation of NF-kB signaling. Approximately 30% of all cases harbor inactivating aberrations of TNFAIP3 (A20), which is an ubiquitin-modifying enzyme that inhibits NF-kB activation. In about 20% of samples, the amplification and overexpression of the REL locus have been identified. REL is a transcription factor that is a member of the REL/NF-kB family which is responsible for DNA binding, dimerization, nuclear localization, and binding to the NF-kB inhibitor. In addition, a recurrent genetic aberration detected in approximately 45% of PMBL samples is the gain or amplification of JAK2. SOCS1, which is a negative regulator of the JAK2/STAT pathway, is recurrently inactivated in PMBL. Thus, the overexpression of JAK2 and/or inactivation of SOCS1 contribute to continuous JAK2/STAT signaling in PMBL. Furthermore, CIITA translocations are highly recurrent in PMBL and occur in roughly 40% of cases. Overall, PMBL may be successfully treated with conventional chemotherapy plus rituximab.    Outcome effects: CIITA translocations are associated with the downregulation of surface HLA class II expression and the overexpression of ligands of the receptor molecule programmed cell death 1 or PDL1 (CD274) and PDCD1LG2 (CD273 and PDL2). This leads to the suppression of antitumor T-cell response. CD274 and PDCD1LG2 may also be overexpressed due to mutations. In conclusion, the resulting signaling in PMBL inhibits plasma cell differentiation and leads to increased proliferation and block of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) is a subtype of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL). PMBL mainly affects young female patients with a median age of 30-35 years with the mediastinum being the predominant site of lymphoma manifestation. PMBL tumor cells seem to originate from a rare B-cell subpopulation which resides in the thymus. In the past, PMBL patients were mainly diagnosed by clinical and pathological criteria. Several gene expression profiling studies have demonstrated that PMBLs are characterized by a specific gene expression profile that clearly distinguishes them from GCB and ABC DLBCLs. However, these studies also indicate that PMBL cannot be diagnosed reliably by current diagnostic methods alone since approximately 25% of cases assigned as PMBL by conventional criteria was not confirmed by gene expression profiling. These cases represented other DLBCL subtypes with mediastinal involvement. Interestingly, PMBLs clinically and biologically resemble nodular sclerosis Hodgkin's lymphoma.    Signaling description: PMBL is characterized by the constitutive activation of NF-kB signaling. Approximately 30% of all cases harbor inactivating aberrations of TNFAIP3 (A20), which is an ubiquitin-modifying enzyme that inhibits NF-kB activation. In about 20% of samples, the amplification and overexpression of the REL locus have been identified. REL is a transcription factor that is a member of the REL/NF-kB family which is responsible for DNA binding, dimerization, nuclear localization, and binding to the NF-kB inhibitor. In addition, a recurrent genetic aberration detected in approximately 45% of PMBL samples is the gain or amplification of JAK2. SOCS1, which is a negative regulator of the JAK2/STAT pathway, is recurrently inactivated in PMBL. Thus, the overexpression of JAK2 and/or inactivation of SOCS1 contribute to continuous JAK2/STAT signaling in PMBL. Furthermore, CIITA translocations are highly recurrent in PMBL and occur in roughly 40% of cases. Overall, PMBL may be successfully treated with conventional chemotherapy plus rituximab.    Outcome effects: CIITA translocations are associated with the downregulation of surface HLA class II expression and the overexpression of ligands of the receptor molecule programmed cell death 1 or PDL1 (CD274) and PDCD1LG2 (CD273 and PDL2). This leads to the suppression of antitumor T-cell response. CD274 and PDCD1LG2 may also be overexpressed due to mutations. In conclusion, the resulting signaling in PMBL inhibits plasma cell differentiation and leads to increased proliferation and block of apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

