xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. Dysregulation of cell cycle is a key point in melanomagenesis.    Signaling description: Many melanocytic signaling pathways are initiated through the activation of the KIT receptor, which in turn triggers MAPK and PI3K pathways activation. Notably, KIT aberrations are found in 28-39% of melanoma cases, while the mutagenic activation of the MAPK pathway is observed in up to 90% of melanoma cases and is most commonly achieved via mutations in NRAS or BRAF. The latter occur at the stage of the benign nevus. As a result, constant MAPK activation drives cell cycle progression. MAPK may also be activated via UV/ROS-induced PKC-activation. In addition, 43-50% of melanoma cases have selective constitutive activity in AKT3 due to a mutation or gene amplification. Furthermore, CDKN2A and CDK4 mutations are associated with the familial form of melanoma, while CCND1 and CDKN2B may be sporadically mutated. Lastly, MITF directly binds to the promoter regions of CDKN2A, CDKN1A, and CCND1 and positively regulates their transcription.    Outcome effects: Defects in cell cycle checkpoint control are associated with the pathogenesis of melanoma. As a result of NRAS and BRAF gene mutations frequently found in melanoma cells, constant MAPK activation drives cell cycle progression. Genes directly involved in cell cycle G1-S transition including CDKN2A, CDK4, CCND1, and CDKN2B are often mutated in melanoma.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Genes mutated in melanoma are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. Dysregulation of cell cycle is a key point in melanomagenesis.    Signaling description: Many melanocytic signaling pathways are initiated through the activation of the KIT receptor, which in turn triggers MAPK and PI3K pathways activation. Notably, KIT aberrations are found in 28-39% of melanoma cases, while the mutagenic activation of the MAPK pathway is observed in up to 90% of melanoma cases and is most commonly achieved via mutations in NRAS or BRAF. The latter occur at the stage of the benign nevus. As a result, constant MAPK activation drives cell cycle progression. MAPK may also be activated via UV/ROS-induced PKC-activation. In addition, 43-50% of melanoma cases have selective constitutive activity in AKT3 due to a mutation or gene amplification. Furthermore, CDKN2A and CDK4 mutations are associated with the familial form of melanoma, while CCND1 and CDKN2B may be sporadically mutated. Lastly, MITF directly binds to the promoter regions of CDKN2A, CDKN1A, and CCND1 and positively regulates their transcription.    Outcome effects: Defects in cell cycle checkpoint control are associated with the pathogenesis of melanoma. As a result of NRAS and BRAF gene mutations frequently found in melanoma cells, constant MAPK activation drives cell cycle progression. Genes directly involved in cell cycle G1-S transition including CDKN2A, CDK4, CCND1, and CDKN2B are often mutated in melanoma.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Genes mutated in melanoma are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

