xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma.    Signaling description: Apoptosis is constantly downregulated in melanoma. BCL2, MCL1, BCL2L1, BCL2L11, and BIRC7 are overexpressed, while the expression of BAX and APAF1 is downregulated. This activation/inhibition results from constant KIT/PI3K and RAS/MAPK activation due to KIT, BRAF, NRAS, and PTEN mutations. TP53 and MDM2 are found to be mutated in 10-15% of melanoma cases. MITF regulates the transcription of BCL2, BIRC7, and HIF1A and may have an antiapoptotic effect.    Outcome effects: The molecular mechanisms underlying the block of apoptosis in melanoma include down-regulation of apoptotic factors and activation of anti-apoptotic factors e.g. MCL1, BIRC7, as well as TP53, MDM2 and others.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Genes mutated in melanoma are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma.    Signaling description: Apoptosis is constantly downregulated in melanoma. BCL2, MCL1, BCL2L1, BCL2L11, and BIRC7 are overexpressed, while the expression of BAX and APAF1 is downregulated. This activation/inhibition results from constant KIT/PI3K and RAS/MAPK activation due to KIT, BRAF, NRAS, and PTEN mutations. TP53 and MDM2 are found to be mutated in 10-15% of melanoma cases. MITF regulates the transcription of BCL2, BIRC7, and HIF1A and may have an antiapoptotic effect.    Outcome effects: The molecular mechanisms underlying the block of apoptosis in melanoma include down-regulation of apoptotic factors and activation of anti-apoptotic factors e.g. MCL1, BIRC7, as well as TP53, MDM2 and others.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Genes mutated in melanoma are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

