xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. The metastatic melanoma is the most fatal stage of disease.    Signaling description: The molecular changes that lead to the metastatic progression of melanoma include loss of E-cadherin (CDH1), as well as an increased expression of N-cadherin (CDH2), vitronectin receptor (integrin), and matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). Normally, CDH1 is expressed in melanocytes and keratinocytes where it positively regulates the association of the two cell types. CDH2 is responsible for interacting with other CDH2-expressing cells such as dermal fibroblasts and vascular endothelium and enables the metastatic spread. Vitronectin receptor (integrin) induces the expression of MMP2 which is an enzyme that degrades collagen in basement membranes, increases BCL2 expression and stimulates the motility of melanoma cells through reorganization of cytoskeleton. In addition, the activation of NODAL has a role in melanocyte dedifferentiation and is regulated by NOTCH4 signaling. POU5F1 driven by EPAS1 is also involved in dedifferentiation. Furthermore, WNT5A is over-expressed in melanoma. It downregulates CDH1, while, KISS, which is a metastasis suppressor, up-regulates VIM and tumor homing and metastasis antigen (CD44). Finally, overexpressed SNAI1 and SNAI2 are the key players in epithelial-mesenchymal transition which is a crucial phenomenon during invasion and metastasis of melanoma.    Outcome effects: The progression of melanoma to an invasive and metastatic phase is associated with the loss of normal intercellular connections, increased motility, and dedifferentiation caused by a number of molecular alterations including loss of E-cadherin (CDH1) and an increased expression of N-cadherin (CDH2), vitronectin receptor (integrin), and matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). The over-expression of SNAI1 and SNAI2 proteins in melanoma activates the epithelial-mesenchymal transition and metastatis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Genes mutated in melanoma are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. The metastatic melanoma is the most fatal stage of disease.    Signaling description: The molecular changes that lead to the metastatic progression of melanoma include loss of E-cadherin (CDH1), as well as an increased expression of N-cadherin (CDH2), vitronectin receptor (integrin), and matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). Normally, CDH1 is expressed in melanocytes and keratinocytes where it positively regulates the association of the two cell types. CDH2 is responsible for interacting with other CDH2-expressing cells such as dermal fibroblasts and vascular endothelium and enables the metastatic spread. Vitronectin receptor (integrin) induces the expression of MMP2 which is an enzyme that degrades collagen in basement membranes, increases BCL2 expression and stimulates the motility of melanoma cells through reorganization of cytoskeleton. In addition, the activation of NODAL has a role in melanocyte dedifferentiation and is regulated by NOTCH4 signaling. POU5F1 driven by EPAS1 is also involved in dedifferentiation. Furthermore, WNT5A is over-expressed in melanoma. It downregulates CDH1, while, KISS, which is a metastasis suppressor, up-regulates VIM and tumor homing and metastasis antigen (CD44). Finally, overexpressed SNAI1 and SNAI2 are the key players in epithelial-mesenchymal transition which is a crucial phenomenon during invasion and metastasis of melanoma.    Outcome effects: The progression of melanoma to an invasive and metastatic phase is associated with the loss of normal intercellular connections, increased motility, and dedifferentiation caused by a number of molecular alterations including loss of E-cadherin (CDH1) and an increased expression of N-cadherin (CDH2), vitronectin receptor (integrin), and matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). The over-expression of SNAI1 and SNAI2 proteins in melanoma activates the epithelial-mesenchymal transition and metastatis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Genes mutated in melanoma are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

