xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. MITF is a key regulator of melanocyte development and differentiation and also functions as an oncogene in melanoma cells.    Signaling description: Ultraviolet (UV) radiation stimulates skin keratinocytes to release POMC, which binds to the melanocyte receptor MC1R resulting in the expression of MITF. WNT3A/CTNNB1 signaling also induces MITF expression. PAX3, SOX10, CREB1, and TCF4/LEF1 bind directly to the MITF promoter. These proteins along with MITF are tightly involved in the melanocyte development and melanomagenesis. Firstly, MITF regulates the expression of genes responsible for melanin production and transportation to keratinocytes such as TYR, TYRP1, DCT, MLANA, PMEL, RAB27A, and others. Melanin is an important chromophore in the skin and is able to absorb UV radiation and visible light. It is also able to scavenge molecular oxygen and hydroxyl radicals thereby protecting DNA from UV damage. In addition, MITF plays both cancer-promoting and cancer-inhibiting roles in melanoma. Most likely, a low level of MITF expression promotes proliferation in melanoma, whereas a high level of MITF expression promotes differentiation through the induction of cellular senescence and melanin production. MITF also has an ability to up-regulate the transcription of both melanoma-promoting genes (CCND1, BCL2, and MET) and melanoma-repressing genes (CDKN2A, CDKN1A, and DIAPH1). APEX1 is regulated by MITF and is involved in DNA repair. SNAI1 and SNAI2 are the key players in epithelial-mesenchymal transition, which is a crucial phenomenon during invasion and metastasis of melanoma. SNAI1 and SNAI2 are directly activated by MITF and represses E-cadherin (CDH1) transcription. Finally, MITF may be overexpressed due to a mutation or gene amplification in about 10% of melanoma cases.    Outcome effects: Following exposure to UV radiation, multiple molecular alterations of the MITF related signaling lead to dysregulation of melanocyte differentiation and proliferation and melanomagenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. MITF is a key regulator of melanocyte development and differentiation and also functions as an oncogene in melanoma cells.    Signaling description: Ultraviolet (UV) radiation stimulates skin keratinocytes to release POMC, which binds to the melanocyte receptor MC1R resulting in the expression of MITF. WNT3A/CTNNB1 signaling also induces MITF expression. PAX3, SOX10, CREB1, and TCF4/LEF1 bind directly to the MITF promoter. These proteins along with MITF are tightly involved in the melanocyte development and melanomagenesis. Firstly, MITF regulates the expression of genes responsible for melanin production and transportation to keratinocytes such as TYR, TYRP1, DCT, MLANA, PMEL, RAB27A, and others. Melanin is an important chromophore in the skin and is able to absorb UV radiation and visible light. It is also able to scavenge molecular oxygen and hydroxyl radicals thereby protecting DNA from UV damage. In addition, MITF plays both cancer-promoting and cancer-inhibiting roles in melanoma. Most likely, a low level of MITF expression promotes proliferation in melanoma, whereas a high level of MITF expression promotes differentiation through the induction of cellular senescence and melanin production. MITF also has an ability to up-regulate the transcription of both melanoma-promoting genes (CCND1, BCL2, and MET) and melanoma-repressing genes (CDKN2A, CDKN1A, and DIAPH1). APEX1 is regulated by MITF and is involved in DNA repair. SNAI1 and SNAI2 are the key players in epithelial-mesenchymal transition, which is a crucial phenomenon during invasion and metastasis of melanoma. SNAI1 and SNAI2 are directly activated by MITF and represses E-cadherin (CDH1) transcription. Finally, MITF may be overexpressed due to a mutation or gene amplification in about 10% of melanoma cases.    Outcome effects: Following exposure to UV radiation, multiple molecular alterations of the MITF related signaling lead to dysregulation of melanocyte differentiation and proliferation and melanomagenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

