xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. Studies have shown that dysregulation of the expression of microRNA (miRNA) genes contribute to melanoma development and progression.    Signaling description: MiRNAs are a set of small (ca 22 nucleotides) single-stranded non-protein-coding RNA molecules, which can recognize and bind 3'-untranslated regions of mRNA and block translation of the gene or induce cleavage of the mRNA. Relatively few studies have focused on the role of miRNAs in the pathogenesis and development of melanoma. MIR137 has been identified as a trigger for micropthalmia-associated transcription factor (MITF), a master regulator of melanocyte development and survival of significant functional importance in melanoma. In addition, MIR221 and MIR222 possess an oncogenic role in melanoma and other cancers when their expression is deregulated. Furthermore, it is likely that MIR34B, MIR34C, and MIR199A1 have the capacity to impair MET-mediated invasive growth.    Outcome effects: MIR137, MIR221, MIR222, MIR34B, MIR34C, MIR199A1 and others possess an oncogenic role in melanoma. MiRNAs interact with melanoma related-signaling hubs including the key regulator of melanocyte development MITF, and thus contribute to the biology and progression of melanoma.    Highlighted proteins: MiRNAs with increased expression are highlighted in red, and miRNAs with decreased expression are highlighted in blue. Double highlighted miRNAs can be either over- or underexpressed."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Melanoma is the most aggressive form of skin cancer caused by malignant transformation of melanocytes. It accounts for only 4-7% of all skin malignancies; yet, it is responsible for about 80% of all skin tumor deaths. The strongest risk factors for melanoma are a family history of melanoma, multiple benign or atypical nevi, and a previous melanoma. The exposure to ultraviolet radiation is one of the primary triggers of melanoma. Studies have shown that dysregulation of the expression of microRNA (miRNA) genes contribute to melanoma development and progression.    Signaling description: MiRNAs are a set of small (ca 22 nucleotides) single-stranded non-protein-coding RNA molecules, which can recognize and bind 3'-untranslated regions of mRNA and block translation of the gene or induce cleavage of the mRNA. Relatively few studies have focused on the role of miRNAs in the pathogenesis and development of melanoma. MIR137 has been identified as a trigger for micropthalmia-associated transcription factor (MITF), a master regulator of melanocyte development and survival of significant functional importance in melanoma. In addition, MIR221 and MIR222 possess an oncogenic role in melanoma and other cancers when their expression is deregulated. Furthermore, it is likely that MIR34B, MIR34C, and MIR199A1 have the capacity to impair MET-mediated invasive growth.    Outcome effects: MIR137, MIR221, MIR222, MIR34B, MIR34C, MIR199A1 and others possess an oncogenic role in melanoma. MiRNAs interact with melanoma related-signaling hubs including the key regulator of melanocyte development MITF, and thus contribute to the biology and progression of melanoma.    Highlighted proteins: MiRNAs with increased expression are highlighted in red, and miRNAs with decreased expression are highlighted in blue. Double highlighted miRNAs can be either over- or underexpressed." EXACT []

