xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acute monocytic leukemia (AML-M5) accounts for 10% of adult acute myeloid leukemia. Based on World Health Organization (WHO) criteria, AML-M5 is diagnosed when more than 80% of leukemic blasts are of the monocytic lineage. A further subclassification (M5a and M5b) depends on whether the monocytic cells are predominantly monoblasts (>80%) (acute monoblastic leukemia) or a mixture of monoblasts and promonocytes (<80% blasts).    Signaling description: About 40% of AML-M5 patients have FLT3 and KIT mutations. MLL gene translocations occur in up to 50% of cases of AML in infants and in 80% of monoblastic variants of AML in infants and young children. MLL translocations are also found in approximately 10% of AML cases in adults. FLT3 and KIT are hematopoietic growth factor receptors of the same family of tyrosine kinases and are crucial mediators of stem-and progenitor-cell proliferation and survival. The major signal transduction pathways leading from FLT3 and KIT include PI3K/AKT1 and RAS/MAPK. One of the most frequent MLL gene translocation is MLL-AF10.MLL regulates target HOXA9 and MEIS1 gene expression through direct binding to promoter sequences     Outcome effects: The expression of target genes leads to the inhibition of myeloblast differentiation and apoptosis while activating cell cycle and proliferation. MLL regulated HOXA9 and MEIS1 gene expression leads to the block of differentiation, stimulation of proliferation, and self-renewal of leukemic population.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    References: 1). Rice K., Buzzai M., Altman J., Licht J (2011) The molecular basis of acute myeloid leukemia. Hematology Education:the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.5:27-35. 2). Tallman MS, et al. (2004) Acute monocytic leukemia (French-American-British classification M5) does not have a worse prognosis than other subtypes of acute myeloid leukemia: a report from the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group. J Clin Oncol. 22(7):1276-86."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acute monocytic leukemia (AML-M5) accounts for 10% of adult acute myeloid leukemia. Based on World Health Organization (WHO) criteria, AML-M5 is diagnosed when more than 80% of leukemic blasts are of the monocytic lineage. A further subclassification (M5a and M5b) depends on whether the monocytic cells are predominantly monoblasts (>80%) (acute monoblastic leukemia) or a mixture of monoblasts and promonocytes (<80% blasts).    Signaling description: About 40% of AML-M5 patients have FLT3 and KIT mutations. MLL gene translocations occur in up to 50% of cases of AML in infants and in 80% of monoblastic variants of AML in infants and young children. MLL translocations are also found in approximately 10% of AML cases in adults. FLT3 and KIT are hematopoietic growth factor receptors of the same family of tyrosine kinases and are crucial mediators of stem-and progenitor-cell proliferation and survival. The major signal transduction pathways leading from FLT3 and KIT include PI3K/AKT1 and RAS/MAPK. One of the most frequent MLL gene translocation is MLL-AF10.MLL regulates target HOXA9 and MEIS1 gene expression through direct binding to promoter sequences     Outcome effects: The expression of target genes leads to the inhibition of myeloblast differentiation and apoptosis while activating cell cycle and proliferation. MLL regulated HOXA9 and MEIS1 gene expression leads to the block of differentiation, stimulation of proliferation, and self-renewal of leukemic population.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    References: 1). Rice K., Buzzai M., Altman J., Licht J (2011) The molecular basis of acute myeloid leukemia. Hematology Education:the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.5:27-35. 2). Tallman MS, et al. (2004) Acute monocytic leukemia (French-American-British classification M5) does not have a worse prognosis than other subtypes of acute myeloid leukemia: a report from the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group. J Clin Oncol. 22(7):1276-86." EXACT []

