xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy that is characterized by the uncontrolled increase in the amount of myeloid progenitors. A block of differentiation may happen at any stage of myeloblast development in different subtypes of AML. The inhibition of apoptosis is one of the key processes in acute AML development and is a collection of several mechanisms.    Signaling description: PML-RAR blocks apoptosis through p53 signaling, while AML1-ETO blocks this process through BCL2. MLL modifies PPP1R15A function and thus inhibits apoptosis. In addition, nucleophosmin (NPM1) mutations are frequent in karyotypically normal AML, occurring in 50-60% of such cases. NPM1 acts as a cellular p53 negative regulator to protect hematopoietic cells from apoptosis. FLT3, a receptor tyrosine kinase is overexpressed in the majority of AML cases and mutated in about 35% of them. FLT3 activates RAS, PI3K, AKT1 and STAT which leads to PIM1/2 activation and BCL2L1 hyperexpression. Furthermore, proto-oncogene, c-kit (KIT) mutations occur in about 20-30% of AML patients. It activates JAK/STAT and PI3K leading to the inhibition of apoptosis. Lastly, KRAS and NRAS mutations are present in approximately 15-25% of AML patients and also have an impact on antiapoptotic signaling.    Outcome effects: In AML, numerous fusion proteins interact with mediators of apoptosis. The inhibition of apoptosis leads to the immortalization of leukemia cells and development of the disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy that is characterized by the uncontrolled increase in the amount of myeloid progenitors. A block of differentiation may happen at any stage of myeloblast development in different subtypes of AML. The inhibition of apoptosis is one of the key processes in acute AML development and is a collection of several mechanisms.    Signaling description: PML-RAR blocks apoptosis through p53 signaling, while AML1-ETO blocks this process through BCL2. MLL modifies PPP1R15A function and thus inhibits apoptosis. In addition, nucleophosmin (NPM1) mutations are frequent in karyotypically normal AML, occurring in 50-60% of such cases. NPM1 acts as a cellular p53 negative regulator to protect hematopoietic cells from apoptosis. FLT3, a receptor tyrosine kinase is overexpressed in the majority of AML cases and mutated in about 35% of them. FLT3 activates RAS, PI3K, AKT1 and STAT which leads to PIM1/2 activation and BCL2L1 hyperexpression. Furthermore, proto-oncogene, c-kit (KIT) mutations occur in about 20-30% of AML patients. It activates JAK/STAT and PI3K leading to the inhibition of apoptosis. Lastly, KRAS and NRAS mutations are present in approximately 15-25% of AML patients and also have an impact on antiapoptotic signaling.    Outcome effects: In AML, numerous fusion proteins interact with mediators of apoptosis. The inhibition of apoptosis leads to the immortalization of leukemia cells and development of the disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

