xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy that is characterized by the uncontrolled increase in the amount of myeloid progenitors. According to the French-American-British (FAB) classification, AML is divided into eight subtypes, M0 through M7, based on the type of cell from which the leukemia developed and its degree of maturity. A block of differentiation may happen at any stage of myeloblast development in the different subtypes. Mutations and translocations of genes responsible for myeloid differentiation and development are the molecular basis of AML development.    Signaling description: The most frequent mutations affect FLT3, KIT, and RAS. FLT3 and KIT are hematopoietic growth factor receptors of the family of tyrosine kinases (RTKs). Major signal transduction pathways leading from FLT3 and KIT include PI3K and RAS/MAPK. The expression of target genes leads to the inhibition of myeloblast differentiation and apoptosis while activating cell cycle and proliferation. RAS oncogenes are key molecules in signal transduction pathways through RTKs. Activating mutations of RAS are found in approximately 25% of AML cases and occur most frequently in NRAS. The hematopoietic factors, CEBPA, SPI1, and RUNX1 may be inactivated due to mutations in AML. The inactivation of CEBPA leads to block of differentiation of blasts in M1, 2, and 4 variants of AML. The inactivation of SPI1 plays the same role in AML M0, 4, 5, and 6 while the inactivation of RUNX1 occurs in AML M0 and 2. Nucleophosmin (NPM1) mutations are common in karyotypically normal AML, occurring in 50-60% of such cases. NPM1 acts as a cellular p53 negative regulator to protect hematopoietic cells from apoptosis. About 50-55% of AML cases are characterized by the presence of specific balanced chromosome abnormalities involved in the formation of specific fusion genes that code for fusion proteins which play a key role in the development of leukemia. The retinoic acid receptor (RARA) chimeric proteins, PML-RAR, PLZF-RAR and NPM-RAR are typical for AML M3 and have been associated with acute promyelocytic leukemia. Translocations involving MLL gene are typical for AML M5, with the most frequent being MLL-AF10. Chromosomal translocation products, AML-ETO1, AML1-MTG16, and AML1-MDS1-EVI1 act as activated oncogenes in AML M2. The chromosomal translocation product, CBFB-MYH11 acts as an oncogene in AML M4.    Outcome effects: The aforementioned genetic events cause block of differentiation, inhibition of apoptosis, and increased cell proliferation which are the main mechanisms of AML development.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Delwel R (2012) Molecular biology of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.6:23-32. 2). Rice K., Buzzai M., Altman J., Licht J (2011) The molecular basis of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.5:27-35."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy that is characterized by the uncontrolled increase in the amount of myeloid progenitors. According to the French-American-British (FAB) classification, AML is divided into eight subtypes, M0 through M7, based on the type of cell from which the leukemia developed and its degree of maturity. A block of differentiation may happen at any stage of myeloblast development in the different subtypes. Mutations and translocations of genes responsible for myeloid differentiation and development are the molecular basis of AML development.    Signaling description: The most frequent mutations affect FLT3, KIT, and RAS. FLT3 and KIT are hematopoietic growth factor receptors of the family of tyrosine kinases (RTKs). Major signal transduction pathways leading from FLT3 and KIT include PI3K and RAS/MAPK. The expression of target genes leads to the inhibition of myeloblast differentiation and apoptosis while activating cell cycle and proliferation. RAS oncogenes are key molecules in signal transduction pathways through RTKs. Activating mutations of RAS are found in approximately 25% of AML cases and occur most frequently in NRAS. The hematopoietic factors, CEBPA, SPI1, and RUNX1 may be inactivated due to mutations in AML. The inactivation of CEBPA leads to block of differentiation of blasts in M1, 2, and 4 variants of AML. The inactivation of SPI1 plays the same role in AML M0, 4, 5, and 6 while the inactivation of RUNX1 occurs in AML M0 and 2. Nucleophosmin (NPM1) mutations are common in karyotypically normal AML, occurring in 50-60% of such cases. NPM1 acts as a cellular p53 negative regulator to protect hematopoietic cells from apoptosis. About 50-55% of AML cases are characterized by the presence of specific balanced chromosome abnormalities involved in the formation of specific fusion genes that code for fusion proteins which play a key role in the development of leukemia. The retinoic acid receptor (RARA) chimeric proteins, PML-RAR, PLZF-RAR and NPM-RAR are typical for AML M3 and have been associated with acute promyelocytic leukemia. Translocations involving MLL gene are typical for AML M5, with the most frequent being MLL-AF10. Chromosomal translocation products, AML-ETO1, AML1-MTG16, and AML1-MDS1-EVI1 act as activated oncogenes in AML M2. The chromosomal translocation product, CBFB-MYH11 acts as an oncogene in AML M4.    Outcome effects: The aforementioned genetic events cause block of differentiation, inhibition of apoptosis, and increased cell proliferation which are the main mechanisms of AML development.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Delwel R (2012) Molecular biology of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.6:23-32. 2). Rice K., Buzzai M., Altman J., Licht J (2011) The molecular basis of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.5:27-35." EXACT []

