xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy that is characterized by the uncontrolled increase in the amount of myeloid progenitors. A block of differentiation may happen at any stage of myeloblast development in different subtypes of AML.    Signaling description: FLT3 is a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) that plays an important role in hematopoietic stem and progenitor cell survival and proliferation. FLT3 mutations occur in approximately 30-40% of all AML cases. These mutations lead to the constitutive activation of the FLT3 signaling pathway and downstream targets which enhances proliferation and survival. RAS oncogenes are key molecules in signal transduction pathways through RTKs. Activating mutations of RAS are found in approximately 25% of AML cases and occur more frequently in NRAS than in KRAS or HRAS. The hematopoietic factors, CEBPA, SPI1, and RUNX1 may be inactivated due to mutations in AML. The inactivation of CEBPA leads to block of differentiation of blasts in M1, 2, and 4 variants of AML. The inactivation of SPI1 plays the same role in AML M0, 4, 5, and 6 while the inactivation of RUNX1 occurs in AML M0 and 2. Chromosomal translocation products, AML-ETO1, AML1-MTG16, and AML1-MDS1-EVI1 act as activated oncogenes in AML M2. The chromosomal translocation product, CBFB-MYH11 acts as an oncogene in AML M4.    Outcome effects: The above mentioned genetic changes enhance cell survival and proliferation and inhibit myeloid differentiation.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Delwel R (2012) Molecular biology of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.6:23-32. 2). Petrie K., Zelent A. (2007) AML1/ETO, a promiscuous fusion oncoprotein. Blood.100(10): 4109-10. 3). Rice K., Buzzai M., Altman J., Licht J (2011) The molecular basis of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.5:27-35. 4). Van Wijnen A., Stein G. (2007) The CBFB-MYH11 butterfly effect in hematopoiesis. Blood. 100(8) 3131-32."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a hematological malignancy that is characterized by the uncontrolled increase in the amount of myeloid progenitors. A block of differentiation may happen at any stage of myeloblast development in different subtypes of AML.    Signaling description: FLT3 is a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) that plays an important role in hematopoietic stem and progenitor cell survival and proliferation. FLT3 mutations occur in approximately 30-40% of all AML cases. These mutations lead to the constitutive activation of the FLT3 signaling pathway and downstream targets which enhances proliferation and survival. RAS oncogenes are key molecules in signal transduction pathways through RTKs. Activating mutations of RAS are found in approximately 25% of AML cases and occur more frequently in NRAS than in KRAS or HRAS. The hematopoietic factors, CEBPA, SPI1, and RUNX1 may be inactivated due to mutations in AML. The inactivation of CEBPA leads to block of differentiation of blasts in M1, 2, and 4 variants of AML. The inactivation of SPI1 plays the same role in AML M0, 4, 5, and 6 while the inactivation of RUNX1 occurs in AML M0 and 2. Chromosomal translocation products, AML-ETO1, AML1-MTG16, and AML1-MDS1-EVI1 act as activated oncogenes in AML M2. The chromosomal translocation product, CBFB-MYH11 acts as an oncogene in AML M4.    Outcome effects: The above mentioned genetic changes enhance cell survival and proliferation and inhibit myeloid differentiation.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Delwel R (2012) Molecular biology of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.6:23-32. 2). Petrie K., Zelent A. (2007) AML1/ETO, a promiscuous fusion oncoprotein. Blood.100(10): 4109-10. 3). Rice K., Buzzai M., Altman J., Licht J (2011) The molecular basis of acute myeloid leukemia.Hematology Education: the education program for the annual congress of the European Hematology Association.5:27-35. 4). Van Wijnen A., Stein G. (2007) The CBFB-MYH11 butterfly effect in hematopoiesis. Blood. 100(8) 3131-32." EXACT []

