xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is the most common type of pancreatic cancer and is the fourth leading cause of cancer related deaths. PDAC has a very poor prognosis due to the inability to diagnose the disease at an early and localized stage. Approximately 90% of all pancreatic tumors metastasize beyond the pancreas. Epidemiological and experimental studies have suggested a link between pancreatic cancer to smoking, diabetes, and a high-fat diet. However, it is not clear how these factors influence pancreatic cancer. It is generally believed that PDAC and cancers of exocrine pancreas originate from ductal cells.    Signaling description: PDAC is characterized by mutations in PTEN, KRAS, BRAF, TP53, SMAD4, ARID1, BRCA1/2, MLH1/2/6, STK11, CDKN2A, ATM, and TGFBR2 genes as well as the overexpression of growth factors and their receptors (EGFRs, VEGFRs, MET, and IGF1R), NOTCH1, WNT, and AKT1.    Outcome effects: These changes cause block of apoptosis, disruption of cell cycle and differentiation, redundant proliferation and migration of epithelial pancreatic cells, block of chromatin remodeling and DNA repair, and vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is the most common type of pancreatic cancer and is the fourth leading cause of cancer related deaths. PDAC has a very poor prognosis due to the inability to diagnose the disease at an early and localized stage. Approximately 90% of all pancreatic tumors metastasize beyond the pancreas. Epidemiological and experimental studies have suggested a link between pancreatic cancer to smoking, diabetes, and a high-fat diet. However, it is not clear how these factors influence pancreatic cancer. It is generally believed that PDAC and cancers of exocrine pancreas originate from ductal cells.    Signaling description: PDAC is characterized by mutations in PTEN, KRAS, BRAF, TP53, SMAD4, ARID1, BRCA1/2, MLH1/2/6, STK11, CDKN2A, ATM, and TGFBR2 genes as well as the overexpression of growth factors and their receptors (EGFRs, VEGFRs, MET, and IGF1R), NOTCH1, WNT, and AKT1.    Outcome effects: These changes cause block of apoptosis, disruption of cell cycle and differentiation, redundant proliferation and migration of epithelial pancreatic cells, block of chromatin remodeling and DNA repair, and vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

