xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic cancer is malignant transformation of the pancreas, and is characterized by mutations in PTEN, KRAS, BRAF, and TP53 genes as well as overexpression of growth factors and their receptors including EGFRs, VEGFRs, MET, and IGF1R.    Signaling description: The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases consists of four related members including EGFR, ERBB2, ERBB3, and ERBB4. The family of vascular endothelial growth factor receptors consists of FLT1, KDR, and FLT4. Ligand-bound EGFRs, VEGFRs, MET, TGFBR1, and IGF1R activate PIK3CA via SRC and IRS1 proteins. PIK3CA, via PIP3 and PDPK1, activates AKT1. Activated AKT1 promotes several essential pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. Further, AKT1 inhibits GSK3B which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). In addition, activated AKT1 inhibits FOXO1 and FOXO3. AKT1 also phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c (CYCS) from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. Finally, growth factor receptors regulate JAK1/2 (Janus kinases 1/2) which are involved in cell growth and activate KRAS and BRAF via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1.    Outcome effects: Overall, these alterations cause constitutive activation of AKT1 cascades and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades which leads to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation, and migration of epithelial pancreatic cells. Specifically, MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. In the nucleus, activated beta-catenin interacts with TCF4 and LEF1 to induce the expression of downstream target genes which are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Further, BRAF activates ERK (MAPK1/3) signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, migration, and vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic cancer is malignant transformation of the pancreas, and is characterized by mutations in PTEN, KRAS, BRAF, and TP53 genes as well as overexpression of growth factors and their receptors including EGFRs, VEGFRs, MET, and IGF1R.    Signaling description: The ERBB family of receptor tyrosine kinases consists of four related members including EGFR, ERBB2, ERBB3, and ERBB4. The family of vascular endothelial growth factor receptors consists of FLT1, KDR, and FLT4. Ligand-bound EGFRs, VEGFRs, MET, TGFBR1, and IGF1R activate PIK3CA via SRC and IRS1 proteins. PIK3CA, via PIP3 and PDPK1, activates AKT1. Activated AKT1 promotes several essential pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates CREB1 and MTOR while inhibiting TP53. CREB1 regulates the transcription of BCL2. Further, AKT1 inhibits GSK3B which leads to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). In addition, activated AKT1 inhibits FOXO1 and FOXO3. AKT1 also phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c (CYCS) from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. Finally, growth factor receptors regulate JAK1/2 (Janus kinases 1/2) which are involved in cell growth and activate KRAS and BRAF via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1.    Outcome effects: Overall, these alterations cause constitutive activation of AKT1 cascades and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling cascades which leads to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation, and migration of epithelial pancreatic cells. Specifically, MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth, and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. In the nucleus, activated beta-catenin interacts with TCF4 and LEF1 to induce the expression of downstream target genes which are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Further, BRAF activates ERK (MAPK1/3) signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, migration, and vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

