xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic cancer is malignant transformation of the pancreas, and is characterized by mutations in TP53, CDKN2A, and ATM genes. TP53 tumor suppressor gene is mutated in over 80% of pancreatic cancer cases. Further, germline and sporadic mutations in CDKN2A and ATM genes have been identified in pancreatic cancer; therefore, it appears they play a central role as pancreatic tumor suppressors. CDKN2A is mutated in most cases of pancreatic ductal carcinoma.    Signaling description: Normally, TP53 stimulates CDKN1A, CDKN2A, and CDKN1B, which in turn inhibit the activity of CDK2, CDK4, and CDK6 which regulate cell cycle and differentiation. In addition, TP53 promotes BAX resulting in the activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. ATM phosphorylates checkpoint kinase CHEK2 which phosphorylates and stabilizes TP53.    Outcome effects: Mutations in the above mentioned genes lead to redundant proliferation, block of apoptosis, as well as disruption of cell cycle and differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic cancer is malignant transformation of the pancreas, and is characterized by mutations in TP53, CDKN2A, and ATM genes. TP53 tumor suppressor gene is mutated in over 80% of pancreatic cancer cases. Further, germline and sporadic mutations in CDKN2A and ATM genes have been identified in pancreatic cancer; therefore, it appears they play a central role as pancreatic tumor suppressors. CDKN2A is mutated in most cases of pancreatic ductal carcinoma.    Signaling description: Normally, TP53 stimulates CDKN1A, CDKN2A, and CDKN1B, which in turn inhibit the activity of CDK2, CDK4, and CDK6 which regulate cell cycle and differentiation. In addition, TP53 promotes BAX resulting in the activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. ATM phosphorylates checkpoint kinase CHEK2 which phosphorylates and stabilizes TP53.    Outcome effects: Mutations in the above mentioned genes lead to redundant proliferation, block of apoptosis, as well as disruption of cell cycle and differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

