xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (PanNETs) are the second most common malignancy of the pancreas. PanNETs have increased in incidence in recent years. These tumors are usually asymptomatic and may be associated with metastasis. PanNETs may occur sporadically or as part of a hereditary syndrome. Patients may present with symptoms related to hormone secretion by functional tumors or to locally advanced or metastatic nonfunctional tumors.    Signaling description: Most frequently PanNETs are related to mutations in DAXX, ATRX, and MEN1 genes and the overexpression of IGF1R, ERBB2, and SRC. MEN1 is a component of a histone methyltransferase complex and also activates many transcription factors involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and cell cycle. TGFBR2 binds to DAXX which activates MAP3K5 and JNK cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10) resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. DAXX interacts with ATRX which binds to RAD51m which is involved in DNA repair, while ATRX participates in chromatin remodeling. Clinically, mutations in MEN1 and DAXX/ATRX genes are associated with a better prognosis. Ligand-bound ERBB2 and IGF1R activate PIK3CA through SRC and IRS1 proteins. Further, activated PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1, and then activated AKT1 phosphorylates and activates MTOR. In addition, growth factor receptors activate KRAS and BRAF via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1.    Outcome effects: Overall, these alterations cause the constitutive activation of AKT1 cascades and mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation and migration of epithelial pancreatic cells, block of chromatin remodeling, and DNA repair. Specifically, MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. BRAF activates MAPK1/3 signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, migration, and vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (PanNETs) are the second most common malignancy of the pancreas. PanNETs have increased in incidence in recent years. These tumors are usually asymptomatic and may be associated with metastasis. PanNETs may occur sporadically or as part of a hereditary syndrome. Patients may present with symptoms related to hormone secretion by functional tumors or to locally advanced or metastatic nonfunctional tumors.    Signaling description: Most frequently PanNETs are related to mutations in DAXX, ATRX, and MEN1 genes and the overexpression of IGF1R, ERBB2, and SRC. MEN1 is a component of a histone methyltransferase complex and also activates many transcription factors involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and cell cycle. TGFBR2 binds to DAXX which activates MAP3K5 and JNK cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10) resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents the formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. DAXX interacts with ATRX which binds to RAD51m which is involved in DNA repair, while ATRX participates in chromatin remodeling. Clinically, mutations in MEN1 and DAXX/ATRX genes are associated with a better prognosis. Ligand-bound ERBB2 and IGF1R activate PIK3CA through SRC and IRS1 proteins. Further, activated PIK3CA activates AKT1 via PIP3 and PDPK1, and then activated AKT1 phosphorylates and activates MTOR. In addition, growth factor receptors activate KRAS and BRAF via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1.    Outcome effects: Overall, these alterations cause the constitutive activation of AKT1 cascades and mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK) signaling cascades that lead to block of apoptosis, loss of differentiation, proliferation and migration of epithelial pancreatic cells, block of chromatin remodeling, and DNA repair. Specifically, MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. BRAF activates MAPK1/3 signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, migration, and vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

