xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic cancer refers to malignant transformation of the pancreas. BRCA1, BRCA2, and PALB2 are important components of DNA repair pathways. Germline mutations in BRCA2 and PALB2 genes have been detected in a large number of known cases of familial pancreatic cancer. At the same time, the risk of pancreatic cancer in carriers of BRCA1 germline mutations is less established. It has been demonstrated that somatic mutations in ATRX and ARID1A genes, somatic and germline mutations in mismatch repair genes including MLH1, MSH2, and MSH6, high levels of microsatellite instability, and loss of MSH2 and MSH6 expression are all characteristic of pancreatic cancer.Mutations of these genes result in an overall increase in genomic instability and catastrophic DNA damage     Signaling description: Normally, BRCA1 and BRCA2 play a central role in DNA repair, response to DNA damage, prevention of chromosome breaks, and genomic stability. BRCA1 interacts with DNA mismatch repair protein complex MLH1/MSH2/MSH3/MSH6 and the genes that participate in homologous recombination and double-strand break repair including BRCA2, RAD51, BRIP1, PALB2, MRN complex (RAD50/MRE11A/NBN), and PARP1/2. RAD51 binds to ATRX which is involved in chromatin remodeling. In addition, BRCA1 interacts with SMARCA4, an essential component of the chromatin remodeling complex SWI/SNF. SMARCA4 interacts with ARID1A which is a member of the SWI/SNF family. Lastly, BRCA2 participates in the formation of KAT2B/MYSM1 and KAT2A/TADA2A/TADA3 chromatin remodeling complexes.    Outcome effects: Mutations in these genes lead to block of DNA repair and disruption of response to DNA damage."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic cancer refers to malignant transformation of the pancreas. BRCA1, BRCA2, and PALB2 are important components of DNA repair pathways. Germline mutations in BRCA2 and PALB2 genes have been detected in a large number of known cases of familial pancreatic cancer. At the same time, the risk of pancreatic cancer in carriers of BRCA1 germline mutations is less established. It has been demonstrated that somatic mutations in ATRX and ARID1A genes, somatic and germline mutations in mismatch repair genes including MLH1, MSH2, and MSH6, high levels of microsatellite instability, and loss of MSH2 and MSH6 expression are all characteristic of pancreatic cancer.Mutations of these genes result in an overall increase in genomic instability and catastrophic DNA damage     Signaling description: Normally, BRCA1 and BRCA2 play a central role in DNA repair, response to DNA damage, prevention of chromosome breaks, and genomic stability. BRCA1 interacts with DNA mismatch repair protein complex MLH1/MSH2/MSH3/MSH6 and the genes that participate in homologous recombination and double-strand break repair including BRCA2, RAD51, BRIP1, PALB2, MRN complex (RAD50/MRE11A/NBN), and PARP1/2. RAD51 binds to ATRX which is involved in chromatin remodeling. In addition, BRCA1 interacts with SMARCA4, an essential component of the chromatin remodeling complex SWI/SNF. SMARCA4 interacts with ARID1A which is a member of the SWI/SNF family. Lastly, BRCA2 participates in the formation of KAT2B/MYSM1 and KAT2A/TADA2A/TADA3 chromatin remodeling complexes.    Outcome effects: Mutations in these genes lead to block of DNA repair and disruption of response to DNA damage." EXACT []

