xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic cancer refers to malignant transformation of the pancreas. A frequent event associated with pancreatic cancer progression is a mutation in the SMAD4 protein gene. In addition, pancreatic cancer is characterized by the overexpression of TGFB1 and mutations in TGFBR2, KRAS, and BRAF genes. Mutations in DAXX and ATRX are characteristic of neuroendocrine pancreatic cancer.    Signaling description: SMAD2, 3, and 4 are the transcriptional regulators, coactivators, and mediators of signal transduction by TGFB proteins, which control proliferation, cellular differentiation,and other cellular functions. Normally, TGFB proteins bind to TGFBR2 which recruits and phosphorylates TGFBR1. TGFBR1 phosphorylates SMAD2 and SMAD3 which bind to and form a heterotrimeric complex with SMAD4. This complex then enters the cell nucleus where it acts as a transcription factor for various genes involved in cell functions. Ligand-bound TGFBR1 activates KRAS and BRAF via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1. BRAF activates MAPK1/3 signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiationand migration. TGFB proteins may also trigger apoptosis via DAXX. TGFBR2 binds to DAXX which activates MAP3K5 and JNK cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10) resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. DAXX interacts with ATRX which binds to RAD51, which is involved in DNA repair while ATRX participates in chromatin remodeling.    Outcome effects: These mutations lead to redundant proliferation, block of apoptosis and DNA repair, as well as disruption of the cell cycle and differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic cancer refers to malignant transformation of the pancreas. A frequent event associated with pancreatic cancer progression is a mutation in the SMAD4 protein gene. In addition, pancreatic cancer is characterized by the overexpression of TGFB1 and mutations in TGFBR2, KRAS, and BRAF genes. Mutations in DAXX and ATRX are characteristic of neuroendocrine pancreatic cancer.    Signaling description: SMAD2, 3, and 4 are the transcriptional regulators, coactivators, and mediators of signal transduction by TGFB proteins, which control proliferation, cellular differentiation,and other cellular functions. Normally, TGFB proteins bind to TGFBR2 which recruits and phosphorylates TGFBR1. TGFBR1 phosphorylates SMAD2 and SMAD3 which bind to and form a heterotrimeric complex with SMAD4. This complex then enters the cell nucleus where it acts as a transcription factor for various genes involved in cell functions. Ligand-bound TGFBR1 activates KRAS and BRAF via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1. BRAF activates MAPK1/3 signaling pathway inducing genes involved in cell proliferation, differentiationand migration. TGFB proteins may also trigger apoptosis via DAXX. TGFBR2 binds to DAXX which activates MAP3K5 and JNK cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10) resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome c from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade. DAXX interacts with ATRX which binds to RAD51, which is involved in DNA repair while ATRX participates in chromatin remodeling.    Outcome effects: These mutations lead to redundant proliferation, block of apoptosis and DNA repair, as well as disruption of the cell cycle and differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

