xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: It is generally accepted that phosphatidylinositol (3,4,5)-triphosphate (PIP3) binds to and activates serine/threonine kinase Akt also known as protein kinase B (AKT1), whereas tumor suppressor PTEN phosphatase dephosphorylates PIP3 (to phosphatidylinositol (4,5)-diphosphate, PIP2), thus decreasing activity of the AKT1 pathway. AKT1 can also be activated by phosphatidylinositol (3,4)-bisphosphate (PtdIns 3,4-P2), that in turn is dephosphorylated by PTEN. Another phosphatase dephosphorylating PtdIns 3,4-P2 is INPP4B (inositol polyphosphate-4-phosphatase), which is considered a tumor suppressor in epithelial carcinomas. It has been shown that androgen receptor (AR) induces the expression of INPP4B in prostate cancer cells, thus negatively regulating the AKT1 pathway. However, INPP4B depletion activates AKT1 and increases cellular proliferation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several anti-apoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates cAMP responsive element binding protein 1 (CREB1), a mechanistic target of rapamycin (serine/threonine kinase (MTOR)) and inhibits tumor protein p53 (TP53). CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). Beta-catenin then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3) and mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5, also referred to as ASK1), mediated by JNK -cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome C from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: It is generally accepted that phosphatidylinositol (3,4,5)-triphosphate (PIP3) binds to and activates serine/threonine kinase Akt also known as protein kinase B (AKT1), whereas tumor suppressor PTEN phosphatase dephosphorylates PIP3 (to phosphatidylinositol (4,5)-diphosphate, PIP2), thus decreasing activity of the AKT1 pathway. AKT1 can also be activated by phosphatidylinositol (3,4)-bisphosphate (PtdIns 3,4-P2), that in turn is dephosphorylated by PTEN. Another phosphatase dephosphorylating PtdIns 3,4-P2 is INPP4B (inositol polyphosphate-4-phosphatase), which is considered a tumor suppressor in epithelial carcinomas. It has been shown that androgen receptor (AR) induces the expression of INPP4B in prostate cancer cells, thus negatively regulating the AKT1 pathway. However, INPP4B depletion activates AKT1 and increases cellular proliferation.    Outcome effects: Activated AKT1 promotes several anti-apoptotic pathways. AKT1 phosphorylates and activates cAMP responsive element binding protein 1 (CREB1), a mechanistic target of rapamycin (serine/threonine kinase (MTOR)) and inhibits tumor protein p53 (TP53). CREB1 regulates transcription of BCL2. MTOR is a central regulator of cellular metabolism, growth and survival in response to hormones and growth factors. AKT1 inhibits glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B) leading to the stabilization of cytosolic beta-catenin (CTNNB1). Beta-catenin then translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with T-cell factor 4 (TCF4) and lymphoid enhancing factor 1 (LEF1) to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. Activated AKT1 inhibits forkhead box O1/O3 (FOXO1/FOXO3) and mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 5 (MAP3K5, also referred to as ASK1), mediated by JNK -cascade (MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10). In addition, AKT1 phosphorylates and inhibits BAD resulting in the activation of BCL2 and BCL2L1 and suppression of BAX. This prevents formation of mitochondrial transition pore, release of cytochrome C from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm, and activation of apoptotic caspase cascade." EXACT []

