xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Fatty acid synthase (FASN) is a multi-functional protein. Its major function is to catalyze the synthesis of palmitate. It has been demonstrated that FASN is overexpressed in many human cancers including prostate cancer. FASN is transcriptionally regulated by nuclear orphan receptor LXR-alpha (NR1H3) and sterol regulatory element binding transcription factor 1 (SREBF1). Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPARG) activates NR1H3, while runt-related transcription factors (Runt) inhibit NR1H3. MAPK1,3 activate SREBF1, whereas serine/threonine protein kinase B (AKT/PCB) inhibits SREBF1 via glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B). FASN participates in the normal functioning of many systems and processes.    Outcome effects: The overexpression of FASN function leads to a reduction in apoptosis and an increase in cell growth, proliferation, invasion, and prostate tumor initiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Fatty acid synthase (FASN) is a multi-functional protein. Its major function is to catalyze the synthesis of palmitate. It has been demonstrated that FASN is overexpressed in many human cancers including prostate cancer. FASN is transcriptionally regulated by nuclear orphan receptor LXR-alpha (NR1H3) and sterol regulatory element binding transcription factor 1 (SREBF1). Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPARG) activates NR1H3, while runt-related transcription factors (Runt) inhibit NR1H3. MAPK1,3 activate SREBF1, whereas serine/threonine protein kinase B (AKT/PCB) inhibits SREBF1 via glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta (GSK3B). FASN participates in the normal functioning of many systems and processes.    Outcome effects: The overexpression of FASN function leads to a reduction in apoptosis and an increase in cell growth, proliferation, invasion, and prostate tumor initiation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

