xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: FK506 binding protein 5 (FKBP5) is a protein that belongs to the immunophilin protein family. The protein is involved in immunosuppressant signaling, protein folding and trafficking. It has been shown that FKBP5 binds to heat shock protein 90 (HSP90AA1) and modulates the affinity of steroid receptors. Particularly, FKBP5 can influence androgen receptor (AR) stability through its binding to HSP90AA1. Another possible role of FKBP5 in AR signaling is the regulation of glucocorticoid action. Under physiological conditions, FKBP5 participates in the formation of the mature glucocorticoid receptor complex, NR3C1 (nuclear receptor subfamily 3, group C, member 1 also known as glucocorticoid receptor). However, overexpression of FKBP5 was found to reduce NR3C1 hormone binding affinity.    Outcome effects: The reduced hormone binding affinity of NR3C1 may casue increased cell proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis since NR3C1 can regulate these processes through the mechanism of transrepression. In particular, NR3C1 can complex with NF-kB preventing them from regulating target genes. NR3C1can also inhibit the MAPK cascade. There is evidence that NR3C1serves as a tumor suppressor in prostate cells and is often downregulated or absent in prostate cancer specimens and cell lines.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: FK506 binding protein 5 (FKBP5) is a protein that belongs to the immunophilin protein family. The protein is involved in immunosuppressant signaling, protein folding and trafficking. It has been shown that FKBP5 binds to heat shock protein 90 (HSP90AA1) and modulates the affinity of steroid receptors. Particularly, FKBP5 can influence androgen receptor (AR) stability through its binding to HSP90AA1. Another possible role of FKBP5 in AR signaling is the regulation of glucocorticoid action. Under physiological conditions, FKBP5 participates in the formation of the mature glucocorticoid receptor complex, NR3C1 (nuclear receptor subfamily 3, group C, member 1 also known as glucocorticoid receptor). However, overexpression of FKBP5 was found to reduce NR3C1 hormone binding affinity.    Outcome effects: The reduced hormone binding affinity of NR3C1 may casue increased cell proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis since NR3C1 can regulate these processes through the mechanism of transrepression. In particular, NR3C1 can complex with NF-kB preventing them from regulating target genes. NR3C1can also inhibit the MAPK cascade. There is evidence that NR3C1serves as a tumor suppressor in prostate cells and is often downregulated or absent in prostate cancer specimens and cell lines.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

