xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Prostate-specific antigen (PSA), also known as kallikrein-3 protein (KLK3), is a secreted peptidase expressed predominantly in the prostatic epithelial cells. The promoter region of the KLK3 gene has an androgen-responsive element and the expression of KLK3 is regulated by the androgen receptor (AR). In the normal prostate, kallikreins are activated in secretory epithelial cells, but are inhibited by Zn2+ in a reversible manner. They become active due to the Zn2+ ion loss promoting proteolysis of the extracellular matrix components, semenogelins (SEMG1/2) and fibronectin (FN1), thus enabling spermatozoa to \"\"swim\"\" freely. This is possible due to the prostate's ability to accumulate cellular levels of zinc that are much higher than in other mammalian cells. However, it has been shown that zinc-accumulating apparatus is often downregulated in prostate cancer cells. This can lead to the decline of Zn2 levels in the cell and as a result increase KLK3 activity.    Outcome effects: Elevated KLK3 activity may result in increased proteolysis of insulin-like growth factor binding protein 3 (IGFBP3) and components of the extracellular matrix contributing to cancer progression and metastatic behavior of prostate cancer cells.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Prostate-specific antigen (PSA), also known as kallikrein-3 protein (KLK3), is a secreted peptidase expressed predominantly in the prostatic epithelial cells. The promoter region of the KLK3 gene has an androgen-responsive element and the expression of KLK3 is regulated by the androgen receptor (AR). In the normal prostate, kallikreins are activated in secretory epithelial cells, but are inhibited by Zn2+ in a reversible manner. They become active due to the Zn2+ ion loss promoting proteolysis of the extracellular matrix components, semenogelins (SEMG1/2) and fibronectin (FN1), thus enabling spermatozoa to \"\"swim\"\" freely. This is possible due to the prostate's ability to accumulate cellular levels of zinc that are much higher than in other mammalian cells. However, it has been shown that zinc-accumulating apparatus is often downregulated in prostate cancer cells. This can lead to the decline of Zn2 levels in the cell and as a result increase KLK3 activity.    Outcome effects: Elevated KLK3 activity may result in increased proteolysis of insulin-like growth factor binding protein 3 (IGFBP3) and components of the extracellular matrix contributing to cancer progression and metastatic behavior of prostate cancer cells.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red." EXACT []

