xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: The transcription factor, NKX3-1 is a tumor suppressor with a prostate-specific and androgen-regulated pattern of expression. The loss of NKX3-1 protein expression is often linked to prostate carcinogenesis. One of the known roles of NKX3-1 is to modulate the activity of TP53 through the interaction with HDAC1 (histone deacetylase 1) and thereby removing it from the p53-MDM2-HDAC1 complex. As a result, TP53 is acetylated and activated to induce apoptosis. Another possible way in which the androgen receptor (AR) could regulate cellular processes such as proliferation and cell growth is to increase the expression via NKX3-1 of IGFBP3 (insulin-like growth factor binding protein 3) that is responsible for decreasing the bioavailability of IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1). AR can also increase the bioavailability of IGF-1 by up-regulating the expression of KLK3 (kallikrein-related peptidase 3, also known as PSA or prostate-specific antigen) which cleaves IGFBP3. Furthermore, NKX3-1 may serve as a transcriptional repressor by decreasing the expression of QSOX1 (pro-oxidant enzyme quiescin Q6).    Outcome effects: The loss of NKX3-1 function due to mutation can lead to an increased level of QSOX1 and tumor initiation. NKX3-1 itself can be regulated by phosphorylation and casein kinase II participates in this process by enhancing the stability of NKX3-1 and also changing its DNA binding affinity. There is also evidence that chronic inflammation may induce cancer initiation and progression due to inflammatory cytokines such as TNF (tumor necrosis factor) and IL1B (interleukin 1, beta), which can enhance NKX3-1 protein loss.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: The transcription factor, NKX3-1 is a tumor suppressor with a prostate-specific and androgen-regulated pattern of expression. The loss of NKX3-1 protein expression is often linked to prostate carcinogenesis. One of the known roles of NKX3-1 is to modulate the activity of TP53 through the interaction with HDAC1 (histone deacetylase 1) and thereby removing it from the p53-MDM2-HDAC1 complex. As a result, TP53 is acetylated and activated to induce apoptosis. Another possible way in which the androgen receptor (AR) could regulate cellular processes such as proliferation and cell growth is to increase the expression via NKX3-1 of IGFBP3 (insulin-like growth factor binding protein 3) that is responsible for decreasing the bioavailability of IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1). AR can also increase the bioavailability of IGF-1 by up-regulating the expression of KLK3 (kallikrein-related peptidase 3, also known as PSA or prostate-specific antigen) which cleaves IGFBP3. Furthermore, NKX3-1 may serve as a transcriptional repressor by decreasing the expression of QSOX1 (pro-oxidant enzyme quiescin Q6).    Outcome effects: The loss of NKX3-1 function due to mutation can lead to an increased level of QSOX1 and tumor initiation. NKX3-1 itself can be regulated by phosphorylation and casein kinase II participates in this process by enhancing the stability of NKX3-1 and also changing its DNA binding affinity. There is also evidence that chronic inflammation may induce cancer initiation and progression due to inflammatory cytokines such as TNF (tumor necrosis factor) and IL1B (interleukin 1, beta), which can enhance NKX3-1 protein loss.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

