xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Beta 2 microglobulin (B2M) is a protein found as a component of the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I on the surface of cells. B2M protein is also known to be a mitogen and increases the growth of prostate cancer cells. The B2M gene has a putative androgen response element and is known to be overexpressed upon androgen stimulation. It is proposed that B2M can activate cAMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) possibly through the binding to G protein-coupled receptor.    Outcome effects: Subsequent phosphorylation and activation of transcription factor CREB (cAMP responsive element binding protein 1) initiates the expression of cyclin A2 (CCNA2), cyclin D1 (CCND1) and vascular endothelial growth factor A (VEGFA), which increase cell proliferation, survival, and angiogenesis. B2M induces integrin-binding sialoprotein (IBSP) and bone gamma-carboxyglutamate (gla) protein (BGLAP) which are structural proteins of the bone matrix. The expression of IBSP and BGLAP is suggested to confer the ability of prostate cancer cells to grow and survive in the bone microenvironment which leads to bone metastasis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Beta 2 microglobulin (B2M) is a protein found as a component of the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I on the surface of cells. B2M protein is also known to be a mitogen and increases the growth of prostate cancer cells. The B2M gene has a putative androgen response element and is known to be overexpressed upon androgen stimulation. It is proposed that B2M can activate cAMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) possibly through the binding to G protein-coupled receptor.    Outcome effects: Subsequent phosphorylation and activation of transcription factor CREB (cAMP responsive element binding protein 1) initiates the expression of cyclin A2 (CCNA2), cyclin D1 (CCND1) and vascular endothelial growth factor A (VEGFA), which increase cell proliferation, survival, and angiogenesis. B2M induces integrin-binding sialoprotein (IBSP) and bone gamma-carboxyglutamate (gla) protein (BGLAP) which are structural proteins of the bone matrix. The expression of IBSP and BGLAP is suggested to confer the ability of prostate cancer cells to grow and survive in the bone microenvironment which leads to bone metastasis." EXACT []

