xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: The transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinase, c-Met (MET) is activated by the hepatocyte growth factor/scatter factor (HGF/SF). It has been shown that the inadequate expression of HGF/SF or MET promotes the growth and invasion of prostate cancer cells. In contrast, androgen receptor (AR) inhibits the expression of MET. This is achieved when AR competes with another transcription factor, SP1, for the binding to the promoter of MET.    Outcome effects: HGF/SF is a multifunctional growth factor that regulates various cell processes such as cell motility, invasive growth, and angiogenesis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: The transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinase, c-Met (MET) is activated by the hepatocyte growth factor/scatter factor (HGF/SF). It has been shown that the inadequate expression of HGF/SF or MET promotes the growth and invasion of prostate cancer cells. In contrast, androgen receptor (AR) inhibits the expression of MET. This is achieved when AR competes with another transcription factor, SP1, for the binding to the promoter of MET.    Outcome effects: HGF/SF is a multifunctional growth factor that regulates various cell processes such as cell motility, invasive growth, and angiogenesis." EXACT []

