xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Androgen receptor (AR) expression and activity level changes along with the cell cycle phases. Upon stimulation by androgens, AR induces cyclin D1 (CCND1) expression possibly through the enhancement of mTOR-dependent translation. It also increases the expression of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1A (CDKN1A, p21Cip1) and induces the degradation of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1B (CDKN1B, p27Kip1) during G1 phase. There is a negative feedback loop to modulate androgen-dependent gene expression performed by cyclin D1 that is bound to AR and abolishes its activity (possibly, with the help of HDAC). Furthermore, increased concentration of E2F1 transcription factor has been found to suppress AR gene expression through binding to the AR promoter during S phase. Androgen receptor can be reactivated later in the G2 phase by direct interaction with the cell division cycle 25 homolog B (CDC25B). Cyclin dependent kinase 1 (CDK1/Cdc2) can also activate AR by phosphorylation. By the end of mitosis, AR is thought to be targeted for elimination by one of the ubiquitin ligases such as MDM2.    Outcome effects: CDKN1B expression is important for the control of G1 phase progression. CDKN1A is thought to have a dual role in the cell cycle performing negative or positive regulation of cyclin D1/CDK4 (assistance in assembling active complex). Activated CDK4 and CDK2 inactivate retinoblastoma 1 tumor suppressor (RB). RB binds with RB-associated KRAB zinc finger (RBAK) and enhances AR transcriptional activity. Cyclin E1 (CCNE1) has been shown to increase the transcriptional activity of AR. Cyclin E1/CDK2 mediates cell cycle progress from G1 phase into S phase.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Androgen receptor (AR) expression and activity level changes along with the cell cycle phases. Upon stimulation by androgens, AR induces cyclin D1 (CCND1) expression possibly through the enhancement of mTOR-dependent translation. It also increases the expression of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1A (CDKN1A, p21Cip1) and induces the degradation of cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1B (CDKN1B, p27Kip1) during G1 phase. There is a negative feedback loop to modulate androgen-dependent gene expression performed by cyclin D1 that is bound to AR and abolishes its activity (possibly, with the help of HDAC). Furthermore, increased concentration of E2F1 transcription factor has been found to suppress AR gene expression through binding to the AR promoter during S phase. Androgen receptor can be reactivated later in the G2 phase by direct interaction with the cell division cycle 25 homolog B (CDC25B). Cyclin dependent kinase 1 (CDK1/Cdc2) can also activate AR by phosphorylation. By the end of mitosis, AR is thought to be targeted for elimination by one of the ubiquitin ligases such as MDM2.    Outcome effects: CDKN1B expression is important for the control of G1 phase progression. CDKN1A is thought to have a dual role in the cell cycle performing negative or positive regulation of cyclin D1/CDK4 (assistance in assembling active complex). Activated CDK4 and CDK2 inactivate retinoblastoma 1 tumor suppressor (RB). RB binds with RB-associated KRAB zinc finger (RBAK) and enhances AR transcriptional activity. Cyclin E1 (CCNE1) has been shown to increase the transcriptional activity of AR. Cyclin E1/CDK2 mediates cell cycle progress from G1 phase into S phase.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

