xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Connexins, or gap-junctional proteins, play a significant role in communication, transport, and signaling between epithelial cells. Connexin 32 (CX32, also known as GJB1) has been shown to be a tumor suppressor in several tissues such as liver and lung. It has been demonstrated that the expression and subsequent functioning of GJA1 (connexin 43, CX43) and GJB1 are down regulated in prostate cancer specimens compared to that in normal prostate. Forced expression of GJA1 and GJB1 in the androgen-responsive prostate cancer cell lines decreases the rate of growth and initiates differentiation.    Outcome effects: There is also evidence that androgens control the expression of GJB1 by increasing its level and inducing gap junction formation. In the absence of androgens, GJB1 is degraded via an endoplasmic reticulum-associated degradation mechanism. In addition to androgens, transcription factor HNF1A is another positive regulator of GJB1 containing a site for this transcription factor in the GJB1 promoter region."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Connexins, or gap-junctional proteins, play a significant role in communication, transport, and signaling between epithelial cells. Connexin 32 (CX32, also known as GJB1) has been shown to be a tumor suppressor in several tissues such as liver and lung. It has been demonstrated that the expression and subsequent functioning of GJA1 (connexin 43, CX43) and GJB1 are down regulated in prostate cancer specimens compared to that in normal prostate. Forced expression of GJA1 and GJB1 in the androgen-responsive prostate cancer cell lines decreases the rate of growth and initiates differentiation.    Outcome effects: There is also evidence that androgens control the expression of GJB1 by increasing its level and inducing gap junction formation. In the absence of androgens, GJB1 is degraded via an endoplasmic reticulum-associated degradation mechanism. In addition to androgens, transcription factor HNF1A is another positive regulator of GJB1 containing a site for this transcription factor in the GJB1 promoter region." EXACT []

