xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is a key androgen hormone. Androgens (DHT, DHEA (dehydroepiandrosterone), and testosterone) competitively bind the androgen receptor (AR). The androgen-AR complex stimulates androgen-regulated processes such as cell proliferation, cell differentiation, and transcription of androgen-regulated genes. The first step of steroidogenesis is the conversion of cholesterol to pregnenolone by CYP11A1, which is then converted to progesterone via hydroxy-delta-5-steroid dehydrogenase (HSD3B2). CYP17A1 catalyzes the 17alpha-hydroxylation of pregnenolone to 17hydroxy-pregnenolone and progesterone to 17alpha-hydroxy-progesterone. 17hydroxy-pregnenolone is then catalyzed by CYP17A1 to DHEA and 17alpha-hydroxy-progesterone is catalyzed by CYP17A1 to androstenedione. Hydroxy-delta-5-steroid dehydrogenases (HSD3B1,2) metabolize DHEA to androstenedione. Testosterone is synthesized by hydroxysteroid (17-beta) dehydrogenase 1,2,3 (HSD17B1,2,3) and aldo-keto reductase (AKR1C3,4) from androstenedione. DHT is formed by the conversion of testosterone by the enzyme 5-a-reductase (SRD5A1,2) in the prostate, testis, adrenal glands, and hair follicles.    Outcome effects: DHT is presumed to play a causative role in the initiation and progression of prostate cancer. Consequently, the androgenic pathway has become an important target for treatment."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is a key androgen hormone. Androgens (DHT, DHEA (dehydroepiandrosterone), and testosterone) competitively bind the androgen receptor (AR). The androgen-AR complex stimulates androgen-regulated processes such as cell proliferation, cell differentiation, and transcription of androgen-regulated genes. The first step of steroidogenesis is the conversion of cholesterol to pregnenolone by CYP11A1, which is then converted to progesterone via hydroxy-delta-5-steroid dehydrogenase (HSD3B2). CYP17A1 catalyzes the 17alpha-hydroxylation of pregnenolone to 17hydroxy-pregnenolone and progesterone to 17alpha-hydroxy-progesterone. 17hydroxy-pregnenolone is then catalyzed by CYP17A1 to DHEA and 17alpha-hydroxy-progesterone is catalyzed by CYP17A1 to androstenedione. Hydroxy-delta-5-steroid dehydrogenases (HSD3B1,2) metabolize DHEA to androstenedione. Testosterone is synthesized by hydroxysteroid (17-beta) dehydrogenase 1,2,3 (HSD17B1,2,3) and aldo-keto reductase (AKR1C3,4) from androstenedione. DHT is formed by the conversion of testosterone by the enzyme 5-a-reductase (SRD5A1,2) in the prostate, testis, adrenal glands, and hair follicles.    Outcome effects: DHT is presumed to play a causative role in the initiation and progression of prostate cancer. Consequently, the androgenic pathway has become an important target for treatment." EXACT []

