xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: The serum and glucocorticoid-regulated kinase 1 (SGK1) is a member of the AGC family of serine/threonine protein kinases. The SGK1 gene is one of the targets of androgen receptor (AR) and its mRNA level is upregulated after androgen stimulation. SGK1 can impact cell physiology through various mechanisms. It can regulate cell cycle and apoptosis indirectly through the inhibition of FOXO3 (forkhead box 03) transcription factor which is responsible for cell cycle arrest and apoptosis. In addition, SGK1 can control cyclin D1 (CCND1) and entry into the cell cycle by regulating the activity of the mammalian target of rapamycin protein (MTOR).    Outcome effects: MTOR regulation can be performed by activating various cellular transporters such as members of solute carrier families (SLC43A2, SLC2A5, SLC2A4, and SLC1A1) and the subsequent influx of nutrients and amino acids (e.g., L-glutamine and D-glucose), which are positive regulators of mTOR."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostate cancer is a cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Prostate cancer cases are characterized by considerable heterogeneity especially during the progression to hormone-refractory status. It is generally accepted that androgen receptor (AR) plays a significant role in prostate cancer even upon transition to an androgen-independent state.    Signaling description: The serum and glucocorticoid-regulated kinase 1 (SGK1) is a member of the AGC family of serine/threonine protein kinases. The SGK1 gene is one of the targets of androgen receptor (AR) and its mRNA level is upregulated after androgen stimulation. SGK1 can impact cell physiology through various mechanisms. It can regulate cell cycle and apoptosis indirectly through the inhibition of FOXO3 (forkhead box 03) transcription factor which is responsible for cell cycle arrest and apoptosis. In addition, SGK1 can control cyclin D1 (CCND1) and entry into the cell cycle by regulating the activity of the mammalian target of rapamycin protein (MTOR).    Outcome effects: MTOR regulation can be performed by activating various cellular transporters such as members of solute carrier families (SLC43A2, SLC2A5, SLC2A4, and SLC1A1) and the subsequent influx of nutrients and amino acids (e.g., L-glutamine and D-glucose), which are positive regulators of mTOR." EXACT []

