xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: HPVs encode two viral oncogenes, HPV16-E6 and HPV16-E7, which can inhibit TP53 and RB1, respectively leading to dysregulation of cell proliferation. The transcription of E6 and E7 is negatively regulated by the viral HPV16-E2 protein, which binds directly to their promoter. In 62% of late stages of cervical carcinomas associated with HPV (types 16, 18, 31, 33, and 45), the viral DNA is integrated into the host cell genome and causes the disruption of the E2 open reading frame and uncontrolled E6 and E7 expression. E1 and E2 are early viral proteins and their expression is required for the replication of viral DNA. E1 binds to and recruits cellular replication proteins to the viral origin of replication, including alpha DNA polymeraseprimase, replication factor A, and human topoisomerase I (TOP1). E2 binds DNA as a homodimer, with a helix of each monomer contacting two consecutive major grooves of its target site. Strong interactions of high-risk HPV E2 with CDC20 and CDH1, the activators of APC/C, result in the stabilization of some APC/C substrates including CCNB2 and AURKA.    Outcome effects: The overexpression of AURKA causes excessive centrosome duplication and genome instability. The blocking of APC/C by E2 also stabilizes SKP2 which leads to increased levels of SKP2 before the cell reaches the G1/S transition. Through negative feedback loop, the accumulation of SKP2 by E2 leads to E2 degradation via the SCF/SKP2 complex.SCF (SKP1/ CUL1/F-box) is a ring-finger ubiquitin ligase composed of four subunits: three invariable components (RBX1, CUL1, and SKP1) and one variable component, specifically, F-box protein SKP2, that binds directly to the substrates. The overexpression of SKP2 also leads to the activation of E2F transcription factors that are required for G1/S transition. The maximum expression of E2 is observed in the G1 phase. This change of E2 expression is explained by its degradation through SCF (SKP2) ubiquitin ligase in late G1 phase. Differentiated cells express SKP2, unlike proliferating cells. This explains why E2 accumulates in the granular layer of HPV-associated cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) cases and is not detected in proliferating cells of the basal layer. APC/C inhibition and thus stabilization of SKP2 are induced at very low levels of E2, whereas E2-induced apoptosis necessitates higher levels of expression. The interaction of E2 with BRD4 is required to ensure the tethering of viral genomes to the host mitotic chromosomes for persistence of viral episomes in HPV-infected cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Exogenous proteins are shown in grey."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: HPVs encode two viral oncogenes, HPV16-E6 and HPV16-E7, which can inhibit TP53 and RB1, respectively leading to dysregulation of cell proliferation. The transcription of E6 and E7 is negatively regulated by the viral HPV16-E2 protein, which binds directly to their promoter. In 62% of late stages of cervical carcinomas associated with HPV (types 16, 18, 31, 33, and 45), the viral DNA is integrated into the host cell genome and causes the disruption of the E2 open reading frame and uncontrolled E6 and E7 expression. E1 and E2 are early viral proteins and their expression is required for the replication of viral DNA. E1 binds to and recruits cellular replication proteins to the viral origin of replication, including alpha DNA polymeraseprimase, replication factor A, and human topoisomerase I (TOP1). E2 binds DNA as a homodimer, with a helix of each monomer contacting two consecutive major grooves of its target site. Strong interactions of high-risk HPV E2 with CDC20 and CDH1, the activators of APC/C, result in the stabilization of some APC/C substrates including CCNB2 and AURKA.    Outcome effects: The overexpression of AURKA causes excessive centrosome duplication and genome instability. The blocking of APC/C by E2 also stabilizes SKP2 which leads to increased levels of SKP2 before the cell reaches the G1/S transition. Through negative feedback loop, the accumulation of SKP2 by E2 leads to E2 degradation via the SCF/SKP2 complex.SCF (SKP1/ CUL1/F-box) is a ring-finger ubiquitin ligase composed of four subunits: three invariable components (RBX1, CUL1, and SKP1) and one variable component, specifically, F-box protein SKP2, that binds directly to the substrates. The overexpression of SKP2 also leads to the activation of E2F transcription factors that are required for G1/S transition. The maximum expression of E2 is observed in the G1 phase. This change of E2 expression is explained by its degradation through SCF (SKP2) ubiquitin ligase in late G1 phase. Differentiated cells express SKP2, unlike proliferating cells. This explains why E2 accumulates in the granular layer of HPV-associated cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) cases and is not detected in proliferating cells of the basal layer. APC/C inhibition and thus stabilization of SKP2 are induced at very low levels of E2, whereas E2-induced apoptosis necessitates higher levels of expression. The interaction of E2 with BRD4 is required to ensure the tethering of viral genomes to the host mitotic chromosomes for persistence of viral episomes in HPV-infected cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Exogenous proteins are shown in grey." EXACT []

