xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: HPVs encode six early viral proteins (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two late proteins (L1 and L2). HPV16-E6 and HPV16-E7 are early viral proteins. The expression of E6 and E7 is required for cell transformation and the production of viral particles. The main target of the viral protein E6 is TP53. E6 protein interacts with and activates UBE3A. In turn, UBE3A deactivates TP53 and NFX1 by ubiquitination. At the same time, the main target of the viral protein E7 is RB1. Dysregulation of RB1 is achieved owing to the activation of CUL2, which is part of the ubiquitination complex. Thus, E7 destabilizes RB1 levels through CUL2-mediated proteasomal degradation.    Outcome effects: NFX1 interacts with SAP30 and SAP25, which leads to repression of transcription of TERT in keratinocytes. TERT regulates many cell processes. When TERT is not repressed by NFX1, activation of the transition of cell cycle from G1 to S phase occurs. The telomerase activity of TERT leads to chromatin remodeling, cell survival, and viral reproduction. The activation of TERT also leads to block of apoptosis. Downregulated TP53 fails to activate CDKN1A and CDKN1B. This leads to the activation of CDK2. CDK2 plays an essential role in regulating the G1-S transition. In addition, the suppression of TP53 does not activate transcription of BAX, which is essential for apoptosis in normal cells. In addition, E7 viral protein negatively interacts with CDKN1A and CDKN1B, which leads to the activation of CDK2 and promotes cell division. Also, in the absence of RB1, DEK oncogene is activated, which blocks apoptosis and activates cell proliferation and chromatin remodeling. Further, due to the absence of interaction between RB1 with E2F4/RBL2, the MYBL2 transcription is not actiavted. MYBL2 is a transcription factor implicated in regulating the cell cycle, apoptosis, and cancer. In conclusion, all these events lead to block of apoptosis, activation G1/S transition, dysregulation of chromatin remodeling as well as the activation of cell survival and viral reproduction in the infected cell.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: HPVs encode six early viral proteins (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two late proteins (L1 and L2). HPV16-E6 and HPV16-E7 are early viral proteins. The expression of E6 and E7 is required for cell transformation and the production of viral particles. The main target of the viral protein E6 is TP53. E6 protein interacts with and activates UBE3A. In turn, UBE3A deactivates TP53 and NFX1 by ubiquitination. At the same time, the main target of the viral protein E7 is RB1. Dysregulation of RB1 is achieved owing to the activation of CUL2, which is part of the ubiquitination complex. Thus, E7 destabilizes RB1 levels through CUL2-mediated proteasomal degradation.    Outcome effects: NFX1 interacts with SAP30 and SAP25, which leads to repression of transcription of TERT in keratinocytes. TERT regulates many cell processes. When TERT is not repressed by NFX1, activation of the transition of cell cycle from G1 to S phase occurs. The telomerase activity of TERT leads to chromatin remodeling, cell survival, and viral reproduction. The activation of TERT also leads to block of apoptosis. Downregulated TP53 fails to activate CDKN1A and CDKN1B. This leads to the activation of CDK2. CDK2 plays an essential role in regulating the G1-S transition. In addition, the suppression of TP53 does not activate transcription of BAX, which is essential for apoptosis in normal cells. In addition, E7 viral protein negatively interacts with CDKN1A and CDKN1B, which leads to the activation of CDK2 and promotes cell division. Also, in the absence of RB1, DEK oncogene is activated, which blocks apoptosis and activates cell proliferation and chromatin remodeling. Further, due to the absence of interaction between RB1 with E2F4/RBL2, the MYBL2 transcription is not actiavted. MYBL2 is a transcription factor implicated in regulating the cell cycle, apoptosis, and cancer. In conclusion, all these events lead to block of apoptosis, activation G1/S transition, dysregulation of chromatin remodeling as well as the activation of cell survival and viral reproduction in the infected cell.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

