xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: The virus genome encodes six early proteins and two late proteins. The early proteins are required for viral replication (E1 and E2), virus particle release (E4), blocking of host immune response (E5), and host cell proliferation (E6 and E7). The late viral proteins (L1 and L2) are necessary for viral capsid formation.The HPV genome is surrounded by an icosahedral capsid consisting of two structural proteins: the major protein L1 and the minor capsid protein L2. The L1 proteins are highly conserved and form 72 five-fold capsomers. The L2 protein binds viral DNA. The L2 protein is an internally located multifunctional protein which takes part in genome encapsidation, L1 interaction and capsid stabilization, endosomal escape of virions, and nuclear transport of the HPV genome. The proteinaceous coat of HPVs encases and protects the viral nucleic acid and provides the initial interaction site of the viral particle with the host cell. Following receptor engagement, the virus is internalized and its coat is disassembled to permit the encapsidated genome access to the cellular transcription and replication machinery. The initial binding to the cell surface is mediated by L1 which interacts with heparan sulfate proteoglycans (HSPGs). L1 can also bind laminin-332 and syndecan-1 (SDC1). Next, conformational changes and proteolytic cleavage of L2 take place, which is essential for successful infection.    Outcome effects: The internalization of virus can occur via different pathways including clathrin and/or caveolae dependent /independent and lipid raft independent routes. L2 then travels with the viral genome to the nucleus, where it accumulates at ND-10 domains. In addition, L2 can interact with karyopherins (importins) and the viral DNA may enter the nucleus via import pathways mediated by karyopherins. The karyopherin beta/importin beta superfamily proteins interact with nucleoporins at the nuclear pore complex to transport the proteins into the nucleus."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: The virus genome encodes six early proteins and two late proteins. The early proteins are required for viral replication (E1 and E2), virus particle release (E4), blocking of host immune response (E5), and host cell proliferation (E6 and E7). The late viral proteins (L1 and L2) are necessary for viral capsid formation.The HPV genome is surrounded by an icosahedral capsid consisting of two structural proteins: the major protein L1 and the minor capsid protein L2. The L1 proteins are highly conserved and form 72 five-fold capsomers. The L2 protein binds viral DNA. The L2 protein is an internally located multifunctional protein which takes part in genome encapsidation, L1 interaction and capsid stabilization, endosomal escape of virions, and nuclear transport of the HPV genome. The proteinaceous coat of HPVs encases and protects the viral nucleic acid and provides the initial interaction site of the viral particle with the host cell. Following receptor engagement, the virus is internalized and its coat is disassembled to permit the encapsidated genome access to the cellular transcription and replication machinery. The initial binding to the cell surface is mediated by L1 which interacts with heparan sulfate proteoglycans (HSPGs). L1 can also bind laminin-332 and syndecan-1 (SDC1). Next, conformational changes and proteolytic cleavage of L2 take place, which is essential for successful infection.    Outcome effects: The internalization of virus can occur via different pathways including clathrin and/or caveolae dependent /independent and lipid raft independent routes. L2 then travels with the viral genome to the nucleus, where it accumulates at ND-10 domains. In addition, L2 can interact with karyopherins (importins) and the viral DNA may enter the nucleus via import pathways mediated by karyopherins. The karyopherin beta/importin beta superfamily proteins interact with nucleoporins at the nuclear pore complex to transport the proteins into the nucleus." EXACT []

