xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type. The virus genome encodes 6 early proteins and 2 late proteins. The early proteins are required for viral replication (E1 and E2), virus particle release (E4), blocking of host immune response (E5) and host cell proliferation (E6 and E7). The late viral proteins (L1 and L2) are necessary for viral capsid formation. The HPV genome is surrounded by an icosahedral capsid consisting of two structural proteins: the major protein L1 and the minor capsid protein L2. The L1 proteins are highly conserved and form 72 five-fold capsomers. The L2 protein binds viral DNA; whereas, L1 can bind laminin-332 and syndecan-1 (SDC1). Conformational changes and proteolytic cleavage of L2 is essential for successful infection.     Signaling description: The internalization of virus can occur via different pathways including clathrin and/or caveolae dependent /independent and lipid raft independent routes. In addition, L2 can interact with karyopherins (importins) and the viral DNA may enter the nucleus via import pathways mediated by karyopherins. The karyopherin beta/importin beta superfamily proteins interact with nucleoporins at the nuclear pore complex to transport the proteins into the nucleus. E1 binds to and recruits cellular replication proteins to the viral origin of replication including alpha DNA polymeraseprimase, replication factor A, and human topoisomerase I (TOP1). E2 binds DNA as a homodimer, with a helix of each monomer contacting two consecutive major grooves of its target site. Strong interactions of high-risk HPV E2 with CDC20 and CDH1, the activators of APC/C, result in the stabilization of some APC/C substrates including CCNB2 and AURKA. The overexpression of AURKA causes excessive centrosome duplication and genome instability. The blocking of APC/C by E2 also stabilizes SKP2, which leads to the increased levels of SKP2 prior to the cell reaching the G1/S transition. The accumulation of SKP2 by E2 leads to E2 degradation via the SCF/SKP2 complex through the negative feedback loop. SCF (SKP1/CUL1/F-box) is a ring-finger ubiquitin ligase composed of four subunits: three invariable components (RBX1, CUL1, and SKP1) and one variable component, specifically, F-box protein SKP2, that binds directly to the substrates. The overexpression of SKP2 also leads to the activation of E2F transcription factors required for G1/S transition. The interaction of E2 with BRD4 is necessary to ensure the tethering of viral genomes to the host mitotic chromosomes for persistence of viral episomes in HPV-infected cells. The E4 protein is detected in the amplification of the viral genome and its greatest amount is observed at the late stage of virus replication prior to assembly and release of viral particles. The protein contributes to viral genome replication and promotes the release of viral particles. E4 binds directly to keratin type I such as KRT18, but not keratin type II or vimentin. The binding is dependent on the 16 amino acids at the N-terminus of the E4 protein, with the leucine cluster being indispensable. The next protein interacting with E4 cellular protein is calpain. Protease calpain cleaves the E4 protein after the amino acid 17 to generate species that lack the N terminus. These C-terminal fragments are able to multimerize and form amyloid-like fibers. This can lead to accumulation of E4 and disruption of the normal dynamics of keratin networks. E4 is also phosphorylated by CDK (CCNB1/CDK1 and CCNA2/ CDK2) in the G2-phase-like/differentiating cells. This causes G2 cell cycle arrest by associating with and preventing the nuclear entry of the CDK1/CCNB1 complexes. E5 protein interacts with IPO5, and the IPO5/E5 complex plays an important role in vesicle trafficking. E5 protein binds and inhibits the activity of ATP6V0C, which alters the endosome"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type. The virus genome encodes 6 early proteins and 2 late proteins. The early proteins are required for viral replication (E1 and E2), virus particle release (E4), blocking of host immune response (E5) and host cell proliferation (E6 and E7). The late viral proteins (L1 and L2) are necessary for viral capsid formation. The HPV genome is surrounded by an icosahedral capsid consisting of two structural proteins: the major protein L1 and the minor capsid protein L2. The L1 proteins are highly conserved and form 72 five-fold capsomers. The L2 protein binds viral DNA; whereas, L1 can bind laminin-332 and syndecan-1 (SDC1). Conformational changes and proteolytic cleavage of L2 is essential for successful infection.     Signaling description: The internalization of virus can occur via different pathways including clathrin and/or caveolae dependent /independent and lipid raft independent routes. In addition, L2 can interact with karyopherins (importins) and the viral DNA may enter the nucleus via import pathways mediated by karyopherins. The karyopherin beta/importin beta superfamily proteins interact with nucleoporins at the nuclear pore complex to transport the proteins into the nucleus. E1 binds to and recruits cellular replication proteins to the viral origin of replication including alpha DNA polymeraseprimase, replication factor A, and human topoisomerase I (TOP1). E2 binds DNA as a homodimer, with a helix of each monomer contacting two consecutive major grooves of its target site. Strong interactions of high-risk HPV E2 with CDC20 and CDH1, the activators of APC/C, result in the stabilization of some APC/C substrates including CCNB2 and AURKA. The overexpression of AURKA causes excessive centrosome duplication and genome instability. The blocking of APC/C by E2 also stabilizes SKP2, which leads to the increased levels of SKP2 prior to the cell reaching the G1/S transition. The accumulation of SKP2 by E2 leads to E2 degradation via the SCF/SKP2 complex through the negative feedback loop. SCF (SKP1/CUL1/F-box) is a ring-finger ubiquitin ligase composed of four subunits: three invariable components (RBX1, CUL1, and SKP1) and one variable component, specifically, F-box protein SKP2, that binds directly to the substrates. The overexpression of SKP2 also leads to the activation of E2F transcription factors required for G1/S transition. The interaction of E2 with BRD4 is necessary to ensure the tethering of viral genomes to the host mitotic chromosomes for persistence of viral episomes in HPV-infected cells. The E4 protein is detected in the amplification of the viral genome and its greatest amount is observed at the late stage of virus replication prior to assembly and release of viral particles. The protein contributes to viral genome replication and promotes the release of viral particles. E4 binds directly to keratin type I such as KRT18, but not keratin type II or vimentin. The binding is dependent on the 16 amino acids at the N-terminus of the E4 protein, with the leucine cluster being indispensable. The next protein interacting with E4 cellular protein is calpain. Protease calpain cleaves the E4 protein after the amino acid 17 to generate species that lack the N terminus. These C-terminal fragments are able to multimerize and form amyloid-like fibers. This can lead to accumulation of E4 and disruption of the normal dynamics of keratin networks. E4 is also phosphorylated by CDK (CCNB1/CDK1 and CCNA2/ CDK2) in the G2-phase-like/differentiating cells. This causes G2 cell cycle arrest by associating with and preventing the nuclear entry of the CDK1/CCNB1 complexes. E5 protein interacts with IPO5, and the IPO5/E5 complex plays an important role in vesicle trafficking. E5 protein binds and inhibits the activity of ATP6V0C, which alters the endosome" EXACT []

