xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: These viruses encode six early viral proteins (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two late proteins (L1 and L2).The HPV16-E5 ORFs have been classified into four groups: alpha, beta, gamma and delta, which correlate with different clinical manifestations including oncogenic potential. The E5 ORF is absent in the genome of several HPVs including beta-, gamma- and mu-HPVs. This indicates that the protein is not essential for the life cycle of these viruses, but rather can provide some benefit in favor of infection and transformation. E5 is expressed during the early phases of the viral life cycle, but only as the fourth ORF on polycistronic transcripts. In contrast, during the epithelial-cell differentiation, E5 is expressed as the second ORF of late transcripts. E5 is a small protein (~85 a.a.), is extremely hydrophobic and has membrane localization. E5 is present in the endoplasmic reticulum, nuclear envelope, and Golgi apparatus, and, when overexpressed, on the plasma membrane. The C-terminal domain is localized in the cytoplasm and interacts with IPO5.    Outcome effects: IPO5/E5 complex plays an important role in vesicle trafficking. E5 protein binds and inhibits the activity of ATP6V0C, which alters endosome transport. This causes altered endosomal acidification and degradation of EGFR, thus enhancing its recycling to the plasma membrane. E5 can also hinder EGFR degradation by interfering with membrane trafficking and the fusion of early and late endosomes. E5 impairs gap junction protein (GJA1), which is necessary for gap junction intercellular communication. In addition, E5 downregulates CDKN1A and CDKN1B, this leads to the reversal of the cell proliferation blockade. This action also occurs through the inhibition of ATP6V0C, which binds CERS2. This protein can inhibit cell proliferation and migration. The lack of EGFR degradation leads to the activation of EGFR/MAPK and PI3K/PKC signaling, which augments the JUN-FOS and MYC activation. This forces the cells through the cell cycle and stimulates transcription of the viral oncogenes E6 and E7. An additional pathway activated by EGFR is the PGE2 synthesis activation. PGE2 interacts with PTGER4 and initiates PKA activation, which leads to CREB1 phosphorylation. CREB1 initiates the transcription of PTGER4, BCL2, and VEGFA. MAPK and AKT1 signaling also elevates the VEGFA expression which leads to vascularization. E5 also enhances the effect of EDNRA signaling. EDNRA activates MAPK and AKT1 pathways and activates EGFR through SRC.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Exogenous protein is shown in grey."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: These viruses encode six early viral proteins (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two late proteins (L1 and L2).The HPV16-E5 ORFs have been classified into four groups: alpha, beta, gamma and delta, which correlate with different clinical manifestations including oncogenic potential. The E5 ORF is absent in the genome of several HPVs including beta-, gamma- and mu-HPVs. This indicates that the protein is not essential for the life cycle of these viruses, but rather can provide some benefit in favor of infection and transformation. E5 is expressed during the early phases of the viral life cycle, but only as the fourth ORF on polycistronic transcripts. In contrast, during the epithelial-cell differentiation, E5 is expressed as the second ORF of late transcripts. E5 is a small protein (~85 a.a.), is extremely hydrophobic and has membrane localization. E5 is present in the endoplasmic reticulum, nuclear envelope, and Golgi apparatus, and, when overexpressed, on the plasma membrane. The C-terminal domain is localized in the cytoplasm and interacts with IPO5.    Outcome effects: IPO5/E5 complex plays an important role in vesicle trafficking. E5 protein binds and inhibits the activity of ATP6V0C, which alters endosome transport. This causes altered endosomal acidification and degradation of EGFR, thus enhancing its recycling to the plasma membrane. E5 can also hinder EGFR degradation by interfering with membrane trafficking and the fusion of early and late endosomes. E5 impairs gap junction protein (GJA1), which is necessary for gap junction intercellular communication. In addition, E5 downregulates CDKN1A and CDKN1B, this leads to the reversal of the cell proliferation blockade. This action also occurs through the inhibition of ATP6V0C, which binds CERS2. This protein can inhibit cell proliferation and migration. The lack of EGFR degradation leads to the activation of EGFR/MAPK and PI3K/PKC signaling, which augments the JUN-FOS and MYC activation. This forces the cells through the cell cycle and stimulates transcription of the viral oncogenes E6 and E7. An additional pathway activated by EGFR is the PGE2 synthesis activation. PGE2 interacts with PTGER4 and initiates PKA activation, which leads to CREB1 phosphorylation. CREB1 initiates the transcription of PTGER4, BCL2, and VEGFA. MAPK and AKT1 signaling also elevates the VEGFA expression which leads to vascularization. E5 also enhances the effect of EDNRA signaling. EDNRA activates MAPK and AKT1 pathways and activates EGFR through SRC.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Exogenous protein is shown in grey." EXACT []

