xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: HPVs encode six early viral proteins (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two later proteins (L1 and L2). The open reading frame (ORF) of HPV16-E4 is contained within the E2 ORF. The primary E4 gene-product (E1-E4) is translated from a spliced mRNA that includes the E1 initiation codon and adjacent sequences. The E4 protein is detected during the amplification of the viral genome and its greatest amount is observed at the late stage of virus replication prior to the assembly and release of viral particles. The protein contributes to viral genome replication and promotes the release of viral particles. The E4 protein is phosphorylated by CDK1, CDK2, MAPK1, PKA, and PRKCA, with CDK1/2 serine 32 and MAPK1 threonine 57 phosphorylations being the two primary events observed in the cells in cycle. These kinases regulate one of the main functions of E4 protein, which is the association with the cellular keratin network and its cleavage by the protease calpain that allows assembly into amyloid-like fibers and re-organization of the keratin network. The phosphorylation of E4 by MAPK1 leads to the stabilization of E4 structure and enhances keratin binding. E4 binds directly to keratin type I such as keratin 18 but not keratin type II or vimentin. The binding is dependent on the 16 amino acids at the N-terminus of the E4 protein, with the leucine cluster being absolutely necessary. The next protein interacting with the E4 cellular protein is calpain. Protease calpain cleaves the E4 protein at the amino acid position 17 to generate species that lack the N terminus. These C-terminal fragments are able to multimerize and form amyloid-like fibers. This can lead to accumulation of E4 and disruption of the normal dynamics of the keratin networks. E4 is also phosphorylated by CDK (CCNB1/CDK1 and CCNA2/ CDK2) in the G2-phase-like/differentiating cells. This causes G2 cell cycle arrest by associating with and preventing the nuclear entry of the CDK1/CCNB1 complexes.    Highlighted proteins: Exogenous proteins are shown in grey."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human papillomaviruses (HPVs) are small DNA viruses that infect differentiated stratified epithelia. They induce benign lesions such as cervical carcinomas depending on the HPV type.    Signaling description: HPVs encode six early viral proteins (E1, E2, E4, E5, E6, and E7) and two later proteins (L1 and L2). The open reading frame (ORF) of HPV16-E4 is contained within the E2 ORF. The primary E4 gene-product (E1-E4) is translated from a spliced mRNA that includes the E1 initiation codon and adjacent sequences. The E4 protein is detected during the amplification of the viral genome and its greatest amount is observed at the late stage of virus replication prior to the assembly and release of viral particles. The protein contributes to viral genome replication and promotes the release of viral particles. The E4 protein is phosphorylated by CDK1, CDK2, MAPK1, PKA, and PRKCA, with CDK1/2 serine 32 and MAPK1 threonine 57 phosphorylations being the two primary events observed in the cells in cycle. These kinases regulate one of the main functions of E4 protein, which is the association with the cellular keratin network and its cleavage by the protease calpain that allows assembly into amyloid-like fibers and re-organization of the keratin network. The phosphorylation of E4 by MAPK1 leads to the stabilization of E4 structure and enhances keratin binding. E4 binds directly to keratin type I such as keratin 18 but not keratin type II or vimentin. The binding is dependent on the 16 amino acids at the N-terminus of the E4 protein, with the leucine cluster being absolutely necessary. The next protein interacting with the E4 cellular protein is calpain. Protease calpain cleaves the E4 protein at the amino acid position 17 to generate species that lack the N terminus. These C-terminal fragments are able to multimerize and form amyloid-like fibers. This can lead to accumulation of E4 and disruption of the normal dynamics of the keratin networks. E4 is also phosphorylated by CDK (CCNB1/CDK1 and CCNA2/ CDK2) in the G2-phase-like/differentiating cells. This causes G2 cell cycle arrest by associating with and preventing the nuclear entry of the CDK1/CCNB1 complexes.    Highlighted proteins: Exogenous proteins are shown in grey." EXACT []

