xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most lethal cancers worldwide, and is most prevalent in Asia and Africa. In more than 80% of cases, HCC is associated with cirrhosis or advanced fibrosis. Cirrhosis typically results from chronic infection with HBV or HCV, alcohol abuse, or metabolic disorders such as hemochromatosis. HCC has a poor prognosis since less than 30% of newly diagnosed patients will be eligible for potential curative treatment.    Signaling description: The formation of new blood vessels, which is initiated by hypoxic or ischemic conditions, is a key mechanism in the pathogenesis of chronic liver diseases (CLDs). Low physiological oxygen levels lead to an accumulation of hypoxia inducible factors (HIFs) that stimulate the expression of angiogenic growth factors. HCC is one of the most vascular solid tumors with angiogenesis playing an important role in its development, progression, and metastasis. HCC cells display rapid growth and thus are in need of a high oxygen and nutrient supply. HCC lesions are characterized by arterial hypervascularity to provide the tumor with highly oxygenated blood. However, these new vessels are marked by a disorganized vasculature which consists of leaky, hemorrhagic, and torturous vessels. The irregularity of tumor vessels results in chaotic blood flow with poorly oxygenated blood. Blood vessel formation is a multifactorial process with the first step being destabilization through ANG and hyperpermeability of the vessel wall. HCC cells secrete a number of angiogenic growth factors such as VEGFs, PDGFs, and TGFs.    Outcome effects: The growth factors activate endothelial cells (ECs) and basement membranes. VEGFA and TGFA induce EC proliferation and migration and cell-to-cell contact is established through cadherins and integrins. Tube formation is completed via TGFB1 and PDGFA while vessel formation is completed following stabilization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most lethal cancers worldwide, and is most prevalent in Asia and Africa. In more than 80% of cases, HCC is associated with cirrhosis or advanced fibrosis. Cirrhosis typically results from chronic infection with HBV or HCV, alcohol abuse, or metabolic disorders such as hemochromatosis. HCC has a poor prognosis since less than 30% of newly diagnosed patients will be eligible for potential curative treatment.    Signaling description: The formation of new blood vessels, which is initiated by hypoxic or ischemic conditions, is a key mechanism in the pathogenesis of chronic liver diseases (CLDs). Low physiological oxygen levels lead to an accumulation of hypoxia inducible factors (HIFs) that stimulate the expression of angiogenic growth factors. HCC is one of the most vascular solid tumors with angiogenesis playing an important role in its development, progression, and metastasis. HCC cells display rapid growth and thus are in need of a high oxygen and nutrient supply. HCC lesions are characterized by arterial hypervascularity to provide the tumor with highly oxygenated blood. However, these new vessels are marked by a disorganized vasculature which consists of leaky, hemorrhagic, and torturous vessels. The irregularity of tumor vessels results in chaotic blood flow with poorly oxygenated blood. Blood vessel formation is a multifactorial process with the first step being destabilization through ANG and hyperpermeability of the vessel wall. HCC cells secrete a number of angiogenic growth factors such as VEGFs, PDGFs, and TGFs.    Outcome effects: The growth factors activate endothelial cells (ECs) and basement membranes. VEGFA and TGFA induce EC proliferation and migration and cell-to-cell contact is established through cadherins and integrins. Tube formation is completed via TGFB1 and PDGFA while vessel formation is completed following stabilization.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

