xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most lethal cancers worldwide, and is most prevalent in Asia and Africa. In more than 80% of cases, HCC is associated with cirrhosis or advanced fibrosis. Cirrhosis typically results from chronic infection with HBV or HCV, alcohol abuse, or metabolic disorders such as hemochromatosis. HCC has a poor prognosis since less than 30% of newly diagnosed patients will be eligible for potential curative treatment.    Signaling description: NOTCH signaling is a crucial regulator of multiple cellular processes including differentiation, proliferation, motility, and survival. The transmembrane receptors NOTCH (1-4) interact with surface-bound ligands including JAG1, JAG2, DLL1, and DLL4. NOTCH1 has been identified as a proto-oncogene HCC is characterized by the overexpression of NOTCH1. Activated membrane-bound NOTCH1 receptor is cleaved by gamma-secretase complex and ADAM17. NOTCH intracellular domain (NICD) is transported to the nucleus and participates in the formation of chromatin remodeling complexes including KAT2B/MYSM1 and KAT2A/TADA2A/TADA3.    Outcome effects: The constitutive activation of NOTCH signaling leads to loss of differentiation and hyperplastic growth of hepatocytes. In addition, NICD interacts with LEF1/TCF4/CTNNB1 and SMAD2/3/4 complexes which induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most lethal cancers worldwide, and is most prevalent in Asia and Africa. In more than 80% of cases, HCC is associated with cirrhosis or advanced fibrosis. Cirrhosis typically results from chronic infection with HBV or HCV, alcohol abuse, or metabolic disorders such as hemochromatosis. HCC has a poor prognosis since less than 30% of newly diagnosed patients will be eligible for potential curative treatment.    Signaling description: NOTCH signaling is a crucial regulator of multiple cellular processes including differentiation, proliferation, motility, and survival. The transmembrane receptors NOTCH (1-4) interact with surface-bound ligands including JAG1, JAG2, DLL1, and DLL4. NOTCH1 has been identified as a proto-oncogene HCC is characterized by the overexpression of NOTCH1. Activated membrane-bound NOTCH1 receptor is cleaved by gamma-secretase complex and ADAM17. NOTCH intracellular domain (NICD) is transported to the nucleus and participates in the formation of chromatin remodeling complexes including KAT2B/MYSM1 and KAT2A/TADA2A/TADA3.    Outcome effects: The constitutive activation of NOTCH signaling leads to loss of differentiation and hyperplastic growth of hepatocytes. In addition, NICD interacts with LEF1/TCF4/CTNNB1 and SMAD2/3/4 complexes which induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

