xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most lethal cancers worldwide, and is most prevalent in Asia and Africa. In more than 80% of cases, HCC is associated with cirrhosis or advanced fibrosis. Cirrhosis typically results from chronic infection with HBV or HCV, alcohol abuse, or metabolic disorders such as hemochromatosis. HCC has a poor prognosis since less than 30% of newly diagnosed patients will be eligible for potential curative treatment.    Signaling description: WNT/beta-catenin (WNT/CTNNB1) signaling pathway controls various cellular functions including proliferation, differentiation, and migration. It has been demonstrated that the overexpression of LRP6, FZD7, TCF4, and HIF1A in addition to mutations of APC, AXIN1, and CTNNB1 genes are associated with HCC. In the absence of WNT proteins, CTNNB1 is related to a complex that consists of APC, AXIN1, GSK3B, and CSNK1A1. This complex formation induces the phosphorylation of CTNNB1 by CSNK1A1 and GSK3B which leads to the ubiquitination and degradation of CTNNB1 protein by the proteasome. When WNT proteins bind to LRP5/6 and FZD7, DVL1 is activated and inhibits GSK3B which results in the stabilization of cytosolic CTNNB1 protein. In addition, transcription factor HIF1A modulates WNT/CTNNB1 signaling by enhancing CTNNB1 activation.    Outcome effects: Activated CTNNB1 protein translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with TCF4/LEF1 to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. The constitutive activation of WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway leads to loss of differentiation, redundant proliferation, and migration of hepatocytes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is one of the most lethal cancers worldwide, and is most prevalent in Asia and Africa. In more than 80% of cases, HCC is associated with cirrhosis or advanced fibrosis. Cirrhosis typically results from chronic infection with HBV or HCV, alcohol abuse, or metabolic disorders such as hemochromatosis. HCC has a poor prognosis since less than 30% of newly diagnosed patients will be eligible for potential curative treatment.    Signaling description: WNT/beta-catenin (WNT/CTNNB1) signaling pathway controls various cellular functions including proliferation, differentiation, and migration. It has been demonstrated that the overexpression of LRP6, FZD7, TCF4, and HIF1A in addition to mutations of APC, AXIN1, and CTNNB1 genes are associated with HCC. In the absence of WNT proteins, CTNNB1 is related to a complex that consists of APC, AXIN1, GSK3B, and CSNK1A1. This complex formation induces the phosphorylation of CTNNB1 by CSNK1A1 and GSK3B which leads to the ubiquitination and degradation of CTNNB1 protein by the proteasome. When WNT proteins bind to LRP5/6 and FZD7, DVL1 is activated and inhibits GSK3B which results in the stabilization of cytosolic CTNNB1 protein. In addition, transcription factor HIF1A modulates WNT/CTNNB1 signaling by enhancing CTNNB1 activation.    Outcome effects: Activated CTNNB1 protein translocates into the nucleus where it interacts with TCF4/LEF1 to induce the expression of downstream target genes that are involved in cell proliferation, differentiation, and migration. The constitutive activation of WNT/CTNNB1 signaling pathway leads to loss of differentiation, redundant proliferation, and migration of hepatocytes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

