xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mantle cell lymphoma (MCL) represents approximately 4-10% of all cases of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. It occurs primarily among elderly individuals with a median age of approximately 60 years (range 29-85). Most MCL patients have disseminated disease including generalized lymphadenopathies and bone marrow involvement. The clinical behavior of MCL patients is aggressive with a median overall survival of approximately 3-4 years. MCL is a mature B-cell neoplasm arising from antigen-experienced B cells.    Signaling description: The canonical WNT pathway has been found to be deregulated in MCL. Nuclear expression of transcriptionally active beta-catenin (CTNNB1) was detected in MCL and correlated with glycogen synthase kinase-3 beta (GSK3B) inactivation. The high level of expression of several FZD receptors and LRP5 has been documented and indicates a possible autocrine loop of WNT signals. Furthermore, casein kinase II, which is constitutively phosphorylated in MCL, phosphorylates CTNNB1, thereby impeding its proteasomal degradation.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, the disruption of canonical WNT signaling reduces proliferation and induces apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mantle cell lymphoma (MCL) represents approximately 4-10% of all cases of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. It occurs primarily among elderly individuals with a median age of approximately 60 years (range 29-85). Most MCL patients have disseminated disease including generalized lymphadenopathies and bone marrow involvement. The clinical behavior of MCL patients is aggressive with a median overall survival of approximately 3-4 years. MCL is a mature B-cell neoplasm arising from antigen-experienced B cells.    Signaling description: The canonical WNT pathway has been found to be deregulated in MCL. Nuclear expression of transcriptionally active beta-catenin (CTNNB1) was detected in MCL and correlated with glycogen synthase kinase-3 beta (GSK3B) inactivation. The high level of expression of several FZD receptors and LRP5 has been documented and indicates a possible autocrine loop of WNT signals. Furthermore, casein kinase II, which is constitutively phosphorylated in MCL, phosphorylates CTNNB1, thereby impeding its proteasomal degradation.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, the disruption of canonical WNT signaling reduces proliferation and induces apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

