xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) is subdivided into three clinically and biologically distinct subtypes including splenic, nodal MZL, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma. MALT lymphoma is a low grade B-cell neoplasm and is the third most common form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. It occurs at different extra-nodal sites with the most common being the gastrointestinal (GI) tract, (50% of all cases), and within the GI tract, the stomach is the most common location (85% of GI MALT lymphomas). Other sites include small intestine, salivary glands, lungs, ocular adnexa, skin, thyroid, and breast.    Signaling description: The development of MALT lymphoma is a multi-stage process which is best understood in the gastric form of disease. The first stage of MALT lymphoma development is associated with chronic infection and inflammation or autoimmune disorders. MALT lymphoma of the stomach is linked to Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) infection and eradication of the bacterium with antibiotics leads to long-term regression of the disease in about 70% of cases. Campylobacter jejuni, Borrelia burgdorferi, and Chlamydia psittaci are associated with MALT lymphoma of the small intestine, skin, and ocular adnexa, respectively. In gastric MALT lymphoma, it has been shown that H. pylori stimulates lymphoma B-cells through tumor infiltrating T-cells involving CD40LG/CD40, CD86/CD28, and ICOSLG/ICOS interactions, CD209 (DC-SIGN receptor), Toll-like receptors, and direct antigen stimulation via B-cell receptor which also plays a role in pathogenesis. The overexpression of CCR7, CCR5, CCR2, CXCR4, and their ligands were also shown in MALT lymphoma. The second stage is characterized by the gain of chromosomal translocations, autonomous growth ability, and lack of response to antibacterial therapy. There are four common translocations occurring at variable incidences in MALT lymphomas of different locations including t(11;18)/API2-MALT1, t(1;14)/IGH-BCL10, t(14;18)/IGH-MALT1, and t(3;14)/IGH-FOXP1. The first three translocations drive the constant activation of NF-kB pathway. The final stage of MALT lymphoma is the transformation to high-grade lymphoma (most often to diffuse B-cell large cell lymphoma) and is associated with inactivating mutations of TP53, CDKN2A, TNFAIP3, and FAS.    Outcome effects: Constant activation of NF-kB pathway leads to block of apoptosis. However, the exact mechanism of IGH-FOXP1 transcript action is not well known; however it may have an antiapoptotic effect through BCL2 activation by overexpressed FOXP1.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) is subdivided into three clinically and biologically distinct subtypes including splenic, nodal MZL, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma. MALT lymphoma is a low grade B-cell neoplasm and is the third most common form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. It occurs at different extra-nodal sites with the most common being the gastrointestinal (GI) tract, (50% of all cases), and within the GI tract, the stomach is the most common location (85% of GI MALT lymphomas). Other sites include small intestine, salivary glands, lungs, ocular adnexa, skin, thyroid, and breast.    Signaling description: The development of MALT lymphoma is a multi-stage process which is best understood in the gastric form of disease. The first stage of MALT lymphoma development is associated with chronic infection and inflammation or autoimmune disorders. MALT lymphoma of the stomach is linked to Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) infection and eradication of the bacterium with antibiotics leads to long-term regression of the disease in about 70% of cases. Campylobacter jejuni, Borrelia burgdorferi, and Chlamydia psittaci are associated with MALT lymphoma of the small intestine, skin, and ocular adnexa, respectively. In gastric MALT lymphoma, it has been shown that H. pylori stimulates lymphoma B-cells through tumor infiltrating T-cells involving CD40LG/CD40, CD86/CD28, and ICOSLG/ICOS interactions, CD209 (DC-SIGN receptor), Toll-like receptors, and direct antigen stimulation via B-cell receptor which also plays a role in pathogenesis. The overexpression of CCR7, CCR5, CCR2, CXCR4, and their ligands were also shown in MALT lymphoma. The second stage is characterized by the gain of chromosomal translocations, autonomous growth ability, and lack of response to antibacterial therapy. There are four common translocations occurring at variable incidences in MALT lymphomas of different locations including t(11;18)/API2-MALT1, t(1;14)/IGH-BCL10, t(14;18)/IGH-MALT1, and t(3;14)/IGH-FOXP1. The first three translocations drive the constant activation of NF-kB pathway. The final stage of MALT lymphoma is the transformation to high-grade lymphoma (most often to diffuse B-cell large cell lymphoma) and is associated with inactivating mutations of TP53, CDKN2A, TNFAIP3, and FAS.    Outcome effects: Constant activation of NF-kB pathway leads to block of apoptosis. However, the exact mechanism of IGH-FOXP1 transcript action is not well known; however it may have an antiapoptotic effect through BCL2 activation by overexpressed FOXP1.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

