xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) is subdivided into three clinically and biologically distinct subtypes including splenic, nodal MZL, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma. Splenic MZL (SMZL) is an indolent B-cell neoplasm involving spleen, bone marrow, and rarely peripheral blood.    Signaling description: The pathogenesis of SMZL is not well known, however, clinical data point to an association with hepatitis C virus. The infection rate of SMZL does not exceed 15-20%. NF-kB is constitutively activated in 60% of SMZL cases due to TNFAIP3, IKBKB, TRAF3, MAP3K14, and BIRC3 mutations. Activating lesions of MYD88 and CARD11 are found in Toll-like receptor (TLR) and B-cell receptor (BCR) pathways. TP53 disruption is found in 10-15% of cases.    Outcome effects: The clinical course of SMZL is heterogeneous, although the majority of cases show an indolent course with a median survival of approximately ten years. Roughly 10% of cases undergo transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. The therapeutic options for SMZL include a \"watch and wait\" approach, splenectomy, chemotherapy, and B-cell-depleting therapy with rituximab.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Marginal zone lymphoma (MZL) is subdivided into three clinically and biologically distinct subtypes including splenic, nodal MZL, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma. Splenic MZL (SMZL) is an indolent B-cell neoplasm involving spleen, bone marrow, and rarely peripheral blood.    Signaling description: The pathogenesis of SMZL is not well known, however, clinical data point to an association with hepatitis C virus. The infection rate of SMZL does not exceed 15-20%. NF-kB is constitutively activated in 60% of SMZL cases due to TNFAIP3, IKBKB, TRAF3, MAP3K14, and BIRC3 mutations. Activating lesions of MYD88 and CARD11 are found in Toll-like receptor (TLR) and B-cell receptor (BCR) pathways. TP53 disruption is found in 10-15% of cases.    Outcome effects: The clinical course of SMZL is heterogeneous, although the majority of cases show an indolent course with a median survival of approximately ten years. Roughly 10% of cases undergo transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. The therapeutic options for SMZL include a \"watch and wait\" approach, splenectomy, chemotherapy, and B-cell-depleting therapy with rituximab.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

