synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: NF-kB classical pathway activation in multiple myeloma (MM) cells results from signaling via TNFSF13 and TNFSF13B ligands that stimulate TNFRSF13B and TNFRSF17 receptors. Following stimulation by TRAF5, TRAF2, RIPK1, and MAP3K7, IKBK family proteins are activated and in turn phosphorylate the inhibitory subunit of NFKBIA leading to its proteasomal degradation. NFKBIA is a negative regulator of NF-kB1/RELA.    Outcome effects: Following NFKBIA removal, the NF-kB1/RELA complex activates the transcription of several downstream target genes (cytokines, adhesion molecules, and cell cycle regulators) resulting in block of apoptosis, tumor cell proliferation, vascularization (angiogenesis), and neoplasm metastasis. The classical NF-kB pathway is negatively regulated by NFKBIA, TNIP1, and CYLD. Lastly, mutations in NF-kB1, TNFRSF13B, and CYLD genes were shown to be important in MM pathogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: NF-kB classical pathway activation in multiple myeloma (MM) cells results from signaling via TNFSF13 and TNFSF13B ligands that stimulate TNFRSF13B and TNFRSF17 receptors. Following stimulation by TRAF5, TRAF2, RIPK1, and MAP3K7, IKBK family proteins are activated and in turn phosphorylate the inhibitory subunit of NFKBIA leading to its proteasomal degradation. NFKBIA is a negative regulator of NF-kB1/RELA.    Outcome effects: Following NFKBIA removal, the NF-kB1/RELA complex activates the transcription of several downstream target genes (cytokines, adhesion molecules, and cell cycle regulators) resulting in block of apoptosis, tumor cell proliferation, vascularization (angiogenesis), and neoplasm metastasis. The classical NF-kB pathway is negatively regulated by NFKBIA, TNIP1, and CYLD. Lastly, mutations in NF-kB1, TNFRSF13B, and CYLD genes were shown to be important in MM pathogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

