xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients. More than 80% of MM patients develop bone disease with severe bone pain and pathologic fractures.    Signaling description: TNFSF11/TNFRSF11A, JAG/Notch, and CCL/CCR interaction play a key role in osteoclast activation in multiple myeloma (MM). Normally, the balance between bone synthesis and resorption is regulated by TNFSF11 and its decoy receptor, TNFRSF11B. In MM bone disease, there is a remarkable imbalance between them with the highly overexpressed TNFSF11 and suppressed TNFRSF11B. TNFSF11 interacts with TNFRSF11A and activates NF-kB, MAPK/FOS-JUN, and calcineurin/NFATC1 pathways. TRAF6 is one of the main regulators in all of these pathways. The activation of Notch by the overexpression of JAG1 and JAG2 results in PI3K/Akt and NF-kB pathway activation. In addition, overexpressed CCL3/20 activates CCR family members leading to signaling via G-proteins/PKC/MAPK and PI3K/Akt.    Outcome effects: The described events result in osteoclast adhesion, activation, and fusion as well as bone resorption and osteoclast apoptosis inhibition leading to the initiation and progression of MM bone disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients. More than 80% of MM patients develop bone disease with severe bone pain and pathologic fractures.    Signaling description: TNFSF11/TNFRSF11A, JAG/Notch, and CCL/CCR interaction play a key role in osteoclast activation in multiple myeloma (MM). Normally, the balance between bone synthesis and resorption is regulated by TNFSF11 and its decoy receptor, TNFRSF11B. In MM bone disease, there is a remarkable imbalance between them with the highly overexpressed TNFSF11 and suppressed TNFRSF11B. TNFSF11 interacts with TNFRSF11A and activates NF-kB, MAPK/FOS-JUN, and calcineurin/NFATC1 pathways. TRAF6 is one of the main regulators in all of these pathways. The activation of Notch by the overexpression of JAG1 and JAG2 results in PI3K/Akt and NF-kB pathway activation. In addition, overexpressed CCL3/20 activates CCR family members leading to signaling via G-proteins/PKC/MAPK and PI3K/Akt.    Outcome effects: The described events result in osteoclast adhesion, activation, and fusion as well as bone resorption and osteoclast apoptosis inhibition leading to the initiation and progression of MM bone disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

