xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: Interleukin 6 (IL6), insulin-like growth factor I (IGF1), and vascular endothelial growth factor A (VEGFA) are all major regulators implicated in multiple myeloma (MM) pathogenesis. These proteins are produced by bone marrow stromal cells and by MM cells. IL6 effects are mediated by JAK/STAT, PI3K/Akt, and RAS/MAPK signaling pathways. IGF1 and VEGFA activate PI3K/Akt and RAS/MAPK pathways.    Outcome effects: The activated pathways lead to the expression of many downstream target genes, which triggers the inhibition of apoptosis as well as the activation of MM cell proliferation and migration, angiogenesis (vascularization), tumor expansion, and invasion.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: Interleukin 6 (IL6), insulin-like growth factor I (IGF1), and vascular endothelial growth factor A (VEGFA) are all major regulators implicated in multiple myeloma (MM) pathogenesis. These proteins are produced by bone marrow stromal cells and by MM cells. IL6 effects are mediated by JAK/STAT, PI3K/Akt, and RAS/MAPK signaling pathways. IGF1 and VEGFA activate PI3K/Akt and RAS/MAPK pathways.    Outcome effects: The activated pathways lead to the expression of many downstream target genes, which triggers the inhibition of apoptosis as well as the activation of MM cell proliferation and migration, angiogenesis (vascularization), tumor expansion, and invasion.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

