xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients. More than 80% of MM patients develop bone disease with severe bone pain and pathologic fractures.    Signaling description: The initiation of WNT signaling occurs following the binding of WNT to FZD and LRP6 receptors, which in turn activates downstream elements (DVL1 and FRAT1) leading to the accumulation of CTNNB1. CTNNB1 translocates to the nucleus where in conjunction with LEF, functions as a transcription activator of target genes such as TNFSF11 and TNFRSF11B. Normally, TNFSF11 promotes bone resorption while TNFRSF11B is involved in bone formation. WNT signaling leads to the increase of TNFRSF11B and the decrease of TNFSF11 production. There are several natural inhibitors of WNT including DKK1 and SFRP1. The disruption of this pathway by myeloma plasma cell-derived overexpressed DKK1 results in the suppression of osteoblast differentiation, enhanced osteoclast function, and bone destruction by the inhibition of the production of regulatory molecules such as TNFRSF11B which is normally produced by osteoblasts.    Outcome effects: In osteoblasts, WNT signaling influences three major developmental functions: the commitment of mesenchymal stem cells to osteoblasts, stimulation of osteoblast proliferation, and promotion of osteoblast and osteocyte survival.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients. More than 80% of MM patients develop bone disease with severe bone pain and pathologic fractures.    Signaling description: The initiation of WNT signaling occurs following the binding of WNT to FZD and LRP6 receptors, which in turn activates downstream elements (DVL1 and FRAT1) leading to the accumulation of CTNNB1. CTNNB1 translocates to the nucleus where in conjunction with LEF, functions as a transcription activator of target genes such as TNFSF11 and TNFRSF11B. Normally, TNFSF11 promotes bone resorption while TNFRSF11B is involved in bone formation. WNT signaling leads to the increase of TNFRSF11B and the decrease of TNFSF11 production. There are several natural inhibitors of WNT including DKK1 and SFRP1. The disruption of this pathway by myeloma plasma cell-derived overexpressed DKK1 results in the suppression of osteoblast differentiation, enhanced osteoclast function, and bone destruction by the inhibition of the production of regulatory molecules such as TNFRSF11B which is normally produced by osteoblasts.    Outcome effects: In osteoblasts, WNT signaling influences three major developmental functions: the commitment of mesenchymal stem cells to osteoblasts, stimulation of osteoblast proliferation, and promotion of osteoblast and osteocyte survival.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

