xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: Multiple myeloma (MM) cells express Notch 1, 2, and 3 receptors which are activated by Jagged-1 and 2 (JAG1 and JAG2) ligands expressed by MM cells and bone marrow stromal cells. JAG2 is overexpressed in MM and plays a central role in this malignancy. Upon ligand binding, ADAM17 metalloproteinase and gamma-secretase cleave the Notch receptors leading to the release of the Notch intracellular portion (ICN) and allowing it to translocate into the nucleus. It has been shown that the Notch pathway may have both a proliferative and anti-proliferative effect. Notch-induced resistance to chemotherapy-induced apoptosis is attributed to the arrest of the cell cycle in G0/G1 phase associated with the up-regulation of CDKN1A. Conversely, CDKN1B, CCND1, and MYC may drive cell cycle progression and proliferation. Further, Notch signaling in MM cells does not affect the expression of major antiapoptotic proteins such as BCL2, BCL2L1, and survivin and also does not activate NF-kB.    Outcome effects: Once ICN is in the nucleus, it recruits co-activators, such as EP300 and MAML1, thereby activating the transcription of several downstream target genes. This results in MM cell proliferation, resistance to apoptosis and chemotherapy as well as the secretion of IL6, VEGFA, and other cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: Multiple myeloma (MM) cells express Notch 1, 2, and 3 receptors which are activated by Jagged-1 and 2 (JAG1 and JAG2) ligands expressed by MM cells and bone marrow stromal cells. JAG2 is overexpressed in MM and plays a central role in this malignancy. Upon ligand binding, ADAM17 metalloproteinase and gamma-secretase cleave the Notch receptors leading to the release of the Notch intracellular portion (ICN) and allowing it to translocate into the nucleus. It has been shown that the Notch pathway may have both a proliferative and anti-proliferative effect. Notch-induced resistance to chemotherapy-induced apoptosis is attributed to the arrest of the cell cycle in G0/G1 phase associated with the up-regulation of CDKN1A. Conversely, CDKN1B, CCND1, and MYC may drive cell cycle progression and proliferation. Further, Notch signaling in MM cells does not affect the expression of major antiapoptotic proteins such as BCL2, BCL2L1, and survivin and also does not activate NF-kB.    Outcome effects: Once ICN is in the nucleus, it recruits co-activators, such as EP300 and MAML1, thereby activating the transcription of several downstream target genes. This results in MM cell proliferation, resistance to apoptosis and chemotherapy as well as the secretion of IL6, VEGFA, and other cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

