xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: The alternative NF-kB pathway in multiple myeloma (MM) cell activation results from signaling by TNFSF13B through the TNFRSF13C receptor. MAP3K14 then activates CHUK, which phosphorylates NFKB2 (p100). This results in the proteasomal removal of the inhibitory C-terminal domain, generating the active NF-kB (p52) subunit. Alternative NF-kB signaling is regulated through the control of MAP3K14 turnover, with TRAF3, TRAF2, and BIRC2 involved in this process. In addition, mutations in MAP3K14, CD40, LTBR, BIRC2, TRAF2, TRAF3, and NFKB2 genes were shown to be important in MM pathogenesis.    Outcome effects: The NF-kB/RELB complex activates the transcription of several downstream target genes (cytokines, adhesion molecules, and cell cycle regulators) resulting in block of apoptosis, tumor cell proliferation, vascularization (angiogenesis), and neoplasm metastasis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Multiple myeloma (MM) is a form of hematologic malignancy in which normal plasma cells transform into malignant myeloma cells and accumulate in the bone marrow. MM is characterized by the presence of monoclonal immunoglobulin in the serum or urine, osteolytic bone lesions, renal disease, and immunodeficiency, and is mainly a disease of old patients.    Signaling description: The alternative NF-kB pathway in multiple myeloma (MM) cell activation results from signaling by TNFSF13B through the TNFRSF13C receptor. MAP3K14 then activates CHUK, which phosphorylates NFKB2 (p100). This results in the proteasomal removal of the inhibitory C-terminal domain, generating the active NF-kB (p52) subunit. Alternative NF-kB signaling is regulated through the control of MAP3K14 turnover, with TRAF3, TRAF2, and BIRC2 involved in this process. In addition, mutations in MAP3K14, CD40, LTBR, BIRC2, TRAF2, TRAF3, and NFKB2 genes were shown to be important in MM pathogenesis.    Outcome effects: The NF-kB/RELB complex activates the transcription of several downstream target genes (cytokines, adhesion molecules, and cell cycle regulators) resulting in block of apoptosis, tumor cell proliferation, vascularization (angiogenesis), and neoplasm metastasis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

