xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cetuximab, a partially humanized monoclonal antibody against epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) is used to treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer. At the same time, only 10-20% of patients with metastatic colorectal cancer will benefit from anti-EGFR therapy. Understanding the reasons of underlying cetuximab resistance and discovering the biomarkers of response will help to predict which patients should receive anti-EGFR therapy. A patient's response to anti-EGFR therapy depends on the importance of the EGFR signal for cancer progression in the given case. EGFR mediates its action through PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/Raf/MAPK pathways.    Outcome effects: Mutations in genes coding for members of the PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/Raf/MAPK pathways may cause their constant activation independent of the EGF/EGFR binding. EGFR is a member of ERBB family of receptors. Changes in expression and mutation status of other ERBB proteins and their ligands affect cetuximab sensitivity through activation of its downstream pathways independent of EGF/EGFR binding. Protein tyrosine kinases, other than ERBB family members, can also activate PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/Raf/MAPK pathways. This contributes to resistance and the inhibition of EGFR which is unable to block pathways leading to cell proliferation. EGFR promotes cell proliferation, not only as a receptor, but can be transported to the nucleus and act on several transcription factors and other nuclear proteins. Cetuximab can also cause antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC). Mutations in FC gamma receptors that are involved in ADCC influence cetuximab sensitivity. Another important mechanism of resistance to anti-EGFR therapies is epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT). The activation of EMT program decreases the cell's dependence on EGF/EGFR signaling and therefore sensitivity to cetuximab.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins highlighted in blue are known to increase sensitivity to cetuximab, and proteins highlighted in red are known to decrease sensitivity to cetuximab."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cetuximab, a partially humanized monoclonal antibody against epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) is used to treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer. At the same time, only 10-20% of patients with metastatic colorectal cancer will benefit from anti-EGFR therapy. Understanding the reasons of underlying cetuximab resistance and discovering the biomarkers of response will help to predict which patients should receive anti-EGFR therapy. A patient's response to anti-EGFR therapy depends on the importance of the EGFR signal for cancer progression in the given case. EGFR mediates its action through PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/Raf/MAPK pathways.    Outcome effects: Mutations in genes coding for members of the PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/Raf/MAPK pathways may cause their constant activation independent of the EGF/EGFR binding. EGFR is a member of ERBB family of receptors. Changes in expression and mutation status of other ERBB proteins and their ligands affect cetuximab sensitivity through activation of its downstream pathways independent of EGF/EGFR binding. Protein tyrosine kinases, other than ERBB family members, can also activate PI3K/AKT1 and Ras/Raf/MAPK pathways. This contributes to resistance and the inhibition of EGFR which is unable to block pathways leading to cell proliferation. EGFR promotes cell proliferation, not only as a receptor, but can be transported to the nucleus and act on several transcription factors and other nuclear proteins. Cetuximab can also cause antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC). Mutations in FC gamma receptors that are involved in ADCC influence cetuximab sensitivity. Another important mechanism of resistance to anti-EGFR therapies is epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT). The activation of EMT program decreases the cell's dependence on EGF/EGFR signaling and therefore sensitivity to cetuximab.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins highlighted in blue are known to increase sensitivity to cetuximab, and proteins highlighted in red are known to decrease sensitivity to cetuximab." EXACT []

