xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Guanylyl cyclase C (GUCY2C) is a transmembrane enzyme expressed in the intestine and brain. Its two ligands, guanylin (GUCA2A) and uroguanylin (GUCA2B) are secreted in the intestine and regulate electrolyte and water transport in intestinal epithelia which has an anti-proliferative effect.    Signaling description: Following ligand binding, GUCY2C becomes activated and converts GTP to cGMP. The increase of cGMP levels results in the activation of downstream effectors including PRKG1, the main intracellular mediator of cGMP signaling. Increased cGMP levels activate PTEN which reduces the levels of phosphorylated AKT1. CDX2 is the transcription factor for several intestinal lineage genes including GUCY2C. CDX2 mutations may cause the inactivation of GUCY2C signaling. Normally, GUCY2C suppresses tumorigenesis by maintaining DNA damage repair mechanisms and restricting proliferation. The loss of GUCA2A/B paracrine hormone expression is a universal early event in colonic neoplasia. Therefore, the anti-proliferative activity of GUCY2C may perhaps be restored by the administration of GUCY2C agonists or by the elevation of intracellular cGMP following inhibition of cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterases, such as PDE5A, which normally degrade cGMP. In addition, a characteristic feature of most colorectal cancers is the high level of activity of SRC tyrosine kinase observed in colon carcinoma cells. GUCY2C is regulated by SRC-mediated inhibitory phosphorylation. Thus, a combinatorial therapy of SRC inhibitors along with activators of GUCY2C may be an effective regimen in the treatment of colon cancers.    Outcome effects: By means of decreasing AKT1 activation, GUCY2C signaling suppresses cell proliferation and helps to protect the tight junctional protein assemblies and intestinal barrier integrity. GUCY2C-induced AKT1 inhibition also prevents MDM2-dependent degradation of the tumor suppressor TP53 and promotes DNA damage repair and genomic stability. Furthermore, GUCY2C signaling reduces the levels of key proteins that promote cell proliferation and cell cycle progression such as CTNNB1 and CCND1. GUCY2C signaling also promotes mitochondrial biogenesis which induces the expression of transcription factors including PPARGC1A, TFAM, and NRF1. Silencing of the GUCY2C signaling reduces mitochondrion number and oxygen consumption in addition to elevating SLC2A1 expression, glucose uptake, and aerobic glycolysis which recapitulates a tumor metabolic phenotype. Silencing GUCY2C in human colon cancer cells increases AKT1-dependent TGFB1 secretion and activates fibroblasts through TGFBR1 and SMAD3 phosphorylation. In turn, activating TGFB1 signaling induces fibroblasts to secrete hepatocyte growth factor (HGF), reciprocally driving colon cancer cell proliferation through MET-dependent signaling.Highlighted protein: Proteins with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Guanylyl cyclase C (GUCY2C) is a transmembrane enzyme expressed in the intestine and brain. Its two ligands, guanylin (GUCA2A) and uroguanylin (GUCA2B) are secreted in the intestine and regulate electrolyte and water transport in intestinal epithelia which has an anti-proliferative effect.    Signaling description: Following ligand binding, GUCY2C becomes activated and converts GTP to cGMP. The increase of cGMP levels results in the activation of downstream effectors including PRKG1, the main intracellular mediator of cGMP signaling. Increased cGMP levels activate PTEN which reduces the levels of phosphorylated AKT1. CDX2 is the transcription factor for several intestinal lineage genes including GUCY2C. CDX2 mutations may cause the inactivation of GUCY2C signaling. Normally, GUCY2C suppresses tumorigenesis by maintaining DNA damage repair mechanisms and restricting proliferation. The loss of GUCA2A/B paracrine hormone expression is a universal early event in colonic neoplasia. Therefore, the anti-proliferative activity of GUCY2C may perhaps be restored by the administration of GUCY2C agonists or by the elevation of intracellular cGMP following inhibition of cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterases, such as PDE5A, which normally degrade cGMP. In addition, a characteristic feature of most colorectal cancers is the high level of activity of SRC tyrosine kinase observed in colon carcinoma cells. GUCY2C is regulated by SRC-mediated inhibitory phosphorylation. Thus, a combinatorial therapy of SRC inhibitors along with activators of GUCY2C may be an effective regimen in the treatment of colon cancers.    Outcome effects: By means of decreasing AKT1 activation, GUCY2C signaling suppresses cell proliferation and helps to protect the tight junctional protein assemblies and intestinal barrier integrity. GUCY2C-induced AKT1 inhibition also prevents MDM2-dependent degradation of the tumor suppressor TP53 and promotes DNA damage repair and genomic stability. Furthermore, GUCY2C signaling reduces the levels of key proteins that promote cell proliferation and cell cycle progression such as CTNNB1 and CCND1. GUCY2C signaling also promotes mitochondrial biogenesis which induces the expression of transcription factors including PPARGC1A, TFAM, and NRF1. Silencing of the GUCY2C signaling reduces mitochondrion number and oxygen consumption in addition to elevating SLC2A1 expression, glucose uptake, and aerobic glycolysis which recapitulates a tumor metabolic phenotype. Silencing GUCY2C in human colon cancer cells increases AKT1-dependent TGFB1 secretion and activates fibroblasts through TGFBR1 and SMAD3 phosphorylation. In turn, activating TGFB1 signaling induces fibroblasts to secrete hepatocyte growth factor (HGF), reciprocally driving colon cancer cell proliferation through MET-dependent signaling.Highlighted protein: Proteins with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

