synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Neutrophils have been implicated in the development of bronchial hyperresponsiveness as the primary cells recruited to the site of an allergic reaction. Neutrophils are the major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils have a role in the development of severe chronic asthma and the onset of sudden severe attacks. Activation of neutrophils through adherence on endothelial cell depends on integrins and selectins expressed on the surface of both neutrophils and endothelial cells. PSGL-1 protein on the surface of neutrophils is the ligand for E-selectin. It is important for neutrophil rolling and integrin-dependent activation of adhesion of neutrophils on cells of blood vessel. Upon binding of PSGL-1 to E-selectin, SYK is activated through LYN/DAP12, which results in activation of complex ITGAL/ITGB2 by MAPK1 kinase (this part of the pathway is truncated here for a better layout). Receptors IRGAL, ITGB2, ITGB1/ITGA9 on the surface of neutrophils facilitate high levels of adhesion between neutrophils and endothelial cells, mostly through their association with ICAM-1. Activation of integrins on neutrophil surface leads to its adhesion to endothelial cells, and at the same time activates signaling pathways in in the neutrophil.    Outcome effects: The major event is the cytoskeleton reorganization which forces neutrophil secretory vesicles to move towards the external membrane. Moreover, integrins trigger intracellular signaling cascades that lead to ROS production through the PI3K/RAC1 related signaling. IL-8 is a principal factor in neutrophil delivery. Other chemokines that are specific for neutrophils include epithelial cell derived neutrophil activating peptide, PPBP, CXCL5, CXCL2, and CXCL6. Neutrophil chemoattractants can increase integrin-dependent adhesion of neutrophils by stimulating integrin synthesis or change the cell shape by directly modulating actin cytoskeleton. IL8 binding to CXCR2 causes the release of MMP9 from tertiary granules. Elevated levels of MMP-9 have been found in patients with asthma. Further, MMP9 might promote eosinophil movement. CR1 and CD11b expression was higher on resting peripheral blood neutrophils in severe steroid-dependent asthmatics compared to normal and mild asthmatics. Surface molecules CR1 and CD11b neutrophil surface mediate binding and phagocytosis of C3b-coated particles and immune complexes. Neutrophil proteases (elastase, cathepsin G, and proteinase-3) may enhance airway inflammation in asthma through the activation of eosinophils to produce superoxide and neutrophilic cytokines and chemokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Neutrophils have been implicated in the development of bronchial hyperresponsiveness as the primary cells recruited to the site of an allergic reaction. Neutrophils are the major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils have a role in the development of severe chronic asthma and the onset of sudden severe attacks. Activation of neutrophils through adherence on endothelial cell depends on integrins and selectins expressed on the surface of both neutrophils and endothelial cells. PSGL-1 protein on the surface of neutrophils is the ligand for E-selectin. It is important for neutrophil rolling and integrin-dependent activation of adhesion of neutrophils on cells of blood vessel. Upon binding of PSGL-1 to E-selectin, SYK is activated through LYN/DAP12, which results in activation of complex ITGAL/ITGB2 by MAPK1 kinase (this part of the pathway is truncated here for a better layout). Receptors IRGAL, ITGB2, ITGB1/ITGA9 on the surface of neutrophils facilitate high levels of adhesion between neutrophils and endothelial cells, mostly through their association with ICAM-1. Activation of integrins on neutrophil surface leads to its adhesion to endothelial cells, and at the same time activates signaling pathways in in the neutrophil.    Outcome effects: The major event is the cytoskeleton reorganization which forces neutrophil secretory vesicles to move towards the external membrane. Moreover, integrins trigger intracellular signaling cascades that lead to ROS production through the PI3K/RAC1 related signaling. IL-8 is a principal factor in neutrophil delivery. Other chemokines that are specific for neutrophils include epithelial cell derived neutrophil activating peptide, PPBP, CXCL5, CXCL2, and CXCL6. Neutrophil chemoattractants can increase integrin-dependent adhesion of neutrophils by stimulating integrin synthesis or change the cell shape by directly modulating actin cytoskeleton. IL8 binding to CXCR2 causes the release of MMP9 from tertiary granules. Elevated levels of MMP-9 have been found in patients with asthma. Further, MMP9 might promote eosinophil movement. CR1 and CD11b expression was higher on resting peripheral blood neutrophils in severe steroid-dependent asthmatics compared to normal and mild asthmatics. Surface molecules CR1 and CD11b neutrophil surface mediate binding and phagocytosis of C3b-coated particles and immune complexes. Neutrophil proteases (elastase, cathepsin G, and proteinase-3) may enhance airway inflammation in asthma through the activation of eosinophils to produce superoxide and neutrophilic cytokines and chemokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

