synonym: "CellType: basophil" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "respiratory system"
synonym: "Organ_System: respiratory system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: The role of basophils in the pathophysiology of allergic asthma is still poorly understood. Human basophils are a major source of IL4 and IL13, the key cytokines implicated in stimulating and amplifying the TH2 response and asthma progress. Activation of basophils, similarly to that of mast cells, is characterized primarily by degranulation and release of heparin, histamine, and leukotrienes from granules, and leads to immediate hypersensitivity reactions. Unlike mast cells, basophils are introduced into blood in an already mature state, thus their effects are faster than those of mast cells. Unlike mast cells, basophils express MHC class II and co-stimulatory molecules which can transmit peptide antigens to Th2-cells and therefore stimulate their differentiation. Similarly to mast cells, which also express the high-affinity IgE receptor FceRI, basophils release inflammatory mediators such as histamine and LTC4 in response to IgE-mediated activation. FceRI is expressed on mast cells and basophils in the form of tetramers (FCER1A / MS4A2/2 of FCER1G). Antigen binding to IgE cross-links two FceRI leading to Lyn phosphorylation. In addition to the well-characterized ability of basophils to be activated by antigen-IgE complexes, basophils have also been shown to be activated by the IgD antibody class molecules. IL-3 is the key cytokine that activate maturation of basophils. IL-3 seems to have a prominent pro-survival effect on basophils that depends on the kinase PIM1. IL-3 also enhances the production of IL-4 and IL-13 from basophils in response to IgE-mediated activation. In basophils, IL-4 and IL-13 production also induced by TLR2 related signaling. IL-33 is also specific to the process of basophil activation. IL-33 is expressed by dermal fibroblasts, airway epithelial cells and bronchial smooth muscle cells and is associated with IL-4, IL-13 and IL-6.    Outcome effects: Basophils are known to contribute to allergic responses by inducing smooth muscle contraction once they migrate to the area of inflammation during the late phase response. Leukotrienes (LT) C4, LTD4, and LTE4, are smooth muscle constrictors.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: The role of basophils in the pathophysiology of allergic asthma is still poorly understood. Human basophils are a major source of IL4 and IL13, the key cytokines implicated in stimulating and amplifying the TH2 response and asthma progress. Activation of basophils, similarly to that of mast cells, is characterized primarily by degranulation and release of heparin, histamine, and leukotrienes from granules, and leads to immediate hypersensitivity reactions. Unlike mast cells, basophils are introduced into blood in an already mature state, thus their effects are faster than those of mast cells. Unlike mast cells, basophils express MHC class II and co-stimulatory molecules which can transmit peptide antigens to Th2-cells and therefore stimulate their differentiation. Similarly to mast cells, which also express the high-affinity IgE receptor FceRI, basophils release inflammatory mediators such as histamine and LTC4 in response to IgE-mediated activation. FceRI is expressed on mast cells and basophils in the form of tetramers (FCER1A / MS4A2/2 of FCER1G). Antigen binding to IgE cross-links two FceRI leading to Lyn phosphorylation. In addition to the well-characterized ability of basophils to be activated by antigen-IgE complexes, basophils have also been shown to be activated by the IgD antibody class molecules. IL-3 is the key cytokine that activate maturation of basophils. IL-3 seems to have a prominent pro-survival effect on basophils that depends on the kinase PIM1. IL-3 also enhances the production of IL-4 and IL-13 from basophils in response to IgE-mediated activation. In basophils, IL-4 and IL-13 production also induced by TLR2 related signaling. IL-33 is also specific to the process of basophil activation. IL-33 is expressed by dermal fibroblasts, airway epithelial cells and bronchial smooth muscle cells and is associated with IL-4, IL-13 and IL-6.    Outcome effects: Basophils are known to contribute to allergic responses by inducing smooth muscle contraction once they migrate to the area of inflammation during the late phase response. Leukotrienes (LT) C4, LTD4, and LTE4, are smooth muscle constrictors.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

