xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Mast cells infiltrate the airway mucous glands in subjects with asthma showing features of degranulation and a positive correlation with the degree of mucus obstruction. The major way of mast cell activation in allergic reactions is through the aggregation of FceRI receptors and IgE. Mast cells contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules, including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases. Upon activation, mast cells release contents of their granules into surrounding tissue (degranulation). IgE - depended degranulation occurs rapidly, and mediators released into extracellular space produce an immediate effect. The content of the granules is pre-synthesized during maturation and proliferation of mast cells.Each FceRI receptor consists of 4 subunits: alpha-particle responsible for IgE binding, one intra-cellular beta-subunit, and two intra-cellular gamma subunits. For IgE-dependent activation, an antigen must bind at least two IgE molecules at the surface of a mast cell. Antigen binding to IgE cross-links two FceRI leading to Lyn phosphorylation. Multi-molecular complex, which includes Gads, SLP-76, PLCc, and Vav form on LAT. FceRI aggregation leads to PI3K, ERK, JNK, NF-?B, PLC and PKC activation. The activation of RAC1, CDC42, Ras lead to MAP kinase activation.    Outcome effects: These pathways lead to degranulation, release of leukotrienes and cytok87 ine synthesis. KITLG is another important protein, which stimulates degranulation and the synthesis of pro-inflammatory cytokines in mast cells, and the intersection of signaling pathways initiated by KITL (SHC1) and FceRI (GRB2) eventually causes degranulation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Mast cells infiltrate the airway mucous glands in subjects with asthma showing features of degranulation and a positive correlation with the degree of mucus obstruction. The major way of mast cell activation in allergic reactions is through the aggregation of FceRI receptors and IgE. Mast cells contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules, including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases. Upon activation, mast cells release contents of their granules into surrounding tissue (degranulation). IgE - depended degranulation occurs rapidly, and mediators released into extracellular space produce an immediate effect. The content of the granules is pre-synthesized during maturation and proliferation of mast cells.Each FceRI receptor consists of 4 subunits: alpha-particle responsible for IgE binding, one intra-cellular beta-subunit, and two intra-cellular gamma subunits. For IgE-dependent activation, an antigen must bind at least two IgE molecules at the surface of a mast cell. Antigen binding to IgE cross-links two FceRI leading to Lyn phosphorylation. Multi-molecular complex, which includes Gads, SLP-76, PLCc, and Vav form on LAT. FceRI aggregation leads to PI3K, ERK, JNK, NF-?B, PLC and PKC activation. The activation of RAC1, CDC42, Ras lead to MAP kinase activation.    Outcome effects: These pathways lead to degranulation, release of leukotrienes and cytok87 ine synthesis. KITLG is another important protein, which stimulates degranulation and the synthesis of pro-inflammatory cytokines in mast cells, and the intersection of signaling pathways initiated by KITL (SHC1) and FceRI (GRB2) eventually causes degranulation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

