xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Asthma is an IgE-mediated sensitization to inhaled allergens which involves a Th2-cell response and subsequent eosinophilic inflammation. Th2 cytokines are believed to regulate IgE synthesis, and eosinophil numbers and activity are thought to play a central role in asthma pathogenesis. Although a number of cytokines are involved in forcing B-cell to produce antibody and generate plasma cells, only IL4 and IL13 are known to promote isotype switching to IgE. B-cells in the lymphoid tissue obtain signals from T-cells acting as antigen presenting cells in B-cell maturation. Th2-cells deliver antigens in the MHC II complex to the B-cell receptor (BCR). Cytokines secreted by T-cells is the second signal provided by T-cells for B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells.    Outcome effects: During B-cell maturation, genetic recombination and isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes occurs. This is necessary to start transcription of immunoglobulins. The binding of CD40 on B-cell surface to the CD40 ligand on T-cells induces isotype switching from the initial IgM response. Start of AICDA expression is the major primary point in this process of isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Asthma is an IgE-mediated sensitization to inhaled allergens which involves a Th2-cell response and subsequent eosinophilic inflammation. Th2 cytokines are believed to regulate IgE synthesis, and eosinophil numbers and activity are thought to play a central role in asthma pathogenesis. Although a number of cytokines are involved in forcing B-cell to produce antibody and generate plasma cells, only IL4 and IL13 are known to promote isotype switching to IgE. B-cells in the lymphoid tissue obtain signals from T-cells acting as antigen presenting cells in B-cell maturation. Th2-cells deliver antigens in the MHC II complex to the B-cell receptor (BCR). Cytokines secreted by T-cells is the second signal provided by T-cells for B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells.    Outcome effects: During B-cell maturation, genetic recombination and isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes occurs. This is necessary to start transcription of immunoglobulins. The binding of CD40 on B-cell surface to the CD40 ligand on T-cells induces isotype switching from the initial IgM response. Start of AICDA expression is the major primary point in this process of isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

