xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Th17 cells produce mostly IL17, a certain amount of IL22 and IL21, small amounts of IFNg, and no IL4. In asthma, IL17 is detectable in inflamed lungs. The percentages of Th17-cells are higher in allergic asthmatics than those in healthy subjects and show a tendency to increase with the disease severity. Th17 are a subset of T-helper cells which primarily produce the two major members of the IL17 family, IL-17A and IL17F involved in the recruitment, activation and neutrophil migration. In addition, these cells also secrete IL21 and IL22. In humans, a combination of TGFB, IL1B and IL23 induces Th17 differentiation from naive T-cells. Interleukin receptor, T-cell receptor and co-activator receptor (such as CD28) pathways activate transcriptional factors NfkB, STAT3, RORalpha and RORgamma for specific Th17 - related cytokines expression.    Outcome effects: H IL17F induces CCL20 in bronchial epithelial cells. Human Th17 cells predominantly express the CCR6 receptor for CCL20 which is able to attract Th17 cells to the site of airway inflammation. This mechanism establishes a positive feedback loop resulting in the recruitment of additional Th17 cells via the induction of IL17F.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Th17 cells produce mostly IL17, a certain amount of IL22 and IL21, small amounts of IFNg, and no IL4. In asthma, IL17 is detectable in inflamed lungs. The percentages of Th17-cells are higher in allergic asthmatics than those in healthy subjects and show a tendency to increase with the disease severity. Th17 are a subset of T-helper cells which primarily produce the two major members of the IL17 family, IL-17A and IL17F involved in the recruitment, activation and neutrophil migration. In addition, these cells also secrete IL21 and IL22. In humans, a combination of TGFB, IL1B and IL23 induces Th17 differentiation from naive T-cells. Interleukin receptor, T-cell receptor and co-activator receptor (such as CD28) pathways activate transcriptional factors NfkB, STAT3, RORalpha and RORgamma for specific Th17 - related cytokines expression.    Outcome effects: H IL17F induces CCL20 in bronchial epithelial cells. Human Th17 cells predominantly express the CCR6 receptor for CCL20 which is able to attract Th17 cells to the site of airway inflammation. This mechanism establishes a positive feedback loop resulting in the recruitment of additional Th17 cells via the induction of IL17F.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

