xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: The antigen presenting cells induce naïve T-cells towards Th1 or Th2 cell response. Mucosal dendritic cells loaded with antigen migrate from the lungs to the T-cell area of mediastinal lymph nodes (MLNs). Mature dendritic cells in the MLNs select naive T-cells from the polyclonal repertoire of cells. Within 4 days, this will lead to clonal expansion of antigen-specific Th-cells. Traditionally, asthma has been known as the Th2 cell-mediated disease. T- helper cells are lymphocytes that release cytokines and present antigen-MHC complex on their surface. T-helper cells developed into two major subtypes of cells referred to as Type 1 and Type 2 helper T-cells. Th2 cells play a key role in directing the immune responses against helminthes. Additionally, Th2 cells are crucial for many types of allergic reactions.    Outcome effects: Th2-cell response is largely responsible for downstream events that include activation of B-cells, production of IgE and binding of IgE to Fce receptor on the cell surface of mast cells, resulting in mast cell degranulation and airway obstruction. IL4 is the major Th2- cytokine. STAT6 participates in Th2 differentiation, in part, by enhancing the expression of GATA3. STAT6 and GATA3 in concert with IL2-mediated STAT5 activation induce the secretion of IL4, IL5, and IL13. IL5 stimulates the differentiation of eosinophils, whereas IL4 and IL13 stimulate contraction of smooth muscle and overproduction of mucus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: The antigen presenting cells induce naïve T-cells towards Th1 or Th2 cell response. Mucosal dendritic cells loaded with antigen migrate from the lungs to the T-cell area of mediastinal lymph nodes (MLNs). Mature dendritic cells in the MLNs select naive T-cells from the polyclonal repertoire of cells. Within 4 days, this will lead to clonal expansion of antigen-specific Th-cells. Traditionally, asthma has been known as the Th2 cell-mediated disease. T- helper cells are lymphocytes that release cytokines and present antigen-MHC complex on their surface. T-helper cells developed into two major subtypes of cells referred to as Type 1 and Type 2 helper T-cells. Th2 cells play a key role in directing the immune responses against helminthes. Additionally, Th2 cells are crucial for many types of allergic reactions.    Outcome effects: Th2-cell response is largely responsible for downstream events that include activation of B-cells, production of IgE and binding of IgE to Fce receptor on the cell surface of mast cells, resulting in mast cell degranulation and airway obstruction. IL4 is the major Th2- cytokine. STAT6 participates in Th2 differentiation, in part, by enhancing the expression of GATA3. STAT6 and GATA3 in concert with IL2-mediated STAT5 activation induce the secretion of IL4, IL5, and IL13. IL5 stimulates the differentiation of eosinophils, whereas IL4 and IL13 stimulate contraction of smooth muscle and overproduction of mucus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

