xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Eosinophils are circulating granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1 to 6 percent of the entire population of white blood cells, and they circulate in blood for several hours. In lung, eosinophils appear only in case of disease (such as asthma) and survive for up to 12 days. The effector function of eosinophils is related to their cytotoxic properties. Allergic asthma is associated with eosinophilic inflammation in the airways. The proinflammatory mediators derived by eosinophils are the major contributors to inflammation in asthma, mucus hypersecretion, and fibrosis. High levels of circulating IL5, IL25 and BDNF have been detected in acute allergic asthmatics.    Outcome effects: The effects of IL25 in eosinophils activation are mediated by the IL25 receptor, composed of two subunits, IL-17RA and IL-17RB. IL5 and GM-CSF promote the local survival of tissue eosinophils during allergic airway inflammation for longer periods. BDNF has been described to modulate airway hyper-responsiveness and was involved in late allergic reaction in asthma. Locally generated IL4 and IL13 promote increased eosinophil adhesion to the airway vasculature. IL5 positively regulates phosphorylation of JAK2, as well as Lyn kinases, decreases BAX translocation and ultimately down-regulates apoptosis, and provokes eosinophil surviving. CSF appears to have a strong role in inhibiting eosinophil apoptosis at the tissue level (via STAT3 depended BAD inhibition). It is possible that IL5 triggers eosinophil differentiation through the activation of SOX4 transcription factor. Siglec8 is selectively expressed on human eosinophils at a higher level and mediates eosinophil apoptosis. Siglec8-induced eosinophils death was enhanced by IL5 and GM-CSF cytokines. The exact biochemical identity of natural tissue ligands for Siglec8 and its signaling in eosinophils remains unknown. A significant association between sequence variants of Siglec8 and asthma was observed for various SNPs among African American, Japanese and Brazilian populations.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Eosinophils are circulating granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1 to 6 percent of the entire population of white blood cells, and they circulate in blood for several hours. In lung, eosinophils appear only in case of disease (such as asthma) and survive for up to 12 days. The effector function of eosinophils is related to their cytotoxic properties. Allergic asthma is associated with eosinophilic inflammation in the airways. The proinflammatory mediators derived by eosinophils are the major contributors to inflammation in asthma, mucus hypersecretion, and fibrosis. High levels of circulating IL5, IL25 and BDNF have been detected in acute allergic asthmatics.    Outcome effects: The effects of IL25 in eosinophils activation are mediated by the IL25 receptor, composed of two subunits, IL-17RA and IL-17RB. IL5 and GM-CSF promote the local survival of tissue eosinophils during allergic airway inflammation for longer periods. BDNF has been described to modulate airway hyper-responsiveness and was involved in late allergic reaction in asthma. Locally generated IL4 and IL13 promote increased eosinophil adhesion to the airway vasculature. IL5 positively regulates phosphorylation of JAK2, as well as Lyn kinases, decreases BAX translocation and ultimately down-regulates apoptosis, and provokes eosinophil surviving. CSF appears to have a strong role in inhibiting eosinophil apoptosis at the tissue level (via STAT3 depended BAD inhibition). It is possible that IL5 triggers eosinophil differentiation through the activation of SOX4 transcription factor. Siglec8 is selectively expressed on human eosinophils at a higher level and mediates eosinophil apoptosis. Siglec8-induced eosinophils death was enhanced by IL5 and GM-CSF cytokines. The exact biochemical identity of natural tissue ligands for Siglec8 and its signaling in eosinophils remains unknown. A significant association between sequence variants of Siglec8 and asthma was observed for various SNPs among African American, Japanese and Brazilian populations.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

