xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties. Airway neutrophilia is one of the hallmarks of severe asthma.    Signaling description: In vitro studies and mouse models show that IL17F is able to act upon eosinophils to promote neutrophil activation. The asthmatic patients have a significantly higher level of serum IL17F protein as compared to that of healthy subjects. IL17F is expressed in activated CD4 T-cells, basophils, and mast cells, the three major cell types involved in allergic airway inflammation. In addition, bronchial epithelial cells are a novel cell source of IL17F, which they produce in response to IL33.    Outcome effects: The I17RA and IL17RC heterodimer recruits TRAF6, leading to the activation of MAP3K7. The Raf1-MEK1/2-ERK1/2-p90RSK/MSK1-CREB is the central signaling pathway activated by I17RA and IL17RC. On the other hand, IL17F also activates transcriptional factors such as NF-?B. Synergistic effects on eosinophil release of CXCL1, IL-8, and CCL4 were observed during the combined treatment by IL17F and IL23. These effects were dose-dependently enhanced by IL-23, but not IL-17F.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties. Airway neutrophilia is one of the hallmarks of severe asthma.    Signaling description: In vitro studies and mouse models show that IL17F is able to act upon eosinophils to promote neutrophil activation. The asthmatic patients have a significantly higher level of serum IL17F protein as compared to that of healthy subjects. IL17F is expressed in activated CD4 T-cells, basophils, and mast cells, the three major cell types involved in allergic airway inflammation. In addition, bronchial epithelial cells are a novel cell source of IL17F, which they produce in response to IL33.    Outcome effects: The I17RA and IL17RC heterodimer recruits TRAF6, leading to the activation of MAP3K7. The Raf1-MEK1/2-ERK1/2-p90RSK/MSK1-CREB is the central signaling pathway activated by I17RA and IL17RC. On the other hand, IL17F also activates transcriptional factors such as NF-?B. Synergistic effects on eosinophil release of CXCL1, IL-8, and CCL4 were observed during the combined treatment by IL17F and IL23. These effects were dose-dependently enhanced by IL-23, but not IL-17F.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

