xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Eosinophils are circulating granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1 to 6 percent of the entire population of white blood cells, and they circulate in blood for several hours. In lung, eosinophils appear only in case of disease (such as asthma) and survive for up to 12 days. The effector function of eosinophils is related to their cytotoxic properties. Allergic asthma is associated with eosinophilic inflammation in the airways. The proinflammatory mediators derived by eosinophils are the major contributors to inflammation in asthma, mucus hypersecretion, and fibrosis. Translocation of eosinophils from bone marrow to their site of action is caused by action of specific chemokines such as CCL24, CCL11. CCL24 and CCL26 mRNA and CCL24 protein were increased in airway epithelial brushings from patients with asthma and were the highest in cases of severe asthma. They bind to the receptor CCR3 which activates migration, degranulation, and synthesis of cytokines in eosinophils via G-protein-dependent mechanism. Degranulation and chemotaxis involve profound rearrangement of the actin cytoskeleton. Precise mechanism of signaling that leads to the remodeling of cytoskeleton in eosinophils is unknown. Key players in this process are small GTPase RhoA and its effector kinases, ROCK1 and ROCK2, which cause an increase in calcium levels under influence of CCR3 ligands. Ligand-bound CCR3 causes activation of MAPK1/MAPK3. Activation of MAPK1/ MAPK3 pathway mediates arachidonic acid release, contributing to the secretion of lipid mediators, leading to degranulation and inflammatory responses. LTB4, and PGD2 and neurokinins are additional chemoattractants and activators of eosinophils in inflammation. Other cytokines, chemokines, small molecules, and inflammatory mediators can also activate eosinophils, but in a much lesser extent. During inflammation, circulating granulocytes first interact with VCAM1, MADCAM1 on the inflamed vascular endothelium of blood vessels. Similarly to other granulocytes, eosinophils are activated by a signal from IgE. Eosinophils express FceRII (CD23), which is a calcium-dependent lectin receptor with less affinity to IgE, as compared to FceRI. Eosinophil expression of FceRI is minimal and does not activate eosinophils. Polymorphisms within FCER2, the gene encoding CD23, have been associated with atopy, increased risk of exacerbations in patients with asthma, and high serum IgE levels.    Outcome effects: Upon activation, eosinophils release the content of their granules in the site of inflammation. Proteins PRG2, RNASE3, EPC, RNASE2 are specific for eosinophils. They are the enzymes directly implicated in the cell damage that possess cytotoxic activities. Upon activation, eosinophils can release free radicals. One of mechanisms for that is via the NADPH oxidase in the plasma membrane under the action of Rac1 and Rac2. EPX also participates in the production of free radicals. Eosinophils do not synthesize and release all of their mediators at once, and the process takes place in a selective manner. Mechanisms that regulate the selective synthesis of pro-inflammatory mediators require further research. Eosinophils are capable of both the release of histamine and its neutralization (enzymatically or through phagocytosis). That is to say, eosinophils can play either pro-allergic or defensive anti-allergic role.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Eosinophils are circulating granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1 to 6 percent of the entire population of white blood cells, and they circulate in blood for several hours. In lung, eosinophils appear only in case of disease (such as asthma) and survive for up to 12 days. The effector function of eosinophils is related to their cytotoxic properties. Allergic asthma is associated with eosinophilic inflammation in the airways. The proinflammatory mediators derived by eosinophils are the major contributors to inflammation in asthma, mucus hypersecretion, and fibrosis. Translocation of eosinophils from bone marrow to their site of action is caused by action of specific chemokines such as CCL24, CCL11. CCL24 and CCL26 mRNA and CCL24 protein were increased in airway epithelial brushings from patients with asthma and were the highest in cases of severe asthma. They bind to the receptor CCR3 which activates migration, degranulation, and synthesis of cytokines in eosinophils via G-protein-dependent mechanism. Degranulation and chemotaxis involve profound rearrangement of the actin cytoskeleton. Precise mechanism of signaling that leads to the remodeling of cytoskeleton in eosinophils is unknown. Key players in this process are small GTPase RhoA and its effector kinases, ROCK1 and ROCK2, which cause an increase in calcium levels under influence of CCR3 ligands. Ligand-bound CCR3 causes activation of MAPK1/MAPK3. Activation of MAPK1/ MAPK3 pathway mediates arachidonic acid release, contributing to the secretion of lipid mediators, leading to degranulation and inflammatory responses. LTB4, and PGD2 and neurokinins are additional chemoattractants and activators of eosinophils in inflammation. Other cytokines, chemokines, small molecules, and inflammatory mediators can also activate eosinophils, but in a much lesser extent. During inflammation, circulating granulocytes first interact with VCAM1, MADCAM1 on the inflamed vascular endothelium of blood vessels. Similarly to other granulocytes, eosinophils are activated by a signal from IgE. Eosinophils express FceRII (CD23), which is a calcium-dependent lectin receptor with less affinity to IgE, as compared to FceRI. Eosinophil expression of FceRI is minimal and does not activate eosinophils. Polymorphisms within FCER2, the gene encoding CD23, have been associated with atopy, increased risk of exacerbations in patients with asthma, and high serum IgE levels.    Outcome effects: Upon activation, eosinophils release the content of their granules in the site of inflammation. Proteins PRG2, RNASE3, EPC, RNASE2 are specific for eosinophils. They are the enzymes directly implicated in the cell damage that possess cytotoxic activities. Upon activation, eosinophils can release free radicals. One of mechanisms for that is via the NADPH oxidase in the plasma membrane under the action of Rac1 and Rac2. EPX also participates in the production of free radicals. Eosinophils do not synthesize and release all of their mediators at once, and the process takes place in a selective manner. Mechanisms that regulate the selective synthesis of pro-inflammatory mediators require further research. Eosinophils are capable of both the release of histamine and its neutralization (enzymatically or through phagocytosis). That is to say, eosinophils can play either pro-allergic or defensive anti-allergic role.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

