xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: TSLP protein is highly expressed in ASM (airway smooth muscle) bundles from asthma and COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) patients. TSLP is produced by epithelial cells, mast cells, ASM cells, and fibroblasts. The inflammatory mediators IL1b and TNFa regulate human TSLP gene expression. Human TSLP mRNA concentrations increase following exposure to TLR2, TLR3, TLR8, and TLR9 ligands in airway bronchial epithelial cells.    Outcome effects: TSLP receptor (TSLPR) activation in bronchial epithelial cells involves Stat3 phosphorylation and induces IL13 production and cell proliferation. TSLP activates cellular senescentin in airway epithelium of patients with asthma. In dendritic cells, TSLP stimulates maturation, which facilitates the generation of effector T-cells, and triggers airway inflammation through subsequent adaptive T-cell responses.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: TSLP protein is highly expressed in ASM (airway smooth muscle) bundles from asthma and COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) patients. TSLP is produced by epithelial cells, mast cells, ASM cells, and fibroblasts. The inflammatory mediators IL1b and TNFa regulate human TSLP gene expression. Human TSLP mRNA concentrations increase following exposure to TLR2, TLR3, TLR8, and TLR9 ligands in airway bronchial epithelial cells.    Outcome effects: TSLP receptor (TSLPR) activation in bronchial epithelial cells involves Stat3 phosphorylation and induces IL13 production and cell proliferation. TSLP activates cellular senescentin in airway epithelium of patients with asthma. In dendritic cells, TSLP stimulates maturation, which facilitates the generation of effector T-cells, and triggers airway inflammation through subsequent adaptive T-cell responses.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

