xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: In asthma, excessive shortening of ASM (airway smooth muscle) cells leading to narrowing of the airways contributes significantly to obstruction. This is partially due to the fact that the smooth muscle mass within the airway wall is increased in asthma via hypertrophy and/or hyperplasia, with the intrinsic contractile properties of individual muscle cells being unchanged. ASM proliferation is induced by mitogens acting mainly via tyrosine kinase receptors and G protein-coupled receptors. Stimulation of these receptors leads to activation of ERK and phosphoinositide 3-PI3K cascades.    Outcome effects: ERK and PI3K-dependent signaling molecules (PDK1, PKB/Akt, p70S6K, Rac1, etc.) cooperate to activate the transcription factors (SP1, CREB, NFkB) required for cyclin D1 gene expression and cell growth. Endothelin-1 is elevated in asthmatic airways and may promote increased ASM mass via its effects on cell growth and apoptosis. ECM components, such as fibronectin, laminin-1 and collagens I and IV, provide strong survival signals to cultured human ASM cells, an effect mediated in part via integrin a5b1.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: In asthma, excessive shortening of ASM (airway smooth muscle) cells leading to narrowing of the airways contributes significantly to obstruction. This is partially due to the fact that the smooth muscle mass within the airway wall is increased in asthma via hypertrophy and/or hyperplasia, with the intrinsic contractile properties of individual muscle cells being unchanged. ASM proliferation is induced by mitogens acting mainly via tyrosine kinase receptors and G protein-coupled receptors. Stimulation of these receptors leads to activation of ERK and phosphoinositide 3-PI3K cascades.    Outcome effects: ERK and PI3K-dependent signaling molecules (PDK1, PKB/Akt, p70S6K, Rac1, etc.) cooperate to activate the transcription factors (SP1, CREB, NFkB) required for cyclin D1 gene expression and cell growth. Endothelin-1 is elevated in asthmatic airways and may promote increased ASM mass via its effects on cell growth and apoptosis. ECM components, such as fibronectin, laminin-1 and collagens I and IV, provide strong survival signals to cultured human ASM cells, an effect mediated in part via integrin a5b1.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

