xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: In asthma, excessive shortening of ASM (airway smooth muscle) cells leading to narrowing of the airways contributes significantly to obstruction. This is partially due to the fact that the intrinsic contractile properties of asthmatic ASM are indeed different from normal. Contraction of smooth muscle, which causes airway narrowing, can be physiologically induced by the release of acetylcholine from efferent parasympathetic nerves or histamine and cysteinyl leukotrienes from mast cells and basophils. The major signaling pathway involved in ASM contraction is a Ca2+ related pathway. Interaction of contractile agonists with their G protein-coupled receptors (GPCR) leads to the activation of PLCb/ Ca2 /calmodulin/ MLCK pathway.    Outcome effects: MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chain (MLC), leading to ASM contraction, which is terminated by MLC phosphatase (MLCP). In addition to IP3, Ca2+ release is also stimulated by agonist-induced activation of CD38. Ca2+ can flow across the plasma membrane through ion channels. MLCK expression was increased in all patients with asthma and COPD compared to controls in a subset of studies. The expression of the ATP2A2 isoform mRNA in human ASM was reduced in patients with moderately severe asthma. The reduction of ATP2A2 expression resulted in phenotypic shifting to an asthmatic ASM type with increased motility, secretion, and slow Ca2+ recovery. Overexpression of ORMDL3 was found to be associated with reduced ATP2A2 activity. NOX4 expression, an important source of ROS, was also increased in asthmatic ASM. ORMDL3 is an asthma candidate gene. SNPs in the region which contains the ORMDL3 gene on chromosome 17q21 were related to childhood asthma risk.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: In asthma, excessive shortening of ASM (airway smooth muscle) cells leading to narrowing of the airways contributes significantly to obstruction. This is partially due to the fact that the intrinsic contractile properties of asthmatic ASM are indeed different from normal. Contraction of smooth muscle, which causes airway narrowing, can be physiologically induced by the release of acetylcholine from efferent parasympathetic nerves or histamine and cysteinyl leukotrienes from mast cells and basophils. The major signaling pathway involved in ASM contraction is a Ca2+ related pathway. Interaction of contractile agonists with their G protein-coupled receptors (GPCR) leads to the activation of PLCb/ Ca2 /calmodulin/ MLCK pathway.    Outcome effects: MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chain (MLC), leading to ASM contraction, which is terminated by MLC phosphatase (MLCP). In addition to IP3, Ca2+ release is also stimulated by agonist-induced activation of CD38. Ca2+ can flow across the plasma membrane through ion channels. MLCK expression was increased in all patients with asthma and COPD compared to controls in a subset of studies. The expression of the ATP2A2 isoform mRNA in human ASM was reduced in patients with moderately severe asthma. The reduction of ATP2A2 expression resulted in phenotypic shifting to an asthmatic ASM type with increased motility, secretion, and slow Ca2+ recovery. Overexpression of ORMDL3 was found to be associated with reduced ATP2A2 activity. NOX4 expression, an important source of ROS, was also increased in asthmatic ASM. ORMDL3 is an asthma candidate gene. SNPs in the region which contains the ORMDL3 gene on chromosome 17q21 were related to childhood asthma risk.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

