xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties. Mucus overproduction is an important feature of bronchial asthma, contributing to mortality in the disease, and goblet cells are major mucus-producing cells.    Signaling description: Airway mucus is a heterogeneous mixture of secreted polypeptides, cells, and cellular debris that are tethered together at the fluid surface by oligomeric mucin complexes. MUC5AC is probably the dominant mucin gene induced in the asthma mouse model. Following their synthesis and post-translational processing, mucins are stored within the intracellular secretory granules until stimulated for release by regulated exocytosis. EGF-R and Th2 cytokine pathways offer the greatest potential for inhibition of excessive mucus production. MARCKS has been shown to be a major regulator of the airway granule exocytosis of mucins, but the precise regulatory mechanism remains unknown. Under normal conditions, intracellular MARCKS is attached to the plasma membrane.    Outcome effects: After being phosphorylated by activated PKC, MARCKS translocates from the plasma membrane to the cytoplasm. HSP70 and cysteine string protein (DNAJC5) associate with MARCKS during mucin secretion. VAMP8 is one of the SNAREs regulating mucin granule exocytosis in the goblet cells. Synaptotagmin 2 is essential for regulated excocytic mucin release from goblet cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties. Mucus overproduction is an important feature of bronchial asthma, contributing to mortality in the disease, and goblet cells are major mucus-producing cells.    Signaling description: Airway mucus is a heterogeneous mixture of secreted polypeptides, cells, and cellular debris that are tethered together at the fluid surface by oligomeric mucin complexes. MUC5AC is probably the dominant mucin gene induced in the asthma mouse model. Following their synthesis and post-translational processing, mucins are stored within the intracellular secretory granules until stimulated for release by regulated exocytosis. EGF-R and Th2 cytokine pathways offer the greatest potential for inhibition of excessive mucus production. MARCKS has been shown to be a major regulator of the airway granule exocytosis of mucins, but the precise regulatory mechanism remains unknown. Under normal conditions, intracellular MARCKS is attached to the plasma membrane.    Outcome effects: After being phosphorylated by activated PKC, MARCKS translocates from the plasma membrane to the cytoplasm. HSP70 and cysteine string protein (DNAJC5) associate with MARCKS during mucin secretion. VAMP8 is one of the SNAREs regulating mucin granule exocytosis in the goblet cells. Synaptotagmin 2 is essential for regulated excocytic mucin release from goblet cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

