xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties. Mucus overproduction is an important feature of bronchial asthma, contributing to mortality in the disease, and goblet cells are major mucus-producing cells.    Signaling description: Airway mucus is a heterogeneous mixture of secreted polypeptides, cells, and cellular debris that are tethered together at the fluid surface by oligomeric mucin complexes. Oversecreted mucins contribute significantly to the viscoelastic properties of the extracellular mucous layer. MUC5AC is probably the dominant mucin gene induced in the asthma mouse model.TNF-alfa, EGFR signaling and Th2 cytokines upregulate mucin synthesis and the development of goblet cell hyperplasia. IL9 may act predominantly via calcium-activated chloride channels (CLCA), IL1beta via PTGS2. IL-4 and IL-13 trigger airway hyperreactivity and goblet cell metaplasia and mucin overproduction through MUC5AC expression and modulation of the expression of a variety of ion channels and transporters (aquaporins (AQPs), CLCA, SLC26A4) within the respiratory epithelium. IL13RA2 has been suggested to act as a 'decoy receptor' in the airway epithelium by inhibiting the IL-13 signal.    Outcome effects: Pendrin (SLC26A4) is an integral membrane protein exchanging anions such as bicarbonate, iodide and others for chloride. SLC26A4 expression increases in respiratory diseases including bronchial asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and rhinovirus infection. SLC26A4 stimulates Cl-/HCO3- exchange activity, which increases the production of mucus and increases viscosity of the airway surface liquid (ASL). Epithelial ALOX15 expression increases in asthma. IL-13 induction of 15-HETE-PE enhances MUC5AC expression in human airway epithelial cells. There is a (Hypothesis) that ALOX15/15HETE-PE could interact with PEBP1 to activate Raf-1/MEK/ERK and amplify the IL-4Ralfa downstream pathways. ALOX15 and PEBP1 interactions occur primarily at the cell membrane. DENND1B, which may be involved in TNF signaling was recently identified as an asthma susceptibility gene in a GWAS on children.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties. Mucus overproduction is an important feature of bronchial asthma, contributing to mortality in the disease, and goblet cells are major mucus-producing cells.    Signaling description: Airway mucus is a heterogeneous mixture of secreted polypeptides, cells, and cellular debris that are tethered together at the fluid surface by oligomeric mucin complexes. Oversecreted mucins contribute significantly to the viscoelastic properties of the extracellular mucous layer. MUC5AC is probably the dominant mucin gene induced in the asthma mouse model.TNF-alfa, EGFR signaling and Th2 cytokines upregulate mucin synthesis and the development of goblet cell hyperplasia. IL9 may act predominantly via calcium-activated chloride channels (CLCA), IL1beta via PTGS2. IL-4 and IL-13 trigger airway hyperreactivity and goblet cell metaplasia and mucin overproduction through MUC5AC expression and modulation of the expression of a variety of ion channels and transporters (aquaporins (AQPs), CLCA, SLC26A4) within the respiratory epithelium. IL13RA2 has been suggested to act as a 'decoy receptor' in the airway epithelium by inhibiting the IL-13 signal.    Outcome effects: Pendrin (SLC26A4) is an integral membrane protein exchanging anions such as bicarbonate, iodide and others for chloride. SLC26A4 expression increases in respiratory diseases including bronchial asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and rhinovirus infection. SLC26A4 stimulates Cl-/HCO3- exchange activity, which increases the production of mucus and increases viscosity of the airway surface liquid (ASL). Epithelial ALOX15 expression increases in asthma. IL-13 induction of 15-HETE-PE enhances MUC5AC expression in human airway epithelial cells. There is a (Hypothesis) that ALOX15/15HETE-PE could interact with PEBP1 to activate Raf-1/MEK/ERK and amplify the IL-4Ralfa downstream pathways. ALOX15 and PEBP1 interactions occur primarily at the cell membrane. DENND1B, which may be involved in TNF signaling was recently identified as an asthma susceptibility gene in a GWAS on children.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

