xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Club cells are a cell type of bronchiolar epithelia which express SCGB1A1 to protect the bronchiole lining. LGALS3 is another club cell specific secreted protein. The role of club cells in asthma is still controversial. Club cells are involved in allergen-induced mucus production, but seem to exert protective effects against asthma. In addition, club cells are the major progenitors for goblet cells. Club cells give rise to goblet cells following an allergen challenge, a process called mucus metaplasia. NGF promotes the proliferation of lung epithelium in vitro and the renewal of club cells following lung injury in vivo. Serum levels of NGF have been shown to be enhanced in asthmatics. Club cell's own mucin expression is limited specifically to a subset of club cells (generations 2-4) that continue to express SCGB1A1. There is a (Hypothesis) that allergen-induced mucus production depends upon IL4R signaling in club cells. In mice, IL13 can induce mucus metaplasia via STAT6. Probably, Notch may silence the ciliated program in the cells that will continue to expand and differentiate into secretory cells in the developing airways. SPDEF is required for goblet cell differentiation induced by allergens. SPDEF regulates a group of genes associated with mucin biosynthesis and secretion, suppressing of TTF1 and FOXA2 and inducting of FOXA3. SPDEF is induced following pulmonary allergen and IL-13 exposure. Expression of Sox2 in club cells is required also for the differentiation of ciliated and goblet cells in the postnatal lung.    Outcome effects: Club cells drive eosinophil accumulation in allergic asthma through CCL11 production.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: Club cells are a cell type of bronchiolar epithelia which express SCGB1A1 to protect the bronchiole lining. LGALS3 is another club cell specific secreted protein. The role of club cells in asthma is still controversial. Club cells are involved in allergen-induced mucus production, but seem to exert protective effects against asthma. In addition, club cells are the major progenitors for goblet cells. Club cells give rise to goblet cells following an allergen challenge, a process called mucus metaplasia. NGF promotes the proliferation of lung epithelium in vitro and the renewal of club cells following lung injury in vivo. Serum levels of NGF have been shown to be enhanced in asthmatics. Club cell's own mucin expression is limited specifically to a subset of club cells (generations 2-4) that continue to express SCGB1A1. There is a (Hypothesis) that allergen-induced mucus production depends upon IL4R signaling in club cells. In mice, IL13 can induce mucus metaplasia via STAT6. Probably, Notch may silence the ciliated program in the cells that will continue to expand and differentiate into secretory cells in the developing airways. SPDEF is required for goblet cell differentiation induced by allergens. SPDEF regulates a group of genes associated with mucin biosynthesis and secretion, suppressing of TTF1 and FOXA2 and inducting of FOXA3. SPDEF is induced following pulmonary allergen and IL-13 exposure. Expression of Sox2 in club cells is required also for the differentiation of ciliated and goblet cells in the postnatal lung.    Outcome effects: Club cells drive eosinophil accumulation in allergic asthma through CCL11 production.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression are highlighted in red." EXACT []

