xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: ADAM33 association with asthma has been replicated in over 33 different population samples worldwide. ADAM33 protein expression is increased in epithelium, smooth muscle and mesenchymal cells of asthmatic patients compared to those of nonasthmatic controls. Most likely, ADAM33 acts as a cell surface sheddase to release growth factors and modify cell surface receptor expression. Sheddases are membrane-bound enzymes that cleave extracellular portions of transmembrane proteins, releasing the soluble ectodomains from the cell surface. ADAM33 might be linked to the action of TGF-ß signaling.    Outcome effects: TGF-ß signaling increases ADAM33 expression during the differentiation of the fibroblastic progenitors among the components of EMTU (epithelial mesenchymal tropic unit) to myofibroblasts.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Asthma is a common long-term lung disorder in which inflammation of the bronchi leads to narrowing of the airways creating breathing difficulties.    Signaling description: ADAM33 association with asthma has been replicated in over 33 different population samples worldwide. ADAM33 protein expression is increased in epithelium, smooth muscle and mesenchymal cells of asthmatic patients compared to those of nonasthmatic controls. Most likely, ADAM33 acts as a cell surface sheddase to release growth factors and modify cell surface receptor expression. Sheddases are membrane-bound enzymes that cleave extracellular portions of transmembrane proteins, releasing the soluble ectodomains from the cell surface. ADAM33 might be linked to the action of TGF-ß signaling.    Outcome effects: TGF-ß signaling increases ADAM33 expression during the differentiation of the fibroblastic progenitors among the components of EMTU (epithelial mesenchymal tropic unit) to myofibroblasts.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

