synonym: "Organ_System: respiratory system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction.    Signaling description: Normally, as soon as the function of alveolar macrophages is complete, they are negatively regulated by soluble mediators and by cell-cell interactions, primarily by interactions with bronchial and alveolar epithelial cells. CD200 receptor (CD200R1) interacts with CD200 on the respiratory epithelium, recruiting DOK2 and Ras GTPase-activating protein RASA2, which inhibits Ras/MAPK/JUN-FOS inflammatory pathways. This inhibitory mechanism is impaired due to the death of bronchial and alveolar epithelial cells in COPD and pulmonary emphysema. IL10 is abundant in the lungs and restricts inflammation by triggering JAK1 signaling and activating the STAT3 pathway to induce the expression of negative regulators such as SOCS3 and MIR146B.    Outcome effects: SOCS3 blocks the expression of pro-inflammatory cytokines, whereas MIR146B directly inhibits TLR4 expression and signaling. The mannose receptor (MRC1) blocks the recognition of TLR4 ligands and restricts phagocytosis of pathogens such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa. SFTPA1 and SFTPD are abundant in the airways and block TLR2 and TLR4 interactions with their respective ligands, as well as their interactions with the TLR coreceptors LY96 and CD14, which prevents the activation of NF-kB and the initiation of the inflammatory response. The binding of surfactant proteins to SIRPA recruits PTPN6 and activates RHOA, which inhibits phagocytosis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction.    Signaling description: Normally, as soon as the function of alveolar macrophages is complete, they are negatively regulated by soluble mediators and by cell-cell interactions, primarily by interactions with bronchial and alveolar epithelial cells. CD200 receptor (CD200R1) interacts with CD200 on the respiratory epithelium, recruiting DOK2 and Ras GTPase-activating protein RASA2, which inhibits Ras/MAPK/JUN-FOS inflammatory pathways. This inhibitory mechanism is impaired due to the death of bronchial and alveolar epithelial cells in COPD and pulmonary emphysema. IL10 is abundant in the lungs and restricts inflammation by triggering JAK1 signaling and activating the STAT3 pathway to induce the expression of negative regulators such as SOCS3 and MIR146B.    Outcome effects: SOCS3 blocks the expression of pro-inflammatory cytokines, whereas MIR146B directly inhibits TLR4 expression and signaling. The mannose receptor (MRC1) blocks the recognition of TLR4 ligands and restricts phagocytosis of pathogens such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa. SFTPA1 and SFTPD are abundant in the airways and block TLR2 and TLR4 interactions with their respective ligands, as well as their interactions with the TLR coreceptors LY96 and CD14, which prevents the activation of NF-kB and the initiation of the inflammatory response. The binding of surfactant proteins to SIRPA recruits PTPN6 and activates RHOA, which inhibits phagocytosis." EXACT []

