synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The airway and alveoli are lined with epithelial cells, which provide a barrier between the host and the environment, and are also an important source of mediator molecules. The alveolar epithelial cells have an important role in the pathogenesis COPD.    Signaling description: There is an increase in alveolar epithelial cell apoptosis in the lungs of COPD patients, and loss of alveolar epithelial cells is one of the major factors in the development of emphysema. FAS ligand (FASLG) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF) trigger apoptosis by binding FAS and TNFRSF1A. The death receptors recruit procaspase-8 through the adaptor protein FADD. In the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) pathway, CASP12 is activated in response to stress signals such as hypoxia. In addition, cigarette smoke induces ER stress response through ATF4-mediated induction of DDIT3 which leads to apoptosis. MAP1LC3B, an autophagic protein, is involved in the regulation of cigarette smoke-induced apoptosis. Under normal conditions, MAP1LC3B forms a multimeric complex with CAV1 and the death receptor FAS in pulmonary epithelial cells. Research has shown that stimulation of epithelial cells with cigarette smoke extract results in rapid dissociation of these complexes. MAP1LC3B facilitates the dissociation of CAV1 from FAS, allowing FAS to stimulate apoptosis through the formation of the death inducing signal complex with FADD and CASP8.    Signaling description: Following cleavage, mature CASP8 then activates CASP3 or cleaves BID. Truncated BID interacts with BAX. CYCS is released through a pore formed in the outer mitochondrial membrane. MMP7 cleaves and activates FASLG, thereby mediating apoptosis. Cytotoxic T-cells can release granzyme B (GZMB) and perforin (PRF1), a pore-forming protein. GZMB activates CASP3 through cleavage and can also cleave CASP8. The effector CASP3 cleaves DFFA and releases it from DFFB, which then translocates from the cytoplasm to the nucleus and acts as an active endonuclease and fragments DNA."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The airway and alveoli are lined with epithelial cells, which provide a barrier between the host and the environment, and are also an important source of mediator molecules. The alveolar epithelial cells have an important role in the pathogenesis COPD.    Signaling description: There is an increase in alveolar epithelial cell apoptosis in the lungs of COPD patients, and loss of alveolar epithelial cells is one of the major factors in the development of emphysema. FAS ligand (FASLG) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF) trigger apoptosis by binding FAS and TNFRSF1A. The death receptors recruit procaspase-8 through the adaptor protein FADD. In the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) pathway, CASP12 is activated in response to stress signals such as hypoxia. In addition, cigarette smoke induces ER stress response through ATF4-mediated induction of DDIT3 which leads to apoptosis. MAP1LC3B, an autophagic protein, is involved in the regulation of cigarette smoke-induced apoptosis. Under normal conditions, MAP1LC3B forms a multimeric complex with CAV1 and the death receptor FAS in pulmonary epithelial cells. Research has shown that stimulation of epithelial cells with cigarette smoke extract results in rapid dissociation of these complexes. MAP1LC3B facilitates the dissociation of CAV1 from FAS, allowing FAS to stimulate apoptosis through the formation of the death inducing signal complex with FADD and CASP8.    Signaling description: Following cleavage, mature CASP8 then activates CASP3 or cleaves BID. Truncated BID interacts with BAX. CYCS is released through a pore formed in the outer mitochondrial membrane. MMP7 cleaves and activates FASLG, thereby mediating apoptosis. Cytotoxic T-cells can release granzyme B (GZMB) and perforin (PRF1), a pore-forming protein. GZMB activates CASP3 through cleavage and can also cleave CASP8. The effector CASP3 cleaves DFFA and releases it from DFFB, which then translocates from the cytoplasm to the nucleus and acts as an active endonuclease and fragments DNA." EXACT []

