xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. Macrophages are believed to play a pivotal role in the pathophysiology of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Their numbers are increased (5-10 fold) in the airways, lung parenchyma, bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, and sputum of smokers and patients with COPD.    Signaling description: Exposure to tobacco and air pollutants is the key provoking factor of COPD development. Later, bacterial and viral infections add to chronic inflammation maintenance. Cigarette smoke activates innate immune cells including epithelial cells and macrophages by triggering pattern recognition receptors (PRRs), such as toll-like receptors (TLRs). PRRs recognize molecules that are exclusive to microbes called pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and specific endogenous molecules, which are normally intracellular, but are released after cell damage called damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). DAMPs include HMGB1, uric acid, extracellular ATP, heat shock proteins, hyaluronic acid, VCAN, and heparan sulfate. One of the most important PAMPs in COPD is bacterial LPS. Downstream pathways that follow DAMP/PAMP/TLR binding include the activation of MYD88, TIRAP, and TICAM1/2 which leads to the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines (IL6, IL8, IL1A, IL1B, and IL18), chemokines (CXCL1 and CCL2), matrix metalloproteinases (MMP2, MMP9, and MMP12), and elastolytic enzymes (CTSL1, CTSS, and CTSK). Most of the inflammatory proteins that are upregulated in COPD macrophages are regulated by NF-kB which is activated in alveolar macrophages of COPD patients. The activation of TLRs and AGER leads to an increased expression of pro-IL1B and pro-IL18, which are subsequently cleaved into mature interleukins by CASP1, which is a member of NLRP3-inflammasome. The inflammasome may be activated by ATP acting through P2RX7 and P2RY2, as well as by ROS and uric acid. NLRP3 inflammasome assembly is also triggered by low intracellular potassium (K+) levels.    Outcome effects: The consequence of sustained macrophage activation in COPD is chronic inflammation and lung destruction by oxygen radicals and proteolytic enzymes.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. Macrophages are believed to play a pivotal role in the pathophysiology of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Their numbers are increased (5-10 fold) in the airways, lung parenchyma, bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, and sputum of smokers and patients with COPD.    Signaling description: Exposure to tobacco and air pollutants is the key provoking factor of COPD development. Later, bacterial and viral infections add to chronic inflammation maintenance. Cigarette smoke activates innate immune cells including epithelial cells and macrophages by triggering pattern recognition receptors (PRRs), such as toll-like receptors (TLRs). PRRs recognize molecules that are exclusive to microbes called pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and specific endogenous molecules, which are normally intracellular, but are released after cell damage called damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). DAMPs include HMGB1, uric acid, extracellular ATP, heat shock proteins, hyaluronic acid, VCAN, and heparan sulfate. One of the most important PAMPs in COPD is bacterial LPS. Downstream pathways that follow DAMP/PAMP/TLR binding include the activation of MYD88, TIRAP, and TICAM1/2 which leads to the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines (IL6, IL8, IL1A, IL1B, and IL18), chemokines (CXCL1 and CCL2), matrix metalloproteinases (MMP2, MMP9, and MMP12), and elastolytic enzymes (CTSL1, CTSS, and CTSK). Most of the inflammatory proteins that are upregulated in COPD macrophages are regulated by NF-kB which is activated in alveolar macrophages of COPD patients. The activation of TLRs and AGER leads to an increased expression of pro-IL1B and pro-IL18, which are subsequently cleaved into mature interleukins by CASP1, which is a member of NLRP3-inflammasome. The inflammasome may be activated by ATP acting through P2RX7 and P2RY2, as well as by ROS and uric acid. NLRP3 inflammasome assembly is also triggered by low intracellular potassium (K+) levels.    Outcome effects: The consequence of sustained macrophage activation in COPD is chronic inflammation and lung destruction by oxygen radicals and proteolytic enzymes.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

