xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction.    Signaling description: The level of acetylcholine in lung tissue is increased in COPD due to its release from nerve terminals and activation of non-neuronal cholinergic system. Acetylcholine is biosynthesized from choline and acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase (CHAT) by several cell types in the respiratory tract. Airway neurons, airway epithelial cells, macrophages, mast cells, and fibroblasts express CHAT. Mediators of inflammation, including TAC1, prostaglandins, and TXA2, enhance the release of acetylcholine from nerve endings. Muscarinic receptor agonists induce the contraction of airway smooth muscle via Ca2+-dependent and Ca2+-independent pathways. Through GNAI1 and GNAQ, the muscarinic M2 and M3 receptors (CHRM2 and CHRM3) activate PLC, which releases IP3 and diacylglycerol following hydrolytic conversion of PIP2. IP3 induces the release of Ca2+ from internal sarcoplasmic reticulum stores. The binding of CHRM3 to CD38, through unidentified mechanisms, contributes to the production of cADPR and the release of Ca2+ through RYR3 in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Ca2+ release increases free cytosolic Ca2+ and promotes calmodulin-dependent activation of MYLK. In addition, PLC-derived diacylglycerol activates and consequently enhances Ca2+ sensitivity. Diacylglycerol activates PKC and results in PPP1R14A phosphorylation and downstream MLCP inhibition. ROCK1, which is activated by RHOA, both phosphorylates PPP1R14A and inhibits MLCP directly.    Outcome effects: MYLK-mediated phosphorylation of MYL9 in the contractile apparatus is necessary to induce smooth muscle contraction. The level of myosin light chain phosphorylation is also controlled by pathways that inhibit MLCP.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction.    Signaling description: The level of acetylcholine in lung tissue is increased in COPD due to its release from nerve terminals and activation of non-neuronal cholinergic system. Acetylcholine is biosynthesized from choline and acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase (CHAT) by several cell types in the respiratory tract. Airway neurons, airway epithelial cells, macrophages, mast cells, and fibroblasts express CHAT. Mediators of inflammation, including TAC1, prostaglandins, and TXA2, enhance the release of acetylcholine from nerve endings. Muscarinic receptor agonists induce the contraction of airway smooth muscle via Ca2+-dependent and Ca2+-independent pathways. Through GNAI1 and GNAQ, the muscarinic M2 and M3 receptors (CHRM2 and CHRM3) activate PLC, which releases IP3 and diacylglycerol following hydrolytic conversion of PIP2. IP3 induces the release of Ca2+ from internal sarcoplasmic reticulum stores. The binding of CHRM3 to CD38, through unidentified mechanisms, contributes to the production of cADPR and the release of Ca2+ through RYR3 in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Ca2+ release increases free cytosolic Ca2+ and promotes calmodulin-dependent activation of MYLK. In addition, PLC-derived diacylglycerol activates and consequently enhances Ca2+ sensitivity. Diacylglycerol activates PKC and results in PPP1R14A phosphorylation and downstream MLCP inhibition. ROCK1, which is activated by RHOA, both phosphorylates PPP1R14A and inhibits MLCP directly.    Outcome effects: MYLK-mediated phosphorylation of MYL9 in the contractile apparatus is necessary to induce smooth muscle contraction. The level of myosin light chain phosphorylation is also controlled by pathways that inhibit MLCP.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

