xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The two major types of COPD are chronic bronchitis and emphysema.    Signaling description: Histological examinations of lungs have shown that the alveolar walls in emphysema appear to be remarkably thin and almost avascular. In endothelial cells, the synthesis of prostacyclin (PGI2) and nitric oxide (NO) is an outcome of KDR activation.    Outcome effects: Normally, NO and PGI2 promote endothelial cell survival. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) can damage the promoter region of the VEGFA gene, and thus impair VEGFA transcription leading to the lack of NO and PGI2 as well as capillary loss.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The two major types of COPD are chronic bronchitis and emphysema.    Signaling description: Histological examinations of lungs have shown that the alveolar walls in emphysema appear to be remarkably thin and almost avascular. In endothelial cells, the synthesis of prostacyclin (PGI2) and nitric oxide (NO) is an outcome of KDR activation.    Outcome effects: Normally, NO and PGI2 promote endothelial cell survival. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) can damage the promoter region of the VEGFA gene, and thus impair VEGFA transcription leading to the lack of NO and PGI2 as well as capillary loss.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

