xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The airway and alveoli are lined with epithelial cells, which provide a barrier between the host and the environment, and are also an important source of mediator molecules. The alveolar epithelial cells have an important role in the pathogenesis COPD.    Signaling description: Cigarette smoke induces both autophagy and apoptosis in alveolar epithelial cells. In lung tissue from patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), autophagy is promoted by increased autophagic vacuoles and activation of autophagic proteins such as ATG4B, ATG5-ATG12, and ATG7. Cigarette smoke induces endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress response and autophagy through the activation of EIF2AK3, ATF4, and ATF6. The autophagic protein, MAP1LC3B has a pivotal role not only in autophagy, but also in apoptosis since it associates with the extrinsic apoptotic factor, FAS. The MAP1LC3B promoter has binding sites for EGR1 and E2F4 transcription factors. Both factors have been shown to bind to the MAP1LC3B promoter in response to cigarette smoke extract in epithelial cells.    Outcome effects: BECN1 interacts with anti-apoptotic BCL2 family members including BCL2 and BCL2L1. The binding of BCL2 family proteins to BECN1 inhibits autophagy by preventing the association of BECN1 with PIK3C3. Autophagy induction correlates with the dissociation of BECN1 from BCL2 or BCL2L1."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The airway and alveoli are lined with epithelial cells, which provide a barrier between the host and the environment, and are also an important source of mediator molecules. The alveolar epithelial cells have an important role in the pathogenesis COPD.    Signaling description: Cigarette smoke induces both autophagy and apoptosis in alveolar epithelial cells. In lung tissue from patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), autophagy is promoted by increased autophagic vacuoles and activation of autophagic proteins such as ATG4B, ATG5-ATG12, and ATG7. Cigarette smoke induces endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress response and autophagy through the activation of EIF2AK3, ATF4, and ATF6. The autophagic protein, MAP1LC3B has a pivotal role not only in autophagy, but also in apoptosis since it associates with the extrinsic apoptotic factor, FAS. The MAP1LC3B promoter has binding sites for EGR1 and E2F4 transcription factors. Both factors have been shown to bind to the MAP1LC3B promoter in response to cigarette smoke extract in epithelial cells.    Outcome effects: BECN1 interacts with anti-apoptotic BCL2 family members including BCL2 and BCL2L1. The binding of BCL2 family proteins to BECN1 inhibits autophagy by preventing the association of BECN1 with PIK3C3. Autophagy induction correlates with the dissociation of BECN1 from BCL2 or BCL2L1." EXACT []

