synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The airway and alveoli are lined with epithelial cells, which provide a barrier between the host and the environment, and are also an important source of mediator molecules. The alveolar epithelial cells have an important role in the pathogenesis COPD.    Signaling description: Cigarette smoke can activate epithelial cells to secrete a variety of inflammatory mediators and proteases including TNF, TGFB1, IL1B, IL8, MMP1, MMP2, and MMP14. Cigarette smoke has been shown to induce intracellular signaling via the TLR4 and AGER receptors. Cigarette smoke extract exposure leads to EGR1 elevation by activating the MAPK1/3 pathway. The upregulated EGR1 can activate transcription of its target gene GGPS1, which is responsible for the reactivation of the MAPK1/3 pathway via Ras forming a vicious circle. EGR1 is a genetic susceptibility factor that can control the production of MMP2 and MMP14 to overwhelm the anti-protease systems and lead to the destruction of alveolar septal architecture. In addition, the expression of AGER receptor, which binds HMGB1, is elevated in airway epithelium in COPD patients. Extracellular ATP causes the release of IL8 and IL6 via activation of P2YR-subtypes from airway epithelial cells. SIRT1 is downregulated in lung cells from COPD patients as a result of post-translational oxidative modification by cigarette smoke-derived components.    Outcome effects: SIRT1 plays an important role in the inflammatory response, regulation of aging, DNA repair, and telomere maintenance which are implicated in the pathogenesis of COPD. The reduction of SIRT1 results in the deacetylation of DNA repair proteins such as FOXO3, TP53, and histone H3 leading to premature lung aging. Normally, pro-inflammatory cytokine release is reduced by SIRT1 via deacetylating RELA, the p65 subunit of NF-kB. When SIRT1 is downregulated, the production of cytokines is elevated. SIRT1 can deacetylate NFE2L2, a master regulator of the cellular redox state, thereby disrupting the antioxidant response. In addition, the levels of CDKN1A and CDKN2A are significantly increased in COPD lung tissue making an impact on cell aging. Cigarette smoke causes DNA damage and impairs double-strand break (DSB) repair, which is aggravated in patients with COPD and emphysema.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction. The airway and alveoli are lined with epithelial cells, which provide a barrier between the host and the environment, and are also an important source of mediator molecules. The alveolar epithelial cells have an important role in the pathogenesis COPD.    Signaling description: Cigarette smoke can activate epithelial cells to secrete a variety of inflammatory mediators and proteases including TNF, TGFB1, IL1B, IL8, MMP1, MMP2, and MMP14. Cigarette smoke has been shown to induce intracellular signaling via the TLR4 and AGER receptors. Cigarette smoke extract exposure leads to EGR1 elevation by activating the MAPK1/3 pathway. The upregulated EGR1 can activate transcription of its target gene GGPS1, which is responsible for the reactivation of the MAPK1/3 pathway via Ras forming a vicious circle. EGR1 is a genetic susceptibility factor that can control the production of MMP2 and MMP14 to overwhelm the anti-protease systems and lead to the destruction of alveolar septal architecture. In addition, the expression of AGER receptor, which binds HMGB1, is elevated in airway epithelium in COPD patients. Extracellular ATP causes the release of IL8 and IL6 via activation of P2YR-subtypes from airway epithelial cells. SIRT1 is downregulated in lung cells from COPD patients as a result of post-translational oxidative modification by cigarette smoke-derived components.    Outcome effects: SIRT1 plays an important role in the inflammatory response, regulation of aging, DNA repair, and telomere maintenance which are implicated in the pathogenesis of COPD. The reduction of SIRT1 results in the deacetylation of DNA repair proteins such as FOXO3, TP53, and histone H3 leading to premature lung aging. Normally, pro-inflammatory cytokine release is reduced by SIRT1 via deacetylating RELA, the p65 subunit of NF-kB. When SIRT1 is downregulated, the production of cytokines is elevated. SIRT1 can deacetylate NFE2L2, a master regulator of the cellular redox state, thereby disrupting the antioxidant response. In addition, the levels of CDKN1A and CDKN2A are significantly increased in COPD lung tissue making an impact on cell aging. Cigarette smoke causes DNA damage and impairs double-strand break (DSB) repair, which is aggravated in patients with COPD and emphysema.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

