xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction.    Signaling description: Mucus production is a primary defense mechanism for maintaining lung health. However, the overproduction of mucin, the chief glycoprotein component of mucus, is a pathological feature in COPD. The most abundant gel forming mucins in tracheobronchial human epithelium are MUC5AC, produced by surface epithelial mucus secreting cells (mainly goblet cells) and MUC5B, mainly produced by submucosal glands. The activation of epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) by EGF and TGFA plays a pivotal role in the synthesis and secretion of mucins. ELANE and ADAM17 are involved in the cleavage and activation of TGFA.    Outcome effects: EGFR activates MAPK- and SP1-dependent signaling pathways leading to MUC5AC and MUC5B production. IL1B, IL13, TNF, and activation of TLRs by pathogens also have a role in mucin production activation. Excessive mucus production and impaired mucociliary clearance contribute to airway obstruction and respiratory infections in patients with COPD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) refers to a group of progressive lung diseases characterized by a long-term worsening airway obstruction.    Signaling description: Mucus production is a primary defense mechanism for maintaining lung health. However, the overproduction of mucin, the chief glycoprotein component of mucus, is a pathological feature in COPD. The most abundant gel forming mucins in tracheobronchial human epithelium are MUC5AC, produced by surface epithelial mucus secreting cells (mainly goblet cells) and MUC5B, mainly produced by submucosal glands. The activation of epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) by EGF and TGFA plays a pivotal role in the synthesis and secretion of mucins. ELANE and ADAM17 are involved in the cleavage and activation of TGFA.    Outcome effects: EGFR activates MAPK- and SP1-dependent signaling pathways leading to MUC5AC and MUC5B production. IL1B, IL13, TNF, and activation of TLRs by pathogens also have a role in mucin production activation. Excessive mucus production and impaired mucociliary clearance contribute to airway obstruction and respiratory infections in patients with COPD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

