xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. One of the general symptoms CF is the increased synthesis and accumulation of mucoproteins in the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts of CF patients. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: In the lung, mucous glycoproteins or mucins are produced by two different cell types including goblet cells of the epithelium and mucous cells of the submucosal gland. TGFA and IL6 are the key cytokines that induce mucin biosynthesis. The expression of these cytokines is significantly increased in patients with CF due to the activation of NF-kB in lung cells. The activation of NF-kB occurs as the result of oxidative stress mediated by chemotaxis of neutrophils and the response of Toll-like receptors (TLRs) to the propagation of pathogens in lung tissues. Helper T-cells increase the production of IL13 in the airway environment in patients with CF. IL13 is a Th2-type cytokine released by activated T-cells, mast cells, and basophils in patients with CF.    Outcome effects: Cytokine receptor signaling pathways activate the main transcription factors for mucin genes including Jun/Fos, STAT3, STAT6, SP1, and NF-kB. TLRs that activate their signaling after contacting pathogens in the lung also stimulate mucin synthesis. cAMP promotes mucin synthesis as well, but the details of this mechanism are poorly studied. In addition, free radicals that are abundant in the lungs of patients with CF also activate mucin synthesis. Excessive amounts of mucins in the lung prevent phagocytes and dendritic cells from binding and removing pathogens and cellular debris which maintains infection and inflammation.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. One of the general symptoms CF is the increased synthesis and accumulation of mucoproteins in the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts of CF patients. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: In the lung, mucous glycoproteins or mucins are produced by two different cell types including goblet cells of the epithelium and mucous cells of the submucosal gland. TGFA and IL6 are the key cytokines that induce mucin biosynthesis. The expression of these cytokines is significantly increased in patients with CF due to the activation of NF-kB in lung cells. The activation of NF-kB occurs as the result of oxidative stress mediated by chemotaxis of neutrophils and the response of Toll-like receptors (TLRs) to the propagation of pathogens in lung tissues. Helper T-cells increase the production of IL13 in the airway environment in patients with CF. IL13 is a Th2-type cytokine released by activated T-cells, mast cells, and basophils in patients with CF.    Outcome effects: Cytokine receptor signaling pathways activate the main transcription factors for mucin genes including Jun/Fos, STAT3, STAT6, SP1, and NF-kB. TLRs that activate their signaling after contacting pathogens in the lung also stimulate mucin synthesis. cAMP promotes mucin synthesis as well, but the details of this mechanism are poorly studied. In addition, free radicals that are abundant in the lungs of patients with CF also activate mucin synthesis. Excessive amounts of mucins in the lung prevent phagocytes and dendritic cells from binding and removing pathogens and cellular debris which maintains infection and inflammation.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

