xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: CFTR regulates the movement of chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial membranes such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs. It is unclear how the disruptions of ion transport cause lung disease in CF. There are several theories of how the defects in the protein and cellular function cause the clinical effects. In the oxidative stress theory, the mutant CFTR channel fails to transport antioxidants to counteract neutrophil-associated oxidative stress. In CF, the constitutive activation of NF-kB and IL8 expression results in increased chemotaxis of neutrophils and increased amounts of reactive oxygen species (ROS) being generated by neutrophils accumulated in the airway. The activation of macrophages, neutrophils, and eosinophils generates oxygen (O2), which is rapidly converted to H2O2 by superoxide dismutase (SOD1). H2O2 activates NF-kB and mediates other oxidative stress responses. Several cytokines such as IL1B, TNF, and TLRs can activate NF-kB signaling in epithelial cells. AGT, VEGFA, P2RY2, ADORA2B, and ADRB2 are important for the activation of epithelial cells and synthesis of free radicals. There are many other activators that are involved. Cellular signaling pathways include the activation of phospholipase C (PLC), adenylate cyclase (ADCY), and protein kinase C (PKC) in addition to the release of calcium (Ca2+).    Outcome effects: In conditions of oxidative stress, epithelial cells begin to produce antioxidants such as glutathione and thiocyanate. The tripeptide, glutathione is formed by the actions of GCLC, GCLM, and glutathione synthetase (GSS). Glutathione reductase (GSR) uses glutathione as a substrate in the detoxification of peroxides such as H2O2 and lipid peroxides. Mutant CFTR in the epithelial cells is unable to channel glutathione and thiocyanate into the airways and decreases the detoxification. Bacterial killing in the airway is diminished as well since thiocyanate also has antimicrobial properties. Thiosulfate sulfurtransferase (TST) expression is induced via cAMP signaling. In addition, glutathione synthesis is regulated by Jun/Fos and NF-kB in alveolar epithelial cells in response to various environmental oxidants and inflammatory stimuli. Low levels of nitric oxide (NO) in CF leads to the decreased activation of membrane cystine and glutamate transport, leading to the reduction of glutathione synthesis in lung cells. The activity of NFE2L2, a transcription factor that regulates antioxidant and cytoprotective protein expression, is diminished in CF. There are no identified explanations for this, but it may be possible that NFE2L2 dysregulation occurs through binding to cAMP-inducible CREBBP, which is competitive to up-regulating NF-kB- CREBBP binding. One potential mechanism for NFE2L2 deficiency is that a decreased interaction with CREBBP reduces nuclear dwell time and DNA binding. Another possible mechanism that inhibits NFE2L2 is its removal from the nucleus via KEAP1 interaction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: CFTR regulates the movement of chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial membranes such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs. It is unclear how the disruptions of ion transport cause lung disease in CF. There are several theories of how the defects in the protein and cellular function cause the clinical effects. In the oxidative stress theory, the mutant CFTR channel fails to transport antioxidants to counteract neutrophil-associated oxidative stress. In CF, the constitutive activation of NF-kB and IL8 expression results in increased chemotaxis of neutrophils and increased amounts of reactive oxygen species (ROS) being generated by neutrophils accumulated in the airway. The activation of macrophages, neutrophils, and eosinophils generates oxygen (O2), which is rapidly converted to H2O2 by superoxide dismutase (SOD1). H2O2 activates NF-kB and mediates other oxidative stress responses. Several cytokines such as IL1B, TNF, and TLRs can activate NF-kB signaling in epithelial cells. AGT, VEGFA, P2RY2, ADORA2B, and ADRB2 are important for the activation of epithelial cells and synthesis of free radicals. There are many other activators that are involved. Cellular signaling pathways include the activation of phospholipase C (PLC), adenylate cyclase (ADCY), and protein kinase C (PKC) in addition to the release of calcium (Ca2+).    Outcome effects: In conditions of oxidative stress, epithelial cells begin to produce antioxidants such as glutathione and thiocyanate. The tripeptide, glutathione is formed by the actions of GCLC, GCLM, and glutathione synthetase (GSS). Glutathione reductase (GSR) uses glutathione as a substrate in the detoxification of peroxides such as H2O2 and lipid peroxides. Mutant CFTR in the epithelial cells is unable to channel glutathione and thiocyanate into the airways and decreases the detoxification. Bacterial killing in the airway is diminished as well since thiocyanate also has antimicrobial properties. Thiosulfate sulfurtransferase (TST) expression is induced via cAMP signaling. In addition, glutathione synthesis is regulated by Jun/Fos and NF-kB in alveolar epithelial cells in response to various environmental oxidants and inflammatory stimuli. Low levels of nitric oxide (NO) in CF leads to the decreased activation of membrane cystine and glutamate transport, leading to the reduction of glutathione synthesis in lung cells. The activity of NFE2L2, a transcription factor that regulates antioxidant and cytoprotective protein expression, is diminished in CF. There are no identified explanations for this, but it may be possible that NFE2L2 dysregulation occurs through binding to cAMP-inducible CREBBP, which is competitive to up-regulating NF-kB- CREBBP binding. One potential mechanism for NFE2L2 deficiency is that a decreased interaction with CREBBP reduces nuclear dwell time and DNA binding. Another possible mechanism that inhibits NFE2L2 is its removal from the nucleus via KEAP1 interaction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

