xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: CFTR is anchored to the outer membrane of cells in the sweat glands, lungs, pancreas, and all other remaining exocrine glands in the body. CFTR regulates the movement of chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial membranes such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs. There are more than a thousand known mutations in the CFTR gene divided into 5 different classes. Class I CFTR mutations affect its biosynthesis; class II mutations affect protein maturation, and aberrantly folded CFTR protein is degraded in the endoplasmic reticulum by endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-associated degradation (ERAD) and the ubiquitin-proteasome system (UPS); class III mutations affect Cl- channel regulation and gating, and in this case CFTR protein is trafficked to the cell membrane, but then fails to respond to cAMP stimulation. The first three classes usually lead to the classic CF phenotype with pancreatic insufficiency. Further, class IV mutations affect Cl- conductance, and class V mutations interfere with normal transcription and thereby reduce the amount of otherwise normal CFTR. The latter two classes are mostly associated with a milder expression of the disease. The diversity of mutations explains the penetrance of the disease. There are several theories of how defects in the CFTR and cellular function cause the clinical effects. One theory is that the lack of Cl-, iodide, and thiocyanate exiting through the CFTR protein channel leads to the accumulation of a more viscous, nutrient-rich mucus in the lungs that allows bacteria, mostly Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas aeruginosa, to hide from the body's immune system. Another theory is that CFTR protein failure leads to an increase in Na+ and Cl- uptake, which results in increased water reabsorption and creates thick, dehydrated mucus. According to this (Hypothesis), mucus on the epithelium forms plaques with hypoxic niches that can harbor bacteria, particularly Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The prevailing (Hypothesis) for pathophysiology in the CF lung has been linked to the disruption of CFTR-mediated negative regulation of ENaC, leading to Na+ hyperabsorption and dehydration of the airway. The oxidative stress theory states that the mutant CFTR channel does not transport antioxidants to counteract neutrophil-associated oxidative stress.    Outcome effects: The constitutive activation of NF-kB signaling results in increased amounts of reactive oxygen species (ROS) being generated by neutrophils accumulated in the airway. Oxidative stress additionally stimulates the synthesis of IL8 and antioxidants such as glutathione and thiocyanate in epithelial cells, and mutant CFTR in epithelial cells is unable to channel these antioxidants into the airways. Bacterial killing in the airway is diminished as well, since thiocyanate also has antimicrobial properties. Pathogens activate the NF-kB pathway resulting in chronic infection, neutrophilic inflammation, and progressive airway destruction. The elevated level of IL8 is a marker of CF. Even in the absence of pathogens, there is an increased expression of IL8 in the lungs of CF patients allowing inflammation to progress. This is possibly due to the ability of functional CFTR protein to inhibit NF-kB through TNFRSF1A or TLR4 pathways. In CF, the expression of TLR4 is reduced on the surface of epithelial cells, resulting in a decreased activation of MYD88 signaling. Further, epithelial cells produce decreased amounts of type I interferon (IFNB1) in response to Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection most likely from the action of PIAS1. IFNB1 response is central to the clearan"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: CFTR is anchored to the outer membrane of cells in the sweat glands, lungs, pancreas, and all other remaining exocrine glands in the body. CFTR regulates the movement of chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial membranes such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs. There are more than a thousand known mutations in the CFTR gene divided into 5 different classes. Class I CFTR mutations affect its biosynthesis; class II mutations affect protein maturation, and aberrantly folded CFTR protein is degraded in the endoplasmic reticulum by endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-associated degradation (ERAD) and the ubiquitin-proteasome system (UPS); class III mutations affect Cl- channel regulation and gating, and in this case CFTR protein is trafficked to the cell membrane, but then fails to respond to cAMP stimulation. The first three classes usually lead to the classic CF phenotype with pancreatic insufficiency. Further, class IV mutations affect Cl- conductance, and class V mutations interfere with normal transcription and thereby reduce the amount of otherwise normal CFTR. The latter two classes are mostly associated with a milder expression of the disease. The diversity of mutations explains the penetrance of the disease. There are several theories of how defects in the CFTR and cellular function cause the clinical effects. One theory is that the lack of Cl-, iodide, and thiocyanate exiting through the CFTR protein channel leads to the accumulation of a more viscous, nutrient-rich mucus in the lungs that allows bacteria, mostly Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas aeruginosa, to hide from the body's immune system. Another theory is that CFTR protein failure leads to an increase in Na+ and Cl- uptake, which results in increased water reabsorption and creates thick, dehydrated mucus. According to this (Hypothesis), mucus on the epithelium forms plaques with hypoxic niches that can harbor bacteria, particularly Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The prevailing (Hypothesis) for pathophysiology in the CF lung has been linked to the disruption of CFTR-mediated negative regulation of ENaC, leading to Na+ hyperabsorption and dehydration of the airway. The oxidative stress theory states that the mutant CFTR channel does not transport antioxidants to counteract neutrophil-associated oxidative stress.    Outcome effects: The constitutive activation of NF-kB signaling results in increased amounts of reactive oxygen species (ROS) being generated by neutrophils accumulated in the airway. Oxidative stress additionally stimulates the synthesis of IL8 and antioxidants such as glutathione and thiocyanate in epithelial cells, and mutant CFTR in epithelial cells is unable to channel these antioxidants into the airways. Bacterial killing in the airway is diminished as well, since thiocyanate also has antimicrobial properties. Pathogens activate the NF-kB pathway resulting in chronic infection, neutrophilic inflammation, and progressive airway destruction. The elevated level of IL8 is a marker of CF. Even in the absence of pathogens, there is an increased expression of IL8 in the lungs of CF patients allowing inflammation to progress. This is possibly due to the ability of functional CFTR protein to inhibit NF-kB through TNFRSF1A or TLR4 pathways. In CF, the expression of TLR4 is reduced on the surface of epithelial cells, resulting in a decreased activation of MYD88 signaling. Further, epithelial cells produce decreased amounts of type I interferon (IFNB1) in response to Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection most likely from the action of PIAS1. IFNB1 response is central to the clearan" EXACT []

