xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. CF is a disease with a multitude of clinical manifestations including infertility and subfertility in both sexes.    Signaling description: About 97 percent of men with CF are infertile due to congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD) with resultant obstructive azoospermia. CFTR mutations may also result in other forms of male infertility. CFTR mediates the uterine HCO3- secretion and is necessary for HCO3-related sperm capacitation. Therefore, mutant CFTR may lead to impaired sperm fertilizing capacity and thus reduced fertility in women with CF. The detailed mechanisms of CFTR participation in reproduction remain largely unknown. Capacitation is the final step in the maturation of mammalian spermatozoa and is required for the fertilization of oocytes. This step occurs in the female reproductive tract following ejaculation. Capacitation results in membrane fluidity due to lipid modifications, an influx of calcium (Ca2+) in the sperm head and flagellum, and the phosphorylation of proteins on serine (Ser), threonine (Thr), and the oocyte tyrosine (Tyr) residues. Firstly, the influx of HCO3- and Ca2+ stimulates ADCY10 and increases intracellular cAMP concentrations. HCO3- entry is mediated by SLC26A3. CFTR may act as a Cl- channel to provide a recycling pathway for Cl-, which is required for the operation of SLC26A3. High cAMP levels activate protein kinase A (PKA) and AKAP6.    Outcome effects: AKAP6, AKAP3, and CABYR all participate in PKA activation. The phosphorylation of PKA substrates is subjected to regulation by a protein phosphatase, likely PP2A. PKA and PP2A lead to Tyr residue phosphorylation of sperm proteins. In addition, the increase in intracellular Ca2+ may affect the axoneme directly, through phospholipids or selective regulation of dyneins. Ca2+ also up-regulates CAMK4, which results in hyperactivation. Furthermore, G-actin is polymerized to F-actin during sperm capacitation and the fibers must undergo depolymerization in order to achieve the acrosome reaction. Actin polymerization is influenced by PLD1 activation, which occurs via the HCO3-/cAMP/PKA pathway or via the G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR). External albumin (ALB) activates capacitation through the interaction with phospholipids. Additionally, capacitated spermatozoa undergo the acrosome reaction when they come in contact with zona pellucida. The acrosome reaction is normally triggered by ACR. In conclusion, capacitation and acrosome reaction are complex processes that appear to be controlled by several kinases and pathways since they are blocked by inhibitors of PKC, PKA, PTK, PI3K, AKT1, and ERK (MAPK), however, the details of these processes are still incomplete.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. CF is a disease with a multitude of clinical manifestations including infertility and subfertility in both sexes.    Signaling description: About 97 percent of men with CF are infertile due to congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD) with resultant obstructive azoospermia. CFTR mutations may also result in other forms of male infertility. CFTR mediates the uterine HCO3- secretion and is necessary for HCO3-related sperm capacitation. Therefore, mutant CFTR may lead to impaired sperm fertilizing capacity and thus reduced fertility in women with CF. The detailed mechanisms of CFTR participation in reproduction remain largely unknown. Capacitation is the final step in the maturation of mammalian spermatozoa and is required for the fertilization of oocytes. This step occurs in the female reproductive tract following ejaculation. Capacitation results in membrane fluidity due to lipid modifications, an influx of calcium (Ca2+) in the sperm head and flagellum, and the phosphorylation of proteins on serine (Ser), threonine (Thr), and the oocyte tyrosine (Tyr) residues. Firstly, the influx of HCO3- and Ca2+ stimulates ADCY10 and increases intracellular cAMP concentrations. HCO3- entry is mediated by SLC26A3. CFTR may act as a Cl- channel to provide a recycling pathway for Cl-, which is required for the operation of SLC26A3. High cAMP levels activate protein kinase A (PKA) and AKAP6.    Outcome effects: AKAP6, AKAP3, and CABYR all participate in PKA activation. The phosphorylation of PKA substrates is subjected to regulation by a protein phosphatase, likely PP2A. PKA and PP2A lead to Tyr residue phosphorylation of sperm proteins. In addition, the increase in intracellular Ca2+ may affect the axoneme directly, through phospholipids or selective regulation of dyneins. Ca2+ also up-regulates CAMK4, which results in hyperactivation. Furthermore, G-actin is polymerized to F-actin during sperm capacitation and the fibers must undergo depolymerization in order to achieve the acrosome reaction. Actin polymerization is influenced by PLD1 activation, which occurs via the HCO3-/cAMP/PKA pathway or via the G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR). External albumin (ALB) activates capacitation through the interaction with phospholipids. Additionally, capacitated spermatozoa undergo the acrosome reaction when they come in contact with zona pellucida. The acrosome reaction is normally triggered by ACR. In conclusion, capacitation and acrosome reaction are complex processes that appear to be controlled by several kinases and pathways since they are blocked by inhibitors of PKC, PKA, PTK, PI3K, AKT1, and ERK (MAPK), however, the details of these processes are still incomplete.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

