xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: There are more than a thousand mutations in the CFTR gene and the diversity of mutations explains the penetrance of the disease. The mutations are divided into 5 classes. Class I CFTR mutations affect its biosynthesis. This type of mutations includes the most severe CF phenotypes since no protein is being synthesized. The most common mutations are G542X, which prevent the synthesis of a stable CFTR protein or result in the production of a truncated protein due to the creation of a premature termination codon. The truncated proteins are usually unstable, rapidly degraded, and recognized by chaperone proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). IL1B receptors and beta-2-adrenergic receptor (ADRB2) stimulate the expression of CFTR via NF-kB and CREB1. The exact signaling leading to CFTR expression is poorly understood. This pathway illustrates the classic signaling route activated by IL1R1 and ADRB2.    Outcome effects: P2RY2 activates Ca2+-dependent signaling that leads to membrane hyperpolarization, CFTR secretion, and possibly CFTR expression activation.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: There are more than a thousand mutations in the CFTR gene and the diversity of mutations explains the penetrance of the disease. The mutations are divided into 5 classes. Class I CFTR mutations affect its biosynthesis. This type of mutations includes the most severe CF phenotypes since no protein is being synthesized. The most common mutations are G542X, which prevent the synthesis of a stable CFTR protein or result in the production of a truncated protein due to the creation of a premature termination codon. The truncated proteins are usually unstable, rapidly degraded, and recognized by chaperone proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). IL1B receptors and beta-2-adrenergic receptor (ADRB2) stimulate the expression of CFTR via NF-kB and CREB1. The exact signaling leading to CFTR expression is poorly understood. This pathway illustrates the classic signaling route activated by IL1R1 and ADRB2.    Outcome effects: P2RY2 activates Ca2+-dependent signaling that leads to membrane hyperpolarization, CFTR secretion, and possibly CFTR expression activation.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

