xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: The most common pathological mutation in the CFTR gene leads to a deletion of phenylalanine at position 508 (DeltaF508). This mutation results in a folding defect, retention of the protein in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), and its subsequent degradation by the proteasome. CF patients exhibit a typical phenotype characterized by thick mucus secretions obstructing the airways and persistent pulmonary infections leading to pulmonary failure and death. CFTR is synthesized in the ER and then begins the process of maturation by folding and N-glycosylation, which is monitored by chaperone proteins. Correctly folded CFTR is transported to the Golgi for maturation before trafficking to the apical cell surface. CANX, COPII, GOPC, and RHOQ regulate the maturation and trafficking of normal CFTR from the ER to the Golgi. Mutant CFTR is degraded via proteasome endopeptidase complex or is transported to aggresomes near the microtubule-organizing center. Most mutant DeltaF508-CFTR are recognized as misfolded by ER-associated degradation (ERAD), shuttled out of the ER, and targeted for proteosomal degradation.    Outcome effects: Two ubiquitin ligase complexes are needed for mutated CFTR degradation including ER membrane-associated ubiquitin ligase complex (DERL1, RNF5, and UBE2J2) and cytosolic ubiquitin ligase complex (STUB1 and UBE2D1). Since improperly folded CFTR undergoes ubiquitination-mediated degradation, it is hypothesized that Hsc70 (HSPA8) promotes ERAD of DeltaF508-CFTR, which involves a co-chaperone known as STUB1. HSPBP1 binds HSPA8 and this binding decreases the ubiquitin ligase activity of STUB1. Since DeltaF508-CFTR is a misfolded protein, it is believed that DeltaF508-CFTR-expressing cells activate the nuclear transcription mechanism of genes called unfolded protein response (UPR). It has been discovered that protein levels of HSPA5 and activated ATF6 are increased in DeltaF508-CFTR-containing cells. The UPR is comprised of the regulator protein, HSPA5 and a number of signal transducers including ATF6 and EIF2AK3, which lead to ERAD, apoptosis, and decreased biosynthesis. ERP29 is ubiquitously expressed and seems to play a role in CFTR trafficking in the lung.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: The most common pathological mutation in the CFTR gene leads to a deletion of phenylalanine at position 508 (DeltaF508). This mutation results in a folding defect, retention of the protein in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), and its subsequent degradation by the proteasome. CF patients exhibit a typical phenotype characterized by thick mucus secretions obstructing the airways and persistent pulmonary infections leading to pulmonary failure and death. CFTR is synthesized in the ER and then begins the process of maturation by folding and N-glycosylation, which is monitored by chaperone proteins. Correctly folded CFTR is transported to the Golgi for maturation before trafficking to the apical cell surface. CANX, COPII, GOPC, and RHOQ regulate the maturation and trafficking of normal CFTR from the ER to the Golgi. Mutant CFTR is degraded via proteasome endopeptidase complex or is transported to aggresomes near the microtubule-organizing center. Most mutant DeltaF508-CFTR are recognized as misfolded by ER-associated degradation (ERAD), shuttled out of the ER, and targeted for proteosomal degradation.    Outcome effects: Two ubiquitin ligase complexes are needed for mutated CFTR degradation including ER membrane-associated ubiquitin ligase complex (DERL1, RNF5, and UBE2J2) and cytosolic ubiquitin ligase complex (STUB1 and UBE2D1). Since improperly folded CFTR undergoes ubiquitination-mediated degradation, it is hypothesized that Hsc70 (HSPA8) promotes ERAD of DeltaF508-CFTR, which involves a co-chaperone known as STUB1. HSPBP1 binds HSPA8 and this binding decreases the ubiquitin ligase activity of STUB1. Since DeltaF508-CFTR is a misfolded protein, it is believed that DeltaF508-CFTR-expressing cells activate the nuclear transcription mechanism of genes called unfolded protein response (UPR). It has been discovered that protein levels of HSPA5 and activated ATF6 are increased in DeltaF508-CFTR-containing cells. The UPR is comprised of the regulator protein, HSPA5 and a number of signal transducers including ATF6 and EIF2AK3, which lead to ERAD, apoptosis, and decreased biosynthesis. ERP29 is ubiquitously expressed and seems to play a role in CFTR trafficking in the lung.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

