xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: CFTR regulates the movement of chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial membranes such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs. There are more than a thousand known mutations in the CFTR gene divided into 5 classes. Class III mutations affect Cl- channel regulation and gating, and in this case CFTR protein is trafficked to the cell membrane, but then fails to respond to cAMP stimulation. Classes I, II, and III usually lead to the classic CF phenotype with pancreatic insufficiency. Class IV mutations affect Cl- conductance, while class V mutations interfere with normal transcription and thereby reduce the amount of otherwise normal CFTR. The latter two classes are mostly associated with a milder expression of the disease. The diversity of mutations explains the penetrance of the disease. It is unclear how the disruption of ion transport causes lung disease in CF. There are several theories of how defects in the protein and cellular function cause the clinical effects. One theory is that the lack of Cl-, iodide, and thiocyanate exiting through the CFTR protein channel leads to the accumulation of a more viscous, nutrient-rich mucus in the lungs that allows bacteria, usually Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas aeruginosa, to hide from the body's immune system. Another theory is that CFTR protein failure leads to an increase in Na+ and Cl- uptake, which results in increased water reabsorption and creates thick, dehydrated mucus. The subsequent decrease in periciliary water volume causes a reduction in the lubricating layer between epithelium and mucus with compression of cilia by mucus resulting in the inhibition of normal ciliary and cough clearance of the mucus. According to this (Hypothesis), mucus on the epithelium forms plaques with hypoxic niches that can harbor bacteria, particularly Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The prevailing (Hypothesis) for pathophysiology in the CF lung has been the disruption of CFTR-mediated negative regulation of ENaC, leading to Na+ hyperabsorption and dehydration of the airway. The adequate hydration of airway surface liquid (ASL) is determined by the balance between Na+ absorption of ENaC and Cl- secretion mediated by CFTR.    Outcome effects: In the absence of CFTR function, unrestrained Na+ absorption and the deficiency of active Cl- secretion lead to decreased ASL volume and subsequent failure of normal mucociliary clearance. Chronic lung diseases burdened by considerable tissue damage recruit additional receptors (P2RY2) responding to higher ATP concentrations. In addition, the lung expresses several AQPs including AQP1 in microvascular endothelia, AQP3 in large airways, AQP4 in large- and small-airway epithelia, and AQP5 in type I alveolar epithelial cells. Furthermore, pathogens activate NF-kB signaling pathway causing chronic infection, neutrophilic inflammation, and progressive airway destruction. The elevated level of IL8 is a marker of CF. The decreased level of nitric oxide (NO) also serves as a marker of inflammation during CF. Other types of inflammation in the lung are usually accompanied by high NO levels. The reason for decreased NO levels in patients with CF remains unknown. One possible explanation may be the increased activity of ARG1, which competes with NOS3 for the substrate during NO synthesis. NO stimulates CFTR via NO-sensitive guanylate cyclase.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disease caused by mutations in the CFTR gene. CFTR is a cyclic AMP (cAMP)-regulated, ATP-gated chloride (Cl-) channel located in the apical membrane of epithelial cells lining the airways, intestines, ducts of the pancreas, and sweat glands. Lung disease is the major cause of mortality of CF.    Signaling description: CFTR regulates the movement of chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial membranes such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs. There are more than a thousand known mutations in the CFTR gene divided into 5 classes. Class III mutations affect Cl- channel regulation and gating, and in this case CFTR protein is trafficked to the cell membrane, but then fails to respond to cAMP stimulation. Classes I, II, and III usually lead to the classic CF phenotype with pancreatic insufficiency. Class IV mutations affect Cl- conductance, while class V mutations interfere with normal transcription and thereby reduce the amount of otherwise normal CFTR. The latter two classes are mostly associated with a milder expression of the disease. The diversity of mutations explains the penetrance of the disease. It is unclear how the disruption of ion transport causes lung disease in CF. There are several theories of how defects in the protein and cellular function cause the clinical effects. One theory is that the lack of Cl-, iodide, and thiocyanate exiting through the CFTR protein channel leads to the accumulation of a more viscous, nutrient-rich mucus in the lungs that allows bacteria, usually Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas aeruginosa, to hide from the body's immune system. Another theory is that CFTR protein failure leads to an increase in Na+ and Cl- uptake, which results in increased water reabsorption and creates thick, dehydrated mucus. The subsequent decrease in periciliary water volume causes a reduction in the lubricating layer between epithelium and mucus with compression of cilia by mucus resulting in the inhibition of normal ciliary and cough clearance of the mucus. According to this (Hypothesis), mucus on the epithelium forms plaques with hypoxic niches that can harbor bacteria, particularly Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The prevailing (Hypothesis) for pathophysiology in the CF lung has been the disruption of CFTR-mediated negative regulation of ENaC, leading to Na+ hyperabsorption and dehydration of the airway. The adequate hydration of airway surface liquid (ASL) is determined by the balance between Na+ absorption of ENaC and Cl- secretion mediated by CFTR.    Outcome effects: In the absence of CFTR function, unrestrained Na+ absorption and the deficiency of active Cl- secretion lead to decreased ASL volume and subsequent failure of normal mucociliary clearance. Chronic lung diseases burdened by considerable tissue damage recruit additional receptors (P2RY2) responding to higher ATP concentrations. In addition, the lung expresses several AQPs including AQP1 in microvascular endothelia, AQP3 in large airways, AQP4 in large- and small-airway epithelia, and AQP5 in type I alveolar epithelial cells. Furthermore, pathogens activate NF-kB signaling pathway causing chronic infection, neutrophilic inflammation, and progressive airway destruction. The elevated level of IL8 is a marker of CF. The decreased level of nitric oxide (NO) also serves as a marker of inflammation during CF. Other types of inflammation in the lung are usually accompanied by high NO levels. The reason for decreased NO levels in patients with CF remains unknown. One possible explanation may be the increased activity of ARG1, which competes with NOS3 for the substrate during NO synthesis. NO stimulates CFTR via NO-sensitive guanylate cyclase.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

