xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery. PAH is sub-classified into familial (FPAH), idiopathic (IPAH), or acquired (APAH).    Signaling description: Mutations in the bone morphogenetic protein receptor II (BMPR2) gene have been identified in over 75% of patients with FPAH and in 10-40% of patients with IPAH. Less common mutations that affect the activin-like kinase-type 1 (ACVRL1) and endoglin (ENG) are associated with both FPAH and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia. There also can be a mutation in SMAD9, although it rarely occurs. In normal pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs), BMP and TGFB1 signaling have an anti-proliferative effect. BMPR1A, BMPR1B, and BMPR2 form a complex and phosphorylate SMAD1, 5, and 9. When activated, these proteins interact with a nuclear chaperone, SMAD4 and are translocated to the nucleus. TGFB1 signaling acts through SMAD 2 and 3 and also enrolls SMAD4 as a co-activator.    Outcome effects: Normally, these events lead to the activation of CDKN2B and ID3 as well as the repression of MYC resulting in the inhibition of proliferation. In addition, BMP2 usually inhibits PASMC proliferation via the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPARG) and apolipoprotein E (APOE). APOE impairs PDGFB signaling by binding to low density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 1 (LRP1), thereby initiating endocytosis and degradation of the LRP1/PDGFRB/PDGFB complex. In addition to APOE, other transcriptional targets of PPARG, such as adiponectin (ADIPOQ), can sequester PDGFB and repress PASMC proliferation. In FPAH and IPAH, these proliferation-inhibiting processes are impaired due to the previously mentioned mutations and PASMC proliferation is activated leading to the thickening of arterial walls, narrowing of the vessel lumen, and PAH development. Lastly, serotonin may antagonize the anti-proliferative BMPR2/SMAD1/5/9 pathway, inhibit ID3 activation, and facilitate proliferation in non-BMPR2-mutated cases. Therefore, there may be evidence linking serotonin to the development of PAH independently of mutations in BMPR2 gene.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery. PAH is sub-classified into familial (FPAH), idiopathic (IPAH), or acquired (APAH).    Signaling description: Mutations in the bone morphogenetic protein receptor II (BMPR2) gene have been identified in over 75% of patients with FPAH and in 10-40% of patients with IPAH. Less common mutations that affect the activin-like kinase-type 1 (ACVRL1) and endoglin (ENG) are associated with both FPAH and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia. There also can be a mutation in SMAD9, although it rarely occurs. In normal pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs), BMP and TGFB1 signaling have an anti-proliferative effect. BMPR1A, BMPR1B, and BMPR2 form a complex and phosphorylate SMAD1, 5, and 9. When activated, these proteins interact with a nuclear chaperone, SMAD4 and are translocated to the nucleus. TGFB1 signaling acts through SMAD 2 and 3 and also enrolls SMAD4 as a co-activator.    Outcome effects: Normally, these events lead to the activation of CDKN2B and ID3 as well as the repression of MYC resulting in the inhibition of proliferation. In addition, BMP2 usually inhibits PASMC proliferation via the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPARG) and apolipoprotein E (APOE). APOE impairs PDGFB signaling by binding to low density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 1 (LRP1), thereby initiating endocytosis and degradation of the LRP1/PDGFRB/PDGFB complex. In addition to APOE, other transcriptional targets of PPARG, such as adiponectin (ADIPOQ), can sequester PDGFB and repress PASMC proliferation. In FPAH and IPAH, these proliferation-inhibiting processes are impaired due to the previously mentioned mutations and PASMC proliferation is activated leading to the thickening of arterial walls, narrowing of the vessel lumen, and PAH development. Lastly, serotonin may antagonize the anti-proliferative BMPR2/SMAD1/5/9 pathway, inhibit ID3 activation, and facilitate proliferation in non-BMPR2-mutated cases. Therefore, there may be evidence linking serotonin to the development of PAH independently of mutations in BMPR2 gene.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

