xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction in PAH is characterized by the decreased production of vasodilators and growth inhibitors such as nitric oxide (NO) and prostacyclin (PGI2) and the increased production of the vasoconstrictor/co-mitogens such as endothelin 1 (EDN1) and thromboxane A2 (TXA2) in endothelial cells (ECs). These factors act on ECs and pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs) in an autocrine and paracrine manner, leading to vasoconstriction and cell proliferation. In normal conditions, endothelial-specific nitric oxide synthase (NOS3) produces nitric oxide (NO) from arginine and oxygen (O2). Soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) is activated in the presence of NO and produces cGMP. In the vascular endothelium, cGMP activates PKG, a cGMP kinase. Consequently, this process leads to a reduction of intracellular calcium (Ca2+) levels via the Ca2+-transporting ATPase, and finally vasodilation. Phosphodiesterase 5A (PDE5A), which is abundant in PAH, is an enzyme that hydrolyzes cGMP. The reduced NO bioavailability in PAH may be due to the decreased expression of NOS3, inhibition of NOS3 enzymatic activity, or the inactivation of NO by superoxide radical. The activity of arginase II (ARG2), a STAT1/STAT3-regulated gene, whose product reduces NO synthesis by competing with NOS3 for arginine, is increased in PAH ECs. Patients with PAH have increased plasma levels of the endogenous inhibitor of NOS3, N,N-dimethylarginine and decreased levels of the enzymes which are responsible for the degradation of dimethylarginine, such as dimethylarginine dimethylaminohydrolases (DDAH1/2). The decreased expression of PGI2 synthase (PTGIS) in PAH ECs has also been reported in patients with PAH. In addition, the local hyperproduction of reactive oxygen spices (ROS) by NADPH oxidases such as CYBB and NOX4, as well as xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH), also have a role in vasoconstriction. Angiopoietin 1 (ANGPT1) is overexpressed in most forms of non-familial PAH.    Outcome effects: ANGPT1 exerts its effects through the endothelial-specific tyrosine kinase receptor (TEK) and stimulates ECs to produce and secrete serotonin and EDN1. The exact mechanism is not clear yet, however it has been shown that ANGPT1 increases the level of tryptophan hydroxylase 1 (TPH1), the rate-limiting enzyme of serotonin synthesis, and endothelin converting enzyme 1 (ECE1). In addition, BMP2 recruits both canonical and non-canonical WNT signaling pathways to promote EC proliferation, survival, and motility. In the case of canonical WNT-activation, BMP2 via BMPR2 activates MAPK1/3 in a SMAD-independent manner. MAPK1/3-mediated inhibition of GSK3B induces beta-catenin (CTNNB1) accumulation and transcriptional activity necessary for EC survival and proliferation in addition to the inhibition of apoptosis. In the case of non-canonical WNT-activation, BMP2 is responsible for SMAD1 phosphorylation, or with the loss of BMPR2 (as in some cases of familial and idiopathic PAH), ACVR2A/SMAD3-dependent recruitment of DVL1 to promote the RHOA/RAC1 signaling. Furthermore, the activation of G-protein-coupled receptors by vasoconstrictors leads to the activation of RHOA, which in turn activates ROCK1. RHOA/ROCK1, acting through LIMK1/2 and CFL1, increases the formation of F-actin stress fibers, cell contraction, and blood vessel permeability. RHOA activates NF-kB and CCND1 and thereby inhibits apoptosis and activates the proliferation of ECs.In addition, RHOA induces the production of SERPINE1, thromboplastin (F3), and selectin P (SELP) and also inhibits thrombomodulin (THBD) formation thereby facilitating thrombosis formation. In conclusion, the final common outcome in PAH is the elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance mediated by vasoconstriction and obstructive cellular proliferation of PASMCs and ECs.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or act"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction in PAH is characterized by the decreased production of vasodilators and growth inhibitors such as nitric oxide (NO) and prostacyclin (PGI2) and the increased production of the vasoconstrictor/co-mitogens such as endothelin 1 (EDN1) and thromboxane A2 (TXA2) in endothelial cells (ECs). These factors act on ECs and pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs) in an autocrine and paracrine manner, leading to vasoconstriction and cell proliferation. In normal conditions, endothelial-specific nitric oxide synthase (NOS3) produces nitric oxide (NO) from arginine and oxygen (O2). Soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) is activated in the presence of NO and produces cGMP. In the vascular endothelium, cGMP activates PKG, a cGMP kinase. Consequently, this process leads to a reduction of intracellular calcium (Ca2+) levels via the Ca2+-transporting ATPase, and finally vasodilation. Phosphodiesterase 5A (PDE5A), which is abundant in PAH, is an enzyme that hydrolyzes cGMP. The reduced NO bioavailability in PAH may be due to the decreased expression of NOS3, inhibition of NOS3 enzymatic activity, or the inactivation of NO by superoxide radical. The activity of arginase II (ARG2), a STAT1/STAT3-regulated gene, whose product reduces NO synthesis by competing with NOS3 for arginine, is increased in PAH ECs. Patients with PAH have increased plasma levels of the endogenous inhibitor of NOS3, N,N-dimethylarginine and decreased levels of the enzymes which are responsible for the degradation of dimethylarginine, such as dimethylarginine dimethylaminohydrolases (DDAH1/2). The decreased expression of PGI2 synthase (PTGIS) in PAH ECs has also been reported in patients with PAH. In addition, the local hyperproduction of reactive oxygen spices (ROS) by NADPH oxidases such as CYBB and NOX4, as well as xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH), also have a role in vasoconstriction. Angiopoietin 1 (ANGPT1) is overexpressed in most forms of non-familial PAH.    Outcome effects: ANGPT1 exerts its effects through the endothelial-specific tyrosine kinase receptor (TEK) and stimulates ECs to produce and secrete serotonin and EDN1. The exact mechanism is not clear yet, however it has been shown that ANGPT1 increases the level of tryptophan hydroxylase 1 (TPH1), the rate-limiting enzyme of serotonin synthesis, and endothelin converting enzyme 1 (ECE1). In addition, BMP2 recruits both canonical and non-canonical WNT signaling pathways to promote EC proliferation, survival, and motility. In the case of canonical WNT-activation, BMP2 via BMPR2 activates MAPK1/3 in a SMAD-independent manner. MAPK1/3-mediated inhibition of GSK3B induces beta-catenin (CTNNB1) accumulation and transcriptional activity necessary for EC survival and proliferation in addition to the inhibition of apoptosis. In the case of non-canonical WNT-activation, BMP2 is responsible for SMAD1 phosphorylation, or with the loss of BMPR2 (as in some cases of familial and idiopathic PAH), ACVR2A/SMAD3-dependent recruitment of DVL1 to promote the RHOA/RAC1 signaling. Furthermore, the activation of G-protein-coupled receptors by vasoconstrictors leads to the activation of RHOA, which in turn activates ROCK1. RHOA/ROCK1, acting through LIMK1/2 and CFL1, increases the formation of F-actin stress fibers, cell contraction, and blood vessel permeability. RHOA activates NF-kB and CCND1 and thereby inhibits apoptosis and activates the proliferation of ECs.In addition, RHOA induces the production of SERPINE1, thromboplastin (F3), and selectin P (SELP) and also inhibits thrombomodulin (THBD) formation thereby facilitating thrombosis formation. In conclusion, the final common outcome in PAH is the elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance mediated by vasoconstriction and obstructive cellular proliferation of PASMCs and ECs.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or act" EXACT []

