xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery.    Signaling description: Chronic hereditary or acquired hemolytic anemias such as sickle cell disease, thalassemia intermedia, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, and other hemoglobinopathies or red blood cell membranopathies are accompanied by PAH development. A central mechanism underlying this disease is intravascular hemolysis, which releases free hemoglobin into plasma.    Outcome effects: Free hemoglobin reacts with and catabolizes nitric oxide (NO) at a very high rate: more than 1000 times faster than hemoglobin within the red blood cell. This reaction oxidizes NO to nitrate and inhibits its ability to vasodilate. In addition to the release of hemoglobin, hemolysis releases red blood cell arginase 1 (ARG1), an enzyme that metabolizes arginine to L-ornithine, reducing its availability for de novo NO synthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery.    Signaling description: Chronic hereditary or acquired hemolytic anemias such as sickle cell disease, thalassemia intermedia, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, and other hemoglobinopathies or red blood cell membranopathies are accompanied by PAH development. A central mechanism underlying this disease is intravascular hemolysis, which releases free hemoglobin into plasma.    Outcome effects: Free hemoglobin reacts with and catabolizes nitric oxide (NO) at a very high rate: more than 1000 times faster than hemoglobin within the red blood cell. This reaction oxidizes NO to nitrate and inhibits its ability to vasodilate. In addition to the release of hemoglobin, hemolysis releases red blood cell arginase 1 (ARG1), an enzyme that metabolizes arginine to L-ornithine, reducing its availability for de novo NO synthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

