xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery. The contraction and proliferation of pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs) are key processes in PAH development. PAH is characterized by the decreased production of vasodilators and growth inhibitors such as nitric oxide (NO) and prostacyclin (PGI2) and the increased production of the vasoconstrictor/co-mitogens such as endothelin 1 (EDN1) and serotonin.    Signaling description: The main mechanisms of contraction and proliferation induction in PASMCs are RHOA/ROCK1 activation, reactive oxygen spices (ROS) hyperproduction, intracellular calcium (Ca2+) overload, and the decreased expression/function of potassium (K+) channels. In PASMCs, vasoconstrictors activate G-protein-coupled receptors which trigger guanine nucleotide exchange factor (GEF) and induce RHOA. Then, RHOA activates ROCK1 which has multiple effects with the most widely studied being the inhibition of myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP). This promotes the phosphorylation of MYL12B and as a result contraction occurs. ROCK1 is also involved in actin cytoskeleton dynamics through the phosphorylation of LIMK1/2, which in turn phosphorylates and inhibits CFL1, leading to smooth muscle cell actin (ACTA2) filament stabilization. This may have an impact on cell differentiation, proliferation, and migration. The expression of TAGLN and CNN1 are regulated by SRF and cofactors such as MYOCD and myocardin-related transcription factors (MKL1/2) which also influence the contractile phenotype in PASMCs. Conversely, the activation of RHOA/ROCK1 pathway increases PASMC proliferation. RHOA has been shown to be required for sustained Ras/MAPK1/3 activation. ROCK1 also causes decreased CDKN1A and CDKN1B expression. Serotonin has both contractile and mitogenic effects in PASMCs. The contractile action of serotonin is mediated via the activation of HTR1B, HTR2A, and HTR2B receptors. The mitogenic effect of serotonin requires internalization through the serotonin transporter, SLC6A4. The breakdown of serotonin by monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) results in the generation of ROS. Serotonin also induces ROS through the activation of NADPH oxidases (CYBB and NOX4) via RAC1. In addition, ROS is responsible for the nuclear translocation of phosphorylated MAPK1/3. Subsequently, phosphorylated MAPK1/3 binds transcription factors such as GATA4, EGR1, and ELK1 and thus increases the expression of proteins which are involved in cellular proliferation. In PASMCs, free Ca2+ concentration in the cytosol is an important determinant of contraction, migration, and proliferation. The cytosolic Ca2+ concentration can be increased by Ca2+ influx through voltage-dependent Ca2+ channels (CACNA1C) and transient receptor potential cation channels (TRPC1/3/6) in addition to Ca2+ release from sarcoplasmic reticulum through ITPR2 and RYR3. Ca2+/calmodulin complex activates MYLK leading to MYL12B phosphorylation and PASMC contraction. Also, the decreased expression and function of potassium (K+) channels (KCNA5 and KCNB1) leads to membrane depolarization and contributes to the sustained elevation of cytosol Ca2+. In addition, PAH is characterized by overexpression of NOTCH3 in PASMCs. It has been demonstrated that there is a link between NOTCH3 receptor signaling through HES5, cell proliferation, and a shift to an undifferentiated PASMC phenotype. Furthermore, TNC, EGF, PDGFA/PDGFB, and IGF1 are all believed to be significant in PAH development.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, the final common outcome in PAH is the elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance mediated by vasoconstriction and obstructive cellular proliferation of PASMCs and endothelial cells.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is increased blood pressure in the pulmonary artery. The contraction and proliferation of pulmonary artery smooth muscle cells (PASMCs) are key processes in PAH development. PAH is characterized by the decreased production of vasodilators and growth inhibitors such as nitric oxide (NO) and prostacyclin (PGI2) and the increased production of the vasoconstrictor/co-mitogens such as endothelin 1 (EDN1) and serotonin.    Signaling description: The main mechanisms of contraction and proliferation induction in PASMCs are RHOA/ROCK1 activation, reactive oxygen spices (ROS) hyperproduction, intracellular calcium (Ca2+) overload, and the decreased expression/function of potassium (K+) channels. In PASMCs, vasoconstrictors activate G-protein-coupled receptors which trigger guanine nucleotide exchange factor (GEF) and induce RHOA. Then, RHOA activates ROCK1 which has multiple effects with the most widely studied being the inhibition of myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP). This promotes the phosphorylation of MYL12B and as a result contraction occurs. ROCK1 is also involved in actin cytoskeleton dynamics through the phosphorylation of LIMK1/2, which in turn phosphorylates and inhibits CFL1, leading to smooth muscle cell actin (ACTA2) filament stabilization. This may have an impact on cell differentiation, proliferation, and migration. The expression of TAGLN and CNN1 are regulated by SRF and cofactors such as MYOCD and myocardin-related transcription factors (MKL1/2) which also influence the contractile phenotype in PASMCs. Conversely, the activation of RHOA/ROCK1 pathway increases PASMC proliferation. RHOA has been shown to be required for sustained Ras/MAPK1/3 activation. ROCK1 also causes decreased CDKN1A and CDKN1B expression. Serotonin has both contractile and mitogenic effects in PASMCs. The contractile action of serotonin is mediated via the activation of HTR1B, HTR2A, and HTR2B receptors. The mitogenic effect of serotonin requires internalization through the serotonin transporter, SLC6A4. The breakdown of serotonin by monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) results in the generation of ROS. Serotonin also induces ROS through the activation of NADPH oxidases (CYBB and NOX4) via RAC1. In addition, ROS is responsible for the nuclear translocation of phosphorylated MAPK1/3. Subsequently, phosphorylated MAPK1/3 binds transcription factors such as GATA4, EGR1, and ELK1 and thus increases the expression of proteins which are involved in cellular proliferation. In PASMCs, free Ca2+ concentration in the cytosol is an important determinant of contraction, migration, and proliferation. The cytosolic Ca2+ concentration can be increased by Ca2+ influx through voltage-dependent Ca2+ channels (CACNA1C) and transient receptor potential cation channels (TRPC1/3/6) in addition to Ca2+ release from sarcoplasmic reticulum through ITPR2 and RYR3. Ca2+/calmodulin complex activates MYLK leading to MYL12B phosphorylation and PASMC contraction. Also, the decreased expression and function of potassium (K+) channels (KCNA5 and KCNB1) leads to membrane depolarization and contributes to the sustained elevation of cytosol Ca2+. In addition, PAH is characterized by overexpression of NOTCH3 in PASMCs. It has been demonstrated that there is a link between NOTCH3 receptor signaling through HES5, cell proliferation, and a shift to an undifferentiated PASMC phenotype. Furthermore, TNC, EGF, PDGFA/PDGFB, and IGF1 are all believed to be significant in PAH development.    Outcome effects: In conclusion, the final common outcome in PAH is the elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance mediated by vasoconstriction and obstructive cellular proliferation of PASMCs and endothelial cells.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

