xref: PS:Organ "gubernaculum"
xref: PS:Organ "testis"
synonym: "Organ: gubernaculum" EXACT []
synonym: "Organ: testis" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "reproductive system"
synonym: "Organ_System: reproductive system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cryptorchidism is one of the most frequent congenital abnormalities, with a recorded frequency of 3-4% among newborn males. Before sex determination, both female and male embryonic gonads are located in the same high intra-abdominal position. The developing testes migrate through a multiphase process of testicular descent (TD), first into a low abdominal position and then into the developing scrotum. A critical role in TD belongs to the gubernacular ligaments. Analysis of mouse mutants revealed a number of genes involved in this process.     Signaling description: Insulin-like factor 3 (INSL3) controls the first abdominal phase of TD through its receptor, leucine-rich-containing repeats G protein-coupled receptor LGR8 (RXFP2). INSL3 signaling is essential for myogenic differentiation in the gubernaculum and involved genes of the WNT and NOTCH pathways. Mutation of INSL3 and RXFP2 result in high intraabdominal cryptorchidism and male sterility. The mutant by INSL3 and RXFP2 genes gubernaculum fails to differentiate. Several transcription factors, such as HOXA10, HOXA11, and ARID5B also were shown to cause the cryptorchidism in mice, suggesting an involvement of multiple signaling pathways in in the control of TD."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cryptorchidism is one of the most frequent congenital abnormalities, with a recorded frequency of 3-4% among newborn males. Before sex determination, both female and male embryonic gonads are located in the same high intra-abdominal position. The developing testes migrate through a multiphase process of testicular descent (TD), first into a low abdominal position and then into the developing scrotum. A critical role in TD belongs to the gubernacular ligaments. Analysis of mouse mutants revealed a number of genes involved in this process.     Signaling description: Insulin-like factor 3 (INSL3) controls the first abdominal phase of TD through its receptor, leucine-rich-containing repeats G protein-coupled receptor LGR8 (RXFP2). INSL3 signaling is essential for myogenic differentiation in the gubernaculum and involved genes of the WNT and NOTCH pathways. Mutation of INSL3 and RXFP2 result in high intraabdominal cryptorchidism and male sterility. The mutant by INSL3 and RXFP2 genes gubernaculum fails to differentiate. Several transcription factors, such as HOXA10, HOXA11, and ARID5B also were shown to cause the cryptorchidism in mice, suggesting an involvement of multiple signaling pathways in in the control of TD." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-ade19d9e-f577-482b-82c2-8f79aa07ef0e
name: Testicular Dysgenesis Causes Male Infertility
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:testicular_male_infertility ! Testicular male infertility
is_a: urn:agi-folder:reproductive_system ! Reproductive system
xref: PS:Description "The early stages of testicular formation in the second month of gestation requires the action of several genes, of which one of the earliest and most important is SRY. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: The early stages of testicular formation in the second month of gestation requires the action of several genes, of which one of the earliest and most important is SRY. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
