synonym: "Organ: paramesonephric duct" EXACT []
synonym: "Organ: testis" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "reproductive system"
synonym: "Organ_System: reproductive system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The first known step of sexual differentiation of a normal XY fetus is the development of testes. The early stages of testicular formation during the second month of gestation requires the action of several genes, of which one of the most important one is SRY, the sex-determining region of the Y chromosome. When SRY a gene is defective, the indifferent gonads fail to differentiate into testes in a genetically male fetus. Mutations of SRY account for many cases of Swyer syndrome or XY gonadal dysgenesis, a type of hypogonadism in a person who has male karyotype (46, XY). This person is externally female with streak gonads, and without treatment will not experience puberty.    Signaling description: When complexed with the SF1 (NR5A1) protein, SRY acts as a transcription factor that can upregulate other transcription factors, most importantly SOX9, which then activates and forms a feedforward loop with FGF9. Mutations in NR5A1 can produce intersex genitals, absence of puberty, and infertility. Mutations in SOX9 lead to the skeletal malformation syndrome campomelic dysplasia, frequently with autosomal sex-reversal and cleft palate. Sertoli cell proliferation and differentiation is mainly activated by FGF9. SOX9 expression causes the development of primary sex cords, which later develop into seminiferous tubules. These cords form in the central part of the yet-undifferentiated gonad, turning it into a testis. The now induced Leydig cells of the testis then start secreting testosterone while the Sertoli cells produce anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) to inhibit the creation of a female reproductive system.Other transcription factors such as SP1, WT1, GATA4, FOG2 (ZFPM2), SIX1/4 play role in the control of the SRY expression. For example, mutation in WT1 results in reduced male gonad size. Deletion of the gene resulted in complete sex reversal. DAX1 (NR0B1) is an antagonist of male development. NR0B1 acts early in human testis determination, as its dysregulation via either a duplication of the Xp21 region encompassing the NR0B1 gene or a large deletion upstream of NR0B1 leads to 46XY male-to-female sex reversal.Other genes which take part in gonadal development and mutations in which lead to the male infertility include CBX2, SOX3, DHH, AMH, AMHR2, CYP17A1, CYP11A1, and STAR.    Outcome effects: Without testes, no testosterone and anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is produced; the Wolffian ducts fail to develop; no internal male organs are formed. Also, the lack of testosterone means that no dihydrotestosterone is formed and consequently the external genitalia fail to virilize, resulting in normal female genitalia. Without AMH, the Müllerian ducts develop into internal female organs."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The first known step of sexual differentiation of a normal XY fetus is the development of testes. The early stages of testicular formation during the second month of gestation requires the action of several genes, of which one of the most important one is SRY, the sex-determining region of the Y chromosome. When SRY a gene is defective, the indifferent gonads fail to differentiate into testes in a genetically male fetus. Mutations of SRY account for many cases of Swyer syndrome or XY gonadal dysgenesis, a type of hypogonadism in a person who has male karyotype (46, XY). This person is externally female with streak gonads, and without treatment will not experience puberty.    Signaling description: When complexed with the SF1 (NR5A1) protein, SRY acts as a transcription factor that can upregulate other transcription factors, most importantly SOX9, which then activates and forms a feedforward loop with FGF9. Mutations in NR5A1 can produce intersex genitals, absence of puberty, and infertility. Mutations in SOX9 lead to the skeletal malformation syndrome campomelic dysplasia, frequently with autosomal sex-reversal and cleft palate. Sertoli cell proliferation and differentiation is mainly activated by FGF9. SOX9 expression causes the development of primary sex cords, which later develop into seminiferous tubules. These cords form in the central part of the yet-undifferentiated gonad, turning it into a testis. The now induced Leydig cells of the testis then start secreting testosterone while the Sertoli cells produce anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) to inhibit the creation of a female reproductive system.Other transcription factors such as SP1, WT1, GATA4, FOG2 (ZFPM2), SIX1/4 play role in the control of the SRY expression. For example, mutation in WT1 results in reduced male gonad size. Deletion of the gene resulted in complete sex reversal. DAX1 (NR0B1) is an antagonist of male development. NR0B1 acts early in human testis determination, as its dysregulation via either a duplication of the Xp21 region encompassing the NR0B1 gene or a large deletion upstream of NR0B1 leads to 46XY male-to-female sex reversal.Other genes which take part in gonadal development and mutations in which lead to the male infertility include CBX2, SOX3, DHH, AMH, AMHR2, CYP17A1, CYP11A1, and STAR.    Outcome effects: Without testes, no testosterone and anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is produced; the Wolffian ducts fail to develop; no internal male organs are formed. Also, the lack of testosterone means that no dihydrotestosterone is formed and consequently the external genitalia fail to virilize, resulting in normal female genitalia. Without AMH, the Müllerian ducts develop into internal female organs." EXACT []

