xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Leydig cells are testicular cells responsible for production of testosterone in the presence of luteinizing hormone (LH). Dysfunction of Leydig cells may result from mutations in luteinizing hormone / choriogonadotropin receptor (LHCGR) or enzymes, taking part in different stages of sex steroids biogenesis: cholesterol monooxygenase (P450SCC, CYP11A1); steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (STAR); 3 beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (HSD3B2); 17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17A1); 17,20-lyase (CYP17A1); and 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase III (HSD17B3).    Signaling description: Leydig cell hypoplasia is caused by mutations in LHCGR. It is characterized by an inability of the body to respond to LH. The condition manifests itself as pseudohermaphroditism, hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, reduced or absent puberty, and infertility. Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an endocrine disorder that is an uncommon and potentially lethal form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH). Lipoid CAH causes mineralocorticoid deficiency in affected infants and children. Male infants are severely undervirilized causing their external genitalia to look feminine. This disorder is caused by mutations in CYP11A1 that converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and STAR that transports cholesterol into the mitochondria.HSD3B2 gene mutations result in the congenital adrenal hyperplasia and sex steroids deficiency. Males with the defect have hypospadias. This form of the adrenal hyperplasia can cause male pseudohermaphroditism."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Leydig cells are testicular cells responsible for production of testosterone in the presence of luteinizing hormone (LH). Dysfunction of Leydig cells may result from mutations in luteinizing hormone / choriogonadotropin receptor (LHCGR) or enzymes, taking part in different stages of sex steroids biogenesis: cholesterol monooxygenase (P450SCC, CYP11A1); steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (STAR); 3 beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (HSD3B2); 17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17A1); 17,20-lyase (CYP17A1); and 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase III (HSD17B3).    Signaling description: Leydig cell hypoplasia is caused by mutations in LHCGR. It is characterized by an inability of the body to respond to LH. The condition manifests itself as pseudohermaphroditism, hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, reduced or absent puberty, and infertility. Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an endocrine disorder that is an uncommon and potentially lethal form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH). Lipoid CAH causes mineralocorticoid deficiency in affected infants and children. Male infants are severely undervirilized causing their external genitalia to look feminine. This disorder is caused by mutations in CYP11A1 that converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and STAR that transports cholesterol into the mitochondria.HSD3B2 gene mutations result in the congenital adrenal hyperplasia and sex steroids deficiency. Males with the defect have hypospadias. This form of the adrenal hyperplasia can cause male pseudohermaphroditism." EXACT []

