xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Sertoli cells are the somatic cells in the testis, which provide structural and metabolic support to the developing germ cells. Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and testosterone are the two major endocrine signals that act in the testis to regulate Sertoli cells function and spermatogenesis efficiency. Testosterone is essential to maintain spermatogenesis and male fertility. Mutations in testosterone receptor androgen receptor (AR) cause androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) in which various phenotypes may be seen such as ambiguous genitalia, partial labialscrotal fusion, hypospadias, bifid scrotum, gynecomastia and infertility.Cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, located on chromosome is mutated in 60-90% of patients with congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD).    Signaling description: The classical mechanism of testosterone action is to activate gene transcription by causing the androgen receptor (AR) to translocate to and bind specific DNA regulatory elements. Numerous genes and proteins are upregulated in response to testosterone but few are known to be induced in Sertoli cells by androgens through AR binding to gene promoter elements. Testosterone has been found to act via two non-classical pathways in Sertoli cells: 1) testosterone-mediated calcium influx, 2) ERK/MAP kinase and CREB transcription factor activation.Testosterone stimulation can depolarize the Sertoli cell and cause calcium influx Ca2+ into Sertoli cells. Testosterone interacts with an unknown receptor in the plasma membrane that has characteristics of a Gq coupled G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR). Phospholipase C (PLC) is activated to cleave PIP2 into IP3 and DAG. Lower concentrations of PIP2 inhibit K+/ATP channels causing membrane depolarization and Ca2+ entry via L-type Ca2+ channels.Testosterone acting via AR is able to recruit and activate SRC, which causes the activation of the EGFR. EGFR then activates the MAPK cascade most probably through RAS, resulting in the subsequent activation of CREB1. CBAVD is a form of obstructive azoospermia in which there is a disconnection betweenthe epididymis and the ejaculatory duct that causes a functional block to natural fertilization. There are experimental data obtained on Sertoli cells indicating the involvement of CFTR in spermatogensis. CFTR mediated entry of HCO3activates the cAMP pathway leading to full activation of the transcription factors CREB and CREM, and subsequent up-regulation of genes involved in spermatogenesis. CREM (cAMP response element modulator) is a key transcription factor in early round spermatids and activates many genes of spermiogenesis, including TNP1/2, PRM1/2, TESK1, and LDHC.CFTR also negatively regulates the NF-kB/COX2/PGE2 pathway, defect of which can disrupt the tight junction (TJs) and thus the blood-testicular barrier (BTB).Genes that regulate cellular differentiation and chromatin remodeling (TNP1/2, PRM1/2) also may be mutated and cause male infertility.    Outcome effects: In the absence of testosterone stimulation, spermatogenesis does not proceed beyond the meiosis stage. After withdrawal of testosterone, germ cells that have progressed beyond meiosis detach from supporting Sertoli cells and die, whereas mature sperm cannot be released from Sertoli cells resulting in infertility."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Sertoli cells are the somatic cells in the testis, which provide structural and metabolic support to the developing germ cells. Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and testosterone are the two major endocrine signals that act in the testis to regulate Sertoli cells function and spermatogenesis efficiency. Testosterone is essential to maintain spermatogenesis and male fertility. Mutations in testosterone receptor androgen receptor (AR) cause androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) in which various phenotypes may be seen such as ambiguous genitalia, partial labialscrotal fusion, hypospadias, bifid scrotum, gynecomastia and infertility.Cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, located on chromosome is mutated in 60-90% of patients with congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD).    Signaling description: The classical mechanism of testosterone action is to activate gene transcription by causing the androgen receptor (AR) to translocate to and bind specific DNA regulatory elements. Numerous genes and proteins are upregulated in response to testosterone but few are known to be induced in Sertoli cells by androgens through AR binding to gene promoter elements. Testosterone has been found to act via two non-classical pathways in Sertoli cells: 1) testosterone-mediated calcium influx, 2) ERK/MAP kinase and CREB transcription factor activation.Testosterone stimulation can depolarize the Sertoli cell and cause calcium influx Ca2+ into Sertoli cells. Testosterone interacts with an unknown receptor in the plasma membrane that has characteristics of a Gq coupled G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR). Phospholipase C (PLC) is activated to cleave PIP2 into IP3 and DAG. Lower concentrations of PIP2 inhibit K+/ATP channels causing membrane depolarization and Ca2+ entry via L-type Ca2+ channels.Testosterone acting via AR is able to recruit and activate SRC, which causes the activation of the EGFR. EGFR then activates the MAPK cascade most probably through RAS, resulting in the subsequent activation of CREB1. CBAVD is a form of obstructive azoospermia in which there is a disconnection betweenthe epididymis and the ejaculatory duct that causes a functional block to natural fertilization. There are experimental data obtained on Sertoli cells indicating the involvement of CFTR in spermatogensis. CFTR mediated entry of HCO3activates the cAMP pathway leading to full activation of the transcription factors CREB and CREM, and subsequent up-regulation of genes involved in spermatogenesis. CREM (cAMP response element modulator) is a key transcription factor in early round spermatids and activates many genes of spermiogenesis, including TNP1/2, PRM1/2, TESK1, and LDHC.CFTR also negatively regulates the NF-kB/COX2/PGE2 pathway, defect of which can disrupt the tight junction (TJs) and thus the blood-testicular barrier (BTB).Genes that regulate cellular differentiation and chromatin remodeling (TNP1/2, PRM1/2) also may be mutated and cause male infertility.    Outcome effects: In the absence of testosterone stimulation, spermatogenesis does not proceed beyond the meiosis stage. After withdrawal of testosterone, germ cells that have progressed beyond meiosis detach from supporting Sertoli cells and die, whereas mature sperm cannot be released from Sertoli cells resulting in infertility." EXACT []

