synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic Kidney Disease (CKD) refers to a large group of diseases in which abnormal cysts are formed in the kidneys. The cysts interfere with normal kidney function and may cause kidney failure. CKD includes Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD), Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD), Medullary Sponge Kidney (MSK), Nephronophthisis. Hereditary factors account for the majority of CKD types. ADPKD and ARPKD are the two most common types of CKD with different modes of inheritance. Nephronophthisis is a rare autosomal recessive disorder; however, it is the most frequent monogenic cause of the end stage renal disease in children. Pathology of CKD may be related to defects in planar cell polarity.    Signaling description: Mutations in PKD1/2 and PKHD1 genes are associated with ADPKD and ARPKD. Mutations in the INVS gene are associated with nephronophthisis. Both PKD1 and INVS proteins interact with the WNT-signaling pathway leading to cyst formation and renal disease. Precise molecular mechanisms of this process are largely unknown. One of hypotheses is depicted on the pathway. The canonical WNT-signaling pathway initiates when WNT4/5A binds to FZD4 receptor in the presence of LRP5/6, resulting in the activation of DVL1/2. The destruction complex comprising GSK3ß, APC, Axin and CK1 proteins is inhibited by DVL1/2. When this pathway is inactive, the destruction complex targets CTNNB1 for proteosomal degradation. When the pathway is active, such destruction is inhibited, resulting in the accumulation of CTNNB1 in the cytosol with subsequent transcriptional activation of target genes. The non-canonical WNT-pathway initiates when WNT4/5A/9B/11 binds to FZD3/4/5/6 receptors leading to the activation of DVL1/2/3, RHOA, RAC1 and downstream signaling cascade, resulting in cytoskeletal rearrangements and cell polarity regulation.    Outcome effects: In the kidney, the interaction of INVS and PKD1 with CTNNB1 functions as a molecular switch between the WNT-signaling pathways. The impaired balance between the WNT/ß-catenin and non-canonical WNT-pathways disturbs planar cell polarity and causes cyst formation.    Mutated proteins: Mutated PKD1, PKD2, PKHD1, and INVS are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic Kidney Disease (CKD) refers to a large group of diseases in which abnormal cysts are formed in the kidneys. The cysts interfere with normal kidney function and may cause kidney failure. CKD includes Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD), Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD), Medullary Sponge Kidney (MSK), Nephronophthisis. Hereditary factors account for the majority of CKD types. ADPKD and ARPKD are the two most common types of CKD with different modes of inheritance. Nephronophthisis is a rare autosomal recessive disorder; however, it is the most frequent monogenic cause of the end stage renal disease in children. Pathology of CKD may be related to defects in planar cell polarity.    Signaling description: Mutations in PKD1/2 and PKHD1 genes are associated with ADPKD and ARPKD. Mutations in the INVS gene are associated with nephronophthisis. Both PKD1 and INVS proteins interact with the WNT-signaling pathway leading to cyst formation and renal disease. Precise molecular mechanisms of this process are largely unknown. One of hypotheses is depicted on the pathway. The canonical WNT-signaling pathway initiates when WNT4/5A binds to FZD4 receptor in the presence of LRP5/6, resulting in the activation of DVL1/2. The destruction complex comprising GSK3ß, APC, Axin and CK1 proteins is inhibited by DVL1/2. When this pathway is inactive, the destruction complex targets CTNNB1 for proteosomal degradation. When the pathway is active, such destruction is inhibited, resulting in the accumulation of CTNNB1 in the cytosol with subsequent transcriptional activation of target genes. The non-canonical WNT-pathway initiates when WNT4/5A/9B/11 binds to FZD3/4/5/6 receptors leading to the activation of DVL1/2/3, RHOA, RAC1 and downstream signaling cascade, resulting in cytoskeletal rearrangements and cell polarity regulation.    Outcome effects: In the kidney, the interaction of INVS and PKD1 with CTNNB1 functions as a molecular switch between the WNT-signaling pathways. The impaired balance between the WNT/ß-catenin and non-canonical WNT-pathways disturbs planar cell polarity and causes cyst formation.    Mutated proteins: Mutated PKD1, PKD2, PKHD1, and INVS are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

