xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic Kidney Disease (CKD) refers to a large group of diseases in which abnormal cysts are formed in the kidneys. The cysts interfere with normal kidney function and may cause kidney failure. CKD includes Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD), Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD), Nephronophthisis, Medullary Sponge Kidney (MSK) etc. Hereditary factors account for the majority of CKD types. ADPKD and ARPKD are the two most common types of CKD with different modes of inheritance; yet in both cases mutations in polycystin genes (PKD1 and 2) are associated with cyst formation.    Signaling description: PKD1 is involved in the regulation of several signaling routes, such as the activation of JUN, EIF4E, and CCND1. PKD2 regulates Ca2+ influx through the interaction with PKD1 and activation of ERK-signaling. The exact mechanisms for PKD1 and PKD2 involvement in renal cyst formation are not well understood. One of the hypotheses is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: PKD1 regulates JUN activity through JNK-signaling in a GNA12, GNA13, GNAQ and GNB1-dependent manner leading to cell proliferation. In renal epithelial cells, PKD1 inhibits cell size due to the down-regulation of MTOR, RPS6KB1 and EIF4E. Cell growth is regulated by PKD1 through JAK2/STAT3/CCND1 signaling. The ability of PKD2 to regulate Ca2+ influx may account for its capacity to activate ERK-signaling. PKD1 also is involved in fluid secretion via stimulation of PKA/CREBA/CFTR signaling. The increased production of AVP stimulates the proliferation of ADPKD cells and drives fluid secretion via V2R/PKA/ERK and V2R/PKA/CREBA/CFTR, presumably, leading to cyst formation and renal disease.Highlighted protein description: AVP with red highlights has increased expression in ADPKD.    Mutated proteins: Mutated PKD1, PKD2, and PKHD1 are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic Kidney Disease (CKD) refers to a large group of diseases in which abnormal cysts are formed in the kidneys. The cysts interfere with normal kidney function and may cause kidney failure. CKD includes Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD), Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD), Nephronophthisis, Medullary Sponge Kidney (MSK) etc. Hereditary factors account for the majority of CKD types. ADPKD and ARPKD are the two most common types of CKD with different modes of inheritance; yet in both cases mutations in polycystin genes (PKD1 and 2) are associated with cyst formation.    Signaling description: PKD1 is involved in the regulation of several signaling routes, such as the activation of JUN, EIF4E, and CCND1. PKD2 regulates Ca2+ influx through the interaction with PKD1 and activation of ERK-signaling. The exact mechanisms for PKD1 and PKD2 involvement in renal cyst formation are not well understood. One of the hypotheses is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: PKD1 regulates JUN activity through JNK-signaling in a GNA12, GNA13, GNAQ and GNB1-dependent manner leading to cell proliferation. In renal epithelial cells, PKD1 inhibits cell size due to the down-regulation of MTOR, RPS6KB1 and EIF4E. Cell growth is regulated by PKD1 through JAK2/STAT3/CCND1 signaling. The ability of PKD2 to regulate Ca2+ influx may account for its capacity to activate ERK-signaling. PKD1 also is involved in fluid secretion via stimulation of PKA/CREBA/CFTR signaling. The increased production of AVP stimulates the proliferation of ADPKD cells and drives fluid secretion via V2R/PKA/ERK and V2R/PKA/CREBA/CFTR, presumably, leading to cyst formation and renal disease.Highlighted protein description: AVP with red highlights has increased expression in ADPKD.    Mutated proteins: Mutated PKD1, PKD2, and PKHD1 are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

