xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cystic Kidney Disease (CKD) refers to a large group of diseases in which abnormal cysts are formed in the kidneys. The cysts interfere with normal kidney function and may cause kidney failure. CKD includes Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD), Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD), Medullary Sponge Kidney (MSK), Nephronophthisis. Hereditary factors account for the majority of CKD types. Pathological processes underneath CKD are associated with cilia disorganization and defects in the planar cell polarity.    Signaling description: Mutations in PKD1/2, PKHD1, DCDC2, KIF3A, CEP290, NPHP1/3/4, and INVS are associated with CKD. The localization of these proteins in the primary cilium is proposed to be crucial for its function as a mechanosensory antenna of fluid flow in the kidney. However, precise mechanisms by which cilia participate in renal cyst formation are largely undefined. One of the hypotheses is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: The PKD1-PKD2 complex located at the primary cilia surface functions as a fluid shear sensor that regulates the calcium signal. KIF3A-KIF3B-KIFAP3 complex is an essential intraflagellar transport-associated kinesin-2 motor. PKHD1 stimulates PKD2 channel activity in the presence of the kinesin-2 motor. CEP290 and DCDC2 interact with KIF3A in microtubule organization. PKD1 binds to the NPHP1-4 complex localized in microtubule-based structures. INVS and KIF3A modulate cytoskeleton organization and cell polarity via the interaction with CTNNB1.    Mutated proteins: Mutated PKD1/2, PKHD1, DCDC2, KIF3A, CEP290, and NPHP1 are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cystic Kidney Disease (CKD) refers to a large group of diseases in which abnormal cysts are formed in the kidneys. The cysts interfere with normal kidney function and may cause kidney failure. CKD includes Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD), Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD), Medullary Sponge Kidney (MSK), Nephronophthisis. Hereditary factors account for the majority of CKD types. Pathological processes underneath CKD are associated with cilia disorganization and defects in the planar cell polarity.    Signaling description: Mutations in PKD1/2, PKHD1, DCDC2, KIF3A, CEP290, NPHP1/3/4, and INVS are associated with CKD. The localization of these proteins in the primary cilium is proposed to be crucial for its function as a mechanosensory antenna of fluid flow in the kidney. However, precise mechanisms by which cilia participate in renal cyst formation are largely undefined. One of the hypotheses is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: The PKD1-PKD2 complex located at the primary cilia surface functions as a fluid shear sensor that regulates the calcium signal. KIF3A-KIF3B-KIFAP3 complex is an essential intraflagellar transport-associated kinesin-2 motor. PKHD1 stimulates PKD2 channel activity in the presence of the kinesin-2 motor. CEP290 and DCDC2 interact with KIF3A in microtubule organization. PKD1 binds to the NPHP1-4 complex localized in microtubule-based structures. INVS and KIF3A modulate cytoskeleton organization and cell polarity via the interaction with CTNNB1.    Mutated proteins: Mutated PKD1/2, PKHD1, DCDC2, KIF3A, CEP290, and NPHP1 are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

