xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Detumescense is subsidence of erection. There is a number of stimuli, including norepinephrine, endothelin 1 (EDN1), angiotensin II regulating the transition from erection to detumescense.     Signaling description: The intracellular mechanism in the absence of arousal stimuli, initiates with activation of G proteins following ligand binding to membrane receptors in order to keep cavernosal arterioles and sinuses constricted, maintaining the penis in the non-erect state. Subsequently to G protein activation, two signaling pathways are brought into play to cause smooth muscle contraction in the arterioles and cavernosum: Ca+2 dependent pathway (phopholipase C, PKC) and RHOA/ROCK pathway. Another significant function of EDN1 and angiotensin II is to activate nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH) oxidase, increasing reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. ROS rapidly react with NO, reducing its bioavailability, and also stimulate RHOA/ROCK activity.     Outcome effects: The levels of circulating EDN1 and angiotensin II and sympathetic nervous system activity are increased in hypertension making an impact in erectile dysfunction.Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red and compound with reduced level is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Detumescense is subsidence of erection. There is a number of stimuli, including norepinephrine, endothelin 1 (EDN1), angiotensin II regulating the transition from erection to detumescense.     Signaling description: The intracellular mechanism in the absence of arousal stimuli, initiates with activation of G proteins following ligand binding to membrane receptors in order to keep cavernosal arterioles and sinuses constricted, maintaining the penis in the non-erect state. Subsequently to G protein activation, two signaling pathways are brought into play to cause smooth muscle contraction in the arterioles and cavernosum: Ca+2 dependent pathway (phopholipase C, PKC) and RHOA/ROCK pathway. Another significant function of EDN1 and angiotensin II is to activate nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH) oxidase, increasing reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. ROS rapidly react with NO, reducing its bioavailability, and also stimulate RHOA/ROCK activity.     Outcome effects: The levels of circulating EDN1 and angiotensin II and sympathetic nervous system activity are increased in hypertension making an impact in erectile dysfunction.Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red and compound with reduced level is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

