xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Erectile dysfunction (ED) is defined as the persistent inability to maintain or achieve a penile erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual performance. Its etiology is multifactorial and risk factors include age, diabetes mellitus, neurologic diseases, smoking and cardiovascular diseases. Penile erection is a complex neurovascular response that involves integration of central nervous system activity and the release of vasodilator factors from nerves innervating the penis and the endothelium of the penile arteries.     Signaling description: Nitric oxide (NO) is the principle mediator of penile erection. NO is produced in endothelial cells by eNOS (NOS3) in response to binding of acetylcholine from cholinergic nerves to muscarinic receptor CHRM3 on the corporal endothelium. NO is also produced by the nonadrenergic noncholinergic (NANC) nerves in response to central or peripheral nerve stimulation and subsequent influx of calcium through N-type calcium channels. The increase in intracellular calcium (Ca2+) results in the formation of calcium-calmodulin complexes, which activate nNOS (NOS1). NO released from NANC nerve terminals or endothelial cells diffuses into corporal smooth muscle and binds to its intracellular receptor, which is sGC. Binding of NO to sGC results in an increase in the conversion of GTP to cGMP leading to activation of protein kinase G (PKG). Activation of downstream targets of PKG results in an overall decrease in intracellular calcium by increasing uptake of intracellular calcium into sarcoplasmic reticulum and inactivating membrane calcium influx channels. Activation of a membrane-bound G-protein-coupled receptor PTGIR by prostaglandin E1 can also induce smooth muscle cell relaxation via activation of adenylate cyclase and the increased conversion of ATP to cAMP. The increase in intracellular cAMP activates PKA, which interacts with downstream targets to inhibit membrane calcium channels and increase uptake of calcium by the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This leads to an overall decrease in intracellular calcium concentration and corporal smooth muscle relaxation.     Outcome effects: In ED the production and release on NO is augmented, the level of intracellular cAMP and cGMP in smooth muscle cells is low, and the level of intracellular Ca2+ is high leading to muscle contraction and lack of erection (detumescence). The activity of PKG can be prolonged via inhibiting the breakdown of cGMP by type 5 phosphodiesterase 5A (PDE5A) by agents like Viagra (Sildenafil), Levitra (Vardenafil) and Cialis (Tadalafil). Nowadays, the PDE5A inhibitors are the first-line treatment for ED.Compound with elevated level is highlighted in red and compound with reduced level is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Erectile dysfunction (ED) is defined as the persistent inability to maintain or achieve a penile erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual performance. Its etiology is multifactorial and risk factors include age, diabetes mellitus, neurologic diseases, smoking and cardiovascular diseases. Penile erection is a complex neurovascular response that involves integration of central nervous system activity and the release of vasodilator factors from nerves innervating the penis and the endothelium of the penile arteries.     Signaling description: Nitric oxide (NO) is the principle mediator of penile erection. NO is produced in endothelial cells by eNOS (NOS3) in response to binding of acetylcholine from cholinergic nerves to muscarinic receptor CHRM3 on the corporal endothelium. NO is also produced by the nonadrenergic noncholinergic (NANC) nerves in response to central or peripheral nerve stimulation and subsequent influx of calcium through N-type calcium channels. The increase in intracellular calcium (Ca2+) results in the formation of calcium-calmodulin complexes, which activate nNOS (NOS1). NO released from NANC nerve terminals or endothelial cells diffuses into corporal smooth muscle and binds to its intracellular receptor, which is sGC. Binding of NO to sGC results in an increase in the conversion of GTP to cGMP leading to activation of protein kinase G (PKG). Activation of downstream targets of PKG results in an overall decrease in intracellular calcium by increasing uptake of intracellular calcium into sarcoplasmic reticulum and inactivating membrane calcium influx channels. Activation of a membrane-bound G-protein-coupled receptor PTGIR by prostaglandin E1 can also induce smooth muscle cell relaxation via activation of adenylate cyclase and the increased conversion of ATP to cAMP. The increase in intracellular cAMP activates PKA, which interacts with downstream targets to inhibit membrane calcium channels and increase uptake of calcium by the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This leads to an overall decrease in intracellular calcium concentration and corporal smooth muscle relaxation.     Outcome effects: In ED the production and release on NO is augmented, the level of intracellular cAMP and cGMP in smooth muscle cells is low, and the level of intracellular Ca2+ is high leading to muscle contraction and lack of erection (detumescence). The activity of PKG can be prolonged via inhibiting the breakdown of cGMP by type 5 phosphodiesterase 5A (PDE5A) by agents like Viagra (Sildenafil), Levitra (Vardenafil) and Cialis (Tadalafil). Nowadays, the PDE5A inhibitors are the first-line treatment for ED.Compound with elevated level is highlighted in red and compound with reduced level is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

