xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia (ARVC/D) is a type of nonischemic cardiomyopathy that involves primarily the right ventricle. It is characterized by hypokinetic areas involving the free wall of the right ventricle, with fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricular myocardium, with associated arrhythmias originating in the right ventricle.    Signaling description: Adherens junctions are cell-cell junctions that are in cardiomyocytes located usually more basally than tight junctions. An adherens junction is defined as a cell junction, whose cytoplasmic face is linked to the actin cytoskeleton. Adherens junctions contain cadherins, a family of transmembrane proteins that form homodimers in a calcium-dependent manner with other cadherin molecules on adjacent cells.    Outcome effects: JUP or gamma-catenin (plakoglobin) binds the catenin-binding region of the cadherin. Alpha-catenin binds the cadherin indirectly via beta-catenin or plakoglobin and links the actin cytoskeleton to cadherin. Recently identified CTNNA3 is a novel ARVC gene. This gene encodes alpha-T-catenin, which binds plakophilin-2 and gamma-catenin and thereby contributes to the formation of the adherens junctions."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia (ARVC/D) is a type of nonischemic cardiomyopathy that involves primarily the right ventricle. It is characterized by hypokinetic areas involving the free wall of the right ventricle, with fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricular myocardium, with associated arrhythmias originating in the right ventricle.    Signaling description: Adherens junctions are cell-cell junctions that are in cardiomyocytes located usually more basally than tight junctions. An adherens junction is defined as a cell junction, whose cytoplasmic face is linked to the actin cytoskeleton. Adherens junctions contain cadherins, a family of transmembrane proteins that form homodimers in a calcium-dependent manner with other cadherin molecules on adjacent cells.    Outcome effects: JUP or gamma-catenin (plakoglobin) binds the catenin-binding region of the cadherin. Alpha-catenin binds the cadherin indirectly via beta-catenin or plakoglobin and links the actin cytoskeleton to cadherin. Recently identified CTNNA3 is a novel ARVC gene. This gene encodes alpha-T-catenin, which binds plakophilin-2 and gamma-catenin and thereby contributes to the formation of the adherens junctions." EXACT []

