xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia (ARVC/D) is a type of nonischemic cardiomyopathy that involves primarily the right ventricle. It is characterized by hypokinetic areas involving the free wall of the right ventricle, with fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricular myocardium, with associated arrhythmias originating in the right ventricle. ARVC is caused by genetic defects in myocardial desmosomes, areas on the surface of heart muscle cells, which link the cells together. The desmosomes are composed of several proteins, and many of those proteins can have harmful mutations.    Signaling description: Desmosomes are major cell-cell junctions, particularly abundant in epidermal cells and cardiomyocytes. The JUP gene comprises 13 exons, coding for 745 amino acids and an 81.75-kd protein. JUP cytoplasmic protein also known as gamma catenin is found in both submembranous plaques of desmosomes and intermediate junctions. The protein, a member of the catenin family, forms distinct complexes with cadherins and desmosomal cadherins. It contains a distinct repeating amino acid motif called the armadillo repeat. Mutations of JUP affect the structure and distribution of mechanical and electrical cell junctions and could interfere with regulatory mechanisms. The DSP gene comprises 24 exons, encoding 2871 amino acids. The wild-type DSP gene product - desmoplakin, along with plakoglobin, anchors desmosomal cadherins, forming an ordered array of non-transmembrane proteins, which then bind to keratin intermediate filaments. The abnormalities in desmoplakin lead to desmosomal instability. Defective desmosomes cannot sustain the constant mechanical stress in contracting cardiomyocytes, resulting in cardiac dysfunction and cell death. The PKP2 gene comprises 14 exons. The wild-type PKP2 gene product is a desmosomal protein, which provides structural and functional integrity to adjacent cells. Abnormalities in plakophilin are thought to perturb intercellular connections and lead to arrhythmia. The DSG2 gene comprises 15 exons spanning 48.6 kb. The wild-type DSG2 gene product, desmoglein-2, is a member of the desmoglein family and is an essential component of the desmosome. DSG2 is expressed in myocardium. The effect of an abnormal gene product is unknown at this point. The DSC2 gene comprises 17 exons spanning 32 kb. The wild-type DSC2 gene product, desmocollin-2, is ubiquitously expressed in desmosomal tissues and is the only one of three desmocollin isoforms present in cardiac tissue. Desmocollins bind desmogleins through their extracellular domains in a Ca2+-dependent manner and their cytoplasmic domains have binding sites for plakoglobin. Desmocollin mutations that result in an isoform lacking the last 37 amino acid residues of the carboxyl-terminal domain of DSC2a are unable to bind plakoglobin.    Outcome effects: The majority of disease-causing mutations detected in patients with ARVC occur in genes encoding desmosomal proteins.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia (ARVC/D) is a type of nonischemic cardiomyopathy that involves primarily the right ventricle. It is characterized by hypokinetic areas involving the free wall of the right ventricle, with fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricular myocardium, with associated arrhythmias originating in the right ventricle. ARVC is caused by genetic defects in myocardial desmosomes, areas on the surface of heart muscle cells, which link the cells together. The desmosomes are composed of several proteins, and many of those proteins can have harmful mutations.    Signaling description: Desmosomes are major cell-cell junctions, particularly abundant in epidermal cells and cardiomyocytes. The JUP gene comprises 13 exons, coding for 745 amino acids and an 81.75-kd protein. JUP cytoplasmic protein also known as gamma catenin is found in both submembranous plaques of desmosomes and intermediate junctions. The protein, a member of the catenin family, forms distinct complexes with cadherins and desmosomal cadherins. It contains a distinct repeating amino acid motif called the armadillo repeat. Mutations of JUP affect the structure and distribution of mechanical and electrical cell junctions and could interfere with regulatory mechanisms. The DSP gene comprises 24 exons, encoding 2871 amino acids. The wild-type DSP gene product - desmoplakin, along with plakoglobin, anchors desmosomal cadherins, forming an ordered array of non-transmembrane proteins, which then bind to keratin intermediate filaments. The abnormalities in desmoplakin lead to desmosomal instability. Defective desmosomes cannot sustain the constant mechanical stress in contracting cardiomyocytes, resulting in cardiac dysfunction and cell death. The PKP2 gene comprises 14 exons. The wild-type PKP2 gene product is a desmosomal protein, which provides structural and functional integrity to adjacent cells. Abnormalities in plakophilin are thought to perturb intercellular connections and lead to arrhythmia. The DSG2 gene comprises 15 exons spanning 48.6 kb. The wild-type DSG2 gene product, desmoglein-2, is a member of the desmoglein family and is an essential component of the desmosome. DSG2 is expressed in myocardium. The effect of an abnormal gene product is unknown at this point. The DSC2 gene comprises 17 exons spanning 32 kb. The wild-type DSC2 gene product, desmocollin-2, is ubiquitously expressed in desmosomal tissues and is the only one of three desmocollin isoforms present in cardiac tissue. Desmocollins bind desmogleins through their extracellular domains in a Ca2+-dependent manner and their cytoplasmic domains have binding sites for plakoglobin. Desmocollin mutations that result in an isoform lacking the last 37 amino acid residues of the carboxyl-terminal domain of DSC2a are unable to bind plakoglobin.    Outcome effects: The majority of disease-causing mutations detected in patients with ARVC occur in genes encoding desmosomal proteins.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

