xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: The endothelial dysfunction disturbs the physiological protective regulatory balance, which is a critical factor in atherosclerotic disease progression and diseases/disorders associated with an increased cardiovascular risk including hypertension, dyslipidemia, smoking, and mental stress. Endothelial dysfunction is associated with the inability of arteries and arterioles to fully dilate in response to a number of vasoactive substances including nitric oxide (NO). The decrease in NO levels is a hallmark of endothelial dysfunction. During endothelial dysfunction, the endothelium reduces anticoagulant properties while increasing the expression of adhesion molecules, chemokines, and reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Under oxidative stress, oxidized LDL (oxLDL) in the vessel wall causes activation followed by endothelial dysfunction. OLR1 (also known as LOX-1) is the main receptor for oxLDL in endothelial cells. In normal endothelium, OLR1 is expressed at low levels, whereas pathological states such as dyslipidemia, inflammation, and oxidative stress dramatically increase OLR1 expression. In vitro, OLR1 expression is induced by several stimuli including pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF, IL1, and IFNG), angiotensin II, EDN1, and free radicals. Moreover, oxLDL can upregulate the expression of its own receptor, OLR1 in a dose-dependent manner. OxLDL, through OLR1, contributes to the induction of endothelial dysfunction by several mechanisms. In vascular endothelial cells, OLR1 activation triggers a major source of intracellular ROS-NAD(P)H oxidase on the cell membrane, which results in the rapid increase of intracellular ROS such as O2 and H2O2. NAD(P)H oxidase consists of four major subunits including CYBB, CYBA, NCF1, and NCF2 and several isoforms of NAD(P)H subunits have been detected in endothelial cells. The exact mechanism of OLR1-dependent NAD(P)H oxidase activation is unknown. OxLDL/OLR1 may induce RAC1 activation via MMP14 axis to modulate RAC1-mediated NAD(P)H oxidase activity and ROS generation. OLR1 forms a complex with MMP14, which is a cellular receptor and activator for MMP2, but is also a potent matrix-degrading enzyme. Human endothelial cells increase the expression of MMP14, when treated with oxLDL, possibly through an EGFR-dependent mechanism. In addition, OLR1 may directly activate the assembly of NAD(P)H oxidase complex via RAC1 regulation. ROS triggers NF-kB activation and the production of chemokines and adhesion molecules including CCL2, IL8, CXCL2, VCAM1, ICAM1, SELE, and SELP, which leads to the recruitment and adhesion of leukocytes to the endothelium. Furthermore, oxLDL, via OLR1 activation, modulates the expression and activity of MMP1 and MMP3 in human coronary artery endothelial cells (HCAECs). In this process, the activation of PRKCB subunit has an important signaling role. The precise mechanism of this signaling has yet to be determined. NO reduction is considered a hallmark of endothelial dysfunction.    Outcome effects: The decrease of intracellular NO results in increased levels of O2. In addition, oxLDL/OLR1 may induce RHOA activation, via MMP1 axis, to modulate RHOA-dependent NOS3 protein downregulation. ARHGEF1 and ROCK2 may be involved in RHOA-dependent NOS3 protein downregulation downstream from the OLR1 receptor complex. OxLDL can inhibit NO generation by displacing NOS3 from its caveolae membrane localization by depleting caveolae of cholesterol. This suggests that DNM2 is involved in scavenger receptor-mediated oxLDL endocytosis. Furthermore, oxLDL activates endothelial ARG2, which regulates NO production by competing with NOS3 for the common substrate L-arginine, through the dissociation of ar"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: The endothelial dysfunction disturbs the physiological protective regulatory balance, which is a critical factor in atherosclerotic disease progression and diseases/disorders associated with an increased cardiovascular risk including hypertension, dyslipidemia, smoking, and mental stress. Endothelial dysfunction is associated with the inability of arteries and arterioles to fully dilate in response to a number of vasoactive substances including nitric oxide (NO). The decrease in NO levels is a hallmark of endothelial dysfunction. During endothelial dysfunction, the endothelium reduces anticoagulant properties while increasing the expression of adhesion molecules, chemokines, and reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Under oxidative stress, oxidized LDL (oxLDL) in the vessel wall causes activation followed by endothelial dysfunction. OLR1 (also known as LOX-1) is the main receptor for oxLDL in endothelial cells. In normal endothelium, OLR1 is expressed at low levels, whereas pathological states such as dyslipidemia, inflammation, and oxidative stress dramatically increase OLR1 expression. In vitro, OLR1 expression is induced by several stimuli including pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF, IL1, and IFNG), angiotensin II, EDN1, and free radicals. Moreover, oxLDL can upregulate the expression of its own receptor, OLR1 in a dose-dependent manner. OxLDL, through OLR1, contributes to the induction of endothelial dysfunction by several mechanisms. In vascular endothelial cells, OLR1 activation triggers a major source of intracellular ROS-NAD(P)H oxidase on the cell membrane, which results in the rapid increase of intracellular ROS such as O2 and H2O2. NAD(P)H oxidase