xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow. Dendritic cells (DCs) have been identified in atherosclerotic plaques and are thought to have an important role in atherogenesis.    Signaling description: DCs are a heterogeneous population that includes conventional dendritic cells (cDCs) subdivided into lymphoid-tissue-resident or migratory subtypes, plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs), and inflammatory DCs, which are not found in steady states. Lesional DCs take up lipids, produce proinflammatory cytokines, and polarize CD4 T-cells to atherogenic Th1 responses, all contributing to their proatherogenic character. Although DCs are antigen-presenting cells, they can also engulf lipids so they share phenotypic and functional features with macrophages. Therefore, the exact distinction between macrophages and DCs remains controversial and confounded by their plasticity. In addition, DCs may be ideally positioned at the crossroads of innate and adaptive immunity, translating innate damage signals, such as oxidized LDL, into an enhanced adaptive immune response.    Outcome effects: Oxidized LDL bind CD36, inducing intracellular CD36-TLR4-TLR6 heterotrimerization, which activates NF-kB and chemokine expression. In advanced plaques, CCL17 DCs limit regulatory T (Treg) cell expansion and exert local immune-regulatory functions by recruiting and activating CD4 T-cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow. Dendritic cells (DCs) have been identified in atherosclerotic plaques and are thought to have an important role in atherogenesis.    Signaling description: DCs are a heterogeneous population that includes conventional dendritic cells (cDCs) subdivided into lymphoid-tissue-resident or migratory subtypes, plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs), and inflammatory DCs, which are not found in steady states. Lesional DCs take up lipids, produce proinflammatory cytokines, and polarize CD4 T-cells to atherogenic Th1 responses, all contributing to their proatherogenic character. Although DCs are antigen-presenting cells, they can also engulf lipids so they share phenotypic and functional features with macrophages. Therefore, the exact distinction between macrophages and DCs remains controversial and confounded by their plasticity. In addition, DCs may be ideally positioned at the crossroads of innate and adaptive immunity, translating innate damage signals, such as oxidized LDL, into an enhanced adaptive immune response.    Outcome effects: Oxidized LDL bind CD36, inducing intracellular CD36-TLR4-TLR6 heterotrimerization, which activates NF-kB and chemokine expression. In advanced plaques, CCL17 DCs limit regulatory T (Treg) cell expansion and exert local immune-regulatory functions by recruiting and activating CD4 T-cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

