xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: Tissue injury leading to the release of danger signals such as S100 proteins, HMGB1, heat shock proteins, phosphatidylserine containing microparticles, and alteration of tissue homestasis with the oxidation of LDL and other proteins are able to activate pattern-recognition receptors to initiate a potent inflammatory response. An increasing number of endogenous ligands are being reported as candidate stimulators of pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors (TLRs) and advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER and TLRs play important roles in mediating acute and chronic inflammation relevant for the initiation and progression of atherosclerosis. AGER activates several signal transduction cascades including the family of MAPKs and PI3K. In addition, transcription factors such as NF-kB and JUN/FOS are targets of AGER signaling. Furthermore, S100A8, S100A9, and S100A12 are endogenously expressed at various levels in cells that are closely linked to vascular disease such as neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, and monocytes.    Outcome effects: The released S100A8 and S100A9 exert extracellular functions, which are mediated by TLR4, AGER, and other receptors. These proteins are released from damaged/dying cells or activated cells and are included in the group of proteins termed damage-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) molecules.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: Tissue injury leading to the release of danger signals such as S100 proteins, HMGB1, heat shock proteins, phosphatidylserine containing microparticles, and alteration of tissue homestasis with the oxidation of LDL and other proteins are able to activate pattern-recognition receptors to initiate a potent inflammatory response. An increasing number of endogenous ligands are being reported as candidate stimulators of pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors (TLRs) and advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER and TLRs play important roles in mediating acute and chronic inflammation relevant for the initiation and progression of atherosclerosis. AGER activates several signal transduction cascades including the family of MAPKs and PI3K. In addition, transcription factors such as NF-kB and JUN/FOS are targets of AGER signaling. Furthermore, S100A8, S100A9, and S100A12 are endogenously expressed at various levels in cells that are closely linked to vascular disease such as neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, and monocytes.    Outcome effects: The released S100A8 and S100A9 exert extracellular functions, which are mediated by TLR4, AGER, and other receptors. These proteins are released from damaged/dying cells or activated cells and are included in the group of proteins termed damage-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) molecules.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

