xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: Atherosclerosis involves the formation of plaques in arterial walls that narrow the arterial passage which restricts blood flow and increases the risk of occlusion of blood flow by a myocardial infarction. One of the first stages in the development of atherosclerosis is the passage of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) out of the arterial lumen and into the arterial wall. Once there, the lipids in LDL are chemically modified and oxidized. Oxidized LDL stimulates inflammatory signaling by neighboring endothelial cells (ECs), releasing chemokines and cytokines such as CCL5 and CCL2 in addition to ICAM1 and recruiting monocytes into the arterial wall.    Outcome effects: Monocytes then differentiate into macrophages and internalize modified LDL. These macrophages continue to internalize LDL and become enlarged and full of lipid. These cells accumulate in tissue and are transformed into foam cells, dying and forming part of the atherogenic plaque. Monocyte recruitment involves their rolling, firm adhesion, lateral migration, and transendothelial diapedesis which are controlled by chemokines. While soluble chemokines mediate direct leukocyte recruitment, chemokines immobilized on the surface of activated ECs, through glycosaminoglycan, trigger leukocyte arrest via G-protein-coupled receptors, which activate leukocyte integrins.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: Atherosclerosis involves the formation of plaques in arterial walls that narrow the arterial passage which restricts blood flow and increases the risk of occlusion of blood flow by a myocardial infarction. One of the first stages in the development of atherosclerosis is the passage of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) out of the arterial lumen and into the arterial wall. Once there, the lipids in LDL are chemically modified and oxidized. Oxidized LDL stimulates inflammatory signaling by neighboring endothelial cells (ECs), releasing chemokines and cytokines such as CCL5 and CCL2 in addition to ICAM1 and recruiting monocytes into the arterial wall.    Outcome effects: Monocytes then differentiate into macrophages and internalize modified LDL. These macrophages continue to internalize LDL and become enlarged and full of lipid. These cells accumulate in tissue and are transformed into foam cells, dying and forming part of the atherogenic plaque. Monocyte recruitment involves their rolling, firm adhesion, lateral migration, and transendothelial diapedesis which are controlled by chemokines. While soluble chemokines mediate direct leukocyte recruitment, chemokines immobilized on the surface of activated ECs, through glycosaminoglycan, trigger leukocyte arrest via G-protein-coupled receptors, which activate leukocyte integrins.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

