xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow. Atherosclerotic lesions frequently become calcified. This process can begin early and accelerates as the disease progresses and more complex lesions develop.    Signaling description: Calcium deposits in coronary arteries indicate the presence of plaques. On the surface of vascular cells, ENPP1 converts ATP to AMP and pyrophosphate (PPi), while NT5E converts AMP to adenosine and inorganic phosphate (Pi). PPi inhibits calcification, while tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) degrades PPi and adenosine inhibits ALPL. A deficiency of NT5E results in decreased adenosine levels, which eliminates the inhibition of ALPL from the pathway either directly or by way of adenosine receptor signaling.    Outcome effects: The higher levels of ALPL results in reduced PPi and increased cell calcification.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow. Atherosclerotic lesions frequently become calcified. This process can begin early and accelerates as the disease progresses and more complex lesions develop.    Signaling description: Calcium deposits in coronary arteries indicate the presence of plaques. On the surface of vascular cells, ENPP1 converts ATP to AMP and pyrophosphate (PPi), while NT5E converts AMP to adenosine and inorganic phosphate (Pi). PPi inhibits calcification, while tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) degrades PPi and adenosine inhibits ALPL. A deficiency of NT5E results in decreased adenosine levels, which eliminates the inhibition of ALPL from the pathway either directly or by way of adenosine receptor signaling.    Outcome effects: The higher levels of ALPL results in reduced PPi and increased cell calcification.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

