xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: The continuous migration and infiltration of activated macrophages and T-cells into atherosclerotic lesions are prominent features in human atherosclerotic disease. The recruitment of these cells to lesions is guided by endothelial leukocyte adhesion molecules and chemoattractants. Chemokines, a family of small chemotactic cytokines, can be detected within atherosclerotic lesions, where they function as messengers to direct leukocytes to sites of inflammation, but may also control homeostasis and other activities of migrated cells.    Outcome effects: Monocytes employ chemokine receptors such as CCR2, CCR5, and CX3CR1 to invade plaques, where they differentiate into macrophages. Neutrophils use CCL5 receptors including CCR1 and CCR5 for entering in atherosclerotic plaques in contrast to the restricted use of CCR2 and CXCR2 for peripheral venous recruitment. T-cells use CXCR3-CXCL10, CXCR6-CXCL16, and CCR5 for entering the atherosclerotic plaques.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atherosclerosis is a progressive chronic disease characterized by the accumulation of lipids and fibrous elements in the large arteries. The buildup of fat and cholesterol leads to the formation of plaques in the artery walls which can hamper the blood flow.    Signaling description: The continuous migration and infiltration of activated macrophages and T-cells into atherosclerotic lesions are prominent features in human atherosclerotic disease. The recruitment of these cells to lesions is guided by endothelial leukocyte adhesion molecules and chemoattractants. Chemokines, a family of small chemotactic cytokines, can be detected within atherosclerotic lesions, where they function as messengers to direct leukocytes to sites of inflammation, but may also control homeostasis and other activities of migrated cells.    Outcome effects: Monocytes employ chemokine receptors such as CCR2, CCR5, and CX3CR1 to invade plaques, where they differentiate into macrophages. Neutrophils use CCL5 receptors including CCR1 and CCR5 for entering in atherosclerotic plaques in contrast to the restricted use of CCR2 and CXCR2 for peripheral venous recruitment. T-cells use CXCR3-CXCL10, CXCR6-CXCL16, and CCR5 for entering the atherosclerotic plaques.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

