xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Aldosterone and angiotensin (AGT) have a central role in the neuro-endocrine regulation of blood pressure. Chronic activation of the angiotensin-aldosterone system (AAS) is a major contributing factor to the pathogenesis and progression of arterial hypertension.    Signaling description: The classic AAS is a cascade, which involves several distinct components. The juxtaglomerular apparatus in the kidney secretes renin (REN). REN cleaves an inactive angiotensinogen (AGT), a high molecular weight protein released from the liver, converting it into angiotensin I, which is then converted into angiotensin II by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). The angiotensin II receptors are located in the arterioles, adrenal gland, and circumventricular regions of the brain. Angiotensin II acts as an endocrine, autocrine/paracrine, and intracrine hormone. These effects include constriction of blood vessels resulting in increased blood pressure, stimulation of aldosterone synthesis and release, and induction of thirst and salt appetite. Angiotensin II also stimulates the secretion of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex, which is involved in the control of extracellular fluid volume and electrolyte homeostasis. CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone. Progesterone is then hydroxylated to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2, while CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. CYP17A1 converts pregnenolone to 17-hydroxypregnenolone, which is hydroxylated to 17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone by HSD3B2. CYP21A2 hydroxylates 17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol. CYP11B1 converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol.    Outcome effects: Aldosterone and cortisol act on the kidney tubules causing them to reabsorb more Na+ and water from urine. K+ is secreted into the tubules in exchange for Na+, which is excreted. This increases the volume of fluid in the body and increases the blood pressure. In addition, aldosterone production is stimulated by adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). ACTH is synthesized from proopiomelanocortin (POMC) in the anterior pituitary gland. Precursor polypeptide POMC is cleaved to ACTH and other hormones. Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is produced by parvocellular neuroendocrine cells of the hypothalamus and released into the primary capillary plexus of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system, where it regulates the production of ACTH. The latter binds MC2R, which activates ADCY, and ADCY produces the second messenger cAMP, thereby stimulating the activity of PKA, which phosphorylates and activates STAR, a crucial protein for the transport of cholesterol mitochondria, where the biosynthesis of steroids is initiated. Aldosterone regulates the activity of ion channels that control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons and is responsible for Na+ reabsorption into plasma and K+ excretion in the distal renal tubule. The transport of Na+ and K+ has a dominant role in blood pressure homeostasis and excessive levels of Na+ in the plasma causes arterial hypertension.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Aldosterone and angiotensin (AGT) have a central role in the neuro-endocrine regulation of blood pressure. Chronic activation of the angiotensin-aldosterone system (AAS) is a major contributing factor to the pathogenesis and progression of arterial hypertension.    Signaling description: The classic AAS is a cascade, which involves several distinct components. The juxtaglomerular apparatus in the kidney secretes renin (REN). REN cleaves an inactive angiotensinogen (AGT), a high molecular weight protein released from the liver, converting it into angiotensin I, which is then converted into angiotensin II by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). The angiotensin II receptors are located in the arterioles, adrenal gland, and circumventricular regions of the brain. Angiotensin II acts as an endocrine, autocrine/paracrine, and intracrine hormone. These effects include constriction of blood vessels resulting in increased blood pressure, stimulation of aldosterone synthesis and release, and induction of thirst and salt appetite. Angiotensin II also stimulates the secretion of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex, which is involved in the control of extracellular fluid volume and electrolyte homeostasis. CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone. Progesterone is then hydroxylated to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2, while CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. CYP17A1 converts pregnenolone to 17-hydroxypregnenolone, which is hydroxylated to 17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone by HSD3B2. CYP21A2 hydroxylates 17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol. CYP11B1 converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol.    Outcome effects: Aldosterone and cortisol act on the kidney tubules causing them to reabsorb more Na+ and water from urine. K+ is secreted into the tubules in exchange for Na+, which is excreted. This increases the volume of fluid in the body and increases the blood pressure. In addition, aldosterone production is stimulated by adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). ACTH is synthesized from proopiomelanocortin (POMC) in the anterior pituitary gland. Precursor polypeptide POMC is cleaved to ACTH and other hormones. Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is produced by parvocellular neuroendocrine cells of the hypothalamus and released into the primary capillary plexus of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system, where it regulates the production of ACTH. The latter binds MC2R, which activates ADCY, and ADCY produces the second messenger cAMP, thereby stimulating the activity of PKA, which phosphorylates and activates STAR, a crucial protein for the transport of cholesterol mitochondria, where the biosynthesis of steroids is initiated. Aldosterone regulates the activity of ion channels that control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons and is responsible for Na+ reabsorption into plasma and K+ excretion in the distal renal tubule. The transport of Na+ and K+ has a dominant role in blood pressure homeostasis and excessive levels of Na+ in the plasma causes arterial hypertension.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

