xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Postmenopause is also frequently associated with arterial hypertension.    Signaling description: The hypertension risk related to postmenopause is due to rapid reduction of estrogen levels, which has a direct effect on blood vessel functions. Estrogens influence the transcription of a number of genes. Once activated by estrogen, the ESR1 is translocated into the nucleus where it interacts with transcription factors including NCOA1/2/3 and EP300 to form a transcription complex to induce the expression of downstream target genes. In addition, rapid non-genomic effects are mediated by ESR1 and GPER. The loss of estrogens contributes to endothelial dysfunction and results in arterial hypertension. Endothelial dysfunction is typically characterized by a reduction in nitric oxide (NO) levels. Estradiol quickly stimulates NO production by increasing intracellular Ca2+ which activates NOS3 and in the long-term by upregulating the transcription of NOS3. Both acute and chronic effects of estradiol on NO promote vasodilation. CAV1 and CAV3 are the primary structural proteins of caveolae, which are vesicular invaginations of the plasma membrane that represent specialized membrane signaling organelles. They are the preferential site of ESR1 signaling complexes in endothelial cells. Activated ESR1 co-localizes with CAV1/3 and activates NOS3. Once activated by estradiol, membrane-bound ESR1 activates PIK3CA via PIK3R1.    Outcome effects: PIK3CA, through PIP3 and PDPK1, activates AKT1, which phosphorylates and activates NOS3 and CREB1, which in turn bind the nuclear ESR1 transcription complex. In addition, GPER, via GNAQ, stimulates PLCB1 and phosphatidyl-inositol-calcium second messenger system resulting in the release of Ca2+ into the cytoplasm from the endoplasmic reticulum. Ca2+ binds the multifunctional binding messenger protein, calmodulin, which activates NOS3. CAV3 inhibits ADCY, which activates NOS3 via PKA. Nuclear ESR1 transcription complex promotes the expression of PTGIS, PTGS2, and PTGS1, which catalyze the synthesis of prostaglandin involved in vasodilation and regulation of platelet function. In addition, nuclear ESR1 transcription complex promotes the expression of blood clotting regulators such as PROS1, THBD, and PROCR. Furthermore, ESR1 and GPER activate MAP kinases cascade via SRC/SHC1 and leads to the activation of IL6, IL8, CCL2, and VEGFA which are involved in the mediation of vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Postmenopause is also frequently associated with arterial hypertension.    Signaling description: The hypertension risk related to postmenopause is due to rapid reduction of estrogen levels, which has a direct effect on blood vessel functions. Estrogens influence the transcription of a number of genes. Once activated by estrogen, the ESR1 is translocated into the nucleus where it interacts with transcription factors including NCOA1/2/3 and EP300 to form a transcription complex to induce the expression of downstream target genes. In addition, rapid non-genomic effects are mediated by ESR1 and GPER. The loss of estrogens contributes to endothelial dysfunction and results in arterial hypertension. Endothelial dysfunction is typically characterized by a reduction in nitric oxide (NO) levels. Estradiol quickly stimulates NO production by increasing intracellular Ca2+ which activates NOS3 and in the long-term by upregulating the transcription of NOS3. Both acute and chronic effects of estradiol on NO promote vasodilation. CAV1 and CAV3 are the primary structural proteins of caveolae, which are vesicular invaginations of the plasma membrane that represent specialized membrane signaling organelles. They are the preferential site of ESR1 signaling complexes in endothelial cells. Activated ESR1 co-localizes with CAV1/3 and activates NOS3. Once activated by estradiol, membrane-bound ESR1 activates PIK3CA via PIK3R1.    Outcome effects: PIK3CA, through PIP3 and PDPK1, activates AKT1, which phosphorylates and activates NOS3 and CREB1, which in turn bind the nuclear ESR1 transcription complex. In addition, GPER, via GNAQ, stimulates PLCB1 and phosphatidyl-inositol-calcium second messenger system resulting in the release of Ca2+ into the cytoplasm from the endoplasmic reticulum. Ca2+ binds the multifunctional binding messenger protein, calmodulin, which activates NOS3. CAV3 inhibits ADCY, which activates NOS3 via PKA. Nuclear ESR1 transcription complex promotes the expression of PTGIS, PTGS2, and PTGS1, which catalyze the synthesis of prostaglandin involved in vasodilation and regulation of platelet function. In addition, nuclear ESR1 transcription complex promotes the expression of blood clotting regulators such as PROS1, THBD, and PROCR. Furthermore, ESR1 and GPER activate MAP kinases cascade via SRC/SHC1 and leads to the activation of IL6, IL8, CCL2, and VEGFA which are involved in the mediation of vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

