xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Aldosterone is a key mineralocorticoid hormone that has a fundamental role in salt and water homeostasis and blood pressure regulation.    Signaling description: Aldosterone production is regulated through the continuing action of the local and systemic angiotensin II, circulating adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and serum potassium levels. All stimuli can have both immediate and continuous effects on aldosterone synthesis and release. Aldosterone synthesis itself involves an acute phase regulated by a steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (STAR), which is crucial for the transport of cholesterol mitochondria, where the biosynthesis of steroids is initiated. AGT binds AGTR1 to activate PLC, which then mediates the cleavage of PIP2 to diacylglycerol and IP3. IP3 binds ITPR1 on the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), which leads to a release calcium (Ca2+) and, thus raises cytosolic Ca2+ levels. Diacylglycerol activates PKC, which phosphorylates PRKD1, which directly phosphorylates and activates CREB1. IP3 mediates the activation of CREB1 via the MAPK pathway.    Outcome effects: CREB1 activates STAR and facilitates a rapid influx of cholesterol into the mitochondria. Cholesterol is the primary precursor for steroidogenesis and aldosterone synthesis. A possible activating mutation in CYP11B2 enhances aldosterone synthesis. Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which is synthesized from proopiomelanocortin (POMC), is able to quickly stimulate aldosterone production. ACTH binds MC2R, which activates ADCY10 via the GNAS complex. ADCY10 produces cAMP, thereby stimulating the activity of PKA, which phosphorylates and activates STAR and CREB1. Angiotensin II and ACTH rapidly increase aldosterone secretion by increasing STAR expression. The overproduction of aldosterone causes aldosteronism and leads to arterial hypertension.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Aldosterone is a key mineralocorticoid hormone that has a fundamental role in salt and water homeostasis and blood pressure regulation.    Signaling description: Aldosterone production is regulated through the continuing action of the local and systemic angiotensin II, circulating adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and serum potassium levels. All stimuli can have both immediate and continuous effects on aldosterone synthesis and release. Aldosterone synthesis itself involves an acute phase regulated by a steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (STAR), which is crucial for the transport of cholesterol mitochondria, where the biosynthesis of steroids is initiated. AGT binds AGTR1 to activate PLC, which then mediates the cleavage of PIP2 to diacylglycerol and IP3. IP3 binds ITPR1 on the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), which leads to a release calcium (Ca2+) and, thus raises cytosolic Ca2+ levels. Diacylglycerol activates PKC, which phosphorylates PRKD1, which directly phosphorylates and activates CREB1. IP3 mediates the activation of CREB1 via the MAPK pathway.    Outcome effects: CREB1 activates STAR and facilitates a rapid influx of cholesterol into the mitochondria. Cholesterol is the primary precursor for steroidogenesis and aldosterone synthesis. A possible activating mutation in CYP11B2 enhances aldosterone synthesis. Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which is synthesized from proopiomelanocortin (POMC), is able to quickly stimulate aldosterone production. ACTH binds MC2R, which activates ADCY10 via the GNAS complex. ADCY10 produces cAMP, thereby stimulating the activity of PKA, which phosphorylates and activates STAR and CREB1. Angiotensin II and ACTH rapidly increase aldosterone secretion by increasing STAR expression. The overproduction of aldosterone causes aldosteronism and leads to arterial hypertension.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

