xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. The pathogenesis of arterial hypertension is associated with mechanisms of vasoconstriction.    Signaling description: The endothelium plays a crucial role in the regulation of vasoconstriction through the synthesis and release of vasodilator substances including nitric oxide (NO) and PGI2. NO and PGI2 are secreted by endothelial cells in response to specific stimuli such as bradykinin (KNG1) and hypoxia and act as local regulators of vascular tone. KNG1 is an endothelium-dependent vasodilator and is produced by kallikrein. Reduced levels of NO, PGI2, and KNG1 elevate vascular tone and promote endothelial cell proliferation. PGI2 binds PTGIR, while KNG1 acts through BDKRB2. The activity of these receptors is mediated by G proteins such as GNA11 and GNAQ, which activate PLCB1 and phosphatidyl-inositol-calcium second messenger system resulting in the release of Ca2+ into the cytoplasm from the endoplasmic reticulum and the activation of PRKCD.    Outcome effects: PRKCD activates AKT1 pathway via PTK2B/SRC. Normally, AKT1 activates NOS3, which produces the main vasodilator, NO, which activates PTGS1/2. However, the lack of constitutional activation of NOS3 leads to the deficiency of NO and PGI2 production and impaired vasodilation. SRC transactivates EGFR that stimulates the MAPK cascade and promotes endothelial cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. The pathogenesis of arterial hypertension is associated with mechanisms of vasoconstriction.    Signaling description: The endothelium plays a crucial role in the regulation of vasoconstriction through the synthesis and release of vasodilator substances including nitric oxide (NO) and PGI2. NO and PGI2 are secreted by endothelial cells in response to specific stimuli such as bradykinin (KNG1) and hypoxia and act as local regulators of vascular tone. KNG1 is an endothelium-dependent vasodilator and is produced by kallikrein. Reduced levels of NO, PGI2, and KNG1 elevate vascular tone and promote endothelial cell proliferation. PGI2 binds PTGIR, while KNG1 acts through BDKRB2. The activity of these receptors is mediated by G proteins such as GNA11 and GNAQ, which activate PLCB1 and phosphatidyl-inositol-calcium second messenger system resulting in the release of Ca2+ into the cytoplasm from the endoplasmic reticulum and the activation of PRKCD.    Outcome effects: PRKCD activates AKT1 pathway via PTK2B/SRC. Normally, AKT1 activates NOS3, which produces the main vasodilator, NO, which activates PTGS1/2. However, the lack of constitutional activation of NOS3 leads to the deficiency of NO and PGI2 production and impaired vasodilation. SRC transactivates EGFR that stimulates the MAPK cascade and promotes endothelial cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

