xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. The pathogenesis of arterial hypertension is associated with mechanisms of vasoconstriction, which is believed to be an early component of the arterial hypertensive process.    Signaling description: Excessive vasoconstriction is a consequence of smooth muscle cell proliferation and dysfunction. Abundant levels of angiotensin II, noradrenaline, AVP, and EDN1 have a central role in smooth muscle cell dysfunction. They function as growth factors influencing smooth muscle cell proliferation, growth, and contraction. Angiotensin II acts through AGTR1, while noradrenaline binds ADRA1A. The receptor for AVP is AVPR1A, whereas EDN1 binds EDNRA/B. The activity of these receptors is mediated by G proteins such as GNA11 and GNAQ, which activate PLC and phosphatidyl-inositol-calcium second messenger system. PLC mediates the cleavage of PIP2 to diacylglycerol and IP3. Further, diacylglycerol activates PRKCD, and IP3 binds ITPR1 on the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), releasing Ca2+ and raising cytosolic Ca2+ concentrations. In addition, Ca2+ enters through the voltage-dependent, L-type calcium channels such as CACNB2, CACNA1C, and CACNA2D1 and is released via RYR3 into the sarcoplasmic reticulum.    Outcome effects: Ca2+ is a highly versatile, intracellular signal that regulates many different cellular processes and proteins such as RASGRP1 and RASGRF1, which activate the MAPK cascade. PRKCD activates the MAPK pathway via PTK2B/SRC, which are involved in smooth muscle cell proliferation and growth. MAPK1 and MAPK3 activate MYLK that phosphorylates MYL12B to facilitate myosin interaction with actin filaments to produce the contractile activity. GNAQ stimulates the activation of RHOA, which, through ROCK1, decreases MYOCD expression. MYOCD is a smooth muscle and cardiac cell specific transcriptional factor that acts as a cofactor of SRF and TAGLN and is involved in smooth muscle cell contraction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. The pathogenesis of arterial hypertension is associated with mechanisms of vasoconstriction, which is believed to be an early component of the arterial hypertensive process.    Signaling description: Excessive vasoconstriction is a consequence of smooth muscle cell proliferation and dysfunction. Abundant levels of angiotensin II, noradrenaline, AVP, and EDN1 have a central role in smooth muscle cell dysfunction. They function as growth factors influencing smooth muscle cell proliferation, growth, and contraction. Angiotensin II acts through AGTR1, while noradrenaline binds ADRA1A. The receptor for AVP is AVPR1A, whereas EDN1 binds EDNRA/B. The activity of these receptors is mediated by G proteins such as GNA11 and GNAQ, which activate PLC and phosphatidyl-inositol-calcium second messenger system. PLC mediates the cleavage of PIP2 to diacylglycerol and IP3. Further, diacylglycerol activates PRKCD, and IP3 binds ITPR1 on the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), releasing Ca2+ and raising cytosolic Ca2+ concentrations. In addition, Ca2+ enters through the voltage-dependent, L-type calcium channels such as CACNB2, CACNA1C, and CACNA2D1 and is released via RYR3 into the sarcoplasmic reticulum.    Outcome effects: Ca2+ is a highly versatile, intracellular signal that regulates many different cellular processes and proteins such as RASGRP1 and RASGRF1, which activate the MAPK cascade. PRKCD activates the MAPK pathway via PTK2B/SRC, which are involved in smooth muscle cell proliferation and growth. MAPK1 and MAPK3 activate MYLK that phosphorylates MYL12B to facilitate myosin interaction with actin filaments to produce the contractile activity. GNAQ stimulates the activation of RHOA, which, through ROCK1, decreases MYOCD expression. MYOCD is a smooth muscle and cardiac cell specific transcriptional factor that acts as a cofactor of SRF and TAGLN and is involved in smooth muscle cell contraction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

