xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Na+/Cl-/K+ homeostasis disorder have a key role in the development of arterial hypertension. Non-voltage gated sodium (Na ) channels including amiloride-sensitive Na channels, epithelial Na channel (ENaC), and Na channel non-neuronal 1 (SCNN1) control Na transport across epithelia in nephron collecting ducts. This channel is a heterotrimeric complex consisting of alpha (SCNN1A), beta (SCNN1B) and gamma (SCNN1G) subunits. It plays a major role in Na homeostasis by reabsorption of Na ions. The activity of ENaC in kidney is modulated by the mineralocorticoid aldosterone. A kidney-specific SLC12A1 plays a key role in Na , Cl-, and K ion balance. KCNJ1 is activated by internal ATP and plays an important role in K homeostasis. SLC12A3 mediates Na and Cl- reabsorption in the distal renal tubule. These channels control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons which are responsible for Na and Cl- reabsorption into plasma and K excretion. The transport of Na and K plays a dominant role in blood pressure and the overproduction of Na in plasma is the cause of arterial hypertension. Aldosterone binds to NR3C2, which interacts with NR3C1, and they both function as transcription factors that bind to corticoid response elements in the promoters of corticoid responsive genes to activate their transcription. NR3C1 promotes TSC22D3 which increases the transcription of SGK1. Subsequently, SGK1 phosphorylates NEDD4L. NEDD4L mediates the ubiquitination of multiple target substrates and plays a critical role in epithelial Na transport by regulating the cell surface expression of ENaC. WNK1/4 regulate blood pressure by controlling the transport of Na and Cl- ions via activation of STK39 and OXSR1. STK39 and OXSR1 stimulate SLC12A1 and SLC12A3. Mutations in SCNN1B/SCNN1G cause Liddle syndrome while excessive levels of aldosterone is the cause of hyperaldosteronism. In addition, mutations in WNK1/4, NR3C2 and excessive levels of dietary sodium cause pseudohyperaldosteronism that are characterized by high blood pressure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Na+/Cl-/K+ homeostasis disorder have a key role in the development of arterial hypertension. Non-voltage gated sodium (Na ) channels including amiloride-sensitive Na channels, epithelial Na channel (ENaC), and Na channel non-neuronal 1 (SCNN1) control Na transport across epithelia in nephron collecting ducts. This channel is a heterotrimeric complex consisting of alpha (SCNN1A), beta (SCNN1B) and gamma (SCNN1G) subunits. It plays a major role in Na homeostasis by reabsorption of Na ions. The activity of ENaC in kidney is modulated by the mineralocorticoid aldosterone. A kidney-specific SLC12A1 plays a key role in Na , Cl-, and K ion balance. KCNJ1 is activated by internal ATP and plays an important role in K homeostasis. SLC12A3 mediates Na and Cl- reabsorption in the distal renal tubule. These channels control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons which are responsible for Na and Cl- reabsorption into plasma and K excretion. The transport of Na and K plays a dominant role in blood pressure and the overproduction of Na in plasma is the cause of arterial hypertension. Aldosterone binds to NR3C2, which interacts with NR3C1, and they both function as transcription factors that bind to corticoid response elements in the promoters of corticoid responsive genes to activate their transcription. NR3C1 promotes TSC22D3 which increases the transcription of SGK1. Subsequently, SGK1 phosphorylates NEDD4L. NEDD4L mediates the ubiquitination of multiple target substrates and plays a critical role in epithelial Na transport by regulating the cell surface expression of ENaC. WNK1/4 regulate blood pressure by controlling the transport of Na and Cl- ions via activation of STK39 and OXSR1. STK39 and OXSR1 stimulate SLC12A1 and SLC12A3. Mutations in SCNN1B/SCNN1G cause Liddle syndrome while excessive levels of aldosterone is the cause of hyperaldosteronism. In addition, mutations in WNK1/4, NR3C2 and excessive levels of dietary sodium cause pseudohyperaldosteronism that are characterized by high blood pressure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

