xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Glucocorticoid-induced hypertension is a poorly understood clinical state and is often associated with the Cushing's disease. This form of hypertension is believed to be mediated by excess sodium (Na+) and water reabsorption by the kidney.    Signaling description: The disruption of Na+/K+ homeostasis plays a key role in the development of arterial hypertension. The activity of channels that control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons is regulated by the mineralocorticoid aldosterone and glucocorticoid cortisol. Excessive secretion of cortisol causes arterial hypertension. Cortisol can bind to the mineralocorticoid receptor, NR3C2 and regulate the transcription of a number of genes. Activated NR3C2 translocates into the nucleus, where it interacts with the transcription complex, NCOA1/2/3/EP300/CREB1/CREBP/SP1, to induce the expression of downstream target genes.    Outcome effects: The NCOA1/2/3/EP300/CREB1/CREBP/SP1 complex activates the transcription of SGK1, ENaC subunits, ATP1B1, and AQP2. The non-catalytic ATP1B1 subunit binds the catalytic ATP1A1 and forms Na+/K+-transporting ATPase, which catalyzes the hydrolysis of ATP coupled with the Na+ and K+ exchange across the plasma membrane. SGK1 activates the alpha subunit (SCNN1A) of ENaC. Non-voltage-gated Na+ channels including amiloride-sensitive Na+ channels, epithelial Na+ channel (ENaC), and Na+ channel non-neuronal 1 (SCNN1), control Na+ transport across epithelia in nephron collecting ducts. This channel is a heterotrimeric complex consisting of alpha (SCNN1A), beta (SCNN1B) and gamma (SCNN1G) subunits. It has a major role in Na+ homeostasis throuth the Na+ reabsorption. FLNA, FURIN, and CTTN interact with ENaC subunits and are involved in cytoskeletal ENaC regulation. Mutations in SCNN1B and SCNN1G cause Liddle syndrome characterized by increased blood pressure. AQP2 forms a water-specific channel on plasma membranes of nephron collecting ducts that provides water to move down the osmotic gradient.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Arterial hypertension is a chronic increase in blood pressure, which may lead to a variety of cardiovascular diseases, stroke, congestive heart failure, peripheral vascular disease, and renal disease. Hypertension risk factors include obesity, alcohol, smoking, kidney or endocrine diseases, diabetes mellitus, and family history. Glucocorticoid-induced hypertension is a poorly understood clinical state and is often associated with the Cushing's disease. This form of hypertension is believed to be mediated by excess sodium (Na+) and water reabsorption by the kidney.    Signaling description: The disruption of Na+/K+ homeostasis plays a key role in the development of arterial hypertension. The activity of channels that control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons is regulated by the mineralocorticoid aldosterone and glucocorticoid cortisol. Excessive secretion of cortisol causes arterial hypertension. Cortisol can bind to the mineralocorticoid receptor, NR3C2 and regulate the transcription of a number of genes. Activated NR3C2 translocates into the nucleus, where it interacts with the transcription complex, NCOA1/2/3/EP300/CREB1/CREBP/SP1, to induce the expression of downstream target genes.    Outcome effects: The NCOA1/2/3/EP300/CREB1/CREBP/SP1 complex activates the transcription of SGK1, ENaC subunits, ATP1B1, and AQP2. The non-catalytic ATP1B1 subunit binds the catalytic ATP1A1 and forms Na+/K+-transporting ATPase, which catalyzes the hydrolysis of ATP coupled with the Na+ and K+ exchange across the plasma membrane. SGK1 activates the alpha subunit (SCNN1A) of ENaC. Non-voltage-gated Na+ channels including amiloride-sensitive Na+ channels, epithelial Na+ channel (ENaC), and Na+ channel non-neuronal 1 (SCNN1), control Na+ transport across epithelia in nephron collecting ducts. This channel is a heterotrimeric complex consisting of alpha (SCNN1A), beta (SCNN1B) and gamma (SCNN1G) subunits. It has a major role in Na+ homeostasis throuth the Na+ reabsorption. FLNA, FURIN, and CTTN interact with ENaC subunits and are involved in cytoskeletal ENaC regulation. Mutations in SCNN1B and SCNN1G cause Liddle syndrome characterized by increased blood pressure. AQP2 forms a water-specific channel on plasma membranes of nephron collecting ducts that provides water to move down the osmotic gradient.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

