xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common monogenically inherited form of heart disease characterized by the thickening of the left ventricular wall, contractile dysfunction, and potentially fatal arrhythmias.    Signaling description: Mutations of sarcomeric and non-sarcomeric protein genes are the major causative factors in the development of HCM. These mutations lead to impaired sarcomere assembly and impaired cardiomyocyte contractility. Angiotensin II (AGT), endothelin-1 (EDN1), TNF, and IL6 are released in response to reduced contractility and mediate contractile adaptation through increased calcium (Ca2+) release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.    Outcome effects: The increase in intracellular Ca2+ activates calmodulin-calcineurin and CAMK- histone deacetylase pathways triggering the transcription of MEF2A, GATA4, and NFATC1, which induces hypertrophy through the expression of contractile proteins. They also induce the expression of fetal isoforms of proteins such as natriuretic peptide A (NPPA), natriuretic peptide B (NPPB), and skeletal muscle actin alpha 1 (ACTA1). AGT, EDN1, TNF, and IL6 activate NF-KB, STAT, JUN/FOS, and SMAD and thereby drive the increase of TIMP1, TIMP2, MMP2, and MMP9 transcription and remodeling of the extracellular matrix. MMPs are inhibited by TIMPs once they are activated, but the gelatinases (MMP2 and MMP9) can form complexes with TIMPs, when the enzymes are in the latent form. In addition, increased myocardial fibrosis occurs due to the increased production of TGFB1 by cardiomyocytes and increased fibroblast proliferation and function. In conclusion, the final morphological events resulting from these molecular mechanisms are prominent myocardial hypertrophy, myocardial disarray, and increased interstitial fibrosis, which all lead to diastolic dysfunction and heart failure     Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common monogenically inherited form of heart disease characterized by the thickening of the left ventricular wall, contractile dysfunction, and potentially fatal arrhythmias.    Signaling description: Mutations of sarcomeric and non-sarcomeric protein genes are the major causative factors in the development of HCM. These mutations lead to impaired sarcomere assembly and impaired cardiomyocyte contractility. Angiotensin II (AGT), endothelin-1 (EDN1), TNF, and IL6 are released in response to reduced contractility and mediate contractile adaptation through increased calcium (Ca2+) release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.    Outcome effects: The increase in intracellular Ca2+ activates calmodulin-calcineurin and CAMK- histone deacetylase pathways triggering the transcription of MEF2A, GATA4, and NFATC1, which induces hypertrophy through the expression of contractile proteins. They also induce the expression of fetal isoforms of proteins such as natriuretic peptide A (NPPA), natriuretic peptide B (NPPB), and skeletal muscle actin alpha 1 (ACTA1). AGT, EDN1, TNF, and IL6 activate NF-KB, STAT, JUN/FOS, and SMAD and thereby drive the increase of TIMP1, TIMP2, MMP2, and MMP9 transcription and remodeling of the extracellular matrix. MMPs are inhibited by TIMPs once they are activated, but the gelatinases (MMP2 and MMP9) can form complexes with TIMPs, when the enzymes are in the latent form. In addition, increased myocardial fibrosis occurs due to the increased production of TGFB1 by cardiomyocytes and increased fibroblast proliferation and function. In conclusion, the final morphological events resulting from these molecular mechanisms are prominent myocardial hypertrophy, myocardial disarray, and increased interstitial fibrosis, which all lead to diastolic dysfunction and heart failure     Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

