xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common monogenically inherited form of heart disease characterized by the thickening of the left ventricular wall, contractile dysfunction, and potentially fatal arrhythmias.    Signaling description: Mutations in more than 20 genes may induce HCM. Most of these genes encode for sarcomeric proteins, however non-sarcomeric protein genes may also be mutated. Beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7), cardiac myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3), and cardiac troponin T (TNNT2) are the three most common causal genes for HCM and collectively account for approximately 75% of all HCM cases. The mutated sarcomeric protein genes are cardiac troponin I (TNNI3), cardiac troponin C (TNNC1), cardiac alpha-actin (ACTC1), alpha-tropomyosin (TPM1), essential myosin light chain (MYL3), regulatory myosin light chain (MYL2), and alpha-myosin heavy chain (MYH6). In addition, HCM-causing mutations have been identified in several genes encoding Z disk proteins including telethonin (TCAP), myozenin 2 (MYOZ2), cardiac muscle LIM protein (CSRP3), and ankyrin repeat and KH domain-containing protein 1 (ANKRD1,) as well as genes coding for proteins that connect plasma membrane and the sarcomere such as vinculin (VCL), junctophilin 2 (JPH2), and caveolin 3 (CAV3). Titin (TTN) spreading from Z- to M-disk may also be mutated. Mutations in both sarcomeric and non-sarcomeric proteins lead to impaired sarcomere assembly and impaired myofibrillar contractile function. These events lead to compensatory hypertrophy. Calcium (Ca2+) also has an important role in the pathogenesis of HCM. Normally, during action potential, Ca2+ enters the cardiomyocyte through the voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels (CACNA1C), which are responsible for Ca2+ influx and to a lesser extent via the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1), thereby triggering Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release through cardiac ryanodine receptor (RYR2) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). This Ca2+ binds TNNC1 and activates the myofilaments during systole leading to contraction. During diastole, Ca2+ is taken back up into the SR by cardiac muscle Ca2+ transporting ATPase (ATP2A2) and is pumped out of the cells by SLC8A1, which results in relaxation. The increase in intracellular Ca2+ in HCM is a result of two distinct mechanisms. The mutated sarcomeric proteins use more adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to produce the same force of contraction. Therefore, it compromises Ca2+ reuptake by the ATP2A2 transporter into the SR and more Ca2+ becomes available inside the cells. Conversely, the activation of a number of different receptor classes leads to an increase in intracellular Ca2+. Angiotensin II (AGT), endothelin-1 (EDN1), TNF, and IL6 are released in response to reduced contractility and mediate contractile adaptation through increased Ca2+ release from the SR.    Outcome effects: The increase in intracellular Ca2+ activates calmodulin-calcineurin and CAMK-histone deacetylase pathways triggering the transcription of MEF2A, GATA4, and NFATC1, which induces hypertrophy through the expression of contractile proteins. They also induce the expression of fetal isoforms of proteins such as natriuretic peptide A (NPPA), natriuretic peptide B (NPPB), and skeletal muscle actin alpha 1 (ACTA1). AGT, EDN1, TNF, and IL6 activate NF-kB, STAT, JUN/FOS, and SMAD and thereby drive the increase of TIMP1, TIMP2, MMP2, and MMP9 transcription and remodeling of the extracellular matrix. MMPs are inhibited by TIMPs once they are activated but the gelatinases (MMP2 and MMP9) can form complexes with TIMPs when the enzymes are in the latent form. In addition, increased myocardial fibrosis occurs due to the increased production of TGFB1 by cardiomyocytes and increased fibroblast proliferation and function. In conclusion, the final morphological events are prominent myocardial hypertrophy, myocardial disarray, and increased interstitial fibrosis which all result in diastolic dysfunction and heart failure."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common monogenically inherited form of heart disease characterized by the thickening of the left ventricular wall, contractile dysfunction, and potentially fatal arrhythmias.    Signaling description: Mutations in more than 20 genes may induce HCM. Most of these genes encode for sarcomeric proteins, however non-sarcomeric protein genes may also be mutated. Beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7), cardiac myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3), and cardiac troponin T (TNNT2) are the three most common causal genes for HCM and collectively account for approximately 75% of all HCM cases. The mutated sarcomeric protein genes are cardiac troponin I (TNNI3), cardiac troponin C (TNNC1), cardiac alpha-actin (ACTC1), alpha-tropomyosin (TPM1), essential myosin light chain (MYL3), regulatory myosin light chain (MYL2), and alpha-myosin heavy chain (MYH6). In addition, HCM-causing mutations have been identified in several genes encoding Z disk proteins including telethonin (TCAP), myozenin 2 (MYOZ2), cardiac muscle LIM protein (CSRP3), and ankyrin repeat and KH domain-containing protein 1 (ANKRD1,) as well as genes coding for proteins that connect plasma membrane and the sarcomere such as vinculin (VCL), junctophilin 2 (JPH2), and caveolin 3 (CAV3). Titin (TTN) spreading from Z- to M-disk may also be mutated. Mutations in both sarcomeric and non-sarcomeric proteins lead to impaired sarcomere assembly and impaired myofibrillar contractile function. These events lead to compensatory hypertrophy. Calcium (Ca2+) also has an important role in the pathogenesis of HCM. Normally, during action potential, Ca2+ enters the cardiomyocyte through the voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels (CACNA1C), which are responsible for Ca2+ influx and to a lesser extent via the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1), thereby triggering Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release through cardiac ryanodine receptor (RYR2) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). This Ca2+ binds TNNC1 and activates the myofilaments during systole leading to contraction. During diastole, Ca2+ is taken back up into the SR by cardiac muscle Ca2+ transporting ATPase (ATP2A2) and is pumped out of the cells by SLC8A1, which results in relaxation. The increase in intracellular Ca2+ in HCM is a result of two distinct mechanisms. The mutated sarcomeric proteins use more adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to produce the same force of contraction. Therefore, it compromises Ca2+ reuptake by the ATP2A2 transporter into the SR and more Ca2+ becomes available inside the cells. Conversely, the activation of a number of different receptor classes leads to an increase in intracellular Ca2+. Angiotensin II (AGT), endothelin-1 (EDN1), TNF, and IL6 are released in response to reduced contractility and mediate contractile adaptation through increased Ca2+ release from the SR.    Outcome effects: The increase in intracellular Ca2+ activates calmodulin-calcineurin and CAMK-histone deacetylase pathways triggering the transcription of MEF2A, GATA4, and NFATC1, which induces hypertrophy through the expression of contractile proteins. They also induce the expression of fetal isoforms of proteins such as natriuretic peptide A (NPPA), natriuretic peptide B (NPPB), and skeletal muscle actin alpha 1 (ACTA1). AGT, EDN1, TNF, and IL6 activate NF-kB, STAT, JUN/FOS, and SMAD and thereby drive the increase of TIMP1, TIMP2, MMP2, and MMP9 transcription and remodeling of the extracellular matrix. MMPs are inhibited by TIMPs once they are activated but the gelatinases (MMP2 and MMP9) can form complexes with TIMPs when the enzymes are in the latent form. In addition, increased myocardial fibrosis occurs due to the increased production of TGFB1 by cardiomyocytes and increased fibroblast proliferation and function. In conclusion, the final morphological events are prominent myocardial hypertrophy, myocardial disarray, and increased interstitial fibrosis which all result in diastolic dysfunction and heart failure." EXACT []

