xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common monogenically inherited form of heart disease characterized by the thickening of the left ventricular wall, contractile dysfunction, and potentially fatal arrhythmias. Mutations in more than 20 genes may induce hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM). Most of these genes encode sarcomeric proteins; however genes of non-sarcomeric proteins may also be mutated.    Signaling description: Beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7), cardiac myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3), and cardiac troponin T (TNNT2) are the three most common causal genes for HCM and collectively account for approximately 75% of all HCM cases. The mutated sarcomeric protein genes are cardiac troponin I (TNNI3), cardiac troponin C (TNNC1), cardiac alpha-actin (ACTC1), alpha-tropomyosin (TPM1), essential myosin light chain (MYL3), regulatory myosin light chain (MYL2), and alpha-myosin heavy chain (MYH6). In addition, HCM-causing mutations have been identified in several genes encoding Z disk proteins including telethonin (TCAP), myozenin 2 (MYOZ2), cardiac muscle LIM protein (CSRP3), and ankyrin repeat and KH domain-containing protein 1 (ANKRD1) as well as in proteins connecting the plasma membrane and sarcomere such as vinculin (VCL), junctophilin 2 (JPH2), and caveolin 3 (CAV3). Titin (TTN) spreading from Z- to M-disk may also be mutated. Calcium (Ca2+) also has an important role in the pathogenesis of HCM. Normally during action potential, Ca2+ enters the cardiomyocyte through the voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels (CACNA1C) which are responsible for Ca2+ influx and to a lesser extent via the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1), thereby triggering Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release through cardiac ryanodine receptor (RYR2) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). This Ca2+ binds TNNC1 and activates the myofilaments during systole leading to contraction.    Outcome effects: During diastole, Ca2+ is taken back up into the SR by cardiac muscle Ca2+ transporting ATPase (ATP2A2) and is pumped out of the cells by SLC8A1 which results in relaxation. The increase in intracellular Ca2+ in HCM is the result of two different mechanisms. The mutated sarcomeric proteins use more adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to produce the same force of contraction. Therefore, it compromises Ca2+ reuptake by the ATP2A2 transporter into the SR and more Ca2+ becomes available inside the cells. Finally, mutations in sarcomeric and non-sarcomeric proteins lead to impaired sarcomere assembly and impaired myofibrillar contractile function. This, in addition to intracellular Ca2+ overload, leads to compensatory cardiomyocyte hypertrophy.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common monogenically inherited form of heart disease characterized by the thickening of the left ventricular wall, contractile dysfunction, and potentially fatal arrhythmias. Mutations in more than 20 genes may induce hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM). Most of these genes encode sarcomeric proteins; however genes of non-sarcomeric proteins may also be mutated.    Signaling description: Beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7), cardiac myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3), and cardiac troponin T (TNNT2) are the three most common causal genes for HCM and collectively account for approximately 75% of all HCM cases. The mutated sarcomeric protein genes are cardiac troponin I (TNNI3), cardiac troponin C (TNNC1), cardiac alpha-actin (ACTC1), alpha-tropomyosin (TPM1), essential myosin light chain (MYL3), regulatory myosin light chain (MYL2), and alpha-myosin heavy chain (MYH6). In addition, HCM-causing mutations have been identified in several genes encoding Z disk proteins including telethonin (TCAP), myozenin 2 (MYOZ2), cardiac muscle LIM protein (CSRP3), and ankyrin repeat and KH domain-containing protein 1 (ANKRD1) as well as in proteins connecting the plasma membrane and sarcomere such as vinculin (VCL), junctophilin 2 (JPH2), and caveolin 3 (CAV3). Titin (TTN) spreading from Z- to M-disk may also be mutated. Calcium (Ca2+) also has an important role in the pathogenesis of HCM. Normally during action potential, Ca2+ enters the cardiomyocyte through the voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels (CACNA1C) which are responsible for Ca2+ influx and to a lesser extent via the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1), thereby triggering Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release through cardiac ryanodine receptor (RYR2) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). This Ca2+ binds TNNC1 and activates the myofilaments during systole leading to contraction.    Outcome effects: During diastole, Ca2+ is taken back up into the SR by cardiac muscle Ca2+ transporting ATPase (ATP2A2) and is pumped out of the cells by SLC8A1 which results in relaxation. The increase in intracellular Ca2+ in HCM is the result of two different mechanisms. The mutated sarcomeric proteins use more adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to produce the same force of contraction. Therefore, it compromises Ca2+ reuptake by the ATP2A2 transporter into the SR and more Ca2+ becomes available inside the cells. Finally, mutations in sarcomeric and non-sarcomeric proteins lead to impaired sarcomere assembly and impaired myofibrillar contractile function. This, in addition to intracellular Ca2+ overload, leads to compensatory cardiomyocyte hypertrophy.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

