xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myocardial ischemia is a restriction of the blood supply to myocardium, which occurs due to a coronary artery obstruction and causes oxygen shortage and myocardial damage.    Signaling description: Cardiomyocytes are very sensitive to ischemic injury. During I/R in humans, 5-30% of cardiac myocytes in the area at risk undergo apoptosis within 16 hours. ROS are critical mediators of I/R injury, which is also associated with intracellular Ca2+ overload in cardiomyocytes. During ischemia, intracellular Ca2+ is elevated with further increase during reperfusion. ROS also participates in lipid hyperoxidation, DNA degradation, and necrotic cell death.    Outcome effects: Ischaemic/hypoxic heart disease induces rapid changes in all membrane systems and causes reversible or irreversible injury including necrotic and apoptotic cell death. Mitochondria play an important role in transmitting and amplifying the death signals. The most important mitochondrial events during apoptosis are structural and functional remodeling of this organelle and subsequent release of apoptogenic proteins into the cytosol and nucleus, such as ENDOG, which activates DNA degradation in the nucleus. In addition, proteins associated with mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP) including VDAC1, SLC25A4, and PPID are also involved in these processes. Channel activity is regulated by interactions of BAX and BAK1 with the outer mitochondrial membrane. The mPTPs are regulated by BCL2 proteins and are opened by Ca2+ ionophores and oxidative stress. The mPTPs open under conditions of ischemia reperfusion when matrix Ca2+ levels increase above a certain threshold. Lastly, HMGB1 is a nuclear protein, which acts as a chromatin-binding factor and also functions as an extracellular signaling molecule, which is released into the blood from cardiomyocytes and associated with inflammation and cardiomyocyte necrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myocardial ischemia is a restriction of the blood supply to myocardium, which occurs due to a coronary artery obstruction and causes oxygen shortage and myocardial damage.    Signaling description: Cardiomyocytes are very sensitive to ischemic injury. During I/R in humans, 5-30% of cardiac myocytes in the area at risk undergo apoptosis within 16 hours. ROS are critical mediators of I/R injury, which is also associated with intracellular Ca2+ overload in cardiomyocytes. During ischemia, intracellular Ca2+ is elevated with further increase during reperfusion. ROS also participates in lipid hyperoxidation, DNA degradation, and necrotic cell death.    Outcome effects: Ischaemic/hypoxic heart disease induces rapid changes in all membrane systems and causes reversible or irreversible injury including necrotic and apoptotic cell death. Mitochondria play an important role in transmitting and amplifying the death signals. The most important mitochondrial events during apoptosis are structural and functional remodeling of this organelle and subsequent release of apoptogenic proteins into the cytosol and nucleus, such as ENDOG, which activates DNA degradation in the nucleus. In addition, proteins associated with mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP) including VDAC1, SLC25A4, and PPID are also involved in these processes. Channel activity is regulated by interactions of BAX and BAK1 with the outer mitochondrial membrane. The mPTPs are regulated by BCL2 proteins and are opened by Ca2+ ionophores and oxidative stress. The mPTPs open under conditions of ischemia reperfusion when matrix Ca2+ levels increase above a certain threshold. Lastly, HMGB1 is a nuclear protein, which acts as a chromatin-binding factor and also functions as an extracellular signaling molecule, which is released into the blood from cardiomyocytes and associated with inflammation and cardiomyocyte necrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

