xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myocardial ischemia is a restriction of the blood supply to myocardium, which occurs due to a coronary artery obstruction and causes oxygen shortage and myocardial damage.    Signaling description: The lack of oxygen leads to the degradation of pyruvic acid to lactic acid (anaerobic glycolysis) and bloackage of tricarboxylic acid cycle (Krebs cycle) and fatty acid oxidation (beta-oxidation of fatty acids). During the initial minutes of ischemia, anaerobic glycolysis and H+ release produce a progressive decline of the intracellular pH and ATP. These initial changes are associated with the activation of the Na+/H+-exchanger (SLC9A6) and a sustained rise of intracellular Na+, which requires Na+ exchange through the activation of the Na+/K+-ATPase (ATP1A1) and Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1). During ischemia, ATP is progressively depleted and energy-dependent ion pumps such as the Na+/K+-ATPase cannot function.    Outcome effects: Cytosolic Na+ overload provides a large driving force for Ca2+ influx through the reverse mode of Na+/Ca2+ exchanger. In addition, Ca2+ entry through CACNs results in substantial Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release via RYR2 in sarcoplasmic reticulum. Ca2+ is a highly versatile intracellular signal that regulates many different cellular processes and cytosolic Ca2+ overload is a key component of cardiomyocyte death. The increase of Ca2+ leads to mitochondrial Ca2+ accumulation, which may result in the opening of mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP), and the later participates in cardiomyocyte apoptosis and necrosis. Low ATP and altered ion homeostasis result in the plasma membrane rupture and cell death. Cytosolic Ca2+ damages the organelles that regulate the contractile cycle, which subsequently results in a defective functioning of the myocardial contractile apparatus.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myocardial ischemia is a restriction of the blood supply to myocardium, which occurs due to a coronary artery obstruction and causes oxygen shortage and myocardial damage.    Signaling description: The lack of oxygen leads to the degradation of pyruvic acid to lactic acid (anaerobic glycolysis) and bloackage of tricarboxylic acid cycle (Krebs cycle) and fatty acid oxidation (beta-oxidation of fatty acids). During the initial minutes of ischemia, anaerobic glycolysis and H+ release produce a progressive decline of the intracellular pH and ATP. These initial changes are associated with the activation of the Na+/H+-exchanger (SLC9A6) and a sustained rise of intracellular Na+, which requires Na+ exchange through the activation of the Na+/K+-ATPase (ATP1A1) and Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (SLC8A1). During ischemia, ATP is progressively depleted and energy-dependent ion pumps such as the Na+/K+-ATPase cannot function.    Outcome effects: Cytosolic Na+ overload provides a large driving force for Ca2+ influx through the reverse mode of Na+/Ca2+ exchanger. In addition, Ca2+ entry through CACNs results in substantial Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release via RYR2 in sarcoplasmic reticulum. Ca2+ is a highly versatile intracellular signal that regulates many different cellular processes and cytosolic Ca2+ overload is a key component of cardiomyocyte death. The increase of Ca2+ leads to mitochondrial Ca2+ accumulation, which may result in the opening of mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP), and the later participates in cardiomyocyte apoptosis and necrosis. Low ATP and altered ion homeostasis result in the plasma membrane rupture and cell death. Cytosolic Ca2+ damages the organelles that regulate the contractile cycle, which subsequently results in a defective functioning of the myocardial contractile apparatus.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

