xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myocardial ischemia is a restriction of the blood supply to myocardium, which occurs due to a coronary artery obstruction and causes oxygen shortage and myocardial damage. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) is a hormone system that regulates system blood pressure and electrolyte homeostasis. RAAS is an important regulatory mechanism involved in heart failure.    Signaling description: Chronic activation of RAAS is a major contributing factor to the pathogenesis and progression of cardiovascular disease, cardiac vasoconstriction, hypertrophy, and fibrosis. The classic RAAS is a cascade comprised of several different components. When blood pressure is low, the juxtaglomerular apparatus in the kidney secretes renin (REN), which cleaves the inactive angiotensinogen (AGT), a high molecular weight protein released from the liver, converting it into angiotensin I. Further, angiotensin I is converted to angiotensin II by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). The angiotensin II receptors are located in arterioles, the adrenal gland, and the circumventricular regions of the brain.    Outcome effects: Angiotensin II regulates the constriction of blood vessels resulting in increased blood pressure, stimulation of aldosterone synthesis and release, and induction of thirst and salt appetite. Angiotensin enhances the release of NPPA and NPPB (natriuretic peptides) implicated in the control of extracellular fluid volume and electrolyte homeostasis. Angiotensin also stimulates the secretion of the hormone aldosterone from the adrenal cortex. CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone, which is then hydrolyzed to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2. Then, CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. Aldosterone acts on the tubules of the kidneys causing them to reabsorb more sodium (Na+) and water from urine. Potassium (K+) is secreted into the tubules in exchange for Na+, which is excreted. This increases the volume of fluid in the body, which additionally increases the blood pressure. It has been recently discovered that RAAS components are expressed in many tissues such as the heart, kidney, placenta, testis, eye, and lymphocytes. These local tissue RAAS components are believed to contribute to tissue damage. In the heart, chymase (CMA1) and cathepsin G (CTSG) convert angiotensin I to angiotensin II.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myocardial ischemia is a restriction of the blood supply to myocardium, which occurs due to a coronary artery obstruction and causes oxygen shortage and myocardial damage. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) is a hormone system that regulates system blood pressure and electrolyte homeostasis. RAAS is an important regulatory mechanism involved in heart failure.    Signaling description: Chronic activation of RAAS is a major contributing factor to the pathogenesis and progression of cardiovascular disease, cardiac vasoconstriction, hypertrophy, and fibrosis. The classic RAAS is a cascade comprised of several different components. When blood pressure is low, the juxtaglomerular apparatus in the kidney secretes renin (REN), which cleaves the inactive angiotensinogen (AGT), a high molecular weight protein released from the liver, converting it into angiotensin I. Further, angiotensin I is converted to angiotensin II by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). The angiotensin II receptors are located in arterioles, the adrenal gland, and the circumventricular regions of the brain.    Outcome effects: Angiotensin II regulates the constriction of blood vessels resulting in increased blood pressure, stimulation of aldosterone synthesis and release, and induction of thirst and salt appetite. Angiotensin enhances the release of NPPA and NPPB (natriuretic peptides) implicated in the control of extracellular fluid volume and electrolyte homeostasis. Angiotensin also stimulates the secretion of the hormone aldosterone from the adrenal cortex. CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone, which is then hydrolyzed to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2. Then, CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. Aldosterone acts on the tubules of the kidneys causing them to reabsorb more sodium (Na+) and water from urine. Potassium (K+) is secreted into the tubules in exchange for Na+, which is excreted. This increases the volume of fluid in the body, which additionally increases the blood pressure. It has been recently discovered that RAAS components are expressed in many tissues such as the heart, kidney, placenta, testis, eye, and lymphocytes. These local tissue RAAS components are believed to contribute to tissue damage. In the heart, chymase (CMA1) and cathepsin G (CTSG) convert angiotensin I to angiotensin II.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

