xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. The pathway illustrates the processes that occur in endothelial cells during the first stages of vasospasm (1-7 days). The products of erythrocyte and thrombocyte decay, mainly Ca2+, histamine, and prostaglandins, initiate the events whih lead to the activation of protein kinase C (PKC), either through an increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels or directly via diacylglycerol. In addition, G-proteins activate adenylate cyclase (ADCY) resulting in cAMP synthesis and the activation of protein kinase A (PKA). G-proteins also activate PIP3 synthesis, which in turn result in the activation of AKT1. Furthermore, PKC and PKA phosphorylate and in turn, activate AKT1. In addition, endothelial nitric oxide synthase (NOS3) may be activated through its phosphorylation by several kinases including PKC, PKA, and AKT1. Activated calmodulin is required for the activation of both NOS3 and inducible NO synthase (NOS2). Their synthesis is triggered through an NF-kB-dependent pathway via the activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: The extensive synthesis of nitric oxide (NO) is needed to provide vasodilation at the site of injury. However, the overproduction of NO and the presence of free radicals in adjacent tissues result in the formation of peroxynitrite, which triggers a number of pathological processes such as vasoconstriction, oxidative stress, disruption of Na+ homeostasis and export, intracellular Ca2+ overload, apoptosis, and necrotic cell death. Additionally, the presence of peroxynitrite with free radicals and free oxyhemoglobin lead to lipid peroxidation in membranes, which also promotes cell death. The role of bilirubin oxidation products currently remains unclear; however, it has been shown that these substances are necessary for vasospasm. The activation of DDAH2 by NO and arginine occurs during vasospasm. This enzyme participates in signaling pathways of oxidative stress and apoptosis. Protein arginine methyltransferase 3 (PRMT3) is another enzyme that is activated by NO and displays altered activity in vasospasm. It activates mRNA processing and arrests translation, which disrupts the normal functioning of endothelial cells. Thus, endothelial cell death occurs in early vasospasm, which upsets the normal function of blood vessels and leads to further pathological processes.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. The pathway illustrates the processes that occur in endothelial cells during the first stages of vasospasm (1-7 days). The products of erythrocyte and thrombocyte decay, mainly Ca2+, histamine, and prostaglandins, initiate the events whih lead to the activation of protein kinase C (PKC), either through an increase of intracellular Ca2+ levels or directly via diacylglycerol. In addition, G-proteins activate adenylate cyclase (ADCY) resulting in cAMP synthesis and the activation of protein kinase A (PKA). G-proteins also activate PIP3 synthesis, which in turn result in the activation of AKT1. Furthermore, PKC and PKA phosphorylate and in turn, activate AKT1. In addition, endothelial nitric oxide synthase (NOS3) may be activated through its phosphorylation by several kinases including PKC, PKA, and AKT1. Activated calmodulin is required for the activation of both NOS3 and inducible NO synthase (NOS2). Their synthesis is triggered through an NF-kB-dependent pathway via the activation of AKT1.    Outcome effects: The extensive synthesis of nitric oxide (NO) is needed to provide vasodilation at the site of injury. However, the overproduction of NO and the presence of free radicals in adjacent tissues result in the formation of peroxynitrite, which triggers a number of pathological processes such as vasoconstriction, oxidative stress, disruption of Na+ homeostasis and export, intracellular Ca2+ overload, apoptosis, and necrotic cell death. Additionally, the presence of peroxynitrite with free radicals and free oxyhemoglobin lead to lipid peroxidation in membranes, which also promotes cell death. The role of bilirubin oxidation products currently remains unclear; however, it has been shown that these substances are necessary for vasospasm. The activation of DDAH2 by NO and arginine occurs during vasospasm. This enzyme participates in signaling pathways of oxidative stress and apoptosis. Protein arginine methyltransferase 3 (PRMT3) is another enzyme that is activated by NO and displays altered activity in vasospasm. It activates mRNA processing and arrests translation, which disrupts the normal functioning of endothelial cells. Thus, endothelial cell death occurs in early vasospasm, which upsets the normal function of blood vessels and leads to further pathological processes.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red." EXACT []

