xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. The chronic vasoconstriction, which lasts several days following SAH results in energy and oxygen (O2) starvation.    Outcome effects: The O2 starvation leads to an ATP deficiency, which in turn inhibits ion pumps, primarily the Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase. The resulting misbalance between K+ and Na+ ions results in uncontrolled neuronal necrosis. In addition, there are other vital processes activated by O2 starvation in cells such as the appearance of H2O2 and a decrease of ATP synthesis. The products of neuronal necrosis lead to vasoconstriction of adjacent blood vessels, which expands the injury. The death of neurons occurs adjacent to SAH lesions in the late stages of vasospasm. The extent of necrosis is directly correlated to the severity of SAH.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. The chronic vasoconstriction, which lasts several days following SAH results in energy and oxygen (O2) starvation.    Outcome effects: The O2 starvation leads to an ATP deficiency, which in turn inhibits ion pumps, primarily the Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase. The resulting misbalance between K+ and Na+ ions results in uncontrolled neuronal necrosis. In addition, there are other vital processes activated by O2 starvation in cells such as the appearance of H2O2 and a decrease of ATP synthesis. The products of neuronal necrosis lead to vasoconstriction of adjacent blood vessels, which expands the injury. The death of neurons occurs adjacent to SAH lesions in the late stages of vasospasm. The extent of necrosis is directly correlated to the severity of SAH.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased levels are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased levels are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

