xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. Increased calcium (Ca2+), noradrenaline, serotonin, thromboxane A2 (TXA2), endothelin 1 and 3 (EDN1 and EDN3), and leukotriene B (LTB4) activate vascular contraction. The receptors for these mediators are G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs). Once they are activated, their action leads to an increase in intracellular Ca2+ via the IP3 pathway and the activation of protein kinase C (PKC). This activates calmodulin and Ras proteins.    Outcome effects: Ras proteins activate the pathway, which results in the CALD1 phosphorylation, required for actin stabilization in the smooth muscle. Calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase (MYLK), resulting in smooth muscle contraction. G-proteins are also capable of inhibiting protein kinase A (PKA) (through cAMP), which may suppress the activity of MYLK and RHOA. The activation of RHOA via the guanine nucleotide exchange factors RASGRF1 and RASGRP1, leads to the inhibition of MYLK and results in the arrest of smooth muscle relaxation. Thus, increased Ca2+, noradrenaline, serotonin, TXA2, EDN1, EDN3, and LTB4 lead to vasoconstriction following SAH. Vasoconstriction results in a decreased cellular nutrition along with dysfunction and death of endothelial cells. The depletion of ATP reduces the activity of myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP), providing further vasoconstriction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. Increased calcium (Ca2+), noradrenaline, serotonin, thromboxane A2 (TXA2), endothelin 1 and 3 (EDN1 and EDN3), and leukotriene B (LTB4) activate vascular contraction. The receptors for these mediators are G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs). Once they are activated, their action leads to an increase in intracellular Ca2+ via the IP3 pathway and the activation of protein kinase C (PKC). This activates calmodulin and Ras proteins.    Outcome effects: Ras proteins activate the pathway, which results in the CALD1 phosphorylation, required for actin stabilization in the smooth muscle. Calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase (MYLK), resulting in smooth muscle contraction. G-proteins are also capable of inhibiting protein kinase A (PKA) (through cAMP), which may suppress the activity of MYLK and RHOA. The activation of RHOA via the guanine nucleotide exchange factors RASGRF1 and RASGRP1, leads to the inhibition of MYLK and results in the arrest of smooth muscle relaxation. Thus, increased Ca2+, noradrenaline, serotonin, TXA2, EDN1, EDN3, and LTB4 lead to vasoconstriction following SAH. Vasoconstriction results in a decreased cellular nutrition along with dysfunction and death of endothelial cells. The depletion of ATP reduces the activity of myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP), providing further vasoconstriction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

