xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. Free hemoglobin and oxyhemoglobin bind to haptoglobin (HP) and the complex is absorbed and degraded by macrophages. In addition, free hemoglobin activates leukocyte adhesion. Free oxyhemoglobin is involved in a number of vital cell processes such as the formation of free radicals, lipid peroxidation, arrest of vasodilation, activation of vasoconstriction, and apoptotic cell death. Mutations that decrease the activity of HP lead to reduced amounts of hemoglobin absorbed by macrophages. This escalates the pathological effects of free hemoglobin and oxyhemoglobin, which increases the probability of a lethal outcome.    Outcome effects: SAH and the formation of hematomas initiate several molecular and cellular processes that lead to cerebral vasospasm. The main pathological processes such as the formation of free radicals and activation of molecular pathways that lead to irreversible vascular spasm, which occurs within the first 10 minutes - 24 hours. Medical assistance is rarely sought at this point, which leads to a high death rate in cerebral vasospasm. According to various estimates, the mortality in progressive cerebral vasospasm after SAH is as high as 20-60% in the first 14 days.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intracranial vasospasm is a severe complication of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH). SAH may occur spontaneously, however it generally results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm or head injury. Neuronal death is the cause of high mortality and disability rate in patients with vasospasm.    Signaling description: Vasospasm can be triggered by products of cellular decay such as serotonin, histamine, norepinephrine, prostaglandins, thromboxanes, endothelin, hemoglobin, nitric oxide, and free radicals. The major pathological processes involved in the development of vasospasm are vasoconstriction, endothelial dysfunction, and neuronal death. Free hemoglobin and oxyhemoglobin bind to haptoglobin (HP) and the complex is absorbed and degraded by macrophages. In addition, free hemoglobin activates leukocyte adhesion. Free oxyhemoglobin is involved in a number of vital cell processes such as the formation of free radicals, lipid peroxidation, arrest of vasodilation, activation of vasoconstriction, and apoptotic cell death. Mutations that decrease the activity of HP lead to reduced amounts of hemoglobin absorbed by macrophages. This escalates the pathological effects of free hemoglobin and oxyhemoglobin, which increases the probability of a lethal outcome.    Outcome effects: SAH and the formation of hematomas initiate several molecular and cellular processes that lead to cerebral vasospasm. The main pathological processes such as the formation of free radicals and activation of molecular pathways that lead to irreversible vascular spasm, which occurs within the first 10 minutes - 24 hours. Medical assistance is rarely sought at this point, which leads to a high death rate in cerebral vasospasm. According to various estimates, the mortality in progressive cerebral vasospasm after SAH is as high as 20-60% in the first 14 days.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or acitivity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

