xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myocarditis is defined as inflammation of the heart muscle. Many viruses have been implicated as causative agents in the development of myocarditis. These most commonly include adenoviruses and enteroviruses, such as the coxsackieviruses.    Signaling description: Recently, parvovirus B19 has been detected in a significant percentage of patients diagnosed with myocarditis and dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM). At the same time, a growing body of data indicates that parvovirus is present in many patients who do not develop myocarditis. In epithelial cells, coxsackievirus initially attaches to DAF on the apical surface of the cell. This facilitates the interaction of the virus with CAR localized at the tight junction of epithelial cells, an area not normally accessible to viral particles.    Outcome effects: The binding of coxsackievirus to DAF triggers actin rearrangement through the AbI kinase activation and permits movement of virus particle into the tight junction. CAR is a transmembrane protein with 2 extracellular immunoglobulin domains. The extracellular region of CAR is thought to bind to another CAR molecule on an adjacent cell as an antiparallel homodimer."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myocarditis is defined as inflammation of the heart muscle. Many viruses have been implicated as causative agents in the development of myocarditis. These most commonly include adenoviruses and enteroviruses, such as the coxsackieviruses.    Signaling description: Recently, parvovirus B19 has been detected in a significant percentage of patients diagnosed with myocarditis and dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM). At the same time, a growing body of data indicates that parvovirus is present in many patients who do not develop myocarditis. In epithelial cells, coxsackievirus initially attaches to DAF on the apical surface of the cell. This facilitates the interaction of the virus with CAR localized at the tight junction of epithelial cells, an area not normally accessible to viral particles.    Outcome effects: The binding of coxsackievirus to DAF triggers actin rearrangement through the AbI kinase activation and permits movement of virus particle into the tight junction. CAR is a transmembrane protein with 2 extracellular immunoglobulin domains. The extracellular region of CAR is thought to bind to another CAR molecule on an adjacent cell as an antiparallel homodimer." EXACT []

