xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss and is due to the shortening or absence of the anagen phase of hair growth.    Signaling description: All cellular layers of hair develop from the niche of dermal papilla (DP) cells, which is the lower part of the hair follicle adjacent to the dermis that provides the induction of hair keratinocyte differentiation. The signaling which follows at the DP and hair follicle interface in a balding individual with androgenetic alopecia (AGA) results in premature termination of anagen associated with premature entry into catagen. The anagen-to-catagen transition is characterized by apoptotic cell death in hair bulb epithelial cells, including outer root sheath (ORS) cells. Androgen receptor (AR)-overexpressing human DP cells from AGA express androgen-inducible TGFB1, DKK1, and IL6 which suppress hair keratinocyte growth derived from DP cells. DKK1 is an inhibitor of canonical WNT/CTNNB1 signaling and hair follicle growth. DKK1 induces the proapoptotic protein, BAX which results in the apoptosis of ORS keratinocytes. An increased expression of cytokines, such as TGFB1 promotes apoptosis.    Outcome effects: IL6 is expressed in the DP of human hair follicles, whereas IL6R and IL6ST are expressed in the inner and ORS of human hair follicles. IL6 promotes anagen-to-catagen transition in vivo. PTGDS is elevated at the mRNA and protein levels in bald scalp compared with haired scalp of men with AGA. Prostaglandin D2 (PGD2) is similarly elevated in the bald scalp. PGD2 is a potent vessel contractor and may contribute to AGA development through the deterioration of blood supply of the hair follicle. PGD2 inhibits hair follicle regeneration through PTGDR2 which suggests that the inhibition of PGD2 production or PTGDR2 signaling may promote skin regeneration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss and is due to the shortening or absence of the anagen phase of hair growth.    Signaling description: All cellular layers of hair develop from the niche of dermal papilla (DP) cells, which is the lower part of the hair follicle adjacent to the dermis that provides the induction of hair keratinocyte differentiation. The signaling which follows at the DP and hair follicle interface in a balding individual with androgenetic alopecia (AGA) results in premature termination of anagen associated with premature entry into catagen. The anagen-to-catagen transition is characterized by apoptotic cell death in hair bulb epithelial cells, including outer root sheath (ORS) cells. Androgen receptor (AR)-overexpressing human DP cells from AGA express androgen-inducible TGFB1, DKK1, and IL6 which suppress hair keratinocyte growth derived from DP cells. DKK1 is an inhibitor of canonical WNT/CTNNB1 signaling and hair follicle growth. DKK1 induces the proapoptotic protein, BAX which results in the apoptosis of ORS keratinocytes. An increased expression of cytokines, such as TGFB1 promotes apoptosis.    Outcome effects: IL6 is expressed in the DP of human hair follicles, whereas IL6R and IL6ST are expressed in the inner and ORS of human hair follicles. IL6 promotes anagen-to-catagen transition in vivo. PTGDS is elevated at the mRNA and protein levels in bald scalp compared with haired scalp of men with AGA. Prostaglandin D2 (PGD2) is similarly elevated in the bald scalp. PGD2 is a potent vessel contractor and may contribute to AGA development through the deterioration of blood supply of the hair follicle. PGD2 inhibits hair follicle regeneration through PTGDR2 which suggests that the inhibition of PGD2 production or PTGDR2 signaling may promote skin regeneration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

