xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss and is due to the shortening or absence of the anagen phase of hair growth.    Signaling description: The three stages of hair growth include: active growth (anagen phase), hair follicle degeneration (catagen phase), and quiescence (telogen phase). At certain point, telogen phase ends and new hair growth cycle begins due to the activity of stem and stem-like cells. In androgenetic alopecia (AGA) patients, the catagen phase is initiated prematurely while the telogen phase is lengthened. Hair follicle miniaturization occurs and hair type changes from a thick and pigmented shaft to thin vellus hair. In the following cycle, anagen phase onset is often absent. The transition from telogen phase to anagen phase occurs due to stem and stem-like cells including the pool of pluripotent cells in the outer root sheath (bulge region) and progenitor cells adjacent to dermal papilla (DP) or secondary germ. Secondary germ cells arise from the bulge at the end of catagen. The activation of anagen occurs in two stages. First, secondary germ cells are activated; then they stimulate activation of multipotent keratinocyte progenitor cells (bulge region), which in turn are the main source for hair follicle matrix cell mass that further form all cellular layers of hair. Defects in the conversion of hair follicle stem cells to progenitor cells play a role in the pathogenesis of AGA. In bald areas in patients with AGA, there is a loss of progenitor cells such as CD200hiItga6hi and CD34hi cells, but not stem cells. A decrease in progenitor cell population by DP, possibly due to a lack of replenishment from bulge cells, could potentially result in diminished numbers of bulb matrix cells in the DP of the hair follicle and as a result the new (lower) hair follicle that forms at anagen onset is smaller than its predecessor in AGA. Telogen is the resting phase for the cells and major signaling pathways within. WNT signaling pathway which leads to cell proliferation is inactive, as well as BMP signaling pathway that leads to cell differentiation. Quiescence is provided by reciprocal inhibiting feedback loops in these signaling pathways where WNT suppresses BMP and BMP suppresses WNT. BMP2 and BMP4 gene expression, as well as the expression of DKK1 and SFRP4 genes inhibit the expression of WNT family genes; and thus, limit hair follicle development to the stage of refractory telogen. In patients with AGA, cells of DP, in the presence of androgens, produce excessive amounts of factors that inhibit WNT, such as DKK1. BMP signaling pathway suppresses itself by triggering the expression of NOG. It has been shown that FGF18 expression in the skin is also required for maintaining refractory telogen stage.    Outcome effects: Typically, telogen ends and anagen begins when the balance shifts towards WNT signaling pathway activity. First, stocks of cells in the DP that produce WNT inhibiting signals become exhausted at the end of telogen. Second, the external signal capable of amplifying WNT expression in secondary germ is required for the initiation of a new cycle. The molecular signal and the cells that initiate the anagen phase are currently unknown. It is possible that the cells of the sebaceous gland or fibroblasts surrounding the hair follicle may be the origin of the initial stimulating signal. For instance, adipocytes secrete PDGFA that can activate WNT10B gene expression through the action of CREB1 transcription factor. WNT10B has been shown to promote hair-shaft growth via WNT/CTNNB1 signaling (canonical WNT signaling) pathway, and maintains the anagen phase. SHH signals may also be involved in the induction of WNT signaling (and anagen) in neighboring telogen hair follicle cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss and is due to the shortening or absence of the anagen phase of hair growth.    Signaling description: The three stages of hair growth include: active growth (anagen phase), hair follicle degeneration (catagen phase), and quiescence (telogen phase). At certain point, telogen phase ends and new hair growth cycle begins due to the activity of stem and stem-like cells. In androgenetic alopecia (AGA) patients, the catagen phase is initiated prematurely while the telogen phase is lengthened. Hair follicle miniaturization occurs and hair type changes from a thick and pigmented shaft to thin vellus hair. In the following cycle, anagen phase onset is often absent. The transition from telogen phase to anagen phase occurs due to stem and stem-like cells including the pool of pluripotent cells in the outer root sheath (bulge region) and progenitor cells adjacent to dermal papilla (DP) or secondary germ. Secondary germ cells arise from the bulge at the end of catagen. The activation of anagen occurs in two stages. First, secondary germ cells are activated; then they stimulate activation of multipotent keratinocyte progenitor cells (bulge region), which in turn are the main source for hair follicle matrix cell mass that further form all cellular layers of hair. Defects in the conversion of hair follicle stem cells to progenitor cells play a role in the pathogenesis of AGA. In bald areas in patients with AGA, there is a loss of progenitor cells such as CD200hiItga6hi and CD34hi cells, but not stem cells. A decrease in progenitor cell population by DP, possibly due to a lack of replenishment from bulge cells, could potentially result in diminished numbers of bulb matrix cells in the DP of the hair follicle and as a result the new (lower) hair follicle that forms at anagen onset is smaller than its predecessor in AGA. Telogen is the resting phase for the cells and major signaling pathways within. WNT signaling pathway which leads to cell proliferation is inactive, as well as BMP signaling pathway that leads to cell differentiation. Quiescence is provided by reciprocal inhibiting feedback loops in these signaling pathways where WNT suppresses BMP and BMP suppresses WNT. BMP2 and BMP4 gene expression, as well as the expression of DKK1 and SFRP4 genes inhibit the expression of WNT family genes; and thus, limit hair follicle development to the stage of refractory telogen. In patients with AGA, cells of DP, in the presence of androgens, produce excessive amounts of factors that inhibit WNT, such as DKK1. BMP signaling pathway suppresses itself by triggering the expression of NOG. It has been shown that FGF18 expression in the skin is also required for maintaining refractory telogen stage.    Outcome effects: Typically, telogen ends and anagen begins when the balance shifts towards WNT signaling pathway activity. First, stocks of cells in the DP that produce WNT inhibiting signals become exhausted at the end of telogen. Second, the external signal capable of amplifying WNT expression in secondary germ is required for the initiation of a new cycle. The molecular signal and the cells that initiate the anagen phase are currently unknown. It is possible that the cells of the sebaceous gland or fibroblasts surrounding the hair follicle may be the origin of the initial stimulating signal. For instance, adipocytes secrete PDGFA that can activate WNT10B gene expression through the action of CREB1 transcription factor. WNT10B has been shown to promote hair-shaft growth via WNT/CTNNB1 signaling (canonical WNT signaling) pathway, and maintains the anagen phase. SHH signals may also be involved in the induction of WNT signaling (and anagen) in neighboring telogen hair follicle cells.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

