xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss. Androgenetic alopecia (AGA), as well as some other cutaneous diseases including seborrhea, acne, and hirsutism, is the prominent symptom of peripheral androgen excess. Androgen excess occurs by the increased synthesis of androgens in the skin called hyperandrogenism.    Signaling description: The sebaceous gland and hair follicles constitute the pilosebaceous unit of the skin. Differentiated sebocytes produce and secrete lipid-rich sebum into the hair canal which empties out to the skin surface. The sebaceous gland is continuously renewed throughout adult life by a population of progenitor cells. It has been shown that the sebum excretion rate is significantly increased in male pattern alopecia. Recent studies suggest that androgens are the positive signal for sebocyte proliferation and the factor that limits their differentiation. However, the mechanism that provides the influence of androgens and androgen receptors (ARs) on proliferation and differentiation is unclear. The peak of AR expression is observed just before the onset of differentiation and then persists during the first accumulation of lipids, marked by FASN and PPARG. Sebocytes derived from bulge stem cells require PPARG for proper homeostasis of the gland. AR functions to prevent TP53 activation, terminate proliferation, and promote MYC-induced differentiation of sebocytes. However, in response to high MYC activity, TP53 can block AR signaling and thereby inhibit differentiation which leads to the expansion of undifferentiated sebocytes. Androgens stimulate sebaceous lipids, while in vivo and in vitro experiments demonstrate that testosterone affects proliferation in human sebocytes, but does not affect lipid synthesis. Therefore, androgens may influence AR cofactors. PPARG is an important AR cofactor which induces the differentiation of sebocytes. The renewal of sebocytes is tightly associated with the regulation of stem cells in the pilosebaceous unit. SOX9 is the earliest marker of both sebocyte progenitor cells and hair follicle stem cells niche. Some patients with androgenetic alopecia (AGA) demonstrate higher SOX9 expression levels in the balding site of the head, as compared to the occipital area. KRT15-positive bulge stem cells have demonstrated their capacity to replenish the sebocyte lineage. Transcription factors, PRDM1 and MYC regulate proliferation and sebaceous gland formation. SHH signaling may be a positive regulator of proliferation of sebocyte progenitor cells.    Outcome effects: Sebocyte cell differentiation requires the suppression of canonical WNT/CTNNB1 signaling-related proliferation of progenitor cells. In mouse model, SMAD7 reduces canonical WNT activity and sebocyte differentiation by directly binding to CTNNB1 and recruiting SMURF2, which leads to the degradation of cytoplasmic CTNNB1. DKK1 is an inhibitor of WNT signaling. DKK1 levels are high in the bald scalp compared to the haired scalp of patients with male pattern baldness. In addition, IGF1 signaling has tremendous implications for the differentiation of pilosebaceous cells. TGFB1 signaling maintains sebocytes in an undifferentiated state by decreasing the expression of FADS2 and PPARG as well as lipid accumulation through the TGFBR2/SMAD2 dependent pathway. Mature sebocytes secrete an oily, waxy substance called sebum. Human sebum consists of squalene, esters of glycerol, wax, cholesterol, free cholesterol, and fatty acids. Squalene accumulation may be considered as a marker of sebocyte differentiation and therefore of sebogenesis. Sapienic acid is a sebum fatty acid that is unique to humans. Circulating fatty acids are selectively utilized by the sebaceous gland. Fatty acid transformation leads to the generation of acetyl-CoA which is incorporated into squalene and wax ester synthesis. DGAT1 and DGAT2"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss. Androgenetic alopecia (AGA), as well as some other cutaneous diseases including seborrhea, acne, and hirsutism, is the prominent symptom of peripheral androgen excess. Androgen excess occurs by the increased synthesis of androgens in the skin called hyperandrogenism.    Signaling description: The sebaceous gland and hair follicles constitute the pilosebaceous unit of the skin. Differentiated sebocytes produce and secrete lipid-rich sebum into the hair canal which empties out to the skin surface. The sebaceous gland is continuously renewed throughout adult life by a population of progenitor cells. It has been shown that the sebum excretion rate is significantly increased in male pattern alopecia. Recent studies suggest that androgens are the positive signal for sebocyte proliferation and the factor that limits their differentiation. However, the mechanism that provides the influence of androgens and androgen receptors (ARs) on proliferation and differentiation is unclear. The peak of AR expression is observed just before the onset of differentiation and then persists during the first accumulation of lipids, marked by FASN and PPARG. Sebocytes derived from bulge stem cells require PPARG for proper homeostasis of the gland. AR functions to prevent TP53 activation, terminate proliferation, and promote MYC-induced differentiation of sebocytes. However, in response to high MYC activity, TP53 can block AR signaling and thereby inhibit differentiation which leads to the expansion of undifferentiated sebocytes. Androgens stimulate sebaceous lipids, while in vivo and in vitro experiments demonstrate that testosterone affects proliferation in human sebocytes, but does not affect lipid synthesis. Therefore, androgens may influence AR cofactors. PPARG is an important AR cofactor which induces the differentiation of sebocytes. The renewal of sebocytes is tightly associated with the regulation of stem cells in the pilosebaceous unit. SOX9 is the earliest marker of both sebocyte progenitor cells and hair follicle stem cells niche. Some patients with androgenetic alopecia (AGA) demonstrate higher SOX9 expression levels in the balding site of the head, as compared to the occipital area. KRT15-positive bulge stem cells have demonstrated their capacity to replenish the sebocyte lineage. Transcription factors, PRDM1 and MYC regulate proliferation and sebaceous gland formation. SHH signaling may be a positive regulator of proliferation of sebocyte progenitor cells.    Outcome effects: Sebocyte cell differentiation requires the suppression of canonical WNT/CTNNB1 signaling-related proliferation of progenitor cells. In mouse model, SMAD7 reduces canonical WNT activity and sebocyte differentiation by directly binding to CTNNB1 and recruiting SMURF2, which leads to the degradation of cytoplasmic CTNNB1. DKK1 is an inhibitor of WNT signaling. DKK1 levels are high in the bald scalp compared to the haired scalp of patients with male pattern baldness. In addition, IGF1 signaling has tremendous implications for the differentiation of pilosebaceous cells. TGFB1 signaling maintains sebocytes in an undifferentiated state by decreasing the expression of FADS2 and PPARG as well as lipid accumulation through the TGFBR2/SMAD2 dependent pathway. Mature sebocytes secrete an oily, waxy substance called sebum. Human sebum consists of squalene, esters of glycerol, wax, cholesterol, free cholesterol, and fatty acids. Squalene accumulation may be considered as a marker of sebocyte differentiation and therefore of sebogenesis. Sapienic acid is a sebum fatty acid that is unique to humans. Circulating fatty acids are selectively utilized by the sebaceous gland. Fatty acid transformation leads to the generation of acetyl-CoA which is incorporated into squalene and wax ester synthesis. DGAT1 and DGAT2" EXACT []

