xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss. Androgenetic alopecia (AGA), as well as some other cutaneous diseases including seborrhea, acne, and hirsutism, is the prominent symptom of peripheral androgen excess. Androgen excess occurs by the increased synthesis of androgens in the skin called hyperandrogenism.    Signaling description: Androgens are major regulators of human androgen-dependent pigmented terminal hair follicle growth such as beard. At the same time, there are androgen-independent hair follicles such as eyelashes. Scalp follicles from frontal and parietal areas of the scalp are considered androgen-sensitive. In androgenetic alopecia (AGA), androgens have paradoxically different effects on hair follicles depending on body site, stimulating androgen-dependent beard growth while inducing regression of follicles from frontal and parietal areas of the scalp. Dermal papilla (DP) cells can synthesize androgens. All types of hair follicle DP cells, including beard, pubic, and scalp, contain testosterone and androstenedione. Normally, only beard cells produce the more potent androgen, dihydrotestosterone by SRD5A2. In AGA, DP cells from androgen-sensitive frontal scalp contain more SRD5A2 than those from androgen-insensitive occipital scalp. Steroid sulfatase, STS in the DP of human terminal hair follicles hydrolyzes dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-sulfate) to DHEA. The follicle DP appears to be the site of androgen action.    Outcome effects: In hair follicles, androgen receptors (ARs) are only found in DP cells. In AGA, DP cells from balding scalp express higher levels of ARs than non-balding scalp cells. AR gene isoforms or functional mutations near the AR gene may explain the aberrant expression of AR protein in the balding scalp and AGA onset and development. Of the many AR gene polymorphisms known, the G allele of StuI and polyQ polymorphisms has the most significant association with AGA, especially in white populations. DNA methylation of the AR promoter is increased in hair follicles from occipital scalp compared with those from vertex AGA scalp, indicating the importance of DNA methylation for protecting occipital hair follicles against AR expression. In AGA, there is simultaneous androgen-dependent stimulation of beard hair growth and the regression of scalp hair which are explained by the difference in DP cell gene expression status due to different schemes of their embryogenesis. In beard and bald frontal scalp, DP cell expression of TGFB1I1 mRNA is higher than in cells from the occipital scalp. TGFB1I1 is a coactivator of AR. TGFB1 can enhance androgen sensitivity in balding DP cells through SMAD3 signaling and checked by TGFB1I1 in an autocrine manner. The expression of NCOA4, a cell growth promoter, has been shown to be reduced in DP of balding areas. The DP and hair bulb express only the short (beta), but not the long (alpha) form of NCOA4. Studies indicate that AR competes with AHR, VDR, and PPARG/ retinoid-X receptor for NCOA4 availability. In addition, in scalp DP cells of AGA patients, increased levels of DNA damage and androgen-accelerated premature senescence (loss of proliferative capacity) induction are observed. The loss of proliferative capacity of balding DP cells is associated with higher expression of CDKN2A, ATM, ATR, HSPB1, SOD; and lower expression of BMI1 and PCNA. CDKN2A protein levels are upregulated in response to androgen and the knockdown of AR diminishes the effects of androgen. Two important DNA damage sensors, H2AFX-ATM and ATR are detected only in balding DP cells. The expression levels of H2AFX proteins are further increased with AR overexpression. In prostate cancer cell lines, androgen signaling has been reported to induce the recruitment of TOP2B complex, which catalyzes DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs) at regulatory regions of AR target ge"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss. Androgenetic alopecia (AGA), as well as some other cutaneous diseases including seborrhea, acne, and hirsutism, is the prominent symptom of peripheral androgen excess. Androgen excess occurs by the increased synthesis of androgens in the skin called hyperandrogenism.    Signaling description: Androgens are major regulators of human androgen-dependent pigmented terminal hair follicle growth such as beard. At the same time, there are androgen-independent hair follicles such as eyelashes. Scalp follicles from frontal and parietal areas of the scalp are considered androgen-sensitive. In androgenetic alopecia (AGA), androgens have paradoxically different effects on hair follicles depending on body site, stimulating androgen-dependent beard growth while inducing regression of follicles from frontal and parietal areas of the scalp. Dermal papilla (DP) cells can synthesize androgens. All types of hair follicle DP cells, including beard, pubic, and scalp, contain testosterone and androstenedione. Normally, only beard cells produce the more potent androgen, dihydrotestosterone by SRD5A2. In AGA, DP cells from androgen-sensitive frontal scalp contain more SRD5A2 than those from androgen-insensitive occipital scalp. Steroid sulfatase, STS in the DP of human terminal hair follicles hydrolyzes dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-sulfate) to DHEA. The follicle DP appears to be the site of androgen action.    Outcome effects: In hair follicles, androgen receptors (ARs) are only found in DP cells. In AGA, DP cells from balding scalp express higher levels of ARs than non-balding scalp cells. AR gene isoforms or functional mutations near the AR gene may explain the aberrant expression of AR protein in the balding scalp and AGA onset and development. Of the many AR gene polymorphisms known, the