xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss. Androgenetic alopecia (AGA), as well as some other cutaneous diseases including seborrhea, acne, and hirsutism, is the prominent symptom of peripheral androgen excess. Androgen excess occurs by the increased synthesis of androgens in the skin called hyperandrogenism.    Signaling description: Androgens are steroid hormones that are derived from cholesterol and differ only in the ring structure and side chains attached to it. Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-sulfate) and androstenedione are the earlier compounds in the synthesis of androgen which are mainly produced in the adrenal gland. The later compounds such as testosterone and dihydrotestosterone (DHT) are mainly produced in the gonads. Free testosterone is the most potent circulating androgen. Skin cells and hair follicles express all androgen metabolizing enzymes required for the independent cutaneous synthesis of androgens. Testosterone in women and DHT in both genders are synthesized in the skin. Sebaceous glands and sweat glands are major contributors to the synthesis of androgens by the skin. In addition, sebocytes are capable of de novo synthesis of cholesterol which can be utilized for the production of androgens. Cholesterol can be made within the cell from acetyl-CoA (de novo synthesis) by HMGCR. There is negative feedback regulation of HMGCR activity by cholesterol, so that high intracellular cholesterol inhibits de novo synthesis. Cholesterol is also taken up by the cell in the form of low density lipoprotein (LDL). LDL is taken into cells via CD36, and broken down into esterified cholesterol, and then free cholesterol. Five major enzymes are involved in androgen synthesis in the skin. The first enzymatic step in steroid synthesis is the conversion of cholesterol into pregnenolone by CYP11A1 in the inner mitochondrial membrane. An additional cytochrome, CYP17A1 is necessary for the synthesis of DHEA and androstenedione. However, sebocytes express low levels of CYP17A1. Steroid sulfatase STS hydrolyzes DHEA-sulfate to DHEA in sebaceous glands. HSD3B1 converts DHEA to androstenedione. Human skin (sebaceous glands) exclusively expresses HSD3B1 isoform. Androstenedione is activated by the conversion to testosterone through HSD17B. HSD17B3 and HSD17B5 form the active androgen, testosterone from androstenedione, whereas the oxidative reaction of HSD17B2 types and HSD17B4 is in the reverse direction, inactivating the potent sexual steroids. SRD5A1 irreversibly converts testosterone to DHT, the most potent naturally occurring androgen in tissue. SRD5A1 isoforms dominate in the skin sebaceous and sweat glands, with lesser activity in epidermal cells and hair follicles.    Outcome effects: Even though sebocytes are known to be the main source of androgen synthesis in the skin, their involvement in the progression of AGA is still unclear. There is no conclusive evidence regarding statistically significant alteration of the expression of steroidogenic enzymes in sebocytes of balding scalp. Some patients with AGA have significantly higher levels of STAR in the basal layer of sebaceous glands in bald parieto-frontal scalp as compared to the occipital scalp. The regulation of STAR expression may be associated with cAMP-PKA pathway, which includes PKA phosphorylation of CREB1, recruitment of CREB binding protein (CREBBP), retinoid X receptor alpha, and RARA to the STAR promoter. If AGA patients have no increase in endogenous androgen synthesis in sebocytes, levels of androgens in the skin may increase due to intensified proliferation or differentiation of sebocytes. Recent studies suggest that androgens are the positive signal for sebocyte proliferation and the factor that limits their differentiation. Nevertheless, the mechanism that provides the influence of androgens and androgen receptors (ARs)"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Androgenetic alopecia refers to hereditary androgen-dependent hair loss that affects both man and women. The process is characterized by moderate to severe hair loss. Androgenetic alopecia (AGA), as well as some other cutaneous diseases including seborrhea, acne, and hirsutism, is the prominent symptom of peripheral androgen excess. Androgen excess occurs by the increased synthesis of androgens in the skin called hyperandrogenism.    Signaling description: Androgens are steroid hormones that are derived from cholesterol and differ only in the ring structure and side chains attached to it. Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-sulfate) and androstenedione are the earlier compounds in the synthesis of androgen which are mainly produced in the adrenal gland. The later compounds such as testosterone and dihydrotestosterone (DHT) are mainly produced in the gonads. Free testosterone is the most potent circulating androgen. Skin cells and hair follicles express all androgen metabolizing enzymes required for the independent cutaneous synthesis of androgens. Testosterone in women and DHT in both genders are synthesized in the skin. Sebaceous glands and sweat glands are major contributors to the synthesis of androgens by the skin. In addition, sebocytes are capable of de novo synthesis of cholesterol which can be utilized for the production of androgens. Cholesterol can be made within the cell from acetyl-CoA (de novo synthesis) by HMGCR. There is negative feedback regulation of HMGCR activity by cholesterol, so that high intracellular cholesterol inhibits de novo synthesis. Cholesterol is also taken up by the cell in the form of low density lipoprotein (LDL). LDL is taken into cells via CD36, and broken down into esterified cholesterol, and then free cholesterol. Five major enzymes are involved in androgen synthesis in the skin. The first enzymatic step in steroid synthesis is the conversion of cholesterol into pregnenolone by CYP11A1 in the inner mitochondrial membrane. An additional cytochrome, CYP17A1 is necessary for the synthesis of DHEA and androstenedione. However, sebocytes express low levels of CYP17A1. Steroid sulfatase STS hydrolyzes DHEA-sulfate to DHEA in sebaceous glands. HSD3B1 converts DHEA to androstenedione. Human skin (sebaceous glands) exclusively expresses HSD3B1 isoform. Androstenedione is activated by the conversion to testosterone through HSD17B. HSD17B3 and HSD17B5 form the active androgen, testosterone from androstenedione, whereas the oxidative reaction of HSD17B2 types and HSD17B4 is in the reverse direction, inactivating the potent sexual steroids. SRD5A1 irreversibly converts testosterone to DHT, the most potent naturally occurring androgen in tissue. SRD5A1 isoforms dominate in the skin sebaceous and sweat glands, with lesser activity in epidermal cells and hair follicles.    Outcome effects: Even though sebocytes are known to be the main source of androgen synthesis in the skin, their involvement in the progression of AGA is still unclear. There is no conclusive evidence regarding statistically significant alteration of the expression of steroidogenic enzymes in sebocytes of balding scalp. Some patients with AGA have significantly higher levels of STAR in the basal layer of sebaceous glands in bald parieto-frontal scalp as compared to the occipital scalp. The regulation of STAR expression may be associated with cAMP-PKA pathway, which includes PKA phosphorylation of CREB1, recruitment of CREB binding protein (CREBBP), retinoid X receptor alpha, and RARA to the STAR promoter. If AGA patients have no increase in endogenous androgen synthesis in sebocytes, levels of androgens in the skin may increase due to intensified proliferation or differentiation of sebocytes. Recent studies suggest that androgens are the positive signal for sebocyte proliferation and the factor that limits their differentiation. Nevertheless, the mechanism that provides the influence of androgens and androgen receptors (ARs)" EXACT []

