xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy rashes. It typically involves a skin reaction similar to an allergy, which leads to an ongoing swelling and redness. AD is associated with the impaired skin barrier function and IgE-mediated sensitization to food and environmental allergens.    Signaling description: Airborne or food allergens may enter the skin with defective barrier function and are picked up by the Langerhans cells (LCs). LCs, inflammatory dendritic epidermal cells, and plasmacytoid dendritic cells play a key role in AD and have an impact on the mechanisms underlying AD such as the recruitment of inflammatory cells, T-cell priming, and the release of cytokines and chemokines.    Outcome effects: Mature LCs express high levels of MHC class II antigens, co-stimulatory molecules such as CD80 and CD86, and chemokine receptors which are all important for antigen presentation. Antigen-loaded LCs migrate to lymph nodes and stimulate naive CD4 T- cells (Th0) to differentiate into Th2-cells, which in turn promote differentiation of B-cells into IgE-producing plasma cells. Furthermore, AD is associated with increased IgE levels and decreased IFNG production.    Highlighted proteins: IFNG with decreased expression is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy rashes. It typically involves a skin reaction similar to an allergy, which leads to an ongoing swelling and redness. AD is associated with the impaired skin barrier function and IgE-mediated sensitization to food and environmental allergens.    Signaling description: Airborne or food allergens may enter the skin with defective barrier function and are picked up by the Langerhans cells (LCs). LCs, inflammatory dendritic epidermal cells, and plasmacytoid dendritic cells play a key role in AD and have an impact on the mechanisms underlying AD such as the recruitment of inflammatory cells, T-cell priming, and the release of cytokines and chemokines.    Outcome effects: Mature LCs express high levels of MHC class II antigens, co-stimulatory molecules such as CD80 and CD86, and chemokine receptors which are all important for antigen presentation. Antigen-loaded LCs migrate to lymph nodes and stimulate naive CD4 T- cells (Th0) to differentiate into Th2-cells, which in turn promote differentiation of B-cells into IgE-producing plasma cells. Furthermore, AD is associated with increased IgE levels and decreased IFNG production.    Highlighted proteins: IFNG with decreased expression is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

