xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy rashes. It typically involves a skin reaction similar to an allergy, which leads to ongoing swelling and redness. AD is associated with impaired skin barrier function and IgE-mediated sensitization to food and environmental allergens. Mast cells serve as the major effector cells in AD associated immediate hypersensitivity through their activation via the high affinity IgE receptor, FCER1.    Signaling description: Mast cells are believed to be involved in the pathogenesis of AD due to the large number of proinflammatory mediators secreted from the FCER1-activated mast cells and their role in IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity, as well as increased number of mast cells in the majority of AD patients. The ethiology of AD is not completely understood. Trigger factors such as allergens, bacteria, mechanical injury, and exogenous proteases induce the release of IL33 and thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) from keratinocytes leading to mast cell activation.    Outcome effects: IL33, alone or in combination with other cytokines and chemokines, activates mast cells and dendritic cells in inflamed skin. IL33-dependent activation of IL1RL1 signaling leads to the activation of MAPK and NF-kB signaling pathways, which are involved in the pathogenesis of AD.    Highlighted proteins: IL33 with increased expression is highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated IL4 is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy rashes. It typically involves a skin reaction similar to an allergy, which leads to ongoing swelling and redness. AD is associated with impaired skin barrier function and IgE-mediated sensitization to food and environmental allergens. Mast cells serve as the major effector cells in AD associated immediate hypersensitivity through their activation via the high affinity IgE receptor, FCER1.    Signaling description: Mast cells are believed to be involved in the pathogenesis of AD due to the large number of proinflammatory mediators secreted from the FCER1-activated mast cells and their role in IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity, as well as increased number of mast cells in the majority of AD patients. The ethiology of AD is not completely understood. Trigger factors such as allergens, bacteria, mechanical injury, and exogenous proteases induce the release of IL33 and thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) from keratinocytes leading to mast cell activation.    Outcome effects: IL33, alone or in combination with other cytokines and chemokines, activates mast cells and dendritic cells in inflamed skin. IL33-dependent activation of IL1RL1 signaling leads to the activation of MAPK and NF-kB signaling pathways, which are involved in the pathogenesis of AD.    Highlighted proteins: IL33 with increased expression is highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated IL4 is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

