xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ichthyosis is a family of genetic disorders with dry, scaly and thickened skin. X-linked ichthyosis is the second most common ichthyosis, with a prevalence of 1:2000 to 1:6000. Recessive X-linked ichthyosis is clinically characterized by widespread, dark brown, polygonal scales and generalized dryness. Cutaneous manifestations are present soon after birth and usually tend not to improve with age. X-linked Ichthyosis is caused by mutations in the STS gene coding for steroid sulfatase located on the X-chromosome.    Signaling description: Cytosolic sulfotransferase SULT2B1 catalyzes the conversion of cholesterol to cholesterol sulfate. Cholesterol sulfotransferase (SULT2B1) activity generates cholesterol sulfate predominately in the lower nucleated cell layers of the epidermis; in contrast, steroid sulfatase (STS), which catalyzes the hydrolysis of cholesterol sulfate peaks in the outer epidermis. Cholesterol sulfate increases the expression of Fra-1, Fra-2, and Jun D members of the AP-1 family of transcription factors leading to enhanced transcriptional regulation of differentiation-linked proteins, such as TG-1 and involucrin, inducing keratinocyte differentiation. Cholesterol sulfate inhibits serine proteases - kallikrein 7 and kallikrein 5 leading to corneodesmosome degradation. Pathological processes underneath the development of X-linked ichthyosis are depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Defective steroid sulfatase is unable to hydrolyze cholesterol sulfate leading to the accumulation of cholesterol sulfate and reducing the available cholesterol needed for the formation of extracellular lamellar bilayers. The accumulating cholesterol sulfate is a potent transcriptional regulator in cutaneous and extracutaneous tissues, where it stimulates epidermal differentiation by several mechanisms. First, it activates the eta isoform of protein kinase C, which in turn stimulates the phosphorylation of epidermal structural proteins, while also increasing cornified envelope formation. In addition, cholesterol sulfate regulates other proteins involved in cornified envelope formation, such as transglutaminase 1 and involucrin, operating through an AP-1 binding site in the promoter region of these proteins. The excess cholesterol sulfate in the stratum corneum in X-linked ichthyosis destabilizes permeability barrier homeostasis. Cholesterol sulfate increases stratum corneum retention through its known function as an inhibitor of serine proteases, kallikrein 7 and kallikrein 5, which mediate the initial stages of corneodesmosome degradation. Also, the stratum corneum in X-linked ichthyosis demonstrates abundant Ca++ in extracellular domains, which preferentially localizes along the external faces of opposing corneodesmosomes. Thus, delayed degradation of corneodesmosomes in X-linked ichthyosis could be due in part to leakage of Ca++ into the lower stratum corneum, with formation of Ca++ bridges between adjacent corneodesmosomes. If Ca++ is present in sufficient quantities, it could stabilize the highly-anionic sulfate groups (from persistent cholesterol sulfate) in the extracellular lipids. The cutaneous clinical phenotype can be explained by the impact of excess cholesterol sulfate on epidermal differentiation and lipid synthesis, as well as on the organization of the lamellar lipids that provide the permeability barrier, and the ability of these lipids to inhibit corneodesmosomes proteolysis. The barrier abnormality in X-linked ichthyosis is also due to the decreased cholesterol content of the stratum corneum.    Animal data: not shown     Highlighted proteins: KLK5, KLK7 and cholesterol are highlighted in blue in order to illustrate the attenuation of corneodesmosome degradation leading to defects in the stratum corneum protective skin barrier.    Mutated proteins: STS gene mutated in X-linked ichthyosis is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ichthyosis is a family of genetic disorders with dry, scaly and thickened skin. X-linked ichthyosis is the second most common ichthyosis, with a prevalence of 1:2000 to 1:6000. Recessive X-linked ichthyosis is clinically characterized by widespread, dark brown, polygonal scales and generalized dryness. Cutaneous manifestations are present soon after birth and usually tend not to improve with age. X-linked Ichthyosis is caused by mutations in the STS gene coding for steroid sulfatase located on the X-chromosome.    Signaling description: Cytosolic sulfotransferase SULT2B1 catalyzes the conversion of cholesterol to cholesterol sulfate. Cholesterol sulfotransferase (SULT2B1) activity generates cholesterol sulfate predominately in the lower nucleated cell layers of the epidermis; in contrast, steroid sulfatase (STS), which catalyzes the hydrolysis of cholesterol sulfate peaks in the outer epidermis. Cholesterol sulfate increases the expression of Fra-1, Fra-2, and Jun D members of the AP-1 family of transcription factors leading to enhanced transcriptional regulation of differentiation-linked proteins, such as TG-1 and involucrin, inducing keratinocyte differentiation. Cholesterol sulfate inhibits serine proteases - kallikrein 7 and kallikrein 5 leading to corneodesmosome degradation. Pathological processes underneath the development of X-linked ichthyosis are depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Defective steroid sulfatase is unable to hydrolyze cholesterol sulfate leading to the accumulation of cholesterol sulfate and reducing the available cholesterol needed for the formation of extracellular lamellar bilayers. The accumulating cholesterol sulfate is a potent transcriptional regulator in cutaneous and extracutaneous tissues, where it stimulates epidermal differentiation by several mechanisms. First, it activates the eta isoform of protein kinase C, which in turn stimulates the phosphorylation of epidermal structural proteins, while also increasing cornified envelope formation. In addition, cholesterol sulfate regulates other proteins involved in cornified envelope formation, such as transglutaminase 1 and involucrin, operating through an AP-1 binding site in the promoter region of these proteins. The excess cholesterol sulfate in the stratum corneum in X-linked ichthyosis destabilizes permeability barrier homeostasis. Cholesterol sulfate increases stratum corneum retention through its known function as an inhibitor of serine proteases, kallikrein 7 and kallikrein 5, which mediate the initial stages of corneodesmosome degradation. Also, the stratum corneum in X-linked ichthyosis demonstrates abundant Ca++ in extracellular domains, which preferentially localizes along the external faces of opposing corneodesmosomes. Thus, delayed degradation of corneodesmosomes in X-linked ichthyosis could be due in part to leakage of Ca++ into the lower stratum corneum, with formation of Ca++ bridges between adjacent corneodesmosomes. If Ca++ is present in sufficient quantities, it could stabilize the highly-anionic sulfate groups (from persistent cholesterol sulfate) in the extracellular lipids. The cutaneous clinical phenotype can be explained by the impact of excess cholesterol sulfate on epidermal differentiation and lipid synthesis, as well as on the organization of the lamellar lipids that provide the permeability barrier, and the ability of these lipids to inhibit corneodesmosomes proteolysis. The barrier abnormality in X-linked ichthyosis is also due to the decreased cholesterol content of the stratum corneum.    Animal data: not shown     Highlighted proteins: KLK5, KLK7 and cholesterol are highlighted in blue in order to illustrate the attenuation of corneodesmosome degradation leading to defects in the stratum corneum protective skin barrier.    Mutated proteins: STS gene mutated in X-linked ichthyosis is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

