xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. The pathogenesis of psoriasis is yet to be fully understood. Studies have shown that dysregulation of the skin T-cells is involved in the development of psoriasis plaques.    Signaling description: Psoriasis skin lesions contain increased amounts of T helper 17 (Th17) cells. Several cytokines are involved in Th17 differentiation. TGFB1 prevents Th1 and Th2 differentiation by suppressing STAT4 and GATA3 expression, thus allowing Th17 differentiation. However, Th17 development also occurs in the absence of TGFB1 signaling. IL18 synergizes with IL23 to promote IL17 production. IL1B negatively regulates Th1 differentiation by inducing cyclooxygenase (COX)-2, which subsequently increases the secretion of prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) that suppresses IFNG production thus allowing Th17 differentiation. In addition, the association between psoriasis and polymorphisms in IL12/23p40 and IL23R was recently discovered. Also, nuclear orphan receptor (RORC) functions to coordinate the diverse cytokine-induced signals and thus controls Th17 cell differentiation. Furthermore, the overreaction of psoriatic T cells to IFNA1 can be explained by the downregulation of SOCS3 and PTPN6. These two proteins are negative regulators of the IFNA1 signaling pathway. PTPN6 inhibits JAK1 and JAK2 that activate STAT1, STAT3, and STAT4 by the dephosphorylation of upstream cytokine receptors and receptor-associated tyrosine kinases. In turn, the suppressors of cytokine signaling (SOCS) constitute a family of STAT-induced negative regulators of cytokine signaling. These proteins inhibit the production of certain proinflammatory cytokines such as IL17. Finally, the deficiency of FOXP3 regulatory T (Treg) cells in psoriasis lesions may be responsible for an acute flare-up of the disease, and thus may predict severity and clinical course.    Outcome effects: The differentiation of naive T-cells towards a Th17 phenotype is promoted by a number of cytokines including transforming growth factor-B (TGFB1), IL1B, IL6, IL21, and IL23. In turn, the interleukin-(IL)-23 and IL-17 expressing Th17 cells are thought to be the specific regulators of the inflammatory reactions in psoriasis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: IL12/23p40 and IL23R mutated in psoriasis are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. The pathogenesis of psoriasis is yet to be fully understood. Studies have shown that dysregulation of the skin T-cells is involved in the development of psoriasis plaques.    Signaling description: Psoriasis skin lesions contain increased amounts of T helper 17 (Th17) cells. Several cytokines are involved in Th17 differentiation. TGFB1 prevents Th1 and Th2 differentiation by suppressing STAT4 and GATA3 expression, thus allowing Th17 differentiation. However, Th17 development also occurs in the absence of TGFB1 signaling. IL18 synergizes with IL23 to promote IL17 production. IL1B negatively regulates Th1 differentiation by inducing cyclooxygenase (COX)-2, which subsequently increases the secretion of prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) that suppresses IFNG production thus allowing Th17 differentiation. In addition, the association between psoriasis and polymorphisms in IL12/23p40 and IL23R was recently discovered. Also, nuclear orphan receptor (RORC) functions to coordinate the diverse cytokine-induced signals and thus controls Th17 cell differentiation. Furthermore, the overreaction of psoriatic T cells to IFNA1 can be explained by the downregulation of SOCS3 and PTPN6. These two proteins are negative regulators of the IFNA1 signaling pathway. PTPN6 inhibits JAK1 and JAK2 that activate STAT1, STAT3, and STAT4 by the dephosphorylation of upstream cytokine receptors and receptor-associated tyrosine kinases. In turn, the suppressors of cytokine signaling (SOCS) constitute a family of STAT-induced negative regulators of cytokine signaling. These proteins inhibit the production of certain proinflammatory cytokines such as IL17. Finally, the deficiency of FOXP3 regulatory T (Treg) cells in psoriasis lesions may be responsible for an acute flare-up of the disease, and thus may predict severity and clinical course.    Outcome effects: The differentiation of naive T-cells towards a Th17 phenotype is promoted by a number of cytokines including transforming growth factor-B (TGFB1), IL1B, IL6, IL21, and IL23. In turn, the interleukin-(IL)-23 and IL-17 expressing Th17 cells are thought to be the specific regulators of the inflammatory reactions in psoriasis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: IL12/23p40 and IL23R mutated in psoriasis are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

