synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "skin"
synonym: "Organ: skin" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "epidermis"
synonym: "Tissue: epidermis" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "integumentary system"
synonym: "Organ_System: integumentary system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. The pathogenesis of psoriasis is yet to be fully understood. IFN-gamma (IFNG) and TNF-alpha (TNF) induced inflammation is implicated in the development of psoriasis.    Signaling description: SOCS1 and SOCS3 are abundantly expressed in IFNG/TNF-activated psoriatic keratinocytes which play an important role in maintaining anti-apoptosis. SOCS1 and SOCS3 suppress cytokine-induced apoptosis by sustaining the activation of the PI3K/AKT1 pathway in keratinocytes. The latter determines the activation of the antiapoptotic NF-kB cascade and, in parallel, the inhibition of the proapoptotic BAD function in keratinocytes. SOCS3 binds RasGAP (RASA1), a negative regulator of Ras signaling, and reduces its activity, thereby promoting activation of the Ras pathway. Moreover, SOCS1 interacts with RASA1, and promotes its degradation after IFNG stimulation.    Outcome effects: Upon IFN-gamma (IFNG) and TNF-alpha (TNF) exposure, psoriatic keratinocytes express exaggerated levels of inflammatory mediators, show aberrant hyperproliferation, and terminal differentiation. The thickening of psoriatic skin also results from an unusual resistance of keratinocytes to cytokine-induced apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. The pathogenesis of psoriasis is yet to be fully understood. IFN-gamma (IFNG) and TNF-alpha (TNF) induced inflammation is implicated in the development of psoriasis.    Signaling description: SOCS1 and SOCS3 are abundantly expressed in IFNG/TNF-activated psoriatic keratinocytes which play an important role in maintaining anti-apoptosis. SOCS1 and SOCS3 suppress cytokine-induced apoptosis by sustaining the activation of the PI3K/AKT1 pathway in keratinocytes. The latter determines the activation of the antiapoptotic NF-kB cascade and, in parallel, the inhibition of the proapoptotic BAD function in keratinocytes. SOCS3 binds RasGAP (RASA1), a negative regulator of Ras signaling, and reduces its activity, thereby promoting activation of the Ras pathway. Moreover, SOCS1 interacts with RASA1, and promotes its degradation after IFNG stimulation.    Outcome effects: Upon IFN-gamma (IFNG) and TNF-alpha (TNF) exposure, psoriatic keratinocytes express exaggerated levels of inflammatory mediators, show aberrant hyperproliferation, and terminal differentiation. The thickening of psoriatic skin also results from an unusual resistance of keratinocytes to cytokine-induced apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

