xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. Interleukin-22 (IL22) is a recently discovered cytokine that has been found to significantly induce the proliferation of human keratinocytes. IL22 is implicated in the pathogenesis of a number of autoimmune diseases characterized by hyperproliferation of keratinocytes including psoriasis.    Signaling description: IL22 is mostly produced by a specific subset of T cells.It acts through the IL22 receptor which is a complex of two subunits: IL22RA1 and IL10RB. Following binding to IL22RA1, IL22 primarily activates Janus kinase 1 (JAK1), signal transducer and activator of transcription protein 3 (STAT3), mitogen activated protein kinases (MAPKs), such as the c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK), p38 and extracellular signal regulated kinase 1/2 (ERK1/2).    Outcome effects: The interaction of IL22 and JAK1 activates the JAK/STAT/ERK/MAPK pathway, as well as the PI3K/AKT1/MTOR signaling pathway leading to the proliferation of keratinocytes. In the PI3K/AKT1/MTOR pathway, PI3K and protein kinase B (AKT1) are the key upstream molecules that link ligation of growth factor receptors and kinases to the activation of MTOR, similarly leading to keratinocyte proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. Interleukin-22 (IL22) is a recently discovered cytokine that has been found to significantly induce the proliferation of human keratinocytes. IL22 is implicated in the pathogenesis of a number of autoimmune diseases characterized by hyperproliferation of keratinocytes including psoriasis.    Signaling description: IL22 is mostly produced by a specific subset of T cells.It acts through the IL22 receptor which is a complex of two subunits: IL22RA1 and IL10RB. Following binding to IL22RA1, IL22 primarily activates Janus kinase 1 (JAK1), signal transducer and activator of transcription protein 3 (STAT3), mitogen activated protein kinases (MAPKs), such as the c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK), p38 and extracellular signal regulated kinase 1/2 (ERK1/2).    Outcome effects: The interaction of IL22 and JAK1 activates the JAK/STAT/ERK/MAPK pathway, as well as the PI3K/AKT1/MTOR signaling pathway leading to the proliferation of keratinocytes. In the PI3K/AKT1/MTOR pathway, PI3K and protein kinase B (AKT1) are the key upstream molecules that link ligation of growth factor receptors and kinases to the activation of MTOR, similarly leading to keratinocyte proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

