xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. The pathogenesis of psoriasis is yet to be fully understood.    Signaling description: The response of dendritic cells (DCs) to extracellular self-DNA and self-RNA are prevented by the endosomal seclusion of nucleic acid-recognizing Toll-like receptors (TLRs). However in psoriasis, plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) sense self-DNA that is transported to endosomal TLR9 upon forming a complex with cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide (CAMP). CAMP can bind self-RNA released by dying cells, protect them from extracellular degradation, and transport them into endosomal compartments of DCs. In pDCs, self-RNA-CAMP complexes activate TLR7 and, similar to the self-DNA-CAMP complexes, trigger the secretion of IFNA1 without inducing maturation or the production of IL6 and TNF. In contrast to self-DNA-CAMP complexes, self-RNA-CAMP complexes also trigger the activation of classical myeloid DCs (mDCs). This occurs through TLR8 and leads to the production of TNF and IL6 as well as the differentiation of mDCs into mature DCs. Further, S100 proteins belong to a multi-gene family of low molecular weight calcium binding proteins encoded within a well-known psoriasis susceptibility locus (PSOR4) on chromosome 1q21.    Outcome effects: TLR7, 8 and 9 were all shown to contribute to autoimmune diseases. S100A7 and S100A8/A9 which are prominent members of this protein family are upregulated in skin inflammation and psoriasis. S100A8/A9 plays a role in myeloid differentiation, inflammation, and exerts antimicrobial activity. S100A7 is produced by keratinocytes surrounding the wound and is released into the wound exudate. S100A8/A9 interacts with AGER and has been shown to interact with TLR4 via the interaction with S100A8. The receptors, AGER and TLR4 are found on the surface of immune system cells and endothelial cells. Although the signaling pathways for both receptor groups are different, they both ultimately activate NF-kB transcription factor which, in turn, activates genes encoding adhesion proteins, proinflammatory cytokines, and proangiogenic factors.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disorder characterized by scaly and itchy patches of red skin, and is generally considered to be a hereditary disease triggered by environmental factors. Most of the genetic changes associated with psoriasis relate to the immune system. The pathogenesis of psoriasis is yet to be fully understood.    Signaling description: The response of dendritic cells (DCs) to extracellular self-DNA and self-RNA are prevented by the endosomal seclusion of nucleic acid-recognizing Toll-like receptors (TLRs). However in psoriasis, plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) sense self-DNA that is transported to endosomal TLR9 upon forming a complex with cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide (CAMP). CAMP can bind self-RNA released by dying cells, protect them from extracellular degradation, and transport them into endosomal compartments of DCs. In pDCs, self-RNA-CAMP complexes activate TLR7 and, similar to the self-DNA-CAMP complexes, trigger the secretion of IFNA1 without inducing maturation or the production of IL6 and TNF. In contrast to self-DNA-CAMP complexes, self-RNA-CAMP complexes also trigger the activation of classical myeloid DCs (mDCs). This occurs through TLR8 and leads to the production of TNF and IL6 as well as the differentiation of mDCs into mature DCs. Further, S100 proteins belong to a multi-gene family of low molecular weight calcium binding proteins encoded within a well-known psoriasis susceptibility locus (PSOR4) on chromosome 1q21.    Outcome effects: TLR7, 8 and 9 were all shown to contribute to autoimmune diseases. S100A7 and S100A8/A9 which are prominent members of this protein family are upregulated in skin inflammation and psoriasis. S100A8/A9 plays a role in myeloid differentiation, inflammation, and exerts antimicrobial activity. S100A7 is produced by keratinocytes surrounding the wound and is released into the wound exudate. S100A8/A9 interacts with AGER and has been shown to interact with TLR4 via the interaction with S100A8. The receptors, AGER and TLR4 are found on the surface of immune system cells and endothelial cells. Although the signaling pathways for both receptor groups are different, they both ultimately activate NF-kB transcription factor which, in turn, activates genes encoding adhesion proteins, proinflammatory cytokines, and proangiogenic factors.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

