xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Photoaging refers to the cumulative specific alterations in the skin caused by chronic exposure to sunlight. The process involves prominent cutaneous transformation that is clinically characterized by fine and coarse wrinkles, blotchy dyspigmentation, telangiectasia, sallowness, increased fragility, and rough skin texture and depends on both environmental and intrinsic factors, with fair-skinned individuals showing more pronounced symptoms of photoaging than people with darker skin.    Signaling description: UV irradiation induces the down-regulation of transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-beta) and connective tissue growth factor in the TGF-beta/Smad/CTGF/procollagen axis and leads to the decreased synthesis of type I collagen. Type I collagen is the major structural protein in human skin, providing skin with strength and resiliency, and is synthesized as a precursor form, type I procollagen, by fibroblasts that reside in skin connective tissue. TGF-beta regulates procollagen production both directly and indirectly, via the TGF-beta regulated connective tissue growth factor (CTGF). UV irradiation impairs the TGF-beta/Smad pathway, and this impairment is associated with reduced CTGF expression and reduced type I procollagen synthesis in human skin in vivo and human skin fibroblasts in vitro. Inhibition of the TGF-beta/Smad pathway by UV radiation occurs primarily as a consequence of down-regulation of the TGF-beta receptor type II.    Outcome effects: TGF-beta plays a dominant role in regulating collagen homeostasis. As a consequence of the passage of time and/or chronic exposure to ultraviolet radiation, type I procollagen synthesis is down-regulated via the TGF-beta dependent signaling. This loss of type I procollagen is a major contributing factor to thinning and increased fragility of naturally aged skin, and the wrinkled appearance of photoaged skin.    Highlighted proteins: TGFBR2, CTGF, COL1A2 and collagen type I are highlighted in blue in order to illustrate the disruption of the protective skin barrier."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Photoaging refers to the cumulative specific alterations in the skin caused by chronic exposure to sunlight. The process involves prominent cutaneous transformation that is clinically characterized by fine and coarse wrinkles, blotchy dyspigmentation, telangiectasia, sallowness, increased fragility, and rough skin texture and depends on both environmental and intrinsic factors, with fair-skinned individuals showing more pronounced symptoms of photoaging than people with darker skin.    Signaling description: UV irradiation induces the down-regulation of transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-beta) and connective tissue growth factor in the TGF-beta/Smad/CTGF/procollagen axis and leads to the decreased synthesis of type I collagen. Type I collagen is the major structural protein in human skin, providing skin with strength and resiliency, and is synthesized as a precursor form, type I procollagen, by fibroblasts that reside in skin connective tissue. TGF-beta regulates procollagen production both directly and indirectly, via the TGF-beta regulated connective tissue growth factor (CTGF). UV irradiation impairs the TGF-beta/Smad pathway, and this impairment is associated with reduced CTGF expression and reduced type I procollagen synthesis in human skin in vivo and human skin fibroblasts in vitro. Inhibition of the TGF-beta/Smad pathway by UV radiation occurs primarily as a consequence of down-regulation of the TGF-beta receptor type II.    Outcome effects: TGF-beta plays a dominant role in regulating collagen homeostasis. As a consequence of the passage of time and/or chronic exposure to ultraviolet radiation, type I procollagen synthesis is down-regulated via the TGF-beta dependent signaling. This loss of type I procollagen is a major contributing factor to thinning and increased fragility of naturally aged skin, and the wrinkled appearance of photoaged skin.    Highlighted proteins: TGFBR2, CTGF, COL1A2 and collagen type I are highlighted in blue in order to illustrate the disruption of the protective skin barrier." EXACT []

