xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Photoaging refers to the cumulative specific alterations in the skin caused by chronic exposure to sunlight. The process involves prominent cutaneous transformation that is clinically characterized by fine and coarse wrinkles, blotchy dyspigmentation, telangiectasia, sallowness, increased fragility, and rough skin texture and depends on both environmental and intrinsic factors, with fair-skinned individuals showing more pronounced symptoms of photoaging than people with darker skin.    Signaling description: UVA radiation is the major component of solar UV radiation; it deeply penetrates into the skin and induces profound alterations in the dermal connective tissue. UVA radiation appears to play a key role in pigmented changes occurring with age, the major sign of skin photoaging. Skin susceptibility to photoaging alterations also depends on constitutive pigmentation. Melanocytes are melanin-producing cells located in the stratum basale of the epidermis. UV radiation mediates the synthesis and release of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone which then promotes pigmentation by binding to the melanocortin 1 receptor on the surface of melanocytes. The production of cyclic adenosine monophosphate downstream of MC1R activates the receptors of MITF. MITF later participates in the conversion of tyrosine into melanin pigments by stimulating the transcription of melanocyte-specific genes. Recent studies have shown that melanin, especially pheomelanin (a yellow/red form of melanin), acts as a potent UV photosensitizer as it produces ROS, induces DNA damage and cause apoptosis of skin cells upon exposure to UV radiation.    Outcome effects: The role of melanin in photoprotection is controversial. Melanin functions as a UV-absorbent and has photoprotective properties, yet recent evidence shows that the yellow/red form of melanin (pheomelanin) can have damaging effect on the skin and contribute to photoaging.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Photoaging refers to the cumulative specific alterations in the skin caused by chronic exposure to sunlight. The process involves prominent cutaneous transformation that is clinically characterized by fine and coarse wrinkles, blotchy dyspigmentation, telangiectasia, sallowness, increased fragility, and rough skin texture and depends on both environmental and intrinsic factors, with fair-skinned individuals showing more pronounced symptoms of photoaging than people with darker skin.    Signaling description: UVA radiation is the major component of solar UV radiation; it deeply penetrates into the skin and induces profound alterations in the dermal connective tissue. UVA radiation appears to play a key role in pigmented changes occurring with age, the major sign of skin photoaging. Skin susceptibility to photoaging alterations also depends on constitutive pigmentation. Melanocytes are melanin-producing cells located in the stratum basale of the epidermis. UV radiation mediates the synthesis and release of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone which then promotes pigmentation by binding to the melanocortin 1 receptor on the surface of melanocytes. The production of cyclic adenosine monophosphate downstream of MC1R activates the receptors of MITF. MITF later participates in the conversion of tyrosine into melanin pigments by stimulating the transcription of melanocyte-specific genes. Recent studies have shown that melanin, especially pheomelanin (a yellow/red form of melanin), acts as a potent UV photosensitizer as it produces ROS, induces DNA damage and cause apoptosis of skin cells upon exposure to UV radiation.    Outcome effects: The role of melanin in photoprotection is controversial. Melanin functions as a UV-absorbent and has photoprotective properties, yet recent evidence shows that the yellow/red form of melanin (pheomelanin) can have damaging effect on the skin and contribute to photoaging.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is in white-out style." EXACT []

