xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Photoaging refers to the cumulative specific alterations in the skin caused by chronic exposure to sunlight. The process involves prominent cutaneous transformation that is clinically characterized by fine and coarse wrinkles, blotchy dyspigmentation, telangiectasia, sallowness, increased fragility, and rough skin texture and depends on both environmental and intrinsic factors, with fair-skinned individuals showing more pronounced symptoms of photoaging than people with darker skin.    Signaling description: Chronic UV exposure causes damage to DNA, and activation of the ATM and ATR kinases. Upon activation, ATM and ATR phosphorylate Chk2 and Chk1, respectively which in turn catalyze the phosphorylation and accumulation of p53. As a transcription factor, p53 binds directly to the POMC gene promoter in keratinocytes and induces POMC, MMP2 and CASP transcription. Ultraviolet (UV) radiation stimulates skin keratinocytes to release POMC, which binds to the melanocyte receptor, MC1R resulting in the expression of MITF which are highly correlated with melanocyte development and melanomagenesis. Upon UVR-induced, damage keratinocytes upregulate their expression of the pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) gene.    Outcome effects: POMC encodes a pro-peptide that is cleaved to generate alpha -MSH, ACTH and ß-endorphin. Together, these peptides have pleiotropic effects on endocrine and neuroendocrine signaling, and the immune system, in addition to the alpha -MSH function in melanogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: POMC with increased expression in photoaging is highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Photoaging refers to the cumulative specific alterations in the skin caused by chronic exposure to sunlight. The process involves prominent cutaneous transformation that is clinically characterized by fine and coarse wrinkles, blotchy dyspigmentation, telangiectasia, sallowness, increased fragility, and rough skin texture and depends on both environmental and intrinsic factors, with fair-skinned individuals showing more pronounced symptoms of photoaging than people with darker skin.    Signaling description: Chronic UV exposure causes damage to DNA, and activation of the ATM and ATR kinases. Upon activation, ATM and ATR phosphorylate Chk2 and Chk1, respectively which in turn catalyze the phosphorylation and accumulation of p53. As a transcription factor, p53 binds directly to the POMC gene promoter in keratinocytes and induces POMC, MMP2 and CASP transcription. Ultraviolet (UV) radiation stimulates skin keratinocytes to release POMC, which binds to the melanocyte receptor, MC1R resulting in the expression of MITF which are highly correlated with melanocyte development and melanomagenesis. Upon UVR-induced, damage keratinocytes upregulate their expression of the pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) gene.    Outcome effects: POMC encodes a pro-peptide that is cleaved to generate alpha -MSH, ACTH and ß-endorphin. Together, these peptides have pleiotropic effects on endocrine and neuroendocrine signaling, and the immune system, in addition to the alpha -MSH function in melanogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: POMC with increased expression in photoaging is highlighted in red." EXACT []

