xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Apoptosis of skin keratinocytes is an important mechanism that triggers SLE.    Signaling description: Apoptotic material of dead or dying keratinocytes plays a central role in the development of innate responses that lead to inflammation in SLE. The direct absorption of ultraviolet (UV) energy in keratinocytes drives biochemical reactions that result in the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and DNA damage. The production of ROS in the skin can also take place through the activation of the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) which leads to the NAD(P)H oxidase activation and delayed ROS production. ROS, which can act as a mediator of indirect photodamage, causes oxidative stress. Following activation by ROS generation and oxidative stress, the keratinocyte dies and releases the apoptotic material that triggers death of neighboring cells. UV radiation activates toll-like receptors (TLRs) leading to increased levels and active secretion of IL1B, IL18, IL33, and IL1A. The activation of different TLRs upon the UV exposure can lead to the opposite cytokine profiles with regard to promoting or inhibiting the immune responses. TLR3 or TLR7 activation by UV radiation produces IL12, while TLR2 or TLR4 activation leads to the IL10 induction. The activation of TLRs also leads to the expression of high-mobility group box 1 (HMGB1) protein which is when secreted causes death of neighboring cells. Other substances released from the apoptotic cell, such as damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) can also activate TLRs. UV damage activates the NLRP1 and NLRP3 inflammasome complexes. The inflammasome actvation may occur via the ATP-related and calcium (Ca2+)-related pathways; however, the exact mechanism of this process is unknown. The AIM2 inflammasome is not present in healthy skin, and is found exclusively in altered keratinocytes. A possible mechanism for the AIM2 inflammasome activation includes UV irradiation-induced or IGF-mediated signaling leading to DNA fragmentation.    Outcome effects: Activated inflammasomes are able to induce IL1B, IL18, IL33, and IL1A production. Dying keratinocytes release danger signals that induce apoptosis of the neighboring keratinocytes and secrete abundant proinflammatory proteins. Secreted IL1B, IL18, IL33, and IL1A in addition to the apoptotic materials of dying keratinocytes lead to the chronic innate activation of the plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs) to secrete type I interferons (IFNs) which is a key early event that triggers autoimmunity in SLE.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Apoptosis of skin keratinocytes is an important mechanism that triggers SLE.    Signaling description: Apoptotic material of dead or dying keratinocytes plays a central role in the development of innate responses that lead to inflammation in SLE. The direct absorption of ultraviolet (UV) energy in keratinocytes drives biochemical reactions that result in the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and DNA damage. The production of ROS in the skin can also take place through the activation of the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) which leads to the NAD(P)H oxidase activation and delayed ROS production. ROS, which can act as a mediator of indirect photodamage, causes oxidative stress. Following activation by ROS generation and oxidative stress, the keratinocyte dies and releases the apoptotic material that triggers death of neighboring cells. UV radiation activates toll-like receptors (TLRs) leading to increased levels and active secretion of IL1B, IL18, IL33, and IL1A. The activation of different TLRs upon the UV exposure can lead to the opposite cytokine profiles with regard to promoting or inhibiting the immune responses. TLR3 or TLR7 activation by UV radiation produces IL12, while TLR2 or TLR4 activation leads to the IL10 induction. The activation of TLRs also leads to the expression of high-mobility group box 1 (HMGB1) protein which is when secreted causes death of neighboring cells. Other substances released from the apoptotic cell, such as damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) can also activate TLRs. UV damage activates the NLRP1 and NLRP3 inflammasome complexes. The inflammasome actvation may occur via the ATP-related and calcium (Ca2+)-related pathways; however, the exact mechanism of this process is unknown. The AIM2 inflammasome is not present in healthy skin, and is found exclusively in altered keratinocytes. A possible mechanism for the AIM2 inflammasome activation includes UV irradiation-induced or IGF-mediated signaling leading to DNA fragmentation.    Outcome effects: Activated inflammasomes are able to induce IL1B, IL18, IL33, and IL1A production. Dying keratinocytes release danger signals that induce apoptosis of the neighboring keratinocytes and secrete abundant proinflammatory proteins. Secreted IL1B, IL18, IL33, and IL1A in addition to the apoptotic materials of dying keratinocytes lead to the chronic innate activation of the plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs) to secrete type I interferons (IFNs) which is a key early event that triggers autoimmunity in SLE.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

