xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Dendritic cells (DCs) play a central role in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) development.    Signaling description: Autoantibodies and apoptotic cell products activate the synthesis of type I interferon and cytokines in plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs) and myeloid dendritic cells (mDCs). IFN alpha (IFNA1) activates mDCs, which express co-stimulatory molecules and trigger the differentiation of autoreactive CD4 and CD8 T cells, thereby maintaining chronic inflammation. Polymorphisms in DNASE1, PARP1, CAMP, and TREX1 genes, which can cause diminished activity of these enzymes, are associated with SLE. DNASE1, PARP1, CAMP, and TREX1 are important proteins for utilizing products of apoptosis. In addition, cellular debris such as fragments of DNA, RNA, or HMGB1 activates Toll-like receptor (TLR) signaling on the cell membrane and inside the cell. TLR signaling leads to the high level of expression of type I interferon and cytokines by a yet to be identified mechanism. A possible signaling route involves the MYD88 and TICAM1-related NF-kB, AP-1 (Jun/Fos), IRF5, and IRF7 activation. DCs have an active inflammasome for the secretion of IL1B, IL18, IL33 and IL1A which support chronic immunological reactions. The details of inflammasome activation are unclear with calcium-related inflammasome complex activation being a possible scenario. Additionally, mutated FCGR2A can cause alterations in endocytosis and the presentation of autoantigens, which then triggers T-cell differentiation.    Outcome effects: The high level of type I interferon recursively activates mDCs. This occurs through the interaction between IFNA1 and IFNAR2 or IFNAR1. The exact mechanism of IFNAR1 signaling in SLE is poorly understood, however signal transduction through JAK1 and TYK2, which phosphorylate IFNAR1 leading to the recruitment and phosphorylation of the signal transducers and activators of transcription (STAT1 and STAT2) is likely. Polymorphisms in TYK2 are highly associated with SLE. STAT heterodimers associate with IRF9. This complex translocates to the nucleus to induce IFN-stimulated genes from IFN-stimulated response elements (ISREs).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Genes with mutations linked to SLE are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Dendritic cells (DCs) play a central role in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) development.    Signaling description: Autoantibodies and apoptotic cell products activate the synthesis of type I interferon and cytokines in plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs) and myeloid dendritic cells (mDCs). IFN alpha (IFNA1) activates mDCs, which express co-stimulatory molecules and trigger the differentiation of autoreactive CD4 and CD8 T cells, thereby maintaining chronic inflammation. Polymorphisms in DNASE1, PARP1, CAMP, and TREX1 genes, which can cause diminished activity of these enzymes, are associated with SLE. DNASE1, PARP1, CAMP, and TREX1 are important proteins for utilizing products of apoptosis. In addition, cellular debris such as fragments of DNA, RNA, or HMGB1 activates Toll-like receptor (TLR) signaling on the cell membrane and inside the cell. TLR signaling leads to the high level of expression of type I interferon and cytokines by a yet to be identified mechanism. A possible signaling route involves the MYD88 and TICAM1-related NF-kB, AP-1 (Jun/Fos), IRF5, and IRF7 activation. DCs have an active inflammasome for the secretion of IL1B, IL18, IL33 and IL1A which support chronic immunological reactions. The details of inflammasome activation are unclear with calcium-related inflammasome complex activation being a possible scenario. Additionally, mutated FCGR2A can cause alterations in endocytosis and the presentation of autoantigens, which then triggers T-cell differentiation.    Outcome effects: The high level of type I interferon recursively activates mDCs. This occurs through the interaction between IFNA1 and IFNAR2 or IFNAR1. The exact mechanism of IFNAR1 signaling in SLE is poorly understood, however signal transduction through JAK1 and TYK2, which phosphorylate IFNAR1 leading to the recruitment and phosphorylation of the signal transducers and activators of transcription (STAT1 and STAT2) is likely. Polymorphisms in TYK2 are highly associated with SLE. STAT heterodimers associate with IRF9. This complex translocates to the nucleus to induce IFN-stimulated genes from IFN-stimulated response elements (ISREs).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Genes with mutations linked to SLE are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

