xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Aberrant T-cell activation due to abnormal antigen presenting cell (APC) function is one of the main molecular pathological mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: T-lymphocytes from SLE patients show a number of abnormalities that cause hypercellular kinetics along with the production of defective interleukin-2 (IL2). The increased level of TNF secreted from dendritic cells activates the FAS/FASLG and CASP3-mediated apoptotic signaling pathways in SLE T-cells. Therefore, apoptosis of the T-cell subset in SLE patients increases. As a result of chronic activation, defective DNA methylation or impaired alternative splicing, the expression and function of proteins in the T-cell receptor (TCR) complex are altered. The exact details of this process are not known. The defective downstream signaling from TCR leads to the aberrant activation of T-cells and low levels of IL2 production.    Outcome effects: SLE-related TCR signaling pathway alterations cause changes in the CD247 and FCER1G expression. As a result, reduced expression of CD247 leads to the increased activation of SYK. FCER1G-SYK signaling replaces normal CD247-ZAP70 signaling and leads to higher calcium levels in the T-cell. In addition, CREM activation leads to down-regulation of IL2. Aberrant DNA methylation affects a number of genes in SLE patients, such as those encoding CD40LG, CD70, CD11A (ITGAL), and PP2A. Finally, aberrant activation Th0-cell causes a reduction in the Th1 cell-mediated cytokine (IL2 and IFNG) secretion.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Aberrant T-cell activation due to abnormal antigen presenting cell (APC) function is one of the main molecular pathological mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: T-lymphocytes from SLE patients show a number of abnormalities that cause hypercellular kinetics along with the production of defective interleukin-2 (IL2). The increased level of TNF secreted from dendritic cells activates the FAS/FASLG and CASP3-mediated apoptotic signaling pathways in SLE T-cells. Therefore, apoptosis of the T-cell subset in SLE patients increases. As a result of chronic activation, defective DNA methylation or impaired alternative splicing, the expression and function of proteins in the T-cell receptor (TCR) complex are altered. The exact details of this process are not known. The defective downstream signaling from TCR leads to the aberrant activation of T-cells and low levels of IL2 production.    Outcome effects: SLE-related TCR signaling pathway alterations cause changes in the CD247 and FCER1G expression. As a result, reduced expression of CD247 leads to the increased activation of SYK. FCER1G-SYK signaling replaces normal CD247-ZAP70 signaling and leads to higher calcium levels in the T-cell. In addition, CREM activation leads to down-regulation of IL2. Aberrant DNA methylation affects a number of genes in SLE patients, such as those encoding CD40LG, CD70, CD11A (ITGAL), and PP2A. Finally, aberrant activation Th0-cell causes a reduction in the Th1 cell-mediated cytokine (IL2 and IFNG) secretion.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

