xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Aberrant T-cell activation due to abnormal antigen presenting cell (APC) function is one of the main molecular pathological mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: T-cells have a central role in the pathogenesis of SLE. The level of serum IL17 is increased in SLE patients. The frequency of IL17-producing T- cells is increased in peripheral blood. T-cell differentiation into IL17-producing Th17 cells requires cytokines IL6, IL23, IL21, and TGFB. High levels of IFNA1, IL1B, and IL18 released from dendritic cells can promote Th17 cell activation. IL6, IL21, and IL23 receptors all activate STAT3, which can bind and activate IL17 and IL21 genes directly. The expression and activity of STAT3 is increased in SLE T-cells. IL17 gene transcription is regulated by the retinoid related orphan receptor, RORC transcription factor. RORC is expressed exclusively in Th17 cells and is necessary for IL17 production. In addition, altered T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling can also lead to Th17 expression.    Outcome effects: There is evidence of an increased level of IL17 in the circulation and tissues of SLE patients. IL17 can induce the production of an array of inflammatory molecules such as cytokines, chemokines, and MMP from immune and non-immune cells, leading to the recruitment and activation of inflammatory cells along with tissue damage in SLE.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Aberrant T-cell activation due to abnormal antigen presenting cell (APC) function is one of the main molecular pathological mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: T-cells have a central role in the pathogenesis of SLE. The level of serum IL17 is increased in SLE patients. The frequency of IL17-producing T- cells is increased in peripheral blood. T-cell differentiation into IL17-producing Th17 cells requires cytokines IL6, IL23, IL21, and TGFB. High levels of IFNA1, IL1B, and IL18 released from dendritic cells can promote Th17 cell activation. IL6, IL21, and IL23 receptors all activate STAT3, which can bind and activate IL17 and IL21 genes directly. The expression and activity of STAT3 is increased in SLE T-cells. IL17 gene transcription is regulated by the retinoid related orphan receptor, RORC transcription factor. RORC is expressed exclusively in Th17 cells and is necessary for IL17 production. In addition, altered T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling can also lead to Th17 expression.    Outcome effects: There is evidence of an increased level of IL17 in the circulation and tissues of SLE patients. IL17 can induce the production of an array of inflammatory molecules such as cytokines, chemokines, and MMP from immune and non-immune cells, leading to the recruitment and activation of inflammatory cells along with tissue damage in SLE.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

