xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Aberrant T-cell activation due to abnormal antigen presenting cell (APC) function is one of the main molecular pathological mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: T-cells have a central role in the pathogenesis of SLE. At the same time, the role of Th1 and Th2 cytokines in the pathogenesis of SLE is controversial. In patients with SLE, Th2-related cytokines are increased, while Th1 cytokines are decreased. Thus, SLE was initially considered to be a Th2 predominant disease. However, it appears that Th1 or Th2 dominance depends on the stage of the disease and involvement. One of the possible mechanisms is through the increase in calcium levels. This occurs due to the pathological activation of SYK signaling.    Outcome effects: At the end of the SYK signaling cascade, the expression of IL4, IL5, IL9, IL13, and CD40LG is up-regulated. Finally, the Th2 response promotes the production of high amounts of antibodies by B lymphocytes and induces the survival of eosinophils.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Aberrant T-cell activation due to abnormal antigen presenting cell (APC) function is one of the main molecular pathological mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: T-cells have a central role in the pathogenesis of SLE. At the same time, the role of Th1 and Th2 cytokines in the pathogenesis of SLE is controversial. In patients with SLE, Th2-related cytokines are increased, while Th1 cytokines are decreased. Thus, SLE was initially considered to be a Th2 predominant disease. However, it appears that Th1 or Th2 dominance depends on the stage of the disease and involvement. One of the possible mechanisms is through the increase in calcium levels. This occurs due to the pathological activation of SYK signaling.    Outcome effects: At the end of the SYK signaling cascade, the expression of IL4, IL5, IL9, IL13, and CD40LG is up-regulated. Finally, the Th2 response promotes the production of high amounts of antibodies by B lymphocytes and induces the survival of eosinophils.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

