xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. High titers of autoantibodies accompanied by abnormal B-cell activation and differentiation are shown in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: Autoantigens observed in SLE comprise nuclear antigens including dsDNA, the ribonucleoprotein (RNP) complex Ro, the RNA-binding protein La, RNPs, the RNA molecule/protein complex Sm, the C1 complement component subunit C1q, and phospholipids. High levels of CD40LG, IFNB1, and IFNA1 in SLE activate the differentiation of B-cells and production of autoantibodies. A high level of TNFSF13B may result in the rescue of self-reactive B-cells from elimination. The mechanism of this process likely includes activation of the non-classical NF-kB2 pathway by TNFSF13B via TNFRSF13C, which facilitates up-regulation of several antiapoptotic BCL2 family members, thus promoting cell survival. Further, polymorphisms in BANK1, LYN, BLK, and RASGRF1 are thought to alter B-cell activation and increase SLE risk by a yet to be identified mechanism. Overall, the exact process of B-cell activation in lupus is poorly understood. The pathway also reflects one of the possible signaling routes through the B-cell receptor (BCR) complex (IgM, CD79A and CD79B). B lymphocytes from patients with SLE display enhanced BCR-mediated fluxes of intracytoplasmic calcium. The FCGR2B receptor is expressed on B-cells and regulates B-cell survival and proliferation by down-modulating BCR signaling. Polymorphisms that alter the inhibitory function of FCGR2B in B-cells are associated with SLE.    Outcome effects: The pathology of SLE involves alterations of B cell activation and survival through a number of signaling paths including the enhanced (BCR)-mediated signal transduction leading to increased intracytoplasmic calcium, as well as the activation of the non-classical NF-kB2 pathway by TNFSF13B via TNFRSF13C and others.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Genes with mutations linked to SLE are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. High titers of autoantibodies accompanied by abnormal B-cell activation and differentiation are shown in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).    Signaling description: Autoantigens observed in SLE comprise nuclear antigens including dsDNA, the ribonucleoprotein (RNP) complex Ro, the RNA-binding protein La, RNPs, the RNA molecule/protein complex Sm, the C1 complement component subunit C1q, and phospholipids. High levels of CD40LG, IFNB1, and IFNA1 in SLE activate the differentiation of B-cells and production of autoantibodies. A high level of TNFSF13B may result in the rescue of self-reactive B-cells from elimination. The mechanism of this process likely includes activation of the non-classical NF-kB2 pathway by TNFSF13B via TNFRSF13C, which facilitates up-regulation of several antiapoptotic BCL2 family members, thus promoting cell survival. Further, polymorphisms in BANK1, LYN, BLK, and RASGRF1 are thought to alter B-cell activation and increase SLE risk by a yet to be identified mechanism. Overall, the exact process of B-cell activation in lupus is poorly understood. The pathway also reflects one of the possible signaling routes through the B-cell receptor (BCR) complex (IgM, CD79A and CD79B). B lymphocytes from patients with SLE display enhanced BCR-mediated fluxes of intracytoplasmic calcium. The FCGR2B receptor is expressed on B-cells and regulates B-cell survival and proliferation by down-modulating BCR signaling. Polymorphisms that alter the inhibitory function of FCGR2B in B-cells are associated with SLE.    Outcome effects: The pathology of SLE involves alterations of B cell activation and survival through a number of signaling paths including the enhanced (BCR)-mediated signal transduction leading to increased intracytoplasmic calcium, as well as the activation of the non-classical NF-kB2 pathway by TNFSF13B via TNFRSF13C and others.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Genes with mutations linked to SLE are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

