xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Defective clearance of apoptotic cells is implicated in the development of SLE.    Signaling description: Macrophages of SLE patients show an increased expression of genes involved in inflammation and autophagy. Neutrophils from patient with SLE exhibit an abnormal phenotype with decreased ability for phagocytosis of dead cells, as well as the increased apoptosis and aggregation. They release NETs (Neutrophil Extracellular Traps), which contribute to the development of autoimmunity.    Outcome effects: Neutrophils and macrophages show a defect in apoptotic cell clearance and overproduce inflammatory cytokines in patients with SLE.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors. Defective clearance of apoptotic cells is implicated in the development of SLE.    Signaling description: Macrophages of SLE patients show an increased expression of genes involved in inflammation and autophagy. Neutrophils from patient with SLE exhibit an abnormal phenotype with decreased ability for phagocytosis of dead cells, as well as the increased apoptosis and aggregation. They release NETs (Neutrophil Extracellular Traps), which contribute to the development of autoimmunity.    Outcome effects: Neutrophils and macrophages show a defect in apoptotic cell clearance and overproduce inflammatory cytokines in patients with SLE.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

