xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors.    Signaling description: Defects in the complement pathway components are linked to SLE. The principal function of the activated components of the complement system is recognition and clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic cells. Complete deficiencies of complement components including C4, C1q and others predispose for the development of SLE.    Outcome effects: The link between SLE and complement deficiencies can be explained by impaired elimination of apoptotic material or dysregulation of B-cell toleration, which trigger autoimmunity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disease that evolves due to a genetic predisposition and environmental or stochastic stimuli. The most common symptoms of SLE are skin lesions such as erythematous rash, as well as fever, joint and muscle pain, swollen lymph nodes, hair loss and fatigue. The disease is believed to be triggered by a combination of genetic, immune and environmental factors.    Signaling description: Defects in the complement pathway components are linked to SLE. The principal function of the activated components of the complement system is recognition and clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic cells. Complete deficiencies of complement components including C4, C1q and others predispose for the development of SLE.    Outcome effects: The link between SLE and complement deficiencies can be explained by impaired elimination of apoptotic material or dysregulation of B-cell toleration, which trigger autoimmunity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

