xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vitiligo is a chronic pigmentary skin disorder in which patches of skin lose their pigmentation. The disease may also involve mucous membranes and normally affects both sides of the body. Vitiligo is thought to be a disease with autoimmune origin that occurs due to a genetic predisposition. The autoimmune process in vitiligo leads to the destruction of melanocytes in the skin which creates the hypopigmented lesions.    Signaling description: Oxidative stress is considered to be one of the possible pathogenic events in melanocyte loss. Hydrogen peroxide has been shown to be a competitive inhibitor of tyrosinase, therefore it may affect melanin production. Numerous other proteins are also affected by oxidative stress, thus multiplying the effect.    Outcome effects: In addition to the disruption of melanogenesis, the accumulation of ROS in melanocytes may lead to melanocyte destruction through apoptosis, as well as the accumulation of misfolded proteins resulting from the endoplasmic reticulum stress and the production of an \"eat-me\" signal.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vitiligo is a chronic pigmentary skin disorder in which patches of skin lose their pigmentation. The disease may also involve mucous membranes and normally affects both sides of the body. Vitiligo is thought to be a disease with autoimmune origin that occurs due to a genetic predisposition. The autoimmune process in vitiligo leads to the destruction of melanocytes in the skin which creates the hypopigmented lesions.    Signaling description: Oxidative stress is considered to be one of the possible pathogenic events in melanocyte loss. Hydrogen peroxide has been shown to be a competitive inhibitor of tyrosinase, therefore it may affect melanin production. Numerous other proteins are also affected by oxidative stress, thus multiplying the effect.    Outcome effects: In addition to the disruption of melanogenesis, the accumulation of ROS in melanocytes may lead to melanocyte destruction through apoptosis, as well as the accumulation of misfolded proteins resulting from the endoplasmic reticulum stress and the production of an \"eat-me\" signal.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

