synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. One of the major pathologic processes underlying acne vulgaris is the enlargement of sebaceous gland related to the increased proliferation of sebocytes.    Signaling description: Various factors provoke the increased sebocyte proliferation leading to the enlargement of sebaceous gland. For instance, milk consumption and growth hormone (GH1) stimulate IGF1 expression. Milk consumption also stimulates the INS expression. IGF1 and INS bind to their receptors and activate the MAPK signaling. The downstream signal of the activated MAPK pathway initiates cyclin D1 (CCND1) transcription via the functional interaction with JUN/FOS transcription factors including CCND1, a key regulator of cell proliferation. FGF7, FGF10, and POMC affect the same signaling through PKC and PKA, respectively. Also, in patients with acne reduced amounts of linoleic acid (which is an inhibitor of ROS formation), cause an increased production of ROS in the damaged follicular wall. G6PD and CAT are normally involved in the reduction of ROS formation; however, these enzymes are downregulated in acne vulgaris. Further, ROS interact with membrane lipids and cause the formation of cytotoxic lipid peroxides; at the same time, ROS can oxidize squalene. This leads to the upregulation of lipoxygenase (ALOX5) activity is through the exposure of peroxidated squalene products to the cell. ALOX5, along with LTA4H, converts arachidonic acid into LTB4. Lipid peroxides and LTB4 promote cell proliferation via PPARA and PPARG activation. These transcription factors are also upregulated by free fatty acids which enter the cell along with LDLR and SLC27A4. Free fatty acids are formed in the by the lipoprotein lipase of the gram-positive anaerobic bacterium Propionibacterium acnes. This is the third pathologic mechanism leading to excessive sebocyte proliferation in acne vulgaris.    Outcome effects: Increased sebocyte proliferation is a key process leading to the formation of acne lesions. Sebocyte proliferation may be induced by milk consumption and GH1 via the stimulation of IGF1 expression. At the same time, ROS formation may contribute to cell proliferation through the induction of PPARG . In addition, Propionibacterium acnes, a gram-positive anaerobic bacterium known to play a role in acne formation produce fatty acids which interact with PRARA and PRARG similarly leading to sebocyte proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. One of the major pathologic processes underlying acne vulgaris is the enlargement of sebaceous gland related to the increased proliferation of sebocytes.    Signaling description: Various factors provoke the increased sebocyte proliferation leading to the enlargement of sebaceous gland. For instance, milk consumption and growth hormone (GH1) stimulate IGF1 expression. Milk consumption also stimulates the INS expression. IGF1 and INS bind to their receptors and activate the MAPK signaling. The downstream signal of the activated MAPK pathway initiates cyclin D1 (CCND1) transcription via the functional interaction with JUN/FOS transcription factors including CCND1, a key regulator of cell proliferation. FGF7, FGF10, and POMC affect the same signaling through PKC and PKA, respectively. Also, in patients with acne reduced amounts of linoleic acid (which is an inhibitor of ROS formation), cause an increased production of ROS in the damaged follicular wall. G6PD and CAT are normally involved in the reduction of ROS formation; however, these enzymes are downregulated in acne vulgaris. Further, ROS interact with membrane lipids and cause the formation of cytotoxic lipid peroxides; at the same time, ROS can oxidize squalene. This leads to the upregulation of lipoxygenase (ALOX5) activity is through the exposure of peroxidated squalene products to the cell. ALOX5, along with LTA4H, converts arachidonic acid into LTB4. Lipid peroxides and LTB4 promote cell proliferation via PPARA and PPARG activation. These transcription factors are also upregulated by free fatty acids which enter the cell along with LDLR and SLC27A4. Free fatty acids are formed in the by the lipoprotein lipase of the gram-positive anaerobic bacterium Propionibacterium acnes. This is the third pathologic mechanism leading to excessive sebocyte proliferation in acne vulgaris.    Outcome effects: Increased sebocyte proliferation is a key process leading to the formation of acne lesions. Sebocyte proliferation may be induced by milk consumption and GH1 via the stimulation of IGF1 expression. At the same time, ROS formation may contribute to cell proliferation through the induction of PPARG . In addition, Propionibacterium acnes, a gram-positive anaerobic bacterium known to play a role in acne formation produce fatty acids which interact with PRARA and PRARG similarly leading to sebocyte proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

