xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. Increased sebum production by the sebaceous gland, known as hyperseborrhea, is an important factor in the formation of acne lesions.    Signaling description: Hyperseborrhea results from the increased fatty acid biosynthesis provoked by the following factors: IGF1, INS, FGF7, FGF10, POMC, and free fatty acids. Milk consumption and growth hormone (GH1) are shown to induce the hepatic synthesis and secretion of IGF1. In turn, IGF1, INS, FGF7, and FGF10 activate PI3K/AKT1 pathway. AKT1-mediated FOXO1 phosphorylation leads to the relief of SREBF1 and promotes fatty acid biosynthesis. In addition, increased MC1R expression is also observed in sebocytes in acne vulgaris. Proopiomelanocortin (POMC) binds to MC1R and via the PKA axis activates CREB1.    Outcome effects: Free fatty acids contribute to the increased lipogenesis in sebocytes. Following their translocation via LDLR and SLC27A4 into the cell, the free fatty acids bind PPARG and PPARA. CREB1, PPARG, and PPARA are transcription factors that promote fatty acid biosynthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. Increased sebum production by the sebaceous gland, known as hyperseborrhea, is an important factor in the formation of acne lesions.    Signaling description: Hyperseborrhea results from the increased fatty acid biosynthesis provoked by the following factors: IGF1, INS, FGF7, FGF10, POMC, and free fatty acids. Milk consumption and growth hormone (GH1) are shown to induce the hepatic synthesis and secretion of IGF1. In turn, IGF1, INS, FGF7, and FGF10 activate PI3K/AKT1 pathway. AKT1-mediated FOXO1 phosphorylation leads to the relief of SREBF1 and promotes fatty acid biosynthesis. In addition, increased MC1R expression is also observed in sebocytes in acne vulgaris. Proopiomelanocortin (POMC) binds to MC1R and via the PKA axis activates CREB1.    Outcome effects: Free fatty acids contribute to the increased lipogenesis in sebocytes. Following their translocation via LDLR and SLC27A4 into the cell, the free fatty acids bind PPARG and PPARA. CREB1, PPARG, and PPARA are transcription factors that promote fatty acid biosynthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

