xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. One of the major pathologic processes in the formation of acne lesions is androgen-induced sebocyte hyperfunction.    Signaling description: In the sebocyte, the circulating androgenic pro-hormone, dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) is converted to dihydrotestosterone. Both testosterone and dihydrotestosterone are able to bind the androgen receptor (AR). However, dihydrotestosterone has a ten-fold higher affinity to AR compared to testosterone. Dihydrotestosterone exerts its effect on sebaceous glands by increasing sebocyte proliferation, and elevating lipid production via SREBF1. The increased conversion of DHEA to dihydrotestosterone is mediated by the IGF1-induced SRD5A1 hyperactivity. IGF1 plays an important role in the pathologic process. It induces AR trans-activation and SREBF1 activation. In the nucleus, AR and SREBF1 bind to the repressive protein, FOXO1. IGF1 activates PI3K and causes the AKT1-mediated FOXO1 phosphorylation. Phosphorylated FOXO1 leaves AR and SREBF1, which induces sebocyte hyperfunction.    Outcome effects: Following binding to the AR, dihydrotestosterone initiates transcription of androgen-responsive genes, and thus promotes increased cell proliferation and fatty acid biosynthesis which leads to comedogenesis and hyperseborrhea, respectively.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. One of the major pathologic processes in the formation of acne lesions is androgen-induced sebocyte hyperfunction.    Signaling description: In the sebocyte, the circulating androgenic pro-hormone, dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) is converted to dihydrotestosterone. Both testosterone and dihydrotestosterone are able to bind the androgen receptor (AR). However, dihydrotestosterone has a ten-fold higher affinity to AR compared to testosterone. Dihydrotestosterone exerts its effect on sebaceous glands by increasing sebocyte proliferation, and elevating lipid production via SREBF1. The increased conversion of DHEA to dihydrotestosterone is mediated by the IGF1-induced SRD5A1 hyperactivity. IGF1 plays an important role in the pathologic process. It induces AR trans-activation and SREBF1 activation. In the nucleus, AR and SREBF1 bind to the repressive protein, FOXO1. IGF1 activates PI3K and causes the AKT1-mediated FOXO1 phosphorylation. Phosphorylated FOXO1 leaves AR and SREBF1, which induces sebocyte hyperfunction.    Outcome effects: Following binding to the AR, dihydrotestosterone initiates transcription of androgen-responsive genes, and thus promotes increased cell proliferation and fatty acid biosynthesis which leads to comedogenesis and hyperseborrhea, respectively.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

