xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. Increased keratinocyte proliferation is a key element in the pathogenesis of acne vulgaris.    Signaling description: Hyperkeratinization occurs when the cells of the follicle become cohesive and do not shed normally onto the skin surface. The process caused by keratin excess and is observed in the primary stages of the disease and causes obstruction of the sebaceous gland duct, which provokes sebum storage in the gland and increases the gland size. The main agents inducing hyperkeratinization are IL1A and androgens. Androgens bind to AR causing increased cell proliferation; whereas, IL1A participates in MAPK pathway activation through MYD88/TRAF6 and RAC1.    Outcome effects: As a result of MAPK pathway activation, NF-kB induction similarly causes increased keratinocyte proliferation leading to hyperkeratinization and ultimately the enlargement of sebaceous gland.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder which occurs due to the blockage of hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands with excess sebum and dead keratinocytes. Increased keratinocyte proliferation is a key element in the pathogenesis of acne vulgaris.    Signaling description: Hyperkeratinization occurs when the cells of the follicle become cohesive and do not shed normally onto the skin surface. The process caused by keratin excess and is observed in the primary stages of the disease and causes obstruction of the sebaceous gland duct, which provokes sebum storage in the gland and increases the gland size. The main agents inducing hyperkeratinization are IL1A and androgens. Androgens bind to AR causing increased cell proliferation; whereas, IL1A participates in MAPK pathway activation through MYD88/TRAF6 and RAC1.    Outcome effects: As a result of MAPK pathway activation, NF-kB induction similarly causes increased keratinocyte proliferation leading to hyperkeratinization and ultimately the enlargement of sebaceous gland.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

