xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In normal conditions circulating androgens control adipocyte size, suppress adipose tissue accumulation and improve insulin sensitivity. Obese men show decreased bioavailable levels of androgens and increased levels of estrogens due to an increase in aromatase (CYP19A1) activity that mediates peripheral conversion of androgens to estrogens.    Signaling description: Testosterone can act to androgen receptor (AR) directly or be converted to the more potent androgen 5-dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha-reductase (SRD5A1/2). Increase in circulating estrogenes level lead to an inhibition of the hypothalamic gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GNRH1) secretion and luteinizing hormone pulse which results in a reduction of gonadal testosterone production. In obesity adipose tissue is also able to affect gonadotropin release through increased secretion of cytokines, in particular TNF and IL6. Insulin excess is known to inhibit hepatic synthesis and secretion of sex-hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), the testosterone transporter.Obesity is characterized by development leptin resistance and high level of leptin. Leptin affects testosterone production directly inhibiting Leydig cell function by blocking the steroidogenesis. Testosterone inhibits the activity of glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH), an adipose-specific glycolysis enzyme. It also suppresses lipoprotein lipase (LPL) activity and lipid uptake in adipocytes. Testosterone was shown to downregulate expression of lipolytic enzyme hormone-sensitive lipase (LIPE) and beta-2-adrenoreceptor (ADRB2), which normally drives the lipolysis in adipocytes (it was shown for subcutaneous fat tissue). Normally AR stimulation results in inhibition of adipogenic differentiation and proliferation by directly activating downstream Wnt effector molecules, including beta-catenin (CTNNB1), T-cell factor 4 (TCF4), and lymphoidenhancer factor 1 (LEF1). Inhibition of adipogenesis involves suppression of key adipogenic factors PPARG, CEBPA, and CEBPD.     Outcome effects: In obesity low testosterone level leads to adipocytes hypertrophy and hyperplasia.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In normal conditions circulating androgens control adipocyte size, suppress adipose tissue accumulation and improve insulin sensitivity. Obese men show decreased bioavailable levels of androgens and increased levels of estrogens due to an increase in aromatase (CYP19A1) activity that mediates peripheral conversion of androgens to estrogens.    Signaling description: Testosterone can act to androgen receptor (AR) directly or be converted to the more potent androgen 5-dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha-reductase (SRD5A1/2). Increase in circulating estrogenes level lead to an inhibition of the hypothalamic gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GNRH1) secretion and luteinizing hormone pulse which results in a reduction of gonadal testosterone production. In obesity adipose tissue is also able to affect gonadotropin release through increased secretion of cytokines, in particular TNF and IL6. Insulin excess is known to inhibit hepatic synthesis and secretion of sex-hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), the testosterone transporter.Obesity is characterized by development leptin resistance and high level of leptin. Leptin affects testosterone production directly inhibiting Leydig cell function by blocking the steroidogenesis. Testosterone inhibits the activity of glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH), an adipose-specific glycolysis enzyme. It also suppresses lipoprotein lipase (LPL) activity and lipid uptake in adipocytes. Testosterone was shown to downregulate expression of lipolytic enzyme hormone-sensitive lipase (LIPE) and beta-2-adrenoreceptor (ADRB2), which normally drives the lipolysis in adipocytes (it was shown for subcutaneous fat tissue). Normally AR stimulation results in inhibition of adipogenic differentiation and proliferation by directly activating downstream Wnt effector molecules, including beta-catenin (CTNNB1), T-cell factor 4 (TCF4), and lymphoidenhancer factor 1 (LEF1). Inhibition of adipogenesis involves suppression of key adipogenic factors PPARG, CEBPA, and CEBPD.     Outcome effects: In obesity low testosterone level leads to adipocytes hypertrophy and hyperplasia.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

