xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Adipose tissue was traditionally considered only as energy storage depot in the form of triglycerides. It is now regarded as an active endocrine organ that, in addition to regulating fat mass and nutrient homeostasis, releases a large number of adipokines (including chemokines, cytokines and hormones) modulating hemostasis. In obesity linked insulin resistance, adiponectin is downregulated. ADIPOQ is a well-known adipose-specific adipokine that produces insulin-sensitizing effects.    Signaling description: Adipokines include leptin (LEP), adiponectin (ADIPOQ), resistin (RETN), visfatin (NAMPT), chemerin (RARRES2), adipsin (CFD), intelectin (ITLN1), retinol binding protein 4 (RBP4), vaspin (SERPINA12), TNF, IL6, IL1B, CCl2 and others. In obese subjects LEP, RETN, TNF, IL6, CCL2, IL1B, RBP4 are upregulated, ADIPOQ is downregulated. LEP is a \"satiety hormone\" and is abundantly expressed in adipose tissue. It inhibits appetite and food intake and stimulates energy expenditure. However, circulating leptin level is increased in obese subjects due to the existence of leptin resistance. Both TNF-alpha and IL-6, the most widely studied cytokines produced by adipose tissue, were reported to modulate insulin resistance.Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Adipose tissue was traditionally considered only as energy storage depot in the form of triglycerides. It is now regarded as an active endocrine organ that, in addition to regulating fat mass and nutrient homeostasis, releases a large number of adipokines (including chemokines, cytokines and hormones) modulating hemostasis. In obesity linked insulin resistance, adiponectin is downregulated. ADIPOQ is a well-known adipose-specific adipokine that produces insulin-sensitizing effects.    Signaling description: Adipokines include leptin (LEP), adiponectin (ADIPOQ), resistin (RETN), visfatin (NAMPT), chemerin (RARRES2), adipsin (CFD), intelectin (ITLN1), retinol binding protein 4 (RBP4), vaspin (SERPINA12), TNF, IL6, IL1B, CCl2 and others. In obese subjects LEP, RETN, TNF, IL6, CCL2, IL1B, RBP4 are upregulated, ADIPOQ is downregulated. LEP is a \"satiety hormone\" and is abundantly expressed in adipose tissue. It inhibits appetite and food intake and stimulates energy expenditure. However, circulating leptin level is increased in obese subjects due to the existence of leptin resistance. Both TNF-alpha and IL-6, the most widely studied cytokines produced by adipose tissue, were reported to modulate insulin resistance.Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

