xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type diabetes.    Signaling description: The activation of autoreactive CD8 T-lymphocytes in type1 diabetes mellitus occurs by T-cell receptor (TCR) recognition of self-determinants. A viral infection may initiate an autoimmune reaction against pancreatic beta-cells and trigger type 1 diabetes mellitus. TCR, which consists of TRA@, TRB@, CD3E, CD3D, CD3G, CD247, and FCER1G, interacts with MHC class I protein complex of the host cell containing self-antigens. The coreceptor, CD8A is necessary for the successful T-cell activation. After binding to an antigen, LCK and intracellular TCR domains phosphorylate and activate ZAP70. ZAP70 transduces the signal to PLCG1 through LAT.    Outcome effects: PLCG1 is responsible for the bulk of antigen-stimulated IP3 production in T-cells. IP3 provokes calcium efflux from endoplasmic reticulum and interacts with calmodulin-calcineurin resulting in NF-AT activation. NF-AT is a transcription factor for IFNG and FASLG which are the main T-cell produced substances involved in T-cell killing mechanisms."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type diabetes.    Signaling description: The activation of autoreactive CD8 T-lymphocytes in type1 diabetes mellitus occurs by T-cell receptor (TCR) recognition of self-determinants. A viral infection may initiate an autoimmune reaction against pancreatic beta-cells and trigger type 1 diabetes mellitus. TCR, which consists of TRA@, TRB@, CD3E, CD3D, CD3G, CD247, and FCER1G, interacts with MHC class I protein complex of the host cell containing self-antigens. The coreceptor, CD8A is necessary for the successful T-cell activation. After binding to an antigen, LCK and intracellular TCR domains phosphorylate and activate ZAP70. ZAP70 transduces the signal to PLCG1 through LAT.    Outcome effects: PLCG1 is responsible for the bulk of antigen-stimulated IP3 production in T-cells. IP3 provokes calcium efflux from endoplasmic reticulum and interacts with calmodulin-calcineurin resulting in NF-AT activation. NF-AT is a transcription factor for IFNG and FASLG which are the main T-cell produced substances involved in T-cell killing mechanisms." EXACT []

