xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Regulatory T-cells (Tregs) play a central role in T-cell-mediated immune responses and the development of type 1A and fulminant type 1 diabetes.CD4 FOXP3 T-cells are composed of three phenotypically and functionally distinct subpopulations: CD45RA FOXP3 low resting Tregs (r-Tregs), CD45RA-FOXP3 high activated Tregs (a-Tregs) and CD45RA-FOXP3 low non-suppressive T cells (non-Tregs). Several islet-related autoantibodies, such as anti-GAD2 antibodies, INS autoantibodies, anti-PTPRN antibodies, and anti-SLC30A8 antibodies have been recognized as markers for type 1A diabetes; however, these antibodies are not cytotoxic. Type 1A diabetes is a T cell-mediated disease with autoimmune response against beta-cells; and T-cells are central players in the development of the autoimmune process.    Outcome effects: Tregs play a vital role in suppressing the T cell-mediated immune response. Their dysfunction can cause T cell-mediated autoimmune disease. Several markers for Tregs, including FOXP3, CTLA4, CD25 high (CD25 is the alpha chain of the IL2 receptor), and CD127 low (CD127 is the alpha subunit of the IL7 receptor), have been identified. Among these molecules, CTLA4 may functionally be crucial for Tregs as they suppress the immune response. r-Tregs are able to convert to a-Tregs after and/or during T-cell receptor (TCR) stimulation and suppress the proliferation of responder cells and themselves in a negative feedback manner.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Regulatory T-cells (Tregs) play a central role in T-cell-mediated immune responses and the development of type 1A and fulminant type 1 diabetes.CD4 FOXP3 T-cells are composed of three phenotypically and functionally distinct subpopulations: CD45RA FOXP3 low resting Tregs (r-Tregs), CD45RA-FOXP3 high activated Tregs (a-Tregs) and CD45RA-FOXP3 low non-suppressive T cells (non-Tregs). Several islet-related autoantibodies, such as anti-GAD2 antibodies, INS autoantibodies, anti-PTPRN antibodies, and anti-SLC30A8 antibodies have been recognized as markers for type 1A diabetes; however, these antibodies are not cytotoxic. Type 1A diabetes is a T cell-mediated disease with autoimmune response against beta-cells; and T-cells are central players in the development of the autoimmune process.    Outcome effects: Tregs play a vital role in suppressing the T cell-mediated immune response. Their dysfunction can cause T cell-mediated autoimmune disease. Several markers for Tregs, including FOXP3, CTLA4, CD25 high (CD25 is the alpha chain of the IL2 receptor), and CD127 low (CD127 is the alpha subunit of the IL7 receptor), have been identified. Among these molecules, CTLA4 may functionally be crucial for Tregs as they suppress the immune response. r-Tregs are able to convert to a-Tregs after and/or during T-cell receptor (TCR) stimulation and suppress the proliferation of responder cells and themselves in a negative feedback manner.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

