xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes. Invariant natural killer T (iNKT) cells were shown to protect against type 1 diabetes in various mouse models.    Signaling description: iNKT cells are a specialized subset of innate T- cells that are highly conserved in mammals. Invariant NKT cells recognize lipids presented by CD1D molecules. CD1D is a non-polymorphic MHC class I-like molecule that is expressed on antigen-presenting cells. CD1D is also expressed on non-hematopoietic cells. The iNKT cells recognize their lipid ligands on CD1D through their semi-invariant T-cell receptor (TCR). In humans, iNKT cells express Valpha24-Jalpha18 chain paired almost exclusively with a Vbeta11 chain. Similarly to iNKT cells, CD1D is highly conserved in mammals. The first lipid to be identified as an antigen for iNKT cells is alpha-galactosylceramide (alphaGalCer), which remains the most potent activator of iNKT cells. In addition to recognition of lipids on CD1D through their TCR, iNKT cells can be activated by co-stimulatory signals specifically cytokines such as IL12 and IL18. These cytokines co-stimulate iNKT cells in many important physiological examples of iNKT cell activation. Invariant NKT cells characteristically express high levels of the transcription factor promyelocytic leukemia zinc finger (PLZF) encoded by ZBTB16. Many studies have revealed the protective role of iNKT cells against autoimmune diseases and particularly type 1 diabetes. The activation of iNKT cells, with a specific agonist such as alphaGalCer or its analogues, inhibits the development of type 1 diabetes.    Outcome effects: It was initially proposed that iNKT cell-mediated protection was associated with the induction of Th2 cell responses to islet autoantigens. However, studies analyzing the protection against diabetes induced by the transfer of anti-islet CD4 and CD8 - cells have revealed that iNKT cells impair the differentiation of these pathogenic T-cells. The autoreactive T-cells become anergic and do not destroy pancreatic islets in mouse model of diabetes. The abortive priming of anti-islet T-cells in pancreatic lymph nodes can be explained by the ability of iNKT cells to promote the recruitment of tolerogenic DCs. It has been shown that iNKT cells prevent and even cure type 1 diabetes in various mouse models. These observations are encouraging for the further development of new therapeutic strategies based on iNKT cell targeting. Upon infection of beta cells, iNKT cells promote the recruitment of plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) specifically in the pancreas and their production of type 1 IFN, a major antiviral cytokine. This local iNKT-pDC crosstalk is dependent on the TNFRSF4-TNFSF4 pathway. Invariant NKT cells specifically express TNFRSF4 in pancreatic tissue but not in lymphoid tissues. As a result, in the pancreas (but not in the spleen), iNKT cells enhance IFNA1 production by pDCs and inhibit local viral replication. Thus, the diabetogenic effect can be controlled by efficient innate immune cell crosstalk, limiting the activation of diabetogenic T-cells. Following viral infection, iNKT cell-pDC crosstalk dampens diabetogenic CD8 T-cell responses in the pancreas. In this infectious context, iNKT cells producing IL10 in the pancreatic lymph nodes promote the production of TGFB1 by pDCs.    Animal data: iNKT cells were shown to protect against type 1 diabetes in various mouse models.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes. Invariant natural killer T (iNKT) cells were shown to protect against type 1 diabetes in various mouse models.    Signaling description: iNKT cells are a specialized subset of innate T- cells that are highly conserved in mammals. Invariant NKT cells recognize lipids presented by CD1D molecules. CD1D is a non-polymorphic MHC class I-like molecule that is expressed on antigen-presenting cells. CD1D is also expressed on non-hematopoietic cells. The iNKT cells recognize their lipid ligands on CD1D through their semi-invariant T-cell receptor (TCR). In humans, iNKT cells express Valpha24-Jalpha18 chain paired almost exclusively with a Vbeta11 chain. Similarly to iNKT cells, CD1D is highly conserved in mammals. The first lipid to be identified as an antigen for iNKT cells is alpha-galactosylceramide (alphaGalCer), which remains the most potent activator of iNKT cells. In addition to recognition of lipids on CD1D through their TCR, iNKT cells can be activated by co-stimulatory signals specifically cytokines such as IL12 and IL18. These cytokines co-stimulate iNKT cells in many important physiological examples of iNKT cell activation. Invariant NKT cells characteristically express high levels of the transcription factor promyelocytic leukemia zinc finger (PLZF) encoded by ZBTB16. Many studies have revealed the protective role of iNKT cells against autoimmune diseases and particularly type 1 diabetes. The activation of iNKT cells, with a specific agonist such as alphaGalCer or its analogues, inhibits the development of type 1 diabetes.    Outcome effects: It was initially proposed that iNKT cell-mediated protection was associated with the induction of Th2 cell responses to islet autoantigens. However, studies analyzing the protection against diabetes induced by the transfer of anti-islet CD4 and CD8 - cells have revealed that iNKT cells impair the differentiation of these pathogenic T-cells. The autoreactive T-cells become anergic and do not destroy pancreatic islets in mouse model of diabetes. The abortive priming of anti-islet T-cells in pancreatic lymph nodes can be explained by the ability of iNKT cells to promote the recruitment of tolerogenic DCs. It has been shown that iNKT cells prevent and even cure type 1 diabetes in various mouse models. These observations are encouraging for the further development of new therapeutic strategies based on iNKT cell targeting. Upon infection of beta cells, iNKT cells promote the recruitment of plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) specifically in the pancreas and their production of type 1 IFN, a major antiviral cytokine. This local iNKT-pDC crosstalk is dependent on the TNFRSF4-TNFSF4 pathway. Invariant NKT cells specifically express TNFRSF4 in pancreatic tissue but not in lymphoid tissues. As a result, in the pancreas (but not in the spleen), iNKT cells enhance IFNA1 production by pDCs and inhibit local viral replication. Thus, the diabetogenic effect can be controlled by efficient innate immune cell crosstalk, limiting the activation of diabetogenic T-cells. Following viral infection, iNKT cell-pDC crosstalk dampens diabetogenic CD8 T-cell responses in the pancreas. In this infectious context, iNKT cells producing IL10 in the pancreatic lymph nodes promote the production of TGFB1 by pDCs.    Animal data: iNKT cells were shown to protect against type 1 diabetes in various mouse models.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

