xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: CD4 FOXP3 T-cells are composed of three phenotypically and functionally distinct subpopulations: CD45RA FOXP3 low resting Tregs (r-Tregs), CD45RA-FOXP3 high activated Tregs (a-Tregs) and CD45RA-FOXP3 low non-suppressive T cells (non-Tregs).    Outcome effects: Non-Tregs, which can produce IL2, IFNG, and IL17A, are not able to suppress the responder cells. Genetic defects in non-Tregs in type 1 diabetes do not reduce the non-suppressive function, yet they increase the expression of proinflammatory cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: CD4 FOXP3 T-cells are composed of three phenotypically and functionally distinct subpopulations: CD45RA FOXP3 low resting Tregs (r-Tregs), CD45RA-FOXP3 high activated Tregs (a-Tregs) and CD45RA-FOXP3 low non-suppressive T cells (non-Tregs).    Outcome effects: Non-Tregs, which can produce IL2, IFNG, and IL17A, are not able to suppress the responder cells. Genetic defects in non-Tregs in type 1 diabetes do not reduce the non-suppressive function, yet they increase the expression of proinflammatory cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

