xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Studies support a diabetogenic role of plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) in the initiatory steps of type 1 diabetes. pDCs are professional antiviral cells that are able to detect viral RNA or DNA through TLR7 and TLR9 and in turn produce large amounts of antiviral cytokines, such as type 1 IFNs. In humans, conflicting results have been obtained regarding the frequency of pDCs in the blood of diabetic patients compared to healthy controls. However, pDCs from early diagnosed patients are able to present antigen to T-cells and activate them.    Outcome effects: In type 1 diabetes, type 1 IFNs produced in pancreatic islets by pDCs may induce or promote the development of the disease.    Animal data: A pathogenic role of pDCs in type 1 diabetes is supported by observations in both humans and rodent models.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Studies support a diabetogenic role of plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) in the initiatory steps of type 1 diabetes. pDCs are professional antiviral cells that are able to detect viral RNA or DNA through TLR7 and TLR9 and in turn produce large amounts of antiviral cytokines, such as type 1 IFNs. In humans, conflicting results have been obtained regarding the frequency of pDCs in the blood of diabetic patients compared to healthy controls. However, pDCs from early diagnosed patients are able to present antigen to T-cells and activate them.    Outcome effects: In type 1 diabetes, type 1 IFNs produced in pancreatic islets by pDCs may induce or promote the development of the disease.    Animal data: A pathogenic role of pDCs in type 1 diabetes is supported by observations in both humans and rodent models.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

