xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: The role of toll-like receptors (TLRs) in the pathogenesis of type 1 diabetes has been extensively studied. As key receptors of the innate immune system, TLRs trigger inflammatory immune responses upon binding to cognate ligands. On a predisposed genetic background, this event may initiate islet inflammation followed by progressive beta-cell destruction and finally overt type 1 diabetes. The accumulation of apoptotic beta-cells, which may undergo secondary necrosis may result in the activation of antigen-presenting cells (APC) via TLR engagement by released endogenous molecules and thereby contributing to the induction of diabetogenic T-cells. Regulatory T-cells (Treg) are TLR-expressing adaptive immune cells. In the presence of beta-cell antigens, TLR2 signaling may, while inducing a pro-inflammatory immune response simultaneously promote suppressive Treg functions.    Outcome effects: Upon their induction, diabetogenic T-cells are recruited to the pancreatic islets by chemokines such as CCL2, CCL5, CXCL9, and CXCL10 which can be released from beta-cells upon TLR ligation. Consistent with this (Hypothesis) is the fact that TLR2 induces apoptosis and promotes diabetes in a mouse model following activation via the synthetic ligand Pam3CSK4. The observation of delayed or reduced diabetes onset in TLR9-deficient mice further supports the theory. With regard to Treg cells, it has been demonstrated that TLR2 stimulation results in a temporary abrogation of the regulatory capacity of Treg. In addition to TLR2, other TLRs also modulate Treg functions. TLR4 and TLR5 ligands are capable of boosting the suppressive function of Treg on T-effector cells. Taken together, TLRs act as important modulators of Treg proliferation and function. Treg function can either be enhanced or attenuated depending on the concentration of TLR ligands and the anatomical site of TLR engagement.    Animal data: TLR2 induces apoptosis and promotes diabetes in mouse model.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: The role of toll-like receptors (TLRs) in the pathogenesis of type 1 diabetes has been extensively studied. As key receptors of the innate immune system, TLRs trigger inflammatory immune responses upon binding to cognate ligands. On a predisposed genetic background, this event may initiate islet inflammation followed by progressive beta-cell destruction and finally overt type 1 diabetes. The accumulation of apoptotic beta-cells, which may undergo secondary necrosis may result in the activation of antigen-presenting cells (APC) via TLR engagement by released endogenous molecules and thereby contributing to the induction of diabetogenic T-cells. Regulatory T-cells (Treg) are TLR-expressing adaptive immune cells. In the presence of beta-cell antigens, TLR2 signaling may, while inducing a pro-inflammatory immune response simultaneously promote suppressive Treg functions.    Outcome effects: Upon their induction, diabetogenic T-cells are recruited to the pancreatic islets by chemokines such as CCL2, CCL5, CXCL9, and CXCL10 which can be released from beta-cells upon TLR ligation. Consistent with this (Hypothesis) is the fact that TLR2 induces apoptosis and promotes diabetes in a mouse model following activation via the synthetic ligand Pam3CSK4. The observation of delayed or reduced diabetes onset in TLR9-deficient mice further supports the theory. With regard to Treg cells, it has been demonstrated that TLR2 stimulation results in a temporary abrogation of the regulatory capacity of Treg. In addition to TLR2, other TLRs also modulate Treg functions. TLR4 and TLR5 ligands are capable of boosting the suppressive function of Treg on T-effector cells. Taken together, TLRs act as important modulators of Treg proliferation and function. Treg function can either be enhanced or attenuated depending on the concentration of TLR ligands and the anatomical site of TLR engagement.    Animal data: TLR2 induces apoptosis and promotes diabetes in mouse model.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

