xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes. Natural killer (NK) cells are implicated in the destruction of beta-cells in type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: NK cells are involved in antiviral and anti-tumor responses directly through killing target cells or indirectly by producing IFNG. NK cells have been detected in the pancreas of patients with type 1 diabetes where they have a higher expression of CD25, CD69, PDCD1, and KLRG1. In addition, natural killer group 2, member D, encoded by KLRK1 and NCR1 are expressed by NK cells. Recent studies suggest that their ligands, ULBP and NCR1 ligand, respectively are expressed by diabetic beta-cells.    Outcome effects: NK cells may contribute directly or indirectly to the destruction of pancreatic beta-cells. In type 1 diabetes, NK cells exhibit a higher proliferation rate, produce higher levels of IFNG and express LAMP1, a marker of granule exocytosis on their cell surface, reflecting their cytotoxic function.    Animal data: NK cells have been detected in the pancreas in large numbers in mouse models of type 1 diabetes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes. Natural killer (NK) cells are implicated in the destruction of beta-cells in type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: NK cells are involved in antiviral and anti-tumor responses directly through killing target cells or indirectly by producing IFNG. NK cells have been detected in the pancreas of patients with type 1 diabetes where they have a higher expression of CD25, CD69, PDCD1, and KLRG1. In addition, natural killer group 2, member D, encoded by KLRK1 and NCR1 are expressed by NK cells. Recent studies suggest that their ligands, ULBP and NCR1 ligand, respectively are expressed by diabetic beta-cells.    Outcome effects: NK cells may contribute directly or indirectly to the destruction of pancreatic beta-cells. In type 1 diabetes, NK cells exhibit a higher proliferation rate, produce higher levels of IFNG and express LAMP1, a marker of granule exocytosis on their cell surface, reflecting their cytotoxic function.    Animal data: NK cells have been detected in the pancreas in large numbers in mouse models of type 1 diabetes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

