xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) are expressed by pancreatic beta-cells, and their function is to detect danger from microbial products. It has been shown that these TLRs may trigger type 1 diabetes, specifically during a localized viral infection in the pancreas.    Outcome effects: Cytokines such as IL1B, IL12B, IFNG, and TNF are secreted by immune cells including macrophages, dendritic cells (DCs), CD4 and CD8 T-cells, and natural killer (NK) cells and may cause direct damage (e.g. apoptosis) of beta-cells. Chemokines such as CXCL10, CCL2, and CCL20 are secreted by beta-cells and can recruit macrophages and other inflammatory cells to the islet area.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) are expressed by pancreatic beta-cells, and their function is to detect danger from microbial products. It has been shown that these TLRs may trigger type 1 diabetes, specifically during a localized viral infection in the pancreas.    Outcome effects: Cytokines such as IL1B, IL12B, IFNG, and TNF are secreted by immune cells including macrophages, dendritic cells (DCs), CD4 and CD8 T-cells, and natural killer (NK) cells and may cause direct damage (e.g. apoptosis) of beta-cells. Chemokines such as CXCL10, CCL2, and CCL20 are secreted by beta-cells and can recruit macrophages and other inflammatory cells to the islet area.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

