xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: It has long been recognized that macrophages are implicated in the pathogenesis of type 1 diabetes. TNF- and IL1B-producing macrophages have been observed in pancreatic islet infiltrates from patients with recent-onset type 1 diabetes. These cells are detected in the islet infiltrates of young NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mice. Inhibition of the influx of these cells into the pancreas by blocking an adhesion-promoting receptor on the cell surface or their depletion, prevents the development of type 1 diabetes. Research suggests that the recruitment of macrophages to islets is mediated by the secretion of CCL1 and CCL2 by CD4 T-cells and pancreatic beta-cells, respectively.    Outcome effects: Macrophages recruited to the pancreas produce IL1B, TNF, and reactive oxygen species (ROS) which may cause beta-cell death. In vivo and in vitro studies in rodents have demonstrated that macrophages may play a pathogenic role on beta-cells via the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as TNF and IL1B. Macrophages produce IL12 and promote efficient differentiation of diabetogenic CD8 cytotoxic T-lymphocytes (CTls) leading the onset of type 1 diabetes.    Animal data: TNF- and IL1B-producing macrophages were detected in the islet infiltrates of young NOD mice.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: It has long been recognized that macrophages are implicated in the pathogenesis of type 1 diabetes. TNF- and IL1B-producing macrophages have been observed in pancreatic islet infiltrates from patients with recent-onset type 1 diabetes. These cells are detected in the islet infiltrates of young NOD (Non-Obese Diabetic) mice. Inhibition of the influx of these cells into the pancreas by blocking an adhesion-promoting receptor on the cell surface or their depletion, prevents the development of type 1 diabetes. Research suggests that the recruitment of macrophages to islets is mediated by the secretion of CCL1 and CCL2 by CD4 T-cells and pancreatic beta-cells, respectively.    Outcome effects: Macrophages recruited to the pancreas produce IL1B, TNF, and reactive oxygen species (ROS) which may cause beta-cell death. In vivo and in vitro studies in rodents have demonstrated that macrophages may play a pathogenic role on beta-cells via the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as TNF and IL1B. Macrophages produce IL12 and promote efficient differentiation of diabetogenic CD8 cytotoxic T-lymphocytes (CTls) leading the onset of type 1 diabetes.    Animal data: TNF- and IL1B-producing macrophages were detected in the islet infiltrates of young NOD mice.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

