xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: In diabetes mellitus type 1, impaired peripheral tolerance to autoantigens including insulin, glutamate decarboxylase 2, and protein tyrosine phosphatase can occur due to the disruption of signal transduction in regulatory T-lymphocytes (Treg), which normally suppress autoaggressive T-cells during maturation. The process occurs in the thymus where MHC class II protein complex (MHCII) with autoantigens on the surface of dendritic cells binds to T-cell receptor (TCR) of Treg cells. In healthy individuals, the signaling triggers FOXP3 activation in Treg cells. FOXP3 is a transcription factor for immunosuppressive cytokines such as interleukin 10 (IL10) and transforming growth factor beta (TGFB).    Outcome effects: The mechanism of FOXP3 in Treg cells is the following: the signal from TCR is transduced to ZAP70 and then to LAT. In turn, LAT activates PLCG1 and GRB2. PLCG1 participates in diacylglycerol and inositol trisphosphate (IP3) generation. IP3 then provokes calcium efflux from endoplasmic reticulum which results in NF-AT activation through calcineurin. Diacylglycerol activates NF-kB through PRKCQ, while LAT induces JUN/FOS through GRB2/SOS1/Ras/RAF1/MAPK and activates NF-AT. Further, NF-AT and NF-kB bind to the promoter region of FOXP3 and initiate its transcription. Treg cells require antigen presentation by MHCII molecules, presence of co-stimulatory molecules, stimulation of high-affinity interleukin 2 (IL2) receptor by exogenous IL2, and cell-to-cell contact for maturation. The stimulation of IL2 receptor activates JAK1 and JAK3 which phosphorylate STAT5A which is then STAT5A translocated into the nucleus where it upregulates the synthesis of intracellular FOXP3. Mutations in HLA-DQA1, HLA-DQB1, HLA-DRB3, HLA-DRB4, and FOXP3 have a strong association with diabetes mellitus type 1.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: In diabetes mellitus type 1, impaired peripheral tolerance to autoantigens including insulin, glutamate decarboxylase 2, and protein tyrosine phosphatase can occur due to the disruption of signal transduction in regulatory T-lymphocytes (Treg), which normally suppress autoaggressive T-cells during maturation. The process occurs in the thymus where MHC class II protein complex (MHCII) with autoantigens on the surface of dendritic cells binds to T-cell receptor (TCR) of Treg cells. In healthy individuals, the signaling triggers FOXP3 activation in Treg cells. FOXP3 is a transcription factor for immunosuppressive cytokines such as interleukin 10 (IL10) and transforming growth factor beta (TGFB).    Outcome effects: The mechanism of FOXP3 in Treg cells is the following: the signal from TCR is transduced to ZAP70 and then to LAT. In turn, LAT activates PLCG1 and GRB2. PLCG1 participates in diacylglycerol and inositol trisphosphate (IP3) generation. IP3 then provokes calcium efflux from endoplasmic reticulum which results in NF-AT activation through calcineurin. Diacylglycerol activates NF-kB through PRKCQ, while LAT induces JUN/FOS through GRB2/SOS1/Ras/RAF1/MAPK and activates NF-AT. Further, NF-AT and NF-kB bind to the promoter region of FOXP3 and initiate its transcription. Treg cells require antigen presentation by MHCII molecules, presence of co-stimulatory molecules, stimulation of high-affinity interleukin 2 (IL2) receptor by exogenous IL2, and cell-to-cell contact for maturation. The stimulation of IL2 receptor activates JAK1 and JAK3 which phosphorylate STAT5A which is then STAT5A translocated into the nucleus where it upregulates the synthesis of intracellular FOXP3. Mutations in HLA-DQA1, HLA-DQB1, HLA-DRB3, HLA-DRB4, and FOXP3 have a strong association with diabetes mellitus type 1.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

