xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) are professional antiviral cells that are primarily located in the blood and lymphoid organs and produce high levels of type 1 interferon (IFN) following the engagement of toll-like receptors (TLRs) with foreign nucleic acids. pDCs express a restricted set of TLRs, including TLR7 and TLR9 which recognize foreign RNA and DNA, respectively.    Outcome effects: Plasmacytoid (p)DC are thought to be involved in both pathogenesis and prevention of type 1 diabetes.    Animal data: The majority of data was collected from experiments in NOD mice.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) are professional antiviral cells that are primarily located in the blood and lymphoid organs and produce high levels of type 1 interferon (IFN) following the engagement of toll-like receptors (TLRs) with foreign nucleic acids. pDCs express a restricted set of TLRs, including TLR7 and TLR9 which recognize foreign RNA and DNA, respectively.    Outcome effects: Plasmacytoid (p)DC are thought to be involved in both pathogenesis and prevention of type 1 diabetes.    Animal data: The majority of data was collected from experiments in NOD mice.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

