xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: FOXP3 regulatory T cells (Treg) can be divided into three different subpopulations namely a-Tregs, r-Tregs, and non-Tregs. Among them, a-Tregs play the most important role as suppressors of immune reactions. The mechanism of suppressive function by a-Tregs has not been fully elucidated, but the increase in the level of soluble CTLA4 may be the result of a compensatory mechanism for the functional defects of a-Tregs in type 1A diabetes. a-Tregs express intracellular CTLA4 and CCR4 to the highest degree among the three subpopulations of Tregs.    Outcome effects: a-Tregs are anergic and suppress the proliferation of responder cells and other FOXP3 Tregs (r-Tregs and non-Tregs). The IL2 signaling pathway in a-Tregs is also important in understanding the impaired function in a-Tregs.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus type 1 is an autoimmune disease in which most of the pancreatic beta-cells are attacked and destroyed by T-cells. As a result, the pancreas does not produce an adequate amount of insulin leading to increased blood sugar levels. There is a genetic predisposition to type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: FOXP3 regulatory T cells (Treg) can be divided into three different subpopulations namely a-Tregs, r-Tregs, and non-Tregs. Among them, a-Tregs play the most important role as suppressors of immune reactions. The mechanism of suppressive function by a-Tregs has not been fully elucidated, but the increase in the level of soluble CTLA4 may be the result of a compensatory mechanism for the functional defects of a-Tregs in type 1A diabetes. a-Tregs express intracellular CTLA4 and CCR4 to the highest degree among the three subpopulations of Tregs.    Outcome effects: a-Tregs are anergic and suppress the proliferation of responder cells and other FOXP3 Tregs (r-Tregs and non-Tregs). The IL2 signaling pathway in a-Tregs is also important in understanding the impaired function in a-Tregs.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

