xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Type 2 diabetes is a chronic metabolic disease characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Early symptoms include frequent urination, increased thirst, weight loss, increased hunger and fatigue. Long-term symptoms include heart disease, strokes, diabetic retinopathy, and kidney failure. Diabetes mellitus type 2 involves impaired insulin sensitivity (insulin resistance) and decreased insulin secretion at the final stages of disease. The origin of the pathology is complex and has various triggers such as a high-fat diet and obesity with a genetic predisposition to hypertension.    Signaling description: There is no strong association between exact mutations and disease, and only the presence of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in several genes in one person could lead to the disease. The disease originates when insulin-dependent tissues suffer from a lack of glucose due to insulin resistance. There is a compensation mechanism that occurs in which insulin secretion increases to maintain normoglycemia. The disease progression is accompanied by endocrine pancreatic cell exhaust resulting in beta-cell destruction. The destruction of pancreatic beta-cells in type 2 diabetes mellitus occurs gradually. The first stage is characterized by the maintenance of differentiated function with intact acute glucose-stimulated insulin. In normal cases, intracellular glucose is metabolized to ATP in glycolysis. The elevation in the ATP/ADP ratio induces the closure of cell-surface ATP-sensitive K channels (KCNJ11) leading to cell membrane depolarization. Cell-surface voltage-dependent calcium (Ca2+) channels open facilitating extracellular Ca2+ influx into the beta-cell. Ca2 activates phospholipase C (PLC) and the additional Ca2+ portion is released from intracellular storage.    Outcome effects: Normally, a rise in free cytosolic Ca2+ triggers the exocytosis of insulin but constant glucose intake through GLUT2 (SLC2A2) leads to a permanent cell membrane depolarization and Ca2+ influx. An excessive amount of intracellular Ca2+ triggers CYCS release and cell apoptosis. The products of glycolysis (NADPH) are involved in the following stages of energy metabolism. NADHP is used in tricarboxylic acid cycle where NADH is formed. NADH is a substrate for ATP production during oxidative phosphorylation. Intensive oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria generates reactive oxygen species (ROS) which are not eliminated due to their abundant amount. ROS trigger oxidative stress in cells. Under physiological conditions, glucose triggers the expression of insulin transcription factors, PDX1 and MAFA which positively regulate islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP). Therefore, elevated intracellular glucose triggers their synthesis and insulin transcription occurs. IAPP then accumulates in the cell disrupting cell metabolism.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Type 2 diabetes is a chronic metabolic disease characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Early symptoms include frequent urination, increased thirst, weight loss, increased hunger and fatigue. Long-term symptoms include heart disease, strokes, diabetic retinopathy, and kidney failure. Diabetes mellitus type 2 involves impaired insulin sensitivity (insulin resistance) and decreased insulin secretion at the final stages of disease. The origin of the pathology is complex and has various triggers such as a high-fat diet and obesity with a genetic predisposition to hypertension.    Signaling description: There is no strong association between exact mutations and disease, and only the presence of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in several genes in one person could lead to the disease. The disease originates when insulin-dependent tissues suffer from a lack of glucose due to insulin resistance. There is a compensation mechanism that occurs in which insulin secretion increases to maintain normoglycemia. The disease progression is accompanied by endocrine pancreatic cell exhaust resulting in beta-cell destruction. The destruction of pancreatic beta-cells in type 2 diabetes mellitus occurs gradually. The first stage is characterized by the maintenance of differentiated function with intact acute glucose-stimulated insulin. In normal cases, intracellular glucose is metabolized to ATP in glycolysis. The elevation in the ATP/ADP ratio induces the closure of cell-surface ATP-sensitive K channels (KCNJ11) leading to cell membrane depolarization. Cell-surface voltage-dependent calcium (Ca2+) channels open facilitating extracellular Ca2+ influx into the beta-cell. Ca2 activates phospholipase C (PLC) and the additional Ca2+ portion is released from intracellular storage.    Outcome effects: Normally, a rise in free cytosolic Ca2+ triggers the exocytosis of insulin but constant glucose intake through GLUT2 (SLC2A2) leads to a permanent cell membrane depolarization and Ca2+ influx. An excessive amount of intracellular Ca2+ triggers CYCS release and cell apoptosis. The products of glycolysis (NADPH) are involved in the following stages of energy metabolism. NADHP is used in tricarboxylic acid cycle where NADH is formed. NADH is a substrate for ATP production during oxidative phosphorylation. Intensive oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria generates reactive oxygen species (ROS) which are not eliminated due to their abundant amount. ROS trigger oxidative stress in cells. Under physiological conditions, glucose triggers the expression of insulin transcription factors, PDX1 and MAFA which positively regulate islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP). Therefore, elevated intracellular glucose triggers their synthesis and insulin transcription occurs. IAPP then accumulates in the cell disrupting cell metabolism.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

