xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Type 2 diabetes is a chronic metabolic disease characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Early symptoms include frequent urination, increased thirst, weight loss, increased hunger and fatigue. Long-term symptoms include heart disease, strokes, diabetic retinopathy, and kidney failure. Diabetes mellitus type 2 involves impaired insulin sensitivity (insulin resistance) and decreased insulin secretion at the final stages of disease. The origin of the pathology is complex and has various triggers such as a high-fat diet and obesity with a genetic predisposition to hypertension.    Signaling description: Dietary carbohydrates in humans are digested prior to absorption. Starch is digested to dextrins by alpha-amylases (salivary and pancreatic). Both maltase-glucoamylase (MGAM) and sucrase-isomaltase (SI) digest starch, dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. SI also converts sucrose to fructose and glucose. LCT converts lactose to galactose and glucose. Glucose and galactose are initially transported into the enterocyte by SLC5A1; while, fructose is transported by SLC2A2 or SLC2A5 located in the apical brush border membrane; and then, all of them exit the enterocyte through the basolateral membrane by either SLC2A2 or exocytosis. Transport by SLC5A1 induces rapid insertion and activation of SLC2A2 in the brush border membrane by a PRKCB-dependent mechanism. Moreover, the trafficking of apical SLC2A2 is rapidly up-regulated by glucose-mediated signaling through TAS1R2/3 activating PLCB2 and PRKCB.    Outcome effects: Due to their function at the final steps of starch digestion MGAM and SI are important target enzymes for the treatment of type-2 diabetes. MGAM inhibitors such as miglitol and acarbose are used in patients with diabetes mellitus type 2 to prevent the breakdown of complex carbohydrates into glucose.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Type 2 diabetes is a chronic metabolic disease characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Early symptoms include frequent urination, increased thirst, weight loss, increased hunger and fatigue. Long-term symptoms include heart disease, strokes, diabetic retinopathy, and kidney failure. Diabetes mellitus type 2 involves impaired insulin sensitivity (insulin resistance) and decreased insulin secretion at the final stages of disease. The origin of the pathology is complex and has various triggers such as a high-fat diet and obesity with a genetic predisposition to hypertension.    Signaling description: Dietary carbohydrates in humans are digested prior to absorption. Starch is digested to dextrins by alpha-amylases (salivary and pancreatic). Both maltase-glucoamylase (MGAM) and sucrase-isomaltase (SI) digest starch, dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. SI also converts sucrose to fructose and glucose. LCT converts lactose to galactose and glucose. Glucose and galactose are initially transported into the enterocyte by SLC5A1; while, fructose is transported by SLC2A2 or SLC2A5 located in the apical brush border membrane; and then, all of them exit the enterocyte through the basolateral membrane by either SLC2A2 or exocytosis. Transport by SLC5A1 induces rapid insertion and activation of SLC2A2 in the brush border membrane by a PRKCB-dependent mechanism. Moreover, the trafficking of apical SLC2A2 is rapidly up-regulated by glucose-mediated signaling through TAS1R2/3 activating PLCB2 and PRKCB.    Outcome effects: Due to their function at the final steps of starch digestion MGAM and SI are important target enzymes for the treatment of type-2 diabetes. MGAM inhibitors such as miglitol and acarbose are used in patients with diabetes mellitus type 2 to prevent the breakdown of complex carbohydrates into glucose.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity is highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

