xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. DN is usually considered a primarily glomerular disease, although this contention has been challenged. The role of tubulointerstitial injury in the development of DN has recently been considered. The tubulointerstitial abnormalities are involved at a later stage in the evolution of DN than the glomerulus disturbances, but the damage to this compartment correlates much better with renal dysfunction.    Signaling description: Many mechanisms relevant to tubulointerstitial pathobiology are similar to those in the renal glomerulus. Epithelial cells of the renal tubules play a central role in the development of tubulointerstitial damage. The activation of the epithelium is due to permanent reabsorption of protein. Persistent albuminuria has a toxic effect on the interstitium. Glucose induces the formation of diacylglycerol which activates PRKCA and stimulates NOX4 which is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER activates mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway and via PI3K activates the AKT1/NF-kB signaling pathway. Overexpressed TGFB1 activates TGFBR2 and interacts with SMAD 2/3/4 which mediates mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) and Janus kinase (JAK) cascades. In the tubular interstitium during progressive DN, there is an increased expression of STAT1,3,4,5A, and 5B and a reduced expression of PIAS1,2,3,4, which inhibit STAT activation.    Outcome effects: The activation of these pathways leads to the epithelial tubular cell proliferation and oxidative stress, the production of collagen (I, III, IV, VI types), fibronectin (FN1), and matrix metallopeptidases (MMP 2, 3, 9, and 12) which are involved in fibrosis and extracellular matrix degradation, macrophage chemotaxis, and ultimately inflammation and tubulointerstitial sclerosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. DN is usually considered a primarily glomerular disease, although this contention has been challenged. The role of tubulointerstitial injury in the development of DN has recently been considered. The tubulointerstitial abnormalities are involved at a later stage in the evolution of DN than the glomerulus disturbances, but the damage to this compartment correlates much better with renal dysfunction.    Signaling description: Many mechanisms relevant to tubulointerstitial pathobiology are similar to those in the renal glomerulus. Epithelial cells of the renal tubules play a central role in the development of tubulointerstitial damage. The activation of the epithelium is due to permanent reabsorption of protein. Persistent albuminuria has a toxic effect on the interstitium. Glucose induces the formation of diacylglycerol which activates PRKCA and stimulates NOX4 which is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER activates mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway and via PI3K activates the AKT1/NF-kB signaling pathway. Overexpressed TGFB1 activates TGFBR2 and interacts with SMAD 2/3/4 which mediates mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) and Janus kinase (JAK) cascades. In the tubular interstitium during progressive DN, there is an increased expression of STAT1,3,4,5A, and 5B and a reduced expression of PIAS1,2,3,4, which inhibit STAT activation.    Outcome effects: The activation of these pathways leads to the epithelial tubular cell proliferation and oxidative stress, the production of collagen (I, III, IV, VI types), fibronectin (FN1), and matrix metallopeptidases (MMP 2, 3, 9, and 12) which are involved in fibrosis and extracellular matrix degradation, macrophage chemotaxis, and ultimately inflammation and tubulointerstitial sclerosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

