xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. Chronic hyperglycemia induces dysfunction in various cell types in the kidney including endothelial cells. Endothelial dysfunction has been implicated as a major mechanism for renal chronic microvascular complications in diabetic patients.    Signaling description: Glomerular endothelial cells are highly specialized cells with regions of attenuated cytoplasm punctuated by numerous circular transcellular pores called fenestrae. High glucose promotes TGFB1 transcription in the kidney and activates TGFBR2 which interacts with SMAD 2/3/4 to form a heterodimeric complex which translocates into the nucleus and regulates target genes such as endothelin (EDN1). In addition, activated TGFBR2 mediates mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs).    Outcome effects: The overproduction of EDN1 causes a reduction of glomerular filtration. Podocytes and proximal tubular epithelial cells are likely the major sources for VEGFA. Further, VEGFA activates vascular endothelial growth factor receptors which mediate MAPK and JAK (Janus kinase) cascades. These kinases participate in endothelial cell proliferation. Progressive endothelial cell proliferation results in decreased glomerular filtration rate and ultimately renal failure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. Chronic hyperglycemia induces dysfunction in various cell types in the kidney including endothelial cells. Endothelial dysfunction has been implicated as a major mechanism for renal chronic microvascular complications in diabetic patients.    Signaling description: Glomerular endothelial cells are highly specialized cells with regions of attenuated cytoplasm punctuated by numerous circular transcellular pores called fenestrae. High glucose promotes TGFB1 transcription in the kidney and activates TGFBR2 which interacts with SMAD 2/3/4 to form a heterodimeric complex which translocates into the nucleus and regulates target genes such as endothelin (EDN1). In addition, activated TGFBR2 mediates mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs).    Outcome effects: The overproduction of EDN1 causes a reduction of glomerular filtration. Podocytes and proximal tubular epithelial cells are likely the major sources for VEGFA. Further, VEGFA activates vascular endothelial growth factor receptors which mediate MAPK and JAK (Janus kinase) cascades. These kinases participate in endothelial cell proliferation. Progressive endothelial cell proliferation results in decreased glomerular filtration rate and ultimately renal failure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

