xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. Podocyte injury contributes to basement membrane thickening and albuminuria as observed in early stages of DN as well as glomerular sclerosis and proteinuria in progressive stages of DN.    Signaling description: Podocytes are specialized cells in the Bowman's capsule in the kidney which surround the capillaries of the glomeruli. Podocytes play a key role in glomerular structural integrity and glomerular filtration barrier. Podocytes express angiotensin II receptors (AGTR1). Over-activation of AGTR1 leads to the activation of HIF1A which inhibits the NPHS1 gene expression which codes for nephrin. Nephrin functions in the glomerular filtration barrier in the kidney and forms the slit diaphragm of glomerular podocytes. The loss of the slit diaphragm and failure of the podocyte structure leads to proteinuria. Glucose induces the formation of diacylglycerol that activates PRKCB. Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER, via PI3K and PRKCB, activate AKT1 which is involved in the inhibition of nephrin expression due to IKBK pathway. High glucose levels promote the transcription of TNF in the kidney. Activated TNFRSF1A up-regulates mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs).    Outcome effects: MAPKs are involved in the activation of caspases, as well as the regulation of podocin (NPHS2) and collagen expression. Further, caspases mediate podocyte apoptosis, whereas, podocin regulates glomerular permeability and collagen (I, III, IV, VI types) is involved in the extracellular matrix polymerization leading to the basement membrane thickening and glomerular sclerosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. Podocyte injury contributes to basement membrane thickening and albuminuria as observed in early stages of DN as well as glomerular sclerosis and proteinuria in progressive stages of DN.    Signaling description: Podocytes are specialized cells in the Bowman's capsule in the kidney which surround the capillaries of the glomeruli. Podocytes play a key role in glomerular structural integrity and glomerular filtration barrier. Podocytes express angiotensin II receptors (AGTR1). Over-activation of AGTR1 leads to the activation of HIF1A which inhibits the NPHS1 gene expression which codes for nephrin. Nephrin functions in the glomerular filtration barrier in the kidney and forms the slit diaphragm of glomerular podocytes. The loss of the slit diaphragm and failure of the podocyte structure leads to proteinuria. Glucose induces the formation of diacylglycerol that activates PRKCB. Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER, via PI3K and PRKCB, activate AKT1 which is involved in the inhibition of nephrin expression due to IKBK pathway. High glucose levels promote the transcription of TNF in the kidney. Activated TNFRSF1A up-regulates mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs).    Outcome effects: MAPKs are involved in the activation of caspases, as well as the regulation of podocin (NPHS2) and collagen expression. Further, caspases mediate podocyte apoptosis, whereas, podocin regulates glomerular permeability and collagen (I, III, IV, VI types) is involved in the extracellular matrix polymerization leading to the basement membrane thickening and glomerular sclerosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

