xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. DN is characterized by renal remodeling which includes hypertrophy, increase in the thickness of glomerular basement membranes, extracellular matrix accumulation, and consequently progressive glomerulosclerosis.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction underlies the basic pathophysiology of microvascular complications of diabetes. Glomerular endothelial cells are highly specialized cells with regions of attenuated cytoplasm punctuated by numerous circular transcellular pores called fenestrae. Early clinical DN is characterized by elevated glomerular filtration associated with vasodilation. Glomerular hyperfiltration leads to the basement membrane thickening, which is the earliest change seen in diabetic nephropathy. Hyperglycemia is the major initiating factor in the pathogenesis of diabetic microvascular complications. Glucose induces the formation of diacylglycerol and activates PRKCA which stimulates NOX4 that is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate the advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). In turn, AGER activates the mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway, and via PI3K activates the AKT1/NF-kB signaling pathway.    Outcome effects: The activation of these pathways leads to endothelial cell proliferation and overexpression of NOS3, adhesion molecules, and interleukins such as IL6 and IL1A. NOS3 produces the main vasodilator, nitric oxide (NO) and adhesion molecules such as ICAM1, VCAM, and SELE which mediate the leukocyte-endothelial cell adhesion and interleukins involved in macrophage chemotaxis. Taken together, these events lead to the progressive abnormality of glomerular filtration and subsequent glomerular and interstitial tissue fibrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. DN is characterized by renal remodeling which includes hypertrophy, increase in the thickness of glomerular basement membranes, extracellular matrix accumulation, and consequently progressive glomerulosclerosis.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction underlies the basic pathophysiology of microvascular complications of diabetes. Glomerular endothelial cells are highly specialized cells with regions of attenuated cytoplasm punctuated by numerous circular transcellular pores called fenestrae. Early clinical DN is characterized by elevated glomerular filtration associated with vasodilation. Glomerular hyperfiltration leads to the basement membrane thickening, which is the earliest change seen in diabetic nephropathy. Hyperglycemia is the major initiating factor in the pathogenesis of diabetic microvascular complications. Glucose induces the formation of diacylglycerol and activates PRKCA which stimulates NOX4 that is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate the advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). In turn, AGER activates the mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway, and via PI3K activates the AKT1/NF-kB signaling pathway.    Outcome effects: The activation of these pathways leads to endothelial cell proliferation and overexpression of NOS3, adhesion molecules, and interleukins such as IL6 and IL1A. NOS3 produces the main vasodilator, nitric oxide (NO) and adhesion molecules such as ICAM1, VCAM, and SELE which mediate the leukocyte-endothelial cell adhesion and interleukins involved in macrophage chemotaxis. Taken together, these events lead to the progressive abnormality of glomerular filtration and subsequent glomerular and interstitial tissue fibrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

