xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. Clinically progressive DN is characterized by the decrease in glomerular filtration and albuminuria which are associated with the development of characteristic histopathologic features including the thickening of the glomerular basement membrane and mesangial expansion due to the accumulation of extracellular matrix proteins. As albuminuria progresses and renal insufficiency ensues, glomerulosclerosis, arteriolar hyalinosis, and interstitial fibrosis develop.    Signaling description: The enhanced expression of TGFB1 in renal cells can lead to glomerular mesangial cell hypertrophy and glomerular fibrosis by extracellular matrix degradation. Mesangial expansion and the degree of fibrosis correlate inversely with glomerular filtration rate in humans with diabetic kidney disease and appear to be critical steps in the progression of diabetic nephropathy to end-stage renal disease. Mesangial cells are specialized cells around blood vessels of the glomeruli which offer structural support and regulate filtration rate. High glucose levels increase the transcription of TGFB1 in the kidney and activate TGFBR2 which interacts with SMAD 2/3/4 to form a heterotetrameric complex that translocates to the nucleus and regulates target genes such as transcription factor SP1 involved in cell proliferation. Activated TGFBR2 mediates mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK) and stress-activated protein kinases (SAPK) cascades. Mesangial cells express angiotensin II receptors (AGTR1). The overactivation of AGTR1 leads to the activation of the MAPK cascade.    Outcome effects: MAP kinases regulate the transcription of fibronectin (FN1) and matrix metallopeptidases (MMP 2, 3, 9, and 12) which are involved in extracellular matrix degradation and inflammation via IL6 and TNF. Diabetes is associated with an increase in PRKCB activity related to increased glucose-induced formation of diacylglycerol (DAG). Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER, via PI3K, activates AKT1 which induces cell growth.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetic nephropathy (DN) is a major diabetic complication. It is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease and a contributor to significant mortality in patients with diabetes. The exact mechanisms leading to the development and progression of renal injury in diabetes are not well understood. Clinically progressive DN is characterized by the decrease in glomerular filtration and albuminuria which are associated with the development of characteristic histopathologic features including the thickening of the glomerular basement membrane and mesangial expansion due to the accumulation of extracellular matrix proteins. As albuminuria progresses and renal insufficiency ensues, glomerulosclerosis, arteriolar hyalinosis, and interstitial fibrosis develop.    Signaling description: The enhanced expression of TGFB1 in renal cells can lead to glomerular mesangial cell hypertrophy and glomerular fibrosis by extracellular matrix degradation. Mesangial expansion and the degree of fibrosis correlate inversely with glomerular filtration rate in humans with diabetic kidney disease and appear to be critical steps in the progression of diabetic nephropathy to end-stage renal disease. Mesangial cells are specialized cells around blood vessels of the glomeruli which offer structural support and regulate filtration rate. High glucose levels increase the transcription of TGFB1 in the kidney and activate TGFBR2 which interacts with SMAD 2/3/4 to form a heterotetrameric complex that translocates to the nucleus and regulates target genes such as transcription factor SP1 involved in cell proliferation. Activated TGFBR2 mediates mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK) and stress-activated protein kinases (SAPK) cascades. Mesangial cells express angiotensin II receptors (AGTR1). The overactivation of AGTR1 leads to the activation of the MAPK cascade.    Outcome effects: MAP kinases regulate the transcription of fibronectin (FN1) and matrix metallopeptidases (MMP 2, 3, 9, and 12) which are involved in extracellular matrix degradation and inflammation via IL6 and TNF. Diabetes is associated with an increase in PRKCB activity related to increased glucose-induced formation of diacylglycerol (DAG). Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER). AGER, via PI3K, activates AKT1 which induces cell growth.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

