xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer.    Signaling description: The hexosamine pathway (HP) is ibvolved in the development of diabetic microangiopathy. HP represents a consecutive conversion of glucose into UDP-acetyl-D-glucosamine (UDP-GlcNAc), which is a substrate for O-GlcNAc transferase (OGT). The enzyme catalyzes the O-linked addition of GlcNAc to serine and threonine residues of cytosolic and nuclear proteins. The mechanism by which an increased flux through HP mediates hyperglycemia-induced transcription activation remains elusive, but there are data suggesting that the covalent modification of SP1 by N-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) may explain the link between the activation of HP and hyperglycemia-induced changes in transcription of SERPINE1 and TGFB1 genes in vascular smooth muscle cells.    Outcome effects: SERPINE1 inhibits plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) disrupting fibrinolysis. The disruption of fibrinolysis is the cause for vascular occlusion in diabetes. TGFB1 causes FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression increasing blood vessel permeability and thickening of capillary basement membranes. These processes are the origin of the edema and the cause of exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in tissue.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, small molecules with elevated level are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer.    Signaling description: The hexosamine pathway (HP) is ibvolved in the development of diabetic microangiopathy. HP represents a consecutive conversion of glucose into UDP-acetyl-D-glucosamine (UDP-GlcNAc), which is a substrate for O-GlcNAc transferase (OGT). The enzyme catalyzes the O-linked addition of GlcNAc to serine and threonine residues of cytosolic and nuclear proteins. The mechanism by which an increased flux through HP mediates hyperglycemia-induced transcription activation remains elusive, but there are data suggesting that the covalent modification of SP1 by N-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) may explain the link between the activation of HP and hyperglycemia-induced changes in transcription of SERPINE1 and TGFB1 genes in vascular smooth muscle cells.    Outcome effects: SERPINE1 inhibits plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) disrupting fibrinolysis. The disruption of fibrinolysis is the cause for vascular occlusion in diabetes. TGFB1 causes FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression increasing blood vessel permeability and thickening of capillary basement membranes. These processes are the origin of the edema and the cause of exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in tissue.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, small molecules with elevated level are highlighted in red." EXACT []

