xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer.    Signaling description: Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) are formed by the non-enzymatic reaction of glucose and other glycating compounds, derived from increased glucose and fatty acid oxidation in endothelial cells. AGE signaling in the vessel wall provokes vascular occlusion and leukocyte recruitment. AGE interacts with the vascular wall by binding to its cellular receptor, AGER. HMGB1, S100A8, and S100A12 also bind and activate AGER in diabetes. The AGE-AGER interaction induces NADPH oxidase activation. The enzyme is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation and thereby cellular oxidant stress. ROS affect HRAS and NF-kB directly. HRAS, along with KRAS also mediates NF-kB activation, transducing the signal through PI3K. Generated by PI3K, PIP3 initiates AKT1 activation by PDPK1. AKT1 provokes the release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA.    Outcome effects: Free NF-kB promotes ICAM1, VCAM1, SERPINE1, and EDN1 transcription. A critical consequence of VCAM1 and ICAM1 expression on the endothelial surface is the attraction of mononuclear phagocytes into the vessel wall that promote inflammation. SERPINE1 transcription contributes to vascular occlusion because it inhibits plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) preventing fibrinolysis. EDN1 expression promotes vasoconstriction. Therefore, impaired blood flow and inflammation in the vessel wall are the main consequences of AGEs.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer.    Signaling description: Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) are formed by the non-enzymatic reaction of glucose and other glycating compounds, derived from increased glucose and fatty acid oxidation in endothelial cells. AGE signaling in the vessel wall provokes vascular occlusion and leukocyte recruitment. AGE interacts with the vascular wall by binding to its cellular receptor, AGER. HMGB1, S100A8, and S100A12 also bind and activate AGER in diabetes. The AGE-AGER interaction induces NADPH oxidase activation. The enzyme is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation and thereby cellular oxidant stress. ROS affect HRAS and NF-kB directly. HRAS, along with KRAS also mediates NF-kB activation, transducing the signal through PI3K. Generated by PI3K, PIP3 initiates AKT1 activation by PDPK1. AKT1 provokes the release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA.    Outcome effects: Free NF-kB promotes ICAM1, VCAM1, SERPINE1, and EDN1 transcription. A critical consequence of VCAM1 and ICAM1 expression on the endothelial surface is the attraction of mononuclear phagocytes into the vessel wall that promote inflammation. SERPINE1 transcription contributes to vascular occlusion because it inhibits plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) preventing fibrinolysis. EDN1 expression promotes vasoconstriction. Therefore, impaired blood flow and inflammation in the vessel wall are the main consequences of AGEs.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

