xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. In diabetes, hyperglycemia is the major underlying cause of diabetic microangiopathy.    Signaling description: Protein kinase C (PKC) activation by diacylglycerol is implicated in diabetic microangiopathy. The major source of diacylglycerol, which is elevated in diabetes, is intracellular hyperglycemia which induces de novo synthesis of diacylglycerol which is formed from the glycolytic intermediate, dihydroxyacetone phosphate, through the reduction of the latter to glycerol-3-phosphate and stepwise acylation (not shown). PKC beta-II (PRKCB) is an essential isozyme among the PKC isoforms, which is activated in vascular tissues during hyperglycemia. Due to the PRKCB activation, NF-kB is released from its inhibitor, NFKBIA and binds to the promoter regions of TGFB1 and EDN1 genes. Elevated EDN1 levels are observed in diabetic patients. TGFB1 triggers FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression. Diacylglycerol similarly primarily affects PRKCD in diabetic edotheliocytes, and via MAPK-cascades, activates JUN which is a transcription factor for FN1.    Outcome effects: Elevation of extracellular FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI proteins leads to the thickening of capillary basement membranes which is the result of exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in tissue. The expression of EDN1, which is a powerful vasoconstrictor, contributes to the elevation of blood pressure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. In diabetes, hyperglycemia is the major underlying cause of diabetic microangiopathy.    Signaling description: Protein kinase C (PKC) activation by diacylglycerol is implicated in diabetic microangiopathy. The major source of diacylglycerol, which is elevated in diabetes, is intracellular hyperglycemia which induces de novo synthesis of diacylglycerol which is formed from the glycolytic intermediate, dihydroxyacetone phosphate, through the reduction of the latter to glycerol-3-phosphate and stepwise acylation (not shown). PKC beta-II (PRKCB) is an essential isozyme among the PKC isoforms, which is activated in vascular tissues during hyperglycemia. Due to the PRKCB activation, NF-kB is released from its inhibitor, NFKBIA and binds to the promoter regions of TGFB1 and EDN1 genes. Elevated EDN1 levels are observed in diabetic patients. TGFB1 triggers FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression. Diacylglycerol similarly primarily affects PRKCD in diabetic edotheliocytes, and via MAPK-cascades, activates JUN which is a transcription factor for FN1.    Outcome effects: Elevation of extracellular FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI proteins leads to the thickening of capillary basement membranes which is the result of exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in tissue. The expression of EDN1, which is a powerful vasoconstrictor, contributes to the elevation of blood pressure.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

