xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer. The polyol pathway is believed to play a role in diabetic microangiopathy.    Signaling description: The polyol pathway of glucose metabolism becomes active only when intracellular glucose levels are elevated. Aldose reductase (AKR1B1), the first and rate-limiting enzyme of the pathway reduces glucose to sorbitol using NADPH as a cofactor. Sorbitol is then metabolized into fructose by sorbitol dehydrogenase (SORD) with NAD usage and NADP production during the reaction. Sorbitol is a hydrophilic molecule and therefore does not diffuse through the cell membrane; thus, it accumulates in the cell inducing osmotic stress. The fructose produced by the polyol pathway can be phosphorylated to fructose-3-phosphate which is broken down to 3-deoxyglucosone. Both compounds are powerful glycosylating agents that are involved in the formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs). The usage of NADPH by AKR1B1 may lead to less cofactor available for glutathione reductase (GSR). GSR is critical for the maintenance of the intracellular pool of reduced glutathione (GSH). This would reduce the ability of the cell to respond to oxidative stress. The usage of NAD by SORD leads to an increased ratio of NADH/NAD, which has been termed \"pseudohypoxia\" and is linked to multiple metabolic and signaling changes known to alter cell functioning. It has been proposed that excess NADH may become a substrate for NADH oxidase and this would be a mechanism for the generation of reactive oxygen species.    Outcome effects: Thus, activation of the polyol pathway by altering intracellular tonicity, generating AGE precursors, and exposing cells to oxidative stress possibly through decreased antioxidant defenses and generation of oxidant species can initiate and multiply several mechanisms of cellular damage.:     Highlighted proteins: Small molcecules with elevated level are highlighted in red, and small moleculces with reduced level is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer. The polyol pathway is believed to play a role in diabetic microangiopathy.    Signaling description: The polyol pathway of glucose metabolism becomes active only when intracellular glucose levels are elevated. Aldose reductase (AKR1B1), the first and rate-limiting enzyme of the pathway reduces glucose to sorbitol using NADPH as a cofactor. Sorbitol is then metabolized into fructose by sorbitol dehydrogenase (SORD) with NAD usage and NADP production during the reaction. Sorbitol is a hydrophilic molecule and therefore does not diffuse through the cell membrane; thus, it accumulates in the cell inducing osmotic stress. The fructose produced by the polyol pathway can be phosphorylated to fructose-3-phosphate which is broken down to 3-deoxyglucosone. Both compounds are powerful glycosylating agents that are involved in the formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs). The usage of NADPH by AKR1B1 may lead to less cofactor available for glutathione reductase (GSR). GSR is critical for the maintenance of the intracellular pool of reduced glutathione (GSH). This would reduce the ability of the cell to respond to oxidative stress. The usage of NAD by SORD leads to an increased ratio of NADH/NAD, which has been termed \"pseudohypoxia\" and is linked to multiple metabolic and signaling changes known to alter cell functioning. It has been proposed that excess NADH may become a substrate for NADH oxidase and this would be a mechanism for the generation of reactive oxygen species.    Outcome effects: Thus, activation of the polyol pathway by altering intracellular tonicity, generating AGE precursors, and exposing cells to oxidative stress possibly through decreased antioxidant defenses and generation of oxidant species can initiate and multiply several mechanisms of cellular damage.:     Highlighted proteins: Small molcecules with elevated level are highlighted in red, and small moleculces with reduced level is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

