xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer.    Signaling description: Several pathways are activated in this complication such as the accelerated formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs), polyol, hexosamine, and protein kinase C (PKC) pathways. AGE, hexosamine, and PKC pathways provoke impairment in blood flow end exchange diffusion and result in PAI-1 (SERPINE1) transcription. SERPINE1 inhibits plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) disrupting fibrinolysis. The disruption of fibrinolysis is the underlying cause for vascular occlusion in diabetes. TGFB1 causes FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression, thus increasing blood vessel permeability and thickening of capillary basement membranes. These processes are the origin of edema and cause of the exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in the tissue. AGE-AGER interaction mediates NF-kB activation. The signal is transduced from AGER to KRAS which affects PI3K. Generated by PI3K, PIP3 initiates AKT1 activation by PDPK1. AKT1 provokes the release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA and free NF-kB promotes ICAM1, VCAM1, SERPINE1, and EDN1 transcription.    Outcome effects: A critical consequence of VCAM1 and ICAM1 expression on the endothelial surface is the attraction of mononuclear phagocytes into the vessel wall that promotes inflammation. EDN1 causes vasoconstriction impairing blood flow in the tissue. Furthermore, the activation of the polyol pathway promotes reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation exposing cells to oxidative stress, AGE precursor formation (methylglyoxal) leading to vessel wall damage. Normally, ischemia (hypoxia) provokes the release of HIF1A from its inhibitor, EGLN2. Free HIF1A with its interacting proteins, EP300 and ARNT trigger the expression of vascularization factors including CXCL12, VEGFA, CXCR4, and NOS3. In the pathology, HIF1A and EP300 are inhibited by methylglyoxal preventing vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of disorders characterized by abnormally high blood sugar levels and insulin resistance. Diabetic microangiopathy is one of the major chronic complications of diabetes affecting small blood vessels. Microangiopathy in peripheral tissue is a diabetic complication which can have severe consequences such as the development of an ulcer.    Signaling description: Several pathways are activated in this complication such as the accelerated formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs), polyol, hexosamine, and protein kinase C (PKC) pathways. AGE, hexosamine, and PKC pathways provoke impairment in blood flow end exchange diffusion and result in PAI-1 (SERPINE1) transcription. SERPINE1 inhibits plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) disrupting fibrinolysis. The disruption of fibrinolysis is the underlying cause for vascular occlusion in diabetes. TGFB1 causes FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression, thus increasing blood vessel permeability and thickening of capillary basement membranes. These processes are the origin of edema and cause of the exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in the tissue. AGE-AGER interaction mediates NF-kB activation. The signal is transduced from AGER to KRAS which affects PI3K. Generated by PI3K, PIP3 initiates AKT1 activation by PDPK1. AKT1 provokes the release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA and free NF-kB promotes ICAM1, VCAM1, SERPINE1, and EDN1 transcription.    Outcome effects: A critical consequence of VCAM1 and ICAM1 expression on the endothelial surface is the attraction of mononuclear phagocytes into the vessel wall that promotes inflammation. EDN1 causes vasoconstriction impairing blood flow in the tissue. Furthermore, the activation of the polyol pathway promotes reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation exposing cells to oxidative stress, AGE precursor formation (methylglyoxal) leading to vessel wall damage. Normally, ischemia (hypoxia) provokes the release of HIF1A from its inhibitor, EGLN2. Free HIF1A with its interacting proteins, EP300 and ARNT trigger the expression of vascularization factors including CXCL12, VEGFA, CXCR4, and NOS3. In the pathology, HIF1A and EP300 are inhibited by methylglyoxal preventing vascularization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

