xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of diabetic neuropathy (DN), resulting in significant morbidity and increased mortality. DN is characterized by a progressive loss of nerve fiber function. Clinically peripheral DN is characterized by pain or burning sensations, numbness, or tingling of limbs. DN is implicated in 50-75% of non-traumatic amputations. DN is characterized by microvascular injury which involves small blood vessels which supply the nerves (vasa nervorum) and results from chronic hyperglycemia, which is considered the major initiating factor in the pathogenesis of DN.    Signaling description: Elevated glucose causes hyperosmotic stress and cell damage due to intracellular accumulation of sorbitol and fructose. Excess glucose enters the polyol pathway where AKR1B1 reduces glucose to sorbitol, and SORD oxidizes sorbitol to fructose. Diabetes is characterized by the disruption of inositol synthesis.    Outcome effects: Disruption of inositol synthesis results in the reduced activity of Na /K -ATPase and PKC, impaired axonal transport, and structural injury of nerves.    Highlighted proteins: Small molecules with elevated level are highlighted in red. Small molecules with decreased level are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of diabetic neuropathy (DN), resulting in significant morbidity and increased mortality. DN is characterized by a progressive loss of nerve fiber function. Clinically peripheral DN is characterized by pain or burning sensations, numbness, or tingling of limbs. DN is implicated in 50-75% of non-traumatic amputations. DN is characterized by microvascular injury which involves small blood vessels which supply the nerves (vasa nervorum) and results from chronic hyperglycemia, which is considered the major initiating factor in the pathogenesis of DN.    Signaling description: Elevated glucose causes hyperosmotic stress and cell damage due to intracellular accumulation of sorbitol and fructose. Excess glucose enters the polyol pathway where AKR1B1 reduces glucose to sorbitol, and SORD oxidizes sorbitol to fructose. Diabetes is characterized by the disruption of inositol synthesis.    Outcome effects: Disruption of inositol synthesis results in the reduced activity of Na /K -ATPase and PKC, impaired axonal transport, and structural injury of nerves.    Highlighted proteins: Small molecules with elevated level are highlighted in red. Small molecules with decreased level are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

