xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of diabetic neuropathy (DN), resulting in significant morbidity and increased mortality. DN is characterized by a progressive loss of nerve fiber function. Clinically peripheral DN is characterized by pain or burning sensations, numbness, or tingling of limbs. DN is implicated in 50-75% of non-traumatic amputations. Oxidative stress in neurons and Schwann cells is implicated in DN.    Signaling description: High intracellular causes the formation of diacylglycerol and activates PRKCA which stimulates NOX4; whereas, high extracellular glucose induces the formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs) which activate a specific cell-surface receptor (AGER). In addition, hyperglycemia causes an increase in glucose flux through the hexosamine pathway. Glucose-6-phosphate is converted to fructose-6-phosphate by GPI.GFPT1 then catalyzes the conversion of glucosamine-6-phosphate: GNPNAT1 converts it to N-acetyl-glucosamine-6-phosphate; PGM3 converts N-acetyl-glucosamine-6-phosphate to N-acetyl-glucosamine-1-phosphate, which is converted to UDP-GlcNAc by UAP1. UDP-GlcNAc stimulates SP1 transcription factor, which regulates the expression of PARP1.    Outcome effects: Following its activation by AGEs, AGER induces NOX4, which is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. In turn, PARP1 is a nuclear enzyme closely associated with oxidative-nitrosative stress.    Highlighted proteins: Small molecules with elevated level are highlighted in red. Small molecules with decreased level are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of diabetic neuropathy (DN), resulting in significant morbidity and increased mortality. DN is characterized by a progressive loss of nerve fiber function. Clinically peripheral DN is characterized by pain or burning sensations, numbness, or tingling of limbs. DN is implicated in 50-75% of non-traumatic amputations. Oxidative stress in neurons and Schwann cells is implicated in DN.    Signaling description: High intracellular causes the formation of diacylglycerol and activates PRKCA which stimulates NOX4; whereas, high extracellular glucose induces the formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs) which activate a specific cell-surface receptor (AGER). In addition, hyperglycemia causes an increase in glucose flux through the hexosamine pathway. Glucose-6-phosphate is converted to fructose-6-phosphate by GPI.GFPT1 then catalyzes the conversion of glucosamine-6-phosphate: GNPNAT1 converts it to N-acetyl-glucosamine-6-phosphate; PGM3 converts N-acetyl-glucosamine-6-phosphate to N-acetyl-glucosamine-1-phosphate, which is converted to UDP-GlcNAc by UAP1. UDP-GlcNAc stimulates SP1 transcription factor, which regulates the expression of PARP1.    Outcome effects: Following its activation by AGEs, AGER induces NOX4, which is involved in reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. In turn, PARP1 is a nuclear enzyme closely associated with oxidative-nitrosative stress.    Highlighted proteins: Small molecules with elevated level are highlighted in red. Small molecules with decreased level are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

