xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of diabetic neuropathy (DN), resulting in significant morbidity and increased mortality. DN is characterized by a progressive loss of nerve fiber function. Clinically peripheral DN is characterized by pain or burning sensations, numbness, or tingling of limbs. DN is implicated in 50-75% of non-traumatic amputations. DN is characterized by microvascular injury which involves small blood vessels which supply the nerves (vasa nervorum) and results from chronic hyperglycemia. Microangiopathy is a major initiating factor in the pathogenesis of nerve ischemia.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction has been implicated as a key mechanism for chronic microangiopathy in diabetic patients. High intracellular glucose causes the formation of diacylglycerol and activates PRKCB which stimulates AKT1. In addition, high extracellular glucose induces the formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs). AGEs activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER), which stimulates mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway and activates AKT1/NF-kB signaling pathway via PI3K.    Outcome effects: The activation of these pathways leads to the overexpression of VEGFA, TGFB1, adhesion molecules (ICAM1 and VCAM1), interleukins (IL6, IL1A), and endothelin 1 (EDN1). TGFB1 and VEGFA increase blood vessel permeability and endothelial cell proliferation. Further, ICAM1 and VCAM1 mediate leukocyte-endothelial cell adhesion; while, interleukins mediate macrophage chemotaxis. Finally, EDN1 is responsible for vasoconstriction which results in microangiopathy.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of diabetic neuropathy (DN), resulting in significant morbidity and increased mortality. DN is characterized by a progressive loss of nerve fiber function. Clinically peripheral DN is characterized by pain or burning sensations, numbness, or tingling of limbs. DN is implicated in 50-75% of non-traumatic amputations. DN is characterized by microvascular injury which involves small blood vessels which supply the nerves (vasa nervorum) and results from chronic hyperglycemia. Microangiopathy is a major initiating factor in the pathogenesis of nerve ischemia.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction has been implicated as a key mechanism for chronic microangiopathy in diabetic patients. High intracellular glucose causes the formation of diacylglycerol and activates PRKCB which stimulates AKT1. In addition, high extracellular glucose induces the formation of advanced glycation end products (AGEs). AGEs activate advanced glycosylation end product-specific receptor (AGER), which stimulates mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) pathway and activates AKT1/NF-kB signaling pathway via PI3K.    Outcome effects: The activation of these pathways leads to the overexpression of VEGFA, TGFB1, adhesion molecules (ICAM1 and VCAM1), interleukins (IL6, IL1A), and endothelin 1 (EDN1). TGFB1 and VEGFA increase blood vessel permeability and endothelial cell proliferation. Further, ICAM1 and VCAM1 mediate leukocyte-endothelial cell adhesion; while, interleukins mediate macrophage chemotaxis. Finally, EDN1 is responsible for vasoconstriction which results in microangiopathy.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

