xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Proliferative diabetic retinopathy is a late diabetic complication caused by microangiopathy in the retina. The pathology includes hypoxia, breakage of newly grown capillaries, bleeding, and subsequent fibrosis.    Signaling description: Hypoxia and high glucose levels in diabetes induce VEGFA, EDN1, and AGT expression. The factors that provoke intensive vascularization in the retina are VEGFA (via NF-kB and FOS), EDN1 (via FOS), JUN, MYC, as well as CREBBP and AGT (via STAT3 transcription factor). EDN1, along with its receptor (EDNRA), promotes PLCB1 and PLCB3 activation. These proteins participate in the IP3 and diacylglycerol generation. IP3 causes the release of calcium (Ca2+) through ITPR1. Ca2+ affects calmodulin which transduces the signal to MYLK and then to MYL9 which causes vasoconstriction. In addition, diacylglycerol, activates CREBBP through the Ras/RAF1/MAPK cascade, which leads to differentiation and cell proliferation. Further, VEGFA promotes vascularization via the activation of JUN/FOS transcription factors through SRC/CTNND1/LEF and PI3K/AKT1/CTNNB1/LEF, which promote PTGS2 and NOS2 expression. Hexosamine and advanced glycation end products (AGE) pathways also participate in the manifestation of the pathology. The activation of the hexosamine pathway occurs when excessive intracellular glucose converts into glucosamine-6-P and then to UDP-acetyl-D-glucosamine (UDP-GlcNAc) which is translocated into the nucleus and presumably activates SP1, which initiates transcription of SERPINE1 and TGFB1. SERPINE1 inhibits the plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) disrupting fibrinolysis. AGE binds to its receptor (AGER) and through PI3K activates AKT1. AKT1 initiates the release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA activating it. NF-kB promotes TGFB1, NOS2, and NOS3 transcription which promotes vascularization. TGFB1 activates FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression increasing blood vessel permeability and thickening of capillary basement membranes.    Outcome effects: The activation of CREBBP through the Ras/RAF1/MAPK cascade plays a central role in cell differentiation and proliferation by interacting with a large number of nuclear factors. The disruption of fibrinolysis by SERPINE1 is the cause of vascular occlusion in diabetes. Increased blood vessel permeability and the thickening of capillary basement membranes are the origins of macular edema and the exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in tissues. Newly grown capillaries have a thin wall and are easily broken. These vessels bleed easily and may also pull on the retina causing retinal traction and detachment. Fibrosis of the hemorrhage causes blindness in diabetic patients.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Proliferative diabetic retinopathy is a late diabetic complication caused by microangiopathy in the retina. The pathology includes hypoxia, breakage of newly grown capillaries, bleeding, and subsequent fibrosis.    Signaling description: Hypoxia and high glucose levels in diabetes induce VEGFA, EDN1, and AGT expression. The factors that provoke intensive vascularization in the retina are VEGFA (via NF-kB and FOS), EDN1 (via FOS), JUN, MYC, as well as CREBBP and AGT (via STAT3 transcription factor). EDN1, along with its receptor (EDNRA), promotes PLCB1 and PLCB3 activation. These proteins participate in the IP3 and diacylglycerol generation. IP3 causes the release of calcium (Ca2+) through ITPR1. Ca2+ affects calmodulin which transduces the signal to MYLK and then to MYL9 which causes vasoconstriction. In addition, diacylglycerol, activates CREBBP through the Ras/RAF1/MAPK cascade, which leads to differentiation and cell proliferation. Further, VEGFA promotes vascularization via the activation of JUN/FOS transcription factors through SRC/CTNND1/LEF and PI3K/AKT1/CTNNB1/LEF, which promote PTGS2 and NOS2 expression. Hexosamine and advanced glycation end products (AGE) pathways also participate in the manifestation of the pathology. The activation of the hexosamine pathway occurs when excessive intracellular glucose converts into glucosamine-6-P and then to UDP-acetyl-D-glucosamine (UDP-GlcNAc) which is translocated into the nucleus and presumably activates SP1, which initiates transcription of SERPINE1 and TGFB1. SERPINE1 inhibits the plasminogen activators (PLAT and PLAU) disrupting fibrinolysis. AGE binds to its receptor (AGER) and through PI3K activates AKT1. AKT1 initiates the release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA activating it. NF-kB promotes TGFB1, NOS2, and NOS3 transcription which promotes vascularization. TGFB1 activates FN1, collagen IV, and collagen VI expression increasing blood vessel permeability and thickening of capillary basement membranes.    Outcome effects: The activation of CREBBP through the Ras/RAF1/MAPK cascade plays a central role in cell differentiation and proliferation by interacting with a large number of nuclear factors. The disruption of fibrinolysis by SERPINE1 is the cause of vascular occlusion in diabetes. Increased blood vessel permeability and the thickening of capillary basement membranes are the origins of macular edema and the exchange diffusion impairment (metabolites and oxygen) in tissues. Newly grown capillaries have a thin wall and are easily broken. These vessels bleed easily and may also pull on the retina causing retinal traction and detachment. Fibrosis of the hemorrhage causes blindness in diabetic patients.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

