xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The endocrine section of the pancreas (islets of Langerhans) consists of insulin-secreting beta cells and four other endocrine cell types. Adult beta cells are heterogeneous, with differences in glucose-responsiveness, insulin promoter activity, and insulin protein levels. The endocrine function of beta cells is to respond to increases in blood glucose levels by secreting insulin. Additional beta cell products are amyloid polypeptide (IAPP or amylin) and C-peptide.    Signaling description: During insulin synthesis, preproinsulin (24-residue signal peptide) is initially synthesized. Proinsulin is formed from preproinsulin in the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER). Proinsulin undergoes maturation into active insulin through the action of cellular endopeptidases such as PCSK1 and CPE. Endopeptidases release a fragment called the C-peptide and leave 2 peptide chains, the B- and A-chains which are linked by 2 disulfide bonds. Mature IAPP is excised by processing proprotein convertase, PCSK1 and PCSK2, in parallel to proinsulin processing. Amylin is secreted together with insulin in a 20 to 1 molar ratio of insulin to amylin. PCSK1 and PCSK2 are themselves synthesized as inactive pro-PCSK1 and pro-PCSK2, which are activated by specific chaperone proteins, PCSK1N and SCG5, respectively. In isolated rat pancreatic islets, the biosynthesis of PCSK1 is specifically stimulated by glucose. In contrast, PCSK2 biosynthesis is not glucose-regulated. Insulin and C-peptide are then stored in the secretory granules together with islet IAPP. Glucose metabolism is the most important physiological event that stimulates insulin gene transcription and mRNA translation. The transcription factors including MAFA, PDX1, FOXO1, NFE2L2, PAX6, and NEUROD1, which are implicated in the regulation of insulin transcription, have been demonstrated to play a crucial role in human pancreatic beta cell function. Increases in glucose levels (10-30 mM) in mouse insulinoma cell line MIN6 disrupt the interaction of another insulin transcription factor, PDX1 with HDAC1 and HDAC2 and promote its association with EP300. EP300 leads to hyperacetylation of histone H4 and induction of insulin gene transcription. Glucose and glucagon (GCG) have been shown to increase PDX1 levels, as well as its nuclear localization. Several signaling pathways including MAPK14, PI3K, and PASK have been implicated in PDX1 phosphorylation and nucleocytoplasmic shuttling. Glucose has also been shown to regulate nuclear localization as well as transactivation capacity of NEUROD1 via post-transcriptional modification. In animal models, NEUROD1 is modified by phosphorylation via MAPK3/MAPK1 and through acetylation by EP300. Under high-glucose conditions (10-30 mM), NEUROD1 interacts with OGT and becomes O-GlcNAc-modified and translocates from the cytosol into the nucleus. NEUROD1, by glucose influence, modulates the expression of ion channels NNAT and ABCC8. This finding suggests that NEUROD1 may regulate insulin exocytosis in pancreatic beta cells by directly inducing the expression of SEC24D, SNAP25, syntaxin1, and STXBP1. In addition, glucose and GCG regulate insulin translation via EIF2AK3 and WFS1. WFS1 is upregulated by ER stress via ERN1 and EIF2AK3. At the beginning of glucose exposure, ERN1 stimulates insulin synthesis via WFS1, while after prolonged exposure; it may reduce insulin production via XBP1. Glucose and GCG stimulate WFS1 translocation from the ER to the plasma membrane, where WFS1 may form a complex with ADCY8 and calmodulin, which stimulates cAMP synthesis and insulin secretion. Lastly, glucose enhances the binding of the RNA binding protein, PTBP1 to the 5-UTRs of mRNAs for insulin and other cargoes of beta cell granules to increase their stability. Insulin is stored as a crystal chelated with Zn2 ."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The endocrine section of the pancreas (islets of Langerhans) consists of insulin-secreting beta cells and four other endocrine cell types. Adult beta cells are heterogeneous, with differences in glucose-responsiveness, insulin promoter activity, and insulin protein levels. The endocrine function of beta cells is to respond to increases in blood glucose levels by secreting insulin. Additional beta cell products are amyloid polypeptide (IAPP or amylin) and C-peptide.    Signaling description: During insulin synthesis, preproinsulin (24-residue signal peptide) is initially synthesized. Proinsulin is formed from preproinsulin in the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER). Proinsulin undergoes maturation into active insulin through the action of cellular endopeptidases such as PCSK1 and CPE. Endopeptidases release a fragment called the C-peptide and leave 2 peptide chains, the B- and A-chains which are linked by 2 disulfide bonds. Mature IAPP is excised by processing proprotein convertase, PCSK1 and PCSK2, in parallel to proinsulin processing. Amylin is secreted together with insulin in a 20 to 1 molar ratio of insulin to amylin. PCSK1 and PCSK2 are themselves synthesized as inactive pro-PCSK1 and pro-PCSK2, which are activated by specific chaperone proteins, PCSK1N and SCG5, respectively. In isolated rat pancreatic islets, the biosynthesis of PCSK1 is specifically stimulated by glucose. In contrast, PCSK2 biosynthesis is not glucose-regulated. Insulin and C-peptide are then stored in the secretory granules together with islet IAPP. Glucose metabolism is the most important physiological event that stimulates insulin gene transcription and mRNA translation. The transcription factors including MAFA, PDX1, FOXO1, NFE2L2, PAX6, and NEUROD1, which are implicated in the regulation of insulin transcription, have been demonstrated to play a crucial role in human pancreatic beta cell function. Increases in glucose levels (10-30 mM) in mouse insulinoma cell line MIN6 disrupt the interaction of another insulin transcription factor, PDX1 with HDAC1 and HDAC2 and promote its association with EP300. EP300 leads to hyperacetylation of histone H4 and induction of insulin gene transcription. Glucose and glucagon (GCG) have been shown to increase PDX1 levels, as well as its nuclear localization. Several signaling pathways including MAPK14, PI3K, and PASK have been implicated in PDX1 phosphorylation and nucleocytoplasmic shuttling. Glucose has also been shown to regulate nuclear localization as well as transactivation capacity of NEUROD1 via post-transcriptional modification. In animal models, NEUROD1 is modified by phosphorylation via MAPK3/MAPK1 and through acetylation by EP300. Under high-glucose conditions (10-30 mM), NEUROD1 interacts with OGT and becomes O-GlcNAc-modified and translocates from the cytosol into the nucleus. NEUROD1, by glucose influence, modulates the expression of ion channels NNAT and ABCC8. This finding suggests that NEUROD1 may regulate insulin exocytosis in pancreatic beta cells by directly inducing the expression of SEC24D, SNAP25, syntaxin1, and STXBP1. In addition, glucose and GCG regulate insulin translation via EIF2AK3 and WFS1. WFS1 is upregulated by ER stress via ERN1 and EIF2AK3. At the beginning of glucose exposure, ERN1 stimulates insulin synthesis via WFS1, while after prolonged exposure; it may reduce insulin production via XBP1. Glucose and GCG stimulate WFS1 translocation from the ER to the plasma membrane, where WFS1 may form a complex with ADCY8 and calmodulin, which stimulates cAMP synthesis and insulin secretion. Lastly, glucose enhances the binding of the RNA binding protein, PTBP1 to the 5-UTRs of mRNAs for insulin and other cargoes of beta cell granules to increase their stability. Insulin is stored as a crystal chelated with Zn2 ." EXACT []

