xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Pro-inflammatory factors such as IL1B, IFNG, and likely TNF induce NOS2 overexpression in beta-cells, leading to the overproduction of NO (nitric oxide) which induces cytotoxicity. The transcription of NOS2 in response to cytokine exposure is regulated by NF-kB (in case of exposure to IL1B) and STAT1 (in case of IFNG). IL1 by itself is sufficient to induce NOS2 expression in rat islets, whereas human and mouse tissues require IL1 and IFNG. IFNG synergizes with IL1B for the induction of beta-cell apoptosis. In human islets and rat beta cells, cytokines induce apoptosis via the canonical intrinsic mitochondrial pathway. IL1B also induces calcineurin-dependent dephosphorylation of BAD/BAX and leads to cytochrome c (CYCS) release and activation of CASP9 and CASP3. In beta-cells, TNF, but not IL1B induces the activation of CASP8, indicating that TNF is able to activate the extrinsic pathway.    Outcome effects: NF-kB functions as a \"master switch\" which controls the effector genes important for maintaining beta-cell homeostasis or apoptosis. Cytokines and hyperglycemia induce NF-kB-related beta-cell apoptosis but simultaneously defend against it. For example, NF-kb induces TNFAIP3 and HMOX1 expression in beta cells leading to an anti-apoptotic effect. Excessive activation of NF-kB by cytokines in beta-cells results in the up-regulated expression of CCL2. Glucose and IL1B can activate TNF expression in pancreatic beta- and alpha-cells in mice and humans. In murine beta-cells, TNF expression is dependent on calcineurin/NFAT/MAPK signaling. NFAT activates TNF expression in cooperation with ATF2 and JUN transcription factors. MAFA/CEBPB interaction could repress NFAT-mediated TNF expression."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Pro-inflammatory factors such as IL1B, IFNG, and likely TNF induce NOS2 overexpression in beta-cells, leading to the overproduction of NO (nitric oxide) which induces cytotoxicity. The transcription of NOS2 in response to cytokine exposure is regulated by NF-kB (in case of exposure to IL1B) and STAT1 (in case of IFNG). IL1 by itself is sufficient to induce NOS2 expression in rat islets, whereas human and mouse tissues require IL1 and IFNG. IFNG synergizes with IL1B for the induction of beta-cell apoptosis. In human islets and rat beta cells, cytokines induce apoptosis via the canonical intrinsic mitochondrial pathway. IL1B also induces calcineurin-dependent dephosphorylation of BAD/BAX and leads to cytochrome c (CYCS) release and activation of CASP9 and CASP3. In beta-cells, TNF, but not IL1B induces the activation of CASP8, indicating that TNF is able to activate the extrinsic pathway.    Outcome effects: NF-kB functions as a \"master switch\" which controls the effector genes important for maintaining beta-cell homeostasis or apoptosis. Cytokines and hyperglycemia induce NF-kB-related beta-cell apoptosis but simultaneously defend against it. For example, NF-kb induces TNFAIP3 and HMOX1 expression in beta cells leading to an anti-apoptotic effect. Excessive activation of NF-kB by cytokines in beta-cells results in the up-regulated expression of CCL2. Glucose and IL1B can activate TNF expression in pancreatic beta- and alpha-cells in mice and humans. In murine beta-cells, TNF expression is dependent on calcineurin/NFAT/MAPK signaling. NFAT activates TNF expression in cooperation with ATF2 and JUN transcription factors. MAFA/CEBPB interaction could repress NFAT-mediated TNF expression." EXACT []

