xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of chronic diseases characterized by a high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period.    Signaling description: In humans, the majority of beta cell replication occurs in early childhood. Some studies suggest that in adults there is no evidence of any beta cell replication. The expansion of beta cells has been detected only in certain conditions including pregnancy, obesity and the neonatal period. A number of factors such as insulin or incretin hormones (GCG and GIP) increase beta-cell mass via the enhanced IGF1R/PI3K/AKT1 signaling. In vitro, IGF1 and IGF2 were shown to enhance beta-cell proliferation in rat islets and insulinoma cell lines. PRL/PRLR triggers beta-cell proliferation through the JAK2/STAT5A signaling pathway resulting in direct activation of the CCND2 expression and adaptive beta-cell hyperplasia during pregnancy. MLXIPL mediates glucose-stimulated proliferation in pancreatic beta cells. Furthermore, BGLAP has been shown to have direct effects on the expression of insulin and beta-cell cycle mediators such as CCND2 and CDK4. WNT has been shown to induce beta-cell proliferation. Further, TCF7L2 is an effector of the WNT signaling pathway in beta-cells. Mutations in TCF7L2 have been linked to type 2 diabetes. The Ca2+/calcineurin/ NFATC1 signaling pathway positively regulates proliferation in rodent beta-cells. Lastly, moderate glucose elevation and incretins promote pancreatic beta-cell survival via the CREB1-mediated induction of IRS2. In mice, under high-fat diet conditions and during pregnancy, incretins induce beta-cell proliferation via the transactivation of EGFR.    Outcome effects: Beta-cell mass is regulated by a number of signaling routes. PI3K/AKT1 signaling is a central pathway in the regulation of beta-cell mass."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of chronic diseases characterized by a high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period.    Signaling description: In humans, the majority of beta cell replication occurs in early childhood. Some studies suggest that in adults there is no evidence of any beta cell replication. The expansion of beta cells has been detected only in certain conditions including pregnancy, obesity and the neonatal period. A number of factors such as insulin or incretin hormones (GCG and GIP) increase beta-cell mass via the enhanced IGF1R/PI3K/AKT1 signaling. In vitro, IGF1 and IGF2 were shown to enhance beta-cell proliferation in rat islets and insulinoma cell lines. PRL/PRLR triggers beta-cell proliferation through the JAK2/STAT5A signaling pathway resulting in direct activation of the CCND2 expression and adaptive beta-cell hyperplasia during pregnancy. MLXIPL mediates glucose-stimulated proliferation in pancreatic beta cells. Furthermore, BGLAP has been shown to have direct effects on the expression of insulin and beta-cell cycle mediators such as CCND2 and CDK4. WNT has been shown to induce beta-cell proliferation. Further, TCF7L2 is an effector of the WNT signaling pathway in beta-cells. Mutations in TCF7L2 have been linked to type 2 diabetes. The Ca2+/calcineurin/ NFATC1 signaling pathway positively regulates proliferation in rodent beta-cells. Lastly, moderate glucose elevation and incretins promote pancreatic beta-cell survival via the CREB1-mediated induction of IRS2. In mice, under high-fat diet conditions and during pregnancy, incretins induce beta-cell proliferation via the transactivation of EGFR.    Outcome effects: Beta-cell mass is regulated by a number of signaling routes. PI3K/AKT1 signaling is a central pathway in the regulation of beta-cell mass." EXACT []

