xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of chronic diseases characterized by a high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period.    Signaling description: Beta-cell function may be improved by the activation of GCK. PFKFB2 acts as a glucose sensor and an endogenous GCK activator. The activation of GCK is promoted by glucose, resulting in an enhancement of insulin secretion. GCK activity is regulated by adaptive translocation between the nucleus and the cytoplasm through binding and dissociation from its regulatory protein (GCKR) in the nucleus. G6PC2 encodes an islet-specific glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit from endoplasmic reticulum. Glucose-6-phosphatase catalyzes the dephosphorylation of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which is the process opposite to glucose utilization. G6PC2 inhibitors may be a new approach for the treatment of insulin secretion defects in type 2 diabetes. Further, GIP and GCG potentiate approximately 70% of the meal-induced insulin secretion in beta-cells. GCG is rapidly inactivated by the ubiquitous proteolytic enzyme DPP4, whose inhibition improves islet and beta-cell functions.    Outcome effects: Mitochondrial respiratory chain is the main source of superoxide in pancreatic beta cells. The levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS)-inactivating enzymes, glutathione peroxidase and CAT are extremely low in pancreatic beta-cells. UCP2 may have an antioxidant role preventing excessive superoxide formation within the respiratory chain. An additional ROS source may be provided by CYP2E1 and the family of NADPH oxidases (NOX). Human beta-cells seem to be less prone to oxidative stress than rodent beta-cells, possibly due to greater catalase and SOD activities. Glutathione peroxidase activity is poorly detectable in human islets. NFE2L2 and PPARG increase antioxidant enzyme activity in beta-cells. Thiazolidinediones are drugs that activate the PPAR group with the highest specificity for PPARG. The activation of PPARG also prevents the NF-kB activation, and the loss of the ATP2A2 pump induced by high glucose concentration and IL1B."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Diabetes mellitus refers to a group of chronic diseases characterized by a high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period.    Signaling description: Beta-cell function may be improved by the activation of GCK. PFKFB2 acts as a glucose sensor and an endogenous GCK activator. The activation of GCK is promoted by glucose, resulting in an enhancement of insulin secretion. GCK activity is regulated by adaptive translocation between the nucleus and the cytoplasm through binding and dissociation from its regulatory protein (GCKR) in the nucleus. G6PC2 encodes an islet-specific glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit from endoplasmic reticulum. Glucose-6-phosphatase catalyzes the dephosphorylation of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which is the process opposite to glucose utilization. G6PC2 inhibitors may be a new approach for the treatment of insulin secretion defects in type 2 diabetes. Further, GIP and GCG potentiate approximately 70% of the meal-induced insulin secretion in beta-cells. GCG is rapidly inactivated by the ubiquitous proteolytic enzyme DPP4, whose inhibition improves islet and beta-cell functions.    Outcome effects: Mitochondrial respiratory chain is the main source of superoxide in pancreatic beta cells. The levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS)-inactivating enzymes, glutathione peroxidase and CAT are extremely low in pancreatic beta-cells. UCP2 may have an antioxidant role preventing excessive superoxide formation within the respiratory chain. An additional ROS source may be provided by CYP2E1 and the family of NADPH oxidases (NOX). Human beta-cells seem to be less prone to oxidative stress than rodent beta-cells, possibly due to greater catalase and SOD activities. Glutathione peroxidase activity is poorly detectable in human islets. NFE2L2 and PPARG increase antioxidant enzyme activity in beta-cells. Thiazolidinediones are drugs that activate the PPAR group with the highest specificity for PPARG. The activation of PPARG also prevents the NF-kB activation, and the loss of the ATP2A2 pump induced by high glucose concentration and IL1B." EXACT []

