xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: The primary function of pancreatic beta-cells is to produce and release insulin in response to glucose. GIP and GCG potentiate as much as 70% of the meal-induced insulin secretion in beta-cells. Various other hormones and neurotransmitters including VIP, CCK and ADCYAP1 potentiate insulin secretion by generating cAMP via the GNAQ stimulation in pancreatic beta-cells. Similarly to hormone stimulation, glucose, acetylcholine, serotonin, and fatty acids induce oscillations of cAMP and Ca2 levels generating pulsatile insulin release.    Outcome effects: G-protein related activation of PIK3CG regulates beta-cell Ca2 -dependent exocytosis by controlling the membrane-associated pool of secretory granules and modulation of cortical F-actin density. PKC activates the rat ion channel TRPM4 following the GNAQ /PLCB1/diacylglycerol pathway stimulation, and this activation leads to membrane depolarization which causes Ca2 influx and insulin release. Osmotic, thermal, or pharmacological activation of another ion channel, TRPV4 enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: The primary function of pancreatic beta-cells is to produce and release insulin in response to glucose. GIP and GCG potentiate as much as 70% of the meal-induced insulin secretion in beta-cells. Various other hormones and neurotransmitters including VIP, CCK and ADCYAP1 potentiate insulin secretion by generating cAMP via the GNAQ stimulation in pancreatic beta-cells. Similarly to hormone stimulation, glucose, acetylcholine, serotonin, and fatty acids induce oscillations of cAMP and Ca2 levels generating pulsatile insulin release.    Outcome effects: G-protein related activation of PIK3CG regulates beta-cell Ca2 -dependent exocytosis by controlling the membrane-associated pool of secretory granules and modulation of cortical F-actin density. PKC activates the rat ion channel TRPM4 following the GNAQ /PLCB1/diacylglycerol pathway stimulation, and this activation leads to membrane depolarization which causes Ca2 influx and insulin release. Osmotic, thermal, or pharmacological activation of another ion channel, TRPV4 enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion." EXACT []

