xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Type 1 diabetes mellitus is an autoimmune pathology in which autoreactive CD8 cytotoxic T-lymphocytes destroy pancreatic beta-cells.    Signaling description: In diabetes, autoreactive CD8 T-lymphocytes do not undergo suppression by regulatory T (Treg) cells due to mutations in genes critical for Treg maturation. A viral infection may serve as a trigger for type 1 diabetes by initiating an autoimmune reaction against pancreatic beta-cells. Autoaggressive CD8 cytotoxic T-lymphocytes kill pancreatic beta-cells in their usual manner. The recognition of the MHC class I protein complex with self-antigens on the surface of beta-cells, as well as CD2-CD58 and ITGAL-ICAM1 interaction result in T-cell activation, release of granzymes (GZMA/GZMB), and the synthesis of FASLG and IFNG.    Outcome effects: Further, FASLG, GZMB, IFNG, IL1B and LTA promote apoptosis. IFNG binds to IFNGR1 on the surface of B-cells. The receptor activates JAK1 and JAK2 which transduce the signal to STAT1, a positive transcriptional regulator of IRF1. In turn, IRF1 binds to the CASP1 promoter, and, further CASP1 cleaves and activates CASP3 which initiates apoptosis. Macrophage-derived TNFB (LTA) and IL1B also contribute to the beta-cell death. LTA binds to its receptor, TNFRSF1A and the resulting signal transduction occurs through the TRADD/FADD/CASP8/CASP10 and TRADD/RIPK1/CRADD/CASP2 pathways. IL1B markedly stimulates the induction of IL1R1 which precedes the induction of NOS2. Nitric oxide (NO) generated by NOS2 triggers TP53 expression which initiates apoptosis. Low amounts of IL1RN (IL1R1 inactivator) were shown to be present in in diabetic patients. GZMA/GZMB and perforin (PRF1) are proteins released by the CD8 cytotoxic T cells which can induce apoptosis in target cells by forming transmembrane pores, as well as through the cleavage of the effector caspases such as CASP3. In addition, there is a caspase-independent mechanism of GZMB-mediated apoptosis with the DFFB involvement. DFFB is activated through the cleavage of its associated inhibitor, DFFA by CASP3. Then, DFFB is able to interact with entities such as topoisomerase II (TOP2B) to condense chromatin leading to DNA fragmentation and ultimately apoptosis. The binding of FAS to oligimerized FASLG on another cell activates apoptotic signaling through the cytoplasmic domain termed the death domain which interacts with signaling adaptors including FADD and DAXX to activate the caspase proteolytic cascade. First, CASP8 and CASP10 are activated then they cleave and activate downstream caspases and several cellular substrates, which leads to cell death.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Type 1 diabetes mellitus is an autoimmune pathology in which autoreactive CD8 cytotoxic T-lymphocytes destroy pancreatic beta-cells.    Signaling description: In diabetes, autoreactive CD8 T-lymphocytes do not undergo suppression by regulatory T (Treg) cells due to mutations in genes critical for Treg maturation. A viral infection may serve as a trigger for type 1 diabetes by initiating an autoimmune reaction against pancreatic beta-cells. Autoaggressive CD8 cytotoxic T-lymphocytes kill pancreatic beta-cells in their usual manner. The recognition of the MHC class I protein complex with self-antigens on the surface of beta-cells, as well as CD2-CD58 and ITGAL-ICAM1 interaction result in T-cell activation, release of granzymes (GZMA/GZMB), and the synthesis of FASLG and IFNG.    Outcome effects: Further, FASLG, GZMB, IFNG, IL1B and LTA promote apoptosis. IFNG binds to IFNGR1 on the surface of B-cells. The receptor activates JAK1 and JAK2 which transduce the signal to STAT1, a positive transcriptional regulator of IRF1. In turn, IRF1 binds to the CASP1 promoter, and, further CASP1 cleaves and activates CASP3 which initiates apoptosis. Macrophage-derived TNFB (LTA) and IL1B also contribute to the beta-cell death. LTA binds to its receptor, TNFRSF1A and the resulting signal transduction occurs through the TRADD/FADD/CASP8/CASP10 and TRADD/RIPK1/CRADD/CASP2 pathways. IL1B markedly stimulates the induction of IL1R1 which precedes the induction of NOS2. Nitric oxide (NO) generated by NOS2 triggers TP53 expression which initiates apoptosis. Low amounts of IL1RN (IL1R1 inactivator) were shown to be present in in diabetic patients. GZMA/GZMB and perforin (PRF1) are proteins released by the CD8 cytotoxic T cells which can induce apoptosis in target cells by forming transmembrane pores, as well as through the cleavage of the effector caspases such as CASP3. In addition, there is a caspase-independent mechanism of GZMB-mediated apoptosis with the DFFB involvement. DFFB is activated through the cleavage of its associated inhibitor, DFFA by CASP3. Then, DFFB is able to interact with entities such as topoisomerase II (TOP2B) to condense chromatin leading to DNA fragmentation and ultimately apoptosis. The binding of FAS to oligimerized FASLG on another cell activates apoptotic signaling through the cytoplasmic domain termed the death domain which interacts with signaling adaptors including FADD and DAXX to activate the caspase proteolytic cascade. First, CASP8 and CASP10 are activated then they cleave and activate downstream caspases and several cellular substrates, which leads to cell death.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

