xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Pro-inflammatory cytokines induce the overexpression of NOS2 in beta cells leading to the overproduction of NO, which decreases mitochondrial respiration, via aconitase inhibition, and prevents accumulation of ATP. In high quantities, NO can nitrosylate specific cysteine residues of various proteins (S-nitrosylation). In most cases S-nitrosylation leads to rapid degradation of the nitrosylated proteins. NO also decreases the expression levels of transcription factors responsible for beta cell differentiation and function including PDX1 and ISL1. Cytokines related to NO increase downregulate the ER Ca2 pump ATP2A2. Decreased ATP2A2 expression leads to ER Ca2 depletion and severe ER stress which results in beta cell apoptosis. Human islets have been shown to be less sensitive to NO-induced damage compared to rodent cells. The resistance of human islets towards NO is speculated to be caused by higher levels of HSPA1A in human beta cells compared to rodent.    Outcome effects: Nitric oxide (NO) can become toxic to beta cells if present at high levels due to the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and protein nitrosylation which causes mitochondrial damage, endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress, and apoptosis.    References: 1). Vincenz L, et al. (2011) Cytokine-Induced - Cell Stress and Death in Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus. In: Type 1 Diabetes - Complications, Pathogenesis, and Alternative Treatments, edited by Chih-Pin Liu, ISBN 978-953-307-756-7. InTech, 2011, 214-222."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: Pro-inflammatory cytokines induce the overexpression of NOS2 in beta cells leading to the overproduction of NO, which decreases mitochondrial respiration, via aconitase inhibition, and prevents accumulation of ATP. In high quantities, NO can nitrosylate specific cysteine residues of various proteins (S-nitrosylation). In most cases S-nitrosylation leads to rapid degradation of the nitrosylated proteins. NO also decreases the expression levels of transcription factors responsible for beta cell differentiation and function including PDX1 and ISL1. Cytokines related to NO increase downregulate the ER Ca2 pump ATP2A2. Decreased ATP2A2 expression leads to ER Ca2 depletion and severe ER stress which results in beta cell apoptosis. Human islets have been shown to be less sensitive to NO-induced damage compared to rodent cells. The resistance of human islets towards NO is speculated to be caused by higher levels of HSPA1A in human beta cells compared to rodent.    Outcome effects: Nitric oxide (NO) can become toxic to beta cells if present at high levels due to the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and protein nitrosylation which causes mitochondrial damage, endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress, and apoptosis.    References: 1). Vincenz L, et al. (2011) Cytokine-Induced - Cell Stress and Death in Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus. In: Type 1 Diabetes - Complications, Pathogenesis, and Alternative Treatments, edited by Chih-Pin Liu, ISBN 978-953-307-756-7. InTech, 2011, 214-222." EXACT []

