xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans.    Signaling description: In beta cells, hyperglycemia increases glucose oxidation and oxidative stress. The major source of oxidative stress is most likely the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Pancreatic islets are known for the extremely low level of expression of H2O2-inactivating enzymes such as catalase and glutathione peroxidase and their unusual susceptibility to reactive oxygen species (ROS). Hyperglycemia has been shown to increase the production of LDHA, UCP2, and CREM in rodent beta-cells. The overexpression of CREM in insulin-secreting rat cells directly suppresses the transcriptional activation of the INS promoter. Chronic exposure to long-chain saturated fatty acids (FFA) is another risk factor for the destruction of beta-cells. When fatty acids are esterified to oleate and palmitate in the ER, the ER stress markers such as XBP1 and ATF6 are induced. ER stress is caused by an accumulation of irreversible misfolded or unfolded protein which leads to beta-cell death. Oleate and palmitate also trigger ER stress through the ER calcium depletion. Palmitate induces a rapid degradation of CPE and this may contribute to ER stress due to an accumulation of unprocessed proinsulin. FFA induces apoptosis in beta cell lines, but the mechanisms are not well understood. The specific toxic effects of FFA may be related to ceramide formation. Ceramide was shown to be implicated in cell apoptosis. PPARD attenuates palmitate-induced ER stress by promoting fatty acid oxidation. In addition, pancreatic beta cells that are chronically exposed to fatty acids show higher CPT1A levels, higher palmitate oxidation rates, and altered insulin response to glucose. Lastly, ST18 is transcriptional factor that is induced by hyperlipidemia and cytokines which triggers beta cell apoptosis.    Outcome effects: Hyperglycemia induced oxidative stress in pancreatic beta-cells leads to their apoptiosis. Hyperlipidemia may trigger beta-cell death through oxidative stress, ceramide formation or the persistent endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans.    Signaling description: In beta cells, hyperglycemia increases glucose oxidation and oxidative stress. The major source of oxidative stress is most likely the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Pancreatic islets are known for the extremely low level of expression of H2O2-inactivating enzymes such as catalase and glutathione peroxidase and their unusual susceptibility to reactive oxygen species (ROS). Hyperglycemia has been shown to increase the production of LDHA, UCP2, and CREM in rodent beta-cells. The overexpression of CREM in insulin-secreting rat cells directly suppresses the transcriptional activation of the INS promoter. Chronic exposure to long-chain saturated fatty acids (FFA) is another risk factor for the destruction of beta-cells. When fatty acids are esterified to oleate and palmitate in the ER, the ER stress markers such as XBP1 and ATF6 are induced. ER stress is caused by an accumulation of irreversible misfolded or unfolded protein which leads to beta-cell death. Oleate and palmitate also trigger ER stress through the ER calcium depletion. Palmitate induces a rapid degradation of CPE and this may contribute to ER stress due to an accumulation of unprocessed proinsulin. FFA induces apoptosis in beta cell lines, but the mechanisms are not well understood. The specific toxic effects of FFA may be related to ceramide formation. Ceramide was shown to be implicated in cell apoptosis. PPARD attenuates palmitate-induced ER stress by promoting fatty acid oxidation. In addition, pancreatic beta cells that are chronically exposed to fatty acids show higher CPT1A levels, higher palmitate oxidation rates, and altered insulin response to glucose. Lastly, ST18 is transcriptional factor that is induced by hyperlipidemia and cytokines which triggers beta cell apoptosis.    Outcome effects: Hyperglycemia induced oxidative stress in pancreatic beta-cells leads to their apoptiosis. Hyperlipidemia may trigger beta-cell death through oxidative stress, ceramide formation or the persistent endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

