xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes. Cyclosporine and tacrolimus are immunosuppressive drugs that may cause post-transplant diabetes.    Signaling description: In animal models, tacrolimus-induced decrease of beta-cell proliferation may be related to CRTC2/CCND1 signaling. In mouse model, calcineurin inhibition leads to age-dependent diabetes which is also characterized by decreased beta-cell proliferation and mass. Tacrolimus and cyclosporine inhibit the calcineurin/NFAT signaling pathway in rodent beta-cells. In a rat insulinoma cell line (INS-1E cells), both drugs equally impair insulin secretion and inhibition of calcineurin activity. In rat islet beta-cells, IRS2 and INS expression is mediated by Ca2 /calcineurin/NFAT pathway. However, in rat cells no transcriptional repression of NFATC1-C4 isoforms was observed despite calcineurin inhibition. CCNDs, which are downstream targets of AKT1, are reduced in rodent islets following calcineurin inhibition. GCG enhances insulin promoter activity via NFAT in rodents. Glucose metabolism activates NFAT pathway by increasing voltage-gated Ca2 channel by affecting the electrical activity of the cell. In rat cells, cyclosporine significantly up-regulates SREBF1 which suppresses genes essential for beta-cell function and proliferation. The role of calcineurin in beta-cell apoptosis is controversial. In rat beta-cells, cyclosporine is connected to the BAX/ BCL2-related apoptotic mechanism. In human islet beta cells, calcineurin has anti-apoptotic effects.    Outcome effects: Cyclosporine and tacrolimus have been shown to impair insulin secretion, decrease insulin content of human pancreatic beta cells, and impair insulin transcription. Tacrolimus works to induce beta cell apoptosis, decrease insulin exocytosis, and reduce insulin gene transcription which ultimately leads to impaired functional beta-cell mass following pancreas transplantation.    Animal data: rodent model     Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes. Cyclosporine and tacrolimus are immunosuppressive drugs that may cause post-transplant diabetes.    Signaling description: In animal models, tacrolimus-induced decrease of beta-cell proliferation may be related to CRTC2/CCND1 signaling. In mouse model, calcineurin inhibition leads to age-dependent diabetes which is also characterized by decreased beta-cell proliferation and mass. Tacrolimus and cyclosporine inhibit the calcineurin/NFAT signaling pathway in rodent beta-cells. In a rat insulinoma cell line (INS-1E cells), both drugs equally impair insulin secretion and inhibition of calcineurin activity. In rat islet beta-cells, IRS2 and INS expression is mediated by Ca2 /calcineurin/NFAT pathway. However, in rat cells no transcriptional repression of NFATC1-C4 isoforms was observed despite calcineurin inhibition. CCNDs, which are downstream targets of AKT1, are reduced in rodent islets following calcineurin inhibition. GCG enhances insulin promoter activity via NFAT in rodents. Glucose metabolism activates NFAT pathway by increasing voltage-gated Ca2 channel by affecting the electrical activity of the cell. In rat cells, cyclosporine significantly up-regulates SREBF1 which suppresses genes essential for beta-cell function and proliferation. The role of calcineurin in beta-cell apoptosis is controversial. In rat beta-cells, cyclosporine is connected to the BAX/ BCL2-related apoptotic mechanism. In human islet beta cells, calcineurin has anti-apoptotic effects.    Outcome effects: Cyclosporine and tacrolimus have been shown to impair insulin secretion, decrease insulin content of human pancreatic beta cells, and impair insulin transcription. Tacrolimus works to induce beta cell apoptosis, decrease insulin exocytosis, and reduce insulin gene transcription which ultimately leads to impaired functional beta-cell mass following pancreas transplantation.    Animal data: rodent model     Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

