synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ghrelin (GHRL) is a gastric peptide hormone and one of the major hormones that stimulate hunger.    Signaling description: Ghrelin peptides exist in two major forms, unacylated ghrelin and acylated ghrelin, and only the latter binds to ghrelin receptor (GHSR). Acylation is catalyzed by MBOAT4. In rat and human pancreatic tissue, GHSR is expressed in most alpha-cells and in certain amount of beta-cells. Despite some controversial results, many studies reveal that ghrelin is able to inhibit insulin secretion in vitro and in vivo. In healthy humans, ghrelin suppresses glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. GHSR is a G-coupled receptor that normally couples to GNAQ which leads to Ca2 mobilization and stimulation rather than inhibition of insulin secretion. However, in rat islet and beta-cells, GHSR couples to GNAI1 instead of GNAQ. GNAI1-related signaling reduces cAMP accumulation and insulin secretion. GHSR coupling to GNAI1 rather than GNAQ requires interaction with SSTR5. When the relative ghrelin/SST ratio is high, ghrelin suppresses cAMP accumulation and when the ratio is low, ghrelin increases Ca2 mobilization. Most of ghrelin's inhibitory influence on insulin secretion is likely due to the enhancement of PTPRN2 expression. PTPRN2 is a membrane glycoprotein localized in secretory granules and is a beta-cell autoantigen for type 1 diabetes. Approximately 50% of newly diagnosed patients have autoantibodies against PTPRN2. In addition, the link between ghrelin and UCP2 has been hypothesized based on the observation that ghrelin knockout mice display a reduced expression of UCP2 in the pancreas.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin may have an inhibitory effect on insulin secretion at low concentrations and a stimulatory effect at high concentrations. Further, ghrelin stimulates glucagon (GCG) secretion from pancreatic alpha cells.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ghrelin (GHRL) is a gastric peptide hormone and one of the major hormones that stimulate hunger.    Signaling description: Ghrelin peptides exist in two major forms, unacylated ghrelin and acylated ghrelin, and only the latter binds to ghrelin receptor (GHSR). Acylation is catalyzed by MBOAT4. In rat and human pancreatic tissue, GHSR is expressed in most alpha-cells and in certain amount of beta-cells. Despite some controversial results, many studies reveal that ghrelin is able to inhibit insulin secretion in vitro and in vivo. In healthy humans, ghrelin suppresses glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. GHSR is a G-coupled receptor that normally couples to GNAQ which leads to Ca2 mobilization and stimulation rather than inhibition of insulin secretion. However, in rat islet and beta-cells, GHSR couples to GNAI1 instead of GNAQ. GNAI1-related signaling reduces cAMP accumulation and insulin secretion. GHSR coupling to GNAI1 rather than GNAQ requires interaction with SSTR5. When the relative ghrelin/SST ratio is high, ghrelin suppresses cAMP accumulation and when the ratio is low, ghrelin increases Ca2 mobilization. Most of ghrelin's inhibitory influence on insulin secretion is likely due to the enhancement of PTPRN2 expression. PTPRN2 is a membrane glycoprotein localized in secretory granules and is a beta-cell autoantigen for type 1 diabetes. Approximately 50% of newly diagnosed patients have autoantibodies against PTPRN2. In addition, the link between ghrelin and UCP2 has been hypothesized based on the observation that ghrelin knockout mice display a reduced expression of UCP2 in the pancreas.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin may have an inhibitory effect on insulin secretion at low concentrations and a stimulatory effect at high concentrations. Further, ghrelin stimulates glucagon (GCG) secretion from pancreatic alpha cells.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

