xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: In pancreatic beta-cells, FOXO1 inhibition is mediated in part by PDX1 inhibition. In response to oxidative stress, FOXO1 increases the expression of INS and two insulin gene transcription factors, NEUROD1 and MAFA. The acetylation/deacetylation state of FOXO1 is crucial in this process. SIRT1 deacetylases FOXO1 and promotes the expression of INS. EIF2AK2 can be activated by glucolipotoxicity and pro-inflammatory cytokines in pancreatic-beta cells. Activated EIF2AK2 significantly inhibits cell proliferation by arresting the cell cycle at G1 phase. Decreased CCND1 and CCND2 levels as well as the increase of CDKN1B and TP53 levels are associated with anti-proliferative effects of EIF2AK2. Proteasome-dependent degradation takes part in reducing the CCND1 and CCND2 levels. Moreover, EIF2AK2 activation abrogates the pro-proliferative effects of IGFI by activating MAPK8 and disrupting the IRS2/PI3K/AKT1 signaling pathway. Further, SREBF1 suppresses INS/IGF1-related insulin secretion and beta-cell proliferation. SREBF1 target genes are involved in lipid synthesis. SREBF1 suppresses IRS2 activity. The overexpression of SREBF1 has been shown to suppress PDX1 expression both in vivo and in vitro. SYTL4 has been discovered as a direct target of SREBF1 and its knockdown restored insulin secretion in animal models. SYTL4 interacts with insulin-containing vesicles for exocytosis. In rat beta cells, SREBF1 is up-regulated by dietary intake of carbohydrates, sugars, and saturated fatty acids and down-regulated by polyunsaturated fatty acids such as eicosapentaenoate (EPA). In a state of high nutrition, SREBF1 was found to accumulate UCP2 in beta cells.    Outcome effects: The INS/IGF1/IRS2/AKT1 pathway deactivates FOXO1 and decreases beta-cell proliferation. However, FOXO1 can still have a role in promoting beta-cell function. The role of FOXO1 in beta-cell function has been studied in mice lacking FOXO1 in their beta-cells. FOXO1 ablation has been shown to cause hyperglycemia with reduced beta-cell mass, physiologic stress, such as multiparity and aging. Surprisingly, lineage-tracing experiments demonstrated that the loss of beta-cell mass was due to beta cell dedifferentiation, and not death. Dedifferentiated beta cells reverted to progenitor-like cells expressing NEUROG3, POU5F1, NANOG, and MYCL. A subset of FOXO1-deficient beta-cells adopted the alpha-cell fate, resulting in hyperglucagonemia. Further, SREBF1 accumulates UCP2 in beta-cells. In turn, UCP2 mediates ATP production from mitochondria and thereby regulates insulin secretion in pancreatic beta-cells.    Animal data: rodent model."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. Cytokine-mediated destruction of beta cells is central in the development of type 1 diabetes.    Signaling description: In pancreatic beta-cells, FOXO1 inhibition is mediated in part by PDX1 inhibition. In response to oxidative stress, FOXO1 increases the expression of INS and two insulin gene transcription factors, NEUROD1 and MAFA. The acetylation/deacetylation state of FOXO1 is crucial in this process. SIRT1 deacetylases FOXO1 and promotes the expression of INS. EIF2AK2 can be activated by glucolipotoxicity and pro-inflammatory cytokines in pancreatic-beta cells. Activated EIF2AK2 significantly inhibits cell proliferation by arresting the cell cycle at G1 phase. Decreased CCND1 and CCND2 levels as well as the increase of CDKN1B and TP53 levels are associated with anti-proliferative effects of EIF2AK2. Proteasome-dependent degradation takes part in reducing the CCND1 and CCND2 levels. Moreover, EIF2AK2 activation abrogates the pro-proliferative effects of IGFI by activating MAPK8 and disrupting the IRS2/PI3K/AKT1 signaling pathway. Further, SREBF1 suppresses INS/IGF1-related insulin secretion and beta-cell proliferation. SREBF1 target genes are involved in lipid synthesis. SREBF1 suppresses IRS2 activity. The overexpression of SREBF1 has been shown to suppress PDX1 expression both in vivo and in vitro. SYTL4 has been discovered as a direct target of SREBF1 and its knockdown restored insulin secretion in animal models. SYTL4 interacts with insulin-containing vesicles for exocytosis. In rat beta cells, SREBF1 is up-regulated by dietary intake of carbohydrates, sugars, and saturated fatty acids and down-regulated by polyunsaturated fatty acids such as eicosapentaenoate (EPA). In a state of high nutrition, SREBF1 was found to accumulate UCP2 in beta cells.    Outcome effects: The INS/IGF1/IRS2/AKT1 pathway deactivates FOXO1 and decreases beta-cell proliferation. However, FOXO1 can still have a role in promoting beta-cell function. The role of FOXO1 in beta-cell function has been studied in mice lacking FOXO1 in their beta-cells. FOXO1 ablation has been shown to cause hyperglycemia with reduced beta-cell mass, physiologic stress, such as multiparity and aging. Surprisingly, lineage-tracing experiments demonstrated that the loss of beta-cell mass was due to beta cell dedifferentiation, and not death. Dedifferentiated beta cells reverted to progenitor-like cells expressing NEUROG3, POU5F1, NANOG, and MYCL. A subset of FOXO1-deficient beta-cells adopted the alpha-cell fate, resulting in hyperglucagonemia. Further, SREBF1 accumulates UCP2 in beta-cells. In turn, UCP2 mediates ATP production from mitochondria and thereby regulates insulin secretion in pancreatic beta-cells.    Animal data: rodent model." EXACT []

