xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. The major hypotheses for the origin of beta cells in adult mammals include replication of pre-existing beta cells, neogenesis from duct epithelium, acini-to-endocrine or acini-to-duct-to-endocrine transdifferentiation, endocrine cell interconversion, and trans-differentiation of pro-alpha-cells to-beta cells.    Signaling description: Alpha cells produce glucagon and are similar to beta cells with regard to intracellular signaling. PCSK2 is an enzyme crucial for proglucagon processing in alpha cells. Fully-differentiated alpha cells express only PCSK2 and produce glucagon, whereas undifferentiated pro-alpha cells express PCSK1/3 as well as PCSK2 and produce GCG in addition to glucagon. The control of glucagon secretion in alpha cells is similar to insulin secretion control in beta cells. Glucagon is released by alpha cells through calcium (Ca2 )-dependent exocytosis of secretory granules. Insulin, somatostatin, GCG, and leptin inhibit whereas amino acids, low glucose, free fatty acids, and catecholamines stimulate glucagon release. Alpha cell development and glucagon gene expression are tightly linked. The differentiated alpha cell phenotype is determined by the expression of ARX, PAX6, POU3F4, IRX2, and FOXA2 as well as the absence of PDX1, PAX4, PCSK1/3, and GCG receptor. PAX6 regulates the expression of the glucagon gene as well as MAF, MAFB, PCSK2, and NEUROD1 genes. Specifically, ARX has been shown to affect alpha cell differentiation but not the glucagon gene expression.    Outcome effects: Alpha cells may transdifferentiate into beta cells, and ARX is crucial for the suppression of beta cell phenotype through the control of PAX4. In addition, PAX4 directly inhibits the expression of ARX. Ectopic expression of PAX4 is sufficient to convert alpha cells into beta cells in vivo. MAFB is essentially absent from mature adult beta cells and its expression is restricted to alpha cells in the adult pancreas. Lastly, the signals that control alpha cell proliferation and their conversion into beta cells remain unknown. The production of GCG by alpha cells is initiated by factors secreted by beta cells in response to injuries, e.g. CXCL12."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are any of the insulin-secreting pancreatic cells in the islets of Langerhans. The major hypotheses for the origin of beta cells in adult mammals include replication of pre-existing beta cells, neogenesis from duct epithelium, acini-to-endocrine or acini-to-duct-to-endocrine transdifferentiation, endocrine cell interconversion, and trans-differentiation of pro-alpha-cells to-beta cells.    Signaling description: Alpha cells produce glucagon and are similar to beta cells with regard to intracellular signaling. PCSK2 is an enzyme crucial for proglucagon processing in alpha cells. Fully-differentiated alpha cells express only PCSK2 and produce glucagon, whereas undifferentiated pro-alpha cells express PCSK1/3 as well as PCSK2 and produce GCG in addition to glucagon. The control of glucagon secretion in alpha cells is similar to insulin secretion control in beta cells. Glucagon is released by alpha cells through calcium (Ca2 )-dependent exocytosis of secretory granules. Insulin, somatostatin, GCG, and leptin inhibit whereas amino acids, low glucose, free fatty acids, and catecholamines stimulate glucagon release. Alpha cell development and glucagon gene expression are tightly linked. The differentiated alpha cell phenotype is determined by the expression of ARX, PAX6, POU3F4, IRX2, and FOXA2 as well as the absence of PDX1, PAX4, PCSK1/3, and GCG receptor. PAX6 regulates the expression of the glucagon gene as well as MAF, MAFB, PCSK2, and NEUROD1 genes. Specifically, ARX has been shown to affect alpha cell differentiation but not the glucagon gene expression.    Outcome effects: Alpha cells may transdifferentiate into beta cells, and ARX is crucial for the suppression of beta cell phenotype through the control of PAX4. In addition, PAX4 directly inhibits the expression of ARX. Ectopic expression of PAX4 is sufficient to convert alpha cells into beta cells in vivo. MAFB is essentially absent from mature adult beta cells and its expression is restricted to alpha cells in the adult pancreas. Lastly, the signals that control alpha cell proliferation and their conversion into beta cells remain unknown. The production of GCG by alpha cells is initiated by factors secreted by beta cells in response to injuries, e.g. CXCL12." EXACT []

