xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are the insulin-secreting cells in the islets of Langerhans. Beta-cell dysfunction is central in the pathogenesis of diabetes mellitus. The drug rapamycin which is used as an immunosuppressant in islet transplantation suppresses beta-cell function.    Signaling description: Rapamycin action is based on its binding to FKBP1A and inhibition of MTOR. The inhibition of MTOR leads to the arrest of the cell cycle at G1 to S phase in activated T-cells, as well as in other cell types.    Outcome effects: Rapamycin is able to reduce the rate of beta-cell proliferation in transplanted rat islets as well as in host murine islets. In vitro, rapamycin treatment induces autophagy in the islets. Currently, MAP1LC3A is the sole marker for autophagosomes. In mice, rapamycin treatment of islets leads to islet dysfunction through upregulation of autophagy and downregulation of insulin production and apoptosis of beta-cells. Rapamycin suppresses high glucose-induced insulin secretion from rat pancreatic islets by reducing mitochondrial ATP production through the suppression of carbohydrate metabolism in the Krebs cycle, together with reduced OGDH activity.    Animal data: rodent model."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pancreatic beta-cells are the insulin-secreting cells in the islets of Langerhans. Beta-cell dysfunction is central in the pathogenesis of diabetes mellitus. The drug rapamycin which is used as an immunosuppressant in islet transplantation suppresses beta-cell function.    Signaling description: Rapamycin action is based on its binding to FKBP1A and inhibition of MTOR. The inhibition of MTOR leads to the arrest of the cell cycle at G1 to S phase in activated T-cells, as well as in other cell types.    Outcome effects: Rapamycin is able to reduce the rate of beta-cell proliferation in transplanted rat islets as well as in host murine islets. In vitro, rapamycin treatment induces autophagy in the islets. Currently, MAP1LC3A is the sole marker for autophagosomes. In mice, rapamycin treatment of islets leads to islet dysfunction through upregulation of autophagy and downregulation of insulin production and apoptosis of beta-cells. Rapamycin suppresses high glucose-induced insulin secretion from rat pancreatic islets by reducing mitochondrial ATP production through the suppression of carbohydrate metabolism in the Krebs cycle, together with reduced OGDH activity.    Animal data: rodent model." EXACT []

