xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles.    Signaling description: Adiponectin (ADIPOQ) is a protein hormone secreted from adipose tissue. It plays a regulatory role in glucose and lipid homeostasis (fatty acid oxidation), preventing insulin resistance. ADIPOQ acts through its receptors, ADIPOR1 and ADIPOR2. Following its binding to ADIPOR2, the hormone induces the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor alpha (PPARA) expression.    Outcome effects: PRARA binds to the promoter regions of lipoprotein lipase (LPL), acyl-CoA oxidase 1 (ACOX1), and uncoupling protein 2 (UCP2) genes promoting their transcription, and thus, up-regulating fatty acid oxidation. ADIPOR1 receptor interacts with adaptor protein (APPL1) to transduce the signal from adiponectin to specific proteins, AMP-dependent protein kinases (AMPK), which negatively regulate the transcription of proteins involved in gluconeogenesis and activate coactivator 1 alpha (PPARGC1A), which is important for the synthesis of proteins participating in mitochondrial organization and biogenesis. In addition, APPL1 provides GLUT4 (SLC2A4) translocation into the cell membrane through AKT1 and MAPK cascade leading to increased glucose intake."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles.    Signaling description: Adiponectin (ADIPOQ) is a protein hormone secreted from adipose tissue. It plays a regulatory role in glucose and lipid homeostasis (fatty acid oxidation), preventing insulin resistance. ADIPOQ acts through its receptors, ADIPOR1 and ADIPOR2. Following its binding to ADIPOR2, the hormone induces the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor alpha (PPARA) expression.    Outcome effects: PRARA binds to the promoter regions of lipoprotein lipase (LPL), acyl-CoA oxidase 1 (ACOX1), and uncoupling protein 2 (UCP2) genes promoting their transcription, and thus, up-regulating fatty acid oxidation. ADIPOR1 receptor interacts with adaptor protein (APPL1) to transduce the signal from adiponectin to specific proteins, AMP-dependent protein kinases (AMPK), which negatively regulate the transcription of proteins involved in gluconeogenesis and activate coactivator 1 alpha (PPARGC1A), which is important for the synthesis of proteins participating in mitochondrial organization and biogenesis. In addition, APPL1 provides GLUT4 (SLC2A4) translocation into the cell membrane through AKT1 and MAPK cascade leading to increased glucose intake." EXACT []

