xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles.    Signaling description: Free fatty acids released from the adipose tissue bind to toll-like receptors, TLR2 and TLR4 and activate MAPK8, MAPK9, and IKBKB via MYD88/IRAK/TRAF6. As a result, IRS is inactivated via phosphorylation. In addition, adipose tissue-derived CXCL5 is a newly discovered cytokine mediating insulin resistance in muscle. CXCL5 is able to inhibit INS action in the muscles by activating the JAK/STAT/SOCS signaling pathway. Furthermore, low adiponectin (ADIPOQ) levels are observed in insulin resistance. Normally, ADIPOQ binds to its receptor, ADIPOR1 and through APPL1/STK11 activates AMPK. AMPK promotes free fatty acid oxidation by downregulation of ACACB and ACLY which participate in the inhibition of CPT1B.    Outcome effects: Substances released from the adipose tissue (including CXCL5 and free fatty acids) provoke inhibition of the insulin (INS) signal transduction leading to the disruption of glycogen synthesis, as well as the essential protein synthesis. The decreased level of ADIPOQ in insulin resistance leads to the disruption of fatty acid oxidation and storage of triglycerides.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles.    Signaling description: Free fatty acids released from the adipose tissue bind to toll-like receptors, TLR2 and TLR4 and activate MAPK8, MAPK9, and IKBKB via MYD88/IRAK/TRAF6. As a result, IRS is inactivated via phosphorylation. In addition, adipose tissue-derived CXCL5 is a newly discovered cytokine mediating insulin resistance in muscle. CXCL5 is able to inhibit INS action in the muscles by activating the JAK/STAT/SOCS signaling pathway. Furthermore, low adiponectin (ADIPOQ) levels are observed in insulin resistance. Normally, ADIPOQ binds to its receptor, ADIPOR1 and through APPL1/STK11 activates AMPK. AMPK promotes free fatty acid oxidation by downregulation of ACACB and ACLY which participate in the inhibition of CPT1B.    Outcome effects: Substances released from the adipose tissue (including CXCL5 and free fatty acids) provoke inhibition of the insulin (INS) signal transduction leading to the disruption of glycogen synthesis, as well as the essential protein synthesis. The decreased level of ADIPOQ in insulin resistance leads to the disruption of fatty acid oxidation and storage of triglycerides.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

