xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles. Inflammation in adipose tissue can cause insulin resistance.    Signaling description: The excess of triglycerides in adipose tissue caused by a high-fat diet provokes inflammation. Triglycerides facilitate MCP-1 (CCL2) expression; however the precise molecular mechanism remains unclear. CCL2 is a chemoattractant recruiting macrophages to adipose tissue. Triglycerides also activate NF-kB triggering TNF synthesis and secretion. The binding of this proinflammatory cytokine to its receptor, TNFRSF1A on the surface of macrophages and adipocytes leads to IL6 and CXCL5 synthesis. The excessive amount of triglycerides in adipose tissue undergoes lysis by the PNPLA2 and LIPE proteins. The released free fatty acids (FFA) are transported via FABP4 to the cell membrane and transported across the cell membrane via SLC27A1.    Outcome effects: The released IL6 provokes insulin resistance in the liver; whereas CXCL5 acts on the muscles. The increased FFA in the blood is exposed to peripheral tissues provoking insulin resistance in these tissues.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles. Inflammation in adipose tissue can cause insulin resistance.    Signaling description: The excess of triglycerides in adipose tissue caused by a high-fat diet provokes inflammation. Triglycerides facilitate MCP-1 (CCL2) expression; however the precise molecular mechanism remains unclear. CCL2 is a chemoattractant recruiting macrophages to adipose tissue. Triglycerides also activate NF-kB triggering TNF synthesis and secretion. The binding of this proinflammatory cytokine to its receptor, TNFRSF1A on the surface of macrophages and adipocytes leads to IL6 and CXCL5 synthesis. The excessive amount of triglycerides in adipose tissue undergoes lysis by the PNPLA2 and LIPE proteins. The released free fatty acids (FFA) are transported via FABP4 to the cell membrane and transported across the cell membrane via SLC27A1.    Outcome effects: The released IL6 provokes insulin resistance in the liver; whereas CXCL5 acts on the muscles. The increased FFA in the blood is exposed to peripheral tissues provoking insulin resistance in these tissues.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

