xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles. Inflammation in adipose tissue can cause insulin resistance.    Signaling description: Normally, adiponectin modulates a number of metabolic processes including glucose regulation and fatty acid oxidation. Adiponectin is exclusively secreted from the adipose tissue. The levels of the hormone are inversely correlated with body fat percentage in adults. Obesity induces the secretion of proinflammatory cytokines, TNF and IL6 by macrophages and adipose tissue itself. These cytokines, through the JAK/STAT3/JUN/FOS/MYC/SIRT1/FOXO1 axis, inhibit peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma protein (PPARG), which forms heterodimers with RXRA and binds to the promoter region of adiponectin (ADIPOQ) resulting in gene transcription. A high-fat diet provokes the activation of protein kinases PRKACA and PKIA by an unknown mechanism leading to the elevated expression of the activating transcription factor 3 (ATF3), which is the negative regulator of adiponectin transcription. A high-fat diet also causes endoplasmic reticulum stress in the adipose tissue. Free fatty acids mediate the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) which interact with proteins leading to un-/misfolded protein formation and the downregulation of adiponectin transcription factors, CEBPA and CEBPB. In addition, increased activation of the negative regulator, NFATC4 occurs in response to insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF1) action in adipocytes.    Outcome effects: Therefore, obesity/high fat diet can disrupt adiponectin synthesis via a number of signaling routes leading to impaired glucose and fatty acid metabolism.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles. Inflammation in adipose tissue can cause insulin resistance.    Signaling description: Normally, adiponectin modulates a number of metabolic processes including glucose regulation and fatty acid oxidation. Adiponectin is exclusively secreted from the adipose tissue. The levels of the hormone are inversely correlated with body fat percentage in adults. Obesity induces the secretion of proinflammatory cytokines, TNF and IL6 by macrophages and adipose tissue itself. These cytokines, through the JAK/STAT3/JUN/FOS/MYC/SIRT1/FOXO1 axis, inhibit peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma protein (PPARG), which forms heterodimers with RXRA and binds to the promoter region of adiponectin (ADIPOQ) resulting in gene transcription. A high-fat diet provokes the activation of protein kinases PRKACA and PKIA by an unknown mechanism leading to the elevated expression of the activating transcription factor 3 (ATF3), which is the negative regulator of adiponectin transcription. A high-fat diet also causes endoplasmic reticulum stress in the adipose tissue. Free fatty acids mediate the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) which interact with proteins leading to un-/misfolded protein formation and the downregulation of adiponectin transcription factors, CEBPA and CEBPB. In addition, increased activation of the negative regulator, NFATC4 occurs in response to insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF1) action in adipocytes.    Outcome effects: Therefore, obesity/high fat diet can disrupt adiponectin synthesis via a number of signaling routes leading to impaired glucose and fatty acid metabolism.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

