synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles.    Signaling description: Proinflammatory cytokines IL6 and TNF are synthesized by the adipose tissue in response to a high-fat diet. IL6 induces SOCS3 overexpression via the JAK/STAT3 axis, as well as the MTOR/PRKCD/MAPK8 mechanism through PTPN11 activation. SOCS3 stops hormone signaling by direct IRS inhibition. Conversely, TNF causes the expression of PTPN1 which dephosphorylates IRS similarly preventing the spread of the signal. In addition, circulating fatty acids are elevated in obese individuals and similarly provoke the insulin resistance causing another pathological feature called hepatic steatosis or fatty liver. The fatty acids which escape oxidation are converted into triglycerides. The precursor of triglycerides, diacylglycerol can activate protein kinase C, theta (PRKCQ). The latter phosphorylates IRS1 and blocks the insulin signal transduction.    Outcome effects: The disruption of hormone signaling in hepatocytes is caused by the inactivation of insulin receptor substrates (IRS1 and IRS2). The decreased levels of adiponectin (ADIPOQ) contribute to the escape of fatty acids from oxidation leading to the accumulation of triglycerides and gluconeogenesis resulting in an additional increase of glucose in the blood.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin resistance (IR) is a chronic condition characterized by a decreased ability of the cells to respond to insulin. IR typically coexists with obesity and announces the onset of diabetes mellitus type 2. Several mechanisms lead to insulin resistance in the liver and muscles.    Signaling description: Proinflammatory cytokines IL6 and TNF are synthesized by the adipose tissue in response to a high-fat diet. IL6 induces SOCS3 overexpression via the JAK/STAT3 axis, as well as the MTOR/PRKCD/MAPK8 mechanism through PTPN11 activation. SOCS3 stops hormone signaling by direct IRS inhibition. Conversely, TNF causes the expression of PTPN1 which dephosphorylates IRS similarly preventing the spread of the signal. In addition, circulating fatty acids are elevated in obese individuals and similarly provoke the insulin resistance causing another pathological feature called hepatic steatosis or fatty liver. The fatty acids which escape oxidation are converted into triglycerides. The precursor of triglycerides, diacylglycerol can activate protein kinase C, theta (PRKCQ). The latter phosphorylates IRS1 and blocks the insulin signal transduction.    Outcome effects: The disruption of hormone signaling in hepatocytes is caused by the inactivation of insulin receptor substrates (IRS1 and IRS2). The decreased levels of adiponectin (ADIPOQ) contribute to the escape of fatty acids from oxidation leading to the accumulation of triglycerides and gluconeogenesis resulting in an additional increase of glucose in the blood.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

