xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected.    Signaling description: Hepatic stellate cells, also known as perisinusoidal cells or Ito cells are pericytes found in the perisinusoidal space of Disse. The stellate cell is the major cell type involved in liver fibrosis, which is formed in response to liver damage in liver diseases. Stellate cells are activated by paracrine stimuli (such as PDGFA/PDGFB, TGFA, and EGF) produced by hepatocytes, endothelial cells, and other cells of the liver. Ligand-bound growth factor receptors activate MAPK1/3 signaling pathway via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1, and STAT3 via JAK2. In turn, STAT3 promotes KLF6.    Outcome effects: The above-mentioned pathways induce matrix metallopeptidases (MMP2/9/13/14), FN1, TIMP1, CDH1, TGFB1, TGFBR1, PLAU, and COL1A1 which are involved in cell proliferation, macrophage chemotaxis, and fibrosis. In addition, fatty acids can modulate inflammation through TLR4. TLR4 activates NF-kB via adaptor proteins and MAPKs. NF-kB promotes interleukins (IL6, IL8, IL2, and IL1A) and CCL2 which are involved in cell proliferation, macrophage chemotaxis, and liver fibrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected.    Signaling description: Hepatic stellate cells, also known as perisinusoidal cells or Ito cells are pericytes found in the perisinusoidal space of Disse. The stellate cell is the major cell type involved in liver fibrosis, which is formed in response to liver damage in liver diseases. Stellate cells are activated by paracrine stimuli (such as PDGFA/PDGFB, TGFA, and EGF) produced by hepatocytes, endothelial cells, and other cells of the liver. Ligand-bound growth factor receptors activate MAPK1/3 signaling pathway via SHC1/GRB2/SOS1, and STAT3 via JAK2. In turn, STAT3 promotes KLF6.    Outcome effects: The above-mentioned pathways induce matrix metallopeptidases (MMP2/9/13/14), FN1, TIMP1, CDH1, TGFB1, TGFBR1, PLAU, and COL1A1 which are involved in cell proliferation, macrophage chemotaxis, and fibrosis. In addition, fatty acids can modulate inflammation through TLR4. TLR4 activates NF-kB via adaptor proteins and MAPKs. NF-kB promotes interleukins (IL6, IL8, IL2, and IL1A) and CCL2 which are involved in cell proliferation, macrophage chemotaxis, and liver fibrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

