xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected.    Signaling description: Insulin resistance results in the activation of gluconeogenesis in the liver, which subsequently leads to the accumulation of fat. Normally, insulin (INS) inhibits gluconeogenesis. Activated INSR and IGF1R stimulate PDPK1 via IRS2, PIK3R1, and the formation of PIP3. In turn, PDPK1 activates AKT1, which inhibits FOXO1.    Outcome effects: FOXO1 promotes CEBPA resulting in the activation of G6PC, PCK1, LPIN1, RETN, and LEP which are involved in gluconeogenesis. Conversely, increased levels of fatty acids lead to the induction of PPARG which promotes CEBPA.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected.    Signaling description: Insulin resistance results in the activation of gluconeogenesis in the liver, which subsequently leads to the accumulation of fat. Normally, insulin (INS) inhibits gluconeogenesis. Activated INSR and IGF1R stimulate PDPK1 via IRS2, PIK3R1, and the formation of PIP3. In turn, PDPK1 activates AKT1, which inhibits FOXO1.    Outcome effects: FOXO1 promotes CEBPA resulting in the activation of G6PC, PCK1, LPIN1, RETN, and LEP which are involved in gluconeogenesis. Conversely, increased levels of fatty acids lead to the induction of PPARG which promotes CEBPA.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

