xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected. NAFLD was shown to be associated with mutations in the PNPLA3 gene.    Signaling description: PNPLA3 plays an important role in hepatocyte lipogenesis. Insulin resistance and disordered lipid metabolism contribute to the development of NAFLD. Normally, insulin (INS) inhibits triglyceride storage via the following mechanism. Activated INSR and IGF1R stimulate PDPK1 via IRS2/PIK3R1 and the formation of PIP3. In turn, PDPK1 activates AKT1 which inhibits FOXO1.    Outcome effects: FOXO1 is the activator of CEBPA and, consequently SREBF1, which is in turn the activator of PNPLA3 via MLXIPL. PNPLA3 is a triacylglycerol lipase which mediates triacylglycerol hydrolysis in hepatocytes. In the liver4, fatty acids induce SREBF1 which promotes PNPLA3 via MLXIPL.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: PNPLA3 mutated in NAFLD is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected. NAFLD was shown to be associated with mutations in the PNPLA3 gene.    Signaling description: PNPLA3 plays an important role in hepatocyte lipogenesis. Insulin resistance and disordered lipid metabolism contribute to the development of NAFLD. Normally, insulin (INS) inhibits triglyceride storage via the following mechanism. Activated INSR and IGF1R stimulate PDPK1 via IRS2/PIK3R1 and the formation of PIP3. In turn, PDPK1 activates AKT1 which inhibits FOXO1.    Outcome effects: FOXO1 is the activator of CEBPA and, consequently SREBF1, which is in turn the activator of PNPLA3 via MLXIPL. PNPLA3 is a triacylglycerol lipase which mediates triacylglycerol hydrolysis in hepatocytes. In the liver4, fatty acids induce SREBF1 which promotes PNPLA3 via MLXIPL.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated proteins: PNPLA3 mutated in NAFLD is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

