xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected. Liver is the major organ for the maintenance of energy homeostasis. A common associated clinical feature of patients with NAFLD is impaired lipid metabolism associated with insulin resistance.I     Signaling description: Insulin resistance results in the inhibition of glycogenesis in the liver, which subsequently leads to the accumulation of fat. Normally, insulin (INS) stimulates glycogenesis. Activated INSR and IGF1R stimulate PDPK1 via IRS2, PIK3R1 and the formation of PIP3. PDPK1 activates AKT1, which phosphorylates GSK3B causing its proteolytic removal. Active GSK3B stimulates CEBPA and inhibits CREB1. CEBPA inactivates NR1H3 and CREB1 promotes SP1 and HIF1A. NR1H3, SP1, and HIF1A promote GCK, PCK2, PGM1, and PFKFB2 which are involved in glycogenesis.    Outcome effects: High fatty acid conditions lead to the induction of PPARG, PPARGC1A, and CYP2E1. CYP2E1 is frequently induced in steatosis. Active CYP2E1 is involved in the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and plays an important role in oxidative stress. PPARGC1B activates SREBF1. SREBF1 and PPARG promote SCD, ACACA, ACSL1, DGAT1, GPAM, FADS2, ACLY, and FASN which are involved in fatty acid and triacylglycerol biosynthesis. The overproduction and deposit of lipids and triglycerides in hepatocytes are the main pathophysiological features of NAFLD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected. Liver is the major organ for the maintenance of energy homeostasis. A common associated clinical feature of patients with NAFLD is impaired lipid metabolism associated with insulin resistance.I     Signaling description: Insulin resistance results in the inhibition of glycogenesis in the liver, which subsequently leads to the accumulation of fat. Normally, insulin (INS) stimulates glycogenesis. Activated INSR and IGF1R stimulate PDPK1 via IRS2, PIK3R1 and the formation of PIP3. PDPK1 activates AKT1, which phosphorylates GSK3B causing its proteolytic removal. Active GSK3B stimulates CEBPA and inhibits CREB1. CEBPA inactivates NR1H3 and CREB1 promotes SP1 and HIF1A. NR1H3, SP1, and HIF1A promote GCK, PCK2, PGM1, and PFKFB2 which are involved in glycogenesis.    Outcome effects: High fatty acid conditions lead to the induction of PPARG, PPARGC1A, and CYP2E1. CYP2E1 is frequently induced in steatosis. Active CYP2E1 is involved in the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and plays an important role in oxidative stress. PPARGC1B activates SREBF1. SREBF1 and PPARG promote SCD, ACACA, ACSL1, DGAT1, GPAM, FADS2, ACLY, and FASN which are involved in fatty acid and triacylglycerol biosynthesis. The overproduction and deposit of lipids and triglycerides in hepatocytes are the main pathophysiological features of NAFLD.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

