xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected. NAFLD involves decreased fatty acid oxidation which leads to the accumulation of triglycerides in hepatocytes.    Signaling description: Obesity and NAFLD are interconnected. Hypertrophic adipocytes release proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF and IL6. TNF promotes inflammatory responses to injury and regulates insulin resistance. The overexpression of TNF plays an important role in the development and progression of metabolic disorders in humans via the following mechanism. Activated TNFRSF1A binds to TRADD/RIPK1/TRAF2 and via MAPKs activates NF-kB. Activated IL6ST and IL6R via JAK1 stimulate STAT3 which promotes CEBPB. CEBPB binds NF-kB and activates HIF1A which inhibits PPARA.    Outcome effects: PPARA is responsible for the induction of ACADM, ACADL, PC, ACOT2, HADH, PDK2/4, ACADVL, CPT2, and ACSL1 all of which are involved in fatty acid oxidation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) refers to a spectrum of liver diseases characterized by abnormal deposition of lipids (triglycerides) in hepatocytes. NAFLD ranges from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) which can have different degrees of fibrosis and progress to liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Type 2 diabetes, obesity, metabolic syndrome, and NAFLD are closely interconnected. NAFLD involves decreased fatty acid oxidation which leads to the accumulation of triglycerides in hepatocytes.    Signaling description: Obesity and NAFLD are interconnected. Hypertrophic adipocytes release proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF and IL6. TNF promotes inflammatory responses to injury and regulates insulin resistance. The overexpression of TNF plays an important role in the development and progression of metabolic disorders in humans via the following mechanism. Activated TNFRSF1A binds to TRADD/RIPK1/TRAF2 and via MAPKs activates NF-kB. Activated IL6ST and IL6R via JAK1 stimulate STAT3 which promotes CEBPB. CEBPB binds NF-kB and activates HIF1A which inhibits PPARA.    Outcome effects: PPARA is responsible for the induction of ACADM, ACADL, PC, ACOT2, HADH, PDK2/4, ACADVL, CPT2, and ACSL1 all of which are involved in fatty acid oxidation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

