xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prolactinoma is a benign tumor of the pituitary that produces prolactin (PRL), it is the most common type of pituitary tumor (about 40% of all pituitary tumors). Prolactinomas are more common in women, with a peak incidence during the childbearing years, but they can also appear in men. Rarely prolactinomas may be malign and metastatic. Prolactinoma symptoms include galactorrhea and amenorrhea in women, gynecomastia, decreased libido an erectile dysfunction in men. In both sexes it may lead to infertility.     Signaling description: A number of genes may be mutated in prolacinoma, including GNAS1, HRAS, PIK3CA, AIP, MEN1. Gain-of-function GNAS1 mutation leads to constitutive activation of the cAMP pathway and PRL hyperproduction. Menin (MEN1) is a tumor suppressor protein that regulates transcription of cyclin kinase inhibitors. Loss-of-function mutations in MEN1 leads to development multiple endocrine neoplasia syndrome type I, including prolactinoma. Aryl hydrocarbon interacting protein (AIP) associates with the cytoplasmic aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR), which is a transcription factor that interacts with proteins which regulate cell cycle such as retinoblastoma protein (RB1). AIP also regulates the localization and activity of phosphodiesterase 4A (PDE4A), an enzyme responsible for the hydrolysis of intracellular cAMP. Loss-of-function AIP mutation leads to decreased PDE4A5 activity resulting in persistent cAMP signaling. A number of factors may be overexpressed in prolactinoma including PTTG1 and HMGA2. Overexpression of PTTG1 leads to high expression of FGF2 and VEGF with subsequent lactotroph hyperplasia and increased angiogenesis. Overexpression of the HMGA2 may be responsible for POU1F1 upregulation. HMGA2 also interacts with RB1 and facilitates the expression of E2F1 target genes through histone modifications and acetylation of E2F1 itself, thereby promoting the G1/S cell phase transition. Recently, HRAS and PIK3CA mutations have been reported in invasive prolactinomas.    Outcome effects: The large majority of patients with prolactinomas can be successfully treated with dopamine D2 receptor (DRD2) agonists as first-line treatment, with normalization of prolactin secretion and gonadal function. However, about 20% of prolactinomas may exhibit either dopamine agonist resistance or high recurrence rates after surgery.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prolactinoma is a benign tumor of the pituitary that produces prolactin (PRL), it is the most common type of pituitary tumor (about 40% of all pituitary tumors). Prolactinomas are more common in women, with a peak incidence during the childbearing years, but they can also appear in men. Rarely prolactinomas may be malign and metastatic. Prolactinoma symptoms include galactorrhea and amenorrhea in women, gynecomastia, decreased libido an erectile dysfunction in men. In both sexes it may lead to infertility.     Signaling description: A number of genes may be mutated in prolacinoma, including GNAS1, HRAS, PIK3CA, AIP, MEN1. Gain-of-function GNAS1 mutation leads to constitutive activation of the cAMP pathway and PRL hyperproduction. Menin (MEN1) is a tumor suppressor protein that regulates transcription of cyclin kinase inhibitors. Loss-of-function mutations in MEN1 leads to development multiple endocrine neoplasia syndrome type I, including prolactinoma. Aryl hydrocarbon interacting protein (AIP) associates with the cytoplasmic aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR), which is a transcription factor that interacts with proteins which regulate cell cycle such as retinoblastoma protein (RB1). AIP also regulates the localization and activity of phosphodiesterase 4A (PDE4A), an enzyme responsible for the hydrolysis of intracellular cAMP. Loss-of-function AIP mutation leads to decreased PDE4A5 activity resulting in persistent cAMP signaling. A number of factors may be overexpressed in prolactinoma including PTTG1 and HMGA2. Overexpression of PTTG1 leads to high expression of FGF2 and VEGF with subsequent lactotroph hyperplasia and increased angiogenesis. Overexpression of the HMGA2 may be responsible for POU1F1 upregulation. HMGA2 also interacts with RB1 and facilitates the expression of E2F1 target genes through histone modifications and acetylation of E2F1 itself, thereby promoting the G1/S cell phase transition. Recently, HRAS and PIK3CA mutations have been reported in invasive prolactinomas.    Outcome effects: The large majority of patients with prolactinomas can be successfully treated with dopamine D2 receptor (DRD2) agonists as first-line treatment, with normalization of prolactin secretion and gonadal function. However, about 20% of prolactinomas may exhibit either dopamine agonist resistance or high recurrence rates after surgery.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

