xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. HT results from a complex combination of genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors which interplay to initiate thyroid autoimmunity. Thyroid follicle damage in HT may be provoked by self-antigen presentation by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) and specific T-lymphocyte activation.    Signaling description: The central phase of HT is characterized by an apparent uncontrolled production of autoreactive CD4 T cells, CD8 cytotoxic T-cells, and immunoglobulin G autoantibodies. Studies involving HT patients indicate a strong association between TAb production and three CTLA4 single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) including -318C/T in the promoter, 49A/G, and CT60. The SNP of the PTPN22 gene, resulting in arginine to tryptophan substitution at codon 620 (R620W), has been demonstrated to be a risk factor for many autoimmune diseases including HT. VDR-FokI SNP in exon 2 has also been identified in HT. The C allele of tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-beta -1031T/C SNP inhibitor of cytokine production is also associated with HT. Accordingly, in patients with HT, serum levels of TGFB1 were lower than in controls which did not change following treatment with levothyroxine.    Outcome effects: In HT, thyrocytes with an aberrant expression of human leukocyte antigen (HLA) class II molecules may act as APCs capable of presenting thyroid autoantigens and initiating autoimmune thyroid disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. HT results from a complex combination of genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors which interplay to initiate thyroid autoimmunity. Thyroid follicle damage in HT may be provoked by self-antigen presentation by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) and specific T-lymphocyte activation.    Signaling description: The central phase of HT is characterized by an apparent uncontrolled production of autoreactive CD4 T cells, CD8 cytotoxic T-cells, and immunoglobulin G autoantibodies. Studies involving HT patients indicate a strong association between TAb production and three CTLA4 single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) including -318C/T in the promoter, 49A/G, and CT60. The SNP of the PTPN22 gene, resulting in arginine to tryptophan substitution at codon 620 (R620W), has been demonstrated to be a risk factor for many autoimmune diseases including HT. VDR-FokI SNP in exon 2 has also been identified in HT. The C allele of tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-beta -1031T/C SNP inhibitor of cytokine production is also associated with HT. Accordingly, in patients with HT, serum levels of TGFB1 were lower than in controls which did not change following treatment with levothyroxine.    Outcome effects: In HT, thyrocytes with an aberrant expression of human leukocyte antigen (HLA) class II molecules may act as APCs capable of presenting thyroid autoantigens and initiating autoimmune thyroid disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

