xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. HT results from a complex combination of genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors which interplay to initiate thyroid autoimmunity. In the final stages, fibrous tissue replaces normal thyroid parenchyma with some people developing hypothyroidism.    Signaling description: The central phase of HT is characterized by an apparent uncontrolled production of auto-reactive CD4 T-cells, CD8 cytotoxic T-cells, and immunoglobulin G autoantibodies. In HT, INF-gamma induced IL18 production activates local immune response. Genetic predisposition of HT requires additional studies.Candidate mutated genes in HT can be classified into two groups: (1) immune regulatory genes (MHC, CTLA-4, PTPN-22, and cytokines) and (2) thyroid-specific genes (TG and TPO).    Outcome effects: HT is considered to be a Th1-mediated disease resulting in the aberrant infiltration of lymphoid cells and destruction of thyroid follicles. IL18 up-regulation by INF-gamma is an immunological feature of HT patients with an important role in promoting the local immune response. Additionally, after IFN-gamma stimulation, thyrocytes secrete CXCR3-binding chemokines which in turn recruit Th1 lymphocytes causing a chronic autoimmune reaction.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. HT results from a complex combination of genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors which interplay to initiate thyroid autoimmunity. In the final stages, fibrous tissue replaces normal thyroid parenchyma with some people developing hypothyroidism.    Signaling description: The central phase of HT is characterized by an apparent uncontrolled production of auto-reactive CD4 T-cells, CD8 cytotoxic T-cells, and immunoglobulin G autoantibodies. In HT, INF-gamma induced IL18 production activates local immune response. Genetic predisposition of HT requires additional studies.Candidate mutated genes in HT can be classified into two groups: (1) immune regulatory genes (MHC, CTLA-4, PTPN-22, and cytokines) and (2) thyroid-specific genes (TG and TPO).    Outcome effects: HT is considered to be a Th1-mediated disease resulting in the aberrant infiltration of lymphoid cells and destruction of thyroid follicles. IL18 up-regulation by INF-gamma is an immunological feature of HT patients with an important role in promoting the local immune response. Additionally, after IFN-gamma stimulation, thyrocytes secrete CXCR3-binding chemokines which in turn recruit Th1 lymphocytes causing a chronic autoimmune reaction.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

