synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. HT results from a complex combination of genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors which interplay to initiate thyroid autoimmunity. HT is considered to be a Th1-mediated disease resulting in the aberrant infiltration of lymphoid cells and destruction of thyroid follicles. Apoptosis or programmed cell death appears to play a major role in the final stage of HT.    Signaling description: An increased number of apoptotic cells is observed in patients with HT; however, the mechanism and regulation of apoptosis in the thyroid gland in HT remains unclear. The interaction with autoantigen leads to the production of different cytokines inducing T-helper type 1 (Th1)-mediated cell immune response. The stimulation of the Fas/Fas ligand apoptotic pathway by pro-inflammatory cytokines is the most important mechanism of T lymphocyte mediated apoptosis. Apoptotic molecules such as FAS and Fas ligand (FASLG) are expressed at low levels by normal thyroid cells compared to patients with HT.    Outcome effects: FAS can facilitate apoptosis in thyroid cells via DAXX and RIPK1 leading to hypothyroidism. In addition, polymorphisms in ENO1, which is involved in triggering apoptosis in thyroid cells, are associated with HT. As a (Hypothesis), single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in ENO1 associated with HT may be related to fibrosis and hypothyroidism.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. HT results from a complex combination of genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors which interplay to initiate thyroid autoimmunity. HT is considered to be a Th1-mediated disease resulting in the aberrant infiltration of lymphoid cells and destruction of thyroid follicles. Apoptosis or programmed cell death appears to play a major role in the final stage of HT.    Signaling description: An increased number of apoptotic cells is observed in patients with HT; however, the mechanism and regulation of apoptosis in the thyroid gland in HT remains unclear. The interaction with autoantigen leads to the production of different cytokines inducing T-helper type 1 (Th1)-mediated cell immune response. The stimulation of the Fas/Fas ligand apoptotic pathway by pro-inflammatory cytokines is the most important mechanism of T lymphocyte mediated apoptosis. Apoptotic molecules such as FAS and Fas ligand (FASLG) are expressed at low levels by normal thyroid cells compared to patients with HT.    Outcome effects: FAS can facilitate apoptosis in thyroid cells via DAXX and RIPK1 leading to hypothyroidism. In addition, polymorphisms in ENO1, which is involved in triggering apoptosis in thyroid cells, are associated with HT. As a (Hypothesis), single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) in ENO1 associated with HT may be related to fibrosis and hypothyroidism.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

