xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. Environmental factors, such as infection, certain drugs, stress or smoking, can play a significant role in HT progression. The close relationship between HT and either excessive iodine levels or selenium deficiency has been demonstrated. High levels of certain chemical agents have also been implicated in the incidence of goiter and autoimmune thyroiditis. Moreover, the components of several viruses (hepatitis C, human parvovirus B19, coxsackie, and herpes viruses) were detected in the thyroid of HT patients. The possible involvement of the oxidative stress profile in HT pathogenesis was also reported.    Signaling description: Several putative mechanisms for iodine involvement in thyroid autoimmunity have been proposed. Firstly, iodine exposure leads to a higher iodination of the major thyroid antigen, thyroglobulin (TG) and thus increases its immunogenicity and enhances the binding affinity of the T-cell receptor which may lead to the specific T-cell activation. Iodine excess has also been shown to promote follicular cell apoptosis by inducing an abnormal expression of tumor necrosis factor-related apoptosis-inducing ligand (TRAIL) in the thyroid. Iodine exposure has been shown to increase the level of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the thyrocyte during TPO oxidation of excessive amounts of iodine. Recent advances have shown that selenium (Se) is very important for thyroid function. There are at least 30 selenium dependent proteins, including the glutathione peroxidase enzyme. A Se deficiency may cause a decrease in the activity of glutathione peroxidase and consequently to an increase in ROS levels. Lastly, chronic IFNA1 and other cytokine treatment may also provoke HT.    Outcome effects: ROS enhance the expression of the intracellular adhesion molecule-1 (ICAM-1) on the thyroid follicular cells which attract the immunocompetent cells into the thyroid gland and initiate an immune reaction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hashimoto's thyroiditis (HT) is an autoimmune thyroid disease in which the thyroid gland is progressively destroyed. HT is one of the most common causes of hypothyroidism affecting 2-10% of the population, with women being more susceptible. Environmental factors, such as infection, certain drugs, stress or smoking, can play a significant role in HT progression. The close relationship between HT and either excessive iodine levels or selenium deficiency has been demonstrated. High levels of certain chemical agents have also been implicated in the incidence of goiter and autoimmune thyroiditis. Moreover, the components of several viruses (hepatitis C, human parvovirus B19, coxsackie, and herpes viruses) were detected in the thyroid of HT patients. The possible involvement of the oxidative stress profile in HT pathogenesis was also reported.    Signaling description: Several putative mechanisms for iodine involvement in thyroid autoimmunity have been proposed. Firstly, iodine exposure leads to a higher iodination of the major thyroid antigen, thyroglobulin (TG) and thus increases its immunogenicity and enhances the binding affinity of the T-cell receptor which may lead to the specific T-cell activation. Iodine excess has also been shown to promote follicular cell apoptosis by inducing an abnormal expression of tumor necrosis factor-related apoptosis-inducing ligand (TRAIL) in the thyroid. Iodine exposure has been shown to increase the level of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the thyrocyte during TPO oxidation of excessive amounts of iodine. Recent advances have shown that selenium (Se) is very important for thyroid function. There are at least 30 selenium dependent proteins, including the glutathione peroxidase enzyme. A Se deficiency may cause a decrease in the activity of glutathione peroxidase and consequently to an increase in ROS levels. Lastly, chronic IFNA1 and other cytokine treatment may also provoke HT.    Outcome effects: ROS enhance the expression of the intracellular adhesion molecule-1 (ICAM-1) on the thyroid follicular cells which attract the immunocompetent cells into the thyroid gland and initiate an immune reaction.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

