xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism is characterized by life-threatening hypersecretion of parathormone which usually manifests itself in the first six months of life. Neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism can be a devastating neurodevelopmental disorder with failure to thrive and demineralization of bone which in some cases is lethal without parathyroidectomy.    Signaling description: Germline-inactivating homozygous CASR mutations result in neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism. Heterozygous mutations in CASR usually result in mild hypercalcemia seen in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia. It is known that mutations in CASR cause the disruption of PTH secretion. At the same time, the precise mechanisms of the PTH expression regulation are not well understood. One of hypotheses is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: CASR is implicated in calcium homeostasis. Under normal conditions, increasing extracellular calcium activates CASR, thus diminishing the PTH release and augmenting renal calcium excretion. Activation of CASR stimulates PLCB1 via GNAQ resulting in the PKC/MAPK/CREB cascade activation. CREB promotes SP1 which interacts with PIN1.In turn, PIN1 regulates KHSRP and HNRNPD involved in PTH expression.    Highlighted proteins: PTH highlighted in red has increased expression in neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism.    Mutated genes: CASR with mutations in neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism is characterized by life-threatening hypersecretion of parathormone which usually manifests itself in the first six months of life. Neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism can be a devastating neurodevelopmental disorder with failure to thrive and demineralization of bone which in some cases is lethal without parathyroidectomy.    Signaling description: Germline-inactivating homozygous CASR mutations result in neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism. Heterozygous mutations in CASR usually result in mild hypercalcemia seen in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia. It is known that mutations in CASR cause the disruption of PTH secretion. At the same time, the precise mechanisms of the PTH expression regulation are not well understood. One of hypotheses is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: CASR is implicated in calcium homeostasis. Under normal conditions, increasing extracellular calcium activates CASR, thus diminishing the PTH release and augmenting renal calcium excretion. Activation of CASR stimulates PLCB1 via GNAQ resulting in the PKC/MAPK/CREB cascade activation. CREB promotes SP1 which interacts with PIN1.In turn, PIN1 regulates KHSRP and HNRNPD involved in PTH expression.    Highlighted proteins: PTH highlighted in red has increased expression in neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism.    Mutated genes: CASR with mutations in neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

