xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyperprolactinaemia refers to abnormally high levels of prolactin in the blood. Inhibition of prolactin (PRL) production and release through activation of DRD2 receptor signaling by dopamine is a well-known mechanism for the development of hyperprolactinaemia. Pharmacological inhibitors of DRD2 were shown to induce hyperprolactinemia.     Signaling description: Several drugs may determine a significant increase in PRL serum concentration. The so-called typical antipsychotics are frequently responsible for drug-related hyperprolactinemia. The second- and third generation of antipsychotics have a weaker affinity for DRD2 dopamine receptors, thus hyperprolactinemia is less common when such medication is used. Other compounds may determine an increase in prolactin levels, including prokinetics, opiates, estrogens, anti-androgens, anti-hypertensive drugs, H2-receptor antagonists, anti-convulsivants and cholinomimetics.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyperprolactinaemia refers to abnormally high levels of prolactin in the blood. Inhibition of prolactin (PRL) production and release through activation of DRD2 receptor signaling by dopamine is a well-known mechanism for the development of hyperprolactinaemia. Pharmacological inhibitors of DRD2 were shown to induce hyperprolactinemia.     Signaling description: Several drugs may determine a significant increase in PRL serum concentration. The so-called typical antipsychotics are frequently responsible for drug-related hyperprolactinemia. The second- and third generation of antipsychotics have a weaker affinity for DRD2 dopamine receptors, thus hyperprolactinemia is less common when such medication is used. Other compounds may determine an increase in prolactin levels, including prokinetics, opiates, estrogens, anti-androgens, anti-hypertensive drugs, H2-receptor antagonists, anti-convulsivants and cholinomimetics.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

