xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Several drugs may increase PRL serum concentration. The so-called typical antipsychotics are frequently responsible for drug-related hyperprolactinemia. The second- and third generation antipsychotics have a weaker affinity for DRD2 dopamine receptors, thus hyperprolactinemia is less common when such medication is used. Other compounds may determine an increase in prolactin levels, including prokinetics, opiates, estrogens, anti-androgens, anti-hypertensive drugs, H2-receptor antagonists, anti-convulsivants and cholinomimetics.     Reference:Castan~o Lo'pez MA, Robles Rodri'guez JL, Garci'a MR (2013). Physiological and Pathological Hyperprolactinemia: Can We Minimize Errors in the Clinical Practice?, Prolactin, Prof. György M. Nagy (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0943-3, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/54758. Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/prolactin/physiological-and-pathological-hyperprolactinemia-can-we-minimize-errors-in-the-clinical-practice-"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Several drugs may increase PRL serum concentration. The so-called typical antipsychotics are frequently responsible for drug-related hyperprolactinemia. The second- and third generation antipsychotics have a weaker affinity for DRD2 dopamine receptors, thus hyperprolactinemia is less common when such medication is used. Other compounds may determine an increase in prolactin levels, including prokinetics, opiates, estrogens, anti-androgens, anti-hypertensive drugs, H2-receptor antagonists, anti-convulsivants and cholinomimetics.     Reference:Castan~o Lo'pez MA, Robles Rodri'guez JL, Garci'a MR (2013). Physiological and Pathological Hyperprolactinemia: Can We Minimize Errors in the Clinical Practice?, Prolactin, Prof. György M. Nagy (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0943-3, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/54758. Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/prolactin/physiological-and-pathological-hyperprolactinemia-can-we-minimize-errors-in-the-clinical-practice-" EXACT []

