xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, muscle weakness, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea and elevated heart rate.    Signaling description: The molecular mechanism of action of thyroid hormones can be genomic or non-genomic. In case of genomic regulation, thyroid hormone nuclear receptors (TH receptors) mediate the biological activities of triiodothyronine (T3) via transcriptional regulation. Two TH receptor genes (THRA and THRB) encode four T3-binding receptor isoforms. TH receptor isoforms have tissue-dependent and time-dependent expression. The major TH receptor isoform in the brain, heart, and bone is THRA, while in the kidney and thyroid it is THRB. T3 and thyroxine (T4) penetrate the target cell with the help of SLCO1C1 and SLC16A2 transporters. The pro-hormone, T4 is converted into the active T3 by type 1 (DIO1) or type 2 (DIO2) deiodinase in the cell. Type 3 deiodinase (DIO3) inactivates T3 and T4. The transcriptional activity of TH receptors is regulated at multiple levels. T3 binds to TH receptor. The binding of T3 causes the dissociation of corepressors and binding of coactivators of TH receptors, which recruit histone acetylases, leading to chromatin remodeling. In addition, TH receptors compete with RA receptor, VDR, and PPAR for retinoid-X receptor (RXR) heterodimerization, but may also form homodimers. Unliganded RXR-TR heterodimers bind thyroid response elements and mediate transcription repression via the nuclear receptor corepressor of RA receptor, which has histone deacetylase activity. The binding of thyroid receptor-associated protein, TRAP220 (MED1) recruits other TRAPs, thereby promoting active transcription. Since these corepressors and coactivators are common for many nuclear receptors, crosstalk may result from competitive binding. Promiscuous binding to thyroid hormone response elements could also mediate nuclear receptor crosstalk.    Outcome effects: The TH receptor can activates various cellular processes including cholesterol and lipid metabolism in the liver.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, muscle weakness, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea and elevated heart rate.    Signaling description: The molecular mechanism of action of thyroid hormones can be genomic or non-genomic. In case of genomic regulation, thyroid hormone nuclear receptors (TH receptors) mediate the biological activities of triiodothyronine (T3) via transcriptional regulation. Two TH receptor genes (THRA and THRB) encode four T3-binding receptor isoforms. TH receptor isoforms have tissue-dependent and time-dependent expression. The major TH receptor isoform in the brain, heart, and bone is THRA, while in the kidney and thyroid it is THRB. T3 and thyroxine (T4) penetrate the target cell with the help of SLCO1C1 and SLC16A2 transporters. The pro-hormone, T4 is converted into the active T3 by type 1 (DIO1) or type 2 (DIO2) deiodinase in the cell. Type 3 deiodinase (DIO3) inactivates T3 and T4. The transcriptional activity of TH receptors is regulated at multiple levels. T3 binds to TH receptor. The binding of T3 causes the dissociation of corepressors and binding of coactivators of TH receptors, which recruit histone acetylases, leading to chromatin remodeling. In addition, TH receptors compete with RA receptor, VDR, and PPAR for retinoid-X receptor (RXR) heterodimerization, but may also form homodimers. Unliganded RXR-TR heterodimers bind thyroid response elements and mediate transcription repression via the nuclear receptor corepressor of RA receptor, which has histone deacetylase activity. The binding of thyroid receptor-associated protein, TRAP220 (MED1) recruits other TRAPs, thereby promoting active transcription. Since these corepressors and coactivators are common for many nuclear receptors, crosstalk may result from competitive binding. Promiscuous binding to thyroid hormone response elements could also mediate nuclear receptor crosstalk.    Outcome effects: The TH receptor can activates various cellular processes including cholesterol and lipid metabolism in the liver.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

