xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, muscle weakness, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea and elevated heart rate.    Signaling description: The molecular mechanism of action of thyroid hormones can be genomic or non-genomic. The non-genomic actions of thyroid hormones may be initiated at the plasma membrane, in the cytoplasm or at intracellular organelles such as mitochondria, and do not primarily involve thyroid hormone nuclear receptors. There are several mechanisms of non-genomic actions of thyroid hormones. Through the vitronectin receptor (integrin), thyroid hormones stimulate MAP2K1 and MAP2K2 (ERK1/2), AKT1, and the release of calcium (Ca2 ) through phospholipase PTK2. Thyroxine (T4) is unable to activate PI3K. In the cytoplasm, plasma membrane ion pumps are critical to cell function. Ca2 -ATPase and Na /K -ATPase are known to be regulated by thyroid hormones by both genomic and non-genomic mechanisms. The non-genomic effects of thyroid hormones on Ca2 pump (Ca2 -ATPase) activity have been documented. Further, triiodothyronine (T3) can bind to THRB in the cytoplasm. Lastly, PIK3CA and HIF1A transcription may be activated.    Outcome effects: Complex cellular events induced by the integrin cell surface receptor include angiogenesis (endothelial and vascular smooth muscle cells) and tumor cell proliferation. Hyper- and hypothyroidism are associated with increased and decreased human erythrocyte Ca2 -ATPase activity, respectively. Cytoplasmic THRB activation is not linked to cell proliferation but to the shuttling of THRA from cytoplasm to nucleus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, muscle weakness, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea and elevated heart rate.    Signaling description: The molecular mechanism of action of thyroid hormones can be genomic or non-genomic. The non-genomic actions of thyroid hormones may be initiated at the plasma membrane, in the cytoplasm or at intracellular organelles such as mitochondria, and do not primarily involve thyroid hormone nuclear receptors. There are several mechanisms of non-genomic actions of thyroid hormones. Through the vitronectin receptor (integrin), thyroid hormones stimulate MAP2K1 and MAP2K2 (ERK1/2), AKT1, and the release of calcium (Ca2 ) through phospholipase PTK2. Thyroxine (T4) is unable to activate PI3K. In the cytoplasm, plasma membrane ion pumps are critical to cell function. Ca2 -ATPase and Na /K -ATPase are known to be regulated by thyroid hormones by both genomic and non-genomic mechanisms. The non-genomic effects of thyroid hormones on Ca2 pump (Ca2 -ATPase) activity have been documented. Further, triiodothyronine (T3) can bind to THRB in the cytoplasm. Lastly, PIK3CA and HIF1A transcription may be activated.    Outcome effects: Complex cellular events induced by the integrin cell surface receptor include angiogenesis (endothelial and vascular smooth muscle cells) and tumor cell proliferation. Hyper- and hypothyroidism are associated with increased and decreased human erythrocyte Ca2 -ATPase activity, respectively. Cytoplasmic THRB activation is not linked to cell proliferation but to the shuttling of THRA from cytoplasm to nucleus.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

