xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea, elevated heart rate and muscle weakness.    Signaling description: Thyroid hormones play a crucial role in skeletal growth, peak bone mass acquisition, and maintenance of bone mass. The thyroid hormone triiodothyronine (T3) has long been considered to play a primordial role in skeletal homeostasis. Bone architecture and strength are maintained by a balanced process of remodeling, which involves the recruitment of osteoclasts and osteoblasts. T3 can induce differentiation and inhibit proliferation of osteoblastic cells and mediate osteoclastic bone resorption through the activation of osteoblasts. However, the detailed mechanism of action of T3 in the bone is not well known.    Outcome effects: T3 is a critical regulator of the IHH-bone morphogenetic protein (BMP2)-PTHLH feedback loop. In hyperthyroidism, the reduced expression of PTHLH associated with the augmentation of BMP2 enhances hypertrophic chondrocyte differentiation through SMADs signaling. The activation of DKK1 and SOST by BMP signaling can lead to the inhibition of osteoblastic cell proliferation. Conversely, thyroid hormones may regulate the differentiation of chondrocytes through the activation of WNT4 and modulating WNT signaling pathways. The expression of THRA and THRB has been described in growth plate chondrocytes, osteoblasts, and stromal cells of bone marrow and may mediate their development through common genomic and non-genomic actions. In addition, mature osteoblasts release TNFSF11, a ligand for TNFRSF11B, which is a cytokine that regulates osteoclastic differentiation and functions as a key factor for osteoclast differentiation and activation by inhibiting osteoclast apoptosis. Furthermore, T3 has been shown to promote the production of IGF1 and its binding proteins, IGFBP2-4 in bone marrow stromal cells and osteoblasts. T3 increases the expression of several bone-related genes through the IGF1R signaling (including osteocalcin (BGLAP), collagen type I, gelatinase B, and collagenase 3 (MMP13)). Finally, an excess of T3 can cause accelerated growth and bone formation in children. In adults, thyrotoxicosis leads to increased bone remodeling and an imbalance between bone resorption and formation which results in net bone loss and osteoporosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea, elevated heart rate and muscle weakness.    Signaling description: Thyroid hormones play a crucial role in skeletal growth, peak bone mass acquisition, and maintenance of bone mass. The thyroid hormone triiodothyronine (T3) has long been considered to play a primordial role in skeletal homeostasis. Bone architecture and strength are maintained by a balanced process of remodeling, which involves the recruitment of osteoclasts and osteoblasts. T3 can induce differentiation and inhibit proliferation of osteoblastic cells and mediate osteoclastic bone resorption through the activation of osteoblasts. However, the detailed mechanism of action of T3 in the bone is not well known.    Outcome effects: T3 is a critical regulator of the IHH-bone morphogenetic protein (BMP2)-PTHLH feedback loop. In hyperthyroidism, the reduced expression of PTHLH associated with the augmentation of BMP2 enhances hypertrophic chondrocyte differentiation through SMADs signaling. The activation of DKK1 and SOST by BMP signaling can lead to the inhibition of osteoblastic cell proliferation. Conversely, thyroid hormones may regulate the differentiation of chondrocytes through the activation of WNT4 and modulating WNT signaling pathways. The expression of THRA and THRB has been described in growth plate chondrocytes, osteoblasts, and stromal cells of bone marrow and may mediate their development through common genomic and non-genomic actions. In addition, mature osteoblasts release TNFSF11, a ligand for TNFRSF11B, which is a cytokine that regulates osteoclastic differentiation and functions as a key factor for osteoclast differentiation and activation by inhibiting osteoclast apoptosis. Furthermore, T3 has been shown to promote the production of IGF1 and its binding proteins, IGFBP2-4 in bone marrow stromal cells and osteoblasts. T3 increases the expression of several bone-related genes through the IGF1R signaling (including osteocalcin (BGLAP), collagen type I, gelatinase B, and collagenase 3 (MMP13)). Finally, an excess of T3 can cause accelerated growth and bone formation in children. In adults, thyrotoxicosis leads to increased bone remodeling and an imbalance between bone resorption and formation which results in net bone loss and osteoporosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

