xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, muscle weakness, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea and elevated heart rate. The heart is a major target organ for thyroid hormone action, and noticeable changes occur in cardiac function in patients with hypo- and hyperthyroidism.    Signaling description: Genomic triiodothyronine (T3) effects are mediated by binding of T3 to specific nuclear receptor proteins, which results in increased transcription of T3-responsive cardiac genes. TR alpha (THRA) is the predominant isoform of thyroid hormone receptor in the heart. It regulates the transcription of several specific proteins critical for cardiac function including MYH genes. Extranuclear non-genomic T3 affects signaling through vitronectin receptor (integrin), which increases protein synthesis in the heart. The adrenergic-receptor complex in plasma membranes has also been shown to be upregulated by thyroid hormones. Beta 1-receptors (ADRB1 and ADRB2) are the predominant beta-adrenoceptors in cells of myocyte origin and may be responsive to T3 regulation through G protein signaling activation.    Outcome effects: T3 treatment decreases MYH6 gene expression leading to enhanced cardiac contractility. The cardiac cycle is divided into systolic contraction and diastolic relaxation. The rate of diastolic relaxation of the heart is related to intracellular calcium (Ca2 ) concentration and SERCA Ca2 -ATPase (ATP2A2) activity. T3 markedly increases the expression of the sarcoplasmic reticulum ATP2A2 gene. T3 activation through integrin and beta-adrenoceptors may stimulate Ca2 -ATPase activity as well as Ca2 movement across the membrane via changes in Ca2 channels. This also contributes to heart relaxation. Finally, T3 significantly shortens diastolic relaxation, and the hyperthyroid heart relaxes at a higher speed (lusitropic activity), while diastole is prolonged in hypothyroid states.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Headnote: Hyperthyroidism refers to overproduction of thyroid hormone by the thyroid gland. Symptoms include the enlargement of the thyroid gland, weight loss, muscle weakness, sleeping difficulties, heat intolerance, diarrhea and elevated heart rate. The heart is a major target organ for thyroid hormone action, and noticeable changes occur in cardiac function in patients with hypo- and hyperthyroidism.    Signaling description: Genomic triiodothyronine (T3) effects are mediated by binding of T3 to specific nuclear receptor proteins, which results in increased transcription of T3-responsive cardiac genes. TR alpha (THRA) is the predominant isoform of thyroid hormone receptor in the heart. It regulates the transcription of several specific proteins critical for cardiac function including MYH genes. Extranuclear non-genomic T3 affects signaling through vitronectin receptor (integrin), which increases protein synthesis in the heart. The adrenergic-receptor complex in plasma membranes has also been shown to be upregulated by thyroid hormones. Beta 1-receptors (ADRB1 and ADRB2) are the predominant beta-adrenoceptors in cells of myocyte origin and may be responsive to T3 regulation through G protein signaling activation.    Outcome effects: T3 treatment decreases MYH6 gene expression leading to enhanced cardiac contractility. The cardiac cycle is divided into systolic contraction and diastolic relaxation. The rate of diastolic relaxation of the heart is related to intracellular calcium (Ca2 ) concentration and SERCA Ca2 -ATPase (ATP2A2) activity. T3 markedly increases the expression of the sarcoplasmic reticulum ATP2A2 gene. T3 activation through integrin and beta-adrenoceptors may stimulate Ca2 -ATPase activity as well as Ca2 movement across the membrane via changes in Ca2 channels. This also contributes to heart relaxation. Finally, T3 significantly shortens diastolic relaxation, and the hyperthyroid heart relaxes at a higher speed (lusitropic activity), while diastole is prolonged in hypothyroid states.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

