synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The primary function of adipose tissue is to store energy in a triacylglycerol form, neutralizing the excess of circulating lipids and saving non-adipose tissues of a fat overload. Thyroid hormone excess is associated with weight loss and reduced muscle and fat mass, demonstrating that thyroid hormones play a central role in regulating adipose tissue metabolism.    Signaling description: Triiodothyronine (T3) can influence the metabolism of cholesterol at several critical proteins in the liver including LDLR which mediates cholesterol uptake from the circulation, ACACA which controls cholesterol biosynthesis, and CYP7A1 which is the rate-limiting enzyme in the synthesis of bile acids where cholesterol is used as a substrate. All of these proteins are regulated on the transcriptional level by thyroid hormone receptor activation. A potential interaction between leptin (LEP) and thyroid hormones has been suggested since both hormones are associated with body weight and energy expenditure regulation. The role of thyroid hormones in LEP modulation remains controversial. In hyperthyroid patients, serum LEP was found to be increased, decreased or unchanged when compared with euthyroid patients. In addition, thyroid hormones appear to regulate resistin (RETN). Some studies report that patients with hyperthyroidism have elevated RETN concentrations when compared to euthyroid control individuals. Furthermore, the levels of G6PD, ME1, and FASN are also increased in hyperthyroidism.    Outcome effects: Finally, a high level of thyroid hormones leads to hypermetabolism and weight loss despite increased energy intake.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Thyroid Hormone. Edited by Neeraj Kumar Agrawal (2012) InTech. 386 p. ISBN 978-953-51-0678-4."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The primary function of adipose tissue is to store energy in a triacylglycerol form, neutralizing the excess of circulating lipids and saving non-adipose tissues of a fat overload. Thyroid hormone excess is associated with weight loss and reduced muscle and fat mass, demonstrating that thyroid hormones play a central role in regulating adipose tissue metabolism.    Signaling description: Triiodothyronine (T3) can influence the metabolism of cholesterol at several critical proteins in the liver including LDLR which mediates cholesterol uptake from the circulation, ACACA which controls cholesterol biosynthesis, and CYP7A1 which is the rate-limiting enzyme in the synthesis of bile acids where cholesterol is used as a substrate. All of these proteins are regulated on the transcriptional level by thyroid hormone receptor activation. A potential interaction between leptin (LEP) and thyroid hormones has been suggested since both hormones are associated with body weight and energy expenditure regulation. The role of thyroid hormones in LEP modulation remains controversial. In hyperthyroid patients, serum LEP was found to be increased, decreased or unchanged when compared with euthyroid patients. In addition, thyroid hormones appear to regulate resistin (RETN). Some studies report that patients with hyperthyroidism have elevated RETN concentrations when compared to euthyroid control individuals. Furthermore, the levels of G6PD, ME1, and FASN are also increased in hyperthyroidism.    Outcome effects: Finally, a high level of thyroid hormones leads to hypermetabolism and weight loss despite increased energy intake.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Thyroid Hormone. Edited by Neeraj Kumar Agrawal (2012) InTech. 386 p. ISBN 978-953-51-0678-4." EXACT []

