xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Graves disease (GD) is characterized by hyperthyroidism, particular ophthalmopathy, and pretibial myxedema. As a result, the synthesis of thyroid hormones, metabolism, and the size of the thyroid gland increase. GD, Hashimoto's thyroiditis, and idiopathic thyroid failure are closely related and are essentially overlapping syndromes.    Signaling description: TSHR may act as an antigen that triggers the division and differentiation of lymphocytes and production of antibodies. Occasionally, thyroid peroxidase (TPO) or thyroglobulin (TG) may also serve as antigens. Autoantibody production in GD may be triggered in several ways. Generally, B-cells are activated through Th2 cells to produce TSHR antibodies. Mutations in MHC components (HLA-B8 and HLA-DRB3), CTLA4, and CD40 genes are considered the major congenital triggers of this process.    Outcome effects: HLA-B8 and HLA-DRB3 are expressed on the surface of antigen-presenting cells, and when mutated can cause recognition of TSHR as an antigen. CTLA4 receptor is an important co-stimulator of the T cell receptor (TCR) complex that initiates T-cell differentiation following antigen binding. The CD40 receptor is expressed on B-cells and is required for their activation. B-cells stimulate the proliferation of T cells predominantly through the CD80 synthesis, thus maintaining the chronic course of the immune response. B-cells may receive a stimulus for differentiation and production of autoantibodies through the direct interaction between antigen (TSHR) and B-cell receptor (BCR) complex. B-cells also produce immunoglobulin to TSHR. Patients have stimulating, blocking and neutral self-antibodies to TSHR simultaneously. The extent of their participation in the development of the disease can vary greatly from patient to patient.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Ben-Skowronek Iwona (2011). Graves Disease - The Interaction of Lymphocytes and Thyroid Cells, Autoimmune Disorders - Pathogenetic Aspects, Dr. Clio Mavragani (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-307-643-0, InTech, Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/autoimmune-disorders-pathogenetic-aspects/gravesdisease-the-interaction-of-lymphocytes-and-thyroid-cells 2). Sattler S, et al. (2012). Regulatory B Cells - Implications in Autoimmune and Allergic Disorders, Recent Advances in Immunology to Target Cancer, Inflammation and Infections, Dr. Jagat Kanwar (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0592- 3, InTech, Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/recent-advances-in-immunology-to-target-cancerinflammation-and-infections/regulatory-B-cells-implications-in-autoimmune-and-allergic-disorders"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Graves disease (GD) is characterized by hyperthyroidism, particular ophthalmopathy, and pretibial myxedema. As a result, the synthesis of thyroid hormones, metabolism, and the size of the thyroid gland increase. GD, Hashimoto's thyroiditis, and idiopathic thyroid failure are closely related and are essentially overlapping syndromes.    Signaling description: TSHR may act as an antigen that triggers the division and differentiation of lymphocytes and production of antibodies. Occasionally, thyroid peroxidase (TPO) or thyroglobulin (TG) may also serve as antigens. Autoantibody production in GD may be triggered in several ways. Generally, B-cells are activated through Th2 cells to produce TSHR antibodies. Mutations in MHC components (HLA-B8 and HLA-DRB3), CTLA4, and CD40 genes are considered the major congenital triggers of this process.    Outcome effects: HLA-B8 and HLA-DRB3 are expressed on the surface of antigen-presenting cells, and when mutated can cause recognition of TSHR as an antigen. CTLA4 receptor is an important co-stimulator of the T cell receptor (TCR) complex that initiates T-cell differentiation following antigen binding. The CD40 receptor is expressed on B-cells and is required for their activation. B-cells stimulate the proliferation of T cells predominantly through the CD80 synthesis, thus maintaining the chronic course of the immune response. B-cells may receive a stimulus for differentiation and production of autoantibodies through the direct interaction between antigen (TSHR) and B-cell receptor (BCR) complex. B-cells also produce immunoglobulin to TSHR. Patients have stimulating, blocking and neutral self-antibodies to TSHR simultaneously. The extent of their participation in the development of the disease can vary greatly from patient to patient.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Ben-Skowronek Iwona (2011). Graves Disease - The Interaction of Lymphocytes and Thyroid Cells, Autoimmune Disorders - Pathogenetic Aspects, Dr. Clio Mavragani (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-307-643-0, InTech, Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/autoimmune-disorders-pathogenetic-aspects/gravesdisease-the-interaction-of-lymphocytes-and-thyroid-cells 2). Sattler S, et al. (2012). Regulatory B Cells - Implications in Autoimmune and Allergic Disorders, Recent Advances in Immunology to Target Cancer, Inflammation and Infections, Dr. Jagat Kanwar (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0592- 3, InTech, Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/recent-advances-in-immunology-to-target-cancerinflammation-and-infections/regulatory-B-cells-implications-in-autoimmune-and-allergic-disorders" EXACT []

