xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (HH) is a condition which is characterized by hypogonadism due to an impaired secretion of gonadotropins by the pituitary gland and a follow-on lack of sex steroids production. Loss-of-function mutations in GNRH1, GNRHR, LHB, and FSHB are the main cause of the hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism progression.    Signaling description: Fertility in mammals is initiated at puberty by the pulsatile secretion of gonadotrophin releasing hormone (GNRH) from neurons in the hypothalamus. The GNRH acts on the anterior pituitary to stimulate the release of the gonadotrophic hormones, luteinizing hormone (LH), and follicle stimulating hormone (FSH). FSH is a dimmer and contains two subunits, FSHB and CGA. LH is also a dimmer and contains LHB and CGA. Upon GNRH binding to the GNRHR in the gonadotroph membrane GNRHR activates GNAS and GNAQ leading to activation of adenylyl cyclase (ADCY) and phospholipase C (PLC). ADCY generates cAMP, stimulating PKA which activates the transcription factor CREB1. PLC leads to activation of PKC, driving activation of MAPK and of MAPK-dependent transcription via EGR1 and JUN-FOS. It also elevates the cytoplasmic Ca2+ concentration, driving activation of calmodulin and its targets. A rise in Ca2+ is the key signal to trigger regulated exocytosis of FSH and LH.Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (HH) is a condition which is characterized by hypogonadism due to an impaired secretion of gonadotropins by the pituitary gland and a follow-on lack of sex steroids production. Loss-of-function mutations in GNRH1, GNRHR, LHB, and FSHB are the main cause of the hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism progression.    Signaling description: Fertility in mammals is initiated at puberty by the pulsatile secretion of gonadotrophin releasing hormone (GNRH) from neurons in the hypothalamus. The GNRH acts on the anterior pituitary to stimulate the release of the gonadotrophic hormones, luteinizing hormone (LH), and follicle stimulating hormone (FSH). FSH is a dimmer and contains two subunits, FSHB and CGA. LH is also a dimmer and contains LHB and CGA. Upon GNRH binding to the GNRHR in the gonadotroph membrane GNRHR activates GNAS and GNAQ leading to activation of adenylyl cyclase (ADCY) and phospholipase C (PLC). ADCY generates cAMP, stimulating PKA which activates the transcription factor CREB1. PLC leads to activation of PKC, driving activation of MAPK and of MAPK-dependent transcription via EGR1 and JUN-FOS. It also elevates the cytoplasmic Ca2+ concentration, driving activation of calmodulin and its targets. A rise in Ca2+ is the key signal to trigger regulated exocytosis of FSH and LH.Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

