xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) is a tripeptide (pGlu-His-Pro-NH2) hormone that is primarily produced in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus. The major recognized function of TRH is the maintenance of thyroid hormone homeostasis via the regulation of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).    Signaling description: The TRH peptide is synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported by the portal vascular system to the anterior pituitary where it acts on thyrotropic and lactotropic cells to promote the secretion of TSH and prolactin, respectively. Hypothyroidism may occur when the hypothalamus fails to produce sufficient levels of TRH. Therefore, it may also be termed hypothalamic-pituitary-axis hypothyroidism or tertiary hypothyroidism (accounts for less than 5% of hypothyroidism cases). The most prevalent cause of tertiary subtypes is a defective development of hypothalamus leading to multiple hormone deficiencies. Defects in the structure of hypothalamic peptides and their receptors have rarely been identified in the case of central congenital hypothyroidism. TRH is synthesized as a large pre-pro-TRH protein. The pre-pro-TRH processing is mostly mediated by prohormone convertases PCSK1 and PCSK2, which are strongly inhibited by triiodothyronine (T3). In case of primary hypothyroidism, the reduced level of T3 may enhance TRH protein processing and synthesis. Thyroid hormone receptor beta-2 (THRB) is the key isoform responsible for theT3-mediated feedback regulation by hypophysiotropic TRH neurons. Further, SLC16A2 is a thyroid hormone transporter expressed primarily in neurons and tanycytes may also be involved physiological feedback regulation. In addition, LEP acts directly on TRH neurons through the long isoforms of leptin receptor (LEPR) which activates STAT3 signaling. LEP can stimulate TRH expression indirectly by affecting alpha-melanotrophin (POMC)-neurons expressing LEPR that in turn release POMC. POMC, through melanocortin 4 receptor (MC4R) related p-CREB signaling, enhances TRH synthesis. Other neurons stimulated by the MC4R pathway may be involved in sympathetic activity or food intake regulation. Neuropeptide Y (NPY) may regulate TRH neurons through a mechanism depending upon the status of the melanocortin system.    Outcome effects: Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) or mutations in THRB cause insufficient TRH production. Then, SLC16A2 mutations result in hypothyroid TRH neurons regardless of the high concentration of circulating T3. Finally, partial and complete deficiencies of leptin (LEP) are associated with hypothyroidism. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) is a tripeptide (pGlu-His-Pro-NH2) hormone that is primarily produced in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus. The major recognized function of TRH is the maintenance of thyroid hormone homeostasis via the regulation of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).    Signaling description: The TRH peptide is synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported by the portal vascular system to the anterior pituitary where it acts on thyrotropic and lactotropic cells to promote the secretion of TSH and prolactin, respectively. Hypothyroidism may occur when the hypothalamus fails to produce sufficient levels of TRH. Therefore, it may also be termed hypothalamic-pituitary-axis hypothyroidism or tertiary hypothyroidism (accounts for less than 5% of hypothyroidism cases). The most prevalent cause of tertiary subtypes is a defective development of hypothalamus leading to multiple hormone deficiencies. Defects in the structure of hypothalamic peptides and their receptors have rarely been identified in the case of central congenital hypothyroidism. TRH is synthesized as a large pre-pro-TRH protein. The pre-pro-TRH processing is mostly mediated by prohormone convertases PCSK1 and PCSK2, which are strongly inhibited by triiodothyronine (T3). In case of primary hypothyroidism, the reduced level of T3 may enhance TRH protein processing and synthesis. Thyroid hormone receptor beta-2 (THRB) is the key isoform responsible for theT3-mediated feedback regulation by hypophysiotropic TRH neurons. Further, SLC16A2 is a thyroid hormone transporter expressed primarily in neurons and tanycytes may also be involved physiological feedback regulation. In addition, LEP acts directly on TRH neurons through the long isoforms of leptin receptor (LEPR) which activates STAT3 signaling. LEP can stimulate TRH expression indirectly by affecting alpha-melanotrophin (POMC)-neurons expressing LEPR that in turn release POMC. POMC, through melanocortin 4 receptor (MC4R) related p-CREB signaling, enhances TRH synthesis. Other neurons stimulated by the MC4R pathway may be involved in sympathetic activity or food intake regulation. Neuropeptide Y (NPY) may regulate TRH neurons through a mechanism depending upon the status of the melanocortin system.    Outcome effects: Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) or mutations in THRB cause insufficient TRH production. Then, SLC16A2 mutations result in hypothyroid TRH neurons regardless of the high concentration of circulating T3. Finally, partial and complete deficiencies of leptin (LEP) are associated with hypothyroidism. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

