xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypothyroidism is a pathological state in which the thyroid gland does not produce a sufficient amount of thyroid hormones. About 95% of hypothyroidism cases are caused by changes which occur in the thyroid gland.    Signaling description: The activity of the thyroid gland is predominantly regulated by the concentration of the pituitary glycoprotein hormone, thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH). TSH is a heterodimer consisting of alpha and beta subunits tightly, but non-covalently, bound. Both thyroid hormones (via their nuclear receptors (THRB and THRA)) directly inhibit the synthesis and release of TSH in pituitary cells. Primary hypothyroidism is the most common cause of elevated serum TSH. In the case of hypothyroidism, the reduced level of thyroid hormones enhances the level of TSH. Thyroid hormones can indirectly affect TSH synthesis via their effects on TRH synthesis and leptin (LEP) synthesis in adipocytes. The second major regulator of TSH production is the thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH). TRH is the major positive regulator of TSH beta gene expression and mainly acts by activating the thyrotropin-releasing hormone receptor (TRHR) which is a G protein-coupled receptor that activates the inositol phospholipid-calcium-protein kinase C transduction pathway upon the binding of TRH. TSH secretion in humans is pulsatile. The pulse frequency is slightly less than 2 hours. Dopamine plays a major role in the cyclic secretion of TSH. In addition, cold stress may play an important role in the regulation of TSH secretion by pituitary cells. The best known mechanism is adrenergic receptor signaling activation. Other hormones/factors such as CCK, GCG, AVP, GAL, GRP, and steroid hormones are also implicated in the complex regulation of TSH beta gene expression.    Outcome effects: Several inherited TSH-beta gene mutations are responsible for familial isolated central hypothyroidism. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Koreaki Sugimoto and Kouki Mori (2012) Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone Regulation and Transcription in Hypothyroidism, Hypothyroidism - Influences and Treatments, Dr. Drahomira Springer (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0021-8, InTech, Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/hypothyroidism-influences-andtreatments/thyroid-stimulating-hormone-regulation-and-transcription-in-hypothyroidism 2). Mariotti S (2011) Physiology of the Hypothalamic-Pituitary Thyroidal System. Available from: www.thyroidmanager.org. Published by ENDOCRINE EDUCATION Inc, South Dartmouth, MA 02748"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypothyroidism is a pathological state in which the thyroid gland does not produce a sufficient amount of thyroid hormones. About 95% of hypothyroidism cases are caused by changes which occur in the thyroid gland.    Signaling description: The activity of the thyroid gland is predominantly regulated by the concentration of the pituitary glycoprotein hormone, thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH). TSH is a heterodimer consisting of alpha and beta subunits tightly, but non-covalently, bound. Both thyroid hormones (via their nuclear receptors (THRB and THRA)) directly inhibit the synthesis and release of TSH in pituitary cells. Primary hypothyroidism is the most common cause of elevated serum TSH. In the case of hypothyroidism, the reduced level of thyroid hormones enhances the level of TSH. Thyroid hormones can indirectly affect TSH synthesis via their effects on TRH synthesis and leptin (LEP) synthesis in adipocytes. The second major regulator of TSH production is the thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH). TRH is the major positive regulator of TSH beta gene expression and mainly acts by activating the thyrotropin-releasing hormone receptor (TRHR) which is a G protein-coupled receptor that activates the inositol phospholipid-calcium-protein kinase C transduction pathway upon the binding of TRH. TSH secretion in humans is pulsatile. The pulse frequency is slightly less than 2 hours. Dopamine plays a major role in the cyclic secretion of TSH. In addition, cold stress may play an important role in the regulation of TSH secretion by pituitary cells. The best known mechanism is adrenergic receptor signaling activation. Other hormones/factors such as CCK, GCG, AVP, GAL, GRP, and steroid hormones are also implicated in the complex regulation of TSH beta gene expression.    Outcome effects: Several inherited TSH-beta gene mutations are responsible for familial isolated central hypothyroidism. .    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Koreaki Sugimoto and Kouki Mori (2012) Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone Regulation and Transcription in Hypothyroidism, Hypothyroidism - Influences and Treatments, Dr. Drahomira Springer (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0021-8, InTech, Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/hypothyroidism-influences-andtreatments/thyroid-stimulating-hormone-regulation-and-transcription-in-hypothyroidism 2). Mariotti S (2011) Physiology of the Hypothalamic-Pituitary Thyroidal System. Available from: www.thyroidmanager.org. Published by ENDOCRINE EDUCATION Inc, South Dartmouth, MA 02748" EXACT []

