xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myasthenia gravis (MG) is an autoimmune neuromuscular disease leading to muscle weakness and fatigue. In most cases, the first noticeable symptom is weakness of the extrinsic ocular muscles (EOMs) called ocular myasthenia.    Signaling description: Muscle weakness is caused by circulating self-antibodies that block the postsynaptic neuromuscular junction. In 80-85% of MG patients, auto-antibodies block the acetylcholine receptor (AChR), whereas in the remaining of MG patients, they block MUSK or LRP4. Thymic hyperplasia is observed in about 65% of MG patients and thymomas are present in about 10% of MG patients with anti-AChR antibodies. According to the nature of the antibodies, MG can be divided into several subtypes that differ by the age at onset, gender predominance, and thymus pathology. The neuromuscular junction is a structure of motoneurones in the presynaptic zone, containing synaptic vesicles filled with acetylcholine and the postsynaptic muscle. Normally, the cumulative activation of nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (nAChR) on the postsynaptic muscle membrane leads to the influx of Na or calcium Ca2+ ions into the muscle cell, which causes membrane depolarization. In addition, nAChR aggregation leads to the release of Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. When the levels of Ca2+ inside the muscle cell are sufficiently high, it will contract. The neuromuscular junction is initiated by the release of acetylcholine and agrin (AGRN) by motor neurons, which eventually leads to the clustering of nAChR, MUSK, and the dystrophin glycoprotein complex. Rapsyn (RAPSN) is a peripheral membrane protein associated with nAChRs and appears to be a central component of the clustering process.    Outcome effects: The neuromuscular junction of the extraocular muscle (EOM) in MG patients contains intrinsic complement regulators C3, C9, and the membrane attack complex. In rodents, anti-nAChR antibodies activate the complement cascade, resulting in the destruction of the postsynaptic neuromuscular junction membrane. The neuromuscular junctions of EOM express fewer intrinsic complement regulators (CD55, CD46, and CD59) which make them more susceptible to complement-mediated injury. In addition, they have less postsynaptic nAChRs and Na channels than skeletal muscle. In skeletal muscles, fast-twitch fibers have neuromuscular junctions with greater quantal contents. These properties make fast-twitch skeletal muscle fibers less susceptible to myasthenic failure than slow-twitch fibers in EOM.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myasthenia gravis (MG) is an autoimmune neuromuscular disease leading to muscle weakness and fatigue. In most cases, the first noticeable symptom is weakness of the extrinsic ocular muscles (EOMs) called ocular myasthenia.    Signaling description: Muscle weakness is caused by circulating self-antibodies that block the postsynaptic neuromuscular junction. In 80-85% of MG patients, auto-antibodies block the acetylcholine receptor (AChR), whereas in the remaining of MG patients, they block MUSK or LRP4. Thymic hyperplasia is observed in about 65% of MG patients and thymomas are present in about 10% of MG patients with anti-AChR antibodies. According to the nature of the antibodies, MG can be divided into several subtypes that differ by the age at onset, gender predominance, and thymus pathology. The neuromuscular junction is a structure of motoneurones in the presynaptic zone, containing synaptic vesicles filled with acetylcholine and the postsynaptic muscle. Normally, the cumulative activation of nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (nAChR) on the postsynaptic muscle membrane leads to the influx of Na or calcium Ca2+ ions into the muscle cell, which causes membrane depolarization. In addition, nAChR aggregation leads to the release of Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. When the levels of Ca2+ inside the muscle cell are sufficiently high, it will contract. The neuromuscular junction is initiated by the release of acetylcholine and agrin (AGRN) by motor neurons, which eventually leads to the clustering of nAChR, MUSK, and the dystrophin glycoprotein complex. Rapsyn (RAPSN) is a peripheral membrane protein associated with nAChRs and appears to be a central component of the clustering process.    Outcome effects: The neuromuscular junction of the extraocular muscle (EOM) in MG patients contains intrinsic complement regulators C3, C9, and the membrane attack complex. In rodents, anti-nAChR antibodies activate the complement cascade, resulting in the destruction of the postsynaptic neuromuscular junction membrane. The neuromuscular junctions of EOM express fewer intrinsic complement regulators (CD55, CD46, and CD59) which make them more susceptible to complement-mediated injury. In addition, they have less postsynaptic nAChRs and Na channels than skeletal muscle. In skeletal muscles, fast-twitch fibers have neuromuscular junctions with greater quantal contents. These properties make fast-twitch skeletal muscle fibers less susceptible to myasthenic failure than slow-twitch fibers in EOM.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

