xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a T-cell dependent, B-cell mediated autoimmune disease in which antibodies are directed against proteins concentrated at the neuromuscular junction, primarily against nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (nAChR). MG patients with the early-onset form have thymic follicular hyperplasia with elevated serum nAChR antibody titers and nAChR-specific CD4 T-cells.    Signaling description: The thymus is essential for T-cell differentiation and for the establishment of central tolerance. Normally, the number of B cells is very small in the thymus; while in the thymus of nAChR-MG patients, there is a presence of a tumor (i.e., thymoma) or germinal centers (GCs) containing a large number of B-cells (i.e., follicular hyperplasia). B-cells from MG thymus are already activated and do not require additional activation to produce anti-nAChR antibodies. Polymorphisms in CHRND, HLA A1-B8-DR3 haplotype, and the TNF-a-308 allele 2 have been shown to increase the risk of early-onset MG. Mutations in the CHRNA1 promoter are associated with early onset of autoimmune MG in two independent human populations (France and U.K.). IRF8 and AIRE are involved in regulating CHRNA1 expression as autoantigens in thymocytes. The inflammatory microenvironment is a strong predisposition factor for MG. The mutant variant of the main potent pro-inflammatory molecule, TNF correlates with overexpression of TNF in MG patients. TNF is significantly increased not only in thymus cells, but also in the sera of MG patients. Many factors trigger MG including infections, hormones, vitamin D, diet, and microbiota. Estrogens have a strong influence on the generation of naïve CD4 and CD8 T-cells in the thymus. Thymocytes from MG patients are more sensitive to the effects of estrogen and display an increased expression of ER-a due to the inflammatory environment. In MG patients, plasma vitamin D levels are significantly lower than in healthy controls.    Outcome effects: The pro-inflammatory effects of TNF are primarily due to its ability to activate NF-kB, leading to the expression of inflammatory genes. Thymic epithelial cells from MG patients overproduce pro-inflammatory IL6, IL1A, and IFNB1. Their expression may be induced by several pathways including LPS/TLR/NF-kB. TLR4 is overexpressed in MG thymus. TLR4 stimulation in MG thymic epithelial cells increases CCL17 and CCL22 expression and induces the production of Th17-related cytokines. IFNB1 induces specific a-AChR, CXCL13 expression in thymic epithelial cells. TNFSF13B, which favors autoreactive B cells, is also induced by IFNB1 in thymic epithelial cell cultures. TNFSF13B and BIRC5 may be involved in promoting the survival and maturation of autoreactive B cells in the disease. TNFSF13B levels are increased in patients with MG. BIRC5-positive cells with B lymphocyte and plasma cell markers are present at significant levels in the circulation of humans with MG. BIRC5 is absent in normal adult tissue. In addition, CXCL13, CCL21, and CXCL12 are involved in the attraction of peripheral B cells to the MG thymus. CXCL13 and CCL21 are overexpressed in the thymus of MG patients. In addition, miRNAs are involved in the control of the development of inflammatory T cells, including T helper 17 (Th17) cell and Th1 cell subsets. The expression of MIR155 and MIR146A in peripheral B cells is higher in MG when compared with healthy controls. Thymic regulatory T-cell (Treg) cells are dysfunctional in MG patients and thymic conventional T (Tconv) cells are resistant to suppression by Treg cells. The resistance of Tconv cells has been explained by the inflammatory microenvironment such as TNF which may contribute to the resistance of Tconv cells to suppression.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myasthenia gravis (MG) is a T-cell dependent, B-cell mediated autoimmune disease in which antibodies are directed against proteins concentrated at the neuromuscular junction, primarily against nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (nAChR). MG patients with the early-onset form have thymic follicular hyperplasia with elevated serum nAChR antibody titers and nAChR-specific CD4 T-cells.    Signaling description: The thymus is essential for T-cell differentiation and for the establishment of central tolerance. Normally, the number of B cells is very small in the thymus; while in the thymus of nAChR-MG patients, there is a presence of a tumor (i.e., thymoma) or germinal centers (GCs) containing a large number of B-cells (i.e., follicular hyperplasia). B-cells from MG thymus are already activated and do not require additional activation to produce anti-nAChR antibodies. Polymorphisms in CHRND, HLA A1-B8-DR3 haplotype, and the TNF-a-308 allele 2 have been shown to increase the risk of early-onset MG. Mutations in the CHRNA1 promoter are associated with early onset of autoimmune MG in two independent human populations (France and U.K.). IRF8 and AIRE are involved in regulating CHRNA1 expression as autoantigens in thymocytes. The inflammatory microenvironment is a strong predisposition factor for MG. The mutant variant of the main potent pro-inflammatory molecule, TNF correlates with overexpression of TNF in MG patients. TNF is significantly increased not only in thymus cells, but also in the sera of MG patients. Many factors trigger MG including infections, hormones, vitamin D, diet, and microbiota. Estrogens have a strong influence on the generation of naïve CD4 and CD8 T-cells in the thymus. Thymocytes from MG patients are more sensitive to the effects of estrogen and display an increased expression of ER-a due to the inflammatory environment. In MG patients, plasma vitamin D levels are significantly lower than in healthy controls.    Outcome effects