xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Celiac disease is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine which affects genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. Celiac disease is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Tissue transglutaminase is a calcium dependent enzyme which mediates ordered and specific deamidation of gliadins, creating an epitope that binds efficiently to DQ2 and is recognized by gut-derived T-cells. Throughout gluten consumption, these tissue transglutaminase autoantibodies are produced by the mucosa of the small intestine, and detected in patients' serum.CD4+ gluten-specific T helper (Th) cells recognize the deamidated gluten-derived peptides presented by HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 and respond by expressing high levels of interleukin 21 and interferon gamma.     Outcome effects: These cytokines affect the epithelial cells and activate intraepithelial lymphocytes, ultimately leading to villous atrophy. This response leads directly to tissue remodelling and flattening of the intestinal mucosa.Description of     Highlighted proteins: IL21, IL2, IL4, IL6, IFN-gamma and gliadin are highlighted in red in order to emphasize their increased expression and role in violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and immune response, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: CTLA4, HLA DQ2, HLA DQ8, ICOS and CD28 genes with white out style have genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Celiac disease is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine which affects genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. Celiac disease is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Tissue transglutaminase is a calcium dependent enzyme which mediates ordered and specific deamidation of gliadins, creating an epitope that binds efficiently to DQ2 and is recognized by gut-derived T-cells. Throughout gluten consumption, these tissue transglutaminase autoantibodies are produced by the mucosa of the small intestine, and detected in patients' serum.CD4+ gluten-specific T helper (Th) cells recognize the deamidated gluten-derived peptides presented by HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 and respond by expressing high levels of interleukin 21 and interferon gamma.     Outcome effects: These cytokines affect the epithelial cells and activate intraepithelial lymphocytes, ultimately leading to villous atrophy. This response leads directly to tissue remodelling and flattening of the intestinal mucosa.Description of     Highlighted proteins: IL21, IL2, IL4, IL6, IFN-gamma and gliadin are highlighted in red in order to emphasize their increased expression and role in violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and immune response, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: CTLA4, HLA DQ2, HLA DQ8, ICOS and CD28 genes with white out style have genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease." EXACT []

