xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Celiac disease (CeD) is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine affecting genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. CeD is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Immunodominant 33-mer peptide from a-gliadin translocates into the lamina propria via IFN? dependent transcytosis, which suggests involvement of the transcellular route. Protected transport pathway, which is driven by retrotranscytosis of secretory IgA through transferrin receptor CD71, promotes the influx of intact, and thus harmful, gluten peptides. Intestinal epithelium plays a central role in Celiac disease pathogenesis. It modulates the intestinal immune system that is acutely altered by gliadin. This indicates that gliadin can gain access to the basal surface of the epithelium, and therefore interact directly with the immune system, via both trans- and paracellular routes of absorption. Gluten peptides can be transported across the intestinal epithelium either paracellularly as a consequence of impaired mucosal integrity attributable to increased release of zonulin upon 19 mer gliadin binding to chemokine (C-X-C motif) receptor 3 (CXCR3 receptor).    Outcome effects: Zonulin induces a protein kinase C-mediated rearrangement of the cytoskeleton, downregulation of ZO-1 and occludin, and disruption of tight junction complex integrity, increasing epithelial permeability. Interleukin 15 (IL-15)-induced perforin/granzyme and homodimeric natural killer-activating receptor-major histocompatibility-class I chain-related gene A complex (NKG2D-MICA) signaling pathways promote increased cytotoxicity of intraepithelial lymphocytes. Intestinal epithelial cells express high levels of interleukin-15, along with MICs and HLA-E. IL-15 causes upregulation of NKG2D expression by intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes and the cytotoxic signalling pathway associated with this receptor. Consequently, intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes acquire killing activity and can target MIC-bearing intestinal epithelial cells. Description of highlighted proteins; IL15, HP, KLRK1 and gliadin are highlighted in red in order to emphasize their increased expression and violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and immune response, resulting in Celiac disease. TJP1 and OCLN are highlighted in blue in order to display decrease of the intestine barrier and the tight junction assembly, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: MICA gene in white out style has genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Celiac disease (CeD) is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine affecting genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. CeD is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Immunodominant 33-mer peptide from a-gliadin translocates into the lamina propria via IFN? dependent transcytosis, which suggests involvement of the transcellular route. Protected transport pathway, which is driven by retrotranscytosis of secretory IgA through transferrin receptor CD71, promotes the influx of intact, and thus harmful, gluten peptides. Intestinal epithelium plays a central role in Celiac disease pathogenesis. It modulates the intestinal immune system that is acutely altered by gliadin. This indicates that gliadin can gain access to the basal surface of the epithelium, and therefore interact directly with the immune system, via both trans- and paracellular routes of absorption. Gluten peptides can be transported across the intestinal epithelium either paracellularly as a consequence of impaired mucosal integrity attributable to increased release of zonulin upon 19 mer gliadin binding to chemokine (C-X-C motif) receptor 3 (CXCR3 receptor).    Outcome effects: Zonulin induces a protein kinase C-mediated rearrangement of the cytoskeleton, downregulation of ZO-1 and occludin, and disruption of tight junction complex integrity, increasing epithelial permeability. Interleukin 15 (IL-15)-induced perforin/granzyme and homodimeric natural killer-activating receptor-major histocompatibility-class I chain-related gene A complex (NKG2D-MICA) signaling pathways promote increased cytotoxicity of intraepithelial lymphocytes. Intestinal epithelial cells express high levels of interleukin-15, along with MICs and HLA-E. IL-15 causes upregulation of NKG2D expression by intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes and the cytotoxic signalling pathway associated with this receptor. Consequently, intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes acquire killing activity and can target MIC-bearing intestinal epithelial cells. Description of highlighted proteins; IL15, HP, KLRK1 and gliadin are highlighted in red in order to emphasize their increased expression and violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and immune response, resulting in Celiac disease. TJP1 and OCLN are highlighted in blue in order to display decrease of the intestine barrier and the tight junction assembly, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: MICA gene in white out style has genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease." EXACT []

