xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Celiac disease is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine which affects genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. Celiac disease is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Intestinal epithelial cells express high levels of interleukin-15, together with MICs and HLA-E. IL-15 leads to the upregulation of NKG2D expression by intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes and the cytotoxic signalling pathway associated with this receptor. Consequently, intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes acquire killing activity and can target MIC-bearing intestinal epithelial cells. Furthermore, intraepithelial cytotoxic T- lymphocytes of patients with coeliac disease express CD94-NKG2C-DAP12 immunoreceptor complexes that recognize HLA-E on intestinal epithelial cells and can promote cytokine secretion, proliferation and cytotoxic activity of T-cells in a T-cell receptor (TCR)-independent manner.    Outcome effects: Activation of NKG2D in conjunction with IL-15 expression leads to the release of arachidonic acids (and possibly leukotrienes) by intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes, which in turn can promote activation of intestinal granulocytes and inflammation.Description of     Highlighted proteins: IL15, IL2, IFN-gamma and FASLG are highlighted in red in order to emphasize their increased expression and role in violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and cell damage, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: MICA gene in white out style has genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Celiac disease is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine which affects genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. Celiac disease is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Intestinal epithelial cells express high levels of interleukin-15, together with MICs and HLA-E. IL-15 leads to the upregulation of NKG2D expression by intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes and the cytotoxic signalling pathway associated with this receptor. Consequently, intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes acquire killing activity and can target MIC-bearing intestinal epithelial cells. Furthermore, intraepithelial cytotoxic T- lymphocytes of patients with coeliac disease express CD94-NKG2C-DAP12 immunoreceptor complexes that recognize HLA-E on intestinal epithelial cells and can promote cytokine secretion, proliferation and cytotoxic activity of T-cells in a T-cell receptor (TCR)-independent manner.    Outcome effects: Activation of NKG2D in conjunction with IL-15 expression leads to the release of arachidonic acids (and possibly leukotrienes) by intraepithelial cytotoxic T-lymphocytes, which in turn can promote activation of intestinal granulocytes and inflammation.Description of     Highlighted proteins: IL15, IL2, IFN-gamma and FASLG are highlighted in red in order to emphasize their increased expression and role in violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and cell damage, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: MICA gene in white out style has genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease." EXACT []

