xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Celiac disease is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine affecting genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. Celiac disease is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Tissue transglutaminase deamidates or crosslinks 33 mer gliadin which is then recognized by human leukocyte antigen (HLA)-DQ2 or -DQ8 molecules of antigen presenting cell (APC). Activated gluten-reactive CD4+ T-cells produce high levels of pro-infl ammatory cytokines, thus inducing a T-helper cell-type-(Th)1 pattern dominated by interferon gamma. Gluten peptides can be transported across the intestinal epithelium either paracellularly as a consequence of impaired mucosal integrity attributable to increased release of zonulin, or via transcytosis, or retrotranscytosis of secretory IgA through transferrin receptor CD71.    Outcome effects: CD4+ gluten-specific T-helper (Th) cells recognize the deamidated gluten-derived peptides presented by HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 and respond by expressing high levels of interleukin 21 and interferon gamma. These cytokines affect the epithelial cells and activate intraepithelial lymphocytes, ultimately leading to villous atrophy. This response leads directly to tissue remodeling and flattening of the intestinal mucosa. Gluten peptides also trigger innate immune response characterized by an elevated expression of interleukin (IL)-15 by intestinal enterocytes. IL21 is critical for Th-cell-driven antibody responses and thus provides a link to B-cells.Description of     Highlighted proteins: IL21, IL15, IFN-gamma and HP highlighted in red in order to emphasize violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and immune response, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: CTLA4, HLA DQ2 and HLA DQ8 genes in whiteout style have genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Celiac disease is a life-long gluten-sensitive autoimmune disease of the small intestine affecting genetically susceptible individuals worldwide. Celiac disease is one of the most common diseases, resulting from both environmental (gluten) and genetic factors (human leukocyte antigen (HLA) and non-HLA genes).     Signaling description: Tissue transglutaminase deamidates or crosslinks 33 mer gliadin which is then recognized by human leukocyte antigen (HLA)-DQ2 or -DQ8 molecules of antigen presenting cell (APC). Activated gluten-reactive CD4+ T-cells produce high levels of pro-infl ammatory cytokines, thus inducing a T-helper cell-type-(Th)1 pattern dominated by interferon gamma. Gluten peptides can be transported across the intestinal epithelium either paracellularly as a consequence of impaired mucosal integrity attributable to increased release of zonulin, or via transcytosis, or retrotranscytosis of secretory IgA through transferrin receptor CD71.    Outcome effects: CD4+ gluten-specific T-helper (Th) cells recognize the deamidated gluten-derived peptides presented by HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 and respond by expressing high levels of interleukin 21 and interferon gamma. These cytokines affect the epithelial cells and activate intraepithelial lymphocytes, ultimately leading to villous atrophy. This response leads directly to tissue remodeling and flattening of the intestinal mucosa. Gluten peptides also trigger innate immune response characterized by an elevated expression of interleukin (IL)-15 by intestinal enterocytes. IL21 is critical for Th-cell-driven antibody responses and thus provides a link to B-cells.Description of     Highlighted proteins: IL21, IL15, IFN-gamma and HP highlighted in red in order to emphasize violation of mucosal integrity, epithelial damage and immune response, resulting in Celiac disease.Description of     Mutated genes: CTLA4, HLA DQ2 and HLA DQ8 genes in whiteout style have genetic variants which increase susceptibility to Celiac disease." EXACT []

