xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). The inflammation affects all levels of the digestive tube where ulcers and scarring of the bowel wall often occur.    Signaling description: It appears that divergent cytokine patterns are involved in the pathogenesis of Crohn's disease. It is primarily a T-helper 1- (Th1) dominant condition while a T-helper 2- (Th2) mediated response results in lesions more closely resembling ulcerative colitis. The chronic inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract in patients with Crohn's disease is thought to be caused by defects in first defense mechanisms to commensal microflora in the gut. The disrupted epithelial and mucosal barrier contributes to increased bacterial penetration. On the pathway, signal transduction cascades and key proteins are shown in a distinct type of cells for simplicity although they act equivalently in each of the cell types. Alterations in the genes (NOD2, TLR4, and CARD9) involved in pathogen pattern recognition in macrophages, Paneth cells and dendritic cells may initiate NF-kB cascade activation leading to the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines.    Outcome effects: The strong expression of tumor necrosis factor (ligand) superfamily, member 11 (TNFSF11) facilitates antigen-presenting cells migrating to Peyer's patches (intestinal mesenteric lymph nodes) to present antigens and stimulate T-cell proliferation and differentiation. In patients with Crohn's disease, the T-cell response is more powerful. Th1-cell and Th17-cell mediated responses are the most characteristic of Crohn's disease. Another major contribution to Crohn's disease pathogenesis comes from autophagy. In autophagosomes, microbes are degraded and their antigens are presented to CD4 cells. The efficiency of autophagy may be diminished due to the possible dysfunction of ATG16L1, IRGM and VAMP3 in Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). The inflammation affects all levels of the digestive tube where ulcers and scarring of the bowel wall often occur.    Signaling description: It appears that divergent cytokine patterns are involved in the pathogenesis of Crohn's disease. It is primarily a T-helper 1- (Th1) dominant condition while a T-helper 2- (Th2) mediated response results in lesions more closely resembling ulcerative colitis. The chronic inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract in patients with Crohn's disease is thought to be caused by defects in first defense mechanisms to commensal microflora in the gut. The disrupted epithelial and mucosal barrier contributes to increased bacterial penetration. On the pathway, signal transduction cascades and key proteins are shown in a distinct type of cells for simplicity although they act equivalently in each of the cell types. Alterations in the genes (NOD2, TLR4, and CARD9) involved in pathogen pattern recognition in macrophages, Paneth cells and dendritic cells may initiate NF-kB cascade activation leading to the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines.    Outcome effects: The strong expression of tumor necrosis factor (ligand) superfamily, member 11 (TNFSF11) facilitates antigen-presenting cells migrating to Peyer's patches (intestinal mesenteric lymph nodes) to present antigens and stimulate T-cell proliferation and differentiation. In patients with Crohn's disease, the T-cell response is more powerful. Th1-cell and Th17-cell mediated responses are the most characteristic of Crohn's disease. Another major contribution to Crohn's disease pathogenesis comes from autophagy. In autophagosomes, microbes are degraded and their antigens are presented to CD4 cells. The efficiency of autophagy may be diminished due to the possible dysfunction of ATG16L1, IRGM and VAMP3 in Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

