xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). The chronic inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract in patients with Crohn's disease is thought to be caused by defects in the first defense mechanisms to commensal microflora in the gut. Paneth cells are granulated epithelial cells of small intestinal crypts which secrete antimicrobial peptides and proteins, including alpha-defensins, lysozyme, and secretory phospholipase A2, in response to bacterial products.    Signaling description: NOD2 and TLR4 gene products are involved in pathogen pattern recognition in Paneth cells. Patients with Crohn's disease who have NOD2 abnormalities manifest more severe defects in alpha-defensin production than normally observed in Crohn's disease. The detailed molecular mechanisms of this effect remain unclear. The existing hypotheses on the NOD2-related regulation of MUC1 and defensins expression are shown on the pathway. TCF4 is a gene associated with Crohn's disease and Paneth cell antimicrobial function. The signaling of WNT-TCF4, TLR4, and phospholipase A2 in Paneth cells in patients with Crohn's disease requires further study.    Outcome effects: Alterations in genes NOD2 and TLR4 are believed to be a cause of weak intestinal barrier and increased T-cell activation in Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). The chronic inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract in patients with Crohn's disease is thought to be caused by defects in the first defense mechanisms to commensal microflora in the gut. Paneth cells are granulated epithelial cells of small intestinal crypts which secrete antimicrobial peptides and proteins, including alpha-defensins, lysozyme, and secretory phospholipase A2, in response to bacterial products.    Signaling description: NOD2 and TLR4 gene products are involved in pathogen pattern recognition in Paneth cells. Patients with Crohn's disease who have NOD2 abnormalities manifest more severe defects in alpha-defensin production than normally observed in Crohn's disease. The detailed molecular mechanisms of this effect remain unclear. The existing hypotheses on the NOD2-related regulation of MUC1 and defensins expression are shown on the pathway. TCF4 is a gene associated with Crohn's disease and Paneth cell antimicrobial function. The signaling of WNT-TCF4, TLR4, and phospholipase A2 in Paneth cells in patients with Crohn's disease requires further study.    Outcome effects: Alterations in genes NOD2 and TLR4 are believed to be a cause of weak intestinal barrier and increased T-cell activation in Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

