xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system. Crohn's disease is primarily a T-helper 1- (Th1) dominant condition; while a T-helper 2- (Th2) mediated response results in lesions more closely resembling ulcerative colitis.    Signaling description: It appears that divergent cytokine patterns are involved in the pathogenesis of Crohn's disease. The Th2-type immune response is characterized by the production of IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13. B-cell activation is especially important in Th2-cell mediated signaling. The detailed mechanisms of Th2-cell mediated molecular signaling in Crohn's disease are still poorly understood. The JAK2 gene provides instructions for making a protein that promotes the growth and proliferation of cells. This protein is part of a signaling pathway called the JAK/STAT pathway, which transmits chemical signals from outside the cell to the cell's nucleus.    Outcome effects: JAK2 polymorphisms have been associated with Crohn's disease. It has also been shown that the expression of CD86 is higher in the intestinal epithelium of patients with Crohn's disease compared to normal controls. Additionally, elevated serum IL6, IL10, IL2 concentrations are found in active Crohn's disease. IL6 and IL10 are cytokines that are mostly important for the Th2-cell mediated immune response. Lastly, increased MAF expression was observed in sections from ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease patients when compared to control patients. MAF is a transcriptional factor necessary for IL4 expression and for Th2-cell mediated immune response."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system. Crohn's disease is primarily a T-helper 1- (Th1) dominant condition; while a T-helper 2- (Th2) mediated response results in lesions more closely resembling ulcerative colitis.    Signaling description: It appears that divergent cytokine patterns are involved in the pathogenesis of Crohn's disease. The Th2-type immune response is characterized by the production of IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13. B-cell activation is especially important in Th2-cell mediated signaling. The detailed mechanisms of Th2-cell mediated molecular signaling in Crohn's disease are still poorly understood. The JAK2 gene provides instructions for making a protein that promotes the growth and proliferation of cells. This protein is part of a signaling pathway called the JAK/STAT pathway, which transmits chemical signals from outside the cell to the cell's nucleus.    Outcome effects: JAK2 polymorphisms have been associated with Crohn's disease. It has also been shown that the expression of CD86 is higher in the intestinal epithelium of patients with Crohn's disease compared to normal controls. Additionally, elevated serum IL6, IL10, IL2 concentrations are found in active Crohn's disease. IL6 and IL10 are cytokines that are mostly important for the Th2-cell mediated immune response. Lastly, increased MAF expression was observed in sections from ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease patients when compared to control patients. MAF is a transcriptional factor necessary for IL4 expression and for Th2-cell mediated immune response." EXACT []

