xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system. Crohn's disease is primarily a T-helper 1- (Th1) dominant condition while a T-helper 2- (Th2) mediated response results in lesions more closely resembling ulcerative colitis.    Signaling description: The increased production of IL12A and IL12B and other cytokines from dendritic cells in patients with Crohn's disease induce both Th1-cell and Th17-cell mediated immune responses. It has been demonstrated that anti-IL12/IL23p40 antibody therapy, which targets both Th1 and Th17 cells, is an effective treatment for Crohn's disease. Th1-cells from lesions of patients with Crohn's disease express high levels of STAT4 and TBX21, transcription factors required for IFGN expression.    Outcome effects: Th1 cells are the primary source of inflammatory cytokines, interferon-gamma (IFNG), interleukin (IL)-2, and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-beta, which activate macrophages and are responsible for cell-mediated immunity and phagocyte-dependent protective responses. Activated Th1-cells constantly express high levels of IFGN which leads to chronic inflammation in the gut of patients with Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system. Crohn's disease is primarily a T-helper 1- (Th1) dominant condition while a T-helper 2- (Th2) mediated response results in lesions more closely resembling ulcerative colitis.    Signaling description: The increased production of IL12A and IL12B and other cytokines from dendritic cells in patients with Crohn's disease induce both Th1-cell and Th17-cell mediated immune responses. It has been demonstrated that anti-IL12/IL23p40 antibody therapy, which targets both Th1 and Th17 cells, is an effective treatment for Crohn's disease. Th1-cells from lesions of patients with Crohn's disease express high levels of STAT4 and TBX21, transcription factors required for IFGN expression.    Outcome effects: Th1 cells are the primary source of inflammatory cytokines, interferon-gamma (IFNG), interleukin (IL)-2, and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-beta, which activate macrophages and are responsible for cell-mediated immunity and phagocyte-dependent protective responses. Activated Th1-cells constantly express high levels of IFGN which leads to chronic inflammation in the gut of patients with Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

