xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system.    Signaling description: The pro-inflammatory activity of T helper 17 (Th17) cells can be beneficial to the host during an infection. However, uncontrolled or inappropriate Th17 activation has been linked to several autoimmune and autoinflammatory pathologies. Th17 cells are activated mainly through IL-23R, which in turn activates the JAK-STAT-TYK pathway that enhances pro-inflammatory cytokine production. The increased production of IL-12 and IL-23 in Crohn's disease induces the Th17-cell immune responses. It has been demonstrated that anti-IL12/IL23p40 antibody therapy, which targets both Th1-cells and Th17-cells, is an effective treatment for Crohn's disease. Th17 cells from patients with Crohn's disease express high levels of STAT3, a transcription factor required for IL17 expression. In addition, polymorphisms in CCR6, JAK2, TYK2, STAT3, IL23R genes have been associated with Crohn's disease and T17-cell mediated effects.    Outcome effects: IL17 is involved in the recruitment, activation and migration of neutrophils. Activated T17cells are involved in maintaining chronic inflammation and ulceration of the gut in patients with Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system.    Signaling description: The pro-inflammatory activity of T helper 17 (Th17) cells can be beneficial to the host during an infection. However, uncontrolled or inappropriate Th17 activation has been linked to several autoimmune and autoinflammatory pathologies. Th17 cells are activated mainly through IL-23R, which in turn activates the JAK-STAT-TYK pathway that enhances pro-inflammatory cytokine production. The increased production of IL-12 and IL-23 in Crohn's disease induces the Th17-cell immune responses. It has been demonstrated that anti-IL12/IL23p40 antibody therapy, which targets both Th1-cells and Th17-cells, is an effective treatment for Crohn's disease. Th17 cells from patients with Crohn's disease express high levels of STAT3, a transcription factor required for IL17 expression. In addition, polymorphisms in CCR6, JAK2, TYK2, STAT3, IL23R genes have been associated with Crohn's disease and T17-cell mediated effects.    Outcome effects: IL17 is involved in the recruitment, activation and migration of neutrophils. Activated T17cells are involved in maintaining chronic inflammation and ulceration of the gut in patients with Crohn's disease.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

