synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). The chronic inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract of patients with Crohn's disease is thought to be triggered by defects in the first defense mechanisms to commensal microflora in the gut.    Signaling description: Alterations in the genes involved in pathogen pattern recognition in macrophages may be the reason for an increased response of antigen-presenting cells to microbes as well as NF-kB cascade activation leading to the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines in Crohn's disease. NOD2 is an intracellular protein expressed in a variety of immune and non-immune cells where it is involved in recognition and defense against pathogens. Polymorphisms in NOD2 gene are strongly associated with Crohn's disease. NOD2 dysfunction results in qualitative or quantitative changes of the commensal organisms present in the terminal ileum. This occurs through three main molecular mechanisms. NOD2 is involved in autophagy and consequently in the trafficking of ingested organisms to lysosomes where they are eliminated. Patients with Crohn's disease-associated NOD2 abnormalities have decreased autophagic responses to infection and defective killing of ingested pathogenic bacteria. Alterations in ATG16L1, a protein required for autophagy results in deficient autophagic responses to bacteria and therefore uncontrolled bacterial expansion. Polymorphisms in ATG16L1 are also associated with Crohn's disease. Through autophagy, antigens from pathogens are transmitted to MHC class II protein complex and activate B cells and a T-cell response. ATG16L1 may also alter the process of apoptosis of macrophages through TRAF2 signaling. Additionally, NOD2 can enhance NF-kB related pro-inflammatory cytokine expression. Cytokines from macrophages activate themselves and T cell maturation. NOD2 also activates NLRP3 inflammasome. NLPR3 inflammasome is required for IL1B, IL18, and IL33 production by macrophages.    Outcome effects: The altered function of macrophages leads to the entry of pathogenic organisms or their parts from the terminal ileum into the lamina propria through Peyer's patches and therefore initiates Crohn's-like inflammation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). The chronic inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract of patients with Crohn's disease is thought to be triggered by defects in the first defense mechanisms to commensal microflora in the gut.    Signaling description: Alterations in the genes involved in pathogen pattern recognition in macrophages may be the reason for an increased response of antigen-presenting cells to microbes as well as NF-kB cascade activation leading to the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines in Crohn's disease. NOD2 is an intracellular protein expressed in a variety of immune and non-immune cells where it is involved in recognition and defense against pathogens. Polymorphisms in NOD2 gene are strongly associated with Crohn's disease. NOD2 dysfunction results in qualitative or quantitative changes of the commensal organisms present in the terminal ileum. This occurs through three main molecular mechanisms. NOD2 is involved in autophagy and consequently in the trafficking of ingested organisms to lysosomes where they are eliminated. Patients with Crohn's disease-associated NOD2 abnormalities have decreased autophagic responses to infection and defective killing of ingested pathogenic bacteria. Alterations in ATG16L1, a protein required for autophagy results in deficient autophagic responses to bacteria and therefore uncontrolled bacterial expansion. Polymorphisms in ATG16L1 are also associated with Crohn's disease. Through autophagy, antigens from pathogens are transmitted to MHC class II protein complex and activate B cells and a T-cell response. ATG16L1 may also alter the process of apoptosis of macrophages through TRAF2 signaling. Additionally, NOD2 can enhance NF-kB related pro-inflammatory cytokine expression. Cytokines from macrophages activate themselves and T cell maturation. NOD2 also activates NLRP3 inflammasome. NLPR3 inflammasome is required for IL1B, IL18, and IL33 production by macrophages.    Outcome effects: The altered function of macrophages leads to the entry of pathogenic organisms or their parts from the terminal ileum into the lamina propria through Peyer's patches and therefore initiates Crohn's-like inflammation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

