xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system. Dendritic cells (DCs) are constituents of this innate system, inducing T-cell activation via antigen presentation. The altered function of proteins involved in pathogen pattern recognition in dendritic cells of patients with Crohn's disease is suggested to be the essential cause of inflammation development.    Signaling description: NOD2 is an intracellular protein expressed in a variety of immune and non-immune cells where it is involved in recognition and defense against pathogens. NOD2 signaling initiates the activation of NF-kB through RIPK2 - MAP3K7 participation. In contrast, NOD2 may be a negative regulator of TLR4-dependent NF-kB activation. RIPK2 - independent NOD2 signaling is initiated by viral ssRNA. This process involves the binding of ssRNA to NOD2 followed by an interaction with MAVS, an adaptor protein associated with mitochondria. This leads to the activation of IRF3 and induction of beta interferon (IFNB1).    Outcome effects: The interaction of NOD2 with CARD9 may determine which downstream components are activated. The strong expression of TNFSF11 in patients with Crohn's disease facilitates antigen-presenting cells migrating to Peyer's patches (intestinal mesenteric lymph nodes) to present antigens and stimulate T-cell proliferation and differentiation. The T-cells of patients with Crohn's disease react more intensely. The activation of TLR4 signaling through NF-kB also stimulates Th1 and Th17 cell differentiation and their pro-inflammatory response. Finally, strong transcriptional activity of NF-kB increases the expression of cytokines and other proteins which in turn activate the immune system.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Crohn's disease is a chronic autoimmune granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. It usually affects the intestines, but may occur anywhere from the mouth to the end of the rectum (anus). Crohn's disease is an inflammatory phenomenon that has been characterized by an adaptive T-cell response in addition to an abnormal function of the innate immune system. Dendritic cells (DCs) are constituents of this innate system, inducing T-cell activation via antigen presentation. The altered function of proteins involved in pathogen pattern recognition in dendritic cells of patients with Crohn's disease is suggested to be the essential cause of inflammation development.    Signaling description: NOD2 is an intracellular protein expressed in a variety of immune and non-immune cells where it is involved in recognition and defense against pathogens. NOD2 signaling initiates the activation of NF-kB through RIPK2 - MAP3K7 participation. In contrast, NOD2 may be a negative regulator of TLR4-dependent NF-kB activation. RIPK2 - independent NOD2 signaling is initiated by viral ssRNA. This process involves the binding of ssRNA to NOD2 followed by an interaction with MAVS, an adaptor protein associated with mitochondria. This leads to the activation of IRF3 and induction of beta interferon (IFNB1).    Outcome effects: The interaction of NOD2 with CARD9 may determine which downstream components are activated. The strong expression of TNFSF11 in patients with Crohn's disease facilitates antigen-presenting cells migrating to Peyer's patches (intestinal mesenteric lymph nodes) to present antigens and stimulate T-cell proliferation and differentiation. The T-cells of patients with Crohn's disease react more intensely. The activation of TLR4 signaling through NF-kB also stimulates Th1 and Th17 cell differentiation and their pro-inflammatory response. Finally, strong transcriptional activity of NF-kB increases the expression of cytokines and other proteins which in turn activate the immune system.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

