xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The antiphospholipid syndrome is characterized by recurrent thrombosis (arterial or venous, or both) and/or morbidity during pregnancy (recurrent miscarriages/stillbirths) associated with high levels of anti-phospholipid antibodies (aPL).     Signaling description: aPL antibodies bind to beta-2GPI (APOH) which in turn binds to platelets receptor LRP8 and GP1BA, and induce cell activation/aggregation through activation of p38 MAPK (MAPK14), phospholipase A2, release of thromboxane A2 and expression of GPIIb/IIIa (alpha2b beta3 integrin), determining a procoagulant state. Additional mechanisms promoting clot formation may be represented by the interaction of aPL with proteins implicated in the coagulation cascade such as PROC, PROS1, F2, and SERPINC1."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The antiphospholipid syndrome is characterized by recurrent thrombosis (arterial or venous, or both) and/or morbidity during pregnancy (recurrent miscarriages/stillbirths) associated with high levels of anti-phospholipid antibodies (aPL).     Signaling description: aPL antibodies bind to beta-2GPI (APOH) which in turn binds to platelets receptor LRP8 and GP1BA, and induce cell activation/aggregation through activation of p38 MAPK (MAPK14), phospholipase A2, release of thromboxane A2 and expression of GPIIb/IIIa (alpha2b beta3 integrin), determining a procoagulant state. Additional mechanisms promoting clot formation may be represented by the interaction of aPL with proteins implicated in the coagulation cascade such as PROC, PROS1, F2, and SERPINC1." EXACT []

