xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyperhomocysteinemia (HHcy) is a condition characterized by an abnormally high level of homocysteine in the blood. Severe homozygous forms of hyperhomocysteinemia cause homocystinuria, which is characterized by mental retardation, ectopia lentis, and skeletal abnormalities, although premature death is typically a consequence of thromboembolic events. Deficiencies of vitamin B6, folic acid (vitamin B9), and vitamin B12 can lead to HHcy. Chronic consumption of alcohol may also result in increased plasma levels of homocysteine due to disruption of folate metabolism. HHcy is strongly associated with end-stage renal disease due to impaired homocysteine excretion. HHcy was shown to cause cypercoagulation conditions.    Signaling description: Homocysteine, a sulfur-containing amino acid, absent in natural diets, synthesized from methionine and either recycled back into methionine or converted into cysteine with the aid of the B-group vitamins. About 50% of homocysteine is converted back to methionine by remethylation. This requires active folate and vitamin B12, in order to donate a methyl group. The remaining homocysteine is transsulfurated to cysteine, with vitamin B6 as the co-factor. Mutations in the enzymes responsible for homocysteine metabolism, particularly cystathionine b-synthase (CBS) or 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR), result in HHcy.     Outcome effects: Homocysteine inhibits the expression of thrombomodulin (THBD), the thrombin cofactor responsible for PROC activation, and inhibits antithrombin III (SERPINC1) binding activity of endothelial heparan sulfate proteoglycan, thereby suppressing the anticoagulant effect of SERPINC1. Homocysteine also increases F3 expression.Homocysteine impairs endothelial cell surface plasminogen activation by posttranslationally modifying annexin A2 (ANXA2), the coreceptor for plasminogen (PLG) and tissue plasminogen activator (PLAT). HHcy is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular disease, including ischemic heart disease, stroke, and peripheral vascular disease.Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyperhomocysteinemia (HHcy) is a condition characterized by an abnormally high level of homocysteine in the blood. Severe homozygous forms of hyperhomocysteinemia cause homocystinuria, which is characterized by mental retardation, ectopia lentis, and skeletal abnormalities, although premature death is typically a consequence of thromboembolic events. Deficiencies of vitamin B6, folic acid (vitamin B9), and vitamin B12 can lead to HHcy. Chronic consumption of alcohol may also result in increased plasma levels of homocysteine due to disruption of folate metabolism. HHcy is strongly associated with end-stage renal disease due to impaired homocysteine excretion. HHcy was shown to cause cypercoagulation conditions.    Signaling description: Homocysteine, a sulfur-containing amino acid, absent in natural diets, synthesized from methionine and either recycled back into methionine or converted into cysteine with the aid of the B-group vitamins. About 50% of homocysteine is converted back to methionine by remethylation. This requires active folate and vitamin B12, in order to donate a methyl group. The remaining homocysteine is transsulfurated to cysteine, with vitamin B6 as the co-factor. Mutations in the enzymes responsible for homocysteine metabolism, particularly cystathionine b-synthase (CBS) or 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR), result in HHcy.     Outcome effects: Homocysteine inhibits the expression of thrombomodulin (THBD), the thrombin cofactor responsible for PROC activation, and inhibits antithrombin III (SERPINC1) binding activity of endothelial heparan sulfate proteoglycan, thereby suppressing the anticoagulant effect of SERPINC1. Homocysteine also increases F3 expression.Homocysteine impairs endothelial cell surface plasminogen activation by posttranslationally modifying annexin A2 (ANXA2), the coreceptor for plasminogen (PLG) and tissue plasminogen activator (PLAT). HHcy is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular disease, including ischemic heart disease, stroke, and peripheral vascular disease.Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

