xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and PsA pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis and PsA, often termed 'psoriatic disease' are currently considered autoimmune diseases which share certain similar pathological events and clinical features. PsA is a seronegative autoimmune inflammatory joint disease that develops in approximately 25 % of psoriasis patients usually within 10 years of the start of skin manifestations.    Signaling description: The innate immune system is the first line of defense against invading organisms, and one of its most important intracellular regulators is NFkB. GWAS studies investigating susceptibility to PsA have identified variants in several genes in the NFkB signaling network, including TNFAIP3, TNIP1, NFKBIA, REL, NOS2 and FBXL19. TNFAIP3 encodes the inducible zinc finger protein A20 (also known as TNF-induced protein 3, TNFAIP3). Ubiquitination of TNFAIP3 in response to NFkB activation prevents subsequent NFkB activation. The product of TNIP1 (TNFAIP3-interacting protein 1) interacts with TNFAIP3 to inhibit TNF-induced NFkB-dependent gene expression, via interference with transactivation signals mediated by RIP (receptor-interacting serine/threonineprotein kinase 1) or TRAF2 (TNF receptor-associated factor 2). Similarly, the product of NFKBIA interferes with nuclear localization signals by inhibiting the activity of dimeric NFkB-REL complexes (REL genes encode a subunit of the NFkB complex that is essential for proper signaling). The product of FBXL19, a member of the F-box family, reversibly inhibits NFkB signaling, and expression of FBXL19 is significantly elevated in psoriatic compared with normal skin. Finally, expression of NOS2 (encoding inducible nitric oxide synthase iNOS) is controlled by NFkB, occurs in inflammatory dendritic cells, and is elevated in lesional psoriatic skin.    Outcome effects: Activated upon liberation NFkB inhibitor, secondary to stimulation by cytokines such as TNF, IL-1 and IL-17, NFkB triggers transcription of numerous target genes involved in the pathogenesis of inflammatory diseases, including PsA, and accumulates as a result of dysregulation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and PsA pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis and PsA, often termed 'psoriatic disease' are currently considered autoimmune diseases which share certain similar pathological events and clinical features. PsA is a seronegative autoimmune inflammatory joint disease that develops in approximately 25 % of psoriasis patients usually within 10 years of the start of skin manifestations.    Signaling description: The innate immune system is the first line of defense against invading organisms, and one of its most important intracellular regulators is NFkB. GWAS studies investigating susceptibility to PsA have identified variants in several genes in the NFkB signaling network, including TNFAIP3, TNIP1, NFKBIA, REL, NOS2 and FBXL19. TNFAIP3 encodes the inducible zinc finger protein A20 (also known as TNF-induced protein 3, TNFAIP3). Ubiquitination of TNFAIP3 in response to NFkB activation prevents subsequent NFkB activation. The product of TNIP1 (TNFAIP3-interacting protein 1) interacts with TNFAIP3 to inhibit TNF-induced NFkB-dependent gene expression, via interference with transactivation signals mediated by RIP (receptor-interacting serine/threonineprotein kinase 1) or TRAF2 (TNF receptor-associated factor 2). Similarly, the product of NFKBIA interferes with nuclear localization signals by inhibiting the activity of dimeric NFkB-REL complexes (REL genes encode a subunit of the NFkB complex that is essential for proper signaling). The product of FBXL19, a member of the F-box family, reversibly inhibits NFkB signaling, and expression of FBXL19 is significantly elevated in psoriatic compared with normal skin. Finally, expression of NOS2 (encoding inducible nitric oxide synthase iNOS) is controlled by NFkB, occurs in inflammatory dendritic cells, and is elevated in lesional psoriatic skin.    Outcome effects: Activated upon liberation NFkB inhibitor, secondary to stimulation by cytokines such as TNF, IL-1 and IL-17, NFkB triggers transcription of numerous target genes involved in the pathogenesis of inflammatory diseases, including PsA, and accumulates as a result of dysregulation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

