xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and PsA pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis and PsA, often termed 'psoriatic disease' are currently considered autoimmune diseases which share certain similar pathological events and clinical features. PsA is a seronegative autoimmune inflammatory joint disease that develops in approximately 25 % of psoriasis patients usually within 10 years of the start of skin manifestations. Psoriatic disease used to be regarded as a Th1-driven disease, and now there is substantial evidence in support for a regulatory role of Th17 cells in the pathogenesis of psoriasis and PsA.    Signaling description: Cytokines play a critical role in the inflammatory and proliferative cascades of both skin and joint tissues. The IL-23/IL-17 axis plays a prominent role in adaptive immunity and forms a complex interplay with TNFalpha and NFkB, and its disruption by genetic variation can contribute to PsA pathogenesis. Several genetic loci involved in Th-17 signaling have reached genome-wide significance (i.e., IL-12B, IL-23R, TYK2, and NFKBIA) or are approaching genomewide significance (i.e., IL-23A) in PsA. IL-23 is a heterodimeric cytokine (composed of a p19 subunit and a p40 subunit), which binds IL-23R and IL-12Rbeta1; the latter being shared with IL-12. IL-23, functioning through its receptor and signaling pathway, induces the production of several proinflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-17, TNFalpha, IL-21, and IL-22).    Outcome effects: IL-23 promotes the expansion and survival of Th-17 cells.IL-23 induced Th17 cytokines (IL-17 and IL-22) can contribute to all four pathologic events in a psoriatic disease: the development of psoriatic plaque, pannus formation in the joint, joint erosion and new bone formation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and PsA pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis and PsA, often termed 'psoriatic disease' are currently considered autoimmune diseases which share certain similar pathological events and clinical features. PsA is a seronegative autoimmune inflammatory joint disease that develops in approximately 25 % of psoriasis patients usually within 10 years of the start of skin manifestations. Psoriatic disease used to be regarded as a Th1-driven disease, and now there is substantial evidence in support for a regulatory role of Th17 cells in the pathogenesis of psoriasis and PsA.    Signaling description: Cytokines play a critical role in the inflammatory and proliferative cascades of both skin and joint tissues. The IL-23/IL-17 axis plays a prominent role in adaptive immunity and forms a complex interplay with TNFalpha and NFkB, and its disruption by genetic variation can contribute to PsA pathogenesis. Several genetic loci involved in Th-17 signaling have reached genome-wide significance (i.e., IL-12B, IL-23R, TYK2, and NFKBIA) or are approaching genomewide significance (i.e., IL-23A) in PsA. IL-23 is a heterodimeric cytokine (composed of a p19 subunit and a p40 subunit), which binds IL-23R and IL-12Rbeta1; the latter being shared with IL-12. IL-23, functioning through its receptor and signaling pathway, induces the production of several proinflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-17, TNFalpha, IL-21, and IL-22).    Outcome effects: IL-23 promotes the expansion and survival of Th-17 cells.IL-23 induced Th17 cytokines (IL-17 and IL-22) can contribute to all four pathologic events in a psoriatic disease: the development of psoriatic plaque, pannus formation in the joint, joint erosion and new bone formation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

