xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Altered bone remodeling is a key feature of PsA and can be present at varying degrees in an individual patient. Altered bone remodeling is a combination of altered bone formation and bone resorption, represented by the extent of bone erosions. Those erosions result from bone resorption by osteoclasts. In PsA, diverse manifestations of dynamic bone involvement including large eccentric erosions, tuft resorption, periostitis, ankylosis and acro-osteolysis are observed.    Signaling description: Two striking features of peripheral involvement in PsA are subchondral perientheseal edema and diffuse bone edema, which are not present in rheumatoid arthritis (RA). The osteoclast formation in PsA patients is supported by data showing the production of osteoclastogenic cytokines, such as RANKL (also known as TNFSF11), which initiates osteoclast differentiation by binding to TNFRSF11A receptor on the osteoclast surface. The signaling involves TRAF1, TRAF2, TRAF3, TRAF5, and TRAF6 proteins and affects NF-kB and promotes osteoclast differentiation and cell survival. In addition, TNFSF11 promotes osteoclast proliferation through the AKT1 pathway. In mature osteoclasts, the same signaling leads to bone resorption. In addition, IL6 initiates NF-kB activation and the previously mentioned processes in osteoclasts through ther JAK-STAT pathway. IL1B also affects NF-kB pathway through the MYD88/IRAK4/IRAK1/TRAF6 axis.    Outcome effects: Elevated levels of IL6, IL1B, and TNFSF11 in PsA promote osteoclast proliferation, differentiation, and their decreased apoptosis via a number of signaling routes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Altered bone remodeling is a key feature of PsA and can be present at varying degrees in an individual patient. Altered bone remodeling is a combination of altered bone formation and bone resorption, represented by the extent of bone erosions. Those erosions result from bone resorption by osteoclasts. In PsA, diverse manifestations of dynamic bone involvement including large eccentric erosions, tuft resorption, periostitis, ankylosis and acro-osteolysis are observed.    Signaling description: Two striking features of peripheral involvement in PsA are subchondral perientheseal edema and diffuse bone edema, which are not present in rheumatoid arthritis (RA). The osteoclast formation in PsA patients is supported by data showing the production of osteoclastogenic cytokines, such as RANKL (also known as TNFSF11), which initiates osteoclast differentiation by binding to TNFRSF11A receptor on the osteoclast surface. The signaling involves TRAF1, TRAF2, TRAF3, TRAF5, and TRAF6 proteins and affects NF-kB and promotes osteoclast differentiation and cell survival. In addition, TNFSF11 promotes osteoclast proliferation through the AKT1 pathway. In mature osteoclasts, the same signaling leads to bone resorption. In addition, IL6 initiates NF-kB activation and the previously mentioned processes in osteoclasts through ther JAK-STAT pathway. IL1B also affects NF-kB pathway through the MYD88/IRAK4/IRAK1/TRAF6 axis.    Outcome effects: Elevated levels of IL6, IL1B, and TNFSF11 in PsA promote osteoclast proliferation, differentiation, and their decreased apoptosis via a number of signaling routes.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

