xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and PsA pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis and PsA, often termed 'psoriatic disease' are currently considered autoimmune diseases which share certain similar pathological events and clinical features. PsA is a seronegative autoimmune inflammatory joint disease that develops in approximately 25 % of psoriasis patients usually within 10 years of the start of skin manifestations.    Signaling description: Synovial tissue in normal joints has few resident immune cells. It consists of an intimal lining layer made up of fibroblast-like synoviocytes and intimal lining layer of macrophages, resting on a subsynovial layer of loose connective tissue, which contains blood vessels, nerve endings, fibroblasts and adipocytes. Inflamed joints are characterised by increased synovial lining cell numbers, increased sublining vascularity, with large numbers of cells within this sub-synovial layer, including fibroblasts. TNF, IL17 and IL1B are the main cytokines that promote cell activation. There are increased levels of TNF and IL17 in the synovium of rheumatoid arthritis patients. Cytokines bind to their receptors on the synovial fibroblast surface and promote JUN, FOS, and NF-kB activation. TNF signaling involves TRAF2 which provides NF-kB activation through the MAPK14 and NF-kB pathway. TRAF2 activates MAPK9 (through MAP3K1), which interacts with JUN and FOS transcription factors. IL1B affects the same pathways through the MYD88/IRAK axis. TRAF3IP2 encodes the TRAF3 interacting protein 2 or NFkB activator 1, which is essential for Th-17 cell-mediated inflammatory responses, and its dysregulation may affect both IL-17 and NFkB signaling cascades.    Outcome effects: JUN, FOS, and NF-kB transcription factors promote the expression of a wide range of cytokines such as MMPs (cartilage erosion), IL8 (recruitment of inflammatory cells in the synovium that leads to pannus formation), IL6 (osteoclast activation and bone resorption), and VEGFA (new vessel growth).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA) refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and PsA pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis and PsA, often termed 'psoriatic disease' are currently considered autoimmune diseases which share certain similar pathological events and clinical features. PsA is a seronegative autoimmune inflammatory joint disease that develops in approximately 25 % of psoriasis patients usually within 10 years of the start of skin manifestations.    Signaling description: Synovial tissue in normal joints has few resident immune cells. It consists of an intimal lining layer made up of fibroblast-like synoviocytes and intimal lining layer of macrophages, resting on a subsynovial layer of loose connective tissue, which contains blood vessels, nerve endings, fibroblasts and adipocytes. Inflamed joints are characterised by increased synovial lining cell numbers, increased sublining vascularity, with large numbers of cells within this sub-synovial layer, including fibroblasts. TNF, IL17 and IL1B are the main cytokines that promote cell activation. There are increased levels of TNF and IL17 in the synovium of rheumatoid arthritis patients. Cytokines bind to their receptors on the synovial fibroblast surface and promote JUN, FOS, and NF-kB activation. TNF signaling involves TRAF2 which provides NF-kB activation through the MAPK14 and NF-kB pathway. TRAF2 activates MAPK9 (through MAP3K1), which interacts with JUN and FOS transcription factors. IL1B affects the same pathways through the MYD88/IRAK axis. TRAF3IP2 encodes the TRAF3 interacting protein 2 or NFkB activator 1, which is essential for Th-17 cell-mediated inflammatory responses, and its dysregulation may affect both IL-17 and NFkB signaling cascades.    Outcome effects: JUN, FOS, and NF-kB transcription factors promote the expression of a wide range of cytokines such as MMPs (cartilage erosion), IL8 (recruitment of inflammatory cells in the synovium that leads to pannus formation), IL6 (osteoclast activation and bone resorption), and VEGFA (new vessel growth).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

