xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis was originally considered a disease of abnormal keratinocyte function. At the same time, keratinocytes, key skin cells mediating many of the functional properties of the skin, can respond to proinflammatory cytokines and generate pro-inflammatory activation loops.    Signaling description: Cytokines IFNgamma, IL-17 and TNFalpha cooperate to induce the production of anti-microbial peptides (AMPs) and chemokines by keratinocytes, thereby enhancing immune-cell recruitment and inflammation in lesions. TNF alpha is found to be at a higher concentration in psoriatic lesions compared to uninvolved skin.    Outcome effects: Collectively, the cytokines IL-17, IFN-gamma, IL-22, and TNF promote keratinocyte activation via a number of signaling routes. IL-22 induces keratinocyte proliferation and epidermal hyperplasia, inhibits keratinocyte terminal differentiation, and promotes antimicrobial protein. TNF alpha increases the production of proinflammatory cytokines IL-6, IL-8, and the adhesion molecules ICAM-1, thus enabling leukocyte infiltration into the skin.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Psoriatic arthritis refers to a form of arthritis that affects people with psoriasis. Symptoms include joint pain, stiffness and swelling. Our understanding of psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis pathogenesis has evolved due to new knowledge on their immunopathology. Psoriasis was originally considered a disease of abnormal keratinocyte function. At the same time, keratinocytes, key skin cells mediating many of the functional properties of the skin, can respond to proinflammatory cytokines and generate pro-inflammatory activation loops.    Signaling description: Cytokines IFNgamma, IL-17 and TNFalpha cooperate to induce the production of anti-microbial peptides (AMPs) and chemokines by keratinocytes, thereby enhancing immune-cell recruitment and inflammation in lesions. TNF alpha is found to be at a higher concentration in psoriatic lesions compared to uninvolved skin.    Outcome effects: Collectively, the cytokines IL-17, IFN-gamma, IL-22, and TNF promote keratinocyte activation via a number of signaling routes. IL-22 induces keratinocyte proliferation and epidermal hyperplasia, inhibits keratinocyte terminal differentiation, and promotes antimicrobial protein. TNF alpha increases the production of proinflammatory cytokines IL-6, IL-8, and the adhesion molecules ICAM-1, thus enabling leukocyte infiltration into the skin.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

