xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement.    Signaling description: IL1A and IL1B are constitutively expressed in SSc fibroblasts. PDGF can induce IL1 in dermal fibroblasts from SSc patients, but not in normal fibroblasts. In turn, IL1 can induce PDGFA mRNA expression and PDGF-AA secretion in normal skin fibroblasts while endogenous IL1A is responsible for higher steady-state PDGFA mRNA levels in SSc compared to normal fibroblasts. In addition, IL1B upregulates PDGFRA and signaling through PDGFR is amplified in SSc fibroblasts. In addition to the direct effects on IL1 and IL1R1 expression, PDGF also increases CCL2 transcription and synthesis. CCL2 can also induce the production of TGFB1. Subsequently, TGFB1 selectively increases the expression of PDGFRA in SSc but not in normal fibroblasts while PDGF induces TGFBR1 and TGFBR2 transcripts enhancing only TGFBR1 surface expression on normal dermal fibroblasts.    Outcome effects: SSc fibroblasts remain persistently activated in the absence of exogenous stimuli due to the constitutive production of endogenous cytokines such as IL1, TGFB1, PDGF, and CCL2 in addition to the upregulation of their receptors. This allows the development of several autocrine cytokine/chemokine loops in SSc fibroblasts that are only partially present in normal fibroblasts. The enhanced expression of TGFBR1 plays a central role in autocrine TGFB1 stimulation that augments SSc fibroblast collagen synthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement.    Signaling description: IL1A and IL1B are constitutively expressed in SSc fibroblasts. PDGF can induce IL1 in dermal fibroblasts from SSc patients, but not in normal fibroblasts. In turn, IL1 can induce PDGFA mRNA expression and PDGF-AA secretion in normal skin fibroblasts while endogenous IL1A is responsible for higher steady-state PDGFA mRNA levels in SSc compared to normal fibroblasts. In addition, IL1B upregulates PDGFRA and signaling through PDGFR is amplified in SSc fibroblasts. In addition to the direct effects on IL1 and IL1R1 expression, PDGF also increases CCL2 transcription and synthesis. CCL2 can also induce the production of TGFB1. Subsequently, TGFB1 selectively increases the expression of PDGFRA in SSc but not in normal fibroblasts while PDGF induces TGFBR1 and TGFBR2 transcripts enhancing only TGFBR1 surface expression on normal dermal fibroblasts.    Outcome effects: SSc fibroblasts remain persistently activated in the absence of exogenous stimuli due to the constitutive production of endogenous cytokines such as IL1, TGFB1, PDGF, and CCL2 in addition to the upregulation of their receptors. This allows the development of several autocrine cytokine/chemokine loops in SSc fibroblasts that are only partially present in normal fibroblasts. The enhanced expression of TGFBR1 plays a central role in autocrine TGFB1 stimulation that augments SSc fibroblast collagen synthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

