xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement. Apoptosis of endothelial cells (ECs) is a key event in the pathogenesis SSc.    Signaling description: FAS-mediated apoptosis of ECs may represent a primary pathogenic event in the skin. FAS induces apoptosis of activated FAS-positive inflammatory and mesenchymal cells after injury and inflammation, thereby limiting the immune response and promoting tissue repair. Prolonged inflammation without apoptosis results in tissue injury as can excessive apoptosis.    Outcome effects: EC apoptosis, possibly mediated by ADCC (antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity), is a key pathogenic event in human SSc. Apoptotic ECs are present in skin biopsies of patients in the early inflammatory stage of SSc and with localized scleroderma but not in those with fibrotic changes or in healthy controls.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement. Apoptosis of endothelial cells (ECs) is a key event in the pathogenesis SSc.    Signaling description: FAS-mediated apoptosis of ECs may represent a primary pathogenic event in the skin. FAS induces apoptosis of activated FAS-positive inflammatory and mesenchymal cells after injury and inflammation, thereby limiting the immune response and promoting tissue repair. Prolonged inflammation without apoptosis results in tissue injury as can excessive apoptosis.    Outcome effects: EC apoptosis, possibly mediated by ADCC (antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity), is a key pathogenic event in human SSc. Apoptotic ECs are present in skin biopsies of patients in the early inflammatory stage of SSc and with localized scleroderma but not in those with fibrotic changes or in healthy controls.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

