xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement. The presence of circulating, potentially functional, autoAbs argues in favor of a pathogenic role of B-cells in SSc.    Signaling description: T-cells activated through interactions between T-cell receptor (TCR) and CD40, interact with B-lymphocytes which leads to B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells. The plasma cells produce autoantibodies (autoAbs) which have a role in SSc development. The protein encoded by IL13RA1 is a subunit of the IL13 receptor. This subunit forms a receptor complex with IL4R, a subunit shared by IL13 and IL4 receptors, which serves as the primary IL13-binding subunit of IL13 receptor and may also be a component of IL4 receptors. IL13 and IL4 stimulate B-cell differentiation with consequent production of autoAbs and isotype switching, in particular during the allergic response (IgM to IgE).    Outcome effects: The plasma cells produce both stimulatory autoAbs including anti-PDGFR Abs that activate PDGF receptors on fibroblasts and inhibitory autoAbs as well as anti-matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) Abs which reduce the inhibition of collagen deposition normally exerted by MMPs. In addition, B-cells and other cell types secrete interleukin-6 (IL6) which stimulates fibroblasts to produce collagen. Serum levels of IL6, an important pro-inflammatory cytokine, have been shown to correlate with skin fibrosis. CD19 expression is also increased in B-cells in systemic scleroderma patients.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement. The presence of circulating, potentially functional, autoAbs argues in favor of a pathogenic role of B-cells in SSc.    Signaling description: T-cells activated through interactions between T-cell receptor (TCR) and CD40, interact with B-lymphocytes which leads to B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells. The plasma cells produce autoantibodies (autoAbs) which have a role in SSc development. The protein encoded by IL13RA1 is a subunit of the IL13 receptor. This subunit forms a receptor complex with IL4R, a subunit shared by IL13 and IL4 receptors, which serves as the primary IL13-binding subunit of IL13 receptor and may also be a component of IL4 receptors. IL13 and IL4 stimulate B-cell differentiation with consequent production of autoAbs and isotype switching, in particular during the allergic response (IgM to IgE).    Outcome effects: The plasma cells produce both stimulatory autoAbs including anti-PDGFR Abs that activate PDGF receptors on fibroblasts and inhibitory autoAbs as well as anti-matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) Abs which reduce the inhibition of collagen deposition normally exerted by MMPs. In addition, B-cells and other cell types secrete interleukin-6 (IL6) which stimulates fibroblasts to produce collagen. Serum levels of IL6, an important pro-inflammatory cytokine, have been shown to correlate with skin fibrosis. CD19 expression is also increased in B-cells in systemic scleroderma patients.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

