xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement.    Signaling description: Unidentified antigens are recognized by antigen presenting cells (APCs) and subsequently presented to and activate CD4 T-cells. T-cells that are activated through interactions between T-cell receptor (TCR) and CD40 interact with B-lymphocytes, leading to B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells. Th2 lymphocytes mainly produce IL4, IL13, IL5, and IL10 cytokines which contribute to B-cell proliferation and differentiation.    Outcome effects: Activated T-helper lymphocytes then secrete profibrotic cytokines including IL4, IL13, and TGFB1 which act on circulating fibroblasts and ultimately lead to collagen deposition. IL4 and IL13 are able to stimulate collagen production and inhibit collagenase synthesis which favors fibrosis. IL10, similar to IL4 and IL13, inhibits Th1 differentiation and reduces the inflammation process.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Systemic scleroderma, also known as systemic sclerosis (SSc) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease of undetermined cause characterized by diffuse fibrosis and vascular abnormalities in the skin, joints and visceral organs. Death usually occurs due to lung, heart, and kidney involvement.    Signaling description: Unidentified antigens are recognized by antigen presenting cells (APCs) and subsequently presented to and activate CD4 T-cells. T-cells that are activated through interactions between T-cell receptor (TCR) and CD40 interact with B-lymphocytes, leading to B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells. Th2 lymphocytes mainly produce IL4, IL13, IL5, and IL10 cytokines which contribute to B-cell proliferation and differentiation.    Outcome effects: Activated T-helper lymphocytes then secrete profibrotic cytokines including IL4, IL13, and TGFB1 which act on circulating fibroblasts and ultimately lead to collagen deposition. IL4 and IL13 are able to stimulate collagen production and inhibit collagenase synthesis which favors fibrosis. IL10, similar to IL4 and IL13, inhibits Th1 differentiation and reduces the inflammation process.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

