xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity.    Signaling description: PTPN22 is a protein tyrosine phosphatase and a critical gatekeeper of T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling. In the TCR signaling pathway, PTPN22 inhibits the activity of key signaling effectors such as ZAP70 and LCK, which attenuates T-cell activation. The genetic variation of the PTPN22 gene leads to increased T-cell activation. TCR binds to MHC class II protein complex of antigen presenting cells (APCs) and transduces the signal through TCR/CD3 complex, which with PTPRC modulates the phosphorylation and activation of LCK and ZAP70. The latter is recruited to the TCR/CD3 complex where it becomes activated which promotes the recruitment and phosphorylation of LAT. The latter promotes activation of the MAPK1/3 pathway and phosphorylation of PLCG1, which results in the hydrolysis of phosphatidylinositol 4,5-biphosphate (PIP2) to produce the second messengers diacylglycerol and inositol triphosphate (IP3). Diacylglycerol activates PRKCQ which leads to transcription factor NF-kB activation. IP3 triggers the release of Ca2+ which binds to calmodulin and activates calcineurin to promote IL2, IL5, and IL4 gene transcription through the transcription factor NF-AT.    Outcome effects: The produced cytokines regulate cell proliferation and differentiation, and the lack of PTPN22 results in increased cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity.    Signaling description: PTPN22 is a protein tyrosine phosphatase and a critical gatekeeper of T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling. In the TCR signaling pathway, PTPN22 inhibits the activity of key signaling effectors such as ZAP70 and LCK, which attenuates T-cell activation. The genetic variation of the PTPN22 gene leads to increased T-cell activation. TCR binds to MHC class II protein complex of antigen presenting cells (APCs) and transduces the signal through TCR/CD3 complex, which with PTPRC modulates the phosphorylation and activation of LCK and ZAP70. The latter is recruited to the TCR/CD3 complex where it becomes activated which promotes the recruitment and phosphorylation of LAT. The latter promotes activation of the MAPK1/3 pathway and phosphorylation of PLCG1, which results in the hydrolysis of phosphatidylinositol 4,5-biphosphate (PIP2) to produce the second messengers diacylglycerol and inositol triphosphate (IP3). Diacylglycerol activates PRKCQ which leads to transcription factor NF-kB activation. IP3 triggers the release of Ca2+ which binds to calmodulin and activates calcineurin to promote IL2, IL5, and IL4 gene transcription through the transcription factor NF-AT.    Outcome effects: The produced cytokines regulate cell proliferation and differentiation, and the lack of PTPN22 results in increased cell proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

