xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity.    Signaling description: TNF and IL1B are the principal cytokines that promote cell activation. In the synovium of RA patients, the levels of TNF and TNFRSF1B receptor are elevated. Cytokines bind to their receptors on the synovial fibroblast surface and promote JUN, FOS, and NF-kB activation. TNF signaling involves TRAF2 which provides NF-kB activation through MAPK14 and NF-kB pathways. TRAF2 activates MAPK9 through binding to MAP3K1. In turn, MAPK9 interacts with JUN and FOS transcription factors. IL1B affects the same pathways through the MYD88/IRAK axis. In addition, WNT pathway is also involved in the pathological process, specifically, WNT5A promotes VEGFA and TNFSF11 expression.    Outcome effects: JUN, FOS, and NF-kB transcription factors promote the expression of a wide range of cytokines such as MMPs (cartilage erosion), IL8 (recruitment of inflammatory cells in the synovium that leads to pannus formation), CXCL12, IL15, IL16, IL17A (T-cell activation), IL6 (osteoclast activation and bone resorption), as well as VEGFA, ANGPT1, ANGPT2, and IL18 (new vessel growth). CXCL10, CCL5, CCL20, and IFNB1 are responsible for additional immune reactions in RA. LIF, IL11, and OSM promote cell invasion in the joint. TNFSF11 increases bone resorption.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity.    Signaling description: TNF and IL1B are the principal cytokines that promote cell activation. In the synovium of RA patients, the levels of TNF and TNFRSF1B receptor are elevated. Cytokines bind to their receptors on the synovial fibroblast surface and promote JUN, FOS, and NF-kB activation. TNF signaling involves TRAF2 which provides NF-kB activation through MAPK14 and NF-kB pathways. TRAF2 activates MAPK9 through binding to MAP3K1. In turn, MAPK9 interacts with JUN and FOS transcription factors. IL1B affects the same pathways through the MYD88/IRAK axis. In addition, WNT pathway is also involved in the pathological process, specifically, WNT5A promotes VEGFA and TNFSF11 expression.    Outcome effects: JUN, FOS, and NF-kB transcription factors promote the expression of a wide range of cytokines such as MMPs (cartilage erosion), IL8 (recruitment of inflammatory cells in the synovium that leads to pannus formation), CXCL12, IL15, IL16, IL17A (T-cell activation), IL6 (osteoclast activation and bone resorption), as well as VEGFA, ANGPT1, ANGPT2, and IL18 (new vessel growth). CXCL10, CCL5, CCL20, and IFNB1 are responsible for additional immune reactions in RA. LIF, IL11, and OSM promote cell invasion in the joint. TNFSF11 increases bone resorption.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

