xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity. The block of apoptosis of synovial fibroblasts is commonly seen in rheumatoid arthritis.    Signaling description: In RA, increased SUMO1 levels are observed in synovial fibroblasts. The protein interacts with FAS and TNF receptors to protect cells from apoptosis. Also, TNFRSF6B and TNFRSF11B bind and inhibit FASLG and TNFSF10, respectively which contributes to the disruption of apoptosis in pathology. Normally, TNF, FASLG, and TNFSF10 interact with their receptors TNFRSF1A/TNFRSF1B, FAS, and TNFRSF10B, respectively on the cell surface to induce apoptosis. The activation of TNFRSF1, FAS, and TNFRSF10 leads to the recruitment of CASP8 and the TRADD-FADD complex. CASP8 activates CASP3 through two pathways. In the first pathway, CASP8 cleaves BID and its active part is translocated into mitochondria where it triggers CYCS release through BAX activation. CYCS affects CASP9 which activates CASP3. In the second pathway, CASP8 activates CASP3 directly. In addition, there is another common pathway for TNF and FASLG leading to apoptosis which involves MAP3K5 activation. MAP3K5, through MAPK8 and MAPK14, inactivates the anti-apoptotic factor BCL2. TNF also activates the recruitment of other TRADD (through binding to TNFRSF1B), leading to the activation of NF-kB via TRAF2/MAP3K14.    Outcome effects: Activation of CASP3 directly promotes apoptosis. NF-kB suppresses apoptosis by inducing the transcription of anti-apoptotic genes such as BIRC2, BIRC3, and BIRC5, which inhibit CASP9.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity. The block of apoptosis of synovial fibroblasts is commonly seen in rheumatoid arthritis.    Signaling description: In RA, increased SUMO1 levels are observed in synovial fibroblasts. The protein interacts with FAS and TNF receptors to protect cells from apoptosis. Also, TNFRSF6B and TNFRSF11B bind and inhibit FASLG and TNFSF10, respectively which contributes to the disruption of apoptosis in pathology. Normally, TNF, FASLG, and TNFSF10 interact with their receptors TNFRSF1A/TNFRSF1B, FAS, and TNFRSF10B, respectively on the cell surface to induce apoptosis. The activation of TNFRSF1, FAS, and TNFRSF10 leads to the recruitment of CASP8 and the TRADD-FADD complex. CASP8 activates CASP3 through two pathways. In the first pathway, CASP8 cleaves BID and its active part is translocated into mitochondria where it triggers CYCS release through BAX activation. CYCS affects CASP9 which activates CASP3. In the second pathway, CASP8 activates CASP3 directly. In addition, there is another common pathway for TNF and FASLG leading to apoptosis which involves MAP3K5 activation. MAP3K5, through MAPK8 and MAPK14, inactivates the anti-apoptotic factor BCL2. TNF also activates the recruitment of other TRADD (through binding to TNFRSF1B), leading to the activation of NF-kB via TRAF2/MAP3K14.    Outcome effects: Activation of CASP3 directly promotes apoptosis. NF-kB suppresses apoptosis by inducing the transcription of anti-apoptotic genes such as BIRC2, BIRC3, and BIRC5, which inhibit CASP9.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

