xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity.    Signaling description: TLR2-induced synovial fibroblast activation is commonly seen in rheumatoid arthritis. IL1B, TNF, and lipopolysaccharide (LPS) induce increased expression of TLR2 on the synovial fibroblast surface in rheumatoid arthritis. Signal transduction occurs through TIRAP, MYD88, and TICAM proteins. MYD88 binds to IRAK4 and thereby facilitates IRAK4 phosphorylation of IRAK1 which triggers IRAK1 activation. IRAK1 and TICAM1 recruit TRAF6, which (through MAP3K7) leads to MAPK1 and MAPK8 activation, which in turn phosphorylate JUN and FOS transcription factors. NF-kB is involved in the signaling through the IKBKB inhibition.    Outcome effects: The transcription factors JUN, FOS, and NF-kB promote the expression of a wide range of cytokines such as MMPs (cartilage erosion), CXCL2, IL8, ICAM1 (recruitment of inflammatory cells in the synovium that leads to pannus formation), IL6 (osteoclast activation and bone resorption), and VEGFA (new vessel growth). Lastly, CXCL10, CCL5, CCL20, and IFNB1 are responsible for other immune reactions in RA.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is an autoimmune disease that affects the joints and causes inflammation and painful swelling. Other symptoms include fatigue, loss of appetite and a low-grade fever. Advanced RA can lead to bone erosion and joint deformity.    Signaling description: TLR2-induced synovial fibroblast activation is commonly seen in rheumatoid arthritis. IL1B, TNF, and lipopolysaccharide (LPS) induce increased expression of TLR2 on the synovial fibroblast surface in rheumatoid arthritis. Signal transduction occurs through TIRAP, MYD88, and TICAM proteins. MYD88 binds to IRAK4 and thereby facilitates IRAK4 phosphorylation of IRAK1 which triggers IRAK1 activation. IRAK1 and TICAM1 recruit TRAF6, which (through MAP3K7) leads to MAPK1 and MAPK8 activation, which in turn phosphorylate JUN and FOS transcription factors. NF-kB is involved in the signaling through the IKBKB inhibition.    Outcome effects: The transcription factors JUN, FOS, and NF-kB promote the expression of a wide range of cytokines such as MMPs (cartilage erosion), CXCL2, IL8, ICAM1 (recruitment of inflammatory cells in the synovium that leads to pannus formation), IL6 (osteoclast activation and bone resorption), and VEGFA (new vessel growth). Lastly, CXCL10, CCL5, CCL20, and IFNB1 are responsible for other immune reactions in RA.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

