xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) monohydrate crystals in the joint with clinical manifestations which include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi and renal functional impairment. Impaired renal uric acid excretion is the prevalent cause of gout.    Signaling description: In general, the process of renal urate transport consists of four steps including glomerular filtration, near-complete reabsorption of filtered urate, subsequent secretion, and postsecretory reabsorption in the remaining proximal tubule. Normally, following glomerular filtration of 100% of urate, 98% of it is reabsorbed in the first part of the proximal tubule, followed by 50% secretion in the second part of the tubule with further reabsorption of approximately 40-48%, resulting in a net reabsorption of approximately 90% leading to only 8-12% of uric acid being excreted by the kidney. In pathology, impaired function of uric acid transporters cause decreased urate excretion.    Outcome effects: Abnormalities in SLC22A12 and SLC2A9 genes cause increased urate reabsorption. SLC2A9 is a high-affinity urate transporter, and it functions in renal urate reabsorption by transporting urate from tubular cells to the peritubular interstitium following the uptake of urate from the luminal space by SLC22A12. Urate secretion from blood into the epithelial cell occurs via several transporters including SLC22A6 and SLC22A8, and from the cell to the tubule space (where urine is located) via another group of transporters including SLC17A1, SLC17A3, ABCG2, ABCC4, and LGALS9. Loss of function mutations in ABCG2 and SLC17A3 genes impair uric acid secretion thereby promoting its accumulation in the serum. Further, downregulation of SLC22 transporter expression by estrogens in premenopausal women diminishes urate reabsorption. As a result, elevated urate levels may cause clinical manifestations that include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi, and renal functional impairment.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) monohydrate crystals in the joint with clinical manifestations which include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi and renal functional impairment. Impaired renal uric acid excretion is the prevalent cause of gout.    Signaling description: In general, the process of renal urate transport consists of four steps including glomerular filtration, near-complete reabsorption of filtered urate, subsequent secretion, and postsecretory reabsorption in the remaining proximal tubule. Normally, following glomerular filtration of 100% of urate, 98% of it is reabsorbed in the first part of the proximal tubule, followed by 50% secretion in the second part of the tubule with further reabsorption of approximately 40-48%, resulting in a net reabsorption of approximately 90% leading to only 8-12% of uric acid being excreted by the kidney. In pathology, impaired function of uric acid transporters cause decreased urate excretion.    Outcome effects: Abnormalities in SLC22A12 and SLC2A9 genes cause increased urate reabsorption. SLC2A9 is a high-affinity urate transporter, and it functions in renal urate reabsorption by transporting urate from tubular cells to the peritubular interstitium following the uptake of urate from the luminal space by SLC22A12. Urate secretion from blood into the epithelial cell occurs via several transporters including SLC22A6 and SLC22A8, and from the cell to the tubule space (where urine is located) via another group of transporters including SLC17A1, SLC17A3, ABCG2, ABCC4, and LGALS9. Loss of function mutations in ABCG2 and SLC17A3 genes impair uric acid secretion thereby promoting its accumulation in the serum. Further, downregulation of SLC22 transporter expression by estrogens in premenopausal women diminishes urate reabsorption. As a result, elevated urate levels may cause clinical manifestations that include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi, and renal functional impairment.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

