xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) monohydrate crystals in the joint with clinical manifestations which include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi and renal functional impairment. The disruption of renal uric acid excretion is the prevalent cause of gout.    Signaling description: Uric acid is generated as a part of normal turnover of nucleic acids or is produced de novo. The first step of de novo synthesis is phosphoribosyl-PP production via the addition of an additional phosphate group (from ATP) to modified sugar ribose-5-phosphate (ribose-5-P). This step is performed by the enzyme PRPS1. Subsequently, several reactions result in inosine monophosphate (IMP) formation which can then be transformed to guanosine monophosphate (GMP). Further, purines (IMP and GMP) are metabolized to nucleosides (inosine and guanosine) and then to nucleotides (hypoxanthine and guanine) by NT5C2 and PNP, respectively. Normally, purine bases derived from nucleic acids are re-used. The enzyme hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HPRT1) salvages hypoxanthine to IMP and guanine to GMP. In a similar salvage pathway, adenosine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT) converts adenine to adenosine monophosphate (AMP). Xanthene dehydrogenase (XDH) is the enzyme that catalyzes the terminal steps in urate production, specifically the oxidation of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid.    Outcome effects: Increased fructose uptake can cause elevated uric acid synthesis as a result of ATP degradation. Specifically, increased net ATP degradation leads to the accumulation of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and AMP which can be rapidly degraded to uric acid. Also, HPRT1 deficiency and phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate synthetase 1 (PRPS1) superactivity can be the cause of secondary gout.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) monohydrate crystals in the joint with clinical manifestations which include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi and renal functional impairment. The disruption of renal uric acid excretion is the prevalent cause of gout.    Signaling description: Uric acid is generated as a part of normal turnover of nucleic acids or is produced de novo. The first step of de novo synthesis is phosphoribosyl-PP production via the addition of an additional phosphate group (from ATP) to modified sugar ribose-5-phosphate (ribose-5-P). This step is performed by the enzyme PRPS1. Subsequently, several reactions result in inosine monophosphate (IMP) formation which can then be transformed to guanosine monophosphate (GMP). Further, purines (IMP and GMP) are metabolized to nucleosides (inosine and guanosine) and then to nucleotides (hypoxanthine and guanine) by NT5C2 and PNP, respectively. Normally, purine bases derived from nucleic acids are re-used. The enzyme hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HPRT1) salvages hypoxanthine to IMP and guanine to GMP. In a similar salvage pathway, adenosine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT) converts adenine to adenosine monophosphate (AMP). Xanthene dehydrogenase (XDH) is the enzyme that catalyzes the terminal steps in urate production, specifically the oxidation of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid.    Outcome effects: Increased fructose uptake can cause elevated uric acid synthesis as a result of ATP degradation. Specifically, increased net ATP degradation leads to the accumulation of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and AMP which can be rapidly degraded to uric acid. Also, HPRT1 deficiency and phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate synthetase 1 (PRPS1) superactivity can be the cause of secondary gout.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

