xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) monohydrate crystals in the joint with clinical manifestations which include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, renal functional impairment, tophi and bone erosion.    Signaling description: Impaired ratio of TNFSF11 and its soluble receptor TNFRSF11B leads to abnormal osteoclast activation in gout. Both proteins are produced by osteoblasts and are designed to maintain bone homeostasis by triggering osteoclast activation (bone resorption) and preventing excessive bone destruction. TNFSF11 also is known as RANKL, initiates osteoclast differentiation by binding to TNFRSF11A receptor on the osteoclast surface. This signaling involves TRAF6, TRAF2, and TRAF5 proteins and affects the NF-kB and MAPK pathways. Also, PGE2 synthesized by monocytes increases TNFSF11 signaling through MAP3K7 activation.    Outcome effects: The resulting signaling promotes osteoclast differentiation and cell survival. At the same time, TNFSF11 signaling promotes osteoclast proliferation through the AKT1 pathway. In mature osteoclasts, the same signaling leads to bone resorption. Also, MSU crystals impair TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio by promoting constant activation of osteoclasts by TNFSF11 leading to pathological bone resorption and bone erosion. Cytokines such as IL1B (through NF-kB pathway) and IL6 (through JAK-STAT pathway) also contribute to osteoclast activation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) monohydrate crystals in the joint with clinical manifestations which include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, renal functional impairment, tophi and bone erosion.    Signaling description: Impaired ratio of TNFSF11 and its soluble receptor TNFRSF11B leads to abnormal osteoclast activation in gout. Both proteins are produced by osteoblasts and are designed to maintain bone homeostasis by triggering osteoclast activation (bone resorption) and preventing excessive bone destruction. TNFSF11 also is known as RANKL, initiates osteoclast differentiation by binding to TNFRSF11A receptor on the osteoclast surface. This signaling involves TRAF6, TRAF2, and TRAF5 proteins and affects the NF-kB and MAPK pathways. Also, PGE2 synthesized by monocytes increases TNFSF11 signaling through MAP3K7 activation.    Outcome effects: The resulting signaling promotes osteoclast differentiation and cell survival. At the same time, TNFSF11 signaling promotes osteoclast proliferation through the AKT1 pathway. In mature osteoclasts, the same signaling leads to bone resorption. Also, MSU crystals impair TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio by promoting constant activation of osteoclasts by TNFSF11 leading to pathological bone resorption and bone erosion. Cytokines such as IL1B (through NF-kB pathway) and IL6 (through JAK-STAT pathway) also contribute to osteoclast activation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

