xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Gout is a crystal deposition disease with clinical manifestations that include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi, and renal functional impairment due to monosodium urate (MSU) crystal deposition. Low pH/temperature and local dehydration contribute to the crystal formation. Monocytes among other cell types are the first to react to the process of urate crystal deposition in the joints.    Signaling description: MSU crystals may activate monocytes via the toll-like receptor (TLR) pathway and the inflammasome. The recognition of extracellular MSU crystals by TLR2 and TLR4 expressed by monocytes induce IL1B transcription. TLR2 and TLR4 signal transduction relies on MYD88, TICAM2, TIRAP and the adaptor proteins. Then, CD14, a phagocyte-expressed pattern recognition receptor functionally interacts with both TLR2 and TLR4 which could bind crystals and promote urate-induced inflammation. This signal transduction causes NF-kB and JUN activation which results in IL1B, TNF, IL6, and IL8 expression. In addition, obesity contributes to the pathology. Specifically, free fatty acids bind to TLR2 and accelerate the activation of monocytes. Lastly, the inflammasome activation depends on the NLRP3 complex assembly promoting CASP1-induced IL1B cleavage to generate the mature cytokine form.    Outcome effects: The cytokines promote the recruitment of neutrophils to the inflamed joint through the activation of endothelial cells. IL1B is the source of pain and inflammation and along with IL6 and PGE2 (synthesized as a result of CD14 signaling) contribute to osteoclast differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Gout is a crystal deposition disease with clinical manifestations that include acute gouty arthritis, chronic gouty arthropathy, tophi, and renal functional impairment due to monosodium urate (MSU) crystal deposition. Low pH/temperature and local dehydration contribute to the crystal formation. Monocytes among other cell types are the first to react to the process of urate crystal deposition in the joints.    Signaling description: MSU crystals may activate monocytes via the toll-like receptor (TLR) pathway and the inflammasome. The recognition of extracellular MSU crystals by TLR2 and TLR4 expressed by monocytes induce IL1B transcription. TLR2 and TLR4 signal transduction relies on MYD88, TICAM2, TIRAP and the adaptor proteins. Then, CD14, a phagocyte-expressed pattern recognition receptor functionally interacts with both TLR2 and TLR4 which could bind crystals and promote urate-induced inflammation. This signal transduction causes NF-kB and JUN activation which results in IL1B, TNF, IL6, and IL8 expression. In addition, obesity contributes to the pathology. Specifically, free fatty acids bind to TLR2 and accelerate the activation of monocytes. Lastly, the inflammasome activation depends on the NLRP3 complex assembly promoting CASP1-induced IL1B cleavage to generate the mature cytokine form.    Outcome effects: The cytokines promote the recruitment of neutrophils to the inflamed joint through the activation of endothelial cells. IL1B is the source of pain and inflammation and along with IL6 and PGE2 (synthesized as a result of CD14 signaling) contribute to osteoclast differentiation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

