xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoarthritis (OA) is a degenerative disease of the joints and is characterized by degradation of the hyaline articular cartilage and remodeling of the subchondral bone.    Signaling description: In OA, cartilage homeostasis is disrupted by mechanisms driven by a combination of biophysical and biochemical factors. OA involves the re-initiation of a process that normally occurs in the embryo and children and ceases in adulthood. This process starts with hypertrophic differentiation of chondrocytes characterized by type X collagen (COL10A1) expression, followed by the conversion of nonvascularized and hypoxic cartilage tissue into highly vascularized bone tissue via the degradation of the cartilage matrix and vascular invasion. The signals to induce endochondral ossification cause cartilage degradation at the center of the joint and osteophyte formation at the periphery. The key chondrocyte signaling pathways include, but are not limited to, WNT/CTNNB1, EPAS1, TGFB2, TNF, and IL1B pathways. The degradation of extracellular matrix (ECM) caused by TNF, IL1B, DDR2, and advanced glycosylation end products are impaired due to mutations in FRZB gene. The loss of FRZB may contribute to cartilage damage by increasing the expression and activity of MMPs, in a WNT-dependent and WNT-independent manner. Excessive mechanical loading causes TNF and IL1B synthesis in cartilage. The cytokines bind to their receptors on the chondrocyte surface and through MAPK8, MAPK p38, and MAPK1 pathways lead to JUN-FOS activation. The release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA is also provoked by these cytokines. In addition, JUN, FOS, and NF-kB bind to the promoter regions of metalloproteinases including MMP1, MMP3, and MMP13 and cause their synthesis. In addition, TNF induces the expression of syndecan 4 (SDC4) in OA.    Outcome effects: The metalloproteinases released into the ECM cleave collagens and aggrecan (ACAN), thereby provoking cartilage erosion. SDC4 directly activates ADAMTS5 which cleaves ACAN, which normally interacts with hyaluronic acid to form large proteoglycan aggregates (not shown). Proteoglycans provide COL2A1 fibrillar formation in articular cartilage; however, decreased proteoglycan aggregates due to the cleavage of ACAN are the reason for nonfibrillar COL2A1 presence. The latter binds to DDR2 receptor and through SHC1/TRAF6/TAB1/MAPK14 causes RUNX2 activation. RUNX2 and EPAS1 induce VEGFA and COL10A1 expression. These proteins are specific for hypertrophic chondrocytes and promote endochondral ossification and osteophyte formation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoarthritis (OA) is a degenerative disease of the joints and is characterized by degradation of the hyaline articular cartilage and remodeling of the subchondral bone.    Signaling description: In OA, cartilage homeostasis is disrupted by mechanisms driven by a combination of biophysical and biochemical factors. OA involves the re-initiation of a process that normally occurs in the embryo and children and ceases in adulthood. This process starts with hypertrophic differentiation of chondrocytes characterized by type X collagen (COL10A1) expression, followed by the conversion of nonvascularized and hypoxic cartilage tissue into highly vascularized bone tissue via the degradation of the cartilage matrix and vascular invasion. The signals to induce endochondral ossification cause cartilage degradation at the center of the joint and osteophyte formation at the periphery. The key chondrocyte signaling pathways include, but are not limited to, WNT/CTNNB1, EPAS1, TGFB2, TNF, and IL1B pathways. The degradation of extracellular matrix (ECM) caused by TNF, IL1B, DDR2, and advanced glycosylation end products are impaired due to mutations in FRZB gene. The loss of FRZB may contribute to cartilage damage by increasing the expression and activity of MMPs, in a WNT-dependent and WNT-independent manner. Excessive mechanical loading causes TNF and IL1B synthesis in cartilage. The cytokines bind to their receptors on the chondrocyte surface and through MAPK8, MAPK p38, and MAPK1 pathways lead to JUN-FOS activation. The release of NF-kB from its inhibitor, NFKBIA is also provoked by these cytokines. In addition, JUN, FOS, and NF-kB bind to the promoter regions of metalloproteinases including MMP1, MMP3, and MMP13 and cause their synthesis. In addition, TNF induces the expression of syndecan 4 (SDC4) in OA.    Outcome effects: The metalloproteinases released into the ECM cleave collagens and aggrecan (ACAN), thereby provoking cartilage erosion. SDC4 directly activates ADAMTS5 which cleaves ACAN, which normally interacts with hyaluronic acid to form large proteoglycan aggregates (not shown). Proteoglycans provide COL2A1 fibrillar formation in articular cartilage; however, decreased proteoglycan aggregates due to the cleavage of ACAN are the reason for nonfibrillar COL2A1 presence. The latter binds to DDR2 receptor and through SHC1/TRAF6/TAB1/MAPK14 causes RUNX2 activation. RUNX2 and EPAS1 induce VEGFA and COL10A1 expression. These proteins are specific for hypertrophic chondrocytes and promote endochondral ossification and osteophyte formation.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

