xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoarthritis (OA) is a degenerative disease of the joints and is characterized by the degradation of the hyaline articular cartilage and remodeling of the subchondral bone. The apoptosis of chondrocytes, the only cells present in cartilage, occurs in OA and is induced by TNF which is synthesized during mechanical stress.    Signaling description: TNF promotes cell death by two mechanisms including direct induction of apoptosis and indirect priming of cells for apoptosis by FASLG presentation. Direct induction includes the activation of caspase cascade. TNF, through TNFRSF1A, leads to the recruitment of TRADD-FADD complex and CASP8, which in turn cleaves BID and then, its active part is translocated into mitochondria where the protein triggers CYCS release through BAX activation. This process is sustained by inhibiting BCL2 with TRAF2/MAP3K5/MAPK8 and MAPK14. In addition, TNF triggers FASLG presentation through NF-kB involvement.    Outcome effects: CYCS affects CASP9 and the latter activates CASP3 leading to apoptosis. Furthermore, FASLG presentation through NF-kB also primes chondrocyte for apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoarthritis (OA) is a degenerative disease of the joints and is characterized by the degradation of the hyaline articular cartilage and remodeling of the subchondral bone. The apoptosis of chondrocytes, the only cells present in cartilage, occurs in OA and is induced by TNF which is synthesized during mechanical stress.    Signaling description: TNF promotes cell death by two mechanisms including direct induction of apoptosis and indirect priming of cells for apoptosis by FASLG presentation. Direct induction includes the activation of caspase cascade. TNF, through TNFRSF1A, leads to the recruitment of TRADD-FADD complex and CASP8, which in turn cleaves BID and then, its active part is translocated into mitochondria where the protein triggers CYCS release through BAX activation. This process is sustained by inhibiting BCL2 with TRAF2/MAP3K5/MAPK8 and MAPK14. In addition, TNF triggers FASLG presentation through NF-kB involvement.    Outcome effects: CYCS affects CASP9 and the latter activates CASP3 leading to apoptosis. Furthermore, FASLG presentation through NF-kB also primes chondrocyte for apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

