synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Growth factor TGFB2, which normally strongly inhibits articular chondrocyte hypertrophy and maturation in children, plays an important role in the development of osteoarthritis (OA)-inducing cartilage degradation and osteophyte formation through the impaired inhibition of chondrocyte hypertrophy in older individuals.    Signaling description: In healthy cartilage, the intracellular signaling initiated by TGFB2 is mediated through the TGFBR1 and TGFBR2 receptors. TGFB2 initially binds to TGFBR2, leading to the recruitment of TGFBR1 which phosphorylates SMAD2 or SMAD3. The phosphorylated SMAD2/3 thus dissociates from the receptor complex and forms a heteromeric complex with SMAD4. This heteromeric SMAD complex translocates into the nucleus where it accumulates and regulates the transcription of ACAN, COL2A1, TIMP1, and TIMP3 genes. In addition, activated TGFBR1 interacts with TAB1 and promotes ATF2 involvement in the signaling through the MAP3K7/MAP2K3/MAPK14 axis. At the same time, SMURF2 is elevated in OA and causes degradation of SMAD2 and TGFBR1.    Outcome effects: ACAN and COL2A1 provide cartilage resistance to compression, while TIMP1 and TIMP3 inhibit metalloproteinases. The expression of ATF2 prevents chondrocyte hypertrophy. As a result of SMAD2 and TGFBR1 degradation, the pathways which promote the synthesis of extracellular matrix proteins and prevent chondrocyte hypertrophy are blocked. Therefore, the activation of signaling through ACVRL1 occurs which involves SMAD1, 5, and 9 and results in degradative enzyme (MMP13) synthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Growth factor TGFB2, which normally strongly inhibits articular chondrocyte hypertrophy and maturation in children, plays an important role in the development of osteoarthritis (OA)-inducing cartilage degradation and osteophyte formation through the impaired inhibition of chondrocyte hypertrophy in older individuals.    Signaling description: In healthy cartilage, the intracellular signaling initiated by TGFB2 is mediated through the TGFBR1 and TGFBR2 receptors. TGFB2 initially binds to TGFBR2, leading to the recruitment of TGFBR1 which phosphorylates SMAD2 or SMAD3. The phosphorylated SMAD2/3 thus dissociates from the receptor complex and forms a heteromeric complex with SMAD4. This heteromeric SMAD complex translocates into the nucleus where it accumulates and regulates the transcription of ACAN, COL2A1, TIMP1, and TIMP3 genes. In addition, activated TGFBR1 interacts with TAB1 and promotes ATF2 involvement in the signaling through the MAP3K7/MAP2K3/MAPK14 axis. At the same time, SMURF2 is elevated in OA and causes degradation of SMAD2 and TGFBR1.    Outcome effects: ACAN and COL2A1 provide cartilage resistance to compression, while TIMP1 and TIMP3 inhibit metalloproteinases. The expression of ATF2 prevents chondrocyte hypertrophy. As a result of SMAD2 and TGFBR1 degradation, the pathways which promote the synthesis of extracellular matrix proteins and prevent chondrocyte hypertrophy are blocked. Therefore, the activation of signaling through ACVRL1 occurs which involves SMAD1, 5, and 9 and results in degradative enzyme (MMP13) synthesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

