xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyper IgM syndrome (HIGM1) is a family of genetic disorders characterized by an abnormally level of immunoglobulin M (IgM) antibodies decreased levels or absence of other antibodies. It is a rare primary immunodeficiency disorder usually inherited in an X-linked recessive manner.    Signaling description: All B-cells initially produce IgM antibodies prior to undergoing class switching due to exposure to a recognized antigen. Healthy B-cells efficiently switch to other types of antibodies necessary to attack invading bacteria, viruses, and other pathogens. In the majority of individuals with HIGM1, B-cells keep producing IgM antibodies as they are unable to switch to a different type of antibody. This results in an overproduction of IgM antibodies and an underproduction of all other immunoglobulin types including IgA, IgG, and IgE. The disorder causes immunodeficiencies including a higher susceptibility to various types of infections. Individuals with HIGM1 also typically have a lowered number of neutrophils and platelets. Conservative treatment consists of prophylactic intravenous immunoglobulin infusions at doses of 400-600 mg/kg per month.There are five identified genes with mutations that lead to HIGM1: 1) CD40 ligand (CD40LG) gene (in this case, which is the most common T-cells cannot induce immunoglobulin class switching in B-cells), 2) CD40 gene (B-cells cannot receive the signal from T-cells to switch classes), 3) Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AICDA) gene (B-cells cannot switch classes), 4) Uracil-DNA glycosylase (UNG) gene (B-cells cannot switch classes, but somatic hypermutation does not cause the effect), and 5) NEMO (IKBKG) gene which causes X-linked HIGM1 with ectodermal dysplasia.    Outcome effects: Due to these mutations, B-cells can no longer recombine genetic material to change heavy chain production which is a required step in class switch recombination.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyper IgM syndrome (HIGM1) is a family of genetic disorders characterized by an abnormally level of immunoglobulin M (IgM) antibodies decreased levels or absence of other antibodies. It is a rare primary immunodeficiency disorder usually inherited in an X-linked recessive manner.    Signaling description: All B-cells initially produce IgM antibodies prior to undergoing class switching due to exposure to a recognized antigen. Healthy B-cells efficiently switch to other types of antibodies necessary to attack invading bacteria, viruses, and other pathogens. In the majority of individuals with HIGM1, B-cells keep producing IgM antibodies as they are unable to switch to a different type of antibody. This results in an overproduction of IgM antibodies and an underproduction of all other immunoglobulin types including IgA, IgG, and IgE. The disorder causes immunodeficiencies including a higher susceptibility to various types of infections. Individuals with HIGM1 also typically have a lowered number of neutrophils and platelets. Conservative treatment consists of prophylactic intravenous immunoglobulin infusions at doses of 400-600 mg/kg per month.There are five identified genes with mutations that lead to HIGM1: 1) CD40 ligand (CD40LG) gene (in this case, which is the most common T-cells cannot induce immunoglobulin class switching in B-cells), 2) CD40 gene (B-cells cannot receive the signal from T-cells to switch classes), 3) Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AICDA) gene (B-cells cannot switch classes), 4) Uracil-DNA glycosylase (UNG) gene (B-cells cannot switch classes, but somatic hypermutation does not cause the effect), and 5) NEMO (IKBKG) gene which causes X-linked HIGM1 with ectodermal dysplasia.    Outcome effects: Due to these mutations, B-cells can no longer recombine genetic material to change heavy chain production which is a required step in class switch recombination.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

