synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur. HIV infection of macrophages is crucial for the disease pathogenesis and progression.    Signaling description: Normally, CCR5 is activated by its peptide ligands and regulates leukocyte chemotaxis and macrophage proliferation during inflammation. In the pathology, CCR5 serves as a coreceptor for macrophage tropic HIV entry. Infected cells serve as a reservoir for viral persistence and participate in person-to-person transmission. The chemokine receptor, CCR5 in macrophages is activated by HIV envelope glycoprotein Gp120 during HIV infection. CCR5 transduces its downstream signals through GNAQ promoting calcium (Ca2) influx into the cell through store-operated Ca2 channels. The signal from GNAQ to the channel is transduced through PLCG2 and IP3. Elevated intracellular Ca2 is required for the activation of calmodulin and the subsequent downstream activation of PTK2B. PTK2B activates two MAPK signaling cascades (including RAC1/MAP3K11 and CDC42/MAP3K1) through SRC and VAV2. Concurrently, MAPK1, MAPK14, MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10 which are downstream of MAP2K4, MAP2K7, MAP2K3, and MAP2K6, are stimulated.    Outcome effects: Stress-activated kinases enhance the transcriptional activity of the key transcription factors including NF-kB, JUN, and FOS, and these promote the proliferation of virally infected cells and lead to the secretion of cellular cytokines which may induce the recruitment of target cells and infected-cell proliferation through paracrine mechanisms. FASLG is also expressed in macrophages during HIV infection (the signal transduction from CCR5 to FASLG occurs through JAK/STAT).Therefore, in HIV infection, macrophages are the reservoir for viral persistence and death signal producers. Finally, mutations in CCR5 gene prevent HIV infection in humans."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur. HIV infection of macrophages is crucial for the disease pathogenesis and progression.    Signaling description: Normally, CCR5 is activated by its peptide ligands and regulates leukocyte chemotaxis and macrophage proliferation during inflammation. In the pathology, CCR5 serves as a coreceptor for macrophage tropic HIV entry. Infected cells serve as a reservoir for viral persistence and participate in person-to-person transmission. The chemokine receptor, CCR5 in macrophages is activated by HIV envelope glycoprotein Gp120 during HIV infection. CCR5 transduces its downstream signals through GNAQ promoting calcium (Ca2) influx into the cell through store-operated Ca2 channels. The signal from GNAQ to the channel is transduced through PLCG2 and IP3. Elevated intracellular Ca2 is required for the activation of calmodulin and the subsequent downstream activation of PTK2B. PTK2B activates two MAPK signaling cascades (including RAC1/MAP3K11 and CDC42/MAP3K1) through SRC and VAV2. Concurrently, MAPK1, MAPK14, MAPK8, MAPK9, and MAPK10 which are downstream of MAP2K4, MAP2K7, MAP2K3, and MAP2K6, are stimulated.    Outcome effects: Stress-activated kinases enhance the transcriptional activity of the key transcription factors including NF-kB, JUN, and FOS, and these promote the proliferation of virally infected cells and lead to the secretion of cellular cytokines which may induce the recruitment of target cells and infected-cell proliferation through paracrine mechanisms. FASLG is also expressed in macrophages during HIV infection (the signal transduction from CCR5 to FASLG occurs through JAK/STAT).Therefore, in HIV infection, macrophages are the reservoir for viral persistence and death signal producers. Finally, mutations in CCR5 gene prevent HIV infection in humans." EXACT []

