synonym: "PMID: 21994747" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4ba1066a-dfdc-42d2-a77e-2fa5dd16a85b"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4ba1066a-dfdc-42d2-a77e-2fa5dd16a85b" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "T-cell"
synonym: "CellType: T-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "blood"
synonym: "Tissue: blood" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur.    Signaling description: Normally, CD4 T-cell signaling includes T-cell receptor (TCR), CD4, CD28, and IL4 and provides B-cell involvement in the immune response to pathogens and contributes to Th17 differentiation which is important for immune defense in the intestine. At the core of signaling is TCR with its coreceptors (CD4 and CD28), which (through ERK (MAPK), JNK (MAPK8), and PI3K pathways) activate Jun/Fos, NF-kB, and NF-AT transcription factors and induce the expression of cytokines for Th17 differentiation and B-cell activation. T-cell activation is initiated by binding of TCR and its coreceptor, CD4 to the appropriate antigen presented by MHC class II protein complex molecules. Signals subsequently phosphorylate the immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs of the CD3 chains, followed by the recruitment and activation of ZAP70. Following phosphorylation by ZAP70, LAT interacts with PLCG1 which hydrolyzes phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (not shown) into secondary messengers, IP3 and diacylglycerol. The latter initiates calcium (Ca2) influx and the activation of PRKCQ and MAPK cascade which result in the activation of transcription through NF-kB and Jun/Fos. The released Ca2 interacts with calmodulin-calcineurin complex and activates NF-AT. CD28 is an important coreceptor for CD4 T-cells regulated by CD80 and CD86. The signal from CD28 is transduced to PI3K, which generates PIP3, which in turn initiates AKT1 activation through PDPK1. AKT1 provokes NF-kB release from its inhibitor, NFKBIA. In addition, IL2 and IL4, through JAK/STAT, promote GATA3 and MAF expression. CD247 (TCR zeta chain) may also activate JAK3 directly.    Outcome effects: NF-AT, NF-kB, Jun/Fos, MAF, and GATA3 trigger the transcription of B-cell activating and Th17-differentiating cytokines. Viral Nef protein downregulates CD28 which blocks signal transduction to NF-AT. Finally, CD4 ligation with HIV envelope glycoprotein Gp120 (env) markedly inhibits TCR-induced activation and expression of JAK3, which transduces the signal to STAT and then to GATA3 and MAF for IL4, IL5, IL10, and IL13 transcription. The block of signaling in CD4 T-cells leads to the decline of the immune response.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Viral proteins are shown in grey.    References: 1). Santos-Costa Q, Gracas-Pereira J, Azevedo-Pereira JM (2007) Death on arrival. HIV and apoptosis - an . VIII Congresso Virtual HIV/AIDS - Current research insights into HIV/AIDS and related diseases. ISBN 978-972-95977-7-0. p. 149-96."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur.    Signaling description: Normally, CD4 T-cell signaling includes T-cell receptor (TCR), CD4, CD28, and IL4 and provides B-cell involvement in the immune response to pathogens and contributes to Th17 differentiation which is important for immune defense in the intestine. At the core of signaling is TCR with its coreceptors (CD4 and CD28), which (through ERK (MAPK), JNK (MAPK8), and PI3K pathways) activate Jun/Fos, NF-kB, and NF-AT transcription factors and induce the expression of cytokines for Th17 differentiation and B-cell activation. T-cell activation is initiated by binding of TCR and its coreceptor, CD4 to the appropriate antigen presented by MHC class II protein complex molecules. Signals subsequently phosphorylate the immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs of the CD3 chains, followed by the recruitment and activation of ZAP70. Following phosphorylation by ZAP70, LAT interacts with PLCG1 which hydrolyzes phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (not shown) into secondary messengers, IP3 and diacylglycerol. The latter initiates calcium (Ca2) influx and the activation of PRKCQ and MAPK cascade which result in the activation of transcription through NF-kB and Jun/Fos. The released Ca2 interacts with calmodulin-calcineurin complex and activates NF-AT. CD28 is an important coreceptor for CD4 T-cells regulated by CD80 and CD86. The signal from CD28 is transduced to PI3K, which generates PIP3, which in turn initiates AKT1 activation through PDPK1. AKT1 provokes NF-kB release from its inhibitor, NFKBIA. In addition, IL2 and IL4, through JAK/STAT, promote GATA3 and MAF expression. CD247 (TCR zeta chain) may also activate JAK3 directly.    Outcome effects: NF-AT, NF-kB, Jun/Fos, MAF, and GATA3 trigger the transcription of B-cell activating and Th17-differentiating cytokines. Viral Nef protein downregulates CD28 which blocks signal transduction to NF-AT. Finally, CD4 ligation with HIV envelope glycoprotein Gp120 (env) markedly inhibits TCR-induced activation and expression of JAK3, which transduces the signal to STAT and then to GATA3 and MAF for IL4, IL5, IL10, and IL13 transcription. The block of signaling in CD4 T-cells leads to the decline of the immune response.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Viral proteins are shown in grey.    References: 1). Santos-Costa Q, Gracas-Pereira J, Azevedo-Pereira JM (2007) Death on arrival. HIV and apoptosis - an . VIII Congresso Virtual HIV/AIDS - Current research insights into HIV/AIDS and related diseases. ISBN 978-972-95977-7-0. p. 149-96." EXACT []

