xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur.    Signaling description: Normally, TNF and FASLG interaction with their receptors, TNFRSF1A/TNFRSF1B and FAS, respectively induce apoptosis. The activation of TNFR1 and FAS leads to the recruitment of TRADD-FADD complex and CASP8. CASP8 activates CASP3 via two signaling routes. In the first pathway, CASP8 cleaves BID and its active part is translocated into the mitochondria where it triggers CYCS release through BAX activation. CYCS affects CASP9 which activates CASP3. In the second pathway, CASP8 directly activates CASP3. There exists a further common pathway for TNF and FASLG which includes MAP3K5 activation and also leads to apoptosis. MAP3K5 inactivates the antiapoptotic factor BCL2 through MAPK8 and MAPK14. Through binding to TNFR1, TNF also activates the recruitment of TRADD, which via TRAF2/ MAP3K14, activates NF-kB which is an antiapoptotic transcription factor. NF-kB suppresses apoptosis by inducing the transcription of antiapoptotic genes including BIRC2, BIRC3, and BIRC5.    Outcome effects: The antiapoptotic proteins BIRC2, BIRC3, and BIRC5 prevent cytochrome c-induced activation of CASP9, and when they are repressed by DIABLO apoptosis is initiated. Viral Nef protein interacts with MAP3K5 and inhibits it which frees BCL2, which in turn inhibits BAX. This leads to the block the following signal transduction needed for the induction of apoptosis, thereby preventing infected cell death.    Highlighted proteins: Viral protein is highlighted in grey.    References: 1). Santos-Costa Q, Gracas-Pereira J, Azevedo-Pereira JM (2007) Death on arrival. HIV and apoptosis - an . VIII Congresso Virtual HIV/AIDS - Current research insights into HIV/AIDS and related diseases. ISBN 978-972-95977-7-0. p. 149-96."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur.    Signaling description: Normally, TNF and FASLG interaction with their receptors, TNFRSF1A/TNFRSF1B and FAS, respectively induce apoptosis. The activation of TNFR1 and FAS leads to the recruitment of TRADD-FADD complex and CASP8. CASP8 activates CASP3 via two signaling routes. In the first pathway, CASP8 cleaves BID and its active part is translocated into the mitochondria where it triggers CYCS release through BAX activation. CYCS affects CASP9 which activates CASP3. In the second pathway, CASP8 directly activates CASP3. There exists a further common pathway for TNF and FASLG which includes MAP3K5 activation and also leads to apoptosis. MAP3K5 inactivates the antiapoptotic factor BCL2 through MAPK8 and MAPK14. Through binding to TNFR1, TNF also activates the recruitment of TRADD, which via TRAF2/ MAP3K14, activates NF-kB which is an antiapoptotic transcription factor. NF-kB suppresses apoptosis by inducing the transcription of antiapoptotic genes including BIRC2, BIRC3, and BIRC5.    Outcome effects: The antiapoptotic proteins BIRC2, BIRC3, and BIRC5 prevent cytochrome c-induced activation of CASP9, and when they are repressed by DIABLO apoptosis is initiated. Viral Nef protein interacts with MAP3K5 and inhibits it which frees BCL2, which in turn inhibits BAX. This leads to the block the following signal transduction needed for the induction of apoptosis, thereby preventing infected cell death.    Highlighted proteins: Viral protein is highlighted in grey.    References: 1). Santos-Costa Q, Gracas-Pereira J, Azevedo-Pereira JM (2007) Death on arrival. HIV and apoptosis - an . VIII Congresso Virtual HIV/AIDS - Current research insights into HIV/AIDS and related diseases. ISBN 978-972-95977-7-0. p. 149-96." EXACT []

