xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur.    Signaling description: HIV uses CD4 and CXCR4 as receptors for entry. Also, HIV envelope glycoprotein Gp120 (env) binds to CD4 to promote FAS synthesis. CD4, through LCK and ZAP70, activates LAT which results in Jun/Fos activation first through PLCG1/ PRKCQ which leads to NF-kB activation, and secondly through GRB2/SOS1/Ras/RAF1/ERK (MAPK).    Outcome effects: NF-kB and Jun/Fos promote FAS transcription which after final editing is translocated and integrated into the membrane. At the same time, env protein binds to CCR5 on the macrophage surface and through JAK/STAT pathway promotes FASLG synthesis. Macrophage-released FASLG binds to FAS of CD4 T-cells causing cell apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Viral proteins are shown in grey.    References: 1). Santos-Costa Q, Gracas-Pereira J, Azevedo-Pereira JM (2007) Death on arrival . HIV and apoptosis - an . VIII Congresso Virtual HIV/AIDS - Current research insights into HIV/AIDS and related diseases. ISBN 978-972-95977-7-0. p. 149-96."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is a lentivirus which causes acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), a condition in which the progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to occur.    Signaling description: HIV uses CD4 and CXCR4 as receptors for entry. Also, HIV envelope glycoprotein Gp120 (env) binds to CD4 to promote FAS synthesis. CD4, through LCK and ZAP70, activates LAT which results in Jun/Fos activation first through PLCG1/ PRKCQ which leads to NF-kB activation, and secondly through GRB2/SOS1/Ras/RAF1/ERK (MAPK).    Outcome effects: NF-kB and Jun/Fos promote FAS transcription which after final editing is translocated and integrated into the membrane. At the same time, env protein binds to CCR5 on the macrophage surface and through JAK/STAT pathway promotes FASLG synthesis. Macrophage-released FASLG binds to FAS of CD4 T-cells causing cell apoptosis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. Viral proteins are shown in grey.    References: 1). Santos-Costa Q, Gracas-Pereira J, Azevedo-Pereira JM (2007) Death on arrival . HIV and apoptosis - an . VIII Congresso Virtual HIV/AIDS - Current research insights into HIV/AIDS and related diseases. ISBN 978-972-95977-7-0. p. 149-96." EXACT []

