xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease involving interactions between complex microbial biofilms, many cell populations and inflammatory mediators, leading to the destruction of the tooth-supporting structures like the periodontal ligament and the alveolar bone. Chronic periodontitis is initiated by colonization with a broad array of bacteria and is perpetuated by an immune-inflammatory response to the changing biofilm. Host recognition of microbes is largely mediated by toll-like receptors (TLRs).     Signaling description: Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) and other virulence factors of gram-negative periodontal bacteria increase the gene expression of TLR2 and TLR4 that play a crucial role in mediating tissue immune responses to periodontitis. These two receptors are expressed in the cells of periodontal tissues such as gingival epithelial cells, gingival fibroblasts, periodontal ligament cells and highly-expressed TLR2 and TLR4 are detected in infected gingival tissues. Mechanism of the molecular signaling of this pathway is known. When virulence factors produced by periodontal pathogens bind to TLRs, they recruit adapter proteins (TICAM1/2, TIRAP, MYD88) and via protein kinases (IKBKE, IRAK1/4, TBK1) and other downstream proteins activate transcription factors NF-kB and IRF3 that induce production of proinflammatory cytokines, chemokine and interferons (IL1B, IL2, IL6, TNF, CXCL8, IFNA1, IFNB1).     Outcome effects: Increased expressions of TLRs participate and persist in inflammatory responses and immune responses following stimulation by bacteria in periodontal tissues.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease involving interactions between complex microbial biofilms, many cell populations and inflammatory mediators, leading to the destruction of the tooth-supporting structures like the periodontal ligament and the alveolar bone. Chronic periodontitis is initiated by colonization with a broad array of bacteria and is perpetuated by an immune-inflammatory response to the changing biofilm. Host recognition of microbes is largely mediated by toll-like receptors (TLRs).     Signaling description: Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) and other virulence factors of gram-negative periodontal bacteria increase the gene expression of TLR2 and TLR4 that play a crucial role in mediating tissue immune responses to periodontitis. These two receptors are expressed in the cells of periodontal tissues such as gingival epithelial cells, gingival fibroblasts, periodontal ligament cells and highly-expressed TLR2 and TLR4 are detected in infected gingival tissues. Mechanism of the molecular signaling of this pathway is known. When virulence factors produced by periodontal pathogens bind to TLRs, they recruit adapter proteins (TICAM1/2, TIRAP, MYD88) and via protein kinases (IKBKE, IRAK1/4, TBK1) and other downstream proteins activate transcription factors NF-kB and IRF3 that induce production of proinflammatory cytokines, chemokine and interferons (IL1B, IL2, IL6, TNF, CXCL8, IFNA1, IFNB1).     Outcome effects: Increased expressions of TLRs participate and persist in inflammatory responses and immune responses following stimulation by bacteria in periodontal tissues.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

