xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Periodontitis - a collective term for separate nosological forms of destruction of the dentition. Characterized by chronic or acute inflammatory process of periodontal tissue and bone atrophy of the alveolar process (alveolar portion) of the jaw. Clinical manifestations of periodontitis are similar, despite the variety of causes leading to these diseases. Diabetes mellitus is a group of metabolic diseases in which there are high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period, can cause many complications, such as cardiovascular disease, stroke, chronic kidney failure, foot ulcers, and damage to the eyes. Large number of investigations has provided the evidence that type 1 and type 2 diabetes increase the risk and severity of periodontitis, and vice-versa, periodontitis is recognized as the serious complication of diabetes.     Signaling description: Despite being extensive research, mechanism underlying the association of periodontitis and diabetes mellitus is not clear. Elevated levels of TNF and IL6 are on the background of diabetes induce the IL6R and TNFR-1 signaling causing the cell proliferation, osteoclast activation, inflammatory and immune responses in periodontal tissue. Hyperglycemia associated with the disturbances in carbohydrates, fat, and protein metabolism leads to non-enzymatic glycation and oxidation of proteins or lipids and to the subsequent formation of advanced glycation endproducts (AGEs). Elevated levels of AGEs are reported in gingival tissues of diabetic patients. It has been shown that AGE-AGER interaction induces the activation of NF?B and subsequent high expression of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF, IL1B, IL2, IL6, CXCL8) and cytotoxic peptides defensins (DEFB4A, DEFB103B).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Periodontitis - a collective term for separate nosological forms of destruction of the dentition. Characterized by chronic or acute inflammatory process of periodontal tissue and bone atrophy of the alveolar process (alveolar portion) of the jaw. Clinical manifestations of periodontitis are similar, despite the variety of causes leading to these diseases. Diabetes mellitus is a group of metabolic diseases in which there are high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period, can cause many complications, such as cardiovascular disease, stroke, chronic kidney failure, foot ulcers, and damage to the eyes. Large number of investigations has provided the evidence that type 1 and type 2 diabetes increase the risk and severity of periodontitis, and vice-versa, periodontitis is recognized as the serious complication of diabetes.     Signaling description: Despite being extensive research, mechanism underlying the association of periodontitis and diabetes mellitus is not clear. Elevated levels of TNF and IL6 are on the background of diabetes induce the IL6R and TNFR-1 signaling causing the cell proliferation, osteoclast activation, inflammatory and immune responses in periodontal tissue. Hyperglycemia associated with the disturbances in carbohydrates, fat, and protein metabolism leads to non-enzymatic glycation and oxidation of proteins or lipids and to the subsequent formation of advanced glycation endproducts (AGEs). Elevated levels of AGEs are reported in gingival tissues of diabetic patients. It has been shown that AGE-AGER interaction induces the activation of NF?B and subsequent high expression of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF, IL1B, IL2, IL6, CXCL8) and cytotoxic peptides defensins (DEFB4A, DEFB103B).    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

