xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Periodontitis is a set of inflammatory diseases affecting the periodontium. Periodontitis involves progressive loss of the alveolar bone around the teeth, and if left untreated, can lead to the loosening and subsequent loss of teeth. Periodontitis is caused by microorganisms that adhere to and grow on the tooth's surfaces, along with an over-aggressive immune response against these microorganisms. Mutated cathepsins cause changes in immune response, degradation of extracellular matrix and osteoclast activation.     Signaling description: The cathepsin-dependent mechanism underlying the progression of periodontitis still remains largely unknown. Mutations in the cathepsin C gene (CTSC) have been identified in prepubertal aggressive periodontitis. Cathepsin C activity has been found to be significantly lower in tissue extract supernatants and gingival crevicular fluid of patients with periodontitis compared with those of healthy controls. Cathepsin C plays essential role in the host defense against bacteria and is the activator of elastase (ELANE) and proteinase 3 (PRTN3) in human. Proteinase 3 is a serine proteinase that is involved in inflammatory response. Elastase is a serine protease hydrolyzes collagen, elastin, fibronectin and many other proteins that leads to extracellular matrix polymerization. Elastase cleaves CXCR1 (IL-8 receptor on neutrophils), macrophage cell surface CD14 and phosphatidylserine receptor (JMJD6) and indirectly regulates neutrophil function and phagocytic recognition of apoptotic lymphocytic cells. Cathepsin K, B, G (CTSK, CTSB, CTSG) concentrations in gingival crevicular fluid of control subjects were below the limit of detection, whereas those for the periodontal patients were significantly higher. Cathepsins K, G, B are key proteases in the osteoclast activation and extracellular matrix degradation via regulation of matrix metalloproteinases.CTSC with white centered color has mutations.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Periodontitis is a set of inflammatory diseases affecting the periodontium. Periodontitis involves progressive loss of the alveolar bone around the teeth, and if left untreated, can lead to the loosening and subsequent loss of teeth. Periodontitis is caused by microorganisms that adhere to and grow on the tooth's surfaces, along with an over-aggressive immune response against these microorganisms. Mutated cathepsins cause changes in immune response, degradation of extracellular matrix and osteoclast activation.     Signaling description: The cathepsin-dependent mechanism underlying the progression of periodontitis still remains largely unknown. Mutations in the cathepsin C gene (CTSC) have been identified in prepubertal aggressive periodontitis. Cathepsin C activity has been found to be significantly lower in tissue extract supernatants and gingival crevicular fluid of patients with periodontitis compared with those of healthy controls. Cathepsin C plays essential role in the host defense against bacteria and is the activator of elastase (ELANE) and proteinase 3 (PRTN3) in human. Proteinase 3 is a serine proteinase that is involved in inflammatory response. Elastase is a serine protease hydrolyzes collagen, elastin, fibronectin and many other proteins that leads to extracellular matrix polymerization. Elastase cleaves CXCR1 (IL-8 receptor on neutrophils), macrophage cell surface CD14 and phosphatidylserine receptor (JMJD6) and indirectly regulates neutrophil function and phagocytic recognition of apoptotic lymphocytic cells. Cathepsin K, B, G (CTSK, CTSB, CTSG) concentrations in gingival crevicular fluid of control subjects were below the limit of detection, whereas those for the periodontal patients were significantly higher. Cathepsins K, G, B are key proteases in the osteoclast activation and extracellular matrix degradation via regulation of matrix metalloproteinases.CTSC with white centered color has mutations.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue, and proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

