xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteopetrosis (known as marble bone disease) is a group of rare, heritable disorders of the skeleton characterized by increased bone density on radiographs. Osteopetrotic conditions vary in their presentation and severity, ranging from neonatal onset with life-threatening complications such as bone marrow failure, to the incidental finding of osteopetrosis on radiographs. Osteopetrosis is caused by the failure of osteoclast development or function. Mutations in at least 10 genes have been identified as causative in humans.    Signaling description: Many forms of osteoclast-rich osteopetrosis are caused by defects in gene products involved in the acidification machinery.Acid secretion is dependent on two key molecules which facilitate proton transport: vacuolar proton-transporting V-ATPase and chloride-specific ion channel, CLCN7. Mutations in the genes encoding the subunit of V-ATPase (TCIRG1) and CLCN7 produce severe malignant osteopetrosis. CLCN7 is closely associated with another membrane protein, OSTM1. Mutations in OSTM1 gene are found in a subset of autosomal recessive osteopetrosis (ARO) patients with neurological involvement. The protons and chloride ions that are expended in the acidification process need to be replenished intracellularly to avoid alkalization. This is achieved by carbonic anhydrase type II (CA2) and an anion exchanger. CA2 plays a key role in kidney function and mutations in CA2 result in ARO with tubular acidosis. In addition, homozygous mutations in CTSK gene lead to pycnodysostosis, a condition accompanied by osteopetrosis. The formation and maintenance of osteoclast polarized membrane domains requires complex vesicular trafficking mechanisms and continuous remodeling of the osteoclast cytoskeleton. PLEKHM1 plays a critical function in vesicle trafficking and acidification. Heterozygous mutations in this gene have been associated with intermediate forms of osteopetrosis. Furthermore, TNFSF11 and TNFRSF11A gene mutations lead to failure of osteoclast differentiation and function. IKBKG mutations cause osteopetrosis with ectodermal dysplasia and immune defects. Osteoblasts regulate osteoclast function through the production of paracrine factors, mainly TNFSF11, which plays a role in WNT signaling.    Outcome effects: The impairment of WNT signaling has been shown to cause osteopetrosis. PORCN mutations are associated with osteopathia striata in Goltz Syndrome, while FAM123B mutations are linked to osteopathia striata with cranial stenosis. OSTM1 regulates CTNNB1/LEF1 interaction and causes rare forms of osteopetrosis. LRP1 mutations also lead to rare forms of the disorder.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteopetrosis (known as marble bone disease) is a group of rare, heritable disorders of the skeleton characterized by increased bone density on radiographs. Osteopetrotic conditions vary in their presentation and severity, ranging from neonatal onset with life-threatening complications such as bone marrow failure, to the incidental finding of osteopetrosis on radiographs. Osteopetrosis is caused by the failure of osteoclast development or function. Mutations in at least 10 genes have been identified as causative in humans.    Signaling description: Many forms of osteoclast-rich osteopetrosis are caused by defects in gene products involved in the acidification machinery.Acid secretion is dependent on two key molecules which facilitate proton transport: vacuolar proton-transporting V-ATPase and chloride-specific ion channel, CLCN7. Mutations in the genes encoding the subunit of V-ATPase (TCIRG1) and CLCN7 produce severe malignant osteopetrosis. CLCN7 is closely associated with another membrane protein, OSTM1. Mutations in OSTM1 gene are found in a subset of autosomal recessive osteopetrosis (ARO) patients with neurological involvement. The protons and chloride ions that are expended in the acidification process need to be replenished intracellularly to avoid alkalization. This is achieved by carbonic anhydrase type II (CA2) and an anion exchanger. CA2 plays a key role in kidney function and mutations in CA2 result in ARO with tubular acidosis. In addition, homozygous mutations in CTSK gene lead to pycnodysostosis, a condition accompanied by osteopetrosis. The formation and maintenance of osteoclast polarized membrane domains requires complex vesicular trafficking mechanisms and continuous remodeling of the osteoclast cytoskeleton. PLEKHM1 plays a critical function in vesicle trafficking and acidification. Heterozygous mutations in this gene have been associated with intermediate forms of osteopetrosis. Furthermore, TNFSF11 and TNFRSF11A gene mutations lead to failure of osteoclast differentiation and function. IKBKG mutations cause osteopetrosis with ectodermal dysplasia and immune defects. Osteoblasts regulate osteoclast function through the production of paracrine factors, mainly TNFSF11, which plays a role in WNT signaling.    Outcome effects: The impairment of WNT signaling has been shown to cause osteopetrosis. PORCN mutations are associated with osteopathia striata in Goltz Syndrome, while FAM123B mutations are linked to osteopathia striata with cranial stenosis. OSTM1 regulates CTNNB1/LEF1 interaction and causes rare forms of osteopetrosis. LRP1 mutations also lead to rare forms of the disorder.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

