synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. In osteoporosis, calcium (Ca2+) deficiency can be the cause of impaired bone mineralization.    Signaling description: Hydroxylapatite is a major component of healthy bones and is formed in membrane-bound matrix vesicles (MV) in osteoblasts. The process includes several steps. ENPP1 is a membrane protein of MV which converts ATP into AMP with PPi release. PPi is hydrolyzed by the ALPL enzyme in the MV outer membrane yielding monophosphate ions (Pi) for incorporation into bone mineral. In addition, another enzyme, PHOSPHO1 hydrolyzes phosphorylcholine and phosphoethanolamine to produce Pi inside the MV.    Outcome effects: Pi complexes with Ca2+ in the form of hydroxylapatite crystal. They initially deposit as non-crystalline compounds and are subsequently converted to hydroxylapatite, the main crystalline salt of the bone. Hydroxylapatite functions to store ions and gives bone its compressional strength. Some of the hydroxylapatite remains in the non-crystalline form to facilitate a rapid resorption when needed. A polymorphism of the first intron of the gene coding for COL1A1 and increased levels of homocysteine can influence fracture risk. This may be due to differences in helix formation or cross-linking of collagen, which challenges the concept of normal mineral and matrix composition in osteoporosis with only structural abnormalities being the cause of skeletal fragility.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Sapir-Koren R, Livshits G (2011) Bone mineralization and regulation of phosphate homeostasis. IBMS BoneKEy. 8, 286-300."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. In osteoporosis, calcium (Ca2+) deficiency can be the cause of impaired bone mineralization.    Signaling description: Hydroxylapatite is a major component of healthy bones and is formed in membrane-bound matrix vesicles (MV) in osteoblasts. The process includes several steps. ENPP1 is a membrane protein of MV which converts ATP into AMP with PPi release. PPi is hydrolyzed by the ALPL enzyme in the MV outer membrane yielding monophosphate ions (Pi) for incorporation into bone mineral. In addition, another enzyme, PHOSPHO1 hydrolyzes phosphorylcholine and phosphoethanolamine to produce Pi inside the MV.    Outcome effects: Pi complexes with Ca2+ in the form of hydroxylapatite crystal. They initially deposit as non-crystalline compounds and are subsequently converted to hydroxylapatite, the main crystalline salt of the bone. Hydroxylapatite functions to store ions and gives bone its compressional strength. Some of the hydroxylapatite remains in the non-crystalline form to facilitate a rapid resorption when needed. A polymorphism of the first intron of the gene coding for COL1A1 and increased levels of homocysteine can influence fracture risk. This may be due to differences in helix formation or cross-linking of collagen, which challenges the concept of normal mineral and matrix composition in osteoporosis with only structural abnormalities being the cause of skeletal fragility.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Sapir-Koren R, Livshits G (2011) Bone mineralization and regulation of phosphate homeostasis. IBMS BoneKEy. 8, 286-300." EXACT []

