xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Following the menopause, risk of bone loss is increased due to lower levels of estrogen. Postmenopausal osteoporosis is characterized by impaired osteoblast function.    Signaling description: Normally, estrogen acts via the estrogen receptor ESR1 to promote bone mineral density. Estrogen binding to ESR1 activates intracellular signaling through IGF1R stabilization in the cell membrane and CTNNB1 translocation to the nucleus. The first route involves IGF1 signaling and the second affects the WNT pathway.    Outcome effects: IGF1R signaling includes downstream activation of two axes: AKT1 pathway and MAPK pathway providing cell proliferation and cell survival. Further, canonical WNT pathway is responsible for osteoblast differentiation involving RUNX2 transcription factor. Estrogen deficiency and/or polymorphisms in the ESR1 lead to impaired osteoblast functioning and bone biomineralization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Bikle DD, Wang Y (2011) Insulin-like growth factor-I and bone. IBMS BoneKEy.8:328-41."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Following the menopause, risk of bone loss is increased due to lower levels of estrogen. Postmenopausal osteoporosis is characterized by impaired osteoblast function.    Signaling description: Normally, estrogen acts via the estrogen receptor ESR1 to promote bone mineral density. Estrogen binding to ESR1 activates intracellular signaling through IGF1R stabilization in the cell membrane and CTNNB1 translocation to the nucleus. The first route involves IGF1 signaling and the second affects the WNT pathway.    Outcome effects: IGF1R signaling includes downstream activation of two axes: AKT1 pathway and MAPK pathway providing cell proliferation and cell survival. Further, canonical WNT pathway is responsible for osteoblast differentiation involving RUNX2 transcription factor. Estrogen deficiency and/or polymorphisms in the ESR1 lead to impaired osteoblast functioning and bone biomineralization.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style.    References: 1). Bikle DD, Wang Y (2011) Insulin-like growth factor-I and bone. IBMS BoneKEy.8:328-41." EXACT []

