xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. In osteoporosis, calcium (Ca2+) deficiency can be the cause of impaired bone mineralization. A reduction of intestinal Ca2 transport is often observed in the elderly.    Signaling description: Ca2+ is absorbed in the small intestine by two general mechanisms: a transcellular active transport process which is located largely in the duodenum and upper jejunum; and a paracellular passive process that functions throughout the length of the intestine. The transcellular process begins with the Ca2+ entry through the brush border via the TRPV5/6 proteins. The expression of TRPV proteins is under the control of VDR and ESR1 transcription factors. In the absence of their ligands (estrogens and/or calcitriol) or due to polymorphisms in the genes TRPV synthesis can be impaired causing decreased Ca2 absorption in the intestine.    Outcome effects: Following its entry through the brush border, Ca2+ is transported to the inner membrane by S100G protein, and then excreted by the ATP2B1 and SLC8A1 transporters into the blood.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. In osteoporosis, calcium (Ca2+) deficiency can be the cause of impaired bone mineralization. A reduction of intestinal Ca2 transport is often observed in the elderly.    Signaling description: Ca2+ is absorbed in the small intestine by two general mechanisms: a transcellular active transport process which is located largely in the duodenum and upper jejunum; and a paracellular passive process that functions throughout the length of the intestine. The transcellular process begins with the Ca2+ entry through the brush border via the TRPV5/6 proteins. The expression of TRPV proteins is under the control of VDR and ESR1 transcription factors. In the absence of their ligands (estrogens and/or calcitriol) or due to polymorphisms in the genes TRPV synthesis can be impaired causing decreased Ca2 absorption in the intestine.    Outcome effects: Following its entry through the brush border, Ca2+ is transported to the inner membrane by S100G protein, and then excreted by the ATP2B1 and SLC8A1 transporters into the blood.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with reduced level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

