synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Systemic osteoporosis refers to pathological bone loss in numerous bones. The process can be triggered by several chronic inflammatory conditions. Inflammation has been shown to lead to excessive bone resorption as well as impaired bone formation.    Signaling description: Under healthy conditions, essential pathways involved in osteoblast functioning are: the bone morphogenetic protein BMP2 and its receptors, which utilize SMAD proteins for direct or transcriptional activation of RUNX2 and subsequent downstream cellular events; the WNT-FZD pathway, which uses CTNNB1 for further activities; as well as parathyroid hormone (PTH) and its receptor signaling mediated by PKA. Pro-inflammatory cytokines promote osteoblast functioning disruption through several mechanisms. TNF causes the expression of SMURF proteins which block BMP2 signaling in osteoblasts by mediating the degradation of SMAD proteins. In addition, TNF induces DKK1 and SOST expression which are inhibitors of WNT signaling. The negative effects of pro-inflammatory cytokines on osteoblasts include IL6-mediated impairment of osteoclast differentiation through JAK-STAT pathway.    Outcome effects: The cytokines activated during the course of inflammatory response have profound effects on the differentiation and activity of osteoblasts by affecting cell signaling.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Systemic osteoporosis refers to pathological bone loss in numerous bones. The process can be triggered by several chronic inflammatory conditions. Inflammation has been shown to lead to excessive bone resorption as well as impaired bone formation.    Signaling description: Under healthy conditions, essential pathways involved in osteoblast functioning are: the bone morphogenetic protein BMP2 and its receptors, which utilize SMAD proteins for direct or transcriptional activation of RUNX2 and subsequent downstream cellular events; the WNT-FZD pathway, which uses CTNNB1 for further activities; as well as parathyroid hormone (PTH) and its receptor signaling mediated by PKA. Pro-inflammatory cytokines promote osteoblast functioning disruption through several mechanisms. TNF causes the expression of SMURF proteins which block BMP2 signaling in osteoblasts by mediating the degradation of SMAD proteins. In addition, TNF induces DKK1 and SOST expression which are inhibitors of WNT signaling. The negative effects of pro-inflammatory cytokines on osteoblasts include IL6-mediated impairment of osteoclast differentiation through JAK-STAT pathway.    Outcome effects: The cytokines activated during the course of inflammatory response have profound effects on the differentiation and activity of osteoblasts by affecting cell signaling.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

