xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is a protein hormone synthesized, processed, and secreted by the parathyroid chief cells in response to changes in serum ionized calcium (Ca2+) levels. The reduction in serum Ca2+ levels triggers the secretion of PTH, whereas, a rise in vitamin D (calcitriol) levels suppresses PTH secretion. Insufficient Ca2+ intake and lack of calcitriol lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which increases the rate of bone remodeling in order to to maintain a normal serum Ca2+ level.    Signaling description: The mechanism of bone resorption mediated by excessive PTH action involves the imbalance of the TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio. Both ligands are produced by osteoblasts, and have the opposite functions. TNFSF11 induces osteoclast activation through its signaling, while TNFRSF11B terminates bone resorption by blocking TNFSF11. PTH causes CREB1 phosphorylation through PKA activation by promoting its binding to the promoter region of the TNFSF11 gene. At the same time, PTH down-regulates TNFRSF11B expression by an unknown mechanism.    Outcome effects: The imbalance of the TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio causes increased osteoclast activation and bone loss.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Kennedy P, Jain A, Srinivasan R, Chaudhry S (2010) Calcium homeostasis and osteoporosis. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol.5 Suppl 1:S23-30."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is a protein hormone synthesized, processed, and secreted by the parathyroid chief cells in response to changes in serum ionized calcium (Ca2+) levels. The reduction in serum Ca2+ levels triggers the secretion of PTH, whereas, a rise in vitamin D (calcitriol) levels suppresses PTH secretion. Insufficient Ca2+ intake and lack of calcitriol lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which increases the rate of bone remodeling in order to to maintain a normal serum Ca2+ level.    Signaling description: The mechanism of bone resorption mediated by excessive PTH action involves the imbalance of the TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio. Both ligands are produced by osteoblasts, and have the opposite functions. TNFSF11 induces osteoclast activation through its signaling, while TNFRSF11B terminates bone resorption by blocking TNFSF11. PTH causes CREB1 phosphorylation through PKA activation by promoting its binding to the promoter region of the TNFSF11 gene. At the same time, PTH down-regulates TNFRSF11B expression by an unknown mechanism.    Outcome effects: The imbalance of the TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio causes increased osteoclast activation and bone loss.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    References: 1). Kennedy P, Jain A, Srinivasan R, Chaudhry S (2010) Calcium homeostasis and osteoporosis. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol.5 Suppl 1:S23-30." EXACT []

