xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Following the menopause, risk of bone loss is increased due to lower levels of estrogen. Postmenopausal osteoporosis is characterized by osteoclast activation.    Signaling description: In postmenopause, the deficiency of estrogens leads to insufficient TNFRSF11B expression. Normally, estrogens prevent bone loss by blocking the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF by bone marrow and bone cells. Therefore, the lack of estrogens in postmenopausal women promotes a decrease of TNFRSF11B, and increase of the TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio. TNFSF11 also known as RANKL, initiates osteoclast differentiation by binding to TNFRSF11A receptor on the osteoclast surface. TNF affects the same pathways through its receptors (TNFRSF1A and TNFRSF1B). In mature osteoclasts, the signaling causes CTSK and MMP13 expression.    Outcome effects: The signaling from TNFSF11 involves TRAF6, TRAF2, and TRAF5 proteins, affects NF-kB and MAPK pathways and eventually triggers osteoclast activation (bone resorption). In addition, TNFSF11 promotes osteoclast survival through the AKT1 pathway. Activation of CTSK and MMP13 expression leads to bone resorption by osteoclasts. Normally, calcitonins inhibit osteoclast-mediated bone resorption through calcitonin receptors (CALCR). Polymorphisms in CALCR may contribute to the pathology.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Following the menopause, risk of bone loss is increased due to lower levels of estrogen. Postmenopausal osteoporosis is characterized by osteoclast activation.    Signaling description: In postmenopause, the deficiency of estrogens leads to insufficient TNFRSF11B expression. Normally, estrogens prevent bone loss by blocking the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF by bone marrow and bone cells. Therefore, the lack of estrogens in postmenopausal women promotes a decrease of TNFRSF11B, and increase of the TNFSF11/TNFRSF11B ratio. TNFSF11 also known as RANKL, initiates osteoclast differentiation by binding to TNFRSF11A receptor on the osteoclast surface. TNF affects the same pathways through its receptors (TNFRSF1A and TNFRSF1B). In mature osteoclasts, the signaling causes CTSK and MMP13 expression.    Outcome effects: The signaling from TNFSF11 involves TRAF6, TRAF2, and TRAF5 proteins, affects NF-kB and MAPK pathways and eventually triggers osteoclast activation (bone resorption). In addition, TNFSF11 promotes osteoclast survival through the AKT1 pathway. Activation of CTSK and MMP13 expression leads to bone resorption by osteoclasts. Normally, calcitonins inhibit osteoclast-mediated bone resorption through calcitonin receptors (CALCR). Polymorphisms in CALCR may contribute to the pathology.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

