xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Excessive bone resorption by osteoclasts is one of the fundamental pathological processes in osteoporosis.    Signaling description: Osteoclast-mediated bone resorption occurs via ruffled-border membrane and can be divided into two steps: demineralization by secretion of protons onto the bone surface and proteolytic resorption of exposed organic matrix primarily composed by collagen type I. Bone surface undergoes demineralization due to increased proton secretion by activated osteoclasts. Protons are formed within osteoclasts as a result of the dissociation of carbonic acid produced by CA2 (carbonic anhydrase 2) from CO2 and H2O and pumped out by V-ATPase. In addition, CTSK elevation occurs in osteoporosis. TNF, IFNG, TNFSF11, and the decrease of estrogens provide increased expression of the protein.    Outcome effects: Protons on the bone surface provide the low pH required for the activation and function of CTSK and also promotes the release of Ca2+ from hydroxylapatite. CTSK degrades collagen type I in the telopeptide regions and cleaves the triple helical domains at multiple sites. The degradation of bone matrix and demineralization causes bone loss in osteoporosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Osteoporosis is a disease characterized by loss of bone strength which increases the risk of fragility fractures. Excessive bone resorption by osteoclasts is one of the fundamental pathological processes in osteoporosis.    Signaling description: Osteoclast-mediated bone resorption occurs via ruffled-border membrane and can be divided into two steps: demineralization by secretion of protons onto the bone surface and proteolytic resorption of exposed organic matrix primarily composed by collagen type I. Bone surface undergoes demineralization due to increased proton secretion by activated osteoclasts. Protons are formed within osteoclasts as a result of the dissociation of carbonic acid produced by CA2 (carbonic anhydrase 2) from CO2 and H2O and pumped out by V-ATPase. In addition, CTSK elevation occurs in osteoporosis. TNF, IFNG, TNFSF11, and the decrease of estrogens provide increased expression of the protein.    Outcome effects: Protons on the bone surface provide the low pH required for the activation and function of CTSK and also promotes the release of Ca2+ from hydroxylapatite. CTSK degrades collagen type I in the telopeptide regions and cleaves the triple helical domains at multiple sites. The degradation of bone matrix and demineralization causes bone loss in osteoporosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue.    Mutated genes: Mutated gene is shown in white-out style." EXACT []

