xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Telomerase (TERT) is an enzyme expressed during the embryonic development that catalyzes the addition of telomere repeats to chromosome ends. Most neoplastic cells de-repress telomerase expression to support immortalization and tumor formation, although some neoplastic cells use telomere recombination or the mechanism of alternative lengthening of telomeres (ALT).    Signaling description: There exist multiple mechanisms of TERT re-activation. A number of transcription factors over-expressed in different tumors are responsible for TERT expression. These include MYC, SP1, STAT3, CTNNB2 and many others. Different oncogenic viral proteins participate in direct or indirect TERT re-activation, including HTLV-1 protein tax, EBV protein LMP1, HPV proteins HPV16-E6/7, KSHV protein ORF73, HBV protein HBx, HCMV protein UL123. In rare cancer cases, TERT may also have activating mutations.    Outcome effects: TERT catalyzes the addition of telomere repeats to chromosome ends supporting cancer cell immortalization.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from experiments on animals.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Telomerase (TERT) is an enzyme expressed during the embryonic development that catalyzes the addition of telomere repeats to chromosome ends. Most neoplastic cells de-repress telomerase expression to support immortalization and tumor formation, although some neoplastic cells use telomere recombination or the mechanism of alternative lengthening of telomeres (ALT).    Signaling description: There exist multiple mechanisms of TERT re-activation. A number of transcription factors over-expressed in different tumors are responsible for TERT expression. These include MYC, SP1, STAT3, CTNNB2 and many others. Different oncogenic viral proteins participate in direct or indirect TERT re-activation, including HTLV-1 protein tax, EBV protein LMP1, HPV proteins HPV16-E6/7, KSHV protein ORF73, HBV protein HBx, HCMV protein UL123. In rare cancer cases, TERT may also have activating mutations.    Outcome effects: TERT catalyzes the addition of telomere repeats to chromosome ends supporting cancer cell immortalization.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from experiments on animals.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details." EXACT []

