xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Telomeres regulate cellular lifespan by maintaining genomic through the protection of chromosomal termini. The maintenance of telomere length is essential for neoplastic cells after they acquire mutations that disrupt cell cycle control regulation and, thus, increase cellular proliferation. Telomere length maintenance is achieved by cancerous cells through the expression of the telomerase enzyme or via alternative lengthening of telomeres (ALT), which is a telomerase-independent mechanism.    Signaling description: The precise mechanism of ALT is poorly understood. One of the hypothesis is the following. ALT relies on TERT (Telomerase Reverse Transcriptase) expression inhibition, and E2F1 and TCEAL7 probably have a role in TERT inactivation. ALT requires permissive mutations that involve p53, ATRX, DAXX and H3F3A. The ATRX-DAXX complex is required for chromatin deposition in histone H3.3, a histone variant associated with transcriptionally active open chromatin, transcription factor binding sites and telomeres. Mutations in H3F3A, encoding H3.3, may affect telomere dynamics by altering histone incorporation throughout the genome with subsequent changes in chromatin remodeling and gene expression. Loss of ATRX or DAXX would limit H3.3 incorporation into telomeric chromatin, which in turn disrupts telomeric heterochromatin and facilitates telomere recombination. Impaired p53 may be also associated with ALT activation.    Outcome effects: ALT promotes maintenance of telomere length in a TERT-independent manner.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Telomeres regulate cellular lifespan by maintaining genomic through the protection of chromosomal termini. The maintenance of telomere length is essential for neoplastic cells after they acquire mutations that disrupt cell cycle control regulation and, thus, increase cellular proliferation. Telomere length maintenance is achieved by cancerous cells through the expression of the telomerase enzyme or via alternative lengthening of telomeres (ALT), which is a telomerase-independent mechanism.    Signaling description: The precise mechanism of ALT is poorly understood. One of the hypothesis is the following. ALT relies on TERT (Telomerase Reverse Transcriptase) expression inhibition, and E2F1 and TCEAL7 probably have a role in TERT inactivation. ALT requires permissive mutations that involve p53, ATRX, DAXX and H3F3A. The ATRX-DAXX complex is required for chromatin deposition in histone H3.3, a histone variant associated with transcriptionally active open chromatin, transcription factor binding sites and telomeres. Mutations in H3F3A, encoding H3.3, may affect telomere dynamics by altering histone incorporation throughout the genome with subsequent changes in chromatin remodeling and gene expression. Loss of ATRX or DAXX would limit H3.3 incorporation into telomeric chromatin, which in turn disrupts telomeric heterochromatin and facilitates telomere recombination. Impaired p53 may be also associated with ALT activation.    Outcome effects: ALT promotes maintenance of telomere length in a TERT-independent manner.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details." EXACT []

