xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The interaction of TERT with WNT signaling facilitates cancer progression by promoting the epithelial-mesenchymal transition and inducing the stem cell phenotype.    Signaling description: In the canonical WNT signaling, WNT binds FZD and LRP5/6, which activates DVL. In turn, DVL inhibits the GSK3B, AXIN1 and APC containing complex, allowing CTNNB1 to be able to enter the nucleus and activate WNT target genes. A direct interplay between TERT and WNT pathway was shown recently with a positive feedback loop between TERT and CTNNB1. TERT forms a complex with the WNT-pathway transcription factor SMARCA4 and enhances the transcription of WNT target genes. CTNNB1 could directly bind the promoter of TERT and upregulate it. Moreover, MYC and KLF4, which are WNT/CTNNB1 target genes, regulate the transcription of TERT.    Outcome effects: At the end, the interplay between TERT- CTNNB1 facilitates cancer progression by promoting the epithelial-mesenchymal transition and inducing the stem cell phenotype.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from experiments on animals.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The interaction of TERT with WNT signaling facilitates cancer progression by promoting the epithelial-mesenchymal transition and inducing the stem cell phenotype.    Signaling description: In the canonical WNT signaling, WNT binds FZD and LRP5/6, which activates DVL. In turn, DVL inhibits the GSK3B, AXIN1 and APC containing complex, allowing CTNNB1 to be able to enter the nucleus and activate WNT target genes. A direct interplay between TERT and WNT pathway was shown recently with a positive feedback loop between TERT and CTNNB1. TERT forms a complex with the WNT-pathway transcription factor SMARCA4 and enhances the transcription of WNT target genes. CTNNB1 could directly bind the promoter of TERT and upregulate it. Moreover, MYC and KLF4, which are WNT/CTNNB1 target genes, regulate the transcription of TERT.    Outcome effects: At the end, the interplay between TERT- CTNNB1 facilitates cancer progression by promoting the epithelial-mesenchymal transition and inducing the stem cell phenotype.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from experiments on animals.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Enabling of Replicative Immortality\" for details." EXACT []

