xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: FOXP3+ Regulatory T cells (Tregs) represent an important component of the immunosuppressive circuitry of the host and it's activation is involved in cancer cell immune escape.     Signaling description: TGFB1/SMAD signaling has a particularly important role in differentiation and activation of Tregs. IL-2 facilitates the induction of FOXP3 via STAT5A. The activation of T-cell receptor induces the NFKB and NFATC signaling. Following cell contact, Tregs may kill the immune cell through PRF1/GZMB or deliver a negative signal via different mechanisms. Tregs express ENTPD1 (CD39) and NT5E (CD73) generating pericellular adenosine and inhibiting the effector T-cell functions. Tregs express CTLA4 stimulating dendritic cells to produce the immunosuppressive enzyme IDO1 via interaction with CD80 and CD86 (see pathway \"IDO1 in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details). Production of TNFSF10 (TRIAL) and LGALS1 by Tregs potentiate cell cycle arrest and apoptosis in NK cells, dendritic cells and effector T-cells. Tregs produce IL10 and TGFB1 that have an immunosuppressive effect.     Outcome effects: Tregs inhibit or induce apoptosis of effector T-cells, dendritic cells, NK cells and some other immune cells.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: FOXP3+ Regulatory T cells (Tregs) represent an important component of the immunosuppressive circuitry of the host and it's activation is involved in cancer cell immune escape.     Signaling description: TGFB1/SMAD signaling has a particularly important role in differentiation and activation of Tregs. IL-2 facilitates the induction of FOXP3 via STAT5A. The activation of T-cell receptor induces the NFKB and NFATC signaling. Following cell contact, Tregs may kill the immune cell through PRF1/GZMB or deliver a negative signal via different mechanisms. Tregs express ENTPD1 (CD39) and NT5E (CD73) generating pericellular adenosine and inhibiting the effector T-cell functions. Tregs express CTLA4 stimulating dendritic cells to produce the immunosuppressive enzyme IDO1 via interaction with CD80 and CD86 (see pathway \"IDO1 in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details). Production of TNFSF10 (TRIAL) and LGALS1 by Tregs potentiate cell cycle arrest and apoptosis in NK cells, dendritic cells and effector T-cells. Tregs produce IL10 and TGFB1 that have an immunosuppressive effect.     Outcome effects: Tregs inhibit or induce apoptosis of effector T-cells, dendritic cells, NK cells and some other immune cells.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details." EXACT []

