xref: PS:Source "Pathological Process"
synonym: "Source: Pathological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) represent the major inflammatory component of the stroma of many tumors, and can affect different aspects of the neoplastic tissue. TAMs express several M2-associated pro-tumoral functions, including promotion of angiogenesis, matrix remodeling and suppression of adaptive immunity.     Signaling description: TAMs are recruited to the tumor, activated and switched to the M2 phenotype via several effectors, including IL10, IL13, IL4, IL1B, TGFB1, CCL2/5, VEGFA, CSF1 and others. The corresponding signaling routes are activated in TAMs for the induction of transcription factors NF-kB, STAT3/6, HIF1A and some others (not shown on the pathway). TAMs produce a number of proteolytic molecules, such as plasmin, PLAU CTSB/S and matrix metalloproteases (MMPs) which may remodel the extracellular matrix and potentiate tumor cell invasion. TAMs promote tumor angiogenesis via the expression of VEGFA/C, PDGFB, TYMP, CCL18, CXCL8 which are directly or indirectly involved in new vessel formation and sprouting. TAMs express immunosuppressive molecules CD274, VTCN1, CD276 which inhibit T-cell activation. TAMs also cause immunosuppression via the production of IL10, IL13, TGFB1, CCL22. They express ARG1 that catabolizes arginine, deplete their environment of arginine and limit arginine availability to T-cells. TAMs also express IDO1 that produces kynurenine, inhibiting T-cell activation and proliferation and inducing apoptosis of T-cells. TAMs potentiate tumor cell proliferation producing EGF, FGF2, IL-6, PDGFB and some other molecules.     Outcome effects: TAMs promote tumor immune escape, tumor cell proliferation, angiogenesis and tumor invasion."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) represent the major inflammatory component of the stroma of many tumors, and can affect different aspects of the neoplastic tissue. TAMs express several M2-associated pro-tumoral functions, including promotion of angiogenesis, matrix remodeling and suppression of adaptive immunity.     Signaling description: TAMs are recruited to the tumor, activated and switched to the M2 phenotype via several effectors, including IL10, IL13, IL4, IL1B, TGFB1, CCL2/5, VEGFA, CSF1 and others. The corresponding signaling routes are activated in TAMs for the induction of transcription factors NF-kB, STAT3/6, HIF1A and some others (not shown on the pathway). TAMs produce a number of proteolytic molecules, such as plasmin, PLAU CTSB/S and matrix metalloproteases (MMPs) which may remodel the extracellular matrix and potentiate tumor cell invasion. TAMs promote tumor angiogenesis via the expression of VEGFA/C, PDGFB, TYMP, CCL18, CXCL8 which are directly or indirectly involved in new vessel formation and sprouting. TAMs express immunosuppressive molecules CD274, VTCN1, CD276 which inhibit T-cell activation. TAMs also cause immunosuppression via the production of IL10, IL13, TGFB1, CCL22. They express ARG1 that catabolizes arginine, deplete their environment of arginine and limit arginine availability to T-cells. TAMs also express IDO1 that produces kynurenine, inhibiting T-cell activation and proliferation and inducing apoptosis of T-cells. TAMs potentiate tumor cell proliferation producing EGF, FGF2, IL-6, PDGFB and some other molecules.     Outcome effects: TAMs promote tumor immune escape, tumor cell proliferation, angiogenesis and tumor invasion." EXACT []

