xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Loss of MHC class I antigens or an impairment of antigen presentation due to mutations/decreased expression of proteasome subunits allow tumor cells to evade immune recognition.     Signaling description: Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) I is a cell surface molecule responsible for the transmission of intracellular antigens. The process of MHC1-mediated antigen presentation is initiated when cells take over antigens and degrade them into peptides by the proteasome. The peptides are then transported into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) by TAP1 and TAP2. The chaperones, calnexin (CANX) and calreticulin (CALR) bind to MHCI and the peptide complex assembly. Other chaperones maintain MHC I as it binds to the TAP complex via tapasin (TAPBP). Subsequently, MHC I and the peptide complex migrate through Golgi vesicles to the outer cell membrane. Cell surface MHC I and the peptide complex, when connected with CD8 and NK cells, activate their differentiation and functions. The loss of MHC I antigens is one of the ways to evade immune recognition. Total loss of MHC I may be a result of structural changes in the beta2-microglobulin (B2M) gene resulting from mutations, deletion or loss of heterozygosity. MHC I inactivation may also result from HLA-A and HLA-B mutation or methylation. Impaired antigen presentation can also be caused by mutations/decreased expression of proteasome subunits PSMB9 and PSMB8, peptide transporters TAP1, and TAP2 as well as TAPBP chaperone. NLRC5, regulating the expression of B2M, TAP1 and PSMB9, may be mutated in cancers. HLA-G is a \"nonclassical\" major histocompatibility complex class Ib gene. The HLA-G presents immunomodulatory functions essential in pregnancy for the tolerance of the semi-allogenic fetus, but an abnormal expression of HLA-G has been observed in numerous pathological conditions, including tumors. HLA-G may be overexpressed in cancers and inhibits the activation of NK cells via interaction with inhibitory receptors LILRB1, LILRB2, and KIR2DL4.     Outcome effects: Due to lack of HLA I, tumor antigens are not presented to NK cells and cytotoxic T-cells, and these cells are unbale to eliminate tumor cells.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Loss of MHC class I antigens or an impairment of antigen presentation due to mutations/decreased expression of proteasome subunits allow tumor cells to evade immune recognition.     Signaling description: Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) I is a cell surface molecule responsible for the transmission of intracellular antigens. The process of MHC1-mediated antigen presentation is initiated when cells take over antigens and degrade them into peptides by the proteasome. The peptides are then transported into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) by TAP1 and TAP2. The chaperones, calnexin (CANX) and calreticulin (CALR) bind to MHCI and the peptide complex assembly. Other chaperones maintain MHC I as it binds to the TAP complex via tapasin (TAPBP). Subsequently, MHC I and the peptide complex migrate through Golgi vesicles to the outer cell membrane. Cell surface MHC I and the peptide complex, when connected with CD8 and NK cells, activate their differentiation and functions. The loss of MHC I antigens is one of the ways to evade immune recognition. Total loss of MHC I may be a result of structural changes in the beta2-microglobulin (B2M) gene resulting from mutations, deletion or loss of heterozygosity. MHC I inactivation may also result from HLA-A and HLA-B mutation or methylation. Impaired antigen presentation can also be caused by mutations/decreased expression of proteasome subunits PSMB9 and PSMB8, peptide transporters TAP1, and TAP2 as well as TAPBP chaperone. NLRC5, regulating the expression of B2M, TAP1 and PSMB9, may be mutated in cancers. HLA-G is a \"nonclassical\" major histocompatibility complex class Ib gene. The HLA-G presents immunomodulatory functions essential in pregnancy for the tolerance of the semi-allogenic fetus, but an abnormal expression of HLA-G has been observed in numerous pathological conditions, including tumors. HLA-G may be overexpressed in cancers and inhibits the activation of NK cells via interaction with inhibitory receptors LILRB1, LILRB2, and KIR2DL4.     Outcome effects: Due to lack of HLA I, tumor antigens are not presented to NK cells and cytotoxic T-cells, and these cells are unbale to eliminate tumor cells.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details." EXACT []

