xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Myeloid-derived suppressor cells (MDSCs) are a heterogeneous population of cells of myeloid origin that consists of myeloid progenitors and immature macrophages, immature granulocytes and immature dendritic cells that expands during cancer, inflammation and infection, and has a remarkable ability to suppress the activation of T-cells and natural killer (NK) cells.     Signaling description: MDSCs use multiple mechanisms for T-cells inactivation, involving transport and metabolism of amino acids, production of ROS and NO and others. MDSCs produce high levels of intracellular ARG1 that catabolizes arginine and import large amounts of arginine through their SLC7A2 transporter. Consequently, they deplete their environment of arginine and limit arginine availability to T-cells. As a result, T-cells deprived of arginine are CD3z chain (CD247) deficient and arrested in the G0-G1 phase of the cell cycle (CDK4 and CCND3 are inhibited). ARG1 expression may be up-regulated by STAT3, STAT1 and STAT6. MDSCs sequestrate cysteine, an amino acid that T-cells are unable to synthesize de novo and that they require for activation. MDSCs import cysteine through SLC3A2 and SLC7A11 and they do not have any transporter for cysteine export. NOS2 is upregulated by STAT in MDSCs, leading to hyper-production of NO which suppresses T-cell function through a variety of mechanisms that involve the inhibition of JAK3 and STAT5 in T-cells, inhibition of MHC class II expression and the induction of T-cell apoptosis. STAT3 contributes to up-regulation of ROS. S100A8 and S100A9 directly bind to NCF1/2, which are crucial components of the NADPH complex. This binding potentiates NADPH oxidase activation in MDSCs, which causes increased production of ROS, leading to the observed suppressive effects on T-cells. MDSCs mediate down-regulation of L-selectin (SELL) via inhibitory effect of ADAM17. T-cells must have a SELL-high phenotype to home to lymph nodes and inflammatory sites where they encounter antigen and are activated. By down-regulating SELL on T-cells, MDSCs perturb the T-cell trafficking patterns and thereby inhibit T-cell activation. MDSCs cleave L-selectin because they constitutively express ADAM17 at their cell surface. In addition to inhibiting T-cell activation, MDSCs also affect the antitumor immunity by perturbing the innate immunity through their interactions with macrophages and natural killer (NK) cells. M1 or classically activated macrophages promote tumor regression; however, in the presence of MDSCs, macrophages are converted to an M2 or alternatively activated phenotype which enhance tumor progression. This re-polarization is a result of the cross-talk between MDSCs and macrophages which increases the production of IL10 by MDSCs and decreases macrophage production of IL12 also necessary for NK cell activation. MDSCs have an ability to promote the de novo development of FOXP3+ regulatory T (TReg) cells. The induction of TReg cells by MDSCs was found to require the activation of tumor-specific T-cells and the presence of IFNG and IL10.     Outcome effects: MDSCs directly or indirectly inactivate T-cells (CD4+ and CD8+) and NK cells and lead to conversion of macrophages to the M2 phenotype. Thereby MDSCs inhibit the immune cells with antitumor functions and promote activation of pro-tumor cells.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Myeloid-derived suppressor cells (MDSCs) are a heterogeneous population of cells of myeloid origin that consists of myeloid progenitors and immature macrophages, immature granulocytes and immature dendritic cells that expands during cancer, inflammation and infection, and has a remarkable ability to suppress the activation of T-cells and natural killer (NK) cells.     Signaling description: MDSCs use multiple mechanisms for T-cells inactivation, involving transport and metabolism of amino acids, production of ROS and NO and others. MDSCs produce high levels of intracellular ARG1 that catabolizes arginine and import large amounts of arginine through their SLC7A2 transporter. Consequently, they deplete their environment of arginine and limit arginine availability to T-cells. As a result, T-cells deprived of arginine are CD3z chain (CD247) deficient and arrested in the G0-G1 phase of the cell cycle (CDK4 and CCND3 are inhibited). ARG1 expression may be up-regulated by STAT3, STAT1 and STAT6. MDSCs sequestrate cysteine, an amino acid that T-cells are unable to synthesize de novo and that they require for activation. MDSCs import cysteine through SLC3A2 and SLC7A11 and they do not have any transporter for cysteine export. NOS2 is upregulated by STAT in MDSCs, leading to hyper-production of NO which suppresses T-cell function through a variety of mechanisms that involve the inhibition of JAK3 and STAT5 in T-cells, inhibition of MHC class II expression and the induction of T-cell apoptosis. STAT3 contributes to up-regulation of ROS. S100A8 and S100A9 directly bind to NCF1/2, which are crucial components of the NADPH complex. This binding potentiates NADPH oxidase activation in MDSCs, which causes increased production of ROS, leading to the observed suppressive effects on T-cells. MDSCs mediate down-regulation of L-selectin (SELL) via inhibitory effect of ADAM17. T-cells must have a SELL-high phenotype to home to lymph nodes and inflammatory sites where they encounter antigen and are activated. By down-regulating SELL on T-cells, MDSCs perturb the T-cell trafficking patterns and thereby inhibit T-cell activation. MDSCs cleave L-selectin because they constitutively express ADAM17 at their cell surface. In addition to inhibiting T-cell activation, MDSCs also affect the antitumor immunity by perturbing the innate immunity through their interactions with macrophages and natural killer (NK) cells. M1 or classically activated macrophages promote tumor regression; however, in the presence of MDSCs, macrophages are converted to an M2 or alternatively activated phenotype which enhance tumor progression. This re-polarization is a result of the cross-talk between MDSCs and macrophages which increases the production of IL10 by MDSCs and decreases macrophage production of IL12 also necessary for NK cell activation. MDSCs have an ability to promote the de novo development of FOXP3+ regulatory T (TReg) cells. The induction of TReg cells by MDSCs was found to require the activation of tumor-specific T-cells and the presence of IFNG and IL10.     Outcome effects: MDSCs directly or indirectly inactivate T-cells (CD4+ and CD8+) and NK cells and lead to conversion of macrophages to the M2 phenotype. Thereby MDSCs inhibit the immune cells with antitumor functions and promote activation of pro-tumor cells.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details." EXACT []

