xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: IDO1 is an enzyme involved in catalyzing tryptophan to kynurenine. Tryptophan depletion and an excess of kynurenine lead to immunosuppression (inhibition of CD4+ T-cells, CD8+ T-cells, NK cells and activation of Treg cells and myeloid-derived suppressor cells). IDO is strongly up-regulated in tumor cells, tumor stromal cells and immune cells, particularly in dendritic cells (DC).     Signaling description: IFNG, IL6, IL1B, TNF and DAMP (ex., HMGB1) activate IDO1 transcription mainly through NFKB and JAK/STAT pathways. In regulatory DC, IDO1 is up-regulated by the reverse signaling from Treg co-regulatory receptors, such as CTLA4 and TNFRSF18 (GITR). CD80/CD86 activates the PI3K/AKT pathway and canonical NF-kappaB pathway. The CD80/CD86-induced PI3K signaling is also affected by a cross-talk with the NOTCH1 signaling. This cross-talk is facilitated by a NOTCH-mediated up-regulation of the expression PIN1, which in turn increases the enzyme activity of casein kinase II. \"Back-signaling\" from CTLA-4 to CD80/CD86 can also be mediated via FOXO3. CD200 and CD40LG also participate in IDO1 activation through NFKB signaling. It is not known yet whether it is the reverse or direct signaling. IDO1 hyperactivity leads to local tryptophan depletion, inhibition of MTOR and activation of EIF2AK4. Kynurenine is produced as a result of IDO-mediated tryptophan catabolism and interacts with AHR. Another tryptophan catabolizing enzyme, TDO2, is also important in driving cancer growth.     Outcome effects: In CD8+ T cells, the IDO1-induced activation of EIF2AK4 leads to cell-cycle arrest and functional anergy. In CD4+ T cells, the activation of EIF2AK4 blocks TH17 differentiation and promotes de novo Treg differentiation and activation of functional suppressor activity in mature Tregs. Overall, EIF2AK4 appears to enhance Treg activity, and inhibit T-effector cells. The depletion of tryptophan and kynurenine excess also induce the inhibition of NK cells and activation of myeloid-derived suppressor cell.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: IDO1 is an enzyme involved in catalyzing tryptophan to kynurenine. Tryptophan depletion and an excess of kynurenine lead to immunosuppression (inhibition of CD4+ T-cells, CD8+ T-cells, NK cells and activation of Treg cells and myeloid-derived suppressor cells). IDO is strongly up-regulated in tumor cells, tumor stromal cells and immune cells, particularly in dendritic cells (DC).     Signaling description: IFNG, IL6, IL1B, TNF and DAMP (ex., HMGB1) activate IDO1 transcription mainly through NFKB and JAK/STAT pathways. In regulatory DC, IDO1 is up-regulated by the reverse signaling from Treg co-regulatory receptors, such as CTLA4 and TNFRSF18 (GITR). CD80/CD86 activates the PI3K/AKT pathway and canonical NF-kappaB pathway. The CD80/CD86-induced PI3K signaling is also affected by a cross-talk with the NOTCH1 signaling. This cross-talk is facilitated by a NOTCH-mediated up-regulation of the expression PIN1, which in turn increases the enzyme activity of casein kinase II. \"Back-signaling\" from CTLA-4 to CD80/CD86 can also be mediated via FOXO3. CD200 and CD40LG also participate in IDO1 activation through NFKB signaling. It is not known yet whether it is the reverse or direct signaling. IDO1 hyperactivity leads to local tryptophan depletion, inhibition of MTOR and activation of EIF2AK4. Kynurenine is produced as a result of IDO-mediated tryptophan catabolism and interacts with AHR. Another tryptophan catabolizing enzyme, TDO2, is also important in driving cancer growth.     Outcome effects: In CD8+ T cells, the IDO1-induced activation of EIF2AK4 leads to cell-cycle arrest and functional anergy. In CD4+ T cells, the activation of EIF2AK4 blocks TH17 differentiation and promotes de novo Treg differentiation and activation of functional suppressor activity in mature Tregs. Overall, EIF2AK4 appears to enhance Treg activity, and inhibit T-effector cells. The depletion of tryptophan and kynurenine excess also induce the inhibition of NK cells and activation of myeloid-derived suppressor cell.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details." EXACT []

