xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: According to the \"two signal\" paradigm, full activation of T-cells in response to antigens requires two separate but complementary signals. Specifically, upon antigen encounter, naive T-cells receive one signal through the interaction of their T-cell receptor with the MHC/antigenic peptide complex on an antigen presenting cell (APC) and another signal through the interaction of a pair of positive costimulatory molecules expressed on the T-cell and APC, respectively, leading to an immune response. At the same time, negative costimulatory signals, downregulate the T-cell immune response. The costimulatory signaling events are not limited to T-cell / APC interactions and also involve T-cell / T-cell, T-cell / B-cell, and T-cell-nonlymphoid cell, including T-cell / cancer cell interactions. The absence of effective co-stimulation can cause T-cell anergy and/or apoptosis.     Signaling description: Tumor cells downregulate the expression of CD80 (B7-1) and CD86 (B7-2) molecules and stimulate the expression of CD274 (B7-H1), CD276 (B7-H3), VTCN1(B7-H4), PDCD1LG2 (B7-DC) molecules in order to inactivate the T- lymphocyte clone. CTLA4 and PDCD1 are expressed on the T-cell surface, they negatively regulate T-cell receptor signaling and may be overexpressed in tumors. One major mechanism of cancer immune escape involves clonal deletion of antigen-reactive T-cells through apoptotic cell death. An important mediator of T-cell deletion is the FASLG/FAS system. Neoplasms may actively destroy T-cells, including cytotoxic T-cells by expressing FASLG. Another tumor-expressed ligand, RCAS1 (EBAG9), was also found to induce apoptotic cell death of T, B, and NK cells expressing its receptor. The precise mechanism of this action is yet to be determined.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: According to the \"two signal\" paradigm, full activation of T-cells in response to antigens requires two separate but complementary signals. Specifically, upon antigen encounter, naive T-cells receive one signal through the interaction of their T-cell receptor with the MHC/antigenic peptide complex on an antigen presenting cell (APC) and another signal through the interaction of a pair of positive costimulatory molecules expressed on the T-cell and APC, respectively, leading to an immune response. At the same time, negative costimulatory signals, downregulate the T-cell immune response. The costimulatory signaling events are not limited to T-cell / APC interactions and also involve T-cell / T-cell, T-cell / B-cell, and T-cell-nonlymphoid cell, including T-cell / cancer cell interactions. The absence of effective co-stimulation can cause T-cell anergy and/or apoptosis.     Signaling description: Tumor cells downregulate the expression of CD80 (B7-1) and CD86 (B7-2) molecules and stimulate the expression of CD274 (B7-H1), CD276 (B7-H3), VTCN1(B7-H4), PDCD1LG2 (B7-DC) molecules in order to inactivate the T- lymphocyte clone. CTLA4 and PDCD1 are expressed on the T-cell surface, they negatively regulate T-cell receptor signaling and may be overexpressed in tumors. One major mechanism of cancer immune escape involves clonal deletion of antigen-reactive T-cells through apoptotic cell death. An important mediator of T-cell deletion is the FASLG/FAS system. Neoplasms may actively destroy T-cells, including cytotoxic T-cells by expressing FASLG. Another tumor-expressed ligand, RCAS1 (EBAG9), was also found to induce apoptotic cell death of T, B, and NK cells expressing its receptor. The precise mechanism of this action is yet to be determined.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details." EXACT []

