xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Adenosine and cAMP are important for Treg-cell-mediated suppression of CD4+ T-cells and antigen-presenting cells.     Signaling description: High intracellular cAMP levels are generated in Treg cells by FOXP3-mediated downregulation of the PDE3B. Furthermore, FOXP3 also down-regulates MIR-142 targeting ADCY9 resulting in upregulated cAMP production. cAMP is transferred from nTreg cells into activated target CD4+ T cells and/or antigen-presenting cells (APCs) via gap junctions to suppress these cells. cAMP facilitates the expression of CREM transcription factor, resulting in the suppression IL-2 directly or via NFATC1. ENTPD1 (CD39) and NT5E (CD73) ectoenzymes on nTreg cells generate extracellular immunosuppressive adenosine from ATP, which adds to the suppressive effects of inflamed-tissue hypoxic adenosinergic signaling on conventional CD4+ T cells acting via ADORA2A. ADORA2A signaling enhances the levels of intracellular cAMP and enforces nuclear localization of CREM.     Outcome effects: Elevated level of intracellular cAMP down-regulates IL2 production which is necessary for CD4+ T-cell and antigen-presenting cell activation thereby providing an immunosuppressive effect.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Adenosine and cAMP are important for Treg-cell-mediated suppression of CD4+ T-cells and antigen-presenting cells.     Signaling description: High intracellular cAMP levels are generated in Treg cells by FOXP3-mediated downregulation of the PDE3B. Furthermore, FOXP3 also down-regulates MIR-142 targeting ADCY9 resulting in upregulated cAMP production. cAMP is transferred from nTreg cells into activated target CD4+ T cells and/or antigen-presenting cells (APCs) via gap junctions to suppress these cells. cAMP facilitates the expression of CREM transcription factor, resulting in the suppression IL-2 directly or via NFATC1. ENTPD1 (CD39) and NT5E (CD73) ectoenzymes on nTreg cells generate extracellular immunosuppressive adenosine from ATP, which adds to the suppressive effects of inflamed-tissue hypoxic adenosinergic signaling on conventional CD4+ T cells acting via ADORA2A. ADORA2A signaling enhances the levels of intracellular cAMP and enforces nuclear localization of CREM.     Outcome effects: Elevated level of intracellular cAMP down-regulates IL2 production which is necessary for CD4+ T-cell and antigen-presenting cell activation thereby providing an immunosuppressive effect.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Immune Escape\" for details." EXACT []

