xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: RAS proteins control cellular signaling pathways responsible for growth, migration, adhesion, cytoskeletal integrity, survival and differentiation. RASSF family proteins are the effectors of RAS and function as tumor suppressors by regulating the cell cycle and apoptosis.    Signaling description: The inactivation of natural inhibitors of RAS signaling - NF1/2 - by mutations leads to its constant activation. Silencing of RASSF proteins by promoter methylation promotes cancer progression.    Outcome effects: RAS signaling is constantly activated leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation and apoptosis of apoptosis.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: RAS proteins control cellular signaling pathways responsible for growth, migration, adhesion, cytoskeletal integrity, survival and differentiation. RASSF family proteins are the effectors of RAS and function as tumor suppressors by regulating the cell cycle and apoptosis.    Signaling description: The inactivation of natural inhibitors of RAS signaling - NF1/2 - by mutations leads to its constant activation. Silencing of RASSF proteins by promoter methylation promotes cancer progression.    Outcome effects: RAS signaling is constantly activated leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation and apoptosis of apoptosis.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details." EXACT []

