xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The canonical WNT signaling pathway promotes the accumulation of CTNNB1 in the cytoplasm and its eventual translocation into the nucleus to act as a transcriptional coactivator of transcription factors that belong to the TCF/LEF family. The conserved WNT/CTNNB1 pathway regulates stem cell pluripotency and cell fate decisions during embryonic development and is often deregulated in cancer.    Signaling description: First, WNT ligands bind to FZD receptors, leading to the formation of a larger cell surface complex with LRP5/6. The activation of the WNT receptor complex triggers displacement of the multifunctional kinase GSK3B from the regulatory APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex. In the absence of WNT-signal (off-state), CTNNB undergoes coordinated phosphorylation by CSNK1A1 and the APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex leading to its ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. In the presence of WNT ligand (on-state), the LRP5/6 co-receptor is complexed with WNT-bound FZD. This leads to activation of DLV by sequential phosphorylation, poly-ubiquitination and polymerization, which displaces GSK3B from APC/AXIN1 through an undetermined mechanism that may involve substrate trapping and/or endosome sequestration. The stabilized CTNNB1 is translocated to the nucleus, where it binds to LEF1/TCF4 transcription factors displacing co-repressors and recruiting additional co-activators to WNT target genes. Mutations in CDK1, APC, AXIN1 and other mutations have also been documented in tumor samples, underscoring the deregulation of this pathway in cancer. In addition, down-regulation of natural WNT inhibitors SFRPs was described in many cancers.    Outcome effects: WNT pathway is kept in the on-state leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The canonical WNT signaling pathway promotes the accumulation of CTNNB1 in the cytoplasm and its eventual translocation into the nucleus to act as a transcriptional coactivator of transcription factors that belong to the TCF/LEF family. The conserved WNT/CTNNB1 pathway regulates stem cell pluripotency and cell fate decisions during embryonic development and is often deregulated in cancer.    Signaling description: First, WNT ligands bind to FZD receptors, leading to the formation of a larger cell surface complex with LRP5/6. The activation of the WNT receptor complex triggers displacement of the multifunctional kinase GSK3B from the regulatory APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex. In the absence of WNT-signal (off-state), CTNNB undergoes coordinated phosphorylation by CSNK1A1 and the APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex leading to its ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. In the presence of WNT ligand (on-state), the LRP5/6 co-receptor is complexed with WNT-bound FZD. This leads to activation of DLV by sequential phosphorylation, poly-ubiquitination and polymerization, which displaces GSK3B from APC/AXIN1 through an undetermined mechanism that may involve substrate trapping and/or endosome sequestration. The stabilized CTNNB1 is translocated to the nucleus, where it binds to LEF1/TCF4 transcription factors displacing co-repressors and recruiting additional co-activators to WNT target genes. Mutations in CDK1, APC, AXIN1 and other mutations have also been documented in tumor samples, underscoring the deregulation of this pathway in cancer. In addition, down-regulation of natural WNT inhibitors SFRPs was described in many cancers.    Outcome effects: WNT pathway is kept in the on-state leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details." EXACT []

