xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The TGFB signaling pathway is involved in various cellular processes including cell growth, differentiation, apoptosis, cellular homeostasis and other vital cellular functions in adult organism, as well as the developing embryo. TGFB induces tumor-suppressive effects that cancer cells must circumvent in order to transform into malignancies. The role of the TGF-beta pathway as a tumor-promoter or suppressor in cancer cells is still a matter of debate, due to its differential effects at the early and late stages of carcinogenesis.    Signaling description: TGF-beta superfamily ligands bind TGFBR2, which recruits and phosphorylates TGFBR1. Then, TGFBR1 phosphorylates receptor-regulated SMADs. Further, SMADs accumulate in the nucleus where they act as transcription factors and participate in the regulation of expression of target genes. Inactivating mutations of TGFBR1/2 or SMAD2/3/4 impair the tumor suppressor function of TGFB signaling leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: SMADs activate transcription factors leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The TGFB signaling pathway is involved in various cellular processes including cell growth, differentiation, apoptosis, cellular homeostasis and other vital cellular functions in adult organism, as well as the developing embryo. TGFB induces tumor-suppressive effects that cancer cells must circumvent in order to transform into malignancies. The role of the TGF-beta pathway as a tumor-promoter or suppressor in cancer cells is still a matter of debate, due to its differential effects at the early and late stages of carcinogenesis.    Signaling description: TGF-beta superfamily ligands bind TGFBR2, which recruits and phosphorylates TGFBR1. Then, TGFBR1 phosphorylates receptor-regulated SMADs. Further, SMADs accumulate in the nucleus where they act as transcription factors and participate in the regulation of expression of target genes. Inactivating mutations of TGFBR1/2 or SMAD2/3/4 impair the tumor suppressor function of TGFB signaling leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: SMADs activate transcription factors leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details." EXACT []

