xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hippo signaling is an evolutionarily conserved pathway that controls organ size by regulating cell proliferation, apoptosis, and stem cell self-renewal. Dysregulation of the Hippo pathway contributes to cancer development.    Signaling description: The essential part of Hippo signaling consists of a kinase cascade in which STK3/4 kinases and SAV1 form a complex and phosphorylate LATS1/2 kinases. Next, activated LATS1/2 phosphorylate and inhibit the transcription of YAP1 and WWTR1 co-activators. When dephosphorylated, YAP1/WWTR1 translocate into the nucleus and interact with TEAD, SMAD, RUNX, TP73 and other transcription factors leading to increased expression of genes that up-regulate cell proliferation and down-regulate apoptosis. GNAQ and GNA11-coupled receptor activate YAP and WWTR1 through the RHO-regulated signaling network. Cancer-related GNAQ and GNA11 mutants stimulate LATS inhibition by RHO via actin cytoskeleton reorganization, dephosphorylation and activation of YAP1 and WWTR1. Tumour suppressor adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) inhibits the transcription of YAP1 and WWTR1 and WNT stimulation or loss of APC/AXIN1 causes nuclear localization and activation of YAP1 and WWTR1. Inactivating mutations of tumor suppressors LATS1/2, MOB1A, NF2 and activating mutations/amplifications of YAP1 and WWTR1 are common in cancer.    Outcome effects: The activation of YAP1 / WWTR1 and downstream transcription factors leads to uncontrolled cellular proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hippo signaling is an evolutionarily conserved pathway that controls organ size by regulating cell proliferation, apoptosis, and stem cell self-renewal. Dysregulation of the Hippo pathway contributes to cancer development.    Signaling description: The essential part of Hippo signaling consists of a kinase cascade in which STK3/4 kinases and SAV1 form a complex and phosphorylate LATS1/2 kinases. Next, activated LATS1/2 phosphorylate and inhibit the transcription of YAP1 and WWTR1 co-activators. When dephosphorylated, YAP1/WWTR1 translocate into the nucleus and interact with TEAD, SMAD, RUNX, TP73 and other transcription factors leading to increased expression of genes that up-regulate cell proliferation and down-regulate apoptosis. GNAQ and GNA11-coupled receptor activate YAP and WWTR1 through the RHO-regulated signaling network. Cancer-related GNAQ and GNA11 mutants stimulate LATS inhibition by RHO via actin cytoskeleton reorganization, dephosphorylation and activation of YAP1 and WWTR1. Tumour suppressor adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) inhibits the transcription of YAP1 and WWTR1 and WNT stimulation or loss of APC/AXIN1 causes nuclear localization and activation of YAP1 and WWTR1. Inactivating mutations of tumor suppressors LATS1/2, MOB1A, NF2 and activating mutations/amplifications of YAP1 and WWTR1 are common in cancer.    Outcome effects: The activation of YAP1 / WWTR1 and downstream transcription factors leads to uncontrolled cellular proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details." EXACT []

