xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: NFKB is a protein complex that controls transcription of DNA, cytokine production and cell survival. NFKB plays a key role in regulating the immune response to infection. Incorrect regulation of NFKB has been linked to cancer.    Signaling description: Mammalian NFKB dimers are generated via homodimeric and heterodimeric interactions of 5 REL subunits expressed from distinct but related genes: RELA, RELB, REL, NFKB1 and NFKB2. The canonical NFKB activation pathway results in activation of gene transcription by the RELA/NFKB1 dimer. This heterodimer cycles between the cytoplasm and the nucleus complexed with the inhibitory protein NFKBIA which reduces the efficiency of its nuclear transport and prevents binding of RELA/NFKB1 to DNA. Extracellular stimuli including bacterial LPS and TNFA activate RELA/NFKB1 dimer via a number of signaling steps facilitated by the IKK complex. This complex is comprised of three major components known as IKBKA (CHUK), IKBKB and IKBKG. The general consensus is that the catalytic component IKBKB and the scaffold component IKBKG transduce signals to the canonical pathway. IKBKB catalyzes NFKBIA phosphorylation which leads to its poly-ubiquitination and degradation by the 26S proteasome to generate \"free\" RELA/NFKB1 and transcriptional activation of target genes. NFKB activation is reported to be associated with tumorigenesis. Inactivation of natural NFKB signaling inhibitors NFKBIA, NFKBIE, FAF1, CYLD, TNFAIP3, TRAF3 leads to constant NFKB activation and cancer progression.    Outcome effects: Constant NFKB activation leads to uncontrolled cellular proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and provokes inflammation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: NFKB is a protein complex that controls transcription of DNA, cytokine production and cell survival. NFKB plays a key role in regulating the immune response to infection. Incorrect regulation of NFKB has been linked to cancer.    Signaling description: Mammalian NFKB dimers are generated via homodimeric and heterodimeric interactions of 5 REL subunits expressed from distinct but related genes: RELA, RELB, REL, NFKB1 and NFKB2. The canonical NFKB activation pathway results in activation of gene transcription by the RELA/NFKB1 dimer. This heterodimer cycles between the cytoplasm and the nucleus complexed with the inhibitory protein NFKBIA which reduces the efficiency of its nuclear transport and prevents binding of RELA/NFKB1 to DNA. Extracellular stimuli including bacterial LPS and TNFA activate RELA/NFKB1 dimer via a number of signaling steps facilitated by the IKK complex. This complex is comprised of three major components known as IKBKA (CHUK), IKBKB and IKBKG. The general consensus is that the catalytic component IKBKB and the scaffold component IKBKG transduce signals to the canonical pathway. IKBKB catalyzes NFKBIA phosphorylation which leads to its poly-ubiquitination and degradation by the 26S proteasome to generate \"free\" RELA/NFKB1 and transcriptional activation of target genes. NFKB activation is reported to be associated with tumorigenesis. Inactivation of natural NFKB signaling inhibitors NFKBIA, NFKBIE, FAF1, CYLD, TNFAIP3, TRAF3 leads to constant NFKB activation and cancer progression.    Outcome effects: Constant NFKB activation leads to uncontrolled cellular proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and provokes inflammation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Evading of Growth Suppressors\" for details." EXACT []

