xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The plasminogen activation system is mostly recognized for its fibrinolytic activity and ability to dissolve blood clots, but it is also involved in inflammation. The interaction between coagulation and inflammation is a bidirectional process. Coagulation factors and plasminogen (PLG) are important players in inflammation.    Signaling description: PLG is an inactive precursor of plasmin that is released from the liver. Upon binding to the cell surface, PLG is converted into active plasmin by PLAT, PLAU, KNG1, F12, and others. Fibrin is a cofactor for PLG activation by PLAT. There are several points of plasmin participation in inflammation. PLG influences inflammatory cell migration. Plasmin degrades a variety of matrix proteins (FN1 and laminin complex) and activates certain matrix metalloproteases (MMP1, MMP3, and MMP13) thereby facilitating cell migration through the extracellular matrix (ECM). The contribution of PLG to inflammatory cell recruitment is dependent upon its tethering to the surface of cells. The binding of PLG to cell surfaces is mediated by different receptors, collectively known as PLG-R. For example, ENO1 and ANXA2 are expressed on monocytoid cells and are directly implicated in macrophage recruitment. PLG also participates in the release or processing of various chemokines/cytokines. For instance, PLG triggers complement activation by classical pathway and triggers monocytes to release TGFB1 and leukotriene B4 (LTB4), thereby promoting an inflammatory response. PLG can also trigger intracellular signaling events in macrophages and neutrophils.    Outcome effects: Plasmin may activate multiple signaling pathways including JAK1/TYK2/STAT3, p38 MAPK, and NF-kB pathway. Plasmin also activates MAPK3/MAPK1. Since a functional plasmin receptor has yet to be identified, the exact mechanisms of signaling pathways activated by PLG are not well known. In addition, macrophages share with monocytes the ANXA2/S100A10 heterotetramer, which can act as a signaling plasmin receptor. In contrast to thrombin (F2), which activates F2R and PARD3 and triggers an increase in Ca2+ and inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate in monocytes, plasmin acts in a different manner. Monocytes express F2R, F2RL1, and PARD3, but it has been suggested that F2RL1 is not involved in monocyte activation. The pathway shows the probable mechanisms of transferring signals from receptors to transcription factors. As with LPS, plasmin preferentially induces the activation of IKBKB in monocytes. Plasmin-induced activation of JAK1, p38, MAPK3/MAPK1, and NF-kB is needed for cytokine expression in macrophages. Plasmin triggers monocytes for the rapid expression of TNF, IL1A, IL1B, and TF on the cell membrane. Plasmin also activates NF-kB and JUN/FOS resulting in CCL2 and CCL20 expression that causes monocytes and neutrophils to migrate into inflammatory foci. With NF-kB and JUN/FOS activation, plasmin leads to CD40 expression which is necessary for T-cell activation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The plasminogen activation system is mostly recognized for its fibrinolytic activity and ability to dissolve blood clots, but it is also involved in inflammation. The interaction between coagulation and inflammation is a bidirectional process. Coagulation factors and plasminogen (PLG) are important players in inflammation.    Signaling description: PLG is an inactive precursor of plasmin that is released from the liver. Upon binding to the cell surface, PLG is converted into active plasmin by PLAT, PLAU, KNG1, F12, and others. Fibrin is a cofactor for PLG activation by PLAT. There are several points of plasmin participation in inflammation. PLG influences inflammatory cell migration. Plasmin degrades a variety of matrix proteins (FN1 and laminin complex) and activates certain matrix metalloproteases (MMP1, MMP3, and MMP13) thereby facilitating cell migration through the extracellular matrix (ECM). The contribution of PLG to inflammatory cell recruitment is dependent upon its tethering to the surface of cells. The binding of PLG to cell surfaces is mediated by different receptors, collectively known as PLG-R. For example, ENO1 and ANXA2 are expressed on monocytoid cells and are directly implicated in macrophage recruitment. PLG also participates in the release or processing of various chemokines/cytokines. For instance, PLG triggers complement activation by classical pathway and triggers monocytes to release TGFB1 and leukotriene B4 (LTB4), thereby promoting an inflammatory response. PLG can also trigger intracellular signaling events in macrophages and neutrophils.    Outcome effects: Plasmin may activate multiple signaling pathways including JAK1/TYK2/STAT3, p38 MAPK, and NF-kB pathway. Plasmin also activates MAPK3/MAPK1. Since a functional plasmin receptor has yet to be identified, the exact mechanisms of signaling pathways activated by PLG are not well known. In addition, macrophages share with monocytes the ANXA2/S100A10 heterotetramer, which can act as a signaling plasmin receptor. In contrast to thrombin (F2), which activates F2R and PARD3 and triggers an increase in Ca2+ and inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate in monocytes, plasmin acts in a different manner. Monocytes express F2R, F2RL1, and PARD3, but it has been suggested that F2RL1 is not involved in monocyte activation. The pathway shows the probable mechanisms of transferring signals from receptors to transcription factors. As with LPS, plasmin preferentially induces the activation of IKBKB in monocytes. Plasmin-induced activation of JAK1, p38, MAPK3/MAPK1, and NF-kB is needed for cytokine expression in macrophages. Plasmin triggers monocytes for the rapid expression of TNF, IL1A, IL1B, and TF on the cell membrane. Plasmin also activates NF-kB and JUN/FOS resulting in CCL2 and CCL20 expression that causes monocytes and neutrophils to migrate into inflammatory foci. With NF-kB and JUN/FOS activation, plasmin leads to CD40 expression which is necessary for T-cell activation." EXACT []

