xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The primary physiological function of platelets is to form hemostatic thrombi that prevent blood loss and maintain vascular integrity. Platelet activation and coagulation normally do not occur within an intact blood vessel.    Signaling description: Following vessel wall injury, platelet-plug formation is initiated by the adherence of platelets to subendothelial collagen. Platelet activation is a dynamic process involving several different pathways. Platelet adhesion receptors are the key initiators of platelet activation at sites of vascular injury, where platelets become exposed to adhesive proteins in the matrix or on endothelial cells. Initial platelet adhesion requires the binding of immobilized VWF from endothelial cells to its platelet receptor, GP1BA. Collagen mediates platelet activation signaling through glycoprotein GP6 and integrin ITGA2B (a2ß1). Fibrinogen also induces platelet activation. All of the major platelet adhesion receptors involve Src family kinases, PI3K, and immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motif (ITAM) signaling pathway. ITAM phosphorylation leads to the binding and activation of the tyrosine kinase SYK, which phosphorylates downstream targets such as LAT. This induces the formation of a signaling complex including LAT, LCP2, and BTK, which further activates PLCG2 leading to TXA2 synthesis, granule secretion, and integrin activation.    Outcome effects: A common platelet response to agonists is the secretion of granule contents. Platelets contain three major types of granules including alpha-granules (containing adhesion proteins, coagulation and fibrinolytic factors, cytokines, growth factors, and adhesion receptors), dense granules (containing nucleotides like ADP, ATP and GTP, serotonin, histamine, pyrophosphates, divalent cations, etc.), and lysosomes. There are multiple pathways that are important for stimulating granule secretion including calcium signaling, PKC-dependent phosphorylation and regulation of SNAP receptor complexes, signaling via small GTPases such as RAC1 and RHOA, the PI3K/AKT1 signaling pathway, and the signaling pathways of MAPK isoforms p38, integrin activation, the NO/cGMP/PKG pathway (not shown), and others. Finally, platelet activation contributes to the inflammatory response by the release of proinflammatory mediator, CD40LG inducing F3 synthesis and increasing IL6 and IL8 levels."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The primary physiological function of platelets is to form hemostatic thrombi that prevent blood loss and maintain vascular integrity. Platelet activation and coagulation normally do not occur within an intact blood vessel.    Signaling description: Following vessel wall injury, platelet-plug formation is initiated by the adherence of platelets to subendothelial collagen. Platelet activation is a dynamic process involving several different pathways. Platelet adhesion receptors are the key initiators of platelet activation at sites of vascular injury, where platelets become exposed to adhesive proteins in the matrix or on endothelial cells. Initial platelet adhesion requires the binding of immobilized VWF from endothelial cells to its platelet receptor, GP1BA. Collagen mediates platelet activation signaling through glycoprotein GP6 and integrin ITGA2B (a2ß1). Fibrinogen also induces platelet activation. All of the major platelet adhesion receptors involve Src family kinases, PI3K, and immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motif (ITAM) signaling pathway. ITAM phosphorylation leads to the binding and activation of the tyrosine kinase SYK, which phosphorylates downstream targets such as LAT. This induces the formation of a signaling complex including LAT, LCP2, and BTK, which further activates PLCG2 leading to TXA2 synthesis, granule secretion, and integrin activation.    Outcome effects: A common platelet response to agonists is the secretion of granule contents. Platelets contain three major types of granules including alpha-granules (containing adhesion proteins, coagulation and fibrinolytic factors, cytokines, growth factors, and adhesion receptors), dense granules (containing nucleotides like ADP, ATP and GTP, serotonin, histamine, pyrophosphates, divalent cations, etc.), and lysosomes. There are multiple pathways that are important for stimulating granule secretion including calcium signaling, PKC-dependent phosphorylation and regulation of SNAP receptor complexes, signaling via small GTPases such as RAC1 and RHOA, the PI3K/AKT1 signaling pathway, and the signaling pathways of MAPK isoforms p38, integrin activation, the NO/cGMP/PKG pathway (not shown), and others. Finally, platelet activation contributes to the inflammatory response by the release of proinflammatory mediator, CD40LG inducing F3 synthesis and increasing IL6 and IL8 levels." EXACT []

