xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In case of infection or oxidative stress, oxidized phospholipids may be found in the blood. It has long been established that oxidized low-density lipoprotein (oxLDL) induces platelet activation/adhesion and enhances platelet aggregation.    Signaling description: Oxidized phospholipids serve as ligands for scavenger receptors and may play a role as pathological, exogenous ligands and activate platelet aggregation. Platelets constitutively express class B scavenger receptors such as CD36 and SCARB1. Class E scavenger receptor, OLR1 (also known as LOX-1) and class D scavenger receptor, CD68 appear on the cell membrane following platelet activation. Scavenger receptor CD36 is the major receptor on platelets that promotes platelet reactivity in response to a group of specific oxidized phospholipids. CD36 recognizes a number of distinct ligands including the family of oxidized choline glycerophospholipids (oxPCCD36), which bind to platelets via CD36 and activates it. CD36 is localized in caveolin-negative, cholesterol-enriched lipid rafts in platelet membranes. The signaling events induced by CD36 are poorly understood. CD36 possibly forms a multi-protein complex in rafts with Src-family kinases (FYN, LYN, and YES1), GP1B, CD9, and alpha 2b beta 3 integrin. LYN and CD36 do not interact directly, but do so through a raft-associated lipid mediator. CD36-anchored LYN may be negatively regulated by MATK in resting platelets. In platelet activation, CD36-anchored LYN is released from suppression through the rapid translocation and subsequent dephosphorylation of MATK. Both oxLDL and oxPCCD36 activate PRKCA and induce the phosphorylation of SYK and PLCG2.    Outcome effects: The downstream targets of LYN and FYN include the VAV family of proteins. VAVs are rapidly phosphorylated in platelets in a CD36-dependent manner. The signaling pathways induced by oxLDL in platelets include the activation of RHOA/ROCK1. In platelets, MAPK14 is also downstream of CD36 signaling. CD36 may lead to the activation of alpha 2b beta 3 integrin complex. Hyperlipidemia in vivo and oxLDL in vitro activate MAPK8 and its upstream activator, MAP2K4 in platelets via CD36. The interaction of CD36 with alpha 2b beta 3 integrin bound to fibrinogen is thought to stabilize platelet aggregation. It is currently unknown whether MAPK14 and MAPK8 work synergistically or independently in the process of platelet activation by oxLDL. The precise function of MAPK8 in platelet biology also remains to be determined. In addition, human CD36-negative platelets exhibit a mild defect at the initial stages of adhesion to fibrillar collagen I. SCARB1 is the major receptor on platelets that recognizes oxHDL. HDL and HDL sub-fractions, HDL2 and HDL3 have no significant effect on the activation and aggregation responses of isolated platelets. However, HDL oxidized by various pathways strongly inhibits platelet activation and interferes with multiple physiologic agonists by inhibiting platelet alpha 2b beta 3 integrin complex activation. The inhibitory effect requires the binding of oxHDL to platelet SCARB1. The mechanism of SCARB1-mediated inhibition of platelet activation by oxHDL is currently under investigation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In case of infection or oxidative stress, oxidized phospholipids may be found in the blood. It has long been established that oxidized low-density lipoprotein (oxLDL) induces platelet activation/adhesion and enhances platelet aggregation.    Signaling description: Oxidized phospholipids serve as ligands for scavenger receptors and may play a role as pathological, exogenous ligands and activate platelet aggregation. Platelets constitutively express class B scavenger receptors such as CD36 and SCARB1. Class E scavenger receptor, OLR1 (also known as LOX-1) and class D scavenger receptor, CD68 appear on the cell membrane following platelet activation. Scavenger receptor CD36 is the major receptor on platelets that promotes platelet reactivity in response to a group of specific oxidized phospholipids. CD36 recognizes a number of distinct ligands including the family of oxidized choline glycerophospholipids (oxPCCD36), which bind to platelets via CD36 and activates it. CD36 is localized in caveolin-negative, cholesterol-enriched lipid rafts in platelet membranes. The signaling events induced by CD36 are poorly understood. CD36 possibly forms a multi-protein complex in rafts with Src-family kinases (FYN, LYN, and YES1), GP1B, CD9, and alpha 2b beta 3 integrin. LYN and CD36 do not interact directly, but do so through a raft-associated lipid mediator. CD36-anchored LYN may be negatively regulated by MATK in resting platelets. In platelet activation, CD36-anchored LYN is released from suppression through the rapid translocation and subsequent dephosphorylation of MATK. Both oxLDL and oxPCCD36 activate PRKCA and induce the phosphorylation of SYK and PLCG2.    Outcome effects: The downstream targets of LYN and FYN include the VAV family of proteins. VAVs are rapidly phosphorylated in platelets in a CD36-dependent manner. The signaling pathways induced by oxLDL in platelets include the activation of RHOA/ROCK1. In platelets, MAPK14 is also downstream of CD36 signaling. CD36 may lead to the activation of alpha 2b beta 3 integrin complex. Hyperlipidemia in vivo and oxLDL in vitro activate MAPK8 and its upstream activator, MAP2K4 in platelets via CD36. The interaction of CD36 with alpha 2b beta 3 integrin bound to fibrinogen is thought to stabilize platelet aggregation. It is currently unknown whether MAPK14 and MAPK8 work synergistically or independently in the process of platelet activation by oxLDL. The precise function of MAPK8 in platelet biology also remains to be determined. In addition, human CD36-negative platelets exhibit a mild defect at the initial stages of adhesion to fibrillar collagen I. SCARB1 is the major receptor on platelets that recognizes oxHDL. HDL and HDL sub-fractions, HDL2 and HDL3 have no significant effect on the activation and aggregation responses of isolated platelets. However, HDL oxidized by various pathways strongly inhibits platelet activation and interferes with multiple physiologic agonists by inhibiting platelet alpha 2b beta 3 integrin complex activation. The inhibitory effect requires the binding of oxHDL to platelet SCARB1. The mechanism of SCARB1-mediated inhibition of platelet activation by oxHDL is currently under investigation." EXACT []

