xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Oxidized low-density lipoprotein (oxLDL) induces platelet activation and increases the ability of platelets to adhere to endothelial vascular cells which induces an inflammatory response and leads to platelet accumulation following vascular injury.    Signaling description: OxLDL induces platelet activation via class B scavenger receptor CD36 signaling. OLR1 (also known as LOX-1) is a class E scavenger receptor that is exposed on the surface of platelets following activation. Activated platelets internalize significant amounts of oxLDL compared to resting platelets. CD36 mediates oxLDL binding to platelets in resting platelets, however in activated platelets; the binding of oxLDL is mediated by both CD36 and OLR1. OxLDL-positive platelets induce platelet aggregation following vascular injury since OLR1can recognize and bind activated platelets. Moreover, oxLDL-positive platelets induce adhesion molecule expression on vascular endothelial cells, reduce regeneration of endothelial cells, and induce foam cell formation. The exact mechanism of intracellular OLR1 signaling in platelets remains unknown. In the plasma membrane, OLR1 is mainly localized in caveolae/lipid rafts, which are cholesterol-enriched membrane microdomains, so its function is modulated by the cholesterol content of the membrane. OxLDL is rapidly internalized by the cell following interaction with OLR1.    Outcome effects: Following the endocytosis of oxLDL-OLR1 complex, the receptor is uncoupled from oxLDL and both are positioned in separate compartments within the cytosol. OxLDL is trafficked through the early endosome and onto the lysosome for degradation. It is believed that the majority of OLR1 is targeted back to the plasma membrane by a recycling pathway from endosome-to-plasma membrane. Cytosolic factors that mediate recognition, endocytosis, and/or recycling of OLR1 have yet to be identified. OxLDL/OLR1 signaling may influence ADP-induced platelet aggregation. OLR1 is important for ADP-stimulated activation of platelet ITGA2B and ITGB3 integrins (fibrinogen receptors). In platelets, ADP/P2RY1 signaling activates PLCB1 and leads to calcium (Ca2+) mobilization and PKC activation which are necessary for shape change and initiation of aggregation. OLR1 is important for ADP-mediated platelet integrin activation, which possibly occurs via PKC. The manner in which OLR1 influences PKC function is currently unknown. Lastly, CD68 is exposed on the surface of activated platelets in parallel with the lysosome membrane. CD68 has been shown to bind oxLDL, although it is unclear whether this plays any role in platelet recognition of oxLDL."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Oxidized low-density lipoprotein (oxLDL) induces platelet activation and increases the ability of platelets to adhere to endothelial vascular cells which induces an inflammatory response and leads to platelet accumulation following vascular injury.    Signaling description: OxLDL induces platelet activation via class B scavenger receptor CD36 signaling. OLR1 (also known as LOX-1) is a class E scavenger receptor that is exposed on the surface of platelets following activation. Activated platelets internalize significant amounts of oxLDL compared to resting platelets. CD36 mediates oxLDL binding to platelets in resting platelets, however in activated platelets; the binding of oxLDL is mediated by both CD36 and OLR1. OxLDL-positive platelets induce platelet aggregation following vascular injury since OLR1can recognize and bind activated platelets. Moreover, oxLDL-positive platelets induce adhesion molecule expression on vascular endothelial cells, reduce regeneration of endothelial cells, and induce foam cell formation. The exact mechanism of intracellular OLR1 signaling in platelets remains unknown. In the plasma membrane, OLR1 is mainly localized in caveolae/lipid rafts, which are cholesterol-enriched membrane microdomains, so its function is modulated by the cholesterol content of the membrane. OxLDL is rapidly internalized by the cell following interaction with OLR1.    Outcome effects: Following the endocytosis of oxLDL-OLR1 complex, the receptor is uncoupled from oxLDL and both are positioned in separate compartments within the cytosol. OxLDL is trafficked through the early endosome and onto the lysosome for degradation. It is believed that the majority of OLR1 is targeted back to the plasma membrane by a recycling pathway from endosome-to-plasma membrane. Cytosolic factors that mediate recognition, endocytosis, and/or recycling of OLR1 have yet to be identified. OxLDL/OLR1 signaling may influence ADP-induced platelet aggregation. OLR1 is important for ADP-stimulated activation of platelet ITGA2B and ITGB3 integrins (fibrinogen receptors). In platelets, ADP/P2RY1 signaling activates PLCB1 and leads to calcium (Ca2+) mobilization and PKC activation which are necessary for shape change and initiation of aggregation. OLR1 is important for ADP-mediated platelet integrin activation, which possibly occurs via PKC. The manner in which OLR1 influences PKC function is currently unknown. Lastly, CD68 is exposed on the surface of activated platelets in parallel with the lysosome membrane. CD68 has been shown to bind oxLDL, although it is unclear whether this plays any role in platelet recognition of oxLDL." EXACT []

