xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Megakaryocytes are large, multinucleate cells that are the precursors of thrombocytes. Thrombocytes lack nuclei and have no genes that need to be transcribed. The primary physiological function of platelets is to form hemostatic thrombi, which prevent blood loss and maintain vascular integrity.    Signaling description: Upon activation due to damage or inflammation, thrombocytes change their shape, release the contents of granules, and become clustered forming hemostatic thrombus. The maturation of thrombocytes consists of two phases, which begins when proplatelets are formed. In the first phase, there is nuclear proliferation to the enlargement of the megakaryocyte cytoplasm as it is filled with cytoskeletal proteins, platelet-specific granules, and granule contents. This phase occurs over a period of days and requires induction by megakaryocyte-specific growth factors. Proplatelets are extended in the second phase and platelets are released. Large proplatelets are released in the bloodstream where they form platelets by fragmentation at their bulbous ends. This phase is completed in several hours. THPO is the main factor of megakaryocyte maturation. In thrombocytes, THPO activates the secretion of alpha granules through PI3K-dependent signaling. THPO acts on the synthesis of thrombocyte-specific proteins via MPL receptor and its signaling. Other factors that are important for thrombocyte maturation are CSF2, IL6, VWF, IL11, and EPO. EPO affects platelet production but its precise role in homeostatic platelet production is unknown. Megakaryocytes mature in the osteoblastic niche and migrate to the vascular niche under a CXCL12 gradient. Some of the strong inhibitors of megakaryocyte differentiation are PACAP, VIP, and PF4. GATA1 and ZFPM1 are considered key transcription factors in the maturation process of thrombocytes. The initial event in megakaryocyte differentiation is the simultaneous inhibition of SPI1 and activation of GATA1. Further, GATA1, FLI1, and KLF1 may be involved in the transcription of megakaryocyte and proplatelet-specific genes. NFE2, MKL1, and RUNX1 control late stages of differentiation, which includes the formation of prothrombocytes and thrombocytes.    Signaling description: As a megakaryocyte begins to migrate towards the endothelium, it interacts with other compounds of the extracellular matrix (ECM) such as fibrinogen or VWF which may increase proplatelet formation. The late stages of differentiation may be regulated by ITGA2B/ITGB3 and GP1BA. In order to migrate through endothelial cells, megakaryocytes require the synthesis of matrix metallopeptidases (MMPs) such as MMP2 or MMP9. In addition, myosin II is crucial in the control of proplatelet formation. It is likely that myosin II interacts with microtubules to generate the forces that impair the protrusion necessary for proplatelet formation. The sliding of overlapping microtubules acts as a primary motor for proplatelet elongation. Furthermore, proteins contained in alpha granules, which are specialized secretory granules, in mature platelets can be synthesized or endocytosed during proplatelet maturation. Most specific proteins, such as PF4 or VWF, are synthesized early in differentiation and localize into alpha granules. Other proteins bind to membrane receptors, such as fibrinogen to ITGA2B/ITGB3, and are endocytosed and packaged into alpha granules."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Megakaryocytes are large, multinucleate cells that are the precursors of thrombocytes. Thrombocytes lack nuclei and have no genes that need to be transcribed. The primary physiological function of platelets is to form hemostatic thrombi, which prevent blood loss and maintain vascular integrity.    Signaling description: Upon activation due to damage or inflammation, thrombocytes change their shape, release the contents of granules, and become clustered forming hemostatic thrombus. The maturation of thrombocytes consists of two phases, which begins when proplatelets are formed. In the first phase, there is nuclear proliferation to the enlargement of the megakaryocyte cytoplasm as it is filled with cytoskeletal proteins, platelet-specific granules, and granule contents. This phase occurs over a period of days and requires induction by megakaryocyte-specific growth factors. Proplatelets are extended in the second phase and platelets are released. Large proplatelets are released in the bloodstream where they form platelets by fragmentation at their bulbous ends. This phase is completed in several hours. THPO is the main factor of megakaryocyte maturation. In thrombocytes, THPO activates the secretion of alpha granules through PI3K-dependent signaling. THPO acts on the synthesis of thrombocyte-specific proteins via MPL receptor and its signaling. Other factors that are important for thrombocyte maturation are CSF2, IL6, VWF, IL11, and EPO. EPO affects platelet production but its precise role in homeostatic platelet production is unknown. Megakaryocytes mature in the osteoblastic niche and migrate to the vascular niche under a CXCL12 gradient. Some of the strong inhibitors of megakaryocyte differentiation are PACAP, VIP, and PF4. GATA1 and ZFPM1 are considered key transcription factors in the maturation process of thrombocytes. The initial event in megakaryocyte differentiation is the simultaneous inhibition of SPI1 and activation of GATA1. Further, GATA1, FLI1, and KLF1 may be involved in the transcription of megakaryocyte and proplatelet-specific genes. NFE2, MKL1, and RUNX1 control late stages of differentiation, which includes the formation of prothrombocytes and thrombocytes.    Signaling description: As a megakaryocyte begins to migrate towards the endothelium, it interacts with other compounds of the extracellular matrix (ECM) such as fibrinogen or VWF which may increase proplatelet formation. The late stages of differentiation may be regulated by ITGA2B/ITGB3 and GP1BA. In order to migrate through endothelial cells, megakaryocytes require the synthesis of matrix metallopeptidases (MMPs) such as MMP2 or MMP9. In addition, myosin II is crucial in the control of proplatelet formation. It is likely that myosin II interacts with microtubules to generate the forces that impair the protrusion necessary for proplatelet formation. The sliding of overlapping microtubules acts as a primary motor for proplatelet elongation. Furthermore, proteins contained in alpha granules, which are specialized secretory granules, in mature platelets can be synthesized or endocytosed during proplatelet maturation. Most specific proteins, such as PF4 or VWF, are synthesized early in differentiation and localize into alpha granules. Other proteins bind to membrane receptors, such as fibrinogen to ITGA2B/ITGB3, and are endocytosed and packaged into alpha granules." EXACT []

