xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The primary physiological function of platelets is to form hemostatic thrombi that prevent blood loss and maintain vascular integrity.    Signaling description: Platelet activation and coagulation normally do not occur within an intact blood vessel. After vessel wall injury, platelet-plug formation is initiated by the adherence of platelets to subendothelial collagen. Platelet activation is a dynamic process involving several different pathways. Soluble platelet agonists that are released from damaged cells (e.g. ADP) and produced during coagulation (e.g. thrombin) and inflammation (e.g. platelet-activating factor), which activate platelets via G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCR). Platelets express GNAQ, Ga12/13, Gi/o, and GNAS. GNAQ, Ga12/13, and Gi/o in platelets are coupled to agonist receptors that stimulate platelet activation. GNAS is coupled to receptors for physiological platelet inhibitors such as PGI2 and adenosine that mediate inhibitory signals by stimulating adenylate cyclase-dependent cAMP signaling. Thrombin-induced platelet activation is mediated via a dual system of G-protein coupled protease-activated receptors (PAR) including F2R and F2RL3 in humans. TXA2 activates platelets via the TXA2 receptor, TBXA2R which couples to GNAQ and GNA13. Serotonin recognizes the GNAQ-coupled receptor, HTR2A. ADP induces platelet activation via P2RY1 (GNAQ-coupled) and P2RY12 (Gi/o-coupled). GNAQ has been shown to transmit cellular signals mainly through its interaction and stimulation of phospholipase C-beta (PLCB1). PLCB1 catalyzes the hydrolysis of phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2) to release inositol 1,4,5 -trisphospate (IP3) and diacylglycerol, which activate calcium (Ca2 ) mobilization and protein kinase C (PKC), respectively. The elevation of Ca2+ levels activates multiple signaling events and molecules including actin-myosin interaction, PKC, calmodulin, nitric oxide synthases (NOS), and Ca2+-dependent proteases. Classical PKCs, particularly PRKCA, play a critical and general role in platelet granule secretion and secretion-dependent aggregation.    Outcome effects: A common platelet response to agonists is the secretion of granule contents. Platelets contain three major types of granules including alpha-granules (containing adhesion proteins, coagulation and fibrinolytic factors, cytokines, growth factors, and adhesion receptors), dense granules (containing nucleotides like ADP, ATP and GTP, serotonin, histamine, pyrophosphates, divalent cations, etc.), and lysosomes. There are multiple pathways that are important for stimulating granule secretion including calcium signaling, PKC-dependent phosphorylation and regulation of SNAP receptor complexes, signaling via small GTPases such as RAC1 and RHOA, the PI3K/AKT1 signaling pathway, and the signaling pathways of MAPK isoforms p38, integrin activation, the NO/cGMP/PKG pathway (not shown), and others. Finally, GNAQ signaling is important for GPCR-stimulated platelet granule secretion, integrin activation, and consequent platelet aggregation. GNA13 stimulates platelet shape change and granule secretion. Importantly, the GPCR and integrin outside-in signaling pathways coordinate with each other to dynamically regulate RHOA-dependent signaling in platelets. This crosstalk is critical for the processes of shape change, granule secretion, spreading, and clot retraction in platelets."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The primary physiological function of platelets is to form hemostatic thrombi that prevent blood loss and maintain vascular integrity.    Signaling description: Platelet activation and coagulation normally do not occur within an intact blood vessel. After vessel wall injury, platelet-plug formation is initiated by the adherence of platelets to subendothelial collagen. Platelet activation is a dynamic process involving several different pathways. Soluble platelet agonists that are released from damaged cells (e.g. ADP) and produced during coagulation (e.g. thrombin) and inflammation (e.g. platelet-activating factor), which activate platelets via G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCR). Platelets express GNAQ, Ga12/13, Gi/o, and GNAS. GNAQ, Ga12/13, and Gi/o in platelets are coupled to agonist receptors that stimulate platelet activation. GNAS is coupled to receptors for physiological platelet inhibitors such as PGI2 and adenosine that mediate inhibitory signals by stimulating adenylate cyclase-dependent cAMP signaling. Thrombin-induced platelet activation is mediated via a dual system of G-protein coupled protease-activated receptors (PAR) including F2R and F2RL3 in humans. TXA2 activates platelets via the TXA2 receptor, TBXA2R which couples to GNAQ and GNA13. Serotonin recognizes the GNAQ-coupled receptor, HTR2A. ADP induces platelet activation via P2RY1 (GNAQ-coupled) and P2RY12 (Gi/o-coupled). GNAQ has been shown to transmit cellular signals mainly through its interaction and stimulation of phospholipase C-beta (PLCB1). PLCB1 catalyzes the hydrolysis of phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2) to release inositol 1,4,5 -trisphospate (IP3) and diacylglycerol, which activate calcium (Ca2 ) mobilization and protein kinase C (PKC), respectively. The elevation of Ca2+ levels activates multiple signaling events and molecules including actin-myosin interaction, PKC, calmodulin, nitric oxide synthases (NOS), and Ca2+-dependent proteases. Classical PKCs, particularly PRKCA, play a critical and general role in platelet granule secretion and secretion-dependent aggregation.    Outcome effects: A common platelet response to agonists is the secretion of granule contents. Platelets contain three major types of granules including alpha-granules (containing adhesion proteins, coagulation and fibrinolytic factors, cytokines, growth factors, and adhesion receptors), dense granules (containing nucleotides like ADP, ATP and GTP, serotonin, histamine, pyrophosphates, divalent cations, etc.), and lysosomes. There are multiple pathways that are important for stimulating granule secretion including calcium signaling, PKC-dependent phosphorylation and regulation of SNAP receptor complexes, signaling via small GTPases such as RAC1 and RHOA, the PI3K/AKT1 signaling pathway, and the signaling pathways of MAPK isoforms p38, integrin activation, the NO/cGMP/PKG pathway (not shown), and others. Finally, GNAQ signaling is important for GPCR-stimulated platelet granule secretion, integrin activation, and consequent platelet aggregation. GNA13 stimulates platelet shape change and granule secretion. Importantly, the GPCR and integrin outside-in signaling pathways coordinate with each other to dynamically regulate RHOA-dependent signaling in platelets. This crosstalk is critical for the processes of shape change, granule secretion, spreading, and clot retraction in platelets." EXACT []

