xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: VEGFA (vascular endothelial growth factor A) is a key mediator of angiogenesis in cancer. The pathology of VEGFA signaling in cancers is characterized with hyperproduction of VEGFA by tumor cells, as well as, tumor-infiltrating cells, including fibroblasts and monocytes/macrophages with subsequent VEGFA signaling hyper-activation.    Signaling description: Tumor-associated hypoxia with increased expression of HIF1A is the most important regulator of VEGFA. Multiple growth factors, including FGF2, FGF4, FGF7, PDGFB and cytokines, including TNF, IL1A, IL1B, IL6 may induce VEGFA hyper-expression. The up-regulation of multiple transcription factors, including JUN-FOS, MYC, MYCN, STAT3, ESR1, AR, PGR may lead to hyper-expression of VEGFA in cancer. The specific role of carcinogenic mutations in TP53, MYC, MYCN, PTEN, KRAS, HRAS, ERBB2, EGFR related to the up-regulation of VEGFA was shown in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: VEGFA expression is up-regulated due to signaling activation. It's altered expression leads to activation of both angiogenesis and vasculogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: VEGFA (vascular endothelial growth factor A) is a key mediator of angiogenesis in cancer. The pathology of VEGFA signaling in cancers is characterized with hyperproduction of VEGFA by tumor cells, as well as, tumor-infiltrating cells, including fibroblasts and monocytes/macrophages with subsequent VEGFA signaling hyper-activation.    Signaling description: Tumor-associated hypoxia with increased expression of HIF1A is the most important regulator of VEGFA. Multiple growth factors, including FGF2, FGF4, FGF7, PDGFB and cytokines, including TNF, IL1A, IL1B, IL6 may induce VEGFA hyper-expression. The up-regulation of multiple transcription factors, including JUN-FOS, MYC, MYCN, STAT3, ESR1, AR, PGR may lead to hyper-expression of VEGFA in cancer. The specific role of carcinogenic mutations in TP53, MYC, MYCN, PTEN, KRAS, HRAS, ERBB2, EGFR related to the up-regulation of VEGFA was shown in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: VEGFA expression is up-regulated due to signaling activation. It's altered expression leads to activation of both angiogenesis and vasculogenesis.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details." EXACT []

