xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: ANGPT/TEK signaling system is a vascular-specific receptor tyrosine kinase pathway that is essential for normal vascular development and is crucial for the angiogenic switch in tumors. ANGPT1/TEK is known to regulate both vascular quiescence and angiogenesis. The pathology of angiogenic switch in tumors is characterized by exit from quiescence state by endothelial cells (ECs), cell proliferation and migration with the formation of new blood vessels. Maintenance of vascular quiescence keeps in with the absence of pro-angiogenic stimuli and ANGPT1/TEK \"trans-association\" state. The acquisition of this quiescence state by ECs is regarded as a potential anti-cancer therapeutic target.    Signaling description: At the cell-cell contacts, oligomerized ANGPT1 bridges TEK expressed in adjoining cells, resulting in the formation of homophilic TEK trans-association. The core of ANGPT1/TEK related signaling, through PI3K and TNIP2, promotes inhibition of ECs activation or apoptosis. The precise mechanism of molecular signaling shown on this pathway is well known and is associated with the activation of AKT and NF-kB.    Outcome effects: KDR, VEGFA, ANGPT2, DLL4, CXCL8 are changing their expression due to AKT signaling activation. Their altered expression leads to ECs quiescence and inhibition of angiogenesis. The inhibition of pro-apoptotic proteins CASP3 and CASP9 and anti-apoptotic BIRC5 activation leads to the suppression of apoptosis and cell survival.    Animal data: The majority of the data on ANGPT/TEK and their role in angiogenesis were revealed from animal model (mice) experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: ANGPT/TEK signaling system is a vascular-specific receptor tyrosine kinase pathway that is essential for normal vascular development and is crucial for the angiogenic switch in tumors. ANGPT1/TEK is known to regulate both vascular quiescence and angiogenesis. The pathology of angiogenic switch in tumors is characterized by exit from quiescence state by endothelial cells (ECs), cell proliferation and migration with the formation of new blood vessels. Maintenance of vascular quiescence keeps in with the absence of pro-angiogenic stimuli and ANGPT1/TEK \"trans-association\" state. The acquisition of this quiescence state by ECs is regarded as a potential anti-cancer therapeutic target.    Signaling description: At the cell-cell contacts, oligomerized ANGPT1 bridges TEK expressed in adjoining cells, resulting in the formation of homophilic TEK trans-association. The core of ANGPT1/TEK related signaling, through PI3K and TNIP2, promotes inhibition of ECs activation or apoptosis. The precise mechanism of molecular signaling shown on this pathway is well known and is associated with the activation of AKT and NF-kB.    Outcome effects: KDR, VEGFA, ANGPT2, DLL4, CXCL8 are changing their expression due to AKT signaling activation. Their altered expression leads to ECs quiescence and inhibition of angiogenesis. The inhibition of pro-apoptotic proteins CASP3 and CASP9 and anti-apoptotic BIRC5 activation leads to the suppression of apoptosis and cell survival.    Animal data: The majority of the data on ANGPT/TEK and their role in angiogenesis were revealed from animal model (mice) experiments." EXACT []

