xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: ANGPT/TEK signaling system is a vascular-specific receptor tyrosine kinase pathway essential for normal vascular development and crucial for the angiogenic switch in tumors. ANGPT1/TEK has been shown to regulate both vascular quiescence and angiogenesis. The pathology of angiogenic switch in tumors is characterized by exit from quiescence state by endothelial cells (ECs), cell proliferation and migration with the formation of new blood vessels. Tumor ECs express elevated ANGPT2 levels, and an increased ANGPT2/ANGPT1 ratio correlates with tumor angiogenesis and poor prognosis in many cancers, making ANGPT2 an attractive therapeutic target.    Signaling description: ANGPT1 is a TIE2 agonist, and ANGPT2 functions as an antagonist or a partial agonist of TIE2 in various settings. ANGPT2 attenuates ANGPT1-TEK activation in the presence of ANGPT1 but activates TEK signaling when ANGPT1 is absent, which suggests that ANGPT2 is a partial agonist. Both ANGPT1 and ANGPT2 have been shown to promote or inhibit tumorigenesis in many cases. The precise mechanism of the molecular signaling shown on this pathway is poorly understood and requires further investigations. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway. In the presence of pro-angiogenic stimuli, ECs escape their quiescent state, loose cell-cell contacts, proliferate and migrate. In the absence of cell-cell adhesions, TEK is anchored to the cell-extracellular matrix (ECM) interface through ANGPT1 binding to the ECM. In cells lacking cell-cell adhesions, ANGPTs induces integrin-dependent focal complex formation, leading to activation of PTK2. ANGPTs activate RAS-MAPK through TEK and integrins leading to cell proliferation and signalling involved in ECs migration. ANGPT2 may also stimulates tip cell migration by activating integrins independently of TEK.    Outcome effects: JUN-FOS is activated leading to increased ECs cell proliferation. F-actin is activated and promotes ECs migration.    Animal data: The majority of the data on ANGPT/TEK and their role in angiogenesis were revealed from animal model (mice) experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: ANGPT/TEK signaling system is a vascular-specific receptor tyrosine kinase pathway essential for normal vascular development and crucial for the angiogenic switch in tumors. ANGPT1/TEK has been shown to regulate both vascular quiescence and angiogenesis. The pathology of angiogenic switch in tumors is characterized by exit from quiescence state by endothelial cells (ECs), cell proliferation and migration with the formation of new blood vessels. Tumor ECs express elevated ANGPT2 levels, and an increased ANGPT2/ANGPT1 ratio correlates with tumor angiogenesis and poor prognosis in many cancers, making ANGPT2 an attractive therapeutic target.    Signaling description: ANGPT1 is a TIE2 agonist, and ANGPT2 functions as an antagonist or a partial agonist of TIE2 in various settings. ANGPT2 attenuates ANGPT1-TEK activation in the presence of ANGPT1 but activates TEK signaling when ANGPT1 is absent, which suggests that ANGPT2 is a partial agonist. Both ANGPT1 and ANGPT2 have been shown to promote or inhibit tumorigenesis in many cases. The precise mechanism of the molecular signaling shown on this pathway is poorly understood and requires further investigations. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway. In the presence of pro-angiogenic stimuli, ECs escape their quiescent state, loose cell-cell contacts, proliferate and migrate. In the absence of cell-cell adhesions, TEK is anchored to the cell-extracellular matrix (ECM) interface through ANGPT1 binding to the ECM. In cells lacking cell-cell adhesions, ANGPTs induces integrin-dependent focal complex formation, leading to activation of PTK2. ANGPTs activate RAS-MAPK through TEK and integrins leading to cell proliferation and signalling involved in ECs migration. ANGPT2 may also stimulates tip cell migration by activating integrins independently of TEK.    Outcome effects: JUN-FOS is activated leading to increased ECs cell proliferation. F-actin is activated and promotes ECs migration.    Animal data: The majority of the data on ANGPT/TEK and their role in angiogenesis were revealed from animal model (mice) experiments." EXACT []

