xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Thrombospondin-1 (THBS1) is a potent endogenous inhibitor of both physiologic and pathologic angiogenesis. THBS1 and THBS2 exert their direct effects through CD36, CD47, and integrins. The pathology of THBS1 signaling in cancers is characterized by down-regulation of THBS1 leading to enhanced angiogenesis.    Signaling description: The activation of MYC and ID1 and inactivation of TP53 in tumors causes down-regulation of THBS1 which results in impairment of pro-apoptotic THBS/CD36/MAPK/CASP signaling, re-activation of proangiogenic KDR/PI3K/AKT1/NO signaling, activation of MMP2/9 and the absence of direct inhibitory effect of THBS1 on VEGFA, FGF2, HGF.    Outcome effects: The re-activation of KDR signaling leads to induction of angiogenesis, and the impairment of CASP activation causes inhibition of apoptosis and endothelial cell survival. Further, activation of MMPs leads to extracellular matrix degradation and invasiveness. VEGFA, FGF2, HGF activation stimulates angiogenesis.    Animal data: The role of integrins in THBS was established mainly in animal (mice) experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Thrombospondin-1 (THBS1) is a potent endogenous inhibitor of both physiologic and pathologic angiogenesis. THBS1 and THBS2 exert their direct effects through CD36, CD47, and integrins. The pathology of THBS1 signaling in cancers is characterized by down-regulation of THBS1 leading to enhanced angiogenesis.    Signaling description: The activation of MYC and ID1 and inactivation of TP53 in tumors causes down-regulation of THBS1 which results in impairment of pro-apoptotic THBS/CD36/MAPK/CASP signaling, re-activation of proangiogenic KDR/PI3K/AKT1/NO signaling, activation of MMP2/9 and the absence of direct inhibitory effect of THBS1 on VEGFA, FGF2, HGF.    Outcome effects: The re-activation of KDR signaling leads to induction of angiogenesis, and the impairment of CASP activation causes inhibition of apoptosis and endothelial cell survival. Further, activation of MMPs leads to extracellular matrix degradation and invasiveness. VEGFA, FGF2, HGF activation stimulates angiogenesis.    Animal data: The role of integrins in THBS was established mainly in animal (mice) experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details." EXACT []

