xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Fibulin 5 (FBLN5) is an extracellular matrix (ECM) protein prominently expressed in developing arteries. FBLN5's function is the stabilization of basement membrane and elastic fibers. FBLN5 enhances attachment of endothelial cell (ECs) to the basement membrane (BM) and inhibits migration and proliferation of ECs in a context-dependent manner.    Signaling description: Generally, FBLN5 blocks angiogenesis by inducing the anti-angiogenic protein THBS1 and by antagonizing pro-angiogenic VEGF and TEK signaling. The precise mechanism of interaction of FBLN5 with THBS1, VEGFA and TEK signaling pathways is not clear. The down-regulation of FBLN5 potentiates angiogenesis in the opposite manner. Normally, FBLN5 enhances the attachment of ECs to BM through binding to ECM proteins (ELN, EMILIN1 and others) and integrins on the cell surface; whereas, down-regulation of FBLN5 potentiates the release of ECs from BM and their migration. FBLN5 may regulate ECs migration and proliferation through integrins, but the exact mechanism of this regulatory action of FBLN5 is yet to be determined.    Outcome effects: The detachment of ECs from BM, their migration and proliferation promoted by down-regulation of FBLN5 signaling leads to activation of angiogenesis.    Animal data: The majority of the data concerning the FBLN5 role in angiogenesis were revealed from animal model experiments. Some data were obtained from human cancer cell experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Fibulin 5 (FBLN5) is an extracellular matrix (ECM) protein prominently expressed in developing arteries. FBLN5's function is the stabilization of basement membrane and elastic fibers. FBLN5 enhances attachment of endothelial cell (ECs) to the basement membrane (BM) and inhibits migration and proliferation of ECs in a context-dependent manner.    Signaling description: Generally, FBLN5 blocks angiogenesis by inducing the anti-angiogenic protein THBS1 and by antagonizing pro-angiogenic VEGF and TEK signaling. The precise mechanism of interaction of FBLN5 with THBS1, VEGFA and TEK signaling pathways is not clear. The down-regulation of FBLN5 potentiates angiogenesis in the opposite manner. Normally, FBLN5 enhances the attachment of ECs to BM through binding to ECM proteins (ELN, EMILIN1 and others) and integrins on the cell surface; whereas, down-regulation of FBLN5 potentiates the release of ECs from BM and their migration. FBLN5 may regulate ECs migration and proliferation through integrins, but the exact mechanism of this regulatory action of FBLN5 is yet to be determined.    Outcome effects: The detachment of ECs from BM, their migration and proliferation promoted by down-regulation of FBLN5 signaling leads to activation of angiogenesis.    Animal data: The majority of the data concerning the FBLN5 role in angiogenesis were revealed from animal model experiments. Some data were obtained from human cancer cell experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details." EXACT []

