xref: PS:Source "Pathological Process"
synonym: "Source: Pathological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The pathology of sustained angiogenesis in tumors is characterized by hyperexpression of multiple pro-angiogenic factors. Activated HIF1A plays a critical role in the expression of pro-angiogenic factors. HIF1A was shown to be upregulated in many cancer types and regarded as a potential anti-angiogenic drug target.    Signaling description: Hypoxia is typical for tumors due to the imbalance between oxygen consumption and supply and is the major inductor of HIF1A. Also, HIF1A can be induced by inactivating mutations of VHL and CDKN2A, BCR-ABL translocation or down-regulation of enzymes and proteins controlling HIF activity (SDHD, SDHB, SDHC, EGLN1, FH). HIF1A in conjunction with EPAS1 (HIF2A) and ARNT (HIF1B) regulate the expression of a suite of pro-angiogenic genes.    Outcome effects: VEGFA, ANGPT1, ANGPT2, TRK, KDR and other pro-angiogenic genes are overexpressed due to the activation of HIFs leading to sustained angiogenesis. CCL2, CXCL12, CXCR4 are also overexpressed due to activation of HIFs facilitating migration of endothelial cells.    Animal data: not shown     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The pathology of sustained angiogenesis in tumors is characterized by hyperexpression of multiple pro-angiogenic factors. Activated HIF1A plays a critical role in the expression of pro-angiogenic factors. HIF1A was shown to be upregulated in many cancer types and regarded as a potential anti-angiogenic drug target.    Signaling description: Hypoxia is typical for tumors due to the imbalance between oxygen consumption and supply and is the major inductor of HIF1A. Also, HIF1A can be induced by inactivating mutations of VHL and CDKN2A, BCR-ABL translocation or down-regulation of enzymes and proteins controlling HIF activity (SDHD, SDHB, SDHC, EGLN1, FH). HIF1A in conjunction with EPAS1 (HIF2A) and ARNT (HIF1B) regulate the expression of a suite of pro-angiogenic genes.    Outcome effects: VEGFA, ANGPT1, ANGPT2, TRK, KDR and other pro-angiogenic genes are overexpressed due to the activation of HIFs leading to sustained angiogenesis. CCL2, CXCL12, CXCR4 are also overexpressed due to activation of HIFs facilitating migration of endothelial cells.    Animal data: not shown     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Induction of Angiogenesis\" for details." EXACT []

