xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mechanisms of apoptosis impairment include down-regulation of pro-apoptotic and upregulation of anti-apoptotic BCL2 family proteins, increased expression of apoptosis inhibitors, disruption of death receptor signaling and impaired function of caspases.    Signaling description: The overexpression of pro-apoptotitc proteins BCL2, BCL2L1, MCL1, BCL2L2, BCL2A1, BCL2L10 and under-expression/inactivation mutations of anti-apoptotic proteins BID, BCL2L11, BBC3, PMAIP1, BAD, BMF, HRK, BAX, BAK1 lead to the impairment of apoptosis. The inactivation of some tumor suppressors (TP53, TP73, WWOX, FOXO3 and others) regulating the expression of BCL2 family proteins is also involved in the suppression of apoptosis. Inhibitors of apoptosis -BIRC2 and 3 - may be activated due to mutations. Other factors participating hyper-expression of IAPs include activation by HIF1A, STAT3, NFKB1, CTNNB1, ARHGEF3 and loss of suppression from TP53, XAF1 and DIABLO. Further, death receptor signaling may be impaired in cancers due to multiple mechanisms. FAS, TNFRSF10A/B, TRADD may be inactivated by mutations or NGFR and FADD may be downregulated. The disruption of caspase cascade may be due to either inactivating mutations (shown for CASP1/4/5/6/8/10) or inactivation by the inhibitors of apoptosis (BIRCs, XIAP, CFLAR). Impairment of CASP3/9 activation may also be due to inactivating mutations in NTN1 and DCC which directly regulates these caspases.    Outcome effects: As a result, caspases evade activation and apoptosis is blocked.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details.    Description of viral proteins: HPV16-E6 shown in grey is viral protein."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mechanisms of apoptosis impairment include down-regulation of pro-apoptotic and upregulation of anti-apoptotic BCL2 family proteins, increased expression of apoptosis inhibitors, disruption of death receptor signaling and impaired function of caspases.    Signaling description: The overexpression of pro-apoptotitc proteins BCL2, BCL2L1, MCL1, BCL2L2, BCL2A1, BCL2L10 and under-expression/inactivation mutations of anti-apoptotic proteins BID, BCL2L11, BBC3, PMAIP1, BAD, BMF, HRK, BAX, BAK1 lead to the impairment of apoptosis. The inactivation of some tumor suppressors (TP53, TP73, WWOX, FOXO3 and others) regulating the expression of BCL2 family proteins is also involved in the suppression of apoptosis. Inhibitors of apoptosis -BIRC2 and 3 - may be activated due to mutations. Other factors participating hyper-expression of IAPs include activation by HIF1A, STAT3, NFKB1, CTNNB1, ARHGEF3 and loss of suppression from TP53, XAF1 and DIABLO. Further, death receptor signaling may be impaired in cancers due to multiple mechanisms. FAS, TNFRSF10A/B, TRADD may be inactivated by mutations or NGFR and FADD may be downregulated. The disruption of caspase cascade may be due to either inactivating mutations (shown for CASP1/4/5/6/8/10) or inactivation by the inhibitors of apoptosis (BIRCs, XIAP, CFLAR). Impairment of CASP3/9 activation may also be due to inactivating mutations in NTN1 and DCC which directly regulates these caspases.    Outcome effects: As a result, caspases evade activation and apoptosis is blocked.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details.    Description of viral proteins: HPV16-E6 shown in grey is viral protein." EXACT []

