xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hypoxia is typical for cancer due to the imbalance between oxygen supply and consumption, and also the formation of aberrant vessels with poor blood flow characteristic to tumors. Normally, hypoxia is linked to cell death. However, cancer cells can avoid hypoxia-induced cell death through a number of mechanisms.    Signaling description: Hypoxia can induce apoptosis via HIF1A-dependent activation of TP53 with downstream signaling through BCL family proteins and the induction of the caspase cascade. HIF1A also regulates the expression of BNIP3L and BNIP3 which may initiate either apoptosis, as well as necrosis or autophagy. The exact mechanism by which cancer cells escape hypoxia-induced cell death is not quite clear. Several mechanisms have been described up to now. First, hyper-activated IGF1/IGFR1/PI3K/AKT1 signaling can inhibit apoptosis. In the same way, TWIST2 can inhibit apoptosis through PI3K/AKT1 activation. Also, CASP9 inhibition by BIRC3 was shown to have a role in evasion of hypoxia-induced apoptosis .    Outcome effects: As a result, cancer cells may avoid hypoxia-induced cell death.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hypoxia is typical for cancer due to the imbalance between oxygen supply and consumption, and also the formation of aberrant vessels with poor blood flow characteristic to tumors. Normally, hypoxia is linked to cell death. However, cancer cells can avoid hypoxia-induced cell death through a number of mechanisms.    Signaling description: Hypoxia can induce apoptosis via HIF1A-dependent activation of TP53 with downstream signaling through BCL family proteins and the induction of the caspase cascade. HIF1A also regulates the expression of BNIP3L and BNIP3 which may initiate either apoptosis, as well as necrosis or autophagy. The exact mechanism by which cancer cells escape hypoxia-induced cell death is not quite clear. Several mechanisms have been described up to now. First, hyper-activated IGF1/IGFR1/PI3K/AKT1 signaling can inhibit apoptosis. In the same way, TWIST2 can inhibit apoptosis through PI3K/AKT1 activation. Also, CASP9 inhibition by BIRC3 was shown to have a role in evasion of hypoxia-induced apoptosis .    Outcome effects: As a result, cancer cells may avoid hypoxia-induced cell death.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details." EXACT []

