xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The BCL2 family of proteins is comprised of pro-apoptotic and anti-apoptotic proteins that play a pivotal role in the regulation of apoptosis. These proteins act upstream of irreversible cellular damage mainly at the level of mitochondria.    Signaling description: Over-expression of pro-apoptotitc proteins BCL2, BCL2L1, MCL1, BCL2L2, BCL2A1, BCL2L10 and under-expression/inactivation mutations of anti-apoptotic proteins BID, BCL2L11, BBC3, PMAIP1, BAD, BMF, HRK, BAX, BAK1 lead to impairment of apoptosis. Inactivation of some tumor suppressors (TP53, TP73, WWOX, FOXO3 and others) regulating the expression of BCL2 family proteins also have a role in apoptosis inactivation.    Outcome effects: Caspases are not activated and apoptosis is not induced.     Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Resisting to Cell Death\" for details."
