xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Many proteins that participate in single-strand mismatch DNA repair can be mutated and down-regulated in cancer, including MSH2/3/6, PMS1/2, MLH1, POLE, LIG1. Some of these proteins can be inhibited by MIR21 or MIR155.    Signaling description: The mismatch repair (MMR) system is responsible for the post-replicative repair of mismatches and small single-stranded DNA loops. MMR recognizes a wide spectrum of mismatches in base-pairing nucleotides and removes a patch of DNA including the misincorporated nucleotide. New DNA is synthesized using the complementary strand as a template. The mismatch is initially bound by MSH2/MSH6 heterodimer for base-base mismatches and one- or two-base loops or MutS beta heterodimer (MSH2/MSH3) for loops of 2-14bp. The mismatch-bound MSH2/MSH6 heterodimer is converted to a sliding clamp capable of translocating along the DNA molecule in an ATP-dependent manner. The MSH2/MSH6/ATP/DNA complex is bound by a second heterodimer, MLH1/PMS2. This complex searches a strand discontinuity and is able to translocate in either direction. MSH2 also forms a heterodimer with MSH3. This MutS beta complex binds to insertion/deletion mispairs but displays little if any affinity for base-base mismatches. In strand resynthesis, the MSH2/MSH6 sliding clamp stimulates the activity of EXO1, starting from a nick situated 5' from the mispair and traveling towards the mispair. Polymerases and their cofactors, PCNA and DNA replication factor C, fill in the resulting single-stranded gap and LIG1 seals the remaining nick. Many members of single-strand mismatch DNA repair can be mutated and downregulated in cancer, including MSH2/3/6, PMS1/2, MLH1, POLE, LIG1. Some of these proteins can be inhibited by MIR21 or MIR155.    Outcome effects: Silencing of single-strand mismatch DNA repair machinery impairs direct DNA repair and promotes microsatellite instability in cancer cells.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Many proteins that participate in single-strand mismatch DNA repair can be mutated and down-regulated in cancer, including MSH2/3/6, PMS1/2, MLH1, POLE, LIG1. Some of these proteins can be inhibited by MIR21 or MIR155.    Signaling description: The mismatch repair (MMR) system is responsible for the post-replicative repair of mismatches and small single-stranded DNA loops. MMR recognizes a wide spectrum of mismatches in base-pairing nucleotides and removes a patch of DNA including the misincorporated nucleotide. New DNA is synthesized using the complementary strand as a template. The mismatch is initially bound by MSH2/MSH6 heterodimer for base-base mismatches and one- or two-base loops or MutS beta heterodimer (MSH2/MSH3) for loops of 2-14bp. The mismatch-bound MSH2/MSH6 heterodimer is converted to a sliding clamp capable of translocating along the DNA molecule in an ATP-dependent manner. The MSH2/MSH6/ATP/DNA complex is bound by a second heterodimer, MLH1/PMS2. This complex searches a strand discontinuity and is able to translocate in either direction. MSH2 also forms a heterodimer with MSH3. This MutS beta complex binds to insertion/deletion mispairs but displays little if any affinity for base-base mismatches. In strand resynthesis, the MSH2/MSH6 sliding clamp stimulates the activity of EXO1, starting from a nick situated 5' from the mispair and traveling towards the mispair. Polymerases and their cofactors, PCNA and DNA replication factor C, fill in the resulting single-stranded gap and LIG1 seals the remaining nick. Many members of single-strand mismatch DNA repair can be mutated and downregulated in cancer, including MSH2/3/6, PMS1/2, MLH1, POLE, LIG1. Some of these proteins can be inhibited by MIR21 or MIR155.    Outcome effects: Silencing of single-strand mismatch DNA repair machinery impairs direct DNA repair and promotes microsatellite instability in cancer cells.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details." EXACT []

