xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Several proteins involved in single-strand nucleotide excision DNA repair may be mutated or deregulated in cancer, including XPC, ERCC1/3/4, LIG1 and POLE    Signaling description: Nucleotide excision repair (NER) involves more than 30 proteins and their action includes several steps: damage recognition, local opening of the DNA duplex around the lesion, dual incision of the damaged DNA strand, gap repair synthesis and strand ligation. The two distinct forms of NER include global genomic NER (GG-NER) (repairs damage in transcriptionally silent regions of the genome) and transcription-coupled NER (TC-NER) (repairs lesions on actively transcribed DNA). The two pathways are very similar with the exception of their mechanisms of damage recognition. In GG-NER, the initial detection of damaged DNA is performed by the XPC protein complex; whereas, TC-NER does not require XPC and occurs when the transcription apparatus stops at the damaged site. The further steps are similar in the two forms of NER. XPA and replication factor A bind at the site of injury and further proceed with damage recognition. Next, the ERCC3 and ERCC2 helicases (components of TFIIH transcription factor) unwind the DNA duplex close to the lesion and enable RNA polymerase II to initiate transcription. Further, one DNA strand is cleaved by ERCC5 and ERCC1/ ERCC4 endonucleases at positions 3' and 5' to the damage, respectively, generating an oligonucleotide containing the lesion. This oligonucleotide is displaced and facilitates the action of the gap repair synthesis machinery, including DNA polymerase delta and epsilon (POLE) and a number of replication accessory factors. Finally, DNA ligase LIG1 seals the nick in the repaired strand. Some members of single-strand nucleotide excision DNA repair may be mutated or deregulated in cancer, including XPC, ERCC1/3/4, LIG1 and POLE.XPC, LIG1 and POLE are inactivated by mutations. RAD25B stabilizes XPC and can be inhibited by MIR373. ERCC3/4 can be inhibited by MIR192. Transcription of ERCC1 is negatively regulated by HMGA2. The latter can be mutated and overexpressed or activated by downregulation of MIR15A/161/26A1, MIRLET7A1.    Outcome effects: The disruption of single-strand nucleotide excision DNA repair machinery leads to genomic instability in cancer cells.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Several proteins involved in single-strand nucleotide excision DNA repair may be mutated or deregulated in cancer, including XPC, ERCC1/3/4, LIG1 and POLE    Signaling description: Nucleotide excision repair (NER) involves more than 30 proteins and their action includes several steps: damage recognition, local opening of the DNA duplex around the lesion, dual incision of the damaged DNA strand, gap repair synthesis and strand ligation. The two distinct forms of NER include global genomic NER (GG-NER) (repairs damage in transcriptionally silent regions of the genome) and transcription-coupled NER (TC-NER) (repairs lesions on actively transcribed DNA). The two pathways are very similar with the exception of their mechanisms of damage recognition. In GG-NER, the initial detection of damaged DNA is performed by the XPC protein complex; whereas, TC-NER does not require XPC and occurs when the transcription apparatus stops at the damaged site. The further steps are similar in the two forms of NER. XPA and replication factor A bind at the site of injury and further proceed with damage recognition. Next, the ERCC3 and ERCC2 helicases (components of TFIIH transcription factor) unwind the DNA duplex close to the lesion and enable RNA polymerase II to initiate transcription. Further, one DNA strand is cleaved by ERCC5 and ERCC1/ ERCC4 endonucleases at positions 3' and 5' to the damage, respectively, generating an oligonucleotide containing the lesion. This oligonucleotide is displaced and facilitates the action of the gap repair synthesis machinery, including DNA polymerase delta and epsilon (POLE) and a number of replication accessory factors. Finally, DNA ligase LIG1 seals the nick in the repaired strand. Some members of single-strand nucleotide excision DNA repair may be mutated or deregulated in cancer, including XPC, ERCC1/3/4, LIG1 and POLE.XPC, LIG1 and POLE are inactivated by mutations. RAD25B stabilizes XPC and can be inhibited by MIR373. ERCC3/4 can be inhibited by MIR192. Transcription of ERCC1 is negatively regulated by HMGA2. The latter can be mutated and overexpressed or activated by downregulation of MIR15A/161/26A1, MIRLET7A1.    Outcome effects: The disruption of single-strand nucleotide excision DNA repair machinery leads to genomic instability in cancer cells.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details." EXACT []

