xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Many proteins that participate in double strand DNA homologous repair can be mutated and dysregulated in cancer: ATM, RAD50/51, BRCA1/2, XRCC2, ATRX, BARD1, H2AFX, MRE11A, NBN, SMC1A, RBBP8. Specifically, ATM can be inhibited by MIR100/421/18A/101-1.     Signaling description: DNA repair by means of homologous recombination takes place when an identical or a near identical sequence is available to provide the template for DNA recovery. The protein machinery responsible for this recovery process is similar to that of the process of meiotic crossing-over when homologous chromatids serve as templates. The double strand bases (DSBs) appear as a result of mistakes in filling single-nucleotide gaps in the replicative fork or as a result of unfixed errors that occurred during single-stranded DNA synthesis. After DSBs are detected by ATM, the 5' strands are removed producing long 3' single-stranded DNA tails. These 3' tails serve as the substrate for RAD51 nucleoprotein filament nucleation. RAD52, BRCA1, and BRCA2 proteins are also recruited in this process. Once assembled, this protein complex screens the genome for homologous DNA sequences that can be used for the recombination.Transcription of BRCA1 is negatively regulated by HMGA2. The latter can be mutated and overexpressed or activated by downregulation of MIR15A/161/26A1, MIRLET7A1. BRCA1 also can be inhibited by MIR182.     Outcome effects: The deregulation of double strand DNA homologous repair machinery leads to genomic instability in cancer cells. Persistent DNA repair affects cell cycle regulation and may be associated with oncogenesis.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Many proteins that participate in double strand DNA homologous repair can be mutated and dysregulated in cancer: ATM, RAD50/51, BRCA1/2, XRCC2, ATRX, BARD1, H2AFX, MRE11A, NBN, SMC1A, RBBP8. Specifically, ATM can be inhibited by MIR100/421/18A/101-1.     Signaling description: DNA repair by means of homologous recombination takes place when an identical or a near identical sequence is available to provide the template for DNA recovery. The protein machinery responsible for this recovery process is similar to that of the process of meiotic crossing-over when homologous chromatids serve as templates. The double strand bases (DSBs) appear as a result of mistakes in filling single-nucleotide gaps in the replicative fork or as a result of unfixed errors that occurred during single-stranded DNA synthesis. After DSBs are detected by ATM, the 5' strands are removed producing long 3' single-stranded DNA tails. These 3' tails serve as the substrate for RAD51 nucleoprotein filament nucleation. RAD52, BRCA1, and BRCA2 proteins are also recruited in this process. Once assembled, this protein complex screens the genome for homologous DNA sequences that can be used for the recombination.Transcription of BRCA1 is negatively regulated by HMGA2. The latter can be mutated and overexpressed or activated by downregulation of MIR15A/161/26A1, MIRLET7A1. BRCA1 also can be inhibited by MIR182.     Outcome effects: The deregulation of double strand DNA homologous repair machinery leads to genomic instability in cancer cells. Persistent DNA repair affects cell cycle regulation and may be associated with oncogenesis.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details." EXACT []

