xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Several proteins involved in double strand DNA non-homologous repair can be mutated in cancer, including ATM, MRE11A, NBN, TP53BP1, RAD50, H2AFX, PRKDC, XRCC5/6, WRN, POLB, FEN1.    Signaling description: Double strand DNA breaks are common in eukaryotic cells and the two major pathways for repairing them are homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous DNA end joining (NHEJ). NHEJ double strand repair recovers two broken DNA ends without using a homologous template. XRCC6/XRCC5 heterodimer protein associates with free DNA ends and recruits other factors including DNA-dependent protein kinase (PRKDC). During DNA binding, PRKDC becomes activated and phosphorylates multiple proteins including DNA ligase IV (LIG4) cofactor XRCC4. The final step of NHEJ involves the ligation of DNA ends by LIG4. Microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) is XRCC6/XRCC5-independent NHEJ and involves short homologous sequences called microhomologies, which exist in single-stranded overhangs on the ends of double-stranded gaps. MMEJ is based on ligating hung mismatched DNA ends that remove overhanging nucleotides and fill the gaps. MMEJ results in removal of mutations from the genetic code. In most cases, MMEJ is used only when NHEJ is impossible.    Outcome effects: Deregulation of double strand DNA non-homologous repair machinery leads to genomic instability in cancer cells. Persistent DNA repair affects cell cycle regulation and may be associated with oncogenesis.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Several proteins involved in double strand DNA non-homologous repair can be mutated in cancer, including ATM, MRE11A, NBN, TP53BP1, RAD50, H2AFX, PRKDC, XRCC5/6, WRN, POLB, FEN1.    Signaling description: Double strand DNA breaks are common in eukaryotic cells and the two major pathways for repairing them are homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous DNA end joining (NHEJ). NHEJ double strand repair recovers two broken DNA ends without using a homologous template. XRCC6/XRCC5 heterodimer protein associates with free DNA ends and recruits other factors including DNA-dependent protein kinase (PRKDC). During DNA binding, PRKDC becomes activated and phosphorylates multiple proteins including DNA ligase IV (LIG4) cofactor XRCC4. The final step of NHEJ involves the ligation of DNA ends by LIG4. Microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) is XRCC6/XRCC5-independent NHEJ and involves short homologous sequences called microhomologies, which exist in single-stranded overhangs on the ends of double-stranded gaps. MMEJ is based on ligating hung mismatched DNA ends that remove overhanging nucleotides and fill the gaps. MMEJ results in removal of mutations from the genetic code. In most cases, MMEJ is used only when NHEJ is impossible.    Outcome effects: Deregulation of double strand DNA non-homologous repair machinery leads to genomic instability in cancer cells. Persistent DNA repair affects cell cycle regulation and may be associated with oncogenesis.     Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details." EXACT []

