xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Direct DNA repair includes single-enzyme mechanisms that can repair lesions without the incision of DNA sugar-phosphate backbone or base excision. In humans, the main type of direct DNA damage reversal systems repair alkylating damage via the action of alkyltransferases and dioxygenases. Biologically important lesions formed in double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) are 6-methoxyguanine, 3-methylcytosine, and 1-methyladenine. Dioxygenases ALKBH2 and ALKBH3 preferentially repair 1-methyladenine and 3-methylcytosine lesions by oxidative demethylation. MGMT (Methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase or O-6-methylguanine-DNA-alkyltransferase) directly dealkylates mutagenic 6-methoxyguanine lesions within DNA.Methylation of MGMT promoter has been associated with several cancer types, including colorectal cancer, lung cancer, lymphoma and glioblastoma.DNMT1 and MECP2 are inhibitors of MGMT. They can be mutated in cancer and exhibit an increased inhibitory activity. MGMT also can be down-regulated by MIR181D in glioma, glioblastoma and other neoplasms.     Outcome effects: silencing of MGMT impairs direct DNA repair and promotes genomic instability in cancer cells.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Direct DNA repair includes single-enzyme mechanisms that can repair lesions without the incision of DNA sugar-phosphate backbone or base excision. In humans, the main type of direct DNA damage reversal systems repair alkylating damage via the action of alkyltransferases and dioxygenases. Biologically important lesions formed in double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) are 6-methoxyguanine, 3-methylcytosine, and 1-methyladenine. Dioxygenases ALKBH2 and ALKBH3 preferentially repair 1-methyladenine and 3-methylcytosine lesions by oxidative demethylation. MGMT (Methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase or O-6-methylguanine-DNA-alkyltransferase) directly dealkylates mutagenic 6-methoxyguanine lesions within DNA.Methylation of MGMT promoter has been associated with several cancer types, including colorectal cancer, lung cancer, lymphoma and glioblastoma.DNMT1 and MECP2 are inhibitors of MGMT. They can be mutated in cancer and exhibit an increased inhibitory activity. MGMT also can be down-regulated by MIR181D in glioma, glioblastoma and other neoplasms.     Outcome effects: silencing of MGMT impairs direct DNA repair and promotes genomic instability in cancer cells.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutation Associated with Impaired DNA Repair in Cancer\" for details." EXACT []

