xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The expression of EZH2 is regulated by HIF1A, E2F1, RAF1/MAPK/ELK1 and other regulators whose pathways can be activated in cancer. Mutation or over-expression of EZH2 has been implicated in many forms of cancer.     Signaling description: EZH2 is a methyltransferase that participates in histone methylation and, consequently, transcriptional repression. EZH2 is the catalytic subunit of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2) involved in embryonic differentiation. The increased levels of EZH2 have been linked to H3K27 (histone H3 family member) trimethylation on promoter regions of several genes. Specifically, EZH1 inhibits the transcription of RAD51, RUNX3, CDKN1C, FOXC1, CDH1, IRF4, PRDM1. Among these, RAD51 controls DNA repair, and, therefore, EZH1 suppresses DNA repair through RAD51 inhibition. Further, RUNX3 controls the expression of CDKN1A, an inhibitor of CCNE1 and CDK2. CDKN1C also inhibits CCNE1. EZH1 activates cell cycle progression through the suppression of RUNX3 and CDKN1C. FOXC1 and CDH1 control cell invasion and metastasis. RUNX3 may influence these processes through FOXC1 and CDH1 inhibition. IRF4 and PRDM1 control B-cell differentiation. EZH1 may block B-cell differentiation through inhibition of these genes in B-cell neoplasms. Elevated concentrations of EZH2 block the phosphorylation of BRCA1 (EZH acts on BRCA1 through PI3K/AKT1). As the negative effect of BRCA1 on CDC25C is eliminated, it can activate CDK1 and thereby trigger mitosis. EZH2 may also directly activate CTNNB1, RELA, STAT3, AR and thus stimulate cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: The activation and overexpression of EZH2 in cancer leads to cell cycle activation, inhibition of DNA repair, activation of cell invasion and inhibition of B-cell differentiation. A large number of EZH2 inhibitors are tested in clinical trials, e.g. 3-deazaneplanocin A.     Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The expression of EZH2 is regulated by HIF1A, E2F1, RAF1/MAPK/ELK1 and other regulators whose pathways can be activated in cancer. Mutation or over-expression of EZH2 has been implicated in many forms of cancer.     Signaling description: EZH2 is a methyltransferase that participates in histone methylation and, consequently, transcriptional repression. EZH2 is the catalytic subunit of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2) involved in embryonic differentiation. The increased levels of EZH2 have been linked to H3K27 (histone H3 family member) trimethylation on promoter regions of several genes. Specifically, EZH1 inhibits the transcription of RAD51, RUNX3, CDKN1C, FOXC1, CDH1, IRF4, PRDM1. Among these, RAD51 controls DNA repair, and, therefore, EZH1 suppresses DNA repair through RAD51 inhibition. Further, RUNX3 controls the expression of CDKN1A, an inhibitor of CCNE1 and CDK2. CDKN1C also inhibits CCNE1. EZH1 activates cell cycle progression through the suppression of RUNX3 and CDKN1C. FOXC1 and CDH1 control cell invasion and metastasis. RUNX3 may influence these processes through FOXC1 and CDH1 inhibition. IRF4 and PRDM1 control B-cell differentiation. EZH1 may block B-cell differentiation through inhibition of these genes in B-cell neoplasms. Elevated concentrations of EZH2 block the phosphorylation of BRCA1 (EZH acts on BRCA1 through PI3K/AKT1). As the negative effect of BRCA1 on CDC25C is eliminated, it can activate CDK1 and thereby trigger mitosis. EZH2 may also directly activate CTNNB1, RELA, STAT3, AR and thus stimulate cell proliferation.    Outcome effects: The activation and overexpression of EZH2 in cancer leads to cell cycle activation, inhibition of DNA repair, activation of cell invasion and inhibition of B-cell differentiation. A large number of EZH2 inhibitors are tested in clinical trials, e.g. 3-deazaneplanocin A.     Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity.     Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

