xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epigenetic changes including DNA methylation participate in the onset of genomic instability and progression of cancer in numerous tumor types. Global DNA hypomethylation and promoter-specific hypermethylation are commonly observed in neoplasias.    Signaling description: The maintenance of appropriate DNA methylation within the CpG dinucleotide islands is involved in regulation of a wide variety of molecular processes including stability of chromosomal structure and control of gene expression. In general, DNA methylation in promoter regions results in gene silencing. Many hundreds of genes can be inactivated in a single cancer by promoter methylation. Tumor cell-specific promoter hypermethylation in genes which affects cell cycle, apoptosis, DNA repair, differentiation, and cell adhesion is often a hallmark of neoplasms.The proteins that mediate these changes are encoded by DNA methyltransferases (DNMT) and methyl-CpG-binding domain (MBD) proteins. DNMT1/3A/3B, MBD4 can be mutated in cancer. DNMT1 can be hyper-activated by PKC, PCNA, AKT1, KDM1A, H.pylori - activated NFkB signaling and other mechanisms. DNMT3A/3B can be activated due to down-regulation of certain MIRs. In turn, DNMTs and MBDs regulate the expression/function of target genes.    Outcome effects: A large number of genes can be inactivated in a single cancer by promoter methylation resulting in their impaired function. We show only selected genes as examples: CDKN2A, CDKN1A (cell cycle), BRCA1 (DNA repair), CDH1 (cell adhesion), MGMT (DNA alkylation), histone H3 proteins (chromatin remodeling).Pharmacological targeting of DNA methylation is a promising therapeutic approach. A number of DNA methylation inhibitors are currently under investigation, including decitabine, 5-azacitidine, hydralazine and others.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations and Altered Expression Associated with Dysregulation of DNA Methylation in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations and Altered Expression Associated with Dysregulation of DNA Methylation in Cancer\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epigenetic changes including DNA methylation participate in the onset of genomic instability and progression of cancer in numerous tumor types. Global DNA hypomethylation and promoter-specific hypermethylation are commonly observed in neoplasias.    Signaling description: The maintenance of appropriate DNA methylation within the CpG dinucleotide islands is involved in regulation of a wide variety of molecular processes including stability of chromosomal structure and control of gene expression. In general, DNA methylation in promoter regions results in gene silencing. Many hundreds of genes can be inactivated in a single cancer by promoter methylation. Tumor cell-specific promoter hypermethylation in genes which affects cell cycle, apoptosis, DNA repair, differentiation, and cell adhesion is often a hallmark of neoplasms.The proteins that mediate these changes are encoded by DNA methyltransferases (DNMT) and methyl-CpG-binding domain (MBD) proteins. DNMT1/3A/3B, MBD4 can be mutated in cancer. DNMT1 can be hyper-activated by PKC, PCNA, AKT1, KDM1A, H.pylori - activated NFkB signaling and other mechanisms. DNMT3A/3B can be activated due to down-regulation of certain MIRs. In turn, DNMTs and MBDs regulate the expression/function of target genes.    Outcome effects: A large number of genes can be inactivated in a single cancer by promoter methylation resulting in their impaired function. We show only selected genes as examples: CDKN2A, CDKN1A (cell cycle), BRCA1 (DNA repair), CDH1 (cell adhesion), MGMT (DNA alkylation), histone H3 proteins (chromatin remodeling).Pharmacological targeting of DNA methylation is a promising therapeutic approach. A number of DNA methylation inhibitors are currently under investigation, including decitabine, 5-azacitidine, hydralazine and others.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. Proteins with blue highlight have decreased expression / activity. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations and Altered Expression Associated with Dysregulation of DNA Methylation in Cancer\" for details.    Mutated proteins: Mutated proteins are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations and Altered Expression Associated with Dysregulation of DNA Methylation in Cancer\" for details." EXACT []

