xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: EPCAM is a transmembrane glycoprotein which mediates intercellular adhesion in epithelia, is involved in cell signaling, migration, proliferation, and differentiation and has oncogenic potential. EPCAM is expressed exclusively in epithelia and epithelial-derived neoplasms.    Signaling description: EPCAM is cleaved by PSEN2 (which is a part of the gamma-secretase complex), ADAM17 and BACE1. Upon cleavage, the extracellular domain (EpEX) is released into the area surrounding the cell, and the intracellular domain (EpICD) is released into the cytoplasm of the cell. EpICD forms a complex with FHL2, CTNNB1, and LEF1 proteins inside the nucleus. This complex then binds to DNA and positively regulates the transcription of various genes including MYC CCND1 and CCNE1, which promotes tumor growth. CTNNB1 is involved in the activation of NANOG, POU5F1 and SOX2, thus, mediating epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT). EPCAM can inhibit CDH1-dependent cell-to-cell adhesion by disrupting the link between CTNNA1 and F-actin. EPCAM potentiates EMT and cell migration through inhibition of CDH1. EPCAM directly binds to KREMLEN1 and disrupts the KREMLEN1-DKK2 interaction, which prevents KREMLEN1-DKK2-mediated removal of LRP6 from the cell surface. In this way, EPCAM mediates WNT signaling activation. EPCAM directly regulates CD44, BSG (CD147), CD9, TSPAN8 and some other proteins implicated in cancer metastatic progression.    Outcome effects: EPCAM potentiates cancer cell migration and proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: EPCAM is a transmembrane glycoprotein which mediates intercellular adhesion in epithelia, is involved in cell signaling, migration, proliferation, and differentiation and has oncogenic potential. EPCAM is expressed exclusively in epithelia and epithelial-derived neoplasms.    Signaling description: EPCAM is cleaved by PSEN2 (which is a part of the gamma-secretase complex), ADAM17 and BACE1. Upon cleavage, the extracellular domain (EpEX) is released into the area surrounding the cell, and the intracellular domain (EpICD) is released into the cytoplasm of the cell. EpICD forms a complex with FHL2, CTNNB1, and LEF1 proteins inside the nucleus. This complex then binds to DNA and positively regulates the transcription of various genes including MYC CCND1 and CCNE1, which promotes tumor growth. CTNNB1 is involved in the activation of NANOG, POU5F1 and SOX2, thus, mediating epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT). EPCAM can inhibit CDH1-dependent cell-to-cell adhesion by disrupting the link between CTNNA1 and F-actin. EPCAM potentiates EMT and cell migration through inhibition of CDH1. EPCAM directly binds to KREMLEN1 and disrupts the KREMLEN1-DKK2 interaction, which prevents KREMLEN1-DKK2-mediated removal of LRP6 from the cell surface. In this way, EPCAM mediates WNT signaling activation. EPCAM directly regulates CD44, BSG (CD147), CD9, TSPAN8 and some other proteins implicated in cancer metastatic progression.    Outcome effects: EPCAM potentiates cancer cell migration and proliferation.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

