xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Integrins are transmembrane receptors that facilitate cell-to-matrix adhesion. Ligands for integrins include fibronectin (FN1), vitronectin (VTN), collagens, laminins, and some other proteins. Upon ligand binding, integrins activate signal transduction pathways that mediate cellular processes such as regulation of cell cycle and organization of cytoskeleton. Integrins are obligate heterodimers, each of them having two subunits, alpha and beta. Mammalian integrins have eighteen alpha and eight beta subunit variants.    Signaling description: A wide variety of integrins contribute to tumor progression including VLA-4 (integrin alpha 4 beta 1), VLA-2 (integrin alpha 2 beta 1), integrin alpha V beta 6, VLA-5 (integrin alpha V beta 1), integrin alpha 5 beta 5, vitronectin receptor (integrin alpha 5 beta 3), integrin alpha 6 beta 4, VLA-6 (integrin alpha 6 beta 1), VLA-3 (integrin alpha 3 beta 1), integrin alpha 11 beta 1, and others. Although integrins themselves lack kinase activity, they recruit and activate kinases, such as PTK2, SRC and some others through clustering. Integrins also couple the ECM to actin cytoskeleton by recruiting proteins such asTLN1, PXN, VCL, and others. In tumor cells, cooperation between integrins and members of the EGF receptor family affect many aspects of tumour progression. Interactions between EGFR / ERBB2 and integrin alpha 6 beta 4 / VLA-6 /VLA-3 were described in various types of cancer. Integrins are prime regulators of adhesion, spreading, and migration of cells. The integrin signaling activates RAC1, CDC42, RHOA through PTK1/BCAR1/DOCK1, resulting in actin polymerization. The MMP-mediated ECM degradation is necessary for cancer cell invasion. MMPs may be overexpressed due to integrin-induced MAPK/JUN-FOS activation. Vitronectin receptor - bound DMP1, IBSP and SPP1 specifically bind and activate MMP2/3/9 respectively. Integrin ligation enhances cell survival through several mechanisms, including increased expression of CFLAR, activation of the PI3K-AKT pathway with subsequent upregulation of BCL2 and downregulation of BAX, BAD, CASP3/8. Integrins promote cell cycle progression through RAC-dependent and JUN/FOS-dependent CCND1 over-expression.    Outcome effects: Activation of cancer cell motility, invasion, proliferation and survival.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Integrins are transmembrane receptors that facilitate cell-to-matrix adhesion. Ligands for integrins include fibronectin (FN1), vitronectin (VTN), collagens, laminins, and some other proteins. Upon ligand binding, integrins activate signal transduction pathways that mediate cellular processes such as regulation of cell cycle and organization of cytoskeleton. Integrins are obligate heterodimers, each of them having two subunits, alpha and beta. Mammalian integrins have eighteen alpha and eight beta subunit variants.    Signaling description: A wide variety of integrins contribute to tumor progression including VLA-4 (integrin alpha 4 beta 1), VLA-2 (integrin alpha 2 beta 1), integrin alpha V beta 6, VLA-5 (integrin alpha V beta 1), integrin alpha 5 beta 5, vitronectin receptor (integrin alpha 5 beta 3), integrin alpha 6 beta 4, VLA-6 (integrin alpha 6 beta 1), VLA-3 (integrin alpha 3 beta 1), integrin alpha 11 beta 1, and others. Although integrins themselves lack kinase activity, they recruit and activate kinases, such as PTK2, SRC and some others through clustering. Integrins also couple the ECM to actin cytoskeleton by recruiting proteins such asTLN1, PXN, VCL, and others. In tumor cells, cooperation between integrins and members of the EGF receptor family affect many aspects of tumour progression. Interactions between EGFR / ERBB2 and integrin alpha 6 beta 4 / VLA-6 /VLA-3 were described in various types of cancer. Integrins are prime regulators of adhesion, spreading, and migration of cells. The integrin signaling activates RAC1, CDC42, RHOA through PTK1/BCAR1/DOCK1, resulting in actin polymerization. The MMP-mediated ECM degradation is necessary for cancer cell invasion. MMPs may be overexpressed due to integrin-induced MAPK/JUN-FOS activation. Vitronectin receptor - bound DMP1, IBSP and SPP1 specifically bind and activate MMP2/3/9 respectively. Integrin ligation enhances cell survival through several mechanisms, including increased expression of CFLAR, activation of the PI3K-AKT pathway with subsequent upregulation of BCL2 and downregulation of BAX, BAD, CASP3/8. Integrins promote cell cycle progression through RAC-dependent and JUN/FOS-dependent CCND1 over-expression.    Outcome effects: Activation of cancer cell motility, invasion, proliferation and survival.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

