xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), the process first noted during embryogenesis, guides the transformation of non-mobile epithelial-like cells into mobile, mesenchymal-like cells that have the potential to travel to distant anatomical sites. This process has also been observed during tumor formation and may lead to the development of metastatic dissemination.    Signaling description: Induction of EMT in cancer cells can occur as a result of embryonic signaling. Activation of SHH, WNT, NOTCH, BMP or TGFB signaling leads to the upregulation of transcriptional factors that drive EMT. Other factors implicated in EMT signaling induction include EGFR/ERBB2, PDGFR, FGFR, DDR1/2, IGF1R, IL6R, integrins and hypoxia-induced HIF1A.Transcriptional factors that drive EMT include SNAI1/2/3, ZEB1/2, TWIST1, GSC, FOXC1/2, FOXQ1, TCF3, KLF8, PRRX1, CTNNB1, ID1/2, SMAD, MYOCD and some others. These transcription factors down-regulate proteins responsible for cell adhesion and inhibiting cell motility (ex., CDH1, OCLN, CLDN1/7, TJP1, KRT18/19) and up-regulate proteins that inhibit cell adhesion and activate cell migration (e. g. CDH2, VIM, FN1, TNC, MMP2/9/15).    Outcome effects: This process leads to the transformation of adhesive, non-mobile, epithelial-like tumor cells into cells with a mobile, invasive phenotype.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), the process first noted during embryogenesis, guides the transformation of non-mobile epithelial-like cells into mobile, mesenchymal-like cells that have the potential to travel to distant anatomical sites. This process has also been observed during tumor formation and may lead to the development of metastatic dissemination.    Signaling description: Induction of EMT in cancer cells can occur as a result of embryonic signaling. Activation of SHH, WNT, NOTCH, BMP or TGFB signaling leads to the upregulation of transcriptional factors that drive EMT. Other factors implicated in EMT signaling induction include EGFR/ERBB2, PDGFR, FGFR, DDR1/2, IGF1R, IL6R, integrins and hypoxia-induced HIF1A.Transcriptional factors that drive EMT include SNAI1/2/3, ZEB1/2, TWIST1, GSC, FOXC1/2, FOXQ1, TCF3, KLF8, PRRX1, CTNNB1, ID1/2, SMAD, MYOCD and some others. These transcription factors down-regulate proteins responsible for cell adhesion and inhibiting cell motility (ex., CDH1, OCLN, CLDN1/7, TJP1, KRT18/19) and up-regulate proteins that inhibit cell adhesion and activate cell migration (e. g. CDH2, VIM, FN1, TNC, MMP2/9/15).    Outcome effects: This process leads to the transformation of adhesive, non-mobile, epithelial-like tumor cells into cells with a mobile, invasive phenotype.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

