xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Basigin (BSG) also known as extracellular matrix metalloproteinase inducer (EMMPRIN) or cluster of differentiation 147 (CD147) is highly up-regulated in malignant cancer cells and stimulates hyaluronic acid production, as well as, several downstream signaling pathways.    Signaling description: BSG is upregulated in cancer by multiple transcription factors including SP1/3, MYC, STAT3, HIF1A, EGR2, SNAI2 and others. BSG stimulates hyaluronic acid production and CD44 activation with subsequent activation of metastatic progression. BSG interacts with integrins VLA2/3/6. The integrin signaling leads to cell migration and activation of cell proliferation. BSG may block apoptosis/anoikis through MAPK1/3 - dependent inactivation of BCL2L11. The precise mechanism of MAPK1/3 activation by BSG is unknown. BSG may also regulate autophagy via BECN1 inactivation. In addition, BSG interacts with monocarboxylate transporters SLC16A1/3. Malignant cancer cells use the glycolytic pathway and require monocarboxylate transporters to efflux lactate that results from glycolysis. Glycolysis and lactate secretion contribute to malignant cell behaviors and drug resistance in tumor cells. BSG promote MMP1/2/3/9/11/13/14/15 production and secretion by fibroblasts, endothelial cells, macrophages and cancer cells, contributing to cell invasion. The precise mechanism of MMPs regulation by BSG is not clear.    Outcome effects: BSG promotes cancer cell motility, invasion, proliferation, survival, drug resistance and cancer-specific metabolism.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Basigin (BSG) also known as extracellular matrix metalloproteinase inducer (EMMPRIN) or cluster of differentiation 147 (CD147) is highly up-regulated in malignant cancer cells and stimulates hyaluronic acid production, as well as, several downstream signaling pathways.    Signaling description: BSG is upregulated in cancer by multiple transcription factors including SP1/3, MYC, STAT3, HIF1A, EGR2, SNAI2 and others. BSG stimulates hyaluronic acid production and CD44 activation with subsequent activation of metastatic progression. BSG interacts with integrins VLA2/3/6. The integrin signaling leads to cell migration and activation of cell proliferation. BSG may block apoptosis/anoikis through MAPK1/3 - dependent inactivation of BCL2L11. The precise mechanism of MAPK1/3 activation by BSG is unknown. BSG may also regulate autophagy via BECN1 inactivation. In addition, BSG interacts with monocarboxylate transporters SLC16A1/3. Malignant cancer cells use the glycolytic pathway and require monocarboxylate transporters to efflux lactate that results from glycolysis. Glycolysis and lactate secretion contribute to malignant cell behaviors and drug resistance in tumor cells. BSG promote MMP1/2/3/9/11/13/14/15 production and secretion by fibroblasts, endothelial cells, macrophages and cancer cells, contributing to cell invasion. The precise mechanism of MMPs regulation by BSG is not clear.    Outcome effects: BSG promotes cancer cell motility, invasion, proliferation, survival, drug resistance and cancer-specific metabolism.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

