xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: CDH1 (cadherin-1, E-cadherin) is a crucial type of cell-cell adhesion molecules and works at adherens junctions to hold epithelial cells tightly together. CDH1 can sequester CTNNB1 to the cell membrane.    Signaling description: CDH1 may be down-regulated in different types of cancers due to transcriptional suppression or mutations. Several transcription factors such as SNAI1/2/3, ZEB1/2, TWIST1, KLF8, TCF3, FOXQ1, PRRX1, FOXC1/2, GSC down-regulate CDH1 expression. EPCAM also may down-regulate CDH1. The loss of CDH1 expression results in CTNNB1 release into the cytoplasm. Normally, upon CDH1 loss, liberated CTNNB1 is rapidly phosphorylated by APC/AXIN1/GSK3B complex and subsequently degraded by the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway. If APC or AXIN1 is nonfunctional, as is the case in many cancer cells, or if GSK3B and AXIN1 is repressed by an activated WNT signaling pathway, CTNNB1 accumulates at high levels in the cytoplasm. Subsequently, it translocates to the nucleus, where it binds to TCF4 and LEF1 and modulates the expression of epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT)-inducing transcription factors. Cytoplasmic CTNND1 is bound to CDH1 and activates the RHO-family GTPases RAC1 and CDC42 and represses RHOA. PI3K is recruited to the membrane by CDH1 and activates TIAM1 and subsequently activates RAC1 and CDC42. cells.    Outcome effects: Taken together, these activities affect the organization of actin cytoskeleton and possibly the migratory behavior of tumour cells, as follows: activated CDC42 and RAC1 promote the formation of filopodia and lamellipodia, respectively.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metastasis\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: CDH1 (cadherin-1, E-cadherin) is a crucial type of cell-cell adhesion molecules and works at adherens junctions to hold epithelial cells tightly together. CDH1 can sequester CTNNB1 to the cell membrane.    Signaling description: CDH1 may be down-regulated in different types of cancers due to transcriptional suppression or mutations. Several transcription factors such as SNAI1/2/3, ZEB1/2, TWIST1, KLF8, TCF3, FOXQ1, PRRX1, FOXC1/2, GSC down-regulate CDH1 expression. EPCAM also may down-regulate CDH1. The loss of CDH1 expression results in CTNNB1 release into the cytoplasm. Normally, upon CDH1 loss, liberated CTNNB1 is rapidly phosphorylated by APC/AXIN1/GSK3B complex and subsequently degraded by the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway. If APC or AXIN1 is nonfunctional, as is the case in many cancer cells, or if GSK3B and AXIN1 is repressed by an activated WNT signaling pathway, CTNNB1 accumulates at high levels in the cytoplasm. Subsequently, it translocates to the nucleus, where it binds to TCF4 and LEF1 and modulates the expression of epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT)-inducing transcription factors. Cytoplasmic CTNND1 is bound to CDH1 and activates the RHO-family GTPases RAC1 and CDC42 and represses RHOA. PI3K is recruited to the membrane by CDH1 and activates TIAM1 and subsequently activates RAC1 and CDC42. cells.    Outcome effects: Taken together, these activities affect the organization of actin cytoskeleton and possibly the migratory behavior of tumour cells, as follows: activated CDC42 and RAC1 promote the formation of filopodia and lamellipodia, respectively.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metastasis\" for details." EXACT []

