xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyaluronic acid (HA) and it's receptors, CD44 and HMMR (CD168) are upregulated in cancers and involved in motility, invasion, proliferation and survival of cancer cells.    Signaling description: HA is produced by hyaluronic acid synthases 1/2/3 (HAS1/2/3) and is involved at all stages of cancer progression. HA associates with CD44, HMMR and LYVE1 receptors. Activated CD44 can act through different signaling pathways. CD44 stimulates PKCE activity, which, in turn, causes NANOG phosphorylation and NANOG-STAT3 complex association with the MIR21 promoter. Then, the upregulation of MIR21 decreases the expression PDCD4 and increases the expression of XIAP, BIRC2/3 and BCL2 thereby inhibiting apoptosis and promoting cell survival. MIR21 also induce chemoresistance through up-regulation of ABCB1 drug transporter. CD44 also can regulate MIR21 expression through MAPK8/JUN pathway by an unknown mechanism. In SRC/TWIST1 signaling, CD44 induces the expression of MIR10B with subsequent over-expression of RHOA/C and induction of cancer cell migration / invasion. SRC along with GRB2 and GAB1 can also activate PI3K/AKT signaling, thus promoting cell proliferation. CD44 upregulates MIR302A through the NANOG/SOX2A/POU5F1 signaling complex. MIR302A then downregulates histone demethylases KDM1A/B and DNA (cytosine-5)-methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) and induces DNA demethylation leading to anti-apoptotic BIRC2/3 and XIAP expression. CD44 can also directly interact with EZR-RDX-MSX complex facilitating actin organization. CD44 directly interacts with EGFR and ERBB2 and activates cancer-related signaling through these receptors. The hyaluronan receptor HMMR acts in tight cooperation with CD44 and enhances it's signaling. It also forms complexes with MAPK1/2 that sustain high basal motility of cancer cells through the activation of microtubules.    Outcome effects: The hyaluronic acid signaling through CD44 and HMMR receptors leads to a great variety of cancer-promoting effects, including activation of cell motility, invasion and proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and induction of drug resistance.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyaluronic acid (HA) and it's receptors, CD44 and HMMR (CD168) are upregulated in cancers and involved in motility, invasion, proliferation and survival of cancer cells.    Signaling description: HA is produced by hyaluronic acid synthases 1/2/3 (HAS1/2/3) and is involved at all stages of cancer progression. HA associates with CD44, HMMR and LYVE1 receptors. Activated CD44 can act through different signaling pathways. CD44 stimulates PKCE activity, which, in turn, causes NANOG phosphorylation and NANOG-STAT3 complex association with the MIR21 promoter. Then, the upregulation of MIR21 decreases the expression PDCD4 and increases the expression of XIAP, BIRC2/3 and BCL2 thereby inhibiting apoptosis and promoting cell survival. MIR21 also induce chemoresistance through up-regulation of ABCB1 drug transporter. CD44 also can regulate MIR21 expression through MAPK8/JUN pathway by an unknown mechanism. In SRC/TWIST1 signaling, CD44 induces the expression of MIR10B with subsequent over-expression of RHOA/C and induction of cancer cell migration / invasion. SRC along with GRB2 and GAB1 can also activate PI3K/AKT signaling, thus promoting cell proliferation. CD44 upregulates MIR302A through the NANOG/SOX2A/POU5F1 signaling complex. MIR302A then downregulates histone demethylases KDM1A/B and DNA (cytosine-5)-methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) and induces DNA demethylation leading to anti-apoptotic BIRC2/3 and XIAP expression. CD44 can also directly interact with EZR-RDX-MSX complex facilitating actin organization. CD44 directly interacts with EGFR and ERBB2 and activates cancer-related signaling through these receptors. The hyaluronan receptor HMMR acts in tight cooperation with CD44 and enhances it's signaling. It also forms complexes with MAPK1/2 that sustain high basal motility of cancer cells through the activation of microtubules.    Outcome effects: The hyaluronic acid signaling through CD44 and HMMR receptors leads to a great variety of cancer-promoting effects, including activation of cell motility, invasion and proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and induction of drug resistance.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

