xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), the process first noted during embryogenesis, guides the transformation of non-mobile epithelial-like cells into mobile, mesenchymal-like cells that have the potential to travel to distant anatomical sites. This process has also been observed during tumor formation and may lead to the development of metastatic dissemination. TGFB signaling has a major role in EMT.    Signaling description: TGFB activates several signaling routes, leading to EMT. The first is TGFBR/SMAD signaling activating specific embryonic transcription factors ZEB1/2 and SNAI1/2. These transcription factors down-regulate proteins responsible for cell adhesion and inhibiting cell motility (ex., CDH1, OCLN, CLDN1/7, TJP1, KRT18/19) and up-regulate proteins inhibiting cell adhesion and activating cell migration (ex., CDH2, VIM, FN1, TNC, MMP2/9/15). The TGFBR/RAS/MAPK activation leads to SMADs regulation as well. The second is PI3K/AKT1/MTOR signaling which has it's own role in cell motility activation and invasion. The third signaling path involves TGFBR interaction with CDC42 and RHOA that activate cell migration through induction of actin polymerization.    Outcome effects: Theses processes lead to the transformation of adhesive, non-mobile, epithelial-like tumor cells into cells with a mobile, invasive phenotype.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), the process first noted during embryogenesis, guides the transformation of non-mobile epithelial-like cells into mobile, mesenchymal-like cells that have the potential to travel to distant anatomical sites. This process has also been observed during tumor formation and may lead to the development of metastatic dissemination. TGFB signaling has a major role in EMT.    Signaling description: TGFB activates several signaling routes, leading to EMT. The first is TGFBR/SMAD signaling activating specific embryonic transcription factors ZEB1/2 and SNAI1/2. These transcription factors down-regulate proteins responsible for cell adhesion and inhibiting cell motility (ex., CDH1, OCLN, CLDN1/7, TJP1, KRT18/19) and up-regulate proteins inhibiting cell adhesion and activating cell migration (ex., CDH2, VIM, FN1, TNC, MMP2/9/15). The TGFBR/RAS/MAPK activation leads to SMADs regulation as well. The second is PI3K/AKT1/MTOR signaling which has it's own role in cell motility activation and invasion. The third signaling path involves TGFBR interaction with CDC42 and RHOA that activate cell migration through induction of actin polymerization.    Outcome effects: Theses processes lead to the transformation of adhesive, non-mobile, epithelial-like tumor cells into cells with a mobile, invasive phenotype.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

