xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Invadopodia are dynamic actin-rich membrane protrusions that degrade the extracellular matrix through the local deposition of proteases and are involved in cell invasion. Invadopodia are found on cancer cells and are similar to the podosomes of normal cells.    Signaling description: The activation of EGFR, PDGFR, TGFBR by their ligands has a crucial role in initiation of invadopodia formation. Integrins may have an additional role in this process. The activation of SRC and ABL kinases, PI3K signaling with the formation of PtdIns(3,4) P2 lead to RHOA, CDC42, SH3PXD2A/2B signaling activation. This causes the formation of CTTN/NCK1/WIPF1/WASL complex, that associates with actin related protein 2/3 complex and induces F-actin polymerization. Ion channels (ORAI1, KCNN3, SLC5A1) and AQP1 are also involved in invadopodia formation and cell migration through increasing cell volume during the formation of protrusions. WNT5A/ROR2/IFT20 signaling may have additional functions in invadopodia formation.    Outcome effects: The polymerization of F-actin promotes the formation of invadopodia structure. The molecular transport of MMP2/9/14 to the leading edge of invadopodia and it's exocytosis by RAB8A/40B, the exocyst complex, and some other proteins cause local destruction of extracellular matrix surrounding invadopodia, and, thus, cancer cell invasion.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Invadopodia are dynamic actin-rich membrane protrusions that degrade the extracellular matrix through the local deposition of proteases and are involved in cell invasion. Invadopodia are found on cancer cells and are similar to the podosomes of normal cells.    Signaling description: The activation of EGFR, PDGFR, TGFBR by their ligands has a crucial role in initiation of invadopodia formation. Integrins may have an additional role in this process. The activation of SRC and ABL kinases, PI3K signaling with the formation of PtdIns(3,4) P2 lead to RHOA, CDC42, SH3PXD2A/2B signaling activation. This causes the formation of CTTN/NCK1/WIPF1/WASL complex, that associates with actin related protein 2/3 complex and induces F-actin polymerization. Ion channels (ORAI1, KCNN3, SLC5A1) and AQP1 are also involved in invadopodia formation and cell migration through increasing cell volume during the formation of protrusions. WNT5A/ROR2/IFT20 signaling may have additional functions in invadopodia formation.    Outcome effects: The polymerization of F-actin promotes the formation of invadopodia structure. The molecular transport of MMP2/9/14 to the leading edge of invadopodia and it's exocytosis by RAB8A/40B, the exocyst complex, and some other proteins cause local destruction of extracellular matrix surrounding invadopodia, and, thus, cancer cell invasion.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metastases\" for details." EXACT []

