xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Small non-coding RNAs or microRNAs (MIRs) are commonly known as potent modifiers of gene expression and are involved in the regulation of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), a transformation process in which non-mobile epithelial-like cells become mobile promoting metastatic dissemination.    Signaling description: MIRs and transcription factors involved in EMT (ZEB1/2, SNAI1/2,EZH2) regulate each other in a double-negative feedback manner thereby regulating the EMT process. MIRs were also shown to negatively regulate CDH1 and BMI1.    Outcome effects: MIRs may positively or negatively regulate EMT. The effect depends on the particular type of cancer.    Animal data: not shown     Highlighted proteins: Not shownThe expression of MIRs may differ between different types of cancer.    Mutated proteins: not shown"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Small non-coding RNAs or microRNAs (MIRs) are commonly known as potent modifiers of gene expression and are involved in the regulation of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), a transformation process in which non-mobile epithelial-like cells become mobile promoting metastatic dissemination.    Signaling description: MIRs and transcription factors involved in EMT (ZEB1/2, SNAI1/2,EZH2) regulate each other in a double-negative feedback manner thereby regulating the EMT process. MIRs were also shown to negatively regulate CDH1 and BMI1.    Outcome effects: MIRs may positively or negatively regulate EMT. The effect depends on the particular type of cancer.    Animal data: not shown     Highlighted proteins: Not shownThe expression of MIRs may differ between different types of cancer.    Mutated proteins: not shown" EXACT []

