xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Metabolic reprogramming is one of the hallmarks of cancer and is represented by up-regulated aerobic glycolysis, glutaminolysis, impairment of Krebs cycle, hyperproduction of lactate, as well as other features.    Signaling description: Cancer cells strongly up-regulate glucose uptake and glycolysis compared to normal cells in order to give rise to an increased yield of intermediate glycolytic metabolites and the end product pyruvate (it is called Warburg effect or aerobic glycolysis). Glucose import to the cancer cell is increased due to activation of SLC2A1 and SLC2A3 transporters (they are activated due to HIF1A, MYC, RELA overexpression and TP53 down-regulation). Many glycolytic enzymes are upregulated in cancer including HK1/2, GPI, PFKM, ALDOA, GAPDH, PGK1, PGAM1, ENO1/2, PKM. Conversion of pyruvate to lactate is activated due to upregulation of LDHA. Activation of HIF1A, AKT1, MYC, SNAI1 and inactivation of TP53 and PTEN play a major role in upregulation of glycolytic enzymes in cancer cells. Several enzymes of the Krebs cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle, TCA) have been found mutated in both sporadic and hereditary forms of cancer. They are IDH1/2, SDHA, SDHB, SDHC, SDHD, SDHAF2 and FH. These impairments may lead to production of oncometabolites. See pathway \"Krebs Cycle Enzyme Mutations in Cancer\" for details. Cancer cells exhibit increased consumption of and dependence on glutamine. Glutamine metabolism fuels the tricarboxylic acid cycle, nucleotide and fatty acid biosynthesis, redox balance in cancer cells and promotes protein synthesis. Glutamine enters the cell through transporters such as SLC1A5 and SLC38A5 (upregulated by MYC) and may be converted to glutamate and alpha-ketoglutarate or amino-acids (see PW \"Metabolic Reprogramming in Cancer Overview\" for details). Lactate shuttle (reverse Warburg effect) is transport of lactate from catabolic/stromal cancer cell to anabolic cancer cell. Lactate can be metabolized back to pyruvate or act as a signalling molecule (see pathway \"Lactate as a Signalling Molecule in Cancer Cells\" for details).    Outcome effects: Glycolytic breakdown of glucose and glutaminolysis produce various intermediate metabolites used for biosynthesis of amino acids and proteins, fatty acids and nucleotides and production of energy.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathways \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" and \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathways \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" and \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Metabolic reprogramming is one of the hallmarks of cancer and is represented by up-regulated aerobic glycolysis, glutaminolysis, impairment of Krebs cycle, hyperproduction of lactate, as well as other features.    Signaling description: Cancer cells strongly up-regulate glucose uptake and glycolysis compared to normal cells in order to give rise to an increased yield of intermediate glycolytic metabolites and the end product pyruvate (it is called Warburg effect or aerobic glycolysis). Glucose import to the cancer cell is increased due to activation of SLC2A1 and SLC2A3 transporters (they are activated due to HIF1A, MYC, RELA overexpression and TP53 down-regulation). Many glycolytic enzymes are upregulated in cancer including HK1/2, GPI, PFKM, ALDOA, GAPDH, PGK1, PGAM1, ENO1/2, PKM. Conversion of pyruvate to lactate is activated due to upregulation of LDHA. Activation of HIF1A, AKT1, MYC, SNAI1 and inactivation of TP53 and PTEN play a major role in upregulation of glycolytic enzymes in cancer cells. Several enzymes of the Krebs cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle, TCA) have been found mutated in both sporadic and hereditary forms of cancer. They are IDH1/2, SDHA, SDHB, SDHC, SDHD, SDHAF2 and FH. These impairments may lead to production of oncometabolites. See pathway \"Krebs Cycle Enzyme Mutations in Cancer\" for details. Cancer cells exhibit increased consumption of and dependence on glutamine. Glutamine metabolism fuels the tricarboxylic acid cycle, nucleotide and fatty acid biosynthesis, redox balance in cancer cells and promotes protein synthesis. Glutamine enters the cell through transporters such as SLC1A5 and SLC38A5 (upregulated by MYC) and may be converted to glutamate and alpha-ketoglutarate or amino-acids (see PW \"Metabolic Reprogramming in Cancer Overview\" for details). Lactate shuttle (reverse Warburg effect) is transport of lactate from catabolic/stromal cancer cell to anabolic cancer cell. Lactate can be metabolized back to pyruvate or act as a signalling molecule (see pathway \"Lactate as a Signalling Molecule in Cancer Cells\" for details).    Outcome effects: Glycolytic breakdown of glucose and glutaminolysis produce various intermediate metabolites used for biosynthesis of amino acids and proteins, fatty acids and nucleotides and production of energy.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with blue highlights have reduced expression/activity. Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathways \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" and \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathways \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" and \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details." EXACT []

