xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mitochondria control essential cellular activities including generation of ATP via oxidative phosphorylation. Mutations in mitochondrial DNA are common in primary human cancers.    Signaling description: Mutations in respiratory chain proteins may affect the components of complex I (NADH dehydrogenase (ubiquinone)) including ND1/2/3/4/4L/5/6, complex III (ubiquinol-cytochrome c reductase) including CYTB, complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) including COX1/2/3, TRNL1 and complex V (H+-transporting two-sector ATPase) including ATP6/8. Complex I may also be inhibited by hypoxia/HIF1A-dependent NDUFA4L2. Subunits of complex IV (COX6C/7A1/7C) may be inhibited by SNAI1 that is activated during epithelial to mesenchymal transition in cancer cells. Complex IV may also be down-regulated in TP53-mutated cancers due to the impairment of TP53/ SCO2 interaction.    Outcome effects: The outcome is mitochondrial dysfunction and impairment of oxidative phosphorylation, decrease in ATP production and increase of ADP amount. Importantly, ADP and AMP can activate AMPK and impair MTOR signaling. Complex I and III produce ROS and thereby modulate diverse cellular functions including apoptosis and HIF activation. It is still unclear whether mitochondrial DNA mutations exert a pro- or anti-tumorigenic effect (both effects are observed in different cases). The effect is cancer-type dependent and mutation-dependent.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Respiratory Chain)\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mitochondria control essential cellular activities including generation of ATP via oxidative phosphorylation. Mutations in mitochondrial DNA are common in primary human cancers.    Signaling description: Mutations in respiratory chain proteins may affect the components of complex I (NADH dehydrogenase (ubiquinone)) including ND1/2/3/4/4L/5/6, complex III (ubiquinol-cytochrome c reductase) including CYTB, complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) including COX1/2/3, TRNL1 and complex V (H+-transporting two-sector ATPase) including ATP6/8. Complex I may also be inhibited by hypoxia/HIF1A-dependent NDUFA4L2. Subunits of complex IV (COX6C/7A1/7C) may be inhibited by SNAI1 that is activated during epithelial to mesenchymal transition in cancer cells. Complex IV may also be down-regulated in TP53-mutated cancers due to the impairment of TP53/ SCO2 interaction.    Outcome effects: The outcome is mitochondrial dysfunction and impairment of oxidative phosphorylation, decrease in ATP production and increase of ADP amount. Importantly, ADP and AMP can activate AMPK and impair MTOR signaling. Complex I and III produce ROS and thereby modulate diverse cellular functions including apoptosis and HIF activation. It is still unclear whether mitochondrial DNA mutations exert a pro- or anti-tumorigenic effect (both effects are observed in different cases). The effect is cancer-type dependent and mutation-dependent.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Respiratory Chain)\" for details." EXACT []

