xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Lactate is the end-product of aerobic glycolysis in cancer cells. It may serve as an important signaling molecule and promote cancer progression.    Signaling description: Lactate is the end-product of aerobic glycolysis obtained from pyruvate by over-expressed LDHA (activated by MYC, HIF1A and HSF1). Also, lactate can be shuttled from catabolic/stromal cancer cells to anabolic cancer cells (this phenomena is called the reverse Warburg effect). Lactate import is performed by multiple transporters (SLC16A1/3/4/7). The activity of transporters is regulated by MYC, BSG and AMPK. In addition, lactate may be converted back to pyruvate by LDHB and enter the Krebs cycle. Lactate serves as an important signaling molecule and promotes cancer progression by regulating cell migration, angiogenesis and the immune escape. Lactate activates HIF1A through EGLN1 inhibition. Further, lactate inhibits NFKBIA thereby activating NFKB. Lactate regulates TGFB2 expression via induction of THBS-1, controls hyaluronic acid synthesis and activates it's receptor CD44. NDRG3 is degraded in an EGLN1/VHL-dependent manner in normoxia but is protected from destruction by binding to lactate that accumulates in hypoxia. NDRG3 activates angiogenesis and cell migration through RAF1/MAPK signaling.    Outcome effects: Overexpressed CD44, TGFB1, CXCL8, KDR, VEGFA and PECAM1 promote cancer cell migration and angiogenesis. CD44 may also potentiate glycolysis. Lactate promotes the immune escape by inhibiting monocyte migration and NK cell mediated cytotoxicity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" and for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Lactate is the end-product of aerobic glycolysis in cancer cells. It may serve as an important signaling molecule and promote cancer progression.    Signaling description: Lactate is the end-product of aerobic glycolysis obtained from pyruvate by over-expressed LDHA (activated by MYC, HIF1A and HSF1). Also, lactate can be shuttled from catabolic/stromal cancer cells to anabolic cancer cells (this phenomena is called the reverse Warburg effect). Lactate import is performed by multiple transporters (SLC16A1/3/4/7). The activity of transporters is regulated by MYC, BSG and AMPK. In addition, lactate may be converted back to pyruvate by LDHB and enter the Krebs cycle. Lactate serves as an important signaling molecule and promotes cancer progression by regulating cell migration, angiogenesis and the immune escape. Lactate activates HIF1A through EGLN1 inhibition. Further, lactate inhibits NFKBIA thereby activating NFKB. Lactate regulates TGFB2 expression via induction of THBS-1, controls hyaluronic acid synthesis and activates it's receptor CD44. NDRG3 is degraded in an EGLN1/VHL-dependent manner in normoxia but is protected from destruction by binding to lactate that accumulates in hypoxia. NDRG3 activates angiogenesis and cell migration through RAF1/MAPK signaling.    Outcome effects: Overexpressed CD44, TGFB1, CXCL8, KDR, VEGFA and PECAM1 promote cancer cell migration and angiogenesis. CD44 may also potentiate glycolysis. Lactate promotes the immune escape by inhibiting monocyte migration and NK cell mediated cytotoxicity.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" and for details." EXACT []

