xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Lipolysis is activated in cancer-associated adipocytes (CAAs) and lipogenesis is activated in cancer cells in order to provide building blocks for membrane biogenesis in actively dividing cancer cells.    Signaling description: Lipolysis is activated in CAAs in order to provide fatty acids and glycerol to actively dividing adjacent cancer cells. It is not quite clear how exactly cancer cells make adipocytes \"melt\", and probably may occur via the production and local action of different cytokines. Lipolytic enzymes ATGL (PNPLA2), LIPE, MGLL and fatty acid transport protein FABP4 were shown to be up-regulated in CAAs. NFKB and PKA may be involved in their activation. Anabolic cancer cells also were shown to be able to produce lipids de novo from glucose. EGFR/PI3K/AKT1 signaling may regulate this process. A number of lipogenic enzymes including ACLY, ACACA/B, FASN, SCD, AGPAT2 were shown to be up-regulated in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: Cancer cells produce triglycerides for membrane synthesis. The activation of ATGL and LIPE has a role in cancer-associated cachexia.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathways \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathways \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Lipolysis is activated in cancer-associated adipocytes (CAAs) and lipogenesis is activated in cancer cells in order to provide building blocks for membrane biogenesis in actively dividing cancer cells.    Signaling description: Lipolysis is activated in CAAs in order to provide fatty acids and glycerol to actively dividing adjacent cancer cells. It is not quite clear how exactly cancer cells make adipocytes \"melt\", and probably may occur via the production and local action of different cytokines. Lipolytic enzymes ATGL (PNPLA2), LIPE, MGLL and fatty acid transport protein FABP4 were shown to be up-regulated in CAAs. NFKB and PKA may be involved in their activation. Anabolic cancer cells also were shown to be able to produce lipids de novo from glucose. EGFR/PI3K/AKT1 signaling may regulate this process. A number of lipogenic enzymes including ACLY, ACACA/B, FASN, SCD, AGPAT2 were shown to be up-regulated in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: Cancer cells produce triglycerides for membrane synthesis. The activation of ATGL and LIPE has a role in cancer-associated cachexia.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathways \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathways \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details." EXACT []

