xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cancer cells display increased consumption of and dependence on glutamine. Glutamine metabolism fuels the tricarboxylic acid cycle (TCA), nucleotide and fatty acid biosynthesis, and redox balance in cancer cells, activates mTOR signaling and promotes protein synthesis.    Signaling description: Glutamine enters the cell through transporters such as SLC1A5 and SLC38A5. Glutamine first loses its amide group to form glutamate by glutaminase (GLS, GLS2), which then loses its amine group to form a-ketoglutarate by glutamate dehydrogenase (GLUD1/2 or GPT). Further, a-ketoglutarate enters TCA. Acetyl-CoA obtained in TCA can be used for fatty acid synthesis. To combat oxidative stress, glutamine can be converted directly into glutathione by the enzyme glutathione cysteine ligase (GCL). Glutathione is a tripeptide composed of glutamate, cysteine and glycine. The reduced form of glutathione has an antioxidant role and is important in controlling cellular redox homeostasis. Glutamate acts as a nitrogen donor for the transamination involved in the production of 'dispensable amino acids' aspartate, alanine and serine through the actions of glutamic-oxaloacetic transaminase (GOT1/2), glutamic pyruvate transaminase (GPT) and phosphoserine aminotransferase 1 (PSAT1), respectively. Glutamine can also act as a nitrogen donor for asparagine through asparagine synthetase (ASNS). In a reaction independent of transamination, proline can be synthesized by conversion of glutamate to pyrroline-5-carboxylate by pyrroline-5-carboxylate synthase (ALDH18A1) and subsequently to proline by pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase 1 (PYCR1). Glutamate can be exchanged for cystine (which is quickly reduced to cysteine inside the cell) by SLC7A11. Glutamine controls MTOR through the synthesis of amino acids. Specifically, arginine and isoleucine are the two amino acids that can stimulate MTOR. Glutamine can contribute to MTOR activation by being exchanged for essential amino acids, including isoleucine, through the large neutral amino acid transporter 1 (LAT1; a heterodimer of SLC7A5 and SLC3A2). The oncogene MYC regulates several steps in the glutamine metabolism. It controls the expression of SLC1A5, SLC38A5, SLC3A2, SLC7A5, PYCR1, ALDH18A1 and GLS (through MIR23A/B). Overexpressed TP63 and TP73 control GLS2 expression. The inactivating mutations in SLC7A11 induce overexpression of SLC7A11, and inactivating mutations in RB1 induce SLC1A5 overexpression. NFE2L2 controls the expression of GCL enzymes.    Outcome effects: Glutamine metabolism impacts the biosynthesis of amino acids and proteins, fatty acids and nucleotides, fuels TCA and regulates redox balance by influencing the glutathione level.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cancer cells display increased consumption of and dependence on glutamine. Glutamine metabolism fuels the tricarboxylic acid cycle (TCA), nucleotide and fatty acid biosynthesis, and redox balance in cancer cells, activates mTOR signaling and promotes protein synthesis.    Signaling description: Glutamine enters the cell through transporters such as SLC1A5 and SLC38A5. Glutamine first loses its amide group to form glutamate by glutaminase (GLS, GLS2), which then loses its amine group to form a-ketoglutarate by glutamate dehydrogenase (GLUD1/2 or GPT). Further, a-ketoglutarate enters TCA. Acetyl-CoA obtained in TCA can be used for fatty acid synthesis. To combat oxidative stress, glutamine can be converted directly into glutathione by the enzyme glutathione cysteine ligase (GCL). Glutathione is a tripeptide composed of glutamate, cysteine and glycine. The reduced form of glutathione has an antioxidant role and is important in controlling cellular redox homeostasis. Glutamate acts as a nitrogen donor for the transamination involved in the production of 'dispensable amino acids' aspartate, alanine and serine through the actions of glutamic-oxaloacetic transaminase (GOT1/2), glutamic pyruvate transaminase (GPT) and phosphoserine aminotransferase 1 (PSAT1), respectively. Glutamine can also act as a nitrogen donor for asparagine through asparagine synthetase (ASNS). In a reaction independent of transamination, proline can be synthesized by conversion of glutamate to pyrroline-5-carboxylate by pyrroline-5-carboxylate synthase (ALDH18A1) and subsequently to proline by pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase 1 (PYCR1). Glutamate can be exchanged for cystine (which is quickly reduced to cysteine inside the cell) by SLC7A11. Glutamine controls MTOR through the synthesis of amino acids. Specifically, arginine and isoleucine are the two amino acids that can stimulate MTOR. Glutamine can contribute to MTOR activation by being exchanged for essential amino acids, including isoleucine, through the large neutral amino acid transporter 1 (LAT1; a heterodimer of SLC7A5 and SLC3A2). The oncogene MYC regulates several steps in the glutamine metabolism. It controls the expression of SLC1A5, SLC38A5, SLC3A2, SLC7A5, PYCR1, ALDH18A1 and GLS (through MIR23A/B). Overexpressed TP63 and TP73 control GLS2 expression. The inactivating mutations in SLC7A11 induce overexpression of SLC7A11, and inactivating mutations in RB1 induce SLC1A5 overexpression. NFE2L2 controls the expression of GCL enzymes.    Outcome effects: Glutamine metabolism impacts the biosynthesis of amino acids and proteins, fatty acids and nucleotides, fuels TCA and regulates redox balance by influencing the glutathione level.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details." EXACT []

