xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Several enzymes of the Krebs cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle, TCA) have been found to be mutated in both sporadic and hereditary forms of cancer. These include IDH1/2, SDHA, SDHB, SDHC, SDHD, SDHAF2 and FH.    Signaling description: The mutated isocitrate dehydrogenase 1/2 (IDH1/2) produces an oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate (2HG) from alpha-ketoglutarate (a-KG), thus, depleting a-KG. The decreased level of a-KG depletes the rest of the cycle (succinate, fumarate, malate). The accumulation of 2HG leads to inhibition of TET1/2/3 enzymes and a decrease of 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) level influences DNA methylation and changes gene expression. The decreased level of a-KG impedes ALKBH2/3 activation and disrupts DNA repair. 2-HG also inhibits collagen prolylhydroxylases involved in collagen maturation thereby promoting cancer cell migration and invasion. The SDH complex is a heterotetramer of SDHA, SDHB, SDHC and SDHD subunits converting succinate to fumarate. SDH5 (SDHAF2) is a factor required for the flavination of SDHA and assembly of the tetramer. FH is an enzyme of the TCA cycle that catalyzes the hydration of fumarate into malate. Loss of SDH leads to an accumulation of succinate, and loss of FH leads to an accumulation of fumarate. Succinate and fumarate at high concentrations are regarded as oncometabolites and inhibit the prolyl hydroxylases EGLN1/3, leading to pseudo-hypoxia and activation if HIF1A and HIF2A (EPAS1). HIF activation provokes multiple events including activation of glycolysis. Succinate, fumarate and 2-HG also inhibit KDM4A leading to the impairment of chromatin remodeling. 2-HG activates MTOR signaling via the KDM4A/DEPTOR/MTOR axis. Fumarate regulates the level of oxidative stress through KEAP1/ NFE2L2.    Outcome effects: The outcome is gene- and mutation-dependent. The effects may include impairment of DNA repair, methylation and chromatin remodeling, induction of pseudo-hypoxia, inhibition of apoptosis, activation of cell motility and invasion and changes in the oxidative stress level.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Several enzymes of the Krebs cycle (tricarboxylic acid cycle, TCA) have been found to be mutated in both sporadic and hereditary forms of cancer. These include IDH1/2, SDHA, SDHB, SDHC, SDHD, SDHAF2 and FH.    Signaling description: The mutated isocitrate dehydrogenase 1/2 (IDH1/2) produces an oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate (2HG) from alpha-ketoglutarate (a-KG), thus, depleting a-KG. The decreased level of a-KG depletes the rest of the cycle (succinate, fumarate, malate). The accumulation of 2HG leads to inhibition of TET1/2/3 enzymes and a decrease of 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) level influences DNA methylation and changes gene expression. The decreased level of a-KG impedes ALKBH2/3 activation and disrupts DNA repair. 2-HG also inhibits collagen prolylhydroxylases involved in collagen maturation thereby promoting cancer cell migration and invasion. The SDH complex is a heterotetramer of SDHA, SDHB, SDHC and SDHD subunits converting succinate to fumarate. SDH5 (SDHAF2) is a factor required for the flavination of SDHA and assembly of the tetramer. FH is an enzyme of the TCA cycle that catalyzes the hydration of fumarate into malate. Loss of SDH leads to an accumulation of succinate, and loss of FH leads to an accumulation of fumarate. Succinate and fumarate at high concentrations are regarded as oncometabolites and inhibit the prolyl hydroxylases EGLN1/3, leading to pseudo-hypoxia and activation if HIF1A and HIF2A (EPAS1). HIF activation provokes multiple events including activation of glycolysis. Succinate, fumarate and 2-HG also inhibit KDM4A leading to the impairment of chromatin remodeling. 2-HG activates MTOR signaling via the KDM4A/DEPTOR/MTOR axis. Fumarate regulates the level of oxidative stress through KEAP1/ NFE2L2.    Outcome effects: The outcome is gene- and mutation-dependent. The effects may include impairment of DNA repair, methylation and chromatin remodeling, induction of pseudo-hypoxia, inhibition of apoptosis, activation of cell motility and invasion and changes in the oxidative stress level.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming (Krebs Cycle)\" for details." EXACT []

