xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In cancer cells, the glucose uptake and glycolysis are strongly un-regulated compared to normal cells, which gives rise to an increased yield of intermediate glycolytic metabolites and the end product, pyruvate. Consequently, the majority of glycolysis-derived pyruvate is diverted to lactate fermentation and kept away from mitochondrial oxidative metabolism. This occurs even in the presence of sufficient oxygen. This metabolic phenotype is known as the Warburg effect or aerobic glycolysis.    Signaling description: In cancer, glucose import is increased through activation of SLC2A1 and SLC2A3 transporters (they are activated due to HIF1A, MYC, RELA over-expression and TP53 down-regulation). Many glycolytic enzymes are up-regulated in cancer including HK1/2, GPI, PFKM, ALDOA, GAPDH, PGK1, PGAM1, ENO1/2, PKM. The conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA is inhibited due to activation of PDK1/3, the inhibitors of PDHA1. The conversion of pyruvate to lactate is activated due to up-regulation of LDHA. The activation of HIF1A, AKT1, MYC, SNAI1 and inactivation of TP53 and PTEN are essential in the up-regulation of glycolytic enzymes in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: Glycolytic breakdown of glucose produces various intermediate metabolites, which, as precursors, can be diverted into anabolic pathways including the pentose phosphate pathway, serine and triacylglycerol synthesis pathways for the de novo synthesis of nucleotides, amino acids, and lipids.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In cancer cells, the glucose uptake and glycolysis are strongly un-regulated compared to normal cells, which gives rise to an increased yield of intermediate glycolytic metabolites and the end product, pyruvate. Consequently, the majority of glycolysis-derived pyruvate is diverted to lactate fermentation and kept away from mitochondrial oxidative metabolism. This occurs even in the presence of sufficient oxygen. This metabolic phenotype is known as the Warburg effect or aerobic glycolysis.    Signaling description: In cancer, glucose import is increased through activation of SLC2A1 and SLC2A3 transporters (they are activated due to HIF1A, MYC, RELA over-expression and TP53 down-regulation). Many glycolytic enzymes are up-regulated in cancer including HK1/2, GPI, PFKM, ALDOA, GAPDH, PGK1, PGAM1, ENO1/2, PKM. The conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA is inhibited due to activation of PDK1/3, the inhibitors of PDHA1. The conversion of pyruvate to lactate is activated due to up-regulation of LDHA. The activation of HIF1A, AKT1, MYC, SNAI1 and inactivation of TP53 and PTEN are essential in the up-regulation of glycolytic enzymes in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: Glycolytic breakdown of glucose produces various intermediate metabolites, which, as precursors, can be diverted into anabolic pathways including the pentose phosphate pathway, serine and triacylglycerol synthesis pathways for the de novo synthesis of nucleotides, amino acids, and lipids.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer Metabolic Reprogramming\" for details." EXACT []

