xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: GPCRs (G-protein-coupled receptors) are able to utilize the EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor) as a downstream signaling partner in mitogenic signal generation.    Signaling description: The EGFR is essential for the regulation of normal cellular functions, as well as pathological processes that underlie the development of hyperproliferative diseases including cancer. GPCRs stimulation leads to activation of second messengers, such as PKC, SRC, PTK2B, Ca2+ and other molecules. GPCR signaling activates EGRF via the 'triple-membrane-passing-signaling' mechanism, in which second messengers activate membrane-bound MMPs (e. g. ADAM family members) which cleave EGF ligands that bind to EGFR (and other EGFR family members) and promote dimerization and activation of EGFR.    Outcome effects: The activation of EGFR promotes the subsequent activation of signaling cascades including the MAPK pathway and the PI3K/AKT pathway, which ultimately stimulates essential cellular responses including cell proliferation, migration, invasion and survival (for details see the \"EGFR Signaling\" pathway).    Animal data: Some data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines. Analysis of GPCR-induced EGFR activation in more than 60 human carcinoma cell lines derived from different tissues has demonstrated broad relevance of this signaling mechanism in cancer.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: GPCRs (G-protein-coupled receptors) are able to utilize the EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor) as a downstream signaling partner in mitogenic signal generation.    Signaling description: The EGFR is essential for the regulation of normal cellular functions, as well as pathological processes that underlie the development of hyperproliferative diseases including cancer. GPCRs stimulation leads to activation of second messengers, such as PKC, SRC, PTK2B, Ca2+ and other molecules. GPCR signaling activates EGRF via the 'triple-membrane-passing-signaling' mechanism, in which second messengers activate membrane-bound MMPs (e. g. ADAM family members) which cleave EGF ligands that bind to EGFR (and other EGFR family members) and promote dimerization and activation of EGFR.    Outcome effects: The activation of EGFR promotes the subsequent activation of signaling cascades including the MAPK pathway and the PI3K/AKT pathway, which ultimately stimulates essential cellular responses including cell proliferation, migration, invasion and survival (for details see the \"EGFR Signaling\" pathway).    Animal data: Some data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines. Analysis of GPCR-induced EGFR activation in more than 60 human carcinoma cell lines derived from different tissues has demonstrated broad relevance of this signaling mechanism in cancer.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

