xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: TYRO3, AXL, and MERTK constitute the TAM family of receptor tyrosine kinases. They regulate a mix of processes, including cell proliferation/survival, cell adhesion and migration, regulation of inflammatory cytokine release and contribute to development of cancer.    Signaling description: Signal-induced stimulation of TAM receptors by GAS6 or PROS1 activate PI3K/AKT, RAS-MAPK, PLCG as well as other pathways. TAM receptors also interplay with other oncogenic receptors such as MET, EGFR, ERBB2. Overexpression of GAS6, PROS1, AXL, MERTK is typical for different types of cancers. AXL may be up-regulated by multiple transcription factors: TP53, SP1/3, EZH2, ESR1 and others.    Outcome effects: The activation of TAM signaling results in increased proliferation and survival, migration and invasiveness of cancer cells.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: TYRO3, AXL, and MERTK constitute the TAM family of receptor tyrosine kinases. They regulate a mix of processes, including cell proliferation/survival, cell adhesion and migration, regulation of inflammatory cytokine release and contribute to development of cancer.    Signaling description: Signal-induced stimulation of TAM receptors by GAS6 or PROS1 activate PI3K/AKT, RAS-MAPK, PLCG as well as other pathways. TAM receptors also interplay with other oncogenic receptors such as MET, EGFR, ERBB2. Overexpression of GAS6, PROS1, AXL, MERTK is typical for different types of cancers. AXL may be up-regulated by multiple transcription factors: TP53, SP1/3, EZH2, ESR1 and others.    Outcome effects: The activation of TAM signaling results in increased proliferation and survival, migration and invasiveness of cancer cells.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

