xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The Hedgehog (Hh) signaling pathway regulates cell proliferation, differentiation and tissue development in embryogenesis and is mostly inhibited in adulthood. However, the Hh cascade can be reactivated in cancer.    Signaling description: In the absence of Hh ligand (SHH), Patched (PTCH) inhibits the activity of Smoothened (SMO), whose localization at the cell surface is essential to its function. Full-length GLI proteins are proteolytically cleaved to produce GLIR, derived mostly from GLI3, which represses Hh target genes. Binding of Hh to PTCH leads to its internalization and destabilization, so it no longer can carry the endogenous SMO agonist molecules outwards. The release of SMO inhibition leads to the production of GLIA, derived mostly from GLI2 ensuing the expression of the Hh target gene. Activating mutations in the SMO gene and overexpression of SHH and GLI1/2 lead to permanent Hh signaling stimulation in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: In cancer, constant activation of Hh leads to uncontrolled cell proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Animal data: Some data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The Hedgehog (Hh) signaling pathway regulates cell proliferation, differentiation and tissue development in embryogenesis and is mostly inhibited in adulthood. However, the Hh cascade can be reactivated in cancer.    Signaling description: In the absence of Hh ligand (SHH), Patched (PTCH) inhibits the activity of Smoothened (SMO), whose localization at the cell surface is essential to its function. Full-length GLI proteins are proteolytically cleaved to produce GLIR, derived mostly from GLI3, which represses Hh target genes. Binding of Hh to PTCH leads to its internalization and destabilization, so it no longer can carry the endogenous SMO agonist molecules outwards. The release of SMO inhibition leads to the production of GLIA, derived mostly from GLI2 ensuing the expression of the Hh target gene. Activating mutations in the SMO gene and overexpression of SHH and GLI1/2 lead to permanent Hh signaling stimulation in cancer cells.    Outcome effects: In cancer, constant activation of Hh leads to uncontrolled cell proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Animal data: Some data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

