xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: A large number of receptor tyrosine-kinases (RTKs), TAM-receptors, estrogen and androgen receptor as well as adapter proteins are overexpressed in different types of cancer and have important roles in cancer progression.    Signaling description: Receptors and adapter proteins can be over-activated due to activating mutations, hyperexpression or chromosomal translocations. Receptors transmit signals through adaptor proteins and activate downstream signaling pathways including PI3K/AKT, RAS/MAPK, PLCG, JAK/STAT pathways and others.    Outcome effects: The abovementioned events lead to uncontrolled proliferation of malignant cells.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: A large number of receptor tyrosine-kinases (RTKs), TAM-receptors, estrogen and androgen receptor as well as adapter proteins are overexpressed in different types of cancer and have important roles in cancer progression.    Signaling description: Receptors and adapter proteins can be over-activated due to activating mutations, hyperexpression or chromosomal translocations. Receptors transmit signals through adaptor proteins and activate downstream signaling pathways including PI3K/AKT, RAS/MAPK, PLCG, JAK/STAT pathways and others.    Outcome effects: The abovementioned events lead to uncontrolled proliferation of malignant cells.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

