xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: NOTCH signaling is an evolutionarily conserved pathway in multicellular organisms that regulates cell-fate determination during embryonic development and maintains adult tissue homeostasis. NOTCH signaling may be oncogenic or tumor suppressive in different cancers.    Signaling description: NOTCH receptors are single-pass transmembrane proteins composed of functional extracellular (NECD), transmembrane (TM), and intracellular (NICD) domains. Delta-like (DLL) and the Jagged (JAG) families serve as ligands for Notch signaling receptors. Upon ligand binding, the NECD is cleaved from the TM-NICD domain by ADAM17. Further, the NECD domain stays bound to the ligand and the NECD-ligand complex undergoes endocytosis and recycling in the signal-emitting cell. Meanwhile, in the signal-perceiving cell, gamma-secretase separates NICD from the TM, which allows for the nuclear translocation of NICD where it associates with the RBPJ transcription factor complex. This leads to activation of target genes of the canonical Notch pathway: MYC, HES-family members and other genes. NOTCH signaling may be oncogenic or tumor suppressive in different cancers. The precise mechanism of this dual action is not clear and seems to be disease-, tissue- and context-dependent. NOTCH1 is hyperactivated due to mutations and serves as an oncogene in T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL), chronic lymphocytic leukemia, non-small cell lung cancer. Also, in T-ALL, inactivating mutations in FBXW7, an E3 ubiquitin ligase responsible for ICN1 degradation and subsequent termination of NOTCH1 signaling were identified. In head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, inactivating mutations in NOTCH1/2/3 are typical. Moreover, in chronic myelomonocytic leukemia, inactivating mutations in NCSTN, APH1A, MAML1 or NOTCH2 (NCSTN, APH1A are components of the gamma-secretase complex) were identified. This confirms the role of NOTCH proteins as tumor suppressors.     Outcome effects: The activation of proliferation and induction of epithelial-mesenchymal transition happen as a result of NOTCH signaling deregulation.    Animal data: The majority of the data were obtained in human cancer lines/specimens.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: NOTCH signaling is an evolutionarily conserved pathway in multicellular organisms that regulates cell-fate determination during embryonic development and maintains adult tissue homeostasis. NOTCH signaling may be oncogenic or tumor suppressive in different cancers.    Signaling description: NOTCH receptors are single-pass transmembrane proteins composed of functional extracellular (NECD), transmembrane (TM), and intracellular (NICD) domains. Delta-like (DLL) and the Jagged (JAG) families serve as ligands for Notch signaling receptors. Upon ligand binding, the NECD is cleaved from the TM-NICD domain by ADAM17. Further, the NECD domain stays bound to the ligand and the NECD-ligand complex undergoes endocytosis and recycling in the signal-emitting cell. Meanwhile, in the signal-perceiving cell, gamma-secretase separates NICD from the TM, which allows for the nuclear translocation of NICD where it associates with the RBPJ transcription factor complex. This leads to activation of target genes of the canonical Notch pathway: MYC, HES-family members and other genes. NOTCH signaling may be oncogenic or tumor suppressive in different cancers. The precise mechanism of this dual action is not clear and seems to be disease-, tissue- and context-dependent. NOTCH1 is hyperactivated due to mutations and serves as an oncogene in T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL), chronic lymphocytic leukemia, non-small cell lung cancer. Also, in T-ALL, inactivating mutations in FBXW7, an E3 ubiquitin ligase responsible for ICN1 degradation and subsequent termination of NOTCH1 signaling were identified. In head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, inactivating mutations in NOTCH1/2/3 are typical. Moreover, in chronic myelomonocytic leukemia, inactivating mutations in NCSTN, APH1A, MAML1 or NOTCH2 (NCSTN, APH1A are components of the gamma-secretase complex) were identified. This confirms the role of NOTCH proteins as tumor suppressors.     Outcome effects: The activation of proliferation and induction of epithelial-mesenchymal transition happen as a result of NOTCH signaling deregulation.    Animal data: The majority of the data were obtained in human cancer lines/specimens.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

