xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: NFKB is a protein complex that controls DNA transcription, cytokine production and cell survival. Pathologic activation of NFKB has been linked to cancer.    Signaling description: Mammalian NFKB dimers are generated via homodimeric and heterodimeric interactions of 5 REL subunits expressed from distinct but related genes: RELA, RELB, REL, NFKB1 and NFKB2. The canonical NFKB activation pathway results in activation of gene transcription by the RELA/NFKB1 dimer. This heterodimer cycles between the cytoplasm and the nucleus complexed with an inhibitory protein NFKBIA which reduces the efficiency of nuclear transport and prevents DNA binding of RELA/NFKB1. Upon interaction with their cell surface receptors, extracellular stimuli such as bacterial LPS and TNFA, activate RELA/NFKB1 dimer via a series of signaling events which are channeled through the IKK complex. This complex is comprised of three major components known as IKBKA (CHUK), IKBKB and IKBKG. The general consensus is that the catalytic component IKBKB and the scaffold component IKBKG transduce signals to the canonical pathway. IKBKB catalyzes phosphorylation of NFKBIA which leads to its poly-ubiquitination and degradation via the 26S proteasome to generate \"free\" RELA/NFKB1 and, as a result, transcriptional activation of target genes. NFKB activation is reported to be associated with tumorigenesis. This may be due to activating mutations in LTBR, CD40, TNFRSF13B, TNFRSF14, MYD88, CD79A/B, IKBKE, REL and other proteins. NFKB signaling may also be activated by viral proteins HHV8GK18_gp80 (HHV-8 protein), tax (HTLV-1 protein), LMP1 (EBV protein) and hepatitis C RNA.    Outcome effects: Constant NFKB activation leads to uncontrolled cellular proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and induction of inflammation.    Animal data: Some data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Description of viral proteins: HHV8GK18_gp80, tax, LMP1 shown in grey are viral proteins."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: NFKB is a protein complex that controls DNA transcription, cytokine production and cell survival. Pathologic activation of NFKB has been linked to cancer.    Signaling description: Mammalian NFKB dimers are generated via homodimeric and heterodimeric interactions of 5 REL subunits expressed from distinct but related genes: RELA, RELB, REL, NFKB1 and NFKB2. The canonical NFKB activation pathway results in activation of gene transcription by the RELA/NFKB1 dimer. This heterodimer cycles between the cytoplasm and the nucleus complexed with an inhibitory protein NFKBIA which reduces the efficiency of nuclear transport and prevents DNA binding of RELA/NFKB1. Upon interaction with their cell surface receptors, extracellular stimuli such as bacterial LPS and TNFA, activate RELA/NFKB1 dimer via a series of signaling events which are channeled through the IKK complex. This complex is comprised of three major components known as IKBKA (CHUK), IKBKB and IKBKG. The general consensus is that the catalytic component IKBKB and the scaffold component IKBKG transduce signals to the canonical pathway. IKBKB catalyzes phosphorylation of NFKBIA which leads to its poly-ubiquitination and degradation via the 26S proteasome to generate \"free\" RELA/NFKB1 and, as a result, transcriptional activation of target genes. NFKB activation is reported to be associated with tumorigenesis. This may be due to activating mutations in LTBR, CD40, TNFRSF13B, TNFRSF14, MYD88, CD79A/B, IKBKE, REL and other proteins. NFKB signaling may also be activated by viral proteins HHV8GK18_gp80 (HHV-8 protein), tax (HTLV-1 protein), LMP1 (EBV protein) and hepatitis C RNA.    Outcome effects: Constant NFKB activation leads to uncontrolled cellular proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and induction of inflammation.    Animal data: Some data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in r