xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: RAS proteins control cellular signaling pathways responsible for growth, migration, adhesion, cytoskeletal integrity, survival and differentiation.    Signaling description: In cancer, RAS/RAF/MAPK signaling may be constitutively activated due to mutations in RAS (KRAS, HRAS, NRAS) or RAF (BRAF1) or MAPK (MAP2K1).    Outcome effects: Constant activation of RAS/RAF/MAPK signaling leads to uncontrolled cell proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
