xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The canonical WNT pathway causes an accumulation of CTNNB1 in the cytoplasm and its eventual translocation into the nucleus to act as a transcriptional co-activator of transcription factors that belong to the TCF/LEF family. The conserved WNT/CTNNB1 pathway regulates stem cell pluripotency and cell fate decisions during development and is often deregulated in cancer.    Signaling description: WNT ligands bind to FZD receptors, leading to the formation of a larger cell surface complex with LRP5/6. Activation of the WNT receptor complex triggers displacement of the multifunctional kinase GSK3B from a regulatory APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex. In the absence of WNT-signal (off-state), CTNNB, undergoes coordinated phosphorylation by CSNK1A1 and the APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex leading to its ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. In the presence of WNT ligand (on-state), the co-receptor LRP5/6 is complexed with WNT-bound FZD. This leads to activation of DLV by sequential phosphorylation, poly-ubiquitination, and polymerization, which displaces GSK3B from APC/AXIN1 through an undetermined mechanism that may involve substrate trapping and/or endosome sequestration. Stabilized CTNNB1 is translocated to the nucleus, where it binds to LEF1/TCF4 transcription factors, displacing co-repressors and recruiting additional co-activators to WNT target genes. Activating mutations in CTNNB1, LEF1, CREBBP genes and hyperexpression of WNT ligands, LRP5/6, FRAT1, FOXM1, BCL9 lead to constant WNT signaling activation.    Outcome effects: WNT pathway is kept in on-state leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The canonical WNT pathway causes an accumulation of CTNNB1 in the cytoplasm and its eventual translocation into the nucleus to act as a transcriptional co-activator of transcription factors that belong to the TCF/LEF family. The conserved WNT/CTNNB1 pathway regulates stem cell pluripotency and cell fate decisions during development and is often deregulated in cancer.    Signaling description: WNT ligands bind to FZD receptors, leading to the formation of a larger cell surface complex with LRP5/6. Activation of the WNT receptor complex triggers displacement of the multifunctional kinase GSK3B from a regulatory APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex. In the absence of WNT-signal (off-state), CTNNB, undergoes coordinated phosphorylation by CSNK1A1 and the APC/AXIN1/GSK3B-complex leading to its ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. In the presence of WNT ligand (on-state), the co-receptor LRP5/6 is complexed with WNT-bound FZD. This leads to activation of DLV by sequential phosphorylation, poly-ubiquitination, and polymerization, which displaces GSK3B from APC/AXIN1 through an undetermined mechanism that may involve substrate trapping and/or endosome sequestration. Stabilized CTNNB1 is translocated to the nucleus, where it binds to LEF1/TCF4 transcription factors, displacing co-repressors and recruiting additional co-activators to WNT target genes. Activating mutations in CTNNB1, LEF1, CREBBP genes and hyperexpression of WNT ligands, LRP5/6, FRAT1, FOXM1, BCL9 lead to constant WNT signaling activation.    Outcome effects: WNT pathway is kept in on-state leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation.    Animal data: Some of the data were obtained from animal experiments or human cancer lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

