xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cell cycle checkpoints are control mechanisms in eukaryotic cells which ensure proper cell division. There exist three major checkpoints: the G1 checkpoint, also known as the restriction or start checkpoint, the G2/M checkpoint, and the metaphase checkpoint. DNA damage response activates the cell cycle checkpoint. Cell cycle progression is controlled by CDKs activated by CDC25A/B/C phosphatases. The induction of double strand breaks in DNA activates the ATM/CHEK2 pathway, while exposed ssDNA initiates the ATR/CHEK1 pathway. Both pathways inactivate CDC25A/B/C leading to a temporal cell cycle arrest. Activation of TP53/ CDKN1A pathway is crucial for the G1 checkpoint. The activation of the MAPKAPK2 pathway contributes to the checkpoint activation and maintenance.    Signaling description: ATR, ATM, BRCA1/2, CHEK1/2 and other proteins critical for DNA damage checkpoint are mutated or downregulated in many types of cancer. CDKs are often mutated or overexpressed, CDC25A/B are often overexpressed in cancers.    Outcome effects: Despite DNA damage, cell cycle is not stopped and apoptosis is not induced allowing the cancer cell to proliferate.    Animal data: The data were obtained both from animal models and human cancer cell lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cell cycle checkpoints are control mechanisms in eukaryotic cells which ensure proper cell division. There exist three major checkpoints: the G1 checkpoint, also known as the restriction or start checkpoint, the G2/M checkpoint, and the metaphase checkpoint. DNA damage response activates the cell cycle checkpoint. Cell cycle progression is controlled by CDKs activated by CDC25A/B/C phosphatases. The induction of double strand breaks in DNA activates the ATM/CHEK2 pathway, while exposed ssDNA initiates the ATR/CHEK1 pathway. Both pathways inactivate CDC25A/B/C leading to a temporal cell cycle arrest. Activation of TP53/ CDKN1A pathway is crucial for the G1 checkpoint. The activation of the MAPKAPK2 pathway contributes to the checkpoint activation and maintenance.    Signaling description: ATR, ATM, BRCA1/2, CHEK1/2 and other proteins critical for DNA damage checkpoint are mutated or downregulated in many types of cancer. CDKs are often mutated or overexpressed, CDC25A/B are often overexpressed in cancers.    Outcome effects: Despite DNA damage, cell cycle is not stopped and apoptosis is not induced allowing the cancer cell to proliferate.    Animal data: The data were obtained both from animal models and human cancer cell lines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression/activity are highlighted in red. Proteins with decreased expression/activity are highlighted in blue. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details.    Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. See pathway \"Genes with Mutations in Cancer-Associated Sustaining of Proliferative Signaling\" for details." EXACT []

