xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Chronic inflammation is one of the leading factors of cancer initiation and progression. Immune cell (predominantly myeloid cells) produce various cytokines and growth factors such as TNF, IL6, IL1B, IL11,IL22, EGF, HGF and others that interact with receptors on the surface of tumor cells. Obesity is also regarded as a chronic inflammation state and makes a great impact into cancer initiation via inflammatory mechanisms. In obesity, adipocytes produce TNF, IL6 and IL1B.    Signaling description: STAT3 and NFkB activation is regarded as the major factors of inflammation-induced tumorigenesis and are activated by cytokines and growth factors in a classical manner. While, IL6/11/22 act through JAK/STAT3 signaling; activation by IL1B involves MYD88/RAC1/IRAC/TRAF6/MAP3K1/STAT3 signaling. Growth factors activate JAK/STAT3 through SRC and ABL1, and TNF activates the classical NFkB pathway.     Outcome effects: Activated STAT3 and NFkB induce expression of multiple proteins involved in cancer progression. Specifically, BCL2, BCL2L1, BIRC2/3/5, MCL1, CFLAR inhibit apoptosis of cancer cells. IL6, PTGS2 and some other cytokines (not shown in order to simplify the pathway) enhance inflammation. VEGFA induce angiogenesis. MMP2/9 cleave extracellular matrix proteins, thus, promoting cancer cell invasion and migration. MYC, CCND1/2, CCNB1, FGF2, HGF, HIF1A directly or indirectly regulate cancer cell proliferation. In addition, HIF1A inhibits MSH2/6 proteins involved in DNA repair and makes impact into genomic instability. Inhibition of CDH1 and TWIST1 expression induce epithelial to mesenchymal transition.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Tumor-Promoting Inflammation\" for details.    Mutated proteins: not shown."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Chronic inflammation is one of the leading factors of cancer initiation and progression. Immune cell (predominantly myeloid cells) produce various cytokines and growth factors such as TNF, IL6, IL1B, IL11,IL22, EGF, HGF and others that interact with receptors on the surface of tumor cells. Obesity is also regarded as a chronic inflammation state and makes a great impact into cancer initiation via inflammatory mechanisms. In obesity, adipocytes produce TNF, IL6 and IL1B.    Signaling description: STAT3 and NFkB activation is regarded as the major factors of inflammation-induced tumorigenesis and are activated by cytokines and growth factors in a classical manner. While, IL6/11/22 act through JAK/STAT3 signaling; activation by IL1B involves MYD88/RAC1/IRAC/TRAF6/MAP3K1/STAT3 signaling. Growth factors activate JAK/STAT3 through SRC and ABL1, and TNF activates the classical NFkB pathway.     Outcome effects: Activated STAT3 and NFkB induce expression of multiple proteins involved in cancer progression. Specifically, BCL2, BCL2L1, BIRC2/3/5, MCL1, CFLAR inhibit apoptosis of cancer cells. IL6, PTGS2 and some other cytokines (not shown in order to simplify the pathway) enhance inflammation. VEGFA induce angiogenesis. MMP2/9 cleave extracellular matrix proteins, thus, promoting cancer cell invasion and migration. MYC, CCND1/2, CCNB1, FGF2, HGF, HIF1A directly or indirectly regulate cancer cell proliferation. In addition, HIF1A inhibits MSH2/6 proteins involved in DNA repair and makes impact into genomic instability. Inhibition of CDH1 and TWIST1 expression induce epithelial to mesenchymal transition.    Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Tumor-Promoting Inflammation\" for details.    Mutated proteins: not shown." EXACT []

