xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Silicosis and asbestosis are two forms of fibrotic lung disease resulting from the inhalation of inert materials indigestible by pulmonary alveolar macrophages - crystalline silica and asbestos respectively. This pathology is predictable among people overexposed to dusts in various industries and occupations such as mining, construction, manufacturing, and building maintenance. Asbestos and silica accumulation in the lung tissue leads to inflammatory response and may cause not only pulmonary fibrosis but also lung cancer or mesothelioma development as a result of chronic inflammation.     Signaling description: Silica and asbestos captured by macrophages activate ROS production by NAD(P)H oxidase. H2O2 may activate both NFkB and MAPK1/JUN-FOS and induce TNF and IL1B production. ROS also activate inflammasome formation. As a result CASP1 promotes the maturation and secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines IL1B and IL18.     Outcome effects: During long-standing chronic inflammation, TNF, IL1B and IL18 can make a significant impact into the development of pulmonary fibrosis, lung cancer and mesothelioma. According to current knowledge, chronic inflammation is regarded as the main underlying mechanism for cancer development in asbestosis and silicosis.     Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Tumor-Promoting Inflammation\" for details.    Mutated proteins: not shown."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Silicosis and asbestosis are two forms of fibrotic lung disease resulting from the inhalation of inert materials indigestible by pulmonary alveolar macrophages - crystalline silica and asbestos respectively. This pathology is predictable among people overexposed to dusts in various industries and occupations such as mining, construction, manufacturing, and building maintenance. Asbestos and silica accumulation in the lung tissue leads to inflammatory response and may cause not only pulmonary fibrosis but also lung cancer or mesothelioma development as a result of chronic inflammation.     Signaling description: Silica and asbestos captured by macrophages activate ROS production by NAD(P)H oxidase. H2O2 may activate both NFkB and MAPK1/JUN-FOS and induce TNF and IL1B production. ROS also activate inflammasome formation. As a result CASP1 promotes the maturation and secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines IL1B and IL18.     Outcome effects: During long-standing chronic inflammation, TNF, IL1B and IL18 can make a significant impact into the development of pulmonary fibrosis, lung cancer and mesothelioma. According to current knowledge, chronic inflammation is regarded as the main underlying mechanism for cancer development in asbestosis and silicosis.     Animal data: not shown    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with red highlights have increased expression/activity. See pathway \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Tumor-Promoting Inflammation\" for details.    Mutated proteins: not shown." EXACT []

