[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-8829fc00-d3d5-40ce-a04f-a4e26d18879b
name: Acetaminophen Induced Hepatotoxicity
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:digestive_system ! Digestive system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:drug_toxicity_pathways ! Drug toxicity pathways
is_a: urn:agi-folder:a ! A
xref: PS:Description "Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is metabolized primarily in the liver by different metabolic pathways. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is metabolized primarily in the liver by different metabolic pathways. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "P. Golovatenko-Abramov"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: P. Golovatenko-Abramov" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-8829fc00-d3d5-40ce-a04f-a4e26d18879b"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-8829fc00-d3d5-40ce-a04f-a4e26d18879b" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "liver"
synonym: "Organ: liver" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "hepatocyte"
synonym: "CellType: hepatocyte" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "digestive system"
synonym: "Organ_System: digestive system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Pathological Process"
synonym: "Source: Pathological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is a widely used antipyretic and analgesic drug which is a major ingredient in cold and flu remedies.    Signaling description: Acetaminophen is metabolized primarily in the liver via different metabolic pathways and approximately 15% of the drug is metabolized by cytochrome CYP2E1 into toxic NAPQI metabolite. This metabolite is capable of directly modifying cysteine residues within a polypeptide chain by arylation mechanism. In hepatocytes, this modification leads to the inactivation of catalase (CAT) and inhibition of Ca2+-transporting ATPase (ATP2B1). The depletion of these proteins causes ROS accumulation, which in turn promotes apoptotic mitochondrial damage.    Outcome effects: The described events along with decreased ATP production induce necrotic transformation. Another target of NAPQI is HMG-CoA synthase (HMGCS2), which is one of the key enzymes in ketone biosynthesis. Therefore, this metabolic pathway also becomes blocked by NAPQI."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is a widely used antipyretic and analgesic drug which is a major ingredient in cold and flu remedies.    Signaling description: Acetaminophen is metabolized primarily in the liver via different metabolic pathways and approximately 15% of the drug is metabolized by cytochrome CYP2E1 into toxic NAPQI metabolite. This metabolite is capable of directly modifying cysteine residues within a polypeptide chain by arylation mechanism. In hepatocytes, this modification leads to the inactivation of catalase (CAT) and inhibition of Ca2+-transporting ATPase (ATP2B1). The depletion of these proteins causes ROS accumulation, which in turn promotes apoptotic mitochondrial damage.    Outcome effects: The described events along with decreased ATP production induce necrotic transformation. Another target of NAPQI is HMG-CoA synthase (HMGCS2), which is one of the key enzymes in ketone biosynthesis. Therefore, this metabolic pathway also becomes blocked by NAPQI." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-d0ac8fc0-e529-4898-827b-49c8dc70149f
name: Ethanol Induced Hepatotoxicity
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:digestive_system ! Digestive system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:drug_toxicity_pathways ! Drug toxicity pathways
is_a: urn:agi-folder:e ! E
xref: PS:Description "Ethanol is metabolized in the liver to produce acetaldehyde followed by acetate with the consumption of NAD and production of NADH. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Ethanol is metabolized in the liver to produce acetaldehyde followed by acetate with the consumption of NAD and production of NADH. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "P. Golovatenko-Abramov"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: P. Golovatenko-Abramov" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-d0ac8fc0-e529-4898-827b-49c8dc70149f"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-d0ac8fc0-e529-4898-827b-49c8dc70149f" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "liver"
synonym: "Organ: liver" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "hepatocyte"
synonym: "CellType: hepatocyte" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "digestive system"
synonym: "Organ_System: digestive system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Pathological Process"
synonym: "Source: Pathological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ethanol is metabolized in the liver to produce acetaldehyde and then acetate with the consumption of NAD and production of NADH.    Signaling description: Therefore, in the case of systematic alcohol abuse, the pool of NAD is exhausted, while NADH concentration increases. This shifts the equilibrium in many metabolic reactions.    Outcome effects: In particular, this results in the inversion of lipid beta-oxidation and promotes the biosynthesis of triacylglycerols. Conversely, in mitochondria, acetaldehyde promotes ROS production and this leads to lipid peroxidation. The overproduction of NADH also promotes lactate synthesis and accumulation which provides the condition for acidosis. Moreover, it is known that in hepatocytes, ethanol upregulates TGFB1 pathway, which induces collagen and fibronectin (FN1) production leading to fibrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with elevated level are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ethanol is metabolized in the liver to produce acetaldehyde and then acetate with the consumption of NAD and production of NADH.    Signaling description: Therefore, in the case of systematic alcohol abuse, the pool of NAD is exhausted, while NADH concentration increases. This shifts the equilibrium in many metabolic reactions.    Outcome effects: In particular, this results in the inversion of lipid beta-oxidation and promotes the biosynthesis of triacylglycerols. Conversely, in mitochondria, acetaldehyde promotes ROS production and this leads to lipid peroxidation. The overproduction of NADH also promotes lactate synthesis and accumulation which provides the condition for acidosis. Moreover, it is known that in hepatocytes, ethanol upregulates TGFB1 pathway, which induces collagen and fibronectin (FN1) production leading to fibrosis.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with elevated level are highlighted in red." EXACT []

