xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Anthracycline antibiotics such as doxorubicin, daunorubicin, epirubicin, and idarubicin are some of the most effective chemotherapeutic agents used in the treatment of cancer.    Signaling description: Anthracycline molecules contain aglyconic and sugar moieties. The aglycone consists of a tetracyclic ring with adjacent quinone-hydroquinone groups. One-electron addition to the quinone moiety results in the formation of a semiquinone that quickly regenerates its parent quinine by reducing oxygen (O2) to reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2). During this cycle, the semiquinone can also oxidize resulting in reductive deglycosidation and the formation of 7-deoxyaglycone. Aglycones intercalate into biologic membranes due to their increased lipid solubility and form ROS in the closest proximity to sensitive targets. The accumulation of the more lipophilic 7-deoxyaglycone in the inner mitochondrial membrane greatly enhances electron deviation from the regular pathway of respiratory chain to O2, leading to an increased formation of superoxide radical and consequent amplification of mitochondrial dysfunction. One-electron redox cycling of doxorubicin is also accompanied by the release of iron (Fe2+) from intracellular stores which results in the formation of drug-iron complexes that convert superoxide radical and H2O2 into more potent hydroxyl radicals. The clinical use of anthracyclines is hindered by serious problems such as the development of resistance in tumor cells or toxicity in healthy tissues, particularly in the form of chronic cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure. The ultrastructural features of anthracycline-induced cardiomyopathy include the loss of myofibrils, dilation of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, cytoplasmic vacuolization, swelling of mitochondria, and increased number of lysosomes. Cardiomyocytes are more susceptible to apoptosis induced by anthracyclines than other tissues since they exhibit low levels of catalase and readily undergo inactivation of glutathione peroxidase following exposure to doxorubicin. The reduction of diverse antioxidant enzymes represents a common response to anthracycline treatment. Mitochondrial death pathway in cardiac myocytes is triggered by oxidative stress. Doxorubicin-induced apoptosis of cardiomyocytes proceeds through redox cycling of anthracycline, consequent formation of ROS, and activation of NF-kB. In contrast, NF-kB activation usually inhibits apoptosis induced by doxorubicin in cancer cells. Chronic cardiomyopathy develops after the summation and mutual feedback of diverse processes such as increased membrane lipid peroxidation, inhibition of nucleic acid and protein synthesis, changes in adrenergic function and adenylate cyclase (ADCY), abnormalities in Ca2+ handling, reduced expression of specific genes, and induction of nitric oxide synthase (NOS2), which leads to nitric oxide (NO), peroxynitrite, and converse nitration/activation of metalloproteinases. Compound with elevated level is highlighted in red.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with elevated level are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Anthracycline antibiotics such as doxorubicin, daunorubicin, epirubicin, and idarubicin are some of the most effective chemotherapeutic agents used in the treatment of cancer.    Signaling description: Anthracycline molecules contain aglyconic and sugar moieties. The aglycone consists of a tetracyclic ring with adjacent quinone-hydroquinone groups. One-electron addition to the quinone moiety results in the formation of a semiquinone that quickly regenerates its parent quinine by reducing oxygen (O2) to reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2). During this cycle, the semiquinone can also oxidize resulting in reductive deglycosidation and the formation of 7-deoxyaglycone. Aglycones intercalate into biologic membranes due to their increased lipid solubility and form ROS in the closest proximity to sensitive targets. The accumulation of the more lipophilic 7-deoxyaglycone in the inner mitochondrial membrane greatly enhances electron deviation from the regular pathway of respiratory chain to O2, leading to an increased formation of superoxide radical and consequent amplification of mitochondrial dysfunction. One-electron redox cycling of doxorubicin is also accompanied by the release of iron (Fe2+) from intracellular stores which results in the formation of drug-iron complexes that convert superoxide radical and H2O2 into more potent hydroxyl radicals. The clinical use of anthracyclines is hindered by serious problems such as the development of resistance in tumor cells or toxicity in healthy tissues, particularly in the form of chronic cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure. The ultrastructural features of anthracycline-induced cardiomyopathy include the loss of myofibrils, dilation of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, cytoplasmic vacuolization, swelling of mitochondria, and increased number of lysosomes. Cardiomyocytes are more susceptible to apoptosis induced by anthracyclines than other tissues since they exhibit low levels of catalase and readily undergo inactivation of glutathione peroxidase following exposure to doxorubicin. The reduction of diverse antioxidant enzymes represents a common response to anthracycline treatment. Mitochondrial death pathway in cardiac myocytes is triggered by oxidative stress. Doxorubicin-induced apoptosis of cardiomyocytes proceeds through redox cycling of anthracycline, consequent formation of ROS, and activation of NF-kB. In contrast, NF-kB activation usually inhibits apoptosis induced by doxorubicin in cancer cells. Chronic cardiomyopathy develops after the summation and mutual feedback of diverse processes such as increased membrane lipid peroxidation, inhibition of nucleic acid and protein synthesis, changes in adrenergic function and adenylate cyclase (ADCY), abnormalities in Ca2+ handling, reduced expression of specific genes, and induction of nitric oxide synthase (NOS2), which leads to nitric oxide (NO), peroxynitrite, and converse nitration/activation of metalloproteinases. Compound with elevated level is highlighted in red.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with elevated level are highlighted in red." EXACT []

