synonym: "Source: Pathological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cyclosporine is an immunosuppressant drug used to reduce the activity of a patient's immune system after an organ transplant to lower the risk of organ rejection.    Signaling description: Cyclosporine therapy is associated with several forms of nephrotoxicity with the most significant being the reversible impairment of glomerular filtration and irreversible interstitial fibrosis. The molecular mechanism underlying chronic cyclosporine nephropathy is multifactorial. Cyclosporine initially binds to a specific family of receptors known as cyclophilins. This drug-receptor complex inhibits activation of the phosphatase calcineurin.    Outcome effects: Subsequently, cyclosporine decreases basolateral Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase and apical Na+-K+-Cl- cotransporter activities, which leads to an enhanced intrarenal renin-angiotensin (REN-AGT) system activity. This, in turn, causes the activation of NF-kB and Jun/Fos transcription factors, resulting in the increased production of EDN1, TGFB1, and the proinflammatory cytokine CCL2."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cyclosporine is an immunosuppressant drug used to reduce the activity of a patient's immune system after an organ transplant to lower the risk of organ rejection.    Signaling description: Cyclosporine therapy is associated with several forms of nephrotoxicity with the most significant being the reversible impairment of glomerular filtration and irreversible interstitial fibrosis. The molecular mechanism underlying chronic cyclosporine nephropathy is multifactorial. Cyclosporine initially binds to a specific family of receptors known as cyclophilins. This drug-receptor complex inhibits activation of the phosphatase calcineurin.    Outcome effects: Subsequently, cyclosporine decreases basolateral Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase and apical Na+-K+-Cl- cotransporter activities, which leads to an enhanced intrarenal renin-angiotensin (REN-AGT) system activity. This, in turn, causes the activation of NF-kB and Jun/Fos transcription factors, resulting in the increased production of EDN1, TGFB1, and the proinflammatory cytokine CCL2." EXACT []

