synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mast cells are dissipated in connective tissue and stored in spleen and bone marrow and contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules, including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases. Upon activation, mast cells release the contents of granules into surrounding tissue by a process known as degranulation.    Signaling description: Mast cells are activated by several factors including cold, injury, and radiation. IgE-dependent activation of mast cells is typical for an allergic reaction. In this case, degranulation occurs quickly and mediators are released into the extracellular space producing an immediate effect. The content of granules within mast cells, which includes histamine, heparin, proteases, and cytokines, is pre-synthesized during maturation and proliferation of mast cells. IgE-dependent activation of mast cells involves the binding of IgE to several FCERI receptors.Each FCERI receptor consists of 4 subunits: alpha-particle which is responsible for IgE binding, intracellular beta-subunit, and two intracellular gamma subunits. For IgE-dependent activation, an antigen must bind at least two IgE molecules at the surface of a mast cell. Antigen binding to IgE results in the crosslinking of two FCERI, which leads to the phosphorylation of LYN. A multi-molecular complex, which includes GRAP2, LCP2, PLCG1, and VAV1, form on LAT.    Outcome effects: FCERI aggregation induces PI3K, MAPK1, MAPK8, NF-kB, PLC, and PKC activation. The activation of RAC1, CDC42, and Ras leads to MAPK activation. These pathways result in degranulation, release of leukotrienes, and cytokine synthesis. An increase in calcium (Ca2+) also leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid and lysophospholipid that support the fusion of mast cell granules with the membrane and release of their contents. In mast cells, STIM1 interacts with ORAI1 membrane protein which opens Ca2+ release-activated Ca2+ (CRAC) channels allowing the increase in intracellular Ca2+. Adaptor complexes such as BCL10-MALT1 permit FCERI-dependent IL6 and TNF release without degranulation. In addition, KITLG is a key protein that stimulates degranulation and synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines in mast cells. The intersection of signaling pathways initiated by KIT receptor (SHC1) and FCERI receptors (GRB2) leads to degranulation. KITLG is a factor of directed movement of mast cells. Upon activation, mast cells secrete numerous vasoactive and proinflammatory mediators. The key pre-stored mast cell mediators are biogenic amines (histamine and serotonin), chemokines (IL8, CCL2, CCL7, CCL5, CCL11, and CCL13), enzymes (chymase, kinogenases, phospholipases, and tryptase), peptides (ANG, CRH, EDN1, bradykinin, LEP, and SST), and proteoglycans (chondroitin sulfate, heparin, and hyaluronic acid). As a class, mast cells are heterogeneous and a single mast cell does not make all of these substances. Histamine and heparin dilate post-capillary venules and increase blood vessel permeability. This leads to local swelling, warmth, redness, and the attraction of other inflammatory cells to the source of inflammation. Mediators that are pre-stored in mast cells also lead to itching or pain."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mast cells are dissipated in connective tissue and stored in spleen and bone marrow and contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules, including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases. Upon activation, mast cells release the contents of granules into surrounding tissue by a process known as degranulation.    Signaling description: Mast cells are activated by several factors including cold, injury, and radiation. IgE-dependent activation of mast cells is typical for an allergic reaction. In this case, degranulation occurs quickly and mediators are released into the extracellular space producing an immediate effect. The content of granules within mast cells, which includes histamine, heparin, proteases, and cytokines, is pre-synthesized during maturation and proliferation of mast cells. IgE-dependent activation of mast cells involves the binding of IgE to several FCERI receptors.Each FCERI receptor consists of 4 subunits: alpha-particle which is responsible for IgE binding, intracellular beta-subunit, and two intracellular gamma subunits. For IgE-dependent activation, an antigen must bind at least two IgE molecules at the surface of a mast cell. Antigen binding to IgE results in the crosslinking of two FCERI, which leads to the phosphorylation of LYN. A multi-molecular complex, which includes GRAP2, LCP2, PLCG1, and VAV1, form on LAT.    Outcome effects: FCERI aggregation induces PI3K, MAPK1, MAPK8, NF-kB, PLC, and PKC activation. The activation of RAC1, CDC42, and Ras leads to MAPK activation. These pathways result in degranulation, release of leukotrienes, and cytokine synthesis. An increase in calcium (Ca2+) also leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid and lysophospholipid that support the fusion of mast cell granules with the membrane and release of their contents. In mast cells, STIM1 interacts with ORAI1 membrane protein which opens Ca2+ release-activated Ca2+ (CRAC) channels allowing the increase in intracellular Ca2+. Adaptor complexes such as BCL10-MALT1 permit FCERI-dependent IL6 and TNF release without degranulation. In addition, KITLG is a key protein that stimulates degranulation and synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines in mast cells. The intersection of signaling pathways initiated by KIT receptor (SHC1) and FCERI receptors (GRB2) leads to degranulation. KITLG is a factor of directed movement of mast cells. Upon activation, mast cells secrete numerous vasoactive and proinflammatory mediators. The key pre-stored mast cell mediators are biogenic amines (histamine and serotonin), chemokines (IL8, CCL2, CCL7, CCL5, CCL11, and CCL13), enzymes (chymase, kinogenases, phospholipases, and tryptase), peptides (ANG, CRH, EDN1, bradykinin, LEP, and SST), and proteoglycans (chondroitin sulfate, heparin, and hyaluronic acid). As a class, mast cells are heterogeneous and a single mast cell does not make all of these substances. Histamine and heparin dilate post-capillary venules and increase blood vessel permeability. This leads to local swelling, warmth, redness, and the attraction of other inflammatory cells to the source of inflammation. Mediators that are pre-stored in mast cells also lead to itching or pain." EXACT []

