xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mast cells are dissipated in connective tissue and stored in the spleen and bone marrow and contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases. Upon activation, mast cells release the contents of granules into surrounding tissue by a process known as degranulation.    Signaling description: Mast cells are activated by several causes including external factors such as cold, injury, and radiation. Mast cell activation, through the aggregation of FCeR receptors with IgE, is the main path of activation in allergic reactions. The synthesis of mediators involved in inflammation in mast cells is triggered by alternative FCeR receptors. Determining the number of participating ligands and patterns of intersection between relevant signaling pathways is an important research task. The signaling pathways link mast cell activation with known major triggers. Anaphylatoxins, including C5a and C3, are peptides that are formed upon activation of the complement system by antigens, products of necrosis or apoptosis, antibodies, and pathogens. In addition, thrombin, kallikrein, and house dust mite protease have anaphylatoxin-like activity. TPSAB1, which is released during mast cell activation, leads to the additional production of C3 and C5a. C5a and C3 receptors (C5AR1 and C3AR1) trigger mast cell degranulation as well as chemokine synthesis. The signaling cascade is initiated by a G-protein-dependent mechanism. C5a induces degranulation in mast cells via signaling pathways that require PLCB1 but not PI3K or MAPK1. In contrast, C3 promotes cytokine gene expression in mast cells via signaling pathways that require PLCB1, PI3K, and MAPK1/2. The neuropeptides, NGF and NTF3, activate the functions of mast cells. NGF is the peptide that stimulates growth of several neural cells. Under inflammatory conditions such as allergy and arthritis, NGF is produced, stored, and released by mast cells, as well as other cells. The causes of enhanced NGF concentration are far from being fully understood. The binding interaction between NGF and NTRK1 receptor facilitates the activation of PI3K, AKT1, and RAF1 (MAPK cascade). Neuropeptides stimulate mast cell degranulation and the release of histamine. NGF has recently been shown to stimulate the expression of IL6 in bone marrow-derived mast cells. NGF accumulates in the focus of an allergic reaction or inflammation and stimulates directed motion of mast cells. The increased concentration of NGF causes pain associated with inflammation. NTF3 activates mast cells via NTRK2 and NTRK3 receptors. Anandamide is another neuropeptide that participates in mast cell activation. It is a cannabinoid neurotransmitter that affects behavior and perception. Presumably, lipopolysaccharides, which are inflammatory agents, stimulate the synthesis of anandamide in lymphocytes. Cannabinoid receptors (CNR2) are present in mast cells, but their action regarding mast cell function is poorly understood. It has been suggested that CNR2 can either inhibit or stimulate the release of histamine. Serotonin, which is released upon activation of thrombocytes in inflammation or injury, is capable of activating mast cell degranulation. Mast cells in humans express HTR1A subtype. However, the details of serotonin receptor signaling in mast cells are unknown. It is hypothesized that HTR1A promotes inflammation by increasing mast cells at the site of tissue injury. In addition, lysophosphatidic acid (LPA) is a prominent lipid component of serum that has pleiotropic effects in inflammation. In response to inflammation, LPA diffuses into the extravascular space where it is a potent mast cell activator, which unlike other triggers favors the release of tryptase. In mast cells, LPA acts through LPAR5, which is involved in LPA-induced calcium flux, particularly at low ligand concentrations and crucial for the generation of CCL4 response to LPA. CCL4 is a potent chemoatt"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mast cells are dissipated in connective tissue and stored in the spleen and bone marrow and contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases. Upon activation, mast cells release the contents of granules into surrounding tissue by a process known as degranulation.    Signaling description: Mast cells are activated by several causes including external factors such as cold, injury, and radiation. Mast cell activation, through the aggregation of FCeR receptors with IgE, is the main path of activation in allergic reactions. The synthesis of mediators involved in inflammation in mast cells is triggered by alternative FCeR receptors. Determining the number of participating ligands and patterns of intersection between relevant signaling pathways is an important research task. The signaling pathways link mast cell activation with known major triggers. Anaphylatoxins, including C5a and C3, are peptides that are formed upon activation of the complement system by antigens, products of necrosis or apoptosis, antibodies, and pathogens. In addition, thrombin, kallikrein, and house dust mite protease have anaphylatoxin-like activity. TPSAB1, which is released during mast cell activation, leads to the additional production of C3 and C5a. C5a and C3 receptors (C5AR1 and C3AR1) trigger mast cell degranulation as well as chemokine synthesis. The signaling cascade is initiated by a G-protein-dependent mechanism. C5a induces degranulation in mast cells via signaling pathways that require PLCB1 but not PI3K or MAPK1. In contrast, C3 promotes cytokine gene expression in mast cells via signaling pathways that require PLCB1, PI3K, and MAPK1/2. The neuropeptides, NGF and NTF3, activate the functions of mast cells. NGF is the peptide that stimulates growth of several neural cells. Under inflammatory conditions such as allergy and arthritis, NGF is produced, stored, and released by mast cells, as well as other cells. The causes of enhanced NGF concentration are far from being fully understood. The binding interaction between NGF and NTRK1 receptor facilitates the activation of PI3K, AKT1, and RAF1 (MAPK cascade). Neuropeptides stimulate mast cell degranulation and the release of histamine. NGF has recently been shown to stimulate the expression of IL6 in bone marrow-derived mast cells. NGF accumulates in the focus of an allergic reaction or inflammation and stimulates directed motion of mast cells. The increased concentration of NGF causes pain associated with inflammation. NTF3 activates mast cells via NTRK2 and NTRK3 receptors. Anandamide is another neuropeptide that participates in mast cell activation. It is a cannabinoid neurotransmitter that affects behavior and perception. Presumably, lipopolysaccharides, which are inflammatory agents, stimulate the synthesis of anandamide in lymphocytes. Cannabinoid receptors (CNR2) are present in mast cells, but their action regarding mast cell function is poorly understood. It has been suggested that CNR2 can either inhibit or stimulate the release of histamine. Serotonin, which is released upon activation of thrombocytes in inflammation or injury, is capable of activating mast cell degranulation. Mast cells in humans express HTR1A subtype. However, the details of serotonin receptor signaling in mast cells are unknown. It is hypothesized that HTR1A promotes inflammation by increasing mast cells at the site of tissue injury. In addition, lysophosphatidic acid (LPA) is a prominent lipid component of serum that has pleiotropic effects in inflammation. In response to inflammation, LPA diffuses into the extravascular space where it is a potent mast cell activator, which unlike other triggers favors the release of tryptase. In mast cells, LPA acts through LPAR5, which is involved in LPA-induced calcium flux, particularly at low ligand concentrations and crucial for the generation of CCL4 response to LPA. CCL4 is a potent chemoatt" EXACT []

