xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mast cells are dissipated in connective tissue and stored in the spleen and bone marrow and contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases.    Signaling description: Upon activation, mast cells release the contents of granules into surrounding tissue by a process known as degranulation. At the same time, the activation of mast cells can lead to the synthesis of large amounts of mediators without degranulation. In inflammation, mast cells rarely release the entire contents of granules and synthesize de novo large amounts of molecules that control immune system activation and inflammatory response at the site of injury. It has been shown that specific stimuli (or their combinations) can selectively trigger the expression of specific mediators. The issue of selective synthesis of mediators in mast cells requires further research.    Outcome effects: IL1A and bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) result in selective release of IL6, TNF, and IL8 without degranulation through vesicles (40-80 nm) much smaller than the secretory granules (800-1000 nm). TLR activation on mast cells causes the release of several cytokines including IL6, IL8, TNF, and IL13. Affected by infection-derived triggers, mast cells also synthesize nitric oxide (NO) through TLR pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mast cells are dissipated in connective tissue and stored in the spleen and bone marrow and contain large amounts of cytoplasmic granules including proteoglycans (heparin), histamine, interleukins, and neutral proteases.    Signaling description: Upon activation, mast cells release the contents of granules into surrounding tissue by a process known as degranulation. At the same time, the activation of mast cells can lead to the synthesis of large amounts of mediators without degranulation. In inflammation, mast cells rarely release the entire contents of granules and synthesize de novo large amounts of molecules that control immune system activation and inflammatory response at the site of injury. It has been shown that specific stimuli (or their combinations) can selectively trigger the expression of specific mediators. The issue of selective synthesis of mediators in mast cells requires further research.    Outcome effects: IL1A and bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) result in selective release of IL6, TNF, and IL8 without degranulation through vesicles (40-80 nm) much smaller than the secretory granules (800-1000 nm). TLR activation on mast cells causes the release of several cytokines including IL6, IL8, TNF, and IL13. Affected by infection-derived triggers, mast cells also synthesize nitric oxide (NO) through TLR pathway." EXACT []

