xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Macrophages are professional phagocytes with the ability to take up pathogens and cell debris during inflammation. Iron is necessary for microbial growth and the competition for iron between a host cell and microorganism is a critical issue during infectious processes.    Signaling description: Macrophage iron uptake is accomplished through the action of TFRC and ITLN1 receptors via erythrophagocytosis and hemoglobin/haptoglobin capture by CD163. TFRC, ITLN1, and CD163-mediated endocytic process, together with erythrophagocytosis, brings iron or hemoglobin into the cytoplasm. Heme oxygenase (HMOX1) catalyzes heme degradation into equimolar amounts of iron, carbon monoxide, and biliverdin. STEAP4 functions as a metalloreductase that has the ability to reduce Fe3+ to Fe2+, using NAD as an acceptor. Macrophages accumulate iron in complexes with ferritin or release it via SLC40A1 on the plasma membrane. M1 macrophages are characterized by high levels of ferritin, which results in iron retention. This may lead to bacteriostatic effects, but also represents the cellular basis for anemia in chronic diseases. M2 macrophages are characterized by high levels of CD163, SLC40A1, and HMOX1 that support iron donation to the microenvironment. This may contribute to tissue repair by providing iron to proliferating parenchymal cells and to fibroblasts for collagen synthesis.    Outcome effects: The levels of iron and iron-related metabolites in macrophages influence their function during activation. The activation of HMOX1 at the resolution phase of inflammation protects tissues from microbial-initiated inflammatory damage and switches macrophages from a proinflammatory state M1 to M2 macrophages. Carbon monoxide can inhibit LPS-induced production of IL1B, IL6, CSF2, TNF, and NO in macrophages and monocyte-derived dendritic cells, possibly by preventing CHUK degradation. Biliverdin exhibits anti-oxidant and anti-inflammatory activities. Iron loading of macrophages inhibits IFNG-mediated secretion of proinflammatory cytokines and reduces microbicidal action of macrophages. The inhibitory effect of iron on CEBPB and HIF1A, which mediate NOS2 inducibility by cytokines, further contributes to the diminished microbicidal activity of iron-loaded macrophages. Furthermore, since NO contributes to the inhibition of ferritin translation, iron further contributes to increased iron accumulation through a feedback loop. Lastly, iron regulatory proteins including ACO1 and IREB2 post-transcriptionally regulate mRNA modification of several iron genes."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Macrophages are professional phagocytes with the ability to take up pathogens and cell debris during inflammation. Iron is necessary for microbial growth and the competition for iron between a host cell and microorganism is a critical issue during infectious processes.    Signaling description: Macrophage iron uptake is accomplished through the action of TFRC and ITLN1 receptors via erythrophagocytosis and hemoglobin/haptoglobin capture by CD163. TFRC, ITLN1, and CD163-mediated endocytic process, together with erythrophagocytosis, brings iron or hemoglobin into the cytoplasm. Heme oxygenase (HMOX1) catalyzes heme degradation into equimolar amounts of iron, carbon monoxide, and biliverdin. STEAP4 functions as a metalloreductase that has the ability to reduce Fe3+ to Fe2+, using NAD as an acceptor. Macrophages accumulate iron in complexes with ferritin or release it via SLC40A1 on the plasma membrane. M1 macrophages are characterized by high levels of ferritin, which results in iron retention. This may lead to bacteriostatic effects, but also represents the cellular basis for anemia in chronic diseases. M2 macrophages are characterized by high levels of CD163, SLC40A1, and HMOX1 that support iron donation to the microenvironment. This may contribute to tissue repair by providing iron to proliferating parenchymal cells and to fibroblasts for collagen synthesis.    Outcome effects: The levels of iron and iron-related metabolites in macrophages influence their function during activation. The activation of HMOX1 at the resolution phase of inflammation protects tissues from microbial-initiated inflammatory damage and switches macrophages from a proinflammatory state M1 to M2 macrophages. Carbon monoxide can inhibit LPS-induced production of IL1B, IL6, CSF2, TNF, and NO in macrophages and monocyte-derived dendritic cells, possibly by preventing CHUK degradation. Biliverdin exhibits anti-oxidant and anti-inflammatory activities. Iron loading of macrophages inhibits IFNG-mediated secretion of proinflammatory cytokines and reduces microbicidal action of macrophages. The inhibitory effect of iron on CEBPB and HIF1A, which mediate NOS2 inducibility by cytokines, further contributes to the diminished microbicidal activity of iron-loaded macrophages. Furthermore, since NO contributes to the inhibition of ferritin translation, iron further contributes to increased iron accumulation through a feedback loop. Lastly, iron regulatory proteins including ACO1 and IREB2 post-transcriptionally regulate mRNA modification of several iron genes." EXACT []

