xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Monocytes differentiate into macrophages in injured tissues under the influence of CSF2. Macrophages are a heterogeneous cell population. The activation of macrophages in response to pathogenic stimuli, endogenous danger signals, and/or type 1 cytokines (IFNG and TNF) is termed classical activation. The resulting macrophages, identified as M1 macrophages, exhibit potent microbicidal activity and promote strong Th1 immune responses.    Signaling description: CSF2 induces monocyte-macrophage M1 lineage differentiation. When CSF2-differentiated human macrophages are engaged with bacterial products and Th1- cytokines such as IFNG and TNF, they are polarized to classically activated M1 macrophages. M1 macrophages are also activated following the recognition of pathogen-associated molecular patterns or PAMPs such as lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and endogenous danger signals such as HMGB1. Additionally, IRF5-mediated cytokine activation is specific for M1 activation.    Outcome effects: IRF3 and NF-kB are responsible for the expression of chemokines specific for M1. Furthermore, M1 population is thought to contribute to macrophage-mediated tissue injury. M1 exerts anti-proliferative and cytotoxic activities, which is due in part to the release of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species and proinflammatory cytokines including TNF, IL1, IL6, and IL12."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Monocytes differentiate into macrophages in injured tissues under the influence of CSF2. Macrophages are a heterogeneous cell population. The activation of macrophages in response to pathogenic stimuli, endogenous danger signals, and/or type 1 cytokines (IFNG and TNF) is termed classical activation. The resulting macrophages, identified as M1 macrophages, exhibit potent microbicidal activity and promote strong Th1 immune responses.    Signaling description: CSF2 induces monocyte-macrophage M1 lineage differentiation. When CSF2-differentiated human macrophages are engaged with bacterial products and Th1- cytokines such as IFNG and TNF, they are polarized to classically activated M1 macrophages. M1 macrophages are also activated following the recognition of pathogen-associated molecular patterns or PAMPs such as lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and endogenous danger signals such as HMGB1. Additionally, IRF5-mediated cytokine activation is specific for M1 activation.    Outcome effects: IRF3 and NF-kB are responsible for the expression of chemokines specific for M1. Furthermore, M1 population is thought to contribute to macrophage-mediated tissue injury. M1 exerts anti-proliferative and cytotoxic activities, which is due in part to the release of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species and proinflammatory cytokines including TNF, IL1, IL6, and IL12." EXACT []

