xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Bloodstream monocytes are a heterogeneous lineage of the cell and distinct cell subsets that can be defined by the expression of several receptors. Monocytes differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells in tissues and focuses of inflammation. Monocytes, and their macrophage and dendritic cell progeny, serve three main functions in the immune system including phagocytosis, antigen presentation, and cytokine production.    Signaling description: Monocytes are divided into subclasses due to the expression levels of CD14 and CD16 membrane receptors. Classical monocytes are characterized by a high expression level of CD14 cell surface receptors including CD14 CD16- monocyte. The non-classical or proinflammatory subclass of monocytes has high levels of CD16 and CD14 expression (CD16 CD14). Other surface molecules that are highly expressed on CD14 CD16 monocytes are chemokine receptors CCR5 and CX3CR1, ITGAX, PECAM1, SPN, MHC class II protein complex, and CXCR4. SPN is a sialylated glycoprotein expressed on the surface of most hematopoietic cells and has been implicated in cell adhesion and signaling. SPN activation enhances hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) production in monocytes; however the detailed mechanisms of this effect are poorly understood. CD16 proinflammatory monocytes express higher levels of CX3CR1 and CSF1R. CSF1 is a potent maturation signal for monocytes and a survival and proliferative factor for macrophages. CSF1 and low levels of CSF2 dramatically increase macrophage generation, whereas higher concentrations of CSF2 result in the inhibition of CSF1-mediated macrophage growth.    Outcome effects: Most inflammatory monocytes are thought to differentiate into macrophages, which are important for the clearance of pathogens and for the resolution of inflammation. The number of proinflammatory monocytes increases in injured tissue. Proinflammatory cytokines may activate the differentiation of this subclass of monocytes. For example, TGFA has been shown to induce CD16 expression on monocytes. Proinflammatory monocytes also produce lower levels of IL10 in response to a TLR4 agonist and higher amounts of TNF and IL12B in response to TLR4 and TLR2 agonists. Monocytes kill bacteria by producing reactive nitrogen intermediates (RNIs) and reactive oxygen intermediates (ROIs) and through the differentiation of macrophages and production of proinflammatory cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Bloodstream monocytes are a heterogeneous lineage of the cell and distinct cell subsets that can be defined by the expression of several receptors. Monocytes differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells in tissues and focuses of inflammation. Monocytes, and their macrophage and dendritic cell progeny, serve three main functions in the immune system including phagocytosis, antigen presentation, and cytokine production.    Signaling description: Monocytes are divided into subclasses due to the expression levels of CD14 and CD16 membrane receptors. Classical monocytes are characterized by a high expression level of CD14 cell surface receptors including CD14 CD16- monocyte. The non-classical or proinflammatory subclass of monocytes has high levels of CD16 and CD14 expression (CD16 CD14). Other surface molecules that are highly expressed on CD14 CD16 monocytes are chemokine receptors CCR5 and CX3CR1, ITGAX, PECAM1, SPN, MHC class II protein complex, and CXCR4. SPN is a sialylated glycoprotein expressed on the surface of most hematopoietic cells and has been implicated in cell adhesion and signaling. SPN activation enhances hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) production in monocytes; however the detailed mechanisms of this effect are poorly understood. CD16 proinflammatory monocytes express higher levels of CX3CR1 and CSF1R. CSF1 is a potent maturation signal for monocytes and a survival and proliferative factor for macrophages. CSF1 and low levels of CSF2 dramatically increase macrophage generation, whereas higher concentrations of CSF2 result in the inhibition of CSF1-mediated macrophage growth.    Outcome effects: Most inflammatory monocytes are thought to differentiate into macrophages, which are important for the clearance of pathogens and for the resolution of inflammation. The number of proinflammatory monocytes increases in injured tissue. Proinflammatory cytokines may activate the differentiation of this subclass of monocytes. For example, TGFA has been shown to induce CD16 expression on monocytes. Proinflammatory monocytes also produce lower levels of IL10 in response to a TLR4 agonist and higher amounts of TNF and IL12B in response to TLR4 and TLR2 agonists. Monocytes kill bacteria by producing reactive nitrogen intermediates (RNIs) and reactive oxygen intermediates (ROIs) and through the differentiation of macrophages and production of proinflammatory cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

