xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Macrophages are professional phagocytes with the ability to take up pathogens and cell debris during inflammation.    Signaling description: During phagocytosis, macrophages engulf microbes into specialized compartments known as phagosomes. Macrophages, similarly to neutrophils, can eat labeled cells by opsonins in Fc receptor-mediated and CR3-mediated processes. In addition, macrophages can swallow cells and debris without opsonin-related mechanisms. Macrophage M1 lineage, activated by classic activation signals such as IFNG, express opsonic receptors including Fcgamma R III/FCGR3A, whereas M2 macrophages are characterized by abundant levels of non-opsonic receptors. MC1R, which is expressed primarily by macrophages, recognizes mannose and fucose on the surface of pathogens and mediates phagocytosis. During MC1R-mediated phagocytosis, the actin cytoskeleton is mobilized around the nascent phagosomes. In addition to the phagocytic signals mediating particle internalization, proinflammatory signals are generated upon MC1R activation. Transcriptional activation most likely occurs via PTPRC-related signaling pathway, which is associated with MC1R receptor; however the specific mechanisms of this are unknown. A possible signaling cascade involving MC1R-dependent phagocytosis produces IL1, IL6, CSF2, TNF, and IL12. Therefore, similar to phagocytosis mediated by Fc receptor, MC1R-mediated phagocytosis is a proinflammatory process due to the production of proinflammatory cytokines. In addition, M2 macrophages play a role in the resolution of inflammation through the phagocytosis of apoptotic neutrophils. The receptors known to participate in the phagocytosis of apoptotic cells by macrophages include MSR1 and CD36 scavenger receptors, which act in conjunction with vitronectin receptor (integrin) and CD14.    Outcome effects: The ligands on the apoptotic cell that are recognized by these receptors include sugars, phosphatidylserine, and surface-bound thrombospondin. CD36 has been identified as one of the receptors for collagen type I, oxidized LDL, and phosphatidylserine. Thrombospondin has been suggested to bridge apoptotic cells, CD36, and the vitronectin receptor (integrin), which results in a phagocytically active ternary complex. Vitronectin receptor (integrin) may stimulate the polymerization of actin which results in CD36-dependent internalization of apoptotic cells. CD14 has also been implicated in the recognition and internalization of apoptotic cells. It may be possible that TLR2 is the transmembrane protein that associates with LPS/LBP/CD14 ternary complex to mediate the proinflammatory response in macrophages."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Macrophages are professional phagocytes with the ability to take up pathogens and cell debris during inflammation.    Signaling description: During phagocytosis, macrophages engulf microbes into specialized compartments known as phagosomes. Macrophages, similarly to neutrophils, can eat labeled cells by opsonins in Fc receptor-mediated and CR3-mediated processes. In addition, macrophages can swallow cells and debris without opsonin-related mechanisms. Macrophage M1 lineage, activated by classic activation signals such as IFNG, express opsonic receptors including Fcgamma R III/FCGR3A, whereas M2 macrophages are characterized by abundant levels of non-opsonic receptors. MC1R, which is expressed primarily by macrophages, recognizes mannose and fucose on the surface of pathogens and mediates phagocytosis. During MC1R-mediated phagocytosis, the actin cytoskeleton is mobilized around the nascent phagosomes. In addition to the phagocytic signals mediating particle internalization, proinflammatory signals are generated upon MC1R activation. Transcriptional activation most likely occurs via PTPRC-related signaling pathway, which is associated with MC1R receptor; however the specific mechanisms of this are unknown. A possible signaling cascade involving MC1R-dependent phagocytosis produces IL1, IL6, CSF2, TNF, and IL12. Therefore, similar to phagocytosis mediated by Fc receptor, MC1R-mediated phagocytosis is a proinflammatory process due to the production of proinflammatory cytokines. In addition, M2 macrophages play a role in the resolution of inflammation through the phagocytosis of apoptotic neutrophils. The receptors known to participate in the phagocytosis of apoptotic cells by macrophages include MSR1 and CD36 scavenger receptors, which act in conjunction with vitronectin receptor (integrin) and CD14.    Outcome effects: The ligands on the apoptotic cell that are recognized by these receptors include sugars, phosphatidylserine, and surface-bound thrombospondin. CD36 has been identified as one of the receptors for collagen type I, oxidized LDL, and phosphatidylserine. Thrombospondin has been suggested to bridge apoptotic cells, CD36, and the vitronectin receptor (integrin), which results in a phagocytically active ternary complex. Vitronectin receptor (integrin) may stimulate the polymerization of actin which results in CD36-dependent internalization of apoptotic cells. CD14 has also been implicated in the recognition and internalization of apoptotic cells. It may be possible that TLR2 is the transmembrane protein that associates with LPS/LBP/CD14 ternary complex to mediate the proinflammatory response in macrophages." EXACT []

