xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Macrophages are professional phagocytes with the ability to take up pathogens and cell debris during inflammation. Macrophages are a heterogeneous cell population. Classically-activated M1 phenotype macrophages display a response to injury by releasing cytotoxic and proinflammatory mediators, which contribute to tissue injury.    Signaling description: M2 phenotype macrophages are activated by Th2 cytokines and have low cytotoxic function, and at the same time high tissue remodeling activity. The molecular mechanisms that regulate the differentiation of M1 and M2 macrophages remain unknown. CSF1 is a potent factor of macrophage differentiation from monocytes in local injury sites. M2 macrophages accumulate at the site of injury later in the inflammatory process. INPP5D appears to repress the generation of M2 macrophages. M2 macrophages down-regulate M1 cell activation and the production of cytotoxic inflammatory mediators. M2 macrophage populations are described as phenotypically distinct; however, they are a cell population with diverse phenotypes that exist in interdependence and are able to transform from one to another. M2 macrophages are subdivided into M2a generation (activated by IL4 or IL13), M2b (activated by immune complexes in combination with IL1B or LPS), and M2c (activated by IL10, TGFB, or glucocorticoids). In general, alternative activation results in M2 macrophages releasing IL10 and exerting selective immunosuppressive activity and inhibiting T-cell proliferation. At the molecular level, IL4 and IL13 signal mediated induction of the transcriptional coactivator, PPARGC1B, which in cooperation with STAT6, appears to be critical for the promotion of alternative macrophage activation. LPS and IL1B trigger M2b-like macrophage polarization via IRF4 signaling.    Outcome effects: M2 macrophages phagocytize apoptotic neutrophils, reduce the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as IL10, and increase the synthesis of mediators that are important in tissue remodeling, angiogenesis, and wound repair including VEGFA and TNF. M2 macrophages also stimulate fibroblasts and macrophages to synthesize extracellular matrix proteins. M2 macrophages express chemokines such as CCL17, CCL22, and CCL24. CCL2 and CXCL10 polarize macrophages to an M2-like phenotype. M2c macrophages that are glucocorticoid-activated up regulate genes with known anti-oxidative, anti-inflammatory, and immunosuppressive functions such as ADORA3 and CD163. M2 macrophages exhibit high levels of pathogen and scavenger receptors, such as MC1R, which provide them with stronger endocytotic and phagocytic activities."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Macrophages are professional phagocytes with the ability to take up pathogens and cell debris during inflammation. Macrophages are a heterogeneous cell population. Classically-activated M1 phenotype macrophages display a response to injury by releasing cytotoxic and proinflammatory mediators, which contribute to tissue injury.    Signaling description: M2 phenotype macrophages are activated by Th2 cytokines and have low cytotoxic function, and at the same time high tissue remodeling activity. The molecular mechanisms that regulate the differentiation of M1 and M2 macrophages remain unknown. CSF1 is a potent factor of macrophage differentiation from monocytes in local injury sites. M2 macrophages accumulate at the site of injury later in the inflammatory process. INPP5D appears to repress the generation of M2 macrophages. M2 macrophages down-regulate M1 cell activation and the production of cytotoxic inflammatory mediators. M2 macrophage populations are described as phenotypically distinct; however, they are a cell population with diverse phenotypes that exist in interdependence and are able to transform from one to another. M2 macrophages are subdivided into M2a generation (activated by IL4 or IL13), M2b (activated by immune complexes in combination with IL1B or LPS), and M2c (activated by IL10, TGFB, or glucocorticoids). In general, alternative activation results in M2 macrophages releasing IL10 and exerting selective immunosuppressive activity and inhibiting T-cell proliferation. At the molecular level, IL4 and IL13 signal mediated induction of the transcriptional coactivator, PPARGC1B, which in cooperation with STAT6, appears to be critical for the promotion of alternative macrophage activation. LPS and IL1B trigger M2b-like macrophage polarization via IRF4 signaling.    Outcome effects: M2 macrophages phagocytize apoptotic neutrophils, reduce the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as IL10, and increase the synthesis of mediators that are important in tissue remodeling, angiogenesis, and wound repair including VEGFA and TNF. M2 macrophages also stimulate fibroblasts and macrophages to synthesize extracellular matrix proteins. M2 macrophages express chemokines such as CCL17, CCL22, and CCL24. CCL2 and CXCL10 polarize macrophages to an M2-like phenotype. M2c macrophages that are glucocorticoid-activated up regulate genes with known anti-oxidative, anti-inflammatory, and immunosuppressive functions such as ADORA3 and CD163. M2 macrophages exhibit high levels of pathogen and scavenger receptors, such as MC1R, which provide them with stronger endocytotic and phagocytic activities." EXACT []

