xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The subfamily of TAM receptors includes AXL, MERTK and TYRO3 receptor protein tyrosine kinases. GAS6 and PROS1 are well known ligands for TAM receptors. TAM receptors have pivotal roles in innate immunity.    Signaling description: Toll-like receptor (TLR4/3/9) signaling is stimulated by pathogens in quiescent macrophages and dendritic cells and initiated burst of pro-inflammatory cytokines expression. These cytokine, such as IFNA1, TNF, IL6, IL12A and IL12B lead to inflammatory response activation. Overexpressed cytokines provide feedback on macrophages and dendritic cells and cause activation of the AXL receptor. TAM receptor signaling through SOCS1/3 leads to inhibition of cytokine expression. Precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway. AXL receptor signaling requires coordinate interaction with both FNAR and STAT1.    Outcome effects: Finally, AXL receptor related inhibition of cytokines expression ends the inflammatory reaction. TAM-deficient mice were shown to develop broad-spectrum autoimmune diseases.    Animal data: Role of TAM receptors in the process of clearance of apoptotic cells was revealed from animal model experiments.    Highlighted proteins: IFNA1, TNF, IL6, IL12A and IL12B are highlighted in blue in order to illustrate AXL receptor signaling effects on their reduced expression. AXL receptor and SOCS1/3 are highlighted in red color in order to show their activated expression."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The subfamily of TAM receptors includes AXL, MERTK and TYRO3 receptor protein tyrosine kinases. GAS6 and PROS1 are well known ligands for TAM receptors. TAM receptors have pivotal roles in innate immunity.    Signaling description: Toll-like receptor (TLR4/3/9) signaling is stimulated by pathogens in quiescent macrophages and dendritic cells and initiated burst of pro-inflammatory cytokines expression. These cytokine, such as IFNA1, TNF, IL6, IL12A and IL12B lead to inflammatory response activation. Overexpressed cytokines provide feedback on macrophages and dendritic cells and cause activation of the AXL receptor. TAM receptor signaling through SOCS1/3 leads to inhibition of cytokine expression. Precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway. AXL receptor signaling requires coordinate interaction with both FNAR and STAT1.    Outcome effects: Finally, AXL receptor related inhibition of cytokines expression ends the inflammatory reaction. TAM-deficient mice were shown to develop broad-spectrum autoimmune diseases.    Animal data: Role of TAM receptors in the process of clearance of apoptotic cells was revealed from animal model experiments.    Highlighted proteins: IFNA1, TNF, IL6, IL12A and IL12B are highlighted in blue in order to illustrate AXL receptor signaling effects on their reduced expression. AXL receptor and SOCS1/3 are highlighted in red color in order to show their activated expression." EXACT []

