xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: L-cells produce multifunctional regulators of gastrointestinal functions, metabolism and eating behavior.    Signaling description: Glucagon-like peptide 1, glucagon-like peptide 2 and oxyntomodulin are released from L-cells following the processing of GCG. The PYY processes to peptide YY(3-36). Products of processing of GCG interact with LP1R and GLP2 in mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons. The peptide YY(3-36) interacts with NPY2R. These receptors via G -proteins initiate 2 pathways: activation of cyclic nucleotide-gated channel by cAMP and PLC/ITPR1 signaling. In both cases a Ca2+ overload occurs leading to cGMP synthesis and K+ channel activation. Serotonin secreted by L-cells interacts with HTR3A which participates in the regulation of the K+ efflux. Transmission of the nerve impulse via the vagal nerve leads to pancreatic juice secretion, intestinal absorption, and the inhibition of gastric motility. Non-vagal actions of the peptides released by L-cells are due to transfer of the peptides through the blood stream. These peptides are positive regulators of the satiety. Glucagon-like peptide 1 and peptide YY(3-36) induce the release of insulin (INS). L-cells' peptides regulate the activation of Paneth cells. In addition, glucagon-like peptide-1 inhibits lymphocyte migration.    Outcome effects: Peptides released by L-cells and serotonin regulate gastrointestinal functions via the vagal nerve. Presence of the peptides in plasma leads to the sense of satiety and insulin release."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: L-cells produce multifunctional regulators of gastrointestinal functions, metabolism and eating behavior.    Signaling description: Glucagon-like peptide 1, glucagon-like peptide 2 and oxyntomodulin are released from L-cells following the processing of GCG. The PYY processes to peptide YY(3-36). Products of processing of GCG interact with LP1R and GLP2 in mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons. The peptide YY(3-36) interacts with NPY2R. These receptors via G -proteins initiate 2 pathways: activation of cyclic nucleotide-gated channel by cAMP and PLC/ITPR1 signaling. In both cases a Ca2+ overload occurs leading to cGMP synthesis and K+ channel activation. Serotonin secreted by L-cells interacts with HTR3A which participates in the regulation of the K+ efflux. Transmission of the nerve impulse via the vagal nerve leads to pancreatic juice secretion, intestinal absorption, and the inhibition of gastric motility. Non-vagal actions of the peptides released by L-cells are due to transfer of the peptides through the blood stream. These peptides are positive regulators of the satiety. Glucagon-like peptide 1 and peptide YY(3-36) induce the release of insulin (INS). L-cells' peptides regulate the activation of Paneth cells. In addition, glucagon-like peptide-1 inhibits lymphocyte migration.    Outcome effects: Peptides released by L-cells and serotonin regulate gastrointestinal functions via the vagal nerve. Presence of the peptides in plasma leads to the sense of satiety and insulin release." EXACT []

