xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Guanylyl cyclase C (GUCY2C), a transmembrane enzyme expressed in the intestine and brain, is the main regulator of intestinal fluidity and secretion of water into the intestinal lumen which is necessary for the lubrication and breakdown of the bolus of food.    Signaling description: GUCY2C is activated by two known endogenous peptide hormones, GUCA2A and GUCA2B, which are also produced in the intestinal epithelium and activate GUCY2C in a paracrine fashion. GUCY2C is also activated by the exogenous ligand, heat-stable enterotoxin, which is produced by enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli which is responsible for travelers or secretory diarrhea. The binding of these three known cognate ligands activates GUCY2C to convert GTP to cGMP. cGMP activates PRKG2 and inhibits the activity of the cAMP-phosphodiesterases PDE3, thereby cross-activating cAMP-dependent protein kinase (PKA). PRKG2 and PKA phosphorylate CFTR and CLCN2 increasing its Cl- secreting activity. cGMP also enhances duodenal bicarbonate secretion in a CFTR-dependent manner. cGMP also inhibits SLC9A2 /SLC9A3 channel, reducing sodium (Na ) uptake. Although this mechanism is well established, it has been recently debated that water accumulation in the intestinal lumen is not as much a result of an active secretion of electrolytes as it is a consequence of impaired fluid absorption. GUCY2C signaling is terminated by the hydrolysis of cGMP to GMP by PDE5A. Heat-stable enterotoxin is a super-agonist of GUCY2C and induces abnormally high levels of intracellular cGMP which causes aberrant fluid-ion efflux, leading to diarrhea.    Outcome effects: As the main regulator of intestinal fluidity, GUCY2C is a therapeutic target for the treatment of constipation-related disorders. Linaclotide, an agonist of GUCY2C, is an investigational drug against constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome and chronic constipation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Guanylyl cyclase C (GUCY2C), a transmembrane enzyme expressed in the intestine and brain, is the main regulator of intestinal fluidity and secretion of water into the intestinal lumen which is necessary for the lubrication and breakdown of the bolus of food.    Signaling description: GUCY2C is activated by two known endogenous peptide hormones, GUCA2A and GUCA2B, which are also produced in the intestinal epithelium and activate GUCY2C in a paracrine fashion. GUCY2C is also activated by the exogenous ligand, heat-stable enterotoxin, which is produced by enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli which is responsible for travelers or secretory diarrhea. The binding of these three known cognate ligands activates GUCY2C to convert GTP to cGMP. cGMP activates PRKG2 and inhibits the activity of the cAMP-phosphodiesterases PDE3, thereby cross-activating cAMP-dependent protein kinase (PKA). PRKG2 and PKA phosphorylate CFTR and CLCN2 increasing its Cl- secreting activity. cGMP also enhances duodenal bicarbonate secretion in a CFTR-dependent manner. cGMP also inhibits SLC9A2 /SLC9A3 channel, reducing sodium (Na ) uptake. Although this mechanism is well established, it has been recently debated that water accumulation in the intestinal lumen is not as much a result of an active secretion of electrolytes as it is a consequence of impaired fluid absorption. GUCY2C signaling is terminated by the hydrolysis of cGMP to GMP by PDE5A. Heat-stable enterotoxin is a super-agonist of GUCY2C and induces abnormally high levels of intracellular cGMP which causes aberrant fluid-ion efflux, leading to diarrhea.    Outcome effects: As the main regulator of intestinal fluidity, GUCY2C is a therapeutic target for the treatment of constipation-related disorders. Linaclotide, an agonist of GUCY2C, is an investigational drug against constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome and chronic constipation." EXACT []

