xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Products released by A-cells (stomach enteroendocrine cells similar to X-cells) regulate stomach functional state and satiety. Activation of A-cells initiates expression and release of ghrelin and nesfatin-1, which activate mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons which transmit the nerve impulse through vagal nerve to the brain.    Signaling description: Activation of GHSR1 by ghrelin induces transmission of the nerve impulse through enhanced PLCb1 and PKC activation and calcium mobilization which stimulate the transmission of nerve impulse by unknown mechanisms. The nerve impulse transmitted through vagal nerve activates gastric motility and pancreatic juice secretion and weakens the feeling of the satiety. Ghrelin is released in the first wave of secretion and nesfatin-1 in the second wave (10-20 minutes after the ghrelin). Nesfatin-1 acts through unknown GPCR that activates cAMP/PKC pathway and extracellular Ca2+ influx. Intracellular calcium interacts and activates GUCA1B /cGMP synthesis and K+ channel opening. Extracellular K+ leads to membrane depolarization and transmission of the nerve impulse through vagal nerve to the brain region which reduces gastric motility, pancreatic juice secretion and satiety.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin and nesfatin-1 activate signaling in mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons. These two neuropeptides have opposite functions.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin and nesfatin-1 activates signaling in mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons. These two neuropeptides have opposite function."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Products released by A-cells (stomach enteroendocrine cells similar to X-cells) regulate stomach functional state and satiety. Activation of A-cells initiates expression and release of ghrelin and nesfatin-1, which activate mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons which transmit the nerve impulse through vagal nerve to the brain.    Signaling description: Activation of GHSR1 by ghrelin induces transmission of the nerve impulse through enhanced PLCb1 and PKC activation and calcium mobilization which stimulate the transmission of nerve impulse by unknown mechanisms. The nerve impulse transmitted through vagal nerve activates gastric motility and pancreatic juice secretion and weakens the feeling of the satiety. Ghrelin is released in the first wave of secretion and nesfatin-1 in the second wave (10-20 minutes after the ghrelin). Nesfatin-1 acts through unknown GPCR that activates cAMP/PKC pathway and extracellular Ca2+ influx. Intracellular calcium interacts and activates GUCA1B /cGMP synthesis and K+ channel opening. Extracellular K+ leads to membrane depolarization and transmission of the nerve impulse through vagal nerve to the brain region which reduces gastric motility, pancreatic juice secretion and satiety.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin and nesfatin-1 activate signaling in mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons. These two neuropeptides have opposite functions.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin and nesfatin-1 activates signaling in mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons. These two neuropeptides have opposite function." EXACT []

