synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Leptin and CCK-8 regulate eating behavior by stimulating the sense of the satiety. Headnote: Leptin and CCK-8 regulate eating behavior by stimulating the sense of the satiety. Receptors to leptin and CCK-8 are located in nodose ganglia neuron (inferior ganglion of the vagus nerve, vagal afferent neurons).    Signaling description: In nodose ganglia neuron CCK-8 interacts with cholecystokinin A receptor (CCKAR) which via GNA11 activates PLCB1. PLCB1/diacylglycerol activate PKC/SRC/HRAS signaling, and HRAS, in turn activates MAPK signaling which leads to FOS activation and EGR1 transcription and translocation to the nucleus with the help of IPO7. Leptin signaling in the nodose ganglia neuron occurs via the leptin receptor (LEPR) and JAK2/IRS2/PI3K signaling. PI3K activates STAT3 mediated EGR1 transcription. STAT3 activates K+ channel and leads to the efflux of K+ and transmission of the nerve impulse. STAT3 activates SOCS3 transcription that inhibits the LEPR signaling. The CCK-8 and leptin signaling pathways enhance each other via co-stimulation. CCKAR signaling also activates PI3K and RHOA by SRC. LEPR signaling pathway also activates PTPN11/MAP2K1 and SRC. The cross-talk of the CCK-8 and leptin signaling enhances the expression of the CART prepropeptide (CARTPT) by EGR1. CARTPT is proteolytically processed to generate multiple biologically active peptides. These peptides are involved in appetite, energy balance, maintenance of body weight, reward and addiction, and the stress response.    Outcome effects: The CCK-8 and leptin signaling pathways co-stimulate each other in the nodal ganglia neurons to induce release of CART prepropeptide.    Animal data: The synergistic action of leptin and CCK-8 in nodose ganglia neuron was established in animal models."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Leptin and CCK-8 regulate eating behavior by stimulating the sense of the satiety. Headnote: Leptin and CCK-8 regulate eating behavior by stimulating the sense of the satiety. Receptors to leptin and CCK-8 are located in nodose ganglia neuron (inferior ganglion of the vagus nerve, vagal afferent neurons).    Signaling description: In nodose ganglia neuron CCK-8 interacts with cholecystokinin A receptor (CCKAR) which via GNA11 activates PLCB1. PLCB1/diacylglycerol activate PKC/SRC/HRAS signaling, and HRAS, in turn activates MAPK signaling which leads to FOS activation and EGR1 transcription and translocation to the nucleus with the help of IPO7. Leptin signaling in the nodose ganglia neuron occurs via the leptin receptor (LEPR) and JAK2/IRS2/PI3K signaling. PI3K activates STAT3 mediated EGR1 transcription. STAT3 activates K+ channel and leads to the efflux of K+ and transmission of the nerve impulse. STAT3 activates SOCS3 transcription that inhibits the LEPR signaling. The CCK-8 and leptin signaling pathways enhance each other via co-stimulation. CCKAR signaling also activates PI3K and RHOA by SRC. LEPR signaling pathway also activates PTPN11/MAP2K1 and SRC. The cross-talk of the CCK-8 and leptin signaling enhances the expression of the CART prepropeptide (CARTPT) by EGR1. CARTPT is proteolytically processed to generate multiple biologically active peptides. These peptides are involved in appetite, energy balance, maintenance of body weight, reward and addiction, and the stress response.    Outcome effects: The CCK-8 and leptin signaling pathways co-stimulate each other in the nodal ganglia neurons to induce release of CART prepropeptide.    Animal data: The synergistic action of leptin and CCK-8 in nodose ganglia neuron was established in animal models." EXACT []

