xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Ghrelin activates the release of GHRH, which in turn activates the release of somatostatin (SST). SST blocks the release of ghrelin and GHRH through a feedback loop mechanism. Ghrelin, GHRH and SST work together in the neurons of hypothalamus.    Signaling description: Ghrelin interacts with the growth hormone secretagogue receptor (GHSR) leading to intracellular Ca2+ influx through the PLC/PKC pathway. Intracellular calcium activates the secretion of growth hormone (GH1). GHRH binds to GHRHR and activates the ADCY/cAMP/PKA pathway. PKA phosphorylates and activates the transcription factors POU1F1 and CREB1 which bind to GH1 promoter and activate its transcription. SST inhibits transcription of GH1 by blocking the ADCY signaling.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin and GHRH are important for the stimulation of neurons in hypothalamus. Effects of ghrelin and GHRH can be inhibited by SST.    Animal data: The effects of ghrelin, GHRH and SST in hypothalamic neurons were revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Ghrelin activates the release of GHRH, which in turn activates the release of somatostatin (SST). SST blocks the release of ghrelin and GHRH through a feedback loop mechanism. Ghrelin, GHRH and SST work together in the neurons of hypothalamus.    Signaling description: Ghrelin interacts with the growth hormone secretagogue receptor (GHSR) leading to intracellular Ca2+ influx through the PLC/PKC pathway. Intracellular calcium activates the secretion of growth hormone (GH1). GHRH binds to GHRHR and activates the ADCY/cAMP/PKA pathway. PKA phosphorylates and activates the transcription factors POU1F1 and CREB1 which bind to GH1 promoter and activate its transcription. SST inhibits transcription of GH1 by blocking the ADCY signaling.    Outcome effects: Ghrelin and GHRH are important for the stimulation of neurons in hypothalamus. Effects of ghrelin and GHRH can be inhibited by SST.    Animal data: The effects of ghrelin, GHRH and SST in hypothalamic neurons were revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

