xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Guanylyl cyclase C (GUCY2C) is a transmembrane enzyme expressed in the intestine and brain. The hypothalamus integrates neurohormonal signaling from the periphery, namely the gut and adipose tissue, to regulate food intake and energy homeostasis. GUCY2C represents one end of a gut-brain endocrine axis, anchored by expression in the hypothalamus.    Signaling description: GUCA2B is produced in the intestine and released postprandially into the systemic circulation to travel to and activate GUCY2C in the hypothalamus to signal energy levels. In the brain, GUCY2C activation by GUCA2B leads to cGMP-dependent CNGA1 activation and calcium (Ca2 ) influx. Furthermore, GUCY2C activation and the resulting increase in intracellular cGMP may induce activation and nuclear translocation of PRKG1 and intracellular Ca2 release. The elevation of intracellular Ca2 levels regulates neurotransmitter release and potentiates synaptic transmission.    Outcome effects: The possible downstream effects may include the depolarization of neurons expressing the anorexigenic neuropeptides, POMC and CARTPT, which increase the firing of anorexigenic neurons and the hyperpolarization of neurons expressing NPY and AGRP, which decrease the firing of orexigenic neurons. Thus, GUCY2C may represent a new target for anti-obesity pharmacotherapy."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Guanylyl cyclase C (GUCY2C) is a transmembrane enzyme expressed in the intestine and brain. The hypothalamus integrates neurohormonal signaling from the periphery, namely the gut and adipose tissue, to regulate food intake and energy homeostasis. GUCY2C represents one end of a gut-brain endocrine axis, anchored by expression in the hypothalamus.    Signaling description: GUCA2B is produced in the intestine and released postprandially into the systemic circulation to travel to and activate GUCY2C in the hypothalamus to signal energy levels. In the brain, GUCY2C activation by GUCA2B leads to cGMP-dependent CNGA1 activation and calcium (Ca2 ) influx. Furthermore, GUCY2C activation and the resulting increase in intracellular cGMP may induce activation and nuclear translocation of PRKG1 and intracellular Ca2 release. The elevation of intracellular Ca2 levels regulates neurotransmitter release and potentiates synaptic transmission.    Outcome effects: The possible downstream effects may include the depolarization of neurons expressing the anorexigenic neuropeptides, POMC and CARTPT, which increase the firing of anorexigenic neurons and the hyperpolarization of neurons expressing NPY and AGRP, which decrease the firing of orexigenic neurons. Thus, GUCY2C may represent a new target for anti-obesity pharmacotherapy." EXACT []

