xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: TP53 regulates cell metabolism by restricting glucose import and glycolysis, and activating lipid transport, fatty acid oxidation and gluconeogenesis.    Signaling description: TP53 is activated by metabolic stress (starvation exposure) through the AMPK/RPTOR/MTOR axis. MTOR/TP53 can also be activated by the amino acids glutamine and isoleucine and the fatty acid product phosphatidic acid. TP53 restricts glucose import by inhibiting the SLC2A1, SLC2A3, SLC2A4 transporters. TP53 restricts glycolysis by inhibiting enzymes PGAM1, HK2 and activating TIGAR (the last one hydrolyses fructose 2,6-bisphosphate, the activator of PFKM). TP53 activates gluconeogenesis through activation of G6PC and PCK1 via SIRT6/FOXO1. TP53 activates lipid transport and fatty acid oxidation via CPT1C, MLYCD, LPIN1 and PANK1 activation. TP53 can inhibit fatty acid synthesis through down-regulation of FASN. TP53 can also activate SCO2 and thereby regulate the respiratory chain.    Outcome effects: The result of TP53 regulation is cell-, tissue- and context-dependent. This pathway represents multiple metabolic effects of TP53 in various tissues and circumstances."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: TP53 regulates cell metabolism by restricting glucose import and glycolysis, and activating lipid transport, fatty acid oxidation and gluconeogenesis.    Signaling description: TP53 is activated by metabolic stress (starvation exposure) through the AMPK/RPTOR/MTOR axis. MTOR/TP53 can also be activated by the amino acids glutamine and isoleucine and the fatty acid product phosphatidic acid. TP53 restricts glucose import by inhibiting the SLC2A1, SLC2A3, SLC2A4 transporters. TP53 restricts glycolysis by inhibiting enzymes PGAM1, HK2 and activating TIGAR (the last one hydrolyses fructose 2,6-bisphosphate, the activator of PFKM). TP53 activates gluconeogenesis through activation of G6PC and PCK1 via SIRT6/FOXO1. TP53 activates lipid transport and fatty acid oxidation via CPT1C, MLYCD, LPIN1 and PANK1 activation. TP53 can inhibit fatty acid synthesis through down-regulation of FASN. TP53 can also activate SCO2 and thereby regulate the respiratory chain.    Outcome effects: The result of TP53 regulation is cell-, tissue- and context-dependent. This pathway represents multiple metabolic effects of TP53 in various tissues and circumstances." EXACT []

