[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-01a88387-f7d5-4d6a-b458-29319caa3eb8
name: Eosinophil Survival by Cytokine Signaling
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:eosinophils_activation ! Eosinophils activation
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
is_a: urn:agi-folder:e ! E
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
xref: PS:Description "Eosinophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Eosinophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-01a88387-f7d5-4d6a-b458-29319caa3eb8"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-01a88387-f7d5-4d6a-b458-29319caa3eb8" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "eosinophil"
synonym: "CellType: eosinophil" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Eosinophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1-6% of all white blood cells. Eosinophils circulate in the blood for several hours, however in lung, skin, and several other organs, eosinophils appear only in the case of disease such as allergy or helminth invasion and survive up to 12 days. The products of eosinophil degranulation are toxic to parasites and decrease the intensity of allergic reactions.+I128    Signaling description: The maturation of eosinophils generally occurs in bone marrow, but can also be completed in the spleen. The differentiation of eosinophils is initiated by cytokines of Th2 type lymphocytes such as IL3, IL5, and CSF2. IL3 and CSF2 result in the proliferation and differentiation of stem cells to the multipotent myeloid and eosinophil progenitor cells, while IL5 supports the expansion, terminal differentiation, and functional activation of eosinophils. IL5 results in the phosphorylation of JAK2, as well as LYN kinases, which decreases BAX translocation and ultimately reduced apoptosis and promotes eosinophil survival. CSF2 appears to have a strong role in inhibiting eosinophil apoptosis at the tissue level via STAT3-dependent BAD inhibition. It is possible that IL5 triggers the differentiation of eosinophils through activation of the transcription factor SOX4. GATA1 and IRF8 are other crucial factors in eosinophil development. The details of regulation of eosinophil differentiation are not well known. Other cytokines play important roles in eosinophil maturation. IL33 induces helper T-cells, mast cells, eosinophils, and basophils to produce type-2 cytokines through binding to the IL1RL1 receptor. IL33 markedly enhances eosinophil survival and upregulates the cell surface expression of ICAM1 on eosinophils, but suppresses ICAM3 and L-selectin. In addition, IL33 mediates the significant release of proinflammatory cytokine IL6 and the chemokines IL8 and CCL2. In addition, leptin (LEP) has been shown to be an eosinophil survival factor.    Outcome effects: LEP upregulates the cell surface expression of adhesion molecules including ICAM1 and CD18 on eosinophils. LEP can also induce the release of inflammatory cytokines IL6, IL8, and CCL2. The precise mechanisms of activation of these proteins are unknown, but may involve the activation of NF-kB and MAPK1 cascades. LEP initiates a signaling cascade through MAPK-dependent pathway in eosinophils and blocks eosinophil apoptosis. Lastly, IL15 reduces spontaneous apoptosis in human eosinophils. The effect of IL15 on spontaneous eosinophil apoptosis possibly requires NF-kB activation and autocrine production of CSF2. In addition, IL9 activates eosinophils, but the mechanism of this is not well established. In addition, IL27 may play a crucial role in allergic diseases by activating eosinophils via differential intracellular signaling cascades. The details of these cascades are poorly studied. TNF induces eosinophil survival and degranulation, which is mediated through the activation of NF-kB pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Eosinophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1-6% of all white blood cells. Eosinophils circulate in the blood for several hours, however in lung, skin, and several other organs, eosinophils appear only in the case of disease such as allergy or helminth invasion and survive up to 12 days. The products of eosinophil degranulation are toxic to parasites and decrease the intensity of allergic reactions.+I128    Signaling description: The maturation of eosinophils generally occurs in bone marrow, but can also be completed in the spleen. The differentiation of eosinophils is initiated by cytokines of Th2 type lymphocytes such as IL3, IL5, and CSF2. IL3 and CSF2 result in the proliferation and differentiation of stem cells to the multipotent myeloid and eosinophil progenitor cells, while IL5 supports the expansion, terminal differentiation, and functional activation of eosinophils. IL5 results in the phosphorylation of JAK2, as well as LYN kinases, which decreases BAX translocation and ultimately reduced apoptosis and promotes eosinophil survival. CSF2 appears to have a strong role in inhibiting eosinophil apoptosis at the tissue level via STAT3-dependent BAD inhibition. It is possible that IL5 triggers the differentiation of eosinophils through activation of the transcription factor SOX4. GATA1 and IRF8 are other crucial factors in eosinophil development. The details of regulation of eosinophil differentiation are not well known. Other cytokines play important roles in eosinophil maturation. IL33 induces helper T-cells, mast cells, eosinophils, and basophils to produce type-2 cytokines through binding to the IL1RL1 receptor. IL33 markedly enhances eosinophil survival and upregulates the cell surface expression of ICAM1 on eosinophils, but suppresses ICAM3 and L-selectin. In addition, IL33 mediates the significant release of proinflammatory cytokine IL6 and the chemokines IL8 and CCL2. In addition, leptin (LEP) has been shown to be an eosinophil survival factor.    Outcome effects: LEP upregulates the cell surface expression of adhesion molecules including ICAM1 and CD18 on eosinophils. LEP can also induce the release of inflammatory cytokines IL6, IL8, and CCL2. The precise mechanisms of activation of these proteins are unknown, but may involve the activation of NF-kB and MAPK1 cascades. LEP initiates a signaling cascade through MAPK-dependent pathway in eosinophils and blocks eosinophil apoptosis. Lastly, IL15 reduces spontaneous apoptosis in human eosinophils. The effect of IL15 on spontaneous eosinophil apoptosis possibly requires NF-kB activation and autocrine production of CSF2. In addition, IL9 activates eosinophils, but the mechanism of this is not well established. In addition, IL27 may play a crucial role in allergic diseases by activating eosinophils via differential intracellular signaling cascades. The details of these cascades are poorly studied. TNF induces eosinophil survival and degranulation, which is mediated through the activation of NF-kB pathway." EXACT []

