xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Eosinophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1-6% of all white blood cells. Eosinophils circulate in the blood for several hours, however in lung, skin, and several other organs, eosinophils appear only in the case of disease such as allergy or helminth invasion and survive up to 12 days. The products of eosinophil degranulation are toxic to parasites and decrease the intensity of allergic reactions.    Signaling description: The maturation of eosinophils generally occurs in bone marrow, but can also take place in the spleen. The translocation of eosinophils from bone marrow to their site of action results from the participation of specific chemokines including CCL11, CCL24, and CCL5. They bind to CCR3 receptor which activates migration, degranulation, and the synthesis of cytokines in eosinophils via a G-protein-dependent mechanism. Degranulation and chemotaxis involve extensive rearrangement of the actin cytoskeleton. The precise mechanism of signaling that leads to remodeling of the cytoskeleton in eosinophils is unknown. The key players in this process are small Rho family of GTPases including RHOA and their effector kinases, ROCK1 and ROCK2, which cause an increase in calcium (Ca2+) levels under the influence of CCR3 ligands. Ligand-bound CCR3 causes activation of MAPK1/MAPK3. The details of MAPK activation signaling are not well established, but it includes RAC1, CDC42, and PAK1 proteins. The activation of MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway mediates the release of arachidonic acid which contributes to the secretion of lipid mediators and leads to inflammatory responses. The additional chemoattractants and activators of eosinophils in inflammation include LTB4, platelet-activating factor, and PGD2. Other cytokines, chemokines, small molecules, and inflammatory mediators also activate eosinophils but to a much lesser extent. During inflammation, the first interaction of circulating granulocytes occurs with the inflamed vascular endothelium of blood vessels. VCAM1, MADCAM1, and CD48, which are expressed on the endothelium, mediate activation through the interaction with alpha4beta7 integrin and CD244 on eosinophils. Similar to other granulocytes, eosinophils are activated by a signal from IgE. Eosinophils express FCER2, which is a Ca2+-dependent lectin receptor with less affinity to IgE as compared to FCER1. Eosinophil expression of FCER1 is minimal and does not activate eosinophils. FCER2 activation pathways lead to degranulation, release of leukotrienes, and cytokine synthesis. An increase in Ca2+ also leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid and lysophospholipid, which support the fusion of granules with membranes and the release of their content. Eosinophils also express immunoglobulin receptors for IgG (FCGR2B and FCGR2A) and IgA (FCAR). When activated by IgG, FCGR2B receptor appears to be the natural inhibitor of eosinophil function. SIGLEC8 is another example of eosinophil function control. SIGLEC8 is selectively expressed on human eosinophils at a high level and mediates eosinophil apoptosis. SIGLEC8-induced eosinophil death is enhanced by IL5 and CSF2 cytokines. The crosslinking of SIGLEC8 results in the profound inhibition of IgE receptor-triggered histamine and PGD2 release, but interestingly not the release of other newly formed mediators such as cytokines. The exact biochemical identity of the natural tissue ligands for SIGLEC8 and its signaling in eosinophils remains unknown. The expression of complement receptors (CR1, CR3, and CD88) and toll-like receptors (TLR7/8) on eosinophils exist but are not presented on this pathway.    Outcome effects: Upon activation, eosinophils release the content of their granules at the site of inflammation. PRG2, RNASE3, EPX, and RNASE2 proteins, which are specific for eosinophils, are the enzymes directly implicated in cell damage that possess neurotoxic, helminthotoxic, and ribonucleolytic act"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Eosinophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. In healthy humans, eosinophils comprise 1-6% of all white blood cells. Eosinophils circulate in the blood for several hours, however in lung, skin, and several other organs, eosinophils appear only in the case of disease such as allergy or helminth invasion and survive up to 12 days. The products of eosinophil degranulation are toxic to parasites and decrease the intensity of allergic reactions.    Signaling description: The maturation of eosinophils generally occurs in bone marrow, but can also take place in the spleen. The translocation of eosinophils from bone marrow to their site of action results from the participation of specific chemokines including CCL11, CCL24, and CCL5. They bind to CCR3 receptor which activates migration, degranulation, and the synthesis of cytokines in eosinophils via a G-protein-dependent mechanism. Degranulation and chemotaxis involve extensive rearrangement of the actin cytoskeleton. The precise mechanism of signaling that leads to remodeling of the cytoskeleton in eosinophils is unknown. The key players in this process are small Rho family of GTPases including RHOA and their effector kinases, ROCK1 and ROCK2, which cause an increase in calcium (Ca2+) levels under the influence of CCR3 ligands. Ligand-bound CCR3 causes activation of MAPK1/MAPK3. The details of MAPK activation signaling are not well established, but it includes RAC1, CDC42, and PAK1 proteins. The activation of MAPK1/MAPK3 pathway mediates the release of arachidonic acid which contributes to the secretion of lipid mediators and leads to inflammatory responses. The additional chemoattractants and activators of eosinophils in inflammation include LTB4, platelet-activating factor, and PGD2. Other cytokines, chemokines, small molecules, and inflammatory mediators also activate eosinophils but to a much lesser extent. During inflammation, the first interaction of circulating granulocytes occurs with the inflamed vascular endothelium of blood vessels. VCAM1, MADCAM1, and CD48, which are expressed on the endothelium, mediate activation through the interaction with alpha4beta7 integrin and CD244 on eosinophils. Similar to other granulocytes, eosinophils are activated by a signal from IgE. Eosinophils express FCER2, which is a Ca2+-dependent lectin receptor with less affinity to IgE as compared to FCER1. Eosinophil expression of FCER1 is minimal and does not activate eosinophils. FCER2 activation pathways lead to degranulation, release of leukotrienes, and cytokine synthesis. An increase in Ca2+ also leads to the synthesis of arachidonic acid and lysophospholipid, which support the fusion of granules with membranes and the release of their content. Eosinophils also express immunoglobulin receptors for IgG (FCGR2B and FCGR2A) and IgA (FCAR). When activated by IgG, FCGR2B receptor appears to be the natural inhibitor of eosinophil function. SIGLEC8 is another example of eosinophil function control. SIGLEC8 is selectively expressed on human eosinophils at a high level and mediates eosinophil apoptosis. SIGLEC8-induced eosinophil death is enhanced by IL5 and CSF2 cytokines. The crosslinking of SIGLEC8 results in the profound inhibition of IgE receptor-triggered histamine and PGD2 release, but interestingly not the release of other newly formed mediators such as cytokines. The exact biochemical identity of the natural tissue ligands for SIGLEC8 and its signaling in eosinophils remains unknown. The expression of complement receptors (CR1, CR3, and CD88) and toll-like receptors (TLR7/8) on eosinophils exist but are not presented on this pathway.    Outcome effects: Upon activation, eosinophils release the content of their granules at the site of inflammation. PRG2, RNASE3, EPX, and RNASE2 proteins, which are specific for eosinophils, are the enzymes directly implicated in cell damage that possess neurotoxic, helminthotoxic, and ribonucleolytic act" EXACT []

