xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Autophagy is the process of utilization of worn-out long-lived proteins and organelles. Abnormal autophagy and protein degradation by lysosomes are related to aging and lead to the accumulation of cross-linked products and cell damage.    Signaling description: In chaperone-mediated autophagy (CMA), substrate proteins labeled with penta-peptide motif KFERQ and are directly transported to lysosomes by HSPA8. The activity of CMA was shown to decrease in old rodents and senescent human fibroblasts in culture. In macroautophagy, phagophora formed from ER surrounds and absorb part of the cytosol and mature to autophagosomes which then fuse with lysosome. Autophagy-related proteins (ATGs) and PIK3C3C with BECN1 induce formation of the autophagosome. Induction of autophagy is negatively regulated by mTOR. Microautophagy is a poorly understood process in mammals and is not shown on the pathway.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. For references see \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Aging\" pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Autophagy is the process of utilization of worn-out long-lived proteins and organelles. Abnormal autophagy and protein degradation by lysosomes are related to aging and lead to the accumulation of cross-linked products and cell damage.    Signaling description: In chaperone-mediated autophagy (CMA), substrate proteins labeled with penta-peptide motif KFERQ and are directly transported to lysosomes by HSPA8. The activity of CMA was shown to decrease in old rodents and senescent human fibroblasts in culture. In macroautophagy, phagophora formed from ER surrounds and absorb part of the cytosol and mature to autophagosomes which then fuse with lysosome. Autophagy-related proteins (ATGs) and PIK3C3C with BECN1 induce formation of the autophagosome. Induction of autophagy is negatively regulated by mTOR. Microautophagy is a poorly understood process in mammals and is not shown on the pathway.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. For references see \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Aging\" pathway." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-bfd8601e-f4a8-46e8-92e3-5b8f46c6ab20
name: Fatty Acids Cause Oxidative Stress and Mitochondria Decline in Aging
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:f ! F
is_a: urn:agi-folder:organelle_instability_associated_with_aging ! Organelle instability associated with aging
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
is_a: urn:agi-folder:mitochondria ! Mitochondria
xref: PS:Description "Long-chain nonesterified (\"free\") fatty acids (FFA) accumulate during starvation, anoxia, exhaustive exercise, and diabetes and able to modulate mitochondrial ROS generation. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Long-chain nonesterified (\"free\") fatty acids (FFA) accumulate during starvation, anoxia, exhaustive exercise, and diabetes and able to modulate mitochondrial ROS generation. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
