synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Oxidative stress is a redox imbalance with an excess of oxidants or a defect in antioxidants (Sies, 1985). Oxidative stress in terms of \"the mitochondrial oxidative stress theory of aging\" is the reaction of reactive oxygen species (ROS), physiologically generated by mitochondrial electron transport chain, with cellular constituents and it initiates the changes associated with aging and determines the maximum longevity (Reinald Pamplona, 2008). Long-chain nonesterified (\"free\") fatty acids (FFA) accumulate during starvation, anoxia, exhaustive exercise, and diabetes and are able to modulate mitochondrial ROS generation. Polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) are able to activate non-mitochondrial ROS production.    Signaling description: FFA is accumulated due the activity of cytosolic phospholipase A2 and secretory phospholipase A2 which are able to modify membrane phospholipids. Despite the well-known effect of FFA on mitochondrial ROS production (both positive and negative), the underlying mechanism is poorly understood. Some of the hypotheses are described on the pathway. FFA is able to modulate non-mitochondrial ROS production by regulating the activity of plasma membrane NAD(P)H oxidase complex in neutrophils. PUFA are more potent inducers of ROS generation.    Outcome effects: PUFA chains (two or more double bonds) are easily attacked by ROS than saturated (no double bonds) or monounsaturated (one double bond) side chains. Cellular membrane PUFAs peroxidation generates mixture of carbonyl compounds (RCS) with three to nine carbons in length. Carbonyl compounds react with proteins or other macromolecules and generate cross-linked unsoluble advanced lipoxidation end-products (ALEs). ALEs may cause structural changes on proteins, formation of protein-protein cross-links and aggregates, alteration of protein degradation and processing and others.    Animal data: The majority of experimental data were obtained in animal models."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Oxidative stress is a redox imbalance with an excess of oxidants or a defect in antioxidants (Sies, 1985). Oxidative stress in terms of \"the mitochondrial oxidative stress theory of aging\" is the reaction of reactive oxygen species (ROS), physiologically generated by mitochondrial electron transport chain, with cellular constituents and it initiates the changes associated with aging and determines the maximum longevity (Reinald Pamplona, 2008). Long-chain nonesterified (\"free\") fatty acids (FFA) accumulate during starvation, anoxia, exhaustive exercise, and diabetes and are able to modulate mitochondrial ROS generation. Polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) are able to activate non-mitochondrial ROS production.    Signaling description: FFA is accumulated due the activity of cytosolic phospholipase A2 and secretory phospholipase A2 which are able to modify membrane phospholipids. Despite the well-known effect of FFA on mitochondrial ROS production (both positive and negative), the underlying mechanism is poorly understood. Some of the hypotheses are described on the pathway. FFA is able to modulate non-mitochondrial ROS production by regulating the activity of plasma membrane NAD(P)H oxidase complex in neutrophils. PUFA are more potent inducers of ROS generation.    Outcome effects: PUFA chains (two or more double bonds) are easily attacked by ROS than saturated (no double bonds) or monounsaturated (one double bond) side chains. Cellular membrane PUFAs peroxidation generates mixture of carbonyl compounds (RCS) with three to nine carbons in length. Carbonyl compounds react with proteins or other macromolecules and generate cross-linked unsoluble advanced lipoxidation end-products (ALEs). ALEs may cause structural changes on proteins, formation of protein-protein cross-links and aggregates, alteration of protein degradation and processing and others.    Animal data: The majority of experimental data were obtained in animal models." EXACT []

