xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. During vesicle docking, the two membranes are held within a bilayer's distance of one another.    Signaling description: Vesicle docking includes molecular interactions underlying the close and tight association of a vesicle with its target and may include the molecular rearrangements needed to trigger bilayer fusion. Docking of a transport vesicle at the target membrane precedes the formation of a tight core SNARE complex. SNARE proteins (soluble NSF attachment receptor) can be divided into two categories: vesicle or v-SNAREs, which are incorporated into the membranes of transport vesicles (synaptobrevin or VAMPs), and target or t-SNAREs, which are located in the membranes of target compartments (syntaxin 1 or STX1A and SNAP25). The described mechanism of the molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Assembly of the SNAREs into the \"\"trans\"\" complexes likely bridges the opposing membranes belonging to cell and secretory granule, bringing them in proximity and inducing their fusion.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. During vesicle docking, the two membranes are held within a bilayer's distance of one another.    Signaling description: Vesicle docking includes molecular interactions underlying the close and tight association of a vesicle with its target and may include the molecular rearrangements needed to trigger bilayer fusion. Docking of a transport vesicle at the target membrane precedes the formation of a tight core SNARE complex. SNARE proteins (soluble NSF attachment receptor) can be divided into two categories: vesicle or v-SNAREs, which are incorporated into the membranes of transport vesicles (synaptobrevin or VAMPs), and target or t-SNAREs, which are located in the membranes of target compartments (syntaxin 1 or STX1A and SNAP25). The described mechanism of the molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Assembly of the SNAREs into the \"\"trans\"\" complexes likely bridges the opposing membranes belonging to cell and secretory granule, bringing them in proximity and inducing their fusion.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

