xref: PS:Source "Cell Process"
synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. Vesicle priming is defined as a Ca2+-dependent mechanism restoring secretion.    Signaling description: In neuronal exocytosis, the priming of the secretory vesicle is mediated by molecular rearrangements and modifications that take place following initial docking of a synaptic vesicle but prior to exocytosis. In other cell types, whose secretion is constitutive (calcium ion independent, non-triggered) there is no priming. The influx of calcium into the cell triggers the completion of the assembly reaction, which is mediated by an interaction between the calcium sensor, synaptotagmin (SYT1), and the partially assembled SNARE complex (SNAP25/STX1A/VAMP2). The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: The influx of calcium ions in the priming step is needed to trigger vesicle fusion and nearly instantaneous neurotransmitter release in neurons.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. Vesicle priming is defined as a Ca2+-dependent mechanism restoring secretion.    Signaling description: In neuronal exocytosis, the priming of the secretory vesicle is mediated by molecular rearrangements and modifications that take place following initial docking of a synaptic vesicle but prior to exocytosis. In other cell types, whose secretion is constitutive (calcium ion independent, non-triggered) there is no priming. The influx of calcium into the cell triggers the completion of the assembly reaction, which is mediated by an interaction between the calcium sensor, synaptotagmin (SYT1), and the partially assembled SNARE complex (SNAP25/STX1A/VAMP2). The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: The influx of calcium ions in the priming step is needed to trigger vesicle fusion and nearly instantaneous neurotransmitter release in neurons.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

