xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. Vesicle trafficking is required for the transportation of a vesicle to the cell surface area.    Signaling description: Loaded secretory vesicles are transported by motor kinesin proteins (KIFs) from trans-Golgi complex to the vicinity of plasma membrane along microtubules and actin filaments. Trafficking is regulated by specific RAB GTPases. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Kinesin proteins, RAB GTPases and cytoskeleton elements are required for the transportation of a vesicle to the cell surface area.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. Vesicle trafficking is required for the transportation of a vesicle to the cell surface area.    Signaling description: Loaded secretory vesicles are transported by motor kinesin proteins (KIFs) from trans-Golgi complex to the vicinity of plasma membrane along microtubules and actin filaments. Trafficking is regulated by specific RAB GTPases. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Kinesin proteins, RAB GTPases and cytoskeleton elements are required for the transportation of a vesicle to the cell surface area.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

