synonym: "Source: Cell Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. Vesicle tethering links the vesicles to the cell membrane at half the diameter of a vesicle.    Signaling description: The tethering of the secretory vesicle before docking and fusion with the targeted plasma membrane is mediated by the exocyst complex, an evolutionary conserved octameric protein complex which consists of EXOC1-8. Exocyst proteins on the plasma membrane (EXOC1 and EXOC7) bind vesicular exocyst proteins, bringing the vesicle very close to the plasma membrane. The exocyst is regulated by activated GTPases, such as RHOA, CDC42, RHOQ (TC10), RALA, and the scaffolding protein IQGAP1, responsible for controlling the activity of the cytoskeleton. Several tethering factors interact with specific Rab GTPases which regulate vesicle transport to the plasma membrane from recycling endosomes during cell surface remodeling and spreading. Prior to SNARE-mediated fusion, the exocyst interacts with the SNAREs and regulates SNARE assembly. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Exocyst proteins provide interaction between the vesicles and the acceptor membrane, regulate cytoskeleton reorganization and mediate SNARE assembly due to tethering of exocytosis.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. Vesicle tethering links the vesicles to the cell membrane at half the diameter of a vesicle.    Signaling description: The tethering of the secretory vesicle before docking and fusion with the targeted plasma membrane is mediated by the exocyst complex, an evolutionary conserved octameric protein complex which consists of EXOC1-8. Exocyst proteins on the plasma membrane (EXOC1 and EXOC7) bind vesicular exocyst proteins, bringing the vesicle very close to the plasma membrane. The exocyst is regulated by activated GTPases, such as RHOA, CDC42, RHOQ (TC10), RALA, and the scaffolding protein IQGAP1, responsible for controlling the activity of the cytoskeleton. Several tethering factors interact with specific Rab GTPases which regulate vesicle transport to the plasma membrane from recycling endosomes during cell surface remodeling and spreading. Prior to SNARE-mediated fusion, the exocyst interacts with the SNAREs and regulates SNARE assembly. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Exocyst proteins provide interaction between the vesicles and the acceptor membrane, regulate cytoskeleton reorganization and mediate SNARE assembly due to tethering of exocytosis.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

