xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. The process of vesicle fusion involves merging the vesicle membrane with the target membrane resulting in release of large biomolecules in the extracellular space (or in the synaptic cleft).    Signaling description: There are several models that explain the formation of the fusion pore, but the exact nature of these processes remains debated. SNARE proteins (syntaxin (STX1A), SNAP-25 and synaptobrevin (VAMP2) form an incredibly stable SNARE complex via a four alpha helical bundle that provides energy for the membranes to fuse. SNARE complex assembly brings the vesicle membrane into close opposition with the plasma membrane promoting lipid mixing leading to vesicle fusion. Complexin (CPLX1) and synaptotagmin (SYT1 ) regulate vesicle release. The post-fusion SNARE complex must be mechanically disassembled. SNARE disassembly can be induced by a single round of ATP hydrolysis supplied by two proteins, ATPase N-ethylmaleimide sensitive factor (NSF) and soluble NSF-attachment protein (NAPA). The precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: SNARE complex formation drives membrane fusion and NAPA/NSF disassemble SNAREs by inducing shearing/rotation with ATP hydrolysis.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Exocytosis is the process of secretory vesicle transport to the cell membrane. These membrane-bound vesicles contain proteins to be secreted to the extracellular environment, and membrane proteins and lipids that are sent to become components of the cell membrane. There are two types of exocytosis: Ca2+ triggered non-constitutive exocytosis and non Ca2+ triggered constitutive exocytosis. The process of exocytosis is includes five major stages: vesicle trafficking, vesicle tethering, vesicle docking, vesicle priming (in neuronal exocytosis) and vesicle fusion. The process of vesicle fusion involves merging the vesicle membrane with the target membrane resulting in release of large biomolecules in the extracellular space (or in the synaptic cleft).    Signaling description: There are several models that explain the formation of the fusion pore, but the exact nature of these processes remains debated. SNARE proteins (syntaxin (STX1A), SNAP-25 and synaptobrevin (VAMP2) form an incredibly stable SNARE complex via a four alpha helical bundle that provides energy for the membranes to fuse. SNARE complex assembly brings the vesicle membrane into close opposition with the plasma membrane promoting lipid mixing leading to vesicle fusion. Complexin (CPLX1) and synaptotagmin (SYT1 ) regulate vesicle release. The post-fusion SNARE complex must be mechanically disassembled. SNARE disassembly can be induced by a single round of ATP hydrolysis supplied by two proteins, ATPase N-ethylmaleimide sensitive factor (NSF) and soluble NSF-attachment protein (NAPA). The precise mechanism of this is poorly understood. One of hypotheses is described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: SNARE complex formation drives membrane fusion and NAPA/NSF disassemble SNAREs by inducing shearing/rotation with ATP hydrolysis.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

