xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) is a prostanoid produced from arachidonic acid in the cell membrane. Prostanoids, including PGG2, PGH2, PGI2, and TXA2, are degraded into inactive products within several minutes. Due to this instability, it is believed that their physiological activity is restricted around the region where they are produced. PGE2 mediates fever generation in response to inflammation by acting primarily through prostaglandin E2 receptor EP3 subtype (PTGER3), which is expressed abundantly in the brain.    Signaling description: The preoptic area of the hypothalamus has been shown to contain a thermocenter since the destruction of this region leads to the inability to generate fever and the preoptic area is sensitive to PGE2-induced fever. In the hypothalamus, PTGER3 mRNA is expressed in neurons in the medial and median preoptic nuclei, especially in neurons surrounding the organum vasculosum lamina terminalis (OVLT). Circulating pyrogens, lipopolysaccharide (LPS), or inflammatory cytokines have easy access to the OVLT since it lacks a blood-brain barrier. Pyrogens induce PTGS2 expression in the OVLT region, which may generate PGE2. It is possible that TNFSF11/TNFRSF11A signaling in astrocytes of the septal/preoptic area region in the hypothalamus mediates the induction of PTGS2/PGE2 generation, most likely through NF-kB signaling activation. PGE2 acts as a final mediator in the induction of fever. PGE2 inhibits GABAergic activity of PTGER3-expressing neurons, which allows the excitation of raphe pallidus neurons in the brain stem and leads to stimulation of thermogenic sympathetic nerves. In addition, GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the mammalian central nervous system. GABA binds to ligand-activated chloride (Cl-) channel transmembrane receptors (GABA-A receptors) in both pre- and post-synaptic neurons. This binding results in a negative change in the transmembrane potential, which usually causes hyperpolarization. Raphe pallidus neurons activate sympathetic nerves responsible for increasing thermoregulatory muscle tone, muscle tremors, and the reduction of heat transfer rate due to vasoconstriction on the body surface. The precise molecular signaling remains unknown following PTGER3 activation via PGE2 in preoptic area neurons which leads to fever generation.    Outcome effects: PTGER3 primarily signals through GNAI1, which leads to decreased cAMP levels. PGE2/PTGER3 signaling rapidly down-regulates the protein expression of GABA-A receptor alpha-2 subunit (GABRA2) in preoptic area neurons. PTGER3-expressing neurons are most likely GABAergic themselves. When cytokine signals stimulate PGE2 production within the preoptic area, PGE2 inhibits GABAergic activity of PTGER3-expressing neurons."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) is a prostanoid produced from arachidonic acid in the cell membrane. Prostanoids, including PGG2, PGH2, PGI2, and TXA2, are degraded into inactive products within several minutes. Due to this instability, it is believed that their physiological activity is restricted around the region where they are produced. PGE2 mediates fever generation in response to inflammation by acting primarily through prostaglandin E2 receptor EP3 subtype (PTGER3), which is expressed abundantly in the brain.    Signaling description: The preoptic area of the hypothalamus has been shown to contain a thermocenter since the destruction of this region leads to the inability to generate fever and the preoptic area is sensitive to PGE2-induced fever. In the hypothalamus, PTGER3 mRNA is expressed in neurons in the medial and median preoptic nuclei, especially in neurons surrounding the organum vasculosum lamina terminalis (OVLT). Circulating pyrogens, lipopolysaccharide (LPS), or inflammatory cytokines have easy access to the OVLT since it lacks a blood-brain barrier. Pyrogens induce PTGS2 expression in the OVLT region, which may generate PGE2. It is possible that TNFSF11/TNFRSF11A signaling in astrocytes of the septal/preoptic area region in the hypothalamus mediates the induction of PTGS2/PGE2 generation, most likely through NF-kB signaling activation. PGE2 acts as a final mediator in the induction of fever. PGE2 inhibits GABAergic activity of PTGER3-expressing neurons, which allows the excitation of raphe pallidus neurons in the brain stem and leads to stimulation of thermogenic sympathetic nerves. In addition, GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the mammalian central nervous system. GABA binds to ligand-activated chloride (Cl-) channel transmembrane receptors (GABA-A receptors) in both pre- and post-synaptic neurons. This binding results in a negative change in the transmembrane potential, which usually causes hyperpolarization. Raphe pallidus neurons activate sympathetic nerves responsible for increasing thermoregulatory muscle tone, muscle tremors, and the reduction of heat transfer rate due to vasoconstriction on the body surface. The precise molecular signaling remains unknown following PTGER3 activation via PGE2 in preoptic area neurons which leads to fever generation.    Outcome effects: PTGER3 primarily signals through GNAI1, which leads to decreased cAMP levels. PGE2/PTGER3 signaling rapidly down-regulates the protein expression of GABA-A receptor alpha-2 subunit (GABRA2) in preoptic area neurons. PTGER3-expressing neurons are most likely GABAergic themselves. When cytokine signals stimulate PGE2 production within the preoptic area, PGE2 inhibits GABAergic activity of PTGER3-expressing neurons." EXACT []

