synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 are potent inflammatory mediators that act through G-protein-coupled receptors, CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2. Leukotrienes have been shown to induce vascular leakage and modulate endothelial function in inflammatory-related disorders.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction includes enhanced endothelial cell proliferation, disruption of the endothelial cell barrier in blood vessels, and increased vascular permeability and attachment of immune cells. CYSLTRs are critical mediators of vascular leakage; however the precise molecular mechanisms are unknown. CYSLTR1 appears to be the preferred receptor for Ca+ influx in epithelial and inflammatory cells. CYSLTR2 is predominantly expressed in human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVECs) and has been shown to be responsible for Ca2+-dependent contraction and increased endothelial permeability. CYSLTR-modulated vascular permeability seems to be mediated through trans-endothelial vesicle transport regulated by Ca2+ signaling. CYSLTR2 activation results in endothelial cell contraction and barrier disruption through ROCK1 pathway and potentiating TNF-induced attachment of leukocytes to the endothelial monolayer through the upregulation of VCAM1. The pathway displays a hypothetical scheme of forcing endothelial permeability under the action of leukotrienes.    Outcome effects: Occludins (OCLN), connexins (GJA1), and cadherins (CDH1) are required for the formation of junctional stability and cell-cell contacts through their connections with the actin cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix. Proteins of the endothelial cell junction are dysregulated through RHOA-related signaling. In addition, HUVECs predominantly express CYSLTR2 and stimulation with LTD4 induces early inflammatory gene expression including EGR1, NR4A1, IL8, RCAN1, SELE, and ADAMTS1. CYSLTR1 activation in endothelial cells mediates their proliferation possibly through the activation of MAPK pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 are potent inflammatory mediators that act through G-protein-coupled receptors, CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2. Leukotrienes have been shown to induce vascular leakage and modulate endothelial function in inflammatory-related disorders.    Signaling description: Endothelial dysfunction includes enhanced endothelial cell proliferation, disruption of the endothelial cell barrier in blood vessels, and increased vascular permeability and attachment of immune cells. CYSLTRs are critical mediators of vascular leakage; however the precise molecular mechanisms are unknown. CYSLTR1 appears to be the preferred receptor for Ca+ influx in epithelial and inflammatory cells. CYSLTR2 is predominantly expressed in human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVECs) and has been shown to be responsible for Ca2+-dependent contraction and increased endothelial permeability. CYSLTR-modulated vascular permeability seems to be mediated through trans-endothelial vesicle transport regulated by Ca2+ signaling. CYSLTR2 activation results in endothelial cell contraction and barrier disruption through ROCK1 pathway and potentiating TNF-induced attachment of leukocytes to the endothelial monolayer through the upregulation of VCAM1. The pathway displays a hypothetical scheme of forcing endothelial permeability under the action of leukotrienes.    Outcome effects: Occludins (OCLN), connexins (GJA1), and cadherins (CDH1) are required for the formation of junctional stability and cell-cell contacts through their connections with the actin cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix. Proteins of the endothelial cell junction are dysregulated through RHOA-related signaling. In addition, HUVECs predominantly express CYSLTR2 and stimulation with LTD4 induces early inflammatory gene expression including EGR1, NR4A1, IL8, RCAN1, SELE, and ADAMTS1. CYSLTR1 activation in endothelial cells mediates their proliferation possibly through the activation of MAPK pathway." EXACT []

