synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Leukotrienes are primarily known for their role in the chemotaxis of myeloid cells.    Signaling description: LTB4 is a powerful chemoattractant for neutrophils. Leukotrienes LTD4 and LTE4, that signal via CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2, can increase proliferation of mast cells through the activation of GNAI1/PRKCE/RAF1/MAPK3/MAPK1, which are downstream signals important for cell-cycle progression. In addition, CYSLTRs are direct chemoattractants for eosinophils and have been shown to enhance eosinophil migration in vivo. PGD2 is a potent stimulator of eosinophil chemotaxis. PGE2 induces both pro- and anti-inflammatory effects. PGE2 acts via PTGER3, PTGER4, PTGER2, and PTGER1 receptors.    Outcome effects: PTGER3 and PTGER4 receptors are widely distributed throughout the body and signal through GNAS, which leads to increased cAMP levels and inhibition of myeloid cell function. Therefore, PGE2 decreases T-cell proliferation and lymphocyte migration via PTGER3 and PTGER4 signaling."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Leukotrienes are primarily known for their role in the chemotaxis of myeloid cells.    Signaling description: LTB4 is a powerful chemoattractant for neutrophils. Leukotrienes LTD4 and LTE4, that signal via CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2, can increase proliferation of mast cells through the activation of GNAI1/PRKCE/RAF1/MAPK3/MAPK1, which are downstream signals important for cell-cycle progression. In addition, CYSLTRs are direct chemoattractants for eosinophils and have been shown to enhance eosinophil migration in vivo. PGD2 is a potent stimulator of eosinophil chemotaxis. PGE2 induces both pro- and anti-inflammatory effects. PGE2 acts via PTGER3, PTGER4, PTGER2, and PTGER1 receptors.    Outcome effects: PTGER3 and PTGER4 receptors are widely distributed throughout the body and signal through GNAS, which leads to increased cAMP levels and inhibition of myeloid cell function. Therefore, PGE2 decreases T-cell proliferation and lymphocyte migration via PTGER3 and PTGER4 signaling." EXACT []

