xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Leukotrienes LTC4 and LTD4 are powerful mediators of asthma, allergies, and airway hypersensitivity. They induce bronchoconstriction, increase microvascular permeability, and enhance mucus secretion.    Signaling description: LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 are endothelial and airway smooth muscle constrictors, which act via CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2 receptors. LTD4 is the preferred ligand for CYSLTR1, while LTC4 and LTE4 bind the receptor with progressively lower affinities. The rank order of affinity for binding CYSLTR2 is LTC4 = LTD4 > LTE4. A portion of CYSLT receptors are constitutively present as heterodimer pairs, thus the interplay of signaling between the two subtypes may regulate immune responses. Despite the clear evidence that leukotrienes are critical mediators of vascular constriction, the exact molecular mechanism by which CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2 mediate these effects are unknown. CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2 are G-protein receptors that induce an intracellular Ca2+ signal. CYSLTR1 receptor-induced intracellular mobilization of Ca2+ has been shown to be mediated by a pertussis toxin (PTX)-insensitive G-protein, while the influx is mediated by a PTX-sensitive G-protein (GNAI1 protein family). It is recognized that an increase in procontractile stimuli in the asthmatic airway promotes greater activation of GPCRs (Gq- and Gi-coupled receptors) mediating contraction. CYSLTR1 appears to be the preferred receptor for Ca2+ influx in epithelial and inflammatory cells. Intracellular Ca2+ plays a major role in airway smooth muscle contraction and relaxation. Phospholipase C beta (PLCB1) is the principal effector of Gq-mediated signaling. Activated PLCB1 hydrolyzes PIP2 into diacylglycerol and IP3. Increased IP3 and diacylglycerol levels lead to increased intracellular Ca2+ through its release from internal stores and influx from membrane bound channels.    Outcome effects: The rise in intracellular Ca2+ promotes the formation of Ca2+-calmodulin complexes that activate myosin light chain kinase (MYLK). MYLK phosphorylates myosin light chains and enables actin to activate the myosin ATPase activity required for cross-bridge cycling and contraction. Additional effects shown to involve Gq activation by G-protein receptors in airway smooth muscle cells include Rho-dependent mechanism of contraction. RHOA activates ROCK1, which in turn phosphorylates the myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP), resulting in increased phosphorylation of myosin light chain. GNAI1 family expressed in airway smooth muscle cells appears capable of activating RHOA through the activation of GEF. Furthermore, leukotrienes in airway smooth muscle cells activate the cell contractile machinery through both Ca2+ and protein kinase C (PKC)-dependent mechanisms. Diacylglycerol promotes the translocation of PKC from the cytoplasm to the membrane and its subsequent activation. Activated PKC is capable of phosphorylating a number of substrates including calponin (CNN1). PKC also phosphorylates intermediates of MAPK signaling pathways, which activate various gene transcription factors involved in promoting airway smooth muscle cell growth. MAPK1/MAPK3 phosphorylate and stimulate the catalytic activity of phospholipase A2. The binding of Ca2+ to phospholipase A2 triggers its association with the plasma or nuclear membrane and the subsequent cleaving and release of arachidonic acid, a precursor in the production of eicosanoids and leukotrienes."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Leukotrienes LTC4 and LTD4 are powerful mediators of asthma, allergies, and airway hypersensitivity. They induce bronchoconstriction, increase microvascular permeability, and enhance mucus secretion.    Signaling description: LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 are endothelial and airway smooth muscle constrictors, which act via CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2 receptors. LTD4 is the preferred ligand for CYSLTR1, while LTC4 and LTE4 bind the receptor with progressively lower affinities. The rank order of affinity for binding CYSLTR2 is LTC4 = LTD4 > LTE4. A portion of CYSLT receptors are constitutively present as heterodimer pairs, thus the interplay of signaling between the two subtypes may regulate immune responses. Despite the clear evidence that leukotrienes are critical mediators of vascular constriction, the exact molecular mechanism by which CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2 mediate these effects are unknown. CYSLTR1 and CYSLTR2 are G-protein receptors that induce an intracellular Ca2+ signal. CYSLTR1 receptor-induced intracellular mobilization of Ca2+ has been shown to be mediated by a pertussis toxin (PTX)-insensitive G-protein, while the influx is mediated by a PTX-sensitive G-protein (GNAI1 protein family). It is recognized that an increase in procontractile stimuli in the asthmatic airway promotes greater activation of GPCRs (Gq- and Gi-coupled receptors) mediating contraction. CYSLTR1 appears to be the preferred receptor for Ca2+ influx in epithelial and inflammatory cells. Intracellular Ca2+ plays a major role in airway smooth muscle contraction and relaxation. Phospholipase C beta (PLCB1) is the principal effector of Gq-mediated signaling. Activated PLCB1 hydrolyzes PIP2 into diacylglycerol and IP3. Increased IP3 and diacylglycerol levels lead to increased intracellular Ca2+ through its release from internal stores and influx from membrane bound channels.    Outcome effects: The rise in intracellular Ca2+ promotes the formation of Ca2+-calmodulin complexes that activate myosin light chain kinase (MYLK). MYLK phosphorylates myosin light chains and enables actin to activate the myosin ATPase activity required for cross-bridge cycling and contraction. Additional effects shown to involve Gq activation by G-protein receptors in airway smooth muscle cells include Rho-dependent mechanism of contraction. RHOA activates ROCK1, which in turn phosphorylates the myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP), resulting in increased phosphorylation of myosin light chain. GNAI1 family expressed in airway smooth muscle cells appears capable of activating RHOA through the activation of GEF. Furthermore, leukotrienes in airway smooth muscle cells activate the cell contractile machinery through both Ca2+ and protein kinase C (PKC)-dependent mechanisms. Diacylglycerol promotes the translocation of PKC from the cytoplasm to the membrane and its subsequent activation. Activated PKC is capable of phosphorylating a number of substrates including calponin (CNN1). PKC also phosphorylates intermediates of MAPK signaling pathways, which activate various gene transcription factors involved in promoting airway smooth muscle cell growth. MAPK1/MAPK3 phosphorylate and stimulate the catalytic activity of phospholipase A2. The binding of Ca2+ to phospholipase A2 triggers its association with the plasma or nuclear membrane and the subsequent cleaving and release of arachidonic acid, a precursor in the production of eicosanoids and leukotrienes." EXACT []

