xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Histamines are nitrogenous neurotransmitters produced during an allergic response. Histamine-mediated responses are facilitated by the stimulus-dependent release of stored histamine or accompanied by the induction of histamine synthesis. In the central nervous system (CNS), histamine is synthesized in histaminergic neurons.    Signaling description: It has been shown that histamine increases the permeability of the blood-brain barrier. Histamine synthesis can be induced upon various combinations of stimuli; however it is largely unknown how tissue-specific expression of histidine decarboxylase (HDC) is regulated. Histamine receptor H3 (HRH3) is expressed in neurons of the CNS and in presynaptic histaminergic neurons of the peripheral nervous system. In histaminergic neurons, HRH3 acts as a presynaptic autoreceptor inhibiting the release and synthesis of histamine. In non-histaminergic neurons, HRH3 functions as a heteroreceptor and inhibits the release of several important neurotransmitters including serotonin, noradrenaline, acetylcholine, and dopamine. HRH3 acts via Gi/o protein signaling and causes the inhibition of adenylate cyclase (ADCY) and synthesis of histamine. In addition, AKT1/GSK3B kinases have been shown to be activated by HRH3 in neurons to occur through PI3K activation via Gb/g.    Outcome effects: In the CNS, AKT1 and GSK3B play a prominent role in brain function and have been implicated in the protection against neuronal apoptosis. The upregulation of AKT1/GSK3B pathway may be the mechanism by which HRH3 exerts its neuroprotective role; however the exact mechanism of this effect remains unknown."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Histamines are nitrogenous neurotransmitters produced during an allergic response. Histamine-mediated responses are facilitated by the stimulus-dependent release of stored histamine or accompanied by the induction of histamine synthesis. In the central nervous system (CNS), histamine is synthesized in histaminergic neurons.    Signaling description: It has been shown that histamine increases the permeability of the blood-brain barrier. Histamine synthesis can be induced upon various combinations of stimuli; however it is largely unknown how tissue-specific expression of histidine decarboxylase (HDC) is regulated. Histamine receptor H3 (HRH3) is expressed in neurons of the CNS and in presynaptic histaminergic neurons of the peripheral nervous system. In histaminergic neurons, HRH3 acts as a presynaptic autoreceptor inhibiting the release and synthesis of histamine. In non-histaminergic neurons, HRH3 functions as a heteroreceptor and inhibits the release of several important neurotransmitters including serotonin, noradrenaline, acetylcholine, and dopamine. HRH3 acts via Gi/o protein signaling and causes the inhibition of adenylate cyclase (ADCY) and synthesis of histamine. In addition, AKT1/GSK3B kinases have been shown to be activated by HRH3 in neurons to occur through PI3K activation via Gb/g.    Outcome effects: In the CNS, AKT1 and GSK3B play a prominent role in brain function and have been implicated in the protection against neuronal apoptosis. The upregulation of AKT1/GSK3B pathway may be the mechanism by which HRH3 exerts its neuroprotective role; however the exact mechanism of this effect remains unknown." EXACT []

