xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Eicosanoids (or icosanoids) are a family of lipophilic small molecules derived from the twenty carbon fatty acid-arachidonic acid (omega-6 fatty acid) and possess mostly proinflammatory properties. These molecules include leukotrienes (LTA4, LTE4, LTD4, LTC4, and LTB4), prostaglandins (PGD2, PGF2, and PGE2), prostacyclins (PGI2), and thromboxanes (TXA2). In addition, eicosanoids also include derivatives of eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA, omega-3 fatty acid), PGG3, PGH2, PGI2, TXA2, and LTB4, and the derivatives of dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA, omega-6 fatty acid), PGE1, and TXA1.    Signaling description: The synthesis of these molecules proceeds simultaneously. Eicosapentaenoic acid-derived eicosanoids participate in similar reactions during inflammation as omega-6 fatty acid-derived ones, but with lower efficiency. This is most likely due to omega-3 fatty acid derivatives competing with omega-6 fatty acid derivatives for receptors; they may be natural inhibitors of inflammation in an organism. Low dietary intake of omega-3 fatty acids has been linked to several inflammation-related diseases. Most eicosanoids act locally on neighboring cells instead of on distant parts of the body. They are not stored in cells so the regulation of their synthesis is the main way to control their activity. Membrane-bound phospholipase A2 in response to extracellular stimuli releases arachidonic acid phospholipids. PTGS2 synthase converts arachidonic acid to PGG2. PTGS2 increases prostaglandin levels in inflammation. ALOX5 acts with the membrane-bound protein ALOX5AP to produce LTA4 which is hydrolyzed to produce LTB4 or LTC4 and LTD4. The most frequently used non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDS) including aspirin, acetaminophen, and ibuprofen inhibit PTGS2. Leukotrienes, produced by mast cells and leukocytes, are primarily known for their role in chemotaxis and vascular permeability activation. LTB4, in response to inflammatory mediators, is able to induce the chemotaxis of leukocytes to the injured endothelium. LTB4 stimulates production by leukocytes IL1/IL2 and IFNG. LTB4 is a powerful chemoattractant for neutrophils where it is able to induce the formation of reactive oxygen species. Leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 induce endothelial vascular permeability. In airway smooth muscle cells, they cause constriction that signal via the CYSLT1 receptor. In alveoli, this function leads to bronchospasm.    Outcome effects: Local inflammatory symtoms produced by prostaglandins include vasoconstriction or vasodilation, coagulation (platelet aggregation), pain, and fever. PGD2 is produced by mast cells and decreases platelet and leukocyte aggregation, T-cell proliferation, and lymphocyte migration. PGD2 induces vasodilation but causes bronchoconstriction. PGD2 is commonly known as a sleep promoter, reduces fever, and acts in an opposite manner as PGE2. PGE2 and PGF2alpha are produced in kidney, spleen, heart, and brain. PGE2 is one of the main prostaglandins involved in inflammation. PGE2 sensitizes pain neurons and causes fever or the heat of localized inflammation. PGE2 increases vasodilation and platelet aggregation induction of uterine contractions. Conversely, it plays an anti-inflammatory role via inhibition of granulocyte functions and decreases T-cell proliferation. PGF2alpha increases vasoconstriction, bronchoconstriction, and smooth muscle contraction. Prostacyclin PGI2, produced in heart and vascular endothelial cells, inhibits coagulation (platelet aggregation) and provokes vasodilation. Thromboxane (TXA2) is produced by platelets and acts as a fast vasoconstrictor and stimulator of coagulation (platelet aggregation). It also causes constriction of blood vessels and bronchioles. All small eicosanoids act through G-protein related signaling pathways. The specificity of eicosanoid action is reached due to cell-specific expression of their receptors and due to the interaction with other active signaling pathways."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Eicosanoids (or icosanoids) are a family of lipophilic small molecules derived from the twenty carbon fatty acid-arachidonic acid (omega-6 fatty acid) and possess mostly proinflammatory properties. These molecules include leukotrienes (LTA4, LTE4, LTD4, LTC4, and LTB4), prostaglandins (PGD2, PGF2, and PGE2), prostacyclins (PGI2), and thromboxanes (TXA2). In addition, eicosanoids also include derivatives of eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA, omega-3 fatty acid), PGG3, PGH2, PGI2, TXA2, and LTB4, and the derivatives of dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA, omega-6 fatty acid), PGE1, and TXA1.    Signaling description: The synthesis of these molecules proceeds simultaneously. Eicosapentaenoic acid-derived eicosanoids participate in similar reactions during inflammation as omega-6 fatty acid-derived ones, but with lower efficiency. This is most likely due to omega-3 fatty acid derivatives competing with omega-6 fatty acid derivatives for receptors; they may be natural inhibitors of inflammation in an organism. Low dietary intake of omega-3 fatty acids has been linked to several inflammation-related diseases. Most eicosanoids act locally on neighboring cells instead of on distant parts of the body. They are not stored in cells so the regulation of their synthesis is the main way to control their activity. Membrane-bound phospholipase A2 in response to extracellular stimuli releases arachidonic acid phospholipids. PTGS2 synthase converts arachidonic acid to PGG2. PTGS2 increases prostaglandin levels in inflammation. ALOX5 acts with the membrane-bound protein ALOX5AP to produce LTA4 which is hydrolyzed to produce LTB4 or LTC4 and LTD4. The most frequently used non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDS) including aspirin, acetaminophen, and ibuprofen inhibit PTGS2. Leukotrienes, produced by mast cells and leukocytes, are primarily known for their role in chemotaxis and vascular permeability activation. LTB4, in response to inflammatory mediators, is able to induce the chemotaxis of leukocytes to the injured endothelium. LTB4 stimulates production by leukocytes IL1/IL2 and IFNG. LTB4 is a powerful chemoattractant for neutrophils where it is able to induce the formation of reactive oxygen species. Leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4 induce endothelial vascular permeability. In airway smooth muscle cells, they cause constriction that signal via the CYSLT1 receptor. In alveoli, this function leads to bronchospasm.    Outcome effects: Local inflammatory symtoms produced by prostaglandins include vasoconstriction or vasodilation, coagulation (platelet aggregation), pain, and fever. PGD2 is produced by mast cells and decreases platelet and leukocyte aggregation, T-cell proliferation, and lymphocyte migration. PGD2 induces vasodilation but causes bronchoconstriction. PGD2 is commonly known as a sleep promoter, reduces fever, and acts in an opposite manner as PGE2. PGE2 and PGF2alpha are produced in kidney, spleen, heart, and brain. PGE2 is one of the main prostaglandins involved in inflammation. PGE2 sensitizes pain neurons and causes fever or the heat of localized inflammation. PGE2 increases vasodilation and platelet aggregation induction of uterine contractions. Conversely, it plays an anti-inflammatory role via inhibition of granulocyte functions and decreases T-cell proliferation. PGF2alpha increases vasoconstriction, bronchoconstriction, and smooth muscle contraction. Prostacyclin PGI2, produced in heart and vascular endothelial cells, inhibits coagulation (platelet aggregation) and provokes vasodilation. Thromboxane (TXA2) is produced by platelets and acts as a fast vasoconstrictor and stimulator of coagulation (platelet aggregation). It also causes constriction of blood vessels and bronchioles. All small eicosanoids act through G-protein related signaling pathways. The specificity of eicosanoid action is reached due to cell-specific expression of their receptors and due to the interaction with other active signaling pathways." EXACT []

