xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Gastric acid secretion represents an important non-immunological first line of defense against ingested microbes. Histamine is also produced by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach, where it controls hydrochloric acid synthesis in neighboring parietal cells through histamine receptor H2 (HRH2) receptor signaling.    Signaling description: Histamine synthesis may be induced by various combinations of stimuli; however it is largely unknown how the tissue-specific expression of histidine decarboxylase (HDC) is regulated. HRH2 activates H /K -exchanging ATPase in the luminal membrane of parietal cells which drives H+ ions into the glandular lumen in exchange for K+. For every H+ ion which is secreted, one HCO3- ion leaves the blood side of the cell and is exchanged for a Cl- ion via an anion exchanger. The HCO3- ions are obtained from CO2 OH-, a reaction catalyzed by CA1. This results in the intracellular accumulation of Cl- ions, which diffuse out of the cell into the lumen via Cl- channels.    Outcome effects: Thus, one Cl- ion reaches the lumen for each H+ ion secreted. PGE2, via PTGER1, induces hydrochloric acid secretions in the stomach and inhibits it via PTGER3/PTGER4 receptors in the duodenum. PGI2 decreases hydrochloric acid secretions indirectly."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Gastric acid secretion represents an important non-immunological first line of defense against ingested microbes. Histamine is also produced by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach, where it controls hydrochloric acid synthesis in neighboring parietal cells through histamine receptor H2 (HRH2) receptor signaling.    Signaling description: Histamine synthesis may be induced by various combinations of stimuli; however it is largely unknown how the tissue-specific expression of histidine decarboxylase (HDC) is regulated. HRH2 activates H /K -exchanging ATPase in the luminal membrane of parietal cells which drives H+ ions into the glandular lumen in exchange for K+. For every H+ ion which is secreted, one HCO3- ion leaves the blood side of the cell and is exchanged for a Cl- ion via an anion exchanger. The HCO3- ions are obtained from CO2 OH-, a reaction catalyzed by CA1. This results in the intracellular accumulation of Cl- ions, which diffuse out of the cell into the lumen via Cl- channels.    Outcome effects: Thus, one Cl- ion reaches the lumen for each H+ ion secreted. PGE2, via PTGER1, induces hydrochloric acid secretions in the stomach and inhibits it via PTGER3/PTGER4 receptors in the duodenum. PGI2 decreases hydrochloric acid secretions indirectly." EXACT []

