xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Histamine is a small molecule, which is produced primarily by mast cells and basophils. Antibodies, mechanical stress, and inflammatory mediators stimulate the release of histamine from secretory granules.    Signaling description: Histamine is produced and stored in granules of mast cells and basophils and released upon stimulation. IL3 or glucocorticoid can augment histamine synthesis in mast cells. Histamine is synthesized by the pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)-containing L-histidine decarboxylase (HDC) from the amino acid histidine. Once formed, histamine is rapidly inactivated by ABP1 or HNMT. MAOB processes the immediate metabolites of histamine for excretion or recycling. The main effects of histamine are associated with allergic and inflammatory reactions and include relaxation of smooth muscles, vasodilation of vessels, and increased blood flow. These effects are caused by the activation of histamine receptors such as HRH1, HRH2, HRH3, and HRH4. HRH1 couples to GNA11, which stimulates phospholipase C (PLC), whereas HRH2 interacts with Gs to activate adenylate cyclase (ADCY). HRH3 and HRH4 couple to Gi proteins to inhibit ADCY and stimulate MAPK in the case of HRH3. HRH1 is widely expressed in different cells, but most specifically in smooth muscles, vascular endothelial cells, heart, and neurons.    Outcome effects: The activation of HRH1, through PLCB1-associated increase in intracellular Ca2+, leads to both smooth muscle contraction and relaxation through nitric oxide (NO) synthesis. The effect of vasodilation prevails in the case of an inflammatory response when NO comes from other cells. In addition, HRH2 effects also lead to vasodilation and relaxation of vascular smooth muscle cells. HRH2 activation leads to PKA-related phosphorylation of MYLK, which decreases its activity and results in contraction inhibition. Histamine is also produced by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach, where it controls hydrochloric acid synthesis through HRH2 receptor signaling. In addition, HRH3 is expressed in neurons of the central nervous system and in presynaptic histaminergic neurons in the peripheral nervous system. HRH3 activation prohibits the release of neurotransmitters into the synapse. HRH3 signaling also regulates the sleep-wake cycle. HRH4 is highly expressed in bone marrow cells, intestine, liver, lung, spleen, trachea, thymus, and scrotum. During inflammatory reactions, HRH4 activation leads to neutrophil attraction from the bone marrow. All histamine receptors act through G proteins to activate cellular signaling. HRH1, HRH4, and HRH3 can inhibit intracellular cascades via ADCY inhibition and activate them via PI3K cascade. The release of phosphatidylinositol-triphosphate (PIP3) activates many cellular responses, the principal of which during inflammation are cytokine synthesis and cell growth stimulation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Histamine is a small molecule, which is produced primarily by mast cells and basophils. Antibodies, mechanical stress, and inflammatory mediators stimulate the release of histamine from secretory granules.    Signaling description: Histamine is produced and stored in granules of mast cells and basophils and released upon stimulation. IL3 or glucocorticoid can augment histamine synthesis in mast cells. Histamine is synthesized by the pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)-containing L-histidine decarboxylase (HDC) from the amino acid histidine. Once formed, histamine is rapidly inactivated by ABP1 or HNMT. MAOB processes the immediate metabolites of histamine for excretion or recycling. The main effects of histamine are associated with allergic and inflammatory reactions and include relaxation of smooth muscles, vasodilation of vessels, and increased blood flow. These effects are caused by the activation of histamine receptors such as HRH1, HRH2, HRH3, and HRH4. HRH1 couples to GNA11, which stimulates phospholipase C (PLC), whereas HRH2 interacts with Gs to activate adenylate cyclase (ADCY). HRH3 and HRH4 couple to Gi proteins to inhibit ADCY and stimulate MAPK in the case of HRH3. HRH1 is widely expressed in different cells, but most specifically in smooth muscles, vascular endothelial cells, heart, and neurons.    Outcome effects: The activation of HRH1, through PLCB1-associated increase in intracellular Ca2+, leads to both smooth muscle contraction and relaxation through nitric oxide (NO) synthesis. The effect of vasodilation prevails in the case of an inflammatory response when NO comes from other cells. In addition, HRH2 effects also lead to vasodilation and relaxation of vascular smooth muscle cells. HRH2 activation leads to PKA-related phosphorylation of MYLK, which decreases its activity and results in contraction inhibition. Histamine is also produced by enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells of the stomach, where it controls hydrochloric acid synthesis through HRH2 receptor signaling. In addition, HRH3 is expressed in neurons of the central nervous system and in presynaptic histaminergic neurons in the peripheral nervous system. HRH3 activation prohibits the release of neurotransmitters into the synapse. HRH3 signaling also regulates the sleep-wake cycle. HRH4 is highly expressed in bone marrow cells, intestine, liver, lung, spleen, trachea, thymus, and scrotum. During inflammatory reactions, HRH4 activation leads to neutrophil attraction from the bone marrow. All histamine receptors act through G proteins to activate cellular signaling. HRH1, HRH4, and HRH3 can inhibit intracellular cascades via ADCY inhibition and activate them via PI3K cascade. The release of phosphatidylinositol-triphosphate (PIP3) activates many cellular responses, the principal of which during inflammation are cytokine synthesis and cell growth stimulation." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-9d1fd5a3-ae1c-4a2e-9933-6996846dba17
name: Leukotriene LTB4 Effect on Neutrophil Activation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:systemic_effects_in_inflammation ! Systemic effects in inflammation
