[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-2c4ece37-e11d-4d60-94b0-1030188d0d39
name: Toll-like Receptors in Sterile Inflammation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:inflammation_initiation ! Inflammation initiation
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
xref: PS:Description "Pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors (TLRs) and NOD-like receptors can recognize non-infectious endogenous molecules that are released during tissue damage and cellular injury. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors (TLRs) and NOD-like receptors can recognize non-infectious endogenous molecules that are released during tissue damage and cellular injury. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-2c4ece37-e11d-4d60-94b0-1030188d0d39"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-2c4ece37-e11d-4d60-94b0-1030188d0d39" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "generic"
synonym: "Organ_System: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "generic"
synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors (TLRs) and NOD-like receptors can recognize non-infectious endogenous molecules that are released during tissue damage and cellular injury. These molecules are known as damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). The most common DAMPs are HMGB1, heat shock proteins, SAP130, fragments of RNA and DNA, hyaluronic acid, VCAN, heparan sulfate, monosodium urate, IL33, and IL1A.    Signaling description: DAMPs act similarly to pathogen pattern molecules that bind to TLRs and activate an immune response and trigger inflammation. TLRs are expressed on the surface of various cells of the immune system and can initiate inflammation after association with DAMPs originating from necrotic cells. TLRs are primarily expressed on the surface of monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells.    Outcome effects: Downstream pathways that follow DAMP/TLR binding are similar to other TLR-activated pathways. These signaling pathways include the activation of MYD88, TIRAP, and TICAM which finally lead to the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines, interferon, and chemokines via the activation of NF-kB, IRFs, and Jun/Fos transcription factors. CD14, CD36, CD40, and CD91 are important co-activators of TLRs. CD14 and CD91 bind to heat shock proteins (HSPs) and promote the expression of IL1B, IL6, TNF, IL12, and NO production through TLR-mediated signaling. These molecules play different roles in the inflammation process. DAMP-specific receptor CD24, which can bind to both HMGB1 and HSPs, negatively regulates sterile inflammatory responses via the inhibition of TLR responses. Intracellular TLRs such as TLR3 and TLR9 can bind to fragments of DNA and RNA originating from dead cells and promote interferon synthesis. In certain cases, AGER may transduce signals by acting cooperatively with TLRs. HMGB1-bound DNA can form a complex with TLR9 and AGER to induce proinflammatory cytokines. Finally, similarly to microbially-induced inflammation, sterile inflammation mediated by TLRs is marked by the recruitment of neutrophils and macrophages and the production of proinflammatory cytokines and chemokines, particularly TNF and IL1."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Pattern recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors (TLRs) and NOD-like receptors can recognize non-infectious endogenous molecules that are released during tissue damage and cellular injury. These molecules are known as damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). The most common DAMPs are HMGB1, heat shock proteins, SAP130, fragments of RNA and DNA, hyaluronic acid, VCAN, heparan sulfate, monosodium urate, IL33, and IL1A.    Signaling description: DAMPs act similarly to pathogen pattern molecules that bind to TLRs and activate an immune response and trigger inflammation. TLRs are expressed on the surface of various cells of the immune system and can initiate inflammation after association with DAMPs originating from necrotic cells. TLRs are primarily expressed on the surface of monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells.    Outcome effects: Downstream pathways that follow DAMP/TLR binding are similar to other TLR-activated pathways. These signaling pathways include the activation of MYD88, TIRAP, and TICAM which finally lead to the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines, interferon, and chemokines via the activation of NF-kB, IRFs, and Jun/Fos transcription factors. CD14, CD36, CD40, and CD91 are important co-activators of TLRs. CD14 and CD91 bind to heat shock proteins (HSPs) and promote the expression of IL1B, IL6, TNF, IL12, and NO production through TLR-mediated signaling. These molecules play different roles in the inflammation process. DAMP-specific receptor CD24, which can bind to both HMGB1 and HSPs, negatively regulates sterile inflammatory responses via the inhibition of TLR responses. Intracellular TLRs such as TLR3 and TLR9 can bind to fragments of DNA and RNA originating from dead cells and promote interferon synthesis. In certain cases, AGER may transduce signals by acting cooperatively with TLRs. HMGB1-bound DNA can form a complex with TLR9 and AGER to induce proinflammatory cytokines. Finally, similarly to microbially-induced inflammation, sterile inflammation mediated by TLRs is marked by the recruitment of neutrophils and macrophages and the production of proinflammatory cytokines and chemokines, particularly TNF and IL1." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-a27206fa-bab6-4af3-b289-3b5fa8219480
name: Toll-like Receptor Independent Sterile Inflammation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:inflammation_initiation ! Inflammation initiation
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
xref: PS:Description "Sterile inflammation is the result of trauma, ischemia-reperfusion injury, or chemically-induced injury and occurs in the absence of pathogens. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Sterile inflammation is the result of trauma, ischemia-reperfusion injury, or chemically-induced injury and occurs in the absence of pathogens. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-a27206fa-bab6-4af3-b289-3b5fa8219480"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-a27206fa-bab6-4af3-b289-3b5fa8219480" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "generic"
synonym: "Organ_System: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "generic"
synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Sterile inflammation is the result of trauma, ischemia-reperfusion injury, or chemically-induced injury and occurs in the absence of pathogens. Sterile inflammation is induced in response to cell death by necrosis or injury. It is the main component of many disorders including pulmonary interstitial fibrosis, ischemia-reperfusion injury, atherosclerosis, Alzheimer's disease, and tumors.    Signaling description: Under conditions of cellular stress or injury, different molecules including damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) can be released into the extracellular environment by dying cells and trigger inflammation under sterile conditions. The most common DAMPs are HMGB1, heat shock proteins, SAP130, fragments of RNA and DNA, hyaluronic acid, VCAN, heparan sulfate, monosodium urate, IL33, and IL1A. Pattern recognition receptors such as NOD-like receptors (NLRs) have an important role in sterile inflammation development. NLRs form inflammasomes, which are multi-protein complexes required for the secretion of IL1B, a key mediator in the sterile inflammation process. There are two inflammasomes that can sense non-microbial molecules including NLRP3 and AIM2. There are three main pathways that have been proposed to mediate sterile activation of NLRP3 including ATP-, lysosomal damage- or ROS-mediated activation. IL1B is produced mainly by the macrophages and has many biological functions such as the upregulation of adhesion molecules on endothelial cells, which are important for the recruitment of neutrophils and monocytes and for the induction of additional proinflammatory mediators. In addition, IL1A and IL33 released from injured endothelial cells during necrosis, but not apoptosis also function to alert innate immune cells to tissue damage. IL1A is crucial for the production of CXCL1 through IL1R signaling by macrophages. IL1A and IL33 signaling results in the activation of NF-kB via TRAF6. AGER is a transmembrane receptor that is expressed by immune cells, endothelial cells, cardiomyocytes, and neurons. AGER recognizes HMGB1 and the S100 family members, which are released during cellular stress and necrotic cell death. The activation of AGER by its ligands results in inflammatory signaling pathways, which lead to the induction of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF. The mechanism, by which AGER activates these proinflammatory signaling pathways is unclear. In certain cases, AGER may transduce signals by acting cooperatively with TLRs. Specifically, HMGB1-bound DNA can form a complex with TLR9 and AGER to induce proinflammatory cytokines. Following sterile inflammatory stimuli such as reactive oxygen species (ROS) and hydrolytic enzymes, native hyaluronic acid of intercellular matrix is degraded into small fragments. In the acute phase of inflammation, hyaluronic acid fragments may prime inflammation via TLR4 and CD44, by different pathways that finally both converge in NF-kB activation. The exact mechanism of CD44 signaling following hyaluronic acid stimuli is currently under investigation. One of the possible mechanisms is through SRC, Ras, and PKC activation. Conversely, the accumulation of high-molecular-weight (HMW) hyaluronan triggers the activation of a phosphatase which causes the formation of a growth-inhibitory complex. ERM (ezrin/radixin/moesin) proteins function as general cross-linkers between actin filaments and specific groups of integral membrane proteins such as CD44. Their carboxy- and amino- terminal halves bind to actin filaments and the cytoplasmic domains of integral membrane proteins, respectively. The amino- and carboxy-terminal halves of ERM proteins have been shown to bind each other and suppress the functions of both halves. This is a prerequisite for the replacement of ERM proteins bound to CD44 by the dephosphorylated active form of NF2. NF2 disrupts the local link to the actin cytoskeleton and blocks Ras activation and signaling. CLEC4E can detect SAP130, which is a component of"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Sterile inflammation is the result of trauma, ischemia-reperfusion injury, or chemically-induced injury and occurs in the absence of pathogens. Sterile inflammation is induced in response to cell death by necrosis or injury. It is the main component of many disorders including pulmonary interstitial fibrosis, ischemia-reperfusion injury, atherosclerosis, Alzheimer's disease, and tumors.    Signaling description: Under conditions of cellular stress or injury, different molecules including damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) can be released into the extracellular environment by dying cells and trigger inflammation under sterile conditions. The most common DAMPs are HMGB1, heat shock proteins, SAP130, fragments of RNA and DNA, hyaluronic acid, VCAN, heparan sulfate, monosodium urate, IL33, and IL1A. Pattern recognition receptors such as NOD-like receptors (NLRs) have an important role in sterile inflammation development. NLRs form inflammasomes, which are multi-protein complexes required for the secretion of IL1B, a key mediator in the sterile inflammation process. There are two inflammasomes that can sense non-microbial molecules including NLRP3 and AIM2. There are three main pathways that have been proposed to mediate sterile activation of NLRP3 including ATP-, lysosomal damage- or ROS-mediated activation. IL1B is produced mainly by the macrophages and has many biological functions such as the upregulation of adhesion molecules on endothelial cells, which are important for the recruitment of neutrophils and monocytes and for the induction of additional proinflammatory mediators. In addition, IL1A and IL33 released from injured endothelial cells during necrosis, but not apoptosis also function to alert innate immune cells to tissue damage. IL1A is crucial for the production of CXCL1 through IL1R signaling by macrophages. IL1A and IL33 signaling results in the activation of NF-kB via TRAF6. AGER is a transmembrane receptor that is expressed by immune cells, endothelial cells, cardiomyocytes, and neurons. AGER recognizes HMGB1 and the S100 family members, which are released during cellular stress and necrotic cell death. The activation of AGER by its ligands results in inflammatory signaling pathways, which lead to the induction of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF. The mechanism, by which AGER activates these proinflammatory signaling pathways is unclear. In certain cases, AGER may transduce signals by acting cooperatively with TLRs. Specifically, HMGB1-bound DNA can form a complex with TLR9 and AGER to induce proinflammatory cytokines. Following sterile inflammatory stimuli such as reactive oxygen species (ROS) and hydrolytic enzymes, native hyaluronic acid of intercellular matrix is degraded into small fragments. In the acute phase of inflammation, hyaluronic acid fragments may prime inflammation via TLR4 and CD44, by different pathways that finally both converge in NF-kB activation. The exact mechanism of CD44 signaling following hyaluronic acid stimuli is currently under investigation. One of the possible mechanisms is through SRC, Ras, and PKC activation. Conversely, the accumulation of high-molecular-weight (HMW) hyaluronan triggers the activation of a phosphatase which causes the formation of a growth-inhibitory complex. ERM (ezrin/radixin/moesin) proteins function as general cross-linkers between actin filaments and specific groups of integral membrane proteins such as CD44. Their carboxy- and amino- terminal halves bind to actin filaments and the cytoplasmic domains of integral membrane proteins, respectively. The amino- and carboxy-terminal halves of ERM proteins have been shown to bind each other and suppress the functions of both halves. This is a prerequisite for the replacement of ERM proteins bound to CD44 by the dephosphorylated active form of NF2. NF2 disrupts the local link to the actin cytoskeleton and blocks Ras activation and signaling. CLEC4E can detect SAP130, which is a component of" EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-22f9e7a9-3073-4f18-b273-5623c70aa07c
name: Coagulation Cascade
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:hematological_system ! Hematological system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:hemostasis_reaction ! Hemostasis reaction
is_a: urn:agi-folder:blood ! Blood
is_a: urn:agi-folder:c ! C
xref: PS:Description "Coagulation results in the formation of thrombus which is a blood clot that prevents further blood loss from damaged tissues. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Coagulation results in the formation of thrombus which is a blood clot that prevents further blood loss from damaged tissues. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
