synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Coagulation results in the formation of thrombus, which is a blood clot that prevents further blood loss from damaged tissues. The coagulation proteins form a cascade in a chain reaction.    Signaling description: There are 12 coagulation factors from F1 to F12; the numbers refer to the order, in which the factors were discovered and not to the order in which they react. The purpose of the pathway is to generate thrombin (F2), which subsequently converts fibrinogen dissolved in plasma into fibrin to form a clot. The process of F2 generation can be divided into three phases including the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways which provide the alternative generation of activated F10 and the final common pathway which results in F2 formation. The intrinsic pathway begins with the formation of the primary complex of F12, KLKB1, and KNG1 on the surface of platelets. During activation, the single chain protein F12 is cleaved into two chains and the light chain (28kDa) is referred to as activated F12. The activated F12 remains in close contact with the activating surface and can activate F11. The subsequent step in the intrinsic pathway requires Ca2+ to proceed efficiently. F11 activates F9 which in turn, activates F10 to its active form through the interactions with Ca2+, membrane phospholipids, and F8.    Outcome effects: The extrinsic pathway provides a very rapid response to tissue injury to activate F10. There are two unique components to the extrinsic pathway including tissue factor (F3) and F7. Following damage to the blood vessel, F7 leaves the circulation and comes into contact with F3 expressed on tissue factor-bearing cells such as stromal fibroblasts and leukocytes. The F3/F7 complex then rapidly activates F10. The activation of F10 is ultimately responsible for the production of F2 which converts fibrinogen to fibrin. In addition, F2 enhances the activation of F5 and F8 in a feedback manner. The polymerized fibrin is stabilized by F13 which is produced by the action of F2 on F13."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Coagulation results in the formation of thrombus, which is a blood clot that prevents further blood loss from damaged tissues. The coagulation proteins form a cascade in a chain reaction.    Signaling description: There are 12 coagulation factors from F1 to F12; the numbers refer to the order, in which the factors were discovered and not to the order in which they react. The purpose of the pathway is to generate thrombin (F2), which subsequently converts fibrinogen dissolved in plasma into fibrin to form a clot. The process of F2 generation can be divided into three phases including the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways which provide the alternative generation of activated F10 and the final common pathway which results in F2 formation. The intrinsic pathway begins with the formation of the primary complex of F12, KLKB1, and KNG1 on the surface of platelets. During activation, the single chain protein F12 is cleaved into two chains and the light chain (28kDa) is referred to as activated F12. The activated F12 remains in close contact with the activating surface and can activate F11. The subsequent step in the intrinsic pathway requires Ca2+ to proceed efficiently. F11 activates F9 which in turn, activates F10 to its active form through the interactions with Ca2+, membrane phospholipids, and F8.    Outcome effects: The extrinsic pathway provides a very rapid response to tissue injury to activate F10. There are two unique components to the extrinsic pathway including tissue factor (F3) and F7. Following damage to the blood vessel, F7 leaves the circulation and comes into contact with F3 expressed on tissue factor-bearing cells such as stromal fibroblasts and leukocytes. The F3/F7 complex then rapidly activates F10. The activation of F10 is ultimately responsible for the production of F2 which converts fibrinogen to fibrin. In addition, F2 enhances the activation of F5 and F8 in a feedback manner. The polymerized fibrin is stabilized by F13 which is produced by the action of F2 on F13." EXACT []

