xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by pancreatic beta-cells located in the islets of Langerhans. It plays a crucial role in carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism in insulin-dependent tissues. Insulin action is similar in both hepatocytes and myocytes. The hormone provides glucose conversion to glycogen and triggers protein synthesis. Insulin influence on protein synthesis is realized in its regulation of translation.    Signaling description: The hormone promotes translation by activating EIF4E and RPS6 in addition to inhibiting EIF2S1. The main signaling occurs through AKT1. Insulin (INS) binds to its receptor, INSR and the signal through PIK3R1 is transduced to IRS which activates PI3K. PI3K-generated PIP3 then activates AKT1. Activated by PIP3, PDPK1 induces the same reaction. AKT1 inhibits proteins which lead to the suppression of the translation responsive elements. AKT1 phosphorylates GSK3B, rendering it inactive. In other cases, GSK3B phosphorylates the protein complex of eukaryotic translation initiation factors (eIF) that negatively regulate the initiation of protein translation. Involved in signaling, MTOR via AKT1 promotes translation by phosphorylation and activation of RPS6KB1 which affects RPS6 and inhibition of EIF4EBP1 which is EIF4E suppressor. EIF4E and RPS6 are key regulators of protein synthesis. Through SHC1/SOS1/RAF1/MAPK3, INS phosphorylates MYC which binds to the promoter region of EIF4E."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by pancreatic beta-cells located in the islets of Langerhans. It plays a crucial role in carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism in insulin-dependent tissues. Insulin action is similar in both hepatocytes and myocytes. The hormone provides glucose conversion to glycogen and triggers protein synthesis. Insulin influence on protein synthesis is realized in its regulation of translation.    Signaling description: The hormone promotes translation by activating EIF4E and RPS6 in addition to inhibiting EIF2S1. The main signaling occurs through AKT1. Insulin (INS) binds to its receptor, INSR and the signal through PIK3R1 is transduced to IRS which activates PI3K. PI3K-generated PIP3 then activates AKT1. Activated by PIP3, PDPK1 induces the same reaction. AKT1 inhibits proteins which lead to the suppression of the translation responsive elements. AKT1 phosphorylates GSK3B, rendering it inactive. In other cases, GSK3B phosphorylates the protein complex of eukaryotic translation initiation factors (eIF) that negatively regulate the initiation of protein translation. Involved in signaling, MTOR via AKT1 promotes translation by phosphorylation and activation of RPS6KB1 which affects RPS6 and inhibition of EIF4EBP1 which is EIF4E suppressor. EIF4E and RPS6 are key regulators of protein synthesis. Through SHC1/SOS1/RAF1/MAPK3, INS phosphorylates MYC which binds to the promoter region of EIF4E." EXACT []

