xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by pancreatic beta-cells located in the islets of Langerhans. Insulin is an anabolic hormone that reduces the level of circulating glucose by allowing its entry into muscle cells and hepatocytes and through its conversion to a storage molecule called glycogen which is metabolized during an energy deficiency.    Signaling description: Insulin (INS) binds to its receptor, INSR and the signal is transduced to IRS which activates PI3K. PI3K-generated PIP3 activates AKT1 leading to TBC1D4 phosphorylation and thus GLUT4 (SLC2A4) translocation into the membrane. Activated by PDPK1, PRKCH induces the same reaction. GLUT1 (SLC2A1) is the specific glucose transporter promoting the entry of glucose into the cell. Subsequently, glucose is converted into glycogen through several stages. The first stage is glucose phosphorylation by hexokinase. As a result, glucose-6-P is formed and then undergoes isomerization by phosphoglucomutase. The translocation of a phosphate group to the first position leads to glucose-1-P formation. UGP2 converts glucose-1-P to UDP-glucose. The last reaction is the addition of UDP-glucose molecules to chains of glycogen catalyzed by glycogen synthase 2 (GYS2). GYS2 activation is also obliged to insulin action through two mechanisms such as AKT1 inhibition of GYS2 inhibitor (GSK3B) and GYS2 phosphorylation by PPP1CC through SHC1/GRB2/ MAPK. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by pancreatic beta-cells located in the islets of Langerhans. Insulin is an anabolic hormone that reduces the level of circulating glucose by allowing its entry into muscle cells and hepatocytes and through its conversion to a storage molecule called glycogen which is metabolized during an energy deficiency.    Signaling description: Insulin (INS) binds to its receptor, INSR and the signal is transduced to IRS which activates PI3K. PI3K-generated PIP3 activates AKT1 leading to TBC1D4 phosphorylation and thus GLUT4 (SLC2A4) translocation into the membrane. Activated by PDPK1, PRKCH induces the same reaction. GLUT1 (SLC2A1) is the specific glucose transporter promoting the entry of glucose into the cell. Subsequently, glucose is converted into glycogen through several stages. The first stage is glucose phosphorylation by hexokinase. As a result, glucose-6-P is formed and then undergoes isomerization by phosphoglucomutase. The translocation of a phosphate group to the first position leads to glucose-1-P formation. UGP2 converts glucose-1-P to UDP-glucose. The last reaction is the addition of UDP-glucose molecules to chains of glycogen catalyzed by glycogen synthase 2 (GYS2). GYS2 activation is also obliged to insulin action through two mechanisms such as AKT1 inhibition of GYS2 inhibitor (GSK3B) and GYS2 phosphorylation by PPP1CC through SHC1/GRB2/ MAPK. Compounds with elevated level are highlighted in red." EXACT []

