xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by pancreatic beta-cells located in the islets of Langerhans. It plays a crucial role in carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism in insulin-dependent tissues. Insulin promotes lipogenesis in hepatocytes and adipocytes resulting in the storage of triglycerides in adipocytes and low density lipoprotein (LDL) in hepatocytes.    Signaling description: Intracellular fat is formed from free fatty acids (FFA) delivered by lipoproteins and glycerol-3-P. Insulin involvement in lipogenesis appears in the regulation of glycerol-3-P synthesis and increasing the level of lipoprotein lipase (LPL) mRNA. The hormone causes GLUT (SLC2A1 and SLC2A4) translocation into the membrane surface involving INSR/IRS mechanism promoting glucose influx through it. Intracellular glucose converts to dihydroxyacetone-P during glycolysis which is transformed into glycerol-3-P by glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (NAD ). In hepatocytes, glycerol-3-P may also be produced from glycerol released from lipoproteins by glycerol kinase. LPL releases FFA from chylomicrons and VLDL which transport FFA from the intestine to the adipose tissue and liver. FFA binds to its receptor, CD36. In the cell, fatty acids are transformed into fatty acyl CoA by ACSL1. Fatty acyl CoA and glycerol-3-P are used to form phosphatidic acid by AGPAT9. Phosphatidic acid is converted into diacylglycerol by phosphatidate phosphatase. Next, the binding of an additional fatty acyl CoA to diacylglycerol results in the formation of triglycerides. In hepatocytes, triglycerides with phospholipids, cholesterol, and APOB are packaged into VLDL. Compound with elevated level is highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by pancreatic beta-cells located in the islets of Langerhans. It plays a crucial role in carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism in insulin-dependent tissues. Insulin promotes lipogenesis in hepatocytes and adipocytes resulting in the storage of triglycerides in adipocytes and low density lipoprotein (LDL) in hepatocytes.    Signaling description: Intracellular fat is formed from free fatty acids (FFA) delivered by lipoproteins and glycerol-3-P. Insulin involvement in lipogenesis appears in the regulation of glycerol-3-P synthesis and increasing the level of lipoprotein lipase (LPL) mRNA. The hormone causes GLUT (SLC2A1 and SLC2A4) translocation into the membrane surface involving INSR/IRS mechanism promoting glucose influx through it. Intracellular glucose converts to dihydroxyacetone-P during glycolysis which is transformed into glycerol-3-P by glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (NAD ). In hepatocytes, glycerol-3-P may also be produced from glycerol released from lipoproteins by glycerol kinase. LPL releases FFA from chylomicrons and VLDL which transport FFA from the intestine to the adipose tissue and liver. FFA binds to its receptor, CD36. In the cell, fatty acids are transformed into fatty acyl CoA by ACSL1. Fatty acyl CoA and glycerol-3-P are used to form phosphatidic acid by AGPAT9. Phosphatidic acid is converted into diacylglycerol by phosphatidate phosphatase. Next, the binding of an additional fatty acyl CoA to diacylglycerol results in the formation of triglycerides. In hepatocytes, triglycerides with phospholipids, cholesterol, and APOB are packaged into VLDL. Compound with elevated level is highlighted in red." EXACT []

[Term]
