[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-b6fb5579-18e3-49e9-a8f9-e590ad9c0e6a
name: Cortisol in Resolving Inflammation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
is_a: urn:agi-folder:c ! C
is_a: urn:agi-folder:steroid_hormones ! Steroid hormones
is_a: urn:agi-folder:endocrine_system ! Endocrine system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:inflammation_resolution ! Inflammation resolution
xref: PS:Description "Natural glucocorticoids, such as cortisol in humans, are cholesterol-derived hormones secreted by the adrenal glands under stress or inflammation. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Natural glucocorticoids, such as cortisol in humans, are cholesterol-derived hormones secreted by the adrenal glands under stress or inflammation. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-b6fb5579-18e3-49e9-a8f9-e590ad9c0e6a"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-b6fb5579-18e3-49e9-a8f9-e590ad9c0e6a" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "generic"
synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "endocrine system"
synonym: "Organ_System: endocrine system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Natural glucocorticoids, such as cortisol in humans, are cholesterol-derived hormones secreted by the adrenal glands under stress or inflammation. The availability of natural cortisol in tissues is regulated by locally expressed 11b-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (HSD11B1).    Signaling description: Both natural and synthetic glucocorticoids transduce their actions by binding to glucocorticoid receptor NR3C1. In the absence of glucocorticoids, NR3C1 resides in the cytoplasm in its inactive form bound to chaperone proteins such as HSP90AA1, HSPA1A, FKBP4, and PTGES3. Following binding to a glucocorticoid molecule, NR3C1 detaches from this multi-protein complex and translocates into the nucleus. The effects of steroids and their receptor complexes can be classified as genomic and non-genomic. In the genomic scenario, cortisol/NR3C1 homodimerizes in the nucleus and exerts its transcriptional activation or repression by direct binding to NR3C1 elements found in the promoters of glucocorticoid target genes. TSC22D3, SGK1, SLPI, ZFP36, and DUSP1 are examples of genes upregulated by activated NR3C1. DUSP1, TSC22D3, SLPI, and ANXA1 function as pivotal anti-inflammatory factors. The mechanism of TSC22D3 action as an anti-inflammatory factor is unclear; however it inhibits the expression of toll-like receptor 2 (TLR2) and reduces the production of proinflammatory mediators. SLPI was originally discovered as a secreted product of epithelial cells that cleaves serine proteases such as elastase (ELANE). SLPI and GRN appear to be a pair of host-derived anti-inflammatory mediators that serve to prevent excess innate immune responses. The binding of GRN by SLPI prevents its conversion into epithelins. Epithelins promote and GRN downregulates inflammatory reactions. SLPI and GRN block TNF-triggered neutrophil activation. SLPI also suppresses the macrophage response to LPS. In addition, ZFP36 binds to AU-rich elements of target mRNAs such as cytokines and growth factors, and then induces mRNA rapid degradation. ZFP36 inhibits TNF production from macrophages by destabilizing its mRNA. Other reported mRNA targets of ZFP36 include CSF2, IL3, IL2, IL6, and PTGS2. DUSP1, a member of the dual-specificity phosphatase family, acts to dephosphorylate and deactivate MAPKs resulting in the inhibition of inflammatory cell activation. The glucocorticoid-antagonizing proinflammatory MIF modulates glucocorticoid sensitivity via inhibition of glucocorticoid-induced DUSP1. NR3C1 also modulates gene transrepression by binding to the target gene containing binding sites for glucocorticoid-responsive elements (GREs) as well as other transcription factors. Thus, NR3C1 can regulate the contribution of other transcription factors to gene expression. The suppression of inflammation via cortisol may occur by NR3C1-mediated transrepression of proinflammatory transcription factor effects such as JUN/FOS, NF-kB, STAT3, and IRF3. The glucocorticoid/NR3C1 complex is able to powerfully inhibit gene transcription entrained by these proteins. Moreover, the glucocorticoid/NR3C1 complex physically interacts with NF-kB in the cytoplasm to block its nuclear complex to prevent gene transcription. The glucocorticoid/NR3C1complex also represses transcriptional activation mediated by JUN/FOS, through a direct interaction between NR3C1 and c-Jun/c-Fos.    Outcome effects: Protein-protein interactions between glucocorticoid/NR3C1 complex and NF-kB and JUN/FOS result in the repression of the production of proinflammatory cytokines including IL2, IL6, IL8, CSF2 as well as PTGS2. NR3C1 isoforms are expressed in nearly all tissue types. Cortisol exerts a classic anti-inflammatory role by inhibiting NF-kB and JUN/FOS expression targets by acting on nearly all cell types of the immune system. Moreover, glucocorticoids suppress dendritic cell maturation. In the macrophage, glucocorticoids inhibit TGFB1 signaling by directly targeting the transcriptional activation function of SMAD3 in"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Natural glucocorticoids, such as cortisol in humans, are cholesterol-derived hormones secreted by the adrenal glands under stress or inflammation. The availability of natural cortisol in tissues is regulated by locally expressed 11b-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (HSD11B1).    Signaling description: Both natural and synthetic glucocorticoids transduce their actions by binding to glucocorticoid receptor NR3C1. In the absence of glucocorticoids, NR3C1 resides in the cytoplasm in its inactive form bound to chaperone proteins such as HSP90AA1, HSPA1A, FKBP4, and PTGES3. Following binding to a glucocorticoid molecule, NR3C1 detaches from this multi-protein complex and translocates into the nucleus. The effects of steroids and their receptor complexes can be classified as genomic and non-genomic. In the genomic scenario, cortisol/NR3C1 homodimerizes in the nucleus and exerts its transcriptional activation or repression by direct binding to NR3C1 elements found in the promoters of glucocorticoid target genes. TSC22D3, SGK1, SLPI, ZFP36, and DUSP1 are examples of genes upregulated by activated NR3C1. DUSP1, TSC22D3, SLPI, and ANXA1 function as pivotal anti-inflammatory factors. The mechanism of TSC22D3 action as an anti-inflammatory factor is unclear; however it inhibits the expression of toll-like receptor 2 (TLR2) and reduces the production of proinflammatory mediators. SLPI was originally discovered as a secreted product of epithelial cells that cleaves serine proteases such as elastase (ELANE). SLPI and GRN appear to be a pair of host-derived anti-inflammatory mediators that serve to prevent excess innate immune responses. The binding of GRN by SLPI prevents its conversion into epithelins. Epithelins promote and GRN downregulates inflammatory reactions. SLPI and GRN block TNF-triggered neutrophil activation. SLPI also suppresses the macrophage response to LPS. In addition, ZFP36 binds to AU-rich elements of target mRNAs such as cytokines and growth factors, and then induces mRNA rapid degradation. ZFP36 inhibits TNF production from macrophages by destabilizing its mRNA. Other reported mRNA targets of ZFP36 include CSF2, IL3, IL2, IL6, and PTGS2. DUSP1, a member of the dual-specificity phosphatase family, acts to dephosphorylate and deactivate MAPKs resulting in the inhibition of inflammatory cell activation. The glucocorticoid-antagonizing proinflammatory MIF modulates glucocorticoid sensitivity via inhibition of glucocorticoid-induced DUSP1. NR3C1 also modulates gene transrepression by binding to the target gene containing binding sites for glucocorticoid-responsive elements (GREs) as well as other transcription factors. Thus, NR3C1 can regulate the contribution of other transcription factors to gene expression. The suppression of inflammation via cortisol may occur by NR3C1-mediated transrepression of proinflammatory transcription factor effects such as JUN/FOS, NF-kB, STAT3, and IRF3. The glucocorticoid/NR3C1 complex is able to powerfully inhibit gene transcription entrained by these proteins. Moreover, the glucocorticoid/NR3C1 complex physically interacts with NF-kB in the cytoplasm to block its nuclear complex to prevent gene transcription. The glucocorticoid/NR3C1complex also represses transcriptional activation mediated by JUN/FOS, through a direct interaction between NR3C1 and c-Jun/c-Fos.    Outcome effects: Protein-protein interactions between glucocorticoid/NR3C1 complex and NF-kB and JUN/FOS result in the repression of the production of proinflammatory cytokines including IL2, IL6, IL8, CSF2 as well as PTGS2. NR3C1 isoforms are expressed in nearly all tissue types. Cortisol exerts a classic anti-inflammatory role by inhibiting NF-kB and JUN/FOS expression targets by acting on nearly all cell types of the immune system. Moreover, glucocorticoids suppress dendritic cell maturation. In the macrophage, glucocorticoids inhibit TGFB1 signaling by directly targeting the transcriptional activation function of SMAD3 in" EXACT []

