xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Lipoxins, such as lipoxin A4 and lipoxin B4, are short-lived bioactive lipid mediators generated from arachidonic acid which has anti-inflammatory effects. FPR2 is the receptor for stereospecific lipoxin A4 binding. The interaction between FPR2 and lipoxin A4 is primarily dependent on stereoselectivity and chirality, respectively. Human FPR2 is present in several types of leukocytes, including monocytes and T-cells, as well as resident cells such as macrophages, synovial fibroblasts, and intestinal epithelial cells.    Signaling description: The anti-inflammatory action of lipoxin A4 is likely to be a summation of multiple lipid domain FPR2 signaling events in several cell types. In general, lipoxin A4 promotes the resolution of inflammation since it blocks polymorphonuclear neutrophil (PMN) transmigration and causes a reduction in their numbers, recruitment of monocytes to the sites of inflammation and injury, and clearance of apoptotic PMNs by macrophages. The current understanding of lipoxin A4/FPR2 intracellular signaling remains incomplete. In neutrophils, lipoxins fail to promote a sustained mobilization of intracellular Ca2+ or generation of cAMP. However, the binding of lipoxin A4 to its receptor triggers the activation of GTPase, phospholipase A2, and phospholipase D. In neutrophils, FPR2/ lipoxin A4 regulates presqualene-PP conversions. Presqualene-PP is a constituent of PMN cell membranes, which lack squalene cyclase, and therefore, cannot use it for cholesterol biosynthesis. The activating positive signals for PMN, such as LTB4, initiate the rapid degradation of presqualene-PP. Presqualene-PP directly inhibits PIK3CG and PLD1. Presqualene-PP degradation under LTB4 unlocks these regulatory enzymes and leads to neutrophil function activation. Presqualene-PP also interacts with GRB2 and blocks NAD(P)H oxidase assembly for reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation. FPR2 activation causes the accumulation of presqualene-PP and blocks neutrophil function, but the signaling mechanisms of this effect remain unknown. The degradation of neutrophil presqualene-PP does not occur when PPAPDC2 or PRKCB is inhibited.    Outcome effects: Neutrophil exposure to 15-epi-lipoxin A4 triggers PRKCB phosphorylation and its interactions with PPAPDC2. It is hypothesized that FPR2-related accumulation of presqualene-PP is dependent on the phosphorylation status of PPAPDC2. Peptide PBP10, derived from PIP2, may participate in FPR2-signaling in PMN. PBP10 binds to gelsolin (GSN), which is a key regulator of actin filament assembly and disassembly. GSN also blocks granule mobilization and the secretion of oxygen radicals. Thus, FPR2 activation by lipoxin A4 in human neutrophils blocks ROS generation and migration. Lipoxin A4 causes low expression of NF-kB, JUN/FOS, and IL8. It is noted that lipoxins block the phosphorylation of LSP1 and upregulate NAB1. In addition, lipoxin A4/FPR2 signaling may inhibit LTB4-related proinflammatory pathway in neutrophils by uncoupling polyisoprenyl phosphate (PIPP) signaling pathway. This is demonstrated by the down-regulation of ITGAM/ITGB2-dependent PMN adhesion and decreased IP3 formation. In monocytes, lipoxin A4 stimulates chemotaxis and adherence. Lipoxin A4 stimulates the uptake of apoptotic PMN by monocyte-derived macrophages thus decreasing inflammation. This action seems to be coupled to changes in the actin cytoskeleton in macrophages.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Lipoxins, such as lipoxin A4 and lipoxin B4, are short-lived bioactive lipid mediators generated from arachidonic acid which has anti-inflammatory effects. FPR2 is the receptor for stereospecific lipoxin A4 binding. The interaction between FPR2 and lipoxin A4 is primarily dependent on stereoselectivity and chirality, respectively. Human FPR2 is present in several types of leukocytes, including monocytes and T-cells, as well as resident cells such as macrophages, synovial fibroblasts, and intestinal epithelial cells.    Signaling description: The anti-inflammatory action of lipoxin A4 is likely to be a summation of multiple lipid domain FPR2 signaling events in several cell types. In general, lipoxin A4 promotes the resolution of inflammation since it blocks polymorphonuclear neutrophil (PMN) transmigration and causes a reduction in their numbers, recruitment of monocytes to the sites of inflammation and injury, and clearance of apoptotic PMNs by macrophages. The current understanding of lipoxin A4/FPR2 intracellular signaling remains incomplete. In neutrophils, lipoxins fail to promote a sustained mobilization of intracellular Ca2+ or generation of cAMP. However, the binding of lipoxin A4 to its receptor triggers the activation of GTPase, phospholipase A2, and phospholipase D. In neutrophils, FPR2/ lipoxin A4 regulates presqualene-PP conversions. Presqualene-PP is a constituent of PMN cell membranes, which lack squalene cyclase, and therefore, cannot use it for cholesterol biosynthesis. The activating positive signals for PMN, such as LTB4, initiate the rapid degradation of presqualene-PP. Presqualene-PP directly inhibits PIK3CG and PLD1. Presqualene-PP degradation under LTB4 unlocks these regulatory enzymes and leads to neutrophil function activation. Presqualene-PP also interacts with GRB2 and blocks NAD(P)H oxidase assembly for reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation. FPR2 activation causes the accumulation of presqualene-PP and blocks neutrophil function, but the signaling mechanisms of this effect remain unknown. The degradation of neutrophil presqualene-PP does not occur when PPAPDC2 or PRKCB is inhibited.    Outcome effects: Neutrophil exposure to 15-epi-lipoxin A4 triggers PRKCB phosphorylation and its interactions with PPAPDC2. It is hypothesized that FPR2-related accumulation of presqualene-PP is dependent on the phosphorylation status of PPAPDC2. Peptide PBP10, derived from PIP2, may participate in FPR2-signaling in PMN. PBP10 binds to gelsolin (GSN), which is a key regulator of actin filament assembly and disassembly. GSN also blocks granule mobilization and the secretion of oxygen radicals. Thus, FPR2 activation by lipoxin A4 in human neutrophils blocks ROS generation and migration. Lipoxin A4 causes low expression of NF-kB, JUN/FOS, and IL8. It is noted that lipoxins block the phosphorylation of LSP1 and upregulate NAB1. In addition, lipoxin A4/FPR2 signaling may inhibit LTB4-related proinflammatory pathway in neutrophils by uncoupling polyisoprenyl phosphate (PIPP) signaling pathway. This is demonstrated by the down-regulation of ITGAM/ITGB2-dependent PMN adhesion and decreased IP3 formation. In monocytes, lipoxin A4 stimulates chemotaxis and adherence. Lipoxin A4 stimulates the uptake of apoptotic PMN by monocyte-derived macrophages thus decreasing inflammation. This action seems to be coupled to changes in the actin cytoskeleton in macrophages.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

