xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The acute phase response (APR) is a prominent systemic reaction of the organism to an infection and tissue injury. One of the mechanisms of APR is increased values of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) and glucocorticoids via the activation of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis.    Signaling description: Glucocorticoids mediate anti-inflammatory effects in the body. Hypothalamic hormone CRH induces ACTH production by adenohypophysis and then ACTH stimulates the secretion of glucocorticoids by the adrenal cortex. The expression of neurohormones CRH and ACTH are stress-induced. During inflammation, IL1, IL6, and TNF as well as neurotransmitters and neuropeptides such as serotonin, acetylcholine, histamine, and noradrenaline stimulate CRH production. Histamine is one of the few central nervous system neurotransmitters that can cause consistent blood-brain barrier opening. Transport systems from blood to the central nervous system (CNS) have been identified for IL1, IL6, and TNF. PGE2 also plays an important role in IL1B-induced upregulation of CRH. CRH provides an anti-inflammatory response by CRHR1 signaling in the adrenal gland and stimulation of ACTH release. ACTH is a melanocortin that is derived from proopiomelanocortin (POMC) proteolytic cleavage by PCSK1. MC2R is the specific receptor for ACTH in the adrenal gland where it leads to the production and release of steroids. MC2R requires the expression of MRAP, which interacts with MC2R and facilitates MC2R cell surface expression. The classical signaling pathway for MCRs passes through G-proteins, which activate adenylate cyclase (ADCY), increase intracellular cAMP production, and activate PKA. The stimulation of all MCRs leads to the activation of MAPK3/MAPK1. The activation of PKA has been shown to counteract Ras/RAF1/ MAPK3/MAPK1 signaling pathway. RAPGEF4/RAPGEF3 is a RAP1A/RAP2A-specific GEF, which translates cAMP signal to MAPKs via BRAF pathway in a PKA- and Ras-independent manner. BRAF is the dominant molecular switch that permits differential cAMP-dependent regulation of MAPKs.    Outcome effects: Glucocorticoids are produced in the adrenal gland and exert potent anti-inflammatory effects by inhibiting IL2 gene expression by lymphocytes, IL1 gene expression by macrophages, MHC class II antigen expression, and phospholipase A2 activity. Glucocorticoids activate the expression of TSC22D3, a protein whose mechanism of action is unclear, but it is known that it interacts with and inhibits the function of NF-kB, Jun/Fos, and ANXA1. Furthermore, glucocorticoids have been shown to inhibit T-cell binding to endothelial cells by modulating the expression of adhesion molecules."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The acute phase response (APR) is a prominent systemic reaction of the organism to an infection and tissue injury. One of the mechanisms of APR is increased values of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) and glucocorticoids via the activation of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis.    Signaling description: Glucocorticoids mediate anti-inflammatory effects in the body. Hypothalamic hormone CRH induces ACTH production by adenohypophysis and then ACTH stimulates the secretion of glucocorticoids by the adrenal cortex. The expression of neurohormones CRH and ACTH are stress-induced. During inflammation, IL1, IL6, and TNF as well as neurotransmitters and neuropeptides such as serotonin, acetylcholine, histamine, and noradrenaline stimulate CRH production. Histamine is one of the few central nervous system neurotransmitters that can cause consistent blood-brain barrier opening. Transport systems from blood to the central nervous system (CNS) have been identified for IL1, IL6, and TNF. PGE2 also plays an important role in IL1B-induced upregulation of CRH. CRH provides an anti-inflammatory response by CRHR1 signaling in the adrenal gland and stimulation of ACTH release. ACTH is a melanocortin that is derived from proopiomelanocortin (POMC) proteolytic cleavage by PCSK1. MC2R is the specific receptor for ACTH in the adrenal gland where it leads to the production and release of steroids. MC2R requires the expression of MRAP, which interacts with MC2R and facilitates MC2R cell surface expression. The classical signaling pathway for MCRs passes through G-proteins, which activate adenylate cyclase (ADCY), increase intracellular cAMP production, and activate PKA. The stimulation of all MCRs leads to the activation of MAPK3/MAPK1. The activation of PKA has been shown to counteract Ras/RAF1/ MAPK3/MAPK1 signaling pathway. RAPGEF4/RAPGEF3 is a RAP1A/RAP2A-specific GEF, which translates cAMP signal to MAPKs via BRAF pathway in a PKA- and Ras-independent manner. BRAF is the dominant molecular switch that permits differential cAMP-dependent regulation of MAPKs.    Outcome effects: Glucocorticoids are produced in the adrenal gland and exert potent anti-inflammatory effects by inhibiting IL2 gene expression by lymphocytes, IL1 gene expression by macrophages, MHC class II antigen expression, and phospholipase A2 activity. Glucocorticoids activate the expression of TSC22D3, a protein whose mechanism of action is unclear, but it is known that it interacts with and inhibits the function of NF-kB, Jun/Fos, and ANXA1. Furthermore, glucocorticoids have been shown to inhibit T-cell binding to endothelial cells by modulating the expression of adhesion molecules." EXACT []

