xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The production of immunosuppressing neuropeptides, such as alpha melanocyte stimulating hormone (aMSH), may play an essential role in the pathogenesis of immune and inflammatory reactions. In addition to the pituitary gland in the brain, aMSH is synthesized in several tissues including skin, placenta, testis, ovary, kidney, and adrenal gland.    Signaling description: aMSH and ACTH neuropeptides are derived from proopiomelanocortin (POMC) proteolytic cleavage. PCSK2, PCSK1, and CPE are necessary to produce mature aMSH. aMSH modulates immune reactions through the activation of MC1R signaling. MC1R, specific for aMSH and ACTH, is expressed in the skin on keratinocytes, dendritic cells, macrophages, and endothelial cells. aMSH suppresses the proinflammatory activity of macrophages. In macrophages, aMSH inhibits LPS-stimulated NO generation and the production of TNF and IL1.    Outcome effects: aMSH engagement of MC1R activates cAMP-dependent PKA, which mediates intracellular signaling leading to the suppression of p38 MAPK and NF-kB activation. aMSH most likely suppresses NF-kB through IRAK1/IRAK3 interactions in activated macrophages. It is also known that MC1R expression requires aMSH inhibition effects on the inflammatory activity of macrophages. aMSH also downregulates CD14 on the surface of macrophages, however the exact mechanism is currently unknown. In dendritic cells, aMSH downregulates the expression of co-stimulatory molecules such as CD86 and CD40, but induces the production of suppressor factor IL10 via poorly understood signaling. The activation of RAPGEF4/RAPGEF3, which translates cAMP signal to MAPK3/MAPK1 via BRAF pathway in a PKA-independent manner, is likely essential for the induction of IL10 synthesis. aMSH also inhibits NF-kB activation not only in macrophages, but also in a T-cell line and human neutrophils. Thus, aMSH suppresses systemic acute-phase response to infection. On endothelial cells, aMSH is capable of down- regulating LPS-induced expression of adhesion molecules such as VCAM1 and SELE.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The production of immunosuppressing neuropeptides, such as alpha melanocyte stimulating hormone (aMSH), may play an essential role in the pathogenesis of immune and inflammatory reactions. In addition to the pituitary gland in the brain, aMSH is synthesized in several tissues including skin, placenta, testis, ovary, kidney, and adrenal gland.    Signaling description: aMSH and ACTH neuropeptides are derived from proopiomelanocortin (POMC) proteolytic cleavage. PCSK2, PCSK1, and CPE are necessary to produce mature aMSH. aMSH modulates immune reactions through the activation of MC1R signaling. MC1R, specific for aMSH and ACTH, is expressed in the skin on keratinocytes, dendritic cells, macrophages, and endothelial cells. aMSH suppresses the proinflammatory activity of macrophages. In macrophages, aMSH inhibits LPS-stimulated NO generation and the production of TNF and IL1.    Outcome effects: aMSH engagement of MC1R activates cAMP-dependent PKA, which mediates intracellular signaling leading to the suppression of p38 MAPK and NF-kB activation. aMSH most likely suppresses NF-kB through IRAK1/IRAK3 interactions in activated macrophages. It is also known that MC1R expression requires aMSH inhibition effects on the inflammatory activity of macrophages. aMSH also downregulates CD14 on the surface of macrophages, however the exact mechanism is currently unknown. In dendritic cells, aMSH downregulates the expression of co-stimulatory molecules such as CD86 and CD40, but induces the production of suppressor factor IL10 via poorly understood signaling. The activation of RAPGEF4/RAPGEF3, which translates cAMP signal to MAPK3/MAPK1 via BRAF pathway in a PKA-independent manner, is likely essential for the induction of IL10 synthesis. aMSH also inhibits NF-kB activation not only in macrophages, but also in a T-cell line and human neutrophils. Thus, aMSH suppresses systemic acute-phase response to infection. On endothelial cells, aMSH is capable of down- regulating LPS-induced expression of adhesion molecules such as VCAM1 and SELE.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red, and proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

