xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Annexins are a group of proteins that contain a conserved structural element, known as the annexin repeat, and are capable of binding to negatively charged phospholipids in a Ca2+ -dependent manner. ANXA1 is the central annexin involved in anti-inflammatory responses.    Signaling description: In resting polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMN), approximately 60% of intracellular ANXA1 is localized in gelatinase and azurophilic granules. PMN adhesion to endothelial cell monolayers provokes the rapid mobilization of ANXA1 onto the cell surface. The engagement of FPR2/ lipoxin A4 by selective agonists may induce ANXA1 phosphorylation and mobilization in human PMNs. The mobilization of ANXA1 is downstream of PKC activation. Glucocorticoids regulate the synthesis and function of ANXA1 possibly through non-genomic processes, depending on the cell type and the time of induction. Interestingly, studies on the cytokine responsiveness of the ANXA1 promoter have revealed that the gene is induced by IL6.    Outcome effects: ANXA1 and its derived peptides display potent proinflammatory activity and activate neutrophils in vitro by acting on FPR1. In contrast, other studies have shown that these peptides use FPR2 for their anti-inflammatory actions. ANXA1 was the first identified inhibitor of phospholipase A2. ANXA1 binds to F-actin and also interacts with profilin, a G-actin binding protein and regulator of actin polymerization. ANXA5 affects the mechanisms of coagulation and has been observed to relocate to the membrane cytoskeleton following platelet activation. This relocation appears to involve both binding to the plasma membrane and to a specific actin isoform, ACTG1. This is paralleled by an association with the platelet membrane of cytosolic phospholipase A2 which suggests an interaction between this phospholipase and ANXA5. The expression of ANXA2 on the cell surface is believed to serve as a receptor for plasminogen (PLG), which functions to produce plasmin. The production of plasmin counteracts fibrinolysis by promoting the degradation of fibrin."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Annexins are a group of proteins that contain a conserved structural element, known as the annexin repeat, and are capable of binding to negatively charged phospholipids in a Ca2+ -dependent manner. ANXA1 is the central annexin involved in anti-inflammatory responses.    Signaling description: In resting polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMN), approximately 60% of intracellular ANXA1 is localized in gelatinase and azurophilic granules. PMN adhesion to endothelial cell monolayers provokes the rapid mobilization of ANXA1 onto the cell surface. The engagement of FPR2/ lipoxin A4 by selective agonists may induce ANXA1 phosphorylation and mobilization in human PMNs. The mobilization of ANXA1 is downstream of PKC activation. Glucocorticoids regulate the synthesis and function of ANXA1 possibly through non-genomic processes, depending on the cell type and the time of induction. Interestingly, studies on the cytokine responsiveness of the ANXA1 promoter have revealed that the gene is induced by IL6.    Outcome effects: ANXA1 and its derived peptides display potent proinflammatory activity and activate neutrophils in vitro by acting on FPR1. In contrast, other studies have shown that these peptides use FPR2 for their anti-inflammatory actions. ANXA1 was the first identified inhibitor of phospholipase A2. ANXA1 binds to F-actin and also interacts with profilin, a G-actin binding protein and regulator of actin polymerization. ANXA5 affects the mechanisms of coagulation and has been observed to relocate to the membrane cytoskeleton following platelet activation. This relocation appears to involve both binding to the plasma membrane and to a specific actin isoform, ACTG1. This is paralleled by an association with the platelet membrane of cytosolic phospholipase A2 which suggests an interaction between this phospholipase and ANXA5. The expression of ANXA2 on the cell surface is believed to serve as a receptor for plasminogen (PLG), which functions to produce plasmin. The production of plasmin counteracts fibrinolysis by promoting the degradation of fibrin." EXACT []

