xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Lipid-derived mediators such as lipoxins, resolvins, and protectins play an important role in the resolution of inflammation. The process of the resolution of inflammation is now considered an active process, where antagonists of proinflammatory mediators lead to phagocytosis of cellular debris and blockage of leukocyte infiltration. During extended inflammation, polymorphonuclear neutrophils (PMNs) may eventually cause trauma and tissue damage through infiltration into tissues.    Signaling description: Lipoxins and resolvins turn off the influx of neutrophils while the influx of monocytes and macrophages is further promoted. Lipoxins, such as lipoxin A4 and lipoxin B4, are short-lived bioactive lipid mediators generated from arachidonic acid which has anti-inflammatory effects. The negative regulators of the inflammatory response such as IL4, IL13, and proinflammatory cytokines are crucial to the formation of lipoxins. Lipoxins are rapidly biosynthesized in response to stimuli, act locally, and then are rapidly inactivated enzymatically by HPGD. There are two main pathways of lipoxin signaling that require transcellular interactions. One pathway involves platelet-leukocyte (PMN) interactions. Human platelets do not produce lipoxins on their own, but become a major source of lipoxins when platelet-PMN adhesion occurs. In neutrophils, ALOX5 converts arachidonic acid to LTA4, which is then released and further transformed by adherent platelets to lipoxin A4 via ALOX12. A second pathway is initiated at the epithelial mucosal surfaces by arachidonate 15-lipoxygenase that converts arachidonic acid to 15(S)-HPETE. Neutrophils take up 15(S)-HPETE and convert it to lipoxins by ALOX5. Lipoxin B4 is a positional isomer of lipoxin A4. This event not only leads to lipoxin biosynthesis, but also reduces LTA4 formation due to competition effects. The reduction of leukotriene production is the first and most rapid method by which lipoxins reduce inflammation. A third major pathway for lipoxin generation involves aspirin, PTGS2, and ALOX5. Endothelial and epithelial cells express PTGS2 in response to diverse stimuli such as cytokines, hypoxia, and bacterial infections. The action of aspirin involves the inhibition of PTGS2, biosynthesis of prostaglandin and thromboxane, and the generation of endogenous anti-inflammatory aspirin-triggered lipoxins. When aspirin is administered, PTGS2 is irreversibly acetylated, but remains active and changes their products from intermediates for prostaglandin and thromboxane to precursors for aspirin-triggered lipoxin A, specifically 15(R)-HPETE. This precursor carries a carbon-15 alcohol in the R configuration and is rapidly converted by ALOX5 in activated PMN to 15-epimeric-lipoxin A4 (ATL), which carries the C-15 hydroxyl group in the R configuration rather than native 15S-lipoxin A4. ATL, with the 15-hydroxyl group in the R configuration, has been shown to directly bind and signal with ALX with more potent action than the native 15S-lipoxin A4 in vitro and in vivo. ALX is the first cloned and identified lipoxygenase-derived eicosanoid receptor with cell type-specific signaling pathways. In addition, resolvins are a family of potent lipid mediators derived from omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids from both eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA). The other family of bioactive chemical signals from DHA such as docosanoids, which possess a conjugated triene double-bond system in their structure, is denoted protectins. EPA-series resolvin synthesis is dependent on aspirin acetylation of PTGS2 in vascular endothelial cells to produce 18(R)-HPEPE, which is further converted to resolvin E1 via ALOX5 in neutrophils. DHA is converted to 17R resolvins by a similar aspirin-triggered PTGS2 mechanism. In the absence of aspirin, PTGS2 in human microvascular endothelial cells converts DHA to 13S-hydroxy-DHA. In an alternative reaction in the absence of aspirin, arachidonate 15-lipoxygenase"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Lipid-derived mediators such as lipoxins, resolvins, and protectins play an important role in the resolution of inflammation. The process of the resolution of inflammation is now considered an active process, where antagonists of proinflammatory mediators lead to phagocytosis of cellular debris and blockage of leukocyte infiltration. During extended inflammation, polymorphonuclear neutrophils (PMNs) may eventually cause trauma and tissue damage through infiltration into tissues.    Signaling description: Lipoxins and resolvins turn off the influx of neutrophils while the influx of monocytes and macrophages is further promoted. Lipoxins, such as lipoxin A4 and lipoxin B4, are short-lived bioactive lipid mediators generated from arachidonic acid which has anti-inflammatory effects. The negative regulators of the inflammatory response such as IL4, IL13, and proinflammatory cytokines are crucial to the formation of lipoxins. Lipoxins are rapidly biosynthesized in response to stimuli, act locally, and then are rapidly inactivated enzymatically by HPGD. There are two main pathways of lipoxin signaling that require transcellular interactions. One pathway involves platelet-leukocyte (PMN) interactions. Human platelets do not produce lipoxins on their own, but become a major source of lipoxins when platelet-PMN adhesion occurs. In neutrophils, ALOX5 converts arachidonic acid to LTA4, which is then released and further transformed by adherent platelets to lipoxin A4 via ALOX12. A second pathway is initiated at the epithelial mucosal surfaces by arachidonate 15-lipoxygenase that converts arachidonic acid to 15(S)-HPETE. Neutrophils take up 15(S)-HPETE and convert it to lipoxins by ALOX5. Lipoxin B4 is a positional isomer of lipoxin A4. This event not only leads to lipoxin biosynthesis, but also reduces LTA4 formation due to competition effects. The reduction of leukotriene production is the first and most rapid method by which lipoxins reduce inflammation. A third major pathway for lipoxin generation involves aspirin, PTGS2, and ALOX5. Endothelial and epithelial cells express PTGS2 in response to diverse stimuli such as cytokines, hypoxia, and bacterial infections. The action of aspirin involves the inhibition of PTGS2, biosynthesis of prostaglandin and thromboxane, and the generation of endogenous anti-inflammatory aspirin-triggered lipoxins. When aspirin is administered, PTGS2 is irreversibly acetylated, but remains active and changes their products from intermediates for prostaglandin and thromboxane to precursors for aspirin-triggered lipoxin A, specifically 15(R)-HPETE. This precursor carries a carbon-15 alcohol in the R configuration and is rapidly converted by ALOX5 in activated PMN to 15-epimeric-lipoxin A4 (ATL), which carries the C-15 hydroxyl group in the R configuration rather than native 15S-lipoxin A4. ATL, with the 15-hydroxyl group in the R configuration, has been shown to directly bind and signal with ALX with more potent action than the native 15S-lipoxin A4 in vitro and in vivo. ALX is the first cloned and identified lipoxygenase-derived eicosanoid receptor with cell type-specific signaling pathways. In addition, resolvins are a family of potent lipid mediators derived from omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids from both eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA). The other family of bioactive chemical signals from DHA such as docosanoids, which possess a conjugated triene double-bond system in their structure, is denoted protectins. EPA-series resolvin synthesis is dependent on aspirin acetylation of PTGS2 in vascular endothelial cells to produce 18(R)-HPEPE, which is further converted to resolvin E1 via ALOX5 in neutrophils. DHA is converted to 17R resolvins by a similar aspirin-triggered PTGS2 mechanism. In the absence of aspirin, PTGS2 in human microvascular endothelial cells converts DHA to 13S-hydroxy-DHA. In an alternative reaction in the absence of aspirin, arachidonate 15-lipoxygenase" EXACT []

