xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Protein oxidation refers to the oxidative damage which occurs as a result of direct or indirect reactions of proteins with reactive oxygen species (ROS). The pathology of oxidative damage is characterized with changes in the protein function, unfolding of the protein, formation of a variety of various toxicity products. According to the \"oxidative stress theory of aging\", oxidative stress initiates changes associated with aging and determine the maximum longevity (Reinald Pamplona, 2008). The aging of proteome in terms of the \"oxidative stress theory\" may be caused by a disturbed balance between the production of oxidized proteins and their clearance, and the accumulation of damaged, oxidized, and aggregated proteins such as lipofuscin.    Signaling description: ROS harms the cell directly by damaging the amino acids (protein side chains) or protein backbones and indirectly by lipid peroxidation products. For example, methionine sulfoxide is the major amino acid oxidation product, which results from methionine oxidation. Oxidized proteins could be repaired by specific enzymes such as TXN or peptide-methionine (S)-S-oxide reductase. The 20S core proteasome is involved in the proteolic degradation of oxidized proteins via the ubiquitin-proteasome system (UPS). Oxidative stress and an excessive amount of oxidized proteins can lead to UPS dysfunction. Oxidized proteins which were not eliminated or repaired may form aggregates and accumulate within the cell at different compartments. Such protein aggregates are insoluble and nondegradable. Lipofuscin is a highly oxidized aggregate formed by covalently cross-linked proteins (30-70%) and lipids (20-50%). Lipofuscin accumulates in the lysosomal lumen and the cytosol. The \"mitochondrial-lysosomal axis theory of aging\" (Brunk and Terman) suggests that lipofuscin is formed in lysosomes as result of lysosomal membrane permeabilization (LMP); in addition, highly reactive ROS are generated within lysosomes.     Outcome effects: Aggregates of oxidized proteins inhibit the proteasomal system which, in turn leads to further protein aggregation. Lipofuscin inhibits both proteasomal and lysosomal-autophagic degradation pathways. Insoluble aggregations of proteins impair the cell function and may lead to the cell senescence. Lipofuscin may be involved in lysosomal membrane degradation, release of the lysosomal content and cell death."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Protein oxidation refers to the oxidative damage which occurs as a result of direct or indirect reactions of proteins with reactive oxygen species (ROS). The pathology of oxidative damage is characterized with changes in the protein function, unfolding of the protein, formation of a variety of various toxicity products. According to the \"oxidative stress theory of aging\", oxidative stress initiates changes associated with aging and determine the maximum longevity (Reinald Pamplona, 2008). The aging of proteome in terms of the \"oxidative stress theory\" may be caused by a disturbed balance between the production of oxidized proteins and their clearance, and the accumulation of damaged, oxidized, and aggregated proteins such as lipofuscin.    Signaling description: ROS harms the cell directly by damaging the amino acids (protein side chains) or protein backbones and indirectly by lipid peroxidation products. For example, methionine sulfoxide is the major amino acid oxidation product, which results from methionine oxidation. Oxidized proteins could be repaired by specific enzymes such as TXN or peptide-methionine (S)-S-oxide reductase. The 20S core proteasome is involved in the proteolic degradation of oxidized proteins via the ubiquitin-proteasome system (UPS). Oxidative stress and an excessive amount of oxidized proteins can lead to UPS dysfunction. Oxidized proteins which were not eliminated or repaired may form aggregates and accumulate within the cell at different compartments. Such protein aggregates are insoluble and nondegradable. Lipofuscin is a highly oxidized aggregate formed by covalently cross-linked proteins (30-70%) and lipids (20-50%). Lipofuscin accumulates in the lysosomal lumen and the cytosol. The \"mitochondrial-lysosomal axis theory of aging\" (Brunk and Terman) suggests that lipofuscin is formed in lysosomes as result of lysosomal membrane permeabilization (LMP); in addition, highly reactive ROS are generated within lysosomes.     Outcome effects: Aggregates of oxidized proteins inhibit the proteasomal system which, in turn leads to further protein aggregation. Lipofuscin inhibits both proteasomal and lysosomal-autophagic degradation pathways. Insoluble aggregations of proteins impair the cell function and may lead to the cell senescence. Lipofuscin may be involved in lysosomal membrane degradation, release of the lysosomal content and cell death." EXACT []

