xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Extracellular Matrix (ECM) Reformation in aging is a changing of quantitative and structural characteristics of proteins secreted from cells. Pathology of extracellular matrix reformation in aging is characterized with reduction in the collagenous content and progressive degradation of the elastic fibers in derma; forming of insoluble proteins and cross-links between damaged and modified proteins; increase of connective tissue stiffness. Cross-links accumulation is caused with high frequency in such long-lived proteins as collagen by oxidative reactions with carbohydrates which lead to the formation of advanced glycation end-products (AGEs). Also hyperglycemia influences the formation of AGEs.    Signaling description: The glycation reaction between a carbohydrate (e.g. glucose) - and a protein amino group (e.g., a collagen lysine side-chain) forms unstable Schiff base. The unstable Schiff base becomes a stable intermediate keto amine (Amadori product). After several months or years chemical reactions finish with generation of metabolic by-products of glycolysis, which interact with ECM and form AGEs. Glucosepane (a lysinearginine crosslink) is the most abundant AGEs in collagen tissues. LOX is needed for regulation the formation of stable inter-molecular crosslinks within individual type-I collagen fibrils. AGEs can bind to different types of receptors on cell surfaces and altered their signaling.    Outcome effects: How AGEs and collagen-related altered extracellular matrix mechanics exactly links with increases tissue stiffness in aging is poorly understood. AGEs leads to mechanical cell damage and the impairment of the interaction of collagen with other molecules such as LOX and cell integrins. Such AGEs as carboxyethyllysine (CEL), carboxymethyllysine (CML), glyoxal lysine dimers (GOLD), methylglyoxal lysine dimers (MOLD) and pentosidine are increased in tissue collagen with age. CEL and CML could also be generated during peroxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids in phospholipids.    Animal data: Experiments data obtained mostly on animal models.    Highlighted proteins: Small molecules with red highlights have increased quantity in aged organisms. For references see \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cell Processes Associated with Aging\" pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Extracellular Matrix (ECM) Reformation in aging is a changing of quantitative and structural characteristics of proteins secreted from cells. Pathology of extracellular matrix reformation in aging is characterized with reduction in the collagenous content and progressive degradation of the elastic fibers in derma; forming of insoluble proteins and cross-links between damaged and modified proteins; increase of connective tissue stiffness. Cross-links accumulation is caused with high frequency in such long-lived proteins as collagen by oxidative reactions with carbohydrates which lead to the formation of advanced glycation end-products (AGEs). Also hyperglycemia influences the formation of AGEs.    Signaling description: The glycation reaction between a carbohydrate (e.g. glucose) - and a protein amino group (e.g., a collagen lysine side-chain) forms unstable Schiff base. The unstable Schiff base becomes a stable intermediate keto amine (Amadori product). After several months or years chemical reactions finish with generation of metabolic by-products of glycolysis, which interact with ECM and form AGEs. Glucosepane (a lysinearginine crosslink) is the most abundant AGEs in collagen tissues. LOX is needed for regulation the formation of stable inter-molecular crosslinks within individual type-I collagen fibrils. AGEs can bind to different types of receptors on cell surfaces and altered their signaling.    Outcome effects: How AGEs and collagen-related altered extracellular matrix mechanics exactly links with increases tissue stiffness in aging is poorly understood. AGEs leads to mechanical cell damage and the impairment of the interaction of collagen with other molecules such as LOX and cell integrins. Such AGEs as carboxyethyllysine (CEL), carboxymethyllysine (CML), glyoxal lysine dimers (GOLD), methylglyoxal lysine dimers (MOLD) and pentosidine are increased in tissue collagen with age. CEL and CML could also be generated during peroxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids in phospholipids.    Animal data: Experiments data obtained mostly on animal models.    Highlighted proteins: Small molecules with red highlights have increased quantity in aged organisms. For references see \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Cell Processes Associated with Aging\" pathway." EXACT []

