xref: PS:Pathway_Author "M. Zharkova  ORCID:0000-0001-8706-9411"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: M. Zharkova  ORCID:0000-0001-8706-9411" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-bae9e3a9-7998-48a2-a0a0-775982d05586"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-bae9e3a9-7998-48a2-a0a0-775982d05586" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "neuron"
synonym: "CellType: neuron" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "brain"
xref: PS:Organ "hypothalamus"
synonym: "Organ: brain" EXACT []
synonym: "Organ: hypothalamus" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "nervous system"
synonym: "Organ_System: nervous system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is produced by parvocellular neuroendocrine cells (neurons) of the hypothalamus and released into the primary capillary plexus of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system, where it regulates the production of ACTH and MSHs.    Signaling description: Pathways activating CRH release are stress dependent: reactive responses to homeostatic disruption frequently involve direct noradrenergic or peptidergic drive of PVN neurons. Norepinephrine/epinephrine, ADCYAP1 (adenylate cyclase-activating polypeptide) and beta-endorphin bind with ADRA1B, ADCYAP1R1 and OPRD1 and cause PLCB1/DAG/PKC/MAPKs/CREB1 signaling activation. Serotonin and ADCYAP1 stimulates ADCY/cAMP/PKA/CREB1 signaling via HTR4 and ADCYAP1R1. CREB1 promotes CRH expression. Glutamate and GABA through GRIN1 and GABRA1 regulate intracellular levels of Ca2+ and Cl- ions required for neuron function. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: CRH secretion is regulated by catecholamines, serotonin and several other biologically active substances.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is produced by parvocellular neuroendocrine cells (neurons) of the hypothalamus and released into the primary capillary plexus of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system, where it regulates the production of ACTH and MSHs.    Signaling description: Pathways activating CRH release are stress dependent: reactive responses to homeostatic disruption frequently involve direct noradrenergic or peptidergic drive of PVN neurons. Norepinephrine/epinephrine, ADCYAP1 (adenylate cyclase-activating polypeptide) and beta-endorphin bind with ADRA1B, ADCYAP1R1 and OPRD1 and cause PLCB1/DAG/PKC/MAPKs/CREB1 signaling activation. Serotonin and ADCYAP1 stimulates ADCY/cAMP/PKA/CREB1 signaling via HTR4 and ADCYAP1R1. CREB1 promotes CRH expression. Glutamate and GABA through GRIN1 and GABRA1 regulate intracellular levels of Ca2+ and Cl- ions required for neuron function. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: CRH secretion is regulated by catecholamines, serotonin and several other biologically active substances.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments" EXACT []

