[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-33da5579-4ae8-4ee8-baaf-ef84b7cfedab
name: Natural Killer Cell Activation through C-Type Lectin-like Receptors
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:natural_killer_cell_receptors_signaling ! Natural killer cell receptors signaling
is_a: urn:agi-folder:n ! N
xref: PS:Description "Natural killer (NK) cells can specifically destroy infected and transformed cells. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Natural killer (NK) cells can specifically destroy infected and transformed cells. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-33da5579-4ae8-4ee8-baaf-ef84b7cfedab"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-33da5579-4ae8-4ee8-baaf-ef84b7cfedab" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "natural killer cell"
synonym: "CellType: natural killer cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Natural killer (NK) cells can specifically destroy infected and transformed cells. The activation of NK cells depends on stimulatory and inhibitory receptors, whose type and copy number vary from cell to cell. NK stimulatory receptors belong to lectin-like type II transmembrane proteins that have a C-type lectin domain.    Signaling description: The C-type lectin superfamily consists of a disulfide-linked heterodimer of CD94 glycoprotein (KLRD1) and one member of NKG2-D (KLRK1). KLRK1 acts as a receptor for the recognition of MHC class I HLA molecules expressed by NK cells and some cytotoxic T-cells. Activated KLRK1 induces the phosphorylation of VAV1, resulting in the actin-dependent clustering of activator receptors and their recruitment to lipid rafts. This leads to more VAV1 phosphorylation, inducing a positive feedback loop, which ultimately results in NK cell cytotoxicity.    Outcome effects: The YINM motif of KLRK1 adaptor, HCST is phosphorylated by Src family kinases (LCK) and can recruit PI3K and VAV1 via GRB2. These proteins activate two main NK cell-mediated responses which are transcription of FAS receptor ligands and the formation of immunological synapses with granules of perforins (PRF1) and granzymes. FAS, PRF1, and granzymes result in the death of target cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Natural killer (NK) cells can specifically destroy infected and transformed cells. The activation of NK cells depends on stimulatory and inhibitory receptors, whose type and copy number vary from cell to cell. NK stimulatory receptors belong to lectin-like type II transmembrane proteins that have a C-type lectin domain.    Signaling description: The C-type lectin superfamily consists of a disulfide-linked heterodimer of CD94 glycoprotein (KLRD1) and one member of NKG2-D (KLRK1). KLRK1 acts as a receptor for the recognition of MHC class I HLA molecules expressed by NK cells and some cytotoxic T-cells. Activated KLRK1 induces the phosphorylation of VAV1, resulting in the actin-dependent clustering of activator receptors and their recruitment to lipid rafts. This leads to more VAV1 phosphorylation, inducing a positive feedback loop, which ultimately results in NK cell cytotoxicity.    Outcome effects: The YINM motif of KLRK1 adaptor, HCST is phosphorylated by Src family kinases (LCK) and can recruit PI3K and VAV1 via GRB2. These proteins activate two main NK cell-mediated responses which are transcription of FAS receptor ligands and the formation of immunological synapses with granules of perforins (PRF1) and granzymes. FAS, PRF1, and granzymes result in the death of target cells." EXACT []

