xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Natural killer (NK) cells can specifically destroy infected and transformed cells. The activation of NK cells depends of stimulatory and inhibitory receptors whose type and copy number vary from cell to cell.    Signaling description: A group of inhibitory receptors called killer-cell immunoglobulin-like receptors (KIRs) belong to the immunoglobulin superfamily (IgSF) proteins with either two (KIR2D) or three (KIR3D) extracellular immunoglobulin domains. NK cell inhibitory receptors via their PTPN11 and PTPN6 cytoplasmic adaptors block intracellular signaling, which is necessary for NK cell function. VAV signaling and actin remodeling are also blocked through dephosphorylation by PTPN11 and PTPN6.    Outcome effects: If the activity of inhibitory receptors in NK cells is higher than the activity of stimulatory receptors, the production of cytotoxic perforins (PRF1), granzymes, and cytokines is diminished."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Natural killer (NK) cells can specifically destroy infected and transformed cells. The activation of NK cells depends of stimulatory and inhibitory receptors whose type and copy number vary from cell to cell.    Signaling description: A group of inhibitory receptors called killer-cell immunoglobulin-like receptors (KIRs) belong to the immunoglobulin superfamily (IgSF) proteins with either two (KIR2D) or three (KIR3D) extracellular immunoglobulin domains. NK cell inhibitory receptors via their PTPN11 and PTPN6 cytoplasmic adaptors block intracellular signaling, which is necessary for NK cell function. VAV signaling and actin remodeling are also blocked through dephosphorylation by PTPN11 and PTPN6.    Outcome effects: If the activity of inhibitory receptors in NK cells is higher than the activity of stimulatory receptors, the production of cytotoxic perforins (PRF1), granzymes, and cytokines is diminished." EXACT []

