xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: Neutrophils are released into the bloodstream, where they circulate and die by apoptosis (half-life ~7 hours) or respond to signals of localized infection. They migrate through vascular endothelial walls into tight tissue spaces seeking out the infectious agents for phagocytosis and destruction. Mature neutrophils in the blood differ significantly from those in the focus of inflammation in terms of their membrane receptor pattern and the state of activation of their intracellular signaling pathways. The activation extent can vary from priming up to complete activation. A high level of neutrophil activation leads to the destruction of pathogens due to degranulation and the production of large amounts of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. Priming is defined as the enhancement or amplification of neutrophil respiratory burst in response to a given activating stimulus following exposure to the priming agent. Neutrophil degranulation and activation of NAD(P)H oxidase occurs only in neutrophils that have been primed before activation. The factors activating neutrophil priming do not cause the release of ROS and the content of granules directly, but trigger signaling cascades required for the initiation of these processes. These factors can be divided into early-phase cytokines including TNF, IL2, and TLR ligands or late-phase chemoattractants and growth factors including IL8, LTB4 and CSF3. Some priming agents, including LTB4 and platelet-activating factor, cause an immediate and rapid rise in intracellular Ca2+. Other priming agents, such as TNF, CSF3, and LPS, are associated with less rapid increases in intracellular Ca2+ and require longer incubation periods to achieve the priming effect. The mechanism of regulating priming is poorly understood. It is possible that priming is connected to the transcriptional regulation of membrane receptors and control of the synthesis of small molecules such as arachidonic acid derivatives.    Outcome effects: Neutrophils in the priming state are characterized by the expression of ITGAM, SELL, FPR1, CXCR2, C5AR1, FASLG, CD14, and other receptors. The precise signaling pathways of neutrophil priming are not well known. It is possible that TNF promotes firm neutrophil adhesion through ITGAM upregulation on the neutrophil surface through the MAPK14 pathway. In addition, CSF3 enhances the expression of ITGB2. CSF3 has been shown to enhance the expression of PCNA in mature neutrophils, which in turn supports the strategy of neutrophil survival. Lastly, IL4 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that inhibits the expression of IL8 in neutrophils.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: Neutrophils are released into the bloodstream, where they circulate and die by apoptosis (half-life ~7 hours) or respond to signals of localized infection. They migrate through vascular endothelial walls into tight tissue spaces seeking out the infectious agents for phagocytosis and destruction. Mature neutrophils in the blood differ significantly from those in the focus of inflammation in terms of their membrane receptor pattern and the state of activation of their intracellular signaling pathways. The activation extent can vary from priming up to complete activation. A high level of neutrophil activation leads to the destruction of pathogens due to degranulation and the production of large amounts of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. Priming is defined as the enhancement or amplification of neutrophil respiratory burst in response to a given activating stimulus following exposure to the priming agent. Neutrophil degranulation and activation of NAD(P)H oxidase occurs only in neutrophils that have been primed before activation. The factors activating neutrophil priming do not cause the release of ROS and the content of granules directly, but trigger signaling cascades required for the initiation of these processes. These factors can be divided into early-phase cytokines including TNF, IL2, and TLR ligands or late-phase chemoattractants and growth factors including IL8, LTB4 and CSF3. Some priming agents, including LTB4 and platelet-activating factor, cause an immediate and rapid rise in intracellular Ca2+. Other priming agents, such as TNF, CSF3, and LPS, are associated with less rapid increases in intracellular Ca2+ and require longer incubation periods to achieve the priming effect. The mechanism of regulating priming is poorly understood. It is possible that priming is connected to the transcriptional regulation of membrane receptors and control of the synthesis of small molecules such as arachidonic acid derivatives.    Outcome effects: Neutrophils in the priming state are characterized by the expression of ITGAM, SELL, FPR1, CXCR2, C5AR1, FASLG, CD14, and other receptors. The precise signaling pathways of neutrophil priming are not well known. It is possible that TNF promotes firm neutrophil adhesion through ITGAM upregulation on the neutrophil surface through the MAPK14 pathway. In addition, CSF3 enhances the expression of ITGB2. CSF3 has been shown to enhance the expression of PCNA in mature neutrophils, which in turn supports the strategy of neutrophil survival. Lastly, IL4 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that inhibits the expression of IL8 in neutrophils.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

