xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: During phagocytosis, neutrophils engulf and take up microbes into specialized compartments known as phagosomes in a receptor-mediated, clathrin-independent process. Neutrophils absorb pathogens tagged with opsonins, which are molecules that bind the cell surface and facilitate phagocytosis. One of the main opsonins, IgG, promotes neutrophil phagocytosis through the activation of Fc-gamma receptors. Unlike macrophages, neutrophils express a low affinity to FCGR3B and FCGR2A receptors. FCGR-dependent phagocytosis is associated with pseudopod extension. When activated by IgG, these receptors facilitate the polymerization of actin filaments, membrane extension outward to surround the particle, and particle engulfment. The initial tyrosine phosphorylation event is carried out by HCK and LYN. Tyrosine kinase SYK is then recruited to the phosphorylated ITAM domain. Active SYK then transmits downstream signals leading to actin polymerization and particle internalization.    Outcome effects: The downstream effectors of SYK are PI3K and PLCG1. Further, VAV1 and motor proteins are involved in the polymerization of actin and phagosome formation. Various members of the Rho family, including RAC1, CDC42, and RHOA, are activated by VAV1. CDC42 participates in the formation of filopodia while RAC1 and RHOA stimulate membrane ruffling. VCL and PXN can also be activated via PI3K/ARF6 signaling and can participate in the organization of polymerized microfilaments. Occasionally, myosin participates in the process of pseudopod extension and phagosome formation. All of these events culminate in actin nucleation, reorganization, and actin-dependent extension of the plasma membrane around the particle. The synthesis of arachidonic acid derivatives also stimulates membrane motility during phagocytosis. The pathogen, absorbed by neutrophils, undergoes respiratory burst in phagolysosomes. In addition, the synthesis of ROS can be activated as the result of the FCGR-related cascade. In addition, the fusion of neutrophil granules with a phagosome results in the formation of a phagolysosome and allows for the assembly of the NAD(P)H oxidase complex, which is a transmembrane multi-protein complex that generates superoxide anions into the lumen of the phagolysosome. Superoxide is generated following the activation of CYBB in the NAD(P)H oxidase complex by RAC1 and RAC2 at the plasma membrane and as a result of the FCGR-related cascade."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: During phagocytosis, neutrophils engulf and take up microbes into specialized compartments known as phagosomes in a receptor-mediated, clathrin-independent process. Neutrophils absorb pathogens tagged with opsonins, which are molecules that bind the cell surface and facilitate phagocytosis. One of the main opsonins, IgG, promotes neutrophil phagocytosis through the activation of Fc-gamma receptors. Unlike macrophages, neutrophils express a low affinity to FCGR3B and FCGR2A receptors. FCGR-dependent phagocytosis is associated with pseudopod extension. When activated by IgG, these receptors facilitate the polymerization of actin filaments, membrane extension outward to surround the particle, and particle engulfment. The initial tyrosine phosphorylation event is carried out by HCK and LYN. Tyrosine kinase SYK is then recruited to the phosphorylated ITAM domain. Active SYK then transmits downstream signals leading to actin polymerization and particle internalization.    Outcome effects: The downstream effectors of SYK are PI3K and PLCG1. Further, VAV1 and motor proteins are involved in the polymerization of actin and phagosome formation. Various members of the Rho family, including RAC1, CDC42, and RHOA, are activated by VAV1. CDC42 participates in the formation of filopodia while RAC1 and RHOA stimulate membrane ruffling. VCL and PXN can also be activated via PI3K/ARF6 signaling and can participate in the organization of polymerized microfilaments. Occasionally, myosin participates in the process of pseudopod extension and phagosome formation. All of these events culminate in actin nucleation, reorganization, and actin-dependent extension of the plasma membrane around the particle. The synthesis of arachidonic acid derivatives also stimulates membrane motility during phagocytosis. The pathogen, absorbed by neutrophils, undergoes respiratory burst in phagolysosomes. In addition, the synthesis of ROS can be activated as the result of the FCGR-related cascade. In addition, the fusion of neutrophil granules with a phagosome results in the formation of a phagolysosome and allows for the assembly of the NAD(P)H oxidase complex, which is a transmembrane multi-protein complex that generates superoxide anions into the lumen of the phagolysosome. Superoxide is generated following the activation of CYBB in the NAD(P)H oxidase complex by RAC1 and RAC2 at the plasma membrane and as a result of the FCGR-related cascade." EXACT []

