synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: The cells at the center of inflammation synthesize a large amount of proinflammatory molecules that activate the chemotaxis of neutrophils to the inflammation site and their degranulation. Upon degranulation (exocytosis), neutrophils release the content of their granules, proteins, and small molecules which possess antibacterial properties and slow down the inflammatory reaction. There are four recognized types of neutrophil granules including primary or azurophilic granules, secondary or specific granules, and tertiary or gelatinase granules that contain few antimicrobials but store metalloproteases such as gelatinase. Finally, secretory granules carry cytokines and important phagocytic receptors that are exposed to the neutrophil surface upon priming and fusion of these granules with the plasma membrane. This pathway illustrates the general mechanism of neutrophil degranulation, featuring the example with FPR1. Like other neutrophil activators of bacterial origin, fMLP is normally associated with the degranulation of azurophilic and specific granules, which both primarily contain antimicrobial components. fMLP is a small molecule that is produced during the degradation of bacterial proteins. fMLP is a strong chemoattractant for neutrophils and can trigger release of the contents of neutrophil granules into the extracellular space. Activated FPR1 receptor induces actin polymerization, which leads to cell migration toward a concentration gradient or granule membrane and cell membrane ruffling (degranulation). FPR1, via G protein related signaling, activates Rho family GTPases such as RAC1, RAC2, RHOA, and CDC42, which cause the actin related protein 2/3 complex to promote actin polymerization. Actin cytoskeleton remodeling is responsible for the first phase of exocytosis, which involves the binding of granules to the membrane. RAC1 and RAC2 differentially regulate actin polymerization in neutrophils through uncapping and actin related protein 2/3 complex/CFL1, respectively. RAC2 selectively mobilizes primary granules. In addition, RAC2 is the preferential activator of NAD(P)H oxidase and promotes the respiratory burst of the target cell mediated by neutrophils. Three Src family members, including HCK, FGR, and LYN, have been implicated in the control of exocytosis of granule products from neutrophils by FPR1 stimulation. HCK is associated with the primary granule, whereas FGR becomes associated with secondary granule exocytosis. The selective recruitment of Src kinases indicates that different signaling pathways exist in neutrophils to induce the release of each granule population. It is likely that the process of granule translocation and exocytosis involves the activation and recruitment of several signaling molecules. The FPR1-dependent cascade leads to an increase in calcium (Ca2+) levels, which is required for degranulation.    Outcome effects: Increases in intracellular Ca2+ alone are sufficient to induce the release of many granule types in neutrophils. The specific target molecules for Ca2+ in neutrophil degranulation have yet to be identified. For instance, Ca2+ ions can activate proteins such as ANXA5, which results in remodeling of the actin cytoskeleton. A hierarchy of granule release exists in response to elevating concentrations of Ca2+. The order of release is secretory vesicles > tertiary granules > secondary granules > primary granules. The final step of exocytosis involves the formation structure of a fusion complex of membrane bound proteins (SNAP receptors) essential for vesicular docking and fusion. Neutrophils"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: The cells at the center of inflammation synthesize a large amount of proinflammatory molecules that activate the chemotaxis of neutrophils to the inflammation site and their degranulation. Upon degranulation (exocytosis), neutrophils release the content of their granules, proteins, and small molecules which possess antibacterial properties and slow down the inflammatory reaction. There are four recognized types of neutrophil granules including primary or azurophilic granules, secondary or specific granules, and tertiary or gelatinase granules that contain few antimicrobials but store metalloproteases such as gelatinase. Finally, secretory granules carry cytokines and important phagocytic receptors that are exposed to the neutrophil surface upon priming and fusion of these granules with the plasma membrane. This pathway illustrates the general mechanism of neutrophil degranulation, featuring the example with FPR1. Like other neutrophil activators of bacterial origin, fMLP is normally associated with the degranulation of azurophilic and specific granules, which both primarily contain antimicrobial components. fMLP is a small molecule that is produced during the degradation of bacterial proteins. fMLP is a strong chemoattractant for neutrophils and can trigger release of the contents of neutrophil granules into the extracellular space. Activated FPR1 receptor induces actin polymerization, which leads to cell migration toward a concentration gradient or granule membrane and cell membrane ruffling (degranulation). FPR1, via G protein related signaling, activates Rho family GTPases such as RAC1, RAC2, RHOA, and CDC42, which cause the actin related protein 2/3 complex to promote actin polymerization. Actin cytoskeleton remodeling is responsible for the first phase of exocytosis, which involves the binding of granules to the membrane. RAC1 and RAC2 differentially regulate actin polymerization in neutrophils through uncapping and actin related protein 2/3 complex/CFL1, respectively. RAC2 selectively mobilizes primary granules. In addition, RAC2 is the preferential activator of NAD(P)H oxidase and promotes the respiratory burst of the target cell mediated by neutrophils. Three Src family members, including HCK, FGR, and LYN, have been implicated in the control of exocytosis of granule products from neutrophils by FPR1 stimulation. HCK is associated with the primary granule, whereas FGR becomes associated with secondary granule exocytosis. The selective recruitment of Src kinases indicates that different signaling pathways exist in neutrophils to induce the release of each granule population. It is likely that the process of granule translocation and exocytosis involves the activation and recruitment of several signaling molecules. The FPR1-dependent cascade leads to an increase in calcium (Ca2+) levels, which is required for degranulation.    Outcome effects: Increases in intracellular Ca2+ alone are sufficient to induce the release of many granule types in neutrophils. The specific target molecules for Ca2+ in neutrophil degranulation have yet to be identified. For instance, Ca2+ ions can activate proteins such as ANXA5, which results in remodeling of the actin cytoskeleton. A hierarchy of granule release exists in response to elevating concentrations of Ca2+. The order of release is secretory vesicles > tertiary granules > secondary granules > primary granules. The final step of exocytosis involves the formation structure of a fusion complex of membrane bound proteins (SNAP receptors) essential for vesicular docking and fusion. Neutrophils" EXACT []

