xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes. The phagocytosis and degradation of pathogens is one of the main functions of neutrophils.    Signaling description: During phagocytosis, neutrophils engulf and take up microbes into specialized compartments known as phagosomes in a receptor-mediated, clathrin-independent process. Neutrophils absorb pathogens tagged with opsonins, which are molecules that attach to the cell surface and facilitate phagocytosis. Opsonin receptors initiate phagocytosis in neutrophils that are presented by FCGR3B, FCGR2A, and CR3. FCGR-mediated phagocytosis is associated with pseudopod extension. CR3-mediated phagocytosis occurs without pseudopod formation, but requires microtubules.    Outcome effects: VAV1-related signaling is active in pseudopod formation and inactive in complement signaling. Complement-dependent phagocytosis in neutrophils is determined by the activation of RASGRF1 signaling."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes. The phagocytosis and degradation of pathogens is one of the main functions of neutrophils.    Signaling description: During phagocytosis, neutrophils engulf and take up microbes into specialized compartments known as phagosomes in a receptor-mediated, clathrin-independent process. Neutrophils absorb pathogens tagged with opsonins, which are molecules that attach to the cell surface and facilitate phagocytosis. Opsonin receptors initiate phagocytosis in neutrophils that are presented by FCGR3B, FCGR2A, and CR3. FCGR-mediated phagocytosis is associated with pseudopod extension. CR3-mediated phagocytosis occurs without pseudopod formation, but requires microtubules.    Outcome effects: VAV1-related signaling is active in pseudopod formation and inactive in complement signaling. Complement-dependent phagocytosis in neutrophils is determined by the activation of RASGRF1 signaling." EXACT []

