xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: Neutrophils can move towards a chemoattractant gradient both in the bloodstream and by transmigration through the blood vessel wall. Chemoattractants are soluble molecules that guide cell movement; moving in the direction of increasing their concentration in a process called chemotaxis. CXCL12 is the chemoattractant that facilitates the exit of neutrophils to the blood marrow niche and their entrance into the bloodstream. It is possible that CXCL12-related signaling leads to changes in the binding affinity state of ITGA4 and significantly increases VCAM1 binding by ITGA4, although the details of this mechanism are poorly understood. Chemoattractants for neutrophils in the bloodstream are proinflammatory cytokines and molecules associated with cell degradation in the focus of inflammation. Non-specific chemoattractants for all leukocytes are fMLP, polypeptides (C5a), lipids (LTB4), and PF4. IL8 is the principal factor in neutrophil delivery. Other chemokines that are specific for neutrophils include PPBP, CXCL5, CXCL2, and CXCL6.    Outcome effects: Chemoattractant receptors mediate the process of chemotaxis mainly through the signaling of G-protein related actin reorganization. G-protein related signaling activates Rho family GTPases including RAC1, RAC2, RHOA, and CDC42, which cause the actin related protein 2/3 complex to promote actin polymerization. Actin cytoskeleton remodeling is required for the first phase of exocytosis, which is the binding of granules to the plasma membrane. RAC1 and RAC2 differentially regulate actin polymerization in neutrophils through uncapping and actin related protein 2/3 complex/CFL1, respectively. An increase in the level of calcium ions is also required for cytoskeleton activation during chemotaxis. The synthesis of arachidonic acid derivatives, as well as the modulation of myosin proteins, stimulates the motility of membranes and cellular migration. The general mechanism of neutrophil degranulation is also associated with cytoskeleton reorganization. However, degranulation does not occur spontaneously during chemotaxis, but is dependent on the combination, pattern of interactions, and concentrations of regulating factors. This also applies to another function of neutrophils, which is the synthesis of reactive oxygen species (ROS)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: Neutrophils can move towards a chemoattractant gradient both in the bloodstream and by transmigration through the blood vessel wall. Chemoattractants are soluble molecules that guide cell movement; moving in the direction of increasing their concentration in a process called chemotaxis. CXCL12 is the chemoattractant that facilitates the exit of neutrophils to the blood marrow niche and their entrance into the bloodstream. It is possible that CXCL12-related signaling leads to changes in the binding affinity state of ITGA4 and significantly increases VCAM1 binding by ITGA4, although the details of this mechanism are poorly understood. Chemoattractants for neutrophils in the bloodstream are proinflammatory cytokines and molecules associated with cell degradation in the focus of inflammation. Non-specific chemoattractants for all leukocytes are fMLP, polypeptides (C5a), lipids (LTB4), and PF4. IL8 is the principal factor in neutrophil delivery. Other chemokines that are specific for neutrophils include PPBP, CXCL5, CXCL2, and CXCL6.    Outcome effects: Chemoattractant receptors mediate the process of chemotaxis mainly through the signaling of G-protein related actin reorganization. G-protein related signaling activates Rho family GTPases including RAC1, RAC2, RHOA, and CDC42, which cause the actin related protein 2/3 complex to promote actin polymerization. Actin cytoskeleton remodeling is required for the first phase of exocytosis, which is the binding of granules to the plasma membrane. RAC1 and RAC2 differentially regulate actin polymerization in neutrophils through uncapping and actin related protein 2/3 complex/CFL1, respectively. An increase in the level of calcium ions is also required for cytoskeleton activation during chemotaxis. The synthesis of arachidonic acid derivatives, as well as the modulation of myosin proteins, stimulates the motility of membranes and cellular migration. The general mechanism of neutrophil degranulation is also associated with cytoskeleton reorganization. However, degranulation does not occur spontaneously during chemotaxis, but is dependent on the combination, pattern of interactions, and concentrations of regulating factors. This also applies to another function of neutrophils, which is the synthesis of reactive oxygen species (ROS)." EXACT []

