xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: Following contact with the endothelial cells of blood vessels, mature neutrophils change their shape and become capable of penetrating blood vessels towards the focus of inflammation. The activation of neutrophils through adherence to endothelial cells is dependent on integrins and selectins that are expressed on the surface of neutrophils and endothelial cells. L-selectin (SELL) is expressed in neutrophils and associates with P-selectin (SELP) expressed on the surface of endothelial cells, therefore providing rolling of neutrophils on the surface of blood vessels. There are other ligands for SELL that facilitate weak neutrophil binding. SELPLG is the ligand for E-selectin (SELE) on the surface of neutrophils. It is important for neutrophil rolling and for integrin-dependent activation of the adhesion of neutrophils on blood vessel cells. Following the binding of SELPLG to SELE, SYK is activated through LYN/TYROBP, which results in the activation of ITGAL/ITGB2 complex by MAPK1. In addition, ITGAL, ITGB2, and ITGB1/ITGA9 receptors on the neutrophil surface facilitate high levels of adhesion between neutrophils and endothelial cells, mainly through the association with ICAM1. In addition, chemoattractants for neutrophils, such as IL8, can increase integrin-dependent adhesion of neutrophils by stimulating the synthesis of integrins or by changing the cell shape through direct reorganization of the actin cytoskeleton. IL8-triggered binding of TLN1 can induce ITGB3 integrin clustering.    Signaling description: The activation of integrins on the neutrophil surface not only leads to adhesion to endothelial cells, but also activates signaling pathways in the neutrophil itself. The main result is the alteration of the cytoskeleton, which causes the movement of secretory vesicles of neutrophils toward the external membrane and enrichment of the membrane with specific proteins that initiate transmigration of neutrophils through the blood vessel. Secretory vesicles, a subtype of neutrophilic granules, form in the late stages of neutrophil maturation and include proteins such as CR1, FCGR2A, MME, and FPR1. The modulation of the actin cytoskeleton and migration/degranulation of neutrophils is carried out by several methods and the pathway illustrates only some of the possible signaling cascades. Furthermore, integrins trigger intracellular signaling cascades that lead to ROS production through PI3K/RAC1-related signaling. Integrins also stimulate the synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines. The stage of neutrophil movement through the vascular basement membrane is facilitated by the release of MMP9 from tertiary granules. Tertiary granules contain metalloproteases required for the degradation of the extracellular matrix and transmigration of neutrophils. The binding of IL8 to CXCR2 causes the release of MMP9 from the tertiary granules. The degranulation of tertiary granules may be a result of the synthesis of arachidonic acid derivatives, which hypothetically can be stimulated by the activation of intracellular integrin-dependent signaling pathways. The mechanisms of the transmigration of neutrophils through the blood vessel wall are not well established."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Neutrophils belong to a subtype of leukocytes that contain granules known as granulocytes. Neutrophils, the largest class of granulocytes, are major antibacterial cellular agents in inflammation since they combine the functions of granulocytes and phagocytes. Neutrophils comprise more than 50% of circulating leukocytes and more than 90% of circulating phagocytes.    Signaling description: Following contact with the endothelial cells of blood vessels, mature neutrophils change their shape and become capable of penetrating blood vessels towards the focus of inflammation. The activation of neutrophils through adherence to endothelial cells is dependent on integrins and selectins that are expressed on the surface of neutrophils and endothelial cells. L-selectin (SELL) is expressed in neutrophils and associates with P-selectin (SELP) expressed on the surface of endothelial cells, therefore providing rolling of neutrophils on the surface of blood vessels. There are other ligands for SELL that facilitate weak neutrophil binding. SELPLG is the ligand for E-selectin (SELE) on the surface of neutrophils. It is important for neutrophil rolling and for integrin-dependent activation of the adhesion of neutrophils on blood vessel cells. Following the binding of SELPLG to SELE, SYK is activated through LYN/TYROBP, which results in the activation of ITGAL/ITGB2 complex by MAPK1. In addition, ITGAL, ITGB2, and ITGB1/ITGA9 receptors on the neutrophil surface facilitate high levels of adhesion between neutrophils and endothelial cells, mainly through the association with ICAM1. In addition, chemoattractants for neutrophils, such as IL8, can increase integrin-dependent adhesion of neutrophils by stimulating the synthesis of integrins or by changing the cell shape through direct reorganization of the actin cytoskeleton. IL8-triggered binding of TLN1 can induce ITGB3 integrin clustering.    Signaling description: The activation of integrins on the neutrophil surface not only leads to adhesion to endothelial cells, but also activates signaling pathways in the neutrophil itself. The main result is the alteration of the cytoskeleton, which causes the movement of secretory vesicles of neutrophils toward the external membrane and enrichment of the membrane with specific proteins that initiate transmigration of neutrophils through the blood vessel. Secretory vesicles, a subtype of neutrophilic granules, form in the late stages of neutrophil maturation and include proteins such as CR1, FCGR2A, MME, and FPR1. The modulation of the actin cytoskeleton and migration/degranulation of neutrophils is carried out by several methods and the pathway illustrates only some of the possible signaling cascades. Furthermore, integrins trigger intracellular signaling cascades that lead to ROS production through PI3K/RAC1-related signaling. Integrins also stimulate the synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines. The stage of neutrophil movement through the vascular basement membrane is facilitated by the release of MMP9 from tertiary granules. Tertiary granules contain metalloproteases required for the degradation of the extracellular matrix and transmigration of neutrophils. The binding of IL8 to CXCR2 causes the release of MMP9 from the tertiary granules. The degranulation of tertiary granules may be a result of the synthesis of arachidonic acid derivatives, which hypothetically can be stimulated by the activation of intracellular integrin-dependent signaling pathways. The mechanisms of the transmigration of neutrophils through the blood vessel wall are not well established." EXACT []

