xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Beta-catenin signaling plays major role in female phenotype development.     Signaling description: In the absence of SRY, levels of RSPO1 rise and cause increased WNT4.Beta-catenin begins to accumulate as a response to RSPO1-WNT4 signaling. In XX germ cells, beta-catenin levels could accumulate sufficiently to repress SOX9 activity. At later stages, FOXL2 increases, which might help, perhaps in concert with estrogen receptor, to maintain granulosa (follicle) cell differentiation by repressing SOX9 and FGF9 expression. It is hypothesized that in the XX individual, the expression of WNT4 leads to the expression of DAX1 (NR0B1), which in turn blocks the expression of SOX9 and thus any testicular differentiation. WNT4 antagonizes the expression of activin B (INHBB) and induces the activin B antagonist, follistatin (FST), theoretically through beta-catenin, preventing testis vasculature development.    Highlighted proteins: Key players of female sex determination are highlighted in red. Key players of male sex determination (repressed) are highlighted in blue. SRY is white-out because it is located in chromosome Y and absent in females."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Beta-catenin signaling plays major role in female phenotype development.     Signaling description: In the absence of SRY, levels of RSPO1 rise and cause increased WNT4.Beta-catenin begins to accumulate as a response to RSPO1-WNT4 signaling. In XX germ cells, beta-catenin levels could accumulate sufficiently to repress SOX9 activity. At later stages, FOXL2 increases, which might help, perhaps in concert with estrogen receptor, to maintain granulosa (follicle) cell differentiation by repressing SOX9 and FGF9 expression. It is hypothesized that in the XX individual, the expression of WNT4 leads to the expression of DAX1 (NR0B1), which in turn blocks the expression of SOX9 and thus any testicular differentiation. WNT4 antagonizes the expression of activin B (INHBB) and induces the activin B antagonist, follistatin (FST), theoretically through beta-catenin, preventing testis vasculature development.    Highlighted proteins: Key players of female sex determination are highlighted in red. Key players of male sex determination (repressed) are highlighted in blue. SRY is white-out because it is located in chromosome Y and absent in females." EXACT []

