[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-0823be29-79a0-492c-8dea-d12fa1d9fdfa
name: Acid Secretion by Collecting Duct of Kidney Nephron
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:urinary_tract ! Urinary tract
is_a: urn:agi-folder:renal_physiology ! Renal physiology
is_a: urn:agi-folder:a ! A
xref: PS:Description "Acid secretion is one of the most important functions of the collecting duct segment of the kidney nephron. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Acid secretion is one of the most important functions of the collecting duct segment of the kidney nephron. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:PMID "17164835"
xref: PS:PMID "19948674"
synonym: "PMID: 17164835" EXACT []
synonym: "PMID: 19948674" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "M. Zharkova  ORCID:0000-0001-8706-9411"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: M. Zharkova  ORCID:0000-0001-8706-9411" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-0823be29-79a0-492c-8dea-d12fa1d9fdfa"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-0823be29-79a0-492c-8dea-d12fa1d9fdfa" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "kidney"
synonym: "Organ: kidney" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "urinary tract"
synonym: "Organ_System: urinary tract" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Acid secretion is one of the most important functions of the collecting duct segment of the kidney nephron.    Signaling description: As daily food intake loads acid into the body, urinary acid excretion is essential. The alpha-intercalated cell of the collecting duct is mainly responsible for hydrogen secretion into the urine. Carbon dioxide (CO2), which is generated in the cell and enters from the blood, is converted to carbonic acid. Carbonic acid is divided into hydrogen ion (H+) and bicarbonate ion (HCO3-). Intracellular carbonic anhydrase II (CA2) catalyzes the formation of these ions.    Outcome effects: Hydrogen ions are secreted into the lumen by H -transporting two-sector ATPase and H /K -exchanging ATPase. The bicarbonate ion is transported to the blood side by the anion exchanger type 1 (SLC4A1)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Acid secretion is one of the most important functions of the collecting duct segment of the kidney nephron.    Signaling description: As daily food intake loads acid into the body, urinary acid excretion is essential. The alpha-intercalated cell of the collecting duct is mainly responsible for hydrogen secretion into the urine. Carbon dioxide (CO2), which is generated in the cell and enters from the blood, is converted to carbonic acid. Carbonic acid is divided into hydrogen ion (H+) and bicarbonate ion (HCO3-). Intracellular carbonic anhydrase II (CA2) catalyzes the formation of these ions.    Outcome effects: Hydrogen ions are secreted into the lumen by H -transporting two-sector ATPase and H /K -exchanging ATPase. The bicarbonate ion is transported to the blood side by the anion exchanger type 1 (SLC4A1)." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-392563b2-f137-426c-a142-6d721688af10
name: Renal Proximal Tubule Acid-Base Homeostasis
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:renal_physiology ! Renal physiology
is_a: urn:agi-folder:r ! R
is_a: urn:agi-folder:urinary_tract ! Urinary tract
xref: PS:Description "The proximal tubule is responsible for approximately 80? of bicarbonate ion (HCO3-) reabsorption in the kidney. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: The proximal tubule is responsible for approximately 80? of bicarbonate ion (HCO3-) reabsorption in the kidney. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:PMID "17164835"
xref: PS:PMID "19948674"
synonym: "PMID: 17164835" EXACT []
synonym: "PMID: 19948674" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "M. Zharkova  ORCID:0000-0001-8706-9411"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: M. Zharkova  ORCID:0000-0001-8706-9411" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-392563b2-f137-426c-a142-6d721688af10"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-392563b2-f137-426c-a142-6d721688af10" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "kidney"
synonym: "Organ: kidney" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "urinary tract"
synonym: "Organ_System: urinary tract" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The proximal tubule is responsible for approximately 80% of bicarbonate ion (HCO3-) reabsorption in the kidney.    Signaling description: The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule, and cortical collecting duct are responsible for reabsorption of 10%, 6%, and 4% of HCO3-, respectively. In the tubular lumen, filtered HCO3- combines with H in a reaction catalyzed by CA4. The carbon dioxide (CO2) produced rapidly diffuses into the tubular cells and is combined with water (H2O) to produce intracellular H and HCO3-, which is catalyzed by soluble cytoplasmic CA2.    Outcome effects: HCO3- is then co-transported with Na into the blood via SLC4A4. The intracellular H produced by CA2 is secreted into the tubular lumen primarily via SLC9A3. Basolaterally expressed CA4 and possibly CA12 facilitate reabsorption by dissipating bicarbonate at the basolateral surface."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The proximal tubule is responsible for approximately 80% of bicarbonate ion (HCO3-) reabsorption in the kidney.    Signaling description: The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule, and cortical collecting duct are responsible for reabsorption of 10%, 6%, and 4% of HCO3-, respectively. In the tubular lumen, filtered HCO3- combines with H in a reaction catalyzed by CA4. The carbon dioxide (CO2) produced rapidly diffuses into the tubular cells and is combined with water (H2O) to produce intracellular H and HCO3-, which is catalyzed by soluble cytoplasmic CA2.    Outcome effects: HCO3- is then co-transported with Na into the blood via SLC4A4. The intracellular H produced by CA2 is secreted into the tubular lumen primarily via SLC9A3. Basolaterally expressed CA4 and possibly CA12 facilitate reabsorption by dissipating bicarbonate at the basolateral surface." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-6c45d065-05db-4b6b-b58c-07aa6b3c7e32
name: Circadian Clock and Sleep Regulation by Melatonin
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:nervous_system ! Nervous system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:sleep_regulation ! Sleep regulation
