xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Circadian rhythm is generated in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) in the hypothalamus, as well as in individual cells called peripheral oscillators. The SCN is known as the master clock and organizes the cycle of peripheral oscillators throughout the body. However, both the SCN and the peripheral oscillators are capable of affecting each other.    Signaling description: The transcription factors that turn on the light-induced promoters are dimers of CLOCK-ARNTL or NPAS2-ARNTL. They activate the negative regulators of the circadian cycle such as period circadian proteins (PER1/2/3), cryptochrome (CRY1/2) genes, positive regulators such as retinoic acid-related orphan receptor A (RORA) gene, and hundreds of effector genes whose products control a wide variety of metabolic functions including cellular respiration, glycolysis, gluconeogenesis, and lipid metabolism. PER and CRY mRNAs are exported to the cytoplasm where they are translated. The PER and CRY proteins then enter the nucleus where they inhibit CLOCK-ARNTL or NPAS2-ARNTL thus turning off transcription of PER and CRY. The PER-CRY complex is then progressively removed by CSNK1D/CSNK1E-mediated phosphorylation and ubiquitin-proteasome-mediated degradation.    Outcome effects: These described events allow CLOCK and ARNTL to once again stimulate transcription of PER and CRY. However, the negative feedback loop causes the level of ARNTL and PER/CRY to oscillate in opposite phases. A second autoregulatory feedback loop is induced by the transcription of NR1D1 and NR1D2. The NR1D1-NR1D2 complex represses ARNTL transcription. This pathway stabilizes CLOCK and can also directly drive circadian rhythms. Circadian rhythms are controlled by external cues, with light being the major contributor. Light is processed in the retina and via the retinohypothalamic tract leads to the synchronization of rhythms in hypothalamic SCN, which constitutes the master clock of the organism. SCN then projects to the subparaventricular zone and subsequently to the rest of the hypothalamus including ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO), the lateral hypothalamus, and the paraventricular nucleus to control hormone, temperature, feeding, and sleep-arousal cycles. VLPO also connects to tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN), which is important for sleep-wake rhythms. The exact connection between the sleep hormone, melatonin and circadian rhythm has yet to be determined, but there are two putative pathways. The first pathway is the repression of adenylate cyclase (ADCY) and protein kinase A (PKA), which influences CREB1 activation. The second pathway is the direct activation of nuclear receptors, RORA and RORB by melatonin."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Circadian rhythm is generated in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) in the hypothalamus, as well as in individual cells called peripheral oscillators. The SCN is known as the master clock and organizes the cycle of peripheral oscillators throughout the body. However, both the SCN and the peripheral oscillators are capable of affecting each other.    Signaling description: The transcription factors that turn on the light-induced promoters are dimers of CLOCK-ARNTL or NPAS2-ARNTL. They activate the negative regulators of the circadian cycle such as period circadian proteins (PER1/2/3), cryptochrome (CRY1/2) genes, positive regulators such as retinoic acid-related orphan receptor A (RORA) gene, and hundreds of effector genes whose products control a wide variety of metabolic functions including cellular respiration, glycolysis, gluconeogenesis, and lipid metabolism. PER and CRY mRNAs are exported to the cytoplasm where they are translated. The PER and CRY proteins then enter the nucleus where they inhibit CLOCK-ARNTL or NPAS2-ARNTL thus turning off transcription of PER and CRY. The PER-CRY complex is then progressively removed by CSNK1D/CSNK1E-mediated phosphorylation and ubiquitin-proteasome-mediated degradation.    Outcome effects: These described events allow CLOCK and ARNTL to once again stimulate transcription of PER and CRY. However, the negative feedback loop causes the level of ARNTL and PER/CRY to oscillate in opposite phases. A second autoregulatory feedback loop is induced by the transcription of NR1D1 and NR1D2. The NR1D1-NR1D2 complex represses ARNTL transcription. This pathway stabilizes CLOCK and can also directly drive circadian rhythms. Circadian rhythms are controlled by external cues, with light being the major contributor. Light is processed in the retina and via the retinohypothalamic tract leads to the synchronization of rhythms in hypothalamic SCN, which constitutes the master clock of the organism. SCN then projects to the subparaventricular zone and subsequently to the rest of the hypothalamus including ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO), the lateral hypothalamus, and the paraventricular nucleus to control hormone, temperature, feeding, and sleep-arousal cycles. VLPO also connects to tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN), which is important for sleep-wake rhythms. The exact connection between the sleep hormone, melatonin and circadian rhythm has yet to be determined, but there are two putative pathways. The first pathway is the repression of adenylate cyclase (ADCY) and protein kinase A (PKA), which influences CREB1 activation. The second pathway is the direct activation of nuclear receptors, RORA and RORB by melatonin." EXACT []

