xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Histamine plays a critical role in mediating wakefulness in mammals. Histamine release in the hypothalamus and other target regions is highest during wakefulness.    Signaling description: The histaminergic neurons display maximal activity during the state of vigilance and cease their activity during NREM and REM sleep. The cerebrospinal levels of histamine are reduced in diseased states where hypersomnolence is a major symptom. Histaminergic neurons are exclusively localized in the tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN) in the posterior hypothalamus and send widespread projections to various brain areas. Histaminergic neurons activate or facilitate large brain areas through postsynaptic H1 and H2 receptors, thus contributing to cortical activation.    Outcome effects: The administration of histamine or H1 receptor (HRH1) agonists induces wakefulness, whereas administration of HRH1 antagonists promotes sleep. The histamine H3 receptor (HRH3) functions as an auto-receptor and regulates the synthesis and release of histamine. The activation of HRH3 decreases histamine production and release, thus promoting sleep. Conversely, the blockade of HRH3 promotes wakefulness. Therefore, HRH3 constitutes a brain target for the treatment of sleep-wake disorders."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Histamine plays a critical role in mediating wakefulness in mammals. Histamine release in the hypothalamus and other target regions is highest during wakefulness.    Signaling description: The histaminergic neurons display maximal activity during the state of vigilance and cease their activity during NREM and REM sleep. The cerebrospinal levels of histamine are reduced in diseased states where hypersomnolence is a major symptom. Histaminergic neurons are exclusively localized in the tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN) in the posterior hypothalamus and send widespread projections to various brain areas. Histaminergic neurons activate or facilitate large brain areas through postsynaptic H1 and H2 receptors, thus contributing to cortical activation.    Outcome effects: The administration of histamine or H1 receptor (HRH1) agonists induces wakefulness, whereas administration of HRH1 antagonists promotes sleep. The histamine H3 receptor (HRH3) functions as an auto-receptor and regulates the synthesis and release of histamine. The activation of HRH3 decreases histamine production and release, thus promoting sleep. Conversely, the blockade of HRH3 promotes wakefulness. Therefore, HRH3 constitutes a brain target for the treatment of sleep-wake disorders." EXACT []

