xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Circadian clock is a molecular cell-autonomous regulator that maintains its own 24-hour molecular rhythm and drives the rhythmic expression of genes critical for the proper regulation of behavioral and physiological rhythms.    Signaling description: Light is the most prominent stimulus capable of synchronizing circadian oscillations to the environment. The primary positive transcriptional elements in the circadian oscillator include CLOCK and ARNTL proteins. In neurons of suprachiasmatic nucleus of the anterior hypathalamus, the intracellular levels of CLOCK remain steady throughout the 24-hour period, whereas ARNTL expression levels are high at the beginning of a subjective day and low at the beginning of a subjective night.    Outcome effects: The increased levels of ARNTL promote the formation of ARNTL-CLOCK heterodimers, which initiate the transcription of target genes containing CACGTG E-Box elements present in the promoters of CLOCK-driven genes. In some brain regions, NPAS2 substitutes for CLOCK. ARNTL-CLOCK activates the transcription of PER1/2/3 and CRY1/2 genes. PER proteins accumulate in the cytoplasm and can be phosphorylated by CSNK1E. The phosphorylated forms of PER are unstable and degraded by means of ubiquitylation. However late in the subjective day, CRY accumulates in the cytoplasm and promotes the formation of stable CSNK1E/PER/CRY complexes, which translocate back to the nucleus to repress their own transcription by disrupting the ARNTL-CLOCK complex. The interacting positive and negative feedback loops of circadian genes establish low levels of PER and CRY and a high level of ARNTL at the beginning of a new circadian day. Another regulatory loop is induced when ARNTL-CLOCK heterodimers activate the transcription of NR1D1 and RORA, two retinoic acid-related orphan nuclear receptors. NR1D1 and RORA compete to bind to the promoter of ARNTL. Since ARNTL homodimers are incapable of activating transcription, the susceptibility of the heterodimeric ARNTL-CLOCK to redox electron transfer through NADH and NAD potential establishes a molecular switch for activating the ARNTL-CLOCK transcription complex."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Circadian clock is a molecular cell-autonomous regulator that maintains its own 24-hour molecular rhythm and drives the rhythmic expression of genes critical for the proper regulation of behavioral and physiological rhythms.    Signaling description: Light is the most prominent stimulus capable of synchronizing circadian oscillations to the environment. The primary positive transcriptional elements in the circadian oscillator include CLOCK and ARNTL proteins. In neurons of suprachiasmatic nucleus of the anterior hypathalamus, the intracellular levels of CLOCK remain steady throughout the 24-hour period, whereas ARNTL expression levels are high at the beginning of a subjective day and low at the beginning of a subjective night.    Outcome effects: The increased levels of ARNTL promote the formation of ARNTL-CLOCK heterodimers, which initiate the transcription of target genes containing CACGTG E-Box elements present in the promoters of CLOCK-driven genes. In some brain regions, NPAS2 substitutes for CLOCK. ARNTL-CLOCK activates the transcription of PER1/2/3 and CRY1/2 genes. PER proteins accumulate in the cytoplasm and can be phosphorylated by CSNK1E. The phosphorylated forms of PER are unstable and degraded by means of ubiquitylation. However late in the subjective day, CRY accumulates in the cytoplasm and promotes the formation of stable CSNK1E/PER/CRY complexes, which translocate back to the nucleus to repress their own transcription by disrupting the ARNTL-CLOCK complex. The interacting positive and negative feedback loops of circadian genes establish low levels of PER and CRY and a high level of ARNTL at the beginning of a new circadian day. Another regulatory loop is induced when ARNTL-CLOCK heterodimers activate the transcription of NR1D1 and RORA, two retinoic acid-related orphan nuclear receptors. NR1D1 and RORA compete to bind to the promoter of ARNTL. Since ARNTL homodimers are incapable of activating transcription, the susceptibility of the heterodimeric ARNTL-CLOCK to redox electron transfer through NADH and NAD potential establishes a molecular switch for activating the ARNTL-CLOCK transcription complex." EXACT []

