xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: It is widely accepted that cannabinoids modulate the sleep-wake cycle.    Signaling description: Postsynaptic neurons synthesize and release endocannabinoids including anandamide and 2-arachidonylglycerol (2-AG). Anandamide is synthesized by NAPEPLD, while 2-AG is synthesized by PLCB1 and diacylglycerol lipases (DAGLs). Endocannabinoids subsequently act as retrograde messengers by binding to presynaptic cannabinoid receptor 1 (CNR1) coupled to the inhibition of voltage-sensitive Ca2+ channels and the activation of K+ channels. This reduces membrane depolarization and exocytosis, thereby inhibiting the release of neurotransmitters such as glutamate. MGLL inactivates 2-AG in presynaptic neurons. Fatty acid amide hydrolase (FAAH) inactivates anandamide in postsynaptic neurons. The transport of endocannabinoids facilitates both endocannabinoid release and re-uptake.    Outcome effects: The development of drugs aimed to block CNR1 may be considered as new candidates for treating excessive somnolence. Moreover, the use of drugs that block the anandamide membrane transporter or inhibit FAAH activity may represent an opportunity to treat insomnia by enhancing the endogenous levels of anandamide."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: It is widely accepted that cannabinoids modulate the sleep-wake cycle.    Signaling description: Postsynaptic neurons synthesize and release endocannabinoids including anandamide and 2-arachidonylglycerol (2-AG). Anandamide is synthesized by NAPEPLD, while 2-AG is synthesized by PLCB1 and diacylglycerol lipases (DAGLs). Endocannabinoids subsequently act as retrograde messengers by binding to presynaptic cannabinoid receptor 1 (CNR1) coupled to the inhibition of voltage-sensitive Ca2+ channels and the activation of K+ channels. This reduces membrane depolarization and exocytosis, thereby inhibiting the release of neurotransmitters such as glutamate. MGLL inactivates 2-AG in presynaptic neurons. Fatty acid amide hydrolase (FAAH) inactivates anandamide in postsynaptic neurons. The transport of endocannabinoids facilitates both endocannabinoid release and re-uptake.    Outcome effects: The development of drugs aimed to block CNR1 may be considered as new candidates for treating excessive somnolence. Moreover, the use of drugs that block the anandamide membrane transporter or inhibit FAAH activity may represent an opportunity to treat insomnia by enhancing the endogenous levels of anandamide." EXACT []

