xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: In humans, there are two subtypes of melatonin receptors including MTNR1A and MTNR1B. These are G protein-coupled receptors and are expressed in various tissues of the body, as a group or individually.    Signaling description: Melatonin activates a variety of second messenger cascades following binding to membrane receptors contingent on the specific cell and species. The activation of MTNR1A and MTNR1B inhibits adenylyl cyclase (ADCY) and its inhibition causes a rippling effect of non-activation, which commences with the decreased formation of cAMP and then progressing to diminished PKA activity, which in turn hinders the phosphorylation of CREB1. MTNR1A also activates PLCB1.    Outcome effects: The main effect of MTNR1A receptor activation is the inhibition of cAMP accumulation, however in different cell types melatonin may cause either inhibition or stimulation of cAMP. Melatonin has both receptor-mediated and receptor-independent actions. Nuclear binding sites are members of the retinoic acid receptor superfamily and include RORA and RORB. Glutamate activates N-methyl-D-aspartate selective glutamate receptor-induced calcium (Ca2+) influx which, along with other second messengers, triggers the activation of diverse signal transduction cascades including neuronal nitric oxide synthase (NOS1) activity, guanylate cyclase, and CREB1, which in turn activates PER1 and PER2 transcription. Mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs) in suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) neurons are activated by light-induced neurotransmission and/or extracellular signals including trophic factors such as brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and nerve growth factor (NGF). MAPKs regulate the activity of CLOCK, ARNTL, and other circadian rhythm proteins. The activation of MTNR1A increases potassium (K+) conductance by activating KCNJ3-inward rectifier K channels via Gb/g proteins. This may play a role in the inhibition of neuronal firing in the SCN. In addition, melatonin interacts with intracellular proteins such as calmodulin and antagonizes the binding of Ca2+ to calmodulin."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: In humans, there are two subtypes of melatonin receptors including MTNR1A and MTNR1B. These are G protein-coupled receptors and are expressed in various tissues of the body, as a group or individually.    Signaling description: Melatonin activates a variety of second messenger cascades following binding to membrane receptors contingent on the specific cell and species. The activation of MTNR1A and MTNR1B inhibits adenylyl cyclase (ADCY) and its inhibition causes a rippling effect of non-activation, which commences with the decreased formation of cAMP and then progressing to diminished PKA activity, which in turn hinders the phosphorylation of CREB1. MTNR1A also activates PLCB1.    Outcome effects: The main effect of MTNR1A receptor activation is the inhibition of cAMP accumulation, however in different cell types melatonin may cause either inhibition or stimulation of cAMP. Melatonin has both receptor-mediated and receptor-independent actions. Nuclear binding sites are members of the retinoic acid receptor superfamily and include RORA and RORB. Glutamate activates N-methyl-D-aspartate selective glutamate receptor-induced calcium (Ca2+) influx which, along with other second messengers, triggers the activation of diverse signal transduction cascades including neuronal nitric oxide synthase (NOS1) activity, guanylate cyclase, and CREB1, which in turn activates PER1 and PER2 transcription. Mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs) in suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) neurons are activated by light-induced neurotransmission and/or extracellular signals including trophic factors such as brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and nerve growth factor (NGF). MAPKs regulate the activity of CLOCK, ARNTL, and other circadian rhythm proteins. The activation of MTNR1A increases potassium (K+) conductance by activating KCNJ3-inward rectifier K channels via Gb/g proteins. This may play a role in the inhibition of neuronal firing in the SCN. In addition, melatonin interacts with intracellular proteins such as calmodulin and antagonizes the binding of Ca2+ to calmodulin." EXACT []

