xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Orexin A/B (HCRT) is a neuropeptide responsible for the regulation of sleep/arousal, feeding behavior, coordination of emotion, energy homeostasis, reward system, and is also implicated in the mechanisms of drug addiction.    Signaling description: Orexin is produced exclusively in orexinergic neurons in the lateral hypothalamic area and interacts with HCRTR1 and HCRTR2 receptors. The absence or deficiency of HCRT or HCRTR1/2 leads to narcolepsy. Individuals with narcolepsy often experience disturbed nocturnal sleep and an abnormal daytime sleep pattern. HCRTR1 and HCRTR2 are G protein-coupled receptors. HCRTR1 is coupled exclusively to GNAQ whereas HCRTR2 couples to Gi/o and/or GNAQ. One prominent method of signaling for these receptors is via phospholipase (PLC) cascades, with the most important one being phospholipase C (PLCB1). PLC signaling has multiple roles in orexin receptor responses. Diacylglycerol activates PKC, which in turn activates multiple calcium (Ca2+) transporters (voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, Ca2+/Na+ antiporters, TRPMs, TRPVs, and TRPCs) and inhibits inward rectifier potassium (K+) channels.    Outcome effects: As a result of massive Ca2+ influx, depolarization occurs and neuron action potential is generated. PLA2G4A, activated by PKC, is a central enzyme for arachidonic acid production. PLA2G4A signaling contributes to Ca2+ influx into the cell. Orexins have an excitatory activity in monoaminergic neurons. Noradrenergic cells of the locus coeruleus, dopaminergic cells of the ventral tegmental area, serotonergic cells of the dorsal raphe, histaminergic cells from tuberomammillary nucleus, and cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain have been shown to be activated by orexins and are implicated in the regulation of arousal. During sleep, orexin neurons and monoaminergic neurons are inhibited by GABAergic neurons of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO). While awake, monoaminergic neurons send inhibitory projections to the VLPO sleep center."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Orexin A/B (HCRT) is a neuropeptide responsible for the regulation of sleep/arousal, feeding behavior, coordination of emotion, energy homeostasis, reward system, and is also implicated in the mechanisms of drug addiction.    Signaling description: Orexin is produced exclusively in orexinergic neurons in the lateral hypothalamic area and interacts with HCRTR1 and HCRTR2 receptors. The absence or deficiency of HCRT or HCRTR1/2 leads to narcolepsy. Individuals with narcolepsy often experience disturbed nocturnal sleep and an abnormal daytime sleep pattern. HCRTR1 and HCRTR2 are G protein-coupled receptors. HCRTR1 is coupled exclusively to GNAQ whereas HCRTR2 couples to Gi/o and/or GNAQ. One prominent method of signaling for these receptors is via phospholipase (PLC) cascades, with the most important one being phospholipase C (PLCB1). PLC signaling has multiple roles in orexin receptor responses. Diacylglycerol activates PKC, which in turn activates multiple calcium (Ca2+) transporters (voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, Ca2+/Na+ antiporters, TRPMs, TRPVs, and TRPCs) and inhibits inward rectifier potassium (K+) channels.    Outcome effects: As a result of massive Ca2+ influx, depolarization occurs and neuron action potential is generated. PLA2G4A, activated by PKC, is a central enzyme for arachidonic acid production. PLA2G4A signaling contributes to Ca2+ influx into the cell. Orexins have an excitatory activity in monoaminergic neurons. Noradrenergic cells of the locus coeruleus, dopaminergic cells of the ventral tegmental area, serotonergic cells of the dorsal raphe, histaminergic cells from tuberomammillary nucleus, and cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain have been shown to be activated by orexins and are implicated in the regulation of arousal. During sleep, orexin neurons and monoaminergic neurons are inhibited by GABAergic neurons of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO). While awake, monoaminergic neurons send inhibitory projections to the VLPO sleep center." EXACT []

