xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Specific wavelengths of blue light (roughly 460-480 nm) are detected by photopigment melanopsin in a small percentage (1-2%) of the retinal ganglion cells (non-rod and cone cells). The axons of the melanopsin-containing ganglion cells project to the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) via the retinohypothalamic tract in the optic nerve. These neurons release L-glutamate and pituitary adenylate cyclase activating polypeptide (ADCYAP1), which causes the entrainment of CLOCK gene expression in the SCN.    Signaling description: In order to inhibit pineal melatonin production, axons from SCN neurons project to the paraventricular nucleus neuron of the hypothalamus, where they release gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA). Nerve cell bodies in the paraventricular nucleus have axons that descend through the brain stem and eventually form a synapse with neurons in the intermediolateral cell column which are preganglionic sympathetic neurons. The axons of these neurons eventually terminate on pinealocytes following an additional synapse in the superior cervical ganglion.    Outcome effects: At night, melanopsin is not activated by light and inhibitory GABAergic SCN neurons are not activated, which results in noradrenaline release by sympathetic superior cervical ganglion resulting in melatonin production and release by pinealocytes."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Specific wavelengths of blue light (roughly 460-480 nm) are detected by photopigment melanopsin in a small percentage (1-2%) of the retinal ganglion cells (non-rod and cone cells). The axons of the melanopsin-containing ganglion cells project to the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) via the retinohypothalamic tract in the optic nerve. These neurons release L-glutamate and pituitary adenylate cyclase activating polypeptide (ADCYAP1), which causes the entrainment of CLOCK gene expression in the SCN.    Signaling description: In order to inhibit pineal melatonin production, axons from SCN neurons project to the paraventricular nucleus neuron of the hypothalamus, where they release gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA). Nerve cell bodies in the paraventricular nucleus have axons that descend through the brain stem and eventually form a synapse with neurons in the intermediolateral cell column which are preganglionic sympathetic neurons. The axons of these neurons eventually terminate on pinealocytes following an additional synapse in the superior cervical ganglion.    Outcome effects: At night, melanopsin is not activated by light and inhibitory GABAergic SCN neurons are not activated, which results in noradrenaline release by sympathetic superior cervical ganglion resulting in melatonin production and release by pinealocytes." EXACT []

