xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Melatonin is synthesized from L-tryptophan through the actions of four enzymes including TPH2, DDC, AANAT, and ASMT.    Signaling description: TPH2 is responsible for the 5' hydroxylation of tryptophan yielding 5-hydroxytryptophan. DDC converts 5-hydroxytryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptamine, while AANAT acetylates serotonin to form N-acetylserotonin. ASMT catalyzes the conversion of N-acetylserotonin to melatonin. The 24-hour oscillations in AANAT activity and melatonin production are driven by the master circadian clock in the anterior hypothalamus, which is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN).    Outcome effects: At night during darkness, retinal ganglion cells send a signal to SCN and then SCN sends a signal to the paraventricular nucleus, intermediolateral cell column, and superior cervical ganglion. The signal comes to the pineal gland as a release of noradrenaline from post-ganglionic sympathetic nerve endings onto the pinealocytes, which initiates and sustains, primarily via ADRB1, elevated melatonin production. ADRB1 sends a signal to AANAT via GNAS, adenylate cyclase (ADCY), PKA, and CREB1. Following synthesis, melatonin is secreted from the pineal gland into the blood. Importantly, the pineal gland does not store residual amounts of its secretory product (melatonin) as other endocrine glands do."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Melatonin is synthesized from L-tryptophan through the actions of four enzymes including TPH2, DDC, AANAT, and ASMT.    Signaling description: TPH2 is responsible for the 5' hydroxylation of tryptophan yielding 5-hydroxytryptophan. DDC converts 5-hydroxytryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptamine, while AANAT acetylates serotonin to form N-acetylserotonin. ASMT catalyzes the conversion of N-acetylserotonin to melatonin. The 24-hour oscillations in AANAT activity and melatonin production are driven by the master circadian clock in the anterior hypothalamus, which is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN).    Outcome effects: At night during darkness, retinal ganglion cells send a signal to SCN and then SCN sends a signal to the paraventricular nucleus, intermediolateral cell column, and superior cervical ganglion. The signal comes to the pineal gland as a release of noradrenaline from post-ganglionic sympathetic nerve endings onto the pinealocytes, which initiates and sustains, primarily via ADRB1, elevated melatonin production. ADRB1 sends a signal to AANAT via GNAS, adenylate cyclase (ADCY), PKA, and CREB1. Following synthesis, melatonin is secreted from the pineal gland into the blood. Importantly, the pineal gland does not store residual amounts of its secretory product (melatonin) as other endocrine glands do." EXACT []

