xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Histidine-rich glycoprotein (HRG), also known as histidine-proline-rich glycoprotein, is an abundant in protein plasma. Plasma proteins are essential for several normal physiological processes including coagulation, fibrinolysis, angiogenesis, tissue remodeling, transport of nutrients, and the detection and clearance of pathogens and dead cells.    Signaling description: In healthy human adults, proteins such as HRG that maintain homeostasis of the vascular system are often present at relatively high concentrations (100-150ng/mL) in the plasma to provide immediate responses to foreign pathogens and vascular damage. It has been reported that HRG acts as a negative acute phase protein (APP) and circulating HRG levels are significantly lower during acute inflammation. HRG serves multiple functions including regulating blood clotting and fibrinolysis.    Outcome effects: HRG strongly inhibits F12 autoactivation via increased Zn2+ levels in response to local platelet activation. Through plasminogen (PLG) binding, HRG inhibits fibrinolysis by reducing the amount of circulating PLG available for activation into plasmin. Second to SERPINC1, HRG is one of the most abundant heparin-binding proteins in human plasma. It has been shown to neutralize the anticoagulant activity of heparin by preventing the formation of heparin/SERPINC1 complexes. In the presence of heparin, HRG inhibits the activity of SERPIND1. In addition, HRG inhibits human T-lymphocyte activation and inhibits the expression of IL2R on activated T-cells, which causes the declined release of T-cell IFNG and altered T-cell-dependent inhibition of erythropoiesis. HRG is also involved in the detection and clearance of pathogens and dead cells and has been shown to facilitate the removal of late (permeabilized plasma membrane) apoptotic and necrotic cells. Under normal physiological conditions, the rapid removal of dead cells from the circulation and tissues by phagocytic cells plays a critical role in maintaining tissue turnover. IgG forms immune complexes with target antigen and facilitates the clearance and neutralization of microorganisms, foreign substances, or necrotic cells. HRG has been shown to interact with IgG and complement component C1q and regulate the formation of insoluble immune complexes. A complex consisting of both HRG and IgG is necessary to aid necrotic cell uptake by macrophages. HRG also regulates macrophage function by modulating Fc-gamma receptor (FcgammaR) expression. Furthermore, HRG has been shown to inhibit angiogenesis by modulating various signaling events that are important for endothelial cell survival, proliferation, and cell migration. For example, HRG is involved in inhibiting proliferation and inducing apoptosis in endothelial cells in vitro through the activation of CASP3. HRG may act as pro-angiogenic modulator through the inhibition of the anti-angiogenic effect of THBS1. It also blocks the anti-angiogenic activity of THBS2 by preventing it from binding to CD36 receptor expressed on endothelial cells. Lastly, HRG can exhibit pro- or anti-angiogenic effects by competing with FGF2, an angiogenesis activator, for binding heparan sulfate on endothelial cells, which has been shown to be necessary for activating FGFR.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Histidine-rich glycoprotein (HRG), also known as histidine-proline-rich glycoprotein, is an abundant in protein plasma. Plasma proteins are essential for several normal physiological processes including coagulation, fibrinolysis, angiogenesis, tissue remodeling, transport of nutrients, and the detection and clearance of pathogens and dead cells.    Signaling description: In healthy human adults, proteins such as HRG that maintain homeostasis of the vascular system are often present at relatively high concentrations (100-150ng/mL) in the plasma to provide immediate responses to foreign pathogens and vascular damage. It has been reported that HRG acts as a negative acute phase protein (APP) and circulating HRG levels are significantly lower during acute inflammation. HRG serves multiple functions including regulating blood clotting and fibrinolysis.    Outcome effects: HRG strongly inhibits F12 autoactivation via increased Zn2+ levels in response to local platelet activation. Through plasminogen (PLG) binding, HRG inhibits fibrinolysis by reducing the amount of circulating PLG available for activation into plasmin. Second to SERPINC1, HRG is one of the most abundant heparin-binding proteins in human plasma. It has been shown to neutralize the anticoagulant activity of heparin by preventing the formation of heparin/SERPINC1 complexes. In the presence of heparin, HRG inhibits the activity of SERPIND1. In addition, HRG inhibits human T-lymphocyte activation and inhibits the expression of IL2R on activated T-cells, which causes the declined release of T-cell IFNG and altered T-cell-dependent inhibition of erythropoiesis. HRG is also involved in the detection and clearance of pathogens and dead cells and has been shown to facilitate the removal of late (permeabilized plasma membrane) apoptotic and necrotic cells. Under normal physiological conditions, the rapid removal of dead cells from the circulation and tissues by phagocytic cells plays a critical role in maintaining tissue turnover. IgG forms immune complexes with target antigen and facilitates the clearance and neutralization of microorganisms, foreign substances, or necrotic cells. HRG has been shown to interact with IgG and complement component C1q and regulate the formation of insoluble immune complexes. A complex consisting of both HRG and IgG is necessary to aid necrotic cell uptake by macrophages. HRG also regulates macrophage function by modulating Fc-gamma receptor (FcgammaR) expression. Furthermore, HRG has been shown to inhibit angiogenesis by modulating various signaling events that are important for endothelial cell survival, proliferation, and cell migration. For example, HRG is involved in inhibiting proliferation and inducing apoptosis in endothelial cells in vitro through the activation of CASP3. HRG may act as pro-angiogenic modulator through the inhibition of the anti-angiogenic effect of THBS1. It also blocks the anti-angiogenic activity of THBS2 by preventing it from binding to CD36 receptor expressed on endothelial cells. Lastly, HRG can exhibit pro- or anti-angiogenic effects by competing with FGF2, an angiogenesis activator, for binding heparan sulfate on endothelial cells, which has been shown to be necessary for activating FGFR.    Highlighted proteins: Protein with decreased expression or activity is highlighted in blue." EXACT []

