xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: C-reactive protein (CRP) and amyloid P component, serum (APCS) belong to an ancient family of proteins known as pentraxins, which are serum proteins with a relatively uncommon pentameric structure. They are positive acute phase proteins (APPs) with elevated expression during inflammation.    Signaling description: CRP levels of 10 mkg/mL-500 mkg/mL are observed in the acute phase response (APR). Although APCS is not an APP in humans, it is a very strong acute phase marker in mice. Pentraxins function as opsonins and recognize foreign antigens, phospholipids, or nuclear components released by damaged cells. CRP has a specific interaction with SNRPN, whereas APCS is a major recognition molecule for DNA. Similar to other APPs such as MBL2, CRP and APCS promote the clearance of pathogens following their association with phagocytes and trigger an immune response through the activation of the complement cascade.    Outcome effects: CRP activates complement via the classical pathway at the level of C1QA, but inhibits the alternative pathway. CRP inhibits complement-mediated cytolysis via the lectin pathway by increasing CFH binding to C3. It is unclear how CRP enhances the binding of CFH to C3. Pentraxins also activate the innate immune system through its interaction with FCG receptors (FCGRs). It is well established that FCGR aggregation by immune complexes is necessary for receptor activation. FcgammaRII is the main receptor for pentraxins. In addition, APCS may stabilize amyloid fibrils against degradative enzymes. APCS may prevent the generation of fibrocytes and it has been suggested that this may contribute to the inhibition of fibrosis. Lastly, PTX3 is a long pentraxin produced locally in response to inflammatory stimuli such as TNF. Similar to the classical pentraxins, PTX3 activates complement and binds to FCGR3A.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: C-reactive protein (CRP) and amyloid P component, serum (APCS) belong to an ancient family of proteins known as pentraxins, which are serum proteins with a relatively uncommon pentameric structure. They are positive acute phase proteins (APPs) with elevated expression during inflammation.    Signaling description: CRP levels of 10 mkg/mL-500 mkg/mL are observed in the acute phase response (APR). Although APCS is not an APP in humans, it is a very strong acute phase marker in mice. Pentraxins function as opsonins and recognize foreign antigens, phospholipids, or nuclear components released by damaged cells. CRP has a specific interaction with SNRPN, whereas APCS is a major recognition molecule for DNA. Similar to other APPs such as MBL2, CRP and APCS promote the clearance of pathogens following their association with phagocytes and trigger an immune response through the activation of the complement cascade.    Outcome effects: CRP activates complement via the classical pathway at the level of C1QA, but inhibits the alternative pathway. CRP inhibits complement-mediated cytolysis via the lectin pathway by increasing CFH binding to C3. It is unclear how CRP enhances the binding of CFH to C3. Pentraxins also activate the innate immune system through its interaction with FCG receptors (FCGRs). It is well established that FCGR aggregation by immune complexes is necessary for receptor activation. FcgammaRII is the main receptor for pentraxins. In addition, APCS may stabilize amyloid fibrils against degradative enzymes. APCS may prevent the generation of fibrocytes and it has been suggested that this may contribute to the inhibition of fibrosis. Lastly, PTX3 is a long pentraxin produced locally in response to inflammatory stimuli such as TNF. Similar to the classical pentraxins, PTX3 activates complement and binds to FCGR3A.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

