synonym: "Organ_System: digestive system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The acute phase response (APR) is a prominent systemic reaction of an organism to infection and tissue injury.    Signaling description: One of the first signs of APR is the synthesis of acute phase proteins (APPs) in the liver. The release of proinflammatory cytokines, nitric oxide, and glucocorticoids initiates the synthesis of APPs. IL6 is the key mediator for hepatocytic secretion of most APPs. An APP has been defined as one, whose plasma concentration increases (positive APP) or decreases (negative APP) by at least 25% during inflammatory disorders. Some examples of negative APPs include albumin (ALB), transferrin (TF), transthyretin (TTR), RBP1, antithrombin (SERPINC1), and transcortin (SERPINA6). The decrease of these proteins may be used as markers of inflammation. The physiological role of the reduced synthesis of these proteins is to reserve amino acids to produce positive APPs more efficiently. Some transcription factors that may be involved in regulating the synthesis of negative APPs in liver are described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: It is recognized that CEBPA is downregulated in APR. ALB is an example of a negative APP and constitutes about half of blood serum protein. It transports hormones, fatty acids and other compounds, buffers pH, and maintains osmotic pressure. SERPINA6, RBP1, TTR, and TF are major transport proteins in the blood. SERPINA6, which is an alpha-globulin protein, has corticosteroid-binding properties and is a key transport protein for glucocorticoids and progestins in the blood. SERPINA6 binds to corticosteroid, glucocorticoids, progestins, retinol, thyroxine (T4), and iron and their deficiency may lead to protein malnutrition. CPB2 and SERPINC1 inhibit fibrinolysis and blood coagulation. The negative expression of SERPINC1 in APR can reduce the increased degradation by proteolytic enzymes (elastase released from activated neutrophils). SERPINC1 also appears to directly act as an anti-inflammatory agent by binding to leukocyte receptors and blocking their communication with endothelial cells and limiting their adhesion and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The acute phase response (APR) is a prominent systemic reaction of an organism to infection and tissue injury.    Signaling description: One of the first signs of APR is the synthesis of acute phase proteins (APPs) in the liver. The release of proinflammatory cytokines, nitric oxide, and glucocorticoids initiates the synthesis of APPs. IL6 is the key mediator for hepatocytic secretion of most APPs. An APP has been defined as one, whose plasma concentration increases (positive APP) or decreases (negative APP) by at least 25% during inflammatory disorders. Some examples of negative APPs include albumin (ALB), transferrin (TF), transthyretin (TTR), RBP1, antithrombin (SERPINC1), and transcortin (SERPINA6). The decrease of these proteins may be used as markers of inflammation. The physiological role of the reduced synthesis of these proteins is to reserve amino acids to produce positive APPs more efficiently. Some transcription factors that may be involved in regulating the synthesis of negative APPs in liver are described on the pathway.    Outcome effects: It is recognized that CEBPA is downregulated in APR. ALB is an example of a negative APP and constitutes about half of blood serum protein. It transports hormones, fatty acids and other compounds, buffers pH, and maintains osmotic pressure. SERPINA6, RBP1, TTR, and TF are major transport proteins in the blood. SERPINA6, which is an alpha-globulin protein, has corticosteroid-binding properties and is a key transport protein for glucocorticoids and progestins in the blood. SERPINA6 binds to corticosteroid, glucocorticoids, progestins, retinol, thyroxine (T4), and iron and their deficiency may lead to protein malnutrition. CPB2 and SERPINC1 inhibit fibrinolysis and blood coagulation. The negative expression of SERPINC1 in APR can reduce the increased degradation by proteolytic enzymes (elastase released from activated neutrophils). SERPINC1 also appears to directly act as an anti-inflammatory agent by binding to leukocyte receptors and blocking their communication with endothelial cells and limiting their adhesion and migration.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

