xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The acute phase response (APR) is a prominent systemic reaction of an organism to infection and tissue injury.    Signaling description: One of the first signs of APR is synthesis of acute phase proteins (APPs) in the liver. The release of proinflammatory cytokines, nitric oxide, and glucocorticoids, initiates the synthesis of APPs. IL6 is the key mediator for hepatocytic secretion of most APPs. Kupffer cells, which are specialized macrophages located in the liver, may be an additional source of proinflammatory cytokines. The physiological role of highly expressed (positive) APPs is to reduce the systemic effects of tissue damage by enhancing clot formation, reducing proteolysis, and facilitating the clearance of toxic metabolites. During APR, plasma viscosity increases as a result of changes in total blood protein concentration including the increase of fibrinogen, which influences the erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR). Systemic APR is profound in bacterial infections, while generally milder in viral infections. Among APPs, CP and C3 expressions are increased by approximately 50% in APR. Further, HP, fibrinogen, A2M, and LBP are increased by 2-3-fold, while CRP and SAA1 are rapidly increased up to 5-1000-fold. This pathway describes the most common signaling that leads to the expression of APPs in the liver. The transcription factors that induce the expression of APPs include CEBPB, STAT1, STAT3, STAT5, NF-kB, HNF4A, SP1, and Jun/Fos. CEBPA is down-regulated in APR, while glucocorticoids are positive effectors of APP expression.    Outcome effects: SERPINE2 is an important component of hepatic APR and a major physiological inhibitor of plasminogen (PLG) activation and inducer of blood coagulation. Furthermore, FN1, fibrinogen, F8, and VWF, cause clot formation. LBP, which is an opsonin, transfers LPS to CD14 on macrophages. CRP acts as an opsonin as well and activates complement by the classical C1q pathway. CRP is a member of the pentraxin family, which function as soluble pattern recognition molecules and one of the earliest and most important roles for these proteins is host defense primarily against pathogenic bacteria. IL6 induction is mediated by STAT3 and CEBPB, which leads to CRP expression. In addition, SERPINA1 is a serine protease inhibitor with targets that include elastase, plasmin, thrombin, trypsin, chymotrypsin, and plasminogen activator. A2M has many diversified and complex functions, but is primarily known for its ability to inhibit a broad spectrum of proteases. It functions as an inhibitor of fibrinolysis by inhibiting plasmin and kallikrein. A2M acts as a carrier protein because it also binds to numerous growth factors and cytokines. In addition, the physiological function of CP includes copper transport, coagulation, angiogenesis, defense against oxidant stress, and iron homeostasis. HP binds free hemoglobin in blood plasma released from erythrocytes with high affinity and thereby inhibits its oxidative activity. SAA1 is thought to influence high density lipoprotein-cholesterol transport. In tissues, it attracts inflammatory cells and inhibits the respiratory burst of leukocytes. Following secretion into the circulation, it associates with high-density lipoprotein (HDL) particles. During acute inflammation, serum SAA1 levels may rise up to 1000-fold. SAA1 exhibits significant immunological activity by being chemotactic for neutrophils and mast cells. ORM1 is one of the major APPs in humans and regulates injury-induced angiogenesis by promoting the downstream stimulation of the angiogenic process by VEGF. SERPINA3 is a plasma protease inhibitor and has a suppressive effect on neutrophil chemotaxis. Lastly, SERPING1 is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) and major inhibitor of the contact coagulation system. It is also an important regulator of complement activation inhibiting the first component of complement C1. Another important biological role of SERPING1 is the regulation of vascular pe"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The acute phase response (APR) is a prominent systemic reaction of an organism to infection and tissue injury.    Signaling description: One of the first signs of APR is synthesis of acute phase proteins (APPs) in the liver. The release of proinflammatory cytokines, nitric oxide, and glucocorticoids, initiates the synthesis of APPs. IL6 is the key mediator for hepatocytic secretion of most APPs. Kupffer cells, which are specialized macrophages located in the liver, may be an additional source of proinflammatory cytokines. The physiological role of highly expressed (positive) APPs is to reduce the systemic effects of tissue damage by enhancing clot formation, reducing proteolysis, and facilitating the clearance of toxic metabolites. During APR, plasma viscosity increases as a result of changes in total blood protein concentration including the increase of fibrinogen, which influences the erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR). Systemic APR is profound in bacterial infections, while generally milder in viral infections. Among APPs, CP and C3 expressions are increased by approximately 50% in APR. Further, HP, fibrinogen, A2M, and LBP are increased by 2-3-fold, while CRP and SAA1 are rapidly increased up to 5-1000-fold. This pathway describes the most common signaling that leads to the expression of APPs in the liver. The transcription factors that induce the expression of APPs include CEBPB, STAT1, STAT3, STAT5, NF-kB, HNF4A, SP1, and Jun/Fos. CEBPA is down-regulated in APR, while glucocorticoids are positive effectors of APP expression.    Outcome effects: SERPINE2 is an important component of hepatic APR and a major physiological inhibitor of plasminogen (PLG) activation and inducer of blood coagulation. Furthermore, FN1, fibrinogen, F8, and VWF, cause clot formation. LBP, which is an opsonin, transfers LPS to CD14 on macrophages. CRP acts as an opsonin as well and activates complement by the classical C1q pathway. CRP is a member of the pentraxin family, which function as soluble pattern recognition molecules and one of the earliest and most important roles for these proteins is host defense primarily against pathogenic bacteria. IL6 induction is mediated by STAT3 and CEBPB, which leads to CRP expression. In addition, SERPINA1 is a serine protease inhibitor with targets that include elastase, plasmin, thrombin, trypsin, chymotrypsin, and plasminogen activator. A2M has many diversified and complex functions, but is primarily known for its ability to inhibit a broad spectrum of proteases. It functions as an inhibitor of fibrinolysis by inhibiting plasmin and kallikrein. A2M acts as a carrier protein because it also binds to numerous growth factors and cytokines. In addition, the physiological function of CP includes copper transport, coagulation, angiogenesis, defense against oxidant stress, and iron homeostasis. HP binds free hemoglobin in blood plasma released from erythrocytes with high affinity and thereby inhibits its oxidative activity. SAA1 is thought to influence high density lipoprotein-cholesterol transport. In tissues, it attracts inflammatory cells and inhibits the respiratory burst of leukocytes. Following secretion into the circulation, it associates with high-density lipoprotein (HDL) particles. During acute inflammation, serum SAA1 levels may rise up to 1000-fold. SAA1 exhibits significant immunological activity by being chemotactic for neutrophils and mast cells. ORM1 is one of the major APPs in humans and regulates injury-induced angiogenesis by promoting the downstream stimulation of the angiogenic process by VEGF. SERPINA3 is a plasma protease inhibitor and has a suppressive effect on neutrophil chemotaxis. Lastly, SERPING1 is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) and major inhibitor of the contact coagulation system. It is also an important regulator of complement activation inhibiting the first component of complement C1. Another important biological role of SERPING1 is the regulation of vascular pe" EXACT []

