xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: T-cells originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and go through positive or negative selection in the thymus to mature.    Signaling description: Upon entry into the thymus, precursors lack expression of CD4 and CD8 and are called double negative (DN). Progenitor cells enter the thymus at the cortico-medullary junction (CMJ) and move through the cortex as the cells progress through DN1 (CD44 /CD25-) and DN2 (CD44 /CD25), arriving at the subcapsular zone as they enter DN3 (CD44-/CD25). At the DN3/DN4 stage of T-cell maturation, proteins produced from effectively rearranged T-cell receptor (TCR) beta genes must be assembled into the pre-TCR complex, which consists of a TCR beta chain (TRB@), the invariant pre-TCR alpha chain, and proteins of the CD3 receptor complex.    Outcome effects: The assembly of a functional pre-TCR complex leads to alpha/beta lineage commitment and involves expansion, expression of CD4 and CD8 coreceptors, and rearrangement of the TCR alpha locus. Thus, double positive (DP) thymocytes that reside in the cortex are the first population capable of expressing mature TCRs at the cell surface. Only T-cells that have a functional pre-TCR survive the transition from DN4 to DP thymocytes, in a process also known as beta-selection. If the pre-TCR complex is not assembled normally, TP53 activation causes apoptosis and negative selection of pre-T cells. In addition, IL7R signaling is important for triggering the synthesis of invariant pre-TCR alpha chain."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: T-cells originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and go through positive or negative selection in the thymus to mature.    Signaling description: Upon entry into the thymus, precursors lack expression of CD4 and CD8 and are called double negative (DN). Progenitor cells enter the thymus at the cortico-medullary junction (CMJ) and move through the cortex as the cells progress through DN1 (CD44 /CD25-) and DN2 (CD44 /CD25), arriving at the subcapsular zone as they enter DN3 (CD44-/CD25). At the DN3/DN4 stage of T-cell maturation, proteins produced from effectively rearranged T-cell receptor (TCR) beta genes must be assembled into the pre-TCR complex, which consists of a TCR beta chain (TRB@), the invariant pre-TCR alpha chain, and proteins of the CD3 receptor complex.    Outcome effects: The assembly of a functional pre-TCR complex leads to alpha/beta lineage commitment and involves expansion, expression of CD4 and CD8 coreceptors, and rearrangement of the TCR alpha locus. Thus, double positive (DP) thymocytes that reside in the cortex are the first population capable of expressing mature TCRs at the cell surface. Only T-cells that have a functional pre-TCR survive the transition from DN4 to DP thymocytes, in a process also known as beta-selection. If the pre-TCR complex is not assembled normally, TP53 activation causes apoptosis and negative selection of pre-T cells. In addition, IL7R signaling is important for triggering the synthesis of invariant pre-TCR alpha chain." EXACT []

