xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Natural killer (NK) cells recognize and attack damaged or infected cells in an organism in the absence of antibodies. NK cells provide rapid responses to virally infected cells and cancer cells. NK cells can recognize stressed cells in the absence of antigen-MHC complex, allowing for a much faster immune reaction.    Signaling description: The activation of NK cells depends on the strong activation of stimulatory NK cell receptors. ITGAL and ITGB2 can rapidly induce signals for activation by promoting adhesion between NK cells and a target cell. FCGR3A and FCER1G provide antibody-dependent T-cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC). FCGR3A and FCER1G recognize and bind to the Fc region of IgG which has bound to the surface of a target cell. KLRK1 binds to non-classical (also non-polymorphic) MHC1 molecules such as MICA. Engaged KLRK1 and other stimulatory receptors on the NK cell surface induces the phosphorylation of VAV1, resulting in the actin-dependent clustering of stimulatory receptors and their recruitment to lipid rafts. This leads to more VAV1 phosphorylation, inducing a positive feedback loop which ultimately results in NK cell cytotoxicity.    Outcome effects: The stimulatory receptors of NK cells cause the release of calcium and activate JUN/FOS transcriptional complex. This leads to key NK cell-mediated responses including the transcription of cytokines and FASLG in addition to the formation of immunological synapses with granules of perforins (PRF1) and granzymes (GZMA/GZMB). FASLG, PRF1, and GZMA/GZMB all result in the death of target cells. Finally, cytokine-mediated STAT5 and STAT1 activation also results in the production of PRF1 and GZMA/GZMB."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Natural killer (NK) cells recognize and attack damaged or infected cells in an organism in the absence of antibodies. NK cells provide rapid responses to virally infected cells and cancer cells. NK cells can recognize stressed cells in the absence of antigen-MHC complex, allowing for a much faster immune reaction.    Signaling description: The activation of NK cells depends on the strong activation of stimulatory NK cell receptors. ITGAL and ITGB2 can rapidly induce signals for activation by promoting adhesion between NK cells and a target cell. FCGR3A and FCER1G provide antibody-dependent T-cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC). FCGR3A and FCER1G recognize and bind to the Fc region of IgG which has bound to the surface of a target cell. KLRK1 binds to non-classical (also non-polymorphic) MHC1 molecules such as MICA. Engaged KLRK1 and other stimulatory receptors on the NK cell surface induces the phosphorylation of VAV1, resulting in the actin-dependent clustering of stimulatory receptors and their recruitment to lipid rafts. This leads to more VAV1 phosphorylation, inducing a positive feedback loop which ultimately results in NK cell cytotoxicity.    Outcome effects: The stimulatory receptors of NK cells cause the release of calcium and activate JUN/FOS transcriptional complex. This leads to key NK cell-mediated responses including the transcription of cytokines and FASLG in addition to the formation of immunological synapses with granules of perforins (PRF1) and granzymes (GZMA/GZMB). FASLG, PRF1, and GZMA/GZMB all result in the death of target cells. Finally, cytokine-mediated STAT5 and STAT1 activation also results in the production of PRF1 and GZMA/GZMB." EXACT []

