xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: T-cells originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and go through positive or negative selection in the thymus to mature. Peripheral tolerance occurs in the peripheral lymphoid organs (lymph node and spleen) following T-cell maturation.    Signaling description: It is crucial to control the tolerance of lymphocytes that first encounter their cognate self-antigens outside of the thymus such as in the case of food antigens, developmental antigens, and antigens displayed during chronic infection. Both anergy and deletion of self-reactive T-cells can occur in the periphery. There are several key mechanisms of T- and B-cell tolerance. Inhibitory receptor engagement and strong activation lead to T-cell tolerance. PDCD1, BTLA, and CTLA4 receptors block T-cell proliferation through the dephosphorylation of ZAP70 and AKT1. The lack of co-stimulation occurs when the expression of ligands for co-stimulating T-cell receptor (TCR) complex receptors, CD40 and CD28 is downregulated.    Outcome effects: T-cells, without a positive signal from the TCR complex, undergo apoptosis. In addition, high amounts of ligands for death receptors such as FAS, TNFRSF25, and TNFRSF10A lead to the activation of death receptor-mediated apoptosis. Lastly, regulatory T-lymphocytes (Treg) function to prevent, downregulate, or limit autoaggressive immune responses in the immune system."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: T-cells originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and go through positive or negative selection in the thymus to mature. Peripheral tolerance occurs in the peripheral lymphoid organs (lymph node and spleen) following T-cell maturation.    Signaling description: It is crucial to control the tolerance of lymphocytes that first encounter their cognate self-antigens outside of the thymus such as in the case of food antigens, developmental antigens, and antigens displayed during chronic infection. Both anergy and deletion of self-reactive T-cells can occur in the periphery. There are several key mechanisms of T- and B-cell tolerance. Inhibitory receptor engagement and strong activation lead to T-cell tolerance. PDCD1, BTLA, and CTLA4 receptors block T-cell proliferation through the dephosphorylation of ZAP70 and AKT1. The lack of co-stimulation occurs when the expression of ligands for co-stimulating T-cell receptor (TCR) complex receptors, CD40 and CD28 is downregulated.    Outcome effects: T-cells, without a positive signal from the TCR complex, undergo apoptosis. In addition, high amounts of ligands for death receptors such as FAS, TNFRSF25, and TNFRSF10A lead to the activation of death receptor-mediated apoptosis. Lastly, regulatory T-lymphocytes (Treg) function to prevent, downregulate, or limit autoaggressive immune responses in the immune system." EXACT []

