xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: T-cells originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and go through positive or negative selection in the thymus to mature.    Signaling description: When CD4 CD8 T-cells bind to antigen, they enter the next stage of differentiation which is the process of positive selection of T- cells. In some cases, T-cell receptors (TCRs) in T-cells remain non-functional or non-activated by antigen-MHC protein complex. The unoccupied TCR complex fails to transmit the survival signal through ATK1 signaling, thereby inducing death by neglect. EGR1 is one of the earliest transcription factors expressed after TCR stimulation on double positive (DP) CD4 CD8 thymocytes. EGR1 enhances the expression of negative regulators of differentiation pathways such as ID3 and the anti-apoptosis molecule, BCL2. BCL11B is the second most important transcription factor in the control of both positive selection and survival of DP thymocytes. Other transcription factors such as RORC, HNF1A, and more recently MYB, have also been implicated in positive selection.    Outcome effects: It has been proposed that glucocorticoids (GCs) induce apoptosis and death by neglect, acting as regulators of the differentiation and selection of developing thymocytes at this stage of maturation. GCs control the selection of DP thymocytes by modifying their TCR-related signaling."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: T-cells originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and go through positive or negative selection in the thymus to mature.    Signaling description: When CD4 CD8 T-cells bind to antigen, they enter the next stage of differentiation which is the process of positive selection of T- cells. In some cases, T-cell receptors (TCRs) in T-cells remain non-functional or non-activated by antigen-MHC protein complex. The unoccupied TCR complex fails to transmit the survival signal through ATK1 signaling, thereby inducing death by neglect. EGR1 is one of the earliest transcription factors expressed after TCR stimulation on double positive (DP) CD4 CD8 thymocytes. EGR1 enhances the expression of negative regulators of differentiation pathways such as ID3 and the anti-apoptosis molecule, BCL2. BCL11B is the second most important transcription factor in the control of both positive selection and survival of DP thymocytes. Other transcription factors such as RORC, HNF1A, and more recently MYB, have also been implicated in positive selection.    Outcome effects: It has been proposed that glucocorticoids (GCs) induce apoptosis and death by neglect, acting as regulators of the differentiation and selection of developing thymocytes at this stage of maturation. GCs control the selection of DP thymocytes by modifying their TCR-related signaling." EXACT []

