synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-463ea690-1c95-492d-9b04-a20c97abe920" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "T-cell"
synonym: "CellType: T-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "bone marrow"
synonym: "Tissue: bone marrow" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "lymph node"
synonym: "Organ: lymph node" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: CD8 cells and NK cells kill damaged or infected cells. CD8 T-cells (cytotoxic T-lymphocytes) attack and kill cells that are infected particularly by viruses or cells that are damaged in other ways such as cancer cells.    Signaling description: Most CD8 T cells express T-cell receptors (TCRs) that can recognize a specific antigen and stimulate CD8 cell differentiation. The binding between TCR complex coactivator, CD8A and the MHC class I protein complex keeps CD8 T-cells and the target cell bound closely together during antigen-specific activation. CD8 cell activation occurs following antigen recognition by the TCR complex and CD8A coactivator. CD28 receptor signaling also contributes to CD8 cell activation. Once activated, CD8 cells undergo proliferation with the help of growth factors for T-cells such as IL2 or IL12. These cytokines increase the number of CD8 cells specific for the target antigen.    Outcome effects: Lastly, the expression of FAS receptor ligand (FASLG) and production of granzyme B (GZMB) and perforin (PRF1) are the main mechanisms of CD8 T-cell cytotoxicity. Cytotoxicity may be mediated indirectly by the release of cytokines such as TNF-beta (LTA) and IFNG from cytotoxic cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: CD8 cells and NK cells kill damaged or infected cells. CD8 T-cells (cytotoxic T-lymphocytes) attack and kill cells that are infected particularly by viruses or cells that are damaged in other ways such as cancer cells.    Signaling description: Most CD8 T cells express T-cell receptors (TCRs) that can recognize a specific antigen and stimulate CD8 cell differentiation. The binding between TCR complex coactivator, CD8A and the MHC class I protein complex keeps CD8 T-cells and the target cell bound closely together during antigen-specific activation. CD8 cell activation occurs following antigen recognition by the TCR complex and CD8A coactivator. CD28 receptor signaling also contributes to CD8 cell activation. Once activated, CD8 cells undergo proliferation with the help of growth factors for T-cells such as IL2 or IL12. These cytokines increase the number of CD8 cells specific for the target antigen.    Outcome effects: Lastly, the expression of FAS receptor ligand (FASLG) and production of granzyme B (GZMB) and perforin (PRF1) are the main mechanisms of CD8 T-cell cytotoxicity. Cytotoxicity may be mediated indirectly by the release of cytokines such as TNF-beta (LTA) and IFNG from cytotoxic cells." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-493908b7-dd04-4504-94cf-0360921ebda6
name: T-Cell Cytotoxic Mediated Cell Death
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t-cell_self_tolerance ! T-cell self tolerance
xref: PS:Description "CD8 cells and NK cells kill damaged or infected cells in an organism. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
