synonym: "Tissue: bone marrow" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "lymph node"
synonym: "Organ: lymph node" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: CD8 cells and NK cells kill damaged or infected cells in an organism. Most CD8 T-cells express T-cell receptors (TCRs) that can recognize a specific antigen. NK cells can recognize a pathogen and/or damaged cell in the absence of antibodies. FAS-like death receptors and the action of granzyme B (GZMB) and perforin (PRF1) on the cell membrane are the main mechanisms of CD8 T-cell and NK-cell cytotoxicity.    Signaling description: Cytotoxicity may be mediated by the release of cytokines such as IFNG from cytotoxic cells. GZMB and PRF1 production can induce apoptosis in target cells by forming transmembrane pores and through the cleavage and activation of caspases. DNase (DFFB) is activated through the cleavage of its associated inhibitor, DFFA by caspase-3 (CASP3). DFFB is then able to interact with topoisomerase II (TOP2B) to condense chromatin, leading to DNA fragmentation and target cell apoptosis. When CD8 cells and NK cells are activated, they begin to express the surface protein, FASLG which can bind to FAS expressed on the target cell and trigger apoptosis. The engagement of FAS with FASLG allows for the recruitment of procaspases CASP8 and CASP10. These caspases then activate the effector caspases CASP3, CASP6, and CASP7, leading to the cleavage of death substrates such as LMNB1, PARP1, and PRKDC.    Outcome effects: The final result is apoptosis of the cell that expresses FAS. However, FAS-FASLG interaction is thought to be more important to T-lymphocyte development rather than to the cytolytic activity of CD8 cells and NK cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: CD8 cells and NK cells kill damaged or infected cells in an organism. Most CD8 T-cells express T-cell receptors (TCRs) that can recognize a specific antigen. NK cells can recognize a pathogen and/or damaged cell in the absence of antibodies. FAS-like death receptors and the action of granzyme B (GZMB) and perforin (PRF1) on the cell membrane are the main mechanisms of CD8 T-cell and NK-cell cytotoxicity.    Signaling description: Cytotoxicity may be mediated by the release of cytokines such as IFNG from cytotoxic cells. GZMB and PRF1 production can induce apoptosis in target cells by forming transmembrane pores and through the cleavage and activation of caspases. DNase (DFFB) is activated through the cleavage of its associated inhibitor, DFFA by caspase-3 (CASP3). DFFB is then able to interact with topoisomerase II (TOP2B) to condense chromatin, leading to DNA fragmentation and target cell apoptosis. When CD8 cells and NK cells are activated, they begin to express the surface protein, FASLG which can bind to FAS expressed on the target cell and trigger apoptosis. The engagement of FAS with FASLG allows for the recruitment of procaspases CASP8 and CASP10. These caspases then activate the effector caspases CASP3, CASP6, and CASP7, leading to the cleavage of death substrates such as LMNB1, PARP1, and PRKDC.    Outcome effects: The final result is apoptosis of the cell that expresses FAS. However, FAS-FASLG interaction is thought to be more important to T-lymphocyte development rather than to the cytolytic activity of CD8 cells and NK cells." EXACT []

