synonym: "Organ_System: endocrine system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prolactin (PRL) is a protein enabling female mammals to produce milk. Estrogens stimulate PRL expression via ESR1.    Signaling description: The classical dopaminergic inhibition of PRL release from the anterior pituitary is mediated through DRD2 receptor, G-proteins and adenylate cyclase (ADCY). Action of dopamine induces a rapid change in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration, due to the coupling of DRD2 receptor to voltage-gated calcium channels or via indirect action on GIRK and resulting in a marked decline in intracellular Ca2+. Estrogen effects on lactotroph cell growth are mediated through estrogen-induced stimulatory or inhibitory action of TGFs and other growth factors. Increasing estrogen concentrations reduce the intrapituitary production of TGFB1 and stimulate the synthesis of TGFB3. These two factors have opposite effects on lactotroph cell growth, since TGFB1 inhibits and TGFB3 stimulates the proliferation of these cells. Interestingly, these estradiol-induced changes are dependent on the presence of folliculostellate cells (FS). In particular, in FS cells estradiol-induced TGFB3 stimulates the production of FGF2, which is a potent stimulator of PRL production and lactotroph cell proliferation. Alternatively, estrogens may affect lactotroph growth and function through altering the dopaminergic control. It is well known that estradiol downregulates hypothalamic DA production and suppresses the activity of DRD2 in lactotrophs by uncoupling the receptors from G proteins. Whereas estrogens predominantly stimulate PRL synthesis and release, this hormone was shown to differently affect lactotroph cell growth by acting either proliferatively or apoptotically. Regarding apoptosis, it has been demonstrated that estrogens upregulate the Fas/FasL apoptotic pathway in lactotrophs and induce the intrapituitary production of TNF, which acts apoptotically through the TNF receptor 1 (TNFR1) expressed on lactotrophs. Whether lactotrophs undergo proliferation or apoptosis will then depend on the actual dominance of a specific factor during the cycle.    Outcome effects: PRL stimulates lactation, has a role in many other processes and regulates fertility."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prolactin (PRL) is a protein enabling female mammals to produce milk. Estrogens stimulate PRL expression via ESR1.    Signaling description: The classical dopaminergic inhibition of PRL release from the anterior pituitary is mediated through DRD2 receptor, G-proteins and adenylate cyclase (ADCY). Action of dopamine induces a rapid change in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration, due to the coupling of DRD2 receptor to voltage-gated calcium channels or via indirect action on GIRK and resulting in a marked decline in intracellular Ca2+. Estrogen effects on lactotroph cell growth are mediated through estrogen-induced stimulatory or inhibitory action of TGFs and other growth factors. Increasing estrogen concentrations reduce the intrapituitary production of TGFB1 and stimulate the synthesis of TGFB3. These two factors have opposite effects on lactotroph cell growth, since TGFB1 inhibits and TGFB3 stimulates the proliferation of these cells. Interestingly, these estradiol-induced changes are dependent on the presence of folliculostellate cells (FS). In particular, in FS cells estradiol-induced TGFB3 stimulates the production of FGF2, which is a potent stimulator of PRL production and lactotroph cell proliferation. Alternatively, estrogens may affect lactotroph growth and function through altering the dopaminergic control. It is well known that estradiol downregulates hypothalamic DA production and suppresses the activity of DRD2 in lactotrophs by uncoupling the receptors from G proteins. Whereas estrogens predominantly stimulate PRL synthesis and release, this hormone was shown to differently affect lactotroph cell growth by acting either proliferatively or apoptotically. Regarding apoptosis, it has been demonstrated that estrogens upregulate the Fas/FasL apoptotic pathway in lactotrophs and induce the intrapituitary production of TNF, which acts apoptotically through the TNF receptor 1 (TNFR1) expressed on lactotrophs. Whether lactotrophs undergo proliferation or apoptosis will then depend on the actual dominance of a specific factor during the cycle.    Outcome effects: PRL stimulates lactation, has a role in many other processes and regulates fertility." EXACT []

