xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Aldosterone regulates the activity of ion channels that control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons and is responsible for Na+ reabsorption into plasma and K+ excretion in the distal renal tubule. The transport of Na+ and K+ has a dominant role in blood pressure regulation.    Signaling description: Non-voltage gated sodium channels SCNN1A/1B/1G and kidney-specific SLC12A1/A3 and KCNJ1 play a key role in Na+, Cl-, and K+ ion homeostasis. These channels control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons which are responsible for Na+ and Cl- reabsorption into plasma and K+ excretion. Aldosterone modulates the activity of these channels in kidney. Aldosterone binds to NR3C2, which interacts with NR3C1, and they both function as transcription factors that bind to corticoid response elements in the promoters of corticoid responsive genes to activate their transcription. NR3C1 promotes TSC22D3 which increases the transcription of SGK1. Subsequently, SGK1 phosphorylates NEDD4L which mediates the ubiquitination of multiple target substrates and has a critical role in epithelial Na+ transport. WNK1/4 control the transport of Na+ and Cl- ions via activation of STK39 and OXSR1 that stimulate SLC12A1 and SLC12A3. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Aldosterone regulates Na+ and Cl- reabsorption and K+ excretion in nephrons."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Aldosterone regulates the activity of ion channels that control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons and is responsible for Na+ reabsorption into plasma and K+ excretion in the distal renal tubule. The transport of Na+ and K+ has a dominant role in blood pressure regulation.    Signaling description: Non-voltage gated sodium channels SCNN1A/1B/1G and kidney-specific SLC12A1/A3 and KCNJ1 play a key role in Na+, Cl-, and K+ ion homeostasis. These channels control electrolyte transport across epithelia in nephrons which are responsible for Na+ and Cl- reabsorption into plasma and K+ excretion. Aldosterone modulates the activity of these channels in kidney. Aldosterone binds to NR3C2, which interacts with NR3C1, and they both function as transcription factors that bind to corticoid response elements in the promoters of corticoid responsive genes to activate their transcription. NR3C1 promotes TSC22D3 which increases the transcription of SGK1. Subsequently, SGK1 phosphorylates NEDD4L which mediates the ubiquitination of multiple target substrates and has a critical role in epithelial Na+ transport. WNK1/4 control the transport of Na+ and Cl- ions via activation of STK39 and OXSR1 that stimulate SLC12A1 and SLC12A3. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Aldosterone regulates Na+ and Cl- reabsorption and K+ excretion in nephrons." EXACT []

