xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Mineralcorticoids are a class of steroid hormones that control salt and water balances. Mineralcorticoids are produced in the zona glomerulosa of the cortex of the adrenal gland and its secretion is principally mediated by angiotensin II (AGT) and adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) but also by other substances.    Signaling description: Cholesterol is the precursor for mineralocorticoids. Cholesterol is transported from cellular stores to the outer mitochondrial membrane, where steroidogenic acute regulatory (STAR) protein facilitates the movement of cholesterol into the inner mitochondrial membrane. STAR mediated transport of cholesterol is the limiting step in corticoid production. In zona glomerulosa of adrenal cortex, CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone. Progesterone is hydrolyzed to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2. CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) and AVP (vasopressin) bind MC2R and AVPR2 and cause the activation of ADCY/cAMP/PKA/STAR signaling. AGT (angiotensin) and EDN1 (endothelin) activate AGTR1 and EDNRA/B and cause PLCB1/DAG/PKC/CREB1 signaling stimulation. Activation of voltage-dependent L-type calcium channels (CACNA1C/CACNA2D1/CACNB2) increases Ca2+ influx that induces CAMK1, which in turn phosphorylates and activates CREB1. Further, CREB1 promotes STAR expression. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: The secretion of mineralocorticoids is regulated by ACTH, AVP, AGT and EDN1."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Mineralcorticoids are a class of steroid hormones that control salt and water balances. Mineralcorticoids are produced in the zona glomerulosa of the cortex of the adrenal gland and its secretion is principally mediated by angiotensin II (AGT) and adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) but also by other substances.    Signaling description: Cholesterol is the precursor for mineralocorticoids. Cholesterol is transported from cellular stores to the outer mitochondrial membrane, where steroidogenic acute regulatory (STAR) protein facilitates the movement of cholesterol into the inner mitochondrial membrane. STAR mediated transport of cholesterol is the limiting step in corticoid production. In zona glomerulosa of adrenal cortex, CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone. Progesterone is hydrolyzed to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2. CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) and AVP (vasopressin) bind MC2R and AVPR2 and cause the activation of ADCY/cAMP/PKA/STAR signaling. AGT (angiotensin) and EDN1 (endothelin) activate AGTR1 and EDNRA/B and cause PLCB1/DAG/PKC/CREB1 signaling stimulation. Activation of voltage-dependent L-type calcium channels (CACNA1C/CACNA2D1/CACNB2) increases Ca2+ influx that induces CAMK1, which in turn phosphorylates and activates CREB1. Further, CREB1 promotes STAR expression. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: The secretion of mineralocorticoids is regulated by ACTH, AVP, AGT and EDN1." EXACT []

