xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Steroidogenesis is the biological process in which steroids are generated from cholesterol and changed into other steroids. The major classes of steroid hormones are the progestogens, corticosteroids (corticoids), androgens, and estrogens. Progestogens are the precursors of all other human steroids, and all human tissues which produce steroids must first convert cholesterol to pregnenolone. This conversion is the rate-limiting step of steroid synthesis. Corticosteroids (glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids) are produced in the adrenal cortex. Estrogen and progesterone are made primarily in the ovary and the placenta during pregnancy, and testosterone in the testes and to a much lesser degree in adrenal cortex. Testosterone is also converted to estrogen in both females and males.     Signaling description: In the adrenal cortex, granulosa cell of the ovary and Leydig cells of the testis, ACTH acts on melanocortin 2 receptors (MC2R). Binding of ACTH to MC2R mediates ADCY/cAMP/PKA signaling which causes a rapid increase in cholesterol biosynthesis. Cholesterol is the precursor for mineralocorticoids (mainly aldosterone) and glucocorticoids (mainly cortisol). Cholesterol is transported from cellular stores to the outer mitochondrial membrane, where steroidogenic acute regulatory (STAR) protein facilitates the movement of cholesterol into the inner mitochondrial membrane. STAR mediated transport of cholesterol is the limiting step of the production of corticoids. In zona glomerulosa of adrenal cortex, CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone. Progesterone is hydrolyzed to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2. CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. In zona fasciculate of adrenal cortex, CYP17A1 converts pregnenolone to 17-hydroxypregnenolone, and HSD3B2 catalyzes 17-hydroxypregnenolone to 17-hydroxyprogesterone which is converted by CYP21A2 to 11-deoxycortisol. CYP11B1 hydroxylates 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol. In zona reticularis of adrenal cortex, CYP17A1 converts 17-hydroxypregnenolone to dehydroepiandrosterone, HSD3B2 catalyzes dehydroepiandrosterone to androstenedione and HSD17B3 converts androstenedione to testosterone. HSD3B2 and HSD17B3 also act in the testis. In granulosa cell of the ovary, CYP19A1 converts testosterone to estrogens. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Steroids are converted to other steroids from cholesterol in adrenal cortex and the gonads.    Animal data: not shown Description of     Highlighted proteins: not shown Description of     Mutated genes: not shown     References: 1) 16426683 2) 25460300 3) 24793988 4) 27252161"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Steroidogenesis is the biological process in which steroids are generated from cholesterol and changed into other steroids. The major classes of steroid hormones are the progestogens, corticosteroids (corticoids), androgens, and estrogens. Progestogens are the precursors of all other human steroids, and all human tissues which produce steroids must first convert cholesterol to pregnenolone. This conversion is the rate-limiting step of steroid synthesis. Corticosteroids (glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids) are produced in the adrenal cortex. Estrogen and progesterone are made primarily in the ovary and the placenta during pregnancy, and testosterone in the testes and to a much lesser degree in adrenal cortex. Testosterone is also converted to estrogen in both females and males.     Signaling description: In the adrenal cortex, granulosa cell of the ovary and Leydig cells of the testis, ACTH acts on melanocortin 2 receptors (MC2R). Binding of ACTH to MC2R mediates ADCY/cAMP/PKA signaling which causes a rapid increase in cholesterol biosynthesis. Cholesterol is the precursor for mineralocorticoids (mainly aldosterone) and glucocorticoids (mainly cortisol). Cholesterol is transported from cellular stores to the outer mitochondrial membrane, where steroidogenic acute regulatory (STAR) protein facilitates the movement of cholesterol into the inner mitochondrial membrane. STAR mediated transport of cholesterol is the limiting step of the production of corticoids. In zona glomerulosa of adrenal cortex, CYP11A1 converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, and HSD3B2 catalyzes the oxidative conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone. Progesterone is hydrolyzed to desoxycorticosterone by CYP21A2. CYP11B1 and CYP11B2 convert desoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and corticosterone to aldosterone, respectively. In zona fasciculate of adrenal cortex, CYP17A1 converts pregnenolone to 17-hydroxypregnenolone, and HSD3B2 catalyzes 17-hydroxypregnenolone to 17-hydroxyprogesterone which is converted by CYP21A2 to 11-deoxycortisol. CYP11B1 hydroxylates 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol. In zona reticularis of adrenal cortex, CYP17A1 converts 17-hydroxypregnenolone to dehydroepiandrosterone, HSD3B2 catalyzes dehydroepiandrosterone to androstenedione and HSD17B3 converts androstenedione to testosterone. HSD3B2 and HSD17B3 also act in the testis. In granulosa cell of the ovary, CYP19A1 converts testosterone to estrogens. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Steroids are converted to other steroids from cholesterol in adrenal cortex and the gonads.    Animal data: not shown Description of     Highlighted proteins: not shown Description of     Mutated genes: not shown     References: 1) 16426683 2) 25460300 3) 24793988 4) 27252161" EXACT []

