xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Among other biomolecules, proteins are the most susceptible to oxidative and nitrosative stress.    Signaling description: An exposure to various free radicals and non-radical oxygen and nitrogen derivatives leads to the modification of amino acid residue side chains, formation of protein-protein cross-links, and oxidation of the protein backbone, which result in protein fragmentation. Protein amino acid residue side chains are oxidized by the hydroxyl radical, which can be produced by Fenton's reagent (H2O2 in the presence of Fe2+ or Cu+). The nitrosative action of non-radical peroxynitrite is mediated by the formation of bicarbonate (CO3-) and nitrogen dioxide (NO2) free radicals in the presence of carbon dioxide (CO2). Free radical-induced protein amino acid side chain modifications are as markers of protein damage induced by oxidative/nitrosative stress.    Outcome effects: The accumulation of oxidized proteins indicates a mismatch between ROS and RNS generation/scavenging and damaged protein utilization capacity. This mismatch is characteristic of a wide range of diseases associated with oxidative and nitrosative stress including atherosclerosis, diabetic complications, various cardiovascular disorders, inflammation and senescence related diseases, as well as neuropathies such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Among other biomolecules, proteins are the most susceptible to oxidative and nitrosative stress.    Signaling description: An exposure to various free radicals and non-radical oxygen and nitrogen derivatives leads to the modification of amino acid residue side chains, formation of protein-protein cross-links, and oxidation of the protein backbone, which result in protein fragmentation. Protein amino acid residue side chains are oxidized by the hydroxyl radical, which can be produced by Fenton's reagent (H2O2 in the presence of Fe2+ or Cu+). The nitrosative action of non-radical peroxynitrite is mediated by the formation of bicarbonate (CO3-) and nitrogen dioxide (NO2) free radicals in the presence of carbon dioxide (CO2). Free radical-induced protein amino acid side chain modifications are as markers of protein damage induced by oxidative/nitrosative stress.    Outcome effects: The accumulation of oxidized proteins indicates a mismatch between ROS and RNS generation/scavenging and damaged protein utilization capacity. This mismatch is characteristic of a wide range of diseases associated with oxidative and nitrosative stress including atherosclerosis, diabetic complications, various cardiovascular disorders, inflammation and senescence related diseases, as well as neuropathies such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease." EXACT []

