xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2), are important signaling molecules in normal cardiovascular function. However, being highly active chemicals, ROS play a crucial role in the development of cardiovascular pathology.    Signaling description: The major source of ROS in the cardiovascular system is NAD(P)H oxidase complex, which is efficiently activated by vasoactive agent AGT, acting through the G-protein-coupled AT1 receptor (AGTR1). The activation of NAD(P)H oxidase complex requires a concerted effort of protein kinase C (PKC), RAC1, and ceramides. The highly reactive and short-living superoxide radical, produced by NAD(P)H oxidase, is rapidly metabolized to H2O2 by Cu2+/Zn2+ superoxide dismutase (SOD1). H2O2 is a stable oxidant which can diffuse through membranes and act at long distances as a modulator of redox-sensitive cellular factors. AGT-induced activation of proinflammatory molecule expression requires NAD(P)H oxidase activity and is mediated through the activation of redox-regulated transcription factor NF-kB.    Outcome effects: NAD(P)H oxidase-derived H2O2 activates IKBK which phosphorylates NFKBIA and releases active NF-kB, which then translocates to the nucleus where it activates target promoters which trigger the transcription of a range of proinflammatory factors."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2), are important signaling molecules in normal cardiovascular function. However, being highly active chemicals, ROS play a crucial role in the development of cardiovascular pathology.    Signaling description: The major source of ROS in the cardiovascular system is NAD(P)H oxidase complex, which is efficiently activated by vasoactive agent AGT, acting through the G-protein-coupled AT1 receptor (AGTR1). The activation of NAD(P)H oxidase complex requires a concerted effort of protein kinase C (PKC), RAC1, and ceramides. The highly reactive and short-living superoxide radical, produced by NAD(P)H oxidase, is rapidly metabolized to H2O2 by Cu2+/Zn2+ superoxide dismutase (SOD1). H2O2 is a stable oxidant which can diffuse through membranes and act at long distances as a modulator of redox-sensitive cellular factors. AGT-induced activation of proinflammatory molecule expression requires NAD(P)H oxidase activity and is mediated through the activation of redox-regulated transcription factor NF-kB.    Outcome effects: NAD(P)H oxidase-derived H2O2 activates IKBK which phosphorylates NFKBIA and releases active NF-kB, which then translocates to the nucleus where it activates target promoters which trigger the transcription of a range of proinflammatory factors." EXACT []

