xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Cardiac hypoxia is the initial step in ischemia/reperfusion injury that results in cardiomyocyte death via apoptosis or necrosis.    Signaling description: Oxygen deficiency triggers the transition of the cells to anaerobic metabolism, which results in lactic acidosis. The accumulation of hydrogen ions (H+) activates the Na+/H+ exchanger (SLC9A1), leading to Na+ and subsequent Ca2+ overload. In mitochondria, Ca2+ induces nitric oxide (NO) production and the opening of mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). This causes the burst of superoxide radical production, peroxynitrite and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) formation, followed by the inhibition of TCA cycle enzymes and ATP depletion.    Outcome effects: Peroxynitrite- and H2O2-induced DNA damage activates PARP1. This results in further drastic NAD and ATP depletion, which leads to energy collapse and necrosis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Cardiac hypoxia is the initial step in ischemia/reperfusion injury that results in cardiomyocyte death via apoptosis or necrosis.    Signaling description: Oxygen deficiency triggers the transition of the cells to anaerobic metabolism, which results in lactic acidosis. The accumulation of hydrogen ions (H+) activates the Na+/H+ exchanger (SLC9A1), leading to Na+ and subsequent Ca2+ overload. In mitochondria, Ca2+ induces nitric oxide (NO) production and the opening of mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). This causes the burst of superoxide radical production, peroxynitrite and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) formation, followed by the inhibition of TCA cycle enzymes and ATP depletion.    Outcome effects: Peroxynitrite- and H2O2-induced DNA damage activates PARP1. This results in further drastic NAD and ATP depletion, which leads to energy collapse and necrosis." EXACT []

