synonym: "Source: Pathological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vasoconstriction and vasodilation are regulated by vascular smooth muscle cell (VSMC) contractility. The phosphorylation of myosin light chain (MYL9) by myosin light chain kinase (MYLK) and its dephosphorylation by myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP) induces VSMC constriction or relaxation, respectively.    Signaling description: Reactive oxygen species (ROS) such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as well as reactive nitrogen species (RNS) such as nitric oxide (NO) and peroxynitrite have an central role in balancing MYLK and MLCP activity, thus regulating VSMC contractility. NO is a vasodilating agent produced in the endothelium by endothelial nitric oxide synthase 3 (NOS3). NO diffuses to VSMCs where it induces cGMP production by the direct activation of soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC). cGMP activates cGMP-dependent protein kinase (PKG), which in turn phosphorylates and activates MLCP. cGMP also causes vasodilation by lowering cytosolic Ca2+ by activating K channels and via PKG-mediated activation of Ca2+ uptake by Ca2+-transporting ATPase. Conversely, NO inhibits MYLK through ADCY/cAMP/PKA pathway. NO mediates ADCY activation through the upregulation of PTGS1/2 and upstream enzymes that convert PGH2 to PGI2, PGE2, and PGD2. NO also reduces the production of TXA2, TXB2, and 20-HETE through inhibiting thromboxane-A synthase and alkane 1-monooxygenase, respectively. In contrast, the inhibition of ALOX15 by NO reduces the production of dilating agents such as 11,12-EET and 12(S)-HPETE. In general, H2O2 acts in an opposite manner as NO. It activates the production of constricting agents such as 12(R)-hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid (12(R)-HETE), TXA2, TXB2, PGH2, and PGF2alpha and inhibits the production of a dilating agent 11,12-EET.    Outcome effects: H2O2 activates phospholipase A2, thus enhancing the production of arachidonic acid, which serves as a precursor for the biosynthesis of prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and tri- and tetraenoic acids. Conversely, arachidonic acid itself acts as a vasoconstrictor through MLCP inhibition. H2O2 induces the inhibitive phosphorylation of MLCP by ROCK1 through the downregulation of upstream PTPN11. In addition, the upregulation of arginase by H2O2 reduces the availability of arginine which is a substrate for NO production by NOS3. Another ROS that interferes with NO production is superoxide radical which reacts with NO to produce the highly reactive peroxynitrite. The oxidation of tetrahydrobiopterin, an essential NOS3 cofactor, by peroxynitrite results in the uncoupling of NOS3 and the production of superoxide radical rather than NO.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with elevated level are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vasoconstriction and vasodilation are regulated by vascular smooth muscle cell (VSMC) contractility. The phosphorylation of myosin light chain (MYL9) by myosin light chain kinase (MYLK) and its dephosphorylation by myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP) induces VSMC constriction or relaxation, respectively.    Signaling description: Reactive oxygen species (ROS) such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as well as reactive nitrogen species (RNS) such as nitric oxide (NO) and peroxynitrite have an central role in balancing MYLK and MLCP activity, thus regulating VSMC contractility. NO is a vasodilating agent produced in the endothelium by endothelial nitric oxide synthase 3 (NOS3). NO diffuses to VSMCs where it induces cGMP production by the direct activation of soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC). cGMP activates cGMP-dependent protein kinase (PKG), which in turn phosphorylates and activates MLCP. cGMP also causes vasodilation by lowering cytosolic Ca2+ by activating K channels and via PKG-mediated activation of Ca2+ uptake by Ca2+-transporting ATPase. Conversely, NO inhibits MYLK through ADCY/cAMP/PKA pathway. NO mediates ADCY activation through the upregulation of PTGS1/2 and upstream enzymes that convert PGH2 to PGI2, PGE2, and PGD2. NO also reduces the production of TXA2, TXB2, and 20-HETE through inhibiting thromboxane-A synthase and alkane 1-monooxygenase, respectively. In contrast, the inhibition of ALOX15 by NO reduces the production of dilating agents such as 11,12-EET and 12(S)-HPETE. In general, H2O2 acts in an opposite manner as NO. It activates the production of constricting agents such as 12(R)-hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid (12(R)-HETE), TXA2, TXB2, PGH2, and PGF2alpha and inhibits the production of a dilating agent 11,12-EET.    Outcome effects: H2O2 activates phospholipase A2, thus enhancing the production of arachidonic acid, which serves as a precursor for the biosynthesis of prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and tri- and tetraenoic acids. Conversely, arachidonic acid itself acts as a vasoconstrictor through MLCP inhibition. H2O2 induces the inhibitive phosphorylation of MLCP by ROCK1 through the downregulation of upstream PTPN11. In addition, the upregulation of arginase by H2O2 reduces the availability of arginine which is a substrate for NO production by NOS3. Another ROS that interferes with NO production is superoxide radical which reacts with NO to produce the highly reactive peroxynitrite. The oxidation of tetrahydrobiopterin, an essential NOS3 cofactor, by peroxynitrite results in the uncoupling of NOS3 and the production of superoxide radical rather than NO.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with elevated level are highlighted in red." EXACT []

