xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Angiotensin II, the active form of the angiotensinogen (AGT) precursor, is one of the central vasoactive agents involved in the development of hypertension, cardiovascular remodeling, and hypertrophy.    Signaling description: Angiotensin II mediates many of its pleiotropic vascular effects through NAD(P)H oxidase-derived reactive oxygen species (ROS). ROS, such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2), are important signaling molecules in the cardiovascular system. The major source of ROS in endothelium, cardiomyocytes, and vascular smooth muscle cells is NAD(P)H oxidase complex, which is efficiently activated by AGT, acting through G-protein-coupled receptor AT1 (AGTR1). The activation of NAD(P)H oxidase complex requires a concerted effort of protein kinase C (PKC), RAC1 and ceramides. The highly reactive and short-living superoxide radical, produced by NAD(P)H oxidase, is rapidly metabolized to H2O2 by Cu2+ /Zn2+ superoxide dismutase (SOD1). H2O2 is a stable oxidant, which can diffuse through membranes and act at long distances as a modulator of redox-sensitive cellular factors.    Outcome effects: Through the upregulation of SRC-kinase, H2O2 is involved in the transactivation of EGFR followed by the activation of MAPK1 cascade. This leads to the intensification of translation, subsequent cell growth, and hypertrophy. MAPK1 is also involved in the activation of matrix metalloproteinases, which results in extracellular matrix remodeling and facilitation of cell migration. Conversely, the SRC activation mediates ACT/AKT1 activation, which enhances cell growth, contributes to cell migration, and activates cell survival through HSPB1 phosphorylation. AGT-mediated activation of cell proliferation involves the induction of redox-sensitive MAP-kinases (MAP3K5, p38, and MAPK8) and kinases of the JAK/STAT pathway (PTK2B and JAK2) by H2O2. H2O2-activated p38 kinase contributes to cardiovascular remodeling and cell survival through the upregulation of extracellular matrix components and phosphorylation of HSPB1, respectively. H2O2 stabilizes and enhances its effect on these pathways by inhibiting phosphatases, which downregulate the action of the targeted kinases."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Angiotensin II, the active form of the angiotensinogen (AGT) precursor, is one of the central vasoactive agents involved in the development of hypertension, cardiovascular remodeling, and hypertrophy.    Signaling description: Angiotensin II mediates many of its pleiotropic vascular effects through NAD(P)H oxidase-derived reactive oxygen species (ROS). ROS, such as superoxide radical and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2), are important signaling molecules in the cardiovascular system. The major source of ROS in endothelium, cardiomyocytes, and vascular smooth muscle cells is NAD(P)H oxidase complex, which is efficiently activated by AGT, acting through G-protein-coupled receptor AT1 (AGTR1). The activation of NAD(P)H oxidase complex requires a concerted effort of protein kinase C (PKC), RAC1 and ceramides. The highly reactive and short-living superoxide radical, produced by NAD(P)H oxidase, is rapidly metabolized to H2O2 by Cu2+ /Zn2+ superoxide dismutase (SOD1). H2O2 is a stable oxidant, which can diffuse through membranes and act at long distances as a modulator of redox-sensitive cellular factors.    Outcome effects: Through the upregulation of SRC-kinase, H2O2 is involved in the transactivation of EGFR followed by the activation of MAPK1 cascade. This leads to the intensification of translation, subsequent cell growth, and hypertrophy. MAPK1 is also involved in the activation of matrix metalloproteinases, which results in extracellular matrix remodeling and facilitation of cell migration. Conversely, the SRC activation mediates ACT/AKT1 activation, which enhances cell growth, contributes to cell migration, and activates cell survival through HSPB1 phosphorylation. AGT-mediated activation of cell proliferation involves the induction of redox-sensitive MAP-kinases (MAP3K5, p38, and MAPK8) and kinases of the JAK/STAT pathway (PTK2B and JAK2) by H2O2. H2O2-activated p38 kinase contributes to cardiovascular remodeling and cell survival through the upregulation of extracellular matrix components and phosphorylation of HSPB1, respectively. H2O2 stabilizes and enhances its effect on these pathways by inhibiting phosphatases, which downregulate the action of the targeted kinases." EXACT []

