synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Th1-cells are very effective in controlling infection of intracellular pathogens. Th1-cells characteristically produce TNF-beta (LTA) and IFN-gamma (IFNG), which induce macrophage activation.    Signaling description: IL12, produced by activated monocytes/macrophages and dendritic cells, is the dominant factor promoting Th1-cell polarization. IFNG itself can further enhance IFNG production and therefore enlarge the pool of Th1-cells. IL1 receptor family member, IL18R through IL18 has been demonstrated to synergize with IL12 in enhancing IFN- production by Th1-cells. Other members of the IL12 cytokine family such as IL27 may also have a role in Th1 development. In general, cytokine signaling requires the heterodimerization of receptor chains. This triggers the phosphorylation of Janus kinases (JAK) and recruitment of signal transducers and activators of transcription (STAT), which translocate to the nucleus and mediate the activation of cytokine-responsive genes. Several STAT molecules have been identified as central players in Th1-cell polarization. STAT4 is activated in response to IL12 via IL12R and mediates the up-regulation of IFNG transcripts, leading to the Th1-cytokine pattern.    Outcome effects: STAT1 is activated by IFNG signaling and upregulates TBX21, a transcription factor that induces IFNG production and Th1 differentiation. IFNG-mediated induction of TBX21 results in the up-regulation of IL12R, which in turn increases TBX21 levels via STAT4 activation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Th1-cells are very effective in controlling infection of intracellular pathogens. Th1-cells characteristically produce TNF-beta (LTA) and IFN-gamma (IFNG), which induce macrophage activation.    Signaling description: IL12, produced by activated monocytes/macrophages and dendritic cells, is the dominant factor promoting Th1-cell polarization. IFNG itself can further enhance IFNG production and therefore enlarge the pool of Th1-cells. IL1 receptor family member, IL18R through IL18 has been demonstrated to synergize with IL12 in enhancing IFN- production by Th1-cells. Other members of the IL12 cytokine family such as IL27 may also have a role in Th1 development. In general, cytokine signaling requires the heterodimerization of receptor chains. This triggers the phosphorylation of Janus kinases (JAK) and recruitment of signal transducers and activators of transcription (STAT), which translocate to the nucleus and mediate the activation of cytokine-responsive genes. Several STAT molecules have been identified as central players in Th1-cell polarization. STAT4 is activated in response to IL12 via IL12R and mediates the up-regulation of IFNG transcripts, leading to the Th1-cytokine pattern.    Outcome effects: STAT1 is activated by IFNG signaling and upregulates TBX21, a transcription factor that induces IFNG production and Th1 differentiation. IFNG-mediated induction of TBX21 results in the up-regulation of IL12R, which in turn increases TBX21 levels via STAT4 activation." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-fb881fd8-5e85-40f4-9f0b-99f963e906b5
name: Treg-Cell Differentiation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t-cell_activation_and_differentiation ! T-cell activation and differentiation
xref: PS:Description "Regulatory T (Treg) cells are a component of the immune system that suppresses immune responses of other cells. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Regulatory T (Treg) cells are a component of the immune system that suppresses immune responses of other cells. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-fb881fd8-5e85-40f4-9f0b-99f963e906b5"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-fb881fd8-5e85-40f4-9f0b-99f963e906b5" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "T-cell"
synonym: "CellType: T-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "lymph node"
synonym: "Organ: lymph node" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Regulatory T (Treg) cells are a component of the immune system that suppresses immune responses of other cells.    Signaling description: The most studied Treg cells are those that express CD4, CD25, and FOXP3. These cells are involved in shutting down immune responses after they have successfully eliminated invading organisms and also participate in preventing autoimmunity. Treg cell differentiation requires STAT5 activation through IL2 signaling. In addition to IL2, \"\"natural\"\" regulatory T-cells (nTregs) and \"\"induced\"\"regulatory T cells (iTregs) need TGFB1 for their survival in addition to FOXP3 expression. FOXP3 is a transcription factor that determines Treg differentiation and function. FOXP3 can act as a transcriptional activator or repressor since it regulates cytokine gene transcription in effector and Treg cells. FOXP3 and GATA3 physically associate with each other and this direct association and cross-inhibition creates a balancing act between transcription factor dominance and T-cell differentiation.    Outcome effects: Treg cells can decrease effector T-lymphocyte IL2 expression, however, it is not known if this is a direct effect by Treg cells on T-cells or an indirect effect caused by Treg inhibition of T-cell proliferation."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Regulatory T (Treg) cells are a component of the immune system that suppresses immune responses of other cells.    Signaling description: The most studied Treg cells are those that express CD4, CD25, and FOXP3. These cells are involved in shutting down immune responses after they have successfully eliminated invading organisms and also participate in preventing autoimmunity. Treg cell differentiation requires STAT5 activation through IL2 signaling. In addition to IL2, \"\"natural\"\" regulatory T-cells (nTregs) and \"\"induced\"\"regulatory T cells (iTregs) need TGFB1 for their survival in addition to FOXP3 expression. FOXP3 is a transcription factor that determines Treg differentiation and function. FOXP3 can act as a transcriptional activator or repressor since it regulates cytokine gene transcription in effector and Treg cells. FOXP3 and GATA3 physically associate with each other and this direct association and cross-inhibition creates a balancing act between transcription factor dominance and T-cell differentiation.    Outcome effects: Treg cells can decrease effector T-lymphocyte IL2 expression, however, it is not known if this is a direct effect by Treg cells on T-cells or an indirect effect caused by Treg inhibition of T-cell proliferation." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-63c6c77f-af3b-4e89-a0d2-27a31f4a0894
name: Th2-Cell Differentiation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t-cell_activation_and_differentiation ! T-cell activation and differentiation
xref: PS:Description "T-helper cells (Th cells) are lymphocytes that release cytokines and present antigen-MHC protein complex on their surface. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: T-helper cells (Th cells) are lymphocytes that release cytokines and present antigen-MHC protein complex on their surface. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-63c6c77f-af3b-4e89-a0d2-27a31f4a0894"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-63c6c77f-af3b-4e89-a0d2-27a31f4a0894" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "T-cell"
synonym: "CellType: T-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "lymph node"
synonym: "Organ: lymph node" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: T-helper cells (Th-cells) are lymphocytes that release cytokines and present antigen-MHC protein complex on their surface. Th-cells develop into two major subtypes of cells including Type 1 (Th1) and Type 2 (Th2) helper T-cells. Th2-cells have a key role in directing immune responses against helminthes. Additionally, Th2-cells are crucial for many types of allergic reactions.    Signaling description: IL4 is a major Th2 cytokine and Th2-cells require IL4 and its downstream effector signal transducer and activator of transcription, STAT6 for their development. STAT6 participates in Th2-differentiation, in part, by enhancing the expression of the master regulator of Th2 differentiation, GATA3. STAT6 and GATA3, in concert with IL2-mediated STAT5 activation, induce the secretion of copious amounts of IL4, IL5, and IL13 by activated Th2-cells. The expression of these transcription factors is also necessary for the Th2-lineage commitment.    Outcome effects: Th2-lymphocytes produce IL4, IL5, and IL13. IL5 stimulates the differentiation of eosinophils, which are important for killing helminthes and other parasites. IL4 and IL13 stimulate the contraction of smooth muscle and overproduction of mucus, which helps in the expulsion of helminthes from the gut and lung."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: T-helper cells (Th-cells) are lymphocytes that release cytokines and present antigen-MHC protein complex on their surface. Th-cells develop into two major subtypes of cells including Type 1 (Th1) and Type 2 (Th2) helper T-cells. Th2-cells have a key role in directing immune responses against helminthes. Additionally, Th2-cells are crucial for many types of allergic reactions.    Signaling description: IL4 is a major Th2 cytokine and Th2-cells require IL4 and its downstream effector signal transducer and activator of transcription, STAT6 for their development. STAT6 participates in Th2-differentiation, in part, by enhancing the expression of the master regulator of Th2 differentiation, GATA3. STAT6 and GATA3, in concert with IL2-mediated STAT5 activation, induce the secretion of copious amounts of IL4, IL5, and IL13 by activated Th2-cells. The expression of these transcription factors is also necessary for the Th2-lineage commitment.    Outcome effects: Th2-lymphocytes produce IL4, IL5, and IL13. IL5 stimulates the differentiation of eosinophils, which are important for killing helminthes and other parasites. IL4 and IL13 stimulate the contraction of smooth muscle and overproduction of mucus, which helps in the expulsion of helminthes from the gut and lung." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-e9d54b7f-58d4-4474-938b-b2f67dabbcbb
name: T-Cell Receptor Signaling
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t-cell_activation_and_differentiation ! T-cell activation and differentiation
xref: PS:Description "The principal function of most T cells is to respond to specific foreign antigens while maintaining self-tolerance. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: The principal function of most T cells is to respond to specific foreign antigens while maintaining self-tolerance. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-e9d54b7f-58d4-4474-938b-b2f67dabbcbb"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-e9d54b7f-58d4-4474-938b-b2f67dabbcbb" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "T-cell"
synonym: "CellType: T-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "lymph node"
synonym: "Organ: lymph node" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The principal function of most T-cells is to respond to specific foreign antigens while maintaining self-tolerance.    Signaling description: T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling in naive T-cells is important to this process, which is initiated by TCR binding to antigen-MHC protein complex on the surface of antigen presenting cells. This leads to a succession of intracellular signaling events that lead to the generation of a T-cell response. Namely, LCK, a member of the Src family protein tyrosine kinases is activated which leads to the phosphorylation of CD3 coreceptor cytoplasmic domains, specifically at immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation (ITAM) motifs. These phosphorylated ITAMs serve as binding sites for ZAP70. ZAP70 is activated by LCK-mediated phosphorylation and subsequent autophosphorylation. Activated ZAP70 then phosphorylates a variety of linker/adapter proteins such as LAT and LCP2. Further signaling proteins are then recruited which results in calcium mobilization, actin cytoskeleton reorganization, and activation of Ras. In addition, a sequential three-part protein kinase cascade is initiated which consists of MAP3K, MAP2K, and MAPK. Enzymatically active transducers such as PI3K and PLCG1 help relay the signal downstream.    Outcome effects: These signals lead to the activation of transcription factors such as NF-AT and NF-kB, which ultimately result in cellular proliferation, differentiation, cytokine production, and activation-induced cell death. These signaling cascades regulate T-cell development, activation, and effector function."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The principal function of most T-cells is to respond to specific foreign antigens while maintaining self-tolerance.    Signaling description: T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling in naive T-cells is important to this process, which is initiated by TCR binding to antigen-MHC protein complex on the surface of antigen presenting cells. This leads to a succession of intracellular signaling events that lead to the generation of a T-cell response. Namely, LCK, a member of the Src family protein tyrosine kinases is activated which leads to the phosphorylation of CD3 coreceptor cytoplasmic domains, specifically at immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation (ITAM) motifs. These phosphorylated ITAMs serve as binding sites for ZAP70. ZAP70 is activated by LCK-mediated phosphorylation and subsequent autophosphorylation. Activated ZAP70 then phosphorylates a variety of linker/adapter proteins such as LAT and LCP2. Further signaling proteins are then recruited which results in calcium mobilization, actin cytoskeleton reorganization, and activation of Ras. In addition, a sequential three-part protein kinase cascade is initiated which consists of MAP3K, MAP2K, and MAPK. Enzymatically active transducers such as PI3K and PLCG1 help relay the signal downstream.    Outcome effects: These signals lead to the activation of transcription factors such as NF-AT and NF-kB, which ultimately result in cellular proliferation, differentiation, cytokine production, and activation-induced cell death. These signaling cascades regulate T-cell development, activation, and effector function." EXACT []

[Term]
