[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-382461c2-ec93-4781-bf4c-74037c21ebaf
name: Vascular Endothelial Cell Activation by NO
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:vascular_system_response ! Vascular system response
is_a: urn:agi-folder:cardiovascular_system ! Cardiovascular system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:v ! V
xref: PS:Description "Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-382461c2-ec93-4781-bf4c-74037c21ebaf"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-382461c2-ec93-4781-bf4c-74037c21ebaf" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "endothelial cell"
synonym: "CellType: endothelial cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "blood vessel"
synonym: "Organ: blood vessel" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "cardiovascular system"
synonym: "Organ_System: cardiovascular system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: The most profound sign of EC activation is the increase in nitric oxide (NO) release. NO production from ECs is stimulated by a variety of mechanical forces such as shear stress or vessel injury and soluble mediators including acetylcholine, VEGFA, bradykinin, sphingosine-1-P, H2O2, and AGT. These factors or vessel damage stimulate ECs to secrete mostly procoagulant or antifibrinolytic components such as VWF, TXA2, or SERPINE1 into the local environment, while the expression or secretion of components with anticoagulant and profibrinolytic properties is significantly reduced. In contrast to the specific activation of membrane receptors by soluble mediators, mechanical forces such as shear stress simultaneously activate many membrane proteins including receptors and mechanosensitive ion channels, via conformational changes or clustering, leading to the initiation of signal transduction.    Outcome effects: However, the downstream signaling events remain to be elucidated. NOS3 is the primary enzyme in NO synthesis in epithelial cells. NOS3 is a peripheral membrane protein that is targeted to endothelial plasmalemmal caveolae through an interaction with CAV1. HSP90AA1 binds to NOS3 and facilitates the displacement of CAV1 by calmodulin. In addition to these protein interactions that modulate calmodulin binding, other cellular signaling cascades also regulate NOS3 activity. NO is the key endothelium-derived relaxing factor that plays a pivotal role in the regulation of vascular tone and vasomotor function. In addition to its vasodilatory effect, NO inhibits leukocyte adhesion to the endothelium, maintains vascular smooth muscle in a nonproliferative state, and limits platelet aggregation. The released NO targets cysteine groups in key regulator molecules such as NF-kB. NF-kB provides the expression of factors that promote thrombocyte activation and inhibit synthesis of coagulation factors in epithelial cells. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) also activate NF-kB. When ROS signaling predominates, it leads to the activation of ECs. Finally, the activation of NOS3 and increased synthesis of NO in epithelial cells cause vasodilation and activation of microcirculation at the site of injury. Nitrogen stimulates the secretion of inflammatory mediators such as histamine, which in turn increase vascular permeability and formation of edema at the injury location.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: The most profound sign of EC activation is the increase in nitric oxide (NO) release. NO production from ECs is stimulated by a variety of mechanical forces such as shear stress or vessel injury and soluble mediators including acetylcholine, VEGFA, bradykinin, sphingosine-1-P, H2O2, and AGT. These factors or vessel damage stimulate ECs to secrete mostly procoagulant or antifibrinolytic components such as VWF, TXA2, or SERPINE1 into the local environment, while the expression or secretion of components with anticoagulant and profibrinolytic properties is significantly reduced. In contrast to the specific activation of membrane receptors by soluble mediators, mechanical forces such as shear stress simultaneously activate many membrane proteins including receptors and mechanosensitive ion channels, via conformational changes or clustering, leading to the initiation of signal transduction.    Outcome effects: However, the downstream signaling events remain to be elucidated. NOS3 is the primary enzyme in NO synthesis in epithelial cells. NOS3 is a peripheral membrane protein that is targeted to endothelial plasmalemmal caveolae through an interaction with CAV1. HSP90AA1 binds to NOS3 and facilitates the displacement of CAV1 by calmodulin. In addition to these protein interactions that modulate calmodulin binding, other cellular signaling cascades also regulate NOS3 activity. NO is the key endothelium-derived relaxing factor that plays a pivotal role in the regulation of vascular tone and vasomotor function. In addition to its vasodilatory effect, NO inhibits leukocyte adhesion to the endothelium, maintains vascular smooth muscle in a nonproliferative state, and limits platelet aggregation. The released NO targets cysteine groups in key regulator molecules such as NF-kB. NF-kB provides the expression of factors that promote thrombocyte activation and inhibit synthesis of coagulation factors in epithelial cells. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) also activate NF-kB. When ROS signaling predominates, it leads to the activation of ECs. Finally, the activation of NOS3 and increased synthesis of NO in epithelial cells cause vasodilation and activation of microcirculation at the site of injury. Nitrogen stimulates the secretion of inflammatory mediators such as histamine, which in turn increase vascular permeability and formation of edema at the injury location.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased level or activity are highlighted in red." EXACT []

