xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) and pericytes belong to the same cell lineage and both cell types are responsible for the contraction of vessels. VSMCs are found in arteries, whereas pericytes are associated with small vessels (arterioles, capillaries, and venules). Endothelial cells and pericytes constantly communicate via reciprocal exchange of growth factors. VSMCs, pericytes and ECs are required for angiogenesis and vasculogenesis and tumor neovascularization.    Signaling description: PDGFB/PDGFRB and HBEGF/EGFR/ERBB2 signaling are central in the regulation of VSMCs/pericyte proliferation and migration. They activate downstream signaling cascades, including PI3K-RAC1, PLCG, SRC pathways and others. IGF1, FGF2, SPP1 may also have an additional role in these processes. VEGFA negatively regulate VSMCs/pericyte proliferation and migration.    Outcome effects: The activation of MAPK/JUN/FOS leads to VSMCs/pericyte proliferation. The activation of actin-myosin system is necessary for the induction of migration.    Animal data: The majority of the data on VSMC and pericyte signaling have been obtained through the analysis of genetic mouse models."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) and pericytes belong to the same cell lineage and both cell types are responsible for the contraction of vessels. VSMCs are found in arteries, whereas pericytes are associated with small vessels (arterioles, capillaries, and venules). Endothelial cells and pericytes constantly communicate via reciprocal exchange of growth factors. VSMCs, pericytes and ECs are required for angiogenesis and vasculogenesis and tumor neovascularization.    Signaling description: PDGFB/PDGFRB and HBEGF/EGFR/ERBB2 signaling are central in the regulation of VSMCs/pericyte proliferation and migration. They activate downstream signaling cascades, including PI3K-RAC1, PLCG, SRC pathways and others. IGF1, FGF2, SPP1 may also have an additional role in these processes. VEGFA negatively regulate VSMCs/pericyte proliferation and migration.    Outcome effects: The activation of MAPK/JUN/FOS leads to VSMCs/pericyte proliferation. The activation of actin-myosin system is necessary for the induction of migration.    Animal data: The majority of the data on VSMC and pericyte signaling have been obtained through the analysis of genetic mouse models." EXACT []

