xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by avessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: When ECs undergo inflammatory activation via proinflammatory cytokines, the increased expression of selectins (SEL), VCAM1, and ICAM1 promotes the adherence of monocytes. Adhesion molecule expression is induced by IL1B, TNF, CD40/CD40LG interactions, CCL2, and CRP, which is produced by the liver in response to IL6. The inflammatory process and action of cytokines shifts the phenotype of epithelia cells from an anticoagulant, anti-inflammatory, and vasodilatatory phenotype to a proinflammatory and procoagulant state. Anti-inflammatory cytokines lead to the reduced expression of components with anticoagulant and profibrinolytic properties such as THBD, PROCR, and PGI2. SP1 and YBX1 most likely play an important role in this process. In contrast, cytokines lead to the increased expression of cell adhesion molecules. IL1, a cytokine secreted by activated platelets, has been shown to promote endothelial activation by inducing the expression of ICAM1, CCL2, and IL6. In addition, the binding of platelet CD40LG to its receptor, CD40 on the endothelium has been shown to induce the expression of adhesion molecules such as SELE, ICAM1, and VCAM1 in addition to cytokines including IL8, CCL2 and also tissue factor on the endothelium. Adhesion molecules are important in mediating the interaction of neutrophils and platelets with endothelium, thus also promoting the inflammatory and hemostatic responses. Adhesion molecules are synthesized by ECs and are subsequently stored in the Weibel-Palade bodies of ECs.    Outcome effects: The release of the Weibel-Palade body contents and relocation of adhesion molecules to the surface of ECs occurs due to increased intracellular calcium (Ca2+). Tissue damage, free radicals, and various mediators including anti-inflammatory cytokines may lead to the increase in Ca2 levels.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by avessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: When ECs undergo inflammatory activation via proinflammatory cytokines, the increased expression of selectins (SEL), VCAM1, and ICAM1 promotes the adherence of monocytes. Adhesion molecule expression is induced by IL1B, TNF, CD40/CD40LG interactions, CCL2, and CRP, which is produced by the liver in response to IL6. The inflammatory process and action of cytokines shifts the phenotype of epithelia cells from an anticoagulant, anti-inflammatory, and vasodilatatory phenotype to a proinflammatory and procoagulant state. Anti-inflammatory cytokines lead to the reduced expression of components with anticoagulant and profibrinolytic properties such as THBD, PROCR, and PGI2. SP1 and YBX1 most likely play an important role in this process. In contrast, cytokines lead to the increased expression of cell adhesion molecules. IL1, a cytokine secreted by activated platelets, has been shown to promote endothelial activation by inducing the expression of ICAM1, CCL2, and IL6. In addition, the binding of platelet CD40LG to its receptor, CD40 on the endothelium has been shown to induce the expression of adhesion molecules such as SELE, ICAM1, and VCAM1 in addition to cytokines including IL8, CCL2 and also tissue factor on the endothelium. Adhesion molecules are important in mediating the interaction of neutrophils and platelets with endothelium, thus also promoting the inflammatory and hemostatic responses. Adhesion molecules are synthesized by ECs and are subsequently stored in the Weibel-Palade bodies of ECs.    Outcome effects: The release of the Weibel-Palade body contents and relocation of adhesion molecules to the surface of ECs occurs due to increased intracellular calcium (Ca2+). Tissue damage, free radicals, and various mediators including anti-inflammatory cytokines may lead to the increase in Ca2 levels.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

