xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by a vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: Endothelium, forming the luminal vascular surface, is continuously interacting with coagulation factors. Coagulation factors are serine proteases and act through the cleavage of protease-activated G-protein coupled receptors (PARs) such as F2R, F2RL1, F2RL2, and F2RL3 on the surface of endothelial cells. F2RL2 does not signal through G-proteins and instead acts as a cofactor that binds and localizes thrombin for activation of F2RL3.    Outcome effects: G-proteins transmit signals through RHOA/Ras/MAPK, PI3K/AKT1 to JUN-FOS and NF-kB. An alternative mechanism by which F10 induces inflammation may rely on EPR1. Fibrinogen may activate inflammation through TLR4. Finally, the interaction of coagulation factors with endothelial cells results in the recruitment of immune cells, disruption of the endothelial cell barrier as well as migration and release of growth factors and cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by a vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: Endothelium, forming the luminal vascular surface, is continuously interacting with coagulation factors. Coagulation factors are serine proteases and act through the cleavage of protease-activated G-protein coupled receptors (PARs) such as F2R, F2RL1, F2RL2, and F2RL3 on the surface of endothelial cells. F2RL2 does not signal through G-proteins and instead acts as a cofactor that binds and localizes thrombin for activation of F2RL3.    Outcome effects: G-proteins transmit signals through RHOA/Ras/MAPK, PI3K/AKT1 to JUN-FOS and NF-kB. An alternative mechanism by which F10 induces inflammation may rely on EPR1. Fibrinogen may activate inflammation through TLR4. Finally, the interaction of coagulation factors with endothelial cells results in the recruitment of immune cells, disruption of the endothelial cell barrier as well as migration and release of growth factors and cytokines.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

