xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by a vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: VEGFA, EGF, and TGFB1 are general growth factors that activate epithelial cells in inflammation. TGFB1 stimulates the expression of endothelin, a potent blood flow control protein, in addition to the synthesis of cell adhesion molecules required for recruiting leukocytes into the site of injury. In addition, EGF and VEGFA stimulate the synthesis of nitrogen, adhesion molecules such as TXA2, and PGE2 through an increase in intracellular calcium (Ca2+), and activation of NF-kB and Jun/Fos transcription factors. Conversely, inflammation and the action of growth factors lead to downregulation of the expression of components with anticoagulant and profibrinolytic properties such as THBD, PROCR, and PGI2 in epithelial cells. SP1 and YBX1 most likely play an important role in this process. Adhesion molecules are synthesized by ECs and are subsequently stored in the Weibel-Palade bodies of ECs.    Outcome effects: Upon stimulation of platelets or ECs, the granules may fuse with the plasma membrane allowing SELP and others to translocate to the cell surface. Both Ca2+ and H+ are able to induce the secretion of Weibel-Palade bodies. Lastly, tissue damage, free radicals, and various mediators including anti-inflammatory cytokines may lead to an increase in Ca2+ levels.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vascular endothelial cells (ECs) form a single layer of simple squamous lining cells and can be activated by a vessel wall injury or soluble mediators of inflammation. The activation of signaling specific to epithelial cells of blood vessels is one of the earliest processes in inflammation.    Signaling description: VEGFA, EGF, and TGFB1 are general growth factors that activate epithelial cells in inflammation. TGFB1 stimulates the expression of endothelin, a potent blood flow control protein, in addition to the synthesis of cell adhesion molecules required for recruiting leukocytes into the site of injury. In addition, EGF and VEGFA stimulate the synthesis of nitrogen, adhesion molecules such as TXA2, and PGE2 through an increase in intracellular calcium (Ca2+), and activation of NF-kB and Jun/Fos transcription factors. Conversely, inflammation and the action of growth factors lead to downregulation of the expression of components with anticoagulant and profibrinolytic properties such as THBD, PROCR, and PGI2 in epithelial cells. SP1 and YBX1 most likely play an important role in this process. Adhesion molecules are synthesized by ECs and are subsequently stored in the Weibel-Palade bodies of ECs.    Outcome effects: Upon stimulation of platelets or ECs, the granules may fuse with the plasma membrane allowing SELP and others to translocate to the cell surface. Both Ca2+ and H+ are able to induce the secretion of Weibel-Palade bodies. Lastly, tissue damage, free radicals, and various mediators including anti-inflammatory cytokines may lead to an increase in Ca2+ levels.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

