xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) and pericytes belong to the same cell lineage and both cell types are responsible for the contraction of vessels. VSMCs are found in arteries, whereas pericytes are associated with small vessels (arterioles, capillaries, and venules). Endothelial cells and pericytes constantly communicate through reciprocal exchange of growth factors. VSMCs, pericytes and ECs are required for angiogenesis and vasculogenesis, including tumor neovascularization.    Signaling description: TGFB1 signaling has a major role in VSMCs/pericytes differentiation, proliferation and migration. Activation of ACVRL1 (ALK1) leads to the phosphorylation of SMAD1/5 and the induction of target genes (ID1/2 and others) that promote cell proliferation and migration, and it opposes VSMCs/pericytes differentiation. Activation of TGFBR1 (ALK5) in mesenchymal cells leads to the phosphorylation of SMAD2/3, thus regulating the expression of specific genes (ACTA2, MYH11, TAGLN, FN1 and others) promoting mitotic and migratory quiescence and differentiation of VSMCs/pericytes. KLF4, GATA6, SRF, MYCOD also be involved in VSMCs/pericytes differentiation.    Outcome effects: ACVRL1 signaling may dominate in the early phase of TGFB stimulation, leading to cell proliferation and migration, whereas TGFBR1 signaling dominates later in this process, leading to cell differentiation and extracellular matrix production.    Animal data: Most of the insight into molecular processes in VSMCs and pericytes comes from the analysis of genetic mouse models."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) and pericytes belong to the same cell lineage and both cell types are responsible for the contraction of vessels. VSMCs are found in arteries, whereas pericytes are associated with small vessels (arterioles, capillaries, and venules). Endothelial cells and pericytes constantly communicate through reciprocal exchange of growth factors. VSMCs, pericytes and ECs are required for angiogenesis and vasculogenesis, including tumor neovascularization.    Signaling description: TGFB1 signaling has a major role in VSMCs/pericytes differentiation, proliferation and migration. Activation of ACVRL1 (ALK1) leads to the phosphorylation of SMAD1/5 and the induction of target genes (ID1/2 and others) that promote cell proliferation and migration, and it opposes VSMCs/pericytes differentiation. Activation of TGFBR1 (ALK5) in mesenchymal cells leads to the phosphorylation of SMAD2/3, thus regulating the expression of specific genes (ACTA2, MYH11, TAGLN, FN1 and others) promoting mitotic and migratory quiescence and differentiation of VSMCs/pericytes. KLF4, GATA6, SRF, MYCOD also be involved in VSMCs/pericytes differentiation.    Outcome effects: ACVRL1 signaling may dominate in the early phase of TGFB stimulation, leading to cell proliferation and migration, whereas TGFBR1 signaling dominates later in this process, leading to cell differentiation and extracellular matrix production.    Animal data: Most of the insight into molecular processes in VSMCs and pericytes comes from the analysis of genetic mouse models." EXACT []

