xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors.    Signaling description: Phototransduction begins when a photon causes isomerization of the chromophore 11-cis-retinal into all-trans-retinal, which induces a rapid conformational change and activation of rhodopsin (RHO). The photoresponse persists until key phototransduction proteins become deactivated through regulatory enzymes.    Outcome effects: The decrease in intracellular Ca2+ during light stimulation affects the level of recoverin (RCVRN). Normally, RCVRN inhibits the ability of G protein-coupled receptor kinase (GRK1) to phosphorylate and inactivate rhodopsin (RHO). When the level of Ca2+ decreases, this inhibition fails to occur and RHO is inactivated. Subsequently, arrestin 1 (SAG) binds to RHO with high affinity and completely blocks subsequent G-protein (transducin) activation. Similarly, G-protein and PDE6 remain active until G-protein hydrolyzes GTP. This hydrolysis is catalyzed by a complex of proteins consisting of RGS9, RGS9BP, and G-beta-5-L subunit of G-protein. It has been shown that illumination causes significant fractions of transducin and RCVRN to exit rod outer segments, whereas SAG translocates in the opposite direction. It is hypothesized that SAG is transported by microtubules and this reaction may be induced by PLC/PKC signaling.    Outcome effects: The final point of photoreceptor cell recovery is restoration of cGMP levels by guanylate cyclases to its dark level.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors.    Signaling description: Phototransduction begins when a photon causes isomerization of the chromophore 11-cis-retinal into all-trans-retinal, which induces a rapid conformational change and activation of rhodopsin (RHO). The photoresponse persists until key phototransduction proteins become deactivated through regulatory enzymes.    Outcome effects: The decrease in intracellular Ca2+ during light stimulation affects the level of recoverin (RCVRN). Normally, RCVRN inhibits the ability of G protein-coupled receptor kinase (GRK1) to phosphorylate and inactivate rhodopsin (RHO). When the level of Ca2+ decreases, this inhibition fails to occur and RHO is inactivated. Subsequently, arrestin 1 (SAG) binds to RHO with high affinity and completely blocks subsequent G-protein (transducin) activation. Similarly, G-protein and PDE6 remain active until G-protein hydrolyzes GTP. This hydrolysis is catalyzed by a complex of proteins consisting of RGS9, RGS9BP, and G-beta-5-L subunit of G-protein. It has been shown that illumination causes significant fractions of transducin and RCVRN to exit rod outer segments, whereas SAG translocates in the opposite direction. It is hypothesized that SAG is transported by microtubules and this reaction may be induced by PLC/PKC signaling.    Outcome effects: The final point of photoreceptor cell recovery is restoration of cGMP levels by guanylate cyclases to its dark level.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

