xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors.    Signaling description: Phototransduction begins when a photon causes isomerization of the chromophore 11-cis-retinal into all-trans-retinal, which induces a rapid conformational change and activation of rhodopsin (RHO). RHO activates molecules of the G-protein transducin by catalyzing GDP/GTP exchange on the transducin alpha-subunit, G-alpha-t. Further, G-alpha-t-GTP separates from the transducing beta/gamma-subunits and binds to the gamma-subunit of its effector, cGMP phosphodiesterase 6 (PDE6), which releases the subunit's inhibitory effect on the catalytic alpha- and beta-subunits of PDE6. Activated PDE6 rapidly hydrolyzes cGMP, thereby reducing its concentration in the cytoplasm and causes closure of cGMP-gated cation channels (CNGA1 and CNGB1) in the plasma membrane. The level of Ca2+ decreases in light because its influx is reduced when cGMP-gated channels close, whereas Ca2+ efflux via the Ca2+/K+/Na+ exchanger continues.    Outcome effects: The resulting closure of the cGMP-sensitive cation channels blocks the dark current and hyperpolarizes the photoreceptor membrane. The rate of glutamate release from the synapses is reduced, thus converting and relaying the light signal to the postsynaptic neurons as electrical signal. The reduction of intracellular Ca2+ is sensed by several different Ca2+-binding proteins including guanylate cyclase activators 1A/B (GUCA1A and GUCA1B), which stimulate cGMP synthesis by GUCY2D and GUCY2F when Ca2+ decreases. The reduction in intracellular Ca2+ also affects other Ca+-binding proteins including recoverin (RCVRN). Normally, RCVRN inhibits the ability of G protein-coupled receptor kinase (GRK1) to phosphorylate and inactivate RHO. When the level of Ca2+ decreases, this inhibition fails to occur and RHO is inactivated.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors.    Signaling description: Phototransduction begins when a photon causes isomerization of the chromophore 11-cis-retinal into all-trans-retinal, which induces a rapid conformational change and activation of rhodopsin (RHO). RHO activates molecules of the G-protein transducin by catalyzing GDP/GTP exchange on the transducin alpha-subunit, G-alpha-t. Further, G-alpha-t-GTP separates from the transducing beta/gamma-subunits and binds to the gamma-subunit of its effector, cGMP phosphodiesterase 6 (PDE6), which releases the subunit's inhibitory effect on the catalytic alpha- and beta-subunits of PDE6. Activated PDE6 rapidly hydrolyzes cGMP, thereby reducing its concentration in the cytoplasm and causes closure of cGMP-gated cation channels (CNGA1 and CNGB1) in the plasma membrane. The level of Ca2+ decreases in light because its influx is reduced when cGMP-gated channels close, whereas Ca2+ efflux via the Ca2+/K+/Na+ exchanger continues.    Outcome effects: The resulting closure of the cGMP-sensitive cation channels blocks the dark current and hyperpolarizes the photoreceptor membrane. The rate of glutamate release from the synapses is reduced, thus converting and relaying the light signal to the postsynaptic neurons as electrical signal. The reduction of intracellular Ca2+ is sensed by several different Ca2+-binding proteins including guanylate cyclase activators 1A/B (GUCA1A and GUCA1B), which stimulate cGMP synthesis by GUCY2D and GUCY2F when Ca2+ decreases. The reduction in intracellular Ca2+ also affects other Ca+-binding proteins including recoverin (RCVRN). Normally, RCVRN inhibits the ability of G protein-coupled receptor kinase (GRK1) to phosphorylate and inactivate RHO. When the level of Ca2+ decreases, this inhibition fails to occur and RHO is inactivated.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

