xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors. Cone cells, or cones, are photoreceptor cells in the retina of the eye, which are responsible for color vision. They function best in relatively bright light, as opposed to rod cells that work better in dim light.    Signaling description: Humans normally have three kinds of cones. The first type of cones peak at the long-wave red color and contain long-wave-sensitive opsin 1 (OPN1LW). Cones of the second type peak at the green part of the spectrum and contain medium-wave-sensitive opsins 1 (OPN1MW and OPN1MW2). The third type of cones peak at the blue part of the spectrum and contain short-wave-sensitive opsin 1 (OPN1SW). As their visual pigment is destroyed in the process of photoactivation, the continuous function of cones imposes the need for rapid recycling of their chromophore. Cones recycle chromophore and restore their activity much faster than rods do. This is why the visual cycle in rods includes an alternative visual cycle in addition to the canonical one (the canonical visual cycle is the same for rods). Following photolysis, all-trans-retinal is reduced to all-trans-retinol (vitamin A) by retinol dehydrogenase RDH8 and DHRS3. Vitamin A is then released and transported across the interphotoreceptor matrix (IPM) to Muller cells, with the help of RBP3.    Outcome effects: In Muller cells, vitamin A is isomerized directly to 11-cis-retinol by retinol isomerase following binding with RBP1. 11-cis-retinol is released from Muller cells to the IPM and transported to the cone. 11-cis retinol is oxidized to 11-cis retinal by an unidentified 11-cis RDH retinol dehydrogenase."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors. Cone cells, or cones, are photoreceptor cells in the retina of the eye, which are responsible for color vision. They function best in relatively bright light, as opposed to rod cells that work better in dim light.    Signaling description: Humans normally have three kinds of cones. The first type of cones peak at the long-wave red color and contain long-wave-sensitive opsin 1 (OPN1LW). Cones of the second type peak at the green part of the spectrum and contain medium-wave-sensitive opsins 1 (OPN1MW and OPN1MW2). The third type of cones peak at the blue part of the spectrum and contain short-wave-sensitive opsin 1 (OPN1SW). As their visual pigment is destroyed in the process of photoactivation, the continuous function of cones imposes the need for rapid recycling of their chromophore. Cones recycle chromophore and restore their activity much faster than rods do. This is why the visual cycle in rods includes an alternative visual cycle in addition to the canonical one (the canonical visual cycle is the same for rods). Following photolysis, all-trans-retinal is reduced to all-trans-retinol (vitamin A) by retinol dehydrogenase RDH8 and DHRS3. Vitamin A is then released and transported across the interphotoreceptor matrix (IPM) to Muller cells, with the help of RBP3.    Outcome effects: In Muller cells, vitamin A is isomerized directly to 11-cis-retinol by retinol isomerase following binding with RBP1. 11-cis-retinol is released from Muller cells to the IPM and transported to the cone. 11-cis retinol is oxidized to 11-cis retinal by an unidentified 11-cis RDH retinol dehydrogenase." EXACT []

