xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Rod cells, or rods, are photoreceptor cells in the retina of the eye that are very sensitive to light (can be induced by a single photon) and support vision at low light levels.    Signaling description: Rods are concentrated at the outer edges of the retina and are used in peripheral vision. As their visual pigment is destroyed in the process of photoactivation, the continuous function of rods imposes the need for rapid recycling of their chromophore and regeneration of their pigment. The visual cycle is the biological conversion of a photon into an electrical signal in the retina. The absorption of a photon by a rhodopsin (RHO) pigment molecule induces the isomerization of 11-cis-retinal to all-trans-retinal resulting in activated metarhodopsin II.    Outcome effects: The decay of metarhodopsin II yields apo-opsin and free all-trans-retinal. The translocation of all-trans-retinal from the disc membrane to the cytoplasmic space is facilitated by an ATP-binding cassette transporter ABCA4. All-trans-retinal is reduced to all-trans-retinol (vitamin A) by retinol dehydrogenases (RDH). Vitamin A is transported from rods to retinal pigment epithelial cells (RPEs) by RBP3. Vitamin A also may come to RPE from the blood stream through STRA6. In RPEs, vitamin A is esterified to a fatty acid by lecithin retinol acyltransferase (LRAT). The isomerase, RPE-specific 65 kDa protein (RPE65) uses an all-trans-retinyl ester as a substrate to form 11-cis-retinol. 11-cis-retinol binds to the cellular retinaldehyde-binding protein (RLBP1). 11-cis-retinol is oxidized to 11-cis-retinal by retinol dehydrogenases (RDH). Non-photoreceptor RGR opsin also takes part in this process. 11-cis- retinol is released by the RPE and taken up by the rod, where it is incorporated into RHO."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Rod cells, or rods, are photoreceptor cells in the retina of the eye that are very sensitive to light (can be induced by a single photon) and support vision at low light levels.    Signaling description: Rods are concentrated at the outer edges of the retina and are used in peripheral vision. As their visual pigment is destroyed in the process of photoactivation, the continuous function of rods imposes the need for rapid recycling of their chromophore and regeneration of their pigment. The visual cycle is the biological conversion of a photon into an electrical signal in the retina. The absorption of a photon by a rhodopsin (RHO) pigment molecule induces the isomerization of 11-cis-retinal to all-trans-retinal resulting in activated metarhodopsin II.    Outcome effects: The decay of metarhodopsin II yields apo-opsin and free all-trans-retinal. The translocation of all-trans-retinal from the disc membrane to the cytoplasmic space is facilitated by an ATP-binding cassette transporter ABCA4. All-trans-retinal is reduced to all-trans-retinol (vitamin A) by retinol dehydrogenases (RDH). Vitamin A is transported from rods to retinal pigment epithelial cells (RPEs) by RBP3. Vitamin A also may come to RPE from the blood stream through STRA6. In RPEs, vitamin A is esterified to a fatty acid by lecithin retinol acyltransferase (LRAT). The isomerase, RPE-specific 65 kDa protein (RPE65) uses an all-trans-retinyl ester as a substrate to form 11-cis-retinol. 11-cis-retinol binds to the cellular retinaldehyde-binding protein (RLBP1). 11-cis-retinol is oxidized to 11-cis-retinal by retinol dehydrogenases (RDH). Non-photoreceptor RGR opsin also takes part in this process. 11-cis- retinol is released by the RPE and taken up by the rod, where it is incorporated into RHO." EXACT []

[Term]
