synonym: "Organ_System: sensory system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors.    Signaling description: The vertebrate retina comprises two different types of photoreceptors including rods and cones. There is a general agreement that the basic molecular mechanism of excitation is similar in both classes of photoreceptors. Photoreceptor cells are unusual in that they depolarize in response to the absence of stimuli or scotopic conditions (darkness). The intracellular cGMP levels are critical for phototransduction. In the photoreceptor cells, cGMP is synthesized by membrane guanylate cyclases (GUCY2D and GUCY2F) and hydrolyzed by PDE6. In the dark, photoreceptor rhodopsin (RHO) dissociates from G-proteins, and G-protein alpha dissociates from PDE6. Thus, PDE6 is inactive and cGMP levels are elevated. This activates cyclic nucleotide gated channels (CNGA1 and CNGB1), which allows a steady inward Ca2+ and Na+ current (known as \"dark current\").    Outcome effects: The dark current keeps the cell depolarized and causes the vesicles containing glutamate to merge with the cell membrane, therefore releasing the neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, which transduces the signal to retinal bipolar cells.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Phototransduction is the conversion of light to electrical nerve signals in the outer segments of photoreceptors.    Signaling description: The vertebrate retina comprises two different types of photoreceptors including rods and cones. There is a general agreement that the basic molecular mechanism of excitation is similar in both classes of photoreceptors. Photoreceptor cells are unusual in that they depolarize in response to the absence of stimuli or scotopic conditions (darkness). The intracellular cGMP levels are critical for phototransduction. In the photoreceptor cells, cGMP is synthesized by membrane guanylate cyclases (GUCY2D and GUCY2F) and hydrolyzed by PDE6. In the dark, photoreceptor rhodopsin (RHO) dissociates from G-proteins, and G-protein alpha dissociates from PDE6. Thus, PDE6 is inactive and cGMP levels are elevated. This activates cyclic nucleotide gated channels (CNGA1 and CNGB1), which allows a steady inward Ca2+ and Na+ current (known as \"dark current\").    Outcome effects: The dark current keeps the cell depolarized and causes the vesicles containing glutamate to merge with the cell membrane, therefore releasing the neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, which transduces the signal to retinal bipolar cells.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with decreased level are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

