xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Physiological responses to stressors, such as inflammation, are principally mediated by two neuroendocrine systems: the sympathetic nervous system (SNS) and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) system.    Signaling description: The SNS is responsible for the acute stress response, which requires energy mobilization from the body stores and diverts the blood flow to skeletal muscles. The SNS effects occur due to the release of noradrenaline (also known as norepinephrine) from the sympathetic nerve terminals and the subsequent secretion of epinephrine (also known as adrenaline) and noradrenaline from chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. Chromaffin cells are similar to postsynaptic sympathetic neurons and release catecholamines (80% epinephrine and 20% noradrenaline) into the circulation. Acetylcholine, the key neurotransmitter in chromaffin cells, binds to postsynaptic nAChR and mAChR on chromaffin cells. nAChR and mAChR are differentially expressed in two separate populations of chromaffin cells (adrenergic and noradrenergic, respectively), which release different substances (epinephrine and noradrenaline). The cells with nAChR are more abundant. The activation of nAChR quickly leads to chromaffin cell depolarization, which in turn triggers electrical activity and calcium (Ca2+) influx from voltage-gated Ca2+ channels.    Outcome effects: Increases in cytosolic Ca2+ lead to the fusion of secretory granules and exocytosis of epinephrine/noradrenaline into the bloodstream. mAChR causes a slow release of Ca2+ from the intracellular storage. ADCYAP1 causes persistent catecholamine release by the adrenal medulla. ADCYAP1 response includes PLC and PKC activation and Na+/Ca2+ exchange-mediated depolarization in chromaffin cells allowing LVACC Ca2+ channels to release catecholamine. In addition, angiotensin II is a potent regulator of catecholamine synthesis and release. Angiotensinogen (AGT), which is produced by the liver, does not appear to be an acute-phase protein. However, some forms of acute inflammation, such as an injection of lipopolysaccharide/cellulite or partial hepatectomy, increases the hepatic synthesis of AGT. AGT is converted to the active form, angiotensin II, by renin (REN) and ACE. The synthesis of catecholamine occurs in advance, and NGF serves as a mitogen for chromaffin cells which may be involved in this process. Catecholamines, such as noradrenaline and epinephrine, mediate adaptive cardiovascular and metabolic effects through interactions with adrenergic receptors. Alpha adrenergic receptors generally produce excitatory responses of smooth muscle in which they are located. Beta adrenergic receptors generally produce inhibitory responses of smooth muscle in response to bronchodilation, vasodilation, and uterine relaxation. ADRB1 produces cardiac positive responses and produces lipolysis in adipose tissue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Physiological responses to stressors, such as inflammation, are principally mediated by two neuroendocrine systems: the sympathetic nervous system (SNS) and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) system.    Signaling description: The SNS is responsible for the acute stress response, which requires energy mobilization from the body stores and diverts the blood flow to skeletal muscles. The SNS effects occur due to the release of noradrenaline (also known as norepinephrine) from the sympathetic nerve terminals and the subsequent secretion of epinephrine (also known as adrenaline) and noradrenaline from chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. Chromaffin cells are similar to postsynaptic sympathetic neurons and release catecholamines (80% epinephrine and 20% noradrenaline) into the circulation. Acetylcholine, the key neurotransmitter in chromaffin cells, binds to postsynaptic nAChR and mAChR on chromaffin cells. nAChR and mAChR are differentially expressed in two separate populations of chromaffin cells (adrenergic and noradrenergic, respectively), which release different substances (epinephrine and noradrenaline). The cells with nAChR are more abundant. The activation of nAChR quickly leads to chromaffin cell depolarization, which in turn triggers electrical activity and calcium (Ca2+) influx from voltage-gated Ca2+ channels.    Outcome effects: Increases in cytosolic Ca2+ lead to the fusion of secretory granules and exocytosis of epinephrine/noradrenaline into the bloodstream. mAChR causes a slow release of Ca2+ from the intracellular storage. ADCYAP1 causes persistent catecholamine release by the adrenal medulla. ADCYAP1 response includes PLC and PKC activation and Na+/Ca2+ exchange-mediated depolarization in chromaffin cells allowing LVACC Ca2+ channels to release catecholamine. In addition, angiotensin II is a potent regulator of catecholamine synthesis and release. Angiotensinogen (AGT), which is produced by the liver, does not appear to be an acute-phase protein. However, some forms of acute inflammation, such as an injection of lipopolysaccharide/cellulite or partial hepatectomy, increases the hepatic synthesis of AGT. AGT is converted to the active form, angiotensin II, by renin (REN) and ACE. The synthesis of catecholamine occurs in advance, and NGF serves as a mitogen for chromaffin cells which may be involved in this process. Catecholamines, such as noradrenaline and epinephrine, mediate adaptive cardiovascular and metabolic effects through interactions with adrenergic receptors. Alpha adrenergic receptors generally produce excitatory responses of smooth muscle in which they are located. Beta adrenergic receptors generally produce inhibitory responses of smooth muscle in response to bronchodilation, vasodilation, and uterine relaxation. ADRB1 produces cardiac positive responses and produces lipolysis in adipose tissue." EXACT []

