xref: PS:Source "Receptor Signaling"
synonym: "Source: Receptor Signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Regulatory T-cells (Tregs) are specialized population of T cells that are engaged in sustaining immunological self-tolerance and homeostasis. Tregs are defined by expression of the transcription factor forkhead box protein 3 (FOXP3).    Signaling description: TGFB1 plays a particularly important role in Tregs differentiation. Additionally, IL-2 and retinoic acid (RA) facilitate induction of FOXP3. The induction of functional FOXP3 Treg cells by the concurrent activation of TGFB1 and AHR signaling is mediated, at least partially, by the transcription factors SMAD1 and AIOLOS (IKZF3). SMAD1 alone or in combination with SMAD3/4 regulates FOXP3 expression. Dendritic cells (DCs), which produce RA and TGFB1, are potent inducers of Foxp3 expression in activated T-cells. Treatment of DCs with the AHR ligand promotes the expression of the retinal dehydrogenases ALDH1A1 that enhances RA secretion. Notably, RA forms a complex with the nuclear receptors, RA receptors (RARs) and retinoid X receptors (RXRs) that controls the transactivation of FOXP3 promoter in coordination with SMAD3, thereby promoting Treg differentiation. Kynurenine, which is produced when tryptophan is catabolized by indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase (IDO), is an AHR agonist that is important for generating Tregs and inhibiting Th17 cell development. DCs produce IDO, and through the production of tryptophan metabolites preferentially induce Tregs. Dietary metabolites are another important environmental factor that influence Treg differentiation and function, especially in the gut. AHR ligands and promote Tregs include indole-3-carbanole and 3,3'-diindolylmethane, derived from cruciferous vegetables. Tryptophan metabolite, cinnabarinic acid, has been identified as a novel AHR ligand.Ouctome effects: Tregs produce IL-10 that oppose function of Th17 cells. IKZF3 interacts with FOXP3 and mediates the repression of IL-2 expression."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Regulatory T-cells (Tregs) are specialized population of T cells that are engaged in sustaining immunological self-tolerance and homeostasis. Tregs are defined by expression of the transcription factor forkhead box protein 3 (FOXP3).    Signaling description: TGFB1 plays a particularly important role in Tregs differentiation. Additionally, IL-2 and retinoic acid (RA) facilitate induction of FOXP3. The induction of functional FOXP3 Treg cells by the concurrent activation of TGFB1 and AHR signaling is mediated, at least partially, by the transcription factors SMAD1 and AIOLOS (IKZF3). SMAD1 alone or in combination with SMAD3/4 regulates FOXP3 expression. Dendritic cells (DCs), which produce RA and TGFB1, are potent inducers of Foxp3 expression in activated T-cells. Treatment of DCs with the AHR ligand promotes the expression of the retinal dehydrogenases ALDH1A1 that enhances RA secretion. Notably, RA forms a complex with the nuclear receptors, RA receptors (RARs) and retinoid X receptors (RXRs) that controls the transactivation of FOXP3 promoter in coordination with SMAD3, thereby promoting Treg differentiation. Kynurenine, which is produced when tryptophan is catabolized by indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase (IDO), is an AHR agonist that is important for generating Tregs and inhibiting Th17 cell development. DCs produce IDO, and through the production of tryptophan metabolites preferentially induce Tregs. Dietary metabolites are another important environmental factor that influence Treg differentiation and function, especially in the gut. AHR ligands and promote Tregs include indole-3-carbanole and 3,3'-diindolylmethane, derived from cruciferous vegetables. Tryptophan metabolite, cinnabarinic acid, has been identified as a novel AHR ligand.Ouctome effects: Tregs produce IL-10 that oppose function of Th17 cells. IKZF3 interacts with FOXP3 and mediates the repression of IL-2 expression." EXACT []

