xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) is a ligand-activated helix-loop-helix transcription factor involved in the regulation of biological responses to planar aromatic hydrocarbons     Signaling description: Tryptophan (trp) metabolites are important endogenous AHR ligands. Most of the free trp in mammals is oxidized along the kynurenine pathway. The first and rate-limiting step in this series of reactions is catalyzed by indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase 1/2 (IDO1/2) and tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase (TDO2), which produce AHR ligand kynurenine. Importantly, IDO1 and IDO2 can be upregulated upon AHR suggesting that AHR activation may result in a positive feedback loop that prolongs and/or amplifies AHR signaling. The downstream metabolites kynurenic acid and xanthurenic acid also act as AHR ligands. The IDO/TDO-independent tryptamine pathway, catalyzed by tryptophan hydroxylase (TPH1/2), and dopamine decarboxylase (DDC) also has been implicated in AHR activation. The most proximal metabolite in that pathway, tryptamine, is a potent AHR activator and may be both a direct AHR ligand and a precursor of a downstream AHR ligands. Of note, tryptamine is a competitive CYP1A1 substrate, a result consistent with the prediction that endogenous AHR ligand is degraded by CYP1 enzymes. In addition, the serotonin pathway is connected to AHR activation. 5-Hydroxytryptophan, a proximal serotonin metabolite, is an AHR agonist of modest potency. Serotonin itself is a weak AHR activator. 6-formylindolo[3,2-b]carbazole (FICZ) and 1-(2H-Indol-3-yl)-9H-pyrido(3,4-b)indole (eudistomin U) are photoproducts of trp and act as AHR ligands.    Outcome effects: FICZ is readily metabolized by CYP1A1, CYP1A2, and CYP1B1. DAO and aspartate aminotransferase take part in metabolizing of trp to indolyl-3-pyruvate, AHR ligand. In the gut, the indole derivative 3-Formylindole, which is generated through conversion from dietary tryptophan by commensal lactobacilli, acts as endogenous ligand for AHR. Other tryptophan metabolites produced by commensal gut flora also may act as AHR ligands."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) is a ligand-activated helix-loop-helix transcription factor involved in the regulation of biological responses to planar aromatic hydrocarbons     Signaling description: Tryptophan (trp) metabolites are important endogenous AHR ligands. Most of the free trp in mammals is oxidized along the kynurenine pathway. The first and rate-limiting step in this series of reactions is catalyzed by indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase 1/2 (IDO1/2) and tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase (TDO2), which produce AHR ligand kynurenine. Importantly, IDO1 and IDO2 can be upregulated upon AHR suggesting that AHR activation may result in a positive feedback loop that prolongs and/or amplifies AHR signaling. The downstream metabolites kynurenic acid and xanthurenic acid also act as AHR ligands. The IDO/TDO-independent tryptamine pathway, catalyzed by tryptophan hydroxylase (TPH1/2), and dopamine decarboxylase (DDC) also has been implicated in AHR activation. The most proximal metabolite in that pathway, tryptamine, is a potent AHR activator and may be both a direct AHR ligand and a precursor of a downstream AHR ligands. Of note, tryptamine is a competitive CYP1A1 substrate, a result consistent with the prediction that endogenous AHR ligand is degraded by CYP1 enzymes. In addition, the serotonin pathway is connected to AHR activation. 5-Hydroxytryptophan, a proximal serotonin metabolite, is an AHR agonist of modest potency. Serotonin itself is a weak AHR activator. 6-formylindolo[3,2-b]carbazole (FICZ) and 1-(2H-Indol-3-yl)-9H-pyrido(3,4-b)indole (eudistomin U) are photoproducts of trp and act as AHR ligands.    Outcome effects: FICZ is readily metabolized by CYP1A1, CYP1A2, and CYP1B1. DAO and aspartate aminotransferase take part in metabolizing of trp to indolyl-3-pyruvate, AHR ligand. In the gut, the indole derivative 3-Formylindole, which is generated through conversion from dietary tryptophan by commensal lactobacilli, acts as endogenous ligand for AHR. Other tryptophan metabolites produced by commensal gut flora also may act as AHR ligands." EXACT []

