synonym: "Source: Receptor Signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) is a ligand-activated helix-loop-helix transcription factor involved in the regulation of biological responses to planar aromatic hydrocarbons     Signaling description: The inactive, cytosolic Aryl Hydrocarbon Receptor (AHR) is part of a protein complex that includes the 90-kDa heat shock protein (HSP90), the SRC protein kinase, and the AHR-interacting protein AIP. Ligand binding triggers conformational changes, dissociation of the AhR/HSP90/SRC complex and translocation of the AHR to the nucleus.    Outcome effects: Once in the nucleus, the AHR establishes protein-protein interactions with the AHR nuclear translocator (ARNT) and with additional coactivators and transcription factors, such as NCOA and GTF2B. AHR interacts with transcription factors with well-characterized functions in the immune system and in other organ systems e.g., MAF, ESR1/2, AR, E2F, RB1, RELB, STAT and others (genomic action). In addition to its direct transcriptional regulation of target genes, the AHR also has been shown to control cellular processes through nongenomic signaling pathways. One of these pathways results from the release of SRC from the AHR/HSP90/SRC complex, enabling SRC to then target multiple cellular targets via interaction with EGFR and downstream MAPK signaling. Transcription of AHR repressor AHRR is controlled by AHR."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) is a ligand-activated helix-loop-helix transcription factor involved in the regulation of biological responses to planar aromatic hydrocarbons     Signaling description: The inactive, cytosolic Aryl Hydrocarbon Receptor (AHR) is part of a protein complex that includes the 90-kDa heat shock protein (HSP90), the SRC protein kinase, and the AHR-interacting protein AIP. Ligand binding triggers conformational changes, dissociation of the AhR/HSP90/SRC complex and translocation of the AHR to the nucleus.    Outcome effects: Once in the nucleus, the AHR establishes protein-protein interactions with the AHR nuclear translocator (ARNT) and with additional coactivators and transcription factors, such as NCOA and GTF2B. AHR interacts with transcription factors with well-characterized functions in the immune system and in other organ systems e.g., MAF, ESR1/2, AR, E2F, RB1, RELB, STAT and others (genomic action). In addition to its direct transcriptional regulation of target genes, the AHR also has been shown to control cellular processes through nongenomic signaling pathways. One of these pathways results from the release of SRC from the AHR/HSP90/SRC complex, enabling SRC to then target multiple cellular targets via interaction with EGFR and downstream MAPK signaling. Transcription of AHR repressor AHRR is controlled by AHR." EXACT []

