xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) 2 is a cell surface molecule responsible for the transmission of exogenous antigens. Antigens are protein fragments that invade the cell by endocytosis and then undergo proteolysis.    Signaling description: Exogenous antigens are usually presented on MHC2 molecules, which interact with CD4 helper T-cells to activate them. MHC2 is synthesized in the Golgi apparatus. During MHC2 fermentative processing, fragment CLIP (CD74) is separated. HLA-DM binds to MHC2-CD74 complexes, catalyzing the release of CD74 molecule, which then allows binding of the antigenic peptides to the MHC2 groove. HLA-DO may regulate the DM-catalyzed removal of CD74. Exogenous antigens invade the cell by endocytosis and then undergo proteolysis. An endosome fuses with a lysosome, the pH decreases, and acidic proteases digest antigens into peptide fragments. Increased intra-vesicular pH stops the processing. Peptides are then carried to the Golgi apparatus where they bind to MHC2.    Outcome effects: Further, MHC2 and the peptide complex migrate through the Golgi vesicles to the outer cell membrane. MHC2 and the peptide complex activate CD4 T-cell differentiation and mediate an immune response."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) 2 is a cell surface molecule responsible for the transmission of exogenous antigens. Antigens are protein fragments that invade the cell by endocytosis and then undergo proteolysis.    Signaling description: Exogenous antigens are usually presented on MHC2 molecules, which interact with CD4 helper T-cells to activate them. MHC2 is synthesized in the Golgi apparatus. During MHC2 fermentative processing, fragment CLIP (CD74) is separated. HLA-DM binds to MHC2-CD74 complexes, catalyzing the release of CD74 molecule, which then allows binding of the antigenic peptides to the MHC2 groove. HLA-DO may regulate the DM-catalyzed removal of CD74. Exogenous antigens invade the cell by endocytosis and then undergo proteolysis. An endosome fuses with a lysosome, the pH decreases, and acidic proteases digest antigens into peptide fragments. Increased intra-vesicular pH stops the processing. Peptides are then carried to the Golgi apparatus where they bind to MHC2.    Outcome effects: Further, MHC2 and the peptide complex migrate through the Golgi vesicles to the outer cell membrane. MHC2 and the peptide complex activate CD4 T-cell differentiation and mediate an immune response." EXACT []

