synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Antigen processing and presentation is a specific immune response to an immunogenic peptide epitope. Antigens initiate certain processes within the cell that result in the proteolysis of infectious proteins (antigen processing) and presentation of their epitopes on the surface of cells (antigen presentation). The result of these actions is the induction of a T-cell response that recruits and engages other molecular participants of the immune response.    Signaling description: The endogenous/intracellular antigen pathway is connected to the processing of intracellular peptide fragments through the action of the ubiquitin-related proteolytic system and MHC class I protein complex. Viral peptides are also presented if a virus have infected the cell. Intracellular proteins are cut into short peptides in the proteasome and proteins bound for degradation are targeted to the proteasome by covalent linkage with ubiquitin. Proteasomes break the protein up into peptides suitable for fitting within MHC class I protein complex. Peptides generated in the cytosol are then transported into the rough endoplasmic reticulum to enable interaction with MHC molecules. TAPs (transporters associated with antigen processing) transport peptides into the rough endoplasmic reticulum. MHC class I molecules are tethered to the TAP transporter via TAPBP. Also, several chaperone proteins including CALR, HSPA5, and PDIA3 within rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER) facilitate the proper folding of MHC class I molecules and its association with beta-2-microglobulin (B2M).    Outcome effects: Once the peptide is transported to the rough ER lumen, it binds to the cleft of the awaiting MHC class I molecule, stabilizing it and allowing it to be transported to the cell surface by the Golgi apparatus. MHC class I molecules on the cell surface are specialized for the presentation of peptides derived from endogenous proteins (intracellular antigens) to the T-cell receptor (TCR) of CD8 T-cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Antigen processing and presentation is a specific immune response to an immunogenic peptide epitope. Antigens initiate certain processes within the cell that result in the proteolysis of infectious proteins (antigen processing) and presentation of their epitopes on the surface of cells (antigen presentation). The result of these actions is the induction of a T-cell response that recruits and engages other molecular participants of the immune response.    Signaling description: The endogenous/intracellular antigen pathway is connected to the processing of intracellular peptide fragments through the action of the ubiquitin-related proteolytic system and MHC class I protein complex. Viral peptides are also presented if a virus have infected the cell. Intracellular proteins are cut into short peptides in the proteasome and proteins bound for degradation are targeted to the proteasome by covalent linkage with ubiquitin. Proteasomes break the protein up into peptides suitable for fitting within MHC class I protein complex. Peptides generated in the cytosol are then transported into the rough endoplasmic reticulum to enable interaction with MHC molecules. TAPs (transporters associated with antigen processing) transport peptides into the rough endoplasmic reticulum. MHC class I molecules are tethered to the TAP transporter via TAPBP. Also, several chaperone proteins including CALR, HSPA5, and PDIA3 within rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER) facilitate the proper folding of MHC class I molecules and its association with beta-2-microglobulin (B2M).    Outcome effects: Once the peptide is transported to the rough ER lumen, it binds to the cleft of the awaiting MHC class I molecule, stabilizing it and allowing it to be transported to the cell surface by the Golgi apparatus. MHC class I molecules on the cell surface are specialized for the presentation of peptides derived from endogenous proteins (intracellular antigens) to the T-cell receptor (TCR) of CD8 T-cells." EXACT []

