xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) 1 is a cell surface molecule that is responsible for the transmission of intracellular antigens. Antigens are fragments of proteins from other organisms or self-proteins in the case of autoimmune diseases.    Signaling description: Intracellular antigens are mainly produced by viruses replicating within a host cell and synthesized proteins such as self-antigens and cancer antigens. The process of MHC1-mediated antigen presentation is initiated when the cell takes over antigens and degrades them into peptides in the proteasome. Proteasomal action alone is not sufficient for peptide generation, therefore TPP2 is also required. Peptides are transported into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) by TAP1 and TAP2. TAPs deliver the peptides to the ER for binding to the MHC1 groove. The chaperones, calnexin (CANX) and calreticulin (CALR) bind to MHC1 and the peptide complex assembly. Other chaperones maintain MHC1 as it binds to the TAP complex via tapasin (TAPBP).    Outcome effects: Subsequently, MHC1 and the peptide complex migrate through Golgi vesicles to the outer cell membrane. Cell surface MHC1 and the peptide complex, when connected to the CD8 and NK cells, activate their differentiation and mediate apoptosis of the infected target cell."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) 1 is a cell surface molecule that is responsible for the transmission of intracellular antigens. Antigens are fragments of proteins from other organisms or self-proteins in the case of autoimmune diseases.    Signaling description: Intracellular antigens are mainly produced by viruses replicating within a host cell and synthesized proteins such as self-antigens and cancer antigens. The process of MHC1-mediated antigen presentation is initiated when the cell takes over antigens and degrades them into peptides in the proteasome. Proteasomal action alone is not sufficient for peptide generation, therefore TPP2 is also required. Peptides are transported into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) by TAP1 and TAP2. TAPs deliver the peptides to the ER for binding to the MHC1 groove. The chaperones, calnexin (CANX) and calreticulin (CALR) bind to MHC1 and the peptide complex assembly. Other chaperones maintain MHC1 as it binds to the TAP complex via tapasin (TAPBP).    Outcome effects: Subsequently, MHC1 and the peptide complex migrate through Golgi vesicles to the outer cell membrane. Cell surface MHC1 and the peptide complex, when connected to the CD8 and NK cells, activate their differentiation and mediate apoptosis of the infected target cell." EXACT []

