synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "brain"
synonym: "Organ: brain" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "nerve tissue"
synonym: "Tissue: nerve tissue" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "nervous system"
synonym: "Organ_System: nervous system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The principal means of communication for all neurons is the release of chemical signals at spatially defined locations: synapses for neurons in the brain, or the vasculature for neurons with neuroendocrine terminals. Neuroendocrine neurons generate action potentials upon appropriate stimulation. These action potentials are responsible for the calcium-dependent release of neurotransmitters at neuroendocrine terminals into the vasculature. Targets of neuroendocrine signals are located at some distance from the release sites, and the transmission path is the vasculature rather than the synapse. Adrenergic neuroendocrine neurons must therefore release larger amounts of signal than those that communicate using synaptically released small molecule transmitters.    Signaling description: In neuroendocrine neurons, secretion is dependent upon membrane depolarization and Ca2+ entry. Neurotransmitter norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is secreted as a result of tyrosine hydroxylase/dopa decarboxylase/dopamine beta-hydroxylase (TH/DOC/DBH) cascade. The vesicular monoamine transporter (SLC18A2) activity is necessary for the uptake of norepinephrine into secretory vesicles. The following exocytosis of secretory vesicles consists of vesicle trafficking, tethering, docking, priming and fusion. The secretory vesicles are transported by motor kinesin proteins (KIFs) from trans-Golgi complex to the vicinity of plasma membrane along microtubules and actin filaments. Trafficking is regulated by specific RAB GTPases. The tethering of the secretory vesicles is mediated by the exocyst complex and RAB proteins, bringing the vesicles very close to the plasma membrane. Docking of a transport vesicle at the target membrane precedes the formation of a tight core SNARE complex (VAMPs/STX1A/SNAP25). The influx of calcium ions in the priming step is needed for membrane depolarization and trigger vesicle fusion in neurons. The influx of Ca2+ from voltage-dependent calcium channels into the cell triggers the completion of the assembly reaction, which is mediated by an interaction between the calcium sensor SYT1 and the partially assembled SNARE complex. SNARE proteins form an incredibly stable SNARE complex which brings the vesicle membrane into close apposition with the plasma membrane promoting lipid mixing leading to vesicle fusion. CPLX1 and SYT1 regulate the norepinephrine release. SNARE disassembly can be induced by a single round of ATP hydrolysis providing by ATPase NSF and NAPA. Norepinephrine binds with adrenoceptors on the effector cell membrane. The extraneuronal norepinephrine uptake is regulated by monoamine oxidase/aldehyde dehydrogenase/catechol O-methyltransferase cascade. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Neurotransmitter norepinephrine is released from presynaptic nerve terminals of adrenergic neuron by synaptic vesicle exocytosis.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The principal means of communication for all neurons is the release of chemical signals at spatially defined locations: synapses for neurons in the brain, or the vasculature for neurons with neuroendocrine terminals. Neuroendocrine neurons generate action potentials upon appropriate stimulation. These action potentials are responsible for the calcium-dependent release of neurotransmitters at neuroendocrine terminals into the vasculature. Targets of neuroendocrine signals are located at some distance from the release sites, and the transmission path is the vasculature rather than the synapse. Adrenergic neuroendocrine neurons must therefore release larger amounts of signal than those that communicate using synaptically released small molecule transmitters.    Signaling description: In neuroendocrine neurons, secretion is dependent upon membrane depolarization and Ca2+ entry. Neurotransmitter norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is secreted as a result of tyrosine hydroxylase/dopa decarboxylase/dopamine beta-hydroxylase (TH/DOC/DBH) cascade. The vesicular monoamine transporter (SLC18A2) activity is necessary for the uptake of norepinephrine into secretory vesicles. The following exocytosis of secretory vesicles consists of vesicle trafficking, tethering, docking, priming and fusion. The secretory vesicles are transported by motor kinesin proteins (KIFs) from trans-Golgi complex to the vicinity of plasma membrane along microtubules and actin filaments. Trafficking is regulated by specific RAB GTPases. The tethering of the secretory vesicles is mediated by the exocyst complex and RAB proteins, bringing the vesicles very close to the plasma membrane. Docking of a transport vesicle at the target membrane precedes the formation of a tight core SNARE complex (VAMPs/STX1A/SNAP25). The influx of calcium ions in the priming step is needed for membrane depolarization and trigger vesicle fusion in neurons. The influx of Ca2+ from voltage-dependent calcium channels into the cell triggers the completion of the assembly reaction, which is mediated by an interaction between the calcium sensor SYT1 and the partially assembled SNARE complex. SNARE proteins form an incredibly stable SNARE complex which brings the vesicle membrane into close apposition with the plasma membrane promoting lipid mixing leading to vesicle fusion. CPLX1 and SYT1 regulate the norepinephrine release. SNARE disassembly can be induced by a single round of ATP hydrolysis providing by ATPase NSF and NAPA. Norepinephrine binds with adrenoceptors on the effector cell membrane. The extraneuronal norepinephrine uptake is regulated by monoamine oxidase/aldehyde dehydrogenase/catechol O-methyltransferase cascade. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Neurotransmitter norepinephrine is released from presynaptic nerve terminals of adrenergic neuron by synaptic vesicle exocytosis.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

