xref: PS:Organ "adrenal gland"
synonym: "Organ: adrenal gland" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "chromaffin cell"
synonym: "CellType: chromaffin cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "endocrine system"
synonym: "Organ_System: endocrine system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine and dopamine) released from adrenal chromaffin cells exert profound effects on cardiovascular, endocrine, immune, and nervous system function. Precise regulation of chromaffin cell exocytosis is key to maintaining normal physiological function.    Signaling description: In exocrine adrenal chromaffin cells, secretion is dependent upon membrane depolarization and Ca2+ entry. AGT binds to G-protein coupled AGTR1 which triggers ADCY and PKA activation to mediate the enzyme cascade catalyzing the secretion of catecholamines. The vesicular monoamine transporter activity is necessary for the uptake of catecholamines into vesicles. The following exocytosis of secretory vesicles consists of vesicle trafficking, tethering, docking and fusion. The secretory dense-core vesicles (LDCV) are transported by motor kinesin proteins (KIFs) from trans-Golgi complex to the vicinity of plasma membrane along microtubules and actin filaments. Trafficking is regulated by a specific RAB GTPases. The tethering of the secretory vesicles is mediated by the exocyst complex and RAB proteins, bringing the vesicles very close to the plasma membrane. Docking of a transport vesicle at the target membrane precedes the formation of a tight core SNARE complex (VAMPs/STX1A/SNAP25). Cholinergic receptor CHRM3 triggers the activation of PLCB1/DAG/PKC signaling and mediates membrane depolarization resulting in Ca2+ entry through voltage-gated channels. The influx of Ca2+ into the cell triggers the completion of the assembly reaction, which is mediated by an interaction between the calcium sensor SYT1 and the partially assembled SNARE complex. SNARE proteins form an incredibly stable SNARE complex which brings the vesicle membrane into close apposition with the plasma membrane promoting lipid mixing leading to vesicle fusion. CPLX1 and SYT1 regulate the catecholamines release. SNARE disassembly can be induced by a single round of ATP hydrolysis provided by ATPase NSF and NAPA. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Chromaffin cells release catecholamine transmitters by Ca2+-regulated exocytosis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine and dopamine) released from adrenal chromaffin cells exert profound effects on cardiovascular, endocrine, immune, and nervous system function. Precise regulation of chromaffin cell exocytosis is key to maintaining normal physiological function.    Signaling description: In exocrine adrenal chromaffin cells, secretion is dependent upon membrane depolarization and Ca2+ entry. AGT binds to G-protein coupled AGTR1 which triggers ADCY and PKA activation to mediate the enzyme cascade catalyzing the secretion of catecholamines. The vesicular monoamine transporter activity is necessary for the uptake of catecholamines into vesicles. The following exocytosis of secretory vesicles consists of vesicle trafficking, tethering, docking and fusion. The secretory dense-core vesicles (LDCV) are transported by motor kinesin proteins (KIFs) from trans-Golgi complex to the vicinity of plasma membrane along microtubules and actin filaments. Trafficking is regulated by a specific RAB GTPases. The tethering of the secretory vesicles is mediated by the exocyst complex and RAB proteins, bringing the vesicles very close to the plasma membrane. Docking of a transport vesicle at the target membrane precedes the formation of a tight core SNARE complex (VAMPs/STX1A/SNAP25). Cholinergic receptor CHRM3 triggers the activation of PLCB1/DAG/PKC signaling and mediates membrane depolarization resulting in Ca2+ entry through voltage-gated channels. The influx of Ca2+ into the cell triggers the completion of the assembly reaction, which is mediated by an interaction between the calcium sensor SYT1 and the partially assembled SNARE complex. SNARE proteins form an incredibly stable SNARE complex which brings the vesicle membrane into close apposition with the plasma membrane promoting lipid mixing leading to vesicle fusion. CPLX1 and SYT1 regulate the catecholamines release. SNARE disassembly can be induced by a single round of ATP hydrolysis provided by ATPase NSF and NAPA. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Chromaffin cells release catecholamine transmitters by Ca2+-regulated exocytosis." EXACT []

