xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Norepinephrine release is regulated by neuronal and extraneuronal norepinephrine uptake, neuropeptides and via adrenoceptors. Presynaptic adrenoceptors are neuronal norepinephrine uptake sites at which transmitters inhibit or facilitate the release of norepinephrine from its terminals by principles of feedback.    Signaling description: Norepinephrine and epinephrine regulate ADCY/cAMP/PKA/MAPKs/CREB1 signaling via ADRA2A and ADRB2. CREB1 promotes tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) expression involved in the conversion of tyrosine to norepinephrine. Also, CREB1 promotes the expression of neurotransmitter transporter (SLC6A2) that regulates the neuronal norepinephrine level and its uptake. Neuropeptides, orexin A and substance P bind to HCRTR1 and TACR1 which mediate PLCB1/DAG/PKC signaling. Activated PKC stimulates voltage-dependent calcium channel required for secretory vesicle fusion and norepinephrine release. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Norepinephrine and epinephrine regulate norepinephrine release from neuroendocrine terminal.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Norepinephrine release is regulated by neuronal and extraneuronal norepinephrine uptake, neuropeptides and via adrenoceptors. Presynaptic adrenoceptors are neuronal norepinephrine uptake sites at which transmitters inhibit or facilitate the release of norepinephrine from its terminals by principles of feedback.    Signaling description: Norepinephrine and epinephrine regulate ADCY/cAMP/PKA/MAPKs/CREB1 signaling via ADRA2A and ADRB2. CREB1 promotes tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) expression involved in the conversion of tyrosine to norepinephrine. Also, CREB1 promotes the expression of neurotransmitter transporter (SLC6A2) that regulates the neuronal norepinephrine level and its uptake. Neuropeptides, orexin A and substance P bind to HCRTR1 and TACR1 which mediate PLCB1/DAG/PKC signaling. Activated PKC stimulates voltage-dependent calcium channel required for secretory vesicle fusion and norepinephrine release. The described mechanism of molecular signaling is known in general terms and is depicted on the pathway.    Outcome effects: Norepinephrine and epinephrine regulate norepinephrine release from neuroendocrine terminal.    Animal data: Some of the data was revealed from animal model experiments." EXACT []

