[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-17e22d93-003d-4d23-a793-7d1f57fad6c8
name: Toll-like Receptors Act through MYD88-TIRAP Signaling
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:secretory_vesicle ! Secretory vesicle
is_a: urn:agi-folder:generic ! Generic
is_a: urn:agi-folder:antigen_recognition_in_innate_immune_system ! Antigen recognition in innate immune system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:plasma_membrane ! Plasma membrane
xref: PS:Description "Toll-like receptors (TLRs) recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) which are small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) which are small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-17e22d93-003d-4d23-a793-7d1f57fad6c8"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-17e22d93-003d-4d23-a793-7d1f57fad6c8" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "endothelial cell"
synonym: "CellType: endothelial cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "generic"
synonym: "Organ_System: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "generic"
synonym: "Tissue: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses, thereby protecting the organism from infection.    Signaling description: Typical PAMPs are bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), bacterial flagellin, lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, peptidoglycan, and nucleic acid variants from viruses such as dsRNA or unmethylated CpG motifs. TLRs can cause autoimmune disease, when they become receptors for fragments of native DNA/RNA and molecules released by apoptotic cells. In humans, ten functional receptors (TLR1-10) have been identified. TLR1, TLR2, and TLR6 are highly similar and specifically recognize components from gram-positive bacteria including LPA, macrophage-activating lipopeptide from Mycoplasma fermentans (MALP2), and diacylated lipopeptides. The specific signaling cascade activated by TLRs depends on the type of cytoplasmic adaptor molecules. TLR1, TLR2, and TLR6 recruit the adaptors, MYD88 and TIRAP. The MYD88-TIRAP-dependent pathway triggers NF-kB, MAPK1, MAPK8, p38 MAPK (MAPK14) activation. All of these transcription factors are responsible for the expression of inflammatory and immune system response genes such as IL12, TNF, IFNs, and CCL5. In addition, TRAF6 directly activates IRF5, which is a transcription factor that is important for the expression of IFNs.    Outcome effects: Finally, TLR1-, TLR2-, and TLR6-mediated pathways lead to immune system activation and result in cellular and humoral defense against viruses and bacteria."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses, thereby protecting the organism from infection.    Signaling description: Typical PAMPs are bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), bacterial flagellin, lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, peptidoglycan, and nucleic acid variants from viruses such as dsRNA or unmethylated CpG motifs. TLRs can cause autoimmune disease, when they become receptors for fragments of native DNA/RNA and molecules released by apoptotic cells. In humans, ten functional receptors (TLR1-10) have been identified. TLR1, TLR2, and TLR6 are highly similar and specifically recognize components from gram-positive bacteria including LPA, macrophage-activating lipopeptide from Mycoplasma fermentans (MALP2), and diacylated lipopeptides. The specific signaling cascade activated by TLRs depends on the type of cytoplasmic adaptor molecules. TLR1, TLR2, and TLR6 recruit the adaptors, MYD88 and TIRAP. The MYD88-TIRAP-dependent pathway triggers NF-kB, MAPK1, MAPK8, p38 MAPK (MAPK14) activation. All of these transcription factors are responsible for the expression of inflammatory and immune system response genes such as IL12, TNF, IFNs, and CCL5. In addition, TRAF6 directly activates IRF5, which is a transcription factor that is important for the expression of IFNs.    Outcome effects: Finally, TLR1-, TLR2-, and TLR6-mediated pathways lead to immune system activation and result in cellular and humoral defense against viruses and bacteria." EXACT []

