xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: NOD-like receptors (NLRs) are cytoplasmic proteins that may bind to pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). NLRs are involved in the regulation of inflammatory and apoptotic responses.    Signaling description: The NLR family consists of several proteins that are divided into subfamilies based on their N-terminal protein-interacting domains. NLRC is a subfamily of NLRs, which includes intracellular receptors that contain a CARD-domain (caspase recruiting domain), namely NOD1 and NOD2. Both NOD1 and NOD2 recognize bacterial molecules produced during peptidoglycan (PGN) synthesis in gram-negative bacteria and in some gram-positive bacteria such as Listeria monocytogenes and Bacillus sp. NOD1 recognizes D-gamma-glutamyl-meso- diaminopimelic acid dipeptide (iE-DAP) found in the PGN of all gram-negative and certain gram-positive bacteria, whereas NOD2 recognizes the muramyl dipeptide (MDP) structure found in almost all bacteria. Thus, NOD2 acts as a general sensor of PGN, while NOD1 is involved in the recognition of a specific subset of bacteria. In addition, ligand bound NOD1 and NOD2 oligomerize and activate signaling via RIPK2. Once activated, RIPK2 mediates the ubiquitination of IKBKG leading to the activation of NF-kB and the production of inflammatory cytokines. Furthermore, poly-ubiquitinated RIPK2 recruits TAK1 (MAP3K7), which leads to IKBKG complex activation and the activation of MAPKs. Also, NOD2 signaling has been shown to involve the adapter protein, CARD9 to mediate MAPK p38 and JNK (MAPK8) signaling through RIPK2. NLRC4 also belongs to the NLRC subfamily. Caspase-1 (CASP1) activation induced by cytosolic flagellin has been shown to be NLRC4-dependent. AIM2 acts as a cytosolic DNA sensor that responds to the release of type I interferon and proinflammatory cytokines. AIM2 also acts through inflammasomes to activate CASP1. The NLRP subfamily consists of 14 members characterized by their PYD effector domains. NLPR3 and NLPR1 are the most studied receptors in this subfamily. NLRP1 oligomerization induced by MDP requires ATP. NLRP3 activation is induced by a wide variety of stimuli including whole bacteria (Listeria monocytogenes and Staphylococcus aureus), bacterial RNA, uric acid crystals, amyloid beta, extracellular ATP, and pore-forming toxins (nigericin and maitotoxin).    Outcome effects: NLRPs form a multiprotein complex called an inflammasome. NLRP1 and NLRP3 recruit PYCARD, which in turn interacts with CASP1 via a CARD-CARD interaction. CASP1 activation in inflammasomes leads to the maturation and activation of IL1B, IL18, and IL33. These cytokines activate macrophages and T-cells. Lastly, NOD9 (NLRX1) is the first NLR protein shown to be localized at the mitochondria. NLRX1 blocks antiviral inflammatory responses via the MAVS-related pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: NOD-like receptors (NLRs) are cytoplasmic proteins that may bind to pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). NLRs are involved in the regulation of inflammatory and apoptotic responses.    Signaling description: The NLR family consists of several proteins that are divided into subfamilies based on their N-terminal protein-interacting domains. NLRC is a subfamily of NLRs, which includes intracellular receptors that contain a CARD-domain (caspase recruiting domain), namely NOD1 and NOD2. Both NOD1 and NOD2 recognize bacterial molecules produced during peptidoglycan (PGN) synthesis in gram-negative bacteria and in some gram-positive bacteria such as Listeria monocytogenes and Bacillus sp. NOD1 recognizes D-gamma-glutamyl-meso- diaminopimelic acid dipeptide (iE-DAP) found in the PGN of all gram-negative and certain gram-positive bacteria, whereas NOD2 recognizes the muramyl dipeptide (MDP) structure found in almost all bacteria. Thus, NOD2 acts as a general sensor of PGN, while NOD1 is involved in the recognition of a specific subset of bacteria. In addition, ligand bound NOD1 and NOD2 oligomerize and activate signaling via RIPK2. Once activated, RIPK2 mediates the ubiquitination of IKBKG leading to the activation of NF-kB and the production of inflammatory cytokines. Furthermore, poly-ubiquitinated RIPK2 recruits TAK1 (MAP3K7), which leads to IKBKG complex activation and the activation of MAPKs. Also, NOD2 signaling has been shown to involve the adapter protein, CARD9 to mediate MAPK p38 and JNK (MAPK8) signaling through RIPK2. NLRC4 also belongs to the NLRC subfamily. Caspase-1 (CASP1) activation induced by cytosolic flagellin has been shown to be NLRC4-dependent. AIM2 acts as a cytosolic DNA sensor that responds to the release of type I interferon and proinflammatory cytokines. AIM2 also acts through inflammasomes to activate CASP1. The NLRP subfamily consists of 14 members characterized by their PYD effector domains. NLPR3 and NLPR1 are the most studied receptors in this subfamily. NLRP1 oligomerization induced by MDP requires ATP. NLRP3 activation is induced by a wide variety of stimuli including whole bacteria (Listeria monocytogenes and Staphylococcus aureus), bacterial RNA, uric acid crystals, amyloid beta, extracellular ATP, and pore-forming toxins (nigericin and maitotoxin).    Outcome effects: NLRPs form a multiprotein complex called an inflammasome. NLRP1 and NLRP3 recruit PYCARD, which in turn interacts with CASP1 via a CARD-CARD interaction. CASP1 activation in inflammasomes leads to the maturation and activation of IL1B, IL18, and IL33. These cytokines activate macrophages and T-cells. Lastly, NOD9 (NLRX1) is the first NLR protein shown to be localized at the mitochondria. NLRX1 blocks antiviral inflammatory responses via the MAVS-related pathway." EXACT []

