xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), which are small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses, thereby protecting the organism from infection.    Signaling description: Typical PAMPs are bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), bacterial flagellin, lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, peptidoglycan, and nucleic acid variants from viruses such as dsRNA or unmethylated CpG motifs. In humans, ten functional receptors (TLR1-10) have been identified. TLR5 recognizes flagellin of bacterial flagella. TLR7 and TLR8 are found in membranes of endosomes and recognize single-stranded RNA from viruses. TLR9 is also found in the membrane of endosomes and recognizes unmethylated CpG islands in bacterial and viral DNA.    Outcome effects: The specific signaling cascade activated by TLRs depends of the type of cytoplasmic adaptor molecules. TLR5, TLR7, TLR8, and TLR9 act through the MYD88 pathway. The MYD88-dependent pathway leads to the activation of NF-kB, MAPK1, MAPK8, p38 MAPK (MAPK14), IRF5, and IRF7 in addition to the transcription of inflammatory genes. The details of TLR5, TLR7, TLR8, and TLR9-related signaling remain unknown."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Toll-like receptors (TLRs) recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs), which are small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses, thereby protecting the organism from infection.    Signaling description: Typical PAMPs are bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), bacterial flagellin, lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, peptidoglycan, and nucleic acid variants from viruses such as dsRNA or unmethylated CpG motifs. In humans, ten functional receptors (TLR1-10) have been identified. TLR5 recognizes flagellin of bacterial flagella. TLR7 and TLR8 are found in membranes of endosomes and recognize single-stranded RNA from viruses. TLR9 is also found in the membrane of endosomes and recognizes unmethylated CpG islands in bacterial and viral DNA.    Outcome effects: The specific signaling cascade activated by TLRs depends of the type of cytoplasmic adaptor molecules. TLR5, TLR7, TLR8, and TLR9 act through the MYD88 pathway. The MYD88-dependent pathway leads to the activation of NF-kB, MAPK1, MAPK8, p38 MAPK (MAPK14), IRF5, and IRF7 in addition to the transcription of inflammatory genes. The details of TLR5, TLR7, TLR8, and TLR9-related signaling remain unknown." EXACT []

