xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: TLR4 is a member of the Toll-like receptor (TLR) family. TLRs recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) which are small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses, thereby protecting the organism from infection.    Signaling description: Typical PAMPs are bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), bacterial flagellin, lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, peptidoglycan, and nucleic acid variants from viruses such as dsRNA or unmethylated CpG motifs. TLR4 is the primary receptor for LPS on gram-negative bacteria. TLR4 can cause autoimmune disease, when it becomes a receptor for fragments of native DNA/RNA and molecules released by apoptotic cells. The specific signaling cascade activated by TLRs depends on the type of cytoplasmic adaptor molecules. TLR4 acts through both MYD88 and TICAM2-dependent pathways. The MYD88-dependent pathway leads to the activation of NF-kB, MAPK1, MAPK8, IRF5, and p38 MAPK (MAPK14) in addition to the transcription of proinflammatory genes. Activated by TLR4, TICAM2 participates in MYD88-dependent pathway by recruiting TRAF6. The TLR4-TICAM2 association also triggers the activation of IRF3 and IRF7, and thereby the expression of IFNs.    Outcome effects: Finally, TLR4 activation results in strong expression of cytokines and proinflammatory proteins by leukocytes."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: TLR4 is a member of the Toll-like receptor (TLR) family. TLRs recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) which are small molecular motifs found on pathogens and activate innate immune responses, thereby protecting the organism from infection.    Signaling description: Typical PAMPs are bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), bacterial flagellin, lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, peptidoglycan, and nucleic acid variants from viruses such as dsRNA or unmethylated CpG motifs. TLR4 is the primary receptor for LPS on gram-negative bacteria. TLR4 can cause autoimmune disease, when it becomes a receptor for fragments of native DNA/RNA and molecules released by apoptotic cells. The specific signaling cascade activated by TLRs depends on the type of cytoplasmic adaptor molecules. TLR4 acts through both MYD88 and TICAM2-dependent pathways. The MYD88-dependent pathway leads to the activation of NF-kB, MAPK1, MAPK8, IRF5, and p38 MAPK (MAPK14) in addition to the transcription of proinflammatory genes. Activated by TLR4, TICAM2 participates in MYD88-dependent pathway by recruiting TRAF6. The TLR4-TICAM2 association also triggers the activation of IRF3 and IRF7, and thereby the expression of IFNs.    Outcome effects: Finally, TLR4 activation results in strong expression of cytokines and proinflammatory proteins by leukocytes." EXACT []

