[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-57a4aead-b02a-4577-8d03-23ea051a3b24
name: T-Cell Dependent B-Cell Activation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:b-cell_activation ! B-cell activation
xref: PS:Description "B cells in lymphoid tissue obtain signals from T cells acting as antigen-presenting cells for the maturation of B cells. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: B cells in lymphoid tissue obtain signals from T cells acting as antigen-presenting cells for the maturation of B cells. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "V. Sobolev ORCID:0000-0003-4779-156X"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: V. Sobolev ORCID:0000-0003-4779-156X" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-57a4aead-b02a-4577-8d03-23ea051a3b24"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-57a4aead-b02a-4577-8d03-23ea051a3b24" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "bone marrow"
synonym: "Tissue: bone marrow" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "B-cell"
synonym: "CellType: B-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: B-cells in lymphoid tissue obtain signals from T-cells acting as antigen-presenting cells for the maturation of B-cells. T-dependent antigens are presented on MHC class II protein complex (MHC2) to a special subtype of T-cell calledTh2-cell, which are the main activators for B-cells.    Signaling description: Th2-cells deliver antigens in the MHC2 complex to the B-cell receptor (BCR). BCR is a transmembrane protein complex composed of membrane immunoglobulin (IgM) and disulfide-linked heterodimers CD79A/CD79B. Activated by antigen, the BCR complex stimulates B-cell proliferation and differentiation. BCR can influence intracellular signaling through PLCG2/calcium/NFATC2, PI3K, IKBK/NF-kB, and canonical ERK (MAPK) signaling. Following antigen internalization and re-expression on their surface, B-cells can in the reverse order activate T-cells, which recognize a different part of the same antigen. In turn, T-cells produce cytokines Il2, Il4, and CD40 (B-cell growth factor), which leads to B-cell division and maturation to antibody secreting cells. The secretion of cytokines by T-cells is the second signal provided by T-cells for B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells.    Outcome effects: Genetic recombination and isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes occurs during B-cell maturation. This is necessary for the initiation of transcription of immunoglobulins. The binding of CD40 on Bcells to the CD40 ligand (CD40LG) on T-cells induces isotype switching from the initial IgM response. AICDA expression is the primary step in the process of isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes. Cytokine-induced STAT6 is a transcription factor that also contributes to AICDA expression and immunoglobulin synthesis."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: B-cells in lymphoid tissue obtain signals from T-cells acting as antigen-presenting cells for the maturation of B-cells. T-dependent antigens are presented on MHC class II protein complex (MHC2) to a special subtype of T-cell calledTh2-cell, which are the main activators for B-cells.    Signaling description: Th2-cells deliver antigens in the MHC2 complex to the B-cell receptor (BCR). BCR is a transmembrane protein complex composed of membrane immunoglobulin (IgM) and disulfide-linked heterodimers CD79A/CD79B. Activated by antigen, the BCR complex stimulates B-cell proliferation and differentiation. BCR can influence intracellular signaling through PLCG2/calcium/NFATC2, PI3K, IKBK/NF-kB, and canonical ERK (MAPK) signaling. Following antigen internalization and re-expression on their surface, B-cells can in the reverse order activate T-cells, which recognize a different part of the same antigen. In turn, T-cells produce cytokines Il2, Il4, and CD40 (B-cell growth factor), which leads to B-cell division and maturation to antibody secreting cells. The secretion of cytokines by T-cells is the second signal provided by T-cells for B-cell proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells.    Outcome effects: Genetic recombination and isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes occurs during B-cell maturation. This is necessary for the initiation of transcription of immunoglobulins. The binding of CD40 on Bcells to the CD40 ligand (CD40LG) on T-cells induces isotype switching from the initial IgM response. AICDA expression is the primary step in the process of isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes. Cytokine-induced STAT6 is a transcription factor that also contributes to AICDA expression and immunoglobulin synthesis." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-2d04614a-9163-47a9-95e5-8d3492e31bcd
name: T-Cell Independent B-Cell Activation
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:t ! T
is_a: urn:agi-folder:lymphatic_system ! Lymphatic system
is_a: urn:agi-folder:b-cell_activation ! B-cell activation
xref: PS:Description "B-cell receptor (BCR) can recognize free (soluble) protein antigens in the blood or lymph with their membrane bound-immunoglobulin. Pathway is built manually using published studies."
synonym: "Description: B-cell receptor (BCR) can recognize free (soluble) protein antigens in the blood or lymph with their membrane bound-immunoglobulin. Pathway is built manually using published studies." EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "V. Sobolev ORCID:0000-0003-4779-156X"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: V. Sobolev ORCID:0000-0003-4779-156X" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-2d04614a-9163-47a9-95e5-8d3492e31bcd"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-2d04614a-9163-47a9-95e5-8d3492e31bcd" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "bone marrow"
synonym: "Tissue: bone marrow" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "B-cell"
synonym: "CellType: B-cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "lymphatic system"
synonym: "Organ_System: lymphatic system" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: B-cell receptor (BCR) can recognize free (soluble) protein antigens in the blood or lymph with their membrane bound-immunoglobulin. BCR is a transmembrane protein complex composed of membrane immunoglobulin and disulfide-linked heterodimers CD79A/CD79B.    Signaling description: Activated by antigen, the BCR complex stimulates B-cell proliferation and differentiation. BCR can influence intracellular signaling through the PLCG2/calcium/NFATC2, PI3K, IKBK/NF-kB, and canonical ERK (MAPK) signaling. Many non-protein antigens such as polysaccharides and lipids stimulate antibody production and B-cell activation in the absence of helper T-cells and activate BCR signaling. Non-protein T-cell independent type I antigens such as LPS stimulates polyclonal B-cell activation directly by binding to CD14 and TLR4 receptors. T-cell independent type I antigens provide a second signal of B-cell activation. The complement fragment, C3 binds to the antigen and provides a second signal of B-cell activation after stimulating complement cascade by soluble immunoglobulin. The secondary responses are very rapid and generate low affinity antibodies, which consist mainly of IgM without isotype switching or affinity maturation. C3 simultaneously binds and engages CR2, CD19, and CD81 complement receptors and the membrane immunoglobulin on the surface of B-cells.    Outcome effects: This leads to the initiation of signaling cascades from both the BCR complex and the CR2 complex. T-cell independent type 2 antigens such as polysaccharides are molecules with multiple repeating subunits that engage the BCR and thus induce antigen-specific B-cell responses. T-cell independent type 2 antigens provide long-lasting humoral immunity and the generation of memory B-cells. FCGR2B is a monomeric receptor with immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibition motif in the cytoplasmic region. The FCGR2B1 isoform, which is predominantly expressed on B-cells, is cross-linked with BCR by IgG immune complexes. FCGR2B1 acts as a negative feedback regulator by inhibiting BCR signaling through the immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibition motif. Signaling through TNFSF13 and TNFSF13B stimulates B-lymphocytes to undergo proliferation. TNFRSF13C, as a nuclear receptor, participates in AICDA production and thereby in the process of isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: B-cell receptor (BCR) can recognize free (soluble) protein antigens in the blood or lymph with their membrane bound-immunoglobulin. BCR is a transmembrane protein complex composed of membrane immunoglobulin and disulfide-linked heterodimers CD79A/CD79B.    Signaling description: Activated by antigen, the BCR complex stimulates B-cell proliferation and differentiation. BCR can influence intracellular signaling through the PLCG2/calcium/NFATC2, PI3K, IKBK/NF-kB, and canonical ERK (MAPK) signaling. Many non-protein antigens such as polysaccharides and lipids stimulate antibody production and B-cell activation in the absence of helper T-cells and activate BCR signaling. Non-protein T-cell independent type I antigens such as LPS stimulates polyclonal B-cell activation directly by binding to CD14 and TLR4 receptors. T-cell independent type I antigens provide a second signal of B-cell activation. The complement fragment, C3 binds to the antigen and provides a second signal of B-cell activation after stimulating complement cascade by soluble immunoglobulin. The secondary responses are very rapid and generate low affinity antibodies, which consist mainly of IgM without isotype switching or affinity maturation. C3 simultaneously binds and engages CR2, CD19, and CD81 complement receptors and the membrane immunoglobulin on the surface of B-cells.    Outcome effects: This leads to the initiation of signaling cascades from both the BCR complex and the CR2 complex. T-cell independent type 2 antigens such as polysaccharides are molecules with multiple repeating subunits that engage the BCR and thus induce antigen-specific B-cell responses. T-cell independent type 2 antigens provide long-lasting humoral immunity and the generation of memory B-cells. FCGR2B is a monomeric receptor with immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibition motif in the cytoplasmic region. The FCGR2B1 isoform, which is predominantly expressed on B-cells, is cross-linked with BCR by IgG immune complexes. FCGR2B1 acts as a negative feedback regulator by inhibiting BCR signaling through the immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibition motif. Signaling through TNFSF13 and TNFSF13B stimulates B-lymphocytes to undergo proliferation. TNFRSF13C, as a nuclear receptor, participates in AICDA production and thereby in the process of isotype switching of immunoglobulin genes." EXACT []

