synonym: "Organ_System: digestive system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: P-cells (gastric parietal cells) are located in the stomach and secrete stomach acid and leptin in response to the neuromediators signals.    Signaling description: Receptors for histamine, somatostatins, acetylcholine and gastrins are present on the basolateral membrane of P-cell. Histamine and somatostatins activate signaling via cAMP/PKC, whereas acetylcholine and gastrins act through PLCB1/diacylglycerol. PKC can mediate the activation of H+/K+-exchanging ATPases which along with Cl- channels produce stomach acid. The anion exchanger is necessary for the accumulation of Cl- anion in P-cells. It exchanges extracellular Cl- for HCO3- which is produced by carbonic anhydrase 1 (CA1) in cells. The HCO3- anion elevates blood pH. Also, gastrins can activate LEP expression via CCKAR/JAK2/STAT3 signaling. Leptin secretion is activated by intracellular calcium influx. cAMP and Ca2+ regulate the secretion of gastric intrinsic factor (GIF) which is necessary for stabilization of vitamin B12. GIF also binds to transcobalamin 1 (TCN1) and this complex transports vitamin B12 to the blood.    Outcome effects: Activation of P-cells by neuromediators leads to the production of stomach acid and leptin expression."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: P-cells (gastric parietal cells) are located in the stomach and secrete stomach acid and leptin in response to the neuromediators signals.    Signaling description: Receptors for histamine, somatostatins, acetylcholine and gastrins are present on the basolateral membrane of P-cell. Histamine and somatostatins activate signaling via cAMP/PKC, whereas acetylcholine and gastrins act through PLCB1/diacylglycerol. PKC can mediate the activation of H+/K+-exchanging ATPases which along with Cl- channels produce stomach acid. The anion exchanger is necessary for the accumulation of Cl- anion in P-cells. It exchanges extracellular Cl- for HCO3- which is produced by carbonic anhydrase 1 (CA1) in cells. The HCO3- anion elevates blood pH. Also, gastrins can activate LEP expression via CCKAR/JAK2/STAT3 signaling. Leptin secretion is activated by intracellular calcium influx. cAMP and Ca2+ regulate the secretion of gastric intrinsic factor (GIF) which is necessary for stabilization of vitamin B12. GIF also binds to transcobalamin 1 (TCN1) and this complex transports vitamin B12 to the blood.    Outcome effects: Activation of P-cells by neuromediators leads to the production of stomach acid and leptin expression." EXACT []

