xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: K-cells of the proximal small intestine are activated by fatty acids. K-cells absorb glucose from intestine. The major product of K-cells is GIP.     Signaling description: Fatty acids interact with FFAR1, FFAR4 and GPR119 G-protein-coupled receptor on the K-cell surface. Through G-proteins, they activate cAMP/PKA signaling. Activated PKA phosphorylates transcription factors GATA4 and CREB1. In turn, CREB1 interacts with ISL1 and activates the expression of PDX1 and PAX6. All these transcription factors are necessary for GIP gene expression activation. PKA activates the expression of Na+/glucose cotransporter (SLC5A1), the major glucose transporter and critical regulator of GIP release. The ATP-activated inward rectifier potassium channel, the second major regulator of GIP release, is also activated by PKA. The Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase regulate Na+/K+ balance through glucose absorption and is necessary for accurate work of ATP-activated inward rectifier potassium channel.    Outcome effects: The GIP release stimulates synthesis and release of insulin by pancreatic beta-cells."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: K-cells of the proximal small intestine are activated by fatty acids. K-cells absorb glucose from intestine. The major product of K-cells is GIP.     Signaling description: Fatty acids interact with FFAR1, FFAR4 and GPR119 G-protein-coupled receptor on the K-cell surface. Through G-proteins, they activate cAMP/PKA signaling. Activated PKA phosphorylates transcription factors GATA4 and CREB1. In turn, CREB1 interacts with ISL1 and activates the expression of PDX1 and PAX6. All these transcription factors are necessary for GIP gene expression activation. PKA activates the expression of Na+/glucose cotransporter (SLC5A1), the major glucose transporter and critical regulator of GIP release. The ATP-activated inward rectifier potassium channel, the second major regulator of GIP release, is also activated by PKA. The Na+/K+-exchanging ATPase regulate Na+/K+ balance through glucose absorption and is necessary for accurate work of ATP-activated inward rectifier potassium channel.    Outcome effects: The GIP release stimulates synthesis and release of insulin by pancreatic beta-cells." EXACT []

