xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Intestinal enteroendocrine cells (I-cells) of proximal small intestine can produce CCK-8 in response to various nutrients. The major function of CCK-8 is inhibition of eating behavior and stimulation of satiety.    Signaling description: Receptors for fatty acids (FFRA1 and FFRA4), aromatic amino acids (CASR), lysophosphatidic acid (LPAR5), as well as taste receptors type 2 (TAS2R) and toll-like receptors (TLR2, TLR4, TLR5 and TLR9) are expressed on the surface of I-cells. These receptors activate intracellular calcium influx through phospholipase C (PLCB2)/ ITPR1 signaling and activate PKC through cAMP signaling. In turn, PKC activates CREB1. The TLR signaling also leads to phospholipase C (PLCG1)/ ITPR1 induced Ca2+ influx and MAPK1/3 (ERK1/2) dependent CREB1 activation. Transcription factor CREB1 binds to CCK gene promoter and activates preprocholecystokinin transcription. Processing of preprocholecystokinin to CCK-8 is dependent upon PCSK1. Calcium overload via ITPR1 and TRPA1 leads to the secretion of CCK-8 and serotonin. At the mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons, CCK-8 interacts with CCKAR and through JAKs and STAT3 signaling activates the opening of K+ channel which leads to potassium flow out of the cell, membrane depolarization, and transmission of nerve impulse.     Outcome effects: Activation of I-cells by nutrients and TLRs signaling leads to synthesis and secretion of CCK-8. This results in gallbladder contraction, pancreatic juice secretion and stimulation of the sense of satiety. Also, CCK-8 is transferred to the brain by blood flow and, thus inhibits eating behavior and gastric motility. Serotonin also activates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic juice secretion."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Intestinal enteroendocrine cells (I-cells) of proximal small intestine can produce CCK-8 in response to various nutrients. The major function of CCK-8 is inhibition of eating behavior and stimulation of satiety.    Signaling description: Receptors for fatty acids (FFRA1 and FFRA4), aromatic amino acids (CASR), lysophosphatidic acid (LPAR5), as well as taste receptors type 2 (TAS2R) and toll-like receptors (TLR2, TLR4, TLR5 and TLR9) are expressed on the surface of I-cells. These receptors activate intracellular calcium influx through phospholipase C (PLCB2)/ ITPR1 signaling and activate PKC through cAMP signaling. In turn, PKC activates CREB1. The TLR signaling also leads to phospholipase C (PLCG1)/ ITPR1 induced Ca2+ influx and MAPK1/3 (ERK1/2) dependent CREB1 activation. Transcription factor CREB1 binds to CCK gene promoter and activates preprocholecystokinin transcription. Processing of preprocholecystokinin to CCK-8 is dependent upon PCSK1. Calcium overload via ITPR1 and TRPA1 leads to the secretion of CCK-8 and serotonin. At the mucosal endings of vagal afferent neurons, CCK-8 interacts with CCKAR and through JAKs and STAT3 signaling activates the opening of K+ channel which leads to potassium flow out of the cell, membrane depolarization, and transmission of nerve impulse.     Outcome effects: Activation of I-cells by nutrients and TLRs signaling leads to synthesis and secretion of CCK-8. This results in gallbladder contraction, pancreatic juice secretion and stimulation of the sense of satiety. Also, CCK-8 is transferred to the brain by blood flow and, thus inhibits eating behavior and gastric motility. Serotonin also activates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic juice secretion." EXACT []

