xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Gastrin is one of the major signaling peptides in the digestive process. The expression and secretion of gastrin by stomach G-cells is regulated by a number of nutrients and signals from the parasympathetic nervous system and also by certain enteroendocrine cells (e.g. D-cells) of the digestive system.    Signaling description: The lysophosphatidic acid (LPAR5) and L-amino acid (GPRC6A) receptors are the major D-cell receptor in the lumen of the stomach. These receptors via G-proteins activate two signaling pathways: cAMP/PKA and phospholipase C (PLCE1) dependent signaling. cAMP induced PKA activation leads to the activation of GAST transcription. PKA inhibits the repressor of GAST transcription (MEN1) and activates SP1 and CTNNB1, which bind to the promoter of GAST gene and activate its transcription. Phospholipase C synthesizes inositol P3 and diacylglycerol, which leads to the accumulation of intracellular calcium and PKC activation, respectively. In turn, PKC activates L-type calcium channels which leads to extracellular calcium influx and gastrin secretion. GAST propeptide is cleaved by carboxypeptidase B2 (CPB2) and proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 2 (PCSK2). Processed gastrins can be hydrolyzed in plasma by plasma membrane-bound oligomer of MEP1B activated by PKC. The release of gastrins is also activated by gastrin releasing peptide (GRP) secreted by post-ganglionic fibers of the vagus nerve. The inhibition of GAST expression occurs due to an activation of SSTR2 by somatostatin (SST) which blocks cAMP signaling via G-proteins GNAI3 and GNAO1.    Outcome effects: Gastrin synthesis and secretion by G-cell is under the control of nutrients and GRP. In ECL-cells, gastrin signaling leads to histamine secretion and activation of stomach secretion (including stomach acid secretion)."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Gastrin is one of the major signaling peptides in the digestive process. The expression and secretion of gastrin by stomach G-cells is regulated by a number of nutrients and signals from the parasympathetic nervous system and also by certain enteroendocrine cells (e.g. D-cells) of the digestive system.    Signaling description: The lysophosphatidic acid (LPAR5) and L-amino acid (GPRC6A) receptors are the major D-cell receptor in the lumen of the stomach. These receptors via G-proteins activate two signaling pathways: cAMP/PKA and phospholipase C (PLCE1) dependent signaling. cAMP induced PKA activation leads to the activation of GAST transcription. PKA inhibits the repressor of GAST transcription (MEN1) and activates SP1 and CTNNB1, which bind to the promoter of GAST gene and activate its transcription. Phospholipase C synthesizes inositol P3 and diacylglycerol, which leads to the accumulation of intracellular calcium and PKC activation, respectively. In turn, PKC activates L-type calcium channels which leads to extracellular calcium influx and gastrin secretion. GAST propeptide is cleaved by carboxypeptidase B2 (CPB2) and proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 2 (PCSK2). Processed gastrins can be hydrolyzed in plasma by plasma membrane-bound oligomer of MEP1B activated by PKC. The release of gastrins is also activated by gastrin releasing peptide (GRP) secreted by post-ganglionic fibers of the vagus nerve. The inhibition of GAST expression occurs due to an activation of SSTR2 by somatostatin (SST) which blocks cAMP signaling via G-proteins GNAI3 and GNAO1.    Outcome effects: Gastrin synthesis and secretion by G-cell is under the control of nutrients and GRP. In ECL-cells, gastrin signaling leads to histamine secretion and activation of stomach secretion (including stomach acid secretion)." EXACT []

