xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: N-cells are located in the small and large intestine and secrete tridecapeptide neurotensin (processed from NTS) released in response to short and medium chain fatty acid signal.    Signaling description: Short and medium chain fatty acids interact with fatty acid receptors. The exact type of fatty acid receptor in the N-cells is not identified (the most common receptors are shown on the pathway). Fatty acid receptors increase cAMP synthesis, PKA and PKC activation via G-proteins. In turn, PKA and PKC phosphorylate and activate STAT3 and JUN/FOS transcription factors and lead to the expression of NTS gene. The processing of NTS propeptide leads to formation of two neuropeptides: neurotensin (13 a.a.) and neuromedin N. The neurotensin peptide blocks intestine motility by inhibition of muscle contraction._    Outcome effects: Short and medium chain fatty acids dependent expression of neurotensin inactivates gastric motility."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: N-cells are located in the small and large intestine and secrete tridecapeptide neurotensin (processed from NTS) released in response to short and medium chain fatty acid signal.    Signaling description: Short and medium chain fatty acids interact with fatty acid receptors. The exact type of fatty acid receptor in the N-cells is not identified (the most common receptors are shown on the pathway). Fatty acid receptors increase cAMP synthesis, PKA and PKC activation via G-proteins. In turn, PKA and PKC phosphorylate and activate STAT3 and JUN/FOS transcription factors and lead to the expression of NTS gene. The processing of NTS propeptide leads to formation of two neuropeptides: neurotensin (13 a.a.) and neuromedin N. The neurotensin peptide blocks intestine motility by inhibition of muscle contraction._    Outcome effects: Short and medium chain fatty acids dependent expression of neurotensin inactivates gastric motility." EXACT []

