synonym: "CellType: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "generic"
synonym: "Organ: generic" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: There are 17 identified CXC chemokines in mammals. They are subdivided into two categories including those with a specific motif of glutamic acid-leucine-arginine (ELR) immediately before the first cysteine residue of the CXC motif (ELR-positive) and those without an ELR motif (ELR-negative).    Signaling description: ELR-positive CXC chemokines specifically induce the migration of neutrophils and interact with chemokine receptors such as CXCR1 and CXCR2. An example of an ELR-positive CXC chemokine is interleukin-8 (IL8), which induces neutrophils to leave the bloodstream and enter into the surrounding tissue. Other CXC chemokines that lack the ELR motif, such as CXCL13, tend to be chemoattractant for lymphocytes. CXC chemokines bind to CXC chemokine receptors, which are integral membrane proteins that specifically bind and respond to cytokines of the CXC chemokine family. They represent one subfamily of chemokine receptors, which is a large family of G-protein-linked receptors known as seven transmembrane (7-TM) proteins since they span the cell membrane seven times. There are currently seven known CXC chemokine receptors in mammals named CXCR1 through 7. Intracellular signaling by chemokine receptors is dependent on neighboring G-proteins. G-proteins exist as heterotrimers and are composed of three distinct subunits. When the molecule GDP is bound to the G-protein subunit, the G-protein is in an inactive state. Following the binding of the chemokine ligand, chemokine receptors associate with G-proteins, allowing the exchange of GDP for GTP, and the dissociation of the different G-protein subunits. The subunit Gb/g activates PLCB1 that is associated with the cell membrane. PLC cleaves phosphatidylinositol (4,5)-bisphosphate (PIP2) to form two second messenger molecules including inositol 1,4,5 -trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol. Diacylglycerol activates protein kinase C (PKC) and IP3 triggers the release of calcium (Ca2+) from intracellular stores.    Outcome effects: The described events promote many signaling cascades, which cause a cellular response. CXCL8 (IL8) binds to its specific receptors, CXCR1 or CXCR2. A rise in intracellular Ca2+ activates phospholipase D (PLD), which in turn, initiates the MAP kinase pathway. Concurrently, the G-protein subunit G alpha i (GNAI1) directly activates protein tyrosine kinase (PTK2) which phosphorylates serine and threonine residues in the tail of the chemokine receptor, causing its desensitization or inactivation. The initiated MAP kinase pathway activates specific cellular mechanisms involved in chemotaxis, degranulation, release of superoxide anions, and changes in the avidity of cell adhesion molecules called integrins."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: There are 17 identified CXC chemokines in mammals. They are subdivided into two categories including those with a specific motif of glutamic acid-leucine-arginine (ELR) immediately before the first cysteine residue of the CXC motif (ELR-positive) and those without an ELR motif (ELR-negative).    Signaling description: ELR-positive CXC chemokines specifically induce the migration of neutrophils and interact with chemokine receptors such as CXCR1 and CXCR2. An example of an ELR-positive CXC chemokine is interleukin-8 (IL8), which induces neutrophils to leave the bloodstream and enter into the surrounding tissue. Other CXC chemokines that lack the ELR motif, such as CXCL13, tend to be chemoattractant for lymphocytes. CXC chemokines bind to CXC chemokine receptors, which are integral membrane proteins that specifically bind and respond to cytokines of the CXC chemokine family. They represent one subfamily of chemokine receptors, which is a large family of G-protein-linked receptors known as seven transmembrane (7-TM) proteins since they span the cell membrane seven times. There are currently seven known CXC chemokine receptors in mammals named CXCR1 through 7. Intracellular signaling by chemokine receptors is dependent on neighboring G-proteins. G-proteins exist as heterotrimers and are composed of three distinct subunits. When the molecule GDP is bound to the G-protein subunit, the G-protein is in an inactive state. Following the binding of the chemokine ligand, chemokine receptors associate with G-proteins, allowing the exchange of GDP for GTP, and the dissociation of the different G-protein subunits. The subunit Gb/g activates PLCB1 that is associated with the cell membrane. PLC cleaves phosphatidylinositol (4,5)-bisphosphate (PIP2) to form two second messenger molecules including inositol 1,4,5 -trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol. Diacylglycerol activates protein kinase C (PKC) and IP3 triggers the release of calcium (Ca2+) from intracellular stores.    Outcome effects: The described events promote many signaling cascades, which cause a cellular response. CXCL8 (IL8) binds to its specific receptors, CXCR1 or CXCR2. A rise in intracellular Ca2+ activates phospholipase D (PLD), which in turn, initiates the MAP kinase pathway. Concurrently, the G-protein subunit G alpha i (GNAI1) directly activates protein tyrosine kinase (PTK2) which phosphorylates serine and threonine residues in the tail of the chemokine receptor, causing its desensitization or inactivation. The initiated MAP kinase pathway activates specific cellular mechanisms involved in chemotaxis, degranulation, release of superoxide anions, and changes in the avidity of cell adhesion molecules called integrins." EXACT []

