xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The CC chemokine proteins have two adjacent cysteine residues near their amino terminus. There have been at least 27 distinct members of this subgroup reported in mammals called CC chemokine ligands (CCL)-1 to -28, where CCL10 is the same as CCL9. The chemokines of this subfamily usually contain four cysteine residues (C4-CC chemokines), but a small number of CC chemokines possess six cysteine residues (C6-CC chemokines). C6-CC chemokines include CCL1, CCL15, CCL21, CCL23, and CCL28.    Signaling description: CC chemokines induce the migration of monocytes and other cell types such as NK cells and dendritic cells. In different cellular environments, chemokines can regulate distinct second messengers or even positively or negatively regulate the same signal transduction pathway. All of the CC chemokine receptors work by activating the G-protein G alpha I (GNAI1). GNAI1 directly activates protein tyrosine kinase (PTK), which phosphorylates serine and threonine residues in the tail of the chemokine receptor, causing its desensitization or inactivation. In addition, this protein is part of a complex that inhibits adenylate cyclase (ADCY).    Outcome effects: Chemokine receptor ligation ultimately leads to cell signaling events via PI3K, MAPK/Jun/Fos, and the NF-kB pathways."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The CC chemokine proteins have two adjacent cysteine residues near their amino terminus. There have been at least 27 distinct members of this subgroup reported in mammals called CC chemokine ligands (CCL)-1 to -28, where CCL10 is the same as CCL9. The chemokines of this subfamily usually contain four cysteine residues (C4-CC chemokines), but a small number of CC chemokines possess six cysteine residues (C6-CC chemokines). C6-CC chemokines include CCL1, CCL15, CCL21, CCL23, and CCL28.    Signaling description: CC chemokines induce the migration of monocytes and other cell types such as NK cells and dendritic cells. In different cellular environments, chemokines can regulate distinct second messengers or even positively or negatively regulate the same signal transduction pathway. All of the CC chemokine receptors work by activating the G-protein G alpha I (GNAI1). GNAI1 directly activates protein tyrosine kinase (PTK), which phosphorylates serine and threonine residues in the tail of the chemokine receptor, causing its desensitization or inactivation. In addition, this protein is part of a complex that inhibits adenylate cyclase (ADCY).    Outcome effects: Chemokine receptor ligation ultimately leads to cell signaling events via PI3K, MAPK/Jun/Fos, and the NF-kB pathways." EXACT []

