xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Hyperhomocysteinemia is elevated plasma level of homocysteine (Hcy) which is synthesized during methionine metabolism. Hyperhomocysteinemia is associated with increased risk of atherosclerosis and arterial and venous thrombosis and other diseases associated with aging. Hyperhomocysteinemia may be caused by mutation in the MTHFR gene, defects in metabolism of vitamin B12, folate, choline or pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.    Signaling description: Transport of folate into the cell initiates the folate cycle in which MTHFR converts folate to its active form, 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. Human Mthfr C677T genotype is associated with mild MTHFR deficiency in the condition of high temperature stress and less production of 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. 5-methyltetrahydrofolate is a necessary co-factor in methionine cycle and play role in production of universal methyl donor S-adenosylmethionine. Less production of 5-methyltetrahydrofolate due altered MTHFR function may lead to accumulation of non-metabolized Hcy. The level of dietary intakes of methionine, folate, and B12 also influences on the physiological levels of Hcy.    Outcome effects: Hyperhomocysteinemia affects DNA methylation and associated with hypo-methylation due to changes in S-adenosylmethionine/S-adenosylhomocysteine methylation ratio. Changing DNA methylation pattern is the documented process in aging. Accumulation of non-metabolized Hcy hurts the cell in different ways and may cause the cell senescence.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. For references see \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Aging\" pathway."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Hyperhomocysteinemia is elevated plasma level of homocysteine (Hcy) which is synthesized during methionine metabolism. Hyperhomocysteinemia is associated with increased risk of atherosclerosis and arterial and venous thrombosis and other diseases associated with aging. Hyperhomocysteinemia may be caused by mutation in the MTHFR gene, defects in metabolism of vitamin B12, folate, choline or pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.    Signaling description: Transport of folate into the cell initiates the folate cycle in which MTHFR converts folate to its active form, 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. Human Mthfr C677T genotype is associated with mild MTHFR deficiency in the condition of high temperature stress and less production of 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. 5-methyltetrahydrofolate is a necessary co-factor in methionine cycle and play role in production of universal methyl donor S-adenosylmethionine. Less production of 5-methyltetrahydrofolate due altered MTHFR function may lead to accumulation of non-metabolized Hcy. The level of dietary intakes of methionine, folate, and B12 also influences on the physiological levels of Hcy.    Outcome effects: Hyperhomocysteinemia affects DNA methylation and associated with hypo-methylation due to changes in S-adenosylmethionine/S-adenosylhomocysteine methylation ratio. Changing DNA methylation pattern is the documented process in aging. Accumulation of non-metabolized Hcy hurts the cell in different ways and may cause the cell senescence.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue. Proteins with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red. For references see \"Proteins with Altered Expression in Aging\" pathway." EXACT []

