xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The complement system is a component of innate immunity that consists of multiple plasma proteins that function either as enzymes or binding proteins.    Signaling description: Most complement proteins are present in plasma in the form of inactive precursors called zymogens. The activation of the complement system initiates a series of enzymatic reactions in which zymogens covalently bind to or fix on the pathogen surface and form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The formation of MAC leads to the lysis of pathogenic cells. There are three categories of complement pathway according to the type of activation including classical pathway, alternative pathway, and lectin pathway. The alternative complement pathway can operate without antibody participation. A variety of activators of this pathway have been described such as certain particulate polysaccharides including bacterial LPS and a number of immunoglobulins. The first step in the alternative pathway is the binding of a thioester bond of C3 to an activator, -OH or -NH2 groups. C3, in the free state, is rapidly inactivated by the serum protein factors CFH and CFI. Bound C3, which is protected from such inactivation, interacts with CFB to form magnesium (Mg2+)-dependent complex C3-CFB. This complex is cleaved by CFD and the resultant cleavage causes the initial alternative complement pathway C3/C5 convertase to form which is confined to the fluid phase. The initial C3/C5 convertase is under positive regulation by CFP, whose function in the alternative pathway is to bind to cell-bound C3 and to stabilize the C3/C5 convertase. At this point, the alternative pathway proceeds in the same manner as the classical pathway and recruits additional complement factors including C6, C7, C8A, and C9 to ultimately form MAC and the lysis of the associated cell.    Outcome effects: The major consequence of activation of the complement system is the uptake and destruction of complement-coated microbes by phagocytes that bear receptors for these complement fragments."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The complement system is a component of innate immunity that consists of multiple plasma proteins that function either as enzymes or binding proteins.    Signaling description: Most complement proteins are present in plasma in the form of inactive precursors called zymogens. The activation of the complement system initiates a series of enzymatic reactions in which zymogens covalently bind to or fix on the pathogen surface and form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The formation of MAC leads to the lysis of pathogenic cells. There are three categories of complement pathway according to the type of activation including classical pathway, alternative pathway, and lectin pathway. The alternative complement pathway can operate without antibody participation. A variety of activators of this pathway have been described such as certain particulate polysaccharides including bacterial LPS and a number of immunoglobulins. The first step in the alternative pathway is the binding of a thioester bond of C3 to an activator, -OH or -NH2 groups. C3, in the free state, is rapidly inactivated by the serum protein factors CFH and CFI. Bound C3, which is protected from such inactivation, interacts with CFB to form magnesium (Mg2+)-dependent complex C3-CFB. This complex is cleaved by CFD and the resultant cleavage causes the initial alternative complement pathway C3/C5 convertase to form which is confined to the fluid phase. The initial C3/C5 convertase is under positive regulation by CFP, whose function in the alternative pathway is to bind to cell-bound C3 and to stabilize the C3/C5 convertase. At this point, the alternative pathway proceeds in the same manner as the classical pathway and recruits additional complement factors including C6, C7, C8A, and C9 to ultimately form MAC and the lysis of the associated cell.    Outcome effects: The major consequence of activation of the complement system is the uptake and destruction of complement-coated microbes by phagocytes that bear receptors for these complement fragments." EXACT []

