xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The complement system is a component of innate immunity that consists of multiple plasma proteins that function either as enzymes or as binding proteins.    Signaling description: Most complement proteins are present in plasma in the form of inactive precursors called zymogens. The activation of the complement system initiates a series of enzymatic reactions in which zymogens covalently bind to or fix on the pathogen surface and form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The formation of MAC leads to the lysis of pathogenic cells. There are three categories of complement pathway according to the type of activation including classical pathway, alternative pathway, and lectin pathway. The lectin-induced complement pathway can activate the complement system in an antibody-independent manner and is activated by the binding of mannose-binding lectin (MBL) to carbohydrates on the surfaces of pathogens. MBL consists of oligomers of several sizes. The C-type lectin domain of MBL is capable of recognizing N-acetylglucosamine or mannose structures on the pathogen surface, resulting in the activation of MBL-associated serine proteases (MASPs). In humans, three types of MASPs (MASP1, MASP2, and MASP3) form complexes with MBL. C3 and C2 are cleaved by activated MASP1, while C4B and C2 are cleaved by MASP2. The cascade is similar to the classical complement cascade following activation.    Outcome effects: The major consequence of complement system activation is the uptake and destruction of complement-coated microbes by phagocytes that bear receptors for these complement fragments. The lectin pathway of complement activation is important not only for killing microorganisms through the interaction of carbohydrates on their surfaces and mannan-binding protein (MBP) or other collectins, which are humoral lectins found in humans and other mammals, but may also significantly contribute to the pathophysiology of cerebral, myocardial and gut infarction, septic shock syndrome, and possibly autoimmune disease."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The complement system is a component of innate immunity that consists of multiple plasma proteins that function either as enzymes or as binding proteins.    Signaling description: Most complement proteins are present in plasma in the form of inactive precursors called zymogens. The activation of the complement system initiates a series of enzymatic reactions in which zymogens covalently bind to or fix on the pathogen surface and form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The formation of MAC leads to the lysis of pathogenic cells. There are three categories of complement pathway according to the type of activation including classical pathway, alternative pathway, and lectin pathway. The lectin-induced complement pathway can activate the complement system in an antibody-independent manner and is activated by the binding of mannose-binding lectin (MBL) to carbohydrates on the surfaces of pathogens. MBL consists of oligomers of several sizes. The C-type lectin domain of MBL is capable of recognizing N-acetylglucosamine or mannose structures on the pathogen surface, resulting in the activation of MBL-associated serine proteases (MASPs). In humans, three types of MASPs (MASP1, MASP2, and MASP3) form complexes with MBL. C3 and C2 are cleaved by activated MASP1, while C4B and C2 are cleaved by MASP2. The cascade is similar to the classical complement cascade following activation.    Outcome effects: The major consequence of complement system activation is the uptake and destruction of complement-coated microbes by phagocytes that bear receptors for these complement fragments. The lectin pathway of complement activation is important not only for killing microorganisms through the interaction of carbohydrates on their surfaces and mannan-binding protein (MBP) or other collectins, which are humoral lectins found in humans and other mammals, but may also significantly contribute to the pathophysiology of cerebral, myocardial and gut infarction, septic shock syndrome, and possibly autoimmune disease." EXACT []

