xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The complement system is a component of innate immunity that consists of multiple plasma proteins that function either as enzymes or as binding proteins.    Signaling description: Most complement proteins are present in plasma in the form of inactive precursors called zymogens. The activation of the complement system initiates a series of enzymatic reactions in which zymogens covalently bind to or fix on the pathogen surface and form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The formation of MAC leads to the lysis of pathogenic cells. There are three categories of complement pathway according to the type of activation including classical pathway, alternative pathway, and lectin pathway. The classical pathway of complement is initiated when an antibody, usually IgM, IgG3, or IgG1, is bound to multiple sites on a cell surface of a pathogen. Free antibodies cannot activate complement in a solution because of the structural requirements of binding to complement component C1q. Activated C1S is capable of binding, cleaving, and activating the serine proteases C4 and C2 which are the subsequent components of the classical pathway. When a C4 molecule interacts with the activated C1S proteases, it is cleaved at a smaller fragment of the alpha chain called C4A and the larger fragment C4B. C2 is also cleaved into C2B and a smaller, inactive fragment of C2A which diffuses away. The C2B fragment associates with a C4B fragment bound to the pathogen and forms C3 convertase which cleaves and activates the complement component C3. C3B binds the C3 convertase to form C5 convertase that cleaves the next component of the cascade which is C5. A single cleavage in the C5 molecule also leads to the formation of two fragments including C5A and C5B.    Outcome effects: The major consequence of activation of the complement system is the uptake and destruction of complement-coated microbes by phagocytes that bear receptors for these complement fragments.The function of C5B is to initiate the formation of MAC, which can make holes in the membranes of bacterial pathogens and eukaryotic cells. The end form of MAC consists of the tetramolecular C5B-C8 complex (approximately 550kD) and a tubular poly-C9 (approximately 1100kD). Once inserted into the cell membranes, it creates complete transmembrane channels leading to osmotic lysis of the cell."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The complement system is a component of innate immunity that consists of multiple plasma proteins that function either as enzymes or as binding proteins.    Signaling description: Most complement proteins are present in plasma in the form of inactive precursors called zymogens. The activation of the complement system initiates a series of enzymatic reactions in which zymogens covalently bind to or fix on the pathogen surface and form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The formation of MAC leads to the lysis of pathogenic cells. There are three categories of complement pathway according to the type of activation including classical pathway, alternative pathway, and lectin pathway. The classical pathway of complement is initiated when an antibody, usually IgM, IgG3, or IgG1, is bound to multiple sites on a cell surface of a pathogen. Free antibodies cannot activate complement in a solution because of the structural requirements of binding to complement component C1q. Activated C1S is capable of binding, cleaving, and activating the serine proteases C4 and C2 which are the subsequent components of the classical pathway. When a C4 molecule interacts with the activated C1S proteases, it is cleaved at a smaller fragment of the alpha chain called C4A and the larger fragment C4B. C2 is also cleaved into C2B and a smaller, inactive fragment of C2A which diffuses away. The C2B fragment associates with a C4B fragment bound to the pathogen and forms C3 convertase which cleaves and activates the complement component C3. C3B binds the C3 convertase to form C5 convertase that cleaves the next component of the cascade which is C5. A single cleavage in the C5 molecule also leads to the formation of two fragments including C5A and C5B.    Outcome effects: The major consequence of activation of the complement system is the uptake and destruction of complement-coated microbes by phagocytes that bear receptors for these complement fragments.The function of C5B is to initiate the formation of MAC, which can make holes in the membranes of bacterial pathogens and eukaryotic cells. The end form of MAC consists of the tetramolecular C5B-C8 complex (approximately 550kD) and a tubular poly-C9 (approximately 1100kD). Once inserted into the cell membranes, it creates complete transmembrane channels leading to osmotic lysis of the cell." EXACT []

