xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Sirtuins are proteins with deacetylase and/or ADP-ribosyltransferase activities. SIRT6 is the deacetylase which is important for chromatin structure maintenance and genomic stability.    Signaling description: SIRT6 requires NAD+ for its function. NAD+ is a classic co-enzyme that is synthesized from three major precursors - tryptophan, nicotinic acid, and nicotinamide. The level of NAD+ changes during oxidative stress or inflammation and probably with aging. SIRT6 is a NAD+-dependent histone deacetylase which has many highly site-specific targets in the nucleus. SIRT6 has specificity for deacetylating lysine 9 of histone H3 (H3K9Ac) and lysine 56 of histone H3 (H3K56).    Outcome effects: NAD+ dependency of sirtuins can provide the link between metabolic activity and genome stability during aging. SIRT6 required for efficient DNA double-strand break repair. SIRT6 also regulates telomere-independent cell aging by inhibiting NF-kB signaling.    Animal data: Deficiency of SIRT6 leads to shortened lifespan and an aging-like phenotype in mice."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Sirtuins are proteins with deacetylase and/or ADP-ribosyltransferase activities. SIRT6 is the deacetylase which is important for chromatin structure maintenance and genomic stability.    Signaling description: SIRT6 requires NAD+ for its function. NAD+ is a classic co-enzyme that is synthesized from three major precursors - tryptophan, nicotinic acid, and nicotinamide. The level of NAD+ changes during oxidative stress or inflammation and probably with aging. SIRT6 is a NAD+-dependent histone deacetylase which has many highly site-specific targets in the nucleus. SIRT6 has specificity for deacetylating lysine 9 of histone H3 (H3K9Ac) and lysine 56 of histone H3 (H3K56).    Outcome effects: NAD+ dependency of sirtuins can provide the link between metabolic activity and genome stability during aging. SIRT6 required for efficient DNA double-strand break repair. SIRT6 also regulates telomere-independent cell aging by inhibiting NF-kB signaling.    Animal data: Deficiency of SIRT6 leads to shortened lifespan and an aging-like phenotype in mice." EXACT []

