xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Sirtuins are proteins with deacetylase and/or ADP-ribosyltransferase activities. SIRT7 is located in nucleolus of cells and regulates genes transcription.    Signaling description: SIRT7 is an NAD+-dependent protein deacetylase which regulates RNA polymerase I transcription and pre-rRNA processing. NAD+ is a classic co-enzyme that is synthesized from three major precursors - tryptophan, nicotinic acid, and nicotinamide. The level of NAD+ changes during oxidative stress or inflammation and probably with aging. SIRT7 interacts with numerous proteins involved in transcriptional regulation and RNA metabolism. SIRT7 also plays a key role in maintenance of the epigenome by deacetylation of histone 3 (H3K18). SIRT7 participation was shown in control of ribosome biogenesis, mitochondrial gene expression, and hepatic lipid metabolism.    Outcome effects: NAD+ dependency of SIRT7 can provide the link between metabolic activity and gene transcription and genome stability during aging.    Animal data: Loss of Sirt7 in mice causes genomic instability and accelerated aging and induces cardiac hypertrophy."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Sirtuins are proteins with deacetylase and/or ADP-ribosyltransferase activities. SIRT7 is located in nucleolus of cells and regulates genes transcription.    Signaling description: SIRT7 is an NAD+-dependent protein deacetylase which regulates RNA polymerase I transcription and pre-rRNA processing. NAD+ is a classic co-enzyme that is synthesized from three major precursors - tryptophan, nicotinic acid, and nicotinamide. The level of NAD+ changes during oxidative stress or inflammation and probably with aging. SIRT7 interacts with numerous proteins involved in transcriptional regulation and RNA metabolism. SIRT7 also plays a key role in maintenance of the epigenome by deacetylation of histone 3 (H3K18). SIRT7 participation was shown in control of ribosome biogenesis, mitochondrial gene expression, and hepatic lipid metabolism.    Outcome effects: NAD+ dependency of SIRT7 can provide the link between metabolic activity and gene transcription and genome stability during aging.    Animal data: Loss of Sirt7 in mice causes genomic instability and accelerated aging and induces cardiac hypertrophy." EXACT []

