xref: PS:Source "Biological Process"
synonym: "Source: Biological Process" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Telomeres are repetitive nucleotide sequences located at the end of chromosomes. Telomeres become shorter with aging of the cell because of the specialties in DNA replication at chromosomal ends during the each replication cycle.     Signaling description: Telomerase complex is the main mechanism of telomeres length restoration and capable to add TTAGGG repeats to the 3' end of chromosomes. hTERT (the part of telomerase complex) is generally repressed in normal non- pluripotent cells. Shelterin complex and telomere capping protects telomeres against double-strand break (DSB) repair by homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous end joining (NHEJ). When telomeres ends become too short and loss their capping they are processed by enzymes of DNA repair mechanism. With continued cell divisions telomere loss became accumulating and not recovering due to the limited capacity of \"recovering mechanisms\". At this point, defective telomeres will trigger DNA damage signals such as TP53. The telomere binding protein TRF2 binds to ATM kinase and can inhibit its function and DNA repair.    Outcome effects: Activated by dysfunctional telomeres TP53 leads to upregulation of functional DNA damage responses. Shorter telomeres during cell division cause genome instability via chromosome fusions, chromosome breaks, etc.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Telomeres are repetitive nucleotide sequences located at the end of chromosomes. Telomeres become shorter with aging of the cell because of the specialties in DNA replication at chromosomal ends during the each replication cycle.     Signaling description: Telomerase complex is the main mechanism of telomeres length restoration and capable to add TTAGGG repeats to the 3' end of chromosomes. hTERT (the part of telomerase complex) is generally repressed in normal non- pluripotent cells. Shelterin complex and telomere capping protects telomeres against double-strand break (DSB) repair by homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous end joining (NHEJ). When telomeres ends become too short and loss their capping they are processed by enzymes of DNA repair mechanism. With continued cell divisions telomere loss became accumulating and not recovering due to the limited capacity of \"recovering mechanisms\". At this point, defective telomeres will trigger DNA damage signals such as TP53. The telomere binding protein TRF2 binds to ATM kinase and can inhibit its function and DNA repair.    Outcome effects: Activated by dysfunctional telomeres TP53 leads to upregulation of functional DNA damage responses. Shorter telomeres during cell division cause genome instability via chromosome fusions, chromosome breaks, etc.    Highlighted proteins: Proteins with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue." EXACT []

