xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: Prolactin (PRL) is the protein hormone that enables female mammals to produce milk.    Signaling description: PRL is secreted from the pituitary gland in response to different stimuli including nursing. Sucking by the baby on the nipple promotes PRL release. The sucking activates mechanoreceptors, and these signals are transferred by nerve fibers throughout the spinal cord to the hypothalamus and regulate PRL secretion. The suckling stimulus also triggers the release of oxytocin from the posterior pituitary gland, which triggers milk ejection.There are several factors that inhibit or activate PRL production and release. Dopamine and leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF) have inhibitory effect; thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), oxytocin (OXT), vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) and estrogens have stimulatory effects. Also some local pituitary factors, such as epidermal growth factor (EGF), fibroblast growth factor-2 (FGF2), endothelin-1 (EDN1) and pituitary adenylate cyclase activating peptide (ADCYAP1) stimulate the release of PRL. The classical dopaminergic inhibition of PRL release from the anterior pituitary is mediated through DRD2 receptor, G-proteins and adenylate cyclase (ADCY).    Outcome effects: Action of dopamine induces a rapid change in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration, due o the fact that DRD2 receptor is coupled to voltage-gated calcium channels or via indirect action on GIRK and resulting in a marked decline in intracellular calcium. Estrogens stimulate PRL expression via ESR1. Estrogen effects on lactotroph cell growth are mediated through estrogen-induced stimulatory or inhibitory action of TGFs and other growth factors. Increasing estrogens concentrations reduce the intrapituitary production of TGFB1 and stimulate the synthesis of TGFB3. These two factors have opposite effects on lactotroph cell growth, since TGFB1 inhibits and TGFB3 stimulates the proliferation of these cells. Interestingly, these estradiol-induced changes are dependent on the presence of folliculostellate cell (FS). In particular, in FS cells, estradiol-induced TGFB3 stimulates the production of FGF2, which is a potent stimulator of PRL production and lactotroph cell proliferation. Alternatively, estrogens may affect lactotroph growth and function through altering the dopaminergic control. It is well known that estradiol downregulates hypothalamic DA production and suppresses the activity of DRD2 in lactotrophs by uncoupling the receptors from G proteins. Whereas estrogens predominantly stimulate PRL synthesis and release, this hormone was shown to differently affect lactotroph cell growth by acting either proliferative or apoptotically. Regarding apoptosis, it has been demonstrated that estrogens upregulate the Fas/FasL apoptotic pathway in lactotrophs and induces the intrapituitary production of TNF, which acts apoptotic through the TNF receptor 1 (TNFR1) expressed on lactotrophs. Whether lactotrophs undergo proliferation or apoptosis will then depend on the actual dominance of the different factors during the cycle. PRL not only stimulates lactation but also plays role in many processes and regulates fertility. It was shown that PRL suppresses gonadotropin-releasing hormones (FSH and LH) secretion by pituitary gonadotroph cells thereby suppressing ovulation in women and spermatogenesis in men.    References: 1). Oner G (2013). Prolactin and Infertility, Prolactin, Prof. György M. Nagy (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0943-3, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/55557. Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/prolactin/prolactin-and-infertility 2). Reinhoffer V, Ola?h M, Vecsernye?s M, To?th EB, Nagy GM (2013 The Regulation of Pituitary Prolactin Secretion: Hypothalamic, Intrapituitary and Intracellular Factors and Signaling Mechanisms, Prolactin, Prof. György M. Nagy (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0943-3, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/5557"
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: Prolactin (PRL) is the protein hormone that enables female mammals to produce milk.    Signaling description: PRL is secreted from the pituitary gland in response to different stimuli including nursing. Sucking by the baby on the nipple promotes PRL release. The sucking activates mechanoreceptors, and these signals are transferred by nerve fibers throughout the spinal cord to the hypothalamus and regulate PRL secretion. The suckling stimulus also triggers the release of oxytocin from the posterior pituitary gland, which triggers milk ejection.There are several factors that inhibit or activate PRL production and release. Dopamine and leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF) have inhibitory effect; thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), oxytocin (OXT), vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) and estrogens have stimulatory effects. Also some local pituitary factors, such as epidermal growth factor (EGF), fibroblast growth factor-2 (FGF2), endothelin-1 (EDN1) and pituitary adenylate cyclase activating peptide (ADCYAP1) stimulate the release of PRL. The classical dopaminergic inhibition of PRL release from the anterior pituitary is mediated through DRD2 receptor, G-proteins and adenylate cyclase (ADCY).    Outcome effects: Action of dopamine induces a rapid change in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration, due o the fact that DRD2 receptor is coupled to voltage-gated calcium channels or via indirect action on GIRK and resulting in a marked decline in intracellular calcium. Estrogens stimulate PRL expression via ESR1. Estrogen effects on lactotroph cell growth are mediated through estrogen-induced stimulatory or inhibitory action of TGFs and other growth factors. Increasing estrogens concentrations reduce the intrapituitary production of TGFB1 and stimulate the synthesis of TGFB3. These two factors have opposite effects on lactotroph cell growth, since TGFB1 inhibits and TGFB3 stimulates the proliferation of these cells. Interestingly, these estradiol-induced changes are dependent on the presence of folliculostellate cell (FS). In particular, in FS cells, estradiol-induced TGFB3 stimulates the production of FGF2, which is a potent stimulator of PRL production and lactotroph cell proliferation. Alternatively, estrogens may affect lactotroph growth and function through altering the dopaminergic control. It is well known that estradiol downregulates hypothalamic DA production and suppresses the activity of DRD2 in lactotrophs by uncoupling the receptors from G proteins. Whereas estrogens predominantly stimulate PRL synthesis and release, this hormone was shown to differently affect lactotroph cell growth by acting either proliferative or apoptotically. Regarding apoptosis, it has been demonstrated that estrogens upregulate the Fas/FasL apoptotic pathway in lactotrophs and induces the intrapituitary production of TNF, which acts apoptotic through the TNF receptor 1 (TNFR1) expressed on lactotrophs. Whether lactotrophs undergo proliferation or apoptosis will then depend on the actual dominance of the different factors during the cycle. PRL not only stimulates lactation but also plays role in many processes and regulates fertility. It was shown that PRL suppresses gonadotropin-releasing hormones (FSH and LH) secretion by pituitary gonadotroph cells thereby suppressing ovulation in women and spermatogenesis in men.    References: 1). Oner G (2013). Prolactin and Infertility, Prolactin, Prof. György M. Nagy (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0943-3, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/55557. Available from: http://www.intechopen.com/books/prolactin/prolactin-and-infertility 2). Reinhoffer V, Ola?h M, Vecsernye?s M, To?th EB, Nagy GM (2013 The Regulation of Pituitary Prolactin Secretion: Hypothalamic, Intrapituitary and Intracellular Factors and Signaling Mechanisms, Prolactin, Prof. György M. Nagy (Ed.), ISBN: 978-953-51-0943-3, InTech, DOI: 10.5772/5557" EXACT []

