[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-0fe1dcc3-b2ba-409c-922e-ac31e0dd3baf
name: Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome Overview
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:w ! W
is_a: urn:agi-folder:wiskott-aldrich_syndrome ! Wiskott-aldrich syndrome
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "S. Sozin www.researchgate.net/profile/Sergey-Sozin"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: S. Sozin www.researchgate.net/profile/Sergey-Sozin" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-0fe1dcc3-b2ba-409c-922e-ac31e0dd3baf"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-0fe1dcc3-b2ba-409c-922e-ac31e0dd3baf" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Diseases"
synonym: "Source: Diseases" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Headnote: The WAS protein plays an active role in the formation of the cytoskeleton of blood cells (mainly lymphocyte and thrombocyte). Mutations in the WAS gene mainly lead to the development of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. At the moment, more than 300 mutations have been described, leading to various forms of severity of the disease. Since the gene is located in the Xp11.23 region, there is an X-linked recessive disease (only boys are sick).    Signaling description: The WAS protein is activated by one of the proteins of the Rho GTPase family, CDC42 (cell division cycle 42). CDC42 in blood cells is activated by a currently unknown pathway from various G-protein coupled receptors. Also, WAS is activated by a complex consisting of one of the profilin proteins and PIP2 (phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate). The main target of WAS is the actin related protein 2/3 complex. Activation of this complex by the WAS protein and its partner, the VASP (vasodilator stimulated phosphoprotein) protein, leads to the activation of the interaction between the actin related protein 2/3 complex and F-actin. This, in turn, leads to the activation of cellular processes of cytoskeleton organization and biogenesis, as well as lamellipodium formation and platelet structure reorganization.    Outcome effects: Mutations in the WAS gene lead to a complete disruption of the activation of the cytoskeleton organization and biogenesis of blood cells. This leads to the development of immunodeficiency and thrombocytopenia, as well as other severe symptoms: chronic diarrhea, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and fever, as the main ones.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.     Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style."
synonym: "Notes: Headnote: The WAS protein plays an active role in the formation of the cytoskeleton of blood cells (mainly lymphocyte and thrombocyte). Mutations in the WAS gene mainly lead to the development of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. At the moment, more than 300 mutations have been described, leading to various forms of severity of the disease. Since the gene is located in the Xp11.23 region, there is an X-linked recessive disease (only boys are sick).    Signaling description: The WAS protein is activated by one of the proteins of the Rho GTPase family, CDC42 (cell division cycle 42). CDC42 in blood cells is activated by a currently unknown pathway from various G-protein coupled receptors. Also, WAS is activated by a complex consisting of one of the profilin proteins and PIP2 (phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate). The main target of WAS is the actin related protein 2/3 complex. Activation of this complex by the WAS protein and its partner, the VASP (vasodilator stimulated phosphoprotein) protein, leads to the activation of the interaction between the actin related protein 2/3 complex and F-actin. This, in turn, leads to the activation of cellular processes of cytoskeleton organization and biogenesis, as well as lamellipodium formation and platelet structure reorganization.    Outcome effects: Mutations in the WAS gene lead to a complete disruption of the activation of the cytoskeleton organization and biogenesis of blood cells. This leads to the development of immunodeficiency and thrombocytopenia, as well as other severe symptoms: chronic diarrhea, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and fever, as the main ones.    Highlighted proteins: Entities with increased expression or activity are highlighted in red, and entities with decreased expression or activity are highlighted in blue.     Mutated genes: Mutated genes are shown in white-out style." EXACT []

