[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-c553a849-57f6-4165-b6e6-959a2f42b1b3
name: 1_Inflammation in keratinocytes in the pathology of acne
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:1 ! 1
is_a: urn:agi-folder:acne_vulgaris ! Acne vulgaris
xref: PS:Description "published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1"
synonym: "Description: published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-c553a849-57f6-4165-b6e6-959a2f42b1b3"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-c553a849-57f6-4165-b6e6-959a2f42b1b3" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "monocyte"
xref: PS:CellType "keratinocyte"
synonym: "CellType: monocyte" EXACT []
synonym: "CellType: keratinocyte" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "skin"
synonym: "Organ: skin" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "epidermis"
synonym: "Tissue: epidermis" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "integumentary system"
synonym: "Organ_System: integumentary system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "The characteristic inflammatory reaction in acne vulgaris is caused  by the bacterium P. acnes that activates Toll-like receptor (TLR2,4) signaling  resulting in the synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines such as  interleukin-1 beta (IL-1B) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF).  Proinflammatory cytokines are highly expressed in acne lesions, and  they stimulate the activation of mast cells, neutrophils, monocytes, and  other cells of the immune system. The inflammatory reaction supported  by immune cells causes redness and pain and the formation of purulence  in acne lesion.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: The characteristic inflammatory reaction in acne vulgaris is caused  by the bacterium P. acnes that activates Toll-like receptor (TLR2,4) signaling  resulting in the synthesis of proinflammatory cytokines such as  interleukin-1 beta (IL-1B) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF).  Proinflammatory cytokines are highly expressed in acne lesions, and  they stimulate the activation of mast cells, neutrophils, monocytes, and  other cells of the immune system. The inflammatory reaction supported  by immune cells causes redness and pain and the formation of purulence  in acne lesion.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4fa584f2-0a6f-44fe-9228-6ac7f56558bc
name: 3_Increased proliferation of sebocytes in acne vulgaris
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:3 ! 3
is_a: urn:agi-folder:acne_vulgaris ! Acne vulgaris
xref: PS:Description "published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1"
synonym: "Description: published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4fa584f2-0a6f-44fe-9228-6ac7f56558bc"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-4fa584f2-0a6f-44fe-9228-6ac7f56558bc" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "skin"
synonym: "Organ: skin" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "epidermis"
synonym: "Tissue: epidermis" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "integumentary system"
synonym: "Organ_System: integumentary system" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "sebocyte"
synonym: "CellType: sebocyte" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Hyperseborrhea, or the excessive production of sebum by sebaceous  glands, results from increased fatty acid biosynthesis in sebocytes.  Hyperseborrhea may be induced by various factors including IGF1, cytokines,  and free fatty acids themselves. Milk consumption and growth hormone  (GH1) expression (not shown) can influence the synthesis and secretion  of IGF1 in the liver. Testosterone also increases sebocyte hyperfunction.  Increased fatty acid biosynthesis by sebocytes and increased sebocyte  proliferation lead to comedogenesis and hyperseborrhea: hyperseborrhea  results in inflammation and abnormal keratinocyte function at the site  that can cause the formation of acne lesions.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: Hyperseborrhea, or the excessive production of sebum by sebaceous  glands, results from increased fatty acid biosynthesis in sebocytes.  Hyperseborrhea may be induced by various factors including IGF1, cytokines,  and free fatty acids themselves. Milk consumption and growth hormone  (GH1) expression (not shown) can influence the synthesis and secretion  of IGF1 in the liver. Testosterone also increases sebocyte hyperfunction.  Increased fatty acid biosynthesis by sebocytes and increased sebocyte  proliferation lead to comedogenesis and hyperseborrhea: hyperseborrhea  results in inflammation and abnormal keratinocyte function at the site  that can cause the formation of acne lesions.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-be37b20e-fdbf-4dfa-9180-78eb02cb6f9f
name: 2_Hyperseborrhea in acne vulgaris
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:2 ! 2
is_a: urn:agi-folder:acne_vulgaris ! Acne vulgaris
xref: PS:Description "published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1"
synonym: "Description: published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-be37b20e-fdbf-4dfa-9180-78eb02cb6f9f"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-be37b20e-fdbf-4dfa-9180-78eb02cb6f9f" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "skin"
synonym: "Organ: skin" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "epidermis"
synonym: "Tissue: epidermis" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "integumentary system"
synonym: "Organ_System: integumentary system" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "sebocyte"
synonym: "CellType: sebocyte" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Various factors stimulate sebocyte proliferation and provoke enlargement  of the sebaceous gland. Among them are proinflammatory proteins,  milk consumption, and hormones. Another inducer of this process is reactive  oxygen species (ROS). P. acnes also contributes to this process by  producing free fatty acids and activating the nuclear transcription factors  PPARA and PPARG.  The amplified proliferation of sebocytes causes growth of the sebaceous  glands and is one of the main reasons for the formation of lesions  in acne vulgaris.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: Various factors stimulate sebocyte proliferation and provoke enlargement  of the sebaceous gland. Among them are proinflammatory proteins,  milk consumption, and hormones. Another inducer of this process is reactive  oxygen species (ROS). P. acnes also contributes to this process by  producing free fatty acids and activating the nuclear transcription factors  PPARA and PPARG.  The amplified proliferation of sebocytes causes growth of the sebaceous  glands and is one of the main reasons for the formation of lesions  in acne vulgaris.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

