xref: PS:Notes "All three of the complement pathways (classical, alternative, and lectin-mediated) are involved  in the pathogenesis of ARMD.  Complement proteins and other molecules take part in drusen formation.  Drusen are small yellow or white accumulations of extracellular material,  proteins and lipids, in the macula (a part of the retina) between  the Bruch’s membrane and the retinal pigment epithelium of the eye. The  presence of a few small drusen is normal with advancing age. However,  the buildup of larger and more numerous drusen in the macula is a hallmark  of ARMD. Also, complement activation may induce retinal pigment  epithelial (RPE) cell death, and neutrophil and macrophage activation.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: All three of the complement pathways (classical, alternative, and lectin-mediated) are involved  in the pathogenesis of ARMD.  Complement proteins and other molecules take part in drusen formation.  Drusen are small yellow or white accumulations of extracellular material,  proteins and lipids, in the macula (a part of the retina) between  the Bruch’s membrane and the retinal pigment epithelium of the eye. The  presence of a few small drusen is normal with advancing age. However,  the buildup of larger and more numerous drusen in the macula is a hallmark  of ARMD. Also, complement activation may induce retinal pigment  epithelial (RPE) cell death, and neutrophil and macrophage activation.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

