synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-20de55bd-ab2d-4b69-bc79-8f607dee5411" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "lung"
synonym: "Organ: lung" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:CellType "epithelial cell"
synonym: "CellType: epithelial cell" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "respiratory system"
synonym: "Organ_System: respiratory system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Alveolar epithelial cells exposed to cigarette smoke often undergo  apoptosis, develop impaired DNA repair, undergo autophagy or express  the ER-stress response, and produce inflammatory mediators and proteases.  Altogether, cigarette smoke provokes destruction of the alveolar septum  and promotes the development of pulmonary emphysema.  Cigarette smoke can activate epithelial cells to secrete a variety of inflammatory  mediators and proteases. Inflammatory mediators are responsible  for macrophage, neutrophil, and T-cell recruitment. Transforming  growth factor beta 1 (TGFB1) plays a significant role in the induction of  pulmonary fibrosis. Proteases are responsible for extracellular matrix  degradation that contributes to alveolar septal destruction.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: Alveolar epithelial cells exposed to cigarette smoke often undergo  apoptosis, develop impaired DNA repair, undergo autophagy or express  the ER-stress response, and produce inflammatory mediators and proteases.  Altogether, cigarette smoke provokes destruction of the alveolar septum  and promotes the development of pulmonary emphysema.  Cigarette smoke can activate epithelial cells to secrete a variety of inflammatory  mediators and proteases. Inflammatory mediators are responsible  for macrophage, neutrophil, and T-cell recruitment. Transforming  growth factor beta 1 (TGFB1) plays a significant role in the induction of  pulmonary fibrosis. Proteases are responsible for extracellular matrix  degradation that contributes to alveolar septal destruction.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

