name: 2_Lipotoxicity in NAFLD
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:non-alcoholic_fatty_liver_disease ! Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease
is_a: urn:agi-folder:nonalcoholic_fatty_liver_disease ! Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease
is_a: urn:agi-folder:2 ! 2
xref: PS:Description "published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1"
synonym: "Description: published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-26c87205-a805-42c8-a10c-0200c722e60f"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-26c87205-a805-42c8-a10c-0200c722e60f" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Excess FFAs can be toxic for hepatocytes. Hepatocytes take up FFAs  and usually process and store them as triglycerides (TGs). But when hepatic  FFAs exceed the accumulation quota, they activate hepatocyte apoptosis  and inflammation.  Hepatocyte apoptosis and inflammation are crucial mechanisms that  promote progression of steatosis in steatohepatitis. Dead hepatocytes are  consumed by macrophages leading to the release of proinflammatory signals.  These stimuli along with proinflammatory cytokines produced by  hepatocytes activate the hepatic stellate cells involved in liver fibrosis.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: Excess FFAs can be toxic for hepatocytes. Hepatocytes take up FFAs  and usually process and store them as triglycerides (TGs). But when hepatic  FFAs exceed the accumulation quota, they activate hepatocyte apoptosis  and inflammation.  Hepatocyte apoptosis and inflammation are crucial mechanisms that  promote progression of steatosis in steatohepatitis. Dead hepatocytes are  consumed by macrophages leading to the release of proinflammatory signals.  These stimuli along with proinflammatory cytokines produced by  hepatocytes activate the hepatic stellate cells involved in liver fibrosis.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

