synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-3b2de982-5704-4d04-bbec-12a6015668a9"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-3b2de982-5704-4d04-bbec-12a6015668a9" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Class III and class IV mutations cause reductions in transmembrane Cl-  conductance. Class III mutations, in a manner similar to class II mutations,  usually lead to a classic CF phenotype with pancreatic insufficiency.  One of the major functions of CFTR is to regulate the movement of  chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial cell membranes,  such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs.  Class III CFTR mutations disturb the protein’s chloride-transporting  function. Mutated CFTR does not respond to cAMP stimulation even  though it is located in the cell membrane.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: Class III and class IV mutations cause reductions in transmembrane Cl-  conductance. Class III mutations, in a manner similar to class II mutations,  usually lead to a classic CF phenotype with pancreatic insufficiency.  One of the major functions of CFTR is to regulate the movement of  chloride (Cl-) and sodium (Na+) ions across epithelial cell membranes,  such as the alveolar epithelia located in the lungs.  Class III CFTR mutations disturb the protein’s chloride-transporting  function. Mutated CFTR does not respond to cAMP stimulation even  though it is located in the cell membrane.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

