synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ "brain"
xref: PS:Organ "spinal cord"
synonym: "Organ: brain" EXACT []
synonym: "Organ: spinal cord" EXACT []
xref: PS:Tissue "nerve tissue"
synonym: "Tissue: nerve tissue" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organ_System "nervous system"
synonym: "Organ_System: nervous system" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Organelle "synapse"
synonym: "Organelle: synapse" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "The precise mechanism of ALS pathogenesis is still unknown. One hypothesis  is that motor neurons can be damaged by glutamate overload.   In lower motor neurons, glutamate excitotoxicity raises intracellular  Ca2+ levels, which in turn facilitates nitric oxide (NO) formation.  Subsequently, peroxynitrite synthesis triggers damage to the lower motor  neurons. The excessive influx of Ca2+ into mitochondria results in  mitochondrial dysfunction and subsequent motor neuron apoptosis.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: The precise mechanism of ALS pathogenesis is still unknown. One hypothesis  is that motor neurons can be damaged by glutamate overload.   In lower motor neurons, glutamate excitotoxicity raises intracellular  Ca2+ levels, which in turn facilitates nitric oxide (NO) formation.  Subsequently, peroxynitrite synthesis triggers damage to the lower motor  neurons. The excessive influx of Ca2+ into mitochondria results in  mitochondrial dysfunction and subsequent motor neuron apoptosis.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

[Term]
id: urn:agi-pathway:uuid-085de9db-2a8b-4ecb-bd32-5defe59ee076
name: 2_Mutations in SOD1 and associated proteins cause motor neuron death in familial ALS
xref: PS:NodeType "Pathway"
synonym: "NodeType: Pathway" EXACT []
is_a: urn:agi-folder:2 ! 2
is_a: urn:agi-folder:sclerosis,_amyotrophic_lateral ! Sclerosis, amyotrophic lateral
xref: PS:Description "published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1"
synonym: "Description: published in: Disease Pathways: An Atlas of Human Disease Pathways, 2019 https://www.elsevier.com/books/disease-pathways/nesterova/978-0-12-817086-1" EXACT []
xref: PS:Pathway_Author "A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101"
synonym: "Pathway_Author: A. Nesterova ORCID:0000-0002-9448-8101" EXACT []
xref: PS:Link "https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-085de9db-2a8b-4ecb-bd32-5defe59ee076"
synonym: "Link: https://mammal-profservices.pathwaystudio.com/app/sd?urn=urn:agi-pathway:uuid-085de9db-2a8b-4ecb-bd32-5defe59ee076" EXACT []
xref: PS:PathwayType "signaling"
synonym: "PathwayType: signaling" EXACT []
xref: PS:Source "Atlas"
synonym: "Source: Atlas" EXACT []
xref: PS:Notes "Mutations in the proteins, which regulate mitochondrial function, endocytosis  and the degradation of misfolded proteins, or RNA metabolism,  can lead to the accumulation of toxic protein aggregates in neurons, which  initiate neuron apoptosis.   The accumulation of misfolded proteins in neurons triggers mitochondrial  dysfunction, which includes the overloading of endoplasmic  reticulum–associated degradation of proteins (ERAD), the unfolded protein  response (UPR), and the impairment of autophagy. The mitochondrial  dysfunction prominent in ALS neurons initiates apoptosis. All this  leads to the further accumulation of insoluble protein aggregates, a reduction  of transcription, and diminished DNA repair leading to neuron death.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey."
synonym: "Notes: Mutations in the proteins, which regulate mitochondrial function, endocytosis  and the degradation of misfolded proteins, or RNA metabolism,  can lead to the accumulation of toxic protein aggregates in neurons, which  initiate neuron apoptosis.   The accumulation of misfolded proteins in neurons triggers mitochondrial  dysfunction, which includes the overloading of endoplasmic  reticulum–associated degradation of proteins (ERAD), the unfolded protein  response (UPR), and the impairment of autophagy. The mitochondrial  dysfunction prominent in ALS neurons initiates apoptosis. All this  leads to the further accumulation of insoluble protein aggregates, a reduction  of transcription, and diminished DNA repair leading to neuron death.    Entitiy with increased expression or activity is highlighted in red. Entity with low activity is highlighted in blue. Mutated genes are shown in white-out style. Viral proteins are grey." EXACT []

